(1928,'biology',11,291,559,'In Sea urchin DNA,which is double stranded $17\\% $ of the bases were shown to be cytosine. The percentages of the other three bases expected to be present in this DNA are','[{"text": "$G\\\\,{\\\\rm{8}}.{\\\\rm{5}}\\\\% ,A\\\\,{\\\\rm{50}}\\\\% ,T\\\\,{\\\\rm{24}}.{\\\\rm{5}}\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$G\\\\,{\\\\rm{34}}\\\\% ,A\\\\,{\\\\rm{24}}.{\\\\rm{5}}\\\\% ,T\\\\,{\\\\rm{24}}.{\\\\rm{5}}\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$G\\\\,17\\\\% ,A\\\\,{\\\\rm{16}}\\\\% ,T\\\\,32.5\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$G\\\\,17\\\\% ,A\\\\,33\\\\% ,T\\\\,33\\\\% $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to Chargaff’s rule, for the doublestranded DNA, the ratios Adenine:Thymine and Cytosine: Guanine are constant and always equal to 1.<br>$A = T$ and $G = C$<br>A is $17\\% $, hence, $T = 17\\%  = 1$\n<br>$A + T + G + C = 100$<br>$A + T + 17 + 17 = 100$<br>$A + T = 100 - 34$<br>$A + T = 66$<br>Since, $A = T$,<br>$2A = 66$<br>$A = 66{\\rm{/}}2 = 33\\% $<br>$T = 33\\% $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
