(4025,'biology',77,412,822,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Sister chromatids occurs in ${G_1}$ and ${G_2}$ phase of cell cycle.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enzyme unwindase seperates chromatid segments','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Sister chromatids appear in S and ${G_2}$ phase after DNA replication is complete and DNA content is doubled. The separation of chromatid segments is regulated by ubiquitin-mediated proteolysis via three protein complexes &ndash; E1, E2 and E3. E3 is also called the anaphasepromoting complex.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(4026,'biology',77,413,826,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The golgi complex remains intact during mitosis<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Mitochondria, chloroplasts or centrioles fuse at cytokinesis','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Golgi complex disintegrates and disappears during mitosis. Cell organelles like mitochondria and chloroplasts or centrioles are distributed during cytokinesis. An exception is the Golgi apparatus, which becomes fragmented and thus difficult to identify during mitosis.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(4027,'biology',74,247,489,'Select the correct answer<br>I. Chemosynthetic autotrophs bacteria oxidise various inorganic substances such as nitrates, nitrites and ammonia and use the released energy for their ATP production.<br>II. The organisms forming blooms in polluted water bodies are nutritionally Photosynthetic autotrophs.','[{"text": "I is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'BGA forms algal bloom, so both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(4028,'biology',75,719,1518,'A flower is called trimerous due to the presence of','[{"text": "Three whorls of floral leaves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three whorls of essential floral leaves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three members in each of the two whorls of perianth in a flower", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Flower can be cut into three equal halves", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A flower is called trimerous due to the presence of three members in each of the two whorls of perianth in a flower.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(4029,'biology',76,853,1897,'Match the columns I and II.<br><div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Dicot stem<br>B. Monocot stem</div><div class="column-right">P. Scattered vascular bundles<br>Q. Sclerenchymatous hypodermis<br>R. Collenchymatous hypodermis<br>S. Ring arrangement of vascular bundles<br>T. Phloem parenchyma absent</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{R}, ; \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{T}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{S}, ; \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{T}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{T} ; \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{S} ; \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{T}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Dicot stem-Collenchymatous hypodermis, Ring arrangement of vascular bundlesMonocot stem - Scattered vascular bundles, Sclerenchymatous hypodermis, Phloem parenchyma absent.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(4030,'biology',77,418,834,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Meiosis II is similar to mitosis.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Meiosis I cannot occur in haploid.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Meiosis consists of two divisions, meiosis I and meiosis II. The first division of meiosis is called heterotypic or reduction division. During this division the number of chromosomes is reduced to half. Hence, meiosis I can occur in diploid cells only. The chromatids are separated in the second division of meiosis, the second meiotic division is known as homotypic or equational division because the chromosome number remains the same as produced after the first division. Therefore, meiosis II is similar to mitosis.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(4031,'biology',74,247,489,'Chemosynthetic bacteria obtain energy from','[{"text": "Sunlight", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Other organsims", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inorganic substances", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Chemosynthetic bacteria obtained energy from inorganic substances.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
