(8626,'biology',83,500,1016,'Read the following five features (i - v)<br>i. Notochord is present in any stage of life cycle<br>ii. Central nerve cord is present at midventral surface which is solid and double in animals<br>iii. Gills slits are present in any stage of life cycle<br>iv. A "post anal tail" is present in animals<br>v. Dorsal heart is present in body of animals. From above features which are not suitable features for chordate animal?','[{"text": "ii, iii, and v", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii and v", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "iii and iv", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only ii", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In chordata, single and hollow nerve cord is present on the dorsal side, heart is on the ventral side',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8627,'biology',86,981,2286,'State the volume of air remaining in the lungs after a normal breathing.','[{"text": "2100 to 2300 ml", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1000 to 2000 ml", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4000 to 5000 ml", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5000 to 6000 ml", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Functional Residual Capacity (FRC): The volume of air which remains in the lungs after a normal expiration is called Functional Residual Capacity.<br>${\\rm{FRC = ERV + RV}}$.<br>$$\\mathrm{ERV}=1000$$ to $$1100 \\mathrm{ml}$$<br>${\\rm{RV = 1100}}\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,{\\rm{1200ml}}$<br>So, ${\\rm{FRC = 2100}}\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,{\\rm{2300ml}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8630,'biology',83,506,1024,'Read the following features\ni. The forelimbs are modified into wings\nii. Endoskeleton is fully ossified and the long bones are hollow with air cavities\niii. Respiration is by lungs along with air sacs\niv. Digestive tract has additional chambers i.e. crop and gizzard.\nOut of these which is notsuitable features according to flying adaptation in birds?','[{"text": "Only iv", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only iii", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only ii", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only i", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'iv option is true but it is not an adaption suitable for flying.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8631,'biology',86,981,2286,'A person breathes in some volume of air by forced inspiration after having a forced expiration. This quantity of air taken in is','[{"text": "Total lung capacity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tidal volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vital capacity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inspiratory capacity", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Vital capacity is the maximum volume of air a person can breathe in after a forced expiration. This includes ERV, TV and IRV or the maximum volume of air a person can breathe out after a forced inspiration.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8634,'biology',83,502,1020,'Read the following statements<br>I. Heart is three chambered, with two auricles and one ventricle<br>II. Ventral mouth with backwardly directed teeth<br>III. Presence of swim bladder<br>IV. In males, pelvic fins bear claspers<br>V. Absence of gill covers<br>Select the option which includes correct statement w.r.t cartilaginous fish.','[{"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, IV and V", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II, III, IV and V", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, IV and V", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Cartilaginous fishes bear placoid scales and the mouth is ventral. Teeth are modified placoid scales which are backwardly directed. In males, pelvic fins bear claspers. Operculum is absent. Air bladder is absent.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8635,'biology',86,981,2286,'The maximum quantity of air one can expire after maximal inspiration is known as','[{"text": "Residual air", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vital capacity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Tidal volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Total lung capacity", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Vital capacity (VC): This is the maximum amount of air that can be expired forcefully from his lungs after first filling these with a maximum deep inspiration. It is about 4600 ${\\rm{ml}}$.<br>${\\rm{VC = IRV + TV + ERV}}$<BR> ${\\rm{ = 3000 + 500 + 1100 = 4600ml}}{\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8638,'biology',83,498,1014,'Find the incorrect statement.','[{"text": "Platyhelminthes is the only triploblastic acoelomate phylum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All members of animalia are multicellular", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ctenophores are exclusively marine and called comb jellies", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Echinoderms are dioecious with internal fertilization", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In Echinoderms, fertilisation is external.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8639,'biology',86,981,2286,'After forceful inspiration, the amount of air that can be breathed out by maximum forced expiration is equal to','[{"text": "ERV + RV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "TV + ERV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IRV + ERV + TV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "TV + RV + ERV", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Vital Capacity (VC) includes ERV, TV and IRV. It can also be defined as the maximum volume of air a person can breathe out after a forced inspiration.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8642,'biology',83,497,1013,'Mark the incorrect combination of organisation and its characteristic feature.','[{"text": "<i>Neopilina</i> - Segmentation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Pila</i> - Torsion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Nautilus</i> - External shell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Earthworm - Radula", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Radula, the rasping organ, is absent in Unio.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8643,'biology',86,981,2286,'Vital capacity refers to','[{"text": "Maximum volume of air a person can breathe in after a forced expiration.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Maximum volume of air a person can breathe out after a forced inspiration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ERV + TV + IRV}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Vital Capacity (VC) is the maximum volume of air a person can breathe in after a forced expiration. This includes ERV, TV and IRV or the maximum volume of air a person can breathe out after a forced inspiration.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8646,'biology',83,501,1019,'Which of the following features is not shared by starfish and hagfish?','[{"text": "Triploblastic body", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "True coelum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Organ system level", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pharyngeal gill-slits", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Hagfish is a chordate (Cyclostomata). Starfish is a nonchordate (Echinodermata). Pharyngeal gillslits are present only in chordates.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8647,'biology',86,981,2286,'Total volume of air accommodated in the lungs at the end of a forced inspiration is called','[{"text": "RV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ERV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "TLC", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "VC", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Total volume of air accommodated in the lunges at the end of a forced inspiration is called TLC.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8650,'biology',83,507,1025,'<em>Ornithorynchus</em> differs from <em>Macropus</em> and <em>Pteropus</em> by the','[{"text": "Absence of mammary glands", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oviparous mode of reproduction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Presence of pinna", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Absence of lungs", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Platypus (Prototheria) is oviparous. <em>Macropus</em> (Metatheria) and <em>Pteropus</em> (Eutheria) are viviparous.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8651,'biology',86,981,2286,'Which of the following is incorrect w.r.t total lung capacity?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{IRV + TV + ERV + RV}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{IC + FRC}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{VC + RV}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{EC + FRC}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Total volume of air accommodated in the lungs at the end of a forced inspiration. This includes RV, ERV, TV and IRV or vital capacity + residual volume.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8654,'biology',83,496,1012,'Which of the following statements are true?<br>I. Open circulatory system is found in most arthropods.<br>II. Arthropods are true coelomates.<br>III. Platyhelminthes are coelomates<br>IV. Ctenophores have asymmetry<br>Choose the correct option','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Ctenophores have radial symmetry. Platyhelminthes are acoelomates. Most arthropods have a closed circulatory system.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8655,'biology',86,981,2286,'Arrange the following in order of increasing volume<br>a. Tidal volume<br>b. Residual volume<br>c. Expiratory reserve volume<br>d. Vital capacity','[{"text": "a $$ < $$ d $$ < $$ c $$ < $$ b", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a $$ < $$ b $$ < $$ c $$ < $$ d", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a $$ < $$ d $$ < $$ b $$ < $$ c", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a $$ < $$ c $$ < $$ b $$ < $$ d", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Respiratory Volumes and Capacities in order to their increasing volumes is as follow :<br>Tidal volume $$(500 \\mathrm{~mL}) < $$ expiratory reserve volume $$(1000-1100 \\mathrm{~mL}) < $$ residual volume < $$(1100-1200 \\mathrm{~mL})$$ < vital capacity (4600mL).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8658,'biology',83,497,1013,'Consider the following statements<br>I. Molluscs possess cellular level of organisation<br>II. Arthropods contain haemolymph which directly bathes the internal tissues and organs','[{"text": "I is true, but II is false", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I is flase, but II is true", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both I and II are true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and II are false", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Molluscs possess organ system level of organisation.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8659,'biology',86,981,2286,'Terms based on their volumes in ascending order is<br>i. Tidal volume<br>ii. Residual volume<br>iii. Inspiratory reserve volume<br>iv. Expiratory capacity','[{"text": "i, ii, iv, iii", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "i, ii, iii, iv", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii, iv, i, iii", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iii, iv, ii, i", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Tidal volume is volume of air that is inspired or expired during normal respiration. It is around 6000 to $$8000 \\mathrm{ml}$$ per minute. Residual volume is the volume of air remaining in lungs even after forceful expiration. Its volume is $$1100-1200 \\mathrm{ml}$$. Inspiratory reserve volume is the additional volume of air a person can inspire by forcible expiration, it is around $$2500-3000 \\mathrm{ml}$$. Expiratory capacity is total volume of air an individual can expire after normal inspiration.<br>It is about $$8000-9000 \\mathrm{ml}$$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8662,'biology',83,494,1010,'Read the given statements.<br>I. Parapodia are respiratory organs found in arthropods.<br>II. In roundworm true coelom is present<br>III. Coelenterates show polymorphism.<br>iv. In Pleurobrachia locomotion takes place by comb plates.<br>Of the above statements','[{"text": "Only I and II are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only I and IV are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II and III are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only  IIIand IV are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Parapodia are locomotory structures found in certain members of Phylum Annelida. Round worm belongs to the Phylum Aschelminthes, they have pseudocoelom. In Coelenterates more than 1 type of individual occurs in a colony and they perform different function, exhibiting division of labour. This is called Polymorphism. Pleurobrachia is member of Phylum Ctenophora. Comb plates help in the locomotion in members of phylum ctenophora.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8663,'biology',86,981,2286,'Which is the correct matching of the respiratory volume and the specific term?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "2500-3000ml-ERV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5000-6000-VC", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3000-3500-IC", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1500-2000-RV", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Total volume of air a person can inspire after a normal expiration is called as Inspiratory Capacity (IC). This includes tidal volume and inspiratory reserve volume ${\\rm{( TV + IRV)}}$.<br>${\\rm{TV = 500\\,mL}}$<br>${\\rm{IRV = 2500}}\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,{\\rm{3000\\,mL}}$<br>${\\rm{TV + IRV = 3000}}$ to ${\\rm{3500\\,mL}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8666,'biology',83,491,1007,'Consider the following four statements (I-IV) forcoelentrates and give the answer of question asked below.<br>I. Polyp produces medusa asexually<br>II. Medusa produces polyp sexually<br>III. Polyp are free swimming and cylindricalin shape<br>IV. Medusa are umbrella shaped and sessile How many option are correct?','[{"text": "III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Coelenterates show metagenesis (Alternation of generation) where asexual polyp phase alternates with sexual medusa phase. Polyp is a sessile form, polyp undergoes asexual reproduction. Medusa undergoes sexual reproduction. Medusa is free living and umbrella shaped.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9681,'biology',84,579,1159,'Which of the following lacks cartilage?','[{"text": "Larynx", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Terminal bronchiole", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Trachea", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tertiary bronchus", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The tracheae, primary, secondary and tertiary bronchi, and initial bronchioles are supported by incomplete cartilaginous rings.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8667,'biology',86,981,2286,'A person has vital capacity of $$5 \\mathrm{~L}$$ and residual volume of $$1.2 \\mathrm{~L}$$. Total lung capacity','[{"text": "$$4.8 \\\\mathrm{~L}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$6.2 \\\\mathrm{~L}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$500 \\\\mathrm{ml}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'vital capacity of $$5 \\mathrm{~L}$$<br>residual volume of $$1.2 \\mathrm{~L}$$<br>Total lung capacity $$=$$ vital capacity + residual volume $$=5+1.2=6.2 \\mathrm{~L}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8670,'biology',83,490,1006,'Which is not a character of phylum porifera?\ni. Generally marine and mostly asymmetrical\nii. Water in canal system moves from osculumspongocoel ostia\niii. Water transport helps to gather food, respiratory exchange and removal of waste\niv. Digestion is intracellular\nv. Sexes are not separate i. e. eggs and sperms are produced by same individual\nvi. Fertilisation is external and development is direct','[{"text": "iii and v", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "v and vi", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii and vi", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ii and v", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In the members of Phylum Porifera, water in thewater canal moves from Ostia to Spongocoel and to Osculum. Sponges undergo internal fertilization and have indirect development with larval stages .',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8671,'biology',86,981,2286,'Which of the following is incorrect?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{IC = TV + IRV}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inspiratory capacity = Inspiratory reserve volume + Tidal volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Total lung capacity = Vital capacity + Functional residual capacity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Minute ventilation $$=$$ (Number of breaths $$/$$ $$\\\\mathrm{min}) *$$ Tidal volume", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Total lung capacity $$=$$ vital capacity + residual volume',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8674,'biology',83,489,1004,'How many of the following features are used as the basis of animal classification?<br>i. Arrangement of cells<br>ii. Body symmetry<br>iii. Nature of coelom<br>iv. Patterns of digestive system<br>v. Patterns of circulatory system<br>vi. Patterns of reproductive system','[{"text": "Four", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Six", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Five", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'All the six characters mentioned are used as a basis of classification.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8675,'biology',86,981,2286,'Match the following:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Tidal volume<br>B. Inspiratory reserve volume<br>C. Expiratory reserve volume<br>D. Residual volume<br>E. Inspiratory reserve capacity<br>F. Vital capacity</div><div class="column-right">P. Tidal volume and inspiratory reserve volume and expiratory reserve volume<br>Q. Additional volume of air a person can inspire by a forcible inspiration<br>R. Volume of air remaining in the lungs even after a forcible expiration<br>S. Tidal volume and inspiratory reserve volume.<br>T. Volume of air inspirited or expired during a normal respiration.<br>U. Additional volume of air a person can expire by a forcible expiration.</div></div>','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{T},  \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R},  \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q},  \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{U},  \\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{S}$$, $$\\\\mathrm{F}-\\\\mathrm{P}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{T},  \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{U},  \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R},  \\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{P}$$, $$\\\\mathrm{F}-\\\\mathrm{S}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{T},  \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R},  \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{U},  \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q},  \\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{S}$$, $$\\\\mathrm{F}-\\\\mathrm{P}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{T},  \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q},  \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{U},  \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R},  \\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{S}$$, $$\\\\mathrm{F}-\\\\mathrm{P}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Tidal volume $$=$$ Volume of air inspired or expired duing normal respiration<br>Inspiratory reserve volume $$=$$ Additional volume of air, a person can inspire by a forcible inspiration<br>Expiratory reserve volume $$=$$ Additional volume of air, a person can expire by a forcible expiration<br>Residual volume $$=$$ Volume of air remaining in the lungs even after a forcible expiration<br>Inspiratory reserve capacity $$=$$ tidal volume and inspiratory reserve volume<br>Vital capacity $$=$$ Tidal volume and inspiratory reserve volume and expiratory reserve volume',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8678,'biology',83,500,1017,'A list of animals in given below. Identify the animals with open circulatory system and choose the correct answer i. <em>Ascidia</em><br>ii. Cockroach iii. Earthworm iv. Prawn v . Silver fish vi. Snail vii. Squid.','[{"text": "i, ii, iv, v, vi", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ii, iv, vi", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii, iv, v, vi", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iii, iv, v, vii", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Cockroach, Prawn belong to Phylum Arthropoda. Snail belongs to Phylum Mollusca. Members of Phylum Arthropoda and Mollusca have an open circulatory system.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8679,'biology',86,981,2286,'Match the following:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Residual volume<br>B. Inspiratory reserve volume<br>C. Expiratory reserve volume<br>D. Tidal Volume<br>E. Vital capacity</div><div class="column-right">P. 2500-3000 ml of air<br>Q. 1000-1100 ml of air<br>R. 500 ml of air<br>S. 3500-4600 ml of air<br>T 1100-1200 ml of air</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R, B - S, C - Q, D - P, E - T}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - T, B - P, C - Q, D - R, E - S}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - T, B - P, C - S, D - R, E - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - T, B - P, C - Q, D - T, E - S}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Residual volume $$=1100-1200 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ air<br>Inspiratory reserve volume $$=2500-300 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ air<br>Expiratory reserve volume $$=1000-1100 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ air<br>Tidal volume $$=500 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ of air<br>Vital capacity $$=3500-4600 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ of air',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8682,'biology',83,495,1011,'Read the given characters about <em>Hirudinaria</em><br>i. Triploblastic, bilateral symmetry.<br>ii. Metamerically segmented and coelomate animals.<br>iii. Dioecious<br>iv. Closed circulatory systems present<br>v. Lateral appendages parapodia present<br>How many of the characters are correct w.r.t <em>Hirudinaria</em>?','[{"text": "Four", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Five", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Two", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<em>Hirudinaria</em> is triploblastic with bilateral symmetry, metamerically segmented coelomate animal with closed circulatory system. <em>Hirudinaria</em> is monoecious (bisexual). Lateral appendage parapodia is absent in <em>Hirudinaria.</em>',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8683,'biology',86,982,2287,'Primary site of the gaseous exchange in human is','[{"text": "Lungs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alveoli", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bronchus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Trachea", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Alveoli are the primary sites of exchange of gases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8686,'biology',83,497,1013,'Which of the following statements are true/ false?<br>I. In higher phyla cellular level of organisation is seen.<br>II. Phylum-Platyhelminthes have cellular level of organisation.<br>III. Cellular level of organisation is seen when the cells are not arranged as loose cell aggregates.<br>IV. Molluscs exhibit tissue level of organisation.<br>Choose the correct option of the following.','[{"text": "All statements are false", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and II are true, but III and IV are fasle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III and IV are true, but I and II are false", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All statements are true", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Members of higher Phyla have an organ and organ system level of organisation. Phylum Platyhelminthes has an organ system level of organisation. Phylum Molluscs has an organ system grade of organisation.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8687,'biology',86,982,2287,'Gaseous $$\\left(\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right.$$ and $$\\left.\\mathrm{CO}_{2}\\right)$$ exchange between alveoli and blood, and blood and tissues takes place by','[{"text": "Active transport", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Facilitated diffusion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Simple diffusion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Gaseous $$\\left(\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right.$$ and $$\\left.\\mathrm{CO}_{2}\\right)$$ exchange between alveoil and blood, and tissues takes place by simple diffusion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8690,'biology',83,505,1023,'Which one of the following categories of animals is correctly described with no single exception in it?','[{"text": "All vertebrates possess paired appendages, jaws and kidneys.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All amphibians have two pairs of limbs and three-chambered heart.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All reptiles are terrestrial with cornified skin and epidermal scales or scutes.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All protochordates are marine and possess notochord, at least at some stage of their life.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both urochordates and cephalochordates are marine. In urochordate, a notochord is present in larval tail. In cephalochordates, notochord extends from head to tail region and is persistent throughout their life.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8691,'biology',86,982,2287,'The exchange of gases in the alveoli of the lungs takes place by','[{"text": "Simple diffusion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Osmosis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Active transport", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Passive transport", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Gaseous exchange occurs in the alveoli by simple diffusion. The blood flowing past the alveoli is rich in carbon dioxide and very poor in oxygen. The gas molecules naturally flow in the direction of lower concentration through the thin gas exchange membrane, which is only two cells thick.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8694,'biology',83,499,1015,'Which of the following statements is incorrect for hemichordates?<br>I. Excretion by general body surface<br>II. Body contains proboscis, collar and trunk<br>III. Body is schizocoelomic<br>IV. Respiration by proboscis gland','[{"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I,III and IV", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In Hemichordates, excretion takes place by the proboscis gland. Body is enterocoelomate. Respiration by gills.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8696,'biology',86,982,2287,'Exchange of gases occurs','[{"text": "By diffusion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Between blood and tissue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Between alveoli and pulmonary blood capillary", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Alveoli are the primary sites of exchange of gases. Exchange of gases occurs between alveoli and pulmonary blood capillary and also occur between blood and tissues. $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ and $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ are exchanged in these sites by simple diffusion mainly based on pressure/ concentration gradient.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8698,'biology',83,498,1014,'Which of the following is not true for echinodermata?\ni. Sexual reproduction\nii. Fertilization usually internal\niii. Development indirect\niv. Sexes are united\nv. Free swimming larva','[{"text": "ii and iv", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "i and v", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iv and v", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iii and v", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Fertilization in Echinodermata is external, sexes are not united, the organism are unisexual.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8700,'biology',86,982,2287,'The partial pressure of oxygen and the partial pressure of carbon dioxide is identical in<br>i. Deoxygenated blood and oxygenated blood<br>ii. Tissues and deoxygenated blood<br>iii. Oxygenated blood and tissues<br>iv. Tissues and alveoli<br>How many of the above pairs are true.','[{"text": "Two", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "One", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Four", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The partial pressure of oxygen and the partial pressure of carbon dioxide is identical in<br>i. Deoxygenated blood and oxygenated blood<br>iv. Tissues and alveoli',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
