(8896,'biology',86,983,2288,'Carbon dioxide entering erythrocytes reacts with water to form carbonic acid. The enzyme is','[{"text": "Oxidoreductase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carboxypeptidase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydrolase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carbonic anhydrase", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Carbon dioxide entering erythrocytes reacts with water to from carbonic acid. The enzyme is carbonic anhydrase.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8897,'biology',83,502,1020,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A shark can stay at a desired level in water without swimming</div><div class="column-right">It has buoyancy regulating organ, the swim bladder</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Shark is a cartilaginous fish which lacks the buoyancy regulating organ called swim bladder. Hence, shark has to swim constantly or it will sink to the bottom. It can\'t stay at a desired level in water without swimming. Therefore, both are wrong',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9077,'biology',87,1122,2694,'Which of the following is correct statement about leucocytes?','[{"text": "Two main types are found, they are granulocytes and agranulocytes.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They are approximately in an average of $$6000-8000 \\\\mathrm{~mm}^{3}$$ of blood cells in numbers.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lymphocytes and monocytes are the agranulocytes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All statements are correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8898,'biology',84,583,1169,'Heart of frog is unique by the presence of','[{"text": "Ventricle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Auricle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pericardium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sinus venosus and truncus arteriosus", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Heart of frog is unique by the presence of sinus venosus and truncus arteriosus. A triangular structure called sinus venosus joins the right atrium. It receives blood through the major veins called vena cava. The ventricle opens into a saclike conus arteriosus on the ventral side of the heart.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8900,'biology',86,983,2288,'Which factors favour the binding of $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ with $$\\mathrm{Hb}$$ in tissues?','[{"text": "High $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ and high $$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ and high $$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ and low $$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "High $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ and low $$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Following factors favours the binding of $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ with $$\\mathrm{Hb}$$.<br>a. Decrease in $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$.<br>b. Increase in $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ (Both effect).<br>c. Increase in body temperature.<br>d. Increase in $$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$ion concentration.<br>e. Decrease in $$\\mathrm{pH}$$.<br>f. Increse in 2,3 diphosphoglycerate',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8901,'biology',83,495,1011,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Earthworm are hermaphrodites</div><div class="column-right">Annelida do not show asexual reproduction generally</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both are correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8902,'biology',84,583,1169,'A triangular structure that joins the right atrium, in frog is called','[{"text": "Conus arteriosus and receives blood from vena cava", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sinus venosus and receives blood from vena cava", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Sinus venosus and receives blood from pulmonary artery", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conus arteriosus and receives blood from pulmonary vein.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A triangular structure that joins the right atrium is called sinus venosus and receives blood from vena cava.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8904,'biology',86,983,2288,'Which of the following statements is false?','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ is low and $$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$ is high as in the tissues, more binding of $$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ with $$\\\\mathrm{Hb}$$ occurs", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$ is the major factor which affects the binding of $$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ with haemoglobin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Every ${\\\\rm{100}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ml}}$ of deoxygenated blood delivers approximately ${\\\\rm{4}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ml}}$ of $$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ to the alveoli", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RBC contains a very high concentration of carbonic anhydrase and minute quantities of the same in the plasma", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ is high and $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$ is low as in the tissues, more binding of carbon dioxide occurs whereas when the $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ is low and $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$ is high as in the alveoli, dissociation of $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ from carbamino-haemoglobin takes place, i.e., $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ which is bound to haemoglobin from the tissues is delivered at the alveoli.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8905,'biology',83,505,1023,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A house lizard can climb on a smooth wall</div><div class="column-right">It has adhesive lamellar pads creating suctions by vacuum</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'On the underside of the toe of a lizard with sticky toes are a series of small horizontal lines, together called a scansor. Each line is called a lamella, and is composed of thousands of setae, or few little spatulas, each of which ends in a broadened terminal pad. These spatulae are so small that they create molecular adhesive forces between the toes of the animals and their substrate called Vander Waals forces. Therefore, Both are true',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8906,'biology',84,583,1169,'In frog, ventricle opens into ____ on the _____ side of hear','[{"text": "Sinus venosus, ventral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sinus venosus, dorsal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conus arteriosus, dorsal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conus arteriosus, ventral", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In frogs the ventricle opens into a sac like conus arteriosus on the ventral side of the heart.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8908,'biology',86,983,2288,'The carbon dioxide is transported via blood to lungs as','[{"text": "Dissolved in blood plasma", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In the form of carbonic acid only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In combination with haemoglobin only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carbaminohaemoglobin and as carbonic acid", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Carbon dioxide can bind to plasma proteins or can enter red blood cells and bind to haemoglobin. This form transports about 10 percent of the carbon dioxide. When carbon dioxide binds to haemoglobin, a molecule called carbaminohaemoglobin is formed. Binding of carbon dioxide to haemoglobin is reversible. Therefore, when it reaches the lungs, the carbon dioxide can freely dissociate from the haemoglobin and be expelled from the body. The majority of carbon dioxide molecules ( 85 percent) are carried as part of the bicarbonate buffer system. In this system, carbon dioxide diffuses into the red blood cells. Carbonic anhydrase (CA) within the red blood cells quickly converts the carbon dioxide into carbonic acid $$\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}\\right)$$. Carbonic acid is an unstable, intermediate molecule that immediately dissociates into bicarbonate ions $$\\left(\\mathrm{HCO}^{-}\\right)$$and hydrogen $$\\left(\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right)$$ions.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8909,'biology',83,494,1010,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The round worms are eucoelomates</div><div class="column-right">They have a fluid filled coelom</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The round worms are pseudocoelomates. They do not have a true coelom. They have a cavity that forms between the mesoderm and the endoderm. Therefore, Both are false.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8910,'biology',84,583,1169,'Which of the special portal circulation is present in frog?','[{"text": "Renal portal system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hepatic portal system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neither hepatic nor renal portal system is present", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The veins collect blood from different parts of body to the heart and form the venous system. Special venous connection between liver and intestine as well as the kidney and lower parts of the body are present in frogs. The former is called hepatic portal system and the latter is called renal portal system.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8912,'biology',86,983,2288,'In lungs there is definite exchange of ions between $$\\mathrm{RBC}$$ and plasma. Removal of $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ from tissue into blood involves','[{"text": "Efflux of $$\\\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}$$ions from $$\\\\mathrm{RBC}$$ into plasma", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Efflux of $$\\\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$$from $$\\\\mathrm{RBC}$$ into plasma", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Influx of $$\\\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}$$ ions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Influx of $$\\\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$$ions", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Bicarbonate ions which are formed inside the $$\\mathrm{RBC}$$ diffuse from $$\\mathrm{RBC}$$ into the plasma. To maintain the ionic balance $$\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$$ionic move from plasma into RBCs. This ionic exchange is called chloride shift or Hamburger\'s phenomenon. <img src=\'/img/data/biology/86_8912_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Chloride shift is required for $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ transport',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8913,'biology',83,495,1011,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Radial symmetry in animals is advantageous in detecting food and danger</div><div class="column-right">Annelida are bilaterally symmetrical acoelomate animals</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Annelida are bilaterally symmetrical and true coelomates.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8914,'biology',84,583,1169,'Special venous connection between liver and intestine as well as the kidney and lower parts of body are present in frog that is called','[{"text": "Hepatic portal system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Renal portal system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Renal portal system and hepatic portal system respectively", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hepatic portal system and renal portal system, respectively", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Special venous connection between liver and intestine as well as the kidney and lower parts of body are present in frog that is called hepatic portal system and renal portal system, respectively',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(8916,'biology',86,983,2288,'In Bohr\'s effect, the curve shifts to right when','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ decrease and $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ increase and $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ increase and $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ decrease and $\\\\mathrm{pH}$ increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ increase and $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ decrease", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Factors which shift oxygen dissociation curve to right<br>a. Decrease in $\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$.<br>b. Increase in $\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$.<br>c. Increase in body temperature.<br>d. Increase in $\\mathrm{H}+$ ion concentration<br>e. Decrease in $\\mathrm{pH}$.<br>f. Increse in 2,3 diphosphoglycerate',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
