(8923,'biology',86,983,2288,'Select the incorrect option from the statements listed below','[{"text": "The oxygen dissociation curve is sigmoidal in nature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The affinity of foetal haemoglobin is more for oxygen than that of adult haemoglobin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acidic $$\\\\mathrm{pH}$$ stimulates formation of oxyhaemoglobin", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Oxygen can bind with haemoglobin in a reversible manner to form oxyhaemoglobin", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'An acidic $$\\mathrm{pH}$$ will shift oxygen dissociation curve to right. An acidic $$\\mathrm{pH}$$ will reduce the formation of oxyhaemoglobin. At an increased acidic condition (less $$\\mathrm{pH}$$ ) of blood, dissociation of haemoglobin takes place.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
