(9372,'biology',86,983,2288,'Which statements are true/false?<br>I. Blood transport of $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ is comparatively easy because of its high solubility.<br>II. Approximately $$7 \\%$$ of $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ is transported dissolved in plasma.<br>III. $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ diffuses into blood, passes into $$\\mathrm{RBCs}$$ and reacts with water to from $$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}$$.<br>IV. Oxyhaemoglobin of erythrocytes is basic.<br>V. Chloride ions diffuse from plasma into erythrocytes to maintain ionic balance.','[{"text": "I, II and V are true, III and IV are false", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, III and V are false, II and IV are true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV are true, III and V are false", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III and V are true, II and IV are false", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Approximately $$7 \\%$$ of $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ is transported dissolved in plasma. Oxyhaemoglobin of erythrocyes is acidic',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9373,'biology',87,1127,2699,'\'Brain of heart\' is','[{"text": "SA node", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "AV node", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bundle of HIS", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Purkinje fibres", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Brain of heart is SA node. The SAN can generate the maximum number of action potentials, i.e., $$70-75 \\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$$, and is responsible for initiating and maintaining the rhythmic contractile activity of the heart. Therefore, it is called the pacemaker. Our heart normally beats 70-75 times in a minute (average 72 beats ${\\rm{mi}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{)}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9376,'biology',86,980,2285,'The given figure illustrates the changes in lung volume during the process of breathing.<br>The change from II to III indicates the','[{"text": "Expansion of the thoracic cavity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Movement of diaphragm away from the lungs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Expansion of ribs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Movement of air out of the lungs", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The given figure illustrates the changes in lung volume during the process of breathing. The change from II to III indicates the movement of air out of the lungs.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/86_9376_question_1.png'),
(9377,'biology',87,1127,2699,'SA node is located in','[{"text": "Upper left corner of right atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lower left corner of left atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lower right corner of left atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Upper right corner of right atrium", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A specialised cardiac musculature called the nodal tissue is also distributed in the heart. A patch of this tissue is present in the right upper corner of the right atrium called the sino-atrial node (SAN).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9378,'biology',83,496,1012,'Which of the following is a unique character of arthropods?','[{"text": "Open circulatory system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chitinous exoskeleton", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Jointed appendages", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Metameric segmentation", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Chitinous exoskeleton is a unique character of arthropods. Open circulatory system is present in molluscs, echinoderms and hemichordates also. We (humans) also have jointed legs and arms. Metameric segmentation is also seen in annelids and chordates.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9380,'biology',86,983,2288,'A large proportion of oxygen is left unused in the human blood even after its uptake by the body tissues. This $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$','[{"text": "Acts as a reserve during muscular exercise", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Helps in releasing more $$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ to the epithelium tissues", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Is enough to keep oxyhaemoglobin saturation at $$96 \\\\%$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Raises the $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ of blood to $$75 \\\\mathrm{~mm}$$ of $$\\\\mathrm{Hg}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A large proportion of oxygen is left unused in the human blood even after its uptake by the body tissues. This $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ acts as a reserve during muscular exercise.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9381,'biology',83,496,1012,'Which of the following characters is not shared by arthropods and molluscs?','[{"text": "Open circulatory system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Organ-system level of organisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Compound eyes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "True coelom", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Compound eyes are present in arthropods but not molluscs.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9382,'biology',87,1127,2699,'The impulse of heartbeat originates from','[{"text": "SAN", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "AVN", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vagus nerve", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cardiac nerve", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A patch of nodal tissue is present in the right upper corner of the right atrium called the sinoatrial node (SAN). The nodal musculature has the ability to generate action potentials without any external stimuli, i.e., it is autoexcitable. However, the number of action potentials that could be generated in a minute vary at different parts of the nodal system.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9383,'biology',84,583,1167,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Frog is poikilothermic<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Frogs do not have constant body temperature','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Frog is a cold blooded animal or poikilotherm.<br>The body temperature changes with temperature of its surroundings. This is because the oxidation of food in their tissues is slow and they are incapable of maintaining a constant body temperature.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9384,'biology',86,983,2288,'For proper transport of $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ and $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ blood should be','[{"text": "Slightly acidic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Strongly alkaline", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Strongly acidic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Slightly alkaline", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Haemoglobin binds oxygen when passing through the alveoli of the lungs and releases oxygen in the warmer, more acidic environment of bodily tissues, via simple diffusion. Most of the carbon dioxide combines with water and is carried in the plasma as bicarbonate ions. An excess of carbon dioxide (through exercise, or from holding ones breath) quickly shifts the blood $$\\mathrm{pH}$$ to being more acidic (acidosis). Chemoreceptors in the brain and major blood vessels detect this shift and stimulate the breathing centre of the brain (the medulla oblongata). Hence, as $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ levels build up and the blood becomes more acidic, we involuntarily breathe faster, thus lowering $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ levels and stabilising blood $$\\mathrm{pH}$$. In contrast, a person who is hyperventilating (such as during a panic attack) will expire more $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ than being produced in the body and the blood will become too alkaline (alkalosis).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9385,'biology',83,502,1020,'Select the correct statement.','[{"text": "Leech has incomplete gut", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Prawn has statocysts", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Snail chitinous shell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Shark has cycloid scales", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Statocysts are the balancing organs present in some arthropods, e.g., prawn. Leech has a complete gut. Molluscs (e.g. snail) have a calcareous shell. Scales of cartilaginous fishes (e.g. shark) are placoid.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9386,'biology',84,583,1170,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> System for control and coordination is highly evolved in frog<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Nervous system and endocrine system are called integrative systems.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'System for control and co-ordination is highly evolved in frog, it includes both nervous system and endocrinal glands. The nervous system is organized into central nervous system (Brain and spinal cord), peripheral nervous system (Cranial and spinal nerves), Antonomic Nervous system (Sympathetic and parasympathetic nerves).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9387,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Rate of heartbeat is determined by','[{"text": "SA node", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "AV node", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Purkinje fibres", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bundle of His", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The SAN can generate the maximum number of action potentials, i.e., 70-75 $$\\mathrm{min}^{-1}$$, and is responsible for initiating and maintaining the rhythmic contractile activity of the heart. Therefore, it is called the pacemaker. Our heart normally beats $$70-75$$ times in a minute (average 72 beats $$\\mathrm{min}^{-1}$$ ).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9388,'biology',86,982,2287,'Read the given statements.<br>I. Partial pressure of oxygen in inspired air is $$104 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$$.<br>II. $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ is less soluble in venous blood than in arterial blood.<br>III. Liver is the chief site of deamination.<br>IV. Pepsin cannot hydrolyse milk proteins. Of the above statements.','[{"text": "III and IV are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only I is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Partial pressure of oxygen in inspired air (alveolar air) is $$104 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9389,'biology',83,489,1003,'Which of the following are radially symmetrical?','[{"text": "Larvae of echinoderms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Larvae of annelids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adult gastropods", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adult echinoderms", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Adults echinoderms are radially symmetrical (their larvae are bilaterally symmetrical).Adultgastropods are asymmetrical. Annelids are bilaterally symmetrical',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9390,'biology',84,583,1169,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Hepatic portal system is present in frog.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Renal portal system is present in frog.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Frogs have 2 portal systems-Renal portal system and hepatic portal system. Hepatic portal system includes veins that collect blood from alimentary canal and carry it to heart through the liver, hepatic veins and post canal.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9391,'biology',87,1127,2699,'A bundle of nodal fibres, ____ continues from the AVN which passes through the atrioventricular septa to emerge on the top of the interventricular septum and immediately divides.','[{"text": "SA node", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AV bundle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Purkinje fibres", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bundle of His", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A bundle of nodal fibres, atrioventricular bundle (AV bundle) continues from the AVN which passes through the atrio-ventricular septa to emerge on the top of the interventricular septum and immediately divides into a right and left bundle.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9392,'biology',86,982,2287,'During the exchanges of gases between lung alveoli and pulmonary capillaries, if the partial pressure of oxygen in alveolar capillaries is ${\\rm{95}}\\,{\\rm{mmHg}}$ and the partial pressure of $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ is ${\\rm{45}}\\,{\\rm{mmHg}}$ in deoxygenated blood. based on this identify the correct statement.','[{"text": "The partial pressure of nitrogen will remain same in both blood and alveoli.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The partial pressure of oxygen will be highest, i.e., $$104 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$$ in alveoli.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The partial pressure of $$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ is less in pulmonary veins as compared to pulmonary arteries.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In the human body, deamination takes place primarily in the liver, however it can also occur in the kidney. In situations of excess protein intake, deamination is used to break down amino acids for energy. The amino group is removed from the amino acid and converted to ammonia.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9395,'biology',87,1127,2699,'SA node is called the pace maker of the heart. Why?','[{"text": "It initiates and maintains the rhythmic contractile activity of heart.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It delays the transmission of impulse between the atria and ventricles.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It gets stimulated when it receives neural signal.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It maintains the pace of the heart beat", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The SAN can generate the maximum number of action potentials, i.e., 70-75 $$\\mathrm{min}^{-1}$$, and is responsible for initiating and maintaining the rhythmic contractile activity of the heart. Therefore, it is called the pacemaker. Our heart normally beats $$70-75$$ times in a minute (average 72 beats $$\\mathrm{min}^{-1}$$ ).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9396,'biology',86,983,2288,'&nbsp;The diagram shows part played by red blood cells in the transport of carbon dioxide. &nbsp; &nbsp;Which row is correct?','[{"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}<br>{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{1}}&amp;{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{2}}&amp;{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{3}}&amp;{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{4}}\\\\\\\\<br>\\\\begin{array}{l}<br>{\\\\rm{carbonic}}\\\\\\\\<br>{\\\\rm{an}}{\\\\rm{hydrase}}<br>\\\\end{array}&amp;{{{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}}&amp;{{{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{H}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}}&amp;{{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{HCO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}<br>\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}<br>\\\\begin{array}{l}<br>{\\\\rm{Carbamino}}\\\\\\\\<br>{\\\\rm{haemoglobin}}<br>\\\\end{array}&amp;\\\\begin{array}{l}<br>{\\\\rm{Haemoglo - }}\\\\\\\\<br>{\\\\rm{binic}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{acid}}<br>\\\\end{array}&amp;{{{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{H}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}}&amp;{{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{HCO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}<br>\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}<br>\\\\begin{array}{l}<br>{\\\\rm{Haemoglo - }}\\\\\\\\<br>{\\\\rm{binic}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{acid}}<br>\\\\end{array}&amp;{{{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{H}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}}&amp;{{{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}}&amp;{{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{HCO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}<br>\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}<br>{{{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{H}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}}&amp;{{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{HCO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}&amp;{{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}}&amp;{{{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}}<br>\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The diagram shows part played by red blood cells in the transport of carbon dioxide. The correct labelling is as follow a- carbonic anhydrase, b- $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}, \\mathrm{c}-\\mathrm{H}^{+}$, $\\mathrm{d}-\\mathrm{HCO}^{3-}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/86_9396_question_1.png'),
(9397,'biology',83,505,1023,'Which of the following is a poikilotherm with four-chambered heart?','[{"text": "Ostrich", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Crocodile", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Platypus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Salamander", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<i>Crocodiles</i> are the cold-blooded animals (reptiles) with a four-chambered heart. <i>Ostrich</i> and platypus are warm-blooded. The heart of salamander has only three-chambers (two atria and one ventricle).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9398,'biology',84,579,1159,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Subcutaneous layer is made up of areolar connective tissue and adipose tissue.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It lacks adipocytes','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'It contains adipocytes and adipocyte is the fat storage tissue.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9399,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Impulse originated from SA node is transmitted to','[{"text": "Pacemaker", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AV node", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bundle of His", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Purkinje system", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The SAN generates an action potential which stimulates both the atria to undergo a simultaneous contraction - the atrial systole. The action potential is conducted to the ventricular side by the AVN and AV bundle from where the bundle of His transmits it through the entire ventricular musculature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9400,'biology',86,983,2288,'Which of the following change is observed as the blood passes through systemic capillaries in tissues?','[{"text": "Oxygen affinity of haemoglobin decreases.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxyhaemoglobin formation occurs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Unloading of oxygen from haemoglobin takes place.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As the blood passes through systemic capillaries in tissues oxygen affinity of haemoglobin decreases and unloading of oxygen from haemoglobin takes place.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9401,'biology',83,505,1023,'Select the correct match.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "<i>Pheretima</i> - Flame cells help in excretion and osmoregulation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Calotes</i> - Body is covered by dermal scales", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Ophiura</i> - Closed circulatory system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Trygon</i> - Possesses a poison sting", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<i>Trygon</i> possesses a poison sting. In Pheretima, nephridia (not flame cells) help for excretion and osmoregulation. Reptiles (e.g., <i>Calotes</i>) have epidermal scales. Circulatory system of echinoderms (e.g., <i>Ophiura</i>) is of open type.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9402,'biology',84,579,1158,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Stomach and intestine of our body have columnar epithelium<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Columnar epithelium helps in secretion and absorption.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Columnar epithelium is composed of single layer of tall and slender cells, their nuclei are located at the base. Free surface may have microvilli. They are found in the lining of stomach and intestine and help in secretion and absorption.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9403,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Purkinje fibres are','[{"text": "Nerve fibres", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Connective tissue fibres", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Modified cardiac fibres", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Dendrites", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Purkinje fibres are modified cardiac fibres. The Purkinje fibers are specialized conducting fibers composed of electrically excitable cells.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(10618,'biology',9,25,48,'Protonema is found in','[{"text": "Mosses - <i>Sphagnum</i>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mosses - <i>Selaginella</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mosses - <i>Marchantia</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mosses - <i>Fucus</i>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Protonema stage of gametophyte is found in mosses (eg. Sphagnum)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9404,'biology',86,983,2288,'Read the following statements:<br>I. Nearly $$25 \\%$$ of $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ is transported as carbaminohaemoglobin in RBCs.<br>II. In the alveoli, where there is high $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$, low $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ lesser $$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$concentration and lower temperature, the factors are favourable for breakdown of oxyhaemoglobin.<br>III Every $$100 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ of oxygenated blood delivers $$15 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ of $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ to tissues during strenuous exercise.<br>IV.With increase in body temperature during exercise, the affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen increases.<br>Which of the above statements are correct?','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In the alveoli, where there is high $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$, low $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ lesser $$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$concentration and lower temperature, the factors are favourable for formation of oxyhaemoglobin. With increase in body temperature during exercise, the affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen decreases.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9405,'biology',83,503,1021,'Select the taxon that represents both marine and freshwater species but not terrestrial species.','[{"text": "Mollusca", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Echinodermata", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Amphibia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Osteichthyes", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Osteichthyes includes both marine and fresh water fishes with bony endoskeleton. Mollusca includes some freshwater and terrestrial form also. Amphibians live in freshwater and terrestrial habitats. All echinoderms are marine.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9406,'biology',84,579,1161,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Neurons protect and support neuroglial cell<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Neuroglial cells make up $90 \\%$ neural tissue in our body','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Neuroglial cell or neuroglia are specialised cells found in brain and spinal cord supporting neurons and their fibres. About $50 \\%$ of brain cells are neuroglial cells.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9407,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Bundle of His is a group of ____ and found in ____','[{"text": "Nerve fibres, left atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nerve fibres, interventricular septum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Muscular fibres, interventricular septum", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Muscular fibres, left atrium", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Bundle of HIS is a group of muscular fibres and found in interventricular septum.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9408,'biology',86,983,2288,'Which of the followig is an incorrect statement?','[{"text": "A sigmoid curve is obtained when percentage saturation of haemoglobin with $$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ is plotted against $$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The oxygen dissociation curve is highly useful in studying the effect of factors like $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$concentration, etc. on oxygen affinity of haemoglobin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$ and $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ of inhaled air is $$159 \\\\mathrm{mmHg}$$ and $$40 \\\\mathrm{mmHg}$$ respectively.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ gets bound to haemoglobin in the lung surface and gets dissociated at the tissues", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$ and $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ of inhaled air is $$104 \\mathrm{mmHg}$$ and $$40 \\mathrm{mmHg}$$ respectively.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9409,'biology',83,492,1008,'Identify the phylum which shows the following characteristics:<br>(i) Animals are exclusively marine, radially symmetrical and diploblastic.<br>(ii) Body bears eight external rows of ciliated comb plates which help in locomotion.<br>(iii) Digestion is both extracellular and intracellular.<br>(iv) Reproduction is only by sexual modes.','[{"text": "Coelenterate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mollusca", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Arthropoda", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ctenophora", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All these characteristics are related to the minor invertebrate phylum-Ctenophora. Ctenophores are commonly called as sea walnuts or comb jellies.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9410,'biology',84,579,1160,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Smooth muscles are known as involuntary muscles<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Smooth muscles are controlled by autonomic nervous system','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Smooth muscles are involuntary as their functioning cannot be consciously controlled. Action of these muscles is controlled by autonomic nervous system.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9411,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Sino-Atrial node (SAN) can generate impulses','[{"text": "$$70-75 \\\\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$50-55 \\\\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$35-40 \\\\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$90-100 \\\\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The SAN can generate the maximum number of action potentials, i.e., $$70-75 \\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$$, and is responsible for initiating and maintaining the rhythmic contractile activity of the heart. Therefore, it is called the pacemaker. Our heart normally beats 70-75 times in a minute (average 72 beats $$\\mathrm{min}^{-1}$$ ).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9412,'biology',86,979,2284,'Which of the following does not occur during breathing?','[{"text": "Humidifies the air", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Brings the air to body temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Diffusion of gases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cleans up the air", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Diffusion of gases is a physical phenomenon that takes place between the tissue and blood vessels, and does not occur during breathing whereas bringing air to the body temperature, its cleaning and warming occurs during the process of breathing.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9422,'biology',86,984,2289,'Respiratory process is regulated by certain specialized centres in the brain. One of the following listed centres can reduce the duration of inspiration upon stimulation','[{"text": "Medullary inspiratory centre", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pneumotaxic centre", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Apneustic centre", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chemosensitive centre", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Respiratory process is regulated by certain specialized centres in the brain. Pneumotaxic centres can reduce the duration of inspiration upon stimulation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9413,'biology',83,503,1021,'Match Column I with Column II :<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below :<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. <i>Pterophyllum</i><br>B. <i>Myxine</i><br>C. <i>Pristis</i><br>D. <i>Exocoetus</i></div><div class="column-right">P. Hag fish<br>Q. Saw fish<br>R. Angel fish<br>S. Flying fish</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  Q, B  -  P, C  -  R, D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  R, B  -  P, C  -  Q, D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ A  -  S, B  -  P, C  -  Q, D  -  R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ A  -  R, B  -  Q, C  -  P, D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{A}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{R,}}\\,{\\rm{B}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{P,}}\\,{\\rm{C}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{Q,}}\\,{\\rm{D}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{S}}$ is the correct option. <i>Pterophyllum</i> is the scientific name for Angel fish.<br><i>Myxine</i> is the scientific name for Hag fish.<br><i>Pristis</i> is the scientific name for Saw fish.<br><i>Exocoetus</i> is the scientific name for Flying fish.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9415,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Bundle of His consists of<br>(A) Purkinje fibres<br>(B) Left bundle branch<br>(C) Right bundle branch<br>(D) AV bundle','[{"text": "A, B and C only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A, B, C, D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B, C and D only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A and D only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Bundle of HIS consists of Purkinje fibres, Left bundle branch, Right bundle branch.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9416,'biology',86,983,2288,'It is known that exposure to carbon monoxide is harmful to animals because','[{"text": "It reduces $$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ transport", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It reduces $$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ transport", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It increases $$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ transport", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It increases $$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ transport", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Carbon monoxide binds with haemoglobin; leaving less haemoglobin free for binding with oxygen.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9417,'biology',83,503,1021,'Match Column I with Column II :<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. <i>Pleurobrachia</i><br>B. Radula<br>C. Stomochord<br>D. Air bladder</div><div class="column-right">P. Mollusca<br>Q. Ctenophora<br>R. Osteichthyes<br>S. Hemichordata</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  S, B  -  Q, C  -  R, D  -  P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  Q, B  -  P, C  -  S, D  -  R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  Q, B  -  S, C  -  P, D  -  R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  S, B  -  R, C  -  Q, D  -  P}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{A}-\\mathrm{Q}, \\mathrm{B}-\\mathrm{P}, \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{S}, \\mathrm{D}-\\mathrm{R}$ is the correct option.<br>A - <i>Pleurobrachia</i> - is a member of phylum Ctenophora.<br>B - Radula - is a rasping feeding organ present in phylum Mollusca.<br>C- Stomochord - Rudimentary structure similar to notochord found in the collar region of members of phylum Hemichordata.<br>D - Air bladder is found in Osteichthyes which provides them buoyancy.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9419,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. SA node<br>B. AV node<br>C. Bundle of His<br>D. Purkinje fibres</div><div class="column-right">P. Minute fibres present throughout the ventricular muscles<br>Q. Lower left corner of the right atrium<br>R. Right upper corner of right atrium<br>S. Fibres alongwith right and left bundle</div></div>','[{"text": "A-R, B-S, C-Q, D-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-R, B-Q, C-S, D-P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-Q, B-R, C-S, D-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-P, C-S, D-R", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'SA node - Right upper corner of right atrium<br>AV node - lower left corner of the right atrium Purkinje fibres - Minute fibres present throughout the ventricular muscles.',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9420,'biology',83,495,1011,'Consider the following statements :<br>I. Annelids are true coelomates<br>II. Poriferans are pseudocoelomates<br>III. Aschelminthes are acoelomates<br>IV. Platyhelminthes are pseudocoelomates<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "II only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "III only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV only", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Annelids are true coelomate animals. Options (1), (3) and (4) are incorrect because poriferans are acoelomates, aschelminths are pseudocoelomates and platyhelminthes are acoelomates.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9423,'biology',87,1127,2699,'The following diagram show section of a human heart. Identify $$\\mathrm{A}$$ and $$\\mathrm{B}$$ in the given diagram?','[{"text": "A - Mitral valve, B - Tricuspid valve", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Tricuspid valve, B - Mitral valve", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Semilunar valve, B - Mitral valve", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Tricuspid valve, B - Semilunar valve", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The following diagram show section of a human heart. It can be labelled as: A- Mitral valve, B- Tricuspid valve,',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/87_9423_question_1.png'),
(9424,'biology',83,500,1016,'The following are the statements about nonchordates:<br>I. Pharynx is perforated by gill slits.<br>II. Notochord is absent.<br>III. Central nervous system is dorsal.<br>IV. Heart is dorsal if present.<br>V. Post anal tail is absent.<br>Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "I & III only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II & IV only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, IV & V only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II, III & IV only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The features of non-chordates among the given statements are:<br>II. Notochord is absent.<br>IV. Heart is dorsal if present.<br>V. Post anal tail is absent.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9426,'biology',86,980,2285,'In breathing movements, air volume can be estimated by','[{"text": "Stethoscope", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hygrometer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sphygmomanometer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Spirometer", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Stethoscope is used for hearing sounds from chest. Hygrometer is used for measuring density of liquid. Sphygmomanometer is used for measuring blood pressure.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9427,'biology',87,1127,2699,'The deoxygenated blood from heart carried by____ to lungs and oxygenated blood from lungs pumped into heart by ____ respectively?','[{"text": "B, A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, B", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}, \\\\mathrm{A}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, B", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The following diagram show section of a human heart. It can be labelled as :<br>A-Pulmonary artery, B- pulmonary veins, C- vena cava.<br>The deoxygenated blood from heart carried by pulmonary artery (A) to lungs and oxygenated blood from lungs pumped into heart by the pulmonary veins (B) respectively .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/87_9427_question_1.png'),
(9428,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Following is the diagram of heart. Identify $$\\mathrm{A}-\\mathrm{C}$$ in the given diagram?','[{"text": "A-AV node, B-Bundle of His, C-SA node", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A- Bundle of His, B- SA node, C-AV node", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-SA node, B- Bundle of His, C-AV node", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-SA node, B-AV node, C-Bundle of His", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The following diagram show section of a human heart. It can be labelled as: A - Sino artrial wall, B - Atrioventricular node, $$\\mathrm{C}-$$ Bundle of his.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/87_9428_question_1.png'),
(9430,'biology',86,983,2288,'The oxygen - haemoglobin dissociation curve will show a right shift in case of','[{"text": "High $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "High $$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less $$\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$concentration", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The oxygen haemoglobin curve shifted to right when $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$ decreases, $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ increases, $$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$concentration increases, $$\\mathrm{pH}$$ decreases and body temperature increases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9431,'biology',84,583,1168,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The alimentary canal of frog is short<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Frogs are carnivores','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Alimentary canal of frog is short because frog are carnivores and therefore the length of intestine is reduced. Meat is easier to be digested than plant material.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9432,'biology',87,1128,2700,'The duration of cardiac cycle in a normal man is','[{"text": "0.8 seconds", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "80 seconds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "60 seconds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "72 seconds", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'the duration of a cardiac cycle is 0.8 seconds.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9433,'biology',86,979,2283,'Respiration in insects is called direct because','[{"text": "The cells exchange $$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2} / \\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ directly with the air in the tubes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The tissues exchange $$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2} / \\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ directly with coelomic fluid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The tissues exchange $$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2} / \\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ directly with the air outside through body surface", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tracheal tubes exchange $$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2} / \\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ directly with the haemocoel which then exchange the same with tissues", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Respiration in insects is called direct because tracheal tubes exchange $$\\mathrm{O}_{2} / \\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ directly with the haemocoel which then exchange the same with tissues.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9434,'biology',86,980,2285,'Mark the true statement among the following with reference to normal breathing','[{"text": "Inspiration is a passive process where as expiration is active", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inspiration is a active process where as expiration is passive", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inspiration and expiration are active processes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inspiration and expiration are passive processes", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Inspiration is a active process whereas expiration is a passive process. Inspiration occur when the muscles of diaphragm contract to increase the overall volume of thoracic cavity. Thus the pressure within the lungs (intra-pulmonary pressure) is less in comparison to the atmospheric pressure, i.e., there is a negative pressure in the lungs with respect to atmospheric pressure. As the muscles use energy for contraction inspiration is called active process. Whereas, during the expiration diaphragm muscles relax without the use of energy intra-pulmonary pressure becomes higher than the atmospheric pressure and air noshes out. Thus, it is a passive process.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9435,'biology',87,1128,2700,'During systole of heart','[{"text": "Only atria contracts", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only ventricles contract", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atria and ventricles contract separately", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Atria and ventricles contract simultaneously", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'During systole of heart atria and ventricles contract separately. The SAN generates an action potential which stimulates both the atria to undergo a simultaneous contraction - the atrial systole. This increases the flow of blood into the ventricles by about 30 per cent. The action potential is conducted to the ventricular side by the AVN and AV bundle from where the bundle of His transmits it through the entire ventricular musculature. This causes the ventricular muscles to contract, (ventricular systole), the atria undergoes relaxation (diastole), coinciding with the ventricular systole.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9436,'biology',84,583,1169,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In frogs vascular system, lymph is same as the blood<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In frog, lymph contains RBC\'s and proteins','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In the vascular system of frog, blood contains of fluid plasma and 3 types of corpuscles (WBC, RBC and platelets). Lymph is a fluid filtered out from blood through capillaries. It consist of plasma and white corpuscles but lack red corpuscles and same proteins. Lymph is different from blood.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9437,'biology',86,983,2288,'Mark the incorrect statement in context to $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ binding to $$\\mathrm{Hb}$$','[{"text": "Low $$\\\\mathrm{pH}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lower temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lower $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Higher $$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Binding of oxygen with haemoglobin is primarily related to partial pressure of $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$. In alveoli, high $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$, low $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$, less $$\\mathrm{H}+$$ concentration and lower temperature facilitate formation of oxyhaemoglobin.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9438,'biology',87,1128,2700,'During diastole, blood','[{"text": "Enters the blood vessels", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Leaves the heart", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Leaves the ventricle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Enters the heart", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'During diastole, blood enters the heart.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9439,'biology',84,583,1171,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Female frog croaks more loudly than male frog<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> During copulation, the female clasps male with the help of forelimbs.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The male frog croaks louder than female because male frog has presence of 2 vocal sacs situated on the posterior part of the throat in a male frog, that act as a resonators. Croaking is a courtship behaviour, it is a mating call to attract female frog. The male frog jumps on the back of the female frog and holds female frog tightly with the help of the fore limbs.Holding female frog by the male frog is aided by the presence of the nuptial pads.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9440,'biology',87,1128,2700,'During atrial contraction flow of blood increases into the ventricles by about','[{"text": "50 percent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "30 percent", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$30 \\\\mathrm{ml}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$50 \\\\mathrm{ml}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The SAN generates an action potential which stimulates both the atria to undergo a simultaneous contraction - the atrial systole. This increases the flow of blood into the ventricles by about 30 percent.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9441,'biology',86,985,2290,'Incidence of emphysema - a respiratory disorder is high in cigarette smokers. In such cases','[{"text": "The bronchioles are found to be damaged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The alveolar walls are found to be damaged", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The plasma membrane is found to be damaged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The respiratory muscles are found to be damaged", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Incidence of Emphysema - a respiratory disorder is high in cigarette smokers. In such cases the alveolar walls are found to be damaged.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9443,'biology',86,983,2288,'$$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ dissociates from carbamino-haemoglobin when','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ is high and $$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$ is low", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$ is high and $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ is low", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ and $$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$ are equal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In alveoli, $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ is low and $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$ is high. Due to this $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ dissociates from haemoglobin.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9444,'biology',87,1128,2700,'During ventricular systole the oxygenated blood is pumped into the ____ and deoxygenated blood is pumped into the ____','[{"text": "Aorta, pulmonary artery.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pulmonary artery, aorta.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aorta, pulmonary vein.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pulmonary vein, pulmonary artery.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'During ventricular systole the oxygenated blood is pumped into the aorta and deoxygenated blood is pumped into the pulmonary aetery.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9445,'biology',84,579,1160,'The type of muscles present in our<br>','[{"text": "Thigh are striated and voluntary", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Intestine are striated and involuntary", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Upper arm are smooth muscle fibres fusiform in shape", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heart are involuntary and unstriated smooth muscle", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The type of muscles present in our thigh are striated/voluntary/skeletal.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9446,'biology',86,981,2286,'From the following relationships between respiratory volume and capacities, mark the correct option<br>i. $$\\quad$$ Inspiratory capacity (IC) $$=$$ Tidal Volume + Residual Volume<br>ii. $$\\quad$$ Vital capacity $$(\\mathrm{VC})=$$ Tidal Volume (TV) + Inspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV) + Expiratory Reserve Volume (ERV).<br>iii. Residual Volume (RV) = Vital capacity (VC) - Inspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV)<br>iv. Tidal Volume (TV) = Inspiratory capacity (IC) - Inspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV) Options:','[{"text": "(i) Incorrect, (ii) Incorrect, (iii) Incorrect, (iv) correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) Incorrect, (ii) correct, (iii) Incorrect, (iv) correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(i) correct, (ii) correct, (iii) Incorrect, (iv) correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) correct, (ii) Incorrect, (iii) correct, (iv) Incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'(i) Inspiratory Capacity (IC) = Tidal Volume + Inspiratory Reserve Volume (TV + IRV).<br>(ii) Vital Capacity (VC) Tidal Volume + Inspiratory Reserve Volume + Expiratory Reserve Volume. (TV + ERV + IRV)<br>(iii) Residual Volume (RV) Volume of air remaining in the lungs after a forcible expiration<br>(iv) TIdal Volume (TV) Volume of air inspired of expired during a normal respiration.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9447,'biology',87,1128,2700,'When ventricular systole occurs','[{"text": "Semilunar valves guarding pulmonary artery and aorta are forced to open", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tricuspid and bicuspid valves close", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atrial diastole coincides", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Ventricular systole increases the ventricular pressure causing the closure of tricuspid and bicuspid valves due to attempted backflow of blood into the atria. As the ventricular pressure increases further, the semilunar valves guarding the pulmonary artery (right side) and the aorta (left side) are forced open, allowing the blood in the ventricles to flow through these vessels into the circulatory pathways.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9448,'biology',84,579,1158,'The ciliated columnar epithelial cells in humans are known to occur in<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Fallopian tubes and urethra", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Eustachian tube and stomach lining", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oviduct and fallopian tube", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bile duct and Oesophagus", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The function of ciliated epithelium is to move particles or mucus in a specific direction over the epithelium. They are mainly present in the inner surface of hollow organs like bronchioles and fallopian tubes.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9449,'biology',86,979,2284,'Lining of trachea is made of','[{"text": "Simple squamous epithelium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Simple cuboidal epithelium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pseudo stratified epithelium", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Stratified cuboidal epithelium", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The trachea is lined with ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium, a type of epithelium with goblet cells that produce mucins, the main component of mucus, to moisten and protect the airways from foreign particles. This type of epithelium can easily sense foreign particles and ciliary movement remove the particles.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9450,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Which of the following events do not occur during joint diastole?<br>i. Blood from the pulmonary veins and vena cava flows into the left and right ventricles respectively through the left and right atria<br>ii. The semilunar valves are closed.<br>iii. Action potential is conducted from SAN to AVN.<br>iv. All four chambers of heart are in relaxed state.<br>v. Tricuspid and bicuspid valves open.','[{"text": "Only i, iv", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only iii", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only i, ii", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only i, ii and v", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'During joint diastole all the four chambers of heart are in a relaxed state. As the tricuspid and bicuspid valves are open, blood from the pulmonary veins and vena cava flows into the left and the right ventricle respectively through the left and right atria. The semilunar valves are closed at this stage. Action potential is not conducted from SAN to AVN during joint diastole.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9462,'biology',84,583,1171,'Ureters act as urinogenital ducts in','[{"text": "Frogs males<br>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Human males", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Human females", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frogs both males and females", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Ureters act as urinogenital duct in male frog. In human beings urethra acts as male urinogenital duct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9452,'biology',86,979,2283,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Lower invertebrates like sponges, coelenterates, flatworms, etc., exchange $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ with $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ by simple diffusion over their entire body surface. <br></div><div class="column-right">Among invertebrates, fishes use gills whereas amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals respire through skin.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Satement A is correct .<br>Among vertebrates, fishes use gills whereas amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals respire through lungs.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9453,'biology',87,1128,2700,'The amount of blood to be pumped out by each ventricle/minute is','[{"text": "Stroke volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cardiac output", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ERV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ZRV", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'During a cardiac cycle, each ventricle pumps out approximately $$70 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ of blood which is called the stroke volume. The stroke volume multiplied by the heart rate (no. of beats per min.) gives the cardiac output. Therefore, the cardiac output can be defined as the volume of blood pumped out by each ventricle per minute and averages $$5000 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ or 5 litres in a healthy individual.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9454,'biology',84,583,1170,'Frog differ from humans in possessing<br>','[{"text": "Nucleated red blood cells", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Paired cerebral hemispheres", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hepatic portal system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thyroid as well as parathyroid", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Human erythrocytes are enucleated, discoidal while erythrocytes in frog are large, oval and biconvex nucleated cells',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9455,'biology',86,979,2284,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Trachea is a straight tube extending up to the midthoracic cavity, which divides at the level of ${{\\rm{6}}^{{\\rm{th}}}}$ thoracic vertebra into a right and left primary bronchi.<br></div><div class="column-right">Larynx is a tendinous box which helps in sound production</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both are incorrect. Larynx is a cartilaginous box which helps in sound production and hence called the sound box Trachea is a straight tube extending up to the mid-thoracic cavity, which divides at the level of 5 th thoracic vertebra into a right and left primary bronchi.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9456,'biology',87,1128,2700,'During cardiac cycle each ventricle pumps out about $$70 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ of blood which is called','[{"text": "Stroke volume", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cardiac output", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tidal volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Residual volume", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'During a cardiac cycle, each ventricle pumps out approximately $$70 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ of blood which is called the stroke volume. The stroke volume multiplied by the heart rate (no. of beats per min.) gives the cardiac output.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9458,'biology',86,979,2284,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The external nostrils up to the terminal bronchioles constitute the conducting part<br></div><div class="column-right">The alveoli and their ducts form the respiratory or exchange part</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9459,'biology',84,579,1158,'The cells lining the blood vessels belong to the category of','[{"text": "Squamous epithelium", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Smooth muscle tissue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Columnar epithelium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Connective tissue<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Simple Squamous epithelium is present where diffusion and filtration is required. Cells lining our blood vessels belong to the category of Simple Squamous epithelium.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9460,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Cardiac output is','[{"text": "Stroke volume $$\\\\times$$ Heart rate $$=5 \\\\mathrm{~L} / \\\\mathrm{m}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Stroke volume $$\\\\times$$ Heart rate $$=500 \\\\mathrm{ml}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stroke volume $$\\\\times$$ Heart rate $$=72 \\\\mathrm{ml} / \\\\mathrm{m}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stroke volume $$\\\\times$$ Heart rate $$=50 \\\\mathrm{~L} / \\\\mathrm{m}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'During a cardiac cycle, each ventricle pumps out approximately $$70 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ of blood which is called the stroke volume. The stroke volume multiplied by the heart rate (no. of beats per min.) gives the cardiac output. Therefore, the cardiac output can be defined as the volume of blood pumped out by each ventricle per minute and averages $$5000 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ or 5 litres in a healthy individual.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9461,'biology',86,979,2284,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The lungs are situated in the thoracic chamber which is anatomically an air-tight chamber.<br></div><div class="column-right">The thoracic chamber is formed ventrally by the vertebral column, dorsally by the sternum, laterally by the ribs and on the lower side by the dome-shaped diaphragm.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Satement A is correct. The thoracic chamber is formed dorsally by the vertebral column, ventrally by the sternum, laterally by the ribs and on the lower side by the domeshaped diaphragm.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9463,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Identify the correct option<br>1) The body has the ability to alter the stroke volume as well as the heart rate and thereby the cardiac output.<br>2) The ventricles relax -ventricular diastole and the ventricular pressure falls causing the closure of semilunar valves which prevents the backflow of blood into the ventricles','[{"text": "1 is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'1) The body has the ability to alter the stroke volume as well as the heart rate and thereby the cardiac output. For example, the cardiac output of an athlete will be much higher than that of an ordinary man.<br>2) Ventricles relax (ventricular diastole) and the ventricular pressure falls causing the closure of semilunar valves which prevents the backflow of blood into the ventricles. Hence both the statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9464,'biology',86,979,2284,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Breathing or pulmonary ventilation by which atmospheric air is drawn in and $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ rich alveolar air is released out.<br></div><div class="column-right">Breathing involves inspiration during which atmospheric air is drawn in and expiration by which the alveolar air is released out</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both are correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9465,'biology',84,583,1171,'Consider the following four statements (A - D) related to the common frog <i>Rana tigrina</i> and select the correct option stating which ones are true $(\\mathrm{T})$ and which ones are false $(\\mathrm{F})$.<br>Statements:<br>(A) On dry land, it would die due to lack of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ if its mouth is forcibly kept closed for a few days.<br>(B) It has the four-chambered heart.<br>(C) On dry land, it turns uricotelic from ureotelic.<br>(D) Its life history is carried out in pond water.<br>Options','[{"text": "A - F, B - F, C - T, D - T", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - F, B - T, C - T, D - F", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - T, B - F, C - F, D - T", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - T, B - T, C - F, D - F", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The frogs take in oxygen and release carbon dioxide. They get oxygen by breathing through lungs, the lining of their mouths and their skin. When a frog is out of the water, its skin takes oxygen from the air. On dry land, they will die if their mouth is forcibly closed for some time.<br>Frogs have a three-chambered heart. It consists of two atria and one ventricle. Tadpoles eliminate nitrogenous wastes as ammonia (ammonotelic), but adult frog mainly eliminates urea (ureotelic). They are not uricotelic.<br>Frogs habitat is in or near pond.<br>So, the correct answer is option (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9466,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Identify the correct sequence of events in a cardiac cycle:','[{"text": "Joint diastole, ventricular diastole, atrial systole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atrial systole, ventricular diastole, ventricular systole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atrial systole, ventricular systole, joint diastole", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Atrial systole,ventricular diastole, joint diastole, ventricular systole,", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The correct sequence of events in a cardiac cycle is as follow : Atrial systole, ventricular systole, joint diastole. The SAN generates an action potential which stimulates both the atria to undergo a simultaneous contraction - the atrial systole. This increases the flow of blood into the ventricles by about 30 percent. The action potential is conducted to the ventricular side by the AVN and AV bundle from where the bundle of His transmits it through the entire ventricular musculature. This causes the ventricular muscles to contract, (ventricular systole), the atria undergoes relaxation (diastole), coinciding with the ventricular systole. i.e. Joint diastole.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9467,'biology',86,980,2285,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Inspiration occurs if the pressure within the lungs is less than the atmospheric pressure<br></div><div class="column-right">Expiration takes place when the intra-pulmonary pressure is higher than the atmospheric pressure.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Expiration takes place when the intrapulmonary pressure is higher than the atmospheric pressure.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9469,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Second cardiac sound (lub) is associated with opening of','[{"text": "Tricuspid and bicuspid- semilunar valve", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pulmonary and aortic- semilunar valve", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mitral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'During each cardiac cycle two prominent sounds are produced which can be easily heard through a stethoscope. The first heart sound (lub) is associated with the closure of the tricuspid and bicuspid valves whereas the second heart sound (dub) is associated with the closure of the semilunar valves.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9480,'biology',86,982,2287,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The solubility of $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ is $$20-25$$ times higher than that of $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$<br></div><div class="column-right">The diffusion membrane is made up of squamous epithelium of alveoli, the endothelium of alveolar capillaries and the basement substance</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both are correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9470,'biology',86,980,2285,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Relaxation of the diaphragm and the inter-costal muscles returns the diaphragm and sternum to their normal positions and increases the thoracic volume and thereby the pulmonary volume.<br></div><div class="column-right">The volume of air involved in breathing movements can be estimated by using a stethoscope</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both are wrong .<br>Statement A: Relaxation of the diaphragm and the inter-costal muscles returns the diaphragm and sternum to their normal positions and reduce the thoracic volume and thereby the pulmonary volume. Statement B: The volume of air involved in breathing movements can be estimated by using a spirometer',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9471,'biology',84,579,1159,'Given below is the diagrammatic sketch of a certain type of connective tissue. Identify the parts labelled A, B, C & D and select the right option<br>','[{"text": "Part-A:Mast cell,<br>Part-B :Marcophage<br>Part-C :Fibroblast<br>Part-D :Collagen fibres", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Part-A : Macrophage<br>Part-B : Collagen<br>Part-C : Fibroblast<br>Part-D : Mastcell fibres", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Part-A : Mast cell<br>Part-B : Collagen<br>Part-C : Fibroblast<br>Part-D : Macrophage fibres", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Part-A : Macrophage<br>Part-B : Fibroblast<br>Part-C : Collagen<br>Part-D : Mast cell", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given below is the diagrammatic sketch of an areolar type of connective tissue which can be labelled as Part-A : Macrophage Part-B : Fibroblast Part-C : collagen Part-D : Mastcell',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2012,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/84_9471_question_1.png'),
(9472,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Which of the following statement is wrong for second cardiac sound?','[{"text": "It is heard as dub", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is produced due to the closure of semilunar valves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is clinically significant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is produced due to the closure of mitral valves", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'During each cardiac cycle two prominent sounds are produced which can be easily heard through a stethoscope. The first heart sound (lub) is associated with the closure of the tricuspid and bicuspid valves whereas the second heart sound (dub) is associated with the closure of the semilunar valves.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9473,'biology',86,981,2286,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">TV is approximately 600 to $$800 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ of air per minute<br></div><div class="column-right">IRV averages $$2500 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ to $$3000 \\mathrm{~mL}$$</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Satement B is correct. TV is approximately 6000 to $$8000 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ of air per minute. IRV averages $$2500 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ to $$3000 \\mathrm{~mL}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9474,'biology',84,583,1169,'Which one of the following characteristics is common both in humans and adult frogs?<br>','[{"text": "Ureotelic mode of excretion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Four - chambered heart", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Internal fertilization", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nucleated RBCs", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Ureotelic mode of excretion in which urea which is the chief component of nitrogenous waste is excreted. It is common both in humans and adult frogs',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9475,'biology',87,1128,2700,'The first heart sound is caused by the closure of the ____ valves.','[{"text": "Tricuspid and bicuspid- semilunar valve", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pulmonary semilunar", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mitral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tricuspid and bicuspid-AV valve", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In healthy adults, there are two normal heart sounds, often described as a lub and a dub (or dup), that occur in sequence with each heartbeat. These are the first heart sound (S1) and second heart sound (S2), produced by the closing of the atrioventricular valves and semilunar valves, respectively.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9476,'biology',86,981,2286,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">IC is total volume of air a person can inspire after forceful expiration<br></div><div class="column-right">EC is total volume of air a person can expire after a forceful inspiration.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both are wrong. IC is total volume of air a person can inspire after a normal expiration.<br>$$\\mathrm{EC}$$ is total volume of air a person can expire after a normal inspiration.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9478,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Joint diastole phase of the cardiac cycle is in between','[{"text": "Beginning of atrial diastole and end of ventricular diastole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "\'Lubb\' and \'Dup\' sound", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Beginning of atrial systole and end of ventricular systole", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'During each cardiac cycle two prominent sounds are produced which can be easily heard through a stethoscope. The first heart sound (lub) is associated with the closure of the tricuspid and bicuspid valves whereas the second heart sound (dub) is associated with the closure of the semilunar valves.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9479,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Name the vessel that carries blood containing more urea to the kidney and the vessel that carries blood containing less urea from the kidney','[{"text": "Aorta, superior venacava", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Renal artery, renal vein", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Aorta, hepatic vein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of them", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Renal artery carries blood with more urea content to the kidney for the process of excretion. Whereas, renal vein carries purified blood away from the kidney just after filtration and hence contains less urea.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9482,'biology',87,1128,2700,'During each cardiac cycle two prominent sounds are produced which can be easily heard through a','[{"text": "Microscope", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Telescope", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sphygmomanometer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stethoscope", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'During each cardiac cycle two prominent sounds are produced which can be easily heard through a stethoscope. The first heart sound (lub) is associated with the closure of the tricuspid and bicuspid valves whereas the second heart sound (dub) is associated with the closure of the semilunar valves.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9483,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Blood vessel leading to glomerulus is called','[{"text": "Renal artery", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Renal vein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Efferent arteriole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Afferent arteriole", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The glomerulus is a tuft of capillaries formed by the branching of afferent arteriole which is a fine branch of the renal artery. Thus, afferent arteriole is the blood vessel that directly leads to the glomerulus.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9484,'biology',86,983,2288,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">About 97 per cent of $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ is transported by RBCs in the blood.<br></div><div class="column-right">The remaining 3 per cent of $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ is carried in a dissolved state through the plasma.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both are correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9485,'biology',84,583,1169,'Compared to those of humans, the erythrocytes<br>in frog are','[{"text": "Without nucleus but with haemoglobin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "nucleated and with haemoglobin", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "very much smaller and fewer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "nucleated and without haemoglobin.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Erythrocytes in frog are large, oval and biconvex nucleated cells while human erythrocytes are enucleated , discoidal.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9486,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Joint diastole<br>B. Atrial systole<br>C. Ventricular systole<br>D. Ventricular diastole</div><div class="column-right">P. Causes the closure of semilunar valves which prevents the backflow of blood<br>Q. Causes closure of tricuspid and bicuspid valves<br>R. All four chambers of heart are in relaxed state<br>S. Increases blood flow into ventricles by about 30%</div></div>','[{"text": "A-P, B-Q, C-R, D-S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-P, C-Q, D-S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-R, B-S, C-Q, D-P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-S, B-Q, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Joint diastole - All four chambers of heart are in relaxed state<br>Atrial systole - Increases blood flow into ventricles by about $$30 \\%$$<br>Ventricular systole-Cause closure of tricuspid and bicuspid valve<br>Ventricular diastole - Cause the closure of semilunar valves which prevents backflow of blood',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9487,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Name the artery and vein that is associated with the kidney','[{"text": "Aorta, superior venacava", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Renal artery, renal vein", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Aorta, hepatic vein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aorta, renal vein", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Renal artery provides oxygenated blood to kidneys , renal vein drains deoxygenated blood from the kidney into inferior venacava.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9488,'biology',86,981,2286,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">VC includes E C+E R V<br></div><div class="column-right">FRC includes ERV and VC</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both are wrong. VC includes TV+ IRV + ERV. FRC includes ERV and RV.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9490,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Heart beat<br>B. Cardiac cycle<br>C. Stroke volume<br>D. Cardiac output</div><div class="column-right">P. 5 L<br>Q. 70 ml<br>R. 0.8 sec<br>S. 72 per minute</div></div>','[{"text": "A-P, B-R, C-Q, D-S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-P, C-R, D-S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-R, C-S, D-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-S, B-R, C-Q, D-P", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Heart beat - 72 Per minute<br>Cardiac cycle $$-0.8 \\mathrm{sec}$$<br>Stroke volume $$-70 \\mathrm{ml}$$<br>Cardiac output $$-5 \\mathrm{~L}$$',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9491,'biology',88,1151,2758,'The right kidney is placed little lower than the left because of the','[{"text": "Presence of ovaries", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Presence of testis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Presence of liver", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Presence of pancreas", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Left kidney is little higher than the right one because of more space being occupied by the liver on right side. So, in human beings, the right kidney is slightly below the left.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9501,'biology',84,579,1158,'Choose the correctly matched pair:-<br><div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Inner lining of salivary ducts - Ciliated epithelium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Moist surface - Glandular epithelium of buccal cavity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tubular parts of nephrons - Cuboidal epithelium", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inner surface of bronchioles - Squamous epithelium", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Inner lining of salivary ducts is made up of Compound epithelium, Inner surface of bronchioles is made up of Ciliated epithelium, Moist surface of buccal cavity is made up of Compound epithelium, whereas tubular parts of nephron contains Cuboidal epithelium.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9492,'biology',86,983,2288,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In the alveoli, there is high $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$, low $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$, lesser $$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$ concentration and higher temperature, the factors are all favourable for the formation of oxyhaemoglobin<br></div><div class="column-right">In the tissues, where low $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$, high $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$, high $$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$ concentration and higher temperature exist, it results in dissociation of oxygen from the oxyhaemoglobin.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Satement B is correct. In the alveoli, there is high $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$, low $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$, lesser $$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$ concentration and lower temperature, the factors are all favourable for the formation of oxyhaemoglobin.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9494,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Heart beat<br>B. Cardiac output<br>C. Cardiac cycle</div><div class="column-right">P. Consists of systole and diastole of both atria and ventricles<br>Q. Many cardiac cycles performed per minute<br>R. Stroke volume multiplied by heart rate</div></div>','[{"text": "A-P, B-R, C-Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-R, C-P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-R, B-P, C-Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-P, C-R", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Heartbeat-Many cardiac cycles performed per unit<br>Cardiac output -Stroke volume multiplied by heart rate<br>Cardiac cycle-Consists of systole and diastole of both atria and ventricle.',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9495,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Mammalian kidney serve to excrete','[{"text": "Excess water, urea and glucose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Excess salts, urea and excess water", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Excess salts, excess water and excess amino acids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Excess salts, urea and ketone", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Vertebrates like mammals have complex tubular organs called kidneys. They serve to perform the excretion of excess salts, excess water and urea after filtration of glucose, amino acids, ketones out of it.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9496,'biology',86,983,2288,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">$$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ is carried by haemoglobin as carbamino-haemoglobin (about 40-45 per cent).<br></div><div class="column-right">Carbonic anhydrase is present in RBC only</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both are wrong $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ is carried by haemoglobin as carbamino-haemoglobin (about 20-25 per cent).Carbonic anhydrase is present in $$\\mathrm{RBC}$$ and in plasma also',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9497,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Electrocardiogram is a','[{"text": "Artificial heart", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Machine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Graph", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The machine (electro-cardiograph) is used to obtain an electrocardiogram (ECG) (graph).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9499,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Which among the following kidney structures form a highly coiled network of tubules?','[{"text": "Proximal convoluted tubule and distal convoluted tubule", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pistal convoluted tubule and distal convoluted tubule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proximal convoluted tubule and droximal convoluted tubule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proximal convoluted tubule and $$\\\\mathrm{LOH}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The descending limb continues as Proximated Convoluted Tubule (PCT) and the ascending limb continues as Distal Convoluted Tubule (DCT) to form a high coiled network of tubules in the kidney.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(9500,'biology',86,984,2289,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A specialised centre present in the medulla region of the brain called pneumotaxic centre is primarily responsible for this regulation.<br></div><div class="column-right">Another centre present in the pons region of the brain called respiratory rhythm centre can moderate the functions of the previous centre.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both are wrong. A specialised centre present in the medulla region of the brain called respiratory rhythm centre is primarily responsible for this regulation.<br>Another centre present in the pons region of the brain called pneumotaxic centre can moderate the functions of the respiratory rhythm centre.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9502,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Electrocardiogram is a measure of','[{"text": "Heart rate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ventricular contraction rate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atrial filling", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electrical activity of heart", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'ECG is a graphical representation of the electrical activity of the heart during a cardiac cycle.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9504,'biology',86,983,2288,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ is carried in the plasma mainly as $$\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}$$ions.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Zinc containing enzyme carbonic anhydrase of Rbcs catalyses the formation of $$\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}{ }^{-}$$ions that enter plasma.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'An anhydrase is defined as an enzyme that catalyses the removal of a water molecule from a compound, and so it is this "reverse" reaction that gives carbonic anhydrase its name, because it removes a water molecule from carbonic acid. The carbonic anhydrases (or carbonate dehydratases) form a family of enzymes that catalyse the rapid interconversion of carbon dioxide and water to bicarbonate and protons (or vice versa), a reversible reaction that occurs relatively slowly in the absence of a catalyst. The active site of most carbonic anhydrases contains a zinc ion; they are therefore, classified as metalloenzymes.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9505,'biology',84,579,1159,'Choose the correctly matched pair:<br><div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Tendon-Specialised connective tissue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adipose tissue - Dense connective tissue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Areolar tissue - Loose connective tissue", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cartilage - Loose connective tissue", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Adipose tissue is a type of loose connective tissue located mainly beneath the cells. Cartilage is a type of specialised connective tissue. Tendon is dense regular connective tissue.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9506,'biology',87,1129,2701,'To obtain standard ECG a patient is connected to the machine with three electrical leads attached to the following parts except','[{"text": "Right ankle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Left wrist", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Right wrist", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Left ankle", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'To obtain standard ECG a patient is connected to the machine with three electrical leads attached to the following parts - one to each wrist and to the left ankle',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9507,'biology',88,1151,2758,'A part of nephron is situated in cortex completely<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{{\\rm{i}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Renal}}\\,{\\rm{corpuscle}}}&{{\\rm{ii}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{PCT}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}\\\\\n{\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{iii}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{DCT}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}\n\\end{array}}&{\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{iv}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Loop}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{Henle}}}\n\\end{array}}\\\\\n{\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{v}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Collecting}}\\,{\\rm{duct}}}\n\\end{array}}&{}\n\\end{array}$','[{"text": "i and ii only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, ii and iii only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "i, ii, iv and v only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii, iii and v only", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The Malpighian corpuscle, PCT and DCT of the nephron are situated in the cortical region of the kidney whereas the loop of Henle dips into the medulla.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9508,'biology',86,979,2284,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Bacteria that reach the lungs rarely survive.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Alveoli of normal lungs are lined by a film of lecithin which kills the bacteria.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Lecithin is the substance of surfactant produced in the foetal lungs to regulate the surface tension of fluid in lungs. Lecithin increases resistance to disease by its function in thymus gland. Essential Fatty Acids induce our immune cells to kill bacteria. Here fatty acid peroxides produce bacteriocidal hydrogen peroxide. But there are some bacteria by secreting lecithinase convert lecithin into chemicals which may cause heart disease.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9510,'biology',87,1129,2701,'QRS wave represents','[{"text": "Atrial electrical activity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ventricular electrical activity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Spread of impulse from SA node", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The QRS complex represents the depolarisation of the ventricles, which initiates the ventricular contraction. The contraction starts shortly after Q and marks the beginning of the systole.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9511,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Select the incorrect statement from the following:','[{"text": "The DCTs of many nephrons opens into a straight tube called collecting duct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In cortical nephrons (majority), the loop of Henle is too short and extended only very little in medulla.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In juxta medullary nephrons (minority), the loop of Henle is very long and runs deeply into medulla.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vasa recta is not a part of peritubular network.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The efferent arteriole emerging from the glomerulus forms a fine capillary network around the renal tubule called the peritubular capillaries. A minute vessel of this network runs parallel to the Henle\'s loop forming a \' $$U$$ \' shaped vasa recta.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9531,'biology',86,983,2288,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Carbamino- haemoglobin transports $20-25 \\%$ of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in blood<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binds reversibly to amino groups of polypeptide chains of haemoglobin.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'&nbsp;Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9512,'biology',86,979,2283,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Earthworms come out of their burrows on rainy days.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Rain water fills up their burrows which deprive them of $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Earthworms, as the common name implies, live underground, and like most terrestrial creatures, breath air. Oxygen uptake, however takes place through the body wall, as earthworms lack lungs. When it rains, the water flows into their burrows (their wormy homes), blocking off their air supply. As you can imagine, this causes the worms a considerable amount of stress. So, the earthworms that you see during or after a big rain are those that either fled to the surface for a breath of air or those that drowned and were washed from their burrows (poor worms).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9513,'biology',84,579,1158,'The function of the gap junction is to:<br>','[{"text": "Stop substance from leaking across a tissue.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Performing cementing to keep neighbouring cells together.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Facilitate communication between adjoining cells by connecting the cytoplasm for rapid transfer of ions, small molecules and some large molecules.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Separate two cells from each other.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Gap junctions are communicating junctions in animals which facilitate communication between two adjoining cells by protein bridges for rapid transfer of ions, small molecules and large molecules.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9514,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Which wave in ECG indicates development of electrical depolarisation in SA node fibres?','[{"text": "P-wave", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Q-wave", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "R-wave", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "S-wave", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The $$P$$-wave represents the electrical excitation (or depolarisation) of the atria, which leads to the contraction of both the atria',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9515,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Glomerulus along with Bowman\'s capsule is called','[{"text": "Renal corpuscle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Malpighian tubule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Malpighian body", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Glomerulus alongwith Bowman\'s capsule, is called the malpighian body or renal corpuscle.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9516,'biology',86,979,2283,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Respiration is most efficient in the insects.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In insects air is carried directly to the cells by tracheoles.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Respiration is more efficient in insects because of following reasons An internal gas exchange system with an impermeable exoskeleton and spiracles that can open and close helps to protect gas exchange system and prevent water loss, allowing insects to live in some of the driest places on earth.<br>• Short diffusion distance for gases to move through tracheoles means that no transport system is required (saves space, weight and energy). Oxygen moves down tracheoles by passive diffusion, dissolves into the fluid at the end of each tube and diffuses directly into each cell. Therefore, oxygen reaches the respiring cells more quickly.<br>• Rhythmic contractions of abdominal muscles compress air sacs increasing ventilation which helps to maintain a concentration gradient during vigorous activity such as flying. This increases the rate of gas exchange when the insect is more active and has an increased metabolic demand (needs more oxygen).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9518,'biology',87,1129,2701,'P-wave represents','[{"text": "Polarization of ventricles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Repolarization of atria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Repolarization of ventricle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depolarization of atria", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The $$\\mathrm{P}$$-wave represents the electrical excitation (or depolarisation) of the atria, which leads to the contraction of both the atria',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9519,'biology',88,1151,2758,'____ A ____ is a tuft of capillaries formed by the ____ B ____ arteriole - a fine branch of ____C ____ Blood from ____ A  ____ is carried away by an ____D ____ arteriole.','[{"text": "Glomerulus, afferent, renal vein and efferent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glomerulus, efferent, renal artery and afferent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glomerulus, afferent, renal artery and efferent", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Glomerulus, efferent, renal vein and afferent", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Glomerulus is a tuft of capillaries formed by the afferent arteriole - a fine branch of renal artery. Blood from the glomerulus is carried away by an efferent arteriole.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9520,'biology',86,979,2283,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> All the terrestrial vertebrates are air breathers.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> They have developed lungs for breathing an adaptation for land life.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Terrestrial vertebrates have developed lungs as their respiratory organs. They breathe in air through nostrils which later enters the lungs. There is utilisation of oxygen from air to exchange with carbon dioxide from tissues. This gaseous exchange happens in the lungs. Aquatic animals have special structures called as gills for respiration. These aquatic organisms take in water through their mouth. Dissolved oxygen in water is utilised near the gills to be exchanged with carbon dioxide from tissues.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9521,'biology',84,579,1160,'Which type of tissue correctly matches with its location?<br>&emsp; &emsp; \\[{\\rm{Tissue}}\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;{\\rm{Location}}\\]<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Areolar}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{tissue}}}&{\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Tendons}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Transitional}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Urinary}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{bladder}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{epithelium}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Cuboidal}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Lining}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{stomach}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{epithelium}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Smooth}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{muscle}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{Wall}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{abdomen}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Transitional epithelium is present in urinary bladder.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9522,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Identify the correct statement<BR>I. The RST complex represents initiation of the ventricular contraction.<BR>II. The contraction starts shortly after Q and marks the beginning of the systole','[{"text": "I is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II is correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'a. The QRS complex represents initiation of the ventricular contraction.<br>b. The contraction starts shortly after Q and marks the beginning of the systole.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9523,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Structural and functional unit of kidney is the','[{"text": "Neuron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nephridia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nephron", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hilum", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The structural and functional units of kidney are nephrons. Each kidney has nearly one million nephrons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9524,'biology',86,984,2289,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Respiratory centres of medulla oblongata regulate breathing rate.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ level of arterial blood control rate of breathing.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Majority of carbon dioxide molecules (85percent) are carried as part of the bicarbonate buffer system. In this system, carbon dioxide diffuses into the red blood cells. Carbonic anhydrase (CA) within the red blood cells quickly converts the carbon dioxide into carbonic acid $$\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}\\right)$$. Carbonic acid is an unstable, intermediate molecule that immediately dissociates into bicarbonate ions $$\\left(\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\\right)$$and hydrogen $$\\left(\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right)$$ions. Since carbon dioxide is quickly converted into bicarbonate ions, this reaction allows for the continued uptake of carbon dioxide into the blood, down its concentration gradient.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9526,'biology',87,1129,2701,'T-wave on an ECG represents','[{"text": "Depolarization of ventricles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Repolarization of ventricles", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Repolarization of atria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depolrization of atria", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The T-wave represents the return of the ventricles from excited to normal state (repolarisation). The end of the T-wave marks the end of systole.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9527,'biology',88,1151,2758,'The human kidney has about','[{"text": "One million nephrons", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ten billion nephrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three thousand nephrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Variable according to age", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Each kidney holds a total of one million nephrons. They are the functional units of a kidney.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9528,'biology',86,980,2285,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Mammals perform negative pressure breathing<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Using muscular movement mammals create a pressure gradient such that they can perform inspiration and expiration','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Mammals can eat and breathe at the same time because mammals have a negative pressure breathing.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9530,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. ECG<br>B. P-wave<br>C. QRS complex<br>D. T- wave</div><div class="column-right">P. Depolarisation of the ventricles<br>Q. Representation of electrical activity of heart during cardiac cycle<br>R. Repolarisation of the ventricles<br>S. Depolarisation of the atria</div></div>','[{"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-R, D-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-S, B-Q, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-P, B-R, C-S, D-Q", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'ECG - Representation of electrical activity of heart during cardiac cycle<br>P-wave - Depolarisation of the atria<br>QRS complex - Depolarisation of ventricles,<br>T-wave - Repolarization of the ventricles',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9533,'biology',84,583,1169,'Frog\'s heart when taken out of the body continues to beat for sometime. Select the best option from the following statements.<br>(A) Frog is a poikilotherm<br>(B) Frog does not have any coronary circulation.<br>(C) Heart is "myogenic" in nature.<br>(D) Heart is autoexcitable','[{"text": "A and B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C and D", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Frog heart is myogenic and autoexcitable. In this conditions, contraction of the heart originates within the muscles itself. When muscles are contracting. They are releasing heat, which keeps the electrochemical reactions in muscles going so the muscles of heart keep contracting after the removal of heart from the body.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9534,'biology',87,1129,2701,'The following figure shows graphical representation of ECG. Identify the waves of ECG (A-C)-','[{"text": "A- QRS complex, B- T-wave, C- P-wave", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A- QRS complex, B- P-wave, C- T-wave", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A- P-wave, B- QRS complex, C- T-wave", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A- T-wave, B- P-wave, C- QRS complex", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The following figure shows graphical representation of ECG. The waves of ECG (A-C) can be labelled as A-P wave, B-QRS complex, C- T-wave.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/87_9534_question_1.png'),
(9535,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Given diagram is longitudinal section of kidney. Identify the labeled (A-D) parts?','[{"text": "A-Cortex, B-Renal capsule, C-Renal column, D-Medullary pyramids", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-Renal column, B-Medullary pyramids, C- Renal capsule, D- Cortex", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Medullary pyramids, B-Renal column, C- Cortex, D-Renal capsule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Renal capsule, B-Cortex, C-Medullary pyramids, D-Renal column", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A. Cortex<BR>B. Renal Capsule<BR>C. Renal Column<BR>D. Medullary Pyramids',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/88_9535_question_1.png'),
(9536,'biology',86,979,2284,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Human lungs have enormous surface area for the exchange of gases.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In human, glottis is guarded by epiglottis.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Gas exchange between the air that humans breathe in and the blood within the circulatory system is vital to survival. Gas exchange describes the exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide across the respiratory membrane. Due to the many surfacewalls of the alveoli, the lungs have a surface area that is approximately the size of a tennis court. This large surface area allows for rapid gas exchange. Carbon is diffused into the alveoli and oxygen is diffused out from the alveoli into the blood. Each alveolus is connected to a web of capillaries which bring oxygen-deficient red blood cells close to the alveoli so that they may absorb diffused oxygen. This intricate and expansive capillary network allows for the continuous exchange of large volumes of gas. The glottis is guarded by epiglottis because it prevents the entry of food in wind pipe. If the food enters wind pipe, then it chokes it, leading to the death of the person.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9537,'biology',87,1129,2701,'The following figure shows graphical representation of ECG. Identify the electrical activity of cardiac cycle in the given graph $$(\\mathrm{A}-\\mathrm{C})$$ ?','[{"text": "A- depolarisation of atria,<br>B- depolarisation of ventricles,<br>C- repolarisation of ventricles", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A- depolarisation of ventricles,<br>B- repolarisation of ventricles,<br>C- depolarisation of atria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-depolarisation of ventricles,<br>B- depolarisation of atria,<br>C- repolarisation of ventricles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A- repolarisation of ventricles,<br>B- depolarisation of ventricles,<br>C- depolarisation of atria", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The following figure shows graphical representation of ECG. The electrical activity of cardiac cycle in the given graph (A-C) can be labelled as - A: depolarisation of atria, B: depolarization of ventricles, $$\\mathrm{C}$$ : repolarization of ventricles.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/87_9537_question_1.png'),
(9539,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Which is the correct order for the path taken by urine after it leaves the kidney?','[{"text": "$$1,2,3$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3,2,1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2,3,1$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$2,1,3$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The path followed by urine after it leaves the kidney is - Kidney $$>$$ (2) Ureters $$>$$ (3) Urinary Bladder $$>$$ (1) Urethra',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/88_9539_question_1.png'),
(9540,'biology',86,979,2284,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Larynx is a cartilaginous box which help in sound production.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Epiglottis flap covers the glottis during swallowing and prevents the entry of food into the larynx.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Larynx: Nasopharynx opens through glottis of the larynx into the trachea. Larynx is a cartilaginous box which helps in sound production. Due to this, larynx is also called the sound box. There is a thin elastic cartilaginous flap; called epiglottis. The epiglottis covers the glottis during swallowing. This prevents the entry of food into the larynx.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9570,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Hepatic portal system starts from','[{"text": "Digestive system to liver", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Kidney to liver", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Liver to heart", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heart to liver", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A unique vascular connection exists between the digestive tract and liver called hepatic portal system. The hepatic portal vein carries blood from intestine to the liver before it is delivered to the systemic circulation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9542,'biology',87,1130,2702,'How many double circulation are normally completed by the human heart in one minute?','[{"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "36", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "72", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'\' 72 \' double circulation are normally completed by the human heart in one minute.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9543,'biology',86,980,2285,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Inspiration can occur if intrapulmonary pressure is less than the atmospheric pressure.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Inspiration is initiated by the relaxation of diaphragm and external intercostal muscle.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Intrapulmonary pressure is the pressure within the lungs. This pressure varies between inspiration and expiration. During inspiration intrapulmonary pressure becomes less than atmospheric pressure, and during expiration it becomes greater than atmospheric pressure. When a person takes a breath, air is moved into the lungs because the diaphragm and the intercostal muscles contract, causing an increase in the thoracic volume. According to Boyle\'s law there is an inverse relationship between the volume and pressure of gases; therefore, an increase in the volume inside of the thorax causes a decrease in the pressure inside of the thorax.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9544,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Identify correctly the labeled parts in the given diagram?<br>','[{"text": "A-Adrenal gland, B-Pelvis, C-Medulla, D-Cortex", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-Cortex, B-Medulla, C-Pelvis, D-Adrenal gland", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Pelvis, B-Cortex, C-Medulla, D- Adrenal gland", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Pelvis, B-Adrenal gland, C-Cortex, D-Medulla", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A. Adrenal Gland<BR>B. Renal Pelvis<BR>C. Medulla<BR>D. Cortex',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/88_9544_question_1.png'),
(9545,'biology',84,579,1158,'Cuboidal epithelium with brush border of microvilli is found in:<br>','[{"text": "Ducts of salivary glands", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proximal convoluted tubule of nephron", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Eustachian tube", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lining of intestine", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Proximal convoluted tubule of nephron',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9546,'biology',87,1130,2702,'The left side of heart, the deoxygenated blood comes from','[{"text": "Lungs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Liver", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Kidneys", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The left side of heart, the oxygenated blood comes from Lungs.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9547,'biology',86,984,2289,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A chemosensitive area is situated adjacent to the rhythm centre which is highly sensitive to $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ and hydrogen ions.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The role of oxygen in the regulated of respiratory rhythm is quite insignificant.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A chemosensitive area is situated adjacent to the rhythm centre. This is highly sensitive to $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ and hydrogen ions. Increase in these substances can activate this chemosensitive area. This; in turn; gives signal to the rhythm centre to make necessary adjustments in the respiratory process so that these substances can be eliminated. The role of oxygen in the regulation of respiratory rhythm is quite insignificant because there is no receptor for oxygen in carotid artery and aortic arch which sends information to respiratory rhythm centre for regulation of respiratory rhythm. There are receptors carbon dioxide and hydrogen ion in oxygen in carotid artery and aortic arch. As soon as they bind, signals are sent to respiratory rhythm centre to control the respiratory process. Therefore role of oxygen is controlling respiratory rhythm process insignificantly.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9548,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Identify correctly labeled parts in the following diagram?','[{"text": "A- Ureter, B- Urinary bladder, C- Urethra, D- Kidney", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Kidney, B-Ureter, C-Urinary bladder, D-Urethra", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A- Urinary bladder, B- Ureter, C- Kidney, D- Urethra", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A- Urethra, B- Kidney, C- Urinary bladder, D- Ureter", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A. Kidney<BR>B. Ureter<BR>C. Urinary bladder<BR>D. Urethra',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/88_9548_question_1.png'),
(9549,'biology',84,579,1160,'Which of the following statements wrongly represents the nature of smooth muscle?','[{"text": "These muscle have no striations", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They are involuntary muscles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Communication among the cells is performed by intercalated discs", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "These muscles are present in the wall of blood vessels", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Intercalated discs are the communication junction between the cells of cardiac muscles not by the smooth muscles. So, it is wrongly matched.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9550,'biology',87,1130,2702,'The oxygenated blood first is available in','[{"text": "Pulmonary artery", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aorta", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pulmonary vein", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of them", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The oxygenated blood first is available in pulmonary vein .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9602,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Capillaries are made up of','[{"text": "Many layers of endothelial cell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Single layer of endothelial cell", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Few layers of endothelial cell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of them", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Single layer of endothelial cell is present in capillaries.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9551,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Carefully observe the given figure and select the correct option?<br>I. In humans, the excretory system consists of a pair of kidneys, a pair of ureter, a urinary bladder and a urethra.<br>II. Kidneys are reddish brown, bean shaped structures between the levels of last thoracic and third lumbar vertebra close to the dorsal inner wall of the abdominal cavity.<br>III. The pineal gland is located on the head of kidney.<br>IV. Each kidney has nearly one million complex tubular structures called neurons.','[{"text": "I, III, IV only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, III only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The adrenal glands are located on the head of each of the kidney. Each kidney has nearly one million complex tubular structures called nephrons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/88_9551_question_1.png'),
(9552,'biology',86,983,2288,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Haemoglobin cannot get saturated with oxygen more than $$97 \\%$$<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> With a rise in $$\\mathrm{PO}_{2}$$, the affinity between $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ and haemoglobin also increases.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Haemoglobin cannot get saturated with oxygen more than $$97 \\%$$. With a rise in $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$, the affinity between $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ and haemoglobin also increases. Both the statements are correct but reason is not correct explanation of assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9554,'biology',87,1130,2702,'A vein that collects blood from all the parts of the body ____','[{"text": "Pulmonary vein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Coronary vein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carotid vein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vena cava", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Vena cava is the vessel that carries deoxygenated blood from all the parts of the body.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9555,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Identify the given diagram, whose function is to remove nitrogenous wastes from the body?','[{"text": "Human excretory system", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Human respiratory system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Human reproductive system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 3", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The given figure represents the Human Excretory System. It consists of a pair of kidneys, one pair of ureters, a urinary bladder and a urethra.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/88_9555_question_1.png'),
(9556,'biology',86,979,2284,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Mammals can eat while breathing<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Mammals have negative pressure breathing','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In mammals it is the secondary palate that permits breathing while eating. Clearly, breathing while eating is necessary to maintain the high rate of metabolism of mammals. Mammals possess negative pressure breathing (air is sucked into the lungs) Breathing in humans is dependent on regular contractions of the thoracic muscles and the diaphragm.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9557,'biology',84,579,1159,'Which of the following is present between th adjacent bones of the vertebral column?<br><div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Cartilage", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Areolar tissue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Smooth muscle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Intercalated discs", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The adjacent vertebrae of the vertebral column are separated by intervertebral discs made up of fibrous cartilage.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9558,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Rishi is studying the different pipelines in her society, she finds a clean water pipe-A, a drainage pipe -B, a STP- C, a clean water tank -D. Can you guess what does this alphabets imply with respect to our body.','[{"text": "A- Pulmonary vein, B- Pulmonary artery, C- Lungs, D- Left atrium", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-Coronary vein, B- Pulmonary artery, C- Lungs, D- Left atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A- Pulmonary vein, B- Vena cava, C- Lungs, D- Right atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A- Vena cava, B- Pulmonary artery, C- Lungs, D-Right atrium", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Rishi is studying the different pipelines in her society, she finds a clean water pipe-A- Pulmonary vein- carrying oxygenated blood, a drainage pipe -B - Pulmonary artery- carries deoxygenated blood, a STP- C - Lungspurifies the blood, a clean water tank -D - Left Atrium -receives oxygenated blood.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9559,'biology',86,985,2290,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Symptoms of emphysema develops when a person living on plains ascends and stays on a mountain.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Air pressure and partial pressure of oxygen falls with the rise in altitude.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When a person living on plains ascend and stays on a mountain above 8000 feet from the sea level, he develops symptoms of mountain sickness which includes breathlessness, headache, dizziness, irritability, nausea, vomiting, mental fatigue and a bluish tinge on the skin, nails and lips.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(10145,'biology',89,1287,3108,'Motor end plate is a','[{"text": "Neuromuscular junction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Plate of motor neuron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Motor neuron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "End of the motor neuron", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The junction between a motor neuron and the sarcolemma of the muscle fibre is called the neuromuscular junction or motor-end plate.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9560,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Kidneys<br>B. Renal pelvis<br>C. Hilum<br>D. Columns of bertini</div><div class="column-right">P. Broad funnel shaped space<br>Q. Notch present towards the centre of the inner concave surface of the kidney<br>R. Situated between the levels of last thoracic and third lumbar vertebrae<br>S. Present between medullary pyramids</div></div>','[{"text": "A-P, B-R, C-Q, D-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-R, B-P, C-Q, D-S", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-S, B-Q, C-R, D-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-R, B-Q, C-P, D-S", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>A. Kidneys - R. Situated between the levels of last thoracic and third lumbar vertebrae<br>B. Renal pelvis - P. Broad funnel shaped space<br>C. Hilum - Q. Notch present towards the centre of the inner concave surface of the kidney<br>D. Columns of Bertini - S. Present between medullary pyramids',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9562,'biology',87,1130,2702,'A vein that starts as capillaries and ends as capillary network is called','[{"text": "Pulmonary vein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Coronary vein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carotid vein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Portal vein", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A vein that collects blood from one network of capillaries and transports it directly to a second capillary network in another region of the body, without returning to the heart is called as Portal vein.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9563,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Which of the following is correct about human kidney?','[{"text": "Kidney is covered by tough capsule", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Kidney is divided into cortex and medulla on the outer side", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The hilum is extended in between the medullary pyramid and the renal column of bertini.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Kidney synthesises and excretes urea.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Kidney is covered by tough capsule. Inside the kidney, there are two zones, an outer cortex and an inner medulla. The cortex extends in between the medullary pyramids as renal columns called Columns of Bertini.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9564,'biology',86,983,2288,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ transport occurs very fast through RBC<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enzyme carbonic anhydrase is absent in the blood plasma','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Carbonic anhydrase enzyme is present in high quantity in $$\\mathrm{RBC}$$ and it is present in minute quantity in plasma. Carbonic anhydrase enzyme catalyses formation and dissociation of carbonic acid. <img src=\'/img/data/biology/86_9564_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9565,'biology',84,579,1159,'Which of the following is not a connective tissue?','[{"text": "Adipose tissue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cartilage", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neuroglia", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Blood<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Neuroglia are the supporting cells of the nervous tissue. They protect and support neurons. Neuroglia make up more than onehalf the volume of neural tissue in our body. Adipose tissue is a loose connective tissue. Cartilage is a skeletal tissue (a type of connective tissue). Blood is a fluid connective tissue.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9566,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Humans have double circulation. It means','[{"text": "Blood flows in both side of heart", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blood is of two types- oxygenated and deoxygenated", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There has two important chambers- atrias and ventricles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blood circulates twice through the heart.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Humans have double circulation. It means blood passes through the heart twice per circuit. The right pump sends deoxygenated blood to the lungs where it becomes oxygenated and returns back to the heart. The left pump sends the newly oxygenated blood around the body.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9567,'biology',86,979,2284,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Trachea and bronchioles are supported by elastic cartilage<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> This support of incomplete cartilages prevents collapse of wind pipe during respiration.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Trachea is the tube that conducts air, it has noncollapsible walls, due to presence of cartilaginous C-shaped rings, it is mainly hyaline cartilage. Trachea enters into lungs after branching into bronchus, it further divides into bronchioles. Branchioles do not have cartilaginous rings. Bronchi walls are supported by cartilaginous rings.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9568,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Which of the following is correct about hilum of kidney?','[{"text": "It is present on the convex outer surface.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is notch through which urethra, nerve and blood vessel enter.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is notch through which uterus, nerve and blood vessel enter.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None is correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Hilum is a notch towards the centre of the inner concave surface of the kidney through which the ureters, blood vessels and nerves enter into the kidneys.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9569,'biology',84,579,1159,'Match Column - I with Column - II.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Bronchioles<br>B. Goblet cell<br>C.Tendons<br>D Adipose Tissue</div><div class="column-right">P.  Dense Regular Connective Tissue<br>Q. Loose Connective Tissue<br>R.Glandular Tissue<br>S. Ciliated Epithelium</div></div>','[{"text": "A - P, B - Q, C - R, D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q, B - P, C - S, D - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - S, C - Q, D - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S, B - R, C - P, D - Q", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>Bronchioles - Ciliated Epithelium<br>Goblet cell - Glandular tissue<br>Tendons - Dense regular connective tissue Adipose tissue - Loose connective tissue',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9571,'biology',86,983,2288,'People living at sea level have around 5 million RBC per cubic millimetre of their blood whereas those living at an altitude of 5400 metre have around 8 million. This is because at high altitude<br>','[{"text": "Atmospheric $$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ level is less and hence, more Rbcs are needed to absorb the required amount of $$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ to survive", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "There is more UV radiation which enhances Rbc production", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "People eat more nutritive food, therefore, more Rbcs are formed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "People get pollution-free air to breathe and more oxygen is available", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At high altitude, the atmospheric $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ level is less and hence, more RBCs are needed to absorb the required amount of $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ to survive. That is why, the people living at sea level have around 5 million RBC/mm3 of their blood whereas, those living at an altitude of 5400 meter have around 8 million $$\\mathrm{RBC} / \\mathrm{mm} 3$$ of their blood.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2006,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9572,'biology',88,1151,2758,'The cortical part that extends between medullary pyramids as renal columns are called<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Columns of renalini", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Columns of martini", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Columns of bertini", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Columns of bellini", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The cortex extends in between the medullary pyramids as renal columns called Columns of Bertini.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9574,'biology',87,1130,2702,'A unique vascular connection that exist between the digestive tract and liver is called','[{"text": "Gastric portal system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hypothalamic-Hypophyseal portal system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hepatic portal system", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Renal portal system", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A unique vascular connection exists between the digestive tract and liver called hepatic portal system. The hepatic portal vein carries blood from intestine to the liver before it is delivered to the systemic circulation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9575,'biology',86,983,2288,'The haemoglobin of a human foetus<br>','[{"text": "Has a lower affinity for oxygen than that of the adult", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its affinity for oxygen is the same as that of an adult", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Has only b protein sub-units instead of $$d$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Has a higher affinity for oxygen than that of an adult", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Haemoglobin is the protein that makes red blood corpuscles red, binds easily and reversibly with oxygen. Normal values for haemoglobin are $$14-20 \\mathrm{~g} / 100 \\mathrm{ml}$$ of blood in infants, $$13-18 \\mathrm{~g} / 100 \\mathrm{ml}$$ in adult male and 12-16 $$\\mathrm{g} / 100 \\mathrm{ml}$$ in adult females. Foetal haemoglobin does not have sickle-shaped cells even in those destined to have sickle-cell anaemia, i.e., haemoglobin of foetus has higher affinity of oxygen than that of an adult.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9576,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Inner to the hilum is a broad funnel shaped space called the ____ with projections called ____','[{"text": "Renal pelvis, calyces", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Renal medulla, notch", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Renal cortex, calyces", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Renal pelvis, notch", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Inner to the hilum is a broad funnel shaped space called the renal pelvis with projections called calyces.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9578,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Pulmonary circulation means','[{"text": "Nutrient supply to lungs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Circulation in heart -oxygenated and deoxygenated blood", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Circulation in lungs -oxygenated and deoxygenated blood", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Nutrient supply to heart", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The blood pumped by the right ventricle enters the pulmonary artery, whereas the left ventricle pumps blood into the aorta. The deoxygenated blood pumped into the pulmonary artery is passed on to the lungs from where the oxygenated blood is carried by the pulmonary veins into the left atrium. This pathway constitutes the pulmonary circulation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9579,'biology',88,1151,2758,'The medullary pyramids project into the','[{"text": "Calyx", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cortex", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Renal pelvis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The medullary pyramids project into the calyces (sing. calyx). The kidney is divided into outer cortex and inner medulla. The medulla is divided into conical masses called medullary pyramids.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9580,'biology',86,981,2286,'What is the vital capacity of our lungs?<br>','[{"text": "Inspiratory reserve volumes plus tidal volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Total lung capacity minus expiratory reserve volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inspiratory reserve volume plus expiratory reserve volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Total lung capacity minus residual volume", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Vital capacity is the sum of inspiratory reserve volume, tidal volume and expiratory reserve volume, it is about $$4800 \\mathrm{ml}$$. Total lung capacity is the sum of vital capacity and residual volume, i.e., vital capacity of our lung is total lung capacity minus residual volumes. Tidal volume is the amount of air which normally passes into and out of the lung cycle of quite breathing. It is about $$800 \\mathrm{ml}$$ in adult person. Inspiratory reserve volume is the extra volume is the lung of air that can be inhaled into lungs during deepest possible inspiration.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9582,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Pulmonary system consists of','[{"text": "Portal system, Pulmonary capillaries", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Coronary system, Pulmonary artery, Pulmonary capillaries", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pulmonary veins, Pulmonary artery, Pulmonary capillaries", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pulmonary veins, Pulmonary artery,", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Pulmonary veins, Pulmonary artery, pulmonary capillaries are a part of the pulmonary system.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9583,'biology',88,1151,2758,'The outer layer of kidney is a ____. Inside the kidney, there are two zones, an inner ____ and an outer ____','[{"text": "Thin capsule, medulla, cortex", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tough capsule, cortex, medulla", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tough capsule, medulla, cortex", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Thin capsule, cortex, medulla", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The outer layer of kidney is a tough capsule. Inside the kidney, there are two zones, an outer cortex and an inner medulla.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9584,'biology',86,983,2288,'Which of the following factors are favourable for the formation of oxyhaemoglobin?<br>Respiratory capacities Respiratory volumes<br>A. High $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$, low $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$<br>B. Low $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$, low $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$<br>C. Lesser $$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$cone, lower temperature<br>D. High $$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$cone, higher temperature','[{"text": "(A) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(A) and (C)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(B) and (C)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(C) and (D)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Association of $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ with $$\\mathrm{Hb}$$ is favoured by,<BR>a. Increase in $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$.<br>b. Decrease in $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$ (Both effect).<br>c. Decrease in body temperature.<br>d. Decrease in $$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$ion concentration. e. Increase in $$\\mathrm{pH}$$.<br>f. Decrese in 2,3 diphosphoglycerate',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9586,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Identify the system which supplies blood to muscles and different parts of heart','[{"text": "Portal system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Coronary system", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pulmonary system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hepatic portal system", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A special coronary system of blood vessels is present in our body exclusively for the circulation of blood to and from the cardiac musculature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9587,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Part of the kidney through which the ureter, blood vessels and nerves enter into it is ____ and is located towards the ____','[{"text": "Hilum, outer concave surface of the kidney", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hilum, inner convex surface of the kidney", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hilum, inner concave surface of the kidney", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hilum, outer convex surface of the kidney", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Hilum is a notch towards the centre of the inner concave surface of the kidney through which the ureters, blood vessels and nerves enter into the kidneys.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9588,'biology',86,983,2288,'Which two of the following changes (a-d) usually tend to occur in the plain dwellers when they move to high altitudes (1,500 m or more)?<br>a. Increase in red blood cell size<br>b. Increase red blood cell production<br>c. Increased breathing rate<br>d. Increase in thrombocyte count changes occurring are','[{"text": "b and c", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "c and d", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a and d", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a and b", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a person moves to higher altitude, the $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$ and total atmospheric pressure decrease. Hypoxia stimulates the JG-cells of the kidney to release erythropoietin hormone which stimulates erythropoesis in bone marrow causing polythemia (increase in $$\\mathrm{RBCs}$$ production). Hypoxia will also increase breathing rate. Initially the size of RBCs will also increase but with increase in the number of RBCs, the size of RBCs become normal.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9590,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Identify the correct statement<br>I. The oxygenated blood is emptied into the right atrium.<br>II. The hepatic portal vein carries blood from intestine to the liver before it is delivered to the systemic circulation.','[{"text": "I is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II is correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The deoxygenated blood is emptied into the right atrium.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(10845,'biology',9,26,49,'The formation of spores by the spore mother cells is the result of','[{"text": "Mitosis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Meiosis followed by mitosis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Meiosis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Amitosis", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The sporangia produce spores by meiosis in spore mother cells.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9591,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Kidneys in human is situated between ____.','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{T}}_{{\\\\rm{12}}}}{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{{\\\\rm{L}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{T}}_{{\\\\rm{10}}}}{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{{\\\\rm{L}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{T}}_{{\\\\rm{12}}}}{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{{\\\\rm{L}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{T}}_3}{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{{\\\\rm{L}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Kidneys in human is situated between ${{\\rm{T}}_{{\\rm{12}}}}{\\rm{ - }}\\,{{\\rm{L}}_{\\rm{3}}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9592,'biology',86,979,2284,'Two friends are eating together on a dining table. One of them suddenly starts coughing while swallowing some food. This coughing would have been due to improper movement of','[{"text": "Diaphragm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neck", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tongue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Epiglottis<br>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The epiglottis is a flap that is made of elastic cartilage tissue covered with a mucous membrane attached to the entrance of the larynx. It prevents the entry of food into the larnyx and directs it to the oesophagus. Due to the improper movement of epiglottis, one may suddenly start coughing while swallowing some food.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9593,'biology',84,579,1159,'Match Column- I with Column- II.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options give below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Mast cells<br>B. Inner surface of bronchiole<br>C. Blood<br>D. Tubular parts of nephron</div><div class="column-right">P. Ciliated epithelium<br>Q. Areolar connective tissue<br>R. Cuboidal epithelium<br>S. Specialised connective tissue</div></div>','[{"text": "A-P, B-Q, C-S, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-R, C-P, D-S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-P, C-S, D-R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-R, B-S, C-Q, D-P", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'● Areolar connective tissue contains fibroblasts (cells that produce and secrete fibres), macrophages and mast cells.<br>● Inner surface of bronchioles is lined by ciliated epithelium.<br>● Blood is a specialised connective tissue.<br>● Tubular parts of nephron are lined by cuboidal epithelium.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9594,'biology',87,1130,2702,'An artery is','[{"text": "Thick walled in which blood flows under low pressure.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thin walled in which blood flows under high pressure.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thin walled in which blood flows under low pressure.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thick walled in which blood flows under high pressure.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'An artery is thick walled in which blood flows under high pressure.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9595,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Kidneys are ____.','[{"text": "Reddish brown, bean shaped structures", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Close to the ventral inner wall of the abdominal cavity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None is correct", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Kidneys in human are reddish brown, bean shaped structures and close to the dorsal inner wall of the abdominal cavity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9596,'biology',86,985,2290,'Which one of the following is the correct statement for respiration in humans?<br>','[{"text": "Cigarette smoking may lead to inflammation of bronchi.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neural signals from pneumotoxic centre in pons region of brain can increase the duration of inspiration.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Workers in grinding and stone breaking industries may suffer from lung fibrosis.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "About $$90 \\\\%$$ of carbon dioxide $$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}\\\\right)$$ carried by haemoglobin.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Irritating gases, fumes, dust etc., present in the work place result in lung disorders. This is because the defence mechanism of the body cannot fully cope with this situation of so much dust. Long exposure can give to fibrous tissue) and thus causing serious lung damage.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9598,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Arteries differs from veins in having','[{"text": "Thicker walls", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Superficially present", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thin walls", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Internal valves", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Arteries differs from veins in having thicker walls because in arteries blood flows under high pressure.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9599,'biology',88,1151,2758,'In humans, the excretory system consists of','[{"text": "Unpaired kidneys", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pair of ureters", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A urinary bladder and a paired urethra", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In humans, the excretory system consists of a pair of kidneys, one pair of ureters, a urinary bladder and a urethra.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9600,'biology',86,983,2288,'Approximately seventy percent of carbon dioxide absorbed by the blood will be transported to lungs<br>','[{"text": "As bicarbonate ions", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In the form of dissolved gas molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "By binding to RBC", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "As carbaminohaemoglobin", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The largest dfraction of carbon dioxide i.e., (about 70%) is converted to bicarbonate $$\\left(\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}{ }^{-}\\right)$$and transported in the plasma. About $$\\left(-23 \\%\\right.$$ of $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ is carried by haemoglobin as carbamionohaemoglobin<br>$$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\\mathrm{Hb}$$ (haemoglobin) $$\\leftarrow \\mathrm{HbCO}_{3}$$ Carboamino haemoglobin',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9601,'biology',84,579,1159,'Given below are two statements:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Ligaments are dense irregular tissue.</div><div class="column-right">Cartilage is dense regular tissue.<br>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are\\ntrue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are\\nfalse", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is true but Statement B is\\nfalse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is false but Statement B is\\ntrue", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ligament is an example of dense regular connective tissue so Statement A is incorrect and cartilage is an example of specialised connective tissue and not dense regular tissue. Therefore Statement B is also incorrect',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9603,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Ammonotelic animals<br>B. Ureotelic animals<br>C. Uricotelic animals</div><div class="column-right">P. Mammals, marine fishes<br>Q. Reptiles, birds<br>R. Aquatic amphibians, aquatic insects</div></div>','[{"text": "A-P, B-R, C-Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-P, C-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-R, B-P, C-Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-P, B-Q, C-R", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A. Ammonotelic animals - R. Aquatic amphibians, Aquatic insects<BR>B. Ureotelic animals - P. Mammals, Marine fishes<BR>C. Uricotelic animals - Q. Reptiles, birds',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9604,'biology',86,984,2289,'When you hold your breath, which of the following gas changes in blood would first lead to the urge to breathe?<br>','[{"text": "Rising $$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ and falling $$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ concentration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Falling $$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ concentration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rising $$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ concentration", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Falling $$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ concentration", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When you hold your breath, falling $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ concentration in blood would first lead to the urge to breathe',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9605,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Arteries carry oxygenated blood except in','[{"text": "Pulmonary", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cardiac", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Umbilical", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Arteries carry oxygenated blood except in pulmonary and umbilical arteries.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9606,'biology',84,583,1167,'A gradual decrease in the size of the tail during metamorphosis in the life cycle of a frog is a good example for','[{"text": "Cell sensescence", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pinocytic activity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Programmed cell death", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cell nerosis", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The phenomenon of apoptosis was first discovered when while studying the metamorphosis of certain frogs, the gradual disapperance of the tadpole\'s tail as it morphed into a frog was observed - an example of programmed cell death essential to frog development. During apoptosis, enzymes called caspases are activated and destroy essential components that would otherwise keep the cell functional.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'kcet','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9607,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Green glands are excretory organs of','[{"text": "Moths", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Prawns", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Scorpions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Spiders", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Green glands or antennal glands perform the excretory function in crustaceans like prawns.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9608,'biology',86,985,2290,'Name the pulmonary disease in which alveolar surface area involved in gas exchange is drastically reduced due to damage in the alveolar walls.<br>','[{"text": "Pleurisy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Emphysema", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pneumonia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "asthma", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Empysema is a chronic respiratory disease where there is over-inflation of the air sacs (alveoli) in the lung, causing a decrease in lung function and often, breathlessness. In this disease, the alveolar walls are damaged leading to drastic reduction in gas exchange.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9609,'biology',87,1130,2702,'The systemic circulation provides','[{"text": "Nutrients, substances that have to be eliminated", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$&nbsp;", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hormones", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The systemic circulation provides nutrients, substances that have to be eliminated, $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$, hormones etc.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9610,'biology',84,583,1168,'Select the correct statement with regard to respiration in frog.','[{"text": "The lungs are a pair of elongated, pink coloured sac-like structures.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The lungs are present in the lower part of the trunk.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "During aestivation and hibernation, frogs do not respire.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "On land, frogs perform pulmonary respiration only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The lungs of frog area pair of elongated, pink coloured sac-like structures. They are present in the upper part of the trunk region (thorax). During aestivation and hibernation frog performs cutaneous respiration. On land, frog performs buccal respiration and cutaneous respiration, in addition to pulmonary respiration.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9611,'biology',88,1150,2757,'The excretory organs in prawn are','[{"text": "Malpighian tubules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nephridia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Kidneys", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Antennal gland", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Antennal glands or green glands perform the excretory function in crustaceans like prawns.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9612,'biology',86,980,2285,'Lungs do not collapse between breaths and some air always remains in the lungs which can never be expelled because','[{"text": "There is a negative pressure in the lungs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There is a negative intrapleural pressure pulling at the lung walls", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "There is a positive intrapleural pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pressure in the lungs is higher than the atmospheric pressure", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Lungs do not collapse between breaths and some air always remain in the lungs which can be never expelled because there is a negative intrapleural pressure pulling at the lung walls.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9613,'biology',84,583,1169,'In frog, blood from venae cavae first enters which of the following structures?','[{"text": "Conus arteriosus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sinus venosus", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Right atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Left atrium", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Blood from two precaval veins and one postcaval vein first enters a triangular structure called sinus venosus, which opens into the right atrium.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9624,'biology',86,985,2290,'Name the chronic respiratory disorder caused mainly by cigarette smoking<br>','[{"text": "Respiratory alkalosis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Emphysema", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Asthma", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Respiratory acidosis", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Emphysema, is a condition where walls seperating the alveoli breaks and results in the reduction of surface area for the exchange of respiratory gases. The main reason for emphysema is smoking of cigarette.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9614,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Systemic circulation<br>B. Hepatic portal system<br>C. Hepatic portal vein<br>D. Coronary system</div><div class="column-right">P. Circulation of blood to and from the cardiac musculature<br>Q. Provides nutrients, ${{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ to the tissues<br>R. Carries blood from intestine to the liver<br>S. Vascular connection exists between digestive tract and liver</div></div>','[{"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-R, D-P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-S, B-Q, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-P, B-R, C-S, D-Q", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Systemic circulation - Provides nutrients, $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ to the tissues<br>Hepatic portal system - vascular connection exists between digestive tract and liver<br>Hepatic portal vein - Carries blood from intestine to liver<br>Coronary system -Circulation of blood to and from the cardiac musculature.',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9615,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Malpighian tubules are the excretory organs in','[{"text": "Planaria- Platyhelminthes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cockroach - arthropoda", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cockroach - annelida", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<em>Ascaris</em>- arthropoda", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Malpighian tubules are the excretory structures of most of the insects (Phylum Arthropoda) including cockroaches.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9616,'biology',86,983,2288,'Reduction in $$\\mathrm{pH}$$ of blood will','[{"text": "Release bicarbonate ions by the liver", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reduce the rate of heart beat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reduce the blood supply to the brain", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decrease the affinity of hemoglobin with oxygen", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'It is associated with the transport of carbon dioxide. According to Haldane\'s effect, as more and more oxyhaemoglobin is formed in the lungs, it will release more $$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$ions, which results in the increase in the acidity of blood. As $$\\mathrm{pH}$$ of blood decreases, more Carbondioxide is released, decreasing the affinity of haemoglobin to blood.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9617,'biology',84,583,1171,'The reproductive system of female frog differs from that of male frog in that','[{"text": "Ovaries are not attached to kidneys by peritoneum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ureter acts as urinogenital duct in female", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ova do not pass through cloaca", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There is no functional connection between ovaries and kidneys", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In female frogs, there is no functional connection between ovaries and kidneys, unlike in male frogs in which vasa efferentia arising from testes enter the kidney and open into Bidder\'s canal. Bidder\'s canal communicates with the urinogenital duct that comes out of the kidneys and opens into the cloaca. Ureters act as urinogenital ducts in male frogs. In females, ureters and oviducts are separate.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9618,'biology',87,1131,2703,'The heartbeat of a person increases at the time of an interview due to the secretion of','[{"text": "Parasympathethic hormones", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adrenal medullary hormones", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{ADH}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{ACTH}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The heartbeat of a person increases at the time of an interview due to the secretion of adrenal medullary hormones- adrenaline and nor adrenaline.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9619,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Which of the following structures help in the removal of nitrogenous waste as well as concerned with osmoregulation?','[{"text": "Kidney", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Protonephridia and Nephridia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Malphighian tubules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Option (4) is correct as Protonephridia, nephridia, kidney and Malpighian tubules all are the structures which help in the removal of nitrogenous waste as well as are concerned with osmoregulation (i.e. regulation ionic and fluid volume). These structures are found in platyhelminths, annelids and insects, respectively.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9620,'biology',86,982,2287,'The partial pressure of oxygen in the alveoli of the lungs is','[{"text": "Equal to that in the blood", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "More than that in the blood", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Less than that in the blood", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less than that of carbon dioxide<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The alveolar oxygen partial pressure is lower than the atmospheric $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ partial pressure for two reasons. Firstly, as the air enters the lungs, it is humidified by the upper airway and thus the partial pressure of water vapour (47 $$\\mathrm{mmHg}$$ ) reduces the oxygen partial pressure to about $$150 \\mathrm{mmHg}$$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9621,'biology',84,583,1169,'Identify the correct path of passage of urine in female frog.','[{"text": "Kidney $\\\\rightarrow$ urinogenital duct $\\\\rightarrow$ cloaca $\\\\rightarrow$ urinary bladder", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Kidney $\\\\rightarrow$ oviduct $\\\\rightarrow$ urinary bladder $\\\\rightarrow$ cloaca", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Kidney $\\\\rightarrow$ ureter $\\\\rightarrow$ cloaca $\\\\rightarrow$ urinary bladder", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Kidney $\\\\rightarrow$ urinary bladder $\\\\rightarrow$ ureter $\\\\rightarrow$ cloaca", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In female frog, urine from kidneys passes through ureters into the cloaca. From the cloaca, urine enters the urinary bladder where it is stored. The urinogenital duct is present in male frog; not female frog.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9622,'biology',87,1131,2703,'A special neural centre in the ____ the cardiac function through ____ can moderate','[{"text": "Medulla oblongata, autonomic nervous system (ANS)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pons, autonomic nervous system (ANS)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Medulla oblongata, sympathetic nervous system only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Medulla oblongata, parasympathetic nervous system only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A special neural centre in the medulla oblongata can moderate the cardiac function through autonomic nervous system (ANS)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9623,'biology',88,1150,2757,'In annelids like earthworm, excretory organs are','[{"text": "Kidneys", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Malpighian tubules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Green glands", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nephridia", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Nephridia are the tubular excretory structures of earthworms and other annelids. Nephridia help to remove nitrogenous wastes and maintain a fluid and ionic balance.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(10242,'biology',9,24,44,'Air bladders are shown by','[{"text": "<i>Dictyota</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Laminaria</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Fucus</i>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Members of Phaeophyceae possess air bladders to provide buoyancy eg. Kelps. It helps the blades of the algae to float on the surface of the ocean and absorb sunlight for photosynthesis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9625,'biology',84,583,1169,'Identify the correct path of blood flow in the frog.','[{"text": "Venae cavae $\\\\rightarrow$ sinus venosus $\\\\rightarrow$ left atrium ventricle $\\\\rightarrow$ conus arteriosus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Venae cavae $\\\\rightarrow$ sinus venosus $\\\\rightarrow$ right atrium ventricle $\\\\rightarrow$ conus arteriosus", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lungs $\\\\rightarrow$ pulmonary artery $\\\\rightarrow$ left atrium ventricle $\\\\rightarrow$ conus arteriosus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Venae cavae $\\\\rightarrow$ conus arteriosus $\\\\rightarrow$ right atrium ventricle $\\\\rightarrow$ sinus venosus", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Blood from two precaval veins and one postcaval vein first enters a triangular structure called sinus venosus, which opens into the right atrium. Blood from the lungs enters the left atrium. Blood from both the atria enters the ventricle. Ventricle pumps blood into conus arteriosus from which three pairs of aortic arches arise. They carry blood to different parts of the body through their branches.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9626,'biology',87,1131,2703,'Cardiac centre can moderate the cardiac functions through','[{"text": "Sympathetic nervous system only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Parasympathetic nervous system only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Autonomic nervous system (ANS)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "CNS only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Cardiac centre can moderate the cardiac functions through autonomic nervous system (ANS).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9627,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Malpighian tubules help in the removal of','[{"text": "Nitrogenous wastes and osmoregulation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Nitrogenous wastes only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Osmoregulation only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Solid waste, nitrogenous wastes and osmoregulation", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Malpighian tubules help in the removal of nitrogenous wastes and osmoregulation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9628,'biology',86,981,2286,'Lungs are made up of air-filled sacs, the alveoli. They do not collapse even after forceful expiration, because of:','[{"text": "Inspiratory Reserve Volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tidal Volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Expiratory Reserve Volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Residual Volume", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Residual volume is the volume of air which will still remain in the lungs even after forceful expiration.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9629,'biology',84,583,1169,'Amphibian heart is','[{"text": "3- chambered", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2- chambered", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1- chambered", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4- chambered", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Heart is a muscular structure situated in the upper part of the body cavity. Amphibian heart has three chambers, two atria and one ventricle and is covered by a membrane called pericardium.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9630,'biology',87,1131,2703,'Neural signal through the sympathetic nervous (part of ANS) increases the cardiac output because of','[{"text": "Increasing the rate of heartbeat", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increasing the strength of atrial contraction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreasing the speed of conduction", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Neural signals through the sympathetic nerves (part of ANS) can increase the rate of heartbeat, the strength of ventricular contraction and thereby the cardiac output.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9631,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Select the correct statement from the following:<br>(A) In most of the invertebrates, excretory structures are in complex tubular forms.<br>(B) Vertebrates have simple tubular organ as excretory structure like kidney.<br>(C) Protonephridia is primarily concerned with excretion.<br>(D) Protonephridia are excretory structure in rotifers, some annelids and cephalochordates (Amphioxus).','[{"text": "A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "D", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In most of the invertebrates, these structures are simple tubular forms whereas vertebrates have complex tubular organs called kidneys. Some of these structures are mentioned here. Protonephridia or flame cells are the excretory structures in Platyhelminthes (Flatworms, e.g., Planaria), rotifers, some annelids and the cephalochordate - Amphioxus. Protonephridia are primarily concerned with ionic and fluid volume regulation, i.e., osmoregulation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9632,'biology',86,985,2290,'Which of the following options correctly represents the lung conditions in asthma and emphysema, respectively?<br>','[{"text": "Increased respiratory surface; Inflammation of bronchioles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increased number of bronchioles; Increased respiratory surface", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inflammation of bronchioles; decreased respiratory surface", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Decreased respiratory surface; Inflammation of bronchioles", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Asthma is a respiratory disorder where there is difficulty in breathing due to inflammation of bronchi and bronchioles. Emphysema is a chronic disorder where the alveolar walls are damaged which results in decrease of respiratory surface. Major reason for emphysema is cigarette smoking.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9633,'biology',84,579,1158,'The dry surface of the skin is covered by','[{"text": "Stratified squamous epithelium", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Simple squamous epithelium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Simple columnar epithelium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transitional epithelium", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The dry surface of the skin is lined by stratified keratinized squamous epithelium (a type of compound epithelium). fibres',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9634,'biology',87,1131,2703,'The parts of ANS are','[{"text": "Sympathetic nervous system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Parasympathetic nervous system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Somatic nervous system", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A special neural centre in the medulla oblongata can moderate the cardiac function through autonomic nervous system (ANS). Neural signals through the sympathetic nerves (part of ANS) can increase the rate of heart beat, the strength of ventricular contraction and thereby the cardiac output. On the other hand, parasympathetic neural signals (another component of ANS) decrease the rate of heartbeat, speed of conduction of action potential and thereby the cardiac output.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9635,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Uric acid is formed in human being from','[{"text": "Proteins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glucose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Purines", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pyrimidines", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Uric acid is created by the metabolism of purines. It is a less toxic and helps conserve water.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9636,'biology',86,985,2290,'Which of the following is an occupational respiratory disorder?','[{"text": "Botulism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Silicosis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Anthrax", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Emphysema<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Silicosis is due to excess inhalation of silica dust caused in the workers who break and grind the stones. Botulism is a food poison caused by the botulinum toxin released by Clostridium botulinum. Anthrax is a life-threatening infectious disease caused by Bacillus anthracis that normally affects animals, especially ruminants (such as goats, cattle, sheep, and horses). Emphysema is a long-term, progressive disease of the lungs that primarily causes shortness of breath due to over-inflation of the alveoli.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9637,'biology',87,1131,2703,'Parasympathetic neural signal decreases the cardiac output by','[{"text": "Decreasing the rate of heartbeat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreasing the speed conduction of action potential", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increasing heart rate", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Parasympathetic neural signal decreases the cardiac output by decreasing the rate of heartbeat, speed of conduction of action potential and thereby the cardiac output.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9638,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Excretion of nitrogenous waste product mainly as uric acid by birds is helpful in','[{"text": "Conserving body heat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conserving water", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Eliminating excess water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conserving fats", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Reptiles, birds, land snails and insects excrete nitrogenous wastes as uric acid in the form of pellet or paste with a minimum loss of water. This helps uricotelic animals conserve water.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9639,'biology',84,579,1158,'Which of the following is not a type of cell junction between epithelial cells?','[{"text": "Gap junction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Synaptic junction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Tight junction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adhering junction", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Three types of cell junctions are found in the epithelium and other tissues. These are called as tight, adhering and gap junctions. Synaptic junctions occur between two neurons (neuronal synapse) or between a neuron and a muscle fibre (neuromuscular junction).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9640,'biology',86,981,2286,'Match the items given in column I with those in column II and select the correct option given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Tidal volume<BR>B. Inspiratory reserve volume<BR>C. Expiratory reserve volume<BR>D. Residual volume</div><div class="column-right">P. 2500-3000 Ml<br>Q. 1100-1200 Ml<br>R. 500-550 mL<br>S. 1000-1100 Ml</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A - P,B - S,C - Q,D - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R,B - P,C - S,D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R,B - Q,C - P,D - S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - S,B - R,C - Q,D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Tidal Volume (TV): Volume of air inspired or expired during a normal respiration. It is approx. $$500 \\mathrm{~mL}$$., i.e., a healthy man can inspire or expire approximately 6000 to 8000 $$\\mathrm{mL}$$ of air per minute. Inspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV): Additional volume of air, a person can inspire by a forcible inspiration. This averages $$2500 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ to $$3000 \\mathrm{~mL}$$. Expiratory Reserve Volume (ERV): Additional volume of air, a person can expire by a forcible expiration. This averages $$1000 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ to $$1100 \\mathrm{~mL}$$. Residual Volume (RV): Volume of air remaining in the lungs even after a forcible expiration. This averages $$1100 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ to $$1200 \\mathrm{~mL}$$.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9641,'biology',87,1131,2703,'Heartbeat increases','[{"text": "On stimulation of sympathetic nerves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "On stimulation of parasympathetic nerve", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adrenalin secretion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Neural signals through the sympathetic nerves (part of ANS) can increase the rate of heartbeat, the strength of ventricular contraction and thereby the cardiac output.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9642,'biology',84,579,1158,'Which one of the following options gives the type of epithelium lining the PCT of the nephron and its function?','[{"text": "Simple cuboidal - Secretion and absorption", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Simple squamous - Filtration and reabsorption", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Simple cuboidal - Protection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Compound - Reabsorption", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The epithelium of proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) of nephron is simple cuboidal epithelium. The cells have microvilli on the free surface. This epithelium serves for selective reabsorption and tubular secretion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9643,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Some amount of ____ may be retained in the kidney matrix of some of these animals to maintain a desired ____.','[{"text": "Urea, osmolarity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Uric acid, osmolarity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Urea, turgidity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Uric acid, turgidity", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Some amount of urea may be retained in the kidney matrix of some of the animals to maintain a desired osmolarity',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9644,'biology',86,979,2284,'The ciliated epithelial cells are required to move particles or mucus in a specific direction. In humans, these cells are mainly present in<br>','[{"text": "Bile duct and bronchioles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fallopian tubes and Pancreatic duct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Eustachian tube and Salivary duct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bronchioles and Fallopian tubes", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Bronchioles and Fallopian tubes are lined with ciliated epithelium to move particles or mucus in a specific direction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9645,'biology',87,1131,2703,'Heartbeat gets affected by the hormones of','[{"text": "Adrenal cortex", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Renal medulla", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cerebral medulla", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Heartbeat gets affected by the hormones of adrenal medulla. Adrenal medullary hormones can also increase the cardiac output.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9646,'biology',84,583,1169,'In frog, ventricle opens into ____ on the ____ side of heart','[{"text": "Sinus venosus, ventral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sinus venosus, dorsal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conus arteriosus, dorsal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conus arteriosus, ventral", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In frogs the ventricle opens into a sac like conus arteriosus on the ventral side of the heart.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9647,'biology',88,1150,2757,'The ammonia formed is converted into urea in which of the following organs','[{"text": "Kidney", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blood", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ornithine cycle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Liver", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Ammonia, urea and uric acid are the major forms of nitrogenous wastes excreted by the animals. Ammonia is the most toxic form and requires large amount of water for its elimination, whereas uric acid, being the least toxic, can be removed with a minimum loss of water. Hence, order of toxicity is - Uric acid  <  Urea $$ < $$ Ammonia.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9648,'biology',86,981,2286,'Tidal volume and expiratory reserve volume of an athlete is $$500 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ and $$1000 \\mathrm{~mL}$$, respectively. What will be his Expiratory capacity if the Residual Volume is $$1200 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ ?<br>','[{"text": "$$1500 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$1700 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2200 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2700 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Tidal Volume $$=500 \\mathrm{ml}$$<br>Expiratory Reserve Volume $$=1000 \\mathrm{ml}$$<br>Expiratory Capacity ${\\rm{ = TV + ERV}}\\,{\\rm{ < br >  = 500 + 1000}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 1500ml}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9649,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Which of the following sets of animals produce the same substances as their chief excretory product?','[{"text": "Humans, pigeon and frog", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Humans, cockroach and snake", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frog, humans and dogs", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Humans, ant and crow", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Frog (terrestrial amphibian), humans (mammal) and dogs (mammal) are all ureotelic.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9650,'biology',84,583,1170,'If cerebellum is removed from the brain of a frog, it looses','[{"text": "Memory", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Equilibrium", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vision", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Cerebellum, is a part of the hind brain, it is associated with the maintenance of equilibrium',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9651,'biology',87,1131,2703,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Myogenic heart<br>B. Medulla oblongata<br>C. Sympathetic nerves<br>D. Adrenal medullary hormones</div><div class="column-right">P. Auto regulated by nodal tissues<br>Q. Increases the cardiac output<br>R. Moderate the cardiac function through ANS<br>S. Increases the rate of heart beat</div></div>','[{"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-R, D-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-S, B-Q, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-P, B-R, C-S, D-Q", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Myogenic heart - Auto regulated by nodal tissues<br>Medulla oblongata - Moderate the cardiac function through ANS<br>Sympathetic nerves - Increase the rate of heartbeat<br>Adrenal medullary hormones - Increase the cardiac output.',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9652,'biology',86,985,2290,'Due to increasing air-borne allergens and pollutants, many people in urban areas are suffering from respiratory disorder causing wheezing due to<br>','[{"text": "Benign growth on mucous lining of nasal cavity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inflammation of bronchi and bronchioles", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Proliferation of fibrous tissues and damage of the alveolar walls", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reduction in the secretion of surfactants by pneumocytes.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Asthma is a difficulty in breathing causing wheezing due to inflammation of bronchi and bronchioles. It can be due to increasing air born allergens and pollutants. Asthma is an allergic condition. Many people in urban areas are suffering from this respiratory disorder.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9653,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Ammonia is obtained from ____ process and finally converted to ____','[{"text": "Amination, urea", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Deamination, urea", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Deamination, uric acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Amination, uric acid", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ammonia is obtained from deamination process and finally converted to urea in liver.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9654,'biology',84,583,1169,'Select the incorrect statement with regard to circulatory system of frog.','[{"text": "The vascular system is of closed type.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hepatic and renal portal systems are present.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The heart is situated in the upper part of the body cavity.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The lymphatic system is absent.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The lymphatic system is present in frogs. It consists of lymph, lymph channels, and lymph nodes.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9655,'biology',87,1132,2704,'In an adult, normal blood pressure is','[{"text": "$$80 / 120 \\\\mathrm{mmHg}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$100 / 60 \\\\mathrm{mmHg}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$120 / 80 \\\\mathrm{mmHg}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$100 / 80 \\\\mathrm{mmHg}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In an adult, normal blood pressure is 120/80. In this measurement $$120 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$$ (millimetres of mercury pressure) is the systolic, or pumping, pressure and $$80 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$$ is the diastolic, or resting, pressure.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9656,'biology',86,983,2288,'Select the favourable conditions required for the formation of oxyhaemoglobin at the alveoli.<br>','[{"text": "High $$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$, low $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$, less $$\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$, lower temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Low $$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$, low $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$, more $$\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$, higher temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "High $$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$, high $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$, less $$\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$, higher temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low $$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$, low $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$, more $$\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$, higher temperature", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In alveoli, where there is high $$\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$$, low $$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$$, lesser $$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$concentration and lower temperature factors are all favourable for the formation of oxyhemoglobin.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9657,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Identify the substance that gets increased in blood if liver becomes functionless?','[{"text": "Urea", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ammonia", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Uric acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proteins", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ammonia is converted into urea in the liver. If liver does not function and carry out the ornithine/urea cycle, ammonia starts accumulating in the body.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9669,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Aquatic animals are mostly ammonotelic because','[{"text": "Ammonia is released from the body through gill surfaces", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Excretion of ammonia requires large amount of water which is available to these animals.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ammonia is soluble in water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 and 3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both 2 and 3 support the explanation for aquatic animals being ammonotelic.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9658,'biology',84,583,1170,'Which of the following are the well-organised sense organs in frog?','[{"text": "Eyes and internal ears", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Taste buds and nasal epithelium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tympanum and taste buds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Organs of touch and vision", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Frog has different types of sense organs, namelyorgans of touch (sensory papillae), taste (taste buds), smell (nasal epithelium), vision (eyes) and hearing (tympanum with internal ears). Out of these, eyes and internal ears are well-organised structures and the rest are cellular aggregations around nerve endings.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9659,'biology',87,1132,2704,'In normal BP measurement upper limit is the ____ pressure and lower limit is ____ pressure.','[{"text": "Diastolic, systolic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pulse, diastolic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Systolic, diastolic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pulse, systolic", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In an adult, normal blood pressure is 120/80. In this measurement $$120 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$$ (millimetres of mercury pressure) is the systolic, or pumping, pressure and $$80 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$$ is the diastolic, or resting, pressure.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9660,'biology',86,982,2287,'The partial pressures ${\\rm{(in}}\\,{\\rm{mmHg)}}$ of oxygen $$\\left(\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right)$$ and carbon dioxide $$\\left(\\mathrm{CO}_{2}\\right)$$ at alveoli (the site of diffusion ) are:<br>','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}=104$$ and $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}=40$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}=40$$ and $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}=45$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}=95$$ and $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}=40$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}=159$$ and $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}=0.3$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Partial pressure of oxygen higher in Alveoli $$104 \\mathrm{mmHg}$$ and Partial pressure of carbon dioxide in alveoli is $$40 \\mathrm{mmHg}$$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9661,'biology',84,583,1171,'Select the correct route for the passage of ova in female frogs','[{"text": "Ovaries $\\\\rightarrow$ body cavity $\\\\rightarrow$ oviducts $\\\\rightarrow$ cloaca $\\\\rightarrow$ exterior", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ovaries $\\\\rightarrow$ Bidder\'s canal $\\\\rightarrow$ oviduct cloaca $\\\\rightarrow$ exterior", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ovaries $\\\\rightarrow$ oviducts $\\\\rightarrow$ body cavity $\\\\rightarrow$ cloaca exterior", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ovaries $\\\\rightarrow$ kidneys $\\\\rightarrow$ cloaca exterior", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In female frog, ova are released from the ovaries into the body cavity from where there are collected by oviducts. The oviducts open into the cloaca.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9662,'biology',88,1150,2757,'The chief nitrogenous waste product present in urine of <i>Rana tigrina</i> is ____ and is known as ____','[{"text": "Ammonia, ureotelic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Urea, ureotelic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Uric acid, uricotelic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Urea, uricotelic", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Rana tigrina i.e. Frog is a terrestrial amphibian. Terrestrial amphibians are ureotelic in nature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9663,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Hypertension means?','[{"text": "Repeated checks of blood pressure of an individual is $$120 / 80 \\\\mathrm{mmHg}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Single check of blood pressure of an individual is $$120 / 80 \\\\mathrm{mmHg}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Repeated checks of blood pressure of an individual is $$140 / 90 \\\\mathrm{mmHg}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Single check of blood pressure of an individual is $$140 / 90$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Hypertension means repeated checks of blood pressure of an individual is 140/90 $$\\mathrm{mmHg}$$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9664,'biology',86,979,2284,'Which of the following is not the function of conducting part of respiratory system?<br>','[{"text": "Inhaled air humidified", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Temperature of inhaled air is brought to body temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Provides surface for diffusion of $$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ and $$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It clears inhaled air from foreign particles", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Exchange part is the site of actual diffusion of $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ and $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ between blood and atmospheric air. The conducting part transports the atmospheric air to the alveoli, clears it from foreign particles, humidifies and also brings the air to body temperature.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9665,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Aquatic animals excrete ammonia. Which of the following statements does not support this fact?','[{"text": "Ammonia is easily soluble in water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Excretion of ammonia requires less amount of water which is available to these animals.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ammonia is highly toxic and needs to be eliminated as and when formed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ammonia is generally excreted by diffusion across body surfaces or through gill surfaces (in fish) as ammonium ions.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Option (2) is incorrect. Ammonia is the excretory waste product of nearly all aquatic animals like bony fishes, aquatic amphibians and aquatic insects. It is excreted by diffusion across body surface or through gill surface as ammonium ions (not by kidney). Excretion of ammonia requires large amount of water which is available to these animals.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9666,'biology',84,583,1169,'Select the wrong statement about the heart of frog.','[{"text": "Conus arteriosus is situated on the dorsal side of the heart.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Sinus venosus receives deoxygenated blood.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The heart is covered by a membrane called pericardium.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two atria open into a single ventricle.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Conus arteriosus is situated on the ventral side of the heart.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9667,'biology',87,1132,2704,'The instrument by which BP is measured is called','[{"text": "Sphygmomanometer", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Manometer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stethoscope", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "X-ray", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The instrument by which BP is measured is called sphygmomanometer.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9668,'biology',86,983,2288,'Under normal physiological conditions in human being every $$100 \\mathrm{ml}$$ of oxygenated blood can deliver ____ $$\\mathrm{ml}$$ of $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ to the tissues.','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{5}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ml}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{4}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ml}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{10}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ml}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{2}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ml}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In human being, every $$100 \\mathrm{ml}$$ of oxygenated blood can deliver $$5 \\mathrm{ml}$$ of $$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$$ to the tissues under normal physiological conditions.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9670,'biology',84,583,1170,'Which of the following is a correct match of an organ of frog and its function(s)?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Kidneys- synthesis of urea", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Eye- mosaic vision", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Liver- secretion of lipase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ear- hearing and equilibrium", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The ear is an organ of hearing as well as balancing (equilibrium). Urea is synthesized by the liver and eliminated by the kidneys. There is no mosaic vision in the frog (eyes are not compound eyes). The liver does not secrete any digestive enzyme.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9671,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Choose the correct statement.<br>I. Heart failure means the state of heart when it is pumping blood effectively enough to meet the needs of the body<br>II. A symptom of acute chest pain appears when no enough oxygen is reaching the heart muscle.','[{"text": "I is correct only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II is correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The correct statement is a. Heart failure means the state of heart when it is not pumping blood effectively enough to meet the needs of the body.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9672,'biology',86,981,2286,'Vital capacity of lung is ____.','[{"text": "IRV + ERV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IRV + ERV + TV + RV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IRV + ERV + TV – RV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IRV + ERV + TV", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Vital capacity is the maximum volume\nof air a person can breathe in after forced\nexpiration. This includes ERV, TV and IRV.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9673,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Ammonia which is produced by metabolism is converted into ____ in the ____ of mammals and released into blood which is filtered and excreted out by ____','[{"text": "Uric acid, liver, kidney", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Urea, liver, kidney", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Amino acid, liver, kidney", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Urea, kidney, liver", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ammonia which is produced by metabolism is converted into urea in the liver of mammals and released into blood which is filtered and excreted out by kidney.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9674,'biology',84,583,1169,'The ____ urinary bladder is present ____to the rectum which also opens in the cloaca in the frog','[{"text": "Thick-walled, ventral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thin-walled, ventral", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Thick-walled, dorsal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thin-walled, dorsal", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The thin-walled urinary bladder is present ventral to the rectum which also opens in the cloaca.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9675,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Which of the following is true about hypertension?','[{"text": "It leads to cardiac diseases.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It affects vital organs like brain.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It affects vital organs like kidney.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If repeated checks of blood pressure of an individual is 140/90 (140 over 90) or higher, it shows hypertension. High blood pressure leads to heart diseases and also affects vital organs like brain and kidney.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9676,'biology',86,981,2286,'Whether a child died after normal birth or died before birth can be confirmed measuring','[{"text": "The weight of the child", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The dead space air", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tidal volume of air", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Residual volume of air", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Right after the birth, the child cries which opens the respiratory passage and some amount of air gets stored in lungs as residual volume. So to check whether the child died before birth a piece of child\'s lung is cut and put in water, if it floats (due to buoyancy because of air in it) then it is - that the mother gave a live birth and if the piece of lung sinks due to lack of air in case of still birth child would have not inhaled any air) then case of still birth is confirmed',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9677,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Which of the following is uricotelic?<BR>(A) Reptiles<BR>(B) Birds<BR>(C) Insects, Land snails<BR>(D) Amphibians and marine fishes','[{"text": "A, B and C only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "B and C only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A and D only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Reptiles, birds, land snails and insects excrete nitrogenous wastes in the form of uric acid. Amphibians and marine fishes excrete urea and are thus ureotelic in nature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9678,'biology',84,579,1158,'Select the correct match.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Tight}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{junctions}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{Help}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{substances}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}}\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{leak}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{across}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{a}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{tissue}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Synaptic}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{junctions}}}&\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n{\\\\rm{Sites }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{communication}}\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\rm{between}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{two}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{muscle}}\\\\,\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\rm{fibres}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Anchoring}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\\\\\\\\\n{{\\\\rm{junctions}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{Permit}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{electrical}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{signals}}}\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{pass}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{between}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{cells}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Gap}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{junctions}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Allow}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{the}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{cells}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{in}}}\\\\\\\\\\n{{\\\\rm{a}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{tissue}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{communicate}}\\\\,}\\\\\\\\\\n{{\\\\rm{with}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{each}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{other}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Gap junctions facilitate the cells to communicate with each other by connecting the cytoplasm of adjoining cells, for rapid transfer of ions, small molecules and sometimes big molecules. Tight junctions help to stop substances from leaking across a tissue. A synapse is a region where communication occurs between two neurons, or between a neuron and an effector cell (e.g. a muscle).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9679,'biology',86,980,2285,'According to Boyle\'s law, the product of pressure and volume is a constant. Hence','[{"text": "If volume of lungs is increased, the pressure also increases proportionately", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "If volume of lungs is increased, the pressure decreases proportionately", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "If volume of lungs is increasaed, the pressure remains the same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "If volume of lungs is increased, the pressure decreases disproportionately", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to Boyle\'s law, if a given amount of gas has a constant temperature, increasing its volume decreases its pressure, and vice - versa.On inhaling, muscles increases the size of the thoracic (chest) cavity and expand the lungs. This increases their volume, so pressure inside the lungs decreases. As a result, outside air rushes into the lungs. That\'s because a gas always flows from an area of higher to lower pressure',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9683,'biology',86,979,2284,'The trachea terminates into','[{"text": "Bronchial tree", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bronchi", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Alveoli", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The trachea (sturdy) or wind pipe, is a tabular passageway for air. It is located to the oesophagus and extends from the larnyx to the superior border of the fifth thoracic vertebra, where it divides into right and left primary bronchi',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9684,'biology',87,1132,2704,'In atherosclerosis','[{"text": "Affects the vessels that supply blood to the heart muscle.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is caused by deposits of calcium, fat, cholesterol and fibrous tissues, which makes the lumen of arteries narrower", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is also known as CAD", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Atherosclerosis, affects the vessels that supply blood to the heart muscle. It is caused by deposits of calcium, fat, cholesterol and fibrous tissues, which makes the lumen of arteries narrower.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9685,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Identify the correct statement<br>\n<div>&nbsp;</div><div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">I. Ammonia, is generally excreted by diffusion across body surfaces or through gill surfaces (in fish) as ammonium ions.</div><div class="column-right">II. Kidneys do play significant role in its removal.</div></div>','[{"text": "I is correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'I. Ammonia, is generally excreted by diffusion across body surfaces or through gill surfaces (in fish) as ammonium ions.<br>II.Kidneys do not play any significant role in its removal.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9686,'biology',84,579,1159,'By which of the following features does bone tissue resemble cartilage?','[{"text": "Matrix is solid and non-pliable", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cells are enclosed in lacunae", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Matrix has numerous blood vessels", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Site of production of blood cells", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Both osteocytes and chondrocytes are enclosed in lacunae. Matrix of cartilage is pliable while that of bone tissue is non-pliable. Unlike bone tissue, cartilage is avascular. Production of blood cells occurs in the bone marrow of some bones but not cartilages.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9687,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Pain in heart muscle is','[{"text": "Pericarditis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Angina cardia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Angina pectoris", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hypoxia", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Angina: It is also called \'angina pectoris\'. A symptom of acute chest pain appears when no enough oxygen is reaching the heart muscle. Angina can occur in men and women of any age but it is more common among the middleaged and elderly.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9688,'biology',86,983,2288,'During the transporation of gases, to maintain the ionic balance chloride ions shift from','[{"text": "RBC\'s plasma", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plasma to RBC\'s", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lungs to blood", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blood to lungs", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Some Hydrocarbondioxide, moves out into the blood plasma as blood picks up carbondioxide, down its concentration gradient. In exchange, chloride ions $(\\mathrm{Cl})$ move from plasma into the RBC\'s . This exchange, negative ions, which maintains the electrical balance between blood plasma and $\\mathrm{RBC}$ cytosol, is known as the chloride shift or Hamburger\'s phenomenon.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9689,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Select the incorrect statement from the following about ammonia','[{"text": "It is formed in the kidneys", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is generally excreted by the process of diffusion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is excreted as ammonium ion through gill surface in fishes.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is readily soluble in water.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The process of excreting ammonia is Ammonotelism. Many bony fishes, aquatic amphibians and aquatic insects are ammonotelic in nature. Ammonia, as it is readily soluble, is generally excreted by diffusion across body surfaces or through gill surfaces (in fish) as ammonium ions. Kidneys do not play any significant role in its removal.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9690,'biology',84,579,1159,'The kind of tissue that supports our ear pinna is also found in the','[{"text": "The dermis of the skin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ducts of the salivary glands", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tip of the nose", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The mucosa of the stomach", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Cartilage is present in the tip of the nose, ear pinna, intervertebral discs, limbs and hands in adults.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9691,'biology',86,981,2286,'Match the items listed under column I with those given under Column II, choose the appropriate option from the given choices<br><div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Residual\nVolume (RV)<br>B. Inspiratory\nReserve\nvolume(IRV)<br>C. Vital\nCapacity\n(VC)<br>D. Expiratory\nReverse\nvolume(ERV)<br>E. Inspiratory\nCapacity (IC)</div><div class="column-right">P. 4000 mL-4600\nmL<br>Q. 1100mL-\n1200mL<br>R. 1000mL-1100\nmL<br>S. 3000 mL-3500\nmL<br>T. 2500mL-3000\nmL</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{T}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{P}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{T}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{T}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{P}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{T}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'• Even after the expiratory reserve volume is expired, considerable air remains in the lungs because, and some air also remains in the noncollapsible airways. This volume is called the residual volume. It amounts to about $1100 \\mathrm{~mL}$ to $1200 \\mathrm{~mL}$<br>•Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV) is the additional amount of air that can be taken in by forced inspiration after normal inspiration of $500 \\mathrm{~mL}$. This additional inspired air is about $2500 \\mathrm{~mL}$ to $3000 \\mathrm{~mL}$<br>• Vital capacity $(\\mathrm{VC})$ is the sum of inspiratory reserve volume,tidal volume and expiratory reserve volume. It varies from 3400 to 4800 $\\mathrm{mL}$<br>•Expiratory reserve volume (ERV) is the extra amount of air that can be inspired if inspiration follows forced expiration. It is about $1000 \\mathrm{~mL}$ to $1100 \\mathrm{~mL}$.- Inspiratory capacity (IC) is the sum of tidal volume and inspiratory reserve volume (TV + IRV) and is the total volume a person can inspire after normal expiration.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9692,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Myocardial infarction is due to','[{"text": "Infection of heart muscles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Weakning of heart muscles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Clot in blood vessel of the heart", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Clot in blood vessel of the lungs", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Myocardial infarction is due to clot in blood vessel of the heart. A heart attack (myocardial infarction) happens when one or more areas of the heart muscle don\'t get enough oxygen. This happens when blood flow to the heart muscle is blocked.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9693,'biology',84,583,1171,'Given below is the diagrammatic sketch of male repro-ductive system of frog. Select the option that correctly identifies one of the parts $(\\mathrm{A}-\\mathrm{D})$ along with its function.','[{"text": "A:Bidder\'s canal - transports sperm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{B}$ :Adrenal gland - exocrine gland", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}$ :Urinogenital duct - transports both urine and sperm", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "D : Urinary bladder - stores urine", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A - Vasa efferentia (not Bidder\'s canal)<br>B - Adrenal gland (endocrine; not exocrine)<br>C -Urinogenital duct (in the male frog, ureters act as urinogenital ducts)<br>D - Cloaca (not urinary bladder)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/84_9693_question_1.png'),
(9694,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Select ammonotelic from the following','[{"text": "Many cartilaginous fishes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aquatic amphibians", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Land insects", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Many bony fishes, aquatic amphibians and aquatic insects are ammonotelic in nature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9695,'biology',86,979,2284,'A person suddenly starts coughing while swallowing food. This coughing would have been due to improper movement of','[{"text": "Neck", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Epiglottis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Tongue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Diaphragm", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Epiglottis is the cartilagenous covering which prevent entry of food into trachea.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9696,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Congestive heart failure','[{"text": "Cardiac arrest", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heart attack", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Myocardial infarction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heart is not pumping blood effectively", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When heart is not pumping blood effectively it is called congestive heart failure.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9697,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Which of the following organisms is/are<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{\\,\\,{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{. ammonotelic}}}&{{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{. uricotelic}}}&{{\\rm{3}}{\\rm{. ureotelic}}}\n\\end{array}\\\\\n\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{\\left( {\\rm{A}} \\right)\\,{\\rm{Land}}\\,{\\rm{snails}}}&{\\left( {\\rm{B}} \\right)\\,{\\rm{Bony}}\\,{\\rm{fishes}}}\n\\end{array}\\\\\n\\left( {\\rm{C}} \\right)\\,{\\rm{Mammals}}\n\\end{array}$','[{"text": "1-C, 2-A, 3- B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1-A, 2-B, 3- C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1-B, 2-A, 3- C", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'1 Ammonotelic - B-Bony fishes,<br>2 Uricotelic - A Land snails,<br>3 Ureotelic - C Mammals',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9698,'biology',84,579,1158,'Select the option that correctly identifies the following tissue and its location.','[{"text": "Cuboidal epithelium PCT of the nephron", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Squamous epithelium - Pancreatic duct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Columnar epithelium Air sacs of the lungs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cuboidal epithelium - Mucosa of the stomach", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The given figure represents simple cuboidal epithelium which is composed of a single layer of cube-like cells. Examples: Ducts of glands and tubular parts of nephrons in kidneys.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/84_9698_question_1.png'),
(9699,'biology',86,979,2283,'Select the correct match.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Locust – Cutaneous respiration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Prawn – Branchial respiration", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Tadpole - Pulmonary respiration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cobra - Buccal respiration", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Many aquatic arthropods (e.g., prawn) and molluscs use gills for respiration (branchial respiration). Arthropods (e.g., locust) do not perform cutaneous respiration. Their cuticle is impermeable to oxygen. Tadpole larva of amphibians respires by gills (branchial respiration). Buccal respiration occurs in amphibians (e.g., frogs).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9700,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Hypertension<br>B. Systolic pressure<br>C. Diastolic pressure<br>D.Normal blood pressure</div><div class="column-right">P. 80 mm Hg<br>Q. 120 mm Hg<br>R. 140/90 mm Hg<br>S. (120/80)</div></div>','[{"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-R, D-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-S, B-Q, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-P, B-R, C-S, D-Q", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Hypertension - $$(120 / 80)$$<br>Systolic pressure - $$120 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$$<br>Diastolic pressure - $$80 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$$<br>High BP value - 140/90',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9701,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Excretion involves','[{"text": "Removal of ammonia, urea, uric acid, carbon dioxide, water and ions like $$\\\\mathrm{Na}^{+}$$, $$\\\\mathrm{K}^{+}, \\\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$$, phosphate, sulphate, etc", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Removal of substances of no further use or those present in excessive quantities are thrown out of the body.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Expulsion of urine from the urinary bladder and sweat from the skin.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All options are correct',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9702,'biology',84,579,1158,'Ciliated epithelial cells are present in','[{"text": "Kidneys", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Intestines", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blood Vessels", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bronchioles", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If the columnar or cuboidal cells bear cilia on their free surface, they are called ciliated epithelium. Their function is to move particles or mucous in a specific direction over the epithelium. They are mainly present in the inner surface of hollow organs like bronchioles and fallopian tubes.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9703,'biology',86,982,2287,'Which of the following are equal?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ in the alveoli and $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ in the resting tissues", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ in the deoxygenated blood and $\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ in the resting tissues", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ in the alveoli and $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ in the oxygenated blood", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ in the carotid artery and $\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ in the pulmonary artery", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ in the alveoli $(40 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg})=\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ in the resting systemic tissues $(40 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$ )',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9767,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Identify the correct statement<br>I. Arteries are blood vessels that carry blood away from the heart<br>II. Aorta is the main artery leaving the heart<br>III. Pulmonary artery is the only artery that carries oxygen','[{"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Pulmonary artery is the only artery that carries deoxygenated blood.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9704,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Coronary artery disease<br>B. Angina pectoris<br>C. Symptoms of high blood pressure<br>D. Congestive heart failure</div><div class="column-right">P. Affects brain and kidney<br>Q. Caused by deposits of calcium, fat, cholesterol which makes the lumen of arteries narrower<br>R. Lungs mainly affected in this disease<br>S. Acute chest pain appears when no enough oxygen is reaching the heart muscle</div></div>','[{"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-R, D-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-S, B-Q, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-P, B-R, C-S, D-Q", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Coronary artery disease - caused by deposits of calcium, fat, cholesterol which makes the lumen of arteries narrower<br>Angina pectoris - Acute chest pain occurs when no enough oxygen is reaching heart muscle<br>Symptoms of high blood pressure - Affects brain and kidney<br>Congestive heat failure - Lungs mainy affected in this disease.',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9705,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Metabolic wastes containing nitrogen in our body are due to','[{"text": "Vitamins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proteins", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Fats", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Amino acids containing nitrogen are building blocks of protein. Proteins after their destruction in our body forms two types of wastes- nitrogenous and non-nitrogenous. Non-nitrogenous wastes constitute carbon dioxide and water. Nitrogenous waste products only relate to proteins since only contain nitrogen.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9707,'biology',86,979,2284,'Which of the following is not a function of the human respiratory system?','[{"text": "Regulation of blood $\\\\mathrm{pH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Detection of olfactory stimuli", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Production of vocal sounds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Protection against blood loss", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Protection against blood loss is the main function of platelets as they play an important role in blood coagulation. The respiratory system regulates blood $\\mathrm{pH}$ by the elimination of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from the body. Olfactory epithelium in the nasal cavity helps in detection of smell.<br>The larynx is the sound-producing organ.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9708,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<br><div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Heart failure<br>B. Cardiac arrest<br>C. Heart attack</div><div class="column-right">P. When heart stops beating<br>Q. When heart is not pumping blood effectively enough to meet the needs of the body<br>R. When the heart muscle is suddenly damaged by an inadequate blood supply</div></div>','[{"text": "A-Q, B-R, C-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-P, C-R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-R, B-Q, C-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-P, B-R, C-Q", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Heart failure - When heart is not pumping blood effectively enough to meet the needs of body.<br>Cardiac arrest - When heart stops beating<br>Heart attack - When the heart muscle is suddenly damaged by an inadequate blood supply',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9709,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Among ammonia, uric acid and urea, which one is the most soluble?','[{"text": "Ammonia", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Uric acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Urea", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Ammonia, as it is readily soluble, is generally excreted by diffusion in the form of ammonium ions.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9711,'biology',86,979,2284,'Common feature in the alveoli of lungs and villi of intestine in mammals is that both','[{"text": "Provide a large surface area.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Have ciliated epithelium.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Are suited for diffusion of gases.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Have rich supply of blood vessels and lymph ducts.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Both Alveoli of lungs and villi of intestine in mammals provide a large surface area.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9712,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Which of the following is correct?','[{"text": "Right atrium - Oxygenated blood", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vitamin, hormones - Not a part of blood", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tricuspid valve - Left side", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bundle of His - Purkinje fibres", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Right atrium receives deoxygenated blood. Vitamins and hormones are transported via blood. Tricuspid valve is present between right atrium and right ventricle. A bundle of nodal fibres, atrioventricular bundle (AV bundle) continues from the AVN which passes through the atrio-ventricular septa to emerge on the top of the interventricular septum and immediately divides into a right and left bundle. These branches give rise to minute fibres throughout the ventricular musculature of the respective sides and are called purkinje fibres.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9714,'biology',84,579,1158,'Match Column I with Column II:<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Unicellular glandular epithelium<br>B. Compound epithelium<br>C. Multicellular glandular epithelium<br>D. Endocrine glandular epithelium</div><div class="column-right">P. Salivary glands<br>Q. Pancreas<br>R. Goblet cells of alimentary canale<br>S. Moist surface of buccal cavity</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  Q, B  -  P, C  -  R, D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  S, B  -  R, C  -  P, D  -  Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  R, B  -  S, C  -  P, D  -  Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  Q, B  -  P, C  -  S, D  -  R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{A}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{R,}}\\,{\\rm{B}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{S,}}\\,{\\rm{C}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{P,}}\\,{\\rm{D}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{Q}}$ is the correct option.<br>A.Unicellular glandular epithelium - R. Goblet cells of alimentary canal<br>B.Compound epithelium - S. Lines moist surface of buccal cavity<br>C. Multicellular glandular epithelium - P. Salivary glands<br>D. Endocrine glandular epithelium - Q - Pancreas',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9715,'biology',86,979,2284,'External gills, tracheae, and lungs all share which of the following sets of characteristics?','[{"text": "Part of gas-exchange system, exchange both $\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$; increase surface area for diffusion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Used by water breathers; based on counter current exchange; use negative pressure breathing.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Exchange only $\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$; are associated with a circulatory system; found in vertebrates.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Found in insects; employ positive pressure pumping based on cross current flow.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The external gill, tracheae, and lungs are all examples of gas-exchange systems used by various animals to exchange both $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$. One of the special properties of all respiratory systems is that they increase surface area for diffusion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9716,'biology',87,1130,2702,'What does hepatic artery carry?','[{"text": "Oxygenated blood from heart to liver", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Deoxygenated blood from heart to liver", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Deoxygenated blood from digestive system to liver", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blood containing nutrients from digestive system to liver", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Hepatic artery carries oxygenated blood from heart to liver',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9717,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Among ammonia, uric acid and urea, which one needs the least amount of water to excrete?','[{"text": "Ammonia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Uric acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Urea", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All require huge amount of water", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Uric acid is the least toxic among ammonia and urea, thus needs very little amount of water for its excretion from the body.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9718,'biology',86,979,2283,'Air sacs supplement ventilation in','[{"text": "<i>Columba</i>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "<i>Canis</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Calotes</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Carcharodon</i>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Air sacs connected to lungs supplement respiration in birds. Columba (pigeon) is a bird.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(9719,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Blood is at its highest pressure in which of the following chambers','[{"text": "Left atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Right atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Left ventricle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Right ventricle", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As left ventricle pumps oxygenated blood by the aorta to all the tissues of body parts. blood is at its highest pressure in left ventricle.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9720,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Animals accumulate wastes like urea, uric acid, $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}, \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$$, ions like $$\\mathrm{Na}^{+}, \\mathrm{K}^{+}, \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$$, phosphate, sulphate, etc, by','[{"text": "Metabolic activities", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Excess ingestion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Excretion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Animals accumulate ammonia, urea, uric acid, carbon dioxide, water and ions like $$\\mathrm{Na}^{+}$$, $$\\mathrm{K}^{+}, \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$$, phosphate, sulphate, etc., either by metabolic activities or by other means like excess ingestion. These substances have to be removed by the process of excretion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9721,'biology',86,979,2284,'Air entering lung is<br>(a) Warmed &emsp; &emsp; (b) Filtered<br>(c) Deprived of some oxygen<br>(d) Enriched with $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$','[{"text": "a, b, c, & d", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a & b", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "b & d", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "b & c", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The air entering into the lung is warm and filtered from dust particles. It is rich with $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and loss with $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9722,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Which one of the following is absent in the human beings?','[{"text": "Nucleated RBC", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conus arteriosus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Renal portal vein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All these structure are a part of the frog circulatory system',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9723,'biology',88,1150,2757,'Identify the correct order of toxicity among ammonia, urea and the uric acid (from lower to higher) is','[{"text": "Ammonia $$ < $$ urea $$ < $$ uric acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Uric acid $$ < $$ ammonia $$ < $$ urea", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Urea $$ < $$ uric acid $$ < $$ ammonia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Uric acid $$ < $$ urea $$ < $$ ammonia", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Ammonia, urea and uric acid are the major forms of nitrogenous wastes excreted by the animals. Ammonia is the most toxic form and requires large amount of water for its elimination, whereas uric acid, being the least toxic, can be removed with a minimum loss of water. Hence, order of toxicity is - Uric acid $$ < $$ Urea $$ < $$ Ammonia.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9734,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Find out the incorrect statement from the following:','[{"text": "The P-wave represents the electrical excitation (or depolarisation) of the atria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "For a detailed evaluation of the heart\'s function, multiple leads are attached to the chest region (chest leads).", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The end of the T-wave marks the end of systole.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Starting of T-wave marks the end of systole of ventricles.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The end of the T-wave marks the end of systole.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9724,'biology',86,980,2285,'Select the correct combination regarding breathing in man.<br>&emsp; &emsp; $\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{Inspiration}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}\n\\end{array}}&{\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{Expiration}}}\n\\end{array}}\n\\end{array}$','[{"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Ribcage}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{lowered}}}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{Ribcage}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{raised}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Volume}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{thorax}}}&{{\\\\rm{Volume}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{thorax}}}\\\\\\\\\\n{{\\\\rm{decreased}}\\\\,}&{{\\\\rm{increased}}\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Passive}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{process}}}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{Active}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{process}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\\\,\\n{{\\\\rm{Diaphragm}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{is}}}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{Diaphragm}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{is}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{arched}}}\\\\\\\\\\n{{\\\\rm{contracted}}}&{{\\\\rm{upwards}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'During inspiration, the diaphragm contracts and becomes flat due to which the volume of the thoracic cavity increases. This leads to an increase in the pulmonary volume and hence a decrease in the intra-pulmonary (alveolar) pressure which forces air from the atmosphere to enter the lungs. During expiration, the diaphragm relaxes and becomes arched upwards.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9725,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Find the incorrect matching:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "CAD-Atherosclerosis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pain in chest-Angina pectoris", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hypertension-BP raised", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heart failure-Heart attack", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Heart failure-Heart attack is an wrong pair. Heart failure means the state of heart when it is not pumping blood effectively enough to meet the needs of the body. when the heart muscle is suddenly damaged by an inadequate blood supply it is called as heart attack. <br>',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9726,'biology',88,1150,2757,'The process of excretion involves','[{"text": "Assimilating some parts of substances that is no longer required by the body.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Removal of nitrogenous wastes from the body", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Removal of useful substances from the body", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Removal of undigested food formed during digestion", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Excretion refers to the process of removal of nitrogenous wastes from the body of living organisms, which get accumulated due to the various metabolic activities and by other means such as excess ingestion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9727,'biology',86,980,2285,'Pressure gradient between the lungs and atmosphere is generated by<br>i. Diaphragm<br>ii. External intercostal muscales<br>iii. Internal intercostal muscles<br>iv. ribs','[{"text": "i and ii", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i,ii and iii", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "i, and iii", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i,ii,iii and iv", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The diaphragm and a specialised set of muscles external and internal intercostals between the ribs, help in generation of pressure gradients.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9728,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Select the incorrect matching from the following:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "RBC-Red Blood Cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AVN-Atrio Ventricular Node", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "SAN-Sino Atrial Node", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "WBC-White Blue Cells", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'WBC - White blood cells',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9729,'biology',88,1151,2758,'In mammalian kidneys, the Malpighian corpuscles are present in','[{"text": "Renal pelvis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Renal medulla", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Renal cortex", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Renal calyx", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Glomerulus alongwith Bowman\'s capsule, is called the malpighian body or renal corpuscle. The Malpighian corpuscle, PCT and DCT of the nephron are situated in the cortical region of the kidney whereas the loop of Henle dips into the medulla.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9730,'biology',86,982,2287,'In the alveoli, the formation of oxyhaemoglobin is favoured by','[{"text": "High $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$, low $\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$, low $\\\\mathrm{pH}$ and low temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "High $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$, low $\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$, low $\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$ concentration and low temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "High $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$, low $\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$, low $\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$ concentration and high temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "High $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$, high $\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$, high $\\\\mathrm{PH}$ and low temperature", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In the alveoli, the formation of oxyhaemoglobin is favoured by high $\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$, low $\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$, low $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$concentration and low temperature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9731,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Which of the following is correct?','[{"text": "Plasma - Blood", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atrioventricular node - Pacemaker", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Leucocytes - Lymphocytes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mitral valve - Bicuspid valve", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Mitral valve-Bicuspid valve is the left atrio-ventricular valve.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9732,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Which one of these is not a part of uriniferous tubule?','[{"text": "Loop of Henle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Renal pelvis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bowman\'s capsule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Distal convoluted tubule", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The uriniferous tubule/ nephron is the structural and functional unit of kidney. It comprises of Glomerulus, Bowman\'s capsule, PCT, Loop of Henle, DCT and collecting tubule. The collecting tubules are various nephrons later come together to constitute the renal pelvis which is not a part of an individual nephron.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9733,'biology',86,982,2287,'The partial pressure of oxygen in the alveoli is','[{"text": "$104 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$45 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$159 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$95 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Partial pressures in $\\mathrm{mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$ :<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}&{{\\rm{pO2}}}&{{\\rm{pCO2}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Atmospheric}}\\,{\\rm{air}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}&{{\\rm{159}}}&{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.3}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Alveolar}}\\,{\\rm{air}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}&{{\\rm{104}}}&{{\\rm{40}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Deoxygenated}}\\,{\\rm{blood}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}&{{\\rm{40}}}&{{\\rm{45}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Oxygenated}}\\,{\\rm{blood}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}&{{\\rm{95}}}&{{\\rm{40}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Resting}}\\,{\\rm{systemic}}\\,{\\rm{tissues}}}&{{\\rm{40}}}&{{\\rm{45}}}\n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(10259,'biology',9,24,44,'The flagella in brown algae is','[{"text": "They have no flagella", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two unequal, laterally arranged", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Many flagella arranged laterally", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two equal, laterally arranged", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Zoospores in brown algae have two unequal, laterally attached flagella.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9736,'biology',86,983,2288,'What will be the $\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ in the atmospheric air as compared to that of alveolar air?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ lesser and $\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ higher", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ higher and $\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ lesser", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ and $\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ lesser", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ and $\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ higher", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Pressure contributed by individual gas in a mixture of gases is called partial pressure and is represented as $\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ for oxygen and $\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ for carbon dioxide. It can be concluded that $\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ is higher $(159 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg})$ and $\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}(0.3 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{~Hg})$ is lesser in the atmospheric air as compared to that of alveolar air.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9737,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Select the incorrect statement from the following:','[{"text": "Each peak in the ECG is identified with a letter from $$\\\\mathrm{P}$$ to $$\\\\mathrm{T}$$ that corresponds to a specific electrical activity of the heart", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "By counting the number of QRS complexes (in given time period), we can the find pulse rate.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The QRS complex represents the depolarisation of the ventricles, which initiates the atrial contraction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "T-wave represent repolarization of ventricle.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The QRS complex represents the depolarisation of the ventricles, which initiates the ventricular contraction.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9738,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Blood vessel draining the glomerulus immediately is called','[{"text": "Afferent arteriole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Efferent venule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Efferent arteriole", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Renal vein", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Glomerular capillaries emerge to form efferent arteriole which drains off the glomerulus immediately.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9739,'biology',86,983,2288,'$\\mathrm{P}_{50}$ value denotes','[{"text": "Percentage of oxygen.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Partial pressure of $\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Percentage of $\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Partial pressure of $\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{p}_{50}$ value is the oxygen tension at half saturation $(50 \\%)$ of blood. It is calculated from the measured oxygen tension and oxygen saturation by extrapolation along the oxygen dissociation curve to $50 \\%$ saturation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9740,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Which of the following is incorrect about human heart?','[{"text": "The volume of both atria $$>$$ the volume of both ventricles", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The volume of both ventricle $$>$$ the volume of both atria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The deoxygenated blood pumped into the pulmonary artery is passed on to the lungs from where the oxygenated blood is carried by the pulmonary veins into the left atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The cardiac output can be defined as the volume of blood pumped out by each ventricle per minute", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The volume of both ventricles is greater than volume of both atria.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9741,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Which of the following is a part of medulla?','[{"text": "Proximal convoluted tubule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Distal convoluted tubule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Malphighian corpuscle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Loop of Henle", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Loop of Henle is a part of medullary pyramid. The Malpighian corpuscle, PCT and DCT of the nephron are situated in the cortical region of the kidney whereas the loop of Henle dips into the medulla.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9742,'biology',86,983,2288,'Which of the following reactions is catalyzed by carbonic anhydrase at the lungs?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CO}_{3} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}+\\\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CO}_{3} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Hb}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{HbO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At the tissues, carbonic anhydrase enzyme present in the erythrocytes catalyzes the formation of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ from $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ and its dissociation into $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$and $\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}$at tissues. At the alveoli, it catalyzes the formation of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ from $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$and $\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}$its dissociation into $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$. <img src=\'/img/data/biology/86_9742_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9743,'biology',87,1122,2693,'Find out the correct statement:','[{"text": "Globulins are primarily involved in the clotting mechanism of body.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Albumin is mainly the defence system of the body.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plasma without clotting factor is called lymph.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The factors for coagulation of blood are also present in the plasma in an inactive form.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Globulins are primarily involved in the defence mechanism of body. Albumin mainly helps in maintaining osmotic balance of the body. Plasma without clotting factor is called as serum.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9744,'biology',88,1151,2758,'The majority of nephrons in which, the loop of Henle found is too short and extend very little into the medulla are','[{"text": "Peritubular nephrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Medullary nephrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Juxta-medullary nephrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cortical nephrons", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In majority of nephrons, the loop of Henle is too short and extends only very little into the medulla. These nephrons are characterised as cortical nephrons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9745,'biology',86,984,2289,'Respiratory rhythm is primarily controlled by','[{"text": "Central nervous system", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Sympathetic nervous system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Parasympathetic nervous system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Autonomic nervous system", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Respiratory centers are the centers in the medulla oblongata and pons varolii that collects sensory information about the level of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the blood and determines the signals to be sent to the respiratory muscles.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9777,'biology',86,981,2286,'When $1200 \\mathrm{~mL}$ air is left in the lungs, it is called','[{"text": "Vital capacity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tidal volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Residual volume reserve", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inspiratory volume", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Residual volume (RV) is the volume of air remaining in lungs even after a forcible expiration. It is about $1100-1200 \\mathrm{ml}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9746,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Which of the following statements is not true?','[{"text": "In amphibians and reptiles, there is complete double circulation.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In human beings the heart is situated in the thoracic cavity, in between the two lungs slightly tilted to the left.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Human heart has the size of a clenched fist.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heart is protected by a double walled membranous bag (pericardium) with pericardial fluid.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In amphibians and reptiles, there is incomplete double circulation.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9747,'biology',88,1151,2758,'In some of the nephrons, the loop of Henle is very long and runs deep into the medulla','[{"text": "Peritubular nephrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Medullary nephrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Juxta-medullary nephrons", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cortical nephrons", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In some of the nephrons, the loop of Henle is very long and runs deep into the medulla. These nephrons are called juxtamedullary nephrons. Due to long Henle\'s loop, they have prominent vasa recta also.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9748,'biology',86,985,2290,'Which of the following is not a lung disorder?','[{"text": "Asthma", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Emphysema", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Silicosis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Angina", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Angina pectoris refers to an acute chest pain that appears when enough oxygen is not reaching the heart muscle due to myocardial ischemia.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9749,'biology',88,1151,2758,'The peritubular capillaries around the renal tubule emerge from','[{"text": "Vasa recta", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Afferent arteriole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Efferent arteriole", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Renal arteriole", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The efferent arteriole emerging from the glomerulus forms a fine capillary network around the renal tubule called the peritubular capillaries.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9750,'biology',87,1124,2696,'Which of the following is the best representation of the enzyme cascade involved in blood coagulation?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Thrombokinase converts prothrombin to thrombin. Thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9751,'biology',88,1151,2758,'The U-shaped minute vessel that runs parallel to another $$\\mathrm{U}$$ shaped tubule is','[{"text": "Loop of Henle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vasa recta", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Glomerulus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A minute vessel of the peritubular capillaries runs parallel to the Henle\'s loop forming a \' $$U$$ \' shaped vasa recta.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9752,'biology',86,979,2284,'In the given diagram of human respiratory system, few parts are marked as I, II, III, IV,<br>V & VI. Choose the correct combination of labelling from the given options.','[{"text": "I- Nose, II- Bronchus, III- Larynx, IV- Diaphragm, V-Trachea, VI- Lung", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I- Nose, II- Larynx, III- Bronchus, IV- Lung, V- Diaphragm, VI- Trachea", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I- Mouth, II- Trachea, III- Larynx, IV- Lung, V- Diaphragm, Vi- Bronchus.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I- Mouth, II- Diaphragm, III- Trachea, IV- Bronchi, V-Larynx, VI- Lung", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The correct labelling of the structure marked in the figure of respiratory system are: I - Nose, II - Bronchus, III - Larynx, IV Diaphragm, V - Trachea, VI - Lung.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/86_9752_question_1.png'),
(9753,'biology',87,1123,2695,'A doctor prescribed an injection of anti $$\\mathrm{Rh}$$ antibodies to a woman soon after testing the blood type of the first child she delivered. Which of the following is true about the blood type of the woman and the newborn child?','[{"text": "She is $$\\\\mathrm{Rh}$$ negative and the child is $$\\\\mathrm{Rh}$$ positive", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "She is $$\\\\mathrm{Rh}$$ negative and the child is $$\\\\mathrm{Rh}$$ negative", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "She is $$\\\\mathrm{Rh}$$ positive and the child is $$\\\\mathrm{Rh}$$ positive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "She is $$\\\\mathrm{Rh}$$ positive and the child is $$\\\\mathrm{Rh}$$ nagative", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Erythroblastosis foetalis can occur when mother is Rh negative and foetus is Rh positive. This can be avoided by administering anti-Rh antibodies to the mother immediately after the delivery of the first child.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9754,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Vasa recta is absent or highly reduced in the','[{"text": "Medullary nephrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cortical nephrons", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Juxta-medullary nephrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In majority of nephrons, the loop of Henle is too short and extends only very little into the medulla. Such nephrons are called cortical nephrons. Vasa recta is absent or highly reduced in cortical nephrons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9755,'biology',86,980,2285,'The figure given below shows the mechanism of breathing. Identify the stage $(\\mathrm{X})$ of breathing explained & A, B and C marked in the figure.','[{"text": "X – Expiration, A– raised, B – decreased,\\nC – relaxed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "X – Inspiration, A – raised,\\nB – decreased, C – relaxed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "X – Expiration, A – raised, B – increased,\\nC – contracted", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "X - Inspiration, A– raised, B – increased,\\nC –contracted", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{X}$ - Inspiration, $\\mathrm{A}-$ raised, $\\mathrm{B}-$ increased, $\\mathrm{C}-$ contracted. The given figure shows the inspiration stage of breathing. During inspiration, the external intercostal muscles contracts and the internal intercostal muscle relax. This pulls the ribcage up. At the same time the diaphragm muscle contracts which flattens the diaphragm. Both actions increase the volume of thorax.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/86_9755_question_1.png'),
(9756,'biology',87,1126,2698,'Consider the following statements<br>I. Vertebrates and a few invertebrates have a closed circulatory system.<br>II. Closed circulatory systems have blood closed at all times within vessels.<br>III. Human circulatory system is called the cardiovascular system.<br>Which of the above are correct?','[{"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Vertebrates, and a few invertebrates, have a closed circulatory system. Closed<br>circulatory systems have the blood closed at all times within vessels of different size and wall thickness. In this type of system, blood is pumped by a heart through vessels, and does not normally fill body cavities. Human circulatory system is called the cardiovascular system.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9757,'biology',88,1151,2758,'A.The ____ 1 ____ arteriole emerging from the glomerulus forms a fine capillary network around the ____ 2 ____ called the peritubular capillaries.<br>B. The Malpighian corpuscle, PCT and DCT of the nephron are situated in the ____ 3 ____of the kidney','[{"text": "1-Afferent, 2 -renal tubule, 3- cortical region", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1- Efferent, 2 -renal tubule, 3- cortical region", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1-Afferent arteriole, 2- Juxta-medullary nephrons 3-cortical region", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>A. The efferent arteriole emerging from the glomerulus forms a fine capillary network around the renal tubule called the peritubular capillaries.<br>B. The Malpighian corpuscle, PCT and DCT of the nephron are situated in the cortical region of the kidney',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9758,'biology',86,983,2288,'The percentage of carbondioxide that is transported from the tissues to the lungs as carbamino compounds is','[{"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20-25", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "70", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'About $20-25 \\%$ of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is transported from the tissue to the lungs by haemoglobin as carbaminohaemoglobin.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9759,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Choose the incorrect statement about blood circulation in humans.','[{"text": "The left side of the heart is the pump for systemic circulation and the right side of the heart is the pump for pulmonary circulation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The output of systemic circuit becomes the input of pulmonary circuit and vice versa.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blood from the right side of the heart must pass through the lungs to reach the left side of the heart", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Per each minute, the amount of blood flowing through systemic circulation is greater than that flowing through pulmonary circulation", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Right ventricle and left ventricle pump the same amount of blood in each heartbeat. Blood pumped by right ventricle passes through the pulmonary circulation and returns to the left ventricle through the left atrium. Left ventricle pumps it to the systemic tissues through the aorta and its branches.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9760,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Malpighian corpuscle, PCT and DCT<br>B. Loop of Henle<br>C. Collecting duct<br>D. Vasa recta</div><div class="column-right">P. Dips into the medulla<br>Q. Situated in cortical region<br>R. U-shaped vessel<br>S. Open into the renal pelvis</div></div>','[{"text": "A-P, B-Q, C-S, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-P, C-S, D-R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-S, B-R, C-Q, D-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-S, B-R, C-P, D-Q", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A. Malpighian Corpuscle, PCT & DCT - Q. Situated in cortical region<br>B. Loop of Henle - P. Dips into the medulla<br>C. Collecting duct - S. Open into the renal pelvis<br>D. Vasa recta - R. U-shaped vessel',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9761,'biology',86,981,2286,'Which of the following pairs is correctly matched?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Vital}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{capacity}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{\\\\,{\\\\rm{:}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ERV + IRV}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Total}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{lung}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{capacity}}}&{\\\\,{\\\\rm{:}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{VC + RV}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Functional}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{residual}}}&{\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{:}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ERV + TV}}}\\\\\\\\\\n{{\\\\rm{capacity}}}&{}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Inspiratory}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{capacity}}}&{{\\\\rm{:}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{TV + ERV}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{TLC}} = {\\rm{VC}} + {\\rm{RV}}\\\\\n{\\rm{VC}} = {\\rm{VC}} + {\\rm{RV}}\\\\\n{\\rm{FRC}} = {\\rm{ERV}} + {\\rm{RV}}\\\\\n{\\rm{IC}} = {\\rm{TV}} + {\\rm{IRV}}\n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9763,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Heart is felt on the left side, though it is centrally located?','[{"text": "Slightly tilted to the right", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Slightly tilted to the left", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Slightly tilted to the down", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Slightly tilted to the up", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Heart is felt on the left side, though it is centrally located because it is slightly tilted to the left.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9764,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Glomerulus<br>B. Bowman’s capsule<br>C. Malpighian body<br>D. PCT</div><div class="column-right">P. Highly coiled network<br>Q. Glomerulus along with Bowman’s capsule<br>R. Double walled cup-like Structure<br>S. Tuft of capillaries formed by the afferent arteriole</div></div>','[{"text": "A-P, B-S, C-Q, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-P, C-R, D-S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-P, B-S, C-R, D-Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-S, B-R, C-Q, D-P", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A. Glomerulus - S. Tuft of capillaries formed by the afferent arteriole<br>B. Bowman\'s capsule - R. Double walled cup- like structure<br>C. Malpighian Body - Q. Glomerulus along with Bowman\'s capsule<br>D. PCT - P. Highly coiled network',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9765,'biology',86,979,2284,'Which one of the following four organs of respiratory system is correctly matched with its characteristics?<br>A. Bronchi- Two branches of the trachea that brings air into the lungs.<br>B. Trachea- Small flap that prevents food from entering<br>C. Diaphragm - Dome shaped muscle that pushes on the lungs during exhalation.<br>D. Alveoli- Pair of organs that inflate as you inhale and deflate as you exhale.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A and B only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C and D only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A and C only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "B and D only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Trachea (also called windpipe) is a tube that connects the larynx to the lungs, allowing the passage of air, and so is present in all air breathing animals with lungs. Alveoli - main site of gaseous exchanges.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9768,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Delivers blood to the kidney<br>B. Carries urine to pelvis<br>C. Collects nitrogenous waste from Bowman&rsquo;s capsule<br>D. Loop of Henle</div><div class="column-right">P. Proximal convoluted tubule<br>Q. Ascending and descending limb<br>R. Collecting duct<br>S. Renal artery</div></div>','[{"text": "A-S, B-R, C-P, D-Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-P, B-S, C-Q, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-S, B-Q, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A. Delivers blood to the kidney - S. Renal artery<br>B. Carries urine to pelvis - R. Collecting duct<br>C. Collects nitrogenous waste from Bowman\'s capsule - P. Proximal convoluted tubule<br>D. Loop of Henle - Q. Ascending and descending limb',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9769,'biology',86,981,2286,'The inspiratory capacity, functional residual capacity and residual volume of a person are $3,500 \\mathrm{~mL}, 2,300 \\mathrm{~mL}$ and $1,200 \\mathrm{~mL}$, respectively. What is his vital capacity?','[{"text": "$7,000 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4,700 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4,600 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2,400 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{IC}=3,500 \\mathrm{~mL} ; \\mathrm{FRC}=2,300 \\mathrm{~mL} ; \\mathrm{RV}=$ $1,200 \\mathrm{~mL} ; \\mathrm{VC}=$ ?<br>$\\mathrm{FRC}=\\mathrm{ERV}+\\mathrm{RV}$<br>${\\rm{2,300}}$ $\\mathrm{mL}=\\mathrm{ERV}+1,200 \\mathrm{~mL}$<br>$\\mathrm{ERV}=2,300 \\mathrm{~mL}-1,200 \\mathrm{~mL}=1,100 \\mathrm{~mL}$<br>$\\mathrm{VC}=\\mathrm{IRV}+\\mathrm{TV}+\\mathrm{ERV}=\\mathrm{IC}+\\mathrm{ERV}$ $=3,500 \\mathrm{~mL}+1,100 \\mathrm{~mL}=4,600 \\mathrm{~mL}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9771,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Pathway of myogenic impulse conduction is','[{"text": "SAN - AVN - Purkinje fibres - Bundle of His", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "SAN - AVN - Bundle of His - Purkinje fibres", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "AVN - Bundle of His - SAN - Purkinje fibers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AVN - SAN - Bundle of His - Purkinje fibers", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The electrical conduction system of the heart transmits signals generated usually by the sinoatrial node to cause contraction of the heart muscle. The pacemaking signal generated in the sinoatrial node travels through the right atrium to the atrioventricular node, along the Bundle of His and through bundle branches to cause contraction of the heart muscle. This signal stimulates contraction first of the right and left atrium, and then the right and left ventricles. This process allows blood to be pumped throughout the body.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9772,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Match the columns I and II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Cortical nephrons<br>B. Juxta medullary nephrons<br>C. Peritubular capillaries<br>D. Vasa recta</div><div class="column-right">P. Loop of Henle is very long<br>Q. U-shaped vessel runs parallel to loop of Henle<br>R. Loop of Henle too short<br>S. Efferent arteriole emerging from the glomerulus</div></div>','[{"text": "A-P, B-Q, C-R, D-S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-P, B-R, C-S, D-Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-R, B-P, C-S, D-Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-Q, B-R, C-S, D-P", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A. Cortical nephrons - R. Loop of Henle too short<br>B. Juxta Medullary nephrons - P. Loop of Henle is very long<br>C. Peritubular capillaries - S. Efferent arteriole emerging from the glomerulus<br>D. Vasa Recta - Q. U-shaped vessel runs parallel to loop of Henle',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9773,'biology',86,983,2288,'Select the correct statement.','[{"text": "Binding of oxygen with haemoglobin is primarily related to the partial pressure of $\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The amount of $\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ that can diffuse through the diffusion membrane per unit difference in partial pressure is much higher compared to that of $\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "About 70 per cent of $\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is transported by $\\\\mathrm{RBCs}$ in the blood and the remaining 30 per cent of $\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is carried in a dissolved state through the plasma.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A sigmoid curve is obtained when the percentage saturation of haemoglobin with $\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is plotted against the $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve is sigmoidal in shape because hemoglobin has an increasing affinity for $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as the number of bound $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules goes up (positive cooperativity). No more binding is possible after four oxygen molecules bind. Binding of oxygen with haemoglobin is primarily related to the partial pressure of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9775,'biology',87,1127,2699,'If due to some injury the chordae tendinae of the bicuspid valve of the human heart is partially nonfunctional. What would be the immediate effect?','[{"text": "Heart beat would become irregular.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blood will tend to move back into the right atrium.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The flow of blood into pulmonary artery will be reduced.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The flow of blood into the aorta will be slowed down.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A bicuspid or mitral valve guards the opening between the left atrium and the left ventricle. If due to some injury the chordae tendinae of the bicuspid valve of the human heart is partially non-functional then the flow of blood into the aorta will be slowed down.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9776,'biology',88,1151,2758,'The given diagram is of nephron. Which part is called as Malpighian body in nephron?','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{B}+\\\\mathrm{A}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{A}+\\\\mathrm{D}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}+\\\\mathrm{D}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{B}+\\\\mathrm{C}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'B represents glomerulus. C represents Bowman\'s capsule. Glomerulus alongwith Bowman\'s capsule $$(\\mathrm{B}+\\mathrm{C})$$, is called the malpighian body or renal corpuscle.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/88_9776_question_1.png'),
(9779,'biology',87,1127,2699,'What will happen if the neuromuscular conduction pathway in heart is broken between SA node and AV node?','[{"text": "No contraction or heart beat will be seen in heart.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Auricles will contract due to impulse generated by SA node while ventricles will not show any activity.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There will be no effect on the activity of heart.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Auricles will contract at the rate set by the SA node while ventricles will contract at the rate of impulse generated by the AV node.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If the neuromuscular conduction pathway in heart is broken between SA node and AV node then auricles will contract at the rate set by the SA node while ventricles will contract at the rate of impulse generated by the AV node.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9780,'biology',88,1151,2758,'The given diagram is of nephron showing blood vessels and ducts. Which part is lined by simple cuboidal brush border epithelium that increases the surface area for reabsorption?','[{"text": "B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "D", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'PCT is lined by simple cuboidal brush border epithelium which increases the surface area for reabsorption. It is represented by $$\\mathrm{D}$$ in the given figure.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/88_9780_question_1.png'),
(9781,'biology',86,983,2288,'Select the wrong statement.','[{"text": "About 20-25 per cent of carbon dioxide is transported from the tissues to the lungs as carbaminohaemoglobin.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Binding of carbon dioxide to haemoglobin is related to $\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Binding of carbon dioxide to haemoglobin is not affected by $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Carbamino-haemoglobin dissociates into $\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and haemoglobin at the alveoli.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The binding of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to haemoglobin is related to the partial pressure of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2} \\cdot \\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ is a major factor which could affect this binding.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9783,'biology',87,1127,2699,'If a person is undergoing artificial pacemaker transplant then at which of the following structures the transplantation occurs?','[{"text": "Right ventricle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "SA node", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Right auricle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "SA bundles", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If a person is undergoing artificial pacemaker transplant then this is transplantation of SA node.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9784,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Given diagram showing nephron structure. Identify the labeled (A-D) parts?','[{"text": "A-Afferent arteriole, B-Glomerulus, C-Bowman\'s capsule, D-Efferent arteriole", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A- Glomerulus, B- Bowman\'s capsule, C- Efferent arteriole, D- Afferent arteriole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A- Bowman\'s capsule, B- Glomerulus, C- Afferent arteriole, D- Efferent arteriole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Efferent arteriole, B-Afferent arteriole, C- Glomerulus, D- Bowman\'s capsule", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A. Afferent arteriole<br>B. Glomerulus<br>C. Bowman\'s capsule<br>D. Efferent arteriole',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/88_9784_question_1.png'),
(9785,'biology',86,983,2288,'What would happen if human blood becomes acidic (low $\\mathrm{pH}$ )?','[{"text": "Oxygen carying capacity of haemoglobin increases.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxygen carrying capacity of haemoglobin decreases.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "WBCs count increases.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RBCs count decreases.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If human blood becomes acidic, oxygen affinity of haemoglobin decreases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9787,'biology',87,1122,2694,'Haemoglobin $$(\\mathrm{Hb})$$ transports oxygen from lungs to tissues. The partial pressure of oxygen in lungs is different from that in tissues. Each $$\\mathrm{Hb}$$ can bind to up to four oxygen molecules. Suppose we have an equal number of $$\\mathrm{Hb}$$ and oxygen molecuels and all the oxygen molecules are in bound form.<br>Then, which of the following is TRUE?','[{"text": "Almost all the $$\\\\mathrm{Hb}$$ molecules have one bound oxygen molecule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nearly half of the $$\\\\mathrm{Hb}$$ molecules are each bound to two oxygen molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Most of the $$\\\\mathrm{Hb}$$ molecules have one bound oxygen molecule each; the rest either have no bound oxygen or have two or more bound oxygen molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nearly one-fourth of all the $$\\\\mathrm{Hb}$$ molecules are bound to four oxygen molecules each.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'4 oxygen molecules bind to the one molecule of haemoglobin therefore nearly one fourth of all the $$\\mathrm{Hb}$$ molecules will bound to four oxygen molecules each',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9788,'biology',88,1151,2758,'Given diagram showing nephron structure. Identify the labeled (A-C) parts?','[{"text": "A- Vasa recta, B- Ascending limb of loop of Henle, C-Descending limb of loop of Henle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Descending limb of loop of Henle, B-Ascending limb of loop of Henle, C-Vasa recta", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-Ascending limb of loop of Henle, B- Descending limb of loop of Henle, C-Vasa recta", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Descending limb of loop of Henle, B-Vasa recta, C-Ascending limb of loop of Henle", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A. Vasa recta<br>B. Ascending limb of Loop of Henle<br>C. Descending limb of Loop of Henle',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/88_9788_question_1.png'),
(9789,'biology',86,985,2290,'In asthma, muscle spasm causes constriction of small bronchioles but not trachea because','[{"text": "Trachea has pseudostratified epithelium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Trachea has cartilaginous rings", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Trachea divides into primary bronchi", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Exchange of gases does not occur in the trachea", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The trachea, primary, secondary and tertiary bronchi, and initial bronchioles are supported by incomplete cartilaginous rings.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9792,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Which of the following statements concerning the cardiac output is correct?','[{"text": "Is increased by stimulation of the vagus nerve.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Is increased by aldosterone released from the adrenal medulla.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Is largely determined by the end diastolic volume", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Can be measured by dividing the oxygen consumption by the difference in $$\\\\mathrm{PO}_{2}$$ of the venous and arterial blood", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Cardiac output is increased by circulating adrenaline (epinephrine) and not by aldosterone, which is secreted by the adrenal cortex. It can be measured by the dividing the oxygen consumption of the body by the difference in oxygen content between the mixed venous and blood arteries.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9793,'biology',86,985,2290,'Asthma may be attributed to','[{"text": "Bacterial infection of lungs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Allergic reaction of the mast cells in the lungs", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inflammation of the trachea", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Accumulation of fluid in the lungs", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Asthma is due to inflammation of bronchi & Bronchioles caused due to mast cells.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9795,'biology',88,1152,2759,'Filtration of blood occurs in','[{"text": "Loop of Henle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bowman\'s capsule", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lungs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Renal papillae", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The first step in urine formation is the filtration of blood, which is carried out by the glomerulus and is called glomerular filtration.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9796,'biology',87,1128,2700,'In a typical heart, if EDV is $$120 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ of blood and $$\\mathrm{ESV}$$ is $$50 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ of blood, the stroke volume (SV) is','[{"text": "$$120+50=70 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$120-50=70 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$120 / 50=2.4 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$120 / 50=6000 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'During diastole, normal filling of the ventricles increases the volume of each ventricle. This volume is called the enddiastolic blood volume (EDV). Then, as the ventricles empty during systole, the volume decreases, which is called stroke volume output (SV). The remaining volume in each ventricle is called the end systolic volume (ESV).<br>Therefore, stroke volume can be calculated by the following formula:<br>$$\\mathrm{EDV}=\\mathrm{ESV}+\\mathrm{SV}$$<br>Here, EDV is $$120 \\mathrm{~mL}$$; ESV is $$50 \\mathrm{~mL}$$ so $$\\mathrm{SV}$$ will become.<br>$$120=50+\\mathrm{SV}$$<br>$$\\mathrm{SV}=(120-50) \\mathrm{mL}$$<br>Therefore, $$\\mathrm{SV}=70 \\mathrm{~mL}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9798,'biology',86,979,2284,'The given figure shows the respiratory system. Identify the correct structure marked as 1, 2, 3and 4 whose contraction initiates the<br>inspiration','[{"text": "1-lung", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-Diaphragm", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3-pleural", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4-Alveoli", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The correct labelling is: 1 - Lung, 2 Diaphragm, 3 Pleural membrane, 4 - Alveoli.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/86_9798_question_1.png'),
(9799,'biology',88,1152,2759,'The correct order of processes that occur in urine formation.','[{"text": "Tubular secretion $$\\\\rightarrow$$ Glomerular filtration $$\\\\rightarrow$$ Reabsorption", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glomerular filtration $$\\\\rightarrow$$ Tubular secretion $$\\\\rightarrow$$ Reabsorption", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glomerular filtration $$\\\\rightarrow$$ Reabsorption $$\\\\rightarrow$$ Tubular secretion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Tubular secretion $$\\\\rightarrow$$ Reabsorption $$\\\\rightarrow$$ Glomerular filtration", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Urine formation involves three main processes namely, glomerular filtration, reabsorption and secretion taking place in the order they are mentioned.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9800,'biology',87,1128,2700,'If the systolic pressure is $$120 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$$ and diastolic pressure is $$80 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$$. The pulse pressure is ____','[{"text": "$$120-{80=40 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$120 \\\\times 80=960 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$120+80=200 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$120 / 80=1.5 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the healthy young adults, the pressure at the top of pressure pulsation record, called the systolic pressure is about $$120 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$$. At the lowest point of pulse record, called diastolic pressure, it is about $$80 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$$. The difference between the two pressures (ie., about $$40 \\mathrm{~mm}$$ $$\\mathrm{Hg}$$ ) is called as pulse pressure.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9833,'biology',87,1130,2702,'The hepatic portal vein is formed by the union of the','[{"text": "Hepatic veins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Internal and external iliac veins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Subclavian and internal jugular veins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gastric, superior mesenteric and splenic veins", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The blood in the hepatic portal vein passes through the liver on its way back to the heart. Nutrients, metabolic wastes and hormones from the digestive tract and pancreas act upon or are acted upon by the liver before they make their way to the heart.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9802,'biology',86,983,2288,'Refer the given figure and answer the question. Which of the following statements is correct regarding the above figure?','[{"text": "When percentage saturation of haemoglobin is plotted against the partial pressure of oxygen, a sigmoid curve is obtained.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Binding of oxygen with haemoglobin is primarily related to partial pressure of carbon monoxide.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The given graph illustrates the amount of $\\\\mathrm{HbO}_{2}$ as similar to $\\\\mathrm{Hb}$ at different $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The given graph shows the oxygen haemoglobin dissociation curve. The relationship between the $\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ and the percent saturation of haemoglobin when represented on a graph is termed as oxygen - haemoglobin dissociation curve. It is $\\mathrm{S}$-shaped because of the behaviour of the $\\mathrm{Hb}$ in different $\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/86_9802_question_1.png'),
(9803,'biology',88,1152,2759,'How much amount of blood is filtered out by kidney\'s/min?','[{"text": "$$500 \\\\mathrm{ml}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1100-1200 \\\\mathrm{ml}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$1500 \\\\mathrm{ml}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$125 \\\\mathrm{ml}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'On an average, $$1100-1200 \\mathrm{ml}$$ of blood is filtered by the kidneys per minute which constitute roughly $$1 / 5$$ th of the blood pumped out by each ventricle of the heart in a minute.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9804,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Identify the correct statements from the following<br>I. The electrocardiogram is primarily used for evaluating the electrical events within the heart<br>II. A normal ECG composed of P, Q, R, S and $\\mathrm{T}$ wave<br>III. The $\\mathrm{P}$ wave indicates the repolarisation of the atria<br>IV. The $\\mathrm{Q}$ wave indicates the atricular repolarisation','[{"text": "II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV and I", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The correct statements are&lt;br&gt;I. The electrocardiogram is primarily used for evaluating&lt;br&gt;the electrical events within the heart&lt;br&gt;II. A normal ECG composed of P, Q, R, S and wave. remaining lines can be corrected as&lt;br&gt;The&lt;br&gt;The P-wave represents the electrical excitation (or depolarisation) of the atriaThe contraction starts shortly after Q and marks the beginning of the systole',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9806,'biology',86,981,2286,'Match Column I with Column II :<br>Choose the correct answer from the options\ngiven below :<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Expiratory capacity<br>B. Functional residual capacity<br>C. Vital capacity<br>D. Inspiratory capacity</div><div class="column-right">P. Expiratory reserve volume + Tidal volume +Inspiratory reserve volume<br>Q. Tidal volume + Expiratory reserve volume<br>R. Tidal volume + Inspiratory reserve volume<br>S. Expiratory reserve\nvolume + Residual volume</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  Q, B  -  S, C  -  P, D  -  R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ A  -  R, B  -  Q, C  -  S, D  -  P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ A  -  Q, B  -  P, C  -  S, D  -  R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ A  -  P, B  -  R, C  -  Q, D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{A}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{Q,}}\\,{\\rm{B}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{S,}}\\,{\\rm{C}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{P,}}\\,{\\rm{D}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{R}}$ is the correct option.<br>Expiratory capacity $=$ Tidal volume + Expiratory reserve volume<br>Functional residual capacity $=$ Expiratory reserve volume + Residual volume<br>Vital capacity $=$ Expiratory reserve volume + Tidal volume + Inspiratory reserve volume<br>Inspiratory capacity $=$ Tidal volume + Inspiratory reserve volume.',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9807,'biology',88,1152,2759,'Filtration membrane consist of','[{"text": "Endothelium of glomerular blood vessels, epithelium of Bowman\'s capsule , glomerulus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Epithelium of Bowman\'s capsule, podocytes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Basement membrane between the above two layers, epithelium of Bowman\'s capsule, endothelium of glomerular blood vessels", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The glomerular capillary blood pressure causes filtration of blood through 3 layers, i.e., the endothelium of glomerular blood vessels, the epithelium of Bowman\'s capsule and a basement membrane between these two layers',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9808,'biology',87,1127,2699,'All the components of the conducting system of the heart can generate action potentials for the contraction of myocardium, but the sinoatrial node (SA node) acts as the pacemaker because','[{"text": "SA node conducts action potentials faster than any other part of the nodal system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only the SA node is auto-excitable and autorhythmic but not AV node", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The SA node is located in the atria whereas $$\\\\mathrm{AV}$$ node is located in the ventricles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The rate of rhythmic discharge of depolarisation is faster in SA node than in any other part of the nodal system", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The SAN can generate the maximum number of action potentials (average $$72 \\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$$ ) and is responsible for initiating and maintaining the rhythmic contractile activity of the heart. Therefore, it is called the pacemaker. The cells of the AV node, bundle of His and Purkinje fibres also produce a spontaneous action potential but at a slower rate. If the SA node fails, AV node becomes the pacemaker. If SA node and AV node both fail to function, Bundle of His or Purkinje fibres become the pacemaker. Conduction velocity is the fastest in Purkinje fibres and not SA node.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9810,'biology',86,983,2288,'Which of the following factors are favourable for the formation of oxyhaemoglobin in alveoli?','[{"text": "High $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ and High $\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "High $\\\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$ and Lesser $\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$concentration", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Low $\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ and $\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$concentration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low $\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ and High temperature", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Conditions favourable for formation of oxyhaemoglobin in alveoli are high $\\mathrm{pO}_{2}$, less $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$concentration low $\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}$ and low temperature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9811,'biology',88,1152,2759,'The correct order of membrane from filtrate to blood that occur in glomerular filtration are.','[{"text": "Basement membrane $\\\\rightarrow$ Epithelium of Bowman\'s capsule $\\\\rightarrow$ Endothelium of glomerular blood vessels", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Epithelium of Bowman\'s capsule $\\\\rightarrow$ Endothelium of glomerular blood vessels $\\\\rightarrow$ Basement membrane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Endothelium of glomerular blood vessels $\\\\rightarrow$ Epithelium of Bowman\'s capsule $\\\\rightarrow$ Basement membrane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Epithelium of Bowman&rsquo;s capsule &rarr; Basement membrane between these two layers &rarr; Endothelium of glomerular blood vessels", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Epithelium of Bowman&rsquo;s capsule (close to filtrate) &rarr; Basement membrane between these two layers &rarr; Endothelium of glomerular blood vessels (close to blood)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9812,'biology',87,1131,2703,'Identify the correct statement regarding cardiac activity:<br>I. Normal activities of the human heart is regulated intrinsically, hence it is neurogenic.<br>II. A special neural centre in the medulla oblongata can moderate the cardiac function through the CNS<br>III. Parasympathetic neural signals increase the rate of heart beat<br>IV. Adrenal medullary hormones can increase cardiac output.','[{"text": "IV only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "IV and I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Cardiac output is increased by circulating adrenaline (epinephrine) and not by aldosterone, which is secreted by the adrenal cortex.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9814,'biology',88,1152,2759,'Cells named podocytes occur in the wall of','[{"text": "Bowman\'s capsule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glomerulus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Outer wall of Bowman\'s capsules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inner wall of Bowman\'s capsules", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The epithelial cells present on the inner wall of Bowman\'s capsule are called podocytes. They are arranged in an intricate manner so as to leave some minute spaces called filtration slits or slit pores.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9815,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Which of the following cardiac events occur between Q wave and T wave on ECG?<br>i. Opening of bicuspid and tricuspid valves<br>ii. Closure of bicuspid and tricuspid valves<br>iii. Opening of semilunar valves<br>iv. Closure of semilunar valves<br>v. Lub sound<br>vi. Dub sound','[{"text": "i, iii and v", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, iv and vi", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ii, iii and v", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii, iv and vi", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'QRS represents ventricular depolarisation and $$\\mathrm{T}$$ wave represents ventricular repolarisation. Ventricular systole starts shortly after Q (NCERT). End of T wave marks the end of systole (NCERT). During ventricular systole, bicuspid and tricuspid valves close (producing lub sound) and semilunar valves open.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9817,'biology',88,1152,2759,'Podocytes are','[{"text": "Endothelial cells of the glomerulus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Endothelial cells of the Bowman\'s capsule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Epithelial cells of the Bowman\'s capsule", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Epithelial cells of the glomerulus", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The epithelial cells of Bowman\'s capsule called podocytes. These are arranged in an intricate manner so as to leave some minute spaces called filtration slits or slit pores.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9818,'biology',87,1128,2700,'During exercise the cardiac output may rise to five times the resting value. In most people this is mainly due to an increase in','[{"text": "Ejection dfraction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stroke volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heart rate", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "End - diastole volume", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'During exercise the cardiac output may rise to five times the resting value. In most people this is mainly due to an increase in heart rate.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(9820,'biology',88,1152,2759,'Bowman\'s capsule is lined by','[{"text": "Ciliated epithelium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Squamous epithelium", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cuboidal epithelium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Brush bordered epithelium", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Bowman\'s capsule is lined by squamous epithelial cells called podocytes, arranged in a manner so as to leave minute spaces for the cause of filtration in between.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
