(12793,'biology',93,284,544,'Queen Victoria was carrier of haemophilia, a sex linked disease. What is true?','[{"text": "Queen’s father must have had haemophilia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of her sons would have had haemophilia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of her daughters would have had haemophilia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Haemophilia would have occurred more in her male than female descendents", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Queen Victoria was carrier of haemophilia, a sex linked disease. This disorder manifests only in males, however the genetic mutation that causes the disease is located on the X chromosome and is easily inherited from the mother by male children or from either mother or father by female children. A male having a mutant allele on his X-chromosome is hemizygous for that allele. This happens because the trait is recessive.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
