(13014,'biology',94,787,1694,'The replication of DNA in eukaryotes commences from','[{"text": "Several sites along the DNA of the chromatid simultaneously", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "One end of chromatid extending to the other end", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The centromeres to either of the ends of chromatids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both ends of the chromatids simultaneously", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The DNA replication begins at some origins before in $\\mathrm{S}$ phase than at others, but by the end of S phase, the process is completed for all. Near to the completion stage of DNA, the bubbles of freshly replicated DNA meet as well as fuse, forming two molecules as a result. This implies that DNA replication starts from many sites 0n the DNA of the chromatid together.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
