(14164,'chemistry',15,187,412,'The equivalent mass of a certain bivalent metal is 20. The molecular mass of its anhydrous chloride is','[{"text": "91", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "111", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "55.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "75.5", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the compound be ${\\rm{MC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>Equivalent mass of a metal ion ${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{Atomic}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mass}}}}{{{\\rm{Total}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{positive}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{valency}}}}$<br>${\\rm{20 = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{Atomic}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mass}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{the}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{metal}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{ion}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{Atomic}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mass}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{the}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{metal = 2 \\times 20 = 40}}$<br>$\\therefore $ Molecular mass of anhydrous chloride<br>${\\rm{(MC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{)}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 40 + (2 \\times 35}}{\\rm{.5) = 40 + 71 = 111}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
