(14188,'chemistry',15,186,405,'10 g of a mixture of BaO and CaO requires ${\\rm{100}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ of 2.5 M HCl to react completely. The percentage of calcium oxide in the mixture is approximately<br>(Given: molar mass of BaO = 153)','[{"text": "52.6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "55.1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "44.9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "47.4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{BaO + 2HCl}} \\to {\\rm{BaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>${\\rm{CaO + 2HCl}} \\to {\\rm{CaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>Reactions show that 2 moles of mix. of BaO and CaO requires 4 moles of HCl to react completely.<br>Hence, no. of moles of HCl<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.5 \\times 100}}}}{{{\\rm{1000}}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.25moles}}$<br>Now,          HCl          BaO + CaO<br>Initial          4 moles          2 moles<br>Final          0.25 moles ?<br>No. of moles of mixture of BaO and CaO<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.25 \\times 2}}}}{{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.125moles}}$<br>Let the mass of CaO be x g and BaO is ${\\rm{(10 - x)g}}$<br>Then,$\\frac{{\\rm{x}}}{{{\\rm{56}}}}{\\rm{ + }}\\frac{{{\\rm{10 - x}}}}{{{\\rm{153}}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.125}}$<br>[Mol. mass of CaO = 56, BaO = 153]<br>${\\rm{153x + 56(10 - x) = 0}}{\\rm{.125 \\times 56 \\times 153}}$<br>${\\rm{153x + 560 = 56x = 0}}{\\rm{.125 \\times 56 \\times 153}}$<br>${\\rm{97x = 1071 - 560}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\rm{97x = 511}} \\Rightarrow {\\rm{x = 5}}{\\rm{.268}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{\\% }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{CaO = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{5}}{\\rm{.268}}}}{{{\\rm{10}}}}{\\rm{ \\times 100 = 52}}{\\rm{.68}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
