(14272,'biology',99,1329,3212,'Name the scientist who put price tags on nature\'s life-support services?','[{"text": "Robert Constanza", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Robert May", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Paul Ehrlich", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alexander Von Humboldt", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Robert Constanza and his colleagues have very recently tried to put price tags on nature\'s life-support services',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14273,'biology',98,1187,2846,'<i>Bacillus thuringiensis</i> forms the protein crystals which contain a toxic insecticidal protein. This protein<br>I. Is activated by alkaline $\\mathrm{pH}$ of the gut of the insect pest.<br>II. Binds with the epithelial cells of the midgut of the insect pest ultimately killing it.<br>III. Does not kill the carrier bacterium<br>Which of the statements given above are correct?','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Some strains of Bacillus thuringiensis produce proteins that kill certain insects. The Bt toxin protein exist as inactive protoxins but once an insect ingest the inactive toxin, it is converted into an active form of toxin due to the alkaline $\\mathrm{pH}$ of the gut which solubilise the crystals. The activated toxin binds to the surface of midgut epithelial cells and create pores that cause cell swelling and lysis and eventually cause death of the insect.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14274,'chemistry',45,426,852,'The electronic configuration of an element is ${\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{3}}}$. The atomic number of the\nelement which is just below the above mentioned element in the periodic table is','[{"text": "34", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "49", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "33", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "31", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ configuration element is Phosphorous (P). The element below the ‘P’ is Arsenic (As) having atomic number 33.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14275,'chemistry',15,186,405,'A mixture of 2.3g formic acid and 4.5g oxalic acid is treated with conc. ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$. The evolved gaseous mixture is passed through KOH pellets. Weight (in g) of the remaining product at STP will be','[{"text": "2.8", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{3}}{\\\\rm{.0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{HCOOHCO(g) + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O(l)}}$<br>${\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.3 = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.3}}}}{{{\\rm{46}}}}{\\rm{ = 1/20mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{20}}}}{\\rm{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{20}}}}{\\rm{mol}}$<br>${\\rm{HOOC - COOHCO(g) + C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g) + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O(l)}}$<br>${\\rm{4}}{\\rm{.5g = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{4}}{\\rm{.5}}}}{{{\\rm{90}}}}{\\rm{ = 1/20mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{20}}}}{\\rm{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{20}}}}{\\rm{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{20}}}}{\\rm{mol}}$<br>Out of ${\\rm{CO}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{and}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$, only ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ is absorbed by KOH. So, the remaining gas is CO.<br>So, weight of remaining gaseous product, CO is ${\\rm{2 \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{28}}}}{{{\\rm{20}}}}{\\rm{ = 2}}{\\rm{.8g}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14276,'biology',99,1329,3212,'What is the average price tag of on the fundamental ecosystems services?','[{"text": "US $ 33 trillion a year", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "US $ 8 trillion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "US $ 18 trillion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "US $ 3 trillion", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Researchers have put an average price tag of US ${\\rm{\\$ }}$ 33 trillion a year on these fundamental ecosystems sevices, which are largely taken for granted because they are free. This is nearly twice the value of the global gross national product GNP which is (US ${\\rm{\\$ }}$ 18 trillion).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14277,'biology',98,1188,2847,'The process of RNA interference takes place in all eukaryotic organisms as a method of cellular defense which involves silencing of a specific mRNA by<br>i. Complementary dsRNA<br>ii. Retroposons<br>iii. Single Stranded DNA<br>iv. Protein<br>v. Carbohydrate','[{"text": "i, ii", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ii, iii, v", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, ii, iii, v", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, ii, iii, iv", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'RNA interference is a method of silencing of a specific mRNA due to a complementary dsRNA molecule that binds to and prevents translation of the mRNA (silencing). Source of this complementary RNA may be from retroposons also.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
