(14851,'biology',99,1324,3207,'Detritivores breakdown detritus into smaller particles. This process is called:','[{"text": "Fragmentation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Humification", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decomposition", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Catabolism", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Detritivores like earthworm breakdown detritus into smaller particles. This process is called fragmentation.<br>Catabolism is breakdown detritus into smaller particles by releasing of enzymes by bacteria and fungi. Humification is the formation of a dark-coloured amorphous substance called humus. Mineralisation is the degradation of humus into minerals by microbes.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14841,'chemistry',15,186,405,'How many molecules of ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ are formed when one milligram of 100% pure ${\\rm{CaC}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}$  is treated with excess hydrochloric acid?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{6}}{\\\\rm{.023 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{23}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{6}}{\\\\rm{.023 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{21}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{6}}{\\\\rm{.023 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{20}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{6}}{\\\\rm{.023 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{18}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{CaC}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ + 2HCl}} \\to {\\rm{CaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O + C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>100 g of ${\\rm{CaC}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}$  gives 1 mole or ${\\rm{6}}{\\rm{.023 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{23}}}}$ molecules of ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$.<br>${\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}{\\rm{g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{CaC}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{gives = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{6}}{\\rm{.023 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{23}}}}}}{{{\\rm{100}}}}{\\rm{ \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 6}}{\\rm{.023 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{18}}}}$ molecules of ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14842,'chemistry',46,445,891,'Which of the following is least ionic?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{Ca}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{CaB}}{{\\\\rm{r}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{CaC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Ca}}{{\\\\rm{I}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'More is the E.N. difference more is ionic character.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14843,'biology',99,1324,3207,'Given below are two statements :<br>Statement I : Decomposition is a process in which the detritus is degraded into simpler substances by microbes.<br>Statement II : Decomposition is faster if the detritus is rich in lignin and chitin.<br>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :','[{"text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Decomposition is a process in which detritus is degraded into simpler substances by microbes. In a particular climatic condition, decomposition rate is slower if detritus is rich in lignin and chitin, and quicker, if detritus is rich in nitrogen and water-soluble substances like sugars.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14844,'chemistry',45,430,859,'The correct order of electron gain enthalpy with negative sign of F, Cl, Br and I, having atomic number 9, 17, 35 and 53 respectively, is :','[{"text": "F > Cl > Br > I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cl > F > Br > I", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Br > Cl > I > F", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I > Br > Cl > F", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As we move down in a group electron gain enthalpy becomes less negative because the size of the atom increases and the distance of added electron from the nucleus increases. Negative electron gain enthalpy of F is less than Cl. This is due to the fact that when an electron is added to F, the added electron goes to the smaller n = 2 energy level and experiences significant repulsion from the other electrons present in this level. In Cl, the electron goes to the larger n = 3 energy level and consequently occupies a larger region of space leading to much less electron - electron repulsion. So the correct order is Cl > F > Br > I.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14845,'chemistry',15,186,405,'Liquid benzene burns in oxygen according to following equation<br>${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{6}}}{\\rm{ + 15}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g)}} \\to {\\rm{12C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g) + 6}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O(g)}}$<br>How many litres of oxygen are required for complete combustion of 39 g of liquid benzene?','[{"text": "11.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "22.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "42", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "84", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From equation, ${\\rm{2 \\times 78g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{6}}}$ requires<br>${\\rm{15 \\times 22}}{\\rm{.4L}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>${\\rm{39}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{6}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{requires}}\\frac{{{\\rm{15 \\times 22}}{\\rm{.4 \\times 39}}}}{{{\\rm{2 \\times 78}}}}{\\rm{ = 84L}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{at}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{STP}}{\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14846,'chemistry',46,445,891,'The strongest ionic bond is present in','[{"text": "LiF", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "NaF", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RbF", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "CsF", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Greater the lattice energy stronger is the Ionic bond.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14847,'chemistry',45,430,859,'Which of the following relation is correct:','[{"text": "2 I.P. - E.A. - E.N. = 0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 I.P. - E.N. + E.A. = 0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 E.N. - I.P. - E.A. = 0", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "E.N. - I.P. - E.A. = 0", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Where E.N. stands for electronegativity E.A. stands for electron affinity and I.P. stands for Ionisation potential.<br>According to Mullikan scale,<br>${\\rm{E}}{\\rm{.N}}{\\rm{. = }}\\frac{{\\left( {{\\rm{I}}{\\rm{.P}}{\\rm{. + E}}{\\rm{.A}}{\\rm{.}}} \\right)}}{{\\rm{2}}}$<br>${\\rm{2E}}{\\rm{.N}}{\\rm{. - I}}{\\rm{.P}}{\\rm{. - E}}{\\rm{.A}}{\\rm{. = 0}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14848,'biology',99,1328,3211,'Which one of the following will accelerate phosphorus cycle?','[{"text": "Volcanic activity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Weathering of rocks", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Rain fall and storms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Burning of fossil fuels", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The natural reservoir of phosphorus is rock, which contains phosphorus in the form of phosphates. When rocks are weathered, these phosphates are dissolved in soil solution.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14849,'chemistry',15,186,405,'40 g of a sample of carbon on combustion left 10% of it unreacted. The volume of oxygen required at STP for this combustion reaction is','[{"text": "22.4 L", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "67.2 L", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "11.2 L", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "44.8 L", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Use the following steps:<br>Step 1: ${\\rm{C}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{ + }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\to \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{12}}\\,\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{22}}{\\rm{.4}}\\,\\,{\\rm{L}}$<br>Mass volume relationship for the chemical equation,<br>Step 2:<br>${\\rm{C(s)}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{ + }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g)}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}  \\to {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g)}}$<br>${\\rm{1}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{1}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{1}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mol}}$<br>40 g<br>Step 3: Calculate the limiting reagent.<br>As 10%, i.e., $\\left( {{\\rm{40 \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{10}}}}{{{\\rm{100}}}}{\\rm{ = 4g}}} \\right)$ of sample of carbon left unreacted. Hence, ${{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ acts as a limiting agent.<br>Step 4: Calculate the volume of ${{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ at STP<br>At STP, for the combustion of 12 g of C, oxygen\nrequired = 22.4 L<br>For the combustion of 36 g of C, oxygen required<br>will be ${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{22}}{\\rm{.4}}}}{{{\\rm{12}}}}{\\rm{ \\times 36L = 67}}{\\rm{.2L}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(14853,'chemistry',15,185,404,'What is the molecular mass of a substance which contains 9 carbon atoms,13 hydrogen atoms and ${\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.33 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 23}}}}$ g of other component?','[{"text": "135", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "108", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "13", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "144", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The molecule has C, H and other component.<br>Mass of 9 C atoms = 12 $ \\times $ 9 = 108 amu<br>Mass of 13 H atoms = 13 $ \\times $ 1 = 13 amu<br>Mass of other component<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.33 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 23}}}}}}{{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.66 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 24}}}}}}{\\rm{ = 14}}{\\rm{.04 = 135}}{\\rm{.04}}\\,\\,{\\rm{amu}}$<br>$\\therefore $ Total mass of one molecule<br>= 108 + 13 + 14.04 = 135.04 amu<br>$\\therefore $ Mol. mass of substance = 135.04',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
