(15022,'chemistry',15,180,393,'3 g of a hydrocarbon on combustion in excess of oxygen produces 8.8 g of ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ and 5.4 g of ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$. The data illustrates the law of','[{"text": "Conservation of mass", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Multiple proportions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Constant proportions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reciprocal proportions", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Mass of carbon in ${\\rm{8}}{\\rm{.8}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 12 \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{8}}{\\rm{.8}}}}{{{\\rm{44}}}}{\\rm{ = 2}}{\\rm{.4}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}$<br>Mass of hydrogen in ${\\rm{5}}{\\rm{.4}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O = 2 \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{5}}{\\rm{.4}}}}{{{\\rm{18}}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.6}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}$<br>Total mass of (C + H) = 2.4 + 0.6 = 3.0 g<br>Hence, obey’s law of conservation of mass.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
