(15093,'chemistry',46,437,873,'An atom with atomic number 20 is most likely to combine chemically with the atom whose atomic number is','[{"text": "11", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "18", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'An atom with atomic number 20 is most likely to combine chemically with the atom whose atomic number is 16, which is sulfur. This is because elements tend to combine in chemical reactions to achieve a stable electron configuration, often by gaining or losing electrons to attain the electron configuration of the nearest noble gas. In this case, an atom with atomic number 20 (calcium, Ca) is likely to combine with an atom with atomic number 16 (sulfur, S) to form an ionic compound such as CaS.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
