(15146,'chemistry',45,430,856,'The van der Waal and covalent radii of fluorine atom respectively from the following figure are','[{"text": "219 pm, 72 pm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "75 pm, 72 pm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "147 pm, 72 pm", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "147 pm, 144 pm", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Covalent radius is radius of an atom in its bound state i.e., in fluorine it is half of distance between two covalently bonded fluorine atoms; vander Wall radii is one-half of the distance between the nuclei of two identical non-bonded isolated isolated atoms. These atoms are attracted toward each other through weak van der Waal’s force hence vander Waal radii are very large.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/45_15146_question_1.png'),
