(15150,'chemistry',45,430,858,'The correct order in which the first ionization potentital increases is','[{"text": "K, Be, Na", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Be, Na, K", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Na, K, Be", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "K, Na, Be", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The electronic configuration of the elements are<br>$_{\\rm{4}}{\\rm{Be - 1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{;}}\\,{\\,_{{\\rm{11}}}}{\\rm{Na - 1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{6}}}{\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{1}}}$<br>$_{{\\rm{19}}}{\\rm{K - 1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{6}}}{\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{6}}}{\\rm{4}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{1}}}$<br>The first ionization energy of Be is maximum because electron is to be drawn from stable (fully filled) orbital. The ${{\\rm{1}}^{{\\rm{st}}}}$  ionization energy of Na is greater than K because size of the K is bigger than Na which facilitates easy removal of electron from its outermost shell. So, the sequence is K < Na < Be.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15152,'chemistry',46,447,893,'According to valence bond theory, water molecule has','[{"text": "Two dative bonds and bond angle 90°", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two covalent bonds and bond angle 90°", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two dative bonds and bond angle 104.5°", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two covalent bonds and bond angle 104.5°", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Due to the presence of two lone pairs',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15153,'chemistry',15,183,402,'The density of ${{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ at ${{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$ and 1 atm is ${\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.429}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g/litre}}$. The molar volume of gas is','[{"text": "22.4 lit.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "11.2 lit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "33.6 lit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5.6 lit.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\because $${\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.429}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{gm}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ gas occupies volume = 1 litre<br>Molar volume means volume occupies by 1 mol of ${{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ gas<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{32}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{gm}}$ of ${{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ gas occupies<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{32}}}}{{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.429}}}}{\\rm{ = 22}}{\\rm{.4}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{litre}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15154,'chemistry',45,430,858,'In the following, the element with the highest ionisation energy is','[{"text": "$\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{Ne}}} \\\\right]{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{1}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{Ne}}} \\\\right]{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{Ne}}} \\\\right]{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{Ne}}} \\\\right]{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Half-filled orbitals and fully filled orbitals are more stable and hence they have higher ionisation energies. So $\\left[ {{\\rm{Ne}}} \\right]{\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ has the highest ionisation energy.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15156,'chemistry',46,447,894,'Sigma bonds are','[{"text": "Cylindrically symmetrical about the bond axis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Perpendicular to the bond axis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Asymmetrical about the bond axis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both b & c", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15157,'chemistry',45,430,858,'Generally, the first ionization energy increases along a period. But there are some exceptions. One which is not an exception is','[{"text": "N and O", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Na and Mg", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mg and Al", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Be and B", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Be, N and Mg have higher values than expected, this is due to stable configurations of these elements. Be and Mg have fully-filled orbitals, in nitrogen 2p - orbitals are half-filled.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15158,'chemistry',15,183,402,'How many years it would take to spend Avogadro’s number of rupees at the rate of 1 million rupees per one second?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{19}}{\\\\rm{.098 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{19}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{years}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{19}}{\\\\rm{.098}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{years}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{19}}{\\\\rm{.098 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{\\\\rm{9}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{years}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\because $${\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{6}}}$ rupees are spent in 1sec<br>$\\therefore {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{6}}{\\rm{.022 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{23}}}}$ rupees are spent in<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1 \\times 6}}{\\rm{.022 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{23}}}}}}{{{\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{6}}}}}{\\rm{sec}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1 \\times 60}}{\\rm{.22 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{23}}}}}}{{{\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{6}}}{\\rm{ \\times 60 \\times 60 \\times 24 \\times 365}}}}{\\rm{years}}$<br>${\\rm{19}}{\\rm{.098 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{9}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{years}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15161,'chemistry',15,183,402,'1 mol of ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ contains same number of molecules as that of','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{6}}{\\\\rm{.02 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{23}}}}$ atoms of H", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4 g atoms of hydrogen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.81 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{23}}}}$ molecules of ${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.0 g of carbon", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'1 mol of ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{ = 4g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{atoms}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{H}}{\\rm{.}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 4 \\times 6}}{\\rm{.023 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{23}}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{atmos}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{H}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 6}}{\\rm{.023 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{23}}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{molecules}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 12}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{carbon}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15162,'chemistry',45,430,859,'Which halogen has the highest value of negative electron gain enthalpy?','[{"text": "Fluorine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chlorine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bromine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Iodine", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Halogens have highest negative electron gain enthalpy in their respective periods, because they have only one electron less than stable noble gas configurations. As a result, they readily accept one electron to acquire the noble gas configuration. Electron gain enthalpy becomes less negative on moving from chlorine to iodine. However, negative electron gain enthalpy of fluorine is less than that of chlorine due to small sized of fluorine atom. It has very high inter electronic repulsion in the relatively small 2p_x0002_orbitals. Hence, incoming electron experience less attraction from the nucleus.<br>$\\therefore $ Chlorine has the highest value of electron\ngain enthalpy.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15164,'chemistry',45,430,860,'Which is true about the electronegativity order of the following elements?','[{"text": "P > Si", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "C > N", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Br > Cl", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sr > Ca", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'P > Si electronegativity increases along the period.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15165,'chemistry',15,183,402,'Four one–litre flasks are separately filled with the gases hydrogen, helium, oxygen and ozone at the same room temperature and pressure. The ratio of total number of atoms of these gases present in the different flasks would be','[{"text": "1 : 1 : 1 : 1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 : 2 : 2 : 3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 : 1 : 2 : 3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3 : 2 : 2 : 1", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Equal volumes of gases contain equal no. of molecules at same room temperature and pressure.<br>Number of molecules ${\\rm{n \\times }}{{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{A}}}$<br>${{\\rm{n}}_{{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{\\rm{:}}{{\\rm{n}}_{{\\rm{He}}}}{\\rm{:}}{{\\rm{n}}_{{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{\\rm{:}}{{\\rm{n}}_{{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}}}{\\rm{ = 1:1:1:1}}$<br>Hence, no. of atoms of ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{,He,}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{and}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}$ will be in the ratio of ${\\rm{2:1:2:3}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15166,'chemistry',46,447,893,'The type of overlap present in the bonds of hydrogen sulphide molecule is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\sigma }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{s - p}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\sigma }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{s - s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\sigma }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{p - p}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\sigma }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{s}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{ - s}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'s-orbital of H and p-orbital of Sulphur involved in the bonds of hydrogen sulphide.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15168,'chemistry',46,447,895,'The number of sigma and pi bonds in a molecule of cyanogen ${\\left( {{\\rm{CN}}} \\right)_{\\rm{2}}}$ are, respectively.','[{"text": "4,3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3,4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5,2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3,5", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The structure of ${\\left( {{\\rm{CN}}} \\right)_{\\rm{2}}}$ is ${\\rm{N}} \\equiv C - C \\equiv N$. It has ${\\rm{3\\sigma }}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{bonds}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{4\\pi }}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{bonds}}{\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15169,'chemistry',15,182,400,'A sample of “He” have as much atoms as there are letters in “ACID”. Then find the mass of “He” sample','[{"text": "16 amu", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "16 gm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20 gm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20 amu", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Mass of He sample =${\\rm{ 4amu \\times 4 = 16amu}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15170,'chemistry',45,432,864,'Identify the element that forms amphoteric oxide','[{"text": "Carbon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zinc", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Calcium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sulphur", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Zn (zinc) element of forms amphoteric oxide i.e. ZnO. This oxide reacts with both acids and bases, exhibiting a dual behaviour. They show characteristics of both acidic as well as basic oxides.<br>Following reactions are given by ZnO<br>${\\rm{ZnO + 2HCl}} \\to {\\rm{ZnC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>${\\rm{ZnO + 2NaOH}} \\to {\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{Zn}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>Note: Among oxides, CO is netural, CaO is basic and ${\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ is acidic in nature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15192,'chemistry',15,177,387,'Which one of the following is not a mixture?','[{"text": "Distilled water", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Sugar dissolved in water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Liquefied Petroleum Gas (LPG)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gasoline", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Distilled water is not a mixture. It is a compound.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
