(15224,'chemistry',15,180,395,'Which of the following statements indicates that law of multiple proporiton is being followed?','[{"text": "Sample of carbon dioxide taken from any source will always have carbon and oxygen in the ratio 1:2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carbon forms two oxides i.e., ${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{CO}}$, where masses of oxygen which combine with fixed mass of carbon are in the simple ratio 2:1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "When magnesium burns in oxygen, the amount of magnesium taken for the reactions is equal to the amount of magnesium in magnesium oxide formed.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At constant temperature and pressure 200 mL of hydrogen will combine with 100 mL oxygen to produce 200 mL of water vapour.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The element, carbon, combines with oxygen to form two compounds namely, carbon dioxide and carbon monoxide. In ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$,  12 parts by mass of carbon combine with 32 parts by mass of oxygen while in CO, 12 parts by mass of carbon combine with 16 parts by mass of oxygen.<br>Therefore, the masses of oxygen combine with a fixed mass of carbon (12 parts) in ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ and CO are 32 and 16 respectively. These masses of oxygen bear a simple ratio of 32 : 16 or 2 : 1 to each other.<br>This is an example of law of multiple proportion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15225,'chemistry',45,425,848,'Electronic configuration of only one p-block element is exceptional. One molecule of that element consists of how many atoms of it?','[{"text": "One", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Two", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Four", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'He is p-block element but its electronic configuration does not follow the normal trend of p-block element electronic configuration. Since, He is monoatomic, hence it consists of one atom of it.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
