(15252,'chemistry',45,430,856,'Covalent radii of atoms varies in range of 72 pm to 133 pm from F to I while that of noble gases He to Xe varies from 120pm to 220pm. This is because in case of noble gases','[{"text": "covalent radius is very large", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "van der Waal radius is considered", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "metallic radii is considered", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In case of halogens covalent radius is considered this bond is formed by overlapping of electron clouds; while noble gases remain monoatomic, in this case only way to obtain radius is through vander Waal radii.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15253,'chemistry',46,446,892,'The molecule exhibiting maximum number of non-bonding electron pairs around the central atom is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{XeO}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Xe}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{XeF}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Xe}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15254,'chemistry',15,187,412,'A metal oxide has the formula $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}}$. It can be reduced by hydrogen to give free metal and water. $\\mathrm{0.1596 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of this metal oxide requires $\\mathrm{6 \\mathrm{mg}}$ of hydrogen for complete reduction. What is the atomic weight of metal?','[{"text": "52.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "57.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "55.8", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "59.3", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\rm{\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{A}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{~A}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\\\& 0.1596 \\mathrm{~g} \\quad 0.006 \\mathrm{~g}\\end{aligned}}$<br>$\\mathrm{0.006 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ reduces $\\mathrm{0.1596 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}}$<br>$\\mathrm{6 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ reduces $\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{0.1596 \\times 6}{0.006}}$<br>$\\mathrm{=159.6 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}}$.<br>Hence, molecular weight of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}=159.6 \\mathrm{~g}}$.<br>Let molecular weight of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A}=\\mathrm{x}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore 2 \\mathrm{x}+3 \\times 16=159.6}$<br>$\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{x}=159.6-48}$<br>$\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{x}=111.6}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{x}=\\dfrac{111.6}{2}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{x}=55.8 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{mol}}$.<br>Hence, atomic mass of metal $\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{A})=55.8 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{mol}}$.<br>',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2012,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15256,'chemistry',45,430,856,'Identify the incorrect statements among the following:','[{"text": "Lanthanoids contraction is the accumulation of successive linkages", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "As a result of lanthanoids contraction, the properties of 4d series of the transition elements have no similarities with 5d series of transition elements.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Shielding power of 4f electrons is quite weak.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There is a decrease in the radii of the atoms or ions as one proceeds from La to Lu.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In each vertical column of transition element, the elements of second and third transition series resemble each other more closely than the elements of first and second transition series on account of lanthanoids con traction. Hence, the properties of elements of 4d series of the transition elements resemble with the properties of the elements of 5d series of thhe transition elements.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15257,'chemistry',46,442,887,'Which of the following statement is true?','[{"text": "Higher the bond order, lesser the bond length", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Higher the bond order, greater the bond length", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Higher the bond order, lesser the bond energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Higher the bond order, lesser the number of bonds.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{Bond}}\\,\\,{\\rm{order}}\\,\\,{\\rm{\\alpha }}\\,\\,{\\rm{Bond}}\\,\\,{\\rm{energy}}$<br>${\\rm{\\alpha }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{Bond}}\\,\\,{\\rm{length}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15258,'chemistry',15,186,405,'$\\mathrm{3.5 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of mixture of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaOH}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KOH}}$ were dissolved and made up to $\\mathrm{250 \\mathrm{~mL} .25 \\mathrm{~mL}}$ of this solution were completely neutralised by $\\mathrm{17 \\mathrm{~mL}}$ of $\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{N} / 2) \\mathrm{HCl}}$ solution. Then, the percentage of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KOH}}$ in mixture is','[{"text": "80", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "34", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "56", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Suppose the amount of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaOH}}$ in the mixture $\\mathrm{=\\mathrm{xg}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ Amount of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KOH}=(3.5-\\mathrm{x}) \\mathrm{g}}$<br>$\\mathrm{25 \\mathrm{~mL}}$ of alkali solution $\\mathrm{=17 \\mathrm{~mL}}$ of $\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{N} / 2) \\mathrm{HCl}}$<br>or $\\mathrm{250 \\mathrm{~mL}}$ of alkali solution $\\mathrm{=170 \\mathrm{~mL}}$ of $\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{N} / 2) \\mathrm{HCl}}$<br>$\\mathrm{1000 \\mathrm{~mL}}$ of $\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{N}) \\mathrm{HCl}}$ contains $\\mathrm{36.5 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCl}}$<br>$\\mathrm{170 \\mathrm{~mL}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N} / 2 \\mathrm{HCl}}$ will contain<br>$\\mathrm{\\dfrac{36.5 \\times 170}{1000 \\times 2}=3.1025 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCl}}$ Eq. mass of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaOH}=40 \\mathrm{geq} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}}$<br>and that of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KOH}=56{\\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{eq} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}}^{-1}}$<br>$\\mathrm{40 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaOH}}$ require $\\mathrm{36.5 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCl}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{xg}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaOH}}$ will require $\\mathrm{\\dfrac{36.5 \\times \\mathrm{x}}{40} \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCl}}$<br>Similarly, $\\mathrm{56 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KOH}}$ require $\\mathrm{36.5 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCl}}$<br>(3.5-x) of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KOH}}$ will require<br>$\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{36.5(3.5-\\mathrm{x})}{56} \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCl}}$<br>Thus, $\\mathrm{\\dfrac{36.5 \\mathrm{x}}{40}+\\dfrac{36.5(3.5-\\mathrm{x})}{56}=3.1025 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCl}}$ or $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{x}=3.15 \\mathrm{~g}}$<br>$\\mathrm{=}$ Amount of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaOH}}$ in the mixture $\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ Amount of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KOH}=(3.5-3.15) \\mathrm{g}=0.35 \\mathrm{~g}}$<br>$\\mathrm{3.5 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of the mixture contain $\\mathrm{0.35 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KOH}}$<br>$\\mathrm{100 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of the mixture contain $\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{0.35 \\mathrm{~g} \\times 100}{3.5}}$<br>$\\mathrm{=10 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KOH}}$<br>Hence, $\\mathrm{\\%}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KOH}=10 \\%}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15260,'chemistry',45,430,857,'Which of the following has same size?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{F}}{{\\\\rm{e}}^{{\\\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\\\rm{,N}}{{\\\\rm{i}}^{{\\\\rm{2 + }}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Z}}{{\\\\rm{r}}^{{\\\\rm{4 + }}}}{\\\\rm{,T}}{{\\\\rm{i}}^{{\\\\rm{4 + }}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Z}}{{\\\\rm{r}}^{{\\\\rm{4 + }}}}{\\\\rm{,H}}{{\\\\rm{f}}^{{\\\\rm{4 + }}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Z}}{{\\\\rm{n}}^{{\\\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\\\rm{,H}}{{\\\\rm{f}}^{{\\\\rm{4 + }}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In general, the atomic and ionic radii increases on moving down in a group. But the element of second transition series (eg.,Zr, Nb, Mo etc.,) have the almost same radii as the elements of third transition series (eg. Hf, Ta, W etc.). This is because of lanthanide contraction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15261,'chemistry',15,186,405,'What amount of oxygen is used at S.T.P. to obtain $\\mathrm{9 \\mathrm{~g}}$ water from sufficient amount of hydrogen gas?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{5.6 \\\\mathrm{dm}^{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{22.4 \\\\mathrm{dm}^{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{16.8 \\\\mathrm{dm}^{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{11.2 \\\\mathrm{dm}^{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(\\ell)}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\dfrac{1}{2}}$ mole oxygen means $\\mathrm{11.2 \\mathrm{~L}}$ (or) $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{dm}^{3}}$ at STP<br>Here, $\\mathrm{11.2 \\mathrm{dm}^{3}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ gives $\\mathrm{18 \\mathrm{~g}}$ water (at STP)<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore 9 \\mathrm{~g}}$ water is obtained from<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{11}}{\\rm{.2}} \\times {\\rm{9}}}}{{{\\rm{18}}}}{\\rm{ = 5}}{\\rm{.6d}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ of }}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15262,'chemistry',46,442,885,'The nitrogen atoms in ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{,}}\\,\\,{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}^{\\rm{ + }}$<br>are all surrounded by eight electrons. When these species are arranged in order of increasing H – N – H bond angle, what is the correct order?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'2 lone pairs in ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}^{\\rm{ - }}$<br>1 lone pair in ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$<br>0 lone pairs in ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}^{\\rm{ + }}$<br>Bond angle is inversely proportional to the number of lone pairs.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
