(15326,'chemistry',15,180,395,'${\\text{4}}{\\text{.4 g}}$  of an oxide of nitrogen gives $\\mathrm{2.24 \\mathrm{~L}}$ of nitrogen and $\\mathrm{60 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of another oxide of nitrogen gives $\\mathrm{22.4 \\mathrm{~L}}$ of nitrogen at S.T.P. The data illustrates:','[{"text": "Law of conservation of mass", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Law of constant proportions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Law of multiple proportions", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Law of reciprocal proportions", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>In first oxide mass of $\\mathrm{2.24 \\mathrm{~L}}$ of<br>nitrogen at $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{STP}=2.8 \\mathrm{~g}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ mass of oxygen $\\mathrm{=4.4-2.8=1.6 \\mathrm{~g}}$<br>In second oxide, mass of $\\mathrm{22.4 \\mathrm{~L}}$ of<br>nitrogen at $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{STP}=28 \\mathrm{~g}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ Mass of oxygen $\\mathrm{=60-28=32 \\mathrm{~g}}$<br>(or) in second oxide $\\mathrm{2.8 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of nitrogen combines with $\\mathrm{3.2 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of oxygen.<br>The masses of oxygen which combines with 2.8 $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{g}}$ of nitrogen in the oxides are $\\mathrm{1.6 \\mathrm{~g}: 3.2 \\mathrm{~g}}$ or 1 $\\mathrm{: 2}$. This is a simple whole number ratio. $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{T} \\mathrm{h}}$ is illustrates the law of multiple proportions.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15328,'chemistry',45,430,858,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> First ionisation energy for nitrogen is lower than that of oxygen<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Across a period, effective nuclear charge decreases.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In a period from left to right, effective nuclear charge increases and size decreases, as a result ionisation energy increases. But elements of II A and V A group on the account of their half - filled and completely filled orbitals have high IE than the elements of III A and VI A group, respectively.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15329,'chemistry',15,180,393,'If law of conservation of mass was to hold true, then $\\mathrm{20.8 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}}$ on reaction with $\\mathrm{9.8 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$ will produce $\\mathrm{7.3 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCl}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}}$ equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{11.65 \\\\mathrm{~g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{23.3 \\\\mathrm{~g}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{25.5 \\\\mathrm{~g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{30.6 \\\\mathrm{~g}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{HCl}}$ $\\mathrm{20.8 \\mathrm{~g} \\quad 9.8 \\mathrm{~g} \\quad \\mathrm{x} \\quad 7.3 \\mathrm{~g}}$ mass of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}}$ produced, $\\mathrm{(x)=(20.8+9.8)-7.3=23.3 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15330,'chemistry',46,449,904,'While filling electrons in ${\\rm{\\pi }}\\,\\,{\\rm{2}}\\,\\,{\\rm{p}}\\,{\\,_{\\rm{x}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{\\pi }}\\,\\,{\\rm{2}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{p}}_{\\,\\,{\\rm{y}}}}$ the electronic configuration rules that have to be followed is','[{"text": "Pauli’s exclusion principle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aufbau principle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Pauli’s and Hund’s rule", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15332,'chemistry',45,430,857,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The ionic size of $\\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}>\\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In isoelectronic species, greater the nuclear charge, less is the size.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The ionic size of $\\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ is larger than $\\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$. Both the species are isoelectronic and in case of isoelectronic species as nuclear charge increases, attractive force between electron of valence shell and nucleus increases which results in decrease in ionic size.<br>Hence, both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15333,'chemistry',46,450,906,'The paramagnetic nature of oxygen is best explained by','[{"text": "V.B.theory", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hybridisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "M.O.theory", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "VSEPR theory", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15374,'chemistry',45,432,864,'Which among the following oxides is acidic in nature?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CO}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}-$ Acidic oxide<br>NO, CO - Neutral oxides<br>$\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ - Basic oxide',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15334,'chemistry',15,180,395,'Hydrogen and oxygen combine to form $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$ containing $\\mathrm{5.93 \\%}$ and $\\mathrm{11.2 \\%}$ hydrogen respectively, the data illustrates:','[{"text": "Law of conservation of mass.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Law of constant proportions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Law of reciprocal proportions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Law of multiple proportions", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{In}_{2} \\mathrm{O}, \\mathrm{H}: \\mathrm{O}}$ (mass ratio) $\\mathrm{=5.93: 94.07}$ In $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}, \\mathrm{H}: \\mathrm{O}}$ (mass ratio) $\\mathrm{=11.2: 88.8}$ (or) $\\mathrm{5.93: 47.0}$<br>With a fixed mass of hydrogen, mass ratio of oxygen combining is $\\mathrm{94.07: 47.0=2: 1}$.<br>It illustrates law of multiple proportions.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15336,'chemistry',45,430,859,'The correct order of electron affinities of $\\mathrm{N}, \\mathrm{O}, \\mathrm{S}$ and $\\mathrm{Cl}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N} < \\\\mathrm{O} < \\\\mathrm{S} < \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O} < \\\\mathrm{N} < \\\\mathrm{Cl} < \\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O} \\\\approx \\\\mathrm{Cl} < \\\\mathrm{N} \\\\approx \\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O} < \\\\mathrm{S} < \\\\mathrm{Cl} < \\\\mathrm{N}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Generally, electron affinity increases on moving from left to right in a period and decreases on moving down in a group. Electron affinities of second period elements (such as $\\mathrm{N}$, O) are less negative as compared to corresponding third period element. This is because of the small atomic size of second period elements. Hence, the correct order of electron affinities is $\\mathrm{N} < \\mathrm{O} < \\mathrm{S} < \\mathrm{Cl}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15337,'chemistry',15,183,402,'10 grams of each $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}, \\mathrm{~N}_{2}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}$ are kept in three bottles. The correct order of arrangement of bottles containing decreasing number of molecules is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{~N}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{~N}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{~N}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'1 mole means $\\mathrm{6.02 \\times 10^{23}}$ molecules<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ No. of molecules $\\mathrm{\\propto}$ no. of moles<br>no. of moles $\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{\\text { mass }}{\\text { molar mass }}}$<br>Here no. of moles $\\mathrm{=\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{N}_{2}}>\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}>\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}}$ (as the given masses are same)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15338,'chemistry',46,449,905,'Which molecule/ion out of the following does not contain unpaired electrons?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{B}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15340,'chemistry',45,430,856,'Which of the following pairs has almost same atomic radius?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Al}, \\\\mathrm{Ga}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Be}, \\\\mathrm{Mg}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mg}, \\\\mathrm{Al}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{B}, \\\\mathrm{Be}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Electronic configuration of $\\mathrm{Al}$ and $\\mathrm{Ga}$ are written as<br>$\\begin{aligned}& { }_{13} \\mathrm{Al}=[\\mathrm{Ne}] 3 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}, 3 \\mathrm{p}^{1} \\\\& { }_{31} \\mathrm{Ga}=[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{10}, 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}, 4 \\mathrm{p}^{1}\\end{aligned}$<br>Electronic configuration of $\\mathrm{Ga}$ exhibits 10 electrons in d-orbitals which do not screen the nucleus effectively because of the diffused shape of d- oribtals.<br>Consequently, the electrons are attracted by a greater force towards nucleus. Hence, the atomic radius of $\\mathrm{Ga}$ decreases or $\\mathrm{Ga}$ has almost same radius as that of $\\mathrm{Al}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15341,'chemistry',46,449,905,'Which of the following cannot be formed?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{H}}{{\\\\rm{e}}^{{\\\\rm{2 + }}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{H}}{{\\\\rm{e}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{He}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{H}}{{\\\\rm{e}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{H}}{{\\rm{e}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ cannot be formed as bond order is zero.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15342,'chemistry',15,183,402,'The number of oxygen atoms present in $\\mathrm{14.6 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of magnesium bicarbonate is:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{6 \\\\mathrm{~N}_{\\\\mathrm{A}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{0.6 \\\\mathrm{~N}_{\\\\mathrm{A}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{\\\\mathrm{A}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{N}_{\\\\mathrm{A}}}{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Magnesium bicarbonate, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mg}\\left(\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}\\right)_{2}}$, molar mass is $\\mathrm{146 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{mole}}$.<br>$\\mathrm{146 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mg}\\left(\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}\\right)_{2}}$ contains 6 mole oxygen atoms.<br>$\\mathrm{14.6 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mg}\\left(\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}\\right)_{2}}$ contains ?<br>$\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{14.6 \\times 6}{146}=0.6 \\mathrm{~mole}=0.6 \\mathrm{~N}_{\\mathrm{A}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15345,'chemistry',15,182,401,'$\\mathrm{5.6 \\mathrm{~L}}$ of a gas at S.T.P. weighs equal to $\\mathrm{8 \\mathrm{~g}}$. The vapour density of gas is','[{"text": "32", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "40", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Mass of $\\mathrm{22.4 \\mathrm{~L}}$ gas at S.T.P. $\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{8 \\times 22.4}{5.6}=}$ $\\mathrm{32 \\mathrm{~g}(1}$ mole $\\mathrm{)}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ Mol. mass $\\mathrm{=32 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{mole}}$<br>Vapour density is half of molar mass<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ V.D $\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{32}{2}=16}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15346,'chemistry',45,430,858,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Removal of $\\mathrm{s}$-electron is relatively difficult than removal of $\\mathrm{p}$ - electron of the same main shell.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{s}$ - electrons are closer to the nucleus than $\\mathrm{p}$ - electrons of the same shell and hence, are more strongly attracted by nucleus.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As s-electrons are more strongly attracted by nucleus, hence they are difficut to remove. Therefore, both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2012,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15348,'chemistry',15,183,402,'If $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$ ionises as<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}+\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}}$. Then total number of ions produced by 0.1 mole $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$ will be','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{9.03 \\\\times 10^{21}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{3.01 \\\\times 10^{22}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{6.02 \\\\times 10^{22}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{1.8 \\\\times 10^{23}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'1 mole $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$ produces 3 mole ions<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore 0.1}$ mole $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$ produces 0.3 mole ions<br>Number of ions $\\mathrm{=0.3 \\mathrm{~mole} \\times 6.02 \\times 10^{23}}$<br>$\\mathrm{=1.8 \\times 10^{23}}$ ions.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15349,'chemistry',46,449,905,'Which one is paramagnetic and has a bond order of $\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}$ ?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Ions &emsp; B.O.<br>&emsp; ${\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}$ &emsp; &emsp; 1.5<br>&emsp; ${\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ + }}$ &emsp; &emsp; 2.5<br>&emsp; ${{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ &emsp; &emsp; 1<br>&emsp; ${\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ + }}$&emsp; &emsp; 0.5',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15350,'chemistry',45,430,856,'Which is the correct order of atomic radius?','[{"text": "Metalic radius > van der Waal\'s radius > covalent radius", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Van der Waal\'s radius > covalent radius $>$ metallic radius", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Van der Waal\'s radius > metallic radius > covalent radius", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Covalent radius > metallic radius > van der Waal\'s radius", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Since, the internuclear distance between the atoms held by van der Waal\'s forces are larger than that between covalently bonded or metallic bonded atoms, van der Waal\'s radius is larger than metallic and covalent radius. In a covalent bond, the electron pairs are strongly attracted by the nuclei of two atoms due to which covalent radii is smaller than metallic radii. The correct order is van der Waals\' radius > metalic radius $>$ covalent radius',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2015,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15352,'chemistry',15,183,402,'Which of the following will not have a mass of $\\mathrm{10 \\mathrm{~g}}$ ?','[{"text": "0.1 mole $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CaCO}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{1.51 \\\\times 10^{23} \\\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}}$ ions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{0.16 \\\\mathrm{~mol}}$ of $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}}$ ions", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{7.525 \\\\times 10^{22} \\\\mathrm{Br}}$ atoms", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'0.1 mole $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CaCO}_{3}=0.1 \\times 100=10 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{CaCO}_{3}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}=\\dfrac{1.51 \\times 10^{23}}{6.023 \\times 10^{23}} \\times 40=10 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{Ca}^{+2}}$ ions<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}=0.16 \\times 60=9.6 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{-2}}$ ions<br>${\\text{Br    =   }}\\frac{{{\\text{7}}{\\text{.525}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{22}}}}}}{{{\\text{6}}{\\text{.2}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{23}}}}}} \\times {\\text{80   =   10}}\\,{\\text{g  Br atoms}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15353,'chemistry',46,449,905,'Which of the following options represents the correct bond order?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ > O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{ < }}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ < O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ < O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{ < }}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ > O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ions &emsp; B.O.<br>&emsp; ${\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}$ &emsp; &emsp; 1.5<br>&emsp; ${{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ &emsp; &emsp; 2<br>&emsp; ${\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ + }}$ &emsp; &emsp; 2.5',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15354,'chemistry',45,430,857,'The ionic radii  ${\\rm{(}}\\mathop {\\rm{A}}\\limits^{\\rm{o}} {\\rm{)}}$ of $\\mathrm{C}^{4-}$ and $\\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ respectively are 2.60 and 1.40 . The ionic radius of the isoelectronic ion $\\mathrm{N}^{3-}$ would be','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.31}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{A}}\\\\limits^{\\\\rm{o}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{2}}{\\\\rm{.83}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{A}}\\\\limits^{\\\\rm{o}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.71}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{A}}\\\\limits^{\\\\rm{o}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{2}}{\\\\rm{.63}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{A}}\\\\limits^{\\\\rm{o}} $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{C}^{4-}, \\mathrm{N}^{3-}$ and $\\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ are isoelectronic species. The ionic radius of isoelectronic species decreases with increase in nuclear charge. Hence, the order of ionic radius is<br>species : $\\mathrm{C}^{4-}>\\mathrm{N}^{3-}>\\mathrm{O}^{2-}$<br>$\\operatorname{Ionic} \\operatorname{radii}(\\stackrel{\\circ}{\\mathrm{A}}): 2.60>1.71>1.40$<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15356,'chemistry',15,183,402,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{xL}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}_{2}}$ at S.T.P. contains $\\mathrm{3 \\times 10^{22}}$ molecules. The number of molecules in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{x} / 2 \\mathrm{~L}}$ of ozone at S.T.P. will be','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{3 \\\\times 10^{22}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{1.5 \\\\times 10^{22}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{1.5 \\\\times 10^{21}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{1.5 \\\\times 10^{11}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Equal volumes of all gases under similar conditions of temperature and pressure will have equal number of molecules.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15357,'chemistry',46,449,903,'Among the following the one that has two nodal planes is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\sigma }}_{{\\\\rm{ns}}}^{\\\\rm{*}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\sigma }}_{{\\\\rm{n}}{{\\\\rm{p}}_{\\\\rm{z}}}}^{\\\\rm{*}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\sigma }}_{{\\\\rm{2}}{{\\\\rm{p}}_{\\\\rm{x}}}}^{\\\\rm{*}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{\\\\sigma }}_{{\\\\rm{2}}{{\\\\rm{p}}_{\\\\rm{x}}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'ABMO ${{\\rm{p}}_{\\rm{x}}}$ have two nodal planes',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15358,'chemistry',45,430,860,'Which pair of elements has maximum electronegativity difference?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Li}$ and $\\\\mathrm{F}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}$ and $\\\\mathrm{F}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}$ and $\\\\mathrm{Br}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}$ and $\\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Electronegativity decreases down the group and increases on moving from left to right in a period. Thus, $\\mathrm{Li}$ is more electronegative than $\\mathrm{Na}$, and $\\mathrm{F}$ is more electronegative than $\\mathrm{Cl}$ and $\\mathrm{Br} . \\mathrm{Br}$ is less electronegative than $\\mathrm{Cl}$ because of its larger size. Also, lesser be the electronegativity of metal element and more is the electronegativity of halogen atom, then more will be the electronegativity difference of a given species.<br>Thus, $\\mathrm{Na}$ and $\\mathrm{F}$ shows the maximum electronegativity difference option (2) correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15360,'chemistry',15,183,402,'If $\\mathrm{224 \\mathrm{ml}}$. of a triatomic gas has a mass of $\\mathrm{1 \\mathrm{~g}}$ at $\\mathrm{273 \\mathrm{~K}}$ and $\\mathrm{1 \\mathrm{~atm}}$ pressure, then the mass of one atom is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{8.30 \\\\times 10^{-23} \\\\mathrm{~g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{6.24 \\\\times 10^{-23} \\\\mathrm{~g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2.08 \\\\times 10^{-23} \\\\mathrm{~g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{5.54 \\\\times 10^{-23} \\\\mathrm{~g}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'No. of moles of triatomic gas<br>${\\text{ = }}\\frac{{{\\text{224}}\\,\\,{\\text{ml}}}}{{{\\text{22400}}\\,\\,{\\text{ml}}\\,{\\text{mol}}{{\\text{e}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}}}{\\text{ = 1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 2}}}}{\\text{mol}}$<br>No. of molecules<br>${\\text{ = 1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 2}}}}{\\text{ mol}} \\times \\left( {{\\text{6}}{\\text{.02}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{23}}}}{\\text{molecules}}\\,\\,{\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}} \\right)$<br>$\\mathrm{=6.02 \\times 10^{21}}$<br>no. of atoms of gas<br>$\\mathrm{=3 \\times 6.02 \\times 10^{21}=18.06 \\times 10^{21}=1.806 \\times 10^{22}}$<br>$\\mathrm{1.806 \\times 10^{22}}$ atoms has mass $\\mathrm{=1 \\mathrm{~g}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore 1}$ atom mass $\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{1}{1.806 \\times 10^{22} \\mathrm{~g}}}$<br>$\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{10^{-22} \\mathrm{~g}}{1.806}}$<br>$\\mathrm{=0.554 \\times 10^{-22} \\mathrm{~g}}$<br>$\\mathrm{=5.54 \\times 10^{-23} \\mathrm{~g}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
