(15406,'chemistry',45,425,851,'Which of the following is not correct for isoelectronic ions?','[{"text": "They have the same number of electrons around their nuclei", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Higher the atomic number, higher will be the positive charge in a series of isoelectronic ions of same period", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isoelectronic ions have same electric charge", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "An isoelectronic series may have both positively and negatively charged ions", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Isoelectronic species have same number of electrons but not same isoelectric charge. For example $\\mathrm{N}^{3-}, \\mathrm{O}^{2-}, \\mathrm{F}^{-}, \\mathrm{Na}^{+}, \\mathrm{Mg}^{+2}, \\mathrm{Al}^{+3}$ are isoelectronic but have different charges.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15408,'chemistry',15,187,410,'What volume of ${\\text{75}}\\% \\,\\,\\,{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}$ by mass is required to prepare 1.5 litres of ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.2}}\\,{\\text{M}}\\,\\,{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}$ ? (Density of the sample is $\\mathrm{1.8 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{cc}}$ )','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{14.2 \\\\mathrm{cc}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{28.4 \\\\mathrm{cc}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{21.7 \\\\mathrm{cc}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{7.1 \\\\mathrm{cc}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Mass of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$ needed to prepare $\\mathrm{0.2 \\mathrm{M}}$<br>$\\mathrm{1.5 \\mathrm{~L}}$ is Molarity $\\mathrm{\\times}$ gram molar mass $\\mathrm{\\times \\mathrm{V}(\\mathrm{L})}$<br>$\\mathrm{=0.2 \\times 98 \\times 1.5=29.4}$ gram $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$<br>given density is $\\mathrm{1.8 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{cc}}$<br>So. volume needed is $\\mathrm{\\dfrac{29.4 \\mathrm{~g}}{1.8 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{cc}}=16.33 \\mathrm{cc}}$<br>But it is $\\mathrm{75 \\%}$ only<br>So, volume needed of $\\mathrm{75 \\%}$ is<br>$\\mathrm{\\dfrac{16.33 \\times 100}{75}=21.77 \\mathrm{cc}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15409,'chemistry',46,434,870,'An atom of element A has three electrons in its outer shell and B has six electrons in its outer most shell. The formula of the compound formed between these will be','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{A}}_{\\\\rm{6}}}{{\\\\rm{B}}_{\\\\rm{6}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{A}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{B}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{A}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{B}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{A}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{B}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In this case the valence electron in the atom A is three and hence its valency is generally 3. In the atom B, the number of valence electron is six. Hence, its valency is usually 2. Hence, the formula of the molecule formed from A and B could be ${{\\rm{A}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{B}}_{\\rm{3}}}$. An example of two such elements are Al and O and the formula of Aluminium oxide is ${\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15410,'chemistry',45,430,858,'The elements which occupy the peaks of ionisation energy curve are','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}, \\\\mathrm{K}, \\\\mathrm{Rb}, \\\\mathrm{Cs}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}, \\\\mathrm{Mg}, \\\\mathrm{Cl}, \\\\mathrm{I}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}, \\\\mathrm{Br}, \\\\mathrm{I}, \\\\mathrm{F}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{He}, \\\\mathrm{Ne}, \\\\mathrm{Ar}, \\\\mathrm{Kr}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'IE of noble gases in highest in the respective periods, so they occupy the peaks of I.E. curve.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15412,'chemistry',46,444,889,'The electronegativity of O, F, N, Cl and H are 3.5, 4.0, 3.0, 3.0 and 2.1 respectively. The strongest bond will be :','[{"text": "F – O", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "O – Cl", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "N – H", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "O – H", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Bond strength $ \\propto $ Difference in electronegativity of atoms.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15413,'chemistry',15,186,405,'The composition of residual mixture will be, if $\\mathrm{30 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mg}}$ combines with $\\mathrm{30 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{40 \\\\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MgO}+20 \\\\mathrm{~g} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{45 \\\\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MgO}+15 \\\\mathrm{~g} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{50 \\\\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MgO}+10 \\\\mathrm{~g} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{60 g  MgO}}$ only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{Mg}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{MgO}}$<br>$\\mathrm{2 \\times 24 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{Mg}}$ reacts with $\\mathrm{32 \\mathrm{~g}}$ oxygen<br>$\\mathrm{30 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{Mg}}$ reacts with<br>$\\mathrm{\\dfrac{32}{2 \\times 24} \\times 30=20 \\mathrm{gO}_{2}}$<br>So, $\\mathrm{10 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ will be left unreacted and<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mg}}$ is the limiting reagent<br>$\\mathrm{2 \\times 24 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{Mg}}$ forms $\\mathrm{2 \\times 40 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{MgO}}$<br>$\\mathrm{30 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{Mg}}$ forms $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MgO}}$<br>$\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{2 \\times 40}{2 \\times 24} \\times 30=50 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{MgO}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15414,'chemistry',45,430,858,'Which of the following does not affect the ionisation potential of the atom?','[{"text": "Nuclear charge", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stable configuration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Penetration effect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'I.E is affected by radii, nuclear charge, stable electron configuration, penetration of orbitals and screening effect.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15416,'chemistry',15,183,402,'The following substances are present in different containers<br>(i) one gram atom of nitrogen<br>(ii) one mole of calcium<br>(iii) One atom of silver<br>(iv) One mole of oxygen molecules<br>(v) $\\mathrm{10^{23}}$ atoms of carbon<br>(vi) One gram of iron<br>The correct order of increasing masses (in grams) is/are','[{"text": "(iii) $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ (iv) $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ (i) $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ (v)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(iii) $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ (vi) $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ (iv) $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ (ii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(vi) $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ (v) $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ (i) $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(2) & (3) both are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'(i) $\\mathrm{14 \\mathrm{~g}}$ Nitrogen (ii) $\\mathrm{40 \\mathrm{~g}}$ Calcium<br>(iii) $\\mathrm{\\dfrac{108}{6.022 \\times 10^{23}}=1.79 \\times 10^{-22} \\mathrm{~g}}$ Silver<br>(iv) $\\mathrm{32 \\mathrm{~g}}$ Oxygen (v) $\\mathrm{1.99 \\mathrm{~g}}$ Carbon (vi) 1 $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{g}}$ Iron<br>Hence the correct order of increasing masses is (iii) $\\mathrm{ < }$ (vi) $\\mathrm{ < }$ (v) $\\mathrm{ < }$ (i) $\\mathrm{ < }$ (iv) $\\mathrm{ < }$ (ii)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15417,'chemistry',46,436,872,'Which of the following molecule(s) obey the octet rule?<br>(i) ${\\left[ {{\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{4}}}} \\right]^{\\rm{ - }}}$<br>(ii) ${\\left[ {{\\rm{AlC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{4}}}} \\right]^{\\rm{ - }}}$<br>(iii) ${\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>(iv) ${\\rm{CC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{4}}}$','[{"text": "(i), (ii), (iii), (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(ii), (iii), (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i), (iii), (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i), (ii), (iv)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_15417_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Total no. of valence electron around sulphur in ${\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ is 10 while in case of other molecules total 8 electrons are present in each.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15418,'chemistry',45,432,864,'Which of the following oxide is amphoteric?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{P}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{5}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ is basic oxide, $\\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}$ are acidic oxides, $\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ can react both with acids and bases. So, it is amphoteric in nature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15419,'chemistry',15,183,402,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> 3 moles of azide ion and 1 mole of nitride ion contain same amount of charge.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> 1 mole of azide ion and 1 mole of nitride ion have equal number of nitrogen atoms.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Azide ion is $\\mathrm{N}_{3}^{-}$<br>$\\text { charge in } 1 \\text { mole }=1 \\mathrm{~F}$<br>$\\therefore$ charge in 3 mole $=3 \\mathrm{~F}$<br>Nitride ion is $\\mathrm{N}^{3-}$<br>charge in 1 mole $=3 \\mathrm{~F}$<br>One mole of azide ion has $3 \\times \\mathrm{N}_{\\mathrm{A}}$ nitrogen atoms but one mole of nitride ion has only $\\mathrm{N}_{\\mathrm{A}}$ atoms. So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15421,'chemistry',46,449,905,'Stability of the species ${\\rm{L}}{{\\rm{i}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{,Li}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Li}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ + }}$ increases in the order of :','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{L}}{{\\\\rm{i}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ < Li}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}{\\\\rm{ < Li}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Li}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{ < Li}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}{\\\\rm{ < L}}{{\\\\rm{i}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{L}}{{\\\\rm{i}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ < Li}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{ < Li}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Li}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{ < L}}{{\\\\rm{i}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ < Li}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{L}}{{\\rm{i}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = \\sigma 1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{\\sigma }}^{\\rm{*}}}{\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{\\sigma 2}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{Bond}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{order = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {{\\rm{4 - 2}}} \\right){\\rm{ = 1}}$<br>${\\rm{Li}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ + }} = \\sigma 1{s^2}{\\sigma ^ * }1{s^2}\\sigma 2{s^1}$<br>${\\rm{B}}{\\rm{.O}}{\\rm{.}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {{\\rm{3 - 2}}} \\right){\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.5}}$<br>${\\rm{Li}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{ = \\sigma 1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{\\sigma }}^{\\rm{*}}}{\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{\\sigma 2}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{\\sigma }}^{\\rm{*}}}{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{1}}}$<br>${\\rm{B}}{\\rm{.O}}{\\rm{. = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {{\\rm{4 - 3}}} \\right){\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.5}}$<br>The bond order of ${\\rm{Li}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ + }}$ and ${\\rm{Li}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}$ is same but ${\\rm{Li}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ + }}$ is more stable than ${\\rm{Li}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}$ because ${\\rm{Li}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ + }}$ is smaller in size and has 2 electrons in antibonding orbitals whereas ${\\rm{Li}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}$ has 3 electrons in antibonding orbitals. Hence ${\\rm{Li}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ + }}$ is more stable than ${\\rm{Li}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15422,'chemistry',45,430,856,'The correct order of atomic radii of  ${\\text{C, N, P, S}}$  following the order:','[{"text": "${\\\\text{N  <  C  <  P  <  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{C  <  N  <  S  <  P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{C  <  N  <  P  <  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{N  <  C  <  S  <  P}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Along the period, atomic radii decreases and down the group increases.<br>$\\mathrm{C}, \\mathrm{N}$ are smaller and in that \' $\\mathrm{C}$ \' is bigger than \' $\\mathrm{N}$ \'. S,P are bigger and in that \' $\\mathrm{P}$ \' is bigger than \' $\\mathrm{S}$ \'.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15424,'chemistry',15,187,410,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Molarity of a solution represents its concentration.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Molarity is the number of moles of solute per litre of solution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The assertion is true because molarity, defined as moles of solute per liter of solution, accurately represents the concentration of a solution. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
