(15430,'chemistry',45,430,858,'A neutral atom $(\\mathrm{A})$ is converted to $\\left(\\mathrm{A}^{3+}\\right)$ by the following process:<br>$\\mathrm{A} \\xrightarrow[-\\mathrm{e}^{-}]{\\mathrm{E}_{1}} \\mathrm{~A}^{+} \\xrightarrow[-\\mathrm{e}^{-}]{\\mathrm{E}_{2}} \\mathrm{~A}^{2+} \\xrightarrow[-\\mathrm{e}^{-}]{\\mathrm{E}_{3}} \\mathrm{~A}^{3+}$<br>The correct order of $\\mathrm{E}_{1}, \\mathrm{E}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{E}_{3}$energies is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{E}_{1} < \\\\mathrm{E}_{2} < \\\\mathrm{E}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{E}_{1}>\\\\mathrm{E}_{2}>\\\\mathrm{E}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{E}_{1}=\\\\mathrm{E}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{E}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{E}_{1}>\\\\mathrm{E}_{2} < \\\\mathrm{E}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For a particular atom, the successive ionisation energy always increases. So, $\\mathrm{E}_{1} < \\mathrm{E}_{2} < \\mathrm{E}_{3}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15433,'chemistry',46,447,895,'Ratio of ${\\rm{\\sigma }}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{\\pi }}$ bonds is in order<br>A : Tetracyanomethane<br>B : Carbondioxide<br>C : Benzene<br>D : 1,3-buta-di-ene','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A = B < C < D}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A = B < D < C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A = B = C = D}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{C < D < A < B}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; ${\\rm{\\sigma }}$ &emsp; ${\\rm{\\pi }}$<br>${\\rm{C}}{\\left( {{\\rm{C}} \\equiv {\\rm{N}}} \\right)_{\\rm{4}}}$&emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; -8 &emsp; 8<br>${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; -2 &emsp; 2<br>${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{6}}}$ &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; -12 &emsp; 3<br>${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{  =  CH  -  CH  =  C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ &emsp; -9 &emsp; 2',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15434,'chemistry',45,426,852,'The element with atomic number 57 belongs to:','[{"text": "s - block", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "p - block", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "d - block", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "f - block", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The element with atomic number 57 is La.<br>The outer electronic configuration of $\\mathrm{La}$ $(\\mathrm{Z}=57)$ is $5 \\mathrm{~d}^{1} 6 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}$.<br>So, it is d-block element.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15660,'chemistry',46,448,899,'In which of the excitation state of chlorine ${\\rm{Cl}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{3}}}$ is formed','[{"text": "In ground state", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In third excitation state", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In first excitation state", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In second excitation state", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$_{{\\rm{17}}}{\\rm{Cl}} \\to {\\left[ {{\\rm{Ne}}} \\right]^{{\\rm{10}}}}{\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{5}}}$<br>In first excited state, it has 3 unpaired electrons and forms 3 bonds <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_15660_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> \'C\' means number of unpaired electrons.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15437,'chemistry',46,446,892,'Molecular shapes of ${\\rm{SC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{,}}\\,\\,{\\rm{CC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{4}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Xe}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ are','[{"text": "Same with 2,0 and 1 lone pairs of electrons respectively", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Same with 1,1 and 1 lone pairs of electrons respectively", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Different with 1,0 and 2 lone pairs of electrons respectively", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Different with 1,1 and 1 lone pairs of electrons respectively", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In ${\\rm{SC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{4}}}$,<br>number of bond pairs = 4<br>number of lone pairs = 1<br>In ${\\rm{CC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{4}}}$,<br>number of bond pairs = 4<br>number of lone pairs = 0<br>In ${\\rm{Xe}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{4}}}$,<br>number of bond pairs = 4<br>number of lone pairs = 2',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15438,'chemistry',45,424,844,'The last element of the p-block in $6^{\\text {th }}$ period is represented by the outermost electronic configuration','[{"text": "$7 \\\\mathrm{~s}^{2} 7 \\\\mathrm{p}^{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\mathrm{f}^{14} 6 \\\\mathrm{~d}^{10} 7 \\\\mathrm{~s}^{2} 7 \\\\mathrm{p}^{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\mathrm{f}^{14} 5 \\\\mathrm{~d}^{10} 6 \\\\mathrm{~s}^{2} 6 \\\\mathrm{p}^{6}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 \\\\mathrm{f}^{14} 5 \\\\mathrm{~d}^{10} 6 \\\\mathrm{~s}^{2} 6 \\\\mathrm{p}^{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$6^{\\text {th }}$ period contains $6 \\mathrm{~s}, 6 \\mathrm{p}, 5 \\mathrm{~d}$, 4 f orbitals. So, last element configuration will be $4 \\mathrm{f}^{14} 5 \\mathrm{~d}^{10} 6 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 6 \\mathrm{p}^{6}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15441,'chemistry',46,446,892,'Among the following species, identity the isostructural pairs ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{,NO}}_{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{,B}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{,}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{{\\rm{O}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{,H}}{{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{3}}}$','[{"text": "$\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{,NO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}} \\\\right]\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{B}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{,}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{O}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{,H}}{{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}} \\\\right]\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{NO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{,B}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{,}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{O}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}} \\\\right]\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{NO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{,B}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{,}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{O}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}} \\\\right]\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{H}}{{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{,B}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15442,'chemistry',45,422,842,'Which of the elements whose atomic numbers are given below, cannot be accommodated in the present set up of the long form of the periodic table?','[{"text": "107", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "118", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "126", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "102", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The present long form of the periodic table contains elements with atomic number 1 to 118 only (in total 7 periods).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15700,'chemistry',45,430,860,'Select the group where electronegativity increases down the group','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ca}, \\\\mathrm{Sr}$, and Ba", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Zn}, \\\\mathrm{Cd}$, and Hg", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{F}, \\\\mathrm{Cl}$, and Br", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Li, Na, and K", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In $\\mathrm{Zn}, \\mathrm{Cd}$, and Hg , electronegativity increases down the group due to lanthanoid effect in group.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15444,'chemistry',15,187,412,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Equivalent weight of $\\mathrm{Cu}$ in $\\mathrm{CuO}$ is 31.8 and in $\\mathrm{Cu}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ is 63.6.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Equivalent weight of an element<br>$=\\dfrac{\\text { Atomic weight }}{\\mathrm{x}-\\text { factor }}$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The equivalent weight (eq. wt.) of copper $(\\mathrm{Cu})$ in copper oxide $(\\mathrm{CuO})$ is calculated as follows:The atomic weight (At.wt) of copper $(\\mathrm{Cu})$ is $63.6 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{mol}$, and its valency is 2 .<br>Equivalent weight of $\\mathrm{Cu}$ in $\\mathrm{CuO}=\\dfrac{63.6}{2}=31.8$<br>Equivalent weight of $\\mathrm{Cu}$ in $\\mathrm{Cu}_{2} \\mathrm{O}=\\dfrac{63.6}{1}=63.6$<br>So, the option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15445,'chemistry',46,446,892,'Which of the following statement is incorrect for ${\\rm{PC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{5}}}$','[{"text": "Its all P–Cl bond lengths are equal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It involves ${\\\\rm{s}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{d}}$ hybridisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It has irregular geometry", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its shape is trigonal bipyramidal.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15446,'chemistry',45,424,845,'The electronic configuration of the element which is just above the element with atomic number 43 in the same group is','[{"text": "$1 \\\\mathrm{~s}^{2} 2 \\\\mathrm{~s}^{2} 2 \\\\mathrm{p}^{6} 3 \\\\mathrm{~s}^{2} 3 \\\\mathrm{p}^{6} 3 \\\\mathrm{~d}^{5} 4 \\\\mathrm{~s}^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{2}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{2}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{6}}}{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{6}}}{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{d}}^{\\\\rm{5}}}{\\\\rm{4}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{4}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{6}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{2}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{2}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{6}}}{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{6}}}{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{d}}^{\\\\rm{6}}}{\\\\rm{4}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{2}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{2}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{6}}}{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{6}}}{\\\\rm{3}}{{\\\\rm{d}}^{\\\\rm{7}}}{\\\\rm{4}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The element with $Z=43$ lies in the 5 th period. Since, the 4th period has 18 elements, therefore, the atomic number of the element which lies immediately above the element with atomic number 43 is $43-18=25$.<br>Now, the electronic configuration of element with $\\mathrm{Z}=25$ is $1 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 2 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 2 \\mathrm{p}^{6} 3 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 3 \\mathrm{p}^{6} 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15447,'chemistry',15,183,402,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Molar volume of gases change considerably with temperature and pressure.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Molar volume of a substance is the volume occupied by 1 mole of that substance.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The volume of a gas is dependen on its temperature and pressure. Temperature effects the kinetic energy. Pressure change effects the intermoelcular attraction, which inturn allows for expansion or contraction. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15449,'chemistry',46,448,898,'The ratio of hybrid and unhybrid orbitals involved in the bonding of a benzene molecule is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{3:2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1:1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{3:1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1:3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Six carbon atoms undergo ${\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}$ hybridization. So, 18 hybrid orbitals & 6 pure orbitals from carbon & 6 pure orbitals from ‘H’ atoms.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15450,'chemistry',45,430,858,'Which of the following has lowest $1^{\\text {st }}$ ionisation energy?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Li}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{F}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Lithium being alkali metal, it has lowest $1^{\\text {st }}$ I.E. among given<br>Order of $1^{\\text {st }}$ IP<br>$\\mathrm{F}>\\mathrm{O}>\\mathrm{C}>\\mathrm{Li}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15451,'chemistry',15,186,405,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A reactant that is entirely consumed when a reaction goes to completion is known as limiting reactant.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The amount of limiting reactant limits the amount of product formed.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The amount of product in a chemical reaction is dictated by the limiting reagent, the reactant fully consumed first, setting the maximum achievable product quantity. Whereas other reactants in excess don\'t impact the upper limit on product yield. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15453,'chemistry',46,448,899,'In which of the following, d - orbitals are not used by central atom in hybridization?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{P}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{5}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{PC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{5}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Xe}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Xe}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{6}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Hybridization of P in ${\\rm{P}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{5}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{PC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{ - s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{d}}$<br>Hybridization of Xe in ${\\rm{Xe}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ - s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{3}}}$<br>Hybridization of Xe in ${\\rm{Xe}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{6}}}{\\rm{ - s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{3}}}{{\\rm{d}}^{\\rm{3}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15454,'chemistry',45,430,858,'Ionisation energies of element ${\\text{AF}}$  are given below in $\\mathrm{kJ} / \\mathrm{mol} . \\mathrm{IE}_{1}=120, \\mathrm{IE}_{2}=430, \\mathrm{IE}_{3}=540$.<br>If A reacts with different elements, which compound of A is not possible?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{AF}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~N}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~N}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The maximum jump is between $1^{\\text {st }}$ and $2^{\\text {nd }}$ I.E. It means element \' $A$ \' loses 1 electron and shows +1 oxidation state. So, $\\mathrm{AF}, \\mathrm{A}_{2} \\mathrm{O}, \\mathrm{A}_{3} \\mathrm{~N}$ are possible but not $\\mathrm{A}_{3} \\mathrm{~N}_{2}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15455,'chemistry',15,182,400,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Mass spectrometer is used for the identification of isotopes.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Isotopes are the atoms of same element differing in mass numbers.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A mass spectrometer determines atomic or molecular weight by ionizing a sample and utilizing magnetic and electric fields to separate ions based on their mass-to-charge ratios,enabling precise measurement of atomic weights and isotopic identification. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15457,'chemistry',46,449,905,'According to molecular orbital theory, which of the following will not be a viable molecule?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{He}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{He}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{2 + }}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The electronic configuration of ${\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\left( {{\\rm{\\sigma 1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}} \\right){\\rm{,}}{\\left( {{{\\rm{\\sigma }}^{\\rm{*}}}{\\rm{1s}}} \\right)^{\\rm{2}}}$<br>Bond order of ${\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{b}}}{\\rm{ - }}{{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{a}}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{2 - 2}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}$<br>Hence ${\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}$ does not exist',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(15458,'chemistry',45,430,859,'The diagram below is a part of the skeleton of the periodic table in which elements are indicated by letters which are not their usual symbols. The correct option is:','[{"text": "${\\\\text{D}}$\' has maximum electron affinity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{B}}$ \' exist in nature in a liquid state", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A}}$ \' is $5^{\\\\text {th }}$ period element in the periodic table", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(2) & (3)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{Cl}$ \' has maximum electron affinity.<br>Bromine is liquid, not iodine.<br>\' ${\\text{A}}$ \' $(\\mathrm{Se})$ is $4^{\\text {th }}$ period element. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/45_15458_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/45_15458_question_1.jpg'),
(15730,'chemistry',46,451,907,'Between any two of the following molecules, hydrogen bonding is not possible','[{"text": "Two primary amine molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two secondary amine molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two tertiary amine molecules", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Two ammonia molecules", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15459,'chemistry',15,182,400,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Atomic mass has no unit but expressed in amu.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It is the average mass of an atom taking care of relative abundance of all its possible isotopes.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The average mass of an atom is determined by the weighted average of its isotopes\' masses. It is calculated using the formula<br>Average atomic mass $=$<br>$\\sum($ Relative Abundance $) \\times($ Atomic mass of isotope)<br>This provides a dimensionless atomic mass that accounts for the relative abundances of isotopes in the element. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15461,'chemistry',46,446,892,'Amongst ${\\rm{NO}}_{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{,AsO}}_{\\rm{3}}^{{\\rm{3 - }}}{\\rm{,CO}}_{\\rm{3}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{,ClO}}_{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{,SO}}_{\\rm{3}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{and}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{BO}}_{\\rm{3}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}$, the non-planar species are','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{CO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}{\\\\rm{,SO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}{\\\\rm{,BO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{{\\\\rm{3 - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{AsO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{{\\\\rm{3 - }}}{\\\\rm{,ClO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{,SO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{NO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{,CO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}{\\\\rm{,BO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{{\\\\rm{3 - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{SO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}{\\\\rm{,NO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{,BO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{{\\\\rm{3 - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15462,'chemistry',45,430,859,'From the following given electronic configurations, identify the correct order of electron affinity<br>${\\text{(I) [He]}}\\,{\\text{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{2}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{5}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(II)}}\\,\\,{\\text{[He]}}\\,{\\text{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{2}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{3}}}$<br>$({\\text{III)}}\\,{\\text{[Ne]}}\\,{\\text{3}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{3}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{5}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(IV) [Ne]}}\\,{\\text{3}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{3}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{3}}}$','[{"text": "I $>$ II $>$ III $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III $>$ I $>$ IV $>$ II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I $ < $ II $ < $ III $ < $ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II $>$ III $>$ IV $>$ I", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{I  =  F, III  =  Cl}}$<br>$\\mathrm{II}=\\mathrm{N} \\quad, \\mathrm{IV}=\\mathrm{P}$<br>$\\mathrm{N}, \\mathrm{P}$ have low E.A. values due to half filled orbitals.<br>\'${\\text{Cl}}$\' E.A. is greater than \' $\\mathrm{F}$ \' due to large size and less electronic repulsion.<br>So, $\\mathrm{Cl}>\\mathrm{F}>\\mathrm{P}>\\mathrm{N}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15463,'chemistry',15,180,393,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The balancing of chemical equations is based on law of conservation of mass.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Total mass of reactants is equal to total mass of a products.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As per the law of coservation of mass, matter can neither be created nor destroyed. Thus total mass of the reactants must be equal to the total mass of the products. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15465,'chemistry',46,446,892,'The ratio of number of lone pairs on central atom in ammonia, Water and ${\\rm{Xe}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ are','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{3:2:1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{2:1:3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1:2:3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{2:3:1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15466,'chemistry',45,430,858,'Few elements are matched with their successive ionisation energies. Identify the elements<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "X-A noble gas, Y-Alkali metal, Z-Alkaline earth metal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "X-Alkali metal, Y-A noble gas, Z-Alkaline earth metal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "X-Alkaline earth metal, Y-Alkali metal, Z-A noble gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "X-Alkali metal, Y-Alkaline earth metal, Z-A noble gas", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{X}}$  has highest ${\\text{I}}{{\\text{E}}_{\\text{1}}}$ and  ${\\text{I}}{{\\text{E}}_{\\text{2}}}$ hence, it is a noble gas. $\\mathrm{Y}$ has low $\\mathrm{IE}_{1}$, and very high $\\mathrm{IE}_{2}$ so, it is an alkali metal. $\\mathrm{Z}$ has low $\\mathrm{IE}_{1}$ than $\\mathrm{IE}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{IE}_{2}$ is even lower than $\\mathrm{IE}_{2}$ of alkali metal hence, it is an alkaline earth metal.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/45_15466_question_1.jpg'),
(15762,'chemistry',45,430,859,'Which of the following has the highest electron affinity?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{F}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{F}^{-}$and $\\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ ions have stable electronic configuration, thus they will not accept further electrons, while $\\mathrm{O}$ needs electrons to achieve stable arrangement, thus it has highest electron affinity. Further, $\\mathrm{Na}$ is a metal, thus it does not have any electron affinity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15468,'chemistry',15,180,398,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Under similar conditions of temperature and pressure, gases always combine in simple ratio of their volumes.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Equal volumes of gases have equal<br>number of molecules at constant temperature and pressure','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Assertion is the statement of Gay-Lussac\'s law of combining volumes. Reason is the statement of Avogadro\'s law. Both Assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15469,'chemistry',46,446,892,'The atomic number of Sn is 50. The shape of gaseous ${\\rm{SnC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ molecule is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{Cl - Sn - Cl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Electronic configuration of Sn = [Kr] <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_15469_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}$ - hybridisation : with 2 bond pairs and 1 lone pair.<br>So the structure is angular.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15470,'chemistry',45,430,861,'In the given graph, a periodic property \\((\\mathrm{R})\\) is plotted against atomic numbers \\((\\mathrm{Z})\\) of the elements. Which property is shown in the graph and how is it correlated with reactivity of the elements?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Ionisation enthalpy in a group decreases and reactivity decrease from $\\\\mathrm{a} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{e}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ionisation enthalpy in a group decreases and reactivity increases from $\\\\mathrm{a} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{e}$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Atomic radius in a group decreases and reactivity decreases from $\\\\mathrm{a} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Metallic character in a group decreases and reactivity increases from $\\\\mathrm{a} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{e}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'I.E. in a group decreases as the atomic radii increases and reactivity down the group increases.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/45_15470_question_1.jpg'),
(15471,'chemistry',15,177,387,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In a given compound, the relative number and kind of atoms are constant.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The properties of a compound are completely different from those of its constituent elements.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to law of definite proportion, a given compound always contains exactly the same proportion of elements by weight. Thus, in a compound the number and kind of atoms are fixed. Acompound is a totally new substance and differs in properties from that of its constituent elements. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15473,'chemistry',46,448,898,'In pyrrole, the hybrid state assumed by N is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{sp}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{s}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{s}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ds}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'‘N’ contain 3 bp and 1 lp. So, the hybridisation is ${\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{3}}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/46_15473_question_1.png'),
(15474,'chemistry',45,430,858,'Five ionization energy values in $\\mathrm{kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$ are listed below: $\\mathrm{E}_{1}=870, \\mathrm{E}_{2}=830, \\mathrm{E}_{3}=1010, \\mathrm{E}_{4}=1290$,<br>$\\mathrm{E}_{5}=376$. These are','[{"text": "Successive ionization energies for the element with atomic number 5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The first I.E. of successive elements in group 15, 16, 17, 18 and 1 respectively", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The first I.E. of elements with atomic number 1 to 5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Successive I.E. for transition elements with four electrons in d-subshell.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In a period, I.E increases from left to right (Group 15 to 18).<br>Group 15 elements have half filled orbitals so their $1^{\\text {st }}$ I.E is greater than group 16 elements. Similarly group 18 elements (due to fulfilled orbitals) $1^{\\text {st }}$ I.E. is greater than group 17 elements.<br>Group 1 elements have lowest $1^{\\text {st }}$ I.E. values as they are metals and have only onevalence electron.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15475,'chemistry',15,182,401,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Molecular weight of a compound is 44 if its vapour density is 22 .<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Vapour density $\\times 2=$ Molecular weight.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'This is the vapor density method for the determination of molecular weight. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15477,'chemistry',46,449,905,'Which one of the following molecules is expected to exhibit diamagnetic behaviour?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The molecular orbital structures of ${{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ is<br>${{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{:}}{\\left( {{\\rm{\\sigma 1s}}} \\right)^{\\rm{2}}}{\\left( {{{\\rm{\\sigma }}^{\\rm{*}}}{\\rm{1s}}} \\right)^{\\rm{2}}}{\\left( {{\\rm{\\sigma 2s}}} \\right)^{\\rm{2}}}{\\left( {{{\\rm{\\sigma }}^{\\rm{*}}}{\\rm{2s}}} \\right)^{\\rm{2}}}$<br>$\\left( {{\\rm{\\pi 2p}}_{\\rm{x}}^{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{ = \\pi 2p}}_{\\rm{y}}^{\\rm{2}}} \\right){\\left( {{\\rm{\\sigma 2}}{{\\rm{p}}_{\\rm{z}}}} \\right)^{\\rm{2}}}$<br>${{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ has only paired electrons, hence it is diamagnetic.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15478,'chemistry',45,432,864,'In the periodic table, the basic character of oxides','[{"text": "Increases from left to right and decreases from top to bottom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases from right to left and increases from top to bottom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases from left to right and increases from top to bottom", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Decreases from left to right and increases from bottom to top", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Down the group metallic nature increases. So, basic nature of oxides increases.<br>Across a period from left to right metallic nature decreases so basic nature of oxides decreases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15479,'chemistry',15,183,402,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Both $44 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{CO}_{2}$, and $16 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ have same number of carbon atoms.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Both contain $1 \\mathrm{~g}$ atom of carbon which contains $6.023 \\times 10^{23}$ carbon atoms.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$44 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2} \\equiv 1$ mole $\\mathrm{CO}_{2} \\equiv 1 \\mathrm{~g}$-atom of " $\\mathrm{C}$ "<br>$16 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{CH}_{4} \\equiv 1$ mole $\\mathrm{CH}_{4} \\equiv 1 \\mathrm{~g}$-atom of " $\\mathrm{C}$ "<br>$12 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{C}(1$ mole $)$ contains $6.023 \\times 10^{23}$ carbon atoms. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15481,'chemistry',46,445,891,'Which of the following has least covalent P-H bond?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{P}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{P}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{6}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{P}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{5}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{PH}}_{\\\\rm{6}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Due to the +ve charge on P, it attracts the electrons of the P-H bond towards itself. As a result, it has some ionic character. In other words, the P-H bond in ${\\rm{PH}}_{\\rm{6}}^{\\rm{ + }}$ is least covalent.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15482,'chemistry',45,431,862,'Metal \' ${\\text{M}}$\' forms a peroxide of the type  ${{\\text{M}}_{\\text{2}}}$. Valency of the metal with respect to oxygen','[{"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In peroxide, each oxygen have -1 oxidation state. So metal will get valency two as two oxygen atoms are present.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15483,'chemistry',15,183,402,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $1 \\mathrm{~L}$ of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$, and $1 \\mathrm{~L}$ of $\\mathrm{O}_{3}$ contain the same number of moles under identical conditions.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Under identical conditions, $1 \\mathrm{~L}$ of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ ,gas and $1 \\mathrm{~L}$ of $\\mathrm{O}_{3}$ gas contain the same number of oxygen atoms.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Under identical conditions, $1 \\mathrm{~L}$ of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ gas and $1 \\mathrm{~L}$ of $\\mathrm{O}_{3}$ gas contain the same number of moles and hence, the same number of molecules but not the same number of atoms.<br>1 mole of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ contains $\\mathrm{N}_{\\mathrm{A}}$ molecules and $2 \\mathrm{~N}$ oxygen atoms.<br>1 mole of $\\mathrm{O}_{3}$ contains $\\mathrm{N}_{\\mathrm{A}}$ molecules and $3 \\mathrm{~N}$ oxygen atoms So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15485,'chemistry',46,444,890,'The dipole moment of chlorobenzene (Figure 1 below) is 1.5D. The dipole moment of (Figure 2 below) is','[{"text": "2.86 D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.25 D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.5 D", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0 D", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${{\\rm{\\mu }}_{\\rm{R}}}{\\rm{ = 2}}\\left( {{\\rm{Bond}}\\,{\\rm{moment}}} \\right){\\rm{cos}}\\frac{{\\rm{\\theta }}}{{\\rm{2}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/46_15485_question_1.png'),
(15486,'chemistry',45,423,843,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Atomic number of the element copernicium is 112.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> IUPAC name of this element is ununbium in which un and bi-are used for 1 and 2 respectively in latin words.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The IUPAC name of the element with atomic number 112 is "copernicium."<br>According to systematic nomenclature root words are put together in order of digits which make up the atomic number and "ium" is added at the end. 1 is reffered as \'un\' and 2 as \'bi\'. Therefore, 112 is Ununbium. Thus option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15487,'chemistry',15,183,402,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Atomicity of oxygen is 2 .<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> 1 mole of an element contains $6.023 \\times 10^{23}$ atoms.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The number of atoms present in a molecule of a gaseous element is called its atomicity. For example, $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ has two atoms, and hence its atomicity is 2 .<br>1 mole of an element contains Avagadro number of atoms. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15489,'chemistry',46,444,890,'The dipole moment of o,p and m-dichlorobenzene will be in the order','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{o > p > m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{p > o > m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{m > o > p}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{o > m > p}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As bond angle increases, dipole moment decreases.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15490,'chemistry',45,422,842,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Elements in the same vertical column have similar properties.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Elements have periodic dependence upon the atomic number.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Elements within the same group (vertical column) in the periodic table have the same number of electrons in their outermost energy level, and consequently, they share similar electronic configurations. As a result, these elements exhibit similar chemical properties and behaviors. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15492,'chemistry',15,182,400,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> 1 amu equals to $1.66 \\times 10^{-24} \\mathrm{~g}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $1.66 \\times 10^{-24} \\mathrm{~g}$. equals to $\\dfrac{1}{12}$ th of mass of a C-12 atom.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The mass of 1 atom of ${\\text{C - 12}}$ is $1.66 \\times 10^{-24} \\mathrm{~g}$. and this value is used as the definition of an atomic mass unit  $(1\\,{\\text{amu}})$, which is equal to $1.66 \\times 10^{-24} \\mathrm{~g}$. So, the option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15493,'chemistry',46,444,890,'The correct sequence of polarity of the following molecules<br>(I) Benzene<br>(II) Inorganic Benzene<br>(III) ${\\rm{PC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>(IV) ${\\rm{PC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{3}}}$','[{"text": "P-I,  NP-II,  NP-III,  P-IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "NP-I,  NP-II,  NP-III,  P-IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "NP-I,  P-II,  NP-III,  P-IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "NP-I,  P-II,  P-III,  NP-IV", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_15493_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15494,'chemistry',45,426,852,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Helium is placed in group 18 along with p-block elements.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It shows properties similar to p-block elements.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Helium is a noble gas with completely filled valence shell $\\left(1 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}\\right)$. Therefore, it is placed in group 18 of p-block. But it does not show properties of p-block elements. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15496,'chemistry',15,187,412,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Equivalent weight of a compound differs in different reactions.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Equivalent weight $=\\dfrac{\\text { Molecular weight }}{\\text { No. of electrons involved }}$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The equivalent weight of a compound can vary in different reactions, as it depends on the number of electrons lost or gained, influencing its ability to donate or accept one mole of electrons during a chemical reaction. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15497,'chemistry',45,425,850,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Chemistry of actinoids is more complicated than lanthanoids.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Actinoid elements are radioactive.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A large number of oxidation states are possible for actinides due to which they have complex chemistry. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15498,'chemistry',46,445,891,'Which of the molecule has maximum covalent character?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{NaCl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{SiC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{AlC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{MgC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Polarisation in the molecule increases with increase of charge and decreases with decrease in size of the cation when the anion is same. Polarisation $ \\propto \\% $ covalent character the order. So, covalent character is ${\\rm{SiC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{ > AlC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ > MgC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ > NaCl}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15499,'chemistry',15,180,393,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In $\\mathrm{CO}$ molecule, 12 parts by mass of carbon combine with 16 parts by mass of oxygen and in $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$, 12 parts by mass of carbon combine with 32 parts by mass of oxygen.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> When two elements combine separately with a fixed mass of a third element,then the ratio of their masses in which they do so is either the same or whole number multiple of the ratio in which they combine with each other','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The given assertion is explained by the law of multiple proportions, which states that "when two elements combine with each other to form two or more compounds, then the masses of one of the elements which combine with a fixed mass of the other have a simple whole number ratio to one another." The reason provided is in line with the statement of the law of reciprocal proportions. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15501,'chemistry',45,430,858,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> First ionization enthalpy of boron is slightly less than that of beryllium.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Ionization enthalpy increases as we go across the period.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The valence electron of Beryllium is in the $\\mathrm{s}$ orbital, where as in Boron it is in p orbital. The $\\mathrm{s}$ orbital has more penetration power than the $\\mathrm{p}$ orbital. Therefore, it is more difficult to renove valence electron of Be than from Boron. Therefore, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15502,'chemistry',46,449,905,'Which one of the following pairs of species have the same bond order?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{N}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{O}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{N}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{N}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{N}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{O}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{N}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For any species to have same bond order we can expect them to have same number of electrons. Calculating the number of electrons in various species.<br>${\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}\\left( {{\\rm{8 + 8 + 1 = 17}}} \\right){\\rm{;C}}{{\\rm{N}}^{\\rm{ - }}}\\left( {{\\rm{6 + 7 + 1 = 14}}} \\right)$<br>${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}^{\\rm{ + }}}\\left( {{\\rm{7 + 8 - 1 = 14}}} \\right){\\rm{;C}}{{\\rm{N}}^{\\rm{ + }}}\\left( {{\\rm{6 + 7 - 1 = 12}}} \\right)$<br>We find ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{N}}^{\\rm{ - }}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}^{\\rm{ + }}}$ both have 14 electrons so they have same bond order.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15503,'chemistry',15,180,397,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The ratio by volume of gaseous reactants and products is in agreement with their molar ratio.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Volume of a gas is inversely proportional to the number of moles of a gas.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The assertion is true as volume ratios in gaseous reactions align with molar ratios due to Avogadro\'s law, but the reason is false; the correct statement is that "volume of a gas is directly proportional to the number of moles of a gas". So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15505,'chemistry',45,430,858,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Alkali metals have least value of ionization energy within a period.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> They precede alkaline earth metals in periodic table.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ionization energy increases across the period with decrease in atomic size. Alkali metals have the least ionization energy in any given period due to their large size. After alkali metals, alkaline earth metals are present across a period.Thus, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15506,'chemistry',46,440,881,'Which of the following molecules does not have co-ordinate bonds ?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{PH}}_{\\\\rm{4}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{CO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_15506_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15507,'chemistry',15,187,410,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The concentration of the dissolved solute is usually specified in terms of Molarity<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Molarity is temperature dependent concentration term.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Molarity is used to describe how much solute is dissolved in a given volume of the solution, because it is expressed in terms of number of moles. "Molarity is temperature dependent  concentration term". So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15509,'chemistry',45,428,854,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Shielding effect increases as we go down the group.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> More is the number of electrons in the penultimate shell, more is shielding.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The decrease in the effective nuclear charge on the valence electrons due to the electrons in the penultimate shell is called shielding effect. This effect increases as the number of penultimate electrons increases. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15510,'chemistry',46,440,881,'Ionic; covalent; dative and hydrogen bonds are present in','[{"text": "Ice", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{BeS}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}{\\\\rm{.4}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{O}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{CuS}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}{\\\\rm{5}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{O}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{BaC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{.2}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{O}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\left[ {{\\rm{Cu}}{{\\left( {{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{4}}}} \\right]{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{.}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{u}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{SO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\,\\,{\\rm{2 - }}}$ bond ionic<br>In ${\\rm{SO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\,\\,{\\rm{2 - }}}$,    S - O bondis covalent<br>Four ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}} \\to {\\rm{Cu}}$  dative bond. One water molecule is forms H - bond.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15511,'chemistry',15,180,395,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A certain element ${\\text{X}}$, forms three binary compounds with chlorine containing $59.68 \\%, 68.95 \\%$ and $74.75 \\%$ chlorine respectively.These data illustrate the law of multiple proportion.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> According to law of multiple proportions. the relative amounts of an element combining with some fixed amount of a second element in a series of compounds are the ratios of small whole numbers.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The data exemplifies the Law of Multiple Proportions, showcasing fixed mass ratios in compounds formed by element $\\mathrm{X}$ and chlorine, as expressed by small whole numbers. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15513,'chemistry',45,430,858,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The highest I.E. in a period is shown by noble gas.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Noble gases are at the extreme right of the period.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Noble gases have completely filled valence shell. Therfore they have the highest I.E in their respective periods. Noble gases are the last elements in their respective periods. Hence option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15514,'chemistry',46,442,885,'The correct order of the bond angles is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{O > P}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{ > P}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{O > }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{S > P}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{O}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{P}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{S > N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{O}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\left( {{\\rm{10}}{{\\rm{7}}^{\\rm{0}}}} \\right){\\rm{,}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}\\left( {{\\rm{104}}{\\rm{.}}{{\\rm{5}}^{\\rm{0}}}} \\right){\\rm{,P}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\left( {{\\rm{9}}{{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{0}}}} \\right){\\rm{,}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}\\left( {{\\rm{92}}{\\rm{.}}{{\\rm{5}}^{\\rm{0}}}} \\right)$<br>The bond angles depend upon electronegativity of the central atom and number of lone pair of electrons.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15515,'chemistry',15,187,411,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The molality of the solution does not change with change in temperature.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The molality is expressed in units of moles per $1000 \\mathrm{~g}$ of solvent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Molality $(\\mathrm{m})$ represents solute concentration in a solution, measured as moles of solute per kilogram of solvent, It is independent of temperature as it remains independent of solution volume, relying on the constant mass of the solvent. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15517,'chemistry',45,430,858,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Order of ionization potential across the period is $\\mathrm{Na} < \\mathrm{Al} < \\mathrm{Mg} < \\mathrm{Si}$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Ionization potential decreases with decrease in atomic size.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'I.E. increases across a period. But $\\mathrm{Mg}$ has higher I.E. than $\\mathrm{Al}$ due to the fullfilled stability of $3 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}$ configuration.Ionization potential decreases as atomic size increases .So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15519,'chemistry',15,180,398,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Atoms can neither be created nor destroyed.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Under similar conditions of temperature and pressure, equal volume of gases does not contain equal number of molecules.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The law of conservation of mass asserts that atoms cannot be created nor destroyed. But Avogadro\'s law states that, under identical conditions of temperature and pressure, equal volumes of gases contain an equal number of molecules. So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15521,'chemistry',45,430,859,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Electron gain enthalpy can be exothermic or endothermic.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electron gain enthalpy provides a measure of the ease with which an atom adds an electron to form anion.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The process of adding an electron to an isolated gaseous atom can be eitherendothermic or exothermic, depending on the specific element involved. Higher the -ve E.A, easier is the addition of electron into an atom. Both are true but there is no relation between the two statements. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15522,'chemistry',46,438,878,'Which of the following compounds contain(s) no covalent bonds (s) ?<br>${\\rm{KCl,P}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{,}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{,}}{{\\rm{B}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{6}}}{\\rm{,}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{KCl,}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{S}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{KCl}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{KCl,}}{{\\\\rm{B}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{6}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{KCl,}}{{\\\\rm{B}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{6}}}{\\\\rm{,P}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\left. \\begin{array}{l}{\\rm{P}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\\\{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\\\{{\\rm{B}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{6}}}\\\\{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}\\end{array} \\right\\}{\\rm{All}}\\,\\,{\\rm{are}}\\,\\,{\\rm{covalent}}\\,\\,{\\rm{compounds}}$<br>KCl is ionic compound.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15523,'chemistry',15,183,402,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> One mole of $\\mathrm{HCl}$ contains $6.023 \\times 10^{23}$ molecules of $\\mathrm{HCl}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $36.5 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{HCl}$ also contains $6.023 \\times 10^{23}$ molecules of $\\mathrm{HCl}$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Molecular mass of $\\mathrm{HCl}=36.5 \\mathrm{~g} /$ mole<br>According to the mole concept, one mole of any substance contains Avogadro\'s number<br>$\\left(6.023 \\times 10^{23}\\right)$ of molecules. So, in the case of $\\mathrm{HCl}, 36.5$ grams of $\\mathrm{HCl}$ also contains $6.023 \\times 10^{23}$ molecules of $\\mathrm{HCl}$. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15525,'chemistry',45,430,859,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Noble gases have large positive electron gain enthalpy.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electron has to enter the next higher principal quantum level.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Noble gases exhibit large positive electron gain enthalpies (EGE) because the addition of an electron requires it to enter the next higher principal quantum level, resulting in a highly unstable electronic configuration. So option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15526,'chemistry',46,449,905,'Which of the following is the wrong statement?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ONCl}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ON}}{{\\\\rm{O}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}$ are not isoelectronic.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$ molecule is bent.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ozone is violet-black in solid state.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ozone is paramagnetic gas.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{(1)}}\\,\\,\\,\\left. {\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}{{\\rm{ONCl = 8 + 7 + 17 = 32}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}}}\\\\{{\\rm{ON}}{{\\rm{O}}^{\\rm{ - }}}{\\rm{ = 8 + 7 + 8 + 1 = 24}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}}}\\end{array}} \\right\\}{\\rm{not}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{isoelectronic}}$<br>(2) <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_15526_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> The central atom is ${\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}$ hybridised with one lone pair.<br>(3) It is a pale blue gas. At $ - {\\rm{249}}{\\rm{.7^\\circ ,}}$  it forms violet black crystals.<br>(4) ${{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}$ is diamagnetic in nature due to absence of unpaired electrons.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15527,'chemistry',15,187,412,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Equivalent weight of $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$, in the reaction $\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is $17 / 3$ while that of $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ is $28 / 6$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Equivalent weight $=\\dfrac{\\text { Molecular weight }}{\\text { Number of } \\mathrm{e}^{-1} \\text { lost }}$ or gained per mole','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{N}_{2}+6 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{-3}$<br>$\\therefore$ Equivalent weight of<br>$\\mathrm{NH}_{3}=\\dfrac{17}{3}$<br>(Mol. wt. of $\\left.\\mathrm{NH}_{3}=17\\right)\\left(3 \\mathrm{e}^{-}\\right.$change for each nitrogen atom)<br>While, equivalent weight of $\\mathrm{N}_{2}=\\dfrac{28}{6}$<br>( $6 \\mathrm{e}^{-}$change for \' $2 \\mathrm{~N}$ \' atoms)<br>So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15529,'chemistry',45,430,857,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{Na}^{+}$is smaller in size than $\\mathrm{Na}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Na}^{+}$has same nuclear charge as that of $\\mathrm{Na}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{Na}^{+}$is smaller in size because it possesses fewer electrons (10) compared to sodium (Na) with 11 electrons, while the nuclear charge remains the same. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15530,'chemistry',46,449,905,'Four diatomic species are listed below in different sequences. The correct order of their bond orders is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}{\\\\rm{ < He}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}{\\\\rm{ < NO < O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{He}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}{\\\\rm{ < O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{ < NO < C}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{ < NO < C}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}{\\\\rm{ < He}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{NO < C}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}{\\\\rm{ < O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{ < He}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Bond orders are : ${\\rm{He}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ + }}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.5;O}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{ = 1}}{\\rm{.5;}}$<br>${\\rm{NO = 2}}{\\rm{.5;C}}_{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{ = 3}}{\\rm{.0}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15533,'chemistry',45,430,857,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{F}^{-}$ion is larger in size compared to F.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electron repulsion increases because of addition of electron which results in decrease in effective nuclear charge.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Anion is always larger than its parent atom. Thus, $\\mathrm{F}^{-}$is larger than $\\mathrm{F}$ atom. Moreover, addition of an electron to small sized $\\mathrm{F}$ atom leads to a larger electron-electron repulsions, thereby increasing the size of $\\mathrm{F}^{-}$ion. Hence option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15534,'chemistry',46,449,905,'The decreasing order of bond length for ${{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ and its ions is correctly shown in','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ > N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{ = N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}{\\\\rm{ > N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}{\\\\rm{ = N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 + }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}{\\\\rm{ = N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}{\\\\rm{ > N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{ = N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}{\\\\rm{ > N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{1 + }}}{\\\\rm{ > N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{ > N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 + }}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}{\\\\rm{ > N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ > N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}{\\\\rm{ > N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 + }}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Bond order of ${\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}$ and ${\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}$ is 2<br>Bond order of ${\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}$ and ${\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ + }}$  is 2.5<br>Bond order of ${{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ is 3.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15562,'chemistry',46,449,905,'Which one of the following properties is not shown by NO?','[{"text": "It is diamagnetic in gaseous state", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is neutral oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It combines with oxygen to form nitrogen dioxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It’s bond order is 2.5", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Nitric oxide is paramagnetic. In the gaseous state because of the presence of one unpaired electron in its outermost shell. The electronic configuration of NO is ${\\left( {{\\rm{\\sigma 1s}}} \\right)^{\\rm{2}}}{\\left( {{{\\rm{\\sigma }}^{\\rm{*}}}{\\rm{2s}}} \\right)^{\\rm{2}}}{\\left( {{{\\rm{\\sigma }}^{\\rm{*}}}{\\rm{2s}}} \\right)^{\\rm{2}}}{\\left( {{\\rm{\\sigma 2}}{{\\rm{p}}_{\\rm{z}}}} \\right)^{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {{\\rm{\\pi 2p}}_{\\rm{x}}^{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{ = \\pi 2p}}_{\\rm{y}}^{\\rm{2}}} \\right)\\left( {{{\\rm{\\pi }}^{\\rm{*}}}{\\rm{2p}}_{\\rm{x}}^{\\rm{1}}} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15537,'chemistry',45,430,859,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Electron affinity of oxygen is more negative than nitrogen but less negative than fluorine.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> N, O, F belong to same period of the periodic table.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Nitrogen has an highly stable half filled configuration of $1 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}, 2 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}, 2 \\mathrm{p}^{3}$ and addition of an electron to it is not easy. But oxygen has a greater tendency to add electron as its p orbital is incomplete $\\left(1 s^{2}, 2 s^{2}, 2 p^{4}\\right)$. Easier the addition of an electron to the atom, more negative the electron gain enthalpy. N, O, F belong to $2^{\\text {nd }}$ period of the periodic table. but there is no relation between these two statements. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15538,'chemistry',46,449,905,'The common features among the species ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{N}}^{\\rm{ - }}}{\\rm{,}}\\,\\,{\\rm{CO}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}^{\\rm{ + }}}$ are','[{"text": "bond order three and isoelectronic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "bond order three and weak field ligands", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "bond order two and ${\\\\rm{\\\\pi }}$ - acceptors", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "isoelectronic and weak field ligands", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{N}}^{\\rm{ - }}}{\\rm{,}}\\,\\,{\\rm{CO}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}^{\\rm{ + }}}$ are isoelectronic $\\left( {{\\rm{14}}\\overline {\\rm{e}} } \\right)$ and has bond order 3.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15541,'chemistry',45,430,858,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> I.E. of $_{\\text{7}}{\\text{N}}$ is more than that of, ${ }_{8} \\mathrm{O}$ as well as ${ }_{6} \\mathrm{C}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> This is due to difference in reactivity towards oxygen.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Nitrogen has half-filled stable configuration. Thus, more energy is needed to remove electron from it as compared to oxygen atom with incompletely filled valence shell. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15542,'chemistry',46,443,888,'A molecule may be represented by three structures having energies ${{\\rm{E}}_{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{,}}{{\\rm{E}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{E}}_{\\rm{3}}}$ respectively. The energies of these structures follow the order ${{\\rm{E}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ < }}{{\\rm{E}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ < }}{{\\rm{E}}_{\\rm{1}}}$ respectively. If the experimental energy of the molecule is ${{\\rm{E}}_{\\rm{0}}}$, the resonance energy is','[{"text": "$\\\\left( {{{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\rm{ + }}{{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ + }}{{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}} \\\\right){\\\\rm{ - }}{{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\rm{0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\rm{0}}}{\\\\rm{ - }}{{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\rm{0}}}{\\\\rm{ - }}{{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\rm{0}}}{\\\\rm{ - }}{{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The difference in energy of the actual molecule (experimental value) and energy of the most stable resonating structure is called resonance energy.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15545,'chemistry',45,430,860,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{NaCl}$ is ionic compound.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electronegativity difference between $\\mathrm{Na}$ and $\\mathrm{Cl}$ is greater than 1.7.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A bond is classified as ionic when the electronegativity difference between the two bonded atoms is greater than 1.7. In contrast, if the electronegativity difference is less than or equal to 1.7 , the bond is considered covalent. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15546,'chemistry',46,449,905,'Incorrect statement when ${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ change to ${\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{ - 2}}}$','[{"text": "Magnetic properties remains same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bond order increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\sigma }}$ bond decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\pi }}$ bond increases", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathop {{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits_{\\left( {{\\rm{2\\pi }}\\,\\,{\\rm{bond}}} \\right)}  \\to \\mathop {{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{ - 2}}}}\\limits_{\\left( {{\\rm{2\\pi  + 1 - bond}}} \\right)} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15641,'chemistry',15,180,394,'$\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ gas was prepared by (i) burning sulphur in oxygen, (ii) reacting sodium sulphite with dilute $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and (iii) heating copper with conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$. It was found that in each case sulphur and oxygen combined in the ratio of $1: 1$. The data illustrates the law of','[{"text": "Conservation of mass", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Multiple proportions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Constant proportions", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Reciprocal proportions", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Irrespective of method of preparation, a compound contains elements that are present in a fixed ratio by mass is law of constant proportions.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15547,'chemistry',15,187,410,'The volume of 0.02 M aqueous HBr required to neutralize 10.0 mL of 0.01 M aqueous ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ba}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}}}}$ is ____ .(Assume complete neutralization.)','[{"text": "10.0 mL", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7.5 mL", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5.0 mL", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.5 mL", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Neutralisation formula,<br>${\\mathrm{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{M}_{1} \\mathrm{~V}_{1}}{\\mathrm{n}_{1}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{M}_{2} \\mathrm{~V}_{2}}{\\mathrm{n}_{2}}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ba}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}+2 \\mathrm{HBr} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BaBr}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{M}_{1}=0.01 \\quad \\mathrm{M}_{2}=0.02}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{V}_{1}=10 \\mathrm{~mL} \\quad \\mathrm{~V}_{2}=}}$ ?<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{n}_{1}=1 \\quad \\mathrm{n}_{2}=2}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\therefore \\mathrm{V}_{2}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{M}_{1} \\mathrm{~V}_{1}}{\\mathrm{n}_{1}} \\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{n}_{2}}{\\mathrm{~m}_{2}}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{0.01 \\times 10}{1} \\times \\dfrac{2}{0.02}=10 \\mathrm{~mL}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15549,'chemistry',45,430,860,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Smaller the size of an atom greater is the electronegativity.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electronegativity refers to the tendency of atom to share electrons with other atom.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Electronegativity is inversely propotional to size. Electronegativity is a measure of an atom\'s tendency to attract bonding electrons in a bond. Hence option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15550,'chemistry',46,446,892,'Total number of lone pair of electrons in ${\\rm{I}}_{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{ - }}\\,\\,{\\rm{ions}}$ is','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "12", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Total number of lone pairs in ${\\rm{I}}_{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{ - }}$ is 9.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15553,'chemistry',45,430,858,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Phosphorus\' has higher I.E. than sulphur.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> I.E. increases from left to right in a period.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Phosphorus $(\\mathrm{P})$ exhibits a higher ionization energy (I.E.) due to its half-filled electron configuration. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15554,'chemistry',46,448,898,'The species in which the N atom is in a state of sp hybridisation is:','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{NO}}_{\\\\rm{3}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{NO}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{NO}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Hybridisation $\\left( {\\rm{H}} \\right){\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}$  [no.of valence electron of central atom + no.of Monovalent atoms attached to it + (-ve charge if any ) - ( +ve charge if any)]<br>${\\rm{NO}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ + }}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}\\left[ {{\\rm{5 + 0 + 0 - 1}}} \\right]{\\rm{ = 2}}$ i.e., sp hybridisation<br>${\\rm{NO}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}\\left[ {{\\rm{5 + 0 + 1 - 0}}} \\right]{\\rm{ = 3}}$  i.e., ${\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}$ hybridisation<br>${\\rm{NO}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}\\left[ {{\\rm{5 + 0 + 1 - 0}}} \\right]{\\rm{ = 3}}$  i.e., ${\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}$ hybridisation<br>The lewis structure of ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ shows a bent molecular geometry with trigonal planar electron pair geometry hence the hybridisation will be ${\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15571,'chemistry',15,185,404,'A metal chloride contains ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{55.0 \\%}}}$ of chlorine by weight. 100 mL vapours of the metal chloride at STP weigh 0.57 g . The molecular formula of the metal chloride is<br>(Given : Atomic mass of chlorine is 35.5 u )','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MCl}_{4}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MCl}_{3}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MCl}_{2}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "MCl", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Molecular mass of metal chloride<br>${\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{0.57}{100} \\times 22400=127.68}}$<br>Mass of chloride in metal chloride<br>${\\mathrm{=127.68 \\times \\dfrac{55}{100}=70 \\mathrm{~g}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\therefore}}$ Moles of chlorine in the given compound<br>${\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{70}{35.5}=2}}$<br>So, formula of metal chloride ${\\mathrm{=\\mathrm{MCl}_{2}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15556,'chemistry',15,183,402,'The number of molecules and moles in 2.8375 litres of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}}}$ at STP are respectively','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{7.527 \\\\times 10^{23}}}}$ and 0.125 mol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{7.527 \\\\times 10^{22}}}}$ and 0.125 mol", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{1.505 \\\\times 10^{23}}}}$ and 0.250 mol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{7.527 \\\\times 10^{22}}}}$ and 0.250 mol", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Number of moles of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$ at ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{STP}=\\dfrac{2.8375 \\mathrm{~L}}{22.4 \\mathrm{~L}}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{=0.126 \\mathrm{~mol} \\simeq 0.125 \\mathrm{~mol}}}$<br>Number of molecules of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$ at STP<br>${\\mathrm{=0.125 \\times 6.022 \\times 10^{23}=0.7527 \\times 10^{23}}}$<br>(or) ${\\mathrm{7.527 \\times 10^{22}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15557,'chemistry',45,430,857,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{CO}$ is isoelectronic with $\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$ion.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Isoelectronic ions have same number of electrons.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Both $\\mathrm{CO}$ and $\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$are isoelectric species, as they contain the same number of electrons (14). Thus option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15558,'chemistry',46,451,907,'The intermolecular interaction that is dependent on the inverse cube of distance between the molecules is:','[{"text": "London force", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydrogen bond", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ion-ion interaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ion-dipole interaction", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Hydrogen bond is a type of strong electrostatic dipole interaction and dependent on the inverse cube of distance between the molecular ion-dipole interaction and dependent on the inverse cube of distance between the molecular ion-dipole interaction $ \\propto \\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{{\\rm{r}}^{\\rm{3}}}}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15559,'chemistry',15,183,402,'Match Column I with Column II<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{P}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{P}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Molar mass of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CH}_{4}=16 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{mol}}}$<br>Electrons in a molecule of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CH}_{4}=6+(1 \\times 4)=10}}$<br>${\\text{16}}\\,{\\text{g}}$  of  ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}$ contains ${\\mathrm{=10 \\times 6.023 \\times 10^{23}}}$electrons<br>${\\mathrm{=60.2 \\times 10^{23}}}$ electrons        so, (A - Q)<br>One mole or 2 g of hydrogen gas occupies 22.4 L volume at STP .<br>${\\mathrm{\\therefore 1 \\mathrm{~g}}}$ of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}}$ gas occupies ${\\mathrm{\\dfrac{22.4}{2}=11.2 \\mathrm{~L}}}$ <br>volume at STP     (B – S)<br>1 mole of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}_{2}}}$ gas weighs 28 g . (C- P )<br>1 mol of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{2}}}$ gas weighs 64 g<br>${\\kern 1pt} \\therefore 0.5\\,\\,{\\rm{mol}}$ of gas weighs ${\\mathrm{\\dfrac{64}{2}=32 \\mathrm{~g}(\\mathrm{D}-\\mathrm{R})}}$',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/15_15559_question_1.png'),
(15561,'chemistry',45,425,846,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Transition elements exhibit variable valency.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Transition elements possess unpaired electrons.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Transition elements shows the variable valency of energy gap between ns and (n-1)d is very less. Transition elements possess unpaired electrons. Both are true but not correct explanation. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
