(15565,'chemistry',45,430,860,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Outermost electronic configuration of most electronegative element is ${\\text{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{2}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{5}}}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $2 \\mathrm{p}^{5}$ is stable due to half filled subshell.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The most electronegative element is Fluorine whose electronic configuration is $1 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}, 2 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}, 2 \\mathrm{p}^{5}$. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15566,'chemistry',46,449,905,'In which of the following pairs of molecules/ ions, both the species are not likely to exist?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}{\\\\rm{,He}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{,He}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 + }}}{\\\\rm{,H}}{{\\\\rm{e}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}{\\\\rm{,He}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 + }}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}{\\rm{ = \\sigma 1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{0}}}{{\\rm{\\sigma }}^{\\rm{*}}}{\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{0}}}$<br>Bond order for ${\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {{\\rm{0 - 0}}} \\right){\\rm{ = 0}}$<br>${\\rm{H}}{{\\rm{e}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = \\sigma 1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{\\sigma }}^{\\rm{*}}}{\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}$<br>Bond order for ${\\rm{H}}{{\\rm{e}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {{\\rm{2 - 2}}} \\right){\\rm{ = 0}}$<br>so both ${\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{H}}{{\\rm{e}}_{\\rm{2}}}$  does not exist.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15569,'chemistry',45,430,858,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}$ for $\\mathrm{Mn}^{3+} / \\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ is more positive than $\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+} / \\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The third ionisation energy of $\\mathrm{Mn}$ is larger than that of $\\mathrm{Cr}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}$ for $\\mathrm{Mn}^{+3} / \\mathrm{Mn}^{+2}$ is more $+\\mathrm{ve}$ than $\\mathrm{Cr}^{+3} / \\mathrm{Cr}^{+2}$, because of required change from $\\mathrm{d}^{5}$ (half filled) to $\\mathrm{d}^{4}$ in  $\\mathrm{Mn}$  needs higher energy. So both are correct and its a correct explanation. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15570,'chemistry',46,451,909,'Ortho-nitrophenol is less soluble in water than p- and m-nitrophenols because:','[{"text": "o-nitrophenol is more volatile steam than p-isomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "o-nitrophenol shows intramolecular H bonding", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "o-nitrophenol shows intermolecular H bonding", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Melting point of o-nitrophenol is lower than those of m- and p-isomers.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Compounds involved in chelation become non-polar.<br>Consequently such compounds are soluble in non-polar solvents like ether, benzene etc. and are only sparingly soluble in water & less soluble in non-polar solvents.<br>intramolecular H-bonding.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15573,'chemistry',45,430,860,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{F}$ is more electronegative than $\\mathrm{Cl}$. <br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{F}$ has high electron affinity than $\\mathrm{Cl}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Fluorine is more electronegative than chlorine, but has low electron affinity than chlorine. This is because addition of an electron to small sized fluorine is very difficult. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15574,'chemistry',46,449,905,'Using MO theory, predict which of the following species has the shortest bond length?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{2 + }}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Bond order<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\left( \\begin{array}{l}{\\rm{No}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{bonding}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{electrons - }}\\\\{\\rm{No}}{\\rm{.}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{anti - bonding}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{electrons}}\\end{array} \\right)}}{{\\rm{2}}}$<br>Bond order in ${\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ + }}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{10 - 5}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 2}}{\\rm{.5}}$<br>Bond order in ${\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{10 - 7}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 1}}{\\rm{.5}}$<br>Bond order in ${\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{10 - 8}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 1}}$<br>Bond order in ${\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{10 - 4}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 3}}$<br>Since Bond order $ \\propto \\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{Bond}}\\,\\,{\\rm{length}}}}$<br>$\\therefore $ Bond length is shortest in ${\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15577,'chemistry',45,430,858,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Helium has the highest value of ionisation energy among all the elements known. <br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Helium has the highest value of electron affinity among all the elements known.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Helium (He) contains a fully filled ${\\text{1}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}$  orbital, which has a more penetrating effect and is positioned very close to the nucleus. This proximity to the nucleus is the reason helium has the highest value of ionization energy among the elements. Chlorine has the highest electron affinity not helium. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
