(15656,'chemistry',15,183,402,'Assuming the human body to be $80 \\%$ of water, the number of molecules of water present in the body of a person, of mass 65 kg , are','[{"text": "$1.73 \\\\times 10^{16}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$17.39 \\\\times 10^{26}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$173.9 \\\\times 10^{28}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.739 \\\\times 10^{51}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'100 kg of human body contains 80 kg of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>65 kg of human body contains<br>$=\\dfrac{80}{100} \\times 65=52 \\mathrm{~kg}$ water<br>18 g of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ contains $6.023 \\times 10^{23}$ molecules<br> $52 \\times 10^{3} \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ contains?<br>$=\\dfrac{52 \\times 10^{3} \\times 6.023 \\times 10^{23}}{18}$<br>$=17.39 \\times 10^{26}$ molecules',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15662,'chemistry',15,183,402,'26.8 g of $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\cdot \\mathrm{nH}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ contains 12.6 g of water. The value of  ${\\rm{n}}$ is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ molar mass is $142\\,\\,{\\text{g}}.\\,{\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^ - }$<br>only $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ mass $=26.8-12.6=14.2 \\mathrm{~g}$<br>$\\therefore 14.2\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,\\,{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ has $12.6\\,\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>$142\\,\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,\\,{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{has}}?$<br>$ = \\frac{{142 \\times 12.6}}{{14.2}} = 126\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}$<br>${{\\rm{n}}_{{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}}} = \\frac{{126}}{{18}} = 7  {\\rm{mole}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15663,'chemistry',45,430,860,'Arrange the following elements in increasing order of electronegativity:<br> N, O, F, C, Si <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "Si ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ C ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ N ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ O ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ F", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Si ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ C ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ O ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ N ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ F", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "O ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ F ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ N ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ C ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ Si", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "F ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ O ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ N ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ C ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ Si", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Electronegativity decreases on moving down the group and increases across the period on moving from left to right. So, the correct order is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Si} < \\mathrm{C} < \\mathrm{N} < \\mathrm{O} < \\mathrm{F}}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15664,'chemistry',46,446,892,'The geometry with respect to the central atom of the following molecules are :<br>${\\rm{N}}{\\left( {{\\rm{Si}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{;M}}{{\\rm{e}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{N;}}{\\left( {{\\rm{Si}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{P}}$','[{"text": "planar, pyramidal, planar", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "planar, pyramidal, pyramidal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "pyramidal, pyramidal, pyramidal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "pyramidal, planar, pyramidal", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15665,'chemistry',15,186,405,'How many moles of $\\mathrm{Zn}\\left(\\mathrm{FeS}_{2}\\right)$ can be made from 2 moles of zinc, 3 moles of iron, and 5 moles of sulphur?','[{"text": "2 moles", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3 moles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4 moles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5 moles", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'1 mole of $\\mathrm{Zn}\\left(\\mathrm{FeS}_{2}\\right)$ contains: 1 mole of Zn , 1 mole of $\\mathrm{Fe}, 2$ moles of S .<br>So, for 2 moles of $\\mathrm{Zn}\\left(\\mathrm{FeS}_{2}\\right)$, we need 2 moles of $\\mathrm{Zn}, 2$ moles of Fe , and 4 moles of S .<br>So maximum 2 moles of $\\mathrm{Zn}\\left(\\mathrm{FeS}_{2}\\right)$ can be made. (1mole \'Fe\' 1mole \' S \' will be unreacted).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15666,'chemistry',45,430,858,'Arrange the following elements in increasing order of first ionization enthalpy: <br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Li}, \\mathrm{Be}, \\mathrm{B}, \\mathrm{C}, \\mathrm{N}}}$ <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Li} < \\\\mathrm{Be} < \\\\mathrm{B} < \\\\mathrm{C} < \\\\mathrm{N}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Li} < \\\\mathrm{B} < \\\\mathrm{Be} < \\\\mathrm{C} < \\\\mathrm{N}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Li} < \\\\mathrm{Be} < \\\\mathrm{C} < \\\\mathrm{B} < \\\\mathrm{N}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Li} < \\\\mathrm{Be} < \\\\mathrm{N} < \\\\mathrm{B} < \\\\mathrm{C}}}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The penetration of a 2 s -electron to the nucleus is more than that of a 2 p -electron; hence the ${\\mathrm{2 p}}$ electron of boron is more shielded from the nucleus by the inner core of electrons than the 2 s electrons of beryllium. Therefore, it is easier to remove the ${\\mathrm{2 p}}$-electron from boron compared to the removal of a 2 s - electron from beryllium. Thus, boron has a smaller first ionization enthalpy than beryllium. From left to right,i.e, increases. Hence ,the order is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Li} < \\mathrm{B} < \\mathrm{Be} < \\mathrm{C} < \\mathrm{N}}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15668,'chemistry',46,446,892,'According to VSEPR theory','[{"text": "The shape of the molecule depends upon the bonded electron pairs.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "pair of electrons attract each other in valence shells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the pairs of electrons tend to occupy such positions that minimize repulsions", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "the pairs of electrons tend to occupy such positions that minimise distance from each other.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15669,'chemistry',15,186,405,'According to following reaction,$\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{BO}_{3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{A}_{3} \\mathrm{O}_{4}+\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ the number of moles of $\\mathrm{A}_{3} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$ produced if 1 mole of A is mixed with 1 mole of $\\mathrm{BO}_{3}$ is','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$9 \\mathrm{~A}+8 \\mathrm{BO}_{3} \\rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{~A}_{3} \\mathrm{O}_{4}+4 \\mathrm{~B}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$<br>9 moles of A needs 8 moles of B<br>1 mole of A needs?<br>$=1 \\times \\dfrac{8}{9}=0.89$ mole B<br>But given \' B \' is 1 mole<br>So, \' A \' is limiting reagnet<br>9 moles of A form 3 moles of $\\mathrm{A}_{3} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$.<br>So, 1 mole of A form?<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{1 \\times 3}}{9} = \\frac{1}{3}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mole}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{A}}_{\\rm{3}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15670,'chemistry',45,421,841,'Mendeleev\'s classification was a great improvement over contemporary tabulation of the elements by Newlands and Lothar Meyer because<br>(I) it grouped the elements together more accurately in vertical columns.<br>(II) it left gaps for elements which were yet to be discovered in nature.<br>(III) it made possible the prediction of the properties of elements that had not yet been discovered.<br>(IV) it predicted the atomic masses of certain elements which were incorrectly determined.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "(I), (II), (III), and (IV)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(II), (III), and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Mendeleev arranged the elements in groups on the basis of similarities in the formulae of compounds formed by these elements. To ensure that the patterns in the properties of elements fitted the table, it was essential to leave spaces.It predicted the atomic masses of Be , In and Os which were incorrectly given. Hence, all the statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15672,'chemistry',46,446,892,'Which of the following molecules has the maximum number of A-X bonds of identical length, where ‘A” is the central atom and ‘X’ is the surrounding atom?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{S}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{6}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{I}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{7}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{P}}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{5}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ClO}}_{\\\\rm{4}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{6}}}$ = Octahedral and all six S-F bond length are same.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15673,'chemistry',15,186,405,'In the formation reaction of $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ from $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2}, 140 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $40\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,{{\\rm{H}}_2}$ were mixed.Select the option which is correct.','[{"text": "Maximum mass of $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ which can be formed is 180 g .", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "If $\\\\% $ yield of reaction is 80 , then $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ consumed will be 32 g .", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Some $\\\\mathrm{N}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g})$ will be left after the reaction.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "If $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ formed is 85 g then $\\\\% $ yield will be $50 \\\\%$.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\underset{28 \\mathrm{~g}}{\\mathrm{~N}_{2}}+\\underset{6 \\mathrm{~g}}{3 \\mathrm{H}_{2}} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$<br>$28 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~N}_{2}$ needs $6\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,{{\\rm{H}}_2}$<br>$140\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,{{\\rm{N}}_2}$ needs ?<br>$ = \\frac{{146 \\times 6}}{{28}} = 30{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{g}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>But given is $40{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{g}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>So, ${{\\text{N}}_2}$ is limiting reagent.<br>$28\\,\\,{\\text{g}}\\,\\,{{\\text{N}}_2}$ gives $2 \\times 17\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{g}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_3}$<br>$140\\,\\,{\\text{g}}\\,\\,{{\\text{N}}_2}$ gives ?<br>$ = \\frac{{140 \\times 2 \\times 17}}{{28}} = 170\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_3}$ is expected<br>If 85 gram ${\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_3}$ is actually formed.<br>$\\% {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{yield}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}  = \\frac{{85}}{{170}} \\times 100 = \\frac{{100}}{2} = 50\\% $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15674,'chemistry',45,422,842,'Which of the following groups of periodic table contains the maximum number of elements with different valence shell electron configuration?','[{"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Group 10 elements are ${\\text{Ni}},{\\text{Pd}}$ & ${\\text{Pt}}{\\text{.}}$<br>$\\operatorname{Nickel}\\left(3 \\mathrm{~d}^{8} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}\\right)$,palladium $\\left(4 \\mathrm{~d}^{10}\\right)$, platinum $\\left(5 \\mathrm{~d}^{9} 6 \\mathrm{~s}^{1}\\right)$. These elements have different electrons in valence shell.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15684,'chemistry',46,444,890,'Which bond angle, ${\\rm{\\theta }}$ would result in the maximum dipole moment for the triatomic molecule ${\\rm{X}}{{\\rm{Y}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ shown below:','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\theta  = 90^\\\\circ }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\theta  = 120^\\\\circ }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\theta  = 150^\\\\circ }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\theta  = 180^\\\\circ }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{\\mu  = }}\\sqrt {{\\rm{\\mu }}_{\\rm{1}}^{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{ + \\mu }}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{\\mu }}_{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{\\mu }}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{cos}}\\,{\\rm{\\theta }}} $<br>If ${\\rm{\\theta  = 90^\\circ ,}}\\,\\,{\\rm{\\mu }}$ is maximum.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/46_15684_question_1.png'),
(15676,'chemistry',46,445,891,'The correct decreasing order of polarisability of ions is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}}{{\\\\rm{r}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{{\\\\rm{I}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{{\\\\rm{F}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{F}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{{\\\\rm{I}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}}{{\\\\rm{r}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{F}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}}{{\\\\rm{r}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{{\\\\rm{I}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{I}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}}{{\\\\rm{r}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\,\\\\,{{\\\\rm{F}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Larger the size of anion the more is the polarisability.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15677,'chemistry',15,181,399,'Read the Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct option given below.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Atoms can neither be created nor destroyed.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Under similar condition of temperature and pressure, equal volumes of gases does not contain equal number of molecules.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of  Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of  Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Dalton\'s atomic theory, atoms can neither be created nor destroyed and according to Berzelius hypothesis, under similar condition of temperature and pressure, equal volumes of all gases contain equal no. of molecules. Therefore, assertion is true but reason is false.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15678,'chemistry',45,422,842,'Each period in the periodic table starts with a subshell of the new shell and ends with','[{"text": "same subshell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "different subshell of the same shell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the subshell of next higher shell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "p -subshell of the same new shell", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All periods end with noble gases and their general electron configuration is ${\\text{n}}{{\\text{s}}^2}{\\text{n}}{{\\text{p}}^6}.$ (except He). So, last $\\mathrm{e}^{-}$is into \' p \' subshell of the same new shell.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15680,'chemistry',46,444,890,'The variation of dipole moment $\\left( {\\rm{\\mu }} \\right)$  and angle $\\left( {\\rm{\\theta }} \\right)$ change with a change in hybridisation from<br>${\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{3}}} \\to {\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}} \\to {\\rm{sp}}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\mu }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{increase,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{\\\\theta }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{decrease}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\mu }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{decrease,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{\\\\theta }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{increase}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\mu }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{\\\\theta }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{both}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{decrease}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\mu }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{\\\\theta }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{both}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{increase}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{3}}}\\left( {{\\rm{109^\\circ 28\'}}} \\right) \\to {\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {{\\rm{120^\\circ }}} \\right) \\to {\\rm{sp}}\\left( {{\\rm{180^\\circ }}} \\right){\\rm{.}}$<br>The magnitude of angle is increasing and hence dipole moment will go on decreasing.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15681,'chemistry',15,180,393,'Read the Statement -A and Statement -B carefully to mark the correct options given below :<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Law of conservation of mass hold good for nuclear reactions.</div><div class="column-right">This law states that mass can neither be created nor be destroyed in a chemical reaction.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Law of conservation of mass is not obeyed by nuclear reactions. And Statement \'B\'  is true.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15682,'chemistry',45,430,856,'Select correct statement about radius of an atom.','[{"text": "Values of vander Waals\' radii are larger than those of covalent radii because the van der Waals\' forces are much weaker than the forces operating between atoms in a covalently bonded molecule.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The metallic radii are smaller than the vander Waals\' radii, since the bonding forces in the metallic crystal lattice are much stronger than the van der Waals\' forces.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None is correct", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Radius of an atom follows the following order: vander Waals\' radii $>$ Metallic radii $>$ Covalent radii<br>So, both the statements are correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15685,'chemistry',15,187,412,'Match Column I with Column II and choose the correct combination from the given options given.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P,Q,R,B}} - {\\\\rm{Q,C}} - {\\\\rm{Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{P,Q,R}}\\\\qquad $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q,B}} - {\\\\rm{P,Q,R,C}} - {\\\\rm{P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{PQ,R}}\\\\qquad $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P,Q,R,B}} - {\\\\rm{R,C}} - {\\\\rm{P,Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}\\\\qquad $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q,B}} - {\\\\rm{P,C}} - {\\\\rm{P,Q,R,D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'(A) Molarity & (D) normality depend on temperature, dilution, and volume i.e.,A-P,Q,R;D-P,Q,R<br>(B) Molality & (C) mole dfraction depend only on dilution i.e.,B-Q;C-Q. So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/15_15685_question_1.png'),
(15686,'chemistry',45,430,858,'A large difference between the fourth and fifth ionization energies indicates the presence of','[{"text": "4 valence electrons in an atom", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5 valence electrons in an atom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 valence electrons in an atom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8 valence electrons in an atom", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'After four electrons, ion gets stability so it has great $\\mathrm{IP}_{5}$. It means it has 4 valence electrons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15687,'chemistry',46,444,890,'The electronegativity difference between N and F is greater than N and H, yet the dipole moment of ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\left( {{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.5}}\\,{\\rm{D}}} \\right)$ is greater than that of $N{F_3}\\left( {0.2\\,D} \\right)$ This is because','[{"text": "In ${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$ as well as $N{F_3}$, the atomic dipole and bond dipole are in opposite direction.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In ${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$, the atomic dipole and bond dipole are in the opposite direction, where as in $N{F_3}$ these are in same direction.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In ${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$ as well as $N{F_3}$ the atomic dipole and bond dipole are in same direction.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In ${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$, the atomic dipole and bond dipole are in same direction where as in $N{F_3}$ these are in opposite direction.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_15687_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15688,'chemistry',15,186,405,'Match Column I with Column II and choose the correct combination from the given options given.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} \\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} {\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} \\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} \\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} \\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} {\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} \\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{P,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} {\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{R,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$(1)\\mathop {2{{\\rm{H}}_2}}\\limits_{1.0{\\rm{g}}}  + \\mathop {{{\\rm{O}}_2}}\\limits_{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.0g}}}  \\to 2{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}{\\rm{mol}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{{\\,32}}\\,{\\rm{mol}}$<br>${{\\text{O}}_2}$ is the limiting reagent :<br>$1\\,{\\text{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\text{O}}_2} = 2\\,{\\text{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{O}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{32}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\text{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\text{O}}_2} \\equiv \\frac{1}{{16}}\\,{\\text{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{O}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{18}}{{16}}\\,{\\text{g}}\\,\\,{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{O}} = 1.125\\,{\\text{g}}\\,\\,{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{O}}$<br>$(2)\\mathop {{{\\rm{N}}_2}}\\limits_{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.0g}}}  + \\mathop {3{{\\rm{H}}_2}}\\limits_{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.0}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}}  \\to 2{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_3}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{{28}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mol}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,{\\rm{mol}}$<br>${{\\text{N}}_2}$ is the limiting reagent.<br>$\\therefore \\frac{1}{{28}}\\,{\\rm{mol}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\equiv \\frac{1}{{14}}\\,{\\rm{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} $<br>${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}} \\equiv \\frac{1}{{14}} \\times 17\\,{\\rm{g}} = 1.214{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{g}}$<br>$(3)\\,\\,\\mathop {{\\text{CaC}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{1}}{\\text{.0}}\\,\\,{\\text{g}}} \\xrightarrow{\\Delta }\\mathop {{\\text{CaO}}}\\limits_{\\text{?}}  + {\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}$<br>$\\frac{1}{{100}}\\,\\,\\,{\\text{mol}}\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{CaC}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}$<br>$\\frac{1}{{100}} \\times 56\\,\\,{\\text{g}}\\,\\,{\\text{CaO}} = 0.56\\,\\,{\\text{g}}\\,\\,{\\text{CaO}}$<br>$(4){\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits_{1.0{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}}  + \\mathop {{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits_{1.0{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}}  \\to {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{{12}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\frac{1}{2}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mol}}$<br>C is the limiting reagent.<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\frac{1}{{12}}\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{mol}}\\,\\,{\\text{C}} \\equiv \\frac{1}{{12}}\\,\\,{\\text{mol}}$<br>$\\,\\,{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}} \\equiv \\frac{{16}}{{12}}\\,\\,\\,{\\text{g}} = 1.333\\,\\,{\\text{g}}\\,\\,{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}$',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/15_15688_question_1.png'),
(15689,'chemistry',45,430,861,'X, Y, and Z are Mulliken electronegativity, ionization potential and electron-affinity, respectively, then the electron affinity $(\\mathrm{z})$ in the terms of electronegativity ( x ) and ionization potential (y) will be','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{z}} = \\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{x}} + {\\\\rm{y}}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{z}} = \\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{x}} - {\\\\rm{y}}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{z}} = \\\\frac{{{{\\\\rm{x}}^2} - {{\\\\rm{y}}^2}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{z}} = 2{\\\\rm{ x}} - {\\\\rm{y}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to Mulliken\'s scale,<br>$\\mathrm{EN}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{IP}_{1}+\\mathrm{EA}}{2}$<br>So, ${\\text{x}} = \\frac{{{\\text{y}} + {\\text{z}}}}{2}$<br>${\\text{Hence}},\\,\\,{\\text{z}} = 2{\\text{x}} - {\\text{y}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15691,'chemistry',46,444,890,'A diatomic molecule has a dipole moment of 1.2 D. If its bond distance is ${\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.0}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{A^\\circ }}$, what fraction of an electronic charge exists on each atom?','[{"text": "0.85", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.65", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.45", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.25", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For a diatomic molecule dipole moment ${\\rm{\\mu  = e \\times d}}$<br>$\\therefore $ electronic charge ${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{\\mu }}}{{\\rm{d}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.2 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 18}}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{esu}}\\,\\,{\\rm{cm}}}}{{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.0 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 8}}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{cm}}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\,\\,{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.2 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 10}}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{esu}}$<br>Actual value of electronic charge<br>${\\rm{ = 4}}{\\rm{.8 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 10}}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{esu}}{\\rm{.}}$<br>$\\therefore $ Fraction of the electronic charge<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.2 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 10}}}}}}{{{\\rm{4}}{\\rm{.8 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 10}}}}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.25}}{\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15692,'chemistry',15,187,412,'Match Column I with Column II and choose the correct combination from the given options given.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{R} ; \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{S} ; \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S} ; \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P;   B - S;   C - P, Q, R;   D - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P, S;  B - P, Q, S;   C - P, R;   D - R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{S}$; B - P,R; $\\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}$; $\\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>(1) $\\mathrm{M}=\\dfrac{9.8 \\times 10 \\times 1.8}{98}=1.8 ; \\mathrm{N}=1.8 \\times 2=3.6$<br>$\\mathrm{d}=\\mathrm{M}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{Mw}_{2}}{1000}+\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{~m}}\\right)$<br>$1.8 = 1.8\\,\\,\\left( {\\frac{{98}}{{1000}} + \\frac{1}{{\\text{m}}}} \\right) \\Rightarrow {\\text{m}} = 1.10$<br>$(2)\\,\\,{\\text{M}} = \\frac{{9.8 \\times 10 \\times 1.2}}{{98}} = 1.2{\\text{M}}$<br>${\\text{N}} = 1.2 \\times 3 = 3.6\\,N$<br>${\\text{d}} = {\\text{M}}\\left( {\\frac{{{\\text{M}}{{\\text{w}}_2}}}{{1000}} + \\frac{1}{{\\,{\\text{m}}}}} \\right)$<br>$1.8 = 1.8\\left( {\\frac{{98}}{{1000}} + \\frac{1}{{\\,{\\text{m}}}}} \\right) \\Rightarrow {\\text{m}} = 1.10$<br>$(3)\\,\\,1.8\\,{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{A}}}{\\text{molecules}} = 1.8\\,$ mol of HCl in 500mL = 1.8 Eq.<br>$\\mathrm{M}=\\dfrac{1.8 \\times 1000}{500}=3.6 \\mathrm{M}=3.6 \\mathrm{~N}(\\mathrm{n}$ factor $=1)$<br>(4) mEq of base $=(250 \\times 4)+(250 \\times 1.6 \\times 2)$<br>$ = 1000 + 800 = 1800\\,{\\text{m}}\\,\\,{\\text{Eq}} = 1.8\\,\\,{\\text{Eq}}$',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/15_15692_question_1.png'),
(15693,'chemistry',45,430,860,'If electronegativity of X is 3.2 and that of Y is 2.2 , then the percentage ionic character of XY is','[{"text": "19.5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "18.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "29.5", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\%$ ionic character of bond<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =16(\\triangle \\mathrm{EN})+3.5(\\Delta \\mathrm{EN})^{2} \\\\& =16(1)+3.5(1)^{2} \\\\& =19.5 \\%\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15694,'chemistry',46,444,890,'Bond angle in ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{is}}\\,\\,{\\rm{97^\\circ }}{\\rm{.}}$ The S - H bond moment is 0.72 D dipole moment of ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}$ molecule is $\\left( {{\\rm{Cos}}\\,\\,{\\rm{48}}{\\rm{.5^\\circ  = 0}}{\\rm{.662}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "0.95 D", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.72 D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.66 D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.36 D", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{Bond}}\\,\\,{\\rm{moment = 2 \\times \\mu  \\times cos}}\\frac{{\\rm{\\theta }}}{{\\rm{2}}}$<br>${\\rm{2 \\times 0}}{\\rm{.72 \\times cos}}\\left( {\\frac{{{\\rm{97^\\circ }}}}{{\\rm{2}}}} \\right){\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.95}}\\,{\\rm{D}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15695,'chemistry',15,183,402,'The volume of a gas at $0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ and 760 mm pressure is 22.4 cc . The no. of molecules present in this volume is','[{"text": "$10^{-3} \\\\mathrm{~N}_{\\\\mathrm{A}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10^{-4} \\\\mathrm{~N}_{\\\\mathrm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10^{-5} \\\\mathrm{~N}_{\\\\mathrm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10^{-2} \\\\mathrm{~N}_{\\\\mathrm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{P}=760 \\mathrm{~mm}=1 \\mathrm{~atm}$ $\\mathrm{T}=0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}=273 \\mathrm{~K}$,<br> Given are NTP. conditions<br>So, At NTP condition no.of moles.<br>${\\rm{n}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{ Vol}}{\\rm{. of gas at NTP }}}}{{22.4\\,{\\rm{L}}}}$<br>${\\rm{n}} = \\frac{{22.4 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{22.4}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {10^{ - 3}}{\\rm{moles}}$<br>No. of molecules $=$ no. of moles $\\times \\mathrm{N}_{\\mathrm{A}}$<br>$\\therefore$ No.of molecules $=10^{-3} \\mathrm{~N}_{\\mathrm{A}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15696,'chemistry',45,430,860,'Which bond is expected to be the least polar?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}-\\\\mathrm{F}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{P}-\\\\mathrm{F}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Si}-\\\\mathrm{N}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Polarity $\\propto \\triangle \\mathrm{EN}$.<br>Since $\\Delta \\mathrm{EN}$ is very low for $\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{F}$ bond polarity will be very less.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15698,'chemistry',46,441,883,'In ${\\rm{PO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{3 - }}}{\\rm{,}}$ the formal charge on each oxygen atom and the P-O bond order respectively are','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 0}}{\\\\rm{.75,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.6}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 0}}{\\\\rm{.75,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 0}}{\\\\rm{.75,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.25}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 3,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.25}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{PO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{3 - }}}$ ion has 4 resonating structures. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_15698_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> In the resonance hybrid structure of ${\\rm{PO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}$, total of $\\left( {{\\rm{ - 3}}} \\right)$ charge is distributed over 4 oxygen atoms. So, the formal charge of each oxygen atom is ${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}{{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.}}$<br>${\\rm{B}}{\\rm{.O}}{\\rm{. = }}\\frac{{\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}{\\rm{No}}{\\rm{.}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{bonds}}\\,\\,{\\rm{between}}\\,\\,{\\rm{any}}\\,\\,{\\rm{two}}\\\\{\\rm{atoms}}\\,\\,{\\rm{in}}\\,\\,{\\rm{all}}\\,\\,{\\rm{resonating}}\\,\\,{\\rm{structures}}\\end{array} \\right]}}{{\\rm{4}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{5}}}{{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{ = 1}}{\\rm{.25}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15699,'chemistry',15,186,405,'$100\\,{\\rm{mL}}$ of ${\\rm{P}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$ when completely decomposed produced phosphorus and hydrogen. The change in volume of the gas is $\\mathrm{PH}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{s})+3 / 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$','[{"text": "50 mL increase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "500 mL decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "900 mL decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Does not change", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{PH}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{s})+\\dfrac{3}{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$<br>1 mole gas changes into $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ moles<br>So, 100 mL will convert to$\\Rightarrow 100 \\times 3=150 \\mathrm{~mL}$<br>$\\therefore$ Change in volume $=150-100=50 \\mathrm{~mL}$ increase.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
