(15811,'chemistry',15,183,402,'The weight of $112 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of oxygen at NTP is','[{"text": "$0.64 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.96 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.32 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.16 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'At NTP/STP<br>${\\text{n}} = \\frac{{\\text{V}}}{{22400(\\,{\\text{1mL}})}} = \\frac{{\\text{w}}}{{\\text{M}}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{w}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{V}} \\times {\\rm{M}}}}{{22,400}}$<br>Given,${\\rm{V}} = 112\\,{\\rm{mL}}$ and molar mass of oxygen$ = 32\\,{\\rm{g}}/{\\rm{mol}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {\\rm{w}} = \\frac{{112 \\times 32}}{{22400\\,{\\rm{mL}}}} \\Rightarrow {\\rm{w}} = 0.16\\,{\\rm{g}}$<br>Hence, weight  of $112\\,{\\rm{mL}}\\,$of oxygen at${\\rm{NTP}}/{\\rm{STP}} = 0.16\\,{\\rm{g}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19074,'chemistry',53,989,2312,'Aluminium deposited as vapours on glass forms a good mirror, essentially because:','[{"text": "It has better shine than $$\\\\mathrm{Ag}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It does not scratch", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Coating is much smoother", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It does not tarnish in air<br>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15812,'chemistry',45,430,860,'The electronegativity of these following elements increases in the order','[{"text": "C, N, Si, P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, Si, C, P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Si}, \\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}, \\\\mathrm{N}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Si}, \\\\mathrm{N}, \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Si and P are in the 3rd period while C and N are in the 2nd period. Elements in 2nd period have higher electronegativities than those in the 3rd period. Since N has smaller size and has higher nuclear charge than C, its electronegativity is higher than that of C. Similarly, the electronegativity of P is higher<br> than that of Si. Thus, the overall order is :<br> Si, P, C, N.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15814,'chemistry',46,446,892,'In ${\\rm{NO}}_{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{ - }}$ ion, the number of bond pairs and lone pairs of electrons on nitrogen atom are','[{"text": "2, 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3, 1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1, 3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4, 0", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Number of bond pairs = 4<br>Number of lone pairs = (6 - 6)/2 = 0',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15815,'chemistry',15,183,402,'For $14 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{CO}$, the wrong statement is','[{"text": "it occupies $2.24 \\\\mathrm{~L}$ at NTP", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "it corresponds to $1 / 2$ mole of $\\\\mathrm{CO}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "it corresponds to same mole of $\\\\mathrm{CO}$ and nitrogen gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "it corresponds to $3.01 \\\\times 10^{23}$ molecules of $\\\\mathrm{CO}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'(1) 28 g of CO occupies<br>$ = 22.4\\,\\,{\\rm{L}}$\\,of CO at NTP<br>$\\therefore $ 14 g of CO occupies<br>$ = \\frac{{22.4}}{{28}} \\times 14\\,{\\rm{L}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{CO}}\\,{\\rm{at}}\\,{\\rm{NTP}}$<br>$ = 11.2\\,{\\rm{L}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{CO}}\\,{\\rm{at}}\\,{\\rm{NTP}}$<br>Thus 14 g of CO occupies 11.2L volume at NTP<br>(2) Number of moles of CO <br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{mass}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{CO}}}}{{{\\rm{molar}}\\,{\\rm{mass}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{CO}}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{14}}\\,{\\rm{g}}}}{{{\\rm{28}}\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}$ mole of CO<br>Thus 14 g CO equal to $\\frac{1}{2}$ mole of CO.<br>(3)As the molar mass of CO and ${{\\rm{N}}_2}$ gas are the same ,thus 14g of CO corresponds to same moles of  ${{\\text{N}}_2}$ gas.<br>(4) 28 g of CO contains<br>$ = 6.022 \\times {10^{23}}{\\rm{molecules}}$<br>$\\therefore $ 14 g of CO contains<br>$ = \\frac{{6.022 \\times {{10}^{23}}}}{{28}} \\times 14$<br>$ = 3.011 \\times {10^{23}}{\\rm{molecules}}$<br>Thus,14 g of CO corresponds to $3.011 \\times {10^{23}}{\\rm{molecules}}$.<br>Hence, option (1) is wrong, while rest of the options are correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(15816,'chemistry',45,430,857,'Which has the smallest size?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{P}^{5+}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For cationic species, atomic size decreases with increase in charge. (or) For isoelectric species, ionic radius is inversly propotional to atomic number.$\\therefore $ Order of radius :${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}^{\\rm{ + }}} > {\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{g}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}} > {\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}} > {{\\rm{P}}^{{\\rm{5 + }}}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
