(16387,'chemistry',48,751,1617,'What will be the work done on an ideal gas enclosed in a cylinder, when it is compressed by a constant external pressure, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{ext}}}$ in a single step as shown in fig?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Cylinder, which contains one mole of an ideal gas fitted with a frictionless piston. Total volume of the gas $\\mathrm{V_{i}}$ and pressure of the gas inside is $\\mathrm{p}$. If external pressure is $\\mathrm{p_{\\text {ext }}}$ which is greater than $\\mathrm{p}$, piston is moved inward till the pressure inside becomes equal to $\\mathrm{p_{\\text {ext }}}$. This change is achieved in a single step and the final volume be $\\mathrm{V_{f}}$. During this compression, suppose piston moves a distance and its cross-sectional area of the piston is $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A}}$. Then<br>Volume change $\\mathrm{=l \\times A=\\Delta V=V_{f}-V_{i}}$<br>Pressure $\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{\\text { force }}{\\text { area }}}$<br>Force on the piston $\\mathrm{=p . A}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{W}}$ is the work done on the system by movement of the piston then<br>$\\rm{ W=\\text { Force } \\times \\text { distance }=p_{\\text {ext }} \\text {.A.l } \\\\ =p_{\\text {ext }}(-\\Delta V)=-p_{\\text {ext }}\\left(V_{f}-V_{i}\\right)}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/48_16387_question_1.png'),
(16388,'chemistry',49,618,1246,'A mixture of 2 moles of $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and 8 moles of $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ are heated in a 2 lit vessel, till equilibrium is established. At equilibrium, 0.4 moles of $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ was present. The equilibrium concentration of $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ will be','[{"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{~mol} / \\\\mathrm{lit}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\mathrm{~mol} / \\\\mathrm{lit}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\mathrm{~mol} / \\\\mathrm{lit}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1 \\\\mathrm{~mol} / \\\\mathrm{lit}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm {\\begin{array}{lllc} & \\mathrm{N}_{2} & +3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} & \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3} \\\\ \\text { Initial (mol/lit) } & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ \\text { At Equilibrium } & \\text {1-x} & \\text {4-3 x} & \\text {2 x}\\end{array}}$<br>But, at equilibrium $\\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]$=0.2${\\text{mol/lit}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\text {1-x}=0.2 \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{lit}}$<br>${\\left[ {{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]_{{\\text{Equilibrium }}}}$=${\\text{43}}\\,\\,{\\text{x = 1}}{\\text{.6 mol/lit}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{x}=0.8 \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16389,'chemistry',46,441,883,'What is the formal charge on \' $\\mathrm{N}$ \' atom in $\\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ ion?','[{"text": "+1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "-3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "-1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ion $\\Rightarrow\\left[\\begin{array}{c}\\mathrm{H} \\\\ \\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{N}-\\mathrm{N}-\\mathrm{H} \\\\ \\mathrm{H}\\end{array}\\right]^{+}$<br>$\\mathrm{FC}=\\mathrm{VE}-\\mathrm{NE}-\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{BE}}{2}\\right)$<br>$\\therefore$ Formal charge of \' $\\mathrm{N}$ \' atom<br>$=5-0-\\left(\\dfrac{8}{2}\\right)=+1$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16391,'chemistry',48,748,1605,'Although heat is a path function but heat absorbed by the system under certain specific conditions is independent of path. The conditions are','[{"text": "High Temperature, High Pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Constant Temperature, Constant Pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low Temperature, Low Pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Constant Volume, Constant Pressure", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'By first law of thermodynamics:<br>$\\mathrm{q=\\Delta U+(-w)}$<br>$\\mathrm{(-w)=p \\Delta V}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore q=\\Delta U+p \\Delta V}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\Delta V=0}$, since volume is constant.<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore q_{v}=\\Delta U+0}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\Rightarrow q_{v}=\\Delta U=}$ change in internal energy<br>At constant pressure<br>$\\mathrm{q_{p}=\\Delta U+p \\Delta V}$<br>But, $\\mathrm{\\Delta U+p \\Delta V=\\Delta H}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore q_{p}=\\Delta H=}$ change in enthalpy<br>So, at constant volume and at constant pressure heat change is a state function because it is equal to change in internal energy and change in enthalpy respectively which are state functions.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16392,'chemistry',49,617,1241,'The compounds${\\text{ A and B}}$ are mixed in equimolar proportion to form the products, $\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{C}+\\mathrm{D}$. At equilibrium, one third of $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{B}$ are consumed. The equilibrium constant for the reaction is','[{"text": "0.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.25", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\rm {\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {}&{\\text{A}}&{\\text{ + }}&{\\text{B}}& \\rightleftharpoons &{\\text{C}}&{\\text{ + }}&{\\text{D}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{Intial}}\\,}&{\\text{1}}&{}&{\\text{1}}&{}&{\\text{0}}&{}&{\\text{0}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{Equilibrium}}\\,}&{{\\text{1 - }}\\dfrac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{3}}}}&{}&{{\\text{1 - }}\\dfrac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{3}}}}&{}&{\\dfrac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{3}}}}&{}&{\\dfrac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{3}}}} \n\\end{array}}$<br>$\\mathrm {K_{C}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3}}{\\dfrac{2}{3} \\times \\dfrac{2}{3}}=\\dfrac{1}{4}=0.25}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
