(16765,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The oxidation number of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}}$ in is $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NOClO}_{4}}$ is','[{"text": "+11", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+7", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "+5", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The compound may be written as $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NO}^{+} \\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}}$<br>For $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}}$, Let Ox. No.of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}=a}$<br>${\\rm{a + 4x( - 2) =  - 1}}$<br>${\\rm{a =  + 7}}$<br>Hence, the oxidation no. of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}}$ in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NOClO}_{4}}$ is +7 .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16767,'chemistry',48,758,1636,'A cooking gas cylinder is assumed to contain $\\mathrm{11.2 \\mathrm{~kg}}$ is obutane. The combustion of isobutane is given by<br>${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{4}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{{\\rm{10}}}}({\\rm{g}}){\\rm{ + }}\\left( {\\frac{{{\\rm{13}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}} \\right){{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}({\\rm{g}}) \\to {\\rm{4C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}({\\rm{g}}){\\rm{ + 5}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}({\\rm{l}});$<br>$\\Delta {\\rm{H =  - 2658}}\\,{\\rm{kJ}}$<br>If a family needs $\\mathrm{15000 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$ of energy per day for cooking, how long would the cylinder last?','[{"text": "22 days", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "28 days", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "32 days", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "34 days", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{\\because 58 \\mathrm{~g}}$ isobutane provides energy $\\mathrm{=2658}$ $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{kJ}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore \\quad 11.2 \\times 10^{3} \\mathrm{~g}}$ isobutane provides energy<br>$\\rm{=\\dfrac{2658 \\times 11.2 \\times 10^{3}}{58} k J=513268.9 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$<br>The daily requirement of energy $\\mathrm{=15000 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ Cylinder will last in $\\mathrm{\\dfrac{513268.9}{15000}=34}$ days',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16769,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'When $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}$ is converted into ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{Cr}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ the change in oxidation number of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}}$ is','[{"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{-2}}$ is converted into $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{-2}}$ the change in oxidation number of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}}$ is 3 <br>${\\rm{In}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{7}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{:oxidation}}\\,{\\rm{state}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{Cr}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{is}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{ + 6}}$<br>${\\rm{In}}\\,{\\rm{CrO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{:}}\\,{\\rm{oxidation}}\\,{\\rm{state}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{Cr}}\\,{\\rm{is}}\\,{\\rm{ + 3}}$<br>Thus when ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_2}{{\\rm{O}}_7}$ is converted to ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{Cr}}{{\\rm{O}}_4}$, oxidation number changes by 3.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16770,'chemistry',46,449,905,'The bond order and magnetic property of acetylide ion are same as that of','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_2^ + $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{O}}^ + }$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_2^ - $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}_2^ + $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{C}}_2^{ - 2} - 14{{\\rm{e}}^ - } - {\\rm{B}}.{\\rm{O}} = 3$; Diamagnetic<br>${\\rm{N}}_2^ +  - 13{{\\rm{e}}^ - } - $ B.O. $=2.5$; Paramagnetic<br>$\\mathrm{NO}^{+}-14 \\mathrm{e}^{-}-$B.O. $=3$; Diamagnetic<br>${\\rm{O}}_2^ -  - 17{{\\rm{e}}^ - }$ – B.O. $=1.5$; Paramagnetic<br>$\\mathrm{O}_{2}^{+}-15 \\mathrm{e}^{-}-$B.O. $=2.5$; Paramagnetic<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16771,'chemistry',48,758,1636,'From the following data of $\\mathrm{\\Delta H}$ of the following reactions,<br>${\\rm{C}}({\\rm{s}}){\\rm{ + }}{\\raise0.5ex\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle 1$}\\kern-0.1em/\\kern-0.15em\\lower0.25ex\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle 2$}}\\,{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}({\\rm{g}}) \\to {\\rm{CO}}({\\rm{g}});\\Delta {\\rm{H =  - 110kJ}}$ and<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}(\\mathrm{s})+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{g}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CO}(\\mathrm{g})+\\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=132 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$<br>calculate the mole composition of the mixture of oxygen and steam on being passed over coke at $\\mathrm{1273 \\mathrm{~K}}$, keeping the reaction temperature constant.','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{0.3: 1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2.1: 1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{0.6: 1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{1: 1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From the question we see that the first reaction is exothermic and the second one is endothermic. Thus, if a mixture of oxygen and steam $\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)}$ is passed over coke and at the same time, temperature does not change, the composition should be such that $\\mathrm{\\Delta H}$ of both the reactions are numerically equal.<br>In the first reaction, consumption of $\\mathrm{1 / 2}$ mole of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ evolves $\\mathrm{110 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$ energy while in the second reaction, for 1 mole of steam $\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right), 132 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$ of energy is absorbed.<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ Mole of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ needed to evolve<br>$\\mathrm{132 \\mathrm{~kJ}=\\dfrac{0.5}{110} \\times 132=0.6}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ Mole ratio of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ and steam $\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)=0.6: 1}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16772,'chemistry',49,626,1271,'The concentration of hydrogen ion in a sample of soft drink is $3.8 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}$. What is its $\\mathrm{pH}$ ? $(\\log 3.8=0.58)$','[{"text": "3.58", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.58", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.42", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3.42", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}=-\\log \\left[3.8 \\times 10^{-3}\\right]}$<br>$\\mathrm {=-\\log [3.8]-\\log \\left[10^{-3}\\right]}$<br>$\\mathrm {=-(0.58)-(-3.0)=-(-2.42)=2.42}$<br>Therefore, the $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}}$ of the soft drink is 2.42 and it can be inferred that it is acidic.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16773,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Oxidation number of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}}$ in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HNC}}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{1+(-3)+a=0}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore \\quad a=+2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16774,'chemistry',46,449,905,'In which of the following processes, the bond order increases and paramagnetic character changes to diamagnetic one?','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{O}}_2} \\\\to {\\\\rm{O}}_2^ + $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{NO}} \\\\to {\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{O}}^ + }$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{O}}_2} \\\\to {\\\\rm{O}}_2^{2 - }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{N}}_2} \\\\to {\\\\rm{N}}_2^ + $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{O}_{2}-16 \\mathrm{e}^{-}-$ B.O. $=2$, Paramagnetic<br>$\\mathrm{O}_{2}^{+}-15 \\mathrm{e}^{-}-$  B.O. $=2.5$, Paramagnetic<br>$\\mathrm{O}_{2}^{2-}-18 \\mathrm{e}^{-}-$ B.O. $=1$, Diamagnetic<br>NO $-15 \\mathrm{e}^{-}-$ B.O. $=2.5$, Paramagnetic<br>$\\mathrm{NO}^{+}-14 \\mathrm{e}^{-}-$ B.O. $=3$, Diamagnetic<br>$\\mathrm{N}_{2}-14 \\mathrm{e}^{-}-$ B.O. $=3$, Diamagnetic<br>$\\mathrm{N}_{2}^{+}-13 \\mathrm{e}^{-}-$ B.O. $=2.5$, Paramagnetic<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16775,'chemistry',48,758,1635,'Enthalpies of formation of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CO}(\\mathrm{g}), \\mathrm{CO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})}$, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{g})}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}(\\mathrm{~g})}$ are $\\mathrm{-110,-393,81}$ and ${\\rm{9}}{\\rm{.7}}\\,{\\rm{k}}\\,{\\rm{J}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$ respectively. The value of $\\mathrm{\\Delta_{\\mathrm{r}} \\mathrm{H}}$ for the reaction is<br>$\\rm{\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4(\\mathrm{~g})}+3 \\mathrm{CO}_{(\\mathrm{g})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(\\mathrm{g})}+3 \\mathrm{CO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{778 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-778 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{678 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-678 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Heat of reaction<br>$\\rm{\\Delta_{r} H^{\\circ}=\\sum \\Delta_{r} H_{\\text {products }}^{o}-\\sum \\Delta_{r} H_{\\text {reactants }}^{o}}$<br>$\\mathrm{=\\left[\\Delta_{r} H^{\\circ}\\left(N_{2} O\\right)+3 \\Delta_{r} H^{\\circ}\\left(C O_{2}\\right)\\right]}$ <br> $\\mathrm{-\\left[\\Delta_{r} H^{\\circ}\\left(N_{2} O_{4}\\right)+3 \\Delta_{r} H^{\\circ}(C O)\\right]}$<br>$\\mathrm{=[81+(3 \\times-393)]-[9.7+(3 \\times-110)] k J}$<br>$\\mathrm{=-777.7 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\approx-778 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16776,'chemistry',49,626,1271,'The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of a solution at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ is ${\\text{2}}$ . If its $\\mathrm{pH}$ is to be changed to ${\\text{4}}$ , then conc. of $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$of the original has to be','[{"text": "Doubled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Halved", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increased by 100 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreased by 100 times", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}}$ is doubled, $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{H}^{+}}$ion concentration decreased by 100 times.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16778,'chemistry',46,446,892,'Read the Statement A and Statement B carefully and mark correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">${\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_2}$ and  ${{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}$ both possess V  shaped structure.</div><div class="column-right">The bond angle of ${\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_2}$ is less than that of ${{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}$.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_16778_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Bond angle of ${\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ is greater than ${{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}$. Hence ,statement B is incorrect.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16779,'chemistry',48,758,1636,'The heats of combustion of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{x} \\mathrm{H}_{y}, \\mathrm{C}}$, and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ are $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{a}, \\mathrm{b}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{c}}$ cal, respectively. The heat of formation $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{x} \\mathrm{H}_{y}}$ of will be','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\left(-x b+\\\\dfrac{y c}{2}+\\\\dfrac{a}{2}\\\\right) \\\\mathrm{cal}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\left(x b-\\\\dfrac{y c}{2}+\\\\dfrac{a}{2}\\\\right) \\\\mathrm{cal}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-\\\\left(x b+\\\\dfrac{y c}{2}-a\\\\right) \\\\mathrm{cal}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\left(x b-\\\\dfrac{y c}{2}-\\\\dfrac{a}{2}\\\\right) \\\\mathrm{cal}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{x C_{(S)}+\\dfrac{y}{2} H_{2}(g) \\rightarrow C_{x} H_{y}}$<br>${\\Delta _{\\text{f}}}{{\\text{H}}^\\circ }\\left( {{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{x}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{y}}}} \\right){\\text{   =   }}\\Delta {\\text{H}^\\circ}_{{{\\text{C}_\\text{x}}{\\text{H}_\\text{y}}}}{\\text{ - x}} \\cdot \\Delta {\\text{H}^\\circ}{_\\text{C}}{\\text{ -   }}\\frac{{\\text{y}}}{{\\text{2}}}\\Delta {\\text{H}^\\circ}{_{{\\text{H}_2}}}$<br>$ = a - xb - \\frac{y}{2} \\cdot c =  - \\left( {bx + \\frac{{yc}}{2} - a} \\right){\\text{cal}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16780,'chemistry',49,626,1271,'Among the following <br>(A) On dilution, the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of an acid increases <br>(B) A solution with $\\mathrm{pH}=5$ is 100 times more basic than a solution with $\\mathrm{pH}=3$ <br>(C) A solution with $\\mathrm{pH}=8$ is 1000 times more acidic than a solution with $\\mathrm{pH}=11$ <br>(D) The${\\text{pH of 1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ }} - {\\text{ 9}}}}{\\text{M KOH}}$ is slightly greater than 7','[{"text": "All are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A, D are only correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, B, C are only correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are wrong", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'All are correct statements',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16781,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Fluorine does not show positive oxidation state due to the','[{"text": "Absence of s-orbitals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Absence of p-orbitals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Absence of d-orbitals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Highest electronegativity", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16782,'chemistry',46,446,892,'Consider the following statements :<br>(I)  ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{F}}_3}$ molecule has a trigonal planar structure.<br>(II) Bond length of  ${{\\rm{N}}_2}$ is shorter than ${{\\rm{O}}_2}$.<br>(III) Isoelectronic molecules or ions have identical bond order.<br>(IV) Dipole moment of ${{\\rm{H}}_2}\\,{\\rm{S}}$ is higher than that of water molecule.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "(I) and (IV) are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II) and (III) are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(I) and (II) are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(III) and (IV) are correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'(I) ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{F}}_3}$ molecule has trigonal pyramidal structure.<br> (II) ${{\\rm{N}}_2}$ molecule has triple bond so its bond length is shorter than ${{\\rm{O}}_2}$, which has a double bond. <br>(III) Isoelectronic molecules or ions have same number of electrons, so they have identical bond order.<br> (IV) Dipole moment of water is more than ${{\\rm{H}}_2}\\,{\\rm{S}}$ because oxygen is more electronegative than sulphur.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16783,'chemistry',48,758,1638,'If the enthalpy of formation of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCl}_{(\\mathrm{g})}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{-}}$are $\\mathrm{-92.3 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}}$ and $\\mathrm{-167.44 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}}$, find the enthalpy of solution of hydrogen chloride gas.','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-45.14 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} / \\\\mathrm{mol}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-85.14 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} / \\\\mathrm{mol}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-75.14 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} / \\\\mathrm{mol}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-65.14 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} / \\\\mathrm{mol}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCl}_{(g)} \\longrightarrow H_{(a q)}^{+}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{(a q)}^{-}}$<br>$\\rm{\\Delta_{\\text {Sol }} H_{(H C l, g)}  =[0+(-167.44)-(-92.3)] \\\\ =-74.14 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16784,'chemistry',49,626,1272,'${\\text{10 mL}}$ of ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.1 N  HCl}}$ is added to ${\\text{ 990 mL}}$  solution of $\\mathrm{NaCl}$, the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of resulting solution is','[{"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm {p H=-\\log \\left(10^{-1}\\right)=1}$<br>$\\rm {p H_{\\text {final }}=p H_{\\text {initial }}+\\log \\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}}$<br>${\\text{ = 1 + log}}\\,\\frac{{{\\text{1000}}}}{{{\\text{10}}}}$<br>${\\text{ = 1 + 2}}$<br>${\\text{ = 3}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16785,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In which of the following molecule, oxidation number of Nitrogen is fractional?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{3} \\\\mathrm{H}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{5}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}_{3} \\mathrm{H}}$, the oxidation state of nitrogen is $\\mathrm{-1 / 3}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16787,'chemistry',49,626,1273,'A monoprotic acid in a $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ solution ionises to $0.001 \\%$. Its ionisation constant is','[{"text": "$1.0 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.0 \\\\times 10^{-6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.0 \\\\times 10^{-8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.0 \\\\times 10^{-11}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given,${\\text{C  =  0}}{\\text{.1}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{M ; }}\\alpha {\\text{  =  0}}.{\\text{001}}\\% $<br>${\\text{ = 1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 5}}}}$<br>$\\therefore {{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}}{\\text{ = C}}{\\alpha ^{\\text{2}}}$<br>${\\text{ = 0}}{\\text{.1}} \\times {\\left( {{\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 5}}}}} \\right)^{\\text{2}}}$<br>${\\text{ = 1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 11}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16788,'chemistry',48,755,1629,'Consider the following reaction between zinc and oxygen and choose the correct options out of the options given below :<br>$\\rm{2 \\mathrm{Zn}_{(s)}+O_{2(g)} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{ZnO}_{(s)} ; \\Delta H=-693.8 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}}$','[{"text": "The enthalpy of two moles of $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{ZnO}}$ is less than the total enthalpy of two moles of $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Zn}}$ and one mole of oxygen by $\\\\mathrm{693.8 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The enthalpy of two moles of $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{ZnO}}$ is more than the total enthalpy of two moles of $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Zn}}$ and one mole of oxygen by $\\\\mathrm{693.8 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{598.3 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$ energy is evolved in the reaction.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{693.8 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$ energy is absorbed in the reaction.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The given reaction is an exothermic reaction, the enthalpy of reactant is more than the enthalpy of product.<br>or<br>Decrease in number of moles decreases entropy.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16789,'chemistry',50,837,1868,'Oxidation number of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Fe}}$ in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Fe}_{3} \\mathrm{O}_{4}}$ is fractional because','[{"text": "It is a mixed $\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{+2}, \\\\mathrm{Fe}^{+4}\\\\right]}$ oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is a non-stoichiometric compound", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is a mixed $\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{+2}, \\\\mathrm{Fe}^{+3}\\\\right]}$ oxide", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16790,'chemistry',46,444,890,'Given below are two statements:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Dipole moment is a vector quantity and by convention it is depicted by a small arrow with tail on the negative centre and head pointing towards the positive centre.</div><div class="column-right">The crossed arrow of the dipole moment symbolizes the direction of the shift of charges in the molecule.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement  A  is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Dipole moment is a vector quantity and by convention it is depicted by a small arrow with tail on positive centre and head pointing towards the negative centre. Hence statement $ - $ A is incorrect.<br>The crossed arrow symbolises the direction of the shift of electron density (charges) in the molecule. Hence, statement $ - $ B is correct.<br>For example :<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_16790_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16791,'chemistry',49,626,1271,'On the basis of the equation, $\\mathrm{pH}=-\\log \\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]$, the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of  ${10^{ - 8}}\\,{\\text{mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{ - 3}}$ is','[{"text": "6.96", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7.04", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6.0", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Concentration of ${\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 8}}}}\\,{\\text{mol  d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}$ indicates that the solution is very dilute. Hence, the contribution of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}}$concentration from water is significant and should also be included for the calculation of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}}$.<br>$\\rm { {\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=10^{-8}+10^{-7} \\Rightarrow\\left(1+10^{-1}\\right) 10^{-7}} \\\\ \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{pH}=-\\log \\left(1+10^{-1}\\right) 10^{-7} \\Rightarrow 7-0.04=6.96 .}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16792,'chemistry',48,763,1661,'The incorrect expression among the following is:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta G_{\\\\text {system }}}{\\\\Delta S_{\\\\text {total }}}=-T}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In isothermal process,<br>$\\\\rm{w_{\\\\text {reversible }}=-n R T \\\\ln \\\\dfrac{V_{f}}{V_{i}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\ln K=\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta H^{\\\\circ}-T \\\\Delta S^{\\\\circ}}{R T}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K=e^{-\\\\Delta G^{\\\\circ} / R T}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{\\Delta G^{\\circ}=\\Delta H^{\\circ}-T \\Delta S^{\\circ}}$<br>$\\mathrm{-R T \\ln K=\\Delta H^{\\circ}-T \\Delta S^{\\circ}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\ln K=\\dfrac{\\Delta H^{\\circ}-T \\Delta S^{\\circ}}{R T}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16793,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In which of the following pairs, there is greatest difference in the oxidation number of the underlined elements ?','[{"text": "$\\\\underline {{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_2}} $ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\underline{\\\\mathrm{N}}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\underline{P}_{2} O_{5}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\underline{P}_{4} O_{10}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\underline{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\underline{\\\\mathrm{NO}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\underline {\\\\rm{S}} {{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\underline {\\\\rm{S}} {{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'O.N. of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}}$ in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}}$ is +4<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ difference is zero<br>O.N. of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{P}}$ in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{P}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{10}}$ is +5<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ difference is zero<br>O.N. of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}}$ in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$ is +1 and in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NO}}$ is +2 . The difference is 1<br>O.N. of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}}$ in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{2}}$ is +4 and in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{3}}$ is +6 . The difference is +2 .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16794,'chemistry',46,449,905,'The magnetic behavior of $\\mathrm{Li}_2 \\mathrm{O}, \\mathrm{Na}_2 \\mathrm{O}_2$ and $\\mathrm{KO}_2$, respectively, are','[{"text": "diamagnetic, paramagnetic and diamagnetic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "diamagnetic, diamagnetic and paramagnetic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "paramagnetic, diamagnetic and paramagnetic.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "paramagnetic, paramagnetic and diamagnetic", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{L}}{{\\rm{i}}_2}{\\rm{O}}$ is normal oxide $\\left(\\mathrm{O}^{2-}\\right)$ and it is diamagnetic. ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_2}{{\\rm{O}}_2}$ is peroxide $\\left(\\mathrm{O}_{2}^{2-}\\right)$ and it is also diamagnetic.<br>${\\rm{K}}{{\\rm{O}}_2}$  is superoxide $\\left( {{\\rm{O}}_2^ - } \\right)$ and it is paramagnetic, due to odd no. of electrons (unpaired electron)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
