(16819,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of $0.1\\,\\,{\\text{M}}$ solution of the following salts increases in the order','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NaCl} < \\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl} < \\\\mathrm{NaCN} < \\\\mathrm{HCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCl} < \\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl} < \\\\mathrm{NaCl} < \\\\mathrm{NaCN}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NaCN} < \\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl} < \\\\mathrm{NaCl} < \\\\mathrm{HCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCl} < \\\\mathrm{NaCN} < \\\\mathrm{NaCN} < \\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{HCl}}$ is a strong acid. In its $\\mathrm {0.1 \\mathrm{M}}$ solution, $\\mathrm {\\left[H^{+}\\right]=0.1 M}$ and hence, $\\mathrm {p H=1, \\quad N H_{4} C l_{(a q)}}$ hydrolyses in solution and give acidic solution which is less acidic than $\\rm {{\\text{0}}{\\text{.1M}}\\,\\,{\\text{HCl}}}$. NaCl is not hydrolysed in aqueous solutions. Its $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}=7}$. $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{NaCN}}$ undergoes hydrolysis in solution to give alkaline solution. So the $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}}$ increases in the order, $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{HCl} < \\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl} < \\mathrm{NaCl} < \\mathrm{NaCN}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
