(16870,'chemistry',49,632,1293,'A sparingly soluble salt gets precipitated only when the product of concentration of its ions in the solution $\\left(\\mathrm{Q}_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\right)$ becomes greater than its solubility product. If the solubility of $\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}$ in water is  ${\\text{8}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{  - 4}}}}{\\text{mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}$  Its solubility in 0.01 ${\\text{mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{ - 3}}$ of ${{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_4}$ in ${\\text{mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{  -  3}}}}{\\text{?}}$','[{"text": "$6.4 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\rm {\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n  {}&{}&{{\\text{BaS}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}}& \\rightleftharpoons &{{\\text{B}}{{\\text{a}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}}_{{\\text{(aq)}}}}&{\\text{ + }}&{{\\text{SO}}_{\\text{4}}^{{\\text{2 - }}}}&{} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{At}}\\,\\,{\\text{t = 0}}}&{}&{\\text{1}}&{}&{\\text{0}}&{}&{\\text{0}}&{} \\\\ \n  \\begin{gathered}\n  {\\text{At}}\\,\\,{\\text{equilibrium}} \\hfill \\\\\n  {\\text{in}}\\,\\,\\,{\\text{water}} \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered} &{}&{{\\text{1 - S}}}&{}&{\\text{S}}&{}&{\\text{S}}&{} \\\\ \n  \\begin{gathered}\n  {\\text{At}}\\,\\,{\\text{equilibrium}} \\hfill \\\\\n  {\\text{in}}\\,\\,\\,{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}} \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered} &{}&{{\\text{1 - S}}}&{}&{\\text{S}}&{}&{{\\text{S + 0}}{\\text{.01}}}&{} \n\\end{array}}$<br>$\\rm {K_{S P} \\text { for } \\mathrm{BaSO}_{4} \\text { in } \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}=}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\left[\\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\\right]=(S)(S)}$<br>But ${\\text{S = 8}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 4}}}}\\,{\\text{mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\therefore K_{s p}=\\left(8 \\times 10^{-4}\\right)^{2}=64 \\times 10^{-8}}$<br>The expression for $\\mathrm {K_{S P}}$ in the presence of sulphuric acid will be as follows $\\mathrm {K_{S P}=(S)(S+0.01)}$<br>$\\mathrm {(S)(S+0.01)=64 \\times 10^{-8}}$<br>Ignore $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{S}^{2}}$ since, the solubility of the salt is very low. Hence, $\\mathrm {S \\times 0.01=64 \\times 10^{-8}}$.<br>$\\rm {{\\text{S = 6}}{\\text{.4 1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 5}}}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
