(16925,'chemistry',48,758,1646,'The standard enthalpy of formation $\\mathrm{\\left(\\Delta_{f} H_{298}^{o}\\right)}$ for methane, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CH}_{4}}$ is $\\mathrm{-74.9 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}}$. In order to calculate the average energy given out in the formation of a $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}}$ bond from this it is necessary to know which one of the following?<br>','[{"text": "The dissociation energy of the hydrogen molecule, $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The first four ionisation energies of carbon.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The dissociation energy of $\\\\mathrm{H_{2}}$ and enthalpy of sublimation of carbon (graphite).", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The first four ionisation energies of carbon and electron affinity of hydrogen.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'To calculate average enthalpy of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}}$ bond in methane following informations are needed<br>(i) dissociation energy of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ i.e.,<br>$\\rm{\\dfrac{1}{2} H_{2}(g) \\longrightarrow H(g) ; \\Delta H=x(\\text { suppose })}$<br>(ii) Sublimation energy of C(graphite) to C(g)<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}}$ (graphite $\\mathrm{) \\longrightarrow C(g) ; \\Delta H=y(}$ Suppose $\\mathrm{)}$<br>Given<br>${\\rm{C}}({\\rm{ graphite }}){\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}(\\,{\\rm{g}}) \\to {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}(\\,{\\rm{g}}){\\rm{;}}$<br>$\\Delta {\\rm{H = 75}}\\,\\,{\\rm{k}}\\,{\\rm{J}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16926,'chemistry',49,619,1247,'The value of ${\\rm{\\Delta G^\\circ }}$ for the phosphorylation of glucose in glycolysis is $13.8 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$. Find the value of ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{C}}}$ at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$.','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm {\\Delta G^{\\circ}=13.8 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}=13.8 \\times 10^{3} \\mathrm{~J} / \\mathrm{mol}}$ <br>Also, $\\mathrm {\\Delta G^{\\circ}=-2.303 \\mathrm{RT} \\log K_{c}}$<br> Hence, $\\rm {\\log K_{C}}=-\\dfrac{13.8 \\times 1000}{2.303 \\times 8.314 \\times 298} \\\\ \\rm{\\log K_{C}}=-2.4 \\\\ \\mathrm{K_{C}}=4 \\times 10^{-3} .$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16929,'chemistry',48,758,1644,'The heat of atomisation of methane and ethane are $\\mathrm{360 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}}$ and $\\mathrm{620 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}}$, respectively. The longest wavelength of light capable of breaking the $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}}$ bond is (Avogadro number $\\rm{\\left.=6.02 \\times 10^{23}, h=6.62 \\times 10^{-34} \\mathrm{Js}\\right) \\text { : }}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2.48 \\\\times 10^{4} \\\\mathrm{~nm}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{1.49 \\\\times 10^{3} \\\\mathrm{~nm}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2.48 \\\\times 10^{3} \\\\mathrm{~nm}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{1.49 \\\\times 10^{4} \\\\mathrm{~nm}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CH}_{4}, 4 \\times B E_{(C-H)}=360 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore B E_{(C-H)}=90 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}}$<br>In $\\mathrm{C_{2} H_{6}, B E_{(C-C)}+6 \\times B E_{(C-H)}=620 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore B E_{(C-C)}=80 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{E}}_{({\\rm{C - C}})}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{80}} \\times {\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{3}}}}}{{{\\rm{6}}.{\\rm{023}} \\times {\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{23}}}}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{J/}}molecule$<br>${\\rm{Now}},{\\rm{E = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{hc}}}}{\\lambda }$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore \\quad \\lambda=\\dfrac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8} \\times 6.023 \\times 10^{23}}{80 \\times 10^{3}}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore \\quad \\lambda=1.49 \\times 10^{3} \\mathrm{~nm}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2015,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16931,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'If a spoon of copper metal is placed in a solution of ferrous sulphate','[{"text": "No reaction will take place", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cu}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Fe}}$ will precipitate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Iron will precipitate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cu will precipitate out", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{E_{\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+} / \\mathrm{Cu}}^{o}=0.34 \\quad E_{\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+/ F e}}^{o}=-0.44 \\mathrm{~V}}$<br>So Cu can\'t displace $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16932,'chemistry',46,436,872,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The Lewis dot structures provide a picture of bonding in molecules and ions in terms of the shared pairs of electrons and the octet rule.</div><div class="column-right">Octet rule explains the formation of compounds of inert gases such as\n$\\mathrm{XeF}_2, \\mathrm{XeO}_4$ etc.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Octet rule is based upon the chemical inertness of noble gases and it can\'t explain super octet like $\\mathrm{XeF}_{2}, \\mathrm{XeO}_{4}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16933,'chemistry',48,762,1659,'A reaction at 1 bar is non-spontaneous at low temperature but becomes spontaneous at high temperature. Identify the correct statement about the reaction among the following:<br>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta H}$ is negative while $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta S}$ is positive.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta H}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta S}$ are negative.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta H}$ is positive while $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta S}$ is negative.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta H}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta S}$ are positive.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{\\Delta G=\\Delta H-T \\Delta S}$<br>For a spontaneous reaction, $\\mathrm{\\Delta G=-v e}$<br>which is possible when both $\\mathrm{\\Delta H}$ and $\\mathrm{\\Delta S}$ are positive at high temperature and hence the reaction becomes spontaneous.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16934,'chemistry',49,617,1240,'Assume that the decomposition of $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ can be represented by the following equation:<br>${\\text{4HN}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{3}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{4N}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + 2}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + }}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}$<br>and the reaction approaches equilibrium at 400 $\\mathrm{K}$ temperature and $30 \\mathrm{~atm}$ pressure. At equilibrium, partial pressure of $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ is $2 \\mathrm{~atm}$.<br>Calculate ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{C}}}$ in $(\\mathrm{mol} / \\mathrm{L})^{3}$ at $400 \\mathrm{~K}$.','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "32", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm {P_{\\text {total }}=p_{\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}+p_{\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}+p_{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}+p_{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$<br>$\\rm {p_{N O_{2}}=4 p_{O_{2}}}$ and $\\rm{p_{H_{2} O}=2 p_{O_{2}}}$ <br> $\\rm{P_{total}=p_{H_{O_{3}}}+7 p_{O_{2}}}$ <br> $30-2=\\rm{p_{O_{2}} \\times 7 \\Rightarrow p_{O_{2}}}=4$ <br> $\\rm{K_{P}=\\dfrac{p_{N O_{2}}^{4} \\cdot p_{H_{2} O}^{2} \\cdot p_{O_{2}}}{p_{H N O_{3}}^{4}}}=\\dfrac{(4 \\times 4)^{2} \\times(2 \\times 4)^{2} \\times 4}{2^{4}}=2^{20}$ <br>$\\rm{K_{P}=K_{C}(R T)^{\\Delta n(g)}}$ <br> $\\rm{K_{C}}=\\dfrac{2^{20}}{(32)^{3}}=32$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16935,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'During developing of an exposed camera film, one step involves in the following reaction?<br>Which of the following best describes the role of hydroquinol?','[{"text": "It acts as an acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It acts as reducing agent", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It acts as oxidant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It acts as a base", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/50_16935_question_1.png'),
(16936,'chemistry',46,444,890,'The dipole moments of the given molecules are such that','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BF}_3>\\\\mathrm{NF}_3>\\\\mathrm{NH}_3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NF}_3>\\\\mathrm{BF}_3>\\\\mathrm{NH}_3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_3>\\\\mathrm{NF}_3>\\\\mathrm{BF}_3$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_3>\\\\mathrm{BF}_3>\\\\mathrm{NF}_3$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_16936_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16996,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'Which of the following salts is the basic in aqueous solution?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COOK}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Pb}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COO}\\\\right)_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Al}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOK}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}+\\mathrm{KOH}}$<br>Hence nature of solution is basic',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16937,'chemistry',48,751,1613,'If 100 mole of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ decomposes at 1 bar and $\\mathrm{300 \\mathrm{~K}}$, then the wok done ${\\rm{(in}}\\,\\,{\\rm{kJ)}}$ by one mole of $\\mathrm{O_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})}$ as it expands against 1 bar pressure is:<br>$\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}(l) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(l)+\\mathrm{O}_{2}(g)}$<br>$\\left( {{\\rm{R = 8}}{\\rm{.3}}\\,\\,{\\rm{J}}\\,{{\\rm{K}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{\\,mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 124}}{\\\\rm{.50}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "249.00", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "498.00", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "62.25", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}(l) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(l)+\\mathrm{O}_{2}(g)}$<br>$\\rm{w=-P_{e x t}(\\Delta V)=n_{O_{2}} \\quad R T}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\because 100}$ mole $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ on decomposition gives 50 mole $\\mathrm{O_{2}}$.<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore w=-(50)(8.3)(300)=-124500 \\mathrm{~J}=-124.5 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16938,'chemistry',49,618,1246,'The gas ${{\\text{A}}_{\\text{2}}}$ in the left flask allowed to react with gas ${{\\text{B}}_{\\text{2}}}$ present in right flask as ${{\\text{A}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)  + }}{{\\text{B}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2AB(g);}}$<br>${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{C}}}$ ${\\rm{  =    4}}$ at ${\\rm{27^\\circ C}}$. What is the concentration of $\\mathrm{AB}$ when equilibrium is established?','[{"text": "${\\\\text{1}}{\\\\text{.33}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{M}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{2}}{\\\\text{.66}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{M}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.66}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{M}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.33}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{M}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\rm {\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {}&{{{\\text{A}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}}}&{\\text{ + }}&{{{\\text{B}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}}}& \\rightleftharpoons &{{\\text{2AB(g)}}}&{} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{Intial}}\\,{\\text{moles}}}&{\\text{2}}&{}&{\\text{4}}&{}&{\\text{0}}&{} \\\\ \n  \\begin{gathered}\n  {\\text{Moles}}\\,{\\text{at}}\\,\\, \\hfill \\\\\n  {\\text{equilibrium}} \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered} &{{\\text{2 - x}}}&{}&{{\\text{4 - x}}}&{}&{{\\text{2x}}}&{} \n\\end{array}}$<br>$\\mathrm {K_{C}=\\dfrac{4 x^{2}}{(2-x)(4-x)}=4}$<br>$\\mathrm {x=1.33}$ mole<br>$\\mathrm {[A B]=\\dfrac{2 \\times 1.33}{4}=0.66 \\mathrm{M}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/49_16938_question_1.png'),
(16939,'chemistry',50,836,1862,'In which of the following reactions the starred element acts as oxidising agent?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{*}+\\\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{Cu}+\\\\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}{ }^{*}+\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{HCl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}^{*}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{CO}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Mn}^{*} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}+4 \\\\mathrm{HCl} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16940,'chemistry',46,436,872,'Which of the following has octet around central atom?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PF}_{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SF}_{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CCl}_{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BF}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In a chemical bond, electrons are transferred or shared such that each atom attains an electronic configuration of a noble gas $\\left(\\mathrm{ns}^{2} \\mathrm{np}\\right.$ ) i.e. having 8 outer shell (valency) electron. This arrangement is in accordance with octet rule. An outermost shell which had eight electrons is called octet. Carbon tertrachloride has octet around central atom. The number of valence electrons in given compounds are as follows:$\\mathrm{PF}_{5}\\left(10 \\mathrm{e}^{-}\\right), \\mathrm{SF}_{6}\\left(12 \\mathrm{e}^{-}\\right), \\mathrm{CCl}_{4}\\left(8 \\mathrm{e}^{-}\\right), \\mathrm{BF}_{3}\\left(6 \\mathrm{e}^{-}\\right)$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16941,'chemistry',48,752,1620,'For the reaction, $\\mathrm{A(g) \\rightarrow A(l) ; \\Delta H=-3 R T}$. The correct statement for the reaction is:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta H=\\\\Delta U \\\\neq O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta H=\\\\Delta U=O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{|\\\\Delta H| < |\\\\Delta U|}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{|\\\\Delta H|>|\\\\Delta U|}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{A(g) \\longrightarrow A(l)}$<br>$\\rm{\\Delta H=\\Delta U+\\Delta n_{g} R T}$<br>Given, $\\mathrm{\\Delta H=-3 R T}$<br>$\\rm{ \\Delta n_{g}=n_{p}-n_{r}=0-1=-1 \\\\ \\Delta H=\\Delta U-R T \\\\ \\Rightarrow-3 R T=\\Delta U-R T \\\\ \\Rightarrow-3 R T+R T=\\Delta U \\\\ \\Rightarrow-2 R T=\\Delta U \\\\ |\\Delta H|>|\\Delta U|}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16942,'chemistry',49,617,1241,'$4.8 \\mathrm{~g}$ of a sample of methanol was placed in an empty one litre vessel and heated to $250^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. Methanol first changed into vapour and then decomposed into $\\mathrm{CO}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$. After equilibrium was reached, a hole was made in the vessel. The gas effusing out showed that it contains 30 times as much $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ as $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}$ vapour. The equilibrium constant for this reaction at $250^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ is','[{"text": "1.1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\rm {\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {}&{{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{OH(g)}}}& \\rightleftharpoons &{{\\text{CO(g)}}}&{\\text{ + }}&{{\\text{2}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}}}&{} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{Intial}}\\,{\\text{moles}}}&{{\\text{4/32 = 0}}{\\text{.15}}}&{}&{\\text{0}}&{}&{\\text{0}}&{} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{Moles}}\\,{\\text{at}}\\,\\,{\\text{eqm}}}&{{\\text{0}}{\\text{.15x}}}&{}&{\\text{x}}&{}&{\\text{x}}&{} \n\\end{array}}$<br>${\\text{Given, 30(0}}{\\text{.15 - x) = 2x}}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\kern 1pt} {\\text{x = 0}}{\\text{.14}}$<br>$\\mathrm {K_{P}=\\dfrac{(0.14)(0.28)^{2}}{(0.15-0.14)}=1.1}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16943,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'Which of the following reactions is an example of a redox reaction?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{XeF}_{4}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~F}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{XeF}_{6}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{XeF}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{PF}_{5} \\\\rightarrow[\\\\mathrm{XeF}]^{+} \\\\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{XeF}_{6}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{XeOF}_{4}+2 \\\\mathrm{HF}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{XeF}_{6}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{XeO}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~F}_{2}+4 \\\\mathrm{HF}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the reaction <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_16943_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(16967,'chemistry',46,445,891,'Which of the following compound is most covalent?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{LiCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{LiF}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{LiBr}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{LiI}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The magnitude of covalent character depends upon a number of factors. These factors are:<br>(I) Small size of cation.<br>(II) Large size of anion.<br>(III) Large charge on either of the two ions.<br>Hence, due to larger size of $\\mathrm{I}^{-}$, LiI is most covalent.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
