(17002,'chemistry',46,446,892,'The geometry of electron pairs around ${\\rm{ I }}$ in $\\mathrm{IF}_{5}$ is','[{"text": "octahedral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "trigonal bipyramidal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "square pyramidal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "pentagonal planar", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{H}=\\dfrac{1}{2}[\\mathrm{~V}+\\mathrm{Y}-\\mathrm{C}+\\mathrm{A}]$<br> $=\\dfrac{1}{2}[5+7]=6$ or alternate way<br>$\\mathrm{IF}_{5}=7+5 \\times 7=\\dfrac{42}{8}=5+1$<br>$\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}^{2}$ hybridisation, hence it should be octahedral. But its actual geometry is square pyramidal due to the presence of one lone pair of electrons. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_17002_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17003,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Of the following reactions, the one in which oxidation number of \' $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}}$ \' does not change is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2 \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{NO}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2 \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{N}_{2}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}$ <br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'(1) $\\mathrm{2 \\stackrel{+4}{\\mathrm{~N}} \\mathrm{O}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\stackrel{+5}{\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}+\\stackrel{+3}{\\mathrm{HNO}}}$<br>(2) $\\mathrm{\\stackrel{0}{N}{ }_{2}+O_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\stackrel{+2}{N} O}$<br>(3) $\\mathrm{2 \\stackrel{+4}{N O_{2}} \\rightarrow \\stackrel{+4}{N_{2}} \\mathrm{O}_{4} \\Rightarrow}$ There is no change in oxidation number<br>(4) $\\mathrm{\\stackrel{-3}{\\mathrm{~N}} \\mathrm{H}_{4} \\stackrel{+3}{\\mathrm{~N}} \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow \\stackrel{0}{\\mathrm{~N}}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17004,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'The dissociation constants of aniline, acetic acid and water at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ are respectively, $3.83 \\times 10^{-10}$, $1.75 \\times 10^{-15}$ and $1.008 \\times 10^{-14}$. What is the percentage hydrolysis of aniline acetate in a decimolar solution?','[{"text": "50", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "55", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "60", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The degree of hydrolysis \' $\\mathrm {h}$ \' of a salt of a weak base is given by  $\\rm {{\\rm{h = }}\\sqrt {\\dfrac{{{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{w}}}}}{{{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{a}}}{\\rm{ \\times }}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{b}}}}}} }$<br>$\\rm {{\\rm{ = }}\\sqrt {\\dfrac{{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.008 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 4}}}}}}{{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.75 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 5}}}}{\\rm{ \\times 3}}{\\rm{.83 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 10}}}}}}} {\\rm{ = 1}}{\\rm{.219}}}$<br>But, \' $\\mathrm {h}$ \' cannot be greater than 1. This indicates that the degree of hydrolysis in this case is very large and therefore, the above formula, which is based on the assumption that \' $\\mathrm {h}$ \' is small, is not applicable.<br>In the present case, therefore, we have to calculate \' $\\mathrm {h}$ \' as follows:<br>${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{h}}}{\\text{  =  }}\\frac{{{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{w}}}}}{{{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}} \\times {{\\text{K}}_{\\text{b}}}}}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{h}}}{\\text{  =  }}\\frac{{{{\\text{h}}^{\\text{2}}}}}{{{{{\\text{(1  -  h)}}}^{\\text{2}}}}}\\frac{{\\text{h}}}{{{\\text{1  -  h}}}}{\\text{  =  }}\\sqrt {{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{h}}}} $<br>${\\text{  =  }}\\sqrt {\\frac{{{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{w}}}}}{{{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}} \\times {{\\text{K}}_{\\text{b}}}}}} {\\text{  =  }}\\sqrt {{\\text{1}}{\\text{.219}}} {\\text{  =  0}}{\\text{.5495}}$<br>Percentage hydrolysis $\\mathrm {=55}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
