(17012,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'The extent of hydrolysis of  ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.0050 M}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\text{K}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{Cr}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}$ is (The ionisation constants of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}$ are ${\\text{K}}{{\\text{a}}_{\\text{1}}}{\\text{ = 1}}{\\text{.6, K}}{{\\text{a}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ =  3}}{\\text{.1 }} \\times {\\text{ 1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{  - 7}}}}{\\text{)}}$','[{"text": "$0.5 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.026 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.26 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.15 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Just as in the ionisation of polyprotic acids, so in the hydrolysis of their salts, the reaction proceeds in successive stages. The extent of the second stage is generally very small compared with the first. This is particularly true in this case, where $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}}$ is essentially a strong acid with respect to its first ionisation. The equation of interest is<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{HCrO}_{4}^{-}+\\mathrm{OH}^{-}}$<br>Which indicates that the conjugate acid of hydrolysing $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}}$ is $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{HCrO}_{4}^{-}}$. As the ionisation constant for $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{HCrO}_{4}^{-}}$is $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{Ka}_{2}}$, the hydrolysis constant for the reaction is $\\mathrm {K_{W} / \\mathrm{Ka}_{2}}$. <br>$\\rm { \\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{HCrO}_{4}^{-}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}\\right]}=K_{h} \\\\ =\\dfrac{K_{w}}{\\mathrm{Ka}}=\\dfrac{10^{-14}}{3.1 \\times 10^{-7}}=3.2 \\times 10^{-8}}$<br>Let $\\mathrm {x=\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=\\left[\\mathrm{HCrO}_{4}^{-}\\right]}$.<br>Then $\\mathrm {\\left[\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}\\right]=0.005-x \\approx 0.005}$ and<br>$\\rm { \\dfrac{x^{2}}{0.005}=3.2 \\times 10^{-8} \\\\ x=1.3 \\times 10^{-5}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\%}$ hydrolysis $\\mathrm {=\\left(1.3 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 100\\right) / 0.005=0.26 \\%}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17021,'chemistry',46,446,892,'In $\\mathrm{BrF}_{3}$ molecule, the lone pairs occupy equatorial positions to minimise','[{"text": "lone pair-bond pair repulsion only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "bond pair-bond pair repulsion only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "lone pair-lone pair repulsion and lone pair-bond pair repulsion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "lone pair-lone pair repulsion only", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{In} \\mathrm{BrF}_{3}$ molecule, $\\mathrm{Br}$ is $\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}$ hybridised, but geometry is $\\mathrm{T}$-shaped due to distortion of geometry from trigonal bipyramidal to T-shaped by the involvement of lone pair-lone pair repulsion. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_17021_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17013,'chemistry',46,446,892,'Among the following, the pair in which the two species are not isostructural, is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SiF}_{4}$ and $\\\\mathrm{SF}_{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{IO}_{3}^{-}$ and $\\\\mathrm{XeO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BH}_{4}^{-}$ and $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PF}_{6}^{-}$ and $\\\\mathrm{SF}_{6}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{SiF}_{4}$ and $\\mathrm{SF}_{4}$ are not isostructural because${\\rm{Si}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{4}}}$  is tetrahedral due to $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$-hybridisation of $\\mathrm{Si}$.<br>${ }_{14} \\mathrm{Si}=1 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}, 2 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}, 2 \\mathrm{p}^{6}, 3 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 3 \\mathrm{p}^{2}$ (in ground state)<br>${ }_{14} \\mathrm{Si}=1 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}, 2 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 2 \\mathrm{p}^{6}, 3 \\mathrm{~s}^{1} 3 \\mathrm{p}^{3} \\quad \\text { (in excited state) }<br>$Hence, four equivalent $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$-hybrid orbitals are obtained and they are overlapped by four $\\mathrm{p}$-orbitals of four fluorine atoms on their axis. Thus, it shows following structures. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_17013_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  While $\\mathrm{SF}_{4}$ is not tetrahedral but it is distorted tetrahedral because in it, $\\mathrm{S}$ is $\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}$ hybridised.<br>$_{{\\rm{16}}}{\\rm{\\,S = 1\\,}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{,2\\,}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{6}}}{\\rm{,3\\,}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{3p}}_{\\rm{x}}^{\\rm{2}}\\,{\\rm{3p}}_{\\rm{y}}^{\\rm{1}}\\,{\\rm{3p}}_{\\rm{z}}^{\\rm{1}}$<br> (in ground state)<br>${\\rm{ = 1\\,}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{,2\\,}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{6}}}{\\rm{,3\\,}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{3p}}_{\\rm{x}}^{\\rm{1}}\\,{\\rm{3p}}_{\\rm{y}}^{\\rm{1}}\\,{\\rm{3p}}_{\\rm{z}}^{\\rm{1}}\\,{\\rm{3\\,d}}_{{\\rm{xy}}}^{\\rm{1}}$<br>$\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}$-hybridisation<br>(in first excitation state)  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_17013_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Hence, five $\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}$ hybrid orbitals are obtained. One orbital is already paired and rest four are overlapped with four $\\mathrm{p}$-orbitals of four fluorine atoms on their axis in trigonal bipyramidal form. This structure is distorted from trigonal bipyramidal to see-saw due to involvement of repulsion between lone pair and bond pair.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17014,'chemistry',48,760,1650,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The phenomenon like flow of water down hill or fall of a stone on to the ground is an example of a spontaneous process.<br></div><div class="column-right">There is a net increase in potential energy in the direction of change.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The phenomenon like flow of water down hill or fall of a stone on to the ground is an example or spontaneous process because there is a net decrease in potential.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17015,'chemistry',50,842,1881,'Atomic mass of trivalent element of equivalent mass 9 is','[{"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "18", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "27", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "36", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Atomic mass $\\mathrm{=}$ equivalent mass $\\mathrm{\\times}$ valency $\\mathrm{=9 \\times 3=27}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17016,'chemistry',49,629,1278,'The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of blood is 7.4. Assuming that the buffer in blood is carbon dioxide, hydrogen carbonate ion, what is the ratio of conjugate base to acid necessary to maintain blood at its proper $\\mathrm{pH} ?\\left(\\mathrm{Ka}=4.5 \\times 10^{-7}\\right)$','[{"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "11", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "12", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}+\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}}$<br>$\\rm {\\mathrm{Ka}_{1}=\\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}\\right]}=4.5 \\times 10^{-7}}$<br>Given that $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}=7.4}$ then $\\mathrm {\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}\\right]}$or $\\mathrm {\\left[H^{+}\\right]=4 \\times 10^{-8}}$<br>$\\rm {\\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{CO}_{2}\\right]}=\\dfrac{4.5 \\times 10^{-7}}{4 \\times 10^{-8}}=11}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17017,'chemistry',46,446,892,'In a regular octahedral molecule $\\mathrm{MX}_{6}$, the number of ${\\rm{ X}} - {\\rm{M}} - {\\rm{X}}$ bonds at $180^{\\circ}$ is','[{"text": "three", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "two", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "six", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "four", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In octahedral molecule ${\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{6}}}$, the six hybrid orbitals $\\left(\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}^{2}\\right)$ are directed towards the corners of a regular octahedral with an angle of $90^{\\circ}$ According to given structure of ${\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{6}}}$, the number of ${\\rm{X}} - {\\rm{M}} - {\\rm{X}}$ bonds at $180^\\circ $must be three. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_17017_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17018,'chemistry',48,762,1659,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">$\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{G}}$ has the unit of energy.<br></div><div class="column-right">$\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S}}$ are the energy dependent terms.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17019,'chemistry',50,842,1881,'The molar mass of a metallic oxide $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MO}}$ is 40 . The equivalent mass of the element will be','[{"text": "12", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "24", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "48", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Molecular formula of the oxide is MO.<br>Atomic mass of the metal $\\mathrm{=40-16=24}$<br>Equivalent mass $\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{\\text { Atomic mass }}{\\text { valency }}=\\dfrac{24}{2}=12}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17020,'chemistry',49,629,1278,'0.1 mole of ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {\\,{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{b}}}{\\rm{ = 5 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 4}}}}} \\right)$ is mixed with 0.08 mole of $\\mathrm{HCl}$ and diluted to one litre. The ${{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}$ concentration in the solution is ${\\text{x}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - y}}}}$  The value of x is','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\rm {\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}&{\\text{ + }}&{{\\text{HCl}}}& \\to &{{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{NH}}_{\\text{3}}^{\\text{ + }}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}^{\\text{ - }}}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{0}}{\\text{.1}}}&{}&{{\\text{0}}{\\text{.08}}}&{}&{\\text{0}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{0}}{\\text{.02}}}&{}&{\\text{0}}&{}&{{\\text{0}}{\\text{.08}}} \n\\end{array}}$<br>As it is a basic buffer solution.<br>$\\mathrm {p O H=p K_{b}+\\log \\dfrac{0.08}{0.02}=-\\log \\left(5 \\times 10^{-4}\\right)+\\log 4}$<br>$\\rm {=3.30+0.602=3.902}$<br>$\\mathrm {p H=14-3.902=10.09}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\left[H^{+}\\right]=8.1 \\times 10^{-11} \\approx 8 \\times 10^{-11} \\mathrm{M}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17044,'chemistry',49,632,1291,'The solubility of $\\mathrm{Ca}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ is $\\sqrt{3}$. The solubility product of $\\mathrm{Ca}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ is','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "27", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12 \\\\sqrt{3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{Ca}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}+2 \\mathrm{OH}_{2 s}^{-}}$<br>$\\mathrm {K_{s p}=(s)(2 s)^{2}=4 s^{2}, s=\\sqrt{3}}$<br>$\\mathrm {K_{s p}=4(\\sqrt{3})^{3}=12 \\sqrt{3}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17022,'chemistry',48,758,1636,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Combustion of all organic compound is an exothermic reaction.<br></div><div class="column-right">The enthalpies of all elements in their standard state are zero.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In a combustion reaction, sum of enthalpies of reactants is greater than the sum of the enthalpies of products and therefore is an exothermic reaction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
