(17036,'chemistry',49,632,1293,'Equal volumes of several solutions at 0.10 molar concentration are mixed. The chart below indicates the interactions that took place between these solutions. What is the formula of the hypothetical compound that has low solubility?<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{}&{{\\rm{CX}}}&{{\\rm{BY}}}&{{\\rm{DY}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{AX}}}&{\\rm{ \\ldots }}&{{\\rm{Ppt}}}&{\\rm{ \\ldots }}\\\\\n{{\\rm{DY}}}&{\\rm{ \\ldots }}&{{\\rm{ \\ldots }}{\\rm{.}}}&{}\\\\\n{{\\rm{CX}}}&{\\rm{ \\ldots }}&{{\\rm{Ppt}}}&{}\n\\end{array}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CX}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BY}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{AX}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BX}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The chart indicates that when $\\mathrm {A X}$ is mixed with BY a precipitate is formed. The precipitation reaction may be one of the following:<br>$\\rm {A X+B Y \\rightarrow A B+X Y}$<br>Or $\\mathrm {\\quad A X+B Y \\rightarrow A Y+B X}$<br>Since $\\mathrm {A X}$ does not form precipitate with DY, precipitate can\'t be $\\mathrm {A Y}$. Also $\\mathrm {C X}$ does not forms precipitate with $\\mathrm { DY}$, precipitate $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{XY}}$ is also ruled out. $\\mathrm {C X}$ forms precipitate with BY, the precipitate must be $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{BX}}$ and it has the lowest solubility.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17037,'chemistry',46,449,905,'Which one of the following has the strong $\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{O}$ bond?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}^{+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}^{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{O}}_2^{2 - }$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Bond order $\\propto$ bond strength<br>From Q. $5, \\mathrm{BO}$ of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}^{+}=2.5$, and rest have $\\mathrm{BO}$ less than 2.5.<br>Thus, it has the strongest $\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{O}$ bond among the given',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17038,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'Hydrogen peroxide in its reaction with $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KIO}_{4}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}}$ respectively, is acting as a','[{"text": "Reducing agent, oxidising agent", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Reducing agent, reducing agent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxidising agent, oxidising agent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxidising agent, reducing agent", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ reduces ${\\rm{KI}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$, hence it behaves as reducing agent.<br>${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ oxidises ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{OH}}$, hence it behaves as oxidising agent.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
