(17150,'chemistry',48,758,1636,'The ratio of heats liberated at $\\mathrm{298 \\mathrm{~K}}$ from the combustion of one $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{kg}}$ of coke and by burning water gas obtained from $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{kg}}$ of coke is<br>(Assume coke to be $\\mathrm{100 \\%}$ carbon)<br>(Given : enthalpies of combustion of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}, \\mathrm{CO}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ as $\\mathrm{393.5 \\mathrm{~kJ}, 285 \\mathrm{~kJ}, 285 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$ respectively all at $\\mathrm{298 \\mathrm{~K})}$.','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{0.79: 1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{0.69: 1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{0.86: 1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{0.96: 1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given information, the enthalpy of combustion of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}}$ is wrong it should be enthalpy of combustion of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}}$.<br>$\\mathrm{1 \\mathrm{~kg}}$ of coke $\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{1000}{12}=83.33 \\mathrm{moles}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow \\underset{83.33 \\times 396.5 \\mathrm{~kJ}}{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\underbrace{\\mathrm{CO}+\\mathrm{H}_{2}}_{\\text {Water gas }}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow \\underset{\\substack{83.33 \\times(285+285) \\\\ 83.33 \\times 570 \\mathrm{~kJ}}}{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$<br>Now, the ratio of<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}{{\\rm{coke}}}&{\\rm{:}}&{{\\rm{water}}\\,\\,{\\rm{gas}}}\\\\{{\\rm{83}}{\\rm{.33 \\times 393}}{\\rm{.5}}}&{\\rm{:}}&{{\\rm{83}}{\\rm{.33 \\times 570}}}\\\\{ \\Rightarrow {\\rm{393}}{\\rm{.5}}}&{\\rm{:}}&{{\\rm{570}}}\\\\{ \\Rightarrow {\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.69}}}&{\\rm{:}}&{\\rm{1}}\\end{array}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17151,'chemistry',49,625,1269,'Ionic product of water at $310 \\mathrm{~K}$ is $2.7 \\times 10^{-14}$. What is the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of neutral water at this temperature?','[{"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6.8", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{w}}=\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}\\right] \\cdot\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=2.7 \\times 10^{-14}}$ at $\\mathrm {310 \\mathrm{~K}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightleftharpoons\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}\\right]=\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]}$<br>Therefore,<br>$\\rm { {\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}\\right]=\\sqrt{2.7 \\times 10^{-14}}=1.643 \\times 10^{-7} \\mathrm{M}} \\\\ \\mathrm{pH}=-\\log \\left[\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}\\right]=-\\log 1.643 \\times 10^{-7} \\\\ \\mathrm{pH}=7+(-0.2156)=6.7844 .}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17153,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Match Column I with Column II for the oxidation states of the central atoms.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. $\\rm{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{7}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}}$<br>B. $\\rm{{\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}}$<br>C. $\\rm{{\\rm{VO}}_{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{ - }}}$<br>D. $\\rm{{\\rm{FeF}}_{\\rm{6}}^{{\\rm{3 - }}}}$</div><div class="column-right">P. $\\rm{{\\rm{ + 3}}}$<br>Q. $\\rm{{\\rm{ + 4}}}$<br>R. $\\rm{{\\rm{ + 5}}}$<br>S. $\\rm{{\\rm{ + 6}}}$<br>T. $\\rm{{\\rm{ + 7}}}$</div></div>','[{"text": "A - S, B - T, C - R, D - P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - P, B - Q, C - R, D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q, B - R, C - S, D - T", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - T, B - S, C - S, D - R", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'O. N. of Cr in ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{7}}^{{\\rm{ - 2}}}$ is +6<br>O. N. of Mn in ${\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}$ is +7<br>O. N. of V in ${\\rm{VO}}_{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{ - }}$ is +5<br>O. N. of Fe in ${\\rm{FeF}}_{\\rm{6}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}$ is +3',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17154,'chemistry',48,757,1634,'Consider the following reaction:<br>(i) $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}^{+}(a q)+\\mathrm{OH}^{-}(a q) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(l) ; \\Delta H=}$<br>$\\rm{-X_{1} \\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}}$<br>(ii) $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}(g)+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{l}) ; \\Delta H=-X_{2} \\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}}$<br>(iii) $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}(g)+\\mathrm{H}_{2}(g) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CO}(g)+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{l})}$;<br>$\\rm{\\Delta H=-X_{3} \\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}}$<br>(iv) $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2}(g)+\\dfrac{5}{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}(g) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{CO}_{2}(g)+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{l})}$;<br>$\\Delta {\\text{H =  + }}{{\\text{X}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{k}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{J}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{{\\text{  -  1}}}}$<br>Enthalpy of formation of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{l})}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-X_{2} \\\\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{+X_{3} \\\\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-X_{4} \\\\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{+X_{1} \\\\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The amount of heat evolved or absorbed during the formation of 1 mole of a compound from its constituent is known as heat of formation. So, the correct answer is <br>$\\rm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}(g)+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}(g) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{l}) ; \\Delta H=-\\mathrm{X}_{2} \\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17155,'chemistry',49,624,1267,'The ionisation constant of dimethylamine is $5.4 \\times 10^{-4}$. Calculate percentage ionisation of ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.02}}\\,\\,{\\text{M}}$ solution in ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.1}}\\,\\,{\\text{M NaOH}}$?','[{"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.54}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5.4 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.4 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.054}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the presence of ${\\text{0}}.{\\text{1 M NaOH}}$, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/49_17155_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />\n[dimethylamine is a weak base and $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{NaOH}}$ is a strong base so we can assume that $\\mathrm {(\\mathrm{C} \\alpha+0.1) \\approx 0.1]}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}}=\\dfrac{\\left[\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{N}} \\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]}{\\left[\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{NH}\\right]}}$<br>or $\\mathrm {5.4 \\times 10^{-4}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{C} \\alpha \\times 0.1}{0.02}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{C} \\alpha=\\dfrac{5.4 \\times 10^{-4} \\times 0.02}{0.1}=108 \\times 10^{-6}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\alpha=\\dfrac{108 \\times 10^{-6}}{0.02}=54 \\times 10^{-4}=5.4 \\times 10^{-3}}$.<br>$\\mathrm {\\alpha=0.54 \\%}$.<br>In the presence of strong base, dissociation of weak base i.e., dimethylamine decreases due to common ion effect.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17156,'chemistry',46,446,892,'In how many of the following, the molecular geometry is NOT linear?<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}, \\mathrm{SO}_{2}, \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2}, \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}, \\mathrm{BeCl}_{2}, \\mathrm{HCl}, \\mathrm{OF}_{2}}}$','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{2}, \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{OF}_{2}}}$, molecular geometry is bent or V -shaped.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17158,'chemistry',48,758,1635,'The enthalpy of formation of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}$ is $\\mathrm{-46 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$ $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{mol}^{-1}}$. The enthalpy change for the reaction :<br>$\\rm{2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{N}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{+23 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{+92 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{+46 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{+184 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The amount of heat absorbed or evolved when 1 mole of the substance is directly obtained from its constituent elements is called heat or enthalpy of formation.<br>The reaction for the formation of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}$ is :<br>$\\rm{\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{~N}_{2}+\\dfrac{3}{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{NH}_{3} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=-46 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$<br>Heat of formation of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}=-46 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$<br>The enthalpy change for the given reaction,<br>$\\rm{ 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{N}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{1}=? \\\\ \\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{1}=-(2 \\times \\Delta \\mathrm{H})=-(2 \\times-46)=92 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
