(17245,'chemistry',48,751,1612,'When $\\mathrm{1 \\mathrm{~mol}}$ of a gas is heated at constant volume, temperature is raised from 298 to 308 $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}}$. If heat supplied to the gas is $\\mathrm{500 \\mathrm{~J}}$, then which statement is correct?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{q}=\\\\mathrm{w}=500 \\\\mathrm{~J}, \\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{U}=0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{q}=\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{U}=500 \\\\mathrm{~J}, \\\\mathrm{w}=0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{q=-\\\\mathrm{w}=500 \\\\mathrm{~J}, \\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{U}=0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{U}=0, \\\\mathrm{q}=\\\\mathrm{w}=-500 \\\\mathrm{~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As volume is constant hence work done in this process is zero therefore heat supplied is equal to change in internal energy.<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{\\rm{\\Delta V}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ = 0;}}\\,\\,{\\rm{w = 0}}$<br>According to ${{\\rm{1}}^{{\\rm{st}}}}$ law of thermodynamics,<br>${\\rm{\\Delta U = q + w = q = 500}}\\,{\\rm{J}}{\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17246,'chemistry',49,617,1241,'$0.96 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{HI}$ was heated till the equilibrium  ${\\text{2HI(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g) + }}{{\\text{I}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}}$ is reached. The reaction mixture was suddenly cooled and the amount of iodine produced required ${\\text{15}}{\\text{.7 }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{mL}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\frac{{\\text{N}}}{{{\\text{10}}}}$  hypo solution. The ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{P}}}$ for the above equilibrium reaction is','[{"text": "$7.5 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.85 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.93 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$17.5 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Initial moles of  $\\rm {{\\rm{ HI}}}$=$\\dfrac{{{\\text{0}}{\\text{.96}}}}{{{\\text{128}}}}{\\text{ = 7}}{\\text{.5  1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}$<br>Moles of  $\\rm {{{\\rm{I}}_{\\rm{2}}}}$ produced $\\mathrm {=\\dfrac{15.7 \\times 0.1 \\times 10^{-3}}{2}}$<br>$\\mathrm {=7.85 \\times 10^{-4}}$<br>Moles of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{HI}}$ left undissociated at equilibrium<br>$\\rm {=7.5 \\times 10^{-3}-2 \\times 7.85 \\times 10^{-4}=5.93 \\times 10^{-3}}$<br>For $\\mathrm {2 \\mathrm{HI} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{H}_{2}+\\mathrm{I}_{2}}$<br>$\\mathrm {K=\\dfrac{\\left(7.85 \\times 10^{-4}\\right)^{2}}{\\left(5.93 \\times 10^{-3}\\right)^{2}}=17.52 \\times 10^{-3}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17248,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'Thiosulphate reacts differently with iodine and bromine in the reactions given below:<br>$\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}^{2-}+\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{S}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{6}^{2-}+2 \\mathrm{I}^{-}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}^{2-}+2 \\mathrm{Br}_{2}+5 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}+10 \\mathrm{H}^{+}+4 \\mathrm{Br}^{-}}$<br>Which of the following statements justifies the above dual behaviour of thiosulphate?','[{"text": "Bromine is a stronger oxidant than iodine.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bromine is a weaker oxidant than iodine.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thiosulphate undergoes oxidation by bromine and reduction by iodine in these reactions.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bromine undergoes oxidation and iodine undergoes reduction in these reactions.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Bromine being stronger oxidising agent than $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}$, it oxidises $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}^{2-}}$ to $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}}$ whereas $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}$ oxidises it only into $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{6}{ }^{2-}}$ ion.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17249,'chemistry',48,751,1614,'A gas expands from a volume of $\\mathrm{1 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}}$ to a volume of $\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}}$ against an external pressure of $\\mathrm{10^{5} \\mathrm{~N} \\mathrm{~m}^{-2}}$. The work done by the gas will be','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{10^{5} \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{10^{2} \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{10^{2} \\\\mathrm{~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{10^{3} \\\\mathrm{~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Work done $\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{w})=\\mathrm{P} \\Delta \\mathrm{V}=\\mathrm{P} \\times\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{2}-\\mathrm{V}_{1}\\right)}$<br>$\\rm{=10^{5} \\times(2-1)=10^{5} \\mathrm{~J} \\text { or } 10^{2} \\mathrm{~kJ}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17250,'chemistry',46,437,875,'The percentage ionic character in $\\mathrm{Cs}-\\mathrm{Cl}$ bond in $\\mathrm{CsCl}$ molecule is _____. The electronegativity values of $\\mathrm{Cs}$ and $\\mathrm{Cl}$ are 0.7 and 3.0 respectively.','[{"text": "55.3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "60", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "45", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "75.7", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{A}}-\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{B}}=\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{Cl}}-\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{Cs}}=(3.0-0.7)=2.3$<br>Percentage ionic character in $\\mathrm{Cs}-\\mathrm{Cl}$ bond<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\left[16 \\times 2.3+3.5 \\times(2.3)^{2}\\right] \\\\& =[36.8+18.51]=55.31\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17251,'chemistry',49,632,1293,'The solubility of anhydrous $\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$ and hydrous $\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$ in diethyl ether are ${{\\text{S}}_{\\text{1}}}$ and ${{\\text{S}}_{\\text{2}}}$, respectively. Then','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\rm{ = }}{{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\rm{ < }}{{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Can\'t be predicted", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Anhyd. $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}}$ is a lewis acid so more soluble in ether.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17252,'chemistry',50,838,1874,'Identify disproportionation reaction','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CH}_{4}+2 \\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CH}_{4}+4 \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{CCl}_{4}+4 \\\\mathrm{HCl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2 \\\\mathrm{~F}_{4}+2 \\\\mathrm{OH}^{-} \\\\longrightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{~F}^{-}+\\\\mathrm{OF}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2 \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}+2 \\\\mathrm{OH}^{-} \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}+\\\\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17253,'chemistry',48,751,1614,'Work done when 2 mole of an ideal gas is compressed from a volume of $\\mathrm{5 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}}$ to $\\mathrm{2.5 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}}$ at $\\mathrm{300 \\mathrm{~K}}$, under a pressure of ${\\text{100}}\\,{\\text{k}}\\,{\\text{pa}}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{49.75 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{248.7 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{497.5 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{99.50 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given,<br>Number of moles $\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{n})=2}$<br>Volume $\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{1}\\right)=5 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}=5000 \\mathrm{dm}^{3}}$<br>Volume $\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{2}\\right)=2.5 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}=2500 \\mathrm{dm}^{3}}$<br>Pressure $\\mathrm{(p)=100 \\mathrm{kPa}}$<br>We havve, work done $\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{W})=-\\mathrm{p}_{\\text {ext }} \\cdot \\mathrm{dV}}$<br>$\\rm{ =-100 \\mathrm{kPa}(2500-5000) \\\\ =-100 \\times-2500 \\mathrm{dm}^{3} \\\\ =250000 \\mathrm{~J}=250 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\simeq 248.9 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17255,'chemistry',49,632,1293,'What is the minimum volume of water required to dissolve $1 \\mathrm{~g}$ of calcium sulphate at 298K?  (Given, For calcium sulphate, ${{\\rm{K}}_{{\\rm{SP}}}}{\\rm{ = 9}}{\\rm{.1 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 6}}}}$','[{"text": "$3.02 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.56 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.43 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\rm {{{\\rm{K}}_{{\\rm{SP}}}}}$=${{\\text{S}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ = 9.1}}\\times 1{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 6}}}}$<br>${\\text{S = 3}}.{\\text{02}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}\\,{\\text{mol/L = 3}}.{\\text{02}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}} \\times {\\text{136}}\\,{\\text{g/L}}$<br>${\\text{ = 0}}.{\\text{411}}\\,{\\text{g/L}}$<br>Thus, for dissolving $\\mathrm {0.411 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CaSO}_{4}}$, water required <br>$\\mathrm {=1 \\mathrm{~L}}$<br>For dissolving $\\mathrm {1 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CaSO}_{4}}$, water required $\\mathrm {=}$ $\\mathrm {1 / 0.411 \\mathrm{~L}=2.43 \\mathrm{~L}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17256,'chemistry',50,838,1874,'Which of the following elements does not show disproportionation tendency?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Br}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{F}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Being the most electronegative element, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{F}}$ can only be reduced and hence it always shows an oxidation number of${\\rm{ - 1}}$. Further, due to the absence of $\\mathrm{d}$ - orbitals, it cannot be oxidised and hence it does not show +ve oxidation numbers. In other words, F cannot be simultaneously oxidised as well as reduced and hence does not show disproportionation reactions.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17257,'chemistry',48,751,1614,'Three moles of an ideal gas are expanded isothermaly from a volume of $\\mathrm{300 \\mathrm{~cm}^{3}}$ to $\\mathrm{2.5 \\mathrm{~L}}$ at $\\mathrm{300 \\mathrm{~K}}$ against a pressure of $\\mathrm{1.9 \\mathrm{~atm}}$. The work done in joules is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-423.56 \\\\mathrm{~J}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{+423.56 \\\\mathrm{~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-4.18 \\\\mathrm{~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{+4.8 \\\\mathrm{~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given,<br>$\\rm{ \\mathrm{V}_{1}=300 \\mathrm{~cm}^{3}=3 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~m}^{3} \\\\ \\mathrm{~V}_{2}=2.5 \\mathrm{~L}=25 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~m}^{3}}$<br>$\\rm{ \\mathrm{T}=300 \\mathrm{~K} \\\\ \\mathrm{p}=1.9 \\mathrm{~atm}=1.9 \\times 1.01325 \\times 10^{5} \\mathrm{~N} / \\mathrm{m}^{2}}$<br>Now, work done during change in volume against constant presusre is<br>$\\rm{ \\mathrm{W}=-\\mathrm{p}\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{2}-\\mathrm{V}_{1}\\right) \\\\ =-1.9 \\times 1.01325 \\times 10^{5} \\mathrm{~N} / \\mathrm{m}^{2} \\\\ \\quad\\left(25 \\times 10^{-4}-3 \\times 10^{-4}\\right) \\mathrm{m}^{3} \\\\ =-1.925 \\times 10^{5}\\left(22 \\times 10^{-4}\\right) \\mathrm{Nm} \\\\ \\Rightarrow-423.56 \\mathrm{Nm}=-423.56 \\mathrm{~J} \\\\ \\qquad(\\because 1 \\mathrm{Nm}=1 \\mathrm{~J})}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17259,'chemistry',49,632,1292,'If ${\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - x}}}}$  is the factor by which solubility of  ${\\rm{AgBr(s)}}\\left[ {{{\\rm{K}}_{{\\rm{SP}}}}{\\rm{ = 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 10}}}}} \\right]$   changes with respect to initial solubility, when it is dissolved in ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.01}}\\,\\,{\\text{M}}\\,\\,{\\text{NaBr}}\\left( {{\\text{aq}}} \\right)$ solution. The value of ${\\text{\'x\'}}$  is','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\rm {{{\\rm{K}}_{{\\rm{SP}}}}}$ of$  {\\text{AgBr = }}\\left[ {{\\text{A}}{{\\text{g}}^{\\text{ + }}}} \\right]\\left[ {{\\text{B}}{{\\text{r}}^{\\text{ - }}}} \\right]{\\text{ = (S)(S + 0}}{\\text{.01)}}$<br>$\\mathrm {0.01 S=10^{-10}}$<br>$\\mathrm {S=10^{-8}}$,<br>Solubility in water $\\mathrm {=\\sqrt{K_{S P}}=\\sqrt{10^{-10}}=10^{-5} \\mathrm{M}}$<br>Factor by which solubility changes $\\mathrm {=\\dfrac{10^{-8}}{10^{-5}}=10^{-3}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\therefore \\quad x=3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17260,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'The more positive the value of $\\mathrm{E^{\\circ}}$, the greater is the tendency of the species to get reduced. Using the standard electrode potential of redox couples given below find out which of the following is the strongest oxidising agent.<br>$\\mathrm{E^{\\Theta}}$ values: $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+} / \\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}=+0.77 ; I_{2}(s) / I^{-}=+0.54}$;<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+} / \\mathrm{Cu}=+0.34 ; \\mathrm{Ag}^{+} / \\mathrm{Ag}=+0.80 \\mathrm{~V}}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{I_{2}(s)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Since $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ag}^{+} / \\mathrm{Ag}}$ has highest positive value of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{o}}}$, therefore, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}}$is the strongest oxidising agent with highest tendency to get reduced.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17261,'chemistry',48,751,1613,'An ideal gas expands isothermally and reversibly from $\\mathrm{10 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}}$ to $\\mathrm{20 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}}$ at $\\mathrm{300 \\mathrm{~K}}$, performing $\\mathrm{5.187 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$ of work on surrounding, Calculate number of moles of gas used.','[{"text": "1.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{W}=-\\int \\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{ext}} \\mathrm{dV}}$<br>Since, the process is reversible, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{p}_{\\text {ext }}=\\mathrm{p}}$, which gives $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{W}=-\\int \\mathrm{p} \\cdot \\mathrm{dV}}$<br>Substituting the ideal gas equation for $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{p}}$ leads to.<br>$\\rm{\\mathrm{W}=-\\int \\dfrac{\\mathrm{nRT}}{\\mathrm{V}} \\cdot \\mathrm{dV}}$<br>Since the process is isothermal,<br>$\\rm{\\begin{gathered}\\mathrm{W}=-\\mathrm{nRT} \\operatorname{In}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{V}_{2}}{\\mathrm{~V}_{1}}\\right) \\\\-5.187 \\times 1000 \\mathrm{~J}=-\\mathrm{n} \\times 8.314 \\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\\\\\text { In }\\left(\\dfrac{20 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}}{10 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}}\\right) \\\\\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{n}=\\dfrac{5.187 \\times 1000}{8.314 \\times \\operatorname{In} 2} \\mathrm{~mol} \\\\=\\dfrac{5.187 \\times 1000}{8.314 \\times 0.6931} \\mathrm{~mol}=3 \\mathrm{~mol}\\end{gathered}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17262,'chemistry',49,632,1293,'How many grams of $\\mathrm{CaC}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$ will dissolve in $1 \\mathrm{~L}$ of saturated solution?  ${{\\rm{K}}_{{\\rm{SP}}}}$  of $\\mathrm{CaC}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is ${\\text{2}}{\\text{.5}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 9}}}}{\\text{ }}{{\\text{M}}^{\\text{2}}}$ and its molecular weight is ${\\text{128}}$.','[{"text": "$6.4 \\\\times 10^{-3} \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.4 \\\\times 10^{-4} \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.8 \\\\times 10^{-3} \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.8 \\\\times 10^{-4} \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CaC}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{Ca}_{s}^{2+}+\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}}$<br>$\\rm {{{\\rm{K}}_{{\\rm{SP}}}}{\\rm{ = S \\times S = }}{{\\rm{S}}^{\\rm{2}}}}$<br>$\\rm {{\\rm{S  =  }}\\sqrt {{{\\rm{K}}_{{\\rm{SP}}}}} {\\rm{ = }}{\\left( {{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.5 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 9}}}}} \\right)^{{\\rm{1/2}}}}}$<br>$\\mathrm {=5 \\times 10^{-5} \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\therefore \\dfrac{w}{128}=5 \\times 10^{-5}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\therefore \\quad w=6.4 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~g}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17263,'chemistry',46,449,905,'How many of the following are paramagnetic? <br>${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}},\\,{{\\rm{B}}_{\\rm{2}}},{\\rm{O}}_2^{ - 2},{\\rm{BN}},{\\rm{Cl}}_2^ + $ and ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}^ + }.$','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Only $\\mathrm{B}_{2}$ and ${\\rm{Cl}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ + }}$ are paramagnetic.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17264,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In which of the following compounds, an element exhibits two different oxidation states.','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{NO}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N_{3} H}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{NO}_{3}}$ is an ionic compound consisting of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}}$and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}}$ion.<br>The O.N. of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}}$ in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}}$ is -3<br>The O.N. of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}_{\\text {in }} \\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}}$ is +5.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17265,'chemistry',48,751,1614,'Calculate the work done during combustion of $\\mathrm{0.138 \\mathrm{~kg}}$ of ethanol, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}(l)}$ at $\\mathrm{300 \\mathrm{~K}}$.<br>Given: $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{R}=8.314 \\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$, molar mass of ehtanol $\\mathrm{=46 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$.','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-7482 \\\\mathrm{~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{7482 \\\\mathrm{~J}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-2494 \\\\mathrm{~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2494 \\\\mathrm{~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The combustion of ethanol, invovles the following reaction.<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}(l)+3 \\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{CO}_{2}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$<br>Given,<br>Mass of ethanol $\\mathrm{=0.138 \\mathrm{~kg}=138 \\mathrm{~g}}$<br>Temperature $\\mathrm{=300 \\mathrm{~K}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{R}=8.314 \\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$<br>Molar mass of ethanol $\\mathrm{=46 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$<br>No. of moles of ethanol $\\mathrm{=}$ $\\frac{{{\\rm{Mass}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ethanol}}}}{{{\\rm{Molar}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mass}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ethanol}}}}$<br>$\\rm{=\\dfrac{138}{46}=3}$<br>Work done (W) during combustion of $\\mathrm{0.138 \\mathrm{~kg}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}=\\mathrm{nRT}}$<br>$\\rm{ \\mathrm{W}=\\mathrm{nRT} \\\\ \\mathrm{W}=3 \\times 8.314 \\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\times 300 \\mathrm{~K} \\\\ \\mathrm{~W}=7482.6 \\mathrm{~J} \\\\ \\approx 7482 \\mathrm{~J}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17267,'chemistry',49,632,1290,'The product of ionic concentration in a saturated solution of an electrolyte at a given temperature is constant and is known as','[{"text": "Ionic product of the electrolyte", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Solubility product", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ionization constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dissociation constant", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Solubility product is the product of ionic concentration in a saturated solution of an electrolyte at a given temperature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17479,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'In the following reaction, which is the species being oxidised?<br>$\\rm{2 \\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}(\\mathrm{aq})+2 \\mathrm{I}^{-}(\\mathrm{aq}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{I}_{2}(\\mathrm{aq})+2 \\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}(\\mathrm{aq})}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{I}^{-}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{I}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'O.N. of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{I}^{-}}$is -1 and in $\\mathrm{I_{2}}$ O.N. is zero (loss of electrons). Hence $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{I}^{-}}$oxidised.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17268,'chemistry',50,839,1878,'In the following balanced reaction,<br>$\\rm{{\\text{XMnO}}_{\\text{4}}^{\\text{ - }}{\\text{ + Y}}{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}^{{\\text{2 - }}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{XM}}{{\\text{n}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}}{\\text{ + YC}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ + }}\\frac{{\\text{Z}}}{{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}$<br>values of X, Y and Z respectively are','[{"text": "2, 5, 16", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8, 2, 5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5, 2, 16", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5, 8, 4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{XMnO}}_{\\text{4}}^{\\text{ - }}{\\text{ + Y}}{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}^{{\\text{2 - }}}{\\text{ + Z}}{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ + }}} \\rightleftharpoons $<br>${\\rm{XM}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{ + 2}}}}{\\rm{ + 2YC}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + }}\\frac{{\\rm{Z}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>First half reaction ${\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }} \\to {\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{ + 2}}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>On balancing<br>${\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{ + 8}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{ + 5}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{ + 2}}}}{\\rm{ + 4}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(2)}}$<br>Second half reaction<br>${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}} \\to {\\rm{2C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>On balancing<br>${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}} \\to {\\rm{2C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(4)$<br>On multiplying eqn. (2) by 5 and (4) by 2 and then adding we get<br>$\\rm{\\begin{aligned}& 2 \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}+5 \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}+16 \\mathrm{H}^{+} \\longrightarrow \\\\& 2 \\mathrm{Mn}^{+2}+10 \\mathrm{CO}_{2}+8 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\end{aligned}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17269,'chemistry',48,751,1614,'The work done during combustion of $\\mathrm{9 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{~kg}}$ of ethane, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}(\\mathrm{~g})}$ at $\\mathrm{300 \\mathrm{~K}}$ is (Given $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{R}=8.314 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{deg}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$, atomic mass $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}=12, \\mathrm{H}}$ $\\mathrm{=1)}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{6.236 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-6.236 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{18.71 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-18.71 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Work done in a chemical reaction,<br>$\\rm{\\mathrm{W}=\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}} \\mathrm{RT}}$<br>Where, $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=}$ number of moles of gaseous products - number of moles of gaseous reactants combustion of ethane is as follows,<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\dfrac{7}{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{CO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(l)}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=2-4.5=-2.5}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{W}=(2.5 \\mathrm{~mol})\\left(8.314 \\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right) \\times 300 \\mathrm{~K}}$<br>$\\rm{=6235.5 \\mathrm{~kJ}=6.2355 \\mathrm{~J}}$<br>Now, 1 mole of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}=30 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}}$<br>$\\rm{=6.2355 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$<br>Work done for combustion of $\\mathrm{30 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}=6.2355 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ Work done for combustion of $\\mathrm{90 \\mathrm{~g}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}}$<br>$\\rm{ =\\dfrac{6.2355 \\times 90}{30} \\\\ =18.7065 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\\\ =18.71 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17271,'chemistry',49,632,1293,'What is the molar solubility of $\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ if ${{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{  SP}}}}$=${\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.0 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 38}}}}{\\rm{?}}$<br>(Given $\\left.(3.7)^{1 / 4}=1.386 ;(2.7)^{1 / 4}=1.28\\right)$','[{"text": "$3.16 \\\\times 10^{-10}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.386 \\\\times 10^{-10}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.45 \\\\times 10^{-9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.28 \\\\times 10^{-11}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\rm {{{\\rm{K}}_{{\\rm{SP}}}}}$ of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{OH})_{3}=27 \\mathrm{~S}^{4}}$<br>${\\text{S = }}{{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{SP}}}}{\\text{27}}\\dfrac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{4}}}{\\text{ = }}\\left( {\\dfrac{{{\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 38}}}}}}{{{\\text{27}}}}} \\right)\\dfrac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{4}}}{\\text{ = 1}}{\\text{.386  1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 10}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17272,'chemistry',50,839,1878,'Given $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{XNa}_{2} \\mathrm{HAsO}_{3}+\\mathrm{YNaBrO}_{3}+\\mathrm{ZHCl} \\rightarrow}$<br>$\\rm{\\mathrm{NaBr}+\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{AsO}_{4}+\\mathrm{NaCl}}$<br>The values of $\\mathrm{X, Y}$ and $\\mathrm{Z}$ in the above redox reaction are respectively.','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2,1,2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2,1,3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{3,1,6}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{3,1,4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'On balancing the given reaction, we find $\\mathrm{3 \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{HAsO}_{3}+\\mathrm{NaBrO}_{3}+6 \\mathrm{HCl}}$<br>$\\rm{\\longrightarrow 6 \\mathrm{NaCl}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{AsO}_{4}+\\mathrm{NaBr}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17273,'chemistry',48,752,1619,'Two moles of an ideal gas is expanded isothermally and reversibly at $\\mathrm{300 \\mathrm{~K}}$ from $\\mathrm{1 \\mathrm{~L}}$ to $\\mathrm{10 \\mathrm{~L}}$. The enthalpy change in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{kJ}}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{11.4 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{4.8 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{-11.4 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{kJ}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The enthalpy change at constant pressure is given as:<br>$\\rm{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{p}} \\Delta \\mathrm{T}}$<br>For isothermal expansion<br>$\\rm{\\Delta \\mathrm{T} =0 \\\\\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H} =0}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17274,'chemistry',46,444,890,'group moment is $2.3 \\,\\mathrm{D}$ and $-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ group moment is $0.4 \\,\\mathrm{D}$. Determine the dipole moment of $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{CO}$ in $\\mathrm{cm} \\times 10^{30}$ in nearest possible integer.','[{"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "17", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_17274_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The resultant dipole moment of two $\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{C}$ bonds.<br>$=\\sqrt{(0.4)^{2}+(0.4)^{2}+2 \\times(0.4)^{2} \\times \\cos 120^{\\circ}}$<br>$ = \\sqrt {0.32 - 0.16}  = 0.4\\,\\,{\\rm{D}}$<br>$\\therefore$ Net dipole moment of the molecule  $ = 2.3 + 0.4 = 2.7\\,\\,{\\rm{D}}$<br>$1 \\mathrm{D}=10^{-18}$ esu $-\\mathrm{cm} .=10^{-20}$ esu-m.<br>$4.8 \\times {10^{ - 10}}\\,\\,{\\rm{esu}} = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}{\\rm{C}}$<br>$\\therefore 1$ esu $=\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times 10^{-9} \\mathrm{C}$.<br>$\\therefore 1 \\mathrm{D}=\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times 10^{-29} \\mathrm{cm}=\\dfrac{10}{3} \\times 10^{-30} \\mathrm{cm}$.<br>$\\therefore$ Net dipole moment of $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{CO}$<br>$=2.7 \\times \\dfrac{10}{3} \\times 10^{-30} \\mathrm{cm}=9 \\times 10^{-30} \\mathrm{cm}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/46_17274_question_1.png'),
(17277,'chemistry',48,752,1620,'The reaction in which $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}>\\Delta \\mathrm{U}}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{NO}_{(\\\\mathrm{g})}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CaCO}_{3(\\\\mathrm{~s})} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{CaO}_{(\\\\mathrm{s})}+\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})}+3 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{NH}_{3(\\\\mathrm{~g})}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CH}_{4(\\\\mathrm{~g})}+2 \\\\mathrm{O}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{CO}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{(1)}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}+\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}} \\mathrm{RT}}$<br>For the reaction,<br>$\\rm{\\mathrm{CaCO}_{3(\\mathrm{~s})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CaO}_{(\\mathrm{s})}+\\mathrm{CO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\\\\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}  =1 \\\\\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H}  >\\Delta \\mathrm{U}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17278,'chemistry',50,839,1878,'How many electrons are involved in the following redox reaction?<br>$\\rm{\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}+\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}+\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\\mathrm{CO}_{2}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {{\\rm{Unbalanced}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The reaction given is<br>${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{7}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{ + F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}} \\to {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\rm{ + F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\rm{ + C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{7}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}} \\to {\\rm{2C}}{{\\rm{r}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}$<br>On balancing<br>${\\rm{14}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{ + C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{7}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{ + 6}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{2C}}{{\\rm{r}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\rm{ + 7}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(1)}}$<br>${\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}} \\to {\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}} \\to {\\rm{2C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>On adding all the three equations<br>${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{7}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{ + F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{ + 14}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{ + 3}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\to {\\rm{2C}}{{\\rm{r}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\rm{ + F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\rm{ + 2C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 7}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>Hence the total no. of electrons involved in the reaction $\\mathrm{=6}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17281,'chemistry',48,752,1620,'Identify the equation in which change in enthalpy is equal to change in internal energy','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}(l) \\\\longrightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}(l)+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g})}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}(\\\\mathrm{s})+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g})}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}(\\\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}(\\\\mathrm{~g})+\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g})}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g})+3 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\longrightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}(\\\\mathrm{~g})}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In the given options,<br>(1) $\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}(l) \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(l)+\\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})}$ $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=1}$ $\\mathrm{\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}+\\mathrm{RT}}$<br>(2) $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}(\\mathrm{s})+\\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{CO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=1-1=0}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}}$<br>(3) $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{PCl}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=2-1=1}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}+\\mathrm{RT}}$<br>(4) $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}} \\mathrm{RT}=2-3=-1}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}-\\mathrm{RT}}$<br>Equation given in option (2) has enthalpy change equal to internal energy change.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17284,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Amongst the following, identically the species with an atom in +6 oxidation state:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}\\\\right]^{-}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cr}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CrO}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}$<br>Let O. No. of $\\mathrm{C r=x}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{\\rm{x + 2}}({\\rm{ - 2}}){\\rm{ + 2}}({\\rm{ - 1}}){\\rm{ = 0}}$<br>${\\rm{x - 4 - 2 = 0}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {\\rm{x =  + 6}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17285,'chemistry',48,752,1620,'Calculate the difference between heat of combustion of carbon monoxide gas at constant pressure and at constant volume at $\\mathrm{27^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}}$ ?<br>$\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{R}=2 \\mathrm{cal} \\mathrm{K}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right)}$','[{"text": "${\\\\text{54}}\\\\,{\\\\text{cal}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ -  300}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{cal}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ -  600}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{cal}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{27}}\\\\,{\\\\text{cal}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given,<br>${\\rm{T = 27}}^\\circ {\\rm{C = 300}}\\,{\\rm{K}}$<br>${\\rm{R = 2}}\\,{\\rm{cal}}\\,{{\\rm{K}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$<br>Reaction for combustion of carbon monoxide is given as:<br>$\\rm{\\begin{gathered}\\mathrm{CO}(\\mathrm{g})+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{CO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=1-15=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\end{gathered}}$<br>Now, the difference between heat of combustion of carbon monoxide gas at constant pressure and at constant volume is given by<br>$\\rm{ \\Delta \\mathrm{H}-\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}} \\mathrm{RT} \\\\ \\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H}-\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 300=-300 \\mathrm{cal}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17287,'chemistry',46,449,905,'In the MOT of $\\mathrm{F}_{2}$ molecule, number of electrons occupying antibonding orbitals are','[{"text": "8", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Fluorine atom have 2 + 7 electrons so an ${{\\text{F}}_{\\text{2}}}$ molecule contain 18 electrons.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_17287_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Hence, 8 electrons occupy the antibonding orbitals.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17320,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'Consider the following redox couples:<br>I. $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+} / \\mathrm{Zn}, \\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{o}}=-0.76}$.<br>II. $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ag}^{2+} / \\mathrm{Ag}, \\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{o}}=+0.80 \\mathrm{~V}}$.<br>III. $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+} / \\mathrm{Cu}, \\mathrm{E}^{o}=+0.34 \\mathrm{v}}$.<br>IV. $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Hg}^{2+} / \\mathrm{Hg} ; \\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{o}}=+0.885}$<br>Which of the above will act as anode when connected to standard hydrogen electrode which has $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{o}}}$ value given as zero?','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the value of standard reduction potential is negative, the electrode undergoes oxidation and acts as anode. Thus, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+} / \\mathrm{Zn}}$; $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{E}^{\\mathrm{o}}=-0.76}$ will acts as a anode, when connected to standard hydrogen electrode.<br>Thus, statement I is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17288,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'Consider the reaction:<br>${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}({\\rm{aq}}){\\rm{ + S}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{4 + }}}}({\\rm{aq}}){\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}({\\rm{l}})$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\to {\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}({\\rm{aq}}){\\rm{ + HSO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}({\\rm{aq}}){\\rm{ + 3}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}({\\rm{aq}})$<br>Which of the following statements is correct?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}}$ is the oxidising agent because it undergoes oxidation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}}$ is the reducing agent because it undergoes oxidation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}}$ is the reducing agent because it undergoes oxidation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}}$ is the reducing agent because it undergoes reduction", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}({\\rm{aq}}){\\rm{ + S}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{4 + }}}}({\\rm{aq}}){\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}({\\rm{l}})\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\to $<br>${\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}({\\rm{aq}}){\\rm{ + }}\\mathop {{\\rm{HSO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 6}}} ({\\rm{aq}}){\\rm{ + 3}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}$<br>Hence $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{3}}$ is the reducing agent because it undergoes oxidation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17289,'chemistry',48,752,1620,'Standard enthalpy of vaporisation $\\mathrm{\\Delta_{v a p} H^{\\circ}}$ for water at $\\mathrm{100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}}$ is $\\mathrm{40.66 \\mathrm{kmol}^{-1}}$. The internal energy of vaporisation of water at $\\mathrm{100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}\\left(\\right.}$ in $\\mathrm{\\left.\\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}\\right)}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\text{ + 43}}{\\\\text{.76}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{ + 40}}{\\\\text{.66}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{ + 37}}{\\\\text{.56}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ -  43}}{\\\\rm{.76}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{l}) \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(v) \\quad \\therefore \\quad \\Delta n=1}$<br>$\\rm{\\begin{gathered}\\Delta H^{o}=\\Delta U^{o}+\\Delta n R T \\\\40.66 \\times 10^{3}=\\Delta U^{o}+1 \\times 8.314 \\times 373\\end{gathered}}$<br>$\\rm{\\therefore \\quad \\Delta U^{o}=37559 \\mathrm{~J}=37.56 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17292,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'In which of the following reactions, hygrogen peroxide acts as an oxidising agent?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HOCl}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{O}^{+}+\\\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{I}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}+2 \\\\mathrm{OH}^{-} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{I}^{-}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2 \\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}+3 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow}$ $\\\\mathrm{2 \\\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+3 \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}+2 \\\\mathrm{OH}^{-}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{PbS}+4 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{PbSO}_{4}+4 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'(1) <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17292_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> (2) <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17292_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> (3) <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17292_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> (4) <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17292_explanation_4.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17293,'chemistry',48,753,1624,'0.06 mole of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}}$ solid is added to $\\mathrm{100 \\mathrm{~cm}^{3}}$ of water at $\\mathrm{298 \\mathrm{~K}}$. The enthalpy of ${\\rm{KN}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\left( {{\\rm{aq}}} \\right)$ solution is $\\mathrm{35.8 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$. After the solute is dissolved the temperature of the solutions will be','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{293 \\\\mathrm{~K}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{298 \\\\mathrm{~K}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{301 \\\\mathrm{~K}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{304 \\\\mathrm{~K}}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Dissoution of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}}$ is endothermic.<br>$\\rm{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{\\mathrm{sol}}=35.8 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ Heat absorbed when 0.06 mole of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}}$ is dissolved $\\mathrm{=35.8 \\times 0.06=2148 \\mathrm{~J}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\because \\quad \\mathrm{q}=\\mathrm{m} \\times \\mathrm{c} \\times \\Delta \\mathrm{T}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore \\quad 2148=100 \\times 4.184 \\times \\Delta \\mathrm{T} \\Rightarrow \\Delta \\mathrm{T}=5}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore \\quad}$ Temperature of solution<br>$\\mathrm{=298-5=293 \\mathrm{~K}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17296,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Oxidation numbers of potassium in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{O}, \\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KO}_{2}}$, respectively, are','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{+1,+2}$ and +4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{+2,+1}$ and $\\\\mathrm{+\\\\dfrac{1}{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{+1,+1}$ and +1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{+1,+4}$ and +2", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Potassium always shows +1 state only with non-metals.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17297,'chemistry',48,752,1622,'Equal volumes of two monoatomic gases, $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{B}}$, at the same temperature and pressure are mixed. The ratio of specific heats $\\mathrm{\\left(C_{P} / C_{V}\\right)}$ of the mixture will be','[{"text": "0.83", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.50", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.67", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For monoatomic gases $\\mathrm{C_{P}: C_{V}}$ is 1.67 . Note that equal volumes are mixed.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
