2{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^ + } + 10{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_2} + 8{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17380,'chemistry',48,762,1659,'The process is spontaneous at the given temperature, if','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}}$ is $\\\\mathrm{+\\\\mathrm{ve}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}}$ is $\\\\mathrm{-\\\\mathrm{ve}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}}$ is${\\\\rm{ -  ve}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}}$ is $\\\\mathrm{+\\\\mathrm{ve}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}}$ is ${\\\\rm{ +  ve}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}}$ is $\\\\mathrm{+\\\\mathrm{ve}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}}$ is ${\\\\rm{ +  ve}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}}$ is equal to zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{G}=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S}}$ (Gibbs\' equation) when, $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}}$ is - ve, but $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S}}$ is $\\mathrm{+\\mathrm{ve} \\rightarrow \\Delta \\mathrm{G}}$ will be $\\mathrm{(-v e)}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17381,'chemistry',46,444,890,'Read the Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct options given below:<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NF}_{3}}}$ molecules have pyramidal shape with a lone pair of electrons on nitrogen atom. The resultant dipole moment of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$ is greater than that of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NF}_{3}}}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$ ,the orbital dipole due to lone pair is in the same direction as the resultant dipole moment of the N-H bonds.F is the most electronegative element.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Both the molecules ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NF}_{3}}}$ have pyramidal shape with a lone pair of electrons on nitrogen atom.<br>Although fluorine is more electronegative than nitrogen, the resultant dipole moment of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\left(4.90 \\times 10^{-30} \\mathrm{Cm}\\right)}}$ is greater than that of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NF}_{3}\\left(0.8 \\times 10^{-30} \\mathrm{Cm}\\right)}}$. This is because, in case of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$ the orbital dipole due to lone pair is in the same direction as the resultant dipole moment of the ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}-\\mathrm{H}}}$ bonds whereas in ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NF}_{3}}}$ the orbital dipole is in the direction opposite to the resultant dipole moment of the three N-F bonds. The orbital dipole because of lone pair decreases the effect of the resultant bond moment which results in low dipole moment of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NF}_{3}}}$ as represented below <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_17381_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17382,'chemistry',49,629,1278,'What would be the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of a solution obtained by mixing $5 \\mathrm{~g}$ of acetic acid and $7.5 \\mathrm{~g}$ of sodium acetate and making the volume equal to $500 \\mathrm{~mL}$ ?<br>$\\left( {{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{a}}}{\\rm{ = 1}}{\\rm{.75 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 5}}}}{\\rm{,p}}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{a}}}{\\rm{ = 4}}{\\rm{.76}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{pH = 4}}{\\\\rm{.70}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{pH < 4}}{\\\\rm{.70}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pH}$ of solution will be equal to $\\\\mathrm{pH}$ of acetic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{4}}{\\\\text{.499}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\rm {{\\text{pH = p}}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}}}$$\\rm {{\\rm{ + log}}\\dfrac{{{\\rm{[ salt ]}}}}{{{\\rm{[ acid ]}}}}}$<br>$\\rm {=4.76+\\log \\dfrac{\\dfrac{7.5}{500}}{\\dfrac{5}{500}}=4.7+\\log 1.5=4.87}$<br>Hence correct answer is $\\mathrm {4.76 < p H < 5.0}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17383,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The correct order of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}}$-compounds in its decreasing order of oxidation states is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}, \\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}, \\\\mathrm{NO}, \\\\mathrm{N}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}, \\\\mathrm{N}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{NO}, \\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}, \\\\mathrm{NO}, \\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}, \\\\mathrm{N}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}, \\\\mathrm{NO}, \\\\mathrm{N}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\rm{\\begin{array}{ccccc} & \\mathrm{HNO}_{3} & \\mathrm{NO} & \\mathrm{N}_{2} & \\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl} \\\\\\text { Oxidation No } & +5 & +2 & 0 & -3\\end{array}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17384,'chemistry',46,445,891,'Arrange the bonds in order of increasing ionic character in the molecules :<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{LiF}, \\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{O}, \\mathrm{N}_{2}, \\mathrm{SO}_{2}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{ClF}_{3}}}$.','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} < \\\\mathrm{SO}_{2} < \\\\mathrm{ClF}_{3} < \\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} < \\\\mathrm{LiF}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{ClF}_{3} < \\\\mathrm{N}_{2} < \\\\mathrm{SO}_{2} < \\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} < \\\\mathrm{LiF}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{LiF} < \\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} < \\\\mathrm{ClF}_{3} < \\\\mathrm{SO}_{2} < \\\\mathrm{N}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} < \\\\mathrm{ClF}_{3} < \\\\mathrm{SO}_{2} < \\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} < \\\\mathrm{LiF}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Greater the electronegativity difference between the atoms, greater will be the ionic character of molecule. So, increasing order of ionic character in the molecules is<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}_{2} < \\mathrm{SO}_{2} < \\mathrm{ClF}_{3} < \\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{O} < \\mathrm{LiF}}}$<br>So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17385,'chemistry',49,617,1237,'${{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)  + 3}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{(g),}}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{1}}}$<br>${{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)   +  }}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2NO(g),}}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{2}}}$<br>${\\text{ }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)  + }}\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O(g),}}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{3}}}$<br>The equation for the equilibrium constant of the reaction<br>${\\text{2N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{(g) + }}\\dfrac{{\\text{5}}}{{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2NO(g) + 3}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O(g),}}\\left( {{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{4}}}} \\right)$<br>in terms of ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{1}}}\\,\\,{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ and }}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ is:}}$','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{{{{\\\\text{K}}_{\\\\text{1}}}{\\\\text{.}}{{\\\\text{K}}_{\\\\text{2}}}}}{{{{\\\\text{K}}_{\\\\text{3}}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{{{{\\\\text{K}}_{\\\\text{1}}}{\\\\text{.K}}_{\\\\text{2}}^{\\\\text{2}}}}{{{{\\\\text{K}}_{\\\\text{2}}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{{{{\\\\text{K}}_{\\\\text{1}}}{\\\\text{.K}}_{\\\\text{3}}^{\\\\text{2}}}}{{{{\\\\text{K}}_{\\\\text{2}}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{{{{\\\\text{K}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{.K}}_{\\\\text{3}}^{\\\\text{3}}}}{{{{\\\\text{K}}_{\\\\text{1}}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'To calculating the value of $\\mathrm {K_{4}}$ in the given equation we should apply:<br>eqn.(2) + eqn.(3) $\\mathrm {\\times 3-}$ eqn.(1)<br>hence, $\\rm {{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{ = }}\\dfrac{{{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{K}}_{\\text{3}}^{\\text{3}}}}{{{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{1}}}}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17386,'chemistry',48,762,1659,'Conversion of oxygen into ozone is non spontaneous at','[{"text": "all temperatures", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "high temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "room temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "low temperature.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{3 \\mathrm{O}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{O}_{3(\\mathrm{~g})} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=+\\mathrm{ve} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{S}=-\\mathrm{ve}}$<br>Equation, $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{G}=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S}=+\\mathrm{ve}-(-\\mathrm{ve})}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{G}=+\\mathrm{ve}}$; Non - spontaneous at all temperatures.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17387,'chemistry',50,838,1874,'Which of the following reactions are disproportionation reaction?<br>(a) $\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{Cu}^{+} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+\\mathrm{Cu}^{\\circ}}$<br>(b) $\\mathrm{3 \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{2-}+4 \\mathrm{H}^{+} \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}+\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$<br>(c) $\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{KMnO}_{4} \\stackrel{\\Delta}{\\longrightarrow} \\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}+\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$<br>(d) $\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}+3 \\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\longrightarrow 5 \\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+4 \\mathrm{H}^{\\oplus}}$<br>Select the correct option from the following','[{"text": "(a) and (b) only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(a), (b) and (c)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(a), (c) and (d)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(a) and (d) only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'(a) $\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{Cu}^{+1} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}{ }^{2+}+\\mathrm{Cu}}$ [Disproportionation]<br>${\\rm{3}}\\mathop {\\rm{M}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 6}}} {\\rm{nO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{ + 4}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}} \\to $<br>(b) ${\\rm{2}}\\mathop {{\\rm{ M}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 7}}} {\\rm{nO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{ + M}}\\mathop {{\\rm{ n}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 4}}} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$ [Disproportionation]<br>$\\mathop {{\\text{2KMn}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}}\\limits^{ + 7} \\xrightarrow{\\Delta }{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{2}}}\\mathop {\\text{M}}\\limits^{{\\text{ + 6}}} {\\text{n}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}$<br>(c) $\\mathop {{\\rm{Mn}}{{\\rm{O}}_2}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 4}}} {\\rm{ + }}\\mathop {{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits^{\\rm{0}} \\therefore [{\\rm{Redox}}]$<br>(d) ${\\rm{2}}\\mathop {\\rm{M}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 7}}} {\\rm{nO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{ +  3M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2  +  }}}}{\\rm{  +  2}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}} \\to {\\rm{5}}\\mathop {\\rm{M}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 4}}} {\\rm{n}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ +  4}}{{\\rm{H}}^{{\\rm{  +  }}}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,[{\\text{redox}}]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17388,'chemistry',46,444,890,'Read the Statement ${\\mathrm{A}}$ and Statement  B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Since fluorine is more electronegative than nitrogen, the net dipole moment of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NF}_{3}}}$ is greater than ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$.</div><div class="column-right">In ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$ ,the orbital dipole due to lone pair and the dipole moment of NH bonds are in opposite direction,but in ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NF}_{3}}}$  the orbital dipole due to lone pair and dipole moments of N-F bonds are in same direction.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_17388_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Resultant dipole moment in<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}=4.90 \\times 10^{-30} \\mathrm{Cm}}}$<br>Resultant dipole moment in ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NF}_{3}=0.80 \\times 10^{-30} \\mathrm{Cm}}}$<br> In ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$, the orbital dipole due to lone pair is in the same direction as the resultant dipole moment of the N-H bonds, whereas in ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NF}_{3}}}$, the orbital dipole is in the direction opposite to the resultant dipole moment of the three N-F bonds.<br>So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17389,'chemistry',49,626,1272,'$\\mathrm{NaOH}$ is a strong base. What will be $\\mathrm{pH}$ of  ${\\text{5}}{\\text{.0}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 2 }}}}{\\text{M }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{NaOH}}$ solution? ${\\text{(log2 = 0}}{\\text{.3)}}$','[{"text": "14.00", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "13.70", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "13.00", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12.70", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, $\\mathrm {\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=5 \\times 10^{-2}}$<br>$\\rm { \\therefore p O H=-\\log 5 \\times 10^{-2} \\\\ =-\\log 5+2 \\log 10=1.30}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\because p H+p O H=14}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\because p H=14-p O H}$<br>$\\mathrm {=14-1.30=12.70}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17390,'chemistry',48,762,1659,'A reaction has both $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{S}}$ ${\\rm{ -  ve}}$. The rate of reaction','[{"text": "Cannot be predicted for change in temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increases with increase in temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increases with decreases in temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "remains unaffected by change in temperature.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$\\rm{\\Delta G=\\Delta H-T \\Delta S=-V e}$<br>Given $\\mathrm{\\Delta S=-V e, \\Delta H=-V e}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ To get $\\mathrm{\\Delta G=-V e}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\Delta S}$ must be less than $\\mathrm{\\Delta H}$ i.e.,<br>$\\rm{\\Delta H>T \\Delta S}$<br>Thus the reaction is exotherms and rate of reaction increases with decreases of temperature.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17434,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The oxidation state of sulphur in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 6}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 5}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 7}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let, the oxidation state of sulphur in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}$ be $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{x}}$.<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore 2(+1)+2(\\mathrm{x})+7(-2)=0}$<br>${\\rm{2 + 2x - 14 = 0}}$<br>${\\rm{2x = 12x =  + 6}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17392,'chemistry',49,626,1271,'What is the ${\\text{pH}}{\\mkern 1mu} $ of a ${\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 4}}}}{\\text{ M}}$  ${\\text{O}}{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ - }}}$ solution at $330 \\mathrm{~K}$, if ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{w}}}$  at $330 \\mathrm{~K}$ is $10^{-13.6}$ ?','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9.6", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given at $\\mathrm {330 \\mathrm{~K}}$,<br>${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{w}}}{\\text{ = 1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 13}}{\\text{.6}}}}$ <br> ${\\text{i}}{\\text{.e}}{\\text{.,}}\\quad {\\text{p}}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{w}}}{\\text{ = pH}}\\,{\\text{ + }}\\,{\\text{pOH}}$ <br> $\\because {\\text{pOH}}\\,{\\text{ = }}\\,{\\text{ - log}}\\left[ {{\\text{O}}{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ - }}}} \\right]$ <br> $13.6 = {\\text{pH}}\\,{\\text{ + }}\\,{\\text{pOH}}$ <br> $ =  - \\log {10^{ - 4}}$ <br> $ = {\\text{pH}} + 4$ <br> $ = 4$ <br> $\\therefore {\\text{pH}} = 13.6 - 4 = 9.6$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17393,'chemistry',46,445,891,'Which of the following is least ionic?','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "KCl", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AgCl", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If electronegativity difference between two atoms is more then compound will be ionic in nature. Electronegativity values of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Co}, \\mathrm{K}, \\mathrm{Ba}, \\mathrm{Ag}}}$ and Cl are respectively ${\\mathrm{1.88,0.82,0.89,1.93}}$ and 3.2.<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}=3.2-1.88=1.32}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KCl}=3.2-0.82=2.38}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}=3.2-0.89=2.31}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{AgCl}=3.2-1.93=1.27}}$<br>Hence, AgCl is least ionic compound. <br>So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17394,'chemistry',48,762,1659,'Match the column I with colum II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. $\\mathrm{\\begin{array}{l}\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=-\\mathrm{ve} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{S}= \\\\-\\mathrm{ve} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{G}=-\\mathrm{ve}\\end{array}}$<br>B. $\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}{{\\rm{\\Delta H =  - ve;\\Delta S = }}}\\\\{{\\rm{ - ve;(\\Delta G) =  + ve}}}\\end{array}$<br>C. $\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}{{\\rm{\\Delta H =  + ve;\\Delta S = }}}\\\\{{\\rm{ + ve;\\Delta G =  + ve}}}\\end{array}$<br>D. $\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}{{\\rm{\\Delta H =  + ve;\\Delta S = }}}\\\\{{\\rm{ + ve;\\Delta G =  - ve}}}\\end{array}$</div><div class="column-right">P. $\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}{{\\rm{Reaction}}\\,{\\rm{will}}\\,{\\rm{be}}\\,{\\rm{non - }}}\\\\{{\\rm{spontaneous}}\\,{\\rm{at}}\\,{\\rm{high}}}\\\\{{\\rm{temperature}}}\\end{array}$<br>Q. $\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{Reaction}}\\,{\\rm{will}}\\,{\\rm{be}}\\,{\\rm{non - }}}\\\\{{\\rm{spontaneous}}\\,{\\rm{at}}\\,{\\rm{low}}}\\\\{{\\rm{temperature}}}\\end{array}$<br>R. $\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{Reaction}}\\,{\\rm{will}}\\,{\\rm{be}}}\\\\{{\\rm{spontaneous}}\\,{\\rm{at}}\\,{\\rm{low}}}\\\\{{\\rm{temperature}}}\\end{array}$<br>S. $\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{Reaction}}\\,{\\rm{will}}\\,{\\rm{be}}}\\\\{{\\rm{spontaneous}}\\,{\\rm{at}}\\,{\\rm{high}}}\\\\{{\\rm{temperature}}}\\end{array}$</div></div>','[{"text": "A - Q, B - R, C - P, D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - P, C - Q, D - S", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - R, B - Q, C - S, D - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q, B - S, C - P, D - R", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17395,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In the neutral or faintly alkaline medium, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}$ oxidses iodide into iodate. The change in oxidation state of manganese in this reaction is from','[{"text": "+6 to +4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+7 to +3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+6 to +5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+7 to +4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\rm{\\underset{\\substack{\\downarrow \\\\+7}}{\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}+\\mathrm{I}^{-} \\dfrac{\\text { Neutral }}{\\text { or weak alkaline medium }} \\underset{\\substack{\\downarrow \\\\+4}}{\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}+\\mathrm{IO}_{3}^{-}}$<br>"Mn" changes O.N. from +7 to +4 .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17396,'chemistry',49,623,1262,'Which one of the following arrangements represents the correct order of the proton affinity of the given species?','[{"text": "${{\\\\text{I}}^{\\\\text{ - }}}{\\\\text{ < }}{{\\\\text{F}}^{\\\\text{ - }}}{\\\\text{ < H}}{{\\\\text{S}}^{\\\\text{ - }}}{\\\\text{ < NH}}_{\\\\text{2}}^{\\\\text{ - }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{H}}{{\\\\text{S}}^{\\\\text{ - }}}{\\\\text{ < NH}}_{\\\\text{2}}^{\\\\text{ - }}{\\\\text{ < }}{{\\\\text{F}}^{\\\\text{ - }}}{\\\\text{ < }}{{\\\\text{I}}^{\\\\text{ - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{F}}^{\\\\text{ - }}}{\\\\text{ < }}{{\\\\text{I}}^{\\\\text{ - }}}{\\\\text{ < NH}}_{\\\\text{2}}^{\\\\text{ - }}{\\\\text{ < H}}{{\\\\text{S}}^{\\\\text{ - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{NH}}_{\\\\text{2}}^{\\\\text{ - }}{\\\\text{ < H}}{{\\\\text{S}}^{\\\\text{ - }}}{\\\\text{ < }}{{\\\\text{I}}^{\\\\text{ - }}}{\\\\text{ < }}{{\\\\text{F}}^{\\\\text{ - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The species with the greatest proton affinity will be the strongest base, and its conjugate acid will be the weakest acid. The weakest acid will have the smallest value of$\\rm {{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}}}$ Since HI is a stronger acid than HF which is a stronger acid than $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}}$, a partial order of proton affinity is<br>$\\rm {{{\\text{I}}^{\\text{ - }}}{\\text{ < }}{{\\text{F}}^{\\text{ - }}}{\\text{ < H}}{{\\text{S}}^{\\text{ - }}}}$<br>Since $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}$ is a very weak acid, $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{-}}$must be a very strong base. Therefore the correct order of proton affinity is<br>$\\rm {{{\\text{I}}^{\\text{ - }}}{\\text{ < }}{{\\text{F}}^{\\text{ - }}}{\\text{ < HS < NH}}_{\\text{2}}^{\\text{ - }}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17398,'chemistry',48,762,1659,'In which of the following reactions, standard reaction entropy change $\\mathrm{\\left(\\Delta S^{o}\\right)}$ is positive and standard Gibb\'s energy change $\\mathrm{\\left(\\Delta G^{o}\\right)}$ decreases sharply with increasing temperature?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{M g(s)+\\\\dfrac{1}{2} O_{2}(g) \\\\longrightarrow M g O(s)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\mathrm{C}}$ (graphite) $\\\\mathrm{+\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}(g) \\\\longrightarrow \\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}(g)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}}$ (graphite) $\\\\mathrm{+\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}(g) \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{CO}(\\\\mathrm{g})}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CO}(g)+\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}(g) \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}(g)}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{C(s)+\\dfrac{1}{2} O_{2}(g) \\longrightarrow C O(g) ; \\Delta n+\\dfrac{1}{2}}$.<br>Also the moles of gases increase and therefore entropy change $\\mathrm{(\\Delta S)}$ is positive. An increase in temperature will cause more change in $\\mathrm{T \\Delta S}$. Also it is a combustion reaction and thus $\\mathrm{\\Delta H=-v e}$<br>Since $\\mathrm{\\Delta G=\\Delta H-T \\Delta S}$<br>$\\rm{=-v e-(+v e)=-v e}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17695,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'When a manganous salt is fused with the mixture of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}}$ and the solid $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaOH}}$, the oxidation number of Mn changes from +2 to','[{"text": "+4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "+7", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}+\\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}+\\mathrm{OH}^{-} \\rightarrow \\stackrel{(+6)}{\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{2-}}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{NO}}$<br>The oxidation number of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}}$ changes from +2 to +6 in this reaction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17399,'chemistry',50,836,1861,'The process in which addition of oxygen or removal of hydrogen or loss of electron observed is known as','[{"text": "oxidation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "reduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "redox reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathop {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 6}}} {\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{7}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{ + 6}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{2C}}{{\\rm{r}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}$<br>Change in oxidation state $\\mathrm{=+3}$ per chromium atom.<br>1 mole of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}}$ required $\\mathrm{=6 \\mathrm{e}^{-}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore 0.2}$ moles of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}}$ required $\\mathrm{=0.2 \\times 6}$<br>$\\rm{=1.2 \\text { moles of electrons }}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17400,'chemistry',49,617,1241,'At a certain temperature, only ${\\text{50% }}\\,\\,{\\text{HI}}$ is dissociated into $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and  ${{\\text{I}}_{\\text{2}}}$ at equilibrium. The equilibrium constant is:','[{"text": "1.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.25", "isCorrect": true}]',3,' <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/49_17400_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Given, $\\alpha  = 50\\%  = 0.5$<br>${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{c}}} = \\frac{{\\left( {\\frac{\\alpha }{2}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{\\alpha }{2}} \\right)}}{{{{\\left( {1 - \\alpha } \\right)}^2}}} = \\frac{{0.25 \\times 0.25}}{{{{\\left( {0.5} \\right)}^2}}} = \\frac{1}{4} = 0.25$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17402,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'The process in which decrease in oxidation number is known as','[{"text": "oxidation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "reduction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "redox reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Decrease in oxidation number is called reduction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17403,'chemistry',48,762,1659,'Considering the reaction:<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{(\\mathrm{s})}+\\mathrm{O}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+393.5 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$<br>the signs of $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}, \\Delta \\mathrm{S}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{G}}$ respectively are','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ + , - , - }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - , + , + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - , - , - }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - , + , - }}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{(\\mathrm{s})}+\\mathrm{O}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+393.5 \\mathrm{~kJ}}$<br>$\\rm{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=-, \\Delta \\mathrm{S}=+, \\Delta \\mathrm{G}=-}$<br>Its an exothermic reaction. Therefore $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=-\\mathrm{ve}}$ as energy is released during an exothermic reaction. As its a spontaneous reaction, $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{G}=-\\mathrm{ve}}$. Also entropy or randomness increases while moving from LHS to RHS. Therefore $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{S}=+\\mathrm{ve}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17405,'chemistry',49,621,1253,'What happens when an inert gas is added to an equilibrium keeping volume unchanged?','[{"text": "More product will form", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less product will form", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "More reactant will form", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Equilibrium will remain unchanged", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'On adding inert gas at constant volume, the total pressure of the system is increased, but the partial pressure of each reactant and product remains the same. Hence no effect on the state of equilibrium.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17406,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'The conversion of sugar $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{12} \\mathrm{H}_{22} \\mathrm{O}_{11} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CO}_{2}}$ is','[{"text": "oxidation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "reduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both oxidation and reduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neither oxidation nor reduction", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In this reaction oxidation occur.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17407,'chemistry',48,761,1655,'Enthalpy of vaporization of benzene is +35.3 $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}}$ at its boiling point, $\\mathrm{80^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}}$. The entropy change in the transition of the vapour to liquid at its boiling point $\\left( {{\\rm{inJ}}{{\\rm{K}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{\\,mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}} \\right)$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 441}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 100}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 441}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 100}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to Gibbs\' formula,<br>$\\rm{\\Delta \\mathrm{G}=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S}}$<br>At equilibrium, $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{G}=0}$<br>And entropy decreases on going from vapour to liquid.i.e., $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S}}$<br>$\\rm{\\Delta \\mathrm{S}=-\\dfrac{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}}{\\mathrm{T}}=-\\dfrac{35300}{80+273}=-100 \\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17408,'chemistry',49,623,1262,'The conjugate base of hydrazoic acid is:','[{"text": "${{\\\\text{N}}^{{\\\\text{ -3}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{N}}_{\\\\text{3}}^{\\\\text{ - }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{N}}_{\\\\text{2}}^{\\\\text{ - }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{HN}}_{\\\\text{3}}^{\\\\text{ - }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm {\\underset{\\text { Hydrazoicacid }}{\\mathrm{N}_{3} H} \\rightleftharpoons N_{3}^{-}+H^{+}}$<br>i.e., conjugate base of hydrazoic acid is $\\mathrm {N_{3}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17410,'chemistry',50,836,1862,'The compound that can work both as an oxidising as well as a reducing agent is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{BaO}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{In}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ the O.N. of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}}$ is ${\\rm{ - 1}}$ which can be increased to 0 or decreased to ${\\rm{ - 2}}$ hence $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ can work as reducing and oxidising agent.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17411,'chemistry',48,762,1659,'For vaporisation of water at 1 atmospheric pressure the values of $\\mathrm{\\Delta H}$ and $\\mathrm{\\Delta S}$ are $\\mathrm{40.63 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$ and ${\\rm{108}}{\\rm{.8}}\\,{\\rm{J}}\\,{{\\rm{K}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{\\,mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$, respectively. The temperature when Gibb\'s energy change $\\mathrm{(\\Delta G)}$ for this transformation will be zero is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{273.4 \\\\mathrm{~K}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{393.4 \\\\mathrm{~K}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{373.4 \\\\mathrm{~K}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{293.4 \\\\mathrm{~K}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{\\Delta G=\\Delta H-T \\Delta S}$<br>$\\Delta {\\rm{G = 0}},$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta {\\rm{H = T}}\\Delta {\\rm{S}}$<br>$\\mathrm{T=\\dfrac{\\Delta H}{\\Delta S}=\\dfrac{40.63 \\times 10^{3}}{108.8}=373.4 K}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17413,'chemistry',49,621,1250,'Consider the following equilibrium $\\mathrm{AgCl} \\downarrow+2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3} \\rightleftharpoons\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{2}\\right]^{+}+\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$<br>White precipitate of $\\mathrm{AgCl}$ appears on adding which of the following?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aqueous $\\\\mathrm{NaCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aqueous $\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Aqueous $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm {2 \\mathrm{HNO}_{3}(\\mathrm{aq})+\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{2}\\right]^{+}+\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\longrightarrow \\mathrm{AgCl}(s) \\downarrow+2 \\mathrm{NH}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}}$<br>When nitric acid is added to amine solution, solution is made acidic and the complex ion dissociates and liberate silver ion to recombine with chloride ion. This is the conformatory test for solver in group 1.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17414,'chemistry',50,836,1861,'When $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}$ acts as an oxidising agent and ultimately forms $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-2}, \\mathrm{MnO}_{2}, \\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}^{+2}}$, then the number of electrons transferred in each case respectively is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{4,3,1,5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{1,5,3,7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{1,3,4,5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{3,5,7,1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}$ the O.N. of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}}$ is +7 , in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-2}+6}$, in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+4}$ in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+3}$ and in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}}$, it is +2 . The difference being 1, 3, 4 and 5 respectively.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17415,'chemistry',48,761,1654,'For the gas phase reaction, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}(g) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{PCl}_{3}(g)+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(g)}$<br>Which of the following conditions are correct?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta H=0}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta S < 0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta H>0}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta S>0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta H < 0}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta S < 0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta H>0}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta S < 0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{\\Delta H=\\Delta E+\\Delta n_{(g)} R T}$<br>For the reaction,<br>$\\rm{ \\mathrm{PCl}_{5}(g) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{PCl}_{3}(g)+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(g) \\\\ \\Delta n=2-1=1}$<br>Thus, the value of $\\mathrm{\\Delta H}$ is positive or $\\mathrm{>0}$.<br>$\\rm{\\Delta G=\\Delta H-T \\Delta S}$<br>For a spontaneous reaction, $\\mathrm{\\Delta G}$ must be negative. Since in this reaction $\\mathrm{\\Delta H}$ is positive, so for thenegative value of $\\mathrm{\\Delta G, \\Delta S}$ must be positive or $\\mathrm{>0}$.<br>Hence $\\mathrm{\\Delta H>0, \\Delta S>0}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17416,'chemistry',49,617,1243,'For the decomposition of the compound, represented as<br>${\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{COON}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{(s)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{(g)  +  C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g); }}$<br>${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{P}}}{\\text{ = 2}}.{\\text{9}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 5}}}}\\,{\\text{at}}{{\\text{m}}^{\\text{3}}}$<br>If the reaction is started with $1 \\mathrm{~mol}$ of the compound, the total pressure at equilibrium would be:','[{"text": "$1.94 \\\\times 10^{-2} \\\\mathrm{~atm}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.82 \\\\times 10^{-2} \\\\mathrm{~atm}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7.66 \\\\times 10^{-2} \\\\mathrm{~atm}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$38.8 \\\\times 10^{-2} \\\\mathrm{~atm}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{NH}_{2} \\mathrm{COONH}_{4}(s) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}(g)+\\mathrm{CO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>$\\rm {K_{P}=\\dfrac{\\left(P_{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}\\right)^{2} \\times\\left(P_{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}}\\right)}{P_{\\mathrm{NH}_{2}} \\mathrm{COONH}_{4}{ }^{(S)}}=\\left(P_{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}\\right)^{2} \\times\\left(P_{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}}\\right)}$<br>As evident by the reaction, $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}$ and $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CO}_{2}}$ are formed in molar ratio of $\\mathrm {2: 1}$. Thus if $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{P}}$ is the total pressure of the system at equilibrium, then<br>$\\rm { P_{N H_{3}}=\\dfrac{2 \\times P}{3} \\quad P_{C O_{2}}=\\dfrac{1 \\times P}{3} \\\\ K_{P}=\\left(\\dfrac{2 P}{3}\\right)^{2} \\times \\dfrac{P}{3}=\\dfrac{4 P^{3}}{27}}$<br>Given $\\mathrm {K_{P}=2.9 \\times 10^{-5}}$<br>$\\rm { \\therefore 2.9 \\times 10^{-5}=\\dfrac{4 P^{3}}{27} \\\\ P^{3}=\\dfrac{2.9 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 27}{4} \\\\ P=\\left(\\dfrac{2.9 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 27}{4}\\right)^{1 / 3}=5.82 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{~atm}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17418,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'Which species are oxidised and reduced in the following reaction?<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{FeC}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}+\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}}$','[{"text": "Oxidised: Fe, C; Reduced: $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Mn}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Oxidised: Fe; Reduced: Mn", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reduced: Fe, Mn; Oxidised: C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reduced: C; Oxidised: Mn, Fe", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Fe}}$ and carbon in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{FeC}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}}$ are oxidised<br>$\\rm{\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\\mathrm{e}}$<br>$\\rm{\\left(\\mathrm{C}^{2+}\\right)_{2} \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{C}^{4+}+2 \\mathrm{e}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}}$ is $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}$ is reduced.<br>$\\rm{5 \\mathrm{e}+\\mathrm{Mn}^{7+} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17419,'chemistry',48,761,1655,'One mole of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaCl}(\\mathrm{s})}$ on melting absorbed 30.5 $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{kJ}}$ of heat and its entropy is increased by $\\mathrm{28.8 \\mathrm{JK}^{-1}}$. The melting point of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaCl}}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{1059 \\\\mathrm{~K}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{30.5 \\\\mathrm{~K}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{28.8 \\\\mathrm{~K}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{28800 \\\\mathrm{~K}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaCl}_{(\\mathrm{s})} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{NaCl}_{(1)}}$<br>Given: $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=30.5 \\mathrm{KJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}}$<br>$\\rm{\\Delta \\mathrm{S}=28.8 \\mathrm{JK}^{-1}=28.8 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{KJK}^{-1}}$<br>By using $\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{S}=\\dfrac{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}}{\\mathrm{T}}}$<br>$\\rm{=\\dfrac{30.5}{28.8 \\times 10^{-3}}=1059 \\mathrm{~K}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17435,'chemistry',49,621,1255,'The increase of pressure on ice $\\rightleftharpoons$ water system at constant temperature will lead to','[{"text": "A decrease in the entropy of the system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An increase in the Gibb\'s energy of the system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No effect on the equilibrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A shift of the equilibrium in the forward direction", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Volume of ice is greater than that of water. The direction in which the reaction will proceed can be predicted by applying Le-Chatelier\'s principle<br>Pressure $\\mathrm {\\alpha \\dfrac{1}{\\text { Volume }}}$<br>So equilibrium, will shift forward.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17421,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'Assuming that the degree of hydrolysis is small, the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of  ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.1}}\\,\\,{\\text{M}}$  solution of sodium acetate $\\left( {{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}}{\\text{ = 1}}{\\text{.0}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 5}}}}} \\right)$  will be:','[{"text": "5.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9.0", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Sodium acetate is a salt of strong base and weak acid.<br>$\\mathrm {\\therefore \\rm {\\,{\\text{pH}}\\,}=7+\\dfrac{1}{2} p K_{a}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\log c}$   where  ${\\text{P}}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}}{\\text{ =  - log}}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}}$<br>${\\text{  = 7 + }}\\frac{{\\text{5}}}{{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ - }}\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{2}}}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\left\\{ {\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {\\text{ =  - log1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 5}}}}{\\text{ = 5}}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{logc = log1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}{\\text{ =  - 1}}} \n\\end{array}} \\right\\}{\\text{  = 9}}{\\text{.0}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17422,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'When ethane is burnt in presence of excess of oxygen, the oxidation number of carbon changes by','[{"text": "+8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+7", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "+3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{CO}_{2}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$<br>O.N. of carbon changes from ${\\rm{ - 3}}$ (in ethane) to $\\mathrm{+4\\left(\\right.}$ in $\\mathrm{\\left.\\mathrm{CO}_{2}\\right)}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17423,'chemistry',48,762,1659,'For the reaction:<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{X}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}(\\mathrm{l}) \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{XO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>${\\rm{\\Delta U = 2}}{\\rm{.1}}\\,{\\rm{cal,\\Delta S = 20}}\\,{\\rm{cal}}\\,{{\\rm{K}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$ at $\\mathrm{300 \\mathrm{~K}}$. Hence<br>$\\mathrm{\\Delta G}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{2}}{\\\\rm{.7}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{k}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{cal}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 2}}{\\\\rm{.7}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{k}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{cal}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{9}}{\\\\rm{.3}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{k}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{cal}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 9}}{\\\\rm{.3}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{k}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{cal}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given $\\mathrm{\\Delta U=2.1 \\mathrm{cal}, \\Delta S=20 \\mathrm{cal} K^{-1}}$<br>${\\rm{T = 300K}}$<br>$\\Delta {\\rm{n = 2 - 0 = e}}$<br>$\\because \\Delta {\\text{H = }}\\Delta {\\text{U + }}\\Delta {{\\text{n}}_{\\text{g}}}{\\text{RT}}$<br>$\\Delta {\\rm{H = 2}}.{\\rm{1 + 2}} \\times \\frac{{\\rm{2}}}{{{\\rm{1000}}}} \\times {\\rm{300}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 2}}.{\\rm{1 + 1}}.{\\rm{2 = 3}}.{\\rm{3}}\\,{\\rm{k}}\\,{\\rm{cal}}$<br>$\\rm{\\text { Now, } \\Delta G=\\Delta H-T \\Delta S}$${\\rm{ = 3}}.{\\rm{3 - 300}} \\times \\frac{{{\\rm{20}}}}{{{\\rm{1000}}}}{\\rm{ =  - 2}}.{\\rm{7}}\\,{\\rm{k}}\\,{\\rm{cal}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
