(17480,'chemistry',46,451,909,'Select the compound from the following that will show intramolecular hydrogen bonding.','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{OH}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If hydrogen bond is formed within the molecule, it is known as intramolecular hydrogen bond.<br>Ex:  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/46_17480_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17481,'chemistry',49,629,1279,'$20 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.1 M  }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}$  solution is added to 30 $\\mathrm{mL}$ of ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.2  M N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{OH}}$ solution. The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of the resultant mixture is $\\left[\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}\\right.$ of $\\left.\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{OH}=4.7\\right]$','[{"text": "9.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9.0", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5.2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.1M}}$ ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$  means $\\mathrm {0.2 \\mathrm{~N} \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$<br>milli equivalents of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}=20 \\times 0.2=4}$<br>milli equivalents of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{OH}=30 \\times 0.2=6}$<br>After neutralisation, milli equivalents of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{OH}}$ are 2 and salt $\\mathrm {\\left[\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right]}$ milli equivalents are 4.<br>$\\rm { p O H=p K_{b}+\\log \\dfrac{[\\text { salt }]}{[\\text { base }]} \\\\ 4.7+\\log \\dfrac{4}{2} \\\\ p O H=5 \\\\\\therefore \\quad  p H=14-p O H=14-5=9}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17482,'chemistry',50,839,1878,'The number of electrons involved in the reduction of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}}$ ion in acidic solution to $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}^{+}}$is','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{Cr}^{6+}\\right)_{2}+6 \\mathrm{e} \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17484,'chemistry',46,449,901,'The linear combination of atomic orbitals to form molecular orbitals takes place only when the combining atomic orbitals<br>(I) have the same energy<br>(II) have the minimum overlap<br>(III) have same symmetry about the molecular axis<br>(IV) have different symmetry about the molecular axis<br>Choose the most appropriate from the options given below :','[{"text": "(I), (II), (III) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II), (III), (IV) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (III) only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(II) and (IV) only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Conditions for the combination of atomic orbitals are as follows :<br>(I) The combining atomic orbitals must have same or nearly same energy.<br>(II) The combining atomic orbitals must have the same symmetry about the molecular axis.<br>(III) The combining atomic orbitals must overlap to the maximum extent.<br>So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17486,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'In the reaction<br>$\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{HCl}+\\mathrm{S}}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}}$ acts as an oxidising agent.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}}$ are oxidised.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}}$ is oxidised while $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}}$ is reduced.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}}$ acts as an oxidising agent.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In $\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{HCl}+\\mathrm{S}}$<br>O.N. of S changes from ${\\rm{ - 2}}$ to 0 (hence oxidised)<br>O.N. of Fe changes from +3 to +2 hence reduced.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17489,'chemistry',49,617,1243,'For a reaction at equilibrium<br>$\\mathrm{A}(\\mathrm{g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{B}(\\mathrm{g})+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{C}(\\mathrm{g})$<br>the relation between dissociation constant $(\\mathrm{K})$, degree of dissociation $(\\alpha)$ and equilibrium pressure ${\\text{(p)}}$ is given by','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{K  = }}\\\\dfrac{{{{\\\\rm{\\\\alpha }}^{\\\\dfrac{{\\\\rm{1}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\dfrac{{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}}}}}{{{{\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{1  +  }}\\\\dfrac{{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{\\\\alpha }}} \\\\right)}^{\\\\dfrac{{\\\\rm{1}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}}}{\\\\rm{(1 - \\\\alpha )}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{K  =  }}\\\\dfrac{{{{\\\\rm{\\\\alpha }}^{\\\\dfrac{{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\dfrac{{\\\\rm{1}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}}}}}{{{{{\\\\rm{(2 + \\\\alpha )}}}^{\\\\dfrac{{\\\\rm{1}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}}}{\\\\rm{(1 - \\\\alpha )}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{K }}\\\\dfrac{{{\\\\rm{ =  (\\\\alpha p}}{{\\\\rm{)}}^{\\\\dfrac{{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}}}}}{{{{\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{1  +  }}\\\\dfrac{{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{\\\\alpha }}} \\\\right)}^{\\\\dfrac{{\\\\rm{1}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}}}{\\\\rm{(1 - \\\\alpha )}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{K  = }}\\\\dfrac{{{{{\\\\rm{(\\\\alpha p)}}}^{\\\\dfrac{{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}}}}}{{{\\\\rm{(1 + \\\\alpha )(1 - \\\\alpha }}{{\\\\rm{)}}^{\\\\dfrac{{\\\\rm{1}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{A(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{B(g) + }}\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C(g}})$ <br> $\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n  {{\\text{Initial}}\\,{\\text{moles}}}&1&0&0 \\\\ \n  {{\\text{Moles}}\\,{\\text{at}}\\,{\\text{Eq}}{\\text{.}}}&{1 - \\alpha }&\\alpha &{\\dfrac{\\alpha }{2}} \n\\end{array}$ <br> ${\\text{K = }}\\dfrac{{{\\text{(}}{{\\text{n}}_{\\text{B}}}{\\text{)}} \\cdot {{{\\text{(}}{{\\text{n}}_{\\text{C}}}{\\text{)}}}^{{\\text{1/2}}}}}}{{{{\\text{n}}_{\\text{A}}}}}{\\left[ {\\frac{{{{\\text{P}}_{{\\text{Total}}}}}}{{\\sum {\\text{n}}}}} \\right]^{\\Delta {{\\text{n}}_{{\\text{(g)}}}}}}$ <br> $ = \\dfrac{{\\alpha \\dfrac{\\alpha }{2}}}{{1 - \\alpha }}{\\left[ {\\dfrac{{\\text{p}}}{{1 - \\alpha  + \\alpha  + \\dfrac{\\alpha }{2}}}} \\right]^{1/2}}$ <br>$ = \\dfrac{{{\\alpha ^{3/2}}}}{{{2^{1/2}}(1 - \\alpha )}}\\left[ {\\dfrac{{{{\\text{p}}^{1/2}}}}{{{{\\left( {1 + \\dfrac{\\alpha }{2}} \\right)}^{1/2}}}}} \\right]$ <br> ${\\text{K}} = \\frac{{{\\alpha ^{3/2}}{{\\text{p}}^{1/2}}}}{{(1 - \\alpha ){{(2 + \\alpha )}^{1/2}}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17490,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'In the reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$ the oxidizing agent is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "both $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ gets reduced to $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17514,'chemistry',49,625,1269,'Which of the following statements are correct?<br>(I) Ionic product of water $\\left(\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{w}}\\right)=\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=$ $10^{-14} \\mathrm{M}^{2}$<br>(II) At $298 \\mathrm{~K},\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=10^{-7}$.<br>(III) $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{W}}$ does not depends upon temperature.<br> (IV) Molarity of pure water ${\\text{ = 55}}{\\text{.55}}\\,\\,{\\text{M}}$','[{"text": "(I), (II) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (II) and (IV)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(I) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\rm {{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{W}}}}$ value depends on temperature. As temperature increases, $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{W}}}$ value also increases. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17494,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'(I) $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}+\\mathrm{O}_{3} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+2 \\mathrm{O}_{2}}$<br>(II) $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}+\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Ag}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$<br>Role of hydrogen peroxide in the above reactions is respectively','[{"text": "Oxidizing in (I) and reducing in (II)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Reducing in (I) and oxidizing in (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reducing in (I) and (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxidizing in (I) and (II)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the reaction, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17494_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Since $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ oxidises, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{3}}$ into $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$, thus it behaves as an oxidising agent.<br>Further, in the reaction, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17494_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Here $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ reduces $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$ into metallic silver [Ag] (as oxidation number is reducing from +1 to 0 ). Thus, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ bahaves as a reducing agent.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17497,'chemistry',50,836,1862,'Which of the following oxides can act both as an oxidising agent as well as reducing agent?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{P}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{5}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{2}}$ oxides can act both as an oxidising agent as well as reducing agent. It behaves as a reducing agent in moist conditions. It convert iron (III) ions to iron (II) ions and decolourises acidified potassium permanganate (VII) solution.<br>$\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\\mathrm{SO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}+4 \\mathrm{H}^{+}}$<br>When $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{2}}$ reacts with $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}}$, it produces sulphur and water. In this reaction, \' $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{2}}$ \' act as an oxidising agent. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17497_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17501,'chemistry',49,616,1233,'Identify the correct statements below regarding chemical equilibrium.<br>(I) All chemical reactions which are in equilibrium are irreversible.<br>(II) Equilibrium is achieved when the forward reaction rate equals the reverse reaction rate.<br>(III) Equilibrium is achieved when the concentrations of reactants and product remain constant.<br>(IV) Equilibrium is dynamic in nature','[{"text": "(I), (II) and (IV) are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(I), (II), (III) and (IV) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "only (II) is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "only III is correct", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'All the statements are correct. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17502,'chemistry',50,836,1862,'Amongst the following, the strongest oxidising agent is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{O}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Among these, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{3}}$ has maximum value of standard reduction potential $\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}=+2.07 \\mathrm{~V}\\right)}$, so it is strongest oxidising agent.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17505,'chemistry',49,619,1247,'Which of the following statement (s) is/are correct?<br>(I) $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}$ is negative, then the reaction is spontaneous and proceeds in the forward direction<br>(II) $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}$ is positive, then reaction is non spontaneous<br>(III) $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}$ is 0, then reaction is at equilibrium','[{"text": "(I), (II) and (III) are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(I) and (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II) and (III) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "only (III) is correct", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'All the three statements are correct. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17506,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Oxidation state of nitrogen in nitric oxide is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Oxidation state of nitrogen in nitric oxide (NO) is +2.<br>Molecular formula of nitric oxide $\\mathrm{=\\mathrm{NO}}$<br>${\\rm{x - 2 = 0}}$<br>${\\rm{x =  + 2}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17509,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In which among the following compounds, oxidation number of nitrogen is +5 ?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Oxidation number of nitrogen is +5 in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}$.<br>${\\rm{1 + x + 3}}({\\rm{ - 2}}){\\rm{ = 0}}$<br>${\\rm{x - 5 = 0}}$<br>${\\rm{x =  + 5}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17510,'chemistry',49,623,1262,'Which of the following statement (s) is/are correct?<br>(I) Water has ability to act both as an acid and a base<br>(II) In pure water one $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ molecule donate proton and acts an acid and another water molecule accepts a proton and acts as a base.','[{"text": "Both (I) and (II)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neither (I) nor (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only (I)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only (II)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Both the statements are correct. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17513,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'What is the oxidation number of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}}$ in ${\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}$','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 6}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 8}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 8}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 6}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Oxidation number of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}}$ in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{2}}$ ion is +6.<br>$\\rm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{2-}}$<br>Let, oxidation of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}=x}$<br>We knwo that,<br>Putting the value in formula<br>${\\rm{x + 4}}({\\rm{ - 2}}){\\rm{ =  - 2}}$<br>${\\rm{x - 8 =  - 2}}$<br>${\\rm{x =  - 2 + 8 =  + 6}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17539,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Atomic number of an element is 22 . The highest O.S. exhibited by it in its compounds is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 4}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The element is Ti (At. no. 22). Electronic configuration is $\\mathrm{1 s^{2}, 2 s^{2} p^{6}, 3 s^{2} p^{6} d^{2}, 4 s^{2}}$. The energy level of $\\mathrm{3 \\mathrm{~d}}$ and $\\mathrm{4 \\mathrm{~s}}$ is very close. It can have $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}}$ O.S.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17517,'chemistry',49,621,1253,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">If the volume is kept constant and an inert gas such as argon is added which does not take part in the reaction, the equilibrium remains undisturbed.<br></div><div class="column-right">The addition of an inert gas at constant volume does not change the partial pressure or the molar concentrations of the substance involved in the reaction.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If the volume is kept constant and an inert gas such as argon is added which does not take part in the reaction, the equilibrium remains undisturbed. It is because the addition of an inert gas at constant volume does not change the partial pressure or the molar concentration of reaction quotient changes only if the added gas is reactant or product involved in the reaction. So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17518,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In the conversion of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Br}_{2}}$ to $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BrO}_{3}^{-}}$, the oxidation number of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Br}}$ changes from','[{"text": "zero to +5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "+1 to +5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero to ${\\\\rm{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+2 to +5", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Br}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BrO}_{3}^{-}}$<br>Oxidation state of an element in its standard state is zero.<br>Therefore, oxidation state of \' $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Br}}$ \' in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Br}_{2}}$ is zero.<br>Let the oxidation number of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Br}}$ in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BrO}_{3}^{-}}$ be $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{x}}$.<br>Hence, $\\mathrm{x+3 \\times(-2)=-1}$<br>or, $\\mathrm{x-6=-1}$or, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{x}=+5}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17521,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In the reaction,<br>${\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}{\\rm{(aq) + B}}{{\\rm{r}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{(aq)}} \\to {\\rm{Mn}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(\\,s)  + BrO}}_{\\rm{3}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}{\\rm{(aq),}}$<br>the correct change in oxidation number of the species invovled is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Br}^{+5}}$ to $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Br}^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Mn}^{+7}}$ to $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Mn}^{+2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Mn}^{+7}}$ to $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Mn}^{+3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Br}^{-1}}$ to $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Br}^{+5}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-1}(\\mathrm{aq})+\\mathrm{Br}^{-1}(\\mathrm{aq}) \\longrightarrow}$<br>$\\mathop {{\\rm{Mn}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 4}}} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(\\,s) + }}\\mathop {{\\rm{Br}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 5}}} {\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}{\\rm{(aq)}}$<br>In the above reaction, oxidation number of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}}$ in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-1}(\\mathrm{aq})}$ decreases from +7 to +4 in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~s})}$ and oxidation number of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Br}}$ increase from ${\\rm{ - 1}}$ (in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Br}^{-}}$(aq) to +5 (in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BrO}_{3}^{-1}(\\mathrm{aq})}$ ).<br>Thus, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17522,'chemistry',49,629,1281,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Buffer system of carbonic acid and sodium bicarbonate is used for the precipitation of hydroxides of third group basic radicals.<br></div><div class="column-right">Buffer system of carbonic acid and sodium bicarbonate maitains the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of blood to a constant value about 7.4.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Buffer system of carbonic acid and bicarbonate maintains the $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}}$ of blood to a constant value of 7.4, but it is not a group reagent for third group basic radicals. The third group reagent is ammonium hydroxide. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17525,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The highest oxidation state in plutonium (At. no. $\\mathrm{=94)}$ is','[{"text": "+6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+7", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The highest oxidation state in plutonium (Atomic number $\\mathrm{=94}$ ) is +7 . It can show a greater range of oxidation states which (in part) is attributed to the fact that $\\mathrm{5 f, 6 d}$ and $\\mathrm{7 s}$ levels are of comparable energies. Plutonium $\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{Pu})}$ can show oxidation state of $\\mathrm{+3,+4,+5,+6}$ and +7 in its compound.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17526,'chemistry',49,615,1229,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">For dissolution of gases in liquids, on increasing the temperature or decreasing the pressure the amount of gas dissolved decreases.<br></div><div class="column-right">For dissolution of gases in liquids, the concentration of a gas in liquid is inversely proportional to the pressure (concentration) of the gas over the liquid.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Dissolution of gases in liquid is that the concentration of a gas in liquid is proportional to the pressure (concentration) of the gas over the liquid. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17528,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In which substance does nitrogen exhibit the lowest oxidation state?','[{"text": "Nitrogen gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ammonia", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Nitrous oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nitric oxide", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Oxidation state of nitrogen gas $\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\right)}$ is zero, as both the nitrogen atom possess same electronegativity as well as same number of electrons. The oxidation states of ammonia, nitrous oxide and nitric oxide are given below.<br>Ammonia $\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)}$<br>${\\rm{x + 3}}({\\rm{1}}){\\rm{ = 0}}$<br>${\\rm{x =  - 3}}$<br>Nitrous oxide $\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)}$<br>${\\rm{2}}({\\rm{x}}){\\rm{ + 1}}({\\rm{ - 2}}){\\rm{ = 0}}$<br>${\\rm{2x = 2}}$<br>${\\rm{x =  + 1}}$<br>Nitric oxide (NO)<br>${\\rm{x + }}({\\rm{ - 2}}){\\rm{ = 0}}$<br>${\\rm{x =  + 2}}$<br>Thus, nitrogen exhibits lowest oxidation state in case of ammonia.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18383,'chemistry',48,758,1648,'The energy that opposes dissolution of a solvent is','[{"text": "hydration energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "lattice energy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "internal energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "bond energy", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Lattice energy opposes dissolution of a solvent. Lattice energy is the amount of energy required to dissociate 1 mole of ionic crystals into ions. If hydration energy of solid is more than lattice energy, then solid dissolves.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17529,'chemistry',49,617,1239,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">${{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}}$ is the example of homogeneous equilibria.<br></div><div class="column-right">For this reaction, $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}$ has unit L/mol and $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{P}}$ has unit bar.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For the homogeneous equilibrium,<br>$\\rm {\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}}$ has unit $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{mol} / \\mathrm{L}}$ and $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{P}}}$ has unit bar.<br>So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17531,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Match the column I (compound) with column II (oxidation state of underline element) and choose correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{4}}}\\left[ {\\underline {{\\rm{Fe}}} {{\\left( {{\\rm{CN}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{6}}}} \\right]$<br>B. ${\\left[ {\\underline {{\\rm{Ni}}} {{\\left( {{\\rm{CN}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{4}}}} \\right]^{{\\rm{2 - }}}}$<br>C. $\\underline {\\rm{N}} {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}\\underline {\\rm{N}} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}$<br>D. ${\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\underline {{\\rm{Xe}}} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{6}}}$</div><div class="column-right">P. ${\\rm{ - 3,}}\\,{\\rm{ + 5}}$<br>Q. ${\\rm{ + 8}}$<br>R. ${\\rm{ + 2}}$<br>S. ${\\rm{ - 2}}$</div></div>','[{"text": "A - R, B - R, C - P, D - Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - R, B - S, C - Q, D - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q, B - P, C - R, D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P, B - Q, C - S, D - R", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The correct match is A - R, B - R, C - P, D - Q<br>The oxidation states of underlined elements in the given compounds are as follows: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17531_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17532,'chemistry',49,621,1253,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In the dissociation of $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ at constant pressure and temperature, addition of helium at equilibrium increases the dissociation $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$.<br></div><div class="column-right">Helium removes $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ from the field of action.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{PCl}_{5} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{PCl}_{3}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}$<br>At constant pressure, when $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{He}}$ is added to the equilibrium, volume increases. Thus, in order to maintain the $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}}$ constant, degree of dissociation of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}}$ increases. Moreover, He (helium) is unreactive towards chlorine gas.<br>So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17533,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In which of the following compounds, nitrogen exhibits the highest oxidation state?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{3} \\\\mathrm{H}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let the oxidation state of nitrogen in the given compounds be $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{x}}$.<br>$\\mathop {{N_3}}\\limits^x \\mathop H\\limits^{ + 1}  \\Rightarrow \\left( x \\right)3 + \\left( { + 1} \\right) = 0 \\Rightarrow 3x =  - 1$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,x =  - \\frac{1}{3}$<br>(b) $\\mathrm{\\stackrel{x+1-2+1}{\\mathrm{NH}}{ }_{2} \\mathrm{OH} \\Rightarrow x+(+1) 2+(-2)+(+1)=0}$<br>$\\rm{x+2-2+1=0 \\Rightarrow x=-1}$<br>(c) $\\mathrm{\\stackrel{x+1}{N_{2}} H_{4} \\Rightarrow 2(x)+(+1) 4=0}$<br>$\\rm{\\Rightarrow 2 x=-4 \\Rightarrow x=-2}$<br>(d) $\\mathrm{\\stackrel{x+1}{N H_{3}} \\Rightarrow x+(+1) 3=0}$$\\rm{x=-3}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ Oxidation state of nitrogen is highest in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}_{3} \\mathrm{H}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17535,'chemistry',49,628,1276,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Higher order ionisation constant ${\\rm{K}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{\\rm{K}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{3}}}$  are smaller than the lower order ionisation constant ${\\rm{K}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{1}}}$of a polyprotic acid</div><div class="column-right">It is more difficult to remove a positively charged proton from a negative ion due to Coulombic forces.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Higher order ionisation constants $\\mathrm {\\left(\\mathrm{K} a_{2}\\right.}$, $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K} a_{3}}$ ) are smaller than the lower order ionisation constant $\\mathrm {\\left(\\mathrm{K} a_{1}\\right)}$ of a polyprotic acid. The reason for this is that, it is more difficult to remove a positively charged proton from a negative ion due to electrostatic forces. This can be seen in the case of removing a proton from the uncharged $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}}$ as compared from a negatively charged $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-1}}$. So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17536,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Which of the following oxidation state is the most common among the lanthanides?','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'+3 is the most common oxidation state among the lanthanides.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17538,'chemistry',49,626,1274,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The ionisation of hydrogen sulphide in water is low in the presence of hydrochloric acid.<br></div><div class="column-right">Hydrogen sulphide is a weak acid.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}$ is a weak acid and also a weak electrolyte and its ionisation is suppressed, when small amount of strong electrolyte like $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{HCl}}$ is added due to common ion effect.<br>$\\rm {\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{H}^{+}+\\mathrm{S}^{2-}}$<br>$\\rm {\\mathrm{HCl} \\rightarrow \\underset{\\substack{\\text { Common } \\\\ \\text { ion }}}{\\mathrm{H}^{+}}+\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}}$<br>So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17541,'chemistry',49,629,1277,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">An aqueous solution of ammonium acetate can act as a buffer.<br></div><div class="column-right">Acetic acid is a weak acid and $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{OH}$ is a weak base.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'An aqueous solution of ammonium acetate can act as a natural buffer as it resist changes in $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}}$ on dilution or an addition of small amount of acids or alkalies.<br>Moreover, ammonium acetate is a salt of weak acid $\\mathrm {\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}\\right)}$ and weak base $\\mathrm {\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{OH}\\right)}$. So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17542,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Oxidation state of nitrogen is incorrectly given in<br>$\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{Compound}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{Oxidation}}\\,\\,{\\rm{state}}$','[{"text": "$\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{Co}}{{\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}} \\\\right)}_{\\\\rm{5}}}{\\\\rm{Cl}}} \\\\right]{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{OH}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {{{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{5}}}} \\\\right){\\\\rm{S}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ + 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{M}}{{\\\\rm{g}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}}$ in $\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$ has ${\\rm{ - 2}}$ oxidation number',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17544,'chemistry',49,629,1278,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A solution containing a mixture of acetic acid and sodium acetate maintains a constant value of $\\mathrm{pH}$ on additon of small amount of acid or alkali.<br></div><div class="column-right">A solution containing a mixture of acetic acid and sodium acetate acts as a buffer solution of around $\\mathrm{pH}=4.75$.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A solution containing a mixture of acetic acid and the sodium acetate acts as a buffer solution as it maintains a constant value of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}(=4.75)}$ and its $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}}$ is not affected on addition of small amounts of acid or alkali.<br>So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17545,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'What is the oxidation state of nitrogen in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaN}_{3}}$ ?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 3/1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 1/3}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaN}_{3} \\Rightarrow+1+3 \\mathrm{x}=0}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\Rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{x}=-1 \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x}=-\\dfrac{1}{3}}$<br>So, oxidation number of nitrogen in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaN}_{3}}$ is $\\mathrm{-1 / 3}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17547,'chemistry',49,618,1245,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If reaction quotient $\\left( {{{\\text{Q}}_{\\text{c}}}} \\right)$ is less than the equilibrium constant $\\left( {{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{c}}}} \\right)$, the equilibrium tends to shift in the direction of products.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The expression for equilibrium constant is different than the expression for reaction quotient.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The expression for equilibrium constant is same as the expression for reaction quotient. So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17548,'chemistry',50,838,1874,'$\\mathrm{3 \\mathrm{ClO}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{-} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{ClO}_{3}^{-}+2 \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}}$is an example of','[{"text": "oxidation reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "reduction reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "disproportionation reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "decomposition reaction", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{3 \\stackrel{+1}{\\mathrm{Cl}} \\mathrm{O}^{-} \\rightarrow \\stackrel{+5}{\\mathrm{ClO}} \\mathrm{O}_{3}^{-}+2 \\mathrm{Cl}^{-1}}$\'<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}}$ \' changes it\'s O.N. from +1 to +5 (oxidation) and +1 to (-1) [Reduction] so, disproportionation reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17550,'chemistry',49,616,1235,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The active mass of pure solids and pure liquids is taken  as unity.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The active mass of pure solids and liquids depends on density and molecular mass. The density and molecular mass of pure liquids and solids are constant.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion. So the option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17551,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'Which of the following does not represent redox reaction?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2 \\\\mathrm{Ca}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{2}+\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{Ca}(\\\\mathrm{ClO})_{2}}$ $\\\\mathrm{+\\\\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}+2 \\\\mathrm{OH}^{-} \\\\longrightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NaIO}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{NaHSO}_{3} \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{NaHSO}_{4}}$ $\\\\mathrm{+\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\\\mathrm{I}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{2 \\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{4} \\\\mathrm{O}_{6}+2 \\\\mathrm{NaI}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'No element in ${{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{nd}}}}$ reaction has undergone a change in oxidation state.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17553,'chemistry',49,617,1238,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The equilibrium constant of the exothermic reaction at high temperature decreases.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Since $\\ln \\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}_{2}}{\\mathrm{~K}_{1}}=\\dfrac{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}^{0}}{\\mathrm{R}}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{~T}_{1}}-\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{~T}_{2}}\\right]$ and for exothermic reaction, $\\Delta H^{0}=-\\mathrm{ve}$ and thereby $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}_{2}}{\\mathrm{~K}_{1}} < 1$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion. So the option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17554,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'Standard reduction potentials of the half reactions are given below<br>${{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{2}}}({\\rm{g}}){\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{F}}^{\\rm{ - }}}({\\rm{aq}});{\\rm{E}}^\\circ {\\rm{ =  + 2}}.{\\rm{85}}\\,{\\rm{V}}$<br>${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}(\\,{\\rm{g}}){\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{2C}}{{\\rm{l}}^{\\rm{ - }}}({\\rm{aq}});{\\rm{E}}^\\circ {\\rm{ =  + 1}}.{\\rm{36}}\\,{\\rm{V}}$<br>${\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}({\\rm{l}}){\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{2B}}{{\\rm{r}}^{\\rm{ - }}}({\\rm{aq}});{\\rm{E}}^\\circ {\\rm{ =  + 1}}.{\\rm{06}}\\,{\\rm{V}}$<br>${{\\rm{I}}_{\\rm{2}}}({\\rm{s}}){\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{I}}^{\\rm{ - }}}({\\rm{aq}});{\\rm{E}}^\\circ {\\rm{ =  + 0}}.{\\rm{53}}\\,{\\rm{V}}$<br>The strongest oxidising and reducing agents respectively are:','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{{\\\\rm{I}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{B}}{{\\\\rm{r}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}}{{\\\\rm{r}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{{\\\\rm{I}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The higher the value of reduction potential, the higher will be the oxidising pwer whereas the lower the value of reduction potential the higher will be reducing power.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17556,'chemistry',49,618,1245,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The reaction quotient, $\\mathrm{Q}$ has the same form as the equilibrium constant $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{eq}}$ and is evaluated using any given concentrations of the species involved in the reaction, and not necessarily equilibrium concentrations.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> If the numerical value of $\\mathrm{Q}$ is not the same as the value of equilibrium constant, a reaction will proceed.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Both assertion and reason are true and reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion. So the option (2) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17557,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'The standard electrode potentials of four elements A, B, C and D are ${\\rm{ - 3}}{\\rm{.05, - 1}}{\\rm{.66, - 0}}{\\rm{.40}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ + 0}}{\\rm{.80}}$. The highest chemical reactivity will be exhibited by :','[{"text": "A", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "D", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Standard electrode potential i.e. reduction potential of $\\mathrm{A}$ is minimum $\\mathrm{(-3.05 \\mathrm{~V})}$ i.e. its oxidation potential is maximum which implies \' $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A}}$ \' is most reactive chemically.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17559,'chemistry',49,617,1242,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Connecting a flask at vacuum to another flask containing solid $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}$ in equilibrium with its dissociation products ${\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{(g)}}\\,\\,{\\text{and HCl(g)}}$ decreases the amount of solid $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}$. Both the flasks are at same temperature<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}$ for the decomposition process ${\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{Cl(s)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{(g) + HCl(g)}}$ decreases in the above process.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}}$ value increases in the given process. So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17560,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'Zn gives $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ gas with $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCl}}$ but not with $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}$ because','[{"text": "Zn acts as an oxidising agent when it reacts with $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}$ is weaker acid than $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HCl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In electrochemical series, $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Zn}}$ is above hydrogen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}}$ is reduced in preference to hydronium ion", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Zinc gives $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ gas with dil $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} / \\mathrm{HCl}}$ but not with $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}$ because in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}, \\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}}$ ion is reduced and give $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{NO}_{3}, \\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}, \\mathrm{NO}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}$ (based upon the concentration of $\\mathrm{\\left.\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}\\right)}$<br>$\\rm{\\left[\\mathrm{Zn}+\\underset{(\\text { nearly } 6 \\%)}{2 \\mathrm{HNO}_{3}} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Zn}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}\\right] \\times 4}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}+8 \\mathrm{H} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{NH}_{3}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}+\\mathrm{HNO}_{3} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{NO}_{3}}$<br>$\\mathrm{4 \\mathrm{Zn}+\\mathrm{IOHNO}_{3} \\longrightarrow 4 \\mathrm{Zn}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}}$<br>$\\mathrm{+\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{NO}_{3}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Zn}}$ is on the top position of hydrogen in electrochemical series. So Zn displaces $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$ dilute $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCl}}$ with liberation of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}$.<br>$\\rm{\\mathrm{Zn}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{ZnSO}_{4}+\\mathrm{H}_{2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17562,'chemistry',49,621,1251,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The degree of decomposition of $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ is more at low pressures.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In a reversible reaction, on increasing the pressure the equilibrium shifts in the direction in which decrease in volume takes place.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.<br>So the option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17563,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'The standard reduction potential at $\\mathrm{298 \\mathrm{~K}}$ for the following half cell reaction:<br>${\\rm{Zn}}_{({\\rm{aq}})}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}_{({\\rm{s}})}};{\\rm{E}}^\\circ {\\rm{ =  - 0}}.{\\rm{762}}\\,{\\rm{V}}$<br>${\\rm{Cr}}_{({\\rm{aq}})}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}{\\rm{ + 3}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{({\\rm{s}})}};{\\rm{E}}^\\circ {\\rm{ = 0}}.{\\rm{740}}\\,{\\rm{V}}$<br>${\\rm{2H}}_{({\\rm{aq}})}^{\\rm{ + }}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {{\\rm{H}}_{{\\rm{2}}(\\,{\\rm{g}})}};{\\rm{E}}^\\circ {\\rm{ = 0}}.{\\rm{0}}\\,{\\rm{V}}$<br>Which of the following is the strongest reducing agent?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{(\\\\mathrm{s})}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Zn}_{(\\\\mathrm{s})}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{F}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Lower the reduction potential, more easily it is oxidised and stronger is the reducing agent. As zinc has high negative value of reduction potential hence, it is the strongest reducing agent.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17564,'chemistry',49,618,1244,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Stability of $\\mathrm{K}_{4}\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]$ and $\\mathrm{K}_{3}\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]$ are same<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Equilibrium constant for ${{\\text{K}}_4}\\left[ {{\\text{Fe}}{{({\\text{CN}})}_6}} \\right]{\\text{ is }}\\left( {2.6 \\times {{10}^{37}}} \\right){\\text{ and for }}$<br>${{\\text{K}}_3}\\left[ {{\\text{Fe}}{{({\\text{CN}})}_6}} \\right]{\\text{ is }}1.9 \\times {10^{17}}{\\text{ }}$ for their disscociation','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{4}\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]}$ is more stable than $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{3}\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]}$. So, the option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17566,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'In which of the following cases a chemical reaction is possible?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}}$ solution is stirred with a copper spoon", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Conc. $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}$ is stored in a platinum vessel", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gold ornaments are washed with dil. $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HCl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{ZnSO}_{4(\\\\mathrm{aq})}}$ is placed in a copper vessel", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}}$ solution is stirred with a copper spoon, displacement reaction will takes place i.e., $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}}$ displace $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ag}}$ from $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}}$ solution<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}+2 \\mathrm{AgNO}_{3} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}+2 \\mathrm{Ag}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17567,'chemistry',49,616,1235,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> No term in the concentration of a pure solid or a pure liquid appear in constant $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{eq}}$ and is evaluated using any given concentrations of the species involved, in the reaction, and not necessarily equilibrium concentrations.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Each pure solid or pure liquid is in a phase by itself and has a constant concentration at constant temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion. So the option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17570,'chemistry',49,621,1252,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> On cooling in a freezing mixture, colour of the following mixture turns to pink from deep blue for the reaction ${\\text{Co}}\\left( {{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}} \\right)_{{\\text{6(aq)}}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}{\\text{ + 4Cl}}_{{\\text{(aq)}}}^{\\text{ - }} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{CoCl}}_{{\\text{4(aq)}}}^{{\\text{2 - }}}{\\text{ + 6}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{(1)}}}}$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Reaction is endothermic. So on cooling, the reaction moves to backward direction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}^{2+}}$ is pink while $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CoCl}_{4}^{2-}}$ is blue. So, on cooling the reaction tries to overcome the effect of temperature and goes in backward direction (Le - Chatelier\'s principle). Both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion. So the option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
