(17708,'chemistry',48,751,1613,'1 mole of an ideal gas expands isothermally and reversibly by decreasing pressure from $210 \\mathrm{kPa}$ to $105 \\mathrm{kPa}$ at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$. What is the work done? $\\left(\\mathrm{R}=8.314 \\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right)$','[{"text": "$1960 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$864 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1296 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1729 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{P}_{1}=210 \\mathrm{kPa}, \\mathrm{P}_{2}=105 \\mathrm{kPa}, \\mathrm{T}=300 \\mathrm{~K}$.<br>$\\mathrm{n}=1, \\mathrm{R}=8.314 \\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}, \\mathrm{~W}=?$<br>Workdone in isothermal reversible expansion,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{W}_{\\text {max }}=-2.303 \\mathrm{nRT} \\log _{10} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}_{1}}{\\mathrm{P}_{2}} \\\\& =-2.303 \\times 1 \\times 8.314 \\times 300 \\times \\log _{10} \\dfrac{210}{105} \\\\& =-2.303 \\times 1 \\times 8.314 \\times 300 \\times \\log _{10} 2 \\\\& =2.303 \\times 1 \\times 8.314 \\times 300 \\times 0.3010 \\\\& =1728.99 \\mathrm{~J} \\\\& \\mathrm{~W}_{\\max }=1729 \\mathrm{~J}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17709,'chemistry',49,617,1237,'The dissociation constants for acetic acid and $\\mathrm{HCN}$ at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ are $1.5 \\times 10^{-5}$ and $4.5 \\times 10^{-10}$, respectively. The equilibrium constant for the equilibrium<br>$\\mathrm{CN}^{-}+\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{HCN}+\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COO}^{-}$would be','[{"text": "$3.0 \\\\times 10^{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.0 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.0 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.0 \\\\times 10^{4}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COO}^{-}+\\mathrm{H}^{+}}$;<br>${{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{a1}}}}{\\text{ = 1}}{\\text{.5}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 5}}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(I)}}$<br>${\\text{HCN}} \\rightleftharpoons {{\\text{H}}^{{\\text{   +   }}}}{\\text{ + C}}{{\\text{N}}^{{\\text{   -   }}}}$<br>${{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{a2}}}}{\\text{  =  4}}{\\text{.5}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{  -  10}}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{(II)}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CN}^{-}+\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{HCN}+\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COO}^{-} \\mathrm{K}=?}$<br> On subtracting Eq. (II) from Eq. (I), we get<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{COOH}+\\mathrm{CN}^{-} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{HCN}+\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COO}^{-}}$<br>$\\mathrm {K=\\dfrac{K_{a 1}}{K_{a 2}}=\\dfrac{1.5 \\times 10^{-5}}{4.5 \\times 10^{-10}}=\\dfrac{10^{5}}{3}=3.33 \\times 10^{4}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17711,'chemistry',50,842,1881,'In ionic equation,<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BiO}_{3}^{-}+6 \\mathrm{H}^{+}+\\mathrm{X} \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Bi}^{3+}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$<br>the value of \' $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{X}}$ \' is','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Balanced equation is<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BiO}_{3}^{-}+6 \\mathrm{H}^{+}+2 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Bi}^{+3}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore}$ the value of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{x}}$ is 2 .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18386,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'The aqueous solution of $\\mathrm{HCOONa}$,<br>$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+} \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$and $\\mathrm{KCN}$ are respectively','[{"text": "Acidic, acidic, basic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acidic, basic, neutral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Basic, neutral, neutral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Basic, acidic, basic", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/49_18386_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17712,'chemistry',48,752,1619,'Formation of $\\mathrm{NO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}$ from $\\mathrm{N}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}$ and $\\mathrm{O}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}$ is an endothermic process. Which of the following is true for this reaction?','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H} < 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}=\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{U}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}>0$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{N}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+2 \\mathrm{O}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>$\\Delta_{\\mathrm{n}}=\\mathrm{n}_{\\text {products }}-\\mathrm{n}_{\\text {reactants }}$<br>$\\Delta_{\\mathrm{n}(\\mathrm{g})}=2-3=-1$<br>According to first law of thermodynamics,<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}+\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{(\\mathrm{g})} \\mathrm{RT}$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}-\\mathrm{RT}$<br>and $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}>0$, for endothermic process',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17713,'chemistry',49,617,1240,'The relationship between $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}$ and $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}$ is $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}(\\mathrm{RT})^{\\mathrm{\\Delta n}}$. What would be the value of $\\Delta \\mathrm{n}$ for the reaction,<br>${\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{3}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + HC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}$?','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{p}}}{\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{c}}}{{\\rm{(RT)}}^{{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{n}}_{\\rm{g}}}}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=}$ sum of the stoichiometric coefficient of <br>gaseous products - sum of stoichiometric coefficients of gaseous reactants<br>$\\mathrm {\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=2-0=2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17715,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'Thiosulphate reacts differently with iodine and bromine in the reactions given below<br>$\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}^{2-}+\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{S}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{6}^{2-}+2 \\mathrm{I}^{-}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}^{2-}+2 \\mathrm{Br}_{2}+5 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}+4 \\mathrm{Br}^{-}+10 \\mathrm{H}^{+}}$<br>Which of the following statements justifies the above dual behaviour of thiosulphate?','[{"text": "Bromine is a stronger oxidant than iodine.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bromine is a weaker oxidant than iodine.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thiosulphate undergoes oxidation by bromine and reduction by iodine in these reactions.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bromine undergoes oxidation and iodine undergoes reduction in these reactions.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{2}}{\\mathop {\\text{S}}\\limits^{{\\text{ + 2}}} _{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}^{\\mathop {{\\text{2 - }}}\\limits^{{\\text{ + 2}}} }{\\text{(aq) + I}}_{\\text{2}}^{\\text{0}}{\\text{(s)}} \\to \\mathop {{\\text{ }}{{\\text{S}}_{\\text{4}}}}\\limits^{{\\text{2}}{\\text{.5}}} {\\text{O}}_{\\text{6}}^{{\\text{2 - }}}{\\text{(aq) + 2}}{{\\text{I}}^{\\text{ - }}}{\\text{(aq)}}$<br>$\\mathop {{\\text{ }}{{\\text{S}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits^{{\\text{ + 2}}} {\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}^{\\mathop {{\\text{2 - }}}\\limits^{{\\text{ + 2}}} }{\\text{(aq)}}\\,{\\text{ + }}\\,{\\text{2}}\\mathop {\\text{B}}\\limits^{\\text{0}} {{\\text{r}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(l)  +  5}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O(l)}} \\to $<br>${\\text{2SO}}_{\\text{4}}^{{\\text{2 - }}}{\\text{(aq)  +  4B}}{{\\text{r}}^{\\text{ - }}}{\\text{(aq)  +  10}}{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ + }}}{\\text{(aq)}}$<br>Bromine being stronger oxidising agent than $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}$, oxidises $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}^{2-}}$ to $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}}$ whereas $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}$ oxidises it only into $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{6}^{2-}}$ ion.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17716,'chemistry',48,758,1646,'What is enthalpy of formation of $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ if bond enthalpies are as<br>$(\\mathrm{N} \\equiv \\mathrm{N})=941 \\mathrm{~kJ},(\\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{H})=436 \\mathrm{~kJ}$,<br>$(\\mathrm{N}-\\mathrm{H})=389 \\mathrm{~kJ}$ ?','[{"text": "$-84.5 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-21.25 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-42.5 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$-63.45 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{N}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>$\\Delta_{\\mathrm{r}} \\mathrm{H}^{0}=\\sum \\Delta \\mathrm{H}^{\\circ}($ reactant $)-\\sum \\Delta \\mathrm{H}^{\\circ}($ Product $)$<br>$=\\left[\\Delta \\mathrm{H}^{\\mathrm{o}}(\\mathrm{N} \\equiv \\mathrm{N})+3 \\Delta \\mathrm{H}^{\\mathrm{o}}(\\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{H})\\right]$<br>$-\\left[6 \\Delta \\mathrm{H}^{\\circ}(\\mathrm{N}-\\mathrm{H})\\right]=941+3(436)-6(389)$<br>$\\Delta_{\\mathrm{r}} \\mathrm{H}^{\\mathrm{o}}=-85 \\mathrm{~kJ}$ for 2 moles of $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$.<br>$\\therefore$ for 1 mole of ammonia, i.e.,enthalpy of formation of<br>$\\mathrm{NH}_{3}=-\\dfrac{85}{2}=-42.5 \\mathrm{~kJ}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17717,'chemistry',49,617,1237,'The following equilibrium constant were determined at $1120 \\mathrm{~K}$ :<br>${\\text{2C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}} \\rightleftharpoons {{\\text{C}}_{\\left( {\\text{s}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + C}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}{\\text{;}}{{\\text{K}}_{{{\\text{p}}_{\\text{1}}}}}{\\text{ = 1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 14}}}}{\\text{at}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$  &<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + C}}{{\\text{l}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{COC}}{{\\text{l}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}\\,\\,{\\text{& }}{{\\text{K}}_{{{\\text{p}}_{\\text{2}}}}}{\\text{  =  6}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{   - 3}}}}{\\text{ at}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{   - 1}}}}$<br>What is the equilibrium constant $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}$ for the following reaction at $1120 \\mathrm{~K}$ ?<br>$\\mathrm{C}_{(\\mathrm{s})}+\\mathrm{CO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+2 \\mathrm{Cl}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{COCl}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}$','[{"text": "$3.31 \\\\times {10^{11}}{\\\\text{ }}{{\\\\text{M}}^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5.5 \\\\times {10^{10}}{\\\\text{ }}{{\\\\text{M}}^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.51 \\\\times {10^6}{\\\\text{ }}{{\\\\text{M}}^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${{\\text{C}}_{\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + C}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(I)}}$<br>$\\rm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p} 1}=10^{14} \\mathrm{~atm}}$<br>${\\text{2C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + 2C}}{{\\text{l}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2COC}}{{\\text{l}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(II)}}$<br>$\\rm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p} 2}=\\left(6 \\times 10^{-3}\\right)^{2} \\mathrm{~atm}^{-2}}$<br>Add (1) and (2),<br>${\\text{ }}{{\\text{C}}_{\\left( {\\text{s}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + C}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + 2C}}{{\\text{l}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2COC}}{{\\text{l}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}$<br>${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{p}}} = {10^{14}} \\times 36 \\times {10^{ - 6}} = 36 \\times {10^8}$<br>For given reaction, $\\mathrm {\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=-1}$<br>$\\rm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}=3.31 \\times 10^{11} \\mathrm{M}^{-1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17719,'chemistry',50,842,1881,'Calculate the equivalent weight of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KHC}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}}$ when it is oxidised by $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}}$in basic medium','[{"text": "64", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "128", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "32", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "15", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+3 \\mathrm{HC}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{-}+2 \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-} \\rightarrow}$<br>${\\text{2Mn}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ +  6C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ +  5O}}{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ - }}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\Rightarrow \\underset{(+3)}{\\mathrm{HC}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{-}} \\rightarrow \\underset{(+4)}{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}}}$<br>Change in oxidation state for two carbon atoms $\\mathrm{=2}$<br>Eq. wt. of<br>${\\rm{KH}}{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{ M}}{\\rm{.wt }}}}{{{\\rm{ Change}}\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{in   }}\\,\\,{\\rm{oxidation   }}\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{state }}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{128}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 64}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17720,'chemistry',48,751,1612,'A system gives out ${\\text{x J}}$ of heat and does ${\\text{y J}}$ of work on it\'s surrounding. What is the internal energy change?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ - x - yJ}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{y - x J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{x - y J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{x + y J}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{Q  =   - xJ, W  =   - yJ}}$<br>According to first law of thermodynamics,<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=\\mathrm{Q}+\\mathrm{W}$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{U}=-\\mathrm{x}-\\mathrm{y} \\mathrm{J}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17721,'chemistry',49,617,1241,'In the reaction, ${{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g) + }}{{\\text{I}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2HI(g)}}$,<br>the concentrations of $\\mathrm{H}_{2}, \\mathrm{I}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{HI}$ at equilibrium are 8.0, 3.0 and 28.0 moles per litre respectively. What will be the equilibrium constant?','[{"text": "30.61", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "32.66", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "29.40", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20.90", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\rm{{H}_{2} (g) + {I}_{2} (g)} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\rm{HI (g)}$<br>Applying law of mass action<br>$\\rm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{HI}]^{2}}{\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{I}_{2}\\right]}}$<br>Given<br>$\\rm {\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]=8.0 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\left[\\mathrm{I}_{2}\\right]=3.0 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}}$<br>$\\mathrm {[\\mathrm{HI}]=28.0 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}}$<br>So, $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{(28.0)^{2}}{(8.0) \\times(3.0)}=32.66}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17723,'chemistry',50,845,1887,'If ${{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}$  gas is passed through acidified solution of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}$, the colour of the solution will','[{"text": "remain unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "become deep red", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "become green", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "become deep gray", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}+4 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S} \\rightarrow}$<br>$\\rm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}+7 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+3 \\mathrm{~S}}$<br>Green colour appears because of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}}$ in solution.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17724,'chemistry',48,751,1612,'A system does $394 \\mathrm{~J}$ of work on surrounding by absorbing $701 \\mathrm{~J}$ heat. What is the change in internal energy of the system?','[{"text": "$547 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1095 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$307 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$394 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{W}=-394 \\mathrm{~J}($ work done by the system),<br>$\\mathrm{Q}=701 \\mathrm{~J}$ (heat absorbed)<br>According to first law of thermodynamics<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=\\mathrm{Q}+\\mathrm{W}$<br>$=701-394=307 \\mathrm{~J}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17725,'chemistry',49,617,1241,'For the reaction equilibrium:<br>${{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{4}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2N}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}$ the concentration of $\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ at equilibrium are $4.8 \\times 10^{-2}$ and $1.2 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$, respectively. The value of $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}$ for the reaction is','[{"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}{\\\\text{ M}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^3}{\\\\text{ M}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.3 \\\\times {10^2}{\\\\text{ M}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 1}}{\\\\text{ M}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}=\\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right]^{2}}{\\left[\\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right]}=\\dfrac{\\left(1.2 \\times 10^{-2}\\right)^{2}}{4.8 \\times 10^{-2}}=3.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17735,'chemistry',50,839,1878,'In the balanced chemical reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{IO}_{3}^{-}+\\mathrm{pI}^{-}+\\mathrm{qH}^{+} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{rH}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{sI}_{2}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{p}, \\mathrm{q}, \\mathrm{r}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{s}}$ respectively correspond to','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{5,6,4,3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{5,6,3,3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{4,3,5,6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{4,5,6,4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{IO}_{3}^{-}+\\underset{\\mathrm{P}}{5 \\mathrm{I}^{-}}+\\underset{\\mathrm{Q}}{6 \\mathrm{H}^{+}} \\rightarrow \\underset{\\mathrm{s}}{3 \\mathrm{I}_{2}}+\\underset{\\mathrm{R}}{3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$<br>So, P,Q,R,S are 5,6,3,3',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17727,'chemistry',49,618,1246,'Three moles of $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$, three moles of $\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}$ and two moles of $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ are taken in a closed vessel. If at equilibrium the vessel has 1.5 moles of $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$, the number of moles of $\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}$ present in it is','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {{\\text{PC}}{{\\text{l}}_{{\\text{5(g)}}}} \\rightleftharpoons }&{{\\text{PC}}{{\\text{l}}_{{\\text{3(g)}}}}}&{\\text{ + }}&{{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}_{{\\text{2(g)}}}}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{Initially:}}}&{\\text{3}}&{\\text{3}}&{\\text{2}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{At}}\\,{\\text{eqm:}}}&{{\\text{(3 - x)}}}&{{\\text{(3 + x)}}}&{{\\text{(2 + x)}}} \n\\end{array}$<br>Given that, $\\mathrm {(3-\\mathrm{x})=1.5 \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x}=1.5}$<br>Therefore no. of moles of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{PCl}_{3(\\mathrm{~g})}}$ present is<br>$\\rm { =(3+x) \\\\ =3+1.5=4.5}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17728,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'Consider the following statement(s)<br>(A) Iron reacts with dilute $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$ to evolve $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})}$ but $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ag}}$ does not<br>(B) Copper dissolves in dilute $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}$ but not in dilute $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCl}}$.<br>(C) Amongst the halogens, fluorine is the best oxidant because it is the most known as electronegative element<br>Choose the correct statement(s)','[{"text": "A & B", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "B & C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A & C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, B & C", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Iron releases $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ gas from $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$ as it is above $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}^{+}}$in electrochemical series.<br>Copper  is less electropositive metal.<br>Fluorine is the best oxident due to its high reduction potential.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17729,'chemistry',48,751,1614,'What is the constant external pressure of an ideal gas when expanded from $2 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{~m}^{3}$ to $3 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{~m}^{3}$, if the work done by the gas is $-5.09 \\mathrm{~kJ}$ ?','[{"text": "$5.09 \\\\times 10^{5} \\\\mathrm{Nm}^{-2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.01 \\\\times 10^{5} \\\\mathrm{Nm}^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.02 \\\\times 10^{5} \\\\mathrm{Nm}^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.60 \\\\times 10^{5} \\\\mathrm{Nm}^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${{\\text{V}}_{\\text{1}}}{\\text{ = 2}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{  -  2}}}} {{\\text{m}}^{\\text{3}}}{\\text{,}}{{\\text{V}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ = 3}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{  -  2}}}} {{\\text{m}}^{\\text{3}}}$<br>$\\Delta {\\text{V}} = {{\\text{V}}_2} - {{\\text{V}}_1} = \\left( {3 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right) - \\left( {2 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right)$<br>$ = 1 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,{{\\text{m}}^3}$<br>${\\text{W}} =  - 5.09\\,{\\text{kJ}} =  - 5090\\,{\\text{J}},{{\\text{P}}_{{\\text{ext}}}} = ?$<br>$\\,{\\text{W}} =  - {{\\text{P}}_{{\\text{ext }}}}\\Delta {\\text{V}}$<br>$\\therefore {{\\text{P}}_{{\\text{ext }}}} =  - \\frac{{\\text{W}}}{{\\Delta {\\text{V}}}} = \\frac{{5090}}{{1 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}$<br>$ = 5.09 \\times {10^5}{\\text{N}}{{\\text{m}}^{ - 2}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17731,'chemistry',49,618,1246,'4 moles each of $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ gases are allowed to react to form $\\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ in a closed vessel. At equilibrium $25 \\%$ of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is used up. The total number of moles of all the gases at equilibrium is','[{"text": "6.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7.0", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.0", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/49_17731_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Total number of moles $\\mathrm {=2+3+2=7}$ moles.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17732,'chemistry',50,838,1874,'Which of the following reaction is an example of redox reaction?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g})+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}(\\\\mathrm{~g})}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NaOH}(}$ aq $\\\\mathrm{)+\\\\mathrm{HCl}(}$ aq $\\\\mathrm{) \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{NaCl}(}$ aq $\\\\mathrm{)}$<br>$\\\\mathrm{+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}(1)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{XeF}_{6}(\\\\mathrm{~g})+3 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}(1) \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{XeO}_{3}+6 \\\\mathrm{HF}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ =  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{C}} \\to {\\text{ - 2}}} {\\text{ + }}\\mathop {{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{H}} \\to {\\text{0}}}  \\to \\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{C}} \\to {\\text{ - 3,H}} \\to {\\text{ + 1 (oxidation state) }}} $<br>So, it is disproportionation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17733,'chemistry',48,751,1612,'During a process, system absorbs $710 \\mathrm{~J}$ of heat and increases the internal energy by $460 \\mathrm{~J}$. What is the work performed by system?','[{"text": "$-250 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$-1170 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-710 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-460 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{Q}=710 \\mathrm{~J}$ (heat given to system),<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=460 \\mathrm{~J}($ increase in internal energy $)$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=460 \\mathrm{~J}($ increase in internal energy $)$<br>According to first law of thermodynamics<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta \\mathrm{U}=\\mathrm{Q}+\\mathrm{W} \\\\& \\therefore \\mathrm{W}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}-\\mathrm{Q} \\\\& =460-710=-250 \\mathrm{~J}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17736,'chemistry',49,618,1246,'${\\text{x}}$ moles and ${\\text{y}}$ moles per litre of $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{B}$, respectivley, were allowed to react. At the equilibrium ${{\\text{A}}_{\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + 2}}{{\\text{B}}_{\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}} \\rightleftharpoons \\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{C}}_{\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}$. The concentration of A, B and C were found to be  4,2 and $2 \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$, respectively. values of ${\\text{x}}$ and ${\\text{y}}$ are','[{"text": "6 and 4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8 and 10", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6 and 10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8 and 4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{A}+2 \\mathrm{~B} \\rightleftharpoons \\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{C}}$<br>At equilibrium if \' $\\mathrm {x}$ \' moles of $\\mathrm {A}$ is reacted<br>$\\mathrm {\\left(x-x^{\\prime}\\right)\\left(y-2 x^{\\prime}\\right)=\\dfrac{1}{2} x^{\\prime}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\left(x-x^{\\prime}\\right)=4}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\left(y-2 x^{\\prime}\\right)=2}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\dfrac{1}{2} x^{\\prime}=2 ; x^{\\prime}=4}$<br>$\\mathrm {[\\mathrm{A}]=\\mathrm{x}-\\mathrm{x}^{\\prime}=\\mathrm{x}-4=4}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{x}=8}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{y}-8=2 ; \\mathrm{y}=10}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17737,'chemistry',48,748,1606,'Which among the following is NOT an intensive property?','[{"text": "Surface tension", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heat capacity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Viscosity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Temperature", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Surface tension, viscosity, temperature are intensive properties but heat capacity is extensive property.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17739,'chemistry',49,617,1243,'One mole of ammonium carbonate dissociates as shown below at $500 \\mathrm{~K}$ :<br>${\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{COON}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{(s)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{(g) + C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}}$<br>If the pressure exerted by the released gases is $3.0 \\mathrm{~atm}$, then the value of $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}$ is','[{"text": "$7 \\\\mathrm{~atm}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\mathrm{~atm}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\mathrm{~atm}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8 \\\\mathrm{~atm}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Applying the law of chemical equilibrium, we get<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}=\\left(\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}\\right)^{2}\\left(\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}}\\right)}$<br>Since total pressure is 3 atm, the partial pressures of ${\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{(g)}}\\,\\,{\\text{and C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}}$ are<br>$\\mathrm {\\left[\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}\\right]=3 \\times \\dfrac{2}{3}=2 \\mathrm{~atm}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\left[\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}}\\right]=3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{3}=1 \\mathrm{~atm}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}=[2.0]^{2}[1.0]=4.0 \\mathrm{~atm}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17740,'chemistry',50,836,1862,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In the reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{Mg}_{(\\mathrm{s})}+\\mathrm{F}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{MgF}_{2(\\mathrm{~s})}$<br>Magnesium loses electrons and acts as a reducing agent.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Reduction in general means acceptance of electron(s) by a reactant.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Oxidation is the loss of electrons, exemplified by magnesium in this reaction, which serves as a reducing agent by providing electrons to fluorine, the substance undergoing reduction.. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17741,'chemistry',48,751,1614,'A gas is allowed to expand against a constant external pressure of 2.5 bar from an initial volume \' $\\mathrm{x}$ \' $\\mathrm{L}$ to final volume of $4.5 \\mathrm{~L}$. If amount of work done is $5 \\mathrm{dm}^{3}$ bar, what is the value of \' $\\mathrm{x}$ \'?','[{"text": "$2.5 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.5 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.0 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.2 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${{\\text{P}}_{{\\text{ext }}}}{\\text{ = 2}}{\\text{.5}}\\,{\\text{bar, }}{{\\text{V}}_{\\text{1}}}{\\text{ = x L,}}{{\\text{V}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ = 4}}{\\text{.5 L}}$<br>$\\mathrm{W}=-5 \\mathrm{dm}^{3}$ bar $=-5 \\mathrm{~L}$ bar<br>Workdone, $\\mathrm{W}=-\\mathrm{P}_{\\text {ext }}\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{2}-\\mathrm{V}_{1}\\right)$<br>$\\therefore-5 \\mathrm{Lbar}=-2.5 \\operatorname{bar}(4.5-\\mathrm{x}) \\mathrm{L}$<br>$\\therefore 5 \\mathrm{Lbar}=11.25 \\mathrm{~L}$ bar $-2.5 \\times \\mathrm{Lbar}$<br>$\\therefore 2.5 \\times \\mathrm{Lbar}=(11.25-5) \\mathrm{Lbar}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{x}=\\dfrac{6.25}{2.5}=2.5 \\mathrm{~L}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17752,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Oxidation number of $\\mathrm{S}$ in<br>$\\mathrm{HSO}_{3}^{-}$is +4 .<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Sulphur is in different oxidation states in different compounds.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let oxidation number of $\\mathrm{S}$ in $\\mathrm{HSO}_{3}^{-}$be $\\mathrm{x}$.<br>$\\begin{gathered}+1+\\mathrm{x}+3(-2)=-1 \\\\\\therefore \\mathrm{x}=+4\\end{gathered}$<br>Sulphur can show variable O.N. from $(- 2)$ to $(+ 6)$ in different compounds So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17743,'chemistry',49,618,1246,'In a 2 litre flask, the reaction takes place as:<br>${\\text{COC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{CO(g) + C}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}}$<br>The equilibrium conc. of $\\mathrm{COCl}_{2}$ was found to be 0.4. If excess of $\\mathrm{COCl}_{2}$ is added to the system, the equilibrium re-establish and ${\\kern 1pt} \\left[ {{\\text{COC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]$ becomes 1.6. What is the equilibrium conc. of $[\\mathrm{CO}]$ ?','[{"text": "Half of the former value", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thrice of the former value", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains unaltered", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Twice of the former value", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\rm {{\\text{COC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{CO(g) + C}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}}}$<br>$\\rm {\\therefore \\dfrac{[\\mathrm{CO}]\\left[\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{COCl}_{2}\\right]}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}}$<br>Let $\\mathrm {[\\mathrm{CO}]=\\mathrm{x}}$, then $\\mathrm {\\left[\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right]=\\mathrm{x}}$ <br>$\\mathrm {\\therefore \\dfrac{\\mathrm{x}^{2}}{\\left[\\mathrm{COCl}_{2}\\right]}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}}$ or $\\mathrm {\\dfrac{\\mathrm{x}^{2}}{0.4}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}}$<br>Again $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{x}^{2}}{0.4}=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{CO}]\\left[\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{COCl}_{2}\\right]}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\dfrac{\\mathrm{x}^{2}}{0.4}=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{CO}]^{2}}{1.6}[}$ as $\\mathrm {[\\mathrm{CO}]=[\\mathrm{Cl}]]}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\therefore \\mathrm{x}^{2} \\times 4=[\\mathrm{CO}]^{2}}$<br>$\\mathrm {2 \\mathrm{x}=[\\mathrm{CO}]}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17744,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Oxidation number of magnesium in $\\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ is +2 .<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Magnesium belongs to $2^{\\text {nd }}$ group of the periodic table.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In a charged monoatomic ion, the oxidation number of an atom equals the charge on the ion, exemplified by magnesium $(+2)$, aluminum $(+3)$, and sulfur $(-2)$ in their respective ions. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17745,'chemistry',48,751,1612,'In a process, a system performs $238 \\mathrm{~J}$ of work on it\'s surrounding by absorbing $54 \\mathrm{~J}$ of heat. What is the change in internal energy of system during this operation?','[{"text": "$222 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-192 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$54 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-184 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{W}=-238 \\mathrm{~J}$ (workdone by the system), $\\mathrm{Q}=54 \\mathrm{~J}$ (heat absorbed $)$<br>According to first law of thermodynamics,<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=\\mathrm{Q}+\\mathrm{W}$<br>$=54-238=-184 \\mathrm{~J}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17747,'chemistry',49,618,1245,'When two reactants ${\\text{A}}$ and ${\\text{B}}$ are mixed to give products $\\mathrm{C}$ and $\\mathrm{D}$, the reaction quotient $\\mathrm{Q}$, at the initial stages of the reaction','[{"text": "is zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "decreases with time", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "is independent of time", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increases with time", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'At the initial state if only reactants are used ${\\text{Q = 0}},$ <br>but from initial stage to attain equilibrium <br>it continuously increase with time till it becomes equal to $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17748,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Oxidation state of hydrogen is $ + 1$ in $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ while $ - 1$ in $\\mathrm{CaH}_{2}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{CaH}_{2}$ is a metal hydride and for hydrides, hydrogen is assigned the oxidation number of $ - 1$ .','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Assigning oxidation numbers to elements in compounds or ions follows specific rules, like hydrogen generally having an oxidation number of $(+ 1)$ but $(- 1)$ in metal hydrides. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17749,'chemistry',48,751,1612,'When ${\\text{x kJ}}$ heat is provided to a system, work equivalent to $\\mathrm{yJ}$ is done on it. What is internal energy change during this operation?','[{"text": "$(1000 \\\\mathrm{x}+\\\\mathrm{y}) \\\\mathrm{J}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{1000(x + y) J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{(x + 1000 y) J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{x  +  yJ}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{Q =+ x k J  = 1000 x J, W = +y J}}$<br>According to first law of thermodynamics,<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=\\mathrm{Q}+\\mathrm{W}$<br>$=(1000 \\mathrm{x}+\\mathrm{y}) \\mathrm{J}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17751,'chemistry',49,619,1247,'The standard state Gibb\'s energy change for the isomerisation reaction, cis-2-pentene $\\rightleftharpoons$ trans-2-pentene is $-3.67 \\mathrm{~kJ}$ mol at $400 \\mathrm{~K}$. If more trans-2-pentene is added to the reaction vessel, then','[{"text": "more cis-2-pentene is formed", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "equilibrium shifts in the forward reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "equilibrium remains unaltered", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "more trans-2-pentene is produced", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'cis-2-pentene $\\mathrm {\\rightarrow}$ trans-2-pentene<br>$\\mathrm {\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\mathrm{o}}=-3.67 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$ at $\\mathrm {400 \\mathrm{~K}}$<br>Addition of product always favours backward reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17754,'chemistry',48,751,1612,'For isochoric process, the first law of thermodynamics can be expressed as','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{U}=\\\\mathrm{Q}_{\\\\mathrm{v}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$-\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{U}=\\\\mathrm{Q}-\\\\mathrm{P} \\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{U}=-\\\\mathrm{W}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{W}=-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to first law of thermodynamics,<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=\\mathrm{Q}+\\mathrm{W}$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{U}=\\mathrm{Q}-\\mathrm{P}_{\\text {ext }} \\Delta \\mathrm{V} \\quad\\left(\\because \\mathrm{W}=-\\mathrm{P}_{\\text {ext }} \\Delta \\mathrm{V}\\right)$<br>But for isochoric process, (volume is constant)<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{V}=0$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{U}=\\mathrm{Q}_{\\mathrm{v}}$ (at constant volume)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17755,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Oxidation number of carbon in $\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ is zero.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ (formaldehyde) is a covalent compound.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Oxidation numbers are determined by specific rules. Hydrogen $(\\mathrm{H})$ is assigned an oxidation state of $(+ 1)$ , and oxygen $(\\mathrm{O})$ of $(- 2)$ . Let the oxidation number of carbon $(\\mathrm{C})$ in $\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ be $\\mathrm{x}$. Then<br>$\\mathrm{x}+2(+1)+(-2)=0$<br>$\\mathrm{x}=0$<br>So, in this compound, carbon $(\\mathrm{C})$ has an oxidation number of $0 . \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ is a covalent compound. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17756,'chemistry',49,621,1252,'${{\\text{N}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + 3}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2N}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{3}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + }}$ heat<br>What is the effect of the increase of temperature on the equilibrium of the reaction?','[{"text": "Equilibrium is unaltered", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reaction rates does not change", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Equilibrium is shifted to the left", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Equilibrium is shfited to the right", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${{\\text{N}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + 3}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2N}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{3}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + }}$ heat<br>Formation of ammonia is exothermic reaction so increase in temperature favours backward reaction and equilibrium is shifted to the left.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17758,'chemistry',48,751,1614,'When certain volume of gas expands against a constant external pressure of $2.40 \\times 10^{5} \\mathrm{~Pa}$ at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$ to $2.2 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~m}^{3}$. If the work obtained is $-0.048 \\mathrm{~kJ}$. What is the initial volume of the gas?','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{-3} \\\\mathrm{~m}^{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.5 \\\\times 10^{-2} \\\\mathrm{~m}^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5 \\\\times 10^{-3} \\\\mathrm{~m}^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.8 \\\\times 10^{-2} \\\\mathrm{~m}^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{P}_{\\text {ext }}=2.40 \\times 10^{5} \\mathrm{~Pa}, \\mathrm{~V}_{2}=2.2 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~m}^{3}$<br>$\\mathrm{W}=-0.048 \\mathrm{~kJ}=-48 \\mathrm{~J}, \\mathrm{~V}_{1}=$ ?<br>$\\mathrm{W}=-\\mathrm{P}_{\\text {ext }} \\Delta \\mathrm{V}$ <br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{W}=-\\mathrm{P}_{\\text {ext }}\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{2}-\\mathrm{V}_{1}\\right) \\\\& \\therefore-48=-2.40 \\times 10^{5}\\left(2.2 \\times 10^{-3}-\\mathrm{V}_{1}\\right) \\\\& \\therefore 48=5.28 \\times 10^{2}-\\left(2.40 \\times 10^{5} \\mathrm{~V}_{1}\\right) \\\\& \\therefore\\left(2.40 \\times 10^{5}\\right) \\mathrm{V}_{1}=528-48 \\\\& \\left(2.40 \\times 10^{5}\\right) \\mathrm{V}_{1}=480 \\\\& \\therefore \\mathrm{V}_{1}=\\dfrac{480}{2.40 \\times 10^{5}}=200 \\times 10^{-5} \\\\& \\therefore \\mathrm{V}_{1}=2.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~m}^{3}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17759,'chemistry',50,838,1874,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When white $\\mathrm{P}_{4}$ reacts with caustic soda, the products are $\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{NaH}_{2} \\mathrm{PO}_{2}$. This reaction is an example of disproportionation reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In disproportionation reaction, the same substance may act simultaneously as an oxidising agent and as a reducing agent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17759_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Thus, phosphorus undergoes both oxidation as well as reduction simultaneously in the same reaction. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17760,'chemistry',49,621,1255,'The conditions favourable for the following reaction are:<br>${\\text{2S}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}}{\\text{ + }}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{2}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2S}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{3}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)}};\\Delta {{\\text{H}}^{\\text{o}}} =  - 198\\,{\\text{kJ}}$','[{"text": "low temperature, high pressure", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "any value of ${\\\\text{T}}$ and ${\\\\text{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "low temperature and low pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "high temperature and high pressure", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'High pressure favours the reaction showing decrease in mole or volume, i.e., forward reaction. Also low temperature favours exothermic reaction, i.e., forward reaction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17762,'chemistry',48,751,1612,'What is internal energy change when $62 \\mathrm{~J}$ of work is done on the sytem and $128 \\mathrm{~J}$ of heat is transferred to surrounding?','[{"text": "$-62 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-190 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-128 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-66 J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Workdone on the system, so ${\\text{  +  ve}}$ $\\mathrm{W}=62 \\mathrm{~J}$.<br>Heat is given out, so ${\\text{  -  ve}}$<br>$\\mathrm{Q}=-128 \\mathrm{~J}$<br>According to first law of thermodynamics,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta \\mathrm{U}=\\mathrm{Q}+\\mathrm{W} \\\\& =-128+62=-66 \\mathrm{~J}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17763,'chemistry',50,836,1862,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{Na}^{+}$ion cannot be further oxidised or reduced by water.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> When $\\mathrm{NaCl}$ is dissolved in water sodium ion is hydrolysed.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When $\\mathrm{NaCl}$ is dissolved in water, the $\\mathrm{Na}+$ ions are hydrated and not hydrolysed.<br>$\\mathrm{NaCl}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{NaOH}+\\mathrm{HCl}$<br>So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17764,'chemistry',49,621,1255,'The volume of a closed reaction vessel in which the following equilibrium reaction occurs is halved:<br>$2 \\mathrm{SO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{SO}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g})$<br>As a result,','[{"text": "The rates of forward and backward the same reactions will remain", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The equilibrium will not shift", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The equilibrium will shfit to the right", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The rate of forward reaction will become double that of reverse reaction and the equilibrium will shift to the right.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{SO}_{3}\\right]^{2}}{\\left[\\mathrm{SO}_{2}\\right]^{2}\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right]}}$<br>If volume is reduced to half, $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}}$ will decrease to half. Thus, to maintain the equilibrium, the reaciton should shift in the forward direction, i.e. towards right. Also, to attain equilibrium back, the rate of forward direction will become double the rate of backward direction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17766,'chemistry',48,752,1620,'When $2.0 \\mathrm{~g}$ sucrose is oxidised forming $\\mathrm{CO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(1)}$, the internal energy changes by 24 $\\mathrm{kJ}$. Calculate the value of $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$ in $\\mathrm{kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$.<br>$\\left(\\right.$ Molar mass of sucrose $=342 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ )','[{"text": "$2014 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-4104 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$-5612 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8208 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{C}_{12} \\mathrm{H}_{22} \\mathrm{O}_{11(\\mathrm{~s})}+12 \\mathrm{O}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow 12 \\mathrm{CO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+11 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(1)}$<br>Given, $\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=-24 \\mathrm{~kJ}$ per $2 \\mathrm{gram}$<br>$\\therefore$ for 1 mole sucrose (342 gram),<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=-\\dfrac{24 \\times 342}{2} \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}=-4104 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}+\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}} \\mathrm{RT}$<br>Here, $\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=12-12=0$, so $\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}} \\mathrm{RT}=0$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=-4104 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17767,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Oxidation state of nitrogen in $\\mathrm{N}_{3} \\mathrm{H}$ is $-1 / 3$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Nitrogen is less electronegative than hydrogen.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let oxidation state of $\\mathrm{N}$ in $\\mathrm{N}_{3} \\mathrm{H}$ be x<br>$3 \\mathrm{x}+1=0$<br>$\\mathrm{x}=-1 / 3$<br>Nitrogen is more electronegative than hydrogen.<br>So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17768,'chemistry',49,619,1247,'Equilibrium constant for a reaction is 20. What is the value of $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}$ at $300 \\mathrm{~K} ?\\left(\\mathrm{R}=8 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~kJ}\\right)$','[{"text": "$16.63 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-5.527 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-2.763 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-7.191 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given,<br>$\\rm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{eq}}=20}$<br>Temperature $\\mathrm {(\\mathrm{T})=300 \\mathrm{~K}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{R}=8 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~kJ}}$<br>We have, $\\mathrm {\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}=-2.303 \\times \\mathrm{R} \\times \\mathrm{T} \\times \\log \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{eq}}}$<br>$\\mathrm {=-2.303 \\times 8 \\times 10^{-3} \\times \\log 20 \\times 300}$<br>$\\mathrm {(\\log 20=1.301)}$<br>$\\mathrm {=-7.91 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'2020','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17770,'chemistry',48,751,1614,'A gas is allowed to expand in an insulated container against a constant external pressure of 2.5 bar from $4.5 \\mathrm{dm}^{3}$ to $7 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~m}^{3}$. What is the change in internal energy of the gas?','[{"text": "$-3.25 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-312.5 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$112.3 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-625 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\Delta {\\text{U  =  q  +  w}}$<br>Since the container is insulated, ${\\text{q  =  0}}$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=\\mathrm{W}=-\\mathrm{P} \\Delta \\mathrm{V}=-\\mathrm{P}\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{2}-\\mathrm{V}_{1}\\right)$<br>Given: $\\mathrm{P}=2.5$ bar, $\\mathrm{V}_{2}=7 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~m}^{3}$<br>$\\begin{gathered}=7 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 10^{3} \\mathrm{~L}=7 \\mathrm{~L} \\\\\\mathrm{~V}_{1}=4.5 \\mathrm{dm}^{3}=4.5 \\mathrm{~L} \\\\\\therefore \\quad \\Delta \\mathrm{U}=-2.5(7-4.5) \\mathrm{L} \\text { bar }=-6.25 \\mathrm{Lbar} \\\\=-6.25 \\times 100 \\mathrm{~J}=-625 \\mathrm{~J}[1 \\mathrm{~L} \\text { bar }=100 \\mathrm{~J}]\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17771,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In a redox reaction, the oxidation number of the oxidant decreases, while that of reductant increases.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Oxidant gains electron(s) and reductant loses electron(s).','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'An oxident will oxidize another compound by gaining electrons. Thus, its O.N. decreases. On the other hand, the reductant reduces another compound by loosing electrons. Thus, its O.N. increases. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17772,'chemistry',49,623,1263,'Acidity of $\\mathrm{BF}_{3}$ can be explained on which of the following concepts?','[{"text": "Arrhenius concept", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bronsted Lowry concept", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lewis concept", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bronsted Lowry as well as Lewis concept", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Lewis concept, acid can accept a pair of electrons and base can donate a pair of electrons. <br>Thus, as $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{BF}_{3}}$ has six electrons in its valence shell, <br>so it can accept an electrons pair and acts as Lewis acid.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'2018','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17774,'chemistry',48,761,1654,'A container has hydrogen and oxygen mixture in the ratio of $4: 1$ by weight then','[{"text": "Internal energy of the mixture decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Internal energy of the mixture increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Entropy of the mixture increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Entropy of the mixture decreases", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Entropy of the mixture increases due to increase in disorder. (mixing of gases increases entropy).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17775,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Bromide ion is serving as a reducing agent in the reaction,<br>$2{\\text{MnO}}_{4({\\text{ aq) }}}^ -  + {\\text{Br}}_{{\\text{(aq) }}}^ -  + {{\\text{H}}_2}{{\\text{O}}_{({\\text{l}})}} \\to $<br>$2{\\text{Mn}}{{\\text{O}}_{2{\\text{ (aq) }}}} + {\\text{BrO}}_{3({\\text{aq}})}^ -  + 2{\\text{OH}}_{({\\text{aq}})}^ - $<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Oxidation number of $\\mathrm{Br}$ increases from -1 to 5 .','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17775_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Oxidation number of $\\mathrm{Br}$ changes from $ - {\\text{1}}$ to $5 .\\left(\\because\\right.$ Loss of $\\left.6 \\mathrm{e}^{-}\\right)$  $\\mathrm{Br}$  undergoes oxidation, hence, acts as reducing agent. Thus, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17776,'chemistry',49,623,1262,'Conjugate base of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{-}$is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HPO}_{4}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HPO}_{4}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{-} \\stackrel{-\\mathrm{H}^{+}}{\\longrightarrow} \\mathrm{HPO}_{4}^{2-}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'2013','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17778,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Nitrous acid $\\left(\\mathrm{HNO}_{2}\\right)$ may act as an oxidising as well as a reducing agent.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The oxidation number of nitrogen remains same in all the compounds.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Nitrogen typically exhibits oxidation states ranging from $ - {\\text{3}}$ to $ + {\\text{5}}$ , and the most common ones are $-3,-2,-1,0,+1,+3$, and $ + {\\text{5}}$ .<br>O.N. of \' $\\mathrm{N}$ \' in $\\mathrm{HNO}_{2}$ is $ + {\\text{3}}$ . Hence it can undergo both oxidation and reduction. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18244,'chemistry',48,751,1613,'To calculate the amount of work done in joules during reversible expansion of an ideal gas, the volume must be expressed in','[{"text": "Only m ${ }^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only dm ${ }^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only cm ${ }^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Work done (W)$<br>=-2.303 \\mathrm{nRT} \\log \\dfrac{\\mathrm{V}_{2}}{\\mathrm{~V}_{1}}$<br>$\\mathrm{V}_{1}$ and $\\mathrm{V}_{2}$ are in ratio in the relation. So, unit may be expressed in any one of $\\mathrm{m}^{3}, \\mathrm{dm}^{3}$ or $\\mathrm{cm}^{3}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17779,'chemistry',48,758,1635,'If  ${\\text{C + }}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}} \\to {\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ +  94}}{\\text{.2}}\\,\\,{\\text{kcal}}$<br>${{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ + }}\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}} \\to {{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O  +  68}}{\\text{.3}}\\,\\,{\\text{kcal & }}$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{ + 2}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}} \\to {\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ +  2}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O  +  210}}{\\text{.8}}\\,\\,{\\text{kcal}}$<br>then the possible heat of formation of methane will be','[{"text": "$47.3 \\\\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-20.0 \\\\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$45.9 \\\\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-47.3 \\\\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'To get $\\mathrm{C}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CH}_{4}$<br>$[$ eq1] $+[2 \\times$ eq 2$]-$ reverse of eq 3<br>$\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=-94.2+(2 \\times 68.3)-(-210.8)$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=-20 \\mathrm{k} . \\mathrm{cal}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17780,'chemistry',49,623,1262,'Which of the following is the strongest base?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_3}{{\\\\text{O}}^ - }$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_3}{\\\\text{CO}}{{\\\\text{O}}^ - }$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{-}}$is the strong conjugate base of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}}$ (weak acid).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17782,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Lithium ion is a poor oxidising agent.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Lithium ion has the lowest electrode potential.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In electrochemical series lithium ion is placed at the bottom with a lowest reduction potential of $-3.04 \\mathrm{~V}$. This means it is stable in its oxidised state and is a poor oxiding agent. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17783,'chemistry',49,626,1271,'$\\mathrm{pOH}$ of ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.002}}\\,\\,{\\text{M}}\\,\\,{\\text{HN}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}$  is','[{"text": "$11+\\\\log 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$11-\\\\log 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-3+\\\\log 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "none of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm {\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=0.002 \\mathrm{M}}$<br>$\\rm { \\mathrm{pH}=\\log 2 \\times 10^{-3} \\\\ =3-\\log 2 \\\\ \\therefore \\mathrm{pOH}=11+\\log 2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17784,'chemistry',48,748,1606,'Which among the following state functions is an extensive property of system?','[{"text": "Temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Volume", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Redfractive index", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Viscosity", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Volume depends upon the amount of the substance so it is an extensive property. Rest are intensive properties.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17786,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ cannot act as a reducing agent.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Sulphur cannot increase its oxidation number beyond +6 .','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The maximum oxidation state of sulfur is $ + {\\text{6}}$ , determined by its six valence electrons in group ${\\text{16}}$ of the periodic table. It cannot exceed this value due to the valence shell\'s capacity, rendering sulfur incapable of acting as a reducing agent in this state. So the option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17787,'chemistry',49,626,1271,'${\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 6}}}}$ ${\\rm{M}}$ ${\\rm{NaOH}}$ ${\\rm{is}}\\,$ ${\\rm{diluted}}\\,$ $\\,{\\rm{100}}$ ${\\rm{times}}$. ${\\rm{The }}$ ${\\rm{pH}}$$\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,$ $\\,{\\rm{the}}\\,$ ${\\rm{diluted}}$ ${\\rm{base}}$ $\\,{\\rm{is}}$','[{"text": "between 5 and 6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "between 6 and 7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "between 10 and 11", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "between 7 and 8", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\left[ {{\\text{O}}{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ - }}}} \\right]{\\text{of 1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 6}}}}{\\text{M NaOH = 1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 6}}}}$<br>when 100 times diluted,<br>$\\rm {\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=\\dfrac{10^{-6}}{10^{2}}=10^{-8}}$<br>As it is a weak base, autoprotolysis of water <br>will also take place. So, the contribution of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{OH}^{-}}$ <br>ions from water must also be considered.<br>$\\mathrm {\\therefore}$ Total $\\mathrm {\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=10^{-8}+\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]_{\\text {water }}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17788,'chemistry',48,748,1603,'A well stoppered thermo flask contains some ice cubes. This is an example of:','[{"text": "Closed system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Open system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isolated system", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Non-thermodynamic system", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Thermos flask does not allow exchange of heat and matter. So it is an isolated system.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17790,'chemistry',50,836,1862,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Sn reacts with $\\mathrm{HCl}$ to produce $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Sn}$ is a better reducing agent than $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the reaction of $\\operatorname{tin}(\\mathrm{Sn})$ with hydrochloric acid, tin acts as a better reducing agent than hydrogen due to its higher oxidation potential, facilitating the reduction of hydrogen ions $\\left(\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right)$ to form hydrogen gas $\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right)$ while undergoing oxidation itself. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17791,'chemistry',49,626,1271,'$0.023 \\mathrm{~g}$ of sodium metal is reacted with $100 \\mathrm{~cm}^{3}$ of water. The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of the resulting solution is:','[{"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "11", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{M}=\\dfrac{0.023 \\times 1000}{23 \\times 100}=0.01 \\mathrm{M}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{Na}^{+}+\\mathrm{OH}^{-} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{NaOH}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=0.01 \\mathrm{M}=10^{-2} \\mathrm{M}}$,<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pOH}=-\\log \\left(10^{-2}\\right)=2}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}+\\mathrm{pOH}=14}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\therefore \\mathrm{pH}=14-2 \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{pH}=12}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17792,'chemistry',48,751,1612,'The volume of a gas decreases from $500 \\mathrm{cc}$ to $300 \\mathrm{cc}$ when a sample of gas is compressed by an average pressure of $0.6 \\mathrm{~atm}$. During this process $10 \\mathrm{~J}$ of heat is liberated. The change in internal energy is','[{"text": "$-2.16 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.156 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.16 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$101.3 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Change in<br>Volume $(\\mathrm{V})=500-300=200 \\mathrm{cc}=0.2$ litre,<br>Pressure $(\\mathrm{P})=0.6 \\mathrm{~atm}$<br>$\\therefore$ Work done $(\\mathrm{W})=\\mathrm{P} \\Delta \\mathrm{V}=(0.2 \\times 0.6)=0.12$<br>litre-atm or $0.12 \\times 101.3 \\mathrm{~J}=12.156 \\mathrm{~J}$<br>${\\text{q  =   - 10J}}$  liberated heat $)$<br>$\\therefore$ Acc to 1st law of thermodynamics,<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{E}=\\mathrm{q}+\\mathrm{W}=-10+12.16=2.16 \\mathrm{~J}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17794,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$is reduced to $\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ in acidic medium.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In acidic medium, following reaction takes place. $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The reduction of $\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ions can occur differently depending on the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of the medium. In a mild basic or neutral medium:<br>$\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}+\\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-2}$<br>In an acidic medium:<br>$\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}+5 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Mn}^{+2}$<br>In a strong basic medium:<br>$\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}+3 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Mn}^{+4}$<br>Thus, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17795,'chemistry',49,626,1274,'${\\text{30 cc of }}\\frac{{\\text{M}}}{{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ HCl, 20 cc of  }}{\\mkern 1mu} \\frac{{\\text{M}}}{{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ HN}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}$ and ${\\text{40}}\\,\\,{\\text{cc}}$ of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{M}}{4} \\mathrm{NaOH}$ solutions are mixed and the volume was made up to ${\\text{1}}\\,\\,{\\text{d}}{{\\text{m}}^{\\text{3}}}$. The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of the resulting solution is','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Total milliequivalents of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{H}^{+}}$<br>$\\mathrm {=20 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2}+30 \\times \\dfrac{1}{3}=20}$<br>Total milliequivalents of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{OH}^{-}=40 \\times \\dfrac{1}{4}=10}$<br>Thus, milliequivalents of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{H}^{+}}$left $\\mathrm {=20-10=10}$<br>That volume of solution ${\\text{ = 1 d}}{{\\text{m}}^{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ = 1000 mL}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\therefore\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=\\dfrac{10}{1000}=10^{-2}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{pH}=-\\log \\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=-\\log \\left[10^{-2}\\right]=2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17796,'chemistry',48,758,1638,'When ammonium chloride is dissolved in water, the solution becomes cold. The change is:','[{"text": "Endothermic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Exothermic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Supercooling", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Heat is absorbed as solution becomes cold, so it is endothermic.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17797,'chemistry',50,844,1883,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In a galvanic cell, the salt bridge is used to complete the circuit.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The completion of circuit is important for the flow of electric current.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The salt bridge facilitates ion flow between half-cells, preventing excess charge accumulation and maintaining a balanced circuit. This is essential for continuous electron flow and a stable electric current in electrochemical cells. So the option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19071,'chemistry',53,989,2312,'Aluminium (III) chloride forms a dimer because aluminium','[{"text": "Cannot form a trimer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Has high ionisation energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Belongs to third group", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Can increase coordination number", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17799,'chemistry',49,626,1274,'$5 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of  ${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.4\\,N}}\\,{\\rm{NaOH}}$ is mxied with $20 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of  ${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.1\\,N}}\\,\\,{\\rm{HCl}}$  The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of the resulting soluiton will be','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Millimoles of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{NaOH}=5 \\times 0.4=2}$<br>Millimoles of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{HCl}=20 \\times 0.1=2}$<br>So, 2 millimoles of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{NaOH}}$ and 2 millimoles of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{HCl}}$ completely neutralise each other, resulting into a neutral solution $\\mathrm {(\\mathrm{pH}=7)}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17800,'chemistry',48,748,1605,'In a change from state $\\mathrm{A}$ to state $\\mathrm{B}$ :','[{"text": "\\" ${\\\\text{q}}$\\" depends only on the initial and final states.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "\\"${\\\\text{W}}$\\" depends only on the initial and final states.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{E}$ depends only on the initial and final states.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta$${\\\\text{E}}$ depends upon the path adopted by \\" ${\\\\text{A}}$ \\" to change into \\" ${\\\\text{B}}$ \\"", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Because $\\Delta \\mathrm{E}$ is a state function. Heat and work are path functions.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17801,'chemistry',50,837,1868,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The two $\\mathrm{Fe}$ atoms in $\\mathrm{Fe}_{3} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$ have different oxidation numbers.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ ions decolourise $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ solution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{F}}{{\\text{e}}_{\\text{3}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}} \\equiv \\left( {\\mathop {{\\text{Fe}}}\\limits^{{\\text{ + 2}}} {\\text{O }}{\\text{. F}}\\mathop {{{\\text{e}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}}\\limits^{{\\text{ + 3}}} } \\right)$<br>The oxidation states of $\\mathrm{Fe}$ in $\\mathrm{FeO}$ and $\\mathrm{Fe}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ are  $ + {\\text{2}}$  and  $ + {\\text{3}}$ .<br>$\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+\\underset{\\text { (Pink) }}{\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+\\underset{\\text { (Colourless) }}{\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}}$<br>Hence, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17803,'chemistry',49,626,1274,'Calculate the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of a solution which contains ${\\text{10}}\\,\\,{\\text{ml}}$ of ${\\text{1 M}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\text{HCl}}$ and ${\\text{10}}\\,\\,{\\text{ml}}$ of ${\\text{2 M}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\text{NaOH}}$','[{"text": "11.7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12.7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "13.7", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10.7", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{HCl}+\\mathrm{NaOH} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{NaCl}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$<br>$\\mathrm {M_{\\text {eq. }}}$ before $\\mathrm {10 \\times 1 \\quad 10 \\times 2}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\begin{array}{lllll}\\text { reaction } & =10 & =20 & 0 & 0\\end{array}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\begin{array}{lllll}\\mathrm{M}_{\\text {eq. }} \\text { After } & 0 & 10 & 10 & 10\\end{array}}$<br>reaction $\\mathrm {\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]}$left from $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{NaOH}=\\dfrac{10}{20}=0.5 \\mathrm{M}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pOH}=-\\log [\\mathrm{OH}]^{-}=-\\log 0.5}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pOH}=0.3}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}=13.7}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17804,'chemistry',48,752,1620,'For the reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{PCl}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}=\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{E}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}>\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{E}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H} < \\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{E}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these .", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{PCl}_{5(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{PCl}_{3(\\mathrm{~g})}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>For this reaction $\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=2-1=1$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}-\\Delta \\mathrm{E}=\\Delta \\mathrm{nRT}($ or $) \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{E}+\\Delta \\mathrm{nRT}$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H}>\\Delta \\mathrm{E}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17805,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A metal having negative reduction potential when dipped in the solution of its own ions has a tendency to pass into the solution.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Metal having negative reduction potential has large hydration energy.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A metal with a negative reduction potential indicates a greater likelihood of oxidation, as it has a higher tendency to lose electrons and form cations. This is influenced by its hydration energy, where a higher hydration energy results in a more negative reduction potential. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17838,'chemistry',49,632,1291,'$\\mathrm{M}(\\mathrm{OH})_{\\mathrm{x}}$ has $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}=4 \\times 10^{-12}$ and solubility $10^{-4} \\mathrm{M}$. Hence, $\\mathrm{x}$ is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{M(OH}}{{\\text{)}}_{\\text{x}}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{M}}_{\\text{s}}^{{{\\text{x}}^{\\text{ + }}}}{\\text{ + x}}\\mathop {{\\text{OH}}}\\limits_{{\\text{xS}}} $<br>$\\rm {\\therefore \\quad  (\\mathrm{s})(\\mathrm{xs})^{\\mathrm{x}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}} \\\\ \\mathrm{X}^{\\mathrm{x}} \\mathrm{S}^{\\mathrm{x}+1}=4 \\times 10^{-12} \\\\ \\mathrm{X}^{\\mathrm{x}}\\left(10^{-4}\\right)^{\\mathrm{x}+1}=4 \\times 10^{-12} \\\\ \\therefore \\quad \\mathrm{X}=2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17807,'chemistry',49,624,1266,'What is the percent ionization $(\\alpha)$ of  ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.01}}\\,\\,{\\text{M}}$ HA solution? $\\left(\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=10^{-4}\\right)$','[{"text": "$9.5 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.5 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {}&{{\\text{HA}}}& \\rightleftharpoons &{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{(aq)}}}^{{\\text{   +   }}}}&{{\\text{   +   }}}&{{{\\text{A}}^{{\\text{   -   (aq)}}}}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{At Equilibrium}}}&{{\\text{C(1  -  }}\\alpha {\\text{)}}}&{}&{{\\text{C}}\\alpha }&{}&{{\\text{C}}\\alpha } \n\\end{array}$<br>${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}} = \\frac{{{\\text{C}}{\\alpha ^2}}}{{1 - \\alpha }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {10^{ - 4}} = \\frac{{(0.01){\\alpha ^2}}}{{1 - \\alpha }}$<br>$\\alpha  = 0.095$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\% \\alpha  = 9.5$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17808,'chemistry',48,758,1645,'The reaction given below<br>${\\text{BaC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(s)  +  2}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O(l)}} \\to $<br>${\\text{BaC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{.2}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O(s) ; }}\\Delta {\\text{H  =   - xkJ}}$<br>The value of $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ represents:','[{"text": "Enthalpy of solution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Enthalpy of dilution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Enthalpy of combustion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Enthalpy of hydration", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The released enthalpy is called enthalpy of hydration as the compound formed is due to hydration.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17810,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If a copper wire is immersed in a solution of $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$, the colour of the solution becomes blue.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Copper splits up into atomic form and dissolves.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Copper is more reactive than silver. Hence when copper wire is dipped in silver nitrate solution it displaces silver from silver nitrate $\\left(\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}\\right)$ solution forming copper nitrate which is bluish green in color.<br>$\\mathrm{Cu}_{(\\mathrm{s})}+2 \\mathrm{AgNO}_{3(\\mathrm{aq})} \\rightarrow \\underset{\\text { Bluish green }}{\\mathrm{Cu}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}}+2 \\mathrm{Ag}$<br>So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17811,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'What is the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.10 M}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COONa}}$ solution? Hydrolysis constant of sodium acetate is $5.6 \\times 10^{-10}$','[{"text": "8.874", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "88.74", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "887.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.88", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Hydrolysis of the salt may be represented as <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/49_17811_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{h}}} = \\frac{{{{\\text{C}}^2}{\\alpha ^2}}}{{{\\text{C}}(1 - \\alpha )}} = {\\text{C}}{\\alpha ^2}{\\text{ when }}\\alpha  <  < 1$ <br>$\\alpha  = \\sqrt {\\left( {\\frac{{{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{h}}}}}{{\\text{C}}}} \\right)}  = \\sqrt {\\left( {\\frac{{5.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}{{0.10}}} \\right)}  = 7.5 \\times {10^{ - 5}}{\\text{ }}$ <br>$\\left[ {{\\text{O}}{{\\text{H}}^ - }} \\right] = {\\text{C}}\\alpha  = 0.10 \\times 7.5 \\times {10^{ - 5}}$<br>${\\text{pOH}} =  - \\log \\left( {7.5 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} \\right) = 5.126$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}=14-\\mathrm{pOH}=14-5.126=8.874}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17812,'chemistry',48,758,1637,'When $50 \\mathrm{~cm}^{3}$ of $0.2 {\\text{N  }}{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_4}$ is mixed with $50 \\mathrm{~cm}^{3}$ of $1 \\mathrm{~N} \\mathrm{KOH}$, the heat liberated is','[{"text": "$11.46 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$57.3 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$573 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$573 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For complete neutralization of strong acid and strong base energy released is $57.32 \\mathrm{KJ} /$ $\\mathrm{mol}$<br>No. of mole of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}=\\dfrac{0.2 \\times 50}{1000}=10^{-2}$<br>No. of mole of $\\mathrm{KOH}=\\dfrac{1}{1000} \\times 50=5 \\times 10^{-2}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ is limiting reagent as 1 mole $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ needs 2 moles $\\mathrm{KOH}$<br>So $=57.32 \\times 10^{-2}=0.5732 \\mathrm{~kJ}=573.2 \\mathrm{~J}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17813,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Amongst the halogens, fluorine can oxidise the elements to the highest oxidation state.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Due to small size of fluoride ion, it is difficult to oxidise fluoride ion to fluorine. Hence, reverse reaction takes place more easily.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Fluorine, owing to its high electronegativity and heat of dissociation, acts as a potent oxidizing agent by attracting electrons from other elements, causing their oxidation to the highest possible oxidation state, and exhibiting strong reactivity due to the energy required to break fluorine molecule bonds. So the option (2) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18387,'chemistry',48,756,1630,'In the reaction<br> $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{CO}(\\mathrm{g})+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{g})$<br> $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=40 \\mathrm{~kJ} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ represents :','[{"text": "Heat of formation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heat of combustion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heat of neutralisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heat of reaction", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Heat of reaction',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17815,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of  ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.1}}\\,\\,{\\text{M}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{Cl}}$ solution is 5.13. What will be the dissociation constant of $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{OH}$ ?','[{"text": "$1.8 \\\\times 10^{-7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.8 \\\\times 10^{-9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.8 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}}$ is salt of weak base and strong acid, formula for $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}}$ of its hydrolysis may be calculated as,<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left[\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{w}}-\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}-\\log \\mathrm{C}\\right]}$<br>where $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}=5.13}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{W}}=-\\log \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{W}}=-\\log 10^{-14}=14}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\log \\mathrm{C}=\\log 0.1=-1}$<br>Substituting the values in eq. (1) we get<br>$\\rm { 5.13=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left[14-\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}+1\\right] \\\\ 10.26=15-\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}} \\\\ \\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}=4.74 \\\\ \\therefore-\\log \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}}=\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}=4.74 \\\\ \\mathrm{~K}_{\\mathrm{b}}=\\operatorname{anti} \\log (-4.74) \\\\ \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}}=1.8 \\times 10^{-5}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17816,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ is a stronger oxidising agent than $\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> This is due to the increasing stability of the lower species to which they are reduced.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Potassium permanganate $\\left(\\mathrm{KMNO}_{4}\\right)$ is a stronger and more versatile oxidizing agent than potassium dichromate $\\left(\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}\\right)$ due to factors including the higher oxidation state of manganese $(+7)$, its effectiveness under both acidic and basic conditions, larger atom size, greater stability of oxidation states, and higher redox potential. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17818,'chemistry',48,756,1630,'Which of the following statements / relationship is not correct?','[{"text": "In an exothermic reaction, the enthalpy of products is less than that of the reactants.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\text {fusion }}=\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\text {sublimation }}-\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\text {vapourisation }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A reaction for which $\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}^{0} < 0$ and $\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}>0$, is possible at all temperatures", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}$ is less than $\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{E}$ for combustion of carbon to carbon dioxide", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In the formation of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from carbon,<br>$\\mathrm{C}_{(\\mathrm{s})}+\\mathrm{O}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>$\\Delta_{\\mathrm{ng}}=1-1=0$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{E}$<br>Rest statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17819,'chemistry',49,629,1279,'Calculate $\\mathrm{pH}$ of the buffer solution containing 0.15 moles of $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{OH}$ and 0.25 moles of $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}, \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}}$ for $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{OH}$ is $1.8 \\times 10^{-5}$','[{"text": "7.034", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9.04", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8.043", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Applying the equation,<br>$\\rm { \\mathrm{pOH}=\\log \\dfrac{[\\text { Salt }]}{[\\text { Base }]}-\\log \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}} \\\\ =\\log \\dfrac{0.25}{0.15}-\\log 1.8 \\times 10^{-5} \\\\ =\\log 5-\\log 3-\\log 1.8 \\times 10^{-5}=4.96 \\\\ \\mathrm{pH}=(14-4.96)=9.04}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17820,'chemistry',48,753,1625,'"${\\text{X}}$" gm of ethanal was subjected to combustion in a bomb calorimeter and the heat produced is "${\\text{Y}}$" Joules. Then:','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{E}_{\\\\text {(combustion) }}=-\\\\mathrm{XJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{E}_{\\\\text {(combustion) }}=-\\\\mathrm{YJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{E}_{\\\\text {(combustion) }}=-44 \\\\mathrm{Y} / \\\\mathrm{XJ} \\\\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\text {(combustion) }}=+44 \\\\mathrm{Y} / \\\\mathrm{XJ} \\\\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In bomb calorimeter, as volume is constant the heat change is $\\Delta \\mathrm{E}$<br>Given, \' $\\mathrm{X}$ \'g ethanal gives $\\mathrm{Y}$ Joules heat<br>$\\therefore 44\\,{\\text{g }}\\mathop {{\\text{ethanal}}}\\limits_{{\\text{(1}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{mole)}}} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{gives }} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{44{\\text{Y}}}}{{\\text{X}}}{\\text{Joules mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$<br>As reaction is exothermic, it\'s given with -ve sign.<br>$\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{E}=-\\dfrac{44 \\mathrm{Y}}{\\mathrm{X}} \\mathrm{J} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17822,'chemistry',50,844,1883,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Salts like $\\mathrm{KCl}, \\mathrm{KNO}_{3}$ i.e., inert electrolytes are used in salt bridge.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> An inert electrolyte can easily be filled in the U-tube.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Inert electrolytes, such as salts like potassium chloride $(\\mathrm{KCl})$ or sodium sulfate $\\left(\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)$, are used in salt bridge because they enhance electrical conductivity, maintain charge neutrality, minimize interference in redox reactions, and provide a stable background for monitoring electrochemical changes without actively participating in chemical transformations. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17823,'chemistry',49,629,1278,'A buffer solution contains 0.1 mole of sodium acetate in $1000 \\mathrm{~cm}^{3}$ of ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.1}}\\,\\,{\\text{M}}$ acetic acid. To the above buffer solution, 0.1 mole of sodium acetate is further added and dissolved. The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of the resulting buffer is equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pK}_{\\\\mathrm{a}}-\\\\log 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pK}_{\\\\mathrm{a}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pK}_{\\\\mathrm{a}}+2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{pK}_{\\\\mathrm{a}}+\\\\log 2$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}=\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}+\\log \\dfrac{[\\text { Salt }]}{[\\text { Acid }]}}$<br>$\\mathrm {[}$ Salt $\\mathrm {]=\\dfrac{0.1+0.1}{1000 \\mathrm{~mL}}=\\dfrac{0.2}{1}=0.2}$ <br>$\\mathrm {[}$ Acid $\\mathrm {]=\\dfrac{0.1}{1000 \\mathrm{~mL}}=\\dfrac{0.1}{1}=0.1}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{pH}=\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}+\\log 2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17825,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{PbCl}_{4}$ is a powerful oxidising agent.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{PbCl}_{4}$ is more stable than $\\mathrm{PbCl}_{2}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{Pb}$ loses two p-electrons to form stable $\\mathrm{PbCl}_{2}$. It has to loose its s electrons as well to form $\\mathrm{PbCl}_{4}$, which is less stable. This is due to inert pair effect (reluctance of ns elecrons to take part in the reaction). Thus, due to less stability, $\\mathrm{PbCl}_{4}$ easily gains $2 \\mathrm{e}$ - to became more stable $\\mathrm{PbCl}_{2}$. And in turn behaves as a strong oxidising agent. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17826,'chemistry',48,752,1621,'The value of $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ for cooling 2 moles of an ideal monoatomic gas from $225^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ to $125^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ at constant pressure will be:<br>(given, $\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{P}}=5 \\mathrm{R} / 2$ )','[{"text": "$250 \\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-500 \\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$500 \\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-250 \\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=-\\mathrm{nC}_{\\mathrm{p}} \\Delta \\mathrm{T}=-2 \\times \\dfrac{5}{2} \\mathrm{R}(225-125)$<br>$=-5 \\mathrm{R}(100)=-500 \\mathrm{R}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17827,'chemistry',49,632,1291,'Consider the following sparingly soluble salts having same solubility product in their saturated solution in water:<br>(A) ${\\text{XY}}$  <br>(B) $\\mathrm{X}_{2} \\mathrm{Y}$<br>(C) $\\mathrm{XY}_{3}$<br>The correct order of increasing solubility of these salts in water will be','[{"text": "A $ < $ B $ < $ C", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "C $ < $ B $ < $ A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B $ < $ A $ < $ C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B $ < $ C $ < $ A", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $\\mathrm {S_{1}, S_{2}, S_{3}}$ be the solubility of ${\\text{XY}}$,  $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{X}_{2} \\mathrm{Y}}$ and $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{XY}_{3}}$ in water, respectively<br>${\\text{Salt       Solubality product}}$<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {{\\text{XY}}}&{{\\text{             S}}_{\\text{1}}^{\\text{2}}} \\\\ \n  {{{\\text{X}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{Y}}}&{{\\text{              3S}}_{\\text{2}}^{\\text{3}}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{X}}{{\\text{Y}}_{\\text{3}}}}&{{\\text{                27S}}_{\\text{3}}^{\\text{4}}} \n\\end{array}$<br>According to given condition,<br>${\\text{S}}_{\\text{1}}^{\\text{2}}{\\text{ = 4S}}_{\\text{2}}^{\\text{3}}{\\text{ = 27S}}_{\\text{3}}^{\\text{4}}\\,\\,{\\text{or }}{{\\text{S}}_{\\text{1}}}{\\text{ = 2S}}_{\\text{2}}^{{\\text{3/2}}}{\\text{ = 3}}\\sqrt {\\text{3}} {\\text{S}}_{\\text{3}}^{\\text{2}}$<br>Since the practical value of solubility product is found to be very small. So, $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{S}_{1} < \\mathrm{S}_{2} < \\mathrm{S}_{3}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17829,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The oxidation number of $\\mathrm{O}$ in $\\mathrm{O}_{3}$ is zero and the oxidation number of $\\mathrm{S}$ in $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ is +4 .<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{O}_{3}$ can act as an oxidising agent as well as a reducing agent but $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ can act only as an oxidising agent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The oxidation state of Oxygen in $\\mathrm{O}_{3}$ is zero, while the oxidation state of $\\mathrm{S}$ in $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ is ${\\text{ + 4}}$ . Sulfur dioxide can function as both oxidising and reducing agent whereas ozone can only act as an oxidizing agent due to oxygen\'s oxidation state which decreases but not increases in reactions. So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17830,'chemistry',48,758,1635,'In an order to decompose $9 \\mathrm{~g}$ water, $142.5 \\mathrm{~kJ}$ heat is required. Hence enthalpy of formation of water is:','[{"text": "$-142.5 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$+142.5 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-285 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$+285 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For the decomposition of ${\\text{9 g}}$ of water, heat required $=142.5 \\mathrm{~kJ}$<br>$\\therefore$ heat required for decomposition of $18 \\mathrm{~g}$ of water (1 mole)<br>$=\\dfrac{18}{9} \\times 142.5=285 \\mathrm{~kJ}$<br>and enthalpy of formation of water $=-285 \\mathrm{KJ}$ (sign changed as decomposition and formation are reverse reactions)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17831,'chemistry',49,632,1293,'The solubility product of chalk is $9.3 \\times 10^{-8}$. Calculate its solubility in gram per litre','[{"text": "0.3040 gram/litre", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.0304 gram/litre", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.0304 gram/litre", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.0304 gram/litre", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CaCO}_{3} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}+\\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}}$<br>Let the solubility of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{CaCO}_{3}}$ be \' $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{s}}$ \' mole per litre<br>$\\mathrm {\\therefore \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}=\\left[\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}\\right]=\\mathrm{s} . \\mathrm{s}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\therefore \\mathrm{s}=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}}=\\sqrt{9.3 \\times 10^{-8}}=0.000304 \\mathrm{~mole} / \\mathrm{litre}}$<br>Solubility in $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{g} / \\mathrm{l}=}$ mole/litre $\\mathrm {\\times}$ Molecular wt.<br>${\\text{  =  0}}.{\\text{000304}} \\times {\\text{100 = 0}}{\\text{.0304 gram/litre}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17832,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In some cases oxygen shows positive oxidation number though it is an electronegative elements.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Fluorine is more electronegative than oxygen.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Despite being highly electronegative and typically exhibiting negative oxidation states, oxygen can display positive oxidation states, such as ${\\text{ + 2}}$ in oxygen difluoride $\\left(\\mathrm{OF}_{2}\\right)$, when bonded to more electronegative elements like fluorine. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17834,'chemistry',48,757,1634,'Given:<br>${\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_3}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right) + 3{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}_2}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right) \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{NC}}{{\\text{l}}_3}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right) + 3{\\text{HCl}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right), - \\Delta {{\\text{H}}_1}$<br>${{\\text{N}}_2}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right) + 3{{\\text{H}}_2}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right) \\rightleftharpoons 2{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_3}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)\\,; - \\Delta {{\\text{H}}_2}$<br>${{\\text{H}}_2}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right) + {\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}_2}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right) \\rightleftharpoons 2{\\text{HCl}}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right);\\Delta {{\\text{H}}_3}$<br>The heat of formation of $\\mathrm{NCl}_{3(\\mathrm{~g})}$ in terms of<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{1}, \\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{3}$ is:','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{f}}=-\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{1}+\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}}{2}-\\\\dfrac{3}{2} \\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{f}}=\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{1}+\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}}{2}-\\\\dfrac{3}{2} \\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{f}}=\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{1}+\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}}{2}+\\\\dfrac{3}{2} \\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'To get $\\dfrac{1}{2} \\underset{(\\mathrm{g})}{\\mathrm{N}_{2}}+\\dfrac{3}{2} \\underset{(\\mathrm{g})}{\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}} \\rightarrow \\underset{(\\mathrm{g})}{\\mathrm{NCl}_{3}}$<br>$[\\mathrm{eq}(1)]+\\left[\\dfrac{\\mathrm{eq}(2)}{2}\\right]+\\left[\\mathrm{eq}(3) \\times \\dfrac{3}{2}\\right]$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{\\mathrm{f}}=-\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{1}-\\dfrac{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{2}}{2}-\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{3} \\times \\dfrac{3}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17835,'chemistry',49,632,1291,'Given the solubility product of ${{\\text{A}}_{\\text{3}}}{{\\text{B}}_{\\text{2}}}$  is $2 \\times 10^{-30}$. What will will be the solubility in moles/litre?','[{"text": "$\\\\left(1.85 \\\\times 10^{-32}\\\\right)^{1 / 5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\times 10^{-30}}{108}\\\\right)^{1 / 5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{10-28}{5400}\\\\right)^{1 / 5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}}$ of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{A}_{3} \\mathrm{~B}_{2}=2 \\times 10^{-30}}$<br>${{\\text{A}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{}}{{\\text{B}}_{\\text{2}}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{3}}{{\\text{A}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}}{\\text{ + 2}}{{\\text{B}}^{{\\text{3 - }}}}$<br>Assuming \' $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{s}}$ \' is the solubility of  ${{\\text{A}}_{\\text{3}}}{{\\text{B}}_{\\text{2}}}$ then<br>${{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{sp}}}}{\\text{ = (3s}}{{\\text{)}}^{\\text{3}}}{{\\text{(2s)}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ = 108}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{5}}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{s}=5 \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}}{108}} \\quad \\therefore \\mathrm{s}=\\left(\\dfrac{2 \\times 10^{-30}}{108}\\right)^{1 / 5}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17836,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Stannous chloride gives grey precipitate with mercuric chloride, but stannic chloride does not do so.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Stannous chloride is a powerful oxidising agent which oxidises mercuric chloride to metallic mercury.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}$ (stannous chloride) on reaction with mercuric chloride $\\left(\\mathrm{HgCl}_{2}\\right)$, oxidises to $\\mathrm{SnCl}_{4}$ (stannic chloride)<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{HgCl}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{SnCl}_{4}+\\mathrm{Hg}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\\\& \\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}+\\mathrm{Hg}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\rightarrow \\underset{\\text { (Greyish) }}{2 \\mathrm{Hg}}+\\mathrm{SnCl}_{4}\\end{aligned}$<br>So in this reaction, stannous chloride is acting as reducing agent that reduces $\\mathrm{Hg}$ (II) to $\\mathrm{Hg}$ (I) and then to $\\mathrm{Hg}(0)$ So the option (3) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17839,'chemistry',48,753,1625,'$4 \\mathrm{~g}$ of graphite is burnt in a bomb calorimeter of heat capacity $30 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~K}^{-1}$ in excess of oxygen at $1 \\mathrm{~atm}$ pressure. The temperature rises from 300 $\\mathrm{K}$ to $304 \\mathrm{~K}$. What is the enthalpy of combustion of graphite $\\left( {in} \\right.\\left. {{\\text{ kJ mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{ - 1}}} \\right)$ ?','[{"text": "${\\\\text{360}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{1440}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - {\\\\text{360}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - {\\\\text{1440}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\Delta \\mathrm{E}=\\mathrm{C} \\times \\Delta \\mathrm{t} \\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{M}}{\\mathrm{m}}=30 \\times 4 \\times \\dfrac{12}{4}=360$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{E}=-360 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$ (as it is combustion, sign is ${\\text{ -  ve}}$)<br>for $\\underset{(\\mathrm{s})}{\\mathrm{C}}+\\underset{(\\mathrm{g})}{\\mathrm{O}_{2}} \\rightarrow \\underset{(\\mathrm{g})}{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}} ; \\underset{(\\mathrm{g})}{\\Delta \\mathrm{n}}=1-1=0$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{E}=-360 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17841,'chemistry',50,836,1862,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Reaction of $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ and ${{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}$ in the presence of $\\mathrm{Fe}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ catalyst gives elemental sulphur.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ is a reducing agent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In the given reaction, sulfur dioxide $\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{2}\\right)$ acts as an oxidizing agent, causing hydrogen sulfide to oxidize, leading to the reduction of sulfur dioxide from an oxidation state of ${\\text{ + 4}}$ to ${\\text{ 0}}$ , and the oxidation of hydrogen sulfide from ${\\text{ - 2}}$ to ${\\text{ 0}}$ , forming water as a product. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17842,'chemistry',49,632,1293,'Solubility product of a salt ${\\text{AB}}$  is ${\\text{1}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 8}}}}{\\text{ }}{{\\text{M}}^{\\text{2}}}$ in a solution in which the concentration of $\\mathrm{A}^{+}$ions is ${10^{ - 3}}{\\text{ M}}$. The salt will precipitate when the concentration of $\\mathrm{B}^{-}$ ions is kept','[{"text": "${\\\\text{Between 1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{{\\\\text{ - 8}}}}{\\\\text{ M to 1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{{\\\\text{ - 7}}}}{\\\\text{ M}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{Between 1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{{\\\\text{ - 7}}}}{\\\\text{ M to 1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{{\\\\text{ - 8}}}}{\\\\text{ M}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{ > 1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{{\\\\text{ - 5}}}}{\\\\text{ M}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{ < 1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{{\\\\text{ - 8}}}}{\\\\text{ M}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}=1 \\times 10^{-8} \\mathrm{M}^{2}}$<br>$\\rm {\\begin{gathered}{\\left[\\mathrm{A}^{+}\\right]=10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}} \\\\\\therefore\\left[\\mathrm{B}^{-}\\right]=\\dfrac{1 \\times 10^{-8}}{10^{-3}} \\mathrm{M}=10^{-5} \\mathrm{M}\\end{gathered}}$<br>$\\mathrm {\\mathrm{AB}}$ will be precipitated if ionic product will be greater than solubility product. That means when the concentration of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{B}^{-}}$ions will be more than $\\mathrm {10^{-5} \\mathrm{M}}$, then $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{AB}}$ will be precipitated.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17843,'chemistry',48,762,1659,'For a certain process, $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=178 \\mathrm{~kJ}$ and $\\Delta \\mathrm{S}=160 \\mathrm{~J} / \\mathrm{K}$. What is the minimum temperature at which the process is spontaneous (assuming that $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ and $\\Delta \\mathrm{S}$ do not vary with temperature?','[{"text": "$2112.3 \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$136.7 \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$275.8 \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1112.5 \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'To be at equilibrium,<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{G}=0$ and<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{G}=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S}$<br>So, $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S}$<br>$\\mathrm{T}=\\dfrac{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}}{\\Delta \\mathrm{S}}=\\dfrac{178000}{160}=1112.5 \\mathrm{~K}$<br>So the process to be spontaneous, temperature should be more than 1112.5 K (in order to maintain $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}-\\mathrm{ve})$ i.e., 2112.3 K',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17844,'chemistry',50,837,1868,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The formal oxidation number of sulphur in $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{6}$ is 2.5 .<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Two S-atoms are not directly linked with O-atoms.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Formal oxidation number of<br>sulphur $=\\dfrac{2 \\times 5+2 \\times 0}{4}=2.5$.<br>Therefore, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17846,'chemistry',49,632,1293,'One $\\mathrm{dm}^{3}$ solution containing $10^{-5}$ moles each of $\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ions and $\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}$ ions is treated with $10^{-4}$ moles of silver nitrate. Which one of the following observations is made?<br>$\\begin{aligned}& {\\left[\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}} \\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}=4 \\times 10^{-12}\\right],} <br>{\\left[\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}} \\mathrm{AgCl}=1 \\times 10^{-10}\\right]}\\end{aligned}$','[{"text": "Precipitation does not occur.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Silver chromate gets precipitated first", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Silver chloride gets precipitated first", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both silver chromate and silver chloride start precipitating simultaneously", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Ionic product of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{AgCl}=\\left[10^{-4}\\right]\\left[10^{-5}\\right]}$<br>$\\rm {=10^{-9}}$<br>Ionic product of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}=\\left[10^{-8}\\right]\\left[10^{-5}\\right]=10^{-13}}$<br>Ionic product of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{AgCl}>\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}}$ of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{AgCl}}$<br>So, precipitation will take place.<br>Ionic product of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4} < \\mathrm{K}_{\\text {sp }}}$ of $\\mathrm {\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}}$ So, precipitation will not take place.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17847,'chemistry',48,761,1654,'If the enthalpy change for the transition of liquid water to steam is $300 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ at $27^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, the entropy change for the process would be:','[{"text": "$1000 \\\\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10 \\\\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 \\\\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.1 \\\\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\Delta {{\\text{S}}_{{\\text{vap }}}} = \\frac{{\\Delta {{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{vap }}}}}}{{\\text{T}}} = $<br>$\\frac{{300\\,{\\rm{kJ}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}}}{{300\\,{\\rm{K}}}} = \\frac{{300000}}{{300}}\\frac{{{\\rm{J}} \\cdot {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}}}{{\\rm{K}}}$<br>$ = 1000{\\text{J}}{{\\text{K}}^{ - 1}}\\,{\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17848,'chemistry',50,836,1862,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Fluorine is a stronger oxidising agent than iodine.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Fluorine has greater electronegativity than iodine.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Fluorine, with a higher standard electrode potential, is a more potent oxidizing agent compared to iodine, indicating its greater tendency to gain electrons and undergo reduction. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17850,'chemistry',49,632,1291,'Match the column I with column II and mark the appropriate choice.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. ${\\rm{Fe}}{\\left( {{\\rm{OH}}} \\right)_{\\rm{3}}}$<br>B.${\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{g}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{Cr}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$<br>C.${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{COOAg}}$<br>D.${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\left( {{\\rm{P}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{2}}}$</div><div class="column-right">P ${{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{sp}}}}{\\text{ = }}{{\\text{S}}^{\\text{2}}}$<br>Q ${{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{sp}}}} = 27\\,{{\\text{S}}^4}$ <br> R ${{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{sp}}}}{\\text{ = 108}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\text{S}}^{\\text{5}}}$ <br> S $\\,{{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{sp}}}}{\\text{ = 4}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\text{S}}^{\\text{3}}}$</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A - R,    B - Q,    C - S,    D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - Q,    B - S,    C - P,    D - R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - P,    B - R,    C - Q,    D - S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - S,    B - P,    C - R,    D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'(A) ${\\text{Fe}}{({\\text{OH}})_{\\text{3}}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{F}}{{\\text{e}}^{{\\text{3 + }}}}{\\text{ + 3O}}{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ - }}};{{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{sp}}}}{\\text{ = 27}}\\,{{\\text{S}}^{\\text{4}}}$<br>(B) ${\\text{A}}{{\\text{g}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{Cr}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2A}}{{\\text{g}}^{\\text{ + }}}{\\text{ + CrO}}_{\\text{4}}^{{\\text{2 - }}};{{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{sp }}}}{\\text{ = 4}}{{\\text{S}}^{\\text{3}}}$<br>(C) ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COOAg}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CO}}{{\\text{O}}^{\\text{ - }}}{\\text{ + A}}{{\\text{g}}^{\\text{ + }}};{{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{sp}}}}{\\text{ = }}{{\\text{S}}^{\\text{2}}}$<br>(D) ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{a}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\left( {{\\text{P}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}} \\right)_{\\text{2}}} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathop {{\\text{3C}}{{\\text{a}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}}}\\limits_{\\left( {{\\text{3}}\\,{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}} \\right)} {\\text{ + }}\\mathop {{\\text{2PO}}_{\\text{4}}^{{\\text{3 - }}}}\\limits_{\\left( {{\\text{2}}\\,{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}} \\right)} ;{{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{sp}}}}{\\text{ = 108}}\\,{{\\text{S}}^{\\text{5}}}$<br>So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17851,'chemistry',48,758,1637,'If the heat of neutralisation for a strong acidbase reaction is $-57.1 \\mathrm{~kJ}$, what would be the amount of heat released when $350 \\mathrm{~cm}^{3}$ of ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.20}}\\,{\\text{M}}\\,\\,{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}$ is mixed with $650 \\mathrm{~cm}^{3}$ of $0.10 \\mathrm{M}$ $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ ?','[{"text": "$37.1 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.71 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.17 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.317 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Moles of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}: 0.2=\\mathrm{n} \\times \\dfrac{1000}{350}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{n}=0.07 \\mathrm{~mole}$<br>Moles of $\\mathrm{NaOH}: 0.1=\\mathrm{n} \\times \\dfrac{1000}{650}$<br>$\\mathrm{n}=0.065 \\mathrm{~mole}$<br>1 mole $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ needs 2 mole $\\mathrm{NaOH}$<br>0.07 mole $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ needs ?<br>$=\\dfrac{0.07 \\times 2}{1}=0.14 \\mathrm{~mole} \\mathrm{NaOH}$<br>But given has only 0.065 moles $\\mathrm{NaOH}$. So, it is limiting reagent.<br>1 mole gives $57.1 \\mathrm{~kJ}$<br>0.065 mole gives?<br>$=\\dfrac{0.065 \\times 57.1}{1}=3.7115$<br>$=3.71 \\mathrm{~kJ}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17852,'chemistry',50,836,1862,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ gas bleaches the articles permanently.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ is a strong reducing agent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ is an effective oxidizing agent. When it comes into contact with various substances, it can cause oxidation reactions, leading to the removal of color from these materials. In the presence of moisture, $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ exhibits its bleaching properties by oxidizing the substances. So the option (3) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17855,'chemistry',48,763,1661,'For a hypothetical reaction:<br>$\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}$, if $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}>0$','[{"text": "The reaction tends to proceed slowly towards the formation of  ${\\\\text{C}}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The increase in temperature increases the yield of ${\\\\text{C}}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A}}$ and  ${\\\\text{B}}$ predominate in the reaction mixture.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{C}}$ predominates in the reaction mixture.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}$ for the formation of $\\mathrm{C}$ is $+\\mathrm{ve} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}$ for the formation of $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{B}$ is -ve. Hence A and $\\mathrm{B}$ predominate in the reaction mixture.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17859,'chemistry',48,762,1659,'The occurrence of a reaction is impossible if','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}$ is ${\\\\text{ +  ve}}$; $\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}$ is also ${\\\\text{ +  ve}}$ but $\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H} < \\\\mathrm{T} \\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}$ is ${\\\\text{ -  ve}}$; $\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}$ is also ${\\\\text{ -  ve}}$ but $\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}>\\\\mathrm{T} \\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}$ is ${\\\\text{ -  ve}}$; $\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}$ is ${\\\\text{ +  ve}}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}$ is ${\\\\text{ -  ve}}$; $\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}$ is ${\\\\text{ -  ve}}$.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ is +ve and $\\Delta \\mathrm{S}$ is -ve, $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}$ will be always positive and reaction will be non spontaneous.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17860,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'Which of the following cannot act as an oxidising agent?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}^{3-}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BrO}_{3}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'An oxidizing agent must undergo reduction. Here, $\\mathrm{N}^{3-}$ is already in its lowest oxidation state of -3 and cannot be reduced further. Thus, it cannot be used as an oxidizig agent. On the other hand, $\\mathrm{Mn}^{7+}, \\mathrm{S}^{6+}$ and $\\mathrm{Br}^{5+}$ can be reduced to lower states and behave as oxidising agents.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17863,'chemistry',48,761,1652,'For chemical reactions, the calculation of change in entropy is normally done','[{"text": "at constant pressure.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "at constant temperature.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "at both constant temperature and pressure.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "at constant volume.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Calculation of change in entropy is done at constant temperature and pressure.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17864,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'In which of the following reactions, the hydrogen peroxide acts as a reducing agent?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PbS}+4 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{PbSO}_{4}+4 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+2 \\\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HOCl}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{O}^{+}+\\\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{Mn}^{4+}+2 \\\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17864_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Chlorine is reduced from +1 to –1 state.<br>In the remaining reactions, PbS is oxidised to $\\mathrm{PbSO}_{4}(-2$ to +6$), \\mathrm{Fe}^{+2}$ to $\\mathrm{Fe}^{+3}(+2$ to +3$)$ and $\\mathrm{Mn}^{+2}$ to $\\mathrm{Mn}^{+4}(+2$ to +4 ).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17867,'chemistry',48,756,1630,'Which of the following statement is correct?','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}$ is positive for exothermic reactions.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}$ is negative for endothermic reactions.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The heat of neutralization of strong acid and strong base is always the same.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The enthalpy of fusion is negative.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For exothermic reactions, $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ is $-\\mathrm{ve}$ and for endothermic reactions, $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=+\\mathrm{ve}$ The enthalpy of fusion is $+\\mathrm{ve}$ (endothermic)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17869,'chemistry',50,838,1873,'Which of the following options are correct for the reaction<br>$2\\left[\\mathrm{Au}(\\mathrm{CN})_{2}\\right]_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{-}+\\mathrm{Zn}_{(\\mathrm{s})} \\rightarrow$  $2 \\mathrm{Au}_{(\\mathrm{s})}+\\left[\\mathrm{Zn}(\\mathrm{CN})_{4}\\right]_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{2-}$<br>(A) Redox reaction<br>(B) Displacement reaction<br>(C) Decomposition reaction<br>(D) Combination reaction<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "A and B only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A and D only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C and D only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{Au}^{+}$  to Au (reduction)  &  Zn to $\\mathrm{Zn}^{+2}$ (oxidation). So, it is a redox reaction as well as displacement reaction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17871,'chemistry',48,761,1654,'Consider the reactions at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$<br>(a) $4{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_2}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right) + {{\\text{O}}_2}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right) \\to 2\\,{{\\text{N}}_2}{{\\text{O}}_5}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right);\\Delta {\\text{H}} =  - 111\\,\\,{\\text{kJ}}$<br>(b) $4{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_2}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right) + {{\\text{O}}_2}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right) \\to 2 {{\\text{N}}_2}{{\\text{O}}_5}\\left( s \\right);\\Delta {\\text{H}} =  - 219\\,{\\text{kJ}}$<br>Thus entropy change for the sublimation of one mole ${{\\text{N}}_2}{{\\text{O}}_5}\\left( s \\right)$ is :','[{"text": "$180 \\\\mathrm{~J} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~K}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$360 \\\\mathrm{~J} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~K}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$108 \\\\mathrm{~J} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~K}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$54 \\\\mathrm{~J} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~K}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Reverse the reaction<br>(b) and add reaction<br>(a) to get $2\\,\\mathop {{{\\text{N}}_2}{{\\text{O}}_5}}\\limits_{\\left( {\\text{s}} \\right)}  \\to 2\\,\\mathop {{{\\text{N}}_2}{{\\text{O}}_5}}\\limits_{\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)} {\\mkern 1mu} ;$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=+219-111=+108 \\mathrm{~kJ}$ for 2 moles<br>$\\therefore$ for $1 \\mathrm{~mole}=+54 \\mathrm{~kJ}=54000 \\mathrm{~J}$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{S}_{\\text {sub }}=\\dfrac{\\Delta_{\\text {sub }} \\mathrm{H}}{\\mathrm{T}}=\\dfrac{54000}{300}=180 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\mathrm{~K}^{-1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17875,'chemistry',48,758,1635,'The enthalpy of formation of ammonia is $ - 46$  $\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$. The enthalpy for the reaction $2\\,{{\\text{N}}_2}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right) + 6{{\\text{H}}_2}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right) \\to 4{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_3}\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)$ is equal to -','[{"text": "$-46.0 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$46.0 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$184.0 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-184.0 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Enthalpy of formation of 1 mole<br>$\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is $-46 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mole}$<br>$\\therefore$ for 4 mole $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}=-4 \\times 46$<br>$=-184 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17876,'chemistry',50,839,1878,'$2 \\mathrm{IO}_{3}^{-}+\\mathrm{xI}^{-}+12 \\mathrm{H}^{+} \\rightarrow 6 \\mathrm{I}_{2}+6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>What is the value of x ?','[{"text": "10", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "12", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Balanced chemical equation is  $2 \\mathrm{IO}_{3}^{-}+10 \\mathrm{I}^{-}+12 \\mathrm{H}^{+} \\rightarrow 6 \\mathrm{I}_{2}+6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17883,'chemistry',48,748,1603,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A cup of tea placed in a room is an example of open system.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Transfer of heat and water vapour can take place with the surrounding.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'An open system has the capacity to exchange or transfer both matter and energy between the system and its surroundings. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17884,'chemistry',50,837,1869,'The covalency and oxidation state respectively of boron in ${\\left[ {{\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{F}}_4}} \\right]^ - }$, are','[{"text": "4 and 4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 and 5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4 and 3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3 and 4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In $\\left[\\mathrm{BF}_{4}\\right]^{-}$,B forms 4 covalent bonds with 4 fluorine atoms, so its covalency is 4 .<br>Let \' x \' be the oxidation state of boron.<br>$\\mathrm{x}+4(-1)=-1$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{x}=+3$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17887,'chemistry',48,748,1608,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Thermodynamic process means change in at least one of the state variables of the system.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Process is accompanied by an exchange of matter or/and energy between the system and the surroundings.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In thermodynamics, a system\'s state is defined by variables like temperature and pressure, while a thermodynamic process involves changes in these state variables, such as compressing a gas or undergoing a phase transition. Thus, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17891,'chemistry',48,751,1612,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> First law of thermodynamics is identical to the law of conservation of energy.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It states that the total energy of the universe is constant.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The first law of thermodynamics and the law of conservation of energy are equivalent, stating that the total energy in an isolated system remains constant, undergoing only transformations or transfers between the system and its surroundings. Thus, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17895,'chemistry',48,748,1608,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> There is no exchange in internal energy in a cyclic process.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Cyclic process is the one in which the system returns to its initial state after a number of reactions and internal energy is a state function.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Since internal energy is a state function, its value is dependent on the initial and final states of the system. In the case of a cyclic system, the initial and final states are identical. Therefore, $\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=0$. So, option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17896,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}$ acts as a reducing agent in','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}+4 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}+4 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{NaOCl}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{NaCl}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17896_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Here, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}$ acts as reducing agent.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17899,'chemistry',50,836,1861,'Which reaction is NOT a redox reaction?','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Zn}+\\\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{ZnSO}_{4}+\\\\mathrm{Cu}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{2 \\\\mathrm{KClO}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{KIO}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{HCl}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}+2 \\\\mathrm{NaCl}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17899_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  This is not a redox reaction as there is no change in the oxidation states of any element.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17900,'chemistry',48,751,1612,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For an isothermal reversible process, $\\mathrm{q}=-\\mathrm{w}$ i.e.,work done by the system equals the heat absorbed by the system.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enthalpy change $(\\Delta \\mathrm{H})$ is not zero for isothermal process.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In an isothermal process, there is no change in the internal energy $(\\mathrm{U})$ as it is a function of temperature. Therefore, $\\Delta \\mathrm{U}$ is zero.<br>According to the first law of thermodynamics, $\\mathrm{q}+\\mathrm{w}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}$, this implies that if $\\Delta \\mathrm{U}$ is $0, \\mathrm{q}+\\mathrm{w}$ $=0$. Therefore, when $\\left( {\\Delta {\\text{U}} = 0} \\right), - {\\text{w}} = {\\text{q}}$. Thus work done by the system is equal to the heat absorbed by the system. Thus, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17903,'chemistry',50,837,1865,'Which of the following is not a redox reaction?','[{"text": "$\\\\underline 3 {\\\\rm{Mg}} + {{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}} \\\\to {\\\\rm{M}}{{\\\\rm{g}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}\\\\,{{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{MnO}}_4^ -  + {{\\\\rm{C}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{O}}_4^{2 - } \\\\to {\\\\rm{M}}{{\\\\rm{n}}^{2 + }} + {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\underline 2 {\\\\rm{CuS}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_4} + \\\\underline 4 \\\\,{\\\\rm{KI}} \\\\to {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{u}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{I}}_{\\\\rm{2}}} + {{\\\\rm{I}}_{\\\\rm{2}}} + {{\\\\rm{K}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{S}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{AgCl}} + \\\\underline 2 {\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}} \\\\to \\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{Ag}}{{\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_3}} \\\\right)}_2}} \\\\right]{\\\\rm{Cl}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathop {{\\rm{Ag}}}\\limits^{ + 1} \\mathop {{\\rm{Cl}}}\\limits^{ - 1}  + \\mathop {\\rm{N}}\\limits^{ - 3} \\mathop {{{\\rm{H}}_3}}\\limits^{ + 1}  \\to \\left[ {\\mathop {{\\rm{Ag}}\\,}\\limits^{ + 1} {{(\\mathop {\\rm{N}}\\limits^{ - 3} \\mathop {{{\\rm{H}}_3}}\\limits^{ + 1} )}_2}} \\right]\\mathop {{\\rm{Cl}}}\\limits^{ - 1} $<br>In this reaction, no atom is undergoing oxidation or reduction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17904,'chemistry',48,751,1612,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> At constant temperature and pressure, whatever heat is absorbed by the system is used in doing work.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Internal energy change is zero.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{q}} =  - {\\text{w (if }}\\Delta {\\text{U}} = 0).$ So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17907,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'In which of the following reactions, the underlined substance is oxidized?','[{"text": "$3{\\\\rm{Mg}} + {\\\\underline {\\\\rm{N}} _{\\\\underline {\\\\rm{2}} }} \\\\to {\\\\rm{M}}{{\\\\rm{g}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2{\\\\rm{KI}} + {\\\\underline {{\\\\rm{Br}}} _{\\\\underline {\\\\rm{2}} }} \\\\to 2{\\\\rm{KBr}} + {{\\\\rm{I}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\underline {{\\\\rm{Cu}}} {\\\\rm{O}} + {{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}} \\\\to {\\\\rm{Cu}} + {{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{O}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\underline {\\\\rm{C}} {\\\\rm{O}} + {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}} \\\\to {\\\\rm{COC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\stackrel{(+2)}{\\mathrm{CO}}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\rightarrow \\stackrel{(+4)}{\\mathrm{CO}} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$<br>+ 2 to + 4 change in oxidation state. So, carbon oxidised in the above reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17908,'chemistry',48,748,1606,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Enthalpy change is an extensive property.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enthalpy change is a state function.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ (enthalpy change) is considered as an extensive property since its value is contingent upon the quantity or amount of the substance.Enthalpy change depend on intial  & final states so it\'s a state function. Both statements are correct but there is no relation between them. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17911,'chemistry',50,837,1869,'In which of the following reactions, no change in valency occurs?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S} \\\\rightarrow 3 \\\\mathrm{~S}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{Na}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+2 \\\\mathrm{NaOH} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{NaClO}+\\\\mathrm{NaCl}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{KCl} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{AgCl}+\\\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathop {{\\rm{Ag}}}\\limits^{ + 1} \\mathop {\\rm{N}}\\limits^{ + 5} \\mathop {{{\\rm{O}}_3}}\\limits^{ - 2}  + \\mathop {\\rm{K}}\\limits^{ + 1} \\mathop {{\\rm{Cl}}}\\limits^{ - 1}  \\to \\mathop {{\\rm{Ag}}}\\limits^{ + 1} \\mathop {{\\rm{Cl}}}\\limits^{ - 1}  + \\mathop {\\rm{K}}\\limits^{ + 1} \\mathop {\\rm{N}}\\limits^{ + 5} \\mathop {{{\\rm{O}}_3}}\\limits^{ - 1} $<br>Valency of all atoms is same before and after the reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17913,'chemistry',48,752,1620,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}$, if $\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}$ (difference in number of moles of gaseous reactants and products) is zero.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}+\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}} \\mathrm{RT}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ represents the change in enthalpy,<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{U}$ represents the internal energy of a system and $\\Delta \\mathrm{n}$ represents the difference in the number of moles of gaseous reactants and products in a chemical reaction.<br>They are related as $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}+\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{(\\mathrm{g})} \\mathrm{RT}$<br>Thus when $\\Delta \\mathrm{n}=0, \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}$. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17915,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'In the reaction, $2 \\mathrm{Ag}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow$$\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{SO}_{2}, \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ acts as','[{"text": "Reducing agent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "oxidising agent", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "dehydrating agent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neither oxidizing nor reducing agent", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ is Oxidising $\\mathrm{Ag}(0)$ to $\\mathrm{Ag}(+1)$ and  itself undergoing reduction to $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$. So, oxidising agent.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17917,'chemistry',48,752,1621,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Molar heat capacities of solids are approximately equal to one another.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Molar heat capacity of a substance is the quantity of heat required to raise the temperature of unit mass of a substance by one degree celcius.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The quantity of heat needed to increase the temperature of one gram of a substance by one degree celsius is referred to as specific heat capacity. Molar heat capacity is for one mole of the substance and is the quantity of heat needed to raise the temperature of one mole by one degree celsius (or one kelvin). So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17919,'chemistry',50,838,1874,'Which of the acid possesses oxidizing, reducing and complex forming properties?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "HCl", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'An element in its intermediate oxidation state acts as reducing, oxidizing and complex forming agent.<br>Here, in $\\mathrm{HNO}_{2}$, \' N \' is in +3 state  (Intermediate oxidation state).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17921,'chemistry',48,758,1635,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Enthalpy of formation of graphite is zero.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enthalpy of formation of most stable allotrope is taken as zero.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The statement "Enthalpy of formation of graphite is zero" indicates that the formation of graphite from its elements involves no net change in enthalpy. This signifies an energetically neutral process due to the stability of graphite as the most stable allotrope of carbon. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17923,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'What is the oxidation number of As in the compound $\\mathrm{K}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{AsO}_{4} \\cdot 6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ ?','[{"text": "$ - 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{K} \\rightarrow+1, \\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\rightarrow+1, \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow-2$<br>so, $+1+2+\\mathrm{x}-8=0$<br>(or) $\\mathrm{x}=+5$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(17925,'chemistry',48,758,1636,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Heat of combustion is always negative.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Heat of combustion is used to calculate calorific value of fuels.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Heat of combustion is defined as the enthalpy change per mole (or per unit amount) of a substance when it undergoes combustion. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17927,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In which of the following compounds, iron has the lowest oxidation state?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{FeSO}_{4} \\\\cdot\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\\\cdot 6 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Fe}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}_{0.94} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}(\\\\mathrm{CO})_{5}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CO})_{5} \\rightarrow$ Oxidation no. of $\\mathrm{Fe}=0$ as CO is neutral<br>${\\mathop{{\\rm{Fe}}_{0.94}}\\limits^{\\rm{x}}\\mathop {\\rm{O}}\\limits^{ - 2}  \\to 0.94{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{x}} - 2 = 0}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\rm{x}} = 2/0.94 =  + \\,2.18{\\rm{ }}\\,{\\rm{or }}\\frac{{200}}{{94}}$<br>$\\mathop {{\\rm{K}} _4}\\limits^{ + 1}\\mathop {{\\rm{Fe}}}\\limits^{\\rm{x}} {({\\rm{C}}\\mathop {\\,{\\rm{N}}}\\limits^{ - 1} )_6} \\to 4 + {\\rm{x}} - 6 = 0$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\rm{x}} = 2$<br>$\\mathop {{\\rm{Fe}}}\\limits^{\\rm{x}} {\\rm{S}}\\mathop {{{\\rm{O}}_4}}\\limits^{ - 2} .{({\\rm{N}}{\\mathop {{\\rm{H}} _4}\\limits^{ + 1}})_2}{\\rm{S}}{\\mathop {{\\rm{O}} _4}\\limits^{ - 2}} \\cdot 6{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}} \\to $<br>${\\rm{x}} - 2 + (1 \\times 2) - 2 = 0$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\rm{x}} =  + \\,\\,2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17929,'chemistry',48,758,1644,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Gaseous molecules are far apart from each other due to less attractions.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enthalpy of atomisation is the heat of the reaction, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(\\mathrm{l})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(\\mathrm{g})}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The enthalpy of atomization is defined as the enthalpy change that occurs when one mole of a substance is broken up into its individual atoms in the gas phase. Consider the following example of atomization of dihydrogen<br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{H}_{(\\mathrm{g})} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=435.0 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} .$<br>$\\mathrm{H}$ atoms are formed by breaking $\\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{H}$ bonds in dihydrogen by absorbing $435.0 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17931,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The oxidation number of Pt in $\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{4}\\right) \\mathrm{Cl}_{3}\\right]^{-}$ is','[{"text": "+1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "+3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\left[ {\\mathop {{\\rm{Pt}}}\\limits^{\\rm{x}} \\left( {{{\\rm{C}}_2}{{\\mathop {\\rm{H}}\\limits^0 }_4}} \\right){\\rm{C}}{{\\mathop {\\rm{l}}\\limits^{ - 1} }_{\\rm{3}}}} \\right]^ - }$<br>${\\rm{x}} + 0 + ( - 1 \\times 3) =  - 1$<br>${\\rm{x}} =  + \\,2$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17934,'chemistry',48,752,1619,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For isothermal expansion of an ideal gas, its enthalpy decreases.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enthalpy depends on temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'During the isothermal expansion of an ideal gas, enthalpy remains constant. This is because enthalpy is a function of temperature.  So, option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17935,'chemistry',50,838,1874,'During the disproportionation of iodine to iodide and iodate ions, the ratio of iodate and iodide ions formed in alkaline medium is','[{"text": "$1: 5$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 3$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$3 \\mathrm{I}_{2}+\\underline{6} \\mathrm{OH}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{IO}_{3}^{-}+5 \\mathrm{I}^{-}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>So, ratio of iodate to iodide ion is $1: 5$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17938,'chemistry',48,751,1612,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If a refrigerator\'s door is kept open, room gets warmed.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Material kept inside the refrigerator remains cool.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In a refrigerator, heat removal from the interior is achieved by the appliance actively transferring heat to the outside. However, leaving the door open allows warm room air to enter inside preventing effective cooling and causing the refrigerator to act as a source of heat, raising both its internal temperature and the room temperature.So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17939,'chemistry',50,839,1878,'The values of ${\\rm{x}},{\\rm{y}}$ and ${\\rm{z}}$ in the reaction are respectively<br>$4 \\mathrm{KO}_{2}+\\mathrm{xH}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{yCO}_{2} \\rightarrow 4 \\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}+\\mathrm{zO}_{2}$','[{"text": "$3,6,6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2,4,3$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3,2,5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4,3,6$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$4 \\mathrm{KO}_{2}+\\mathrm{xH}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{yCO}_{2} \\rightarrow 4 \\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}+\\mathrm{zO}_{2}$<br>Equating H on both sides: $2 \\mathrm{x}=4 \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x}=2$<br>Equating C on both sides: $\\mathrm{y}=4$<br>Equating O on both sides: $8+\\mathrm{x}+2 \\mathrm{y}$<br>$ = (4 \\times 3) + 2{\\rm{z}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow 8+2+(2 \\times 4)=12+2 \\mathrm{z}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{z}=3$<br>$4 \\mathrm{KO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+4 \\mathrm{CO}_{2} \\rightarrow 4 \\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}+3 \\mathrm{O}_{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17942,'chemistry',48,758,1638,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Ammonium chloride dissolved in water results in cooling of solution.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Reaction is exothermic.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The reaction is endothermic.<br>$\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{NH}_{4 \\text { (aq) }}^{+}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{\\text {(aq) }}^{-} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=+15.1 \\mathrm{KJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$<br>So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17943,'chemistry',50,839,1878,'When the equation for the reaction of $\\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}(\\mathrm{aq})$ and $\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}(\\mathrm{aq})$ is balanced correctly,<br>$\\_{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{ + 2}}}}({\\rm{aq}}) + \\_{\\rm{MnO}}_4^ - ({\\rm{aq}}) + \\_{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}({\\rm{aq}}) \\to $<br>$\\_{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{4 + }}}}({\\rm{aq}}) + \\_{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}({\\rm{aq}}) + \\_{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}({\\rm{l}})$<br>what is the ratio of $\\mathrm{Sn}^{2+} / \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$5\\,\\,({\\text{S}}{{\\text{n}}^{ + 2}}\\xrightarrow{{ + 2}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{n}}^{ + 4}}$<br>$2\\,({\\text{MnO}}_4^ - \\xrightarrow{{ - 5}}{\\text{M}}{{\\text{n}}^{ + 2}}$<br>and the balanced equation is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& 5 \\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}+2 \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}+16 \\mathrm{H}^{+} \\rightarrow \\\\& 5 \\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}+2 \\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}+7 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\end{aligned}$<br>so, the ratio is $5 / 2$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17946,'chemistry',48,762,1658,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Heat absorbed in a reaction at constant temperature and constant volume is $-\\Delta \\mathrm{G}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}$ should be negative for the reaction to be spontaneous.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The heat absorbed in a reaction at constant temperature and constant volume (q) is equal to the change in internal energy  $\\left( {\\Delta {\\text{U}}} \\right)$. For a reaction to be spontaneous, $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}$ should be negative. So, option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17947,'chemistry',50,841,1880,'Number of moles of $\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ required to oxidize one mole of ferrous oxalate completely in acidic medium will be','[{"text": "7.5 moles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.2 moles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.6 moles", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.4 moles", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$5 \\mathrm{FeC}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}+3 \\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}+24 \\mathrm{H}^{+} \\rightarrow$$10 \\mathrm{CO}_{2}+5 \\mathrm{Fe}^{+3}+3 \\mathrm{Mn}^{+2}+3 \\mathrm{~K}^{+}+12 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>5 moles of $\\mathrm{Fe}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$ are oxidized by $=3$ moles of $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$<br>$\\Rightarrow 1$ mole of $\\mathrm{FeC}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is oxidized by $=\\dfrac{3}{5}=0.6$<br>moles of $\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17950,'chemistry',48,758,1641,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Heat of sublimation is the sum of heat of fusion and heat of vaporisation.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Heat of the reaction or enthalpy change is same whether reaction takes place in one step or in a number of steps.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Sublimation involves the transition from solid to vapor with enthalpy change of $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{\\text {Sub }}$. Similarly, the transition from solid to liquid has an enthalpy change of $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{\\text {Fus }}$ and from liquid to gas it is $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{\\text {vap }}$. Therefore, the overall enthalpy change $(\\Delta \\mathrm{H})$ can be calculated as the sum of these changes: $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{\\text {Sub }}=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{\\text {Fus }}+\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{\\text {Vap }}$. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17951,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Oxidation number of P in $\\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}, \\mathrm{S}$ in $\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$ and Cr in $\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ are, respectively','[{"text": "$+3,+6$ and +5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$+5,+3$, and +6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-3,+6$ and +6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$+5,+6$ and +6", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17951_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17954,'chemistry',48,758,1636,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For the combustion reactions, the value of $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ is always negative.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The combustion reactions are always endothermic.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Combustion reactions are invariably associated with the release of heat, and thus, for such reactions, the value of $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ is always negative. Combustion reactions are exothermic.<br>So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17955,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In acidic medium, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}$ changes $\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ to $\\mathrm{CrO}_{5}$ which has two $(-\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{O}-$ ) bonds.Oxidation state of Cr in $\\mathrm{CrO}_{5}$ is','[{"text": "+5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 10$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{CrO}_{5}$ has butterfly shape with two peroxy linkages.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17955_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>${\\rm{x}} + 4\\,( - 1) + 1\\,( - 2) = 0$<br>${\\rm{x}} =  + \\,6.$<br>$\\therefore$ Oxidation no. of $\\mathrm{Cr}=+6$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18002,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'In the reaction,$2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ oxygen is','[{"text": "oxidised only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "reduced only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neither oxidised nor reduced", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both oxidised and reduced", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-1} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}^{-2}+\\mathrm{O}_{2}^{0}$<br>Hence, oxygen is both oxidised and reduced.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17958,'chemistry',48,762,1658,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The thermodynamic function which determines the spontaneity of a process is the free energy. For a process to be spontaneous, the change in free energy must be negative.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The change in free energy is related to the change in enthalpy and change in entropy. The change in entropy for a process must always be positive if it is spontaneous.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The change in entropy can have either a positive or a negative value. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17959,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'(I) $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}+\\mathrm{O}_{3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+2 \\mathrm{O}_{2}$<br>(II) $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}+\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Ag}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{O}_{2}$<br>Role of hydrogen peroxide in the above reactions is, respectively','[{"text": "Oxidizing agent in (I) and reducing agent in (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reducing agent in (I) and oxidizing agent in (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reducing agent in (I) and (II)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Oxidizing agent in (I) and (II)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reduces $\\mathrm{O}_{3}$ to $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in (I) and $\\mathrm{Ag}^{+1}$ to Ag in (II)<br>So, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is acts as reducing agent in both reactions.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17962,'chemistry',48,758,1639,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Enthalpy changes are positive when $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\cdot \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}, \\mathrm{CuSO}_{4} \\cdot 5 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ and salt like $\\mathrm{NaCl}, \\mathrm{KCl}$ etc. which do not form hydrates, are dissolved in water. But enthalpy changes are negative when anhydrous salts capable of forming hydrates are dissolved in water.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The difference in the behaviour is due to large difference in the molecular weights of hydrated and anhydrous salts. The substance with larger molecular weights usually show positive enthalpy changes on dissolution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The exothermic or endothermic behaviour of dissolution depends only upon the nature of the salt and not on its molecular weight. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17963,'chemistry',50,837,1869,'Consider the salt $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{x}} \\mathrm{H}_{\\mathrm{y}}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)_{z} \\cdot 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$. The relationship between $\\mathrm{x}, \\mathrm{y}$ and z is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{x}} + {\\\\rm{y}} - {\\\\rm{z}} = 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{x}} + {\\\\rm{y}} = 2{\\\\rm{x}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{x}} + {\\\\rm{y}} + {\\\\rm{z}} = 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{K}^{+}, \\mathrm{H}^{+}, \\&\\,\\, \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{-2}$, So, compound is  $\\mathrm{KHC}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{x}} = 1,\\,{\\rm{y}} = 1,\\,\\,\\&\\,\\, {\\rm{ z}} = 1$<br>$\\mathrm{x}+\\mathrm{y}=2 \\mathrm{z}$ is correct relation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17966,'chemistry',48,761,1654,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Enthalpy of graphite is lower than that of diamond.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Entropy of graphite is greater than that of diamond.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}$ bond is more strong in graphite than in diamond, i.e. during combustion less heat is released from graphite and hence enthalpy of graphite is lower. Diamond is crystalline solid which is highly ordered structure while such arrangements are not found in the graphite. So, the degree of disorder is less in diamond. Hence, an entropy of the diamond is lesser than graphite. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17967,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Which of the following is correctly matched?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - P,\\tB - Q,\\tC - R,\\tD - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S,\\tB - R,\\tC - Q,\\tD - P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - S,\\tB - R,\\tC - P,\\t\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P,\\tB - Q,\\tC - S,\\t\\tD - R", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{AlF}_{3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{x}+3(-1)=0 \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x}=+3$<br>$\\mathrm{P}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{10} \\Rightarrow 4 \\mathrm{x}+10(-2)=0$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x}=+5$<br>$\\mathrm{HClO}_{4}:+1+\\mathrm{x}+4(-2)=0$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x}=+7$<br>$\\underline{\\mathrm{S}_{4}} \\mathrm{O}_{6}^{-2}: 4 \\mathrm{x}+6(-2)=-2$<br>$\\mathrm{x}=+2.5$',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/50_17967_question_1.png'),
(17970,'chemistry',48,761,1654,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Chlorine when solidified does not have zero entropy even at absolute zero.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Chlorine is a pungent smelling gas and it is difficult to solidify it.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For substance to have zero entropy zero, it must be a perfectly substance. Chlorine is a diatomic molecule and does not form a perfect crystal structure when solidified. This means that even when chlorine is solidified, it does not achieve the perfect order required for its entropy to be zero. Therefore, the assertion is correct. $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ has a pungent smell and is difficult to be solidified. But, it does not explain why its entropy does not reach zero when solidified. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17971,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Which one of the following statements is not correct?','[{"text": "Oxidation number of S in $\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{8}$ is +6 .", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxidation number of Os in $\\\\mathrm{OsO}_{4}$ is +8 .", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxidation number of S in $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{5}$ is +8 .", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Oxidation number of O in $\\\\mathrm{KO}_{2}$ is $-\\\\dfrac{1}{2}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17971_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{5}:+2+\\mathrm{x}+3(-2)+2(-1)=0$<br>$\\mathrm{x}=+6$<br>So, oxidation no. of S is +6 in $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{5}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17974,'chemistry',48,758,1635,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The enthalpy of formation of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(1)}$ is greater than that of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(\\mathrm{g})}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enthalpy change is negative for the condensation reaction, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(\\mathrm{g})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(1)}$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Difference between the heat of reaction of liquid water and steam is the heat of condensation, which is negative. Hence standard heat of formation is more negative for liquid water than steam. Some amount of energy is used in the vapourization. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17975,'chemistry',50,841,1880,'$\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$  is oxidized by $\\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$  in presence of acid :<br>${\\rm{aC}}{{\\rm{N}}^ - } + {\\rm{bNO}}_3^ -  + {\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{H}}^ + } \\to $<br>$({\\rm{a}} + {\\rm{b}}){\\rm{NO}} + {\\rm{aC}}{{\\rm{O}}_2} + \\frac{{\\rm{c}}}{2}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>What are the values of $\\mathrm{a}, \\mathrm{b}$, and c in that order?','[{"text": "3,7 , and 7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3,10 , and 7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3,10 , and 10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3,7 , and 10", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Oxidation half:<br>$3{\\left( {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{N}}^ - } + 3{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}} \\to {\\rm{NO}} + {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_2} + 6{{\\rm{H}}^ + } + 7{\\rm{e}}} \\right.^ - }$<br>Reduction half:<br>$7\\left( {{\\rm{NO}}_3^ -  + 4{{\\rm{H}}^ + } + 3{{\\rm{e}}^ - } \\to {\\rm{NO}} + 2{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}} \\right.$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 3{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{N}}^ - } + 7{\\rm{NO}}_3^ -  + 10{{\\rm{H}}^ + } \\to $<br>$10{\\rm{NO}} + 3{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}} + 5{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>so, $\\mathrm{a}=3, \\mathrm{~b}=7, \\mathrm{c}=10$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17978,'chemistry',48,758,1637,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Heat of neutralisation of perchloric acid, $\\mathrm{HClO}_{4}$ with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ is same as that of $\\mathrm{HCl}$ with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Both $\\mathrm{HCl}$ and $\\mathrm{HClO}_{4}$ are strong acids.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The heat of neutralization is the energy released or absorbed when an acid reacts with a base to form water and a salt. It depends on stoichiometry, and in reactions like that of $\\mathrm{HCl}$ with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$, the stoichiometry is the same, making their heat of neutralization equivalent. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17979,'chemistry',50,839,1878,'Consider the balanced chemical reaction:<br>$\\mathrm{aI}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}+\\mathrm{bBrF}_{3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{cIF}_{5}+\\mathrm{dO}_{2}+\\mathrm{eBr}_{2}$<br>Calculate the value of $(b+c+e) /(a)$.','[{"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\left.2 \\mathrm{I}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5} \\xrightarrow{+20} 5 \\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right) 6$<br>$\\left.2 \\mathrm{BrF}_{3} \\xrightarrow{-6} \\mathrm{Br}_{2}\\right) 20$<br>$12 \\mathrm{I}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}+40 \\mathrm{BrF}_{3} \\rightarrow 24 \\mathrm{IF}_{5}+30 \\,\\,\\mathrm{O}_{2}+20 \\mathrm{Br}_{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{40+24+20}{12}=7$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18021,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'What is the oxidation number of $\\mathrm{Cr}$ in $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the oxidation number of ${\\rm{Cr  in}}\\,\\,{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_7}$ be x.<br>${\\kern 1pt} \\therefore 2( + 1) + 2{\\rm{x}} + 7( - 2) = 0$<br>$2 + 2{\\rm{x}} - 14 = 0$<br>$2{\\rm{x}} = 12 \\Rightarrow {\\rm{x}} = 6$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17982,'chemistry',50,842,1881,'In the reaction: <br>$\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+4 \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+5 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow$ $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+8 \\mathrm{HCl}$ the equivalent weight of $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ will be: $(\\mathrm{M}=$ molecular weight of $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ )','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{M}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{M}{8}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{M}{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{M}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_2}{\\mathop {\\,{\\rm{S}}}\\limits^{ + 2} _2}{{\\rm{O}}_3} + 4{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_2} + 5{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}} \\to \\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_2}\\mathop {\\rm{S}}\\limits^{ + 6} {{\\rm{O}}_4} + {{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_4} + 8{\\rm{HCl}}\n\\end{array}$<br>Net change in O.N. of S is +2 to +6 i.e., 4<br>So, for two \' S \' atoms change is $8 . \\therefore \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{E}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{M}}{8}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17983,'chemistry',48,762,1658,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> An exothermic process, non-spontaneous at high temperature, may become spontaneous at low temperature.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> With decrease in temperature. randomness (entropy) increases.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'An exothermic reaction can shift from non-spontaneous to spontaneous at lower temperatures due to the reduction in the product of temperature and entropy change $(\\Delta \\mathrm{S})$, making the Gibbs free energy change $(\\Delta \\mathrm{G})$ negative and overcoming the enthalpy change $(\\Delta \\mathrm{H})$.Entropy decreases with decrease in temperature. Therefore, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17986,'chemistry',50,839,1878,'Boron carbide, $\\mathrm{B}_{4} \\mathrm{C}$, is made by the high temperature reaction of boron oxide with graphite, yielding carbon monoxide as a byproduct.<br>${ }_{-} \\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+{ }_{-} \\mathrm{C} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}_{4} \\mathrm{C}+$ _CO<br>What is the total of the smallest coefficients for the reactants and products in the balanced equation?','[{"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "15", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$2 \\mathrm{~B}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+7 \\mathrm{C} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}_{4} \\mathrm{C}+6 \\mathrm{CO}$<br>Total sum of coefficients $=2+7+1+6=16$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17987,'chemistry',48,759,1649,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The entropy of the universe is continuously increasing.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> All naturally occurring processes are accompanied by increase in entropy.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The second law of thermodynamics asserts that the entropy of the universe consistently increases, signifying a natural tendency for systems to evolve towards greater disorder and randomness over time. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17990,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'Given the reaction for the discharge of a cobalt cadmium battery<br>$2 \\mathrm{Co}(\\mathrm{OH})_{3}+\\mathrm{Cd}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Co}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}+\\mathrm{Cd}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$<br> Which species is oxidised during the discharge of the battery?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cd}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17990_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Here, oxidation number of $\\mathrm{Cd}$ increases from 0 to +2 , hence $\\mathrm{Cd}$ is oxidised.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18022,'chemistry',49,618,1244,'In which of the following reactions, the concentration of product is higher than the concentration of reactant at equilibrium? \\((\\mathrm{K}=\\) equilibrium constant)<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A} \\\\rightleftharpoons \\\\mathrm{B}, \\\\mathrm{K}=0.001$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{M} \\\\rightleftharpoons \\\\mathrm{N}, \\\\mathrm{K}=10$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{X} \\\\rightleftharpoons \\\\mathrm{Y}, \\\\mathrm{K}=0.005$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{R} \\\\rightleftharpoons \\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{K}=0.01$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Higher ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{C}}}$ value indicates more concentration of products. Higher ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{C}}}$ is satisfied by option (2).',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17991,'chemistry',48,748,1608,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> During an adiabatic process, heat energy is not exchanged between system and its surroundings.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The temperature of a gas increases when it undergoes an adiabatic expansion.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In adiabatic process, no heat enters or leaves the system. From first law of thermodynamics, change in internal energy $(\\Delta {\\text{U}}) = {\\text{q}} + {\\text{w}}$. Since $\\mathrm{q}=0$, work is done at the expense of internal energy. Therefore, internal energy of the system decreases and hence temperature of the gas falls. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17994,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'In this reaction, $\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Ag}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}$ acts as a/an.','[{"text": "reducing agent", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "oxidising agent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "bleaching agent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_17994_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br> The oxidation number of $\\mathrm{O}$ in $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}$ increases. Hence, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}$ acts as a reducing agent as it is undergoing oxidation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17995,'chemistry',48,748,1606,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Mass and volume are extensive properties.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Mass/volume is also an extensive property.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Extensive properties are contingent upon the quantity of the substance, such as mass and volume. In contrast, intensive properties, like temperature and density (mass / volume) remain constant and are not influenced by the quantity of the substance. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17998,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'Which change requires an oxidising agent?','[{"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}^{2-} \\\\rightleftharpoons \\\\mathrm{S}_{4} \\\\mathrm{O}_{6}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \\\\rightleftharpoons \\\\mathrm{Zn}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{ClO}^{-} \\\\rightleftharpoons \\\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{3} \\\\rightleftharpoons \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The reaction which involves oxidation, requires an oxidising agent.<br>$\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}^{2-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{S}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{6}^{2-}$<br>Oxidation state of $\\mathrm{S}$ changes from 2 to 2.5 .Thus, $\\quad \\mathrm{S}$ is undergoing oxidation and hence, requires an oxidising agent.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(17999,'chemistry',48,750,1611,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Absolute values of internal energy of substances cannot be determined.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It is impossible to determine exact values of constituent energies of the substances.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Internal energy $({\\text{U}})$ is the sum of various energy components, including translational, rotational, vibrational, bonding, electronic, potential, and nuclear energy. It is represented as $\\mathrm{U}=\\mathrm{Et}+\\mathrm{Er}+\\mathrm{Ev}+\\mathrm{Eb}+\\mathrm{Ee}+\\mathrm{Ep}+\\mathrm{En}$, with the difference between initial and final states denoted as $\\Delta \\mathrm{U}$ for accurate measurement due to challenges in isolating individual energy components. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18003,'chemistry',48,750,1611,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The increase in internal energy $(\\Delta \\mathrm{U})$ for the vaporization of one mole of water at $1 \\mathrm{~atm}$ and $373 \\mathrm{~K}$ is zero.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> For all isothermal processes, $\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=0$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At $373 \\mathrm{~K}$ ( or $100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ ), water begins to boil, and at this temperature, the vaporization of water is considered as an isothermal process. The change in internal energy $(\\Delta \\mathrm{U})$ depends solely on temperature. Under constant temperature conditions, the internal energy of the gas remains constant, meaning that $\\Delta \\mathrm{U}$ is zero. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18005,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'In which of the following reactions, the underlined substance has been oxidised?','[{"text": "$\\\\underline{\\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\underline{\\\\mathrm{HBr}}+\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{HgCl}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{SnCl}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\underline{\\\\mathrm{Hg}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}+\\\\mathrm{SnCl}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+\\\\underline{2 \\\\mathrm{KI}} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{KCl}+\\\\underline{\\\\mathrm{I}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\underline{2 \\\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}}+4 \\\\mathrm{I}^{-} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{Cu}_{2} \\\\mathrm{I}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{I}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Oxidation is the addition of electronegative element or removal of electropositive element or increase in oxidation number.<br>$\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{KI} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{KCl}+\\mathrm{I}_{2}$<br>In this reaction, the underlined species is oxidised as it involves the removal of electropositive element, i.e. $\\mathrm{K}^{+}$.<br>[Note: However, in the option (4), on the basis of applied concept, $\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ seems to undergo oxidation but in actual, is undergoing reduction and reduces to $\\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$.<br>Thus, in the reaction,]<br> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18005_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18007,'chemistry',48,761,1654,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Molar entropy of vaporisation of water is different from ethanol.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Water is more polar than ethanol.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The molar entropy of vaporization varies among substances due to differences in molecular structure and intermolecular forces. Water, with strong hydrogen bonding, exhibits a relatively high molar entropy during vaporization compared to ethanol, which, with weaker hydrogen bonding, generally has a lower molar entropy.So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18008,'chemistry',49,623,1263,'Amongst the given options which of the following molecules/ions atcs as a Lewis acid?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BF}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Electron pair acceptor is Lewis acid. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/49_18008_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  can not act as Lewis acids because<br>donate a pair of $\\mathrm{e}^{-}$.(Lewis base)<br>$\\mathrm{BF}_{3} \\rightarrow$ Central atom Boron has incomplete<br>octect. Thus, accepts a pair of $\\mathrm{e}^{-}$.(Lewis acid).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18009,'chemistry',50,844,1885,'Strongest reducing agent is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mg}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Al", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ba", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Since, ${\\rm{K}}$ contains only one electron in its outermost shell, it has higher tendency to donate it, i.e. has higher tendency to get oxidised. Therefore, it is the strongest reducing agent among the given elements.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18011,'chemistry',49,619,1247,'The equilibrium concentrations of the species in the reaction $\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{C}+\\mathrm{D}$ are $2,3,10$ and $6 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}$, respectively at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$. $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}$ for the reaction is $(\\mathrm{R}=2 \\mathrm{cal} / \\mathrm{mol} \\mathrm{K})$','[{"text": "$ - 137.26\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{cal}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 1381.80\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{cal}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 13.73\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{cal}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1372.60\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{cal}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{C}+\\mathrm{D}$<br>Given<br>$[\\mathrm{A}]=2 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}$<br>$[\\mathrm{B}]=3 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}$<br>$[\\mathrm{C}]=10 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}$<br>$[\\mathrm{D}]=6 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}, \\mathrm{~K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{C}][\\mathrm{D}]}{[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}]}=\\dfrac{10 \\times 6}{2 \\times 3}=10$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}=-2.303 \\mathrm{RT} \\log \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{eq}}$<br>$=-2.303 \\times 2 \\times 300 \\times \\log 10$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}=-1381.80 \\mathrm{cal}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18012,'chemistry',48,762,1658,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Salt such as $\\mathrm{NaCl}$ dissolves, the $\\mathrm{Na}^{+}$and $\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ions leaving the crystal lattice acquire far greater freedom.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In thermodyanamic terms, the formation of solution occurs with a favourable change in free energy, i.e., $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ has high positive value and ${\\text{T}}\\Delta {\\text{S}}$ has a low negative value.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In thermodynamic terms, the formation of a solution is associated with a favorable change in free energy, $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S}$, where $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ has a small positive value and $\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S}$ has a large positive value. As a result, $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}$ becomes negative. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18013,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'Which of the following substances acts as an oxidising as well as reducing agent?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NaNO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18013_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\mathrm{NaNO}_{2}$ (sodium nitrite) acts both as an oxidising as well as reducing agent ${\\rm{NaN}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$. as an oxidising agent.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18013_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18015,'chemistry',49,617,1236,'At $500 \\mathrm{~K}$, for a reversible reaction<br>$\\mathrm{A}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+\\mathrm{B}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{AB}_{(\\mathrm{g})}$ in a closed container,<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=2 \\times 10^{-5}$. In the presence of catalyst, the equilibrium is attaining 10 times faster. The equilibrium constant ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{C}}}$ in the presence of catalyst at the same temperature is','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{-10}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{-6}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Catalyst does not affect the ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{C}}}$. Only Temp. can alter the value of $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}$ of a given reaction. Hence, the equilibrium constant value in the presence of catalyst is $2 \\times 10^{-5}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18016,'chemistry',48,762,1658,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Many endothermic reactions that are not spontaneous at room temperature become spontaneous at high temperature<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Entropy of the system decreases with inerease in temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Assertion is true but reason is false. The factor ${\\text{T}}\\Delta {\\text{S}}$ increase with increase in temperature. When $\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S}$ is more than $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$, $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}$ will become -ve and process become spontaneous and entropy is directly propotional to temperature. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18017,'chemistry',50,837,1864,'Which is the best description of behaviour of bromine in the reaction given below?$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{Br}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{HBr}+\\mathrm{HOBr}$','[{"text": "Proton accepted only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both oxidised and reduced", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Oxidised only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reduced only", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18017_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Thus, bromine is oxidised as well as reduced in this reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18019,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'A weak acid with ${\\text{p}}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}}\\,\\,5.9$ and weak base with  ${\\text{p}}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{b}}}\\,\\,5.8$ are mixed in equal proportions. $\\mathrm{pH}$ of the resulting solution is','[{"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7.05", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7.005", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7.5", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For salts of weak acid and weak base,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\quad \\mathrm{pH}=7+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\mathrm{pk}_{\\mathrm{a}}-\\mathrm{pk}_{\\mathrm{b}}\\right) \\\\& =7+\\dfrac{1}{2}(5.9-5.8) \\\\& =7.05\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18020,'chemistry',48,759,1649,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> There is a natural asymmetry between converting work to heat and converting heat to work.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> No process is possible in which the sole result is the absorption of heat from a reservoir and its complete conversion into work.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion. Second law of thermodynamics states that total heat can never be converted into equivalent amount of work. Thus, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18024,'chemistry',48,758,1637,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Heat of neutralisation of nitric acid with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ is same as that of $\\mathrm{HCl}$ and $\\mathrm{NaOH}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In both cases strong acid and strong bases are neutralised. $\\mathrm{H}^{+}+\\mathrm{OH}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The heat of neutralization remains constant for both nitric acid with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ and $\\mathrm{HCl}$ with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$, as they have identical stoichiometry in neutralization reactions, involving the donation of one mole of proton to $\\mathrm{NaOH}$, ensures the same heat release despite the specific identities of the acids and bases.So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18025,'chemistry',50,838,1875,'In the complex, $\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right]^{+}$, the oxidation number of $\\mathrm{Cr}$ is','[{"text": "+4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "+2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the oxidation number of $\\mathrm{Cr}$ is $\\mathrm{x}$ in the complex ${\\left[ {{\\rm{Cr}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right]^{\\rm{ + }}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{x}} + 4 \\times (0) + 2 \\times ( - 1) =  + 1$<br>${\\rm{x}} - 2 =  + 1$<br>${\\rm{x}} =  + 3$<br>Thus, oxidation number of $\\mathrm{Cr}$ is +3 .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18026,'chemistry',49,617,1239,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For the reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{N}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g})$<br>Unit of $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\mathrm{L}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-2}$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> For the reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{N}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g})$<br>Equilibrium constant, $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right]^{2}}{\\left[\\mathrm{~N}_{2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]^{3}}$','[{"text": "If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is  not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "If Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion andt reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{N}_{2}+3 \\underset{(\\mathrm{g})}{3 \\mathrm{H}_{2}} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right]^{2}}{\\left[\\mathrm{~N}_{2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{3}\\right]^{3}}$<br>So, unit of $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{mol}}{\\mathrm{L}}\\right)^{2}}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{mol}}{\\mathrm{L}}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{mol}}{\\mathrm{L}}\\right)^{3}}=\\mathrm{L}^{2} \\cdot \\mathrm{mol}^{-2}$<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18028,'chemistry',48,760,1651,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A reaction which is spontaneous and accompanied by decrease of randomness must be exothermic.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> All exothermic reactions are accompanied by decrease in randomness.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Exothermic reactions may be accompanied by increase or decrease in randomness. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18029,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'What is the oxidation number of vanadium in $\\mathrm{Rb}_{4} \\mathrm{Na}\\left[\\mathrm{HV}_{10} \\mathrm{O}_{28}\\right]$ ?','[{"text": "+8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "+3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+1", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Algebraic sum of oxidation number of all the atoms in a neutral molecule is zero.Let the oxidation number of vanadium be x , then $4 \\times 1 + 1 + 1 + 10{\\rm{x}} + ( - 2) \\times 28 = 0$<br>$10{\\rm{x}} = 56 - 6$<br>${\\rm{x}} =  + 5$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18030,'chemistry',49,617,1241,'Two moles of each reactant A and B are taken in a reaction flask. They react in the following manner,<br>${{\\text{A}}_{({\\text{g}})}} + {{\\text{B}}_{({\\text{g}})}} \\rightleftharpoons {{\\text{C}}_{({\\text{g}})}} + {{\\text{D}}_{({\\text{g}})}}$<br>At equilibrium, it was found that the concentration of $\\mathrm{C}$ is triple to that of $\\mathrm{B}$. The equilibrium constant for the reaction is','[{"text": "4.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{6}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\text{A}}({\\text{g}}) + {\\text{B}}({\\text{g}}) \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{C}}({\\text{g}}) + {\\text{D}}({\\text{g}})$<br>Initial moles $2 \\quad 2 \\quad 0 \\quad 0$<br>At equm. $\\quad(2-\\mathrm{x})(2-\\mathrm{x}) \\quad \\mathrm{x} \\quad \\mathrm{x}$<br>At equilibrium, $3[\\mathrm{~B}]=[\\mathrm{C}]$ (given)<br>$3(2-x)=x \\Rightarrow x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{C}][\\mathrm{D}]}{[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}]}$<br>Here no.of moles of gaseous reactants and products are same. So, no need of considering volume.<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^{2}}{\\left(2-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^{2}}=\\dfrac{(1.5)^{2}}{(0.5)^{2}}=9 \\text {. }$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18041,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Oxidation number of $\\mathrm{N}$ in $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ is','[{"text": "-3.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+3.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-3,+5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+5", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let the oxidation state of  N  in<br>${\\rm{HN}}{{\\rm{O}}_3} = {\\rm{x}}$<br>$ + 1 + {\\rm{x}} + ( - 2 \\times 3) = 0$<br>${\\rm{ or }}\\mathop {\\rm{x}}\\limits_{( + 2)}  + 1 - 6 = 0 \\Rightarrow {\\rm{x}} =  + 5$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18032,'chemistry',48,748,1603,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For an isolated system, ${\\text{q}}$ is zero.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In an isolated system, change in ${\\text{U}}$ and $\\mathrm{V}$ is zero.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In an isolated system, where there is no heat exchange with the surroundings ${\\text{(q  =  0)}}$, the work done $(\\mathrm{w})$ is also zero. Therefore, the change in internal energy ${\\text{(}}\\Delta {\\text{U)}}$ is equal to zero. Consequently, for such a system, the change in pressure $(\\mathrm{P})$ and volume $(\\mathrm{V})$ is zero, as work (w) does not contribute to any changes in the system. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18033,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The oxidation number of $\\mathrm{P}$ in $\\mathrm{Mg}_{2} \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ is','[{"text": "+2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "-3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The oxidation number of $\\mathrm{P}$ in $\\mathrm{Mg}_{2} \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ can be calculated as follows: Suppose the oxidation number of $\\mathrm{P}$ be $\\mathrm{x}$<br>then, $2(+2)+2 \\mathrm{x}+(-14)=0$<br>$4 + 2{\\rm{x}} - 14 = 0$<br>$2{\\rm{x}} = 10$<br>${\\rm{x}} =  + 5$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18034,'chemistry',49,617,1241,'For the reaction $\\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\mathrm{CO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{CO}(\\mathrm{g})+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{g})$. If the initial concentration of $\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]=\\left[\\mathrm{CO}_{2}\\right]$ and $\\mathrm{x} \\mathrm{mol} / \\mathrm{L}$ of hydrogen is consumed at equilibrium, the correct expression of $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{x^{2}}{(1-x)^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{x^{2}}{(2+x)^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{x^{2}}{1-x^{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(1+x)^{2}}{(1-x)^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For the reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\mathrm{CO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{CO}(\\mathrm{g})+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{g})$<br>$\\begin{array}{lllll}\\text { Initial conc. } & 1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{array}$<br>At equm. $1-\\mathrm{x} \\quad 1-\\mathrm{x} \\quad \\mathrm{x} \\quad \\mathrm{x}$<br>Thus, $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{x}^{2}}{(1-\\mathrm{x})(1-\\mathrm{x})}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{x}^{2}}{(1-\\mathrm{x})^{2}}$<br>Here, $\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{(\\mathrm{g})}=2-2=0$<br>So, $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{P}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}$<br>Hence ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{P}}}{\\text{ =  }}\\frac{{{{\\text{x}}^{\\text{2}}}}}{{{{{\\text{(1 - x)}}}^{\\text{2}}}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18036,'chemistry',48,760,1651,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Entropy of system increases for spontaneous reaction at all temperatures.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enthalpy of reaction always decreases for spontaneous reaction at all temperatures.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The change in entropy ${\\text{(}}\\Delta {\\text{S)}}$ will be positive and the change in enthalpy $(\\Delta \\mathrm{H})$ will be negative for a spontaneous reaction at all temperatures. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18037,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Highest oxidation state of $\\mathrm{Mn}$ is present in','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let the oxidation state of $\\mathrm{Mn}$ be $\\mathrm{x}$.<br>(1)${\\rm{KMn}}{{\\rm{O}}_4},$<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{ + 1 + {\\rm{x}} + ( - 2) \\times 4 = 0}\\\\\n{{\\rm{x}} - 7 = 0}\\\\\n{{\\rm{x}} =  + 7}\\\\\n{(2){{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{Mn}}{{\\rm{O}}_4},}\\\\\n{( + 1) \\times 2 + {\\rm{x}} + ( - 2) \\times 4 = 0}\\\\\n{{\\rm{x}} - 6 = 0}\\\\\n{{\\rm{x}} =  + 6}\\\\\n{(3){\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}},}\\\\\n{{\\rm{x}} \\times 2 + ( - 2) \\times 3 = 0}\\\\\n{2{\\rm{x}} =  + 6}\\\\\n{{\\rm{x}} =  + 3}\\\\\n{(4){\\rm{Mn}}{{\\rm{O}}_2},}\\\\\n{{\\rm{x}} + ( - 2) \\times 2 = 0}\\\\\n{{\\rm{x}} =  + 4}\n\\end{array}$<br>Thus,oxidation state of Mn is highest in ${\\rm{KMn}}{{\\rm{O}}_4}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18038,'chemistry',49,617,1241,'At equilibrium, the concentration of<br>${{\\text{N}}_2} = 3.0 \\times {10^{ - 3}}{\\text{M}},{\\text{  &   }}{{\\text{O}}_2} = 4.2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}{\\text{M}}$<br>and $\\mathrm{NO}=2.8 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}$<br>in a sealed vessel at $800 \\mathrm{~K}$ and $1 \\mathrm{~atm}$ pressure. What will be $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}$ for the given reaction?  ${{\\text{N}}_2}_{(\\,{\\text{g}})} + {{\\text{O}}_2}_{(\\,{\\text{g}})} \\rightleftharpoons 2{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{({\\text{g}})}}$','[{"text": "$0.328 \\\\mathrm{~atm}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.622 \\\\mathrm{~atm}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.483 \\\\mathrm{~atm}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.712 \\\\mathrm{~atm}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given, $\\mathrm{N}_{2}=3.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}$<br>$\\mathrm{O}_{2}=4.2 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}$<br>$\\mathrm{NO}=2.8 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}$<br>For the given reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{N}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NO}(\\mathrm{g})$<br>equilibrium constant $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}$ can be written as<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{NO}]^{2}}{\\left[\\mathrm{~N}_{2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right]}$<br>$=\\dfrac{\\left(2.8 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}\\right)^{2}}{\\left(3.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}\\right)\\left(4.2 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}\\right)}=0.622$<br>and $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{P}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}} \\cdot(\\mathrm{RT})^{\\Delta \\mathrm{n}}$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=$ Number of moles of gaseous products<br>- number of moles of gaseous reactants.<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{n}=2-2=0$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{P}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}} \\cdot(\\mathrm{RT})^{\\mathrm{o}}$<br>or $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{P}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}=0.622 \\mathrm{~atm}$<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18042,'chemistry',49,621,1255,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For the reaction,<br>$2 \\mathrm{NO}(\\mathrm{g})+\\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$<br>increase in pressure favours the formation of $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The reaction is exothermic.','[{"text": "If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is  not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "If Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion andt reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\underset{(\\mathrm{g})}{2 \\mathrm{NO}}+\\underset{(\\mathrm{g})}{\\mathrm{O}_{2}} \\rightleftharpoons \\underset{(\\mathrm{g})}{2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}}$<br>High pressure favours less volumes side, here products no.of moles are less, so increase in pressure favours forward reaction. The reaction given above is exothermic as it is combustion reaction.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18044,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Which of the following have been arranged in the decreasing order of oxidation number of sulphur?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{4} \\\\mathrm{O}_{6}>\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}>\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{S}_{8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}>\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}>\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}>\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}^{2-}>\\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}>\\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{2-}>\\\\mathrm{HSO}_{4}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{5}>\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{SCl}_{2}>\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n(1){\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\mathop {{{\\rm{S}}_4}}\\limits^{{\\rm{2}}.{\\rm{5}}} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{6}}};{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\mathop {{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits^{ + 6} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{7}}};{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\mathop {{{\\rm{S}}_2}}\\limits^{ + 2} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}};{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{8}}^{\\rm{0}}\\\\\n(2){{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\mathop {{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_4}}\\limits^{ + 6} ;\\mathop {\\,{\\rm{S}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 4}}} {{\\rm{O}}_2};{{\\rm{H}}_2}\\mathop {\\,{\\rm{S}}}\\limits^{ - 2}  > {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\mathop {\\,{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 6}}} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{8}}}\\\\\n(3)\\mathop {{\\rm{SO}}_2^2}\\limits^{ + 2} ;{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\mathop {{\\rm{SO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 6}}} ;\\mathop {{\\rm{SO}}_3^{2 - }}\\limits^{ + 4} {\\mkern 1mu} ;{\\rm{HS}}\\mathop {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}^ - }\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 6}}} \\\\\n(4){{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\mathop {\\rm{S}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 6}}} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{5}}};{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\mathop {{\\rm{SO}}}\\limits^{ + 4} _{\\rm{3}}};{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\mathop {\\rm{S}}\\limits^{ + 2} {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}};{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\mathop {\\rm{S}}\\limits^{ - {\\rm{2}}} \n\\end{array}$<br>Thus, in option (4), compounds are arranged in the decreasing order of oxidation number of sulphur.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18045,'chemistry',48,763,1661,'If values of  $\\Delta {\\text{H}}^\\circ {\\text{ &   }}\\Delta {\\text{S}}^\\circ $   for a process/reaction are $77.2 \\mathrm{~kJ}, 48 \\mathrm{~J} / \\mathrm{k}$ respectively. Then find of $\\log \\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{k}}$. Given : Temp is $300 \\mathrm{~K}$','[{"text": "11", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "15", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "100", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}^{\\circ}-\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S}^{\\circ}$<br>$ = 77.2 - \\left( {300 \\times 48 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right)$<br>$=77.2-14.4$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}=62.8 \\mathrm{~kJ}$<br>$\\Delta {\\text{G}}^\\circ  =  - 2.303{\\text{RT  }}\\log {\\text{k}}$<br>$=77.2-\\left(300 \\times 48 \\times 10^{-3}\\right)$<br>$=77.2-14.4$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& -\\log \\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{62.8}{2.303 \\times 8.314 \\times 300 \\times 10^{-3}} \\\\& =0.0109 \\times 10^{3} \\\\& =10.9 \\simeq 11 \\\\& \\log \\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{k}}=11\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18046,'chemistry',49,617,1240,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}$ can be equal to or less than or even greater than the value of $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{P}}}{\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{C}}}{{\\rm{(RT)}}^{\\Delta {\\rm{n}}}}$<br>Relation between $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{P}}$ and $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}$ depends on the change in the number of moles of gaseous reactants and products.','[{"text": "If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is  not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "If Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion andt reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{P}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}(\\mathrm{RT})^{\\Delta \\mathrm{n}}$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{n}=$ number of moles of gaseous products - number of moles of gaseous reactants.<br>When, $\\Delta \\mathrm{n}>1, \\mathrm{~K}_{\\mathrm{P}}>\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}$<br>When, $\\Delta \\mathrm{n} < 1, \\mathrm{~K}_{\\mathrm{P}} < \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}$<br>When, $\\Delta \\mathrm{n}=0, \\mathrm{~K}_{\\mathrm{P}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}$<br>',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18048,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In which of the following compounds, iron has an oxidation number of +3 ?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\\\right)_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{FeC}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\\\cdot \\\\mathrm{FeSO}_{4} \\\\cdot \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$has a -1 charge.<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{\\rm{In}}\\,{\\rm{Fe}}{\\left( {{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{2}}}$, Fe has oxidation number +2 .<br>$\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}$ has -2 charge.<br> $\\therefore $ In $\\mathrm{FeC}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$, has oxidation number +2 .<br>In, $\\left[ {{\\rm{Fe}}{{\\left( {{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{6}}}} \\right]{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}},{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{the}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{cation}}\\left[ {{\\rm{Fe}}{{\\left( {{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{6}}}} \\right]{{\\mkern 1mu} ^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}$ is present and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ is neutral.<br>$\\therefore $Fe has oxidation number +3 .<br>In Mohr\'s salt\' $\\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}$ has $\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$ ion which has -2 charge.<br>Therefore, Fe has +2 oxidation number.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18050,'chemistry',49,623,1262,'The pair of amphoteric hydroxides is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Al}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{3}, \\\\mathrm{LiOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Be}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Mg}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{B}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{3}, \\\\mathrm{Be}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Be}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Zn}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{Be}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{Zn}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ are amphoteric hydroxides in nature.<br>$\\mathrm{Be}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}+2 \\mathrm{HCl} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BeCl}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>$\\mathrm{Be}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}+2 \\mathrm{NaOH} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{BeO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>$\\mathrm{Zn}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}+2 \\mathrm{HCl} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>$\\mathrm{Zn}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}+2 \\mathrm{NaOH} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{ZnO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18052,'chemistry',50,837,1869,'In which of the following reactions, there is no change in valency?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}+2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}+3 \\\\mathrm{~S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{Na}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 \\\\mathrm{KClO}_{3} \\\\rightarrow 3 \\\\mathrm{KClO}_{4}+\\\\mathrm{KCl}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$(1)\\mathop {{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits^{ + 4}  + 2{{\\rm{H}}_2}\\mathop {\\,{\\rm{S}}}\\limits^{ - 2}  \\to 2{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}} + 3\\mathop {\\,{\\rm{S}}}\\limits^0 $<br>$(2){\\rm{2}}\\mathop {{\\rm{Na}}}\\limits^{\\rm{0}} {\\rm{ + }}\\mathop {{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits^{\\rm{0}}  \\to {\\rm{2}}{\\mathop {{\\rm{Na}}}\\limits^{ + 1} _2}\\mathop {{{\\rm{O}}_2}}\\limits^{ - 1} $<br>$(3){\\rm{2}}\\mathop {{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 1}}} \\mathop {{{\\rm{O}}_2}}\\limits^{ - 1}  + {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}} \\to $<br>${\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_2}}\\limits^{ + 1} {\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}} + {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\mathop {{{\\rm{O}}_2}}\\limits^{ - 1} {\\mkern 1mu} $<br>${\\mkern 1mu} ({\\rm{no}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{change}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{in}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{valency}})$<br>${\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} (4){\\rm{4K}}\\mathop {{\\rm{Cl}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 5}}} {{\\rm{O}}_3} \\to {\\rm{3}}\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{KCl}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 7}}} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{ + K}}\\mathop {{\\rm{Cl}}}\\limits^{ - 1} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18054,'chemistry',49,623,1264,'The correct order of basic strength is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} < \\\\mathrm{OH}^{-} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{O}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH} < \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{O}^{-} < \\\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH} < \\\\mathrm{OH}^{-} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{O}^{-}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{OH}^{-} < \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{O}^{-} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Strong acid has a weak conjugate base and vice - versa.<br>$\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{ Increasing order of basic strength }}}]{{{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O  <  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{OH  <  O}}{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ - }}}{\\text{ <  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{{\\text{O}}^{\\text{ - }}}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18056,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'+3 oxidation state is most common in','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ni}(28)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}$ (26)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Zn}(30)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu}(29)$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'(1) $\\mathrm{Ni}($ III $)=3 \\mathrm{~d}^{7} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{0}=$<br>  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18056_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>(2) $\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{III})=3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{0}=$<br> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18056_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br> +3 oxidation state of Fe provides the extra stability due to half-filled d-orbitals.<br>(3) $\\mathrm{Zn}($ III $)=3 \\mathrm{~d}^{9} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{0}=$<br> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18056_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br> (4) $\\mathrm{Cu}($ III $)=3 \\mathrm{~d}^{8} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{0}=$<br> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18056_explanation_4.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18058,'chemistry',49,623,1263,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\left[\\mathrm{Al}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$ is a stronger acid than $\\left[\\mathrm{Mg}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Size of $\\left[\\mathrm{Al}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$ is smaller than $\\left[\\mathrm{Mg}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}$ and possesses more effective nuclear charge.','[{"text": "If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "If both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is  not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "If Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion andt reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The size of $\\left[\\mathrm{Al}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$ is smaller than that of $\\left[\\mathrm{Mg}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}$. Also the former possesses more effective nuclear charge and thus, attracts electron pair from donor more effectively.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18060,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The oxidation state of chlorine in $\\mathrm{KClO}_{4}$ is','[{"text": "-1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+7", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "-7", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{KClO}_{4}$ Let the oxidation state of $\\mathrm{Cl}$ be $x$.<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n1 + {\\rm{x}} + 4( - 2) = 0\\\\\n1 + {\\rm{x}} - 8 = 0\\\\\n{\\rm{x}} - 7 = 0\\\\\n{\\rm{x}} =  + 7\n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18062,'chemistry',49,623,1262,'Ammonia gas dissolves in water to form $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{OH}$. In this reaction, water acts as','[{"text": "Conjugate base", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A non - polar molecule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A base<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{NH}_{3} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}+\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$<br>In this reaction, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ acts as an acid, as it donates a proton.<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2012,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18064,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Oxidation number of \' $\\mathrm{N}$ \' in $\\mathrm{N}_{3} \\mathrm{H}$ (hydrazoic acid) is','[{"text": "$-\\\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "+3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "-3", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{N}_{3} \\mathrm{H}$ (hydrozoic acid)<br> Let the oxidation state of $\\mathrm{N}$ be $\\mathrm{x}$.<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n + 3({\\rm{x}}) + 1 = 0\\\\\n3{\\rm{x}} + 1 = 0\\,\\\\\n \\Rightarrow \\,{\\rm{x}} =  - \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18066,'chemistry',49,632,1292,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> ${\\text{NaCl}}$  is precipitated when $\\mathrm{HCl}$ gas is passed in a saturated solution of $\\mathrm{NaCl}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{HCl}$ is a strong acid.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Pure ${\\text{NaCl}}$ is precipitated when $\\mathrm{HCl}$ gas is passed in a saturated solution of $\\mathrm{NaCl}$.<br>This is due to the common ion effect. The concentration of $\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$increases in the solution, due to which the reaction of dissociation of $\\mathrm{NaCl}$ moves in the backward direction leading to precipitation of $\\mathrm{NaCl}$.<br>$\\mathrm{HCl}$ is strong acid in aq.solution but the strength of it has no relation with assertion.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18068,'chemistry',50,839,1878,'The coefficients of $\\mathrm{I}^{-}, \\mathrm{IO}_{3}^{-}$and $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$in the redox reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{I}^{-}+\\mathrm{IO}_{3}^{-}+\\mathrm{H}^{+} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{I}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>in the balanced form respectively are','[{"text": "$5,1,6$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1,5,6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6,1,5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5,6,1$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${{\\rm{I}}^ - }{\\rm{ + }}\\left( {\\mathop {\\rm{I}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 5}}} {\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}^ - } \\right){\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}} \\to \\mathop {{{\\rm{I}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits^{\\rm{0}} {\\rm{ + }}\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br> Oxidation half reaction:<br>$2{{\\rm{I}}^ - } \\to {{\\rm{I}}_2} + 2{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Reduction half reaction<br>$10{{\\rm{e}}^ - } + 2(\\mathop {\\rm{I}}\\limits^{ + 5} {\\rm{O}}_3^ - ) \\to \\mathop {{{\\rm{I}}_2}}\\limits^0 {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} (2)$<br>In order to cancel the electrons, multiplying Eq. (1) by 5 and then adding to Eq. (2), we get<br>$10 \\mathrm{I}^{-}+2 \\mathrm{IO}_{3}^{-} \\rightarrow 6 \\mathrm{I}_{2}$<br>To balance $\\mathrm{O}$-atom, add $6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ molecules on RHS and $12 \\mathrm{H}^{+}$on LHS, then<br>$10 \\mathrm{I}^{-}+2 \\mathrm{IO}_{3}^{-}+12 \\mathrm{H}^{+} \\rightarrow 6 \\mathrm{I}_{2}+6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>or $5 \\mathrm{I}^{-}+\\mathrm{IO}_{3}^{-}+6 \\mathrm{H}^{+} \\rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{I}_{2}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18070,'chemistry',49,632,1293,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> On mixing $500 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of ${10^{ - 6}}{\\text{M  C}}{{\\text{a}}^{2 + }}$ ion and $500 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of ${10^{ - 6}}{\\text{ M }}{{\\text{F}}^ - }$ ion, a precipitate of $\\mathrm{CaF}_{2}$ will be obtained. $\\left(\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\right.$ of $\\mathrm{CaF}_{2}=10^{-18}$ ).<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> If $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}$ is greater than ionic product, a precipitate will develop.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{CaF}_{2} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}+2 \\mathrm{~F}^{-}$<br>$\\mathrm{K}=\\left(10^{-6}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{10^{-6}}{2}\\right)^{2}=2.5 \\times 10^{-19}$ which is less than $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\left(10^{-18}\\right)$.<br>Hence, no precipitate will develop. Precipitate will develop only when ionic product is more than solubility product.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18072,'chemistry',50,839,1878,'For the redox reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{Zn}+\\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}+\\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$<br>In basic medium, coefficients of $\\mathrm{Zn}, \\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$and$\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$in the balanced reaction respectively, are','[{"text": "$4,1,7$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7,4,1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4,1,10$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1,4,10$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The balanced reaction is,<br>${\\rm{4Zn}} + {\\rm{NO}}_3^ -  + 7{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}} \\to 4{\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}} + {\\rm{NH}}_4^ +  + {\\rm{10O}}{{\\rm{H}}^ - }$<br>Thus, the coefficients of $\\mathrm{Zn}, \\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$and $\\overline{\\mathrm{O}} \\mathrm{H}$ are $4,1,10$ respectively.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18074,'chemistry',49,632,1293,'On adding $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ solution each of $\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\\right],\\left[\\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}\\right],\\left[\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\\right]$ in a $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution, species first precipitated is $\\left[\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\right.$ of $\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}=10^{-11}, \\mathrm{~K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}$ of $\\mathrm{CaSO}_{4}=10^{-6}, \\mathrm{~K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}$ of $\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}=10^{-5}$ ]','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CaSO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Solubility of $\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}(\\mathrm{x})=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}}=\\sqrt{10^{-11}}$ $=3.6 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$<br>Solubility of<br>$\\mathrm{CaSO}_{4}(\\mathrm{x})=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}}=\\sqrt{10^{-6}}$<br>$=1.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$<br>Solubility of $\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}=\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}}{4}}$<br>$\\left(\\because\\right.$ for $\\left.\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}, 4 \\mathrm{x}^{3}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\text {sp }}\\right)$ $=\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{10^{-5}}{4}}=1.3 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$<br>Least solubility is of $\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}$, hence it will precipitate first.<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18076,'chemistry',50,839,1878,'In the redox reaction,<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{xKMn}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}} + {\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_3} \\to {\\rm{yKN}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}} + {\\rm{Mn}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, + {\\rm{KOH}} + {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}\n\\end{array}$<br>x and y are','[{"text": "x=4,y=6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "x=3,y=8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "x=8,y=6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "x=8,y=3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The balanced reaction is as follows:<br> $8 \\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}+3 \\mathrm{NH}_{3} \\rightarrow 8 \\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$$+3 \\mathrm{KNO}_{3}+5 \\mathrm{KOH}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>$\\because \\mathrm{x}$ and $\\mathrm{y}$ are respectively 8 and 3 .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18077,'chemistry',48,756,1630,'Given<br>(A) $2{\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{({\\text{g}})}} + {{\\text{O}}_{2(\\,{\\text{g}})}} \\to $  $2{\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{2(\\,{\\text{g}})}}\\Delta {\\text{H}}{^\\circ _1} =  - {\\text{x}}\\,\\,{\\text{kJ}}\\,\\,{\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{ - 1}}$<br>(B)  ${{\\text{C}}_{{\\text{(graphitc) }}}} + {{\\text{O}}_{2(\\,{\\text{g}})}} \\to $  ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{2(\\,{\\text{g}})}}\\Delta {\\text{H}}{^\\circ _2} =  - {\\text{y}}\\,\\,{\\text{kJ}}\\,\\,{\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{ - 1}}$<br>The  $\\Delta {\\text{H}}^\\circ $ for the reaction<br>${{\\text{C}}_{{\\text{(graphite) }}}} + \\frac{1}{2}{{\\text{O}}_{2(\\,{\\text{g}})}} \\to {\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{({\\text{g}})}}$  is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\text{x}} + 2{\\\\text{y}}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\text{x}} - 2{\\\\text{y}}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2{\\\\text{x}} - {\\\\text{y}}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2{\\\\text{y}} - {\\\\text{x}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From Hess\'s law,<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/48_18077_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>${\\text{a}} - \\frac{{\\text{x}}}{2} =  - {\\text{y}}$<br>${\\text{a}} = \\frac{{\\text{x}}}{2} - {\\text{y}}$<br>${\\text{a}} = \\frac{{{\\text{x}} - 2{\\text{y}}}}{2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18078,'chemistry',49,632,1293,'The solubility product of a salt having formula $\\mathrm{M}_{2} \\mathrm{X}_{3}$ is $2.2 \\times 10^{-20}$. If the solubility of another salt having formula $\\mathrm{M}_{2} \\mathrm{X}$ is twice the molar solubility of $\\mathrm{M}_{2} \\mathrm{X}_{3}$, the solubility product of $\\mathrm{M}_{2} \\mathrm{X}$ is','[{"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{-12}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9.16 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.58 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.76 \\\\times 10^{-18}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{M}_{2} \\mathrm{X}_{3} \\rightleftharpoons \\underset{2 \\mathrm{~S}}{2 \\mathrm{M}^{3+}}+\\underset{3 \\mathrm{~S}}{3 \\mathrm{X}^{2-}}$<br>(where, $\\mathrm{S}=$ solubility)<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}=(2 \\mathrm{~S})^{2} \\cdot(3 \\mathrm{~S})^{3}=108 \\mathrm{~S}^{5}$<br>Given $2.2 \\times 10^{-20}=108 \\mathrm{~S}^{5}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{S}=4.59 \\times 10^{-5}$ For $\\mathrm{M}_{2} \\mathrm{X}$,<br>${{\\text{M}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{X}} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathop {{\\text{2}}{{\\text{M}}^{\\text{ + }}}}\\limits_{{\\text{2S}}} {\\text{ + X}}_{\\text{S}}^{{\\text{2 - }}}$<br>${{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{sp}}}} = {(2\\,{\\text{S}})^2} \\cdot ({\\text{S}}) = 4\\,{{\\text{S}}^3}$<br>$\\mathrm{M}_{2} \\mathrm{X}$ solubility is twice the molar solubility of $\\mathrm{M}_{2} \\mathrm{X}_{3}$. So, $\\mathrm{S}=2 \\times 4.59 \\times 10^{-5}$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}=4\\left(2 \\times 4.59 \\times 10^{-5}\\right)^{3}=3 \\times 10^{-12}$<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18080,'chemistry',50,842,1881,'During the conversion of $\\,{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{OH}} \\to {{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$ , the equivalent weight of ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{OH}}$ is<br>(molecular weight of ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{OH}}$ is M.)','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{M}}\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{M}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{/}}\\\\,2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{M}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{/}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{M}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{/}}\\\\,5$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathop {\\rm{N}}\\limits^{ - {\\rm{1}}} {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{OH}} \\to {\\mathop {\\rm{N}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 1}}} _{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>Valence factor,n=+1- (-1) = +2<br>Equivalent weight of<br>${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{OH}}\\,{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{molecular}}\\,\\,{\\rm{weight}}}}{{{\\rm{valence}}\\,{\\rm{factor}}}} = \\frac{{\\rm{M}}}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18082,'chemistry',49,632,1293,'The solubility product of sparingly soluble salt $\\mathrm{AX}_{2}$ is $3.2 \\times 10^{-11}$. Its solubility (in $\\mathrm{mol} / \\mathrm{L}$ ) is','[{"text": "$5.6 \\\\times 10^{-6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.1 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{AX}_{2}$ is $\\mathrm{AB}_{2}$ type of salt<br>So, ${{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{SP}}}}{\\text{ =  4}}{{\\text{S}}^{\\text{3}}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{SP}}=3.2 \\times 10^{-11}=32 \\times 10^{-12} \\\\& \\mathrm{~S}=\\left(\\dfrac{32 \\times 10^{-12}}{4}\\right)^{1 / 3}=2 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}\\end{aligned}$<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18084,'chemistry',50,841,1880,'The number of electrons required to reduce $4.5 \\times {10^{ - 5}}{\\rm{g}}$ of Al is','[{"text": "$1.03 \\\\times {10^{18}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.01 \\\\times {10^{18}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.95 \\\\times {10^{26}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.31 \\\\times {10^{20}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}} + {\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{e}}^ - } \\to {\\rm{Al}}\\\\\n27{\\rm{g}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,27{\\rm{g}}\n\\end{array}$<br>27 g of Al is reduced by $3 \\times 6.023 \\times {10^{23}}$ <br>$\\therefore \\,4.5 \\times {10^{ - 5}}{\\rm{g}}\\,$ of Al will be reduced by $ = \\frac{{3 \\times 6.023 \\times {{10}^{23}} \\times 4.5 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}}}{{27}}$ <br>${\\rm{ = }}\\,{\\rm{3}}{\\rm{.01 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{18}}}}{\\rm{electrons}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18086,'chemistry',49,632,1292,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The ionisation of hydrogen sulphide in water is low in the presence of $\\mathrm{HCl}$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> ${{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{S}}$ is a weak acid.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}$  is a weak acid and also a weak electrolyte, thus its ionisation is suppressed when small amount of strong electrolyte (like $\\mathrm{HCl}$ ) is added due to common ion effect.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/49_18086_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18088,'chemistry',50,839,1878,'$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{2 + }} \\to {\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{3 + }} + {{\\rm{e}}^ - }\\\\\n{\\rm{MnO}}_4^ -  + {\\rm{5}}{{\\rm{e}}^ - } \\to {\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{2 + }}\n\\end{array}$ <br>The ratio of stoichiometric coefficient of ${\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{2 + }}$ and ${\\rm{MnO}}_4^ - $','[{"text": "$1:5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5:1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2:3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6:1\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}} \\to {\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}}{\\rm{(Oxidation)}}\\quad \\quad {\\rm{(1)}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{8}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{ + MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{ + 5}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to }\\\\\n{\\quad {\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{ + 4}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{(Reduction)}}\\quad \\quad {\\rm{(2)}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Eq}}{\\rm{.(1)}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{is}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{multiplied}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{by}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{5}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{5F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{ + 5F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\rm{ + MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }} \\to }\\\\\n{{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{5F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{ + M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{ + 4}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}}\\\\\n\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{Thus,}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{the}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{ratio}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{stoichiometric}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\\\\n{\\rm{coefficient}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{and}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{is}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{5:1}}\n\\end{array}\n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18089,'chemistry',49,626,1273,'When $10 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ acetic acid $\\left(\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}=5.0\\right)$ is titrated against $10 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ ammonia solution $\\left(\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}=5.0\\right)$, the equivalence point occurs at $\\mathrm{pH}$.','[{"text": "5.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7.0", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "9.0", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}=-\\log \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}$<br>and $\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}=-\\log \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{pH}=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left[\\log \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}+\\log \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{w}}-\\log \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}}\\right] \\\\& =-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left[-5+\\log 10^{-14}-(-5)\\right] \\\\& =-\\dfrac{1}{2}[-5-14+5]=7\\end{aligned}$<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18097,'chemistry',49,626,1271,'The number of hydroxyl ions in $100 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of a solution having ${\\text{pH  =  }}10$ is','[{"text": "$1 \\\\times 10^{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.012 \\\\times 10^{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.02 \\\\times 10^{18}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.023 \\\\times 10^{19}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, $\\mathrm{pH}=10$<br>$\\therefore\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=1 \\times 10^{-\\mathrm{pH}}=1 \\times 10^{-10} \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$<br>$\\therefore\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=\\dfrac{1 \\times 10^{-14}}{1 \\times 10^{-10}}=1 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$<br>$\\because$ The number of hydroxyl ions present in 1 mole $=6.023 \\times 10^{23}$<br>$\\therefore$ The number of hydroxyl ion in $1 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$<br>$=6.023 \\times 10^{23} \\times 10^{-4} / \\mathrm{L}$<br>$=6.023 \\times 10^{19} \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$<br>$\\because$ In $1000 \\mathrm{~mL}$, number of hydroxyl ion<br>$=6.023 \\times 10^{19}$<br>$\\therefore$ In $100 \\mathrm{~mL}$, number of hydroxyl ions<br>$=\\dfrac{6.023 \\times 10^{19} \\times 100}{1000}=6.02 \\times 10^{18}$<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18092,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The oxidation states of sulphur in the anions<br> $\\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{2-}, \\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}$ and $\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{6}^{2-}$ follow the order.','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{2-} < \\\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-} < \\\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{6}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-} < \\\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{6}^{2-} < \\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{6}^{2-} < \\\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-} < \\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-} < \\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{2-} < \\\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{6}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to chemical bond method: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18092_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18093,'chemistry',49,626,1273,'If the dissociation constant of $5 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{M}$ aqueous solution of diethylamine is $2.5 \\times 10^{-5}$,  its $\\mathrm{pH}$ value is','[{"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.95", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10.04", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Diethylamine is a weak base and from Ostwald\'s dilution law, for weak base,<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}}=\\mathrm{C} \\alpha^{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\alpha=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2.5 \\times 10^{-5}}{5 \\times 10^{-4}}}=0.22$$\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=\\mathrm{C} \\alpha=5 \\times 10^{-4} \\times 0.22$<br>$=1.1 \\times 10^{-4}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{pOH}=-\\log \\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]$<br>$=-\\log \\left(1.1 \\times 10^{-4}\\right)=3.96$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{pH}=14-3.96=10.04$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18094,'chemistry',48,748,1603,'What happens when methane undergoes combustion in system A and B respectively?','[{"text": "System A : Adiabatic system ; Temperature rises.<br>System B : Diathermic container : Temperature remains same.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "System A : Adiabatic system ; Temperature falls.<br>System B : Diathermic container : Temperature rises.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "System A : Adiabatic system; Temperature remains same. <br>System B : Diathermic container : Temperature rises.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "System A : Adiabatic system ; Temperature falls.<br>System B : Diathermic container : Temperature remains same.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When methane undergoes combustion, a certain amount of energy is released. In case of adiabatic system (system A), heat cannot be exchanged with surroundings, so temperature of the system rises. But in diathermic container (system B) heat flow can occur to maintain the temperature constant.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18096,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In which of the following pairs, there is greatest difference in the oxidation number of the underlined elements?','[{"text": "$\\\\underline {\\\\rm{N}} {{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\underline {\\\\rm{N}} _{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\underline {\\\\rm{P}} _{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{5}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\underline {\\\\rm{P}} _{\\\\rm{4}}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{{\\\\rm{10}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\underline {\\\\rm{N}} _{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{O}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\underline {\\\\rm{N}} {\\\\rm{O}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\underline{\\\\text{S}}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{2}}}\\\\,{\\\\text{and}}\\\\,{\\\\underline{\\\\text{S}}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{3}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{O}}{\\rm{.N}}{\\rm{.}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{N}}\\,\\,{\\rm{in}}\\,\\,{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{is + 4}}$<br> $\\therefore \\,\\,{\\rm{difference}}\\,\\,{\\rm{is}}\\,\\,{\\rm{zero}}$.<br> ${\\rm{O}}{\\rm{.N}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{P}}\\,\\,{\\rm{in}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{5}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{4}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{{\\rm{10}}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{is + 5}}$<br> $\\therefore \\,\\,{\\rm{difference}}\\,\\,{\\rm{is}}\\,\\,{\\rm{zero}}$<br> ${\\rm{O}}{\\rm{.N}}{\\rm{.}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{N}}\\,\\,{\\rm{in}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}\\,\\,{\\rm{is + 1}}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{\\rm{in}}\\,\\,{\\rm{NO,it}}\\,{\\rm{is + 2,}}$<br> $\\therefore \\,\\,{\\rm{difference}}\\,\\,{\\rm{is}}\\,\\,{\\rm{1}}$<br>${\\rm{O}}{\\rm{.N}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{in}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{is + 4}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{\\rm{in}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{,it}}\\,{\\rm{is + 6,}}$<br> $\\therefore \\,\\,{\\rm{difference}}\\,\\,{\\rm{is + 2}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18098,'chemistry',48,758,1635,'Identify the correct order of standard enthalpy of formation of sodium halides.','[{"text": "${\\\\text{NaI}} < {\\\\text{NaBr}} < {\\\\text{NaCl}} < {\\\\text{NaF}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{NaF}} < {\\\\text{NaCl}} < {\\\\text{NaBr}} < {\\\\text{NaI}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{NaI}} < {\\\\text{NaBr}} < {\\\\text{NaF}} < {\\\\text{NaCl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{NaCl}} < {\\\\text{NaF}} < {\\\\text{NaBr}} < {\\\\text{NaI}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In case of sodium halides, lesser the size of anion greater will be the enthalpy of formation.<br>${\\text{NaF}} > {\\text{NaCl}} > {\\text{NaBr}} > {\\text{NaI}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18100,'chemistry',50,836,1862,'In the reaction<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_7^{2 - } + {\\rm{14}}{{\\rm{H}}^ + } + {\\rm{6}}{{\\rm{I}}^ - } \\to }\\\\\n{{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{2C}}{{\\rm{r}}^{3 + }}{\\rm{ + 7}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O + 3}}{{\\rm{I}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\n\\end{array}$<br>Which element is reduced','[{"text": "I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{I}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {{\\rm{I}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ is oxidation (loss of electrons); (Cr + 6) changes to Cr (+3) by gain of electrons. Hence Cr is reduced.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18101,'chemistry',49,626,1271,'The $\\mathrm{pH}$ value of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{N}}{10} \\mathrm{NaOH}$ is','[{"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "13", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$0.1 {\\text{N  NaOH}}$ is given,<br>So, $\\mathrm{pOH}=-\\log \\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]$<br>$=-\\log 0.1=1$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{pH}=14-\\mathrm{pOH}=14-1=13$<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18104,'chemistry',50,836,1862,'For the the reaction<br> $\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{-2} \\text { aq. }+2 \\mathrm{H}^{+} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-} \\text { (aq.) }+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$,<br> element oxidised is','[{"text": "Oxygen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chromium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\begin{array}{l}\\mathop {{\\rm{Cr}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 6}}} \\mathop {{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ - 2}}} {\\rm{(aq) + 2}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}} \\to \\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 6}}} {\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{7}}^{{\\rm{ - 2}}}{\\rm{(aq) + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}\n\\end{array}$<br>None of the species is losing ${{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}}$ <br>$\\therefore $ none is showing oxidation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18105,'chemistry',49,629,1277,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Buffer solutions are composed of strong acids and strong bases.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It maintains the $\\mathrm{pH}$ to a constant value of 7.4.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both Assertion and Reason are false as buffer solutions are made by mixing weak acid with its salt of strong base or by mixing weak base with its salt of strong acid. It is never formed by mixing strong acid and strong base.<br>The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of buffer solution remains constant but not always equal to 7.4.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18108,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Consider the following equation, which represents a reaction in the extraction of chromium from its ore<br>$2 \\mathrm{Fe}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3} \\cdot \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+4 \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}+3 \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Fe}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+$<br>$4 \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}+4 \\mathrm{CO}_{2}$<br>Which one of the following statements about the oxidation states of the substances is correct?','[{"text": "The iron has been reduced from +3 to +2 state.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The chromium has been oxidised from +3 to +6 state.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The carbon has been oxidised from +2 to +4 state.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There is no change in the oxidation state of the substances in the reaction.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{2}}\\mathop {{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 3}}} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}.\\mathop {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 3}}} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ + 4N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\mathop {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 4}}} {\\rm{ + 3}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\to $<br>${\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{2}}\\mathop {{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 3}}} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ + 4N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\mathop {{\\rm{Cr}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 6}}} {\\rm{ + 4}}\\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 4}}} {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18109,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'$\\mathrm{pH}$ of a salt solution of weak acid $\\left(\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}=4\\right)$ and weak base $\\left(\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}=5\\right)$ at $25^{\\circ}$ is','[{"text": "6.5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7.5", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{pH}$ of buffer solution of weak acid and weak base is calculated, using the following formula.<br>$\\mathrm{pH}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left[\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{w}}+\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}-\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}\\right]$<br>Given: $\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}=4, \\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}=5, \\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{w}}=14$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{pH}=\\dfrac{1}{2}[14+4-5]=6.5$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18111,'chemistry',48,762,1659,'Which of the following relations are correct?<br>(A)  $\\Delta {\\text{U}} = {\\text{q}} + {\\text{p}}\\,\\Delta \\,{\\text{V}}$<br>(B)  $\\Delta {\\text{G}} = \\Delta {\\text{H}} - {\\text{T}}\\,\\Delta \\,{\\text{S}}$<br>(C)  $\\Delta {\\text{S}} = \\frac{{{{\\text{q}}_{{\\text{rev}}}}}}{{\\text{T}}}$<br>(D)  $\\Delta {\\text{H}} = \\Delta {\\text{U}} - \\Delta \\,{\\text{n}}\\,{\\text{RT}}$<br>Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :','[{"text": "B and C only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "B and D only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A and B only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C and D only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to first law of thermodynamics<br>$\\Delta {\\text{U}} = {\\text{q}} + {\\text{w}}$ and ${\\text{w}} =  - {\\text{P}}\\,\\Delta {\\text{V}}$,<br>So, $\\Delta {\\text{U}} = {\\text{q}} - {\\text{P}}\\,\\Delta {\\text{V}}$,<br>$\\Delta {\\text{H}} = \\Delta {\\text{U}} + \\Delta {{\\text{n}}_{\\text{g}}}{\\text{RT}}$<br>$\\Delta {\\text{S}} = \\frac{{{{\\text{q}}_{{\\text{rev}}}}}}{{\\text{T}}}$,<br>$\\Delta {\\text{G}} = \\Delta {\\text{H}} - {\\text{T}}\\Delta {\\text{S}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18112,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'A weak acid ${\\text{HX}}$ has the dissociation constant $1 \\times 10^{-5} \\mathrm{M}$. It forms a salt $\\mathrm{NaX}$ on reaction with alkali. The degree of hydrolysis of $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ solution of $\\mathrm{NaX}$ is','[{"text": "$0.0001 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.01 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.1 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.15 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{X}^{-}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{HX}+\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{h}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{w}}}{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}}=\\dfrac{10^{-14}}{10^{-5}}=10^{-9}$<br>So, degree of hydrolysis<br>$\\mathrm{h}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{h}}}{\\mathrm{C}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10^{-9}}{10^{-1}}}=10^{-4}$<br>Percentage of degree of hydrolysis<br>$=10^{-4} \\times 100=10^{-2} \\%=0.01 \\%$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2012,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18113,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'Which of the following conversions involves gain of 5 electrons per ion?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{2-} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{MnO}}_4^ -  \\to {\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}$<br>${\\rm{O}}{\\rm{.N}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{Mn}}\\,{\\rm{in}}\\,{\\rm{MnO}}_4^ - $<br>${\\rm{x}} + 4 \\times ( - 2) =  - 1\\quad \\therefore {\\rm{x}} =  + 7$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\mathop {{\\text{MnO}}_4^ - }\\limits^{ + 7} \\xrightarrow{{5{e^ - }}}{\\text{M}}{{\\text{n}}^{2 + }}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18116,'chemistry',49,632,1291,'The solubility of $\\mathrm{AgCl}$ in it\'s solution is $1.25 \\times 10^{-5} \\mathrm{moldm}^{-3}$. What is the solubility product of $\\mathrm{AgCl}$ ?','[{"text": "$1.56 \\\\times 10^{-10}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.50 \\\\times 10^{-6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.10 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.53 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{AgCl}_{\\text {(s) }} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{Ag}_{\\text {(aq) }}^{+}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{(\\text {aq) }}^{-}$<br>It is ${\\text{ AB type}}$  of salt<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{Ksp}=\\mathrm{S}^{2}=\\left(1.25 \\times 10^{-5}\\right)^{2}=1.56 \\times 10^{-10}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18117,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'$4 \\mathrm{P}+3 \\mathrm{NaOH}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{NaH}_{2} \\mathrm{PO}_{2}+\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$<br> In the above reaction, find the element undergoing both oxidation and reduction.','[{"text": "Na", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "H", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "O", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18117_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Hence,P is undergoing both oxidation and reduction in the given reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18120,'chemistry',49,623,1262,'Which from the following compounds accepts proton from water molecule according to Bronsted - Lowry theory?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NaOH}_{(\\\\text {aq })}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCl}_{(\\\\mathrm{aq})}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3(\\\\mathrm{aq})}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{OH}_{(\\\\mathrm{aq})}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Ammonia accepts proton and becomes ammonium.<br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(\\ell)}+\\mathrm{NH}_{3(\\mathrm{aq})} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{NH}_{4(\\mathrm{aq})}^{+}+\\mathrm{OH}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{-}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18121,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'$4 \\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{3} \\rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}+\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$, in this reaction, phosphorus in $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{3}$ undergoes','[{"text": "oxidation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "reduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "hydrolysis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "both (1) and (2).", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18121_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Therefore, P in $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{3}$ undergoes both oxidation as well as reduction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18124,'chemistry',49,624,1266,'The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ solution of monobasic acid is 2.34. Calculate the degree of dissociation of the acid.','[{"text": "$3.1 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.18 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{pH}=2.34, \\mathrm{c}=0.1 \\mathrm{M},\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=\\mathrm{C} \\alpha$<br>(i) $\\mathrm{pH}=-\\log _{10}\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]$<br>$\\therefore\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=4.571 \\mathrm{M}$<br>(ii) $\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=\\propto \\mathrm{c}$<br>$\\therefore \\propto=\\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]}{\\mathrm{c}}=\\dfrac{4.571 \\times 10^{-3}}{0.1}=4.571 \\times 10^{-2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18128,'chemistry',49,632,1291,'Solubility of $\\mathrm{AgCl}$ is $7.2 \\times 10^{-7} \\mathrm{moldm}^{-3}$. What is it\'s solubility product?','[{"text": "$3.6 \\\\times 10^{-13}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.2 \\\\times 10^{-14}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.59 \\\\times 10^{-14}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.18 \\\\times 10^{-13}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For $\\mathrm{AgCl}, \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}=\\mathrm{S}^{2}$ (AB type of salt)<br>$\\mathrm{S}=7.2 \\times 10^{-7} \\mathrm{moldm}^{-3}$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}=\\left(7.2 \\times 10^{-7}\\right)^{2}=5.18 \\times 10^{-13}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18132,'chemistry',49,624,1266,'What is the molar concentration of weak monobasic acid if dissociation constant is $5 \\times 10^{-8}$ and undergoes $0.5 \\%$ dissociation?','[{"text": "$0.03 \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.002 \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.001 \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.005 \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=5 \\times 10^{-8}, \\propto=0.5 \\%=\\dfrac{0.5}{100}=5 \\times 10^{-3}$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\propto^{2} \\mathrm{c}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{c}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{\\propto^{2}}=\\dfrac{5 \\times 10^{-8}}{\\left(5 \\times 10^{-3}\\right)^{2}}=\\dfrac{5 \\times 10^{-8}}{25 \\times 10^{-6}}$<br>$=0.002 \\mathrm{M}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18133,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The number of species from the following having +2 oxidation state of metal ion is ____ .<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{FeO}, \\mathrm{Fe}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}, \\mathrm{CuI}, \\mathrm{CuO}, \\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}, \\mathrm{SnCl}_{4}, \\mathrm{Hg}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}}$, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HgCl}_{2}}}$','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18133_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18136,'chemistry',49,623,1261,'A substance containing hydrogen and releasing $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$in aqueous medium is acid. Identify theory suggesting this concept, from the following','[{"text": "Ostwald theory", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bronsted - Lowry theory", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Arrhenius theory", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lewis theory", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Substance which increase $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$ion concentration in aq.soln is called acid. (Arrhenius theory)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18139,'chemistry',48,758,1636,'From the diagram ${{\\rm{\\Delta }}_{\\rm{r}}}{\\rm{H}}\\,{\\rm{for}}\\,\\,{\\rm{the}}\\,\\,{\\rm{reaction}}\\,{\\rm{C}} \\to {\\rm{A}}\\,{\\rm{is:}}$','[{"text": "+35 J", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - {\\\\rm{15 J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 35\\\\,{\\\\rm{J}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "+15 J", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A} \\xrightarrow{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{1}} 2 \\mathrm{~B} \\xrightarrow{\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{2}} \\mathrm{C}}}$${\\mathrm{\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C} \\quad \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{1}+\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{2}=35 \\mathrm{~J}}}$<br>Then ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{A} \\quad \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=-35 \\mathrm{~J}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/48_18139_question_1.png'),
(18140,'chemistry',49,623,1260,'Which among the following salts turns blue litmus red in it\'s aqueous solution?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{CN}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{~F}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COONa}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COONH}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{CN}$ - almost neutral, $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{~F}$ - Slightly acidic<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONa}$ - (weakacid - strongbase salt),(Basic)<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONH}_{4}$ - almost neutral<br>The solution of $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{~F}$ is only slightly acidic and turns blue litmus red.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18143,'chemistry',48,748,1608,'Match Column I (process) with Column II (conditions).Choose the correct answer from the options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A.Isothermal process<br>B.Isochoric process<br>C.Isobaric process<br>D.Adiabatic process</div><div class="column-right">P.No heat exchange<br>Q.Carried out at  constant temperature<br>R.Carried out at constant volume<br>S.Carried out at constant pressure</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A }} - {\\\\rm{S; }}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B }} - {\\\\rm{ R; C}} - {\\\\rm{ Q;  D}} - {\\\\rm{ P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A }} - {\\\\rm{ S;  B }} - {\\\\rm{ Q; C}} - {\\\\rm{R;  D}} - {\\\\rm{ P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A }} - {\\\\rm{ P;  B }} - {\\\\rm{ Q; C }} - {\\\\rm{R; D }} - {\\\\rm{ S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A }} - {\\\\rm{ Q;  B }} - {\\\\rm{ R; C }} - S{\\\\rm{; D }} - {\\\\rm{ P}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'(A) Isothermal process ${\\mathrm{\\Rightarrow}}$ Temperature is constant throughout the process.<br>(B) Isochoric process ${\\mathrm{\\Rightarrow}}$ Volume is constant throughout the process.<br>${\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{C})}}$ Isobaric process ${\\mathrm{\\Rightarrow}}$ Pressure is constant throughout the process.<br>(D) Adiabatic process ${\\mathrm{\\Rightarrow}}$ No exchange of heat (q) between system and surroundings.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18144,'chemistry',49,626,1272,'What is the  ${\\rm{pH}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.02}}\\,{\\rm{M}}\\,\\,{\\rm{NaOH}}$  solution?','[{"text": "10.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "11.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "11.7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12.3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=2 \\times 10^{-2}$<br>$\\mathrm{P}^{\\mathrm{OH}}=-\\log _{10}\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]$<br>$=-\\log 2 \\times 10^{-2}$<br>$=2-\\log 2$<br>$=2-0.3$<br>$\\mathrm{P}^{\\mathrm{OH}}=1.7$<br>and $\\mathrm{P}^{\\mathrm{H}}=14-\\mathrm{P}^{\\mathrm{OH}}=14-1.7$<br>$\\mathrm{P}^{\\mathrm{H}}=12.3$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18145,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The number of elements from the following which exhibit(s) variable oxidation numbers is ____.<br>${\\mathrm{\\qquad}}$ <br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Be}, \\mathrm{B}, \\mathrm{C}, \\mathrm{Na}, \\mathrm{F}, \\mathrm{Cl}, \\mathrm{Al}, \\mathrm{H}, \\mathrm{Li}, \\mathrm{O}}}$','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18145_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18147,'chemistry',48,761,1654,'In which of the following processes entropy increases?<br>A. A liquid evaporates to vapour.<br>B. Temperature of a crystalline solid lowered from 130 K to 0 K .<br>C.${\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{NaHCO}_{3(\\mathrm{~s})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3(\\mathrm{~s})}+\\mathrm{CO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(\\mathrm{g})}}}$<br>D. ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Cl}_{(\\mathrm{g})}}}$<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "A and C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, B and C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, C and D", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "C and D", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When a liquid evaporates to vapour entropy increases.<br>${\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{NaHCO}_{3(\\mathrm{~s})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3(\\mathrm{~s})}+\\mathrm{CO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(\\mathrm{g})}}}$<br>In this change, number of gaseous product molecules increases, so entropy increases.<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Cl}_{(\\mathrm{g})}}}$<br>1 mole of gaseous ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}}$ molecules form 2 moles of gaseous Cl atoms. So entropy increases. When \' ${\\mathrm{T}}$ \' decreases, entropy decreases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18148,'chemistry',49,624,1266,'Dissociation constant of propionic acid is $1.32 \\times 10^{-5}$. Calculate the degree of dissociation of acid in $0.05 \\mathrm{M}$ solution.<br>[2021]','[{"text": "$2.6 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.61 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.90 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.5 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=1.32 \\times 10^{-5}, \\mathrm{c}=0.05 \\mathrm{M}, \\propto=$ ?<br>$\\propto=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{Ka}}{\\mathrm{c}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.32 \\times 10^{-5}}{0.05}}=\\sqrt{2.64} \\times 10^{-2}$<br>$\\therefore \\propto=1.6 \\times 10^{-2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18151,'chemistry',48,751,1613,'The work done during reversible isothermal expansion of one mole of hydrogen gas at ${\\mathrm{25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}}}$ from pressure of 20 atmosphere to 10 atmosphere is <br>(Given ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{R}=2.0 \\,\\mathrm{cal} \\,\\mathrm{K}^{-1} \\,\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}}$ )','[{"text": "0 calorie", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - {\\\\rm{413}}{\\\\rm{.14 calories}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "413.14 calories", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "100 calories", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{W}_{\\text {rev, iso }}=-2.303 \\,\\mathrm{nRT} \\log \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{i}}}{\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{f}}}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{=-2.303 \\times 1 \\times 2 \\times 298 \\times \\log 2}}$<br>${\\mathrm{=-2.303 \\times 1 \\times 2 \\times 298 \\times 0.3}}$<br>${\\mathrm{=-413.14}}$ calories',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18152,'chemistry',49,625,1269,'The $\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]$in a solution is $1 \\times 10^{-12} \\mathrm{moldm}^{-3}$. What is the concentration of $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$ions?','[{"text": "$0.1 \\\\mathrm{moldm}^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.0 \\\\mathrm{moldm}^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0 \\\\mathrm{moldm}^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.01 \\\\mathrm{moldm}^{-3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=1 \\times 10^{-12} \\mathrm{moldm}{ }^{-3},\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=$?<br>and $\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right] \\times\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=1 \\times 10^{-14}$ at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ (ionic product of water)<br>$\\therefore\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=\\dfrac{1 \\times 10^{-14}}{1 \\times 10^{-12}}=1 \\times 10^{-2}$<br>$=0.01 \\mathrm{moldm}^{-3}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18155,'chemistry',48,752,1621,'The molar heat capacity of water at constant pressure, $\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{p}}$ is $75 \\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$  When 10.0 kJ of heat is supplied to 1000 g of water which is free to expand, increase in the temperature of water is','[{"text": "4.2 K", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.4 K", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3.2 K", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6.2 K", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${{\\rm{q}}_{\\rm{p}}} = {\\rm{n}}{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{p}}}\\Delta {\\rm{T}}$<br>$\\mathrm{q}_{\\mathrm{p}}=10 \\mathrm{KJ}=10000 \\mathrm{~J}, \\mathrm{Cp}=75 \\mathrm{JK}^{-} \\mathrm{mol}^{-}$,<br>No. of moles of water $=1000 / 18=55.5 \\mathrm{~mol}$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{T}=\\dfrac{10000}{55.5 \\times 75}=2.4 \\mathrm{~K}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18156,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'Which among the following salt solution in water shows $\\mathrm{pH}$ greater than 7 ?','[{"text": "sodium acetate", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "sodium sulphate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "copper sulphate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ammonium chloride", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Sodium acetate is a salt of weak acid and strong base. Therefore, it is basic in nature with $\\mathrm{pH}$ greater than 7.<br>$\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}, \\mathrm{pH}=7 ; \\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}, \\mathrm{pH}$ is less than 7;<br>$\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}, \\mathrm{pH}$ is less than 7 .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18159,'chemistry',49,626,1272,'The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of $0.005\\,\\,{\\text{M}}\\,\\,{\\text{KOH}}$ is 9.95 . Calculate the $\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]$?','[{"text": "$6.71 \\\\times 10^{-4} \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.12 \\\\times 10^{-4} \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.45 \\\\times 10^{-5} \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.91 \\\\times 10^{-5} \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{pH}=9.95, \\mathrm{c}=0.05 \\mathrm{M}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{pOH}=14-\\mathrm{pH}=14-9.95=4.05 \\\\& \\mathrm{pOH}=-\\log _{10}\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right] \\\\& \\log _{10}\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=-4.05 \\\\& =-4-0.05-1+1 \\\\& =-5+0.95=\\overline{5} .95 \\\\& {\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=\\operatorname{antilog} \\overline{5} .95} \\\\& =8.913 \\times 10^{-5} \\mathrm{M}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18160,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The average oxidation number of sulphur in ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{6}}}$ is ____ .','[{"text": "$ - \\\\frac{1}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{2}{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{8}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\mathop {\\rm{S}}\\limits_{\\rm{4}}^ *  {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{6}}}$<br>$2\\left( { + 1} \\right) + 4x + 6( - 2) = 0$<br>$4x = 10 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{{10}}{4} = \\frac{5}{2} =  + 2.5$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18161,'chemistry',48,753,1625,'One gram sample of $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{NO}_{3}$ is decomposed in a bomb calorimeter. The temperature of the calorimeter increased by 6.12 K . The heat capacity of the system is $1.23 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{g}^{-1} \\mathrm{~K}^{-1}$. What is the molar heat of decomposition for $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{NO}_{3}$.','[{"text": "$7.53 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} / \\\\mathrm{mol}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$398.1 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} / \\\\mathrm{mol}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16.1 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} / \\\\mathrm{mol}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$602 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} / \\\\mathrm{mol}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The heat evolved, $\\mathrm{q}=\\mathrm{mS} \\Delta \\mathrm{T}$<br>$\\mathrm{m}=\\lg ; \\mathrm{S}=1.23 \\mathrm{~kJ} . \\mathrm{g}^{-} \\mathrm{K}^{-}$;<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{T}=6.12 \\mathrm{~K}$<br>When 1 g of ammonium nitrate decomposes, the heat evolved is $\\mathrm{q}=1 \\times 1.23 \\times 6.12 \\mathrm{~kJ} /$ gram<br>When 1 mole of ammonium nitrate decomposes, the heat evolved is $\\mathrm{q}=1 \\times 1.23 \\times 6.12 \\times 80 \\mathrm{~kJ} /$ $\\mathrm{mol}=602.21 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$<br>$\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right.$ molar mass is 80 g.mol $\\left.{ }^{-}\\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18163,'chemistry',49,624,1266,'The dissociation constant of weak monobasic acid is $2.7 \\times 10^{-5}$. If degree of dissociation of acid is $3 \\times 10^{-2}$, what is the concentration of acid?','[{"text": "$0.24 \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.03 \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.3 \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.11 \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=2.7 \\times 10^{-5}, \\propto c=3 \\times 10^{-2}, \\mathrm{c}=$ ?<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\mathrm{c} \\propto^{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{c}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}}{\\propto^{2}}=\\dfrac{2.7 \\times 10^{-5}}{\\left(3 \\times 10^{-2}\\right)^{2}}=\\dfrac{2.7 \\times 10^{-5}}{9 \\times 10^{-4}}=0.03 \\mathrm{M}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18165,'chemistry',48,752,1620,'Assuming that water vapour is an ideal gas, the internal energy change  $(\\Delta U)$  when 1 mol of water is vapourized at 1 bar pressure and  $100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$  (Given molar enthalpy of vaporization of water at 1 bar and 373 K is $41\\,{\\rm{kJ}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}$  and  $\\mathrm{R}=8.314 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\mathrm{~K}^{-1}$ ) will be','[{"text": "$41.00 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.100 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.7899 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$37.899 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$<br>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{l}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\text { (vapour) }$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=1-0=1$;<br>${\\rm{R}} = 8.314 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{\\rm{kJ}}\\,.\\,\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^ - }{{\\rm{K}}^ - }$<br>$\\mathrm{T}=373 \\mathrm{~K}$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}+\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}} \\mathrm{RT}$<br>$=41-\\left(1 \\times 8.314 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 373\\right)$<br>$=37.899 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18167,'chemistry',49,632,1291,'The solubility product of a sparingly soluble salt $\\mathrm{AX}_{2}$ is $3.2 \\times 10^{-8}$. What is it\'s solubility in $\\mathrm{moldm}^{-3}$ ?','[{"text": "$2.8 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.0 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}=3.2 \\times 10^{-8}, \\mathrm{~S}=$ ?<br>$\\mathrm{AX}_{2}$ is $\\mathrm{AB}_{2}$ type salt; so $K_{s p}=4 \\mathrm{~s}^{3}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{S}^{3}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}}{4}=\\dfrac{3.2 \\times 10^{-8}}{4}=0.8 \\times 10^{-8}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{S}=\\sqrt[3]{8 \\times 10^{-9}}=2 \\times 10^{-3}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18169,'chemistry',48,758,1636,'For the allotropic change represented by equation, C (diamond) $\\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}$ (graphite) ; the enthalpy change is $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=-1.89 \\mathrm{~kJ}$. If 6 g of diamond and 6 g of graphite are separately burnt to yield carbon dioxide, the heat liberated in the first case is','[{"text": "Less than in the second case by 1.89 kJ", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "More than in the second case by 1.89 kJ", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less than in the second case by 11.34 kJ", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less than in the second case by 0.945 kJ", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${{\\rm{C}}_{{\\rm{(diamond) }}}} \\to {{\\rm{C}}_{{\\rm{(graphite) }}}};\\Delta {\\rm{H}} =  - 1.89\\,{\\rm{kJ}}\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>${{\\rm{C}}_{{\\rm{(diamond) }}}} + {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\to {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}};\\Delta {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{1}}}\\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>${{\\rm{C}}_{{\\rm{graphite }}}} + {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\to {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}};\\Delta {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\,\\,\\,\\, \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>On subtracting Eq. (3) from Eq. (2), we get Eq. (1)<br>$\\Rightarrow-1.86=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{1}-\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{2}$ <br>$\\Rightarrow \\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{2}=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{1}+1.86$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{2}>\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{1}$<br>So, heat released in second case will be more. Heat released by 1 mole ( 12 g ) transition $=1.89 \\mathrm{~kJ}$<br>Also heat released by $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ mole (6 gram) transition<br>$=\\dfrac{1.89}{2}=0.945 \\mathrm{~kJ}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18171,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'Which of the following salt solutions is highly acidic?','[{"text": "Ammonium acetate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ammonium cyanide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sodium chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ammonium nitrate", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{NO}_{3}$ is a salt of strong acid, $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ and weak base, $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{OH}$. So highly acidic $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONH}_{4}, \\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{CN}$-almost neutral (WAWB salts)<br>$\\mathrm{NaCl}$ (SA-SB salt), neutral',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18172,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The oxidation state of Cr in ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right]^{+}}}$is ____ .','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$-$1", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\left[ {{\\rm{Cr}}{{\\left( {{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right]^{\\rm{ + }}}$<br>${\\text{x}} + 4 \\times (0) - 2{\\text{ }} = {\\text{ }}1$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\text{x + 0}} - 2 = 1$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\text{x = 1 + 2 =  + 3}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18173,'chemistry',48,758,1635,'${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}} + \\frac{1}{2}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\to {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}};\\Delta {\\rm{H}} =  - 68.39\\,{\\rm{kcal}}$<br>${\\rm{K}} + {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}} + {\\rm{water}} \\to {\\rm{KOH}}({\\rm{aq}}) + \\frac{1}{2}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}};$<br>$\\Delta {\\rm{H}} =  - 48{\\rm{kcal}}$<br>${\\rm{KOH}} + {\\rm{water}} \\to {\\rm{KOH}}({\\rm{aq}});\\Delta {\\rm{H}} =  - 14{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{kcal}}$<br>The heat of formation of KOH is (in kcal)','[{"text": "$-68.39+48-14$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-68.39-48+14$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$68.39-48+14$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$68.39+48+14$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{H}_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{1}$     (1)<br>$\\mathrm{K}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+$ water $\\rightarrow \\mathrm{KOH}(\\mathrm{aq})+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{2}$     (2)<br>$\\mathrm{KOH}+$ water $\\rightarrow \\mathrm{KOH}(\\mathrm{aq}) ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{3}    (3)$<br>By adding equation (1) & (2) and subtracting equation 3 , we get<br>${\\rm{K}} + \\frac{1}{2}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}} + {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\to {\\rm{KOH}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(4)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta {\\rm{H}}{^\\circ _{\\rm{f}}} = \\Delta {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{1}}} + \\Delta {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}} - \\Delta {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$<br>$ =  - 68.39 - 48 + 14 = 102.39\\,{\\rm{kcal}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18175,'chemistry',49,632,1291,'Solubility product of $\\mathrm{AgBr}$ is $4.9 \\times 10^{-13}$. What is its solubility?','[{"text": "$2.4 \\\\times 10^{-7} \\\\mathrm{~mol} \\\\mathrm{dm}^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times 10^{-7} \\\\mathrm{~mol} \\\\mathrm{dm}^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.9 \\\\times 10^{-7} \\\\mathrm{~mol} \\\\mathrm{dm}^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.0 \\\\times 10^{-7} \\\\mathrm{moldm}^{-3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}=4.9 \\times 10^{-13}, \\mathrm{~S}=$ ?<br>$\\mathrm{AgBr}$ is $\\mathrm{AB}$ type salt.<br>$\\text { So, } \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}=\\mathrm{S}^{2} \\quad \\therefore \\mathrm{S}=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{S}=\\sqrt{49 \\times 10^{-14}}=7.0 \\times 10^{-7} \\mathrm{moldm}^{-3}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18177,'chemistry',48,758,1636,'2.2 g of acetaldehyde produced 13.95 kcal of enthalpy on combustion in $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$. Calculate the enthalpy of combustion of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CHO}$.','[{"text": "279 kcal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "972 kcal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "27.9 kcal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.79 kcal", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Enthalpy of combustion means heat released by the combustion of 1 mole substance.<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CHO}+3 \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{CO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>${\\rm{molar}}\\,{\\rm{mass}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{CHO}}\\,{\\rm{ = }}\\,{\\rm{44\\,g}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^ - }$<br>$2.2\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{CHO}}$ gives 13.95 kcal<br>44 g ____ ?<br>$ = \\frac{{44 \\times 13.95}}{{2.2}} = 279\\,\\,{\\rm{kcal}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18179,'chemistry',49,624,1266,'Which of the following is NOT a correct mathematical equation for Ostwald dilution law?','[{"text": "$ \\\\propto {\\\\rm{ = }}\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{{{\\\\rm{K}}_{\\\\rm{a}}}}}{{\\\\rm{c}}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{K = }}\\\\frac{{{ \\\\propto ^2}}}{{\\\\rm{V}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{K = }}{ \\\\propto ^2}{\\\\rm{c}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ \\\\propto {\\\\rm{ = }}\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{{{\\\\rm{K}}_{\\\\rm{a}}}}}{{\\\\rm{V}}}} $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\propto=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}} \\cdot V}, \\mathrm{~K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\mathrm{C} \\alpha^{2}, \\alpha=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{\\mathrm{C}}}$ are correct expressions.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18181,'chemistry',48,758,1635,'The equation,<br>$\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{HCl} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{298}$<br>$ =  - 22.060\\,\\,{\\rm{kcal}}$ means','[{"text": "The heat absorbed when one gram molecule of HCl is formed from its elements at $25^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$ is 22.060 kcal.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The heat given out when one gram molecule of HCl is formed from its elements at 298 K is 22.060 kcal.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The heat absorbed when one atom of hydrogen reacts with one atom of chlorine to form one molecule of HCl at $25^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$ and one atom of chlorine to form one molecule of HCl at $25^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$ and one atmosphere pressure is 22.060 kcal.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The heat absorbed when one gram equivalent of HCl is formed from its elements at 298 K is 22.060 kcal .", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\frac{1}{2}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}} + \\frac{1}{2}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\to {\\rm{HCl}},\\,\\,\\Delta {\\rm{H}} =  - 22.060{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{kcal}}$<br>Negative sign indicates the reaction is exothermic, So this equation indicates that 22060 cal or 22.06 kcal heat is given out on formation of 1 mole of HCl from its constituent elements.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18183,'chemistry',49,624,1266,'A weak monobasic acid is $10 \\%$ dissociated in $0.05 \\mathrm{M}$ solution. What is its percentage dissociation in $0.10 \\mathrm{M}$ solution?','[{"text": "$5.27 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.17 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10.3 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\propto_{1}=10 \\%=\\dfrac{10}{100}=0.1, \\mathrm{c}_{1}=0.05 \\mathrm{M}$<br>$\\mathrm{c}_{2}=0.10 \\mathrm{M}, \\propto_{2}=?$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\mathrm{C} \\alpha^{2}$ So, $\\mathrm{C}_{1} \\alpha_{1}^{2}=\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\alpha_{2}^{2}$<br>$\\therefore(0.1)^{2} \\times 0.05=\\propto_{2}^{2} \\times 0.10$<br>$\\therefore \\propto_{2}^{2}=\\dfrac{10^{-2} \\times 5 \\times 10^{-2}}{10^{-1}}=50 \\times 10^{-4}$<br>$\\therefore \\propto_{2}=\\sqrt{50 \\times 10^{-4}}=7.17 \\times 10^{-2}=7.17 \\%$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18184,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In ${\\text{TeO}}_{{\\text{3(aq) }}}^{{\\text{2 - }}} \\to {\\text{TeO}}_{{\\text{4(aq)}}}^{{\\text{2 - }}}$, the oxidation number of Te changes by ____ unit(s).','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The oxidation number of Te increases from +4 to +6 . Hence, oxidation number of Te changes by 2 units.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18185,'chemistry',48,748,1606,'Read Assertion and Reason carefully to  mark the correct option  given  below.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Enthalpy and entropy of any elementary substance in the standard state are taken as zero.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enthalpy and entropy are intensive properties.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Enthalpy is zero, but entropy is not zero, in the standard state. Enthalpy and entropy are intensive properties.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18187,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'Which among the following salt solution in water shows $\\mathrm{pH}$ less than 7 ?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COONH}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ is a salt of strong acid, $\\mathrm{HCl}$ and weak base, $\\mathrm{Cu}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$. Therefore, $\\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ salt solution is acidic and shows pH less than 7 . $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONH}_{4}$-almost neutral (WA-WB salt); $\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}$ - neutral ; (SA-SB salt)<br>$\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ (SB-WA salt)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18203,'chemistry',49,624,1266,'At $298 \\mathrm{~K}, 0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ solutin of acetic acid is $1.34 \\%$ ionized. What is the dissociation constant of acetic acid?','[{"text": "$1.4 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.8 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.34 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{c}=0.1 \\mathrm{M}, \\propto \\mathrm{c}=1.34 \\%=\\dfrac{1.34}{100}=1.34 \\times 10^{-2}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\propto^{2} \\mathrm{c} \\\\& =\\left(1.34 \\times 10^{-2}\\right)^{2} \\times 0.1 \\\\& =1.8 \\times 10^{-5}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18189,'chemistry',48,748,1608,'Match Column I with Column II and choose the correct combination from the options given. Thermodynamic changes in gases can be expressed graphically between changes in pressure and corresponding changes in volume. Column I gives the thermodynamic change while column II shows the P - V graphs marked in serial no. 1 to 4 .<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - 1,\\tB - 2,\\tC - 3,\\t\\tD - 4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - 4, \\tB - 3,\\tC - 2, \\t\\tD - 1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - 3,\\tB - 2,\\tC - 4, \\t\\tD - 1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - 1,\\tB - 4,\\tC - 2,\\t\\tD - 3", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Isothermal changes are changes in pressure and volume where temperature remains constant.<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{P} \\propto 1 / \\mathrm{V}$<br>The graph is a hyperbolic graph but the slope of adiabatic is steeper than slope of isotherm. Adiabatic changes are expressed by the equation $\\mathrm{PV}=$ constant<br>The graph is hyperbolic, but its slope is larger than slope of isothermal curve.<br>Isobaric changes are those changes in which pressure remains constant.<br>Isochoric changes are those changes in which volume remains constant.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/48_18189_question_1.png'),
(18191,'chemistry',49,632,1291,'The solubility of $\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is $2 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}$ at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$. What is it\'s solubility product?','[{"text": "$1.6 \\\\times 10^{-6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times 10^{-11}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times 10^{-11}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times 10^{-6}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{S}=2 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}, \\mathrm{~K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}=$ ?<br>$\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is $\\mathrm{A}_{2} \\mathrm{~B}$ type salt<br>So, $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}=4 \\mathrm{~s}^{3}$<br>$=4 \\times\\left(2 \\times 10^{-4}\\right)^{3}=3.2 \\times 10^{-11}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18193,'chemistry',48,761,1655,'What are the signs of the entropy change (+ or - ) in the following cases.<br>I: A liquid crystallizes into a solid<br>II: Temperature of a crystalline solid is raised from 0 K to 115 K<br>III. $2{\\rm{NaHC}}{{\\rm{O}}_3}(\\,{\\rm{s}}) \\to $<br>${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}({\\rm{s}}) + {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}({\\rm{g}}) + {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}({\\rm{g}})$<br>IV. $\\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{H}(\\mathrm{g})$','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{I}} - ,{\\\\rm{II + }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{,III + }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{,IV + }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{I}} - ,{\\\\rm{II}} - \\\\,{\\\\rm{,III}} - {\\\\rm{,IV + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{I}} - ,{\\\\rm{II}} - \\\\,{\\\\rm{,III}} - {\\\\rm{,IV + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{I + }},{\\\\rm{II}} - \\\\,{\\\\rm{,III}} - {\\\\rm{,IV}} - $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'(1) When liquid crystallizes into soild, its entropy decreases. $\\left(\\mathrm{S}_{\\text {gas }}>\\mathrm{S}_{\\text {liquid }}>\\mathrm{S}_{\\text {solid }}\\right)$<br>(2) If temperature increases, entropy increases.<br>(3) If gaseous moles increases after reaction, then entropy increases.<br>(4) $1 \\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule breaks into 2 gaseous H - atoms, so entropy increases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18195,'chemistry',49,624,1266,'A weak monobasic acid is $3.0 \\%$ dissociated in it\'s $0.04 \\mathrm{M}$ solution. What is the dissociaiton constant of acid?','[{"text": "$9 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.6 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\propto=3.0 \\%=\\dfrac{3}{100}=3 \\times 10^{-2}, \\mathrm{c}=0.04 \\mathrm{M}$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\propto^{2} \\mathrm{c}=\\left(3 \\times 10^{-2}\\right)^{2} \\times 0.04=3.6 \\times 10^{-5}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18196,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'In how many of the following, chlorine has positive oxidation number?<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCl}, \\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\mathrm{O}, \\mathrm{KCl}, \\mathrm{HOCl}, \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}, \\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}, \\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}, \\mathrm{ClO}_{2}}}$.','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Oxidation numbers of Cl in the following compounds are positive.<br>$\\begin{array}{lll}\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\Rightarrow+1 & ; \\quad \\mathrm{HOCl} \\Rightarrow+1 \\\\\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-} \\Rightarrow+7 & ; \\quad \\mathrm{ClO}_{2} \\Rightarrow+4\\end{array}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18198,'chemistry',48,758,1635,'The enthalpy change for which of the following processes represents the enthalpy of formation of AgCl ?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}(\\\\mathrm{aq})+\\\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}(\\\\mathrm{aq}) \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{AgCl}(\\\\mathrm{s})$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ag}(\\\\mathrm{s})+\\\\mathrm{AuCl}(\\\\mathrm{s}) \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{Au}(\\\\mathrm{s})+\\\\mathrm{AgCl}(\\\\mathrm{s})$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ag}(\\\\mathrm{s})+\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{AgCl}(\\\\mathrm{s})$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{AgCl}(\\\\mathrm{s}) \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{Ag}(\\\\mathrm{s})+\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g})$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Enthalpy of formation means the heat released or absorbed when 1 mole of compound is formed from its constituent elements. <br>$\\mathrm{Ag}(\\mathrm{s})+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{AgCl}(\\mathrm{s})$<br>It represents $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{\\mathrm{f}}$ of AgCl as 1 mole AgCl is formed from its components in stable elemental form.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18199,'chemistry',49,630,1283,'Which among the following salts undergoes hydrolysis?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{KCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}$ is a salt of weak base, $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{OH}$ and strong acid, $\\mathrm{HCl}$. Hence, it undergoes hydrolysis. While $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}, \\mathrm{KCl}$ and $\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}$ are salts of strong acids and strong bases and does not undergo hydrolysis.<br>Note: Salts of SA and SB do not undergo hydrolysis.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18202,'chemistry',48,748,1606,'Which of the following options consist of only intensive parameters?','[{"text": "pH of solution, temperature, and volume.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Specific heat capacity, molar internal energy, and E.M.F", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Resistance, molar mass, and vapour density", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Density, mass, and temperature.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Specific heat capacity, molar internal energy, and EMF are intensive properties.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18215,'chemistry',49,624,1266,'What is percent dissociation of acetic acid in it\'s $0.01 \\mathrm{M}$ solution if dissociation of acid is $1.34 \\times 10^{-2}$ ?','[{"text": "$13.4 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.02 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.68 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.34 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Percent dissociation $=\\propto \\times 100$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =1.34 \\times 10^{-2} \\times 100 \\\\& =1.34\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18206,'chemistry',48,758,1638,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Enthalpy of solution of a substance is the enthalpy change when one mole of it dissolves in a specified amount of solvent.</div><div class="column-right">The enthalpy of solution of $\\mathrm{AB}(\\mathrm{s})$ in water is determined by the selective values of the lattice enthalpy and enthalpy of hydration of ions.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18207,'chemistry',49,626,1271,'$\\mathrm{pH}$ of soft drink is 3.6. Calculate the concentration of hydrogen ions in it.','[{"text": "$2.51 \\\\times 10^{-4} \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.3 \\\\times 10^{-3} \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0 \\\\times 10^{-3} \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.81 \\\\times 10^{-4} \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{pH}=3.6,\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=$?<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{pH}=-\\log _{10}\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right] \\\\& \\therefore \\log _{10}\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=-\\mathrm{pH} \\\\& =-3.6 \\\\& =-3-0.6-1+1 \\\\& =-4+0.4=\\overline{4} .4\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\therefore\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=\\operatorname{Antilog}(\\overline{4} .4)=2.512 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{M}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18208,'chemistry',50,838,1872,'The oxidation number of $\\mathrm{Mn}$ in the product formed on strongly heating $\\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ is ____.','[{"text": "$ + 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ + 4$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$2 \\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7} \\xrightarrow{\\triangle} 4 \\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+3 \\mathrm{O}_{2}$<br>$\\therefore$ Oxidation number of $\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ is<br>$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x}+2 \\times(-2)=0$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x}=+4$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18210,'chemistry',48,757,1634,'Use the following data to calculate x and y in $\\mathrm{kcal} / \\mathrm{mol}$<br>(1)  $\\mathrm{Na}(\\mathrm{g}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Na}^{\\oplus}(\\mathrm{g})+\\mathrm{e}^{-} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=119.5$ $\\mathrm{kcal} / \\mathrm{mol}$<br>(2)  $\\frac{1}{2}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_2}(\\,{\\rm{g}}) \\to {\\rm{Cl}}({\\rm{g}});\\Delta {\\rm{H}} = {\\rm{x}}\\,\\,{\\rm{kcal}}/{\\rm{mol}}$<br>(3)  ${\\text{Na}}({\\text{s}}) \\to {\\text{Na}}({\\text{g}});\\Delta {\\text{H}} = {\\text{y}}\\,\\,{\\text{kcal}}/{\\text{mol}}$<br>(4)  $\\mathrm{Cl}(\\mathrm{g})+\\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}(\\mathrm{g}) ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=-87.3 \\mathrm{kcal} /$ mol<br>$(5)\\,\\,{\\rm{Na}}({\\rm{s}}) + \\frac{1}{2}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_2}(\\,{\\rm{g}}) \\to {\\rm{NaCl}}({\\rm{s}});$<br>$\\Delta {\\rm{H}} =  - 98.3\\,{\\rm{kcal}}/{\\rm{mol}}$<br>$(6)\\,\\,{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}^ \\oplus }({\\rm{g}}) + {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}^ - }({\\rm{g}}) \\to {\\rm{NaCl}}({\\rm{s}});$<br>$\\Delta {\\rm{H}} =  - 185.3\\,\\,{\\rm{kcal}}/{\\rm{mol}}$<br>(7)  $({\\rm{x}} - {\\rm{y}}) = 3\\,{\\rm{kcal}}/{\\rm{mol}}$','[{"text": "x = 30.3,   y = 27.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "x = 22.3,   y = 19.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "x = 43.2,   y = 40.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "x = 28.9,   y = 25.9", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From Hess\'s law of heat of summation,<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/48_18210_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{1}+\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{2}+\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{3}+\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{4}+\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{5}$<br>$ - 98.3 = {\\rm{y}} + 119.5 + {\\rm{x}} - 87.3 - 185.3$<br>${\\text{x}} + {\\text{y}} = 54.8\\,{\\text{kcal}}/{\\text{mol}}$ and ${\\rm{x}} - {\\rm{y}} = 3\\,\\,{\\rm{kcal}}/{\\rm{mole}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\text{x}} = 28.9\\,\\,{\\text{k}}{\\text{.}}\\,{\\text{cal}}/{\\text{mole}}$  and ${\\rm{y}} = 25.9\\,\\,{\\rm{kcal}}/{\\rm{mole}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18211,'chemistry',49,631,1284,'Identify the change in colour when $\\mathrm{NaCl}$ solution is titrated against $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ solution using fluorescein indicator.','[{"text": "Colourless to reddish pink", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pale yellow to reddish pink", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reddish pink to colourless", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Colourless to pale yellow", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{NaCl}$ is colourless and after equivalence point, the $\\mathrm{AgCl}$ particles adsorb $\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}$ions (present in slight excess) and then fluorescein anion. The precipitate particles turn reddish pink due to complex formation between $\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}$and fluorescein anion.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18214,'chemistry',48,751,1617,'The four curves $\\mathrm{A}_{1}, \\mathrm{~A}_{2}, \\mathrm{~A}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{A}_{4}$ are shown on $\\mathrm{p}-\\mathrm{V}$ diagram. Which of the curves represents adiabatic process?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'An adiabatic process $(\\mathrm{dq}=0)$ curve in a $\\mathrm{p}-\\mathrm{V}$ diagram approaches both $\\mathrm{V}$-axis and $\\mathrm{p}$-axis. It represents a steep hyperbola, i.e. $\\mathrm{A}_{3}$ in the given diagram.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/48_18214_question_1.png'),
(18218,'chemistry',49,624,1266,'The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of monoacidic weak base is 10.9 . Calculate the percent dissociation in $0.02 \\mathrm{M}$ solution.','[{"text": "$7.92 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.95 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.25 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.51 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{pOH}=14-\\mathrm{pH}=14-10.9=3.1$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{pOH}=-\\log _{10}\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right] \\\\& \\begin{aligned}\\therefore \\log _{10}\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right] & =-\\mathrm{pOH} \\\\& =-3.1 \\\\& =-3-0.1-1+1 \\\\& =\\overline{4}+0.9=\\overline{4} .9\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\therefore\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=\\operatorname{antilog}(\\overline{4} .9)=7.943 \\times 10^{-4}$<br>For monoacidic base,<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=\\propto \\mathrm{c}$<br>$\\therefore \\propto=\\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]}{\\mathrm{c}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{7.943 \\times 10^{-4}}{0.02}=3.97 \\times 10^{-2}$<br>Percent dissociation $=\\propto \\times 100$<br>$=3.97 \\times 10^{-2} \\times 100$<br>$=3.97$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18219,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The difference in oxidation state of $\\mathrm{Cl}$ in $\\mathrm{CaOCl}_{2}$ (bleaching powder) is ____.','[{"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-$1", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18219_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18220,'chemistry',48,748,1608,'A chemical process is carried out in a thermostat maintained at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, it is known as','[{"text": "isothermal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "isobaric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "adiabatic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "isotropic", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A chemical process is carried out in a thermostat maintained at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, i.e. at constant temperature so the process is isothermal.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18222,'chemistry',49,632,1291,'The solubility of sparingly soluble salt $\\mathrm{AB}_{2}$ is $1.0 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{dm}^{-3}$. What is it\'s solubility product?','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{-12}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times 10^{-8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times 10^{-12}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{-8}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{S}=1.0 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{moldm}^{-3}, \\mathrm{~K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}=$ ? for $\\mathrm{AB}_{2}$ type salts, $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}=4 \\mathrm{~s}^{3}$<br>$=4 \\times\\left(1.0 \\times 10^{-4}\\right)^{3}=4 \\times 10^{-12}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18224,'chemistry',48,748,1603,'If $\\mathrm{W}$ is the amount of work done by the system and $\\mathrm{q}$ is the amount of heat supplied to the system, identify the type of the system.','[{"text": "Isolated system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Closed system", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Open system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "System with thermally conducting walls", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When amount of heat (q) is given to the system, the system does work (W), i.e. utilises heat but does not exchange matter, thus system is identified as closed system.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18226,'chemistry',49,626,1271,'What is the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of  $0.005\\,\\,{\\text{M}}\\,\\,{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_4}$  solution?','[{"text": "5.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.0", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ is dibasic acid, so $0.005 \\mathrm{M}=0.01 \\mathrm{~N}$<br>$\\begin{gathered}=10^{-2} \\mathrm{~N} \\\\\\mathrm{pH}=-\\log _{10}\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right] \\\\=-\\log _{10}\\left[10^{-2}\\right]=2.0\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18228,'chemistry',48,751,1613,'If 1 mole of an ideal gas expands isothermally at $37^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ from $15 \\mathrm{~L}$ to $25 \\mathrm{~L}$, the maximum work obtained is','[{"text": "$12.87 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.43 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.57 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.92 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${{\\rm{W}}_{{\\rm{rev }}}} =  - 2.303\\,{\\rm{nRT}}\\,{\\rm{log}}\\,\\,\\frac{{{{\\rm{V}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{{{{\\rm{V}}_{\\rm{1}}}}}$<br>$=-2.303 \\times 1 \\times 0.0821 \\times(273+37) \\log (25 / 15)$<br>$=-2.303 \\times 0.0821 \\times 310 \\log \\dfrac{5}{3}$<br>$=-13.003 \\mathrm{~J}$<br>(-ve sign shows work of expansion)This value is closer to the value given in option (1).<br>$\\therefore$ Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18230,'chemistry',49,632,1290,'The solubility product expression for $\\mathrm{Ca}_{3}\\left(\\mathrm{PO}_{4}\\right)_{2}$ is represented as','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{\\\\mathrm{sp}}=\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\\\\right]^{3}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\\\\right]^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{\\\\mathrm{sp}}=\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\\\\right]^{2}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\\\\right]^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{\\\\mathrm{sp}}=\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\\\\right]\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{2-}\\\\right]^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{\\\\mathrm{sp}}=\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\\\\right]\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{2-}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{Ca}_{3}\\left(\\mathrm{PO}_{4}\\right)_{2} \\rightleftharpoons 3 \\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}+2 \\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{K}_{\\text {sp }}=\\left[\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\\right]^{3}\\left[\\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}\\right]^{2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18232,'chemistry',48,751,1613,'Calculate the work done when 1 mole of an ideal gas is compressed reversibly from 1 bar to 4 bar at a constant temperature of $300 \\mathrm{~K}$','[{"text": "$4.01 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-8.02 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18.02 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Work done $=-2.303 \\mathrm{nRT} \\log \\dfrac{\\mathrm{p}_{1}}{\\mathrm{P}_{2}}$<br>$=  - 2.303 \\times 8.314 \\times 300\\log \\frac{1}{4}$<br>$ =  - 3458.3\\,{\\rm{J}}\\,{\\rm{or}}\\,3.458\\,{\\rm{kJ}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18234,'chemistry',49,626,1271,'What is the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of millimolar solution of $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ ?','[{"text": "13", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "11", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'One millimolar solution of $\\mathrm{NaOH}=1 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$ of $\\mathrm{NaOH}$<br>$\\therefore\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=1 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~mole} / \\mathrm{L}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{pOH}=-\\log \\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]=-\\log \\left(1 \\times 10^{-3}\\right)=3$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{pH}=14-\\mathrm{pOH}=14-3=11$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18236,'chemistry',48,748,1605,'Which of the given properties depends on the manner in which a change is brought about?<br>I.$\\Delta \\mathrm{E} \\quad$   II. q   III. W','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\Delta \\mathrm{E}$ is a state variable, i.e. it is independent of the manner in which change is brought about while $\\mathrm{q}$ heat) and $\\mathrm{W}$ (work done) are path dependent, i.e. path functions.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18238,'chemistry',49,621,1250,'Which of the following reactions can be followed by its colour change?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_2(\\\\mathrm{~g})+1 / 2 \\\\mathrm{O}_2(\\\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\rightleftharpoons \\\\mathrm{SO}_3(\\\\mathrm{~g})$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}_2(\\\\mathrm{~g})+3 \\\\mathrm{H}_2(\\\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\rightleftharpoons 2 \\\\mathrm{NH}_3(\\\\mathrm{~g})$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{HI}(\\\\mathrm{g}) \\\\rightleftharpoons \\\\mathrm{H}_2(\\\\mathrm{~g})+\\\\mathrm{I}_2(\\\\mathrm{~g})$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}$ (graphite $)+\\\\mathrm{CO}_2(\\\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\rightleftharpoons 2 \\\\mathrm{CO}(\\\\mathrm{g})$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Vapours of iodine are in violet colour.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18240,'chemistry',48,751,1614,'The work done during the expansion of a gas from a volume of  ${\\rm{4}}\\,{\\rm{d}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,\\,{\\rm{6}}\\,{\\rm{d}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ against a constant external pressure of $3 \\mathrm{~atm}$ is','[{"text": "$-6 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-608 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$+304 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-304 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Work done $(\\mathrm{W})=-\\mathrm{p}_{\\mathrm{ext}}\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{2}-\\mathrm{V}_{1}\\right)$<br> $=  - 3 \\times (6 - 4) =  - 6\\,{\\rm{L}} - {\\rm{atm}}$<br>$ =  - 6 \\times 101.32\\,{\\rm{J}}$$(\\because {\\text{IL}} - {\\text{atm}} = 101.32\\,{\\text{J}})$<br>$ =  - 607.92 \\approx  - 608\\,{\\rm{J}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18242,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The oxidation number of $\\mathrm{Mn}$ in the product of alkaline oxidative fusion of $\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ is _____.','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/50_18242_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $ + 2 + {\\rm{x}} - 8 = 0$<br>${\\rm{x}} =  + 6$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18243,'chemistry',49,617,1239,'For reactions involving gaseous reactants and products, the equilibrium constant $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}$ is written in terms of','[{"text": "the pressure  of the gases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the molar volumes of the gases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the partial pressures of the gases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "the mole fraction of the gases", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For gas phase reactions, ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{P}}}$  is expressed in terms of partial pressures.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18246,'chemistry',49,617,1237,'The equilibrium constant for the reaction $\\mathrm{A} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{B}$, is $\\mathrm{K}$. The equilibrium constant for the reaction $\\mathrm{mA} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{mB}$, is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{mK}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K} / \\\\mathrm{m}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}^{\\\\mathrm{m}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}^{-\\\\mathrm{m}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For $\\mathrm{A} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{B} ; \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{B}]}{[\\mathrm{A}]}=\\mathrm{K}$ and for<br>$\\mathrm{mA} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{mB} ; \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{B}]^{\\mathrm{m}}}{[\\mathrm{A}]^{\\mathrm{m}}}=\\left(\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{B}]}{[\\mathrm{A}]}\\right)^{\\mathrm{m}}=\\mathrm{K}^{\\mathrm{m}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18248,'chemistry',48,751,1616,'In adiabatic conditions, 2 moles of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$ is expanded such that its volume becomes 27 times. Then, the work done is $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{v}}=6 \\mathrm{calmol}^{-1}\\right.$ and $\\left.\\gamma=1.33\\right)$','[{"text": "$1400 \\\\mathrm{cal}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1000 \\\\mathrm{cal}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$900 \\\\mathrm{cal}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1200 \\\\mathrm{cal}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, $T_{1}=300 \\mathrm{~K}, \\mathrm{~V}_{2}=27 \\mathrm{~V}_{1}$<br>$\\mathrm{n}=1 \\mathrm{~mol}, \\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{v}}=6 \\mathrm{calmol}^{-1}, \\gamma=1.33$<br>In adiabatic conditions, $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{T}_{2}}{\\mathrm{~T}_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{V}_{1}}{\\mathrm{~V}_{2}}\\right)^{\\gamma-1}$<br>${\\rm{ or, }}\\frac{{{{\\rm{T}}_2}}}{{\\,{{\\rm{T}}_1}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{{27}}} \\right)^{1.33 - 1}}$<br>$ = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{{27}}} \\right)^{0.33}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{{27}}} \\right)^{1/3}} = \\frac{1}{3}{\\rm{ }}$<br>${\\rm{or, }}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {{\\rm{T}}_2} = 300 \\times \\frac{1}{3} = 100\\,{\\rm{K}}$<br>Thus, $\\mathrm{T}_{2} < \\mathrm{T}_{1}$ hence, cooling takes place due to expansion under adiabatic condition.<br> $\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=\\mathrm{q}+\\mathrm{W}$<br>$\\mathrm{q}=0$ for adiabatic change<br>$\\Delta U=-$ ve because gas expands.<br> Then, $\\mathrm{W}=-\\Delta \\mathrm{U}$<br>$ =  - {{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{V}}}\\left( {{{\\rm{T}}_2} - {{\\rm{T}}_1}} \\right)$<br>$ =  - 6(100 - 300) = 1200\\,{\\rm{cal}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18250,'chemistry',49,617,1241,'In a chemical equilibrium $\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{C}+\\mathrm{D}$, when one mole each of the two reactants are mixed, 0.6 mole each of the prodcuts are formed. The equilibrium constant is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.30", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.25", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 / 9$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{C}+\\mathrm{D}$<br>$\\mathrm{t}=0$<br>$\\begin{array}{llll}1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{array}$<br>at equilibrium $(1-0.6)(1-0.6) \\quad 0.6 \\quad 0.6$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{C}][\\mathrm{D}]}{[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}]}=\\dfrac{0.6 \\times 0.6}{0.4 \\times 0.4}$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}=2.25$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18252,'chemistry',48,750,1611,'One mole of an ideal gas at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$ is expanded isothermally from initial volume of $1 \\mathrm{~L}$ to $10 \\mathrm{~L}$. The $\\Delta \\mathrm{E}$ for this process is $\\left( {{\\rm{R}} = 2} \\right.{\\rm{cal}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}\\,{{\\rm{K}}^{ - 1}})$','[{"text": "$163.7 \\\\mathrm{cal}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$138.1 \\\\mathrm{cal}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\mathrm{~L} \\\\mathrm{~atm}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From first law of thermodynamics,<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{E}=\\mathrm{q}+\\mathrm{W}$<br>For isothermal process, $\\Delta \\mathrm{T}=0$<br>Hence, $\\Delta \\mathrm{E}=0$<br>This means that for all isothermal process of an ideal gas, the value of $\\Delta \\mathrm{E}=0$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18254,'chemistry',50,837,1867,'The oxidation number of $\\mathrm{Pt}$ in $\\mathrm{K}\\left[\\mathrm{PtNH}_{3} \\mathrm{Cl}_{5}\\right]$ will be ____.','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\text{K}}\\left[ {{\\text{PtN}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{5}}}} \\right]$<br>$ + 1 + {\\text{x}} + 0 - 5 = 0$<br>${\\text{x}} =  + \\,4.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18255,'chemistry',49,617,1240,'For the equilibrium<br>$2 \\mathrm{NO}_{(\\mathrm{g})}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NOCl}_{(\\mathrm{g})}$<br>$\\mathrm{Kp}$ is related to $\\mathrm{Kc}$ as','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{\\\\mathrm{p}}=\\\\mathrm{K}_{\\\\mathrm{c}}(\\\\mathrm{RT})$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{\\\\mathrm{p}}=\\\\mathrm{K}_{\\\\mathrm{c}}(\\\\mathrm{RT})^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{K}}_{\\\\rm{p}}}{\\\\rm{ = }}\\\\frac{{{{\\\\rm{K}}_{\\\\rm{c}}}}}{{{\\\\rm{RT}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{\\\\mathrm{p}}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{K}_{\\\\mathrm{c}}}{(\\\\mathrm{RT})^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{(\\mathrm{g})}=2-3=1$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{P}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}(\\mathrm{RT})^{\\Delta \\mathrm{n}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}(\\mathrm{RT})^{-1}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}}}{\\mathrm{RT}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18256,'chemistry',48,759,1649,'According to second law of thermodynamics, a process (reaction) is spontaneous, if during the process','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}_{\\\\text {universe }}>0$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}_{\\\\text {universe }}=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\text {system }}>0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}_{\\\\text {universe }}=\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}_{\\\\text {system }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a spontaneous (naturally occurring) process takes place, it is accompanied by increase in entropy,<br>i.e.$\\Delta {{\\rm{S}}_{{\\rm{universe }}}} = \\Delta {{\\rm{S}}_{{\\rm{system }}}} + \\Delta {{\\rm{S}}_{{\\rm{surroundings }}}} > 0$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18258,'chemistry',49,618,1244,'For the reaction<br>$2 \\mathrm{~A}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\mathrm{B}(\\mathrm{g}) \\rightleftharpoons 3 \\mathrm{C}(\\mathrm{g})+4 \\mathrm{D}(\\mathrm{g})$<br>Two moles each of A and B were taken into a $1 \\mathrm{~L}$ flask. The following must always be true when the system attains equilibrium','[{"text": "$[\\\\mathrm{A}]=[\\\\mathrm{B}]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$[\\\\mathrm{A}] < [\\\\mathrm{B}]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$[\\\\mathrm{B}]=[\\\\mathrm{C}]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$[\\\\mathrm{A}]+[\\\\mathrm{B}] < [\\\\mathrm{C}]+[\\\\mathrm{D}]$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$2 \\mathrm{~A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightleftharpoons 3 \\mathrm{C}+4 \\mathrm{D}$<br>Initialmoles 22<br>At equm ${\\text{(2  -  2x) (2  -  x)}}\\,\\,{\\text{3x}}\\,\\,{\\text{4x}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\text{(2 -  x )  >  ( 2  -  2 x)}}$ means  ${\\text{[B]  >  [A]}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18260,'chemistry',48,758,1646,'Given that, for the reaction, $\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{HCl}$<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{(HCl}}) =  - 93\\,{\\rm{kJ}}/{\\rm{mol}}\\\\\n{\\rm{BE}}\\left( {{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right) = 434\\,{\\rm{kJ}}/{\\rm{mol}}\\\\\n{\\rm{BE}}\\left( {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right) = 242\\,{\\rm{kJ}}/{\\rm{mol}}\n\\end{array}$<br>The bond dissociation energy of $\\mathrm{HCl}$ is.','[{"text": "$232 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} / \\\\mathrm{mol}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$331 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} / \\\\mathrm{mol}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$431 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} / \\\\mathrm{mol}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$530 \\\\mathrm{~kJ} / \\\\mathrm{mol}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given,$\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}} + \\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\to {\\rm{HCl}}$<br>${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{f}}} = {\\rm{ - 93\\,kJ}}/{\\rm{mol}}$<br>$\\because$ In this reaction, one mole of $\\mathrm{HCl}$ is formed$\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{\\mathrm{f}}$ of the reaction will remain same,$\\text { i.e. }-93 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$<br>Let the bond dissociation energy of $\\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{Cl}$ bond be $\\mathrm{x}$.<br>For the given reaction,<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{\\mathrm{f}}=\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {(reacanants) }}-\\Sigma \\mathrm{B} \\cdot \\mathrm{E}_{\\text {(products) }}$<br>$ - 93 = \\frac{1}{2}(434) + \\frac{1}{2}(242) - x$<br>$ - 93 =  + 338 - x$<br>$ = 431\\,{\\rm{kJ}}/{\\rm{mol}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18262,'chemistry',49,617,1241,'The dissociation equilibrium of a gas $\\mathrm{AB}_{2}$ can be represented as<br>$2 \\mathrm{AB}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{AB}_{(\\mathrm{g})}+\\mathrm{B}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>The degree of dissociation is "X" and is small compared to 1. The expression relating the degree of dissociation (X) with equilibrium constant ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{P}}}$ and total pressure "P" is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{2}}{{\\\\rm{K}}_{\\\\rm{P}}}}}{{\\\\rm{P}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{2}}{{\\\\rm{K}}_{\\\\rm{P}}}}}{{\\\\rm{P}}}} \\\\right)^{\\\\frac{{\\\\rm{1}}}{{\\\\rm{3}}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{2}}{{\\\\rm{K}}_{\\\\rm{P}}}}}{{\\\\rm{P}}}} \\\\right)^{\\\\frac{{\\\\rm{1}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\frac{{{{\\\\rm{K}}_{\\\\rm{P}}}}}{{\\\\rm{P}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$2 \\mathrm{AB}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{AB}_{(\\mathrm{g})}+\\mathrm{B}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>$\\begin{array}{llll}\\mathrm{t}=0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\\\text { At equilibrium } & 2(1-\\mathrm{x}) & 2 \\mathrm{x} & \\mathrm{x}\\end{array}$<br>Total no. of moles at equilibrium $=$<br>$2-2 \\mathrm{x}+2 \\mathrm{x}+\\mathrm{x}=2+\\mathrm{x}$So; $\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{AB}_{2}} \\dfrac{2(1-\\mathrm{x}) \\mathrm{P}}{2+\\mathrm{x}}, \\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{AB}}=\\dfrac{2 \\mathrm{xP}}{2+\\mathrm{x}}, \\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{B}_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{xP}}{2+\\mathrm{x}}$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{AB}}\\right)^{2}\\left(\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{B}_{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{AB}_{2}}\\right)^{2}}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2 \\mathrm{xP}}{2+\\mathrm{x}}\\right)^{2}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{xP}}{2+\\mathrm{x}}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2(1-\\mathrm{x})}{2+\\mathrm{x}} \\mathrm{P}\\right)^{2}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{4 x^{3} P}{4(2+x)(1-x)^{2}}$<br>${\\text{ = }}\\frac{{{{\\text{x}}^{\\text{3}}}{\\text{P}}}}{{{\\text{(2  +  x)(1  -  x}}{{\\text{)}}^{\\text{2}}}}}$<br>$[\\therefore \\mathrm{x} <  <  < 1$ and 2 , So: $(1+\\mathrm{x}) \\approx 1,(2+\\mathrm{x}) \\approx 2]$<br>${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{p}}}{\\text{ = }}\\frac{{{{\\text{x}}^{\\text{3}}}{\\text{P}}}}{{\\text{2}}}\\,\\,{\\text{x = }}{\\left( {\\frac{{{\\text{2}}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{p}}}}}{{\\text{P}}}} \\right)^{\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{3}}}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18288,'chemistry',48,758,1637,'The heats of neutralisation of four acids $\\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{B}, \\mathrm{C}$ and $\\mathrm{D}$ when neutralised against a common base are $13.7,9.4,11.2$ and 124kcal respectively. The weakest among these acids is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{D}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In case of weak acids, some of the heat evolved during neutralisation reaction is utilised to completely ionise the weak acid. Thus, less heat is evolved. Hence, lower the value of heat of neutralisation, weaker is the acid. Thus, B is the weakest acid.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18264,'chemistry',48,758,1636,'The energy released when 6 moles of octane is burnt in air will be [Given, $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{\\mathrm{f}}$ for $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}), \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{g})$ and $\\mathrm{C}_{8} \\mathrm{H}_{18}$ (l) respectively are $-490,-240$ and $-160 \\mathrm{~J} / \\mathrm{mol}$ ]','[{"text": "$-37.4 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-20 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-6.2 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-35.5 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{C}} + {{\\rm{O}}_2} \\to {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_2};{\\rm{\\Delta H}} =  - 490\\,{\\rm{J}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(1)}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{H}}_2} + \\frac{1}{2}{{\\rm{O}}_2} \\to {{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}};{\\rm{\\Delta H}} =  - 240\\,{\\rm{J}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(2)}}\\\\\n8{\\rm{C}} + 9{{\\rm{H}}_2} \\to {{\\rm{C}}_8}{{\\rm{H}}_{18}};{\\rm{\\Delta H}} =  - 160{\\rm{\\,J}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)\n\\end{array}$<br>On applying, $8 \\times$ Eq. (1) $+9 \\times$ Eq. (2) -Eq. (3), we get<br>${{\\rm{C}}_8}{{\\rm{H}}_{18}} + \\frac{{25}}{2}{{\\rm{O}}_2} \\to 8{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_2} + 9{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}$<br>$\\Delta {\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{R}}^0 = [8 \\times ( - 490)] + [9 \\times ( - 240)] + 160$<br>$ =  - 5920{\\rm{\\,J\\,mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$<br>Hence, energy released when 6 moles of octane is burnt in air is$=-5920 \\times 6=-35520 \\mathrm{~J}=-35.5 \\mathrm{~kJ}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18266,'chemistry',49,621,1251,'In which of the equilibria, the position of the equilibrium shifts towards products, if the total pressure is increased','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g})+3 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\rightleftharpoons 2 \\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}(\\\\mathrm{~g})$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{I}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g})+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\rightleftharpoons 2 \\\\mathrm{HI}(\\\\mathrm{g})$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g})+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\rightleftharpoons 2 \\\\mathrm{NO}(\\\\mathrm{g})$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}(\\\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\rightleftharpoons 2 \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g})$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Increase in pressure favours less moles side.<br>${\\rm{i}}{\\rm{.e}}{\\rm{.,}}\\,\\,\\Delta {\\rm{n}} =  - {\\rm{ve.}}$<br>$(1)\\,\\,{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ +  3}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}\\,;\\,\\,\\Delta {\\text{n = }} - 2$<br>${\\text{(2)}}\\,\\,{{\\text{I}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{  +  }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2HI }}\\,{\\text{; }}\\Delta {\\text{n  =  0}}$<br>${\\text{(3)}}\\,\\,{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ +  }}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2NO}}\\,{\\text{;}}\\Delta {\\text{n  =  0}}$<br>${\\text{(4)}}\\,\\,{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}\\,{\\text{;}}\\,\\Delta {\\text{n  =   + 1.}}$<br>Hence, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18268,'chemistry',48,758,1636,'Enthalpy of combustion of carbon to $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is $-393.5 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$. What amount of heat will be released upon formation of $35.2 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from carbon and oxygen gas?','[{"text": "$214.8 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-314.8 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$414.8 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$514.8 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{\\text {combustion }}=-393.5 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$<br>molar mass of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}=44 \\mathrm{~g}$<br>i.e. 1 mole of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ on combustion releases$=-393.5 \\mathrm{~kJ}$<br>$\\dfrac{35.2}{44}$ mole of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ will release<br>$=-\\dfrac{393.5 \\times 35.2}{44}=314.8 \\mathrm{KJ}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18270,'chemistry',49,621,1250,'When $\\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}$ is heated in a closed vessel, oxygen is liberated and $\\mathrm{NaNO}_{2}$ is left behind at equilibrium','[{"text": "Addition of $\\\\mathrm{NaNO}_{2}$ favours forward reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Addition of $\\\\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}$ favours forward reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase in temperature favours forward reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both addition of $\\\\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}$ and increase in temperature favour forward reaction", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Decomposition of  $\\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}$  is endothermic reaction . Endothermic reaction is favoured by high temperature. Addition of solid compounds have no effect on equilibrium.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18278,'chemistry',49,618,1244,'When the following reaction reaches equilibrium,<br>$\\mathrm{N}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{4}(\\mathrm{~g}) ;$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}=7.4 \\times 10^{-26}$<br>the equilibrium mixture contains','[{"text": "mostly products", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "mostly reactants", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "it can not be predicted", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "some times reactants and sometimes products", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}=7.4 \\times 10^{-26}=\\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{4}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]^{2}}$,<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}$ value is too low which indicates mostly reactants are present.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18272,'chemistry',48,758,1636,'A person inhales $640 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ per day. If all $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is used for converting sugar into $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$, how much sucrose $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{12} \\mathrm{H}_{22} \\mathrm{O}_{11}\\right)$ is consumed in the body in one day and what is the heat evolved? [$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ for combustion of sucrose $\\left.=-5645 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right]$.','[{"text": "$530 \\\\mathrm{~g}, 9403.34 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$570 \\\\mathrm{~g}, 9408.33 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$500 \\\\mathrm{~g}, 9402.27 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1140 \\\\mathrm{~g},-18816 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{C}_{12} \\mathrm{H}_{22} \\mathrm{O}_{11}+12 \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow 12 \\mathrm{CO}_{2}+11 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>12 mole $(12 \\times 32 \\mathrm{~g})=384 \\mathrm{~g}$ of oxygen requires1 mole of sucrose $342 \\mathrm{~g}$<br>Hence, $640 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ requires<br>$\\left(\\dfrac{342}{384} \\times 640 \\mathrm{~g}\\right) 570 \\mathrm{~g}$ of sucrose.<br>$640 \\mathrm{~g} \\,\\mathrm{O}_{2}=20$ mole $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ will require $\\dfrac{20}{12}$ mole sucrose.<br>$\\Delta {\\rm{H}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{evolved}} =  - 5645 \\times \\frac{{20}}{{12}}$<br>$=-9408.33 \\mathrm{KJ}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18274,'chemistry',49,617,1239,'For the homogeneous reaction<br>$4 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g})+5 \\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons$<br>$4 \\mathrm{NO}(\\mathrm{g})+6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{g}) \\text {, }$<br>the equilibrium constant $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}$ has the units of','[{"text": "$(\\\\text { Conc })^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conc", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$(\\\\text { Conc })^{-10}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is dimensionless", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{(\\mathrm{g})}=(4+6)-(4+5)=1$,<br>So, $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{C}} \\Rightarrow(\\text { conc })^{1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18275,'chemistry',50,845,1886,'100 mL of ozone at STP were passed through 100 mL of 10 volume ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$ solution. What is the volume strength of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$ after the reaction?','[{"text": "5.6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "11.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9.5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.8", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\quad \\quad {{\\rm{O}}_3} \\to \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{\\rm{O}}_2} +\\,\\,\\,\\, {\\rm{O}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(1)}}\\\\\n\\quad \\quad {{\\rm{H}}_2}{{\\rm{O}}_2} \\to {{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}} + {\\rm{O}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)\\\\\n\\,\\,{\\rm{O}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, + \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{O}}\\,\\,\\, \\to \\,\\,\\,\\,{{\\rm{O}}_2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)\\\\\n1/2{\\rm{vol}}\\,\\,\\,\\quad 1/2\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{vol}}\\quad \\,\\,\\,1{\\rm{vol}}\n\\end{array}$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2), <br>we infer that ${\\mathrm{100 \\mathrm{~mL}_{\\mathrm{m}}}}$ of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{3}}}$ at STP will produce 100 mL of molecular ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$ as such and 100 mL of oxygen molecule after reaction with ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$.<br>This new volume of 100 mL of molecular oxygen after reaction with ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$ is contributed equally by ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{3}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$. Thus, 50 mL of oxygen have been contributed by ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$.<br>Again, we know <br>Volume of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\times}}$ Volume strength of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$<br>$=\\text { Volume of } \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\text { at STP }$<br>${\\mathrm{\\therefore 100 \\mathrm{~mL}}}$ of ${\\rm{\'10}}\\,{\\rm{V}}\\,{\\rm{\'}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\equiv {\\rm{1000}}\\,{\\rm{mL}}$ of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$ at STP After utilisation of 50 mL of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$, according to Eq. (iii), the balance ${\\mathrm{(1000-50)=950 \\mathrm{~mL}}}$ of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$ at STP are still retainable by 100 mL of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$.<br>Hence volume strength of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$ after reaction$=\\dfrac{\\text { Volume of } \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\text { at STP }}{\\text { Volume of } \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}=\\dfrac{950}{100}=9.5 \\mathrm{~V}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\therefore}}$ Volume strength ${\\mathrm{=9.5}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18276,'chemistry',48,758,1638,'The enthalpy of dissolution of $\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}(\\mathrm{~s})$ and $\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2} \\cdot 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{s})$ are 20.6 and $8.8 \\mathrm{~kJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$ respectively. The enthalpy of hydration for $\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}(\\mathrm{~s})+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BaCl}_{2} \\cdot 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{s})$ is.','[{"text": "$29.4 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-29.4 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$-11.8 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$38.2 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}$ (s) + (aq) $\\rightarrow \\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}$ (aq);<br>${\\rm{\\Delta H}} =  - 20.6{\\rm{\\,kJ}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2} \\cdot 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{s})+(\\mathrm{aq}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}(\\mathrm{aq}) ;$<br>${\\rm{\\Delta H}} =  + 8.8\\,{\\rm{kJ}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Eq. (1) can be split into two steps as,<br>$\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}(\\mathrm{~s})+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{l}) \\rightarrow$$\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2} \\cdot 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{s}) ;\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{1}$<br>$\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2} \\cdot 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(\\mathrm{s})+(\\mathrm{aq}) \\rightarrow$$\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}(\\mathrm{aq}) ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{2}$<br>$\\Delta {{\\rm{H}}_1} + \\Delta {{\\rm{H}}_2} =  - 20.6$<br>$\\Delta {{\\rm{H}}_1} + 8.8 =  - 20.6$<br>$\\Delta {{\\rm{H}}_1} =  - 29.4\\,{\\rm{kJ}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18280,'chemistry',48,757,1634,'Given,<br>${{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_3}(\\,{\\rm{g}}) + 3{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_2}(\\,{\\rm{g}})}$$ \\rightleftharpoons $${{\\rm{NC}}{{\\rm{l}}_3}(\\,{\\rm{g}}) + 3{\\rm{HCl}}({\\rm{g}});}$<br>${ - {\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_1}}$<br>${{{\\rm{N}}_2}({\\rm{g}}) + 3{{\\rm{H}}_2}({\\rm{g}})}$$ \\rightleftharpoons $${2{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_3}({\\rm{g}});}$<br>${ - {\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_2}}$<br>${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g)}} + {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g)}}$$ \\rightleftharpoons $$2{\\rm{HCl(g)}};$<br>${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$<br>The heat of formation of $\\mathrm{NCl}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g})$ <br>in terms of $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{1}, \\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}_{3}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{f}}=-\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{1}+\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}}{2}-\\\\dfrac{3}{2} \\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{f}}=\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{1}+\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}}{2}-\\\\dfrac{3}{2} \\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{f}}=\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{1}+\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}}{2}+\\\\dfrac{3}{2} \\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{I}}.{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_3}(\\,{\\rm{g}}) + 3{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_2}({\\rm{g}})$$ \\rightleftharpoons $${\\rm{NC}}{{\\rm{l}}_3}(\\,{\\rm{g}}) + 3{\\rm{HCl}}({\\rm{g}}),$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, - {\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_1}(1)$<br>${\\rm{II}}.{{\\rm{N}}_2}({\\rm{g}}) + 3{{\\rm{H}}_2}({\\rm{g}})$$ \\rightleftharpoons $$2{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_3}({\\rm{g}}),$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, - {\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_2}(2){\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} $<br>${\\rm{III}}.{{\\rm{H}}_2}({\\rm{g}}) + {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_2}({\\rm{g}})$$ \\rightleftharpoons $$2{\\rm{HCl}}({\\rm{g}}),{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_3}{\\mkern 1mu} (3){\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} $<br>${\\rm{On}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{multiplying}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{Eq}}{\\rm{.}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{(3)by}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{3,we}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{get}}$<br>$3{{\\rm{H}}_2}(\\,{\\rm{g}}) + 3{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_2}(\\,{\\rm{g}})$$ \\rightleftharpoons $$6{\\rm{HCl}}({\\rm{g}});{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} $<br>${\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3 \\times \\Delta {{\\rm{H}}_3}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} (4)$<br>${\\rm{On}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{inverting}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{Eq}}{\\rm{.(4),we}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{get}}$<br>${6{\\rm{HCl}}({\\rm{g}})}$$ \\rightleftharpoons $${3{{\\rm{H}}_2}(\\,{\\rm{g}}) + 3{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_2}(\\,{\\rm{g}})}$<br>${{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, - 3 \\times \\Delta {{\\rm{H}}_3}(5)}$<br>${\\rm{On}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{Multiplying}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{Eq}}{\\rm{.(1)}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{with}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{(2),we}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{get}}$<br>$2{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_3} + 6{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_2}$$ \\rightleftharpoons $$2{\\rm{NC}}{{\\rm{l}}_3} + 6{\\rm{HCl}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, - 2 \\times {\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_1}(6)$<br>${\\rm{On}}\\,{\\rm{adding}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Eq}}.({\\rm{2}}),({\\rm{5}}){\\rm{and}}({\\rm{6}}),{\\rm{we}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{get}}$<br>${{\\rm{N}}_2} + 3{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_2}$$ \\rightleftharpoons $$2{\\rm{NC}}{{\\rm{l}}_3};$<br>${{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{r}}} =  - {\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_2} - 3 \\times {\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_3} - 2 \\times {\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_1}}$<br>${\\therefore {\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{f}}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{r}}}}}{{\\rm{2}}} =  - \\frac{{{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{{\\rm{2}}} - \\frac{3}{2}{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_3} - {\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_1}}$<br>${ =  - {\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_1} - \\frac{{{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{{\\rm{2}}} - \\frac{3}{2}{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_3}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18283,'chemistry',49,618,1244,'In which of the following reactions, the equilibrium mixture contains an appreciable concentration of both reactants and products.','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\rightleftharpoons 2 \\\\mathrm{Cl}(\\\\mathrm{g}), \\\\mathrm{K}_{\\\\mathrm{c}}=6.4 \\\\times 10^{-39}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g})+2 \\\\mathrm{NO}(\\\\mathrm{g}) \\\\rightleftharpoons 2 \\\\mathrm{NOCl}(\\\\mathrm{g})$;<br>$\\\\mathrm{K}_{\\\\mathrm{c}}=3.7 \\\\times 10^{8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g})+2 \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g}) \\\\rightleftharpoons 2 \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}(\\\\mathrm{g})$;<br>$\\\\mathrm{K}_{\\\\mathrm{c}}=1.8$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{~g})+\\\\mathrm{S}(\\\\mathrm{s}) \\\\rightleftharpoons \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}(\\\\mathrm{~g}) ; \\\\mathrm{K}_{\\\\mathrm{c}}=7.8 \\\\times 10^{5}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Compare the values of ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{C}}}$. Too high or too low value(s) of $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}$ will contain mostly products or reactants respectively. Therefore the value of $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}$ as 1.8 will contain appreciable concentration of both reactants and products. $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}$ value $10^{-3}$ to $10^{3}$ indicates appreciable conc. of both reactants and products.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(18284,'chemistry',48,753,1625,'The heat liberated when $1.89 \\mathrm{~g}$ of benzoic acid is burnt in a bomb calorimeter at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ increases the temperature of $18.94 \\mathrm{~kg}$ of water by $0.632^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ If the specific heat of water at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ is $0.998 \\mathrm{cal} / \\mathrm{g}$-deg, the value of the heat of combustion of benzoic acid is','[{"text": "$881.1 \\\\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$771.2 \\\\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$981.1 \\\\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$852.7 \\\\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Heat capacity, ${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{V}}} = 18.94 \\times 0.998 \\times {10^3}$<br>$ = 18.902 \\times {10^3}{\\rm{cal}}/{\\rm{deg}}$<br>Moles of benzoic acid $=\\dfrac{1.89}{122}$<br>$\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{p}}=\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{v}}+\\mathrm{R}=18.902 \\times 10^{3}+2$<br>$ = 18904\\,{\\rm{cal/deg}}$<br>${\\rm{Heat}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{combustion}} = \\frac{{{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{p}}}{\\rm{ \\times \\Delta t}}}}{{{\\rm{moles}}}}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\,$<br>$\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt}  = \\frac{{18904 \\times 0.632 \\times 122}}{{1.89}}$<br>$ = \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} 771.203 \\times {10^3}{\\rm{cal}} = 771.2{\\rm{kcal}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18287,'chemistry',49,621,1253,'Consider the reactions<br>i) $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{PCl}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$<br>ii) $\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$<br>The addition of an inert gas at constant volume','[{"text": "will increase the dissociation of $\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ as well as $\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "will reduce the dissociation of $\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ as well as $\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "will increase the dissociation of $\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ and step up the formation of $\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "will not disturb the equilibrium of the reactions", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Addition of inert gas at constant volume has no effect on equilibrium state.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18290,'chemistry',50,841,1880,'The  volume of HCl of specific gravity ${\\mathrm{1.2 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mL}^{-1}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{4 \\%}}$ nature by weight, needed to produce 1.78 L of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}}$ at STP. The reaction involved is:<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+4 \\mathrm{HCl} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}}$','[{"text": "0.24L", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "24L", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.4mL", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.24mL", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{{\\rm{MnO2}}}&{{\\rm{ + 4HCl}}}&{ \\to {\\rm{MnC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}}&{{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O + C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{moles}}:0.08}&{4 \\times 0.8}&{}&{\\frac{{1.78}}{{22.4}}}\\\\\n{}&{ = 0.32}&{}&{ = 0.08}\\\\\n{}&{}&{}&{}\n\\end{array}$<br>${ \\Rightarrow {{\\rm{W}}_{{\\rm{Mn}}{{\\rm{O}}_2}}} = 0.08 \\times 87\\,{\\rm{g}} = 6.96\\,{\\rm{g}}}$<br>${ \\Rightarrow {{\\rm{W}}_{{\\rm{Mn}}{{\\rm{O}}_2}}} = 0.08 \\times 87\\,{\\rm{g}} = 6.96\\,{\\rm{g}}}$<br>${\\left( {\\frac{{10 \\times 4 \\times 1.2}}{{36.5}}} \\right) \\times {{\\rm{V}}_{\\rm{L}}} = 0.32 \\Rightarrow {{\\rm{V}}_{\\rm{L}}} = 0.24\\,{\\rm{L}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18291,'chemistry',49,621,1255,'For the reaction<br>$\\mathrm{N}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NO}(\\mathrm{g}) ;$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=180 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$. Which of the following statement is not true?','[{"text": "Change in pressure at constant volume does not effect the equilibrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{n}_{(\\\\mathrm{g})}=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The formation of NO is increased at higher temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The formation of NO is decreased at higher temperature.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Formation of NO is endothermic reaction. Hence high temperature favours.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18292,'chemistry',48,758,1635,'Thermodynamically, the most stable form of carbon is','[{"text": "diamond", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "graphite", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "fullerenes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "coal", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Thermodynamically, the most stable form of carbon is graphite.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18295,'chemistry',49,621,1253,'Inert gas has been added to the following equilibrium system at constant volume<br>$\\mathrm{SO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+1 / 2 \\mathrm{O}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{SO}_{3(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>In which direction, the equilibrium will shift?','[{"text": "Forward", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Backward", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No effect", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Unpredictable", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{P}}}$ will remain same as inert gas is added at constant volume.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18296,'chemistry',48,750,1611,'For the reaction of one mole of zinc dust with one mole of sulphuric acid in a bomb calorimeter, $\\Delta \\mathrm{E}$ and $\\mathrm{W}$ corresponds to','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{E} < 0, \\\\mathrm{~W}=0$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{E}=0, \\\\mathrm{~W} < 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{E}>0, \\\\mathrm{~W}=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{E}=0, \\\\mathrm{~W}>0$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Bomb calorimeter is commonly used to find the heat of combustion of organic substances which consist of a sealed combustion chamber called a bomb. If a process is run in a sealed container then no expansion or compression is allowed, so, $\\mathrm{W}=0$ and $\\Delta \\mathrm{E}=\\mathrm{q}$. Thus, it has $\\Delta \\mathrm{E} < 0, \\mathrm{~W}=0$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18299,'chemistry',49,621,1251,'The reaction favored at low pressure is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})}+\\\\mathrm{I}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})} \\\\rightleftharpoons 2 \\\\mathrm{HI}_{(\\\\mathrm{g})}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{5(\\\\mathrm{~g})} \\\\rightleftharpoons \\\\mathrm{PCl}_{3(\\\\mathrm{~g})}+\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})} \\\\rightleftharpoons 2 \\\\mathrm{NO}_{(\\\\mathrm{g})}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})}+3 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})} \\\\rightleftharpoons 2 \\\\mathrm{NH}_{3(\\\\mathrm{~g})}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Low pressure favours high moles side.<br>$\\mathrm{PCl}_{5(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{PCl}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>Here, $\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{p}}-\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{R}}=2-1=1$. It is favored by low pressure.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18300,'chemistry',48,752,1620,'The heat change for the following reaction at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$ and constant pressure is $+7.3 \\mathrm{kcal}$.<br>$\\mathrm{A}_{2} \\mathrm{~B}(\\mathrm{~s}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{~A}(\\mathrm{~s})+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{~B}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=+7.3 \\mathrm{kcal}$ <br>The heat change at constant volume would be','[{"text": "$+7.3 \\\\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "more than $7.3 \\\\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "less than $7.3 \\\\mathrm{kcal}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{\\Delta H}} = {\\rm{\\Delta U}} + {\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{n}}_{\\rm{g}}}{\\rm{RT}}$<br>or $\\Delta \\mathrm{U}=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}-\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}} \\mathrm{RT}$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$<br>$\\Delta {\\rm{U}} = 7.3 \\times {10^3} - \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 298$<br>$ = 7300{\\rm{cal}} - 298$<br>$ = 7002{\\rm{cal}} \\approx 7.002{\\rm{kcal}}$<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18302,'chemistry',50,842,1881,'In <br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{s}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ + N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}^ \\ominus {\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}} \\to \\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{As}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}^{{\\rm{3}} - }{\\rm{ + S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}^{{\\rm{2}} - }{\\rm{ + NO + }}{{\\rm{H}}^ \\oplus }\n\\end{array}$ <br>the number of electrons involved in the reduction reaction is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>i. $3{{\\text{e}}^ - } + \\mathop {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}^ \\ominus }_{\\text{3}}}\\limits_{\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {{\\text{x}} - 6 =  - 1} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{x = 5}}} \n\\end{array}}  \\to \\mathop {{\\text{NO}}}\\limits_{\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {{\\text{x}} - 2 = 0} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{x = 2}}} \n\\end{array}} $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18303,'chemistry',49,618,1244,'The following reactions occur at $500 \\mathrm{~K}$. Arrange them in increasing order of tendency to proceed to completion.<br>a) $2 \\mathrm{NOCl}(\\mathrm{g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NO}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}$;<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}=1.7 \\times 10^{-2}$<br>b) $\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) ; \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}=1.5 \\times 10^{-5}$<br>c) $2 \\mathrm{SO}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{SO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$;<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}=1.3 \\times 10^{-5}$<br>d) $2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NO}(\\mathrm{g})+\\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$;<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}=5.9 \\times 10^{-5}$','[{"text": "b $ < $ a $ < $ d $ < $ c", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "c $ < $ b $ < $ d $ < $ a", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "a $ < $ c $ < $ d $ < $ b", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "c $ < $ d $ < $ b $ < $ a", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Greater the ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{P}}}$, more is the tendency to proceed to completion.<br>Increasing order of  ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{P}}}$ value is: c < b < d < a<br>order of completion towards product:  c < b < d < a.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18304,'chemistry',48,758,1637,'The enthalpy of neutralisation of oxalic acid by a strong base is $-25 \\mathrm{kcalmol}^{-1}$. The enthalpy of neutralisation of strong  acid and strong base is $ - 13.7{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{k}}\\,{\\text{cal}}\\,{\\text{equi}}{\\mkern 1mu} {{\\text{v}}^{ - 1}}$. The enthalpy of dissociation of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{H}^{+}+\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.0}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{k}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{cal}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0\\\\,{\\\\rm{k}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{cal}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$18.55\\\\,{\\\\rm{k}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{cal}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11.7\\\\,{\\\\rm{k}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{cal}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The enthalpy of neutralisation of strong acid and strong base<br>$ =  - 13.7\\,{\\rm{k}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{cal}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{equi}}{\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{v}}^{ - 1}}$<br>${{\\rm{As}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{two}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{equivalents}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{oxalic}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{acid}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{are}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{neutralised}}}$<br>${ =  - 13.7 \\times 2 =  - 27.4{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{k}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{cal}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}}$<br>${{\\rm{Enthalpy}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{neutralisation}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{oxalic}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{acid}}}$<br>${ =  - 25\\,{\\rm{k}}\\,{\\rm{cal}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{Enthalpy}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{dissociation}}$<br>$ =  - 25 - ( - 27.4) = 2.4\\,{\\rm{cal}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}{{\\rm{K}}^{ - 1}}$<br>${\\rm{or}} \\approx 2.0\\,{\\rm{kcal}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18307,'chemistry',49,617,1237,'At $527^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, the reaction given below has $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}=4$<br>$\\mathrm{NH}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{~N}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\dfrac{3}{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$.<br>What is the $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}$ for the reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{N}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g}) ?$','[{"text": "$16 \\\\times(800 \\\\mathrm{R})^{2} \\\\quad$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{800 \\\\mathrm{R}}{4}\\\\right)^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{4 \\\\times 800 \\\\mathrm{R}}\\\\right)^{2} \\\\quad$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If, $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}=\\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\right]^{1 / 2}\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]^{3 / 2}}{\\left[\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right]^{1}}=4$<br>then $\\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right]^{2}}{\\left[\\mathrm{~N}_{2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]^{3}}=\\dfrac{1}{4^{2}}$<br>And $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{p}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}} \\cdot(\\mathrm{RT})^{\\Delta \\mathrm{n}}$;<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{g}}=2-4=-2$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{P}}=\\dfrac{1}{4^{2}}(800 \\mathrm{R})^{-2}=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4 \\times 800 \\mathrm{R}}\\right)^{2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18308,'chemistry',48,752,1620,'An endothermic reaction has a positive internal energy change ${\\rm{\\Delta U}}$. In such a case, what is the minimum value that activation energy can have?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\Delta U}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{U}=\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}+\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{nRT}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{U}=\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}-\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{nRT}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{U}=\\\\mathrm{E}_{\\\\mathrm{a}}+\\\\mathrm{RT}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Activation energy is the additional amount of energy needed to overcome the potential barrier of reaction.Hence, $\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\Delta \\mathrm{U}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18311,'chemistry',49,617,1241,'For the reaction equilibrium,<br>$\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>the concentrations of $\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ at equilibrium are $4.8 \\times 10^{-2}$ and $1.2 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{molL}^{-1}$ respectively. The value of ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{c}}}$ for the reaction is','[{"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{2} \\\\mathrm{molL}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{-1} \\\\mathrm{molL}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{-3} \\\\mathrm{molL}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{3} \\\\mathrm{molL}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{c}}=\\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right]^{2}}{\\left[\\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right]}=\\dfrac{\\left(1.2 \\times 10^{-2}\\right)^{2}}{4.8 \\times 10^{-2}}=3 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{molL}^{-1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
