(18809,'chemistry',49,624,1266,'If $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ of a weak acid is taken and its percentage degree of ionisation is $1.34 \\%$, then its ionisation constant will be','[{"text": "$0.8 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.79 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.182 \\\\times 10^{-5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to Ostwald\'s dilution law, $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{C} \\alpha^{2}}{1-\\alpha}$For very weak acid <br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n  {\\alpha  <  <  <  < 1{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{or}}{\\mkern 1mu} 1 - \\alpha  \\approx 1} \\\\ \n  {\\,{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}} = {\\text{C}}{\\alpha ^2}\\,\\,} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{C}} = 0.1{\\text{M}},\\alpha  = 1.34\\%  = 0.0134} \\\\ \n  {\\therefore {{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}} = 0.1 \\times {{\\left( {1.34 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right)}^2}\\,} \\\\ \n  { = 1.79 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}} \n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
