(18974,'chemistry',49,627,1275,'Calculate the pH of $0.1\\,{\\rm{M C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{COOH}}$ solution. Dissociation constant of acetic acid is $1.8 \\times 10^{-5} \\mathrm{M}$.','[{"text": "2.87", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3.52", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.62", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6.54", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COOH}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CO}}{{\\text{O}}^ - } + {{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ + }}}$<br>${\\rm{Initial}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{conc}}{\\rm{.}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}\\,\\,\\, {\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.1M}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{0}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{0}}$<br>${\\rm{Eq}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{.}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{Conc}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.1}} - {\\rm{0}}.{\\rm{1\\alpha }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{0}}.{\\rm{1\\alpha }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{0}}.{\\rm{1}}\\alpha {\\mkern 1mu} $<br>${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{a}}} = \\frac{{[{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{CO}}{{\\rm{O}}^ - }][{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}]}}{{[{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{COOH}}]}}$<br>$1.8 \\times {10^{ - 5}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{0}}.{\\rm{1\\alpha }}{\\mkern 1mu} \\, \\times {\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{0}}.{\\rm{1\\alpha }}}}{{0.1(1 - {\\rm{\\alpha }})}}$<br>$1.8 \\times {10^{ - 5}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.1}}{{\\rm{\\alpha }}^{\\rm{2}}}}}{{1 - {\\rm{\\alpha }}}}$<br>$1.8 \\times {10^{ - 5}} = 0.1\\,{{\\rm{\\alpha }}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} $$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(\\because {\\rm{\\alpha }} <  <  < 1)$<br>${{\\rm{\\alpha }}^{\\rm{2}}} = 1.8 \\times {10^{ - 4}}<br>{\\rm{\\alpha }} = 1.34 \\times {10^{ - 2}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\left[ {{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}} \\right] = 0.1 \\times 1.34 \\times {10^{ - 2}} = 1.34 \\times {10^{ - 3}}$<br>${\\rm{pH}} =  - {\\rm{log}}\\left[ {{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}} \\right] =  - {\\rm{log}}\\left[ {1.34 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right] = 2.87$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18975,'chemistry',53,986,2295,'Group 13 elements exhibit','[{"text": "Only +3 oxidation state", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only +1 oxidation state", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both +1 and +3 oxidation states", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$+1,+2$$ and +3 oxidation states<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As there are three electrons in the valence shell of group 13 elements they show +3 oxidation state. Also in case of heavier members, due to inert pair effect, +1 oxidation state is most stable (Tl).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(18977,'chemistry',49,621,1250,'Consider the following reaction ${\\text{CaC}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{3(s)}}}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{Ca}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{(s)}}}} + {\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{2(g)}}}}$  in closed container at equilibrium. What would be the effect of addition of $\\mathrm{CaCO}_{3}$ on the equilibrium concentration of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ?','[{"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Data is insufficient", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains unaffected", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{CaC}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{3(s)}}}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{Ca}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{(s)}}}} + {\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{2(g)}}}}$<br>According to Le Chatelier\'s principle on adding $\\mathrm{CaCO}_{3}$, the equilibrium shifts in forward direction so as to decrease the effect of increased concentration of $\\mathrm{CaCO}_{3}$, thus, concentration of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ increases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
