(19720,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'In the following reactions,<br>(1) (2) The major product (A) and (C) are respectively','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_19720_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_19720_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  In this case dehydration is governed by Saytzeff\'s rule according to which hydrogen is preferentially eliminated from the carbon atom fewer number of hydrogen atoms, i.e., poor becomes poorer. Thus, 2-Methyl butane- 2 is the major product.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_19720_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_19720_explanation_4.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    This reaction is governed by Markovnikov\'s rule according to which when an unsymmetrical reagent, e.g., $\\mathrm{HBr}$ adds to an unsymmetrical alkene, then the negative part of the reagent is added to that carbon atom of the double bond which bears the least number of hydrogen atoms. Thus, in the above case 2-Methyl 2-bromo butane will be the major product',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_19720_question_1.png'),
