(19787,'chemistry',53,988,2307,'An alkali metal hydride $$(\\mathrm{NaH})$$ reacts with diborane in \' ${\\text{A}}$ \' to give a tetrahedral compound \' ${\\text{B}}$ \' which is extensively used as reducing agent in organic synthesis. The compound  \' ${\\text{A}}$ \' and  \' ${\\text{B}}$ \'  respectively are','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COCH}_{3}$$ and $$\\\\mathrm{B}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~N}_{3} \\\\mathrm{H}_{6}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$$ and $$\\\\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{6}$$ and $$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{Na}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{6}$$ and $$\\\\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/53_19787_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19789,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'The alkane formed on heating sodium butanoate with sodalime is','[{"text": "methane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ethane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "propane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "butane", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{CO}}{{\\text{O}}^{\\text{ - }}}{\\text{N}}{{\\text{a}}^{\\text{ + }}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Sodium}}\\,\\,{\\text{butanoate}}} {\\text{ + NaOH}}\\xrightarrow[\\Delta ]{{{\\text{CaO}}}}{\\text{ }}}$<br>${\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ Propane }}} {\\text{ + N}}{{\\text{a}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19790,'chemistry',54,1212,2902,'Heterolysis of propane gives','[{"text": "Methyl and ethyl free radicals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Methylcarbocation and ethyl free radicals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Methyl carbanion and ethyl carbocation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Methylfree radical and ethylcarbocation", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Heterolysis of a covalent bond form carbocation and carbanion.<br>${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}} - {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}} - {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}} \\to {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits^{\\rm{ + }} {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}} + \\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits^{\\rm{ - }} {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$<br>Due to inductive effect, ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits^{\\rm{ + }} {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ and $\\mathop {{\\rm{ C}}}\\limits^\\Theta  {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$ are fromed as major species during heterolysis of propane',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19791,'chemistry',53,988,2307,'A Lewis acid \' $$\\mathrm{X}$$ \' reacts with $$\\mathrm{LiAlH}_{4}$$ in ether medium to give a highly toxic gas. This gas when heated with $$\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$$ gives a compound commonly known as inorganic benzene. The gas is','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{6}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{B}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~N}_{3} \\\\mathrm{H}_{6}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{BF}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{3}}{{\\text{B}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{6}}}{\\text{ + 6N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}\\xrightarrow{\\Delta }{\\text{2}}\\mathop {{{\\text{B}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{}}{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{3}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{6}}}{\\text{ + 12}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{(inorganic}}\\,\\,{\\text{benzene)}}} $<br>Diborane (highly toxic gas) is formed when $$\\mathrm{BF}_{3}$$ (Lewis acid) reacts with $$\\mathrm{LiAlH}_{4}$$ in presence of ether.<br>$$\\underset{\\text { (X) }}{4 \\mathrm{BF}_{3}}+3 \\mathrm{LiAlH}_{4} \\stackrel{\\text { ether }}{\\longrightarrow} \\underset{\\text { (Toxic gas) }}{2 \\mathrm{~B}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}}+3 \\mathrm{LiF}+3 \\mathrm{AlF}_{3}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19793,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'n-Propyl chloride reacts with sodium metal in dry ether to give','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ $-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathop {{\\text{2C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ - C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ - C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ - Cl}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Propyl}}\\,\\,{\\text{chloride}}} {\\text{ + 2Na}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ Dryether }}}}$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ - C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ - C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ - }}\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ n - hexane }}} {\\text{ - C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ - C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ + 2NaCl}}$<br> Wurtz reaction',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19794,'chemistry',54,1212,2904,'$\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{~N}^{+}$ is neither an electrophile, nor a nucleophile because it','[{"text": "Does not have electron pair for donation as well as can attract electron pair", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neither has electron pair available for donation nor can accommodate electron since all shells of $\\\\mathrm{N}$ are fully occupied", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Can act as Lewis acid and base", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Can\'t be predicted", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The octet around $\\mathrm{N}$ is complete, hence it has no electrophilic character. $\\mathrm{N}$ has no lone pair of electrons to act as nucleophile.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19795,'chemistry',53,989,2312,'The complex formed when $$\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$$ is leached from bauxite using concentrated $$\\mathrm{NaOH}$$ solution is','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Na}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Al}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{4}\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{NaAl}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Al}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{3}\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{AlO}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$$\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+2 \\mathrm{NaOH}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\underset{\\text { Sodium meta aluminate }}{2 \\mathrm{Na}\\left[\\mathrm{Al}(\\mathrm{OH})_{4}\\right]}$$<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19797,'chemistry',55,1241,3021,'The most reactive among the following towards sulphonation is','[{"text": "toluene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "chlorobenzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "nitrobenzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "m-Xylene", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19798,'chemistry',54,1214,2911,'Which one of the following species is most stable?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{p}-\\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~N}-\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{4}-\\\\stackrel{+}{\\\\mathrm{C}} \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{p}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{O}-\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{4}-\\\\stackrel{+}{\\\\mathrm{C}} \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{p}-\\\\mathrm{Cl}-\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{4}-\\\\stackrel{+}{\\\\mathrm{C}} \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\\\stackrel{+}{\\\\mathrm{C}} \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_19798_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Nitro-group is electron withdrawing; therefore decrease stability. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_19798_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Methoxy group is electron releasing; therefore increases stability by donating electron. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_19798_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Chlorine is also electron withdrawing but its effect is less than - $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ group. Hence, the correct order of stability is <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_19798_explanation_4.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19799,'chemistry',53,989,2312,'Aluminium is extracted from alumina $$\\left(\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}\\right)$$ by electrolysis of molten mixture of','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{Na}_{3} \\\\mathrm{AlF}_{6}+\\\\mathrm{CaF}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{KF}+\\\\mathrm{Na}_{3} \\\\mathrm{AlF}_{6}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{HF}+\\\\mathrm{NaAlF}_{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{CaF}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{NaAlF}_{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Extraction of $$\\mathrm{Al}$$ from $$\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$$ is made by electrolytic reduction of molten mixture of alumina $$\\left(\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}\\right)$$, cryolite $$\\left(\\mathrm{Na}_{3} \\mathrm{AlF}_{6}\\right)$$ and fluorspar $$\\mathrm{CaF}_{2}$$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19800,'chemistry',55,1241,3021,'Among the following compounds the one that is most reactive towards electrophilic nitration is','[{"text": "Toluene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Benzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Benzoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nitrobenzene", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Toluene has electron-donating methyl group (while others have electron-withdrawing groups) and hence most reactive towards electrophilic nitration.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19804,'chemistry',55,1242,3022,'Which of the following statements are correct?<br>(i) polynuclear hydrocarbons contain two or more benzene rings fused together<br>(ii) polynuclear hydrocarbons have carcinogenic property<br>(iii) polynuclear hydrocarbons are formed on in complete combustion of organic materials like tobacco, coal and petroleum<br>(iv) they are also produced in human body due to various biochemical reactions','[{"text": "(i), (ii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i), (iii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(ii), (iii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i), (ii) and (iii)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Poly nuclear hydrocarbons are not produced in human body by any biochemical reaction. However, when they enter into human body they undergo various biochemical reactions which finally damage DNA and cause cancer',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19806,'chemistry',54,1213,2910,'In which of the following molecules all the effects namely inductive, mesomeric, and hyperconjugation operate?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_19806_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19807,'chemistry',55,1240,3018,'Identify \' $\\mathrm{X}$ \' in the following reaction','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_19807_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $ \\cdot $6-H atoms on benzene is replaced by 6- \' $\\mathrm{Cl}$ \' atoms.<br>$ \\cdot $  $3 \\pi$ bonds remains on ring.<br>$ \\cdot $ It is an alicyclic compound (not aromatic)<br>$ \\cdot $  Gammaxene does not contain any $\\pi$ bond.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_19807_question_1.png'),
(19808,'chemistry',53,993,2321,'The inert pair effect is most prominent in','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Pb}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Ge}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Si}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19810,'chemistry',54,1213,2907,'The correct stability order of following species is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{x > y > w > z}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{y > x > w > z}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{x > w > z > y}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{z > x > y > w}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{x}}$ is a conjugated diene system, ${\\rm{w}}$ is an isolated diene system, $\\mathrm{z}$ is a cumulated diene system, y is antiaromatic system. Hence, stability order is<br>$\\mathrm{x}>\\mathrm{w}>\\mathrm{z}>\\mathrm{y}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_19810_question_1.jpg'),
(19811,'chemistry',55,1240,3018,'Toluene reacts with halogen in presence of iron (III) chloride giving ortho- and para- halo compounds. The reaction is','[{"text": "free radical addition reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "electrophilic elimination reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "nucleophilic substitution reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "electrophilic substitution reaction", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Toluene reacts with halogen in presence of Iron (III) chloride giving ortho and para halocompounds, the reaction is electrophilic substitution reaction. The "H" atom or aromatic nucleus is replaced with chlorine atom. The electrophile in this reaction is $\\mathrm{Cl}+$ ion obtained by the reaction of $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ with $\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_19811_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19812,'chemistry',53,994,2326,'Which of the following species exists (A) $$\\left[\\mathrm{SiF}_{6}\\right]^{2-},(\\mathrm{B})\\left[\\mathrm{GeCl}_{6}\\right]^{2-}$$ and $$(\\mathrm{C})\\left[\\mathrm{CCl}_{6}\\right]^{2-}$$ ?','[{"text": "(A) and (B)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(B) and (C)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only (C)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(A) and (C)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Carbon does not contain d-orbital hence it cannot expand its octet.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19814,'chemistry',54,1213,2910,'In the following compounds the order of basicity is:','[{"text": "IV $>$ I $>$ III $>$ II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III $>$ I $>$ IV $>$ II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II $>$ I $>$ III $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I $>$ III $>$ II $>$ IV", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In I, the lone pair of electrons on $\\mathrm{N}$ always available for protonation. In III, due to presence of electro negative $\\mathrm{O}$ atom the electron density on $\\mathrm{N}$ is decreased. In II and IV, resonance suppresses the basic character.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_19814_question_1.jpg'),
(19815,'chemistry',55,1241,3021,'In electrophilic aromatic substituion reaction, the nitro group is meta directing because it','[{"text": "decreases electron density at meta position", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increases electron density at meta position", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increases electron density at ortho and para positions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "decreases electron density at ortho and para positions.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The directive influence of $-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ as an electrophile in electrophilic aromatic substitution is due to the $-\\mathrm{I}$ effect or electron withdrawing effect of $-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$, and can be explained on the basis of the resonating structures obtained by the attack of the group on the benzene ring:  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_19815_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The substituent, i.e., $-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ withdraws electrons from O- and P- positions Thus, m-position becomes a point of relatively high electron density and further substitution by electrophile occurs at m-position.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19816,'chemistry',53,994,2326,'A metal, ${\\text{M}}$ forms chlorides in +2 and +4 oxidation states. Which of the following statements about these chlorides is correct?','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{MCl}_{2}$$ is more volatile than $$\\\\mathrm{MCl}_{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{MCl}_{2}$$ is more soluble in anhydrous ethanol than $$\\\\mathrm{MCl}_{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{MCl}_{2}$$ is more ionic than $$\\\\mathrm{MCl}_{4}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{MCl}_{2}$$ is more easily hydrolysed than $$\\\\mathrm{MCl}_{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Compounds in lower O.S. are more ionic than compounds in higher O.S. Thus, $$\\mathrm{MCl}_{2}$$ is more ionic than $$\\mathrm{MCl}_{4}$$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
