(19853,'chemistry',53,995,2336,'Carbon forms two oxides which have different compositions. The equivalent mass of which remains constant?','[{"text": "Oxygen", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Carbon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both carbon and oxygen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neither carbon nor oxygen", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The equivalent mass of a substance have different value under different conditions, it varies with valency. Let us consider the two oxides of carbon viz. $$\\mathrm{CO}$$ and $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$. In $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2} 12$$ parts of carbon combine with 16 parts of oxygen thus equivalent mass of carbon in $$\\mathrm{CO}$$ is $$\\dfrac{12 \\times 8}{16}=6.0$$. In $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}, 12$$ parts of carbon combine with 32 parts of oxygen, thus the equivalent mass of carbon in this oxide is $$\\dfrac{12 \\times 8}{32}=3.0$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
