(19898,'chemistry',53,991,2314,'The composition of duralumin is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Al} 94 \\\\%, \\\\mathrm{Mg} 6 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{Cu56% ,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{Zn 24% ,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{Ni 20% }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu} 95 \\\\%, \\\\mathrm{Al} \\\\mathrm{5 \\\\%}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Al} 95 \\\\%, \\\\mathrm{Cu} 4 \\\\%, \\\\mathrm{Mn} 0.5 \\\\%, \\\\mathrm{Mg} 0.5 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\text{Duralumin}}$  is an alloy of $\\mathrm{Al}(95 \\%)$, $\\mathrm{Cu}(4 \\%), \\operatorname{Mn}(0.5 \\%)$ and $\\operatorname{Mg}(0.5 \\%)$.<br>${\\text{Magnalium}}$ is an alloy of $\\mathrm{Al}(94 \\%)$ and $\\operatorname{Mg}(6 \\%)$.<br>${\\text{German silver}}$ is an alloy of $\\mathrm{Cu}(56 \\%)$, $\\mathrm{Zn}(24 \\%)$ and $\\mathrm{Ni}(20 \\%)$.<br>${\\text{Aluminium bronze}}$ is an alloy of $\\mathrm{Cu}(95 \\%)$ and $\\operatorname{Al}(5 \\%)$.<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2010,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19900,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'How many gram of sodium ${\\text{(atomic}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{mass 23 u)}}$  is required to prepare one mole of ethane from methyl chloride by Wurtz reaction?','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "23", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "11.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "46", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The Wurtz reaction between methyl chloride and sodium metal in presence of dry ehter can be written as:<br>${\\text{2C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{Cl  +  2Na}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{Dry}}\\,\\,{\\text{ether}}}}\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Ethane}}} {\\text{ +  2NaCl}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow 2$ moles of sodium metal reacts to give 1 mole of ethane.<br>Also,<br>1 mole of Na metal $=23 \\mathrm{~g}$<br>$\\therefore 2$ moles of Na metal $=23 \\times 2=46 \\mathrm{~g}$<br>Thus, $46 \\mathrm{~g}$ of sodium is required to prepare one mole of ethane from methyl chloride by Wurtz reaction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19906,'chemistry',53,988,2305,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Coloured cations can be identified by borax bead test.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Transparent bead $\\left(\\mathrm{NaBO}_{2}+\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}\\right)$ forms coloured bead with coloured cation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason is Incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Borax, when strongly heated, forms a glassy mass like bead.<br>${\\text{N}}{{\\text{a}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{B}}_{\\text{4}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{7}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{740}}^\\circ {\\text{C}}}}\\underbrace {\\mathop {{\\text{2NaB}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Sodium}}\\,\\,{\\text{metaborate}}} {\\text{ + }}\\mathop {{{\\text{B}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Boric}}\\,{\\text{anhydride}}} }_{{\\text{Glassy bead }}}$<br>This glassy bead gives different colour beads with different cations. Hence, it can be used to identify cations in qualitative analysis. This test is called borax bead test.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(19908,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'The alkyl halides required to prepare the following compound by Wurtz reaction are','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In Wurtz reaction, an etheral solution of an alkyl halide forms symmetrical alkane when heated with<br>metallic sodium. Tertiary halides do not undergo this reaction.     <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_19908_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_19908_question_1.png'),
