(20001,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">1-Butene on reaction with $\\mathrm{HBr}$ in the presence of a peroxide produces 1-bromobutane.</div><div class="column-right">The above mentioned reaction involves the free radical mechanism.<br></div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'This reaction is followed by anti Markovnikov\'s rule    <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20001_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    In this reaction, anti-Markovnikov\'s addition is explained on the basis of the fact that in the presence peroxides, addition takes place via free radical mechanism rather than the ionic mechanism.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20002,'chemistry',54,1213,2910,'The increasing order of the boiling points for the following compounds is:','[{"text": "(III) $ < $ (IV) $ < $ (II) $ < $ (I)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(IV) $ < $ (III) $ < $ (I) $ < $ (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II) $ < $ (III) $ < $ (IV) $ < $ (I)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(III) $ < $ (II) $ < $ (I) $ < $ (IV)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'(I) $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$ : Hydrogen bonding >Dipole-Dipole<br>(II) Dipole-Dipole: $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{Cl}$ is more polar than $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$. So, Dipole-Dipole<br>$\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{Cl}>$ Dipole-Dipole $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$<br>(III) $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ : No hydrogen bonding and no dipole-dipole interaction<br>(IV) $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$ : Dipole-Dipole<br>Boiling point order is (III) $ < $ (IV) $ < $ (II) $ < $ (I).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_20002_question_1.jpg'),
(20003,'chemistry',53,988,2310,'The geometry of a complex species can be understood from the knowledge of type of hybridisation of orbitals of central atom. The hybridisation of orbitals of central atom in<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{B}\\left(\\mathrm{OH}_{4}\\right)\\right]^{-}$and the geometry of the complex are respectively','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$, tetrahedral", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$, square planar", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\\\mathrm{~d}^{2}$, octahedral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{dsp}^{2}$, square planar", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Structure of $\\mathrm{B}(\\mathrm{OH})_{4}^{-}$  is <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/53_20003_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Hybridiastion  - ${\\text{s}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{3}}}$<br>Geometry   -  Tetrahedral',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
