(20016,'chemistry',54,1209,2895,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Acetone exists in enolic form ( <  $0.15 \\%$ ) but acetyl acetone predominantly exist in enolic form $(>15 \\%)$<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> H-bonding in enolic form in acetyl acetone favour it while it is absent in acetone.<br>','[{"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is wrong", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not correct explanation of Assertion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is wrong but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2021,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20017,'chemistry',55,1240,3014,'Which of the following statements are correct regarding structure of benzene?<br>I. All the six carbons in benzene are $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}-$ hybridised<br>II. $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$-hybridised orbital of carbon combines with another $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$-hybridised orbital of carbon to give $\\mathrm{C}$ - $\\mathrm{C}$ sigma bonds.<br>III. C-H $\\sigma-$ bond is obtained by lateral overlapping of $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$-hybridised orbital of $\\mathrm{C}$ and 1s-orbital of H-atom.<br>Choose the correct statements.','[{"text": "I, II and III are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and III are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Statements I and II are correct, while the statement III is incorrect.<br>It\'s correct form is as follows:<br>$\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H} \\sigma$ bond is obtained by head on overlapping of $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ hybridised orbital of $\\mathrm{C}$ and $1 \\mathrm{~s}$ orbital of $\\mathrm{H}-\\quad$ atom. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20018,'chemistry',53,988,2305,'${\\text{N}}{{\\text{a}}_2}\\,{{\\text{B}}_4}{{\\text{O}}_7} \\cdot 10{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{O}}\\xrightarrow{\\Delta }{\\text{X}} + {\\text{NaB}}{{\\text{O}}_2} + {{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{O}}$<br>${\\text{X}} + {\\text{C}}{{\\text{r}}_2}{{\\text{O}}_3}\\xrightarrow{\\Delta }{\\text{Y}}$ (green colored)<br>The $\\mathrm{X}$ and $\\mathrm{Y}$ are','[{"text": "${\\\\text{N}}{{\\\\text{a}}_{\\\\text{3}}}{\\\\text{B}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{3}}}{\\\\text{  &  Cr}}{\\\\left( {{\\\\text{B}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{2}}}} \\\\right)_{\\\\text{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{N}}{{\\\\text{a}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{{\\\\text{B}}_{\\\\text{4}}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{7}}}{\\\\text{  &  Cr}}{\\\\left( {{\\\\text{B}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{2}}}} \\\\right)_{\\\\text{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{B}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{3}}}{\\\\text{  &  Cr}}{\\\\left( {{\\\\text{B}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{2}}}} \\\\right)_{\\\\text{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{B}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{3}}}{\\\\text{  &  CrB}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\text{N}}{{\\text{a}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{}}{{\\text{B}}_{\\text{4}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{7}}}{\\text{.10}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}\\xrightarrow{\\Delta }\\mathop {{{\\text{B}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{(x)}}} {\\text{ +  2NaB}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ + 10}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}$<br>${\\text{3}}{{\\text{B}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ + C}}{{\\text{r}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}\\xrightarrow{\\Delta }\\mathop {{\\text{2Cr}}}\\limits_{{\\text{(y)}}} {\\left( {{\\text{B}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right)_{\\text{3}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20020,'chemistry',55,1239,3006,'Which of the following order is correct regarding acidic character of hydrocarbons given below?<br>I. $\\mathrm{CH} \\equiv \\mathrm{CH}>\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}>\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$<br>II. $\\mathrm{HC} \\equiv \\mathrm{CH}>\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{C} \\equiv \\mathrm{CH}>\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{C} \\equiv \\mathrm{CCH}_{3}$','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both the orders regarding acidity of hydrocarbon are correct.<br>I. Acidic strength $\\propto$ percent  ${\\text{s -  character [s -  character }}$  is $50 \\%$ in alkynes, $33 \\%$ in alkanes and $25 \\%$ in alkanes].<br>II. Inductive effect defines acidic strength and polarity of C-H bond of alkyne. Groups having + I-effect increases electron density around  $\\mathrm{C-}$ $\\mathrm{H}$ bond, hence polarity decreases and acidic strength of alkyne decreases.<br>So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20021,'chemistry',53,999,2348,'Match the following:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Diamond<br>B. Graphite<br>C. Silica<br>D.${\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}$</div><div class="column-right">P. Metal electrode <br>Q. sp hybridised<br>R. Acheson\'s process<br>S. Agate<br>T. Cutting of glass</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{T}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{T}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A. Diamond is used to cut the glass<br>B. Graphite is prepared by Acheson\'s method<br>C. Allotrope of silica is agate<br>D. In $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$, carbon atom undergoes $\\mathrm{sp}$ hybridisation',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20023,'chemistry',55,1234,2968,'Which of the following statements are correct regarding relative stability of conformations of ethane?<br>I. There is minimum repulsive force and maximum stability of molecule in the case of staggered form.<br>II. There is maximum repulsive force and minimum stability of molecule in case of eclipsed form.<br>III. Due to repulsive force, eclipsed form posseses more energy as compared to staggered form.<br>IV. Energy of staggered molecule is lower than that of eclipsed form.<br>Choose the correct option.','[{"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All the given statements are correct about conformation of alkane. So, the option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20024,'chemistry',53,994,2329,'Which of the following occurs as a consequence of inert pair effect?<br>(A) $\\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}$ acts as a reducing agent<br>(B) $\\mathrm{SnCl}_{4}$ acts as an oxidising agent<br>(C) $\\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is amphoteric<br>(D) $\\mathrm{PbO}_{2}$ is an oxidant<br>(E) $\\mathrm{CCl}_{2}$ is unstable but $\\mathrm{PbCl}_{2}$ is stable','[{"text": "A, D, E", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "D, E, C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, B, C, D, E", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, B, C", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Inert pair effect explains the stability of +2 & +4 oxidation state. Due to inner pair effect , $\\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}$ acts as a reducing agent; $\\mathrm{PbO}_{2}$ is an oxidant ; carbon is more stable in +4, hence  $\\mathrm{CCl}_{2}$ is unstable  but $\\mathrm{PbCl}_{2}$ is  more stable as it is more stable in +2 state.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20026,'chemistry',55,1234,2971,'Arrange the correct sequence for mechanism of chlorination of methane<br>I. $\\mathrm{CH}_{4}+\\mathrm{Cl}^{\\cdot} \\stackrel{\\text { hv }}{\\longrightarrow} \\dot{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}_{3}+\\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{Cl}$<br>II. $\\mathrm{Cl}-\\mathrm{Cl} \\stackrel{\\text { hv }}{\\longrightarrow} 2 \\dot{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{l}$<br>III. $\\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits^{\\text{.}} {{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ + C}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}} \\to {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ - Cl + C}}{{\\text{l}}^{\\text{.}}}$<br>IV. $\\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits^{\\text{.}} {\\text{1 + }}\\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits^{\\text{.}} {\\text{l }} \\to {\\text{Cl - Cl}}$<br>$\\dot{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}_{3}+\\dot{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}_{3} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$<br>Choose the correct option.','[{"text": "II, I, III and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II , III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV, III, II and I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III, I and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Halogenation (chlorination here) of alkane proceeds through free radical mechanism as follows:<br>II. Initiation Homolysis of chlorine molecule<br>${\\text{Cl}} - {\\text{Cl}} \\to \\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}^ \\bullet }}\\limits_{} + \\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}^{^ \\bullet }}}\\limits_{{\\text{ Chlorine free radicals }}} $<br>I. Propagation ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_4} + {\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}^ \\bullet }\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ hv }}}}\\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits^ \\bullet {{\\text{H}}_3} + {\\text{HCl}}$<br>III. $\\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits^ \\bullet {{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ + Cl - Cl}} \\to {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{Cl + }}\\mathop {{\\text{ C}}}\\limits^ \\bullet {\\text{l}}$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{Cl}} + {\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}^ \\bullet } \\to \\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits^ \\bullet {{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{Cl}} + {\\text{HCl}}$<br>$\\mathop {{\\text{ C}}}\\limits^ \\bullet {{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{Cl + Cl - Cl}} \\to {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ + }}\\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits^ \\bullet {\\text{l }}$<br>IV. Termination: $\\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits^ \\bullet {\\text{l + }}\\mathop {{\\text{ C}}}\\limits^ \\bullet {\\text{l}} \\to {\\text{Cl - Cl}}$<br>${{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}\\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits^ \\bullet {\\text{ + }}\\mathop {{\\text{ C}}}\\limits^ \\bullet {{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}} \\to {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ - C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}$<br>$\\mathop {{\\text{ C}}}\\limits^ \\bullet {{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ + }}\\mathop {{\\text{ C}}}\\limits^ \\bullet {\\text{l}} \\to {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ - Cl}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20027,'chemistry',53,986,2297,'For the properties mentioned, the correct trend for the different species is in','[{"text": "Strength as Lewis acids- $\\\\mathrm{BCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{GaCl}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inert pair effect $-\\\\mathrm{Al}>\\\\mathrm{Ga}>$ In", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxidising property $-\\\\mathrm{Al}^{3+}>\\\\mathrm{In}^{3+}>\\\\mathrm{Tl}^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First ionization enthalpy $-\\\\mathrm{B}>\\\\mathrm{Al}>\\\\mathrm{Tl}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Strength of Lewis acids decreases in the order $\\mathrm{BCl}_{3}>\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}>\\mathrm{GaCl}_{3}$, as boron can accept electron pair easily.<br>${\\text{Al}}$ does not show inert pair effect.<br>$\\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$ has the highest oxidising power.<br>Ionization enthalpy of $\\mathrm{Tl}$ is higher than that of ${\\text{Al}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20028,'chemistry',54,1214,2911,'Arrange the following carbocations in decreasing order of stability','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{B}>\\\\mathrm{A}>\\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}>\\\\mathrm{B}>\\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}>\\\\mathrm{B}>\\\\mathrm{A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}>\\\\mathrm{A}>\\\\mathrm{B}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Carbocation (A) is stabilised by hyperconjugation due to $4 \\alpha$-Hydrogens.<br>Carbocation (B) is most stable as it is stabilised by resonance.<br>Carbocation (C) is stabilised by hyperconjugation due to $4 \\alpha$ - hydrogen atoms but destabilised by -I. effect of O-atom.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_20028_question_1.png'),
