(20120,'chemistry',55,1237,2987,'Arrange the following alkyl halides in decreasing order of the rate of $\\beta$ - elimination reaction with alcoholic $\\mathrm{KOH}$.<br>(A) (B) $\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{Br}$<br>(C) $\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{Br}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}>\\\\mathrm{B}>\\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}>\\\\mathrm{B}>\\\\mathrm{A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{B}>\\\\mathrm{C}>\\\\mathrm{A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}>\\\\mathrm{C}>\\\\mathrm{B}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'More number of alkyl groups, more stable alkene will be formed on elimination and more will be the reactivity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20120_question_1.png'),
(20121,'chemistry',53,993,2319,'The correct order of bond enthalpy $\\left(\\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}\\right)$ is:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{C}>\\\\mathrm{Si}-\\\\mathrm{Si}>\\\\mathrm{Ge}-\\\\mathrm{Ge}>\\\\mathrm{Sn}-\\\\mathrm{Sn}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Si}-\\\\mathrm{Si}>\\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{C}>\\\\mathrm{Ge}-\\\\mathrm{Ge}>\\\\mathrm{Sn}-\\\\mathrm{Sn}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{C}>\\\\mathrm{Si}-\\\\mathrm{Si}>\\\\mathrm{Sn}-\\\\mathrm{Sn}>\\\\mathrm{Ge}-\\\\mathrm{Ge}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Si}-\\\\mathrm{Si}>\\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{C}>\\\\mathrm{Sn}-\\\\mathrm{Sn}>\\\\mathrm{Ge}-\\\\mathrm{Ge}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/53_20121_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>So, the correct order is $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}>\\mathrm{Si}-\\mathrm{Si}>\\mathrm{Ge}-$ $\\mathrm{Ge}>\\mathrm{Sn}-\\mathrm{Sn}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20122,'chemistry',54,1201,2868,'Which of the following molecules represents the order of hybridisation $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}, \\mathrm{sp}^{2}, \\mathrm{sp}, \\mathrm{sp}$ from left to right atoms?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CH}-\\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\mathrm{CH}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CH}-\\\\mathrm{C} \\\\equiv \\\\mathrm{CH}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HC} \\\\equiv \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{C} \\\\equiv \\\\mathrm{CH}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}{{\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}}&{}&{{\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}}&{}&{{\\rm{sp}}}&{}&{{\\rm{sp}}}\\\\{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}}& = &{{\\rm{CH}}}& - &{\\rm{C}}& \\equiv &{{\\rm{CH}}}\\end{array}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20124,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'Arrange the following hydrogen halides in order of their decreasing reactivity with propene.','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCl}>\\\\mathrm{HBr}>\\\\mathrm{HI}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HBr}>\\\\mathrm{HI}>\\\\mathrm{HCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HI}>\\\\mathrm{HBr}>\\\\mathrm{HCl}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCl}>\\\\mathrm{HI}>\\\\mathrm{HBr}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The decreasing order of reactivity of hydrogen halides with propene is $\\mathrm{HI}>\\mathrm{HBr}>$ $\\mathrm{HCl}$. As the size of halogen increases, the strength of $\\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{X}$ bond decreases and hence, reactivity increases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20125,'chemistry',53,987,2298,'Group – 13 elements react with $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in amorphous form to form oxides of type $\\mathrm{M}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}(\\mathrm{M}=$ element). Which among the following is the most basic oxide?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Tl}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ga}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The order of basic character of group – 13 oxides increases with increase in the size of the central atom down the group. Thus, $\\mathrm{Tl}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ is most basic oxide.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/53_20125_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20128,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'The addition of $\\mathrm{HBr}$ to 1-Butene gives a mixture of products $\\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{B}$ and $\\mathrm{C}$<br>(A) (B) <br>$\\begin{gathered} {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ - C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ - C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ - C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ - Br}} \\hfill \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(C)}} \\hfill \\\\ \\end{gathered} $<br>The mixture consists of','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\\\mathrm{B}$ as major and $\\\\mathrm{C}$ as minor products", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{B}$ as major, $\\\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\\\mathrm{C}$ as minor products", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{B}$ as minor, $\\\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\\\mathrm{C}$ as major products", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A and B}}$  as minor and $\\\\mathrm{C}$ as major products", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The alkene is unsymmetrical, hence will follow Markovnikov\'s rule to give major product.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20128_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Compound I contains a chiral carbon, it exists in two enantiomers (A and B) Which are mirror images of each other   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20128_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20128_question_1.png'),
