(20224,'chemistry',54,1213,2906,'Identify the functional group that has electron donating inductive effect','[{"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{CN}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The functional group that has electron donating or positive inductive effect is $-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$. Since, the positive inductive effect is due to the presence of an electron donating groups within the polar bond. Thus, option (3) is correct as rest of the three groups $(-\\mathrm{COOH},-\\mathrm{CN}$, $\\left.-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right)$ are electron withdrawing in nature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20226,'chemistry',53,996,2345,'Read Assertion and Reason carefully to  mark the correct option  given  below.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Si - Si bond is much stronger than$\\mathrm{Si}-\\mathrm{O}$ bond.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Si forms multiple bonds easily.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{Si}-\\mathrm{O}$ bond is stronger than $\\mathrm{Si}-\\mathrm{Si}$ bond because $\\mathrm{p} \\pi-\\mathrm{d} \\pi$ bonding is not possible between silicon atoms due to large size.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20247,'chemistry',54,1213,2907,'In case of substituted aniline the group which decreases the basic strength is','[{"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Electron releasing groups like $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$, $-\\mathrm{OCH}_{3},-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ increase electron density at $\\mathrm{N}$ atom hence, such substituents increase basic nature of aromatic amines.<br>Electron-withdrawing groups like,<br>$-\\mathrm{NO}_{2},-\\mathrm{SO}_{3} \\mathrm{H},-\\mathrm{COOH},-\\mathrm{X},-\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}$ )<br>decrease electron density at $\\mathrm{N}$-atom thus, decreasing basic nature of aromatic amines.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20227,'chemistry',55,1244,3025,'A compound $(\\mathrm{X})$ when passed through dil. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ gives compound (Y), which on reaction with $\\mathrm{HI}$ and red phosphorous gives $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}$. The compound $(\\mathrm{X})$ is','[{"text": "Ethane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethyne", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2-Butene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-Butyne", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The compound (X) is likely to be alkyne which reacts with water in the presence of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and $\\mathrm{HgSO}_{4}$ as catalyst to form a carbonyl compound. $\\mathrm{HI}$ and red phosphorous can reduce a carbonyl compound to alkane having same number of carbon atoms.<br>Therefore (Y) is likely to be acetaldehyde, which is the hydration product of ethyne<br>$\\begin{gathered}\n  {\\text{HC}} \\equiv {\\text{CH   +   }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}\\xrightarrow[{{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}}]{{{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}}}\\left[ {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{   =   CH   -   OH}}} \\right] \\hfill \\\\\n  \\,{\\text{(X)}} \\hfill \\\\\n   \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{  -   CHO}} \\hfill \\\\\n  {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{(Y)}} \\hfill \\\\\n  {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CHO   +   4HI}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{red}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{P}}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{  +  2}}{{\\text{I}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{  +  }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}} \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered} $<br>Therefore, $(\\mathrm{X})$ is ethyne.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20228,'chemistry',54,1214,2912,'The stability of carbanions in the following<br>A) ${\\rm{RC}} \\equiv \\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits^ - $<br>B) C) ${{\\rm{R}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}} = \\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits^ - {\\rm{H}}$<br>D) ${{\\rm{R}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{C}} \\equiv \\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits^ - {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>is in the order of','[{"text": "(B)>(C)>(D)>(A)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(D)>(B)>(C)>(A)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(A)>(C)>(B)>(D)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(A)>(B)>(C)>(D)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_20228_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\% \\mathrm{~s}$ character $\\propto$ E.N. of \'C\' & stability of carbanion.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_20228_question_1.png'),
(20230,'chemistry',53,993,2320,'Read Assertion and Reason carefully to  mark the correct option  given  below.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Carbon exhibits highest catenation property.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Carbon has a valency of and 4 and $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}$ energy is very high.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Carbon shows highest catenation capacity due to high $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}$ bond energy and tetravalency.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20231,'chemistry',55,1237,2991,'Number of different products obtained on reductive ozonolysis of myrcene, a terpeniod is','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20231_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20231_question_1.png'),
(20232,'chemistry',54,1213,2910,'Which of the following has the most acidic hydrogen?','[{"text": "3 - hexanone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2, 4 - hexanedione", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2,5 - hexanedione", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2, 3 - hexanedione", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'(1) <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_20232_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> (2) <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_20232_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> (3) <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_20232_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> (4) <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_20232_explanation_4.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> The most acidic hydrogen is the $\\mathrm{H}$ attached to the $\\mathrm{C}$ in (2) as the negative charge on it after removal of proton is extensively delocalized as compared to the all other due to the two carbonyl groups which are adjacent to this carbon.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20234,'chemistry',53,993,2318,'Read the Statement -A and Statement -B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">From silicon to Pb , there is small increase in radius due to the presence of completely filled d- and f-subshells in heavier elements.</div><div class="column-right">From carbon to Si there is considerable increase in radius.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both statements are correct.<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {}&{\\text{C}}&{{\\text{Si}}}&{{\\text{Ge}}}&{{\\text{Sn}}}&{{\\text{Pb}}} \\\\ \n  \\begin{gathered}\n  {\\text{Covalent}} \\hfill \\\\\n  {\\text{radii}} \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered} &{77}&{118}&{122}&{140}&{146({\\text{Pm}})} \n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20246,'chemistry',53,993,2321,'Aluminium is more reactive than iron because its standard reduction potential is higher still aluminium is less easily corroded than iron because','[{"text": "It has higher reducing power and forms protective layer of $\\\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It has higher reducing power and does not react with oxygen so easily.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aluminium reacts with amphoteric carbon dioxide to form a self-protective layer of $\\\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{Al}_{4} \\\\mathrm{C}_{3}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aluminium reacts with nitrogen in air to form a protective layer of AIN.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$4 \\mathrm{Al}+3 \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow \\underset{\\text { Alumina }}{2 \\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}}$(Passivation of aluminium layer).The formed $\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ protects Al from corrosion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20235,'chemistry',54,1214,2911,'Which of the following represents the correct order of stability of the given carbocations?','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{C}}\\\\limits^{\\\\rm{ + }} {\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{C - }}\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{C}}\\\\limits_{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\left| {} \\\\right.}^{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\left| {\\\\rm{ + }} \\\\right.} {\\\\rm{ > }}\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{C}}\\\\limits^{\\\\rm{ + }} {{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{C}}\\\\limits^{\\\\rm{ + }} {\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{C - }}\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{C}}\\\\limits_{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\left| {} \\\\right.}^{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\left| {\\\\rm{ + }} \\\\right.} {\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{C}}\\\\limits^{\\\\rm{ + }} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{C - }}\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{C}}\\\\limits_{\\\\left| {} \\\\right.}^{\\\\mid {\\\\rm{ + }}} {\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{C}}\\\\limits^{\\\\rm{ + }} {\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{C}}\\\\limits^{\\\\rm{ + }} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{C - }}\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{C}}\\\\limits_{\\\\left| {} \\\\right.}^{\\\\mid {\\\\rm{ + }}} {\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{C}}\\\\limits^{\\\\rm{ + }} {\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{F}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{C}}\\\\limits^{\\\\rm{ + }} $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{ - I}}$ group destablises carbocation and since inductive effect decreases with increasing length of carbon chain. Therefore (2) is the correct option',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20236,'chemistry',55,1237,2992,'The products obtained are','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}+\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CO}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{4} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}+\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "none of these.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The terminal $\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ group $\\left(=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\right)$ is completely oxidised to $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$; a monosubstituted atom of a double bond is converted to aldehyde which is further oxidised to carboxylic acid, and the disubstituted atom of a double bond is oxidised to ketone.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20236_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20236_question_1.jpg'),
(20238,'chemistry',53,1000,2349,'Match Column I with Column II and choose the correct combination from the options given.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P,B}} - {\\\\rm{Q,C}} - {\\\\rm{R,D}} - {\\\\rm{T,S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{T,B}} - {\\\\rm{R,C}} - {\\\\rm{S,D}} - {\\\\rm{P,Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{T,B}} - {\\\\rm{S,C}} - {\\\\rm{P,D}} - {\\\\rm{R,Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R,B}} - {\\\\rm{Q,C}} - {\\\\rm{P,D}} - {\\\\rm{S,T}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{BF}_{4}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ - tetrahedral<br>$\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3} \\rightarrow$ Lewis acid due to electron deficiency.<br>$\\mathrm{SnO} \\rightarrow$ Can be oxidised to $\\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$<br>$\\mathrm{PbO}_{2} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{Pb}$ in +4 oxidation state and changes to +2 oxidation state. So, acts as oxidising agent.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/53_20238_question_1.png'),
(20239,'chemistry',54,1213,2906,'$+\\mathrm{I}$ effect is shown by','[{"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{Br}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$-\\mathrm{Br},-\\mathrm{Cl},-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ show $-\\mathrm{I}$ effect as all are electron attracting or withdrawing. $+\\mathrm{I}$ effect is shown by $-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ group only. This is electron releasing or repelling group.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20240,'chemistry',55,1234,2971,'Which branched chain isomer of the hydrocarbon with molecular mass ${\\text{72}}\\,\\,{\\text{u}}$  gives only one isomer of mono substituted alkyl halide?','[{"text": "Tertiary butyl chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neopentane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Isohexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neohexane", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20240_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20242,'chemistry',53,1000,2349,'Match Column I with Column II and choose the correct combination from the options given.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{P}$, $\\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$, $\\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{P}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{P}$, $\\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$, $\\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Boron in $\\mathrm{B}(\\mathrm{OH})_{4}^{-}$and $\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}$ is $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ hybridised.<br>Silicon in $\\mathrm{SiO}_{4}^{-4}$ is $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ hybridised.<br>Carbon in fullurenes is $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ hybridised.<br>Al in $\\left[\\mathrm{Al}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{+3}$ is $\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}^{2}$ hybrised.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/53_20242_question_1.png'),
(20243,'chemistry',54,1213,2907,'Polarization of electrons is acrolein may be written as','[{"text": "$\\\\stackrel{8+}{\\\\mathrm{C}} \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CH}-\\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\stackrel{8-}{\\\\mathrm{O}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\stackrel{8+}{\\\\mathrm{C}} \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CH}-\\\\stackrel{8-}{\\\\mathrm{C}} \\\\mathrm{H}=\\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\stackrel{8-}{\\\\mathrm{C}} \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}=\\\\stackrel{8+}{\\\\mathrm{C}} \\\\mathrm{H}-\\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\stackrel{8-}{\\\\mathrm{C}} \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CH}-\\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\stackrel{8+}{\\\\mathrm{O}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{O}$ atom is more electronegative. Hence carries negative charge.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20248,'chemistry',55,1237,2989,'The reactivity of the given alkenes towards hydrogen is &nbsp;','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}&gt;\\\\mathrm{B}&gt;\\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}&gt;\\\\mathrm{C}&gt;\\\\mathrm{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}&gt;\\\\mathrm{A}&gt;\\\\mathrm{B}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}&gt;\\\\mathrm{B}&gt;\\\\mathrm{A}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The reactivity of alkenes towards hydrogen depends on the hyperconjugation effect in the alkene. More the hyperconjugation effect, more is the stability of alkene and lesser is the reactivity. The hyperconjugation effect will depend on the number of alpha hydrogen atoms. "A" has three alpha hydrogen atoms, "B" has six and "C" has nine. The hyperconjugation effect in the given alkene is $\\mathrm{B}&gt;\\mathrm{A}&gt;\\mathrm{C}$. Thus, stability is $\\mathrm{B}&gt;$ $\\mathrm{A}&gt;\\mathrm{C}$ and hence reactivity of alkene with $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is in the order $\\mathrm{C}&gt;\\mathrm{A}&gt;$ $\\mathrm{B}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20248_question_1.png'),
(20250,'chemistry',53,1000,2349,'Find the incorrect match.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{6}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} \\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu}  - \\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} 3{\\\\rm{c}} - 4{\\\\rm{e}}$  bond is present", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}} \\\\right)_6}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu}  - \\\\,$  All carbon atoms are ${\\\\rm{s}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{3}}} - $ hybridized", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{B}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{6}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\, - $  Non-planar", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{B}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{6}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} \\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\, - $  Non-planar", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{B}_{3} \\mathrm{~N}_{3} \\mathrm{H}_{6}$ (Inorganic benzene) is a planar compound.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20251,'chemistry',54,1213,2907,'Which one of the following is the most reactive towards electrophilic attack?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The group showing electron-donating field effect (such as $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2},-\\mathrm{OH}$ ) should stabilise the intermediate ions, i.e., makes the ring more reactive towards electrophilic substitution than benzene and are called activating groups, while electron-withdrawing group (such as $-\\mathrm{Cl}, \\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ ) increases the positive charge on ring, thus deactivates the ring. Hence, phenol is more readily attacked by an electrophile.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20252,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'The addition of $\\mathrm{HCl}$ to an alkene proceeds in two steps. The first step is the attack of ${{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ + }}}$ ion to portion which can be shown as','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "all are possible.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Addition of $\\mathrm{HCl}$ to an alkene follows Markovnikov\'s rule and first step is the capture of proton by electron cloud of $\\pi$-bond   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20252_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20252_question_1.png'),
(20254,'chemistry',53,996,2342,'Which of the following statements are true for zeolites?<br>I. They are formed by the replacement of some of the silicon atoms of the $\\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ lattice by aluminium.<br>II. They are used as catalysts for softening of \'hard\' water.<br>III. They can absorb $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}, \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$, and EtOH<br>IV. They can separate straight chain hydrocarbons from a mixture containing both straight, chain, and branched chain hydrocarbons. Of these statements','[{"text": "I, II, and III are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III, and IV are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II, and IV are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III, and IV are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Zeolites are sodium aluminium silicates having pores (voids) which absorb gases like $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}, \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$, and alcohol (vapours). They are also used to separate hydrocarbons due to their closed packing.<br>Zeolites are used as ion exchangers in softening of hard water.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20255,'chemistry',54,1215,2920,'Which of the following is an example of elimination reaction?','[{"text": "Chlorination of methane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dehydration of ethanol", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Nitration of benzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydroxylation of ethylene", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In elimination reactions one or two molecules are lost from the substrate to form a multiple bond. Dehydration of ethanol is an example of elimination reaction<br>$\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH} \\underset{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}{\\stackrel{\\text { conc }}{\\longrightarrow}} \\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22264,'chemistry',58,168,361,'A solution is obtained by mixing 300g of $25\\% $ solution and 400g of $40\\% $ solution by mass. The mass percentage of the resulting solution is:','[{"text": "$66.66\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.36\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$33.6\\\\% $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$22.4\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Mass percentage of solute in the solution',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20258,'chemistry',53,988,2307,'Consider the following statements for diborane:<br>I. Boron is approximately $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$-hybridized<br>II. $\\mathrm{B}-\\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{B}$ angle is $180^{\\circ}$<br>III. There are two terminal $\\mathrm{B}-\\mathrm{H}$ bonds for each boron atom<br>IV. There are only 12 bonding electrons available. Of these statements','[{"text": "I, II, and IV are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, and III are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III, and IV are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III, and IV are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/53_20258_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>There are 12 bonding electrons. (each boron -3 electrons and each hydrogen -1 electron) There are two bridge bonds $(\\mathrm{B}-\\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{B}$ ) and four $\\mathrm{B}-\\mathrm{H}$ terminal bonds (each \' B \' has two $\\mathrm{B}-\\mathrm{H}$ bonds).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20260,'chemistry',55,1237,2987,'1-Chlorobutane on reaction with alcoholic potash gives','[{"text": "But-1-ene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Butan- 1- ol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Butan-2-one", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Butan - 2 - ol", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathop {{\\text{ClC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ }}}\\limits_{{\\text{1 - Chlorobutane}}} \\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ alc}}{\\text{. KOH}}}}$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{  =  }}\\mathop {{\\text{CH}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ But  -  l  -  ene }}} {\\text{  -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{  -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20262,'chemistry',53,994,2326,'Given below are two statements one is labelled as  (A) and the other is labelled as Reason ${\\rm{(R)}}$.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> All MX ${ }_{4}$ of 14th group except  $\\mathrm{CX}_{4}$, are easily hydrolyzed by water.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In cases other than carbon, the central atom of $\\mathrm{MX}_{4}$ has d-subshell to\naccommodate lone pair of electrons from O atom of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$.\n<br>In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is  incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and is reason incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{CX}_{4}$ does not undergo hydrolysis due to nonavailability of vacant d-orbitals in carbon atom.Rest ${\\text{M}}{{\\text{X}}_{\\text{4}}}$ compounds undergo hydrolysis due to the presence of vacant d-orbitals in group 14 element, which accepts e- pairs of water molecule.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20264,'chemistry',55,1237,2995,'Identify ${\\text{\' Z \'}}$ &nbsp;in the following reaction series,<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{Br}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{NaOH(aq)}}}}{\\text{(X)}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{ heat }}}]{{{\\text{A}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}}}{\\text{(Y)}}$<br>${\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\xrightarrow{{{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{/}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}}{\\text{(Z)}}$','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20264_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20277,'chemistry',53,988,2308,'The strongest Lewis acid among boron halides is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BBr}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BCl}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BI}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BF}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Generally, as the electronegativity increases, acidic character increases, but in case of boron halides, $\\mathrm{BI}_{3}$ is most acidic, i.e. strongest Lewis acid.This is because there is back donation of electrons from halogen atom to boron in$\\mathrm{BX}_{3}(\\mathrm{X}=\\mathrm{F}, \\mathrm{Cl}, \\mathrm{Br}, \\mathrm{I})$This makes the $\\mathrm{BX}_{3}$ less electron deficient. The tendency of back donation decreases as the size of halogen atom increases (due to large energy difference).Hence, due to large size of $\\mathrm{I}, \\mathrm{BI}_{3}$ is most electron deficient and thus, the strongest Lewis acid.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
