(20370,'chemistry',55,1237,2986,'The trans-alkenes are formed by the reduction of alkynes with :','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na} /$ liq. $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Sn}-\\\\mathrm{HCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{Pd} / \\\\mathrm{C}, \\\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Birch reduction gives trans alkene. and ${\\text{Na / liq.}}\\,\\,{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}$ used as a reagent',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20371,'chemistry',54,1214,2911,'Which among the following carbocation is most reactive?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}^{+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{C}^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{CH}_{3}^{+}$carbocation is most reactive, because order of stability of carbocation due to hyperconjugation and inductive effect is, $3^{\\circ}$ carbocation $>2^{\\circ}$ carbocation $>1^{\\circ}$ carbocation. So, $1^{\\circ}$ carbocation is least stable due to this, it is most reactive.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20372,'chemistry',53,986,2296,'Boron exhibits maximum covalency of','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Boron exhibits maximum covalency of four (e.g. ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BH}_{4}^{-}}}$ion).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20374,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'3-Methyl-pent-2-ene on reaction with $\\mathrm{HBr}$ in presence of peroxide forms an addition product. The number of possible stereoisomers for the product is','[{"text": "Six", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Four", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If two chirality centers are created as a result of an addition reaction four stereoisomers can be obtained as products. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20374_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20374_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20374_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20375,'chemistry',54,1213,2910,'Which of the following compounds possesses the "C-H" bond with the lowest bond dissociation energy?','[{"text": "Benzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2, 2-Dimethylpropane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Toluene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "n-Pentane", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Toluene will form the most stable carbocation hence, bond dissociation energy of "C-H" bond will be minimum in case of toluene. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_20375_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20377,'chemistry',55,1237,2995,'Westrosol has the following formula','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Acetylene adds up two molecules of chlorine to give tertachloroethane, known as westron industrially. This on dehydrochlorination with lime water gives trichloro ethene, commerically called westrosol.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20377_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20379,'chemistry',54,1213,2906,'Chloroacetic acid is a stronger acid than acetic acid. This can be explained using','[{"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{M}$ effect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - I}}$ effect", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$+\\\\mathrm{M}$ effect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+ I effect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{ClCH}_{2} \\mathrm{COOH}$ is more acidic than acetic acid due to the -I effect of $\\mathrm{Cl}$. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_20379_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20380,'chemistry',55,1237,2995,'Identify ${\\text{B}}$  and ${\\text{D}}$ in the following sequence of reactions.','[{"text": "Ethanol and alc. $\\\\mathrm{KOH}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Methanol and bromoethene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethyl hydrogen sulphate and alc. $\\\\mathrm{KOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethyl hydrogen sulphate and aq. $\\\\mathrm{KOH}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20380_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20380_question_1.png'),
(20383,'chemistry',54,1213,2910,'Benzonic acid is less acidic than salicylic acid because of','[{"text": "Intermolecular hydrogen bond", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inductive effect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Resonance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20384,'chemistry',55,1237,2991,'Two cyclic dienes ${\\text{A}}$  and ${\\text{B}}$ have molecular formula, $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{8}$. On reductive ozonolysis, A  gives succinaldehyde and glyoxal while ${\\text{B}}$  gives propane-1, 3-dial. A and B on hydrogenation produce only cyclohexane. The total sum of the positions of the double bonds in compounds $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{B}$ is','[{"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{A}}\\,\\,{\\text{and B}}$  on hydrogenation give cyclohexane. This implies that both A and B should be cyclohexadienes. The ozonolysis products suggest the presence of double bonds at 1 and 3-positions in one and at 1 and 4 positions in the other. Hence, ${\\text{A}}\\,\\,{\\text{and B}}$ should be  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20384_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  sum of double bonds position = 1+3+1+4=9   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20384_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20386,'chemistry',54,1213,2910,'A strong base can abstract an $\\alpha$-hydrogen from','[{"text": "Amine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ketone", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Alkane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alkene", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Removal of $\\mathrm{H}$ from ketone gives resonance stabilised carbanion. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_20386_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
