(20418,'chemistry',55,1234,2973,'When 2-methylbutane is chlorinated, the percentage of $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}$ is ____<br> Assume reactivity ratio of<br>$3^\\circ {\\text{H}}:2^\\circ {\\text{H}}:1^\\circ {\\text{H}} = 5:3.8:1$','[{"text": "$28 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$35 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$23 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$14 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Chlorination of 2-methylbutane gives 4 monochlorinated products :  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20418_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    The reactivity ratio of $3^{\\circ} \\mathrm{H}: 2^{\\circ} \\mathrm{H}: 1^{\\circ} \\mathrm{H}$ towards chlorination is $5: 3.8: 1$<br>Compound reactivity $\\times$ Probability $=$ Number of parts(1) $1 \\times 6=6$(2) $5 \\times 1=5$(3) $3.8 \\times 2=7.6$(4) $1 \\times 3=3$<br>Total number of parts $=21.6$<br>Percentage of $\\mathrm{D}=\\dfrac{3}{21.6} \\times 100=14 \\%$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20422,'chemistry',55,1234,2971,'Which of the following will give three monobromo derivatives?<br>(A) $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$<br>(B) $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right) \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$<br>(C) $\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{C}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$<br>(D) $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right) \\mathrm{CH}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right) \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$','[{"text": "A, B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B, C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, C", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "B, D", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${{\\text{1}}^{{\\text{st}}}}{\\text{ and  }}{{\\text{6}}^{{\\text{th}}}}$  carbon will give same substitute.<br>${{\\text{2}}^{{\\text{nd}}}}{\\text{ and  }}{{\\text{5}}^{{\\text{th}}}}$  carbon will give same substitute.<br>${{\\text{3}}^{{\\text{ rd }}}}{\\text{and  }}{{\\text{4}}^{{\\text{ th}}}}$ carbon will give same substitute.<br>$1+1+1=3$  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20422_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    $1^{\\circ}$ carbon attached with $2^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ will give one derivative. Three $1^{\\circ}$ carbons attached with $4^{\\circ}$ carbon will give same one derivative $\\&$ one derivative will be from $2^{\\circ}$ carbon.<br>$1+1+1=3($ total $)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
