(20547,'chemistry',55,1239,3001,'The maximum number of carbon atoms arranged linearly in the molecule, $\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{C} \\equiv \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{CH}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ is','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20547_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20550,'chemistry',54,1210,2898,'Total number of optically active forms in molecules with \' ${\\text{n}}$ \' number of asymmetric $\\mathrm{C}$ - atoms and which are not divisible into two equal halves is','[{"text": "${{\\\\text{2}}^{\\\\text{n}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{2}}^{{\\\\text{n - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{2}}^{\\\\frac{{{\\\\text{(n - 1)}}}}{{\\\\text{2}}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{2}}^{\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{\\\\text{n}}}{{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{ - 1}}} \\\\right)}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Total number of optically active forms in molecules with \' $\\mathrm{n}$ \' number of asymmetric $\\mathrm{C}$ atoms and which are not divisible into two equal halves is $2^{\\mathrm{n}}$.<br>When molecules are divisible into two equal halves where, $\\mathrm{n}=$ even number, then number of optically active forms is $2^{\\mathrm{n}-1}$.<br>If $\\mathrm{n}=$ odd number then number, of optically active forms is ${{\\text{2}}^{\\left( {\\frac{{{\\text{n - 1}}}}{{\\text{2}}}} \\right)}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20551,'chemistry',55,1237,2995,'Which reaction proceeds as shown indicate major product?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'NBS undergoes allylic substitution.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20552,'chemistry',53,988,2304,'The number of neutrons present in the more abundant isotope of boron is x . Amorphous boron upon heating with air forms a product, in which the oxidation state of boron is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{y}}}$. The value of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{x+y}}}$ is','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The most abundant isotope of boron is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{{ }_{5}^{11} \\mathrm{~B}}}}$.<br> No. of neutrons in it ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{=11-5=6(\\mathrm{x})}}}$<br>Reactions of boron with air<br>(i) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{~B}_{(\\mathrm{s})}+\\mathrm{N}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} 2 \\mathrm{BN}_{(\\mathrm{s})}}}}$<br>(ii) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{4 \\mathrm{~B}_{(\\mathrm{s})}+3 \\mathrm{O}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{~B}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3(\\mathrm{~s})}}}}$<br>Oxidation state of boron in both BN and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}}}}$ is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{+3(\\mathrm{y})}}}$<br>So, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{x+y=6+3=9}}}$<br>So, the correct option is (4)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20554,'chemistry',54,1210,2898,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The following molecule is non-superimposable on its mirror image, hence it is chiral. https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764953659/preptracker/questions/q_20554_col1_1764953658924.jpg<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> All chiral molecules have chiral centres.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Molecules having perpendicular disymmetric plane are supposed to have chirality without having chiral centre. e.g. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_20554_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Given molecule is chiral due to four different groups attached to carbon atom. Hence it is optically active.<br>Hence,  Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20555,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'Product (B) of the given reaction is :','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Me}_{2} \\\\mathrm{C}(\\\\mathrm{OH}) \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Me}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HO}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right) \\\\mathrm{Me}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20555_question_1.png'),
(20556,'chemistry',53,986,2295,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In group 13, the stability +1 oxidation state increases down the group.</div><div class="column-right">The atomic size of gallium is greater than that of aluminium.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Due to inert pair effect, stability of +1 oxidation state increases down the group 13 elements.<br>The atomic size of aluminium is greater than that of gallium ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{Tl}>\\mathrm{In}>\\mathrm{Al}>\\mathrm{Ga}>\\mathrm{B})}}}$<br>${\\rm{Atomic \\,radius}}\\,{\\rm{(pm):}}\\mathop {{\\rm{Tl}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{(107)}}} {\\rm{ > }}\\mathop {{\\rm{In}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{(167)}}} {\\rm{ > }}\\mathop {{\\rm{Al}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{(143)}}} {\\rm{ > }}\\mathop {{\\rm{Ga}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{(135)}}} {\\rm{ > }}\\mathop {\\rm{B}}\\limits_{{\\rm{(88)}}} $<br>So, the correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20558,'chemistry',54,1213,2910,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A compound with delocalised electrons is more stable than that compound would be if all its electrons were localised.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The extra stability, a compound gains as a result of having delocalised electrons, is called delocalisation energy.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Delocalisation of electrons provide more stability to the compounds. The extra stability which is acquired by a compound as a result of resonance (delocalisation of electrons) is called resonance energy or delocalisation energy. Hence, Assertion and Reason both are correct but Reason is not the required explanation of Assertion.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20559,'chemistry',55,1237,2989,'$2.8 \\mathrm{~g}$ of pure alkene containing only one double bond per molecule, react completely with $8 \\mathrm{~g}$ of bromine (in an inert solvent). What is the molecular formula of the alkene?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\\\mathrm{H}_{8}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{3} \\\\mathrm{H}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{12}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$8 \\mathrm{gBr}_{2}$ react with $2.8 \\mathrm{~g}$ of alkene<br>$\\therefore 160 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ react with $56 \\mathrm{~g}$ alkene<br>Thus the molecular wt. of alkene is 56 which indicates $\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{8}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20561,'chemistry',53,987,2302,'The correct statements from the following are<br>(I) The decreasing order of atomic radii of group 13 elements is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Tl}>\\mathrm{In}>\\mathrm{Ga}>\\mathrm{Al}>\\mathrm{B}}}}$.<br>(II) Down the group 13, electronegativity decreases from top to bottom.<br>(III) Al dissolves in dil. HCl and liberates ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}}}$ but conc. ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}}}$ renders Al passive by forming a protective oxide layer on the surface.<br>(IV) All elements of group 13 exhibits highly stable +1 oxidation state.<br>(V) Hybridisation of Al in ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Al}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}}}}$ ion is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}^{2}}}}$<br >Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "(I), (II), (III) and (V) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (III) and (V) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(III) and (V) only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(I) and (III) only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'(I) The decreasing order of atomic radii of group 13 elements is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Tl}>\\mathrm{In}>\\mathrm{Al}>\\mathrm{Ga}>\\mathrm{B}}}}$. The presence of additional 10 d -electrons offer poor screening for the outer electron in gallium. As a result the atomic radius of Ga is lesser than that of Al.<br>(II) Down the group electronegativity first decreases from B to Al and then increases marginally. ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{B}(2.0), \\mathrm{Al}(1.5), \\mathrm{Ga}(1.6)}}}$, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{In}(1.7)}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Tl}(1.8)}}}$<br>(IV) The relative stability of +1 oxidation state progressively increases for heavier elements.Thus in ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Tl},+1}}}$ oxidation state is predominant and for Al it is +3 . So, +1 oxidation state is highly stable for Tl only.<br>So, the correct option is (3)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20562,'chemistry',54,1212,2904,'Among the following the strongest nucleophile is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{SH}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COO}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NCCH}_{2}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'More the electron density on a species better nucleophile it is. $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{SH}$ is a weak acid so it readily loses a proton to form strong conjugate base, $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{~S}^{-}$, which is a strong nucleophile.<br>(+I effect of $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}$ group).<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20563,'chemistry',55,1237,2993,'The treatment of propene with $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ at $500-600^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ produces :','[{"text": "1, 2-Dichloropropene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Allyl chloride", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2, 3-Dichloropropene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1, 3-Dichloropropene", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At high temperature, $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ does not add to alkenes.<br>Chlorine molecule undergoes homolytic cleavage at high temperature forming $\\mathrm{Cl}$ radicals with initiate  substitution reaction at the allylic position. Therefore, when propene reacts with  $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ at high temperature,allyl chloride is formed.${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ - CH = C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ + C}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}\\xrightarrow{{500 - 600^\\circ {\\text{C}}}}$<br>${\\text{Cl -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ -  CH  =  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20565,'chemistry',54,1214,2911,'The decreasing order of the stability of ions,<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3} - \\mathop {\\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits^ +  }\\limits_{\\text{I}} {\\text{H}} - {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{    C}}{{\\text{H}}_3} - \\mathop {\\mathop {\\text{CH}}\\limits^ +  }\\limits_{{\\text{II}}}  - {\\text{OC}}{{\\text{H}}_3}$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3} - \\mathop {\\mathop {\\text{CH}}\\limits^ +   - {\\text{COC}}{{\\text{H}}_3}}\\limits_{{\\text{ III }}} $','[{"text": "I $>$ II $>$ III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III $>$ II $>$ I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II $>$ III $>$ I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II $>$ I $>$ III<br>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Stability of carbocation $\\alpha {\\text{ +  M,  + I}}\\,\\,{\\text{and }}\\alpha \\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{{\\text{EWG}}}}{\\text{,}}\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{{\\text{ - M}}}}{\\text{,}}\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{{\\text{ - I}}}}$  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_20565_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20566,'chemistry',53,986,2294,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Gallium is used in the manufacturing of thermometers.</div><div class="column-right">A thermometer containing gallium is useful for measuring the freezing point ( 256 K ) of brine solution.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Gallium is used in manufacturing of high temperature thermometers.<br>Statement B is incorrect as gallium remains as solid at 256 K temperature, so can\'t be used to measure freezing point of brine solution. So, the correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20567,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'Addition of $\\mathrm{HI}$ on double bond of propene yields isopropyl iodide as the major product, because addition proceeds through','[{"text": "A more stable carbocation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A more stable carbanion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A more stable free radical", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A more stable carbene", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20567_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20569,'chemistry',54,1214,2911,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Allyl and benzyl carbonium ions are more stable than propyl carbonium ion.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electron releasing group stabilises carbonium ion.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The stability of carbonium ions is influenced by both resonance and inductive effect. Allyl and benzyl carbonium ions are more stable than propyl carbocation due to resonance but not because of inductive effect.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2012,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20570,'chemistry',53,993,2323,'Which of the following material is not a semiconductor?','[{"text": "Copper oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Graphite", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Silicon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Germanium", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Graphite is a conductor. Rest given are semiconductors.So, the correct option is (2)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20571,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'Arrange the following compounds in increasing order of reactivity towards the addition of $\\mathrm{HBr}$.<br>${\\text{RCH}} = {\\text{CHR}},{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_2} = {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_2},{{\\text{R}}_2}{\\text{C}}$<br>$ = {\\text{CHR}},{{\\text{R}}_2}{\\text{C}} = {\\text{C}}{{\\text{R}}_2}$','[{"text": "${\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_2} = {\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_2} &lt; {\\\\text{RCH}} = {\\\\text{CHR}} &lt; {{\\\\text{R}}_2}{\\\\text{C}}$<br>$ = {\\\\text{CHR}} &lt; {{\\\\text{R}}_2}{\\\\text{C}} = {\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{R}}_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{R}}_2}{\\\\text{C}} = {\\\\text{CHR}} &lt; {\\\\text{RCH}} = {\\\\text{CHR}} &lt; {\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_2}$<br>$ = {\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_2} &lt; {{\\\\text{R}}_2}{\\\\text{C}} = {\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{R}}_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{RCH}} = {\\\\text{CHR}} &lt; {{\\\\text{R}}_2}{\\\\text{C}} = {\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{R}}_2} &lt; {{\\\\text{R}}_2}{\\\\text{C}}$<br>$ = {\\\\text{CHR}} &lt; {\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_2} = {\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{R}}_2}{\\\\text{C}} = {\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{R}}_2} &lt; {{\\\\text{R}}_2}{\\\\text{C}} = {\\\\text{CHR}} &lt; {\\\\text{RCH}}$<br>$ = {\\\\text{CHR}} &lt; {\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_2} = {\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Alkyl groups on double bond increases electron density on double bond which is easy target for electrophiles. Hence alkyl substituted alkenes are more reactive towards $\\mathrm{HBr}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20573,'chemistry',54,1214,2911,'The most stable carbocation is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Tropilium carbocation is aromatic $(4 n+2 \\pi$ electrons $)$. So, it is maximum stable.<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20574,'chemistry',55,1237,2989,'Here $\\mathrm{X}$ and $\\mathrm{Y}$ are','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{ClCH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}, \\\\mathrm{ClCH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{ClCH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}, \\\\mathrm{ClCH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\\\mathrm{C}=\\\\mathrm{CCl}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{ClCH}=\\\\mathrm{CHCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\\\mathrm{C}=\\\\mathrm{CCl}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{ClCH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{X}: \\mathrm{Cl}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{Cl}$<br>$\\mathrm{Y}: \\mathrm{HO}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{Cl}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20574_question_1.png'),
(20575,'chemistry',53,993,2323,'Evaluate the following statements related to group 14 elements for their correctness.<br>(I) Covalent radius decreases down the group from C to Pb in a regular manner.<br>(II) Electronegativity decreases from C to Pb down the group gradually.<br>(III) Maximum covalance of carbon is 4 whereas other elements can expand their covalance due to presence of d orbitals.<br>(IV) Heavier elements do not form ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{p} \\pi-\\mathrm{p} \\pi}}}$ bonds.<br>(V) Carbon can exhibit negative oxidation states. <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "(I), (II) and (III) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(III) and (IV) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (II) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(III), (IV) and (V) only", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Covalent radius increases down the group. Electronegativity of group 14 elements decreases from C to Si , then remains constant to ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Sn} .(\\mathrm{C}-2.5)(\\mathrm{Si}, \\mathrm{Ge}, \\mathrm{Sn}-1.8)(\\mathrm{Pb}-}}}$ 1.9). So, the correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20577,'chemistry',54,1214,2913,'Compare stability of free radicals.<br>(I) $\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\dot{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ (III) ${ }^{\\cdot} \\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2}$<br>(IV) $\\dot{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$','[{"text": "II $>$ I $>$ III $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II $>$ I $>$ IV $>$ III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I $>$ II $>$ III $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV $>$ III $>$ I $>$ II", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Among the given free radicals, Benzyl free radical  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_20577_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_20577_question_1.jpg'),
(20578,'chemistry',55,1237,2992,'Propene $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\right)$ can be converted into 1-propanol by oxidation. Which set of reagents among the following is ideal to affect the conversion?','[{"text": "Alkaline $\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{B}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{6}$ and alk. $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}_{3} /$ zinc dust", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{OsO}_{4} / \\\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20578_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20579,'chemistry',53,993,2321,'The number of ions from the following that are expected to behave as oxidising agents is :<br>${\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{4 + }}}}{\\rm{,S}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{,P}}{{\\rm{b}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{,T}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\rm{,P}}{{\\rm{b}}^{{\\rm{4 + }}}}{\\rm{,T}}{{\\rm{l}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{ }}$','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}}}}$ is more stable than ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Pb}^{4-}}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Tl}^{+}}}}$is more stable than ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Tl}^{3+}}}}$ due to inert pair effect. Thus, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Pb}^{4-}}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Tl}^{3+}}}}$ act as oxidising agents and themselves get reduced to ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Tl}^{3+}}}}$ respectively. So, the correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20581,'chemistry',54,1215,2918,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Rate of hydrolysis of methyl chloride to methanol is higher in DMF than in water.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Hydrolysis of methyl chloride follows second order kinetics.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Primary alkyl halides (here $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Cl}$ ) undergo $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ reaction. It is a second order reaction.<br>$\\text { Rate } \\alpha[\\mathrm{RX}]\\left[\\mathrm{Nu}^{-}\\right]$<br>Hence, hydrolysis of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Cl}$ is favoured by non - polar medium or polar non - protic solvent (in which proton is not generated) like DMF.<br>$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1$ reactions are favoured by polar solvents like water.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20582,'chemistry',55,1237,2991,'Which of the following compounds does not evolve $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas, when undergo oxidative ozonolysis?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{C  =  CH  -  CH  =  C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Terminal double bonds $=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ will only gets oxidized to $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20583,'chemistry',53,986,2295,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The stability order of +1 oxidation state of Ga , In and Tl is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ga} < \\mathrm{In} < \\mathrm{Tl}}}}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The inert pair effect stabilizes the lower oxidation state down the group.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Down the group, lower oxidation state stability increases due to inert pair effect.So, given both Assertion and Reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation. So, the correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
