(20869,'chemistry',54,1205,2876,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Two members of a homologous series have similar chemical properties.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Propane is a member of homologous series.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A homologous series is a collection of compounds with the same general formula that differ only in the carbon chain length. Compounds in a homologous series often have a fixed set of functional groups, resulting in chemical and physical properties that are comparable. Propane is a member of homologuus series of alkane. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20871,'chemistry',55,1234,2971,'A hydrocarbon $\\mathrm{A}(\\mathrm{VD}=36)$ forms only one monochloro substitution product. A will be :','[{"text": "Iso-pentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neo-pentane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cyclohexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Methyl-cyclohexane<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Molar mass $2 \\times {\\text{VD  =  2}} \\times {\\text{36  =  72}}$ Molar mass of 72 corresponds to pentane &amp; its isomers. As only one monochloro substitution is formed, the molecule must be highly symmetrical. Out of the given molecule, neo pentane satisfy the monocloro substitution.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20873,'chemistry',54,1207,2880,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The IUPAC name of the given compound is 3-Methylcyclohexene and not Methyl cyclohex-2-ene. https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764953718/preptracker/questions/q_20873_col1_1764953717962.png<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In numbering, double bonded carbon atoms get preference to the alkyl group in cycloalkenes.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_20873_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Double bond is given .preference over the substitution while numbering. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21023,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'Which of the following alkyl halide is treated with sodium metal to obtain<br>2,2,3,3 - Tetramethyl butane?','[{"text": "tert-Butyl bromide", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "n-Propyl bromide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sec-Butyl bromide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "n-Butyl bromide", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'It is Wurtz reaction.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21023_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20875,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'Which of the following compounds cannot be prepared by Wurtz reaction?<br>(A) $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$<br>(B) (C) $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{CHCH}_{3}$<br>(D) $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}, \\\\mathrm{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{B}, \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}, \\\\mathrm{D}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The Wurtz reaction is generally used to form symmetrical alkanes because different alkyl halides such as ${\\text{RX }}\\,\\,{\\text{& }}\\,\\,{\\text{R\' X}}$ form a mixture of ${\\text{RR, R\'R\'  &  RR\'}}{\\text{.}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20875_question_1.png'),
(20877,'chemistry',54,1207,2880,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> IUPAC name of https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764953719/preptracker/questions/q_20877_col1_1764953718929.png<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In polyfunctional group, the substituent should be given higher number than the principal functional group.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In compounds containing the carboxyl functional group $(-\\mathrm{COOH})$, the carboxyl carbon is given the lowest possible number.The given compound is a butanoic acid with the "- $\\mathrm{COOH}$ " carbon as the first carbon. There is a methyl substitution on the third carbon. Therefore, it is named as 3-methylbutanoic acid. So, the option<br>(1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20879,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'Methane can not be prepared by','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Al}_{4} \\\\mathrm{C}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Be}_{2} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CaC}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CS}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Methane cannot be prepared by $\\mathrm{CaC}_{2}$. It on hydrolysis produces acetylene. Remaining other carbides produce alkanes.<br>${\\text{A}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{4}}}{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ + 12}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}} \\to {\\text{4Al(OH}}{{\\text{)}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ + 3C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}$<br>${\\text{B}}{{\\text{e}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C + 2}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}} \\to {\\text{2BeO + C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{S}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ + 2}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{ - 4C}}{{\\text{u}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}}]{{{\\text{ + 8Cu}}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}$<br>${\\text{Ca}}{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ + 2}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}} \\to \\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits^{\\text{.}} {\\text{a(OH}}{{\\text{)}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ + }}{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20881,'chemistry',54,1207,2881,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The IUPAC name for $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}=\\mathrm{CH}-\\mathrm{C} \\equiv \\mathrm{CH}$ is pent-3-en-1-yne and not pent-2-en-4-yne.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> While deciding the locants for double and triple bonds, lowest sum rule is always followed.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When both double and triple bonds, are present the lowest sum rule is applied for numbering locants (positions). The sum of the locants of the double and triple bonds when named as pent-3-ene-1-yne is $(1+3)=4$, while the sum of the locants for the other way of naming is $(2+4)=6$. Following the lowest sum rule, the compound is named as pent-3-en-1yne. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20883,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'Methyl bromide is heated with zinc in closed tube produces','[{"text": "Methane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ethylene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "methanol", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\begin{gathered}\n  {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ -  Br  +  2Zn  +  Br  -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}} \\to  \\hfill \\\\\n  {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ -  Zn  -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ +  ZnB}}{{\\text{r}}_{\\text{2}}} \\hfill \\\\\n  {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ -  Zn  -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ +  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ -  Br}} \\to  \\hfill \\\\\n  \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ +  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ZnBr}} \\hfill \\\\\n  \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{Ethane}} \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20885,'chemistry',54,1201,2869,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> neo-Hydrocarbons contain a quaternary carbon atom.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Whenever a carbon atom is bonded to four carbon atoms it is quatermary.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Neo-hydrocarbons are a specific class of hydrocarbons that contain a quaternary carbon atom. This quaternary carbon atom is bonded to four other carbon atoms. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20887,'chemistry',55,1234,2970,'The increasing order of stability of the alkanes with the molecular formula ${{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{5}}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{12}}}}}$ is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The stability generally increases with increase in branched chain. Thus increasing order of stability is: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20887_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20889,'chemistry',54,1207,2882,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764953721/preptracker/questions/q_20889_col1_1764953720491.png<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Cyclopentane is a cyclic compound.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If the number of carbon atoms in the alkyl group is greater than the number of carbon atoms in the ring, the organic compound is named as a cycloalkyl-substituted alkane. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_20889_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Thus, the given name is correct. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20891,'chemistry',55,1234,2967,'IUPAC name of the following compound is<br>$\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{C}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{2}$','[{"text": "3, 3 - Dimethylpentane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2, 2, 4, 4-Tetramethylpentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3, 3-Di-tert-butyl-2, 2, 4, 4- tetramethylpentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Diethyldimethylmethane", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20895,'chemistry',55,1234,2974,'Which of the following has lowest octane number?','[{"text": "Iso-octane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "n-Heptane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "n-Hexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "n-Hexadecane", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Longer the straight chain alkane, lower is the octane number. Thus, n-hexadecane has even lower octane number than n-heptane.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20899,'chemistry',55,1234,2973,'Which of the following is not true for alkanes?','[{"text": "Are characterised by their general less reactivity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Do not show isomerism as they are saturated molecules", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Undergo substitution reactions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Can be prepared by heating the sodium salt of the corresponding acid with soda lime called decarboxylation", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Alkanes are saturated molecules but they show chain and position isomerism.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20937,'chemistry',55,1244,3025,'Which one of the following has the minimum boiling point?','[{"text": "n-butane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1-butyne", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1-butene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iso - butene", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'iso-butene  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20937_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> possesses branching, thus, minimum van der Waals\' force of attraction are present in it.<br>Hence, possesses minimum boiling point.<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2012,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20903,'chemistry',55,1234,2973,'Which of the following statements are not correct for alkane?<br>(A) All $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$ and $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}$ bonds have a length of ${\\text{1}}{\\text{.112}}\\mathop {\\text{A}}\\limits^{\\text{o}} {\\text{and 1}}{\\text{.54}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\mathop {\\text{A}}\\limits^{\\text{o}} $ respectively<br>(B) All bond angles are tetrahedral, having a value of $109^{\\circ} 28^{\\prime}$<br>(C) The $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}$ chain is linear and not zigzag<br>(D) All alkanes exhibit chain isomerism','[{"text": "(A), (B)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(B), (C)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(C) ,(D)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(B), (D)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}$ chain in alkane is zigzag  & ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{, }}{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{6}}}{\\text{ &  }}{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{3}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{8}}}$ do not exhibit chain isomerism',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20907,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'Weight (g) of two moles of the organic compound, which is obtained by heating sodium ethanoate with sodium hydroxide in presence of calcium oxide is:','[{"text": "32", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "30", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "18", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COONa  +  NaOH}}\\xrightarrow[\\Delta ]{{{\\text{CaO}}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{ + N}}{{\\text{a}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\text{2}}\\,\\,{\\text{moles}}\\,{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{ =  2}} \\times {\\text{16  =  32}}\\,{\\text{g}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20911,'chemistry',55,1240,3015,'Consider the following compounds/species: The number of compounds/species which obey Huckel\'s rule is','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Huckle\'s rule  ${\\text{ = (4n + 2)}}\\,\\pi $  electrons compounds (i), (ii), (v), (vii) obey Huckle\'s rule. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20911_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20911_question_1.png'),
(20914,'chemistry',55,1240,3015,'Among the following: The set which represents aromatic species is','[{"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III, IV and V", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'I, II, IV obey Huckel\'s rule and have<br>conjugation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20914_question_1.png'),
(20918,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'3-phenylpropene on reaction with $\\mathrm{HBr}$ gives (as a major product)','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}(\\\\mathrm{Br}) \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{CH}(\\\\mathrm{Br}) \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Br}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{CH}(\\\\mathrm{Br}) \\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The addition of $\\mathrm{HBr}$ on unsymmetrical alkene takes place on the basis of Markownikoff\'s addition."The negative part of the addendum is added to that carbon atom which has lesser number of $\\mathrm{H}$-atom."<br>$\\mathop {{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{6}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{5}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{CH}}}\\limits_{{\\text{3 - phenylpropane}}} {\\text{ = C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ + HBr}} \\to $  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20918_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20922,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Addition of $\\mathrm{HBr}$ on 2-butene gives two isomeric products.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Addition of $\\mathrm{HBr}$ on 2-butene follows Markownikoff\'s rule','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As 2-butene is a symmetrical molecule, thus it does not give any isomer and also Markownikoff\'s rule is not applicable in this case. (applicable for unsymmetrical alkene).<br>Hence, both Assertion and Reason are incorrect',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20929,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'Formation of alcohol by oxymercuration demercuration of alkenes','[{"text": "Involves carbocations and rearrangement", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Involves carbanions and rearrangement", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Is stereospecific", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Does not involve rearrangement and carbocation.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Oxymercuration-demercuration takes place in the following steps:<br>(I) Generation of an electrophile (mercurinium ion)<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3} - {\\text{COO}} - {\\text{Hg}} - {\\text{OCOC}}{{\\text{H}}_3}$<br>$ \\to \\mathop {\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{COO}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ (electrophile) }}}  - {\\text{H}}{{\\text{g}}^ + } + {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{CO}}{{\\text{O}}^ - }}\\limits_{} $<br>(II) Attack of electrophile on double bond of alkene <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20929_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> (III) Addition of water <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20929_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  (IV) Reduction of ${\\text{C - HgOCOC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}$ bond <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20929_explanation_3.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Hence, oxymercuration-demercuration process involves reduction and bridged mercurinium ion formation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20933,'chemistry',55,1237,2995,'The compound which is obtained by treating chloropropane with alcoholic $\\mathrm{KOH}$, when reacts with ${\\text{B}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{/ THF}}$ followed by acetic acid gives','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}(\\\\mathrm{OH}) \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CHOHCH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{Cl}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Chloropropane}}} \\xrightarrow[{{\\text{ - KCl; - }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}]{{{\\text{Alc}}{\\text{.KOH}}}}$<br>$\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CH = C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{propene}}} \\xrightarrow{{{\\text{B}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{/THF}}}}$<br>${{\\text{(C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{)}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{B}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COOH}}}}\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Propane}}} $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20943,'chemistry',55,1237,2986,'Correct order for reaction with alcoholic $\\mathrm{KOH}$.','[{"text": "A $>$ B $>$ C $>$ D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A $>$ C $>$ B $>$ D", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "D $>$ B $>$ C $>$ A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A $>$ D $>$ B $>$ C", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When alcoholic $\\mathrm{KOH}$ reacts with haloakanes, the reaction (rate) depends on the following factor:<br>(I) Size of halogen<br>(II) Degree of C - atom, to whcih the halogen atom is bonded, i.e. more the degree of haloalkane, more is its reactivity.<br>As, bromine atom is large in size, thus   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20943_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Hence, correct order is A > B > C > D<br>Thus, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20943_question_1.jpg'),
(20945,'chemistry',54,1207,2881,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{NC}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{COOH}$ is called 3 cyanopropanoic acid while $\\mathrm{OHC}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{COOH}$ is called 4-oxobutanoic acid.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> While naming polyfunctional compounds, $-\\mathrm{COOH}$ group gets preference over cyano and oxo groups.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In IUPAC nomenclature the longest carbon chain that includes - $\\mathrm{COOH}$ group is identified and the carboxylic carbon is given the count 1 .<br>In $\\mathrm{NC}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{COOH}$, the longest carbon chain containing the carboxylic acid group is the threecarbon chain and the $-\\mathrm{COOH}$ group is at carbon 1. The nitrile (cyano) group is at carbon 3 . So, the name is 3-cyanopropanoic acid. In $\\mathrm{OHC}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{COOH}$, the longest carbon chain is the four-carbon chain with carboxylic acid group is at carbon 1 . The keto (oxo) group is at carbon 4. So, the name is 4-oxobutanoic acid. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20947,'chemistry',55,1239,3004,'Which of the following gives propyne on hydrolysis?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Al}_{4} \\\\mathrm{C}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mg}_{2} \\\\mathrm{C}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{B}_{4} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{La}_{4} \\\\mathrm{C}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Magnesium carbide on hydrolysis gives propyne,<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20947_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20949,'chemistry',54,1214,2914,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Methylene has sextet of electrons around carbon but bears no formal charge.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Methylene behaves as a nucleophile.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Methylene: $\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ behaves as an electrophile since it needs two more electrons to complete its octet.Thus option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20951,'chemistry',55,1244,3025,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Alkynes are more reactive towards nucleophilic addition reaction as compared to alkenes.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Alkynes contain two pi bonds, while alkenes have only one bond.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20951_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Although there are more $\\pi$ - electrons associated with the triple bond, the sp hybridised carbon exert a strong attraction for these $\\pi$-electrons which are consequently bonded more tightly to the functional group than the $\\pi$-electrons of a double bond. Hence, a nucleophile attack occur more readily on triple bonded carbon atom.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20952,'chemistry',54,1201,2868,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The greater the ${\\text{s}}$ -character of the hybrid orbitals, greater is the electronegativity.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The change in hybridisation is associated with the change in electronegativity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When the $s$-character of the hybrid orbital is high it will be closer to the nucleus and will have a greater tendency to attract the electrons and exhibits greater electronegativity. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20954,'chemistry',55,1239,3007,'With ammonical cuprous chloride solution, a reddish brown precipitate is obtained on treating with','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{3} \\\\mathrm{H}_{6}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Cuprous chloride solution with ammonia is known as ammonical cuprous chloride solution. All terminal alkynes behaves as very weak acids. Ammonical cuprous chloride reacts with acetylene to form reddish brown precipitate.<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{HC} \\equiv \\mathrm{CH}+\\mathrm{Cu}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{OH} \\longrightarrow \\\\\\mathrm{Cu}-\\mathrm{C} \\equiv \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{Cu} \\downarrow+2 \\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\\\\\\\\\text { cuprous acetylide } \\\\\\\\\\quad \\text { (Reddish brown precipitate) }\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20956,'chemistry',54,1201,2868,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The ${\\text{sp}}$ hybrid orbital contains more ${\\text{s}}$ character and hence it is closer to its nucleus.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Hybridisation has no effect on the bond length and bond enthalpy in organic compounds.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Hybridisation influences the bond length and bond enthalpy (strength) in organic compounds. Thus, sp hybridised carbon atom having hybrid orbitals with $50 \\% \\mathrm{~s}$ character is more electronegative than $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ or $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ hybridised carbon atoms. So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20958,'chemistry',55,1239,3009,'1-butyne on oxidation with hot alkaline $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ would yield which of the following as end product?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}+\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}+\\\\mathrm{HCOOH}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'1-butyne or oxidation with alk. $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_2} \\cdot {\\text{C}} \\equiv {\\text{CH}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ Hot KMn}}{{\\text{O}}_4}{\\text{ (alk) }}}}$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{COOH}} + {\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_2} + {{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{O}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20960,'chemistry',54,1213,2906,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Polarisation of one $\\sigma$ bond caused by the polarisation of adjacent $\\sigma$ bond is referred to as the inductive effect.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The substituents can be classified as electron withdrawing or electron donating groups relative to hydrogen.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When a covalent bond is formed between atoms of different electronegativity, the bonding $\\sigma$ electrons are shifted towards the more electronegative atom. This is known as inductive effect and this unequal sharing of electrons results in polar covalent bond.Based on electron attracting ability as compared to hydrogen, groups are classified as $+\\mathrm{I} \\&-\\mathrm{I}$. Therfore, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21011,'chemistry',55,1234,2970,'Which isomer of $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{14}$ has highest boiling point?','[{"text": "Hexane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3-Methylpentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-Methylpentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,2-Dimethylbutane", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Straight chain isomer, i.e., Hexane has highest boiling point as compared to branched chain isomers of $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{14}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20962,'chemistry',55,1240,3015,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Cyclopentadienyl anion is much more stable than allyl anion.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Cyclopentadienyl anion is aromatic in character.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Cyclopentadienyl anion is more stable than allyl anion as the former is aromatic and is stabilised by resonance. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20962_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Thus, Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is correct explanation of Assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20964,'chemistry',54,1212,2902,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Heterolytic fission involves the breaking of a covalent bond in such a way that both the electrons of the shared pair are carried away by one of the atoms<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Heterolytic fission occurs readily in polar covalent bonds.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Heterolytic fission occurs when the two atoms differ considerably in their electronegativities and shared pair of electrons is carried by more electronegative atom. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20966,'chemistry',55,1240,3015,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Tropylium cation is aromatic in nature.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The only property that determines its aromatic behaviour is planar structure.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20966_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> is aromatic, because  it contains  $(4 n+2) \\pi$ electrons and planar structure are essential conditions for aromaticity.<br>Thus, Assertion is correct but Reason  is incorrect.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20968,'chemistry',54,1214,2911,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The stability of carbonium ion is influenced by both resonance and inductive effect.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> More the number of alkyl groups, greater the dispersal of positive charge and more is the stability of carbonium ion observed.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The stability of carbonium ions is influenced by both resonance and inductive effects. The reason is also true, as more alkyl groups lead to greater dispersal of positive charge, enhancing the stability of the carbonium ion. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20970,'chemistry',55,1240,3018,'The treatment of benzene with iso-butene in the presence of sulphuric acid gives.','[{"text": "iso - butyl benzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "tert-butyl benzene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "n-butyl benzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No reaction<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Benzene reacts with iso-butene in the presence of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ to produce $\\mathrm{t}$-butyl benzene. The reaction takes place as follows : <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20970_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20972,'chemistry',54,1214,2913,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Free radicals are short lived and highly reactive.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Free radicals are highly unstable.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Since free radicals contain odd electrons, they are short lived and readily try to pair up these odd electrons to form neutral molecules. This is why they are highly reactive. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20974,'chemistry',55,1240,3018,'Correct order of electrophillic substitution reaction is','[{"text": "A $>$ B $>$ C $>$ D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "D $>$ B $>$ A $>$ C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B $>$ A $>$ C $>$ D", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "B $>$ A $>$ D $>$ C", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Electron donating group when bonded with benzene ring will activate the ring due to $(+\\mathrm{I})$ effect and show better electrophilic substitution reaction, whereas electron withdrawing groups show $(- \\mathrm{I})$ effect, will decreases the nature of electrophilic substitution<br>(1) Here, alkyl group (i.e. $-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ ) show $(+\\mathrm{I})$ effect, but lesser than $-\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$ due to presence of oxygen atom, thus $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$ show better electrophilic substitution than that of $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$.<br>(2) Chlorine, i.e. $\\mathrm{Cl}$ show $-\\mathrm{I}$ and $+\\mathrm{R}$ effect due to presence of lone pair of electrons, while $-\\mathrm{CHO}$ group is of $(-\\mathrm{R})$ nature. thus, $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{Cl}$ show more electrophilic substitution than that of $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\mathrm{CHO}$.<br>Groups showing $(+\\mathrm{I})$ and $(+\\mathrm{R})$ effect have more electrophilic substitution tendency than those that show either or $(-\\mathrm{I})$ and $(+\\mathrm{R})$ effect.<br>Hence, correct order is $\\mathrm{B}>\\mathrm{A}>\\mathrm{C}>\\mathrm{D}$ and option (3) is the correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20974_question_1.jpg'),
(20976,'chemistry',54,1201,2868,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Each carbon in ethylene molecule is $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ hybridised.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The $\\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond angle in ethylene molecule is $120^{\\circ}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Each carbon in an ethylene molecule is $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ hybridized. The reason is also true, as the $\\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond angle in ethylene is indeed $120^{\\circ}$, characteristic of $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ hybridization. But it is not the correct explanation. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20978,'chemistry',55,1239,3008,'A reactant $(\\mathrm{X})$  pass through hot red tube at $1600-1800^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ and produces benzene. Hence $(\\mathrm{X})$ is','[{"text": "Acetylene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ethene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cyclohexane", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Reactant $(X)$ in the reaciton is acetylene overall reaction is as follows :  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20978_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20979,'chemistry',54,1201,2868,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Greater the ${\\text{s}}$ -character of the hybrid orbital, the smaller is its size.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ orbital forms the longest and ${\\text{sp}}$ orbital forms the shortest bond.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The ${\\text{s}}$-orbitals are closer to the nucleus than the ${\\text{p}}$-orbitals. Thus, greater the ${\\text{s}}$-character of the hybrid orbital, the smaller is its size.Therefore, the order of the size of the three hybrid orbitals is $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}>\\mathrm{sp}^{2}>\\mathrm{sp}$.Accordingly, $\\mathrm{sp}$ orbital forms shortest and $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ orbital forms longest bonds with other atoms. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20982,'chemistry',55,1234,2974,'Which of the following has highest knocking property?','[{"text": "Aromatic hydrocarbons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Olefins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Branched chain paraffins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Straight chain paraffins", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Knocking character of fuel increases with decrease in octane number of a fuel. The octane number increases in the following order : straight chain alkanes $ < $ branched chain alkanes $ < $ olefins $ < $ cycloalkanes $ < $ aromatic compounds. Since, straight chain alkane (paraffin) has minimum octane number hence, it produces maximum knocking.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20984,'chemistry',54,1214,2912,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Carbanion has an octet of electrons.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In carbanion, negatively charged carbon atom is $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ hybridised.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A carbanion is indeed a trivalent atom with 8 electrons within the valence shell and is negatively charged. Carbanions are$\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ hybridised with pyramidal shape due to lone pair of electrons. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20986,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'Identify the reagent $\\mathrm{R}$ used in the following reaction?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HBr} / \\\\mathrm{UV}$ light", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HBr}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ / UV light", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20986_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_20986_question_1.png'),
(20987,'chemistry',54,1214,2912,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Benzyl carbanion is more stable than propyl carbanion.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The carbon atom in carbanion is trivalent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Benzyl carbanion $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CH}_{2}^{-}\\right)$can be stabilised by resonance but propyl carbanion $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2}^{-}\\right)$has no resonance forms. Carbanion has 3 bonds and a pair of lone pair of electrons. So, It is trivalent.<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_20987_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20990,'chemistry',55,1234,2968,'How many chiral carbon atoms are present in 2-Bromo-3,4,5-trimethylhexane?','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20990_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20992,'chemistry',54,1210,2898,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Chiral molecules do not have any element of symmetry.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ hybridised carbon atom having four types of substituents is asymmetric and is called chiral.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Chiral molecules contain asymmetric carbon and molecules exhibiting chirality do not have any element of symmetry. Carbon atom with four different groups is called chiral carbon. So , the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20994,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'Identify \' ${\\text{A}}$ \'in the following reaction<br>${\\text{A}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ Na}}{\\text{.dry ether }}}}{\\text{3,4  -  Diethyl - 3,4}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{ - dimethylhexane}}$<br>${\\text{ + 2NaCl}}$','[{"text": "3-Chloro-3-methylpentane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3-Chloro-2-methylpentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-Chloro-3-methylpentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-Chloro-2-methylpentane", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'It is Wurtz reaction. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20994_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20996,'chemistry',54,1209,2892,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Nitroalkanes and alkyl nitrites exhibit functional isomerism.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Compounds having same molecular formula and different functional groups are called functional isomers.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Nitroalkanes and alkyl nitrites exhibit functional isomerism. For example<br>$\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ can be represented as<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{ONO}$.<br>$-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ and $-\\mathrm{ONO}$ are different functional groups. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(20998,'chemistry',55,1234,2971,'The reaction of propane with bromine in presence of UV light predominantly forms','[{"text": "2-Bromopropane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1,2 -Dibromopropane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1,3-Dibromopropane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1-Bromopropane", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Bromination is preferred at $3^{\\circ}$ then at $2^{\\circ}$ and least at $1^{\\circ}$ carbon. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_20998_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21000,'chemistry',54,1209,2895,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{HO}-\\mathrm{CH}=\\mathrm{CH}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ exhibits ketoenol tautomerism.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Intermediate formed is a carbocation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The ketoenol tautomerism usually occurs in compounds with - $\\mathrm{OH}$ group and $\\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{C}$ group (enol form) and a carbon-oxygen double bond (keto form). The reason is false; the intermediate formed in ketoenol tautomerism is not a carbocation but involves the movement of protons and electrons between different constitutional isomers. So, the option (3) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21002,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'Which of the following is NOT formed when a mixture of methyl bromide and n-propyl bromide is treated with sodium metal in dry ether?','[{"text": "Butane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ethane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hexane", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'It is mixed or crossed Wurtz reaction. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21002_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Butane is cross coupling product while ethane and hexane are self -coupling products. Hence , propane is not formed in this mixture.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21004,'chemistry',54,1210,2896,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Staggered form is more stable than the eclipsed form.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The conformation in which the bond pairs of two central atoms are very far from one another is called staggered form.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The staggered form is more stable than the eclipsed form because the potential energy of staggered form in which the bond pairs of two carbons are far away from each other is minimum. Also the $\\mathrm{H}$-atoms are as far apart as possible and non-bonded interaction between them is maximum. In eclipsed form, the $\\mathrm{H}$-atoms are very close and so the increased overlap between the non-bonded orbitals is repulsive. Therefore, the option (2) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21007,'chemistry',55,1234,2970,'Which among the following molecules exhibits strong London forces?','[{"text": "neo-pentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "n-pentane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "isobutane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "isopentane", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'n-pentatne is a straight chain alkane, exhibits<br>strong London forces as compared to other<br>branched chain alkanes.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21008,'chemistry',54,1209,2895,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Enol form of cyclohexane $-1,3,5$ - trione is more stable than its keto form.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enol form contains $\\alpha$-hydrogen atoms.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Enol form of cyclohexane -1,3,5-trione i.e., phloroglucinol. It is stabilized by resonance and possesses aromatic character. Enol forms contains $\\alpha-\\mathrm{H}$ atoms. Hence more stable. But it has no relation with given assertion. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21012,'chemistry',54,1210,2897,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Trans-isomers are more stable than cis isomer.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The cis-isomer is the one in which two similar groups are on the same side of double bond.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Trans-isomer is more stable than cis-isomer because in cis-isomer the bulky groups are on the same side of the double bond. The steric repulsion of the groups makes cis-isomer less stable than the trans-isomer in which the bulky groups are far apart (they are on the opposite side of the double bond). So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21015,'chemistry',55,1237,2991,'What are the final products obtained by ozonolysis of propene?','[{"text": "2 molecules of acetaldehyde", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acetone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 molecules of formaldehyde", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "formaldehyde and acetaldehyde", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21015_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21016,'chemistry',54,1210,2897,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Geometrical isomerism is also called cis-trans isomerism.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Propene shows geometrical isomerism.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Geometrical isomerism is possible only when each double bonded carbon atom is attached to two different atoms or groups.<br>No geometrical isomers are possible for propene<br>$\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\right)$ because one of the double<br>bonded carbon atoms has two identical groups (H atoms) attached to it.<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21016_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   So , the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21019,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'The major product obtained in the following reaction is','[{"text": "4-Iodo -4-methylhexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4-Iodo-3-methylhexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3-Iodo-3-methylhexane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3- Iodo -4-methylhexane", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Peroxide has effect only on $\\mathrm{HBr}$ not on $\\mathrm{HCl}$, HI. It is Markovnikov addition of HI. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21019_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_21019_question_1.png'),
(21020,'chemistry',54,1210,2898,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Propadiene is optically inactive.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Propadiene has a plane of symmetry.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'1,2-propadiene $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\right)$ is opticallyinactive because the terminal $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$-hybridised carbons have the same atoms i.e, $\\mathrm{H}$-atoms. As a result, it has a plane of symmetry passing through the three carbon atoms and hence the molecule is optically inactive. So , the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21024,'chemistry',54,1210,2898,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> 6,6\'- Dinitrodiphenic acid shows enantiomerism.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enantiomers rotate the plane of polarised light in the opposite direction but to the same extent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'6,6\'-Dinitrodiphenic acid has steric hindrance between the bulky ${\\text{o}}$-substituents on the benzene rings. This forces the benzene rings to be perpendicular to each other. As a result two ${\\text{o}}$-substituents in one benzene ring lie in the plane of the paper while the two ${\\text{o}}$- substituents in the other benzene ring lie in a plane perpendicular to the plane of the paper. So, its mirror image is non-superimposable and hence shows enantiomerism. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21027,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'Which of the following is NOT obtained when a mixture of bromomethane and bromoethane is treated with sodium in dry ether?','[{"text": "Propane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Butane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Methane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ethane", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Mixed Wurtz reaction<br>$\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ -  Br}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Bromomethane}}} {\\text{ +  2Na}}\\mathop {{\\text{  +  Br  -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Bromoethane}}} {\\text{ -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{Dry}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{ether}}}}$<br>$\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}} - {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Propane}}}  - {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ + 2NaBr}}$<br> Propane is Cross-coupling product.<br>Ethane and Butane are self - coupling products.<br>Methane can not be prepared by Wurtz reaction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21028,'chemistry',54,1210,2898,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Lactic acid is optically active.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Lactic acid has plane of symmetry.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Lactic acid,  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21028_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   molecule because it has a chiral carbon atom or asymmetric carbon atom and is therefore optically active. So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21031,'chemistry',55,1237,2986,'Which of the following catalyst/reagent is used to convert $\\mathrm{C} \\equiv \\mathrm{C}$ (triple bond) to $\\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{C}$ (double bond) to form cis isomer of alkene?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na} /$ liquid $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Pd}-\\\\mathrm{C}$, quinolone", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2} / \\\\mathrm{HCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na} / \\\\mathrm{Hg}$ in $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Controlled reduction of alkynes. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21031_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\mathrm{Na} /$ liq. $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ gives trans alkene.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21032,'chemistry',54,1210,2897,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Alkynes do not show geometrical isomerism.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Geometrical isomerism arises due to the restricted rotation of a double bond','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Alkynes have linear structure and do not show geometrical isomerism. So , the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21035,'chemistry',55,1240,3020,'Which of the following reactions is used to prepare toluene from bromobenzene?','[{"text": "Wurtz reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fittig reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wurtz - Fittig reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Swarts reaction", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21035_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  When a mixture of an alkyl halide and aryl halide is treated with sodium in dry ether, an alkyl arene is obtained. This reaction is known as Wurtz-Fittig reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21036,'chemistry',54,1213,2907,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Cycloheptatrienyl cation is aromatic.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Aromatic molecules have high degree of thermodynamic stability.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Cycloheptatrienyl cation ,  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21036_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   has  delocalised six $\\pi$ electrons and follows $(4 n+2) \\pi$ electron rule. So, it is aromatic. Aromatic compounds are thermally stable. But it is not the correct explanation.Thus, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21039,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'Sodium salt of ${\\text{X}}$  is heated with soda lime and gives ethane, $\\mathrm{X}$ is:','[{"text": "Ethanoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Methanoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propanoic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Either (1) or (3)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Sodium salt of any carboxylic acid with hot soda lime undergoes decarboxylation to form alkane which has one carbon atom less than the parent molecule.<br>$\\mathop {{{\\text{C}}_2}{{\\text{H}}_5}{\\text{COONa}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ Sodium propionate }}} \\xrightarrow[\\Delta ]{{{\\text{NaOH}}/{\\text{CaO}}}}\\mathop {{{\\text{C}}_2}{{\\text{H}}_6}}\\limits_{{\\text{ ethane }}}  \\uparrow  + {\\text{N}}{{\\text{a}}_2}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_3}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21040,'chemistry',54,1213,2907,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Same number of electron pairs are present in resonating structures.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Resonating structures differ in the location of electrons around the constituent atoms.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Resonating structures may have the same number of electrons but differ in electron arrangement. The reason is true, resonating structures differ in the distribution of electrons around the constituent atoms while maintaining the same overall number of electrons. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21043,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'An alkyl halide by formation of its Grignard reagent and heating with water gives propane, What is the original alkyl halide?','[{"text": "Methyl iodide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethyl iodide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethyl bromide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propyl bromide", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{Br}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Propyl}}\\,\\,{\\text{bromide}}} \\xrightarrow{{{\\text{Mg/ ether }}}}\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{MgBr}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Grignard}}\\,\\,{\\text{reagent}}} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21044,'chemistry',54,1212,2904,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Methyl cyanide is a nucleophile as well as an electrophile.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electrophiles are electron loving and nucleophiles attack positive centre.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{C} \\equiv \\mathrm{N}$ is a nucleophile due to the presence of a lone pair of electrons on $\\mathrm{N}$ and is an electrophile due to the presence of partial positive charge on C. Nucleophiles are electron rich species and like to attack +ve charge. As nuclues is with +ve charge, they are called nucleophiles. So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21047,'chemistry',55,1234,2971,'Which of the following isomeric heptanes can yield seven different monochlorinated products upon free radical chlorination?','[{"text": "3-Methyl hexane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2-2, Dimethyl pentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-Methyl hexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,3-Dimethyl pentane", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'3-Methyl hexane has all the seven carbon atoms different and hence forms seven different mono-chlorination products. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21047_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21048,'chemistry',54,1211,2901,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Butene- 2 exists in two isomeric forms but butene-1 does not.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> But-2-ene and But-1-ene are position isomers.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In but-2-ene, carbon atoms involved in double bond have two different groups or atoms and thus shows geometrical isomerism. But-1-ene has two similar groups on terminal C-atom. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21048_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21048_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21051,'chemistry',55,1234,2973,'Alkyl halides react with dialkyl copper reagents to give:','[{"text": "Alkenes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alkyl copper halides", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alkanes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Alkenyl halides", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Alkyl halides (R\'X) react with dialkyl copper ${{\\text{R}}_2}{\\text{CuLi}}$ reagent to give alkanes and the reaction is known as  Corey House synthesis.<br>${{\\text{R}}_2}{\\text{CuLi}} + {{\\text{R}}^\\prime }{\\text{X}}\\xrightarrow{\\Delta }\\mathop {{\\text{R}}{{\\text{R}}^\\prime }}\\limits_{{\\text{Alkane}}}  + {\\text{RCu}} + {\\text{LiX}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21062,'chemistry',55,1234,2972,'Nitro ethane can be obtained from ethane by','[{"text": "the action of conc. $\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ at $100^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the action of dil $\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ at $200^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the action of conc. $\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ at $450^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "the action of conc. $\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ at $0^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Nitration of alkane is carried out using conc. $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ at $450^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$.<br>${{\\text{C}}_2}{{\\text{H}}_6} + {\\text{HN}}{{\\text{O}}_3}\\xrightarrow{{400 - 450^\\circ {\\text{C}}}}{{\\text{C}}_2}{{\\text{H}}_5}{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_2} + {{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{O}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21052,'chemistry',54,1213,2907,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Carbon-oxygen bonds are of equal length in carbonate ion.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Bond length decreases with the multiplicity of bond between two atoms.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}$ shows resonance and thus all the three bonds are of identical bond length.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21052_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  As the no. of bonds between two atoms increase, bond length decrease. So both statements are true, but its not the correct reason. Thus, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21054,'chemistry',55,1234,2972,'Pyrolysis of methane and ethane respectively are:','[{"text": "Exothermic & Endothermic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Endothermic & Exothermic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are endothermic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both are exothermic", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Pyrolysis is endothermic reaction (heating in the absence of oxygen).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21056,'chemistry',54,1213,2909,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Toluene is ${\\text{o , p}}$-directing.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The hyperconjugation effect of the methyl group increases the electron density at ${\\text{o}}$ - and ${\\text{p}}$-positions in the benzene ring.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Toluene, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21056_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   is o,p- directing in electrophilic aromatic substitution reactions. The reason is also true; the hyperconjugation effect of the methyl group in toluene increases electron density at the ortho (o-) and para (p-) positions in the benzene ring, making these positions more reactive. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21058,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'Which of the following substances reacts with water to give ethane:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{MgBr}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{4} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{OC}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Grignard reagent on hydrolysis gives alkane<br>$\\mathrm{RMgX}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{RH}+\\mathrm{Mg}(\\mathrm{OH}) \\mathrm{X}$<br>${{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{5}}}{\\text{MgBr  +  }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}} \\to {{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{6}}}{\\text{ +  Mg(OH)Br}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21059,'chemistry',54,1214,2916,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Benzyne contains a carbon-carbon triple bond.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Two of its carbon atoms are sp  hybridized.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Benzyne, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21059_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   contains a carbon-carbon triple bond. The reason is false all carbon atoms are $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$-hybridized even though there is a triple bond. So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21064,'chemistry',54,1210,2898,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Molecules that are not superimposable on their mirror images are chiral.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> All chiral molecules have chiral centres.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Molecules that are not superimposable on their mirror images are asymmetric and hence chiral. Such molecules are known as enantiomers. These molecules when placed on top of one another will not give the same molecule hence they are chiral.<br>A molecule can be chiral even if it does not have a stereocenter. A molecule\'s chirality depends entirely on whether it is asymmetrical. Example, Biphenyls do not have a chiral carbon but they cannot rotate freely due to steric crowding. Hence, they are asymmetrical.<br>Suitably substituted allenes (2,3-pentadiene) and biphenyls are optically active although they are dissymmetric, i.e. do not have asymmetric carbon atom. So, the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21065,'chemistry',55,1234,2972,'The volume of oxygen required at STP for the complete combustion of 4 lit of ethane at STP is:','[{"text": "4 lit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8 lit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12 lit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "14 lit", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathop {{\\text{2}}{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{6}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{2 vol}}{\\text{. }}} {\\text{ + }}\\mathop {{\\text{7}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{7 vol}}{\\text{. }}}  \\to {\\text{4C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ +  6}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}$<br>$\\therefore 4 \\mathrm{~L} \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}$ needs $14 \\mathrm{~L} \\mathrm{O}_{2}$ at STP.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21068,'chemistry',54,1214,2911,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Tertiary carbonium ions are generally formed more easily than primary carbonium ions.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Hyperconjugation as well as inductive effect due to additional alkyl groups stabilize tertiary carbonium ions.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Tertiary carbonium ions are generally formed more easily than primary carbonium ions. The reason is also true; hyperconjugation and inductive effects due to additional alkyl groups stabilize tertiary carbonium ions, making them more favorable in terms of stability and formation. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21070,'chemistry',55,1234,2971,'The ratio of products, 1 -Chloropropane to $2-$ Chloropropane respectively formed in the chlorination of propane, if all the hydrogens are abstracted at equal rates is:','[{"text": "$50: 50$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25: 75$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$75: 25$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12.5: 87.5$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'There are 6 primary hydrogen atoms and 2 secondary hydrogen atoms<br>$\\therefore$ Yield of 1-Chloropropane $=(6 / 8) \\times 100=75 \\%$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21072,'chemistry',54,1213,2908,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Electromeric effect is brought into play only at the requirement of the reagent.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It is a temporary effect in which bond pair is shifted to one of the constituent atoms.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Electromeric effect cannot occur without the requirement of a specific reagent; it is a spontaneous and temporary effect. The reason is true; electromeric effect involves the temporary shifting of a bond pair to one of the constituent atoms, leading to a transient charge separation. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21074,'chemistry',55,1243,3024,'Ethylene can be separated from acetylene by passing the mixture through:','[{"text": "Fuming $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pyrogallol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ammoniacal $\\\\mathrm{Cu}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Charcoal powder", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Due to the presence of acidic hydrogen, acetylene will give red copper ppt. with ammoniacal $\\mathrm{Cu}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21075,'chemistry',54,1207,2881,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764953760/preptracker/questions/q_21075_col1_1764953759152.png<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Principal functional group gets lowest number followed by double bond or triple bond.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21075_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Since $-\\mathrm{COOH}$ is the principal functional group, it the gets lowest number than the secondary functional group (3-carbethoxy). So it is 3carbethoxy-2-butenoic acid. So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21078,'chemistry',55,1237,2992,'Ethylene gives epoxy ethane on oxidation with:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4} / \\\\mathrm{OH}^{\\\\Theta}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7} / \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} / 200^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4} / 170^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21078_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21080,'chemistry',54,1214,2911,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764953762/preptracker/questions/q_21080_col1_1764953761851.png<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It follows with the formation of more stable carbocation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21080_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   So, the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21082,'chemistry',55,1237,2995,'What are ${\\text{X}}$ and ${\\text{Y}}$ in the reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{4}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\xrightarrow{80^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}} X \\xrightarrow[\\Delta]{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}} \\mathrm{Y}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{6}, \\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{4}, \\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{SH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{OSO}_{3} \\\\mathrm{H}, \\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{4}, \\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{HSO}_{4}$<br>$\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{HSO}_{4} \\xrightarrow[\\Delta]{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}} \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21086,'chemistry',55,1237,2995,'In the following sequence of reactions, the compound \' $\\mathrm{A}$ \' is<br>${\\text{A}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{HBr}}}}{\\text{B}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{Alc}}{\\text{.KOH}}}}{\\text{C}}\\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{,Zn/}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}}$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CHO  +  HCHO}}$','[{"text": "Ethylene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acetic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1-Butene", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{  -   CH   =  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Propene}}\\,\\,{\\text{(A)}}} \\xrightarrow{{{\\text{HBr}}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{  -   }}\\mathop {{\\text{CH(Br)}}}\\limits_{{\\text{(B)}}} {\\text{  -   C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}$<br>$\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ alcKOH }}}}\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{  -   CH   =   C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{(C)}}} \\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{,Zn/}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}}$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CHO  +  HCHO}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21087,'chemistry',54,1212,2904,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$ion is an ambident nucleophile.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Nucleophiles are electron rich species.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Nucleophilic species which have more than one site of reaction are called ambident nucleophiles.<br>$:\\overline {\\text{C}}  \\equiv \\mathop {\\text{N}}\\limits^{ \\cdot \\,\\, \\cdot } : \\leftrightarrow :{\\text{C}} = \\mathop {\\text{N}}\\limits^{..} $. Therefore, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21090,'chemistry',55,1237,2986,'Z-isomer $\\stackrel{\\mathrm{y}}{\\longleftarrow}$ 2-Butyne $\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{x}} \\mathrm{E}$-isomer $\\mathrm{x}$, $\\mathrm{y}$ respectively are:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na} / \\\\mathrm{NH}_{3 \\\\text { (liq) }}$ and $\\\\mathrm{Pd} / \\\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ni} / 140^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$ and $\\\\mathrm{Pd} / \\\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ni} / 140^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$ and $\\\\mathrm{Na} / \\\\mathrm{NH}_{3(\\\\text { liq })}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\\\\mathrm{Na} / \\\\mathrm{NH}_{3(\\\\mathrm{aq})}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Alkyne on Birch reduction ( $\\mathrm{Na}$ in liq. $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ ) gives $\\mathrm{E}$ (trans) alkene. Alkyne on reduction using Lindlar\'s catalyst gives Z (cis) alkene.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
