(21205,'chemistry',54,1213,2910,'What is the correct order of acidity of the protons marked A to D in the given compound ?','[{"text": "${{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{C}}} > {{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{D}}} > {{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{A}}} > {{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{B}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{C}}>\\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{D}}>\\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{B}}>\\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{C}}>\\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{A}}>\\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{D}}>\\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{B}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{D}}>\\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{C}}>\\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{B}}>\\\\mathrm{H}_{\\\\mathrm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Acidity of an acid depends upon the stability its conjugate base.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21205_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21205_question_1.png'),
(21383,'chemistry',54,1207,2881,'The IUPAC name of the compound having the molecular formula $\\mathrm{Cl}_{3} \\mathrm{C} \\cdot \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CHO}$ is','[{"text": "3,3,3-trichloropropanal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1,1,1-trichloropropanal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,2,2-trichloropropanal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "chloral", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21383_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21207,'chemistry',55,1234,2970,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Boiling point of alkanes increases with increase in molecular weight.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> van der Waals\' forces increase with increase in molecular weight.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The greater the molecular mass of a substance, the larger the magnitude of van der Waals\' forces of attraction between its molecules. Consequently, substances will have boiling point. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21208,'chemistry',54,1214,2911,'The most stable carbocation from the following is','[{"text": "C", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Among the given carbocations, (C) is most stable due to resonance stabilisation by the lone pair of nitrogen atom and greater movement of electrons between charge and electronegative atom.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21208_question_1.png'),
(21210,'chemistry',55,1234,2971,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Chlorination of methane gives a mixture of products.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Chlorination proceeds through the formation of carbanion.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Chlorination of methane occurs through the formation of free radicals and at $520-670 \\mathrm{~K}$ gives a mixture of four products i.e., <br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Cl}, \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}, \\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{CCl}_{4}$. So, option<br>(3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21213,'chemistry',55,1234,2972,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Oxidation of alkanes generates large heat.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Heat generated by burning coal is used to generate electricity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Alkanes burn readily in presence of excess of air or oxygen to produce $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$. This process is called combustion and is accompanied by liberation of large amount of heat or light. $\\mathrm{CH}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} ; \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=-890 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$<br>Given reason is a correct statement. but it\'s not the correct explanation of assertion. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21216,'chemistry',55,1234,2971,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Halogenation of alkanes is catalysed by tetraethyl lead.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Halogenation proceeds through free radical intermediate.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Halogenation of alkanes is catalysed by radical initiators like benzoyl peroxide. Halogenation proceeds through free radical mechanism. Assertion is false and reason is true. thus, option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21220,'chemistry',55,1234,2971,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Order of reactivity of alkanes is $3^{\\circ}>2^{\\circ}>1^{\\circ}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Reactivity depends upon the ${\\text{ - I}}$  effect of the alkyl group.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The order of reactivity of primary $\\left(1^{\\circ}\\right)$, secondary $\\left(2^{\\circ}\\right)$ and tertiary $\\left(3^{\\circ}\\right)$ alkanes is $3^{\\circ}>2^{\\circ}>1^{\\circ}$. Greater the number of alkyl groups present, higher is the reactivity.<br>Alkyl groups have ${\\text{ + I}}$ effect. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21223,'chemistry',55,1234,2972,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Alkanes are stable to acids, alkalies, oxidising agents, etc. at room temperature.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Alkanes are less reactive at room temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond is almost non-polar due to the less electronegativity difference between $\\mathrm{C}$ and $\\mathrm{H}$. The $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}$ bond is completely non-polar and polar reagents find no reaction sites on alkane molecules. This makes alkanes less reactive. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21224,'chemistry',54,1207,2879,'A compound with a molecular formula of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{14}}}$ has two tertiary carbons. Its IUPAC name is :','[{"text": "n-hexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-methylpentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,3-dimethylbutane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2,2-dimethylbutane", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21224_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  has two tertiary carbon atoms *',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21226,'chemistry',55,1234,2971,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Methane does not react with $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ in dark.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It is a free radical substitution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Chlorination of methane is a free radical substitution. To convert $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ molecules into chlorine free radicals, energy is required which is not available in dark. Thus, methane does not react with $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ in dark due to no formation of free radicals. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21227,'chemistry',54,1214,2911,'The most stable carbocation among the following is:','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The stability of carbocation: ${\\mathrm{3^{\\circ}>2^{\\circ}>1^{\\circ}}}$. The stability of carbocation can be described by the hyperconjugation. Greater the extent of hyperconjugation, more is the stability of carbocation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21228,'chemistry',55,1234,2966,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> All the four $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$ bonds of methane are of identical bond length.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Bond angle in methane is $109^{\\circ} 28^{\\prime}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The carbon atom in $\\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ hybridised and all the four sigma bonds are formed as a result of overlapping of $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ hybrid orbital from carbon and s-orbital from hydrogen, So, $\\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ has same bond length and bond angle $\\left(109^{\\circ} 28^{\\prime}\\right)$ . So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21231,'chemistry',55,1234,2968,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Butane is convenient fuel for domestic purposes.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Butane shows isomerism.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Butane is easily liquefied and can be transported easily. Butane can be n-butane and isobutane, which means its shows isomerism. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21233,'chemistry',54,1201,2868,'Bond X is made by the overlap of which type of hybridized orbitals?<br>','[{"text": "sp and $\\\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sp and $\\\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ and $\\\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ and $\\\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21233_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>So, it is because of ${\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^2},$ &  ${\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ orbitals.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21233_question_1.png'),
(21234,'chemistry',55,1234,2971,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> neo-Pentane forms only one monosubstituted compound.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Neopentane is a flammable gas.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'neo-Pentane  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21234_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  has four identical methyl groups attached on $4^{\\circ}$ carbon atom and thus, only one product is possible during monosubstitution. Neo - pentane is a flammable gas. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21237,'chemistry',55,1239,3006,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Acetylene does not react with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Acetylene has ${\\text{sp}}$ hybridisation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Acetylene $(\\mathrm{HC} \\equiv \\mathrm{CH})$ is acidic but very weak acid and thus reacts only with extremely strong bases like $\\mathrm{NaNH}_{2}$ and not with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ or $\\mathrm{KOH}$. Acetylene has two sp hybridised carbon atoms. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21238,'chemistry',54,1203,2874,'Which of the following is heterocyclic compound?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A heterocyclic compound is a cyclic compound that has atoms of at least two different elements as member of its ring(s). Along with carbon, $\\mathrm{N}, \\mathrm{O}, \\mathrm{S}$ may present in the ring in heterocyclic compounds.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21239,'chemistry',55,1234,2970,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Freezing point of neo-pentane is more than ${\\text{n}}$-pentane.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Increase in van der Waals forces increases freezing point.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'neo-Pentane is symmetric than ${\\text{n-}}$ pentane and it is expected to show more stable crystal lattice than that of ${\\text{n}}$-pentane. Thus, it has high melting point, freezing point or vanderwaals forces. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21241,'chemistry',54,1207,2881,'The IUPAC name of the given compound is<br>','[{"text": "Ethylacrylate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethylmethylbutenoate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethylacetoethenoate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethyl-3-methylbut-3-enoate", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21241_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>It is an ester, so name will be in the format of alkyl alkanoate.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21241_question_1.png'),
(21243,'chemistry',55,1234,2970,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Bond fission of $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}$ takes place easily than $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond is polar in nature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The bond energy of C-C (i.e., ${\\text{(s}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ -  s}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{3}}}{\\text{)}}$  is<br>$83\\,\\,{\\text{kcal}}\\,\\,{\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^ - }$ and bond energy of $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$<br>(i.e., $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}-\\mathrm{s}$ ) is $99\\,\\,{\\text{kcal}}\\,\\,{\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^ - }$. Thus, fission of $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}$ bond takes place easily in comparison to $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond in alkanes. The electronegativity difference between carbon and hydrogen is small and thus $\\mathrm{C}$ - $\\mathrm{H}$ bond is almost non-polar. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21244,'chemistry',54,1207,2883,'IUPAC name of the following compound is<br>','[{"text": "2-Hydroxy-5-iodo-toulene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4-Iodo-6-methylphenol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3-Methyl-4-hydroxy-1-iodo benzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4-Iodo-2-methylphenol", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21244_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Phenol is the main functional group.4-Iodo-2methylphenol is the correct IUPAC name.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21244_question_1.png'),
(21246,'chemistry',55,1239,3006,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Acetylene is acidic in nature.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Acetylene is ${\\text{sp}}$  hybridised.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The acidic nature in acetylene is described on the basis of higher electronegativity of \'sp\' hybridised carbon atom which pulls more effectively the $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond pair to lose $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$.So, option (1) is correct .',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21247,'chemistry',54,1207,2883,'IUPAC name of the following compound is<br>','[{"text": "4-Bromo-3-cyanophenol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-Bromo-5-hydroxybenzonnitrile", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2-Cyano-4-hydroxybromobenzen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6-Bromo-3-hydroxybenzonitrile compound is", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$ - {\\rm{CN}}$ is the primary functional group. $ - {\\rm{OH}}$ group name will be hydroxy as in this compound.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21247_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21247_question_1.png'),
(21249,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Methane cannot be obtained by Wurtz reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Wurtz reaction leads to the formation of symmetrical alkanes having an even number of carbon atoms.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Wurtz reaction involves the formation of alkanes by treating alkyl halide with sodium in ether. Two alkyl radicals join together to form an alkane. The net result is the formation of symmetrical alkane (R-R) having an even number of carbon atoms. As this is coupling reaction, methane can\'t be replaced. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21250,'chemistry',54,1209,2895,'<br>(III)The tautomer of II is','[{"text": "I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neither I nor III", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21250_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>So both the forms are tautomers of phenol.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21250_question_1.png'),
(21252,'chemistry',54,1209,2891,'The molecular formula of diphenylmethane,<br> <br>How many structural isomers are possible when one of the hydrogen atoms is replaced by a chlorine atom?','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At the following $1,2,3,4$ positions \' Cl \' atom can be added by removing hydrogen atom.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21252_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21252_question_1.png'),
(21253,'chemistry',55,1234,2972,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Branched chain alkanes have lower boiling point.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> As molecular size increases boiling point increases.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Branched chain alkanes have lower surface are as there shape approaches to a sphere du to branching. Also. they have week intermolecular forces. Therefore, they have lower boiling points in comparison to straight chain isomers. Boiling point is directly propotional to molar mass. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21324,'chemistry',54,1214,2912,'Give the stability order the following:<br>','[{"text": "S $>$ R $>$ Q $>$ P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "P $>$ Q $>$ R $>$ S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Q $>$ P $>$ R $>$ S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{P}>\\\\mathrm{Q}>\\\\mathrm{S}>\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Benzyl Carbanion stability ${\\rm{\\alpha }} - {\\rm{M}}, - {\\rm{I}}$ groups at $\\mathrm{O} / \\mathrm{P}$ positions.<br>$-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}-\\mathrm{CN}$ are $ - {\\rm{M}}$ groups and $-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ is ${\\rm{ + I}}$ group.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21324_question_1.png'),
(21256,'chemistry',55,1237,2982,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Alkenes are reactive than alkanes. <br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Alkenes possess $\\pi$ bonds.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Alkanes have only sigma bonds, but in alkenes, there is a a $\\pi$ bond. Pi-bond is weaker than a $\\sigma$ bond and is easily broken. The $\\pi$-electrons are less firmly bound to carbon nuclei and are thus reactive. So, option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21257,'chemistry',54,1209,2892,'Which of the following pairs of compounds are functional isomers?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The two compounds have different functional groups. $1^{\\text {st }}$ compound is ester and $2^{\\text {nd }}$ compound is with ether and keto groups.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21260,'chemistry',54,1209,2892,'2-Butyne and 1,3-Butadiene are','[{"text": "Chain isomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Position isomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Functional isomers", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Tautomers", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Alkene and alkyne are different functional groups.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21261,'chemistry',55,1243,3024,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Acetylene forms acetylide but ethylene does not.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Acctylene is lighter than air.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The electrons of the $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond in<br>1-alkynes are more strongly held by the carbon nucleus. Thus, there is easy removal of hydrogen<br>as $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$. So, $\\mathrm{CH} \\equiv \\mathrm{CH}$ forms $\\mathrm{CH} \\equiv \\mathrm{C}^{\\Theta}$ easily.<br>Which is not possible for $\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$.<br>Acetylene molar mass $(26 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{mole})$ is less than air (29g / mole). So, option (2) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21264,'chemistry',54,1209,2895,'Tautomerism is not exhibited by','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'It can\'t form enol form as consecutive double bonds are not possible in the ring.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21265,'chemistry',55,1237,2984,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> But-2-ene shows geometrical isomerism but but-1-ene does not show.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Geometrical isomerism arises due to the restricted rotation of the double bond.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In but-1-ene, the carbon atom linked by double bond is attached with two hydrogen atoms (similar groups) and thus, it does not show geometrical isomerism. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21265_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21268,'chemistry',55,1237,2989,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Propene reacts with $\\mathrm{HBr}$ to give isopropyl bromide.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Addition of hydrogen halide to alkenes follows Markownikoff\'s rule.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Addition of unsymmetrical reagent to unsymmetrical alkene takes place according to Markownikoff\'s rule, i.e., negative part of the addendum goes to the carbon atom with less number of hydrogen atoms.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21268_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21270,'chemistry',54,1209,2895,'Which of the following pairs of structures does not represent isomers?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The pair of compounds have different molecular formula, $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{5} \\mathrm{H}_{6} \\mathrm{O}\\right.$ and $\\left.\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{8} \\mathrm{O}\\right)$ so they are not isomers.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21272,'chemistry',54,1212,2904,'The most reactive nucleophile among the following is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\overline{\\\\mathrm{O}}$ :", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\overline{\\\\mathrm{O}}$ :", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{CHO} \\\\overline{\\\\mathrm{O}}$ :", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{CO}$ :", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Nucleophilicity depends on the availability of electron pair on donor atom as well as steric factor.<br>More the availability of electron pair on nucleophile, better will be the nucleophilicity.<br>Lesser the steric hindrance, more will be the nucleophilicity.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21273,'chemistry',55,1234,2972,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Iodination of alkanes is made in presence of $\\mathrm{HgO}$ or $\\mathrm{HIO}_{3}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Iodination is very slow and reversible process.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'During ionisation of alkanes, one of the products (HI) acts as strong reducing agent to reverse the reaction.<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{4}+\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{I}+\\mathrm{I}_{2}$<br>Therefore, iodination is made in presence of $\\mathrm{HgO}$ or $\\mathrm{HIO}_{3}$ which utilises $\\mathrm{HI}$ formed in the reaction and give better yield of iodoalkane.<br>$\\mathrm{HIO}_{3}+5 \\mathrm{HI} \\rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{I}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21276,'chemistry',54,1213,2906,'Arrange the following compounds in the order of decreasing $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}$.<br>(P) $\\mathrm{F}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{COOH}$<br>(Q) <br>(R) $\\mathrm{F}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{COOH}$<br>(S) $\\mathrm{Br}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{COOH}$<br>Correct answer is','[{"text": "Q $>$ S $>$ P $>$ R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{P}>\\\\mathrm{R}>\\\\mathrm{S}>\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "R $>$ Q $>$ P $>$ S", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}>\\\\mathrm{Q}>\\\\mathrm{P}>\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Stronger the acid, larger the $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}$. Strength of acid is directly proportional to $-\\mathrm{I}-$ effect.<br>Strong  $ - {\\rm{I}}$ groups close to - COOH group are more acidic. Higher no.of-I groups causes more acidity.<br>So correct option is 3.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21276_question_1.png'),
(21277,'chemistry',55,1234,2971,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Dipole moment of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Cl}$ is more than $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{~F}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{F}$ is more electronegative than $\\mathrm{Cl}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Dipole moment $=\\delta \\times \\mathrm{d}$;<br>The $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{Cl}$ bond is longer than $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{F}$ bond $(\\mathrm{Cl}$ being larger in size than F). The large value of \' ${\\text{d}}$ \' in  $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Cl}$ predominates over $\\delta $  value to give<br>higher value of $\\mu $ for $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Cl} . \\mathrm{F}$ is more electronegative than \' $\\mathrm{Cl}$ \'. But it is not the correct explanation. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21301,'chemistry',54,1201,2867,'Read the Statement -A and Statement -B carefully to mark the correct options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Sum of ${\\rm{\\pi }}$-electrons and lone pair of electrons in propargyl alcohol are 6.</div><div class="column-right">Propargyl alcohol has one triple bond and OH group.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Propargyl alcohol is<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21301_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>sum of $\\pi$ electrons and lone pair of electrons $=4+4=8$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21280,'chemistry',55,1234,2974,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> TEL is an antiknock compound. <br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> TEL reduces the knocking property of a fuel.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Anti-knocking agent is the substance which increases the octane number of the fuel. It helps in auto-ignition by raising the temperature and pressure. TEL is used as an antiknock compound because it forms ethyl free radical which converts some of the straight chain hydrocarbons into branched chain hydrocarbons, and thus, increases the octane number. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21281,'chemistry',54,1213,2906,'In which of the following compounds, the hydroxylic proton is most acidic?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Stronger - I groups like \'F\' can stabilize $ - {\\rm{ve}}$ charge on oxygen atom after losing proton. Such groups should be close to $ - {\\rm{OH}}$ group to be more acidic.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21284,'chemistry',54,1213,2907,'Compare the relative stability of the following resonating structures:<br>(A)  ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}} - {\\rm{O}} - {\\rm{CH}} = {\\rm{CH}} - {\\rm{C}} \\equiv {\\rm{N}}\\,;$<br>(B)  ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}} - {\\rm{O}} - {\\rm{CH}} = {\\rm{CH}} - \\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits^ \\oplus   = \\mathop {\\rm{N}}\\limits^\\Theta  $<br>(C)  ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}} - \\mathop {\\rm{O}}\\limits^ \\oplus   = {\\rm{CH}} - \\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits^\\Theta  {\\rm{H}} - {\\rm{C}} \\equiv {\\rm{N}}\\,;$<br>(D)  ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}} - \\mathop {\\rm{O}}\\limits^ \\oplus   = {\\rm{CH}} - {\\rm{CH}} = {\\rm{C}} = \\mathop {\\rm{N}}\\limits^\\Theta  $','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}>\\\\mathrm{B}>\\\\mathrm{C}>\\\\mathrm{D}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}>\\\\mathrm{C}>\\\\mathrm{D}>\\\\mathrm{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A $>$ D $>$ C $>$ B", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C > {\\\\rm{D}} > {\\\\rm{B}} > {\\\\rm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Resonating structure with no charge separation is most stable(compound a). Among the resonating structures having charge separation, one having complete octet of each atom is more stable (compounds $\\mathrm{c}, \\mathrm{d}$ )<br>Among the structures with complete octet of each atom, the one having $( - )$ ve charge on more electronegative atom, and ( + ) ve charge on less electronegative atom is more stable. $(\\because \\mathrm{d}>\\mathrm{c})$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21286,'chemistry',55,1237,2987,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> 2-Butanol on heating with $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ gives 1-butene and 2-butene.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Dehydration of 2-butanol follows Saytzeff rule.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the dehydration of secondary and tertiary alcohols, when there is a possibility of formation of two isomers, the hydrogen atom is preferentially eliminated from the adjacent carbon atom with the fewer number of hydrogen atoms. This is known as Saytzeff\'s rule   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21286_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{CH  =  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{1 - Butene(20 % )}}} {\\text{ + }}\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CH  =  CHC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{2 - Butene(80 % )}}} $ <br>So, option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21289,'chemistry',55,1237,2989,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Ethene on treating with $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ in presence of $\\mathrm{NaCl}$ forms $\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{ClCH}_{2} \\mathrm{Br}$ and $\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{BrCH}_{2} \\mathrm{Br}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> This addition involves the formation of free radicals.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The addition of $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ follows ionic mechanism, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21289_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21294,'chemistry',55,1237,2992,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Polymerisation of ethylene gives polythene.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Polythene formation is a substitution reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Alkenes in the presence of traces of oxygen and at high temperature and pressure undergo polymerisation. Its an addition reaction.<br>$\\mathrm{nCH}_{2}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\xrightarrow[\\text { traces of } \\mathrm{O}_{2}]{473 \\mathrm{~K}, 1500 \\mathrm{~atm}} \\underset{\\text { Polyethylene (Polythene) }}{\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\right)_{\\mathrm{n}}}$<br>So, option (3) is correct .',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21298,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Cycloalkanes are formed when $\\alpha, \\omega$-dihalides are treated with sodium or zinc.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> This reaction is intermolecular Wurtz reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\alpha, \\omega$-Dihalides when treated with $\\mathrm{Na} / \\mathrm{Zn}$ form cycloalkanes.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21298_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  This reaction can be regarded as intramolecular Wurtz reaction as cyclization happens within the same molecule. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21304,'chemistry',55,1234,2974,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Straight chain alkanes have very low octane number.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Quality of gasoline is measured in terms of octane number.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Straight chain alkanes have very low octane number because as the length of the chain increases, octane number further decreases. The octane number $(\\mathrm{ON})$ is a measure of the knock resistance of gasoline. It defines a numerical value from 0 to 100 , and describes the behavior of the fuel in the engine during combustion. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21305,'chemistry',54,1205,2876,'The molecular formula of the first member of the family of alkenynes and its name is given by the set','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{3} \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$, allene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{5} \\\\mathrm{H}_{6}, 1$-Penten -3-yne", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{8}, 1$-Hexen -5 - yne", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\\\mathrm{H}_{4}$, Butenyne", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Alkenyne is the hydrocarbon that contains double bond as well as triple bond.<br>$\\mathrm{HC} \\equiv \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{CH}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\quad \\text { (Butenyne) }$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21308,'chemistry',54,1207,2881,'The IUPAC name of the given compound is<br>','[{"text": "3-(1-Butynyl)-3-vinyl-2-pentadienal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5-Ethynyl-3-vinyl-2-pentenal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3-Vinyl-2-heptene-6-ynal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5-Acetylenyl-3-ethenyl-2-pentenal", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21308_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21308_question_1.png'),
(21309,'chemistry',55,1234,2970,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> ${\\text{n}}$-Butane has higher m.pt. than its next lower alkane, propane.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Melting points of alkanes do not show regular variation with increase in molecular size.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Alkanes with even number of carbon atoms have more symmetrical structures that result in closer packing in the crystal structure as compared to alkanes with odd number of carbon atoms. Therefore, the attractive forces in the former are more and the melting points are higher as compared to the alkanes with odd number of carbon atoms. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21312,'chemistry',54,1207,2880,'The IUPAC name of the given compound<br>','[{"text": "N, N, 4 - Trimethylpentanamide", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Dimethylamino-4-methylpentanone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}, \\\\mathrm{N}$ - Dimethylamino-4-methylpentanamide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-Methyl-5-oxodimethylpentanamine", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21312_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>It\'s an amide, so name will be alkanamide. ${\\rm{N}},{\\rm{N}},4 - $ Trimethylpentanamide',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21312_question_1.png'),
(21313,'chemistry',55,1234,2974,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The octane number of branched chain hydrocarbons is higher than that of unbranched chain hydrocarbons.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The branched chain hydrocarbons are more volatile than unbranched chain hydrocarbons.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Octane no. increases with branching. Branched alkanes have a number of tertiary carbon-hydrogen bonds. Such hydrogens are easily abstracted in the combustion chain reactions, and speed up the propagation of the explosive events that take place in the piston chamber. Straight chain "normal" alkanes contain only primary and secondary groups which are given out slowly. Straight chains have higher boiling points due to large surface area. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21316,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Corey- House reaction can be used to prepare both symmetrical and unsymmetrical alkanes.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The reaction involves the interaction between lithium dialkyl copper with an alkyl halide both of which may contain even or odd number of carbon atoms.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The Corey-House reaction is a versatile method for preparing both symmetrical and unsymmetrical alkanes.<br>$\\mathrm{R}_{2} \\mathrm{CuLi}+\\mathrm{R}^{1} \\mathrm{X} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{RR}^{1}+\\mathrm{RCu}+\\mathrm{LiX}$<br>So, option (1) is correct',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21320,'chemistry',54,1213,2909,'The stability order of following alkenes is<br>','[{"text": "I $ < $ II $ < $ III $ < $ IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II $ < $ I $ < $ III $ < $ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II $ < $ III $ < $ I $ < $ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II $ < $ IV $ < $ I $ < $ III", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'More substituted  alkene with conjugation is more stable. Those structures which involve conjugation of two double bonds are most stable. If the conjugation is common, the stability is decided by number of $\\alpha-\\mathrm{H}$-atoms . (IV) and (III) both have double bonds in conjugation.(IV) has $6 - {\\rm{\\alpha }} - {\\rm{H}} - $ atoms while (III)  has  $3-\\alpha-\\mathrm{H}-$ atoms .(I) and (II) don\'t have double bonds in conjugation. (II) has $6-\\alpha-$ Hydrogen atoms while (I) has $3-\\alpha-$ Hydrogen atoms.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21320_question_1.png'),
(21321,'chemistry',55,1234,2972,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Nitroethane $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right)$ shows functional isomerism as well as tautomerism.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Nitroethane shows tautomerism due to presence of $\\alpha$-hydrogens and it also shows functional isomerism with ethyl nitrite.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Nitroethane has hydrogens beside a double bond and can show tautomerism. The $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ group can bind to the $\\alpha$ carbon via $\\mathrm{N}$ atom or $\\mathrm{O}$ atom and can show functional isomerism.<br>${\\text{Tautomers}}$   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21321_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    ${\\text{Functional isomers}}$ <br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\underset{\\text { Nitroethane }}{\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}$ and<br>$\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Ethyl nitrite }}}  - {\\text{O}} - {\\text{N}} = {\\text{O }}$<br>So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21323,'chemistry',55,1237,2984,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{CHBr}=\\mathrm{CHCl}$ exhibits geometrical isomerism but $\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{Br}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}$ does not.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Presence of $\\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{C}$ is one of the conditions of geometrical isomerism.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The molecule may or may not contain a double bond; only restricted rotation about a bond gives rise to geometrical isomerism. Moreover, the groups across the carbon atoms with restricted rotation should be bonded to different atoms or groups. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21327,'chemistry',54,1209,2893,'Which one is not a metamer of<br>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Structure (2) contains a different functional group (phenol). So, it is a functional isomer of given compound.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21327_question_1.png'),
(21328,'chemistry',55,1244,3025,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Both cyclopropane and propene give addition reactions readily.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Cyclopropane and propene are isomers of each other.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Propene being unsaturated undergoes addition reactions, and cyclopropane being strained readily undergoes ring cleavage to yield addition products. Both these have same molecular formula, So can be called as ring chain isomers. Therefore, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21331,'chemistry',54,1214,2911,'Consider the following statements:<br>(I) $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{O} \\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is more stable than $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}_{2}$.<br>(II) $\\mathrm{Me}_{2} \\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}$ is more stable than  $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}_{2}$.<br>(III) $\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\mathrm{CH}-\\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is more stable than$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}_{2} .$<br>(IV) $\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}$ is more stable than $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}_{2}$. of these statements:','[{"text": "I and II are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III and IV are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, and III are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II, III, and IV are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'(I) <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21331_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Stable due to resonance.<br>(II) ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}} - \\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits^ \\oplus  {\\rm{H}} - {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$  Stable due to hyperconjugation and ‘${\\rm{ + I}}$’ effect.<br>(III) allyl carbocation is stable due to resonance.<br>(IV) Vinyl carbocation $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}\\right)$ is least stable as double bonded carbon (more electro negative) has + ve charge.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21332,'chemistry',55,1239,3006,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Acetylene reacts with sodamide to evolve $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ gas.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Acetylene is a weaker acid than ammonia.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\text{HC}} \\equiv {\\text{CH}} + {\\text{NaNH}}_2^ -  \\to {\\text{HC}} \\equiv \\overline {\\text{C}} {\\text{N}}{{\\text{a}}^ + } + {\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_3}$<br>Acetylene is a stronger acid than ammonia. So, option (3) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21335,'chemistry',54,1213,2907,'The decreasing order of electron density on the ring is<br>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{x}} > {\\\\rm{w}} > {\\\\rm{y}} > {\\\\rm{z}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{z}} > {\\\\rm{y}} > {\\\\rm{w}} > {\\\\rm{x}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{x}} > {\\\\rm{z}} > {\\\\rm{y}} > {\\\\rm{w}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{y}} > {\\\\rm{z}} > {\\\\rm{w}} > {\\\\rm{x}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ withdraws electron density, through strong $-\\mathrm{M}-$ and $-\\mathrm{I}-$ effect. So, it reduces maximum amount of electron density.<br>$-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ at meta position withdraws electron density through weak $-\\mathrm{I}-$ effect.<br>$-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ increases electron density through $+\\mathrm{I}-$ effect and hyper conjugation at para position.<br>$-\\mathrm{I}-$ effect of $ - {\\rm{Cl}}$ is stronger than its $+\\mathrm{M}-$ effect. So, it overall withdraws electron density from the ring.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21335_question_1.png'),
(21336,'chemistry',55,1239,3007,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Addition of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ to acetylene occurs in presence of dil. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and $\\mathrm{HgSO}_{4}$ to give acetaldehyde.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The reaction proceeds through  electrophilic addition mechanism.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ion first complexes with the $\\pi$- electrons of the triple bond and then nucleophilic attack of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ occurs. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21336_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21340,'chemistry',55,1241,3021,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Aryl halides are less reactive towards substitution of halogen atom.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Halogens are ${\\text{o, p}}$ -directing in nature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Halogens are ${\\text{o / p}}$  directing in nature. Aryl halide shows resonance stabilisation and thus, resistant to substitution reaction. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21344,'chemistry',55,1240,3020,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Benzene does not undergo addition reactions.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Benzene has lower heat of hydrogenation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Benzene does not undergo addition reactions although it is highly unsaturated due to resonance stabilization. Heat of hydrogenation of benzene is low as it is stable. Thus option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21407,'chemistry',54,1209,2895,'Which of the following compound can exhibit tautomerism?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Presence of $\\alpha-\\mathrm{H}$ atom along with a multibonded functional group is an essential condition for exhibiting tautomerism.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21407_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Acetophenone satisfies this condition, therefore it exhibits tautomerism.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21347,'chemistry',54,1213,2907,'Given below are two statements: one is labelled as assertion and other is labelled as reason.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> There is evidence for existence of N -methylacetamide in two structural froms I and II as shown below:<br>https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764948489/preptracker/questions/q_21347_col1_1764948489432.png<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Rotation about carbon-nitrogen bond is restricted due to resonance.<br>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct option from the following:','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is false but Reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21347_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>due to $>\\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{N}-$, rotation is restricted.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21348,'chemistry',55,1241,3021,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Nitration of toluene is easier than benzene.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The methyl group in toluene is electrons releasing.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In toluene  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21348_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> , the $ - {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}$ group is electron releasing. It is an activating group due to its ${\\text{ + I}}$ effect and hyperconjugation. It activates the benzene ring at ortho and para positions. Due to increased activity towards electrophilic substitution, toluene can be more easily nitrated than benzene. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21351,'chemistry',54,1213,2910,'Correct order of $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}$ values of the following compounds is<br>(I) $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>(II) $\\mathrm{HC} \\equiv \\mathrm{CH}$<br>(III) $\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$<br>(IV) $\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$','[{"text": "I $>$ III $>$ II $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I $>$ II $>$ III $>$ IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "III $>$ I $>$ II $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II $>$ I $>$ III $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Higher the electronegativity of atom,  $ - {\\rm{ve}}$ charge is stable. Higher $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}$ value means higher acidic.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21351_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21352,'chemistry',55,1240,3019,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Benzene reacts with $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ in presence of light to give $\\mathrm{BHC}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{BHC}$ is also known as gammexane.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In presence of light, free radical addition of $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ to benzene gives benzenehexachloride (BHC) (Gammaxene). <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21352_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  so, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21355,'chemistry',54,1209,2895,'Match the following.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{P}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{A - R}}$, Acid and ester are functional isomers<br>$\\mathrm{B}-\\mathrm{P}, \\mathrm{S}$, Ketones show both position and metamers.<br>$\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{Q}, \\mathrm{R}$, Functional isomers and tautomers<br>$\\mathrm{D}-\\mathrm{Q}, \\mathrm{R}$, Functional isomers and tautomers.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21355_question_1.png'),
(21356,'chemistry',55,1241,3021,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Nitrobenzene does not undergo<br>Friedel Crafts reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Nitrobenzene is a ${\\text{m}}$ -director.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The Friedel-Crafts reaction does not occur with nitrobenzene because the benzene ring in nitrobenzene has been significantly deactivated. $-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ group is meta director . But it is not the correct explanation. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21360,'chemistry',55,1240,3014,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Overlapping in benzene is $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}-\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ type.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}$ bond length in benzene is  1.3 $ \\mathop {\\rm{A}}^{\\circ}  $.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Benzene has 6 \' $\\mathrm{C}$ \' atoms with $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ hybridisation. The $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}$ bond length in benzene is $1.39 \\mathrm{~A}$ which is greater than $\\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{C}$ bond length of  1.34 $ \\mathop {\\rm{A}}^{\\circ}  $ and less than C-C bond length of 1.54 $ \\mathop {\\rm{A}}^{\\circ}  $. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21363,'chemistry',54,1207,2880,'The IUPAC name of the following compound is','[{"text": "3-propylpent-1-ene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3-ethylpent-1-ene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4-ethylhex-1-ene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3-ethylhex-1-ene", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, the correct IUPAC name of the compound is <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21363_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Select the longest possible carbon atom chain, number the chain and write the name of compound according to IUPAC rules.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21363_question_1.png'),
(21364,'chemistry',55,1240,3018,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In nitration, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ is used as nitronium ion producer.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Benzene has multi center $\\pi$ bonding.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Benzene has delocalised $3 \\pi$-bonds . In the process of nitration, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ (sulfuric acid) is utilized as the nitronium ion $\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{+}\\right)$ producer. This reaction involves the combination of concentrated nitric acid $\\left(\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}\\right)$ with sulfuric acid $\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)$, resulting in the formation of nitronium ions and other species. The reaction proceeds as follows:<br>$\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{HSO}_{4}^{-}+\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}+\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{+}$<br>So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21365,'chemistry',58,168,367,'The molality of a urea solution in which 0.0100 g of urea, $\\left[ {{{\\left( {{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{CO}}} \\right]$ is added to ${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.3000}}\\,\\,{\\rm{d}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ of water at STP is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{5}}{\\\\rm{.55 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 4}}}}{\\\\rm{m}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{3}}{\\\\rm{.33 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 2}}}}{\\\\rm{m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.555}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{33}}{\\\\rm{.3}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{m}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{Molality = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{moles}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{solute}}}}{{{\\rm{kg}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{water}}}}$<br>${\\rm{Moles}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{urea = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.010}}}}{{{\\rm{60}}}}{\\rm{mol}}$<br>${\\rm{Water}}\\,\\,{\\rm{at}}\\,\\,{\\rm{STP}}\\,\\,\\left( {{\\rm{d = 1g}}\\,{\\rm{/c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ = 1}}\\,{\\rm{kg}}\\,{\\rm{/}}\\,{\\rm{d}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}} \\right)$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.3}}\\,{\\rm{d}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.3}}\\,{\\rm{kg}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{Molality}}\\,{\\rm{ = }}\\,\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.010}}}}{{{\\rm{60 \\times 0}}{\\rm{.3}}}}{\\rm{ = 5}}{\\rm{.55 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 4}}}}{\\rm{m}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21367,'chemistry',54,1207,2883,'The IUPAC name of','[{"text": "2-(1-chlorophenyl) propanoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-methyl-(2-chlorophenyl) ethanoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1-methyl-(2-chlorophenyl) ethanoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-(4-chlorophenyl) propanoic acid", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The IUPAC name of compound is 2-(4-chlorophenyl) propanoic acid. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21367_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21367_question_1.png'),
(21368,'chemistry',55,1241,3021,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Activating groups are electron donors.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Nitroso group is an activating group.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Activating groups on benezene rings are electron donars and similar to halogens, the nitroso group $(-\\mathrm{N}=\\mathrm{O})$ is a deactivating group with $\\mathrm{o}, \\mathrm{p}$-directing tendencies in electrophilic aromatic substitution reactions. This is because the oxygen atom $(\\mathrm{O})$ in the nitroso group is more electronegative than the nitrogen atom $(\\mathrm{N})$. As a result, the -NO group as a whole tends to withdraw electrons from the benzene ring, making it less reactive towards electrophilic attack. So, option (3) is correct .',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21371,'chemistry',54,1207,2879,'For the given alkane,','[{"text": "1,1-dimethyl-5-ethyl octane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6-ethyl-2-methyl nonane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4-ethyl-8-methyl nonane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-methyl-6-propyl octane", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21371_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The correct name is 6-ethyl-2-methyl nonane.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21371_question_1.png'),
(21372,'chemistry',55,1241,3021,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $-\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}$ is an ${\\text{o / p}}$-directing group.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The groups which direct the incoming group to ortho and para positions are called  ${\\text{o / p}}$ -directing groups.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The $-\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}$ is an electron donating group and it activates the benzene ring for electrophilic attack at the ortho and para positions. The groups which direct the incoming group to ortho and para positions are called ortho and para directing groups. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21375,'chemistry',54,1207,2880,'IUPAC nomenclature of','[{"text": "2,4,4-trimethylpent-2-ene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2,4,4-trimethylpent-3-ene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,2,4-trimethylpent-3-ene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,2,4-trimethylpent-2-ene", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21375_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21375_question_1.png'),
(21376,'chemistry',55,1240,3010,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Phenanthrene is an aromatic compound.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Phenanthrene has benzenoid structure.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Phenanthrene, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21376_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   is an aromatic compound as it  satisfies Huckel\'s rule. According to Huckel\'s rule a compound is aromatic if it has a conjugated ring system with $({\\text{4n + 2}})\\pi $ electrons, where " ${\\text{n}}$ " is a whole number. Phenanthrene, has a total of $14 \\pi$ electrons within its conjugated ring system. This fulfills the ${\\text{(4n + 2)}}$ rule where ${\\text{n = }}$ 3 . Phenanthrene has benzene rings so it has benzoid structure. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21379,'chemistry',54,1207,2879,'The IUPAC name of$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right) \\mathrm{CH}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{2}$ is','[{"text": "4-ethyl-3-methylhexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3-ethyl-4-methylhexane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4-methyl-3-ethylhexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,4-diethylpentane", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21379_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21380,'chemistry',55,1240,3015,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> [10]-Annulene is not aromatic though it contains Huckel number of n-electrons. <br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Steric interaction between internal hydrogens makes it non-planar.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The given compound has $10 \\pi \\mathrm{e}^{-}$and follows Huckel\'s rule,<br>$(4 \\mathrm{n}+2) \\pi \\mathrm{e}^{-}$  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21380_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Due to severe non-bonded interactions between the internal hydrogen (as shown in figure), the ring assumes non-planar geometry.<br>So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21384,'chemistry',55,1237,2989,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Addition of bromine to trans-2-butene yields meso-2, 3-dibromobutane.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Bromine addition to an alkene is an electrophilic addition.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Transalkene -anti addition-meso product.<br>It is an example of anti-addition. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21387,'chemistry',54,1207,2881,'The IUPAC name of the compound','[{"text": "2-ethenyl-3-methyl cyclohexa-1,3-diene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,5-dimethyl hepta-2,6-dienoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,6-dimethyl hepta-2,5-dienoic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2,3-dimethyl epoxyethane", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21387_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> The IUPAC name is 2,6-dimethylhepta-2,5-dienoic acid',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21387_question_1.png'),
(21388,'chemistry',55,1240,3018,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Benzene reacts with ${\\text{n}}$-propyl chloride in presence of $\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$ to give isopropyl benzene.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Benzene undergoes electrophilic substitution readily.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Even though benzene has $3 \\pi$ bonds, it is readily undergoes electrophilic substitution reaction. When Friedel Crafts alkylation is carried out with $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}$, the product is isopropyl benzene because the initially formed n-propyl carbocation being less stable first rearranges to the more stable isopropyl carbocation by a 1,2-hydride shift before attacking the benzene ring.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21388_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21391,'chemistry',54,1207,2882,'The IUPAC name of the compound','[{"text": "1,1-dimethyl-3-cyclohexanol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1,1-dimethyl-3-hydroxycyclohexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3,3-dimethyl-1-cyclohexanol", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3,3-dimethyl-1-hydroxycyclohexane", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21391_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> IUPAC name: 3, 3-dimethyl-1-cyclohexanol',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21391_question_1.png'),
(21392,'chemistry',55,1240,3015,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Cycloheptatriene contains $6 \\pi$ electrons but it is not aromatic.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Aromatic compounds follow Huckel\'s rule.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Cycloheptatriene, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21392_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> contains ${\\text{6}}\\pi $ electrons. Huckel\'s rule. $(4 n+2) \\pi e^{-}$. But it is not aromatic because $6 \\pi$ electrons are only localised over 6 carbon atoms and not all over the seven carbon atoms of the ring (no conjugation). So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21395,'chemistry',54,1207,2881,'IUPAC name of the given compound is','[{"text": "2-methylbut-2-enoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3-methylbut-2-enoic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3-methybut-3-enoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-methylbut-3-enoic acid", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21395_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\because$ Order or preference$\\mathrm{COOH}>(=)>\\text { alkyl group. }$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21395_question_1.png'),
(21396,'chemistry',55,1237,2989,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Trans-2-butene on reaction with $\\mathrm{Br}$ gives meso-2,3-dibromobutane.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The reaction involves syn-addition of bromine.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'With trans-2-butene, the product of $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ addition is optically inactive due to the formation of symmetrical meso-compounds. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21396_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Addition of $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ is anti addition. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21397,'chemistry',58,176,385,'The boiling point of a solution containing 0.61 g of benzoic acid in the 50 g of carbon disulphide assuming ${\\rm{84\\% }}$ dimerisation of the acid, is (The boiling point and ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{b}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{are}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{46}}{\\rm{.}}{{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{and}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{46}}{\\rm{.}}{{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{and}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.3}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{K}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{kg}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$  respectively)','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{2}}{{\\\\rm{5}}^{\\\\rm{^\\\\circ }}}{\\\\rm{C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{40}}{\\\\rm{.2}}{{\\\\rm{5}}^{\\\\rm{^\\\\circ }}}{\\\\rm{C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{5}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{\\\\rm{^\\\\circ }}}{\\\\rm{C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{46}}{\\\\rm{.3}}{{\\\\rm{3}}^{\\\\rm{^\\\\circ }}}{\\\\rm{C}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{i = 1 - }}\\frac{{\\rm{\\alpha }}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 1 - }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.84}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 1 - 0}}{\\rm{.42 = 0}}{\\rm{.58}}$<br>${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{b}}}{\\rm{ = i}}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{m}}}{\\rm{m}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.58 \\times 2}}{\\rm{.3 \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.61}}}}{{{\\rm{122}}}}{\\rm{ \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1000}}}}{{{\\rm{50}}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.133}}{{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$<br>${\\rm{Hence,}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{b}}}{\\rm{ = 46}}{\\rm{.2 + 0}}{\\rm{.133 = 46}}{\\rm{.3}}{{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21399,'chemistry',54,1207,2880,'IUPAC name of the following','[{"text": "3-dimethylamino-3-methyl pentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 (N, N-trimethyl)-3-amino pentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\mathrm{~N}-\\\\mathrm{N}$-trimethyl pentammine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3-(N, N-dimethyl) amino-3-methyl pentane", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21399_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> 3-(N,N-dimethyl) amino-3-methylpentane',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21399_question_1.png'),
(21400,'chemistry',55,1240,3018,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Alkyl benzene is not prepared by Friedel-Crafts alkylation of benzene.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Alkyl halides are less reactive than acyl halides.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The monoalkyl derivative first formed readily undergoes further alkylation at a still greater speed to produce polysubstituted products. Also the alkyl halide employed may also undergo an isomeric change. Due to these difficulties alkyl benzene is not generally prepared by Friedel-Crafts alkylation of benzene. Alkyl halides are less reactive than aryl halides but it is not the correct explanation. Thus, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21403,'chemistry',54,1207,2885,'The IUPAC name of the following compound','[{"text": "bicyclo [2.2.0] octane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "bicyclo [0.2.2] hexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "bicyclo[2.1.1] hexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "bicyclo [2.2.0] hexane", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21403_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21403_question_1.png'),
(21404,'chemistry',55,1239,3006,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Acetylene on reaction with sodamide gives sodium acetylide and ammonia.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> sp-Hybridised carbon atoms of acetylene are considerably electronegative.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{HC} \\equiv \\mathrm{CH}+\\mathrm{NaNH}_{2} \\xrightarrow{\\text { Ether }} \\mathrm{HC} \\equiv \\mathrm{C}^{-} \\mathrm{Na}^{+}+\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond is strongly polar due to high electronegativity of carbon atom. Hence, it gives $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$ion on reacting with a base and acetylene behaves as an acid. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21415,'chemistry',54,1211,2901,'The total number of isomers for $\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{8}$ is','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The possible isomers of $\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{8}$ are as  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21415_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21408,'chemistry',55,1237,2992,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Cis-2-butene gives meso-2, 3-butanediol with dilute alkaline $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ solution.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Dilute alkaline $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ solution gives trans addition with alkenes.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Cis alkene - syn addtion - meso product.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21408_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Alkaline $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ always gives syn -addition on olefins. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21410,'chemistry',58,176,385,'Which of the following pair of solutions is isotonic?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.001M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{A}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{S}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}} \\\\right)_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.01M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{BaC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.001M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{CaC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.001M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{A}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{S}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}} \\\\right)_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.01M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{BaC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.001M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{CaC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.01M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{BaC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.015}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{NaCl}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Isotonic solutions are those which have same osmotic pressure at a given temperature.<br>${\\rm{For}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.01M}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{BaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{,i = 3}}$<br>$\\pi  = iCRT$<br>${\\rm{ = 3 \\times 0}}{\\rm{.01RT = 0}}{\\rm{.03RT}}$<br>${\\rm{For}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.015M}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{NaCl,i = 2}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{\\pi  = 2 \\times 0}}{\\rm{.015RT = 0}}{\\rm{.03RT}}$<br>Therefore, ${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.01M}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{BaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{and}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.015M}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{NaCl}}$ are isotonic.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21411,'chemistry',54,1211,2901,'Number of acyclic isomers of the compound having the molecular formula $\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}$ is','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The acyclic isomers of compound having molecular formula $\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}$ are as, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21411_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21412,'chemistry',55,1237,2988,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The presence of $\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}$enhances the solubility of alkenes in water.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Alkenes are weakly polar in nature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}$forms complex with the alkene by $\\mathrm{p} \\pi-\\mathrm{d} \\pi$ bonding giving an ion and, thus the solubility increases.<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21412_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, option (2) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21414,'chemistry',58,176,385,'Solutions A, B, C and D are respectively 0.2 M urea, 0.10 M NaCl, 0.05 M ${\\rm{BaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ and 0.05 M ${\\rm{AlC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}}$. All solutions are isotonic with each other except','[{"text": "B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Isotonic solution have the same osmotic pressure. (The osmotic pressure of 0.2 M urea (A)).<br>${\\rm{\\pi  = iMRT}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 1 \\times 0}}{\\rm{.2RT}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.2RT}}$<br>The osmotic pressure of 0.10 M NaCl(B).<br>${\\rm{\\pi  = iMRT}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 2 \\times 0}}{\\rm{.1RT}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.2RT}}$<br>The osmotic pressure of ${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.05}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{M}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{BaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\left( {\\rm{C}} \\right)$<br>${\\rm{\\pi  = iMRT}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 3 \\times 0}}{\\rm{.05RT}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.15RT}}$<br>The osmotic pressure of ${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.05}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{M}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{AlC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\left( {\\rm{D}} \\right)$.<br>${\\rm{\\pi  = iMRT}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 4 \\times 0}}{\\rm{.05RT}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.2RT}}$<br>Hence, all solution are isotonic with each other except solution C.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21416,'chemistry',55,1240,3018,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Benzene on heating with conc.<br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ gives benzenesulphonic acid which when heated with superheated steam under pressure gives benzene.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Sulphonation is a reversible process.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Sulphonation of benzene is an electrophilic substitution reaction in which $\\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ acts as the electrophile.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21416_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21417,'chemistry',58,176,385,'A 0.0020 m aqueous solution in an ionic compound ${\\rm{Co}}{\\left( {{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{5}}}\\left( {{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right){\\rm{Cl}}$ freezes at ${\\rm{ - 0}}{\\rm{.0073}}{{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$. Number of moles of ions which 1 mol of ionic compound produces on being dissolved in water will be (${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{ = 1}}{\\rm{.86^\\circ }}\\,{\\rm{C/m}}$)','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{ = i \\times K}}_{\\rm{f}}^{\\rm{\'}}\\,{\\rm{olality}}$<br>${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.00732 = i \\times 1}}{\\rm{.86 \\times 0}}{\\rm{.002}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{i = 1}}{\\rm{.96 = 2}}$<br>$\\therefore \\left[ {{\\rm{Co}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{ \\times }}\\left( {{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right)} \\right]{\\rm{Cl}} \\to {\\left[ {{\\rm{Co}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right]^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{ + C}}{{\\rm{l}}^{\\rm{ - }}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21419,'chemistry',54,1209,2893,'The number of metamers of the compound with molecular formula $\\mathrm{C}_{5} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}$ is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Since the compound has $\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{n}} \\mathrm{H}_{2 \\mathrm{n}} \\mathrm{O}$ type general formula, it must be a ketone (or aldehyde). It has the following metamers (the isomers having same functional group, but different alkyl group attached with the same functional group) : <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21419_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Hence,it has three metameters',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21420,'chemistry',55,1237,2985,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> 2,3-Dimethylbut-2-ene is more  stable than but-2-ene.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Six hyperconjugation structures can be written for 2,3-dimcthylbut-2-ene while but-2 -ene has tweleve.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The stability of a compound increases with the number of hyperconjugating structures it possesses. For example, 2,3-dimethyl-but-2-ene can have twelve hyperconjugation structures, whereas but-2-ene has only six. Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect. Hence, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21422,'chemistry',58,176,385,'A non-volatile solute ‘A’ tetramerises in water to the extent of ${\\rm{80\\% }}$ 2.5g of ‘A’ in 100 g of water, lowers the freezing point by ${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.}}{{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$. The molar mass of ${\\rm{A}}\\,\\,{\\rm{in}}\\,\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$ is $\\left( {{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{for}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{water = 1}}{\\rm{.86}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{K}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{kg}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "62", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "221", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "155", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "354", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As non-volatile solute ‘A’ tetramerises,<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{\\alpha }}\\left( {{\\rm{1 - i}}} \\right)\\frac{{\\rm{n}}}{{{\\rm{n - 1}}}}{\\rm{,\\alpha  = 80\\% }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\left( {{\\rm{given}}} \\right)$<br>${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.8 = }}\\left( {{\\rm{1 - i}}} \\right)\\frac{{\\rm{4}}}{{\\rm{3}}} \\Rightarrow {\\rm{i = 0}}{\\rm{.4}}$<br>${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{ = i}}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{m}}$<br>${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.3 = 0}}{\\rm{.4 \\times 1}}{\\rm{.86 \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.5}}}}{{{\\rm{Molar}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mass}}}}{\\rm{ \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1000}}}}{{{\\rm{100}}}}$<br>${\\rm{Molar}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mass = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.4 \\times 1}}{\\rm{.86 \\times 2}}{\\rm{.5 \\times 10}}}}{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.3}}}}{\\rm{ = 62g/mol}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21439,'chemistry',54,1210,2897,'The number of isomers for the compound with the molecular formula $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{BrCl} \\cdot \\mathrm{FI}$ is','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The possible isomers of $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{BrClFI}$ are as <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21439_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21423,'chemistry',54,1211,2901,'Empirical formula of compound having molar mass 58 is $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}$. Number of structural isomers possible, are','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Empirical formula is $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}$.<br>Empirical formula mass $=29 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{mol}$<br>Molar mass $=58 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{mol}$<br>$\\eta=\\dfrac{\\text { molar mass }}{\\text { empirical mass formula }}=\\dfrac{58}{29}=2$<br>Molecular formula $=\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{2}=\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{10}$<br>The molecular formula is $\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{10}$ and the isomers are two (chain isomers) in number. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21423_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21424,'chemistry',55,1244,3025,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{HC} \\equiv \\mathrm{C}^{-}$is more stable than<br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{CH}^{-}$<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{HC} \\equiv \\mathrm{C}^{-}$has more s-character than<br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{CH}^{-}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{HC} \\equiv \\mathrm{C}^{-}$has $50 \\%$ s-character and<br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{CH}^{-}$has $33 \\%$ s-character in hybrid orbitals . Stability of carbanions increases with an increase in the s- character at the carbon containing negative charge (carbanion).<br>So, $\\mathrm{HC} \\equiv \\mathrm{C}^{-}$is more stable than<br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{CH}^{-}$. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21426,'chemistry',58,176,385,'Which of the following aqueous solution will exhibit highest boiling point?','[{"text": "0.01 M urea", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.01}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{KN}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.01}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{a}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{S}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.015}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {{\\\\rm{C}}_{\\\\rm{6}}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{{\\\\rm{12}}}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{6}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{\\Delta T = i \\times }}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{b}}}{\\rm{ \\times m}}$ <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/58_21426_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${\\rm{i \\times m}}$ of ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ is highest, hence its boiling point will also be highest.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21427,'chemistry',54,1211,2901,'How many isomers are possible for the alkane $\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{10}$ ?','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The isomers of  ${{\\rm{C}}_4}{{\\rm{H}}_{10}}$ are :  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21427_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\therefore $Two isomers are possible.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21428,'chemistry',55,1234,2973,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Saturated hydrocarbons are chemically less reactive.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> All isomeric paraffins have same parent name.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Less reactivity of saturated hydrocarbons is due to presence of single bonds between carbon atoms. Paraffins (alkanes) may have straight chain or branched chain isomers which have different parent names. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21430,'chemistry',58,176,385,'Which of the following aqueous solutions has highest freezing point?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.1}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{molal}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{A}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{S}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}} \\\\right)_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.1}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{molal}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{BaC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.1}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{molal}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{AlC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.1}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{molal}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}{\\\\rm{Cl}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{ = i}}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{m}}$<br>As m = 0.1 molal for all given solutions thus, lower the value of i, lower will be the depression in freezing point $\\left( {{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{f}}}} \\right)$ and higher will be the freezing point of the solution.<br>${\\rm{For}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\left( {{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{;i = 5}}$<br>${\\rm{For}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{BaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{;i = 3}}$<br>${\\rm{For}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{AlC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{;i = 4}}$<br>${\\rm{For}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{Cl;i = 2}}$<br>Thus, 0.1 molal ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{Cl}}$ will have highest freezing point.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21431,'chemistry',54,1211,2901,'The pair of structures given below represents<br>','[{"text": "Chain isomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Position isomers", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Tautomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Metamers", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The pair of the given structures, <br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21431_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  represents position isomers. In case of position isomers, the difference lies in the position of substituent groups or atom in the same chain.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21431_question_1.png'),
(21432,'chemistry',55,1234,2968,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Staggered conformation of ethane is $1.25 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ more stable than the eclipsed conformation.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The two conformations of ethane cannot be separated at room temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Staggered conformation is more stable because any two hydrogen atoms on adjacent carbon atoms are as far apart as possible. Also the repulsion between the electron clouds of $\\sigma$ - bonds of two non-bonded hydrogen atoms is minimum. The energy difference of<br>$12.55 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ is not large enough to prevent rotation at room temperature. As a result it is not possible to separate the two conformations of ethane. So, option (2) is correct .',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21434,'chemistry',58,176,385,'Which of the following aqueous solutions should have the highest boiling point ?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.0}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{NaOH}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.0}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{a}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{S}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.0}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.0}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{M}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{KN}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{b}}}{\\rm{ = i}}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{b}}}{\\rm{m}}$<br>Concentration is same for all the solutions. So, ${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{b}}} \\propto {\\rm{i}}$<br>${\\rm{i = 2}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{for}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{NaOH}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{;}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{i = 3}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{for}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{NaS}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$<br>${\\rm{i = 2}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{for}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{;}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{i = 2}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{for}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{KN}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}$<br>So, elevation in boiling point will be maximum in ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ solution or ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ solution will have highest boiling point.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21435,'chemistry',54,1210,2897,'Among the following, the oxime of which shows geometrical isomerism, is','[{"text": "acetone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "diethylketone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "formaldehyde", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "benzaldehyde", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The oxime of benzaldehyde shows geometrical isomerism <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21435_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The isomers are as follows:  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21435_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21436,'chemistry',55,1237,2984,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Trans-Pent-2-ene is polar but transbut-2-ene is non-polar.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The polarity of cis-isomer is more than trans-isomer, which is either non-polar or less polar.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In trans-pent-2-ene, the vector sum of all polar bonds does not cancel out to zero due to unsymmetrical distribution of polar bonds. Thus there is a net dipole moment, making the molecule polar.<br>On the other hand, in trans-but-2-ene, the vector sum of polar bonds is zero due to the symmetrical arrangement of polar bonds that cancel each other out. This results in a nonpolar molecule with no net dipole moment. In general cis alkenes is more polar than trans alkenes but it\'s not the correct explanation. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21438,'chemistry',58,175,383,'Which of the following sets of solutions of urea $\\left( {{\\rm{mol}}{\\rm{.}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mass}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{60}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}} \\right)$ and sucrose $\\left( {{\\rm{mol}}{\\rm{.}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mass}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{342}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}} \\right)$  is isotonic?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{9}}{\\\\rm{.1g}}{{\\\\rm{L}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{urea}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{6}}{\\\\rm{.0g}}{{\\\\rm{L}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{sucrose}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{3}}{\\\\rm{.0g}}{{\\\\rm{L}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{urea}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{3}}{\\\\rm{.0g}}{{\\\\rm{L}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{sucrose}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{6}}{\\\\rm{.0g}}{{\\\\rm{L}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{urea}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{9}}{\\\\rm{.0g}}{{\\\\rm{L}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{sucrose}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{3}}{\\\\rm{.0g}}{{\\\\rm{L}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{urea}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{17}}{\\\\rm{.1g}}{{\\\\rm{L}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{sucrose}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${{\\rm{\\pi }}_{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{\\pi }}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{RT = }}{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{RT}}$<br>${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>$\\frac{{{{\\rm{W}}_{\\rm{1}}}}}{{{{\\rm{M}}_{\\rm{1}}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{{\\rm{W}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{{{{\\rm{M}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}$<br>$\\frac{{\\rm{3}}}{{{\\rm{60}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{{\\rm{W}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{{{\\rm{342}}}}$<br>${{\\rm{W}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 17}}{\\rm{.1}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21440,'chemistry',55,1240,3018,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Benzene reacts with iodine monochloride in presence of anhyd. $\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$ to form iodobenzene.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Iodine monochloride reacts with anhyd $\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$ to produce $\\mathrm{I}^{+}$which attacks the benzene ring.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Benzene reacts with iodine monochloride in presence of anhyd. $\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$ to form iodobenzene. It is an electrophilic substitution reaction. Iodine monochloride reacts with anhyd. $\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$ to produce $\\mathrm{I}^{+}$which acts as electrophile and attacks the benzene ring. Anhyd. $\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$ acts as a catalyst.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}+\\mathrm{ICl} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{I}^{+}+\\mathrm{AlCl}_{4}^{-} \\\\& \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{6}+\\mathrm{I}^{+} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{I}+\\mathrm{H}^{+} \\\\& \\mathrm{H}^{+}+\\mathrm{AlCl}_{4}^{\\Theta} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}+\\mathrm{HCl}\\end{aligned}$<br>So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21442,'chemistry',58,175,383,'A solution has an osmotic pressure of ‘x’ kPa at 300 K having 1 mole of solute in ${\\rm{10}}{\\rm{.5}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ of solution. If it’s osmotic pressure is reduced to $\\left( {\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{10}}}}} \\right){\\rm{th}}$ of it’s initial value, what is the new volume of solution?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{11}}{\\\\rm{.0}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{105}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{30}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{110}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The given values are<br>${{\\rm{\\pi }}_{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{ = \'x\' kPa,}}{{\\rm{\\pi }}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{x}}}{{{\\rm{10}}}}{\\rm{kPa,}}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{ = 300K,}}{{\\rm{n}}_{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{ = 1mole}}$<br>Using formula,<br>${{\\rm{\\pi }}_{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{V = }}{{\\rm{x}}_{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{RT}}$<br>${\\rm{x \\times 10}}{\\rm{.5 = 1 \\times 8}}{\\rm{.314 \\times 300}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{x = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{8}}{\\rm{.314 \\times 300}}}}{{{\\rm{10}}{\\rm{.5}}}}$<br>If osmotic pressure is reduced $\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{10}}}}{\\rm{th}}$ then,<br>value ${{\\rm{\\pi }}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{x}}}{{{\\rm{10}}}}{\\rm{kPa}}$<br>${{\\rm{\\pi }}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{V = nRT}}$<br>$\\frac{{\\rm{X}}}{{{\\rm{10}}}}{\\rm{ \\times V = n \\times R \\times T}}$<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{8}}{\\rm{.314 \\times 300}}}}{{{\\rm{10}}{\\rm{.5 \\times 10}}}}{\\rm{ \\times V = 1 \\times 8}}{\\rm{.314 \\times 300}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{V = 10}}{\\rm{.5 \\times 10 = 105}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21443,'chemistry',54,1210,2897,'Which of the following compounds will show geometrical isomerism?','[{"text": "Cyclohexene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-hexene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3-hexyne", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1,1-diphenylethylene", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Cyclohexene is unsubstituted alicyclic compound. 2-hexene exhibits geometrical isomerism.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21443_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> In 3-hexane,the triply bonded carbon atoms have only one substituent each<br>In 1,1-diphenylethylene,one double bonded carbon atom has two similar groups',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21446,'chemistry',58,175,383,'If M, W and V represent molar mass of solute, the mass of solute and volume of solution in litres respectively, which among following equations is true?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\pi  = }}\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{MWR}}}}{{{\\\\rm{TV}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\pi  = }}\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{TMR}}}}{{{\\\\rm{WV}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\pi  = }}\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{TWR}}}}{{{\\\\rm{VM}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\pi  = }}\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{TRV}}}}{{{\\\\rm{WM}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We have the relation between osmotic<br>pressure and molecular mass as,<br>${\\rm{\\pi  = CRT}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{;}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{where,}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{\\pi  = osmotic}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{pressure}}$<br>${\\rm{C = concentration}}$<br>${\\rm{\\pi  = }}\\frac{{\\rm{n}}}{{\\rm{V}}}{\\rm{RT}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{\\pi  = }}\\frac{{\\frac{{\\rm{W}}}{{\\rm{M}}}}}{{\\rm{V}}}{\\rm{ \\times RT}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{\\rm{TWR}}}}{{{\\rm{MV}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2015,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21447,'chemistry',54,1211,2901,'The total number of acyclic isomers including the stereoisomers (geometrical and optical), with the molecular formula $\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{7} \\mathrm{Cl}$ is','[{"text": "12", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "11", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21447_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21447_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21447_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\therefore $Total 12 isomers are shown.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21451,'chemistry',54,1210,2897,'The number of geometrical isomers in $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}=\\mathrm{CHCH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ are','[{"text": "two", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "three", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "four", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "five", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}=\\mathrm{CHCH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ exhibits two geometrical isomers:  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21451_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21452,'chemistry',55,1234,2968,'The incorrect statement regarding conformations of ethane is :','[{"text": "The dihedral angle in staggered conformation is $60^{\\\\circ}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The conformations of ethane are inter convertible to one-another.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethane has infinite number of conformations.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Eclipsed conformation is the most stable conformation.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Eclipsed form is most unstable conformation due to increased strain.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21454,'chemistry',58,176,385,'The van’t Hoff factor \'I\' accounts for','[{"text": "extent of dissolution of solute", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "extent of dissociation of solute", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "extent of mobility of solute", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "extent of solubility of solute", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'van’t Hoff factor \'i\' expresses the extent of association or dissociation of solute in the solution..',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21455,'chemistry',54,1210,2897,'The number of isomers of the compound with molecular formula $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ is','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Three isomers are possible having molecular formula $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{Br}_{2}$. The isomers are as follows: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21455_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21458,'chemistry',58,176,385,'For which among the following equimolar aqueous solutions Van’t Hoff factor has the lowest value?','[{"text": "Aluminium chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Potassium sulphate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ammonium chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Urea", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Number of total ions present in the solution is known as van’t Hoff factor.<br>Urea is a molecular solid hence, does not undergo association or dissociation therefore, has the lowest value of van’t Hoff factor (i).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21459,'chemistry',54,1211,2901,'Number of isomers possible for $\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{8} \\mathrm{O}$ is','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'There are 6 isomers possible for the compounds having molecular formula $\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{8} \\mathrm{O}$,which are as follows : <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21459_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21460,'chemistry',55,1237,2995,'Find out the major products from the following reactions.','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Mercuration-demercuration is a Markownikoff\'s addition while hydroborationoxidation is an anti-Markownikoff\'s addition.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21460_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_21460_question_1.png'),
(21462,'chemistry',58,176,385,'Relationship between van’t Hoff’s factor (i) and degree of dissociation $\\left( {\\rm{\\alpha }} \\right)$  is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{i = }}\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{\\\\alpha  - 1}}}}{{{\\\\rm{n - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{i = }}\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{\\\\alpha  - 1}}}}{{{\\\\rm{1 - n}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\alpha  = }}\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{1 - i}}}}{{{\\\\rm{n - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\alpha  = }}\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{i - 1}}}}{{{\\\\rm{n - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Relationship between vant Hoff factor (i) and degree of dissociation $\\left( {\\rm{\\alpha }} \\right)$ is given by<br>${\\rm{\\alpha  = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{i - 1}}}}{{{\\rm{n - 1}}}}$<br>where, n is the number of ions formed after dissociation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21463,'chemistry',54,1210,2898,'In which of the following compounds, one of the structural isomers is also capable of showing enantiomorphism','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{3} \\\\mathrm{H}_{8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{3} \\\\mathrm{H}_{6} \\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{5} \\\\mathrm{H}_{12}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{14}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Structural isomer of $\\mathrm{C}_{3} \\mathrm{H}_{6} \\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ are; <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21463_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Out of these four structural isomers,isomer (ii) has a chiral carbon atom and hence,shows enantiomorphism(i.e. optical isomerism)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21464,'chemistry',55,1239,3007,'But-2-yne is reacted separately with one mole of hydrogen as shown below:<br>${\\text{B}}\\xleftarrow[{{\\text{liq}}\\,\\,{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}}]{{{\\text{Na}}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}} - {\\text{C}}\\mathop  \\equiv \\limits_{ + {{\\text{H}}_2}} {\\text{C}} - {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}\\xrightarrow[\\Delta ]{{{\\text{Pd}}/{\\text{C}}}}{\\text{A}}$<br>1.  A is more soluble than B .<br>2.  The boiling point and melting point of ${\\text{A}}$ are higher and lower than B respectively.<br>3.  A is more polar than B because dipole moment of A is zero.<br>4.  $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ adds easily to B than A .<br>Identify the incorrect statements from the options given below.','[{"text": "1,3 and 4 only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 2 only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,3 and 4 only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 and 4 only", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21464_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>cis-2-butene has lower melting point than trans2-butene as trans-form has compactely packed crystal lattice.<br>cis-2-butene is polar so, it has higher boiling point than trans-form. The cis-2-butene being polar is more soluble than trans-form.<br>Addition of $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ to alkene is an anti-addition, so, cis-2-butene is more polar but dipole moment of cis-2-butene is not zero<br>$\\Rightarrow$ cis structure is less stable than "trans" structure. Less stable means more reactive so cis-but-2-ene will react more easily with $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21466,'chemistry',58,176,385,'If the van’t Hoff-factor for 0.1 M Ba ${\\left( {{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{2}}}$ solution is 2.74, the degree of dissociation is','[{"text": "0.87", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.74", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.91", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "87", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given,<br>Molarity = 0.1 M<br>vant Hoff factor (i) = 2.74<br>Since, ${\\rm{i > 1}}$, it means solute is undergoing dissociation.<br>$Ba{\\left( {N{O_3}} \\right)_2} \\rightleftharpoons B{A^{2 + }} + 2NO_3^ - $<br>Number of particles dissociated (n) = 3<br>Now, ${\\rm{\\alpha }}$  (degree of dissociation) ${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\left( {{\\rm{i - 1}}} \\right)}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{n - 1}}} \\right)}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\left( {{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.74 - 1}}} \\right)}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{3 - 1}}} \\right)}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.87}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21467,'chemistry',54,1210,2898,'Which of the following is optically active?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Optically active compounds have at least one asymmetric or chiral carbon atom. Compound given in option (1) contains two chiral carbon atoms as, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21467_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\therefore$ It is optically active.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21468,'chemistry',55,1240,3020,'','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21468_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_21468_question_1.png'),
(21470,'chemistry',58,176,385,'Which of the following 0.10 molal aqueous solutions will have maximum ${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{f}}}$ value?','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{C}}_{{\\\\rm{12}}}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{{\\\\rm{22}}}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{{\\\\rm{11}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{KI}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{S}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}} \\\\right)_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ - CO - N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'On the give 0.10 m aqueous solution, ${\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\left( {{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{3}}}$ will have maximum ${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{f}}}$ value. The expression for the freezing point of the solution is<br>${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{ = i}}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{.m}}$<br>van’t Hoff factor (i), is highest for ${\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\left( {{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{3}}}$, which is 5. Hence, it will give maximum ${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{f}}}$ value.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21471,'chemistry',54,1200,2862,'The first Nobel prize in Chemistry was given to','[{"text": "JH van\'t Hoff", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cannizzaro", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mendeleev\'s", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Moseley", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'JH van\'t Hoff was the scientist who got first nobel prize in Chemistry.He predicted in 1874 that the four bonds of a carbon atom are directed towards the four corners of a regular tetrahedron and the angle between any two adjacent bonds is $109^\\circ {28^\\prime }$. This tetrahedron arrangement of four bonds of carbon laid the foundation of the present day fascinating field of \'stereochemistry\'.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21483,'chemistry',54,1211,2901,'The configuration of the chiral centre and the geometry of the double bond in the following molecule can be described by','[{"text": "R and E", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "S and E", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{R}$ and $\\\\mathrm{Z}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}$ and $\\\\mathrm{Z}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_21483_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> In the above compound, the configuration about the chiral carbon $\\left(\\mathrm{C}^{*}\\right)$ is $\\mathrm{R}$.<br>Since, the groups with higher priority marked as(1) are on the same side of the double bond and those with lower priority are on the other side,thus the geometry about the $\\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{C}$ double bond is $\\mathrm{Z}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_21483_question_1.png'),
(21474,'chemistry',58,175,383,'A 6% solution of urea is isotonic with','[{"text": "0.05 M solution of glucose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6% solution of glucose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "25% solution of glucose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 M solution of glucose.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When a solution of urea (6%) is isotonic with a solution of glucose,<br>${{\\rm{\\pi }}_{{\\rm{glucose}}}}{\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{\\pi }}_{{\\rm{urea}}}} \\Rightarrow {{\\rm{C}}_{{\\rm{glucose}}}}{\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{C}}_{{\\rm{urea}}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\left( {\\frac{{{{\\rm{w}}_{\\rm{g}}}{\\rm{ \\times 100}}}}{{{{\\rm{m}}_{\\rm{g}}}{\\rm{ \\times 100}}}}} \\right){\\rm{ = }}\\left( {\\frac{{{{\\rm{w}}_{\\rm{u}}}{\\rm{ \\times 1000}}}}{{{{\\rm{m}}_{\\rm{u}}}{\\rm{ \\times 100}}}}} \\right)$<br>${\\rm{where,}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{w}}_{\\rm{g}}}{\\rm{ = mass}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{glucose = x}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}$<br>${{\\rm{m}}_{\\rm{g}}}{\\rm{ = molecular}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mass}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{glucose = 180}}$<br>${{\\rm{w}}_{\\rm{u}}}{\\rm{ = mass}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{urea = 6}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}$<br>${{\\rm{m}}_{\\rm{u}}}{\\rm{ = molecular}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mass}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{urea = 60}}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{\\rm{x \\times 100}}}}{{{\\rm{180 \\times 100}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{6 \\times 1000}}}}{{{\\rm{60 \\times 100}}}} \\Rightarrow {\\rm{x = 18g}}$<br>Thus, 18 g of glucose are present in 100 mL of solution.<br>In other words, 1 M solution of glucose (18 g in 100 mL) is isotonic with 6% solution of urea.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21475,'chemistry',54,1210,2898,'Optical isomerism arises from the presence of','[{"text": "a centre of symmetry", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a line of symmetry", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "an asymmetric carbon atom", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A compound could be optically active only when it contains at least one asymmetric carbon atom or a chiral centre.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21477,'chemistry',58,175,383,'The osmotic pressure of solution containing 34.2 g of cane sugar (molar mass ${\\rm{ = 342}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$) in 1L of solution at ${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$  is (Given ${\\rm{R = 0}}{\\rm{.082}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{L}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{atm}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{K}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$)','[{"text": "2.40 atm", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3.6 atm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "24 atm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.0024 atm", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Osmotic pressure, ${\\rm{\\pi  = CRT}}$<br>where, C = concentration of solution.<br>Given, w = 34.3 g, V = 1 L, M ${\\rm{ = 342}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$<br>${\\rm{T = 2}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C, = 20 + 273 = 29}}{{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{\\pi  = }}\\frac{{\\rm{W}}}{{{\\rm{MV}}}}{\\rm{RT = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{34}}{\\rm{.2}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}}}{{{\\rm{342}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}}}$<br>${\\rm{ \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.082L}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{atm}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{K}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{ \\times 293k}}}}{{{\\rm{1L}}}}{\\rm{ = 2}}{\\rm{.40}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{atm}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
