(21572,'chemistry',58,176,385,'Van’t Hoff factor of centimolal solution of ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\left[ {{\\rm{Fe}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{CN}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{6}}}} \\right]$ is 3.333. Calculate the percent dissociation of ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\left[ {{\\rm{Fe}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{CN}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{6}}}} \\right]$.','[{"text": "33.33", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.78", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "78", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "23.33", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, i = 3.333<br>On dissociation of ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\left[ {{\\rm{Fe}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{CN}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{6}}}} \\right]$, we get four ions.<br>${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\left[ {{\\rm{Fe}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{CN}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{6}}}} \\right] \\to {\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{K}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{ + }}{\\left[ {{\\rm{Fe}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{CN}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{6}}}} \\right]^{{\\rm{3 - }}}}$<br>Total number of ions (n) = 4<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{\\alpha  = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{i - 1}}}}{{{\\rm{n - 1}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{3}}{\\rm{.333 - 1}}}}{{{\\rm{4 - 1}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.333}}}}{{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.7776}}$<br>$ \\simeq {\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.78}}$<br>Hence, the per cent dissociation becomes ${\\rm{78\\% }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21573,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'In the preparation of alkanes from hydrogenation of alkenes and alkynes, finely divided catalysts are used. Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct regarding these catalysts?<br>(I) Platinum and palladium catalyze the reaction at room temperature.<br>(II) Nickel catalyzes the reaction at relatively higher temperature and pressure.<br>(III) Platinum and palladium catalyze the reaction at higher temperature.','[{"text": "(I) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II) and (III)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(I) and (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) only", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Pt and Pd catalyze the reaction at room temperature and Ni needs $200^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21576,'chemistry',58,176,385,'2 g of benzoic acid dissolved in 25 g ${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{6}}}$ shows a depression in freezing point equal to 1.62 K. ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{for}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{6}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{is}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{4}}{\\rm{.9}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{K}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{molalit}}{{\\rm{y}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$. If acid forms dimer molecule in solution, then the percentage association of acid is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{90}}{\\\\rm{.2\\\\% }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{99}}{\\\\rm{.2\\\\% }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{9}}{\\\\rm{.8\\\\% }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.8\\\\% }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{\\Delta T = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1000 \\times }}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{ \\times w}}}}{{{\\rm{m \\times W}}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1000 \\times 4}}{\\rm{.9 \\times 2}}}}{{{\\rm{122 \\times 25}}}}{\\rm{ = 3}}{\\rm{.213K}}$<br>${\\rm{i = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{{\\rm{exp}}{\\rm{.}}}}}}{{{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{{\\rm{cal}}}}}}{\\rm{ = 1 - \\alpha  + }}\\frac{{\\rm{\\alpha }}}{{\\rm{n}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.62}}}}{{{\\rm{3}}{\\rm{.12}}}}{\\rm{ = 1 - \\alpha  + }}\\frac{{\\rm{\\alpha }}}{{\\rm{2}}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{\\alpha  = 0}}{\\rm{.992}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{or}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{99}}{\\rm{.2\\% }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21577,'chemistry',55,1237,2989,'the major product of this reaction is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'More stable product is formed. More substituted alkene with maximum $\\alpha$ hydrogen atoms is more stable.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21577_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_21577_question_1.png'),
(21580,'chemistry',58,176,385,'If a centimolal aqueous solution of ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\left[ {{\\rm{Fe}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{CN}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{6}}}} \\right]$ has degree of dissociation 0.78. What is the value of van’t Hoff factor?','[{"text": "1.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.34", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.5", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given,<br>Degree of dissociation $\\left( \\alpha  \\right) = 0.78$<br>${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\left[ {{\\rm{Fe}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{CN}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{6}}}} \\right] \\to {\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{K}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{ + }}{\\left[ {{\\rm{Fe}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{CN}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{6}}}} \\right]^{{\\rm{3 - }}}}$<br>That means, total moles formed after dissociation<br>$\\left( {\\rm{n}} \\right){\\rm{ = 4}}$<br>We know that, van’t Hoff factor (i) ${\\rm{ = \\alpha n + }}\\left( {{\\rm{1 - \\alpha }}} \\right)$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.78 \\times 4 + }}\\left( {{\\rm{1 - 0}}{\\rm{.78}}} \\right)$<br>${\\rm{ = 3}}{\\rm{.122 + 0}}{\\rm{.22 = 3}}{\\rm{.34}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21581,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'<br>The major product $(\\mathrm{X})$ will be','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}} - {\\\\rm{CH}} = {\\\\rm{CH}} - {\\\\rm{CH}} = {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CH}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'It is an example for intramolecular Wurtz reaction.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21581_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_21581_question_1.png'),
(21584,'chemistry',58,176,385,'Calculate Van’t Hoff-factor for 0.2 m aqueous solution of KCl which freezes at ${\\rm{ - 0}}{\\rm{.68}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$.<br>$\\left( {{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{ = 1}}{\\rm{.86K}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "3.72", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.83", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6.8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.86", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given, Molality $ = 0.2\\,\\,m$<br>${K_f} = 1.86K\\,\\,mo{l^{ - }}$<br>$\\Delta {T_f} = 0.680^\\circ C = 0.680K$<br>As, we know that<br>$\\Delta {T_f} = i \\times {K_f} \\times m$<br>$0.680 = i \\times 1.86 \\times 0.2$<br>$\\frac{{0.680}}{{1.86 \\times 0.2}} = i$<br>$1.83 = i$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
