(21732,'chemistry',55,1240,3014,'Which reaction confirms the presence of three double bonds in benzene?','[{"text": "Oxidation reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydrolysis reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Triozonolysis reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Addition reaction", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Triozonolysis confirms the presence of three bonds in benzene.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21735,'chemistry',58,169,372,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">When scuba divers come towards surface, their capillaries get blocked which is painful and dangerous to life.</div><div class="column-right">There occurred release of dissolved gases as the pressure decreases and leads to the formation of bubbles of nitrogen in the blood.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When the divers come towards the surface, pressure gradually decreases. This releases the dissolved gases and leads to the formation of bubbles of nitrogen in the blood.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21736,'chemistry',55,1240,3020,'Read the statements below.<br>(1)  Aromatic hydrocarbons are immiscible with water but readily miscible with organic solvents.<br>(2)  Benzene reacts with excess $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ in presence of anhy. $\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$ to produce $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{Cl}_{6}$.<br>(3)  Acylium ion is a resonance stabilized electrophile.<br>Choose the option containing correct statements.','[{"text": "(1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(2) and (3)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(1) and (3)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(1), (2), and (3)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Aromatic hydrocarbons are covalent in nature, so soluble in organic solvents and insoluble in water.<br>Benzene reacts with excess $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ in the presence of anhy. $\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$ to form $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{Cl}_{6}$. It is electrophilic substitution reaction.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21736_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>so, given all statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21739,'chemistry',58,170,377,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Ethanol and acetone show positive deviation from Raoult’s law.</div><div class="column-right">Pure ethanol molecule show hydrogen bond and on adding acetone hydrogen bond between ethanol molecules breaks.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Ethanol molecules shows hydrogen bonding. On adding acetone, its molecules get in between the host molecule and break some of hydrogen bonds between them. Due to weakening of interaction the mixture of ethanol and acetone shows the positive deviation from Raoult’s law.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21740,'chemistry',55,1241,3021,'<br>Set of reagents for above conversion is / are','[{"text": "(i) Conc ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}+}}$ conc ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}}}$<br>(ii) ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}}$, sunlight", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Fe}}}$, dark<br>(ii) Conc ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}+}}$ conc ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) Conc ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}+}}$ conc ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}}}$<br>(ii) ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} / \\\\mathrm{Fe} /}}$ dark", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(i) ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{2}}}$<br>(ii) ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cu} / \\\\mathrm{HCl}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Nitro group is meta directing so first nitration is to be done.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21740_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_21740_question_1.png'),
(21743,'chemistry',58,176,385,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The molecular weight of acetic acid determined by depression in freezing point method in benzene and water was found to be different.</div><div class="column-right">Water is polar and benzene is non-polar.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In benzene, acetic acid dimerises but in water it does not. This is because benzene is non-polar, whereas water is polar. The number of particles become different in both the solvents.<br>Depression in freezing point is a colligative property, i.e. depends upon the number of particles in solution. Hence, molecular mass determined by depression in freezing point method comes out to be different.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21744,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'Which of the following can be used for the preparation of propane?','[{"text": "${\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{3}}}{\\\\text{CH}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\text{ = }}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}\\\\xrightarrow[{{\\\\text{2}}{\\\\text{.AgN}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{3}}}{\\\\text{/NaOH}}}]{{{\\\\text{1}}{\\\\text{.}}{{\\\\text{B}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{6}}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{3}}}{\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{Cl}}\\\\xrightarrow[{{\\\\text{2}}{\\\\text{.}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{2}}}}]{{{\\\\text{1}}{\\\\text{.Mg/ether}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Alkyl halides undergo reduction with red phosphorus and hydrogen iodide and result in the formation of alkane. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21744_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The products obtained in the other reactions are as follows:  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21744_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21747,'chemistry',58,176,385,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">If 0.1 M NaCl solution boils at ${\\rm{10}}{{\\rm{1}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C,}}$, then ${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.1}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{M}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{BaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}$  solution boil at appr. ${\\rm{101}}{\\rm{.}}{{\\rm{5}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$</div><div class="column-right">${\\rm{BaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ has higher molecular mass than NaCl</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21748,'chemistry',55,1234,2969,'By which one of the following compounds both $\\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ can be prepared in one step ?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{I}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{I}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Methane and Ethane can be prepared in one step from $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{I}$ by reduction with $\\mathrm{Zn}-\\mathrm{Cu} / \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ and Wurtz reaction [Which double the number of $\\mathrm{C}$-atoms] respectively.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21748_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21748_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
