(21760,'chemistry',55,1237,2986,'$\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{Br} \\xrightarrow{\\text { Alc. } \\mathrm{KOH}}$ Final product is','[{"text": "propene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "propanol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "cyclopropane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "propan-1,2-diol", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When haloalkane with ${\\rm{\\beta }}$-hydrogen atom is heated with alc. $\\mathrm{KOH}$ solution, then alkene is formed. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21760_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Carbon and the halogen is lost from $\\alpha$-carbon atom. As a result, an alkene is formed as a product. Due to the involvement of elimination of $\\beta$ hydrogen atom, the process is often called $\\beta$ elimination',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
