(21764,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'Major product $\\mathrm{A}$ of the reaction is $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{ICl}} \\mathrm{A}$','[{"text": "2-chloro-1-iodo-2-methyl propane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1-chloro-2-iodo-2-methyl propane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1,2-dichloro-2-methyl propane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1,2-diiodo-2-methyl propane", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{I}}$-Cl is polarised and undergoes heterolytic fission [as $\\mathrm{Cl}$ being more electronegative than I] to generate iodonium ion( $\\left.\\mathrm{I}^{+}\\right)$and chloride ion $\\left(\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\\right)$.<br>$\\mathrm{I}-\\mathrm{Cl} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{I}^{+}+\\left(\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\\right)$<br>The reaction proceeds through the formation of a bridged iodonium ion which is more stable than carbocation followed by the attack of chloride ion. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21764_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> i.e.,2-chloro-1-iodo-2-methylpropane',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
