(21828,'chemistry',55,1239,3006,'Which of the following compounds reacts with an aqueous solution of $\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{2}\\right] \\mathrm{OH}$ ?','[{"text": "Ethane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1-butyne", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2-butyne", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Due to the acidic nature of the hydrogen atoms attached to a triple bonded carbon atoms, acetylene and terminal alkynes form metal acetylides.<br>Reaction with ammonical $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ solution $\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{2}\\right] \\mathrm{OH}$ is as follows:  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21828_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21831,'chemistry',55,1244,3025,'Which one of the following compounds does not form an ozonide?','[{"text": "Ethene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propyne", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propane", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As propane is a saturated compound. Thus, it does not form an ozonide. Ozonide is formed only by unsaturated compounds, i.e. alkenes or alkynes. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21831_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21832,'chemistry',58,176,385,'The freezing point of benzene decrease by ${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.4}}{{\\rm{5}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$ when 0.2 g of acetic acid is added to 20 g of benzene. If acetic acid associates to form a dimer in benzene will be :- (${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{f}}}$ for benzene ${\\rm{ = 5}}{\\rm{.12K}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{kg}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$)','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{80}}{\\\\rm{.4\\\\% }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{74}}{\\\\rm{.6\\\\% }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{64}}{\\\\rm{.6\\\\% }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{94}}{\\\\rm{.6\\\\% }}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In benzene<br>${\\text{2C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COOH}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\left( {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COOH}}} \\right)_{\\text{2}}}$<br>${\\rm{i = 1 + }}\\left( {\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ - 1}}} \\right){\\rm{\\alpha }}$<br>${\\rm{i = 1 - }}\\frac{{\\rm{\\alpha }}}{{\\rm{2}}}$ Here, ${\\rm{\\alpha }}$ degree of association<br>${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{ = i}}{{\\rm{k}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{m}}$<br>${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.45 = }}\\left( {{\\rm{1 - }}\\frac{{\\rm{\\alpha }}}{{\\rm{2}}}} \\right)\\left( {{\\rm{5}}{\\rm{.12}}} \\right)\\frac{{\\frac{{\\left( {\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.2}}}}{{{\\rm{60}}}}} \\right)}}{{{\\rm{20}}}}}}{{{\\rm{1000}}}}$<br>${\\rm{1 - }}\\frac{{\\rm{\\alpha }}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.527}}$<br>${\\rm{\\alpha  = 0}}{\\rm{.945}}$<br>% degree of association = 94.5%',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21834,'chemistry',54,1201,2869,'Total number of ${\\mathrm{\\alpha-\\mathrm{H}}}$ atoms present in tert-butyl cation and methyl cation are ____ .','[{"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'tert-Butyl cation $\\left[ {{{{\\text{(C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{)}}}_{\\text{3}}}{{\\text{C}}^{\\text{ + }}}} \\right]$ has nine ${\\mathrm{\\alpha-\\mathrm{H}}}$ atoms. Whereas, methyl cation $\\left( {\\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits^{\\text{ + }} {{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}} \\right)$ does not possess any ${\\mathrm{\\alpha-H}}$ atom',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21835,'chemistry',55,1240,3015,'Which of the following is an aromatic compound?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The main condition for a compound is be aromatic is presence of $(4 n+2) \\pi$ electrons.(Huckel rule) <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21835_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  due to the presence of $(4 \\mathrm{n}+2) \\pi \\mathrm{e}^{-}=2, \\mathrm{n}=0$. As it follows Huckel\' rule. Thus, it is aromatic in nature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21836,'chemistry',58,170,375,'Which one of the following statements regarding Henry’s law is not correct?','[{"text": "Different gases have different ${{\\\\rm{K}}_{\\\\rm{H}}}$<br>(Henry’s law constant) values at the same\\ntemperature.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The value of ${{\\\\rm{K}}_{\\\\rm{H}}}$ increases with increase of temperature and ${K_H}$  is function of the nature of the gas.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The partial pressure of the gas in vapour phase is proportional to the mole fraction of the gas in the solution.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Higher the value of ${{\\\\rm{K}}_{\\\\rm{H}}}$ at a given pressure, higher is the solubility of the gas in the liquids.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Higher ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{H}}}$ - Lower is solubility at a given pressure.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21839,'chemistry',55,1240,3018,'During nitration of benzene, the attacking electrophile is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'During nitration of benzene the attacking electrophile is $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{+}$. It is formed by the reaction between $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2}  \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21839_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21840,'chemistry',58,172,380,'The mass of a non-volatile, non-electrolyte solute $\\left( {{\\rm{molar}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mass = 50g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}} \\right)$ needed to be dissolved in 114 g octane to reduce its vapour pressure to 75%, is:','[{"text": "37. 5g", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "75 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "150 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "50/3 g", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\frac{{{{\\rm{P}}^{\\rm{0}}}{\\rm{ - }}{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{8}}}}}{{{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{8}}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{n}}}{{\\rm{N}}}$<br>$\\frac{{100P - 75P}}{{75P}} = \\frac{{\\frac{W}{{50}}}}{1}$<br>$\\frac{{25}}{{75}} = \\frac{W}{{50}}$<br>$W = \\frac{{50}}{3}g$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21843,'chemistry',55,1241,3021,'Which of the following compounds undergoes nitration more readily?','[{"text": "Benzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Benzoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nitrobenzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Toluene", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ group activates the benzene ring, whereas, $-\\mathrm{COOH}$ and $-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ deactivates the ring. Hence, toluene $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)$ undergoes nitration easily.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
