(21860,'chemistry',55,1241,3021,'Which order is correct for the decreasing reactivity towards monobromination of the following compounds?<br>${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}},\\,{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{COOH}},{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{6}}},\\,{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{I}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{II}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{III}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, {\\rm{IV}}$','[{"text": "I $>$ II $>$ III $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I $>$ III $>$ II $>$ IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II $>$ III $>$ IV $>$ I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III $>$ I $>$ II $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Monobromination of the aromatic compound is an electrophilic substitution reaction. The electron donating group (like $-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ ) will increase the reactivity of ring while electron withdrawing groups decreases the reactivity of the ring . Hence, among the given aromatic compounds, the reactivity of $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ is highest. Between $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{COOH}$ and $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{NO}_{2}$, electron withdrawing power of $-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ group is higher than $-\\mathrm{COOH}$ so the reactivity of $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{COOH}$ is higher than $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{NO}_{2}$. Thus, the decreasing reactivity order of ring towards monobromination is <br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}} > {{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{6}}} > {{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{COOH}} > {{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\\\\n\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{I}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{III}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{II}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{1V}}\n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21863,'chemistry',58,169,372,'${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{H}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{value}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{for}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Ar}}({\\rm{g}}),{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}({\\rm{g}}),{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{HCHO}}{\\mkern 1mu} ({\\rm{g}}){\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} $ ${\\rm{and}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{(g)}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{are}}\\,\\,{\\rm{40}}{\\rm{.39,1}}{\\rm{.67,}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.87 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 5}}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} $ ${\\rm{and}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.413}}$ respectively.<br>Arrange these gases in the order of their increasing solubility.','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{HCHO < C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}{\\\\rm{ < C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ < Ar}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{HCHO < C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ < C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}{\\\\rm{ < Ar}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Ar < C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ < C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}{\\\\rm{ < HCHO}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Ar < C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}{\\\\rm{ < C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ < HCHO}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Greater is the value of ${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{H}}}$ lower is its solubility.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exampler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21864,'chemistry',55,1234,2974,'Petrochemicals can be used to prepare','[{"text": "synthetic fibres", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "pesticides", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "plastics", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Petrochemicals can be used to prepare synthetic fibres, pesticides and plastics etc.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21867,'chemistry',58,173,381,'The unit of ebullioscopic constant is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{K}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{kg}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{or}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{K}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{molality}}} \\\\right)^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{mol}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{kg}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {{\\\\rm{K}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{or}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {{\\\\rm{K}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\left( {{\\\\rm{molality}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{kg}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {{\\\\rm{K}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{or}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {{\\\\rm{K}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{molality}}} \\\\right)^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{K}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{mol}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{k}}{{\\\\rm{g}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{or}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{K}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\left( {{\\\\rm{molality}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As we know from elevation in boiling point that<br>${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{b}}}{\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{b}}}{\\rm{m}}$<br>${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{b}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{b}}}}}{{\\rm{m}}}$<br>${\\rm{unit}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{b}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{unit}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{b}}}}}{{{\\rm{unit}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{m}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{K}}}{{{\\rm{molality}}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{K}}}{{{\\rm{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{k}}{{\\rm{g}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}}}{\\rm{ = K}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{kg}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exampler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21868,'chemistry',55,1234,2974,'Octane number can be changed by','[{"text": "isomerisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "alkylation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "cyclisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The octane number of fuel can be improved by increasing the percentage of branched chain alkanes, alkenes and aromatic hydrocarbon. Thus, octane number can be changed by isomerisation, alkylation and aromatisation (cyclisation).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21871,'chemistry',58,175,383,'An unripe mango placed in a concentrated salt solution to prepare pickle shrivels because ..................','[{"text": "It gains water due to osmosis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It loses water due to reverse osmosis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Is gains water due to reverse osmosis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It loses water due to osmosis", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When an unripe mango is placed in a concentrated salt solution to prepare pickle then mango loses water due to osmosis and gets shrivel.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exampler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21872,'chemistry',55,1234,2971,'In the following reaction, the major product is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}} + {\\rm{hv}} \\to {\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{r}}^{\\rm{ \\bullet }}} + {\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{r}}^{\\rm{ \\bullet }}}$ <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_21872_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_21872_question_1.png'),
(21875,'chemistry',58,170,376,'Which of the following represents correctly the changes in thermodynamics properties during the formation of 1 mol of an ideal binary solution?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For ideal solution ${\\rm{\\Delta H = 0,\\Delta G =  - ve}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{from}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{equation}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S}}$<br>${\\rm{i}}{\\rm{.e}}{\\rm{.,}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{T\\Delta S > 0}}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(21879,'chemistry',58,170,377,'Vapour phase diagram for a solution is given below if dotted line represents deviation. Correct observation for this solution is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{{\\\\rm{mix}}}}{\\\\rm{: + ve}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{S}}_{{\\\\rm{mix}}}}{\\\\rm{: + ve}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{V}}_{{\\\\rm{mix}}}}{\\\\rm{: + ve}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/58_21879_question_1.png'),
(21880,'chemistry',55,1241,3021,'The major product of the reaction of','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$-\\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ group given in reactant is electron withdrawing group that deactivates the aromatic ring by decreasing the electron density on the ring through resonance.The resonance only decreases the electron density at the ortho-and para-position hence these sites are less nucleophilic, and so the system tends to react with electrophiles at the meta sites. Hence, ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\left( {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{3}}}} \\right)$ groups attached to benzene ring are meta directors for electrophiles and form compound (b).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_21880_question_1.png'),
(21908,'chemistry',58,169,370,'Solubility of four ionic salts \\[{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{,}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{,}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{4}}}\\] are plotted against temperature in Kelvin. Which of these salts will form exothermic solution \\[\\left( {{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_{{\\rm{sol}}{\\rm{.}}}}{\\rm{ < 0}}} \\right)\\]?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In exothermic solution, solubility will decrease with the increase in temperature.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/58_21908_question_1.png');
