(22173,'chemistry',58,175,383,'Insulin is dissolved in suitable solvent and the osmotic pressure ${\\rm{(atm)\\pi }}$, of solutions of various concentrations $\\left( {{\\rm{g/c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}} \\right)$ C is measured at ${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{7}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$. The slope of $\\left( {\\rm{\\pi }} \\right)$ against C is found to be ${\\rm{4}}{\\rm{.1 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}$. The molecular mass of insulin is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{6 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{\\\\rm{6}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{6 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{3 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Osmotic pressure,<br>${\\rm{\\pi  = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{C}}\\left( {{\\rm{g/cc}}} \\right)}}{{{\\rm{MW}}}}{\\rm{ST}}$<br>The slope of graph plot between osmotic<br>pressure and concentration is $\\frac{{{\\rm{ST}}}}{{{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{W}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}$<br>Given, Slope ${\\rm{ = 4}}{\\rm{.1 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}$<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{ST}}}}{{{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{W}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{\\rm{ = 4}}{\\rm{.1 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}$<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.0821 \\times 300}}}}{{{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{W}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{\\rm{ = 4}}{\\rm{.1 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}$<br>${\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{W}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 6 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{6}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22261,'chemistry',58,168,366,'Equal volumes of 0.1 M ${\\rm{AgN}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.2}}\\,\\,{\\rm{M}}\\,\\,{\\rm{NaCl}}$ are mixed. The concentration of ${\\rm{NO}}_{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{ - }}$ ions in the mixture will be','[{"text": "0.1 M", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.05 M", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.2 M", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.15 M", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When equal volume of two solutions are mixed, volume gets doubled and thus, concentration changes. The new concentration of nitrate ions in the mixture will be<br>$\\frac{{\\rm{M}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.1}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.05M}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22177,'chemistry',58,175,383,'Two solutions of glucose have osmotic pressure 1.5 and 2.5 atm. 1 litre of 1st solution is mixed with 2 litres of 2nd solution.The osmotic pressure of resultant solution will be','[{"text": "21.6 atm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.126 atm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.16 atm", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.216 atm", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{{{{\\rm{\\pi }}_{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{V}}_{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{\\pi }}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{V}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{{{{\\rm{V}}_{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{V}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{\\pi }}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {{\\rm{\\pi }}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\left( {{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.5 \\times 1}}} \\right){\\rm{ + }}\\left( {{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.5 \\times 2}}} \\right)}}{{{\\rm{1 + 2}}}}{\\rm{ = 2}}{\\rm{.16}}\\,{\\rm{atm}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22179,'chemistry',55,1234,2972,'An alkane with molecular mass 72 formed only one substitution product. The number of $1^{\\circ}$ hydrogens in it is.','[{"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let the molecular formula of the alkane be $\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{n}} \\mathrm{H}_{2 \\mathrm{n}+2}$.\nMolecular mass of the alkane $=\\mathrm{nC}+(2 \\mathrm{n}+2) \\mathrm{H}$<br>$ = {\\rm{n}} \\times 12 + (2{\\rm{n}} + 2) \\times 1$<br>$ = 14\\,{\\rm{n}} + 2$<br>$14\\,{\\rm{n}} + 2 = 72$<br>$14\\,{\\rm{n}} = 72 - 2 = 70$<br>${\\rm{n}} = \\frac{{70}}{{14}} = 5$<br>Thus, the molecular formula of the alkane is $\\mathrm{C}_{5} \\mathrm{H}_{12}$. It can have three isomers.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_22179_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>It is neopentane which forms one substitution product as it has only one type of hydrogen.\nNumber of $1^{\\circ}$ hydrogens $=12$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22181,'chemistry',58,175,383,'A solution is prepared by dissolving 1.08 g of human serum albumin. A protein obtained from blood plasma in 50 c.c of aqueous solution. The solution has an osmotic pressure of 5.85 mm of Hg at 298 K . What is the molar mass of albumin?','[{"text": "686.55 g/mole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "68655 g/ mole", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "34328 g/ mole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "343.28 g/mole", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Molecular weight<br>${\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{W}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{R \\times T}}}}{{{\\rm{\\pi  \\times V}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.08 \\times 0}}{\\rm{.0821 \\times 298}}}}{{\\left( {\\frac{{{\\rm{5}}{\\rm{.85}}}}{{{\\rm{760}}}}} \\right)\\left( {{\\rm{50 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}} \\right)}}{\\rm{ = 68655}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22185,'chemistry',58,175,383,'What is the osmotic pressure of the solution obtained by mixing ${\\rm{300c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ of 2% (mass-volume) solution of urea with ${\\rm{300c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ of 3.42% solution of sucrose at ${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$ ?<br>$\\left( {{\\rm{R = 0}}{\\rm{.082Latm}}{{\\rm{K}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "5 atm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5.2 atm", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.6 atm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.5 atm", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{100}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ of urea solution contains 2g of urea<br>${\\rm{300}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ of urea solution contains 6g of urea<br>${\\rm{100}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ of sucrose solution contains 3.42 g of sucrose<br>${\\rm{300}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ of sucrose solution contains 10.26 g<br>${\\rm{\\pi v = }}\\left[ {{{\\left( {\\frac{{\\rm{w}}}{{{\\rm{mw}}}}} \\right)}_{{\\rm{urea}}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\left( {\\frac{{\\rm{w}}}{{{\\rm{mw}}}}} \\right)}_{{\\rm{sucrose}}}}} \\right]{\\rm{RT}}$<br>${\\rm{\\pi  \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{600}}}}{{{\\rm{1000}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\left[ {\\frac{{\\rm{6}}}{{{\\rm{60}}}}{\\rm{ + }}\\frac{{{\\rm{10}}{\\rm{.26}}}}{{{\\rm{342}}}}} \\right]{\\rm{ \\times 0}}{\\rm{.0821 \\times 293}}$<br>${\\rm{\\pi  = 5}}{\\rm{.2}}\\,\\,{\\rm{atm}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22189,'chemistry',58,176,385,'If the various terms in the given below expressions have usual meanings, the van’t Hoff factor (i) cannot be calculated by which one of the following expressions?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\pi V = }}\\\\sqrt {\\\\rm{i}} {\\\\rm{nRT}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{T}}_{\\\\rm{f}}}{\\\\rm{ = i}}{{\\\\rm{K}}_{\\\\rm{f}}}{\\\\rm{.m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{T}}_{\\\\rm{f}}}{\\\\rm{ = i}}{{\\\\rm{K}}_{\\\\rm{f}}}{\\\\rm{.m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{P}}_{{\\\\rm{solvent}}}^{\\\\rm{o}}{\\\\rm{ - }}{{\\\\rm{P}}_{{\\\\rm{solution}}}}}}{{{\\\\rm{P}}_{{\\\\rm{solvent}}}^{\\\\rm{o}}}}{\\\\rm{ = i}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{\\\\rm{n}}}{{{\\\\rm{N + n}}}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Van’t Hoff equation is<br>${\\rm{\\pi V = inRT}}$<br>For depression in freezing point,<br>${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{ = i \\times }}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{f}}}{\\rm{ \\times m}}$<br>For elevation in boiling point,<br>${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{T}}_{\\rm{b}}}{\\rm{ = i \\times }}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{b}}}{\\rm{ \\times m}}$<br>For lowering of vapour pressure,<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{P}}_{{\\rm{solvent}}}^{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{ - }}{{\\rm{P}}_{{\\rm{solution}}}}}}{{{\\rm{P}}_{{\\rm{solvent}}}^{\\rm{0}}}}{\\rm{ = i}}\\left( {\\frac{{\\rm{n}}}{{{\\rm{N + n}}}}} \\right){\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
