(22226,'chemistry',58,170,374,'At ${\\rm{8}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$, the vapour pressure of pure liquid ‘A’ is 520 mm Hg and that of pure liquid ‘B’ is 1000 mm Hg. If a mixture of solution of ‘A’ and ‘B’ boils at ${\\rm{8}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$ and 1 atm pressure, the amount of ‘A’ in the mixture is (1 atm = 760 mm Hg)','[{"text": "50 mole percent", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "52 mole percent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "34 mole percent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "48 mole percent", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{T}}}{\\rm{ = p}}_{\\rm{A}}^{\\rm{0}}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}{\\rm{ + p}}_{\\rm{B}}^{\\rm{0}}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{B}}}$<br>Mixture of solution boils at 1 atm = 760 mm = total pressure<br>${\\rm{760 = 520}}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}{\\rm{ + 1000}}\\left( {{\\rm{1 - }}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}} \\right)$<br>${{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.5}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mol\\% }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{A = 50\\% }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22229,'chemistry',58,172,380,'Vapour pressure of water at 293K is 17.535mm Hg. The vapour pressure of the solution at 293K when 25g of glucose is dissolved in 450g of water is','[{"text": "17.44 mm Hg", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.744 mm Hg", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16.44 mm Hg", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "17.535 mm Hg", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\frac{{{{\\rm{P}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{ - P}}}}{{{{\\rm{P}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{{\\rm{w}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{{{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{w}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{\\rm{ \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{w}}_{\\rm{1}}}}}{{{{\\rm{w}}_{\\rm{1}}}}}$<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{17}}{\\rm{.535 - P}}}}{{{\\rm{17}}{\\rm{.535}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{25}}}}{{{\\rm{180}}}}{\\rm{ \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{18}}}}{{{\\rm{450}}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,{\\rm{P = 17}}{\\rm{.44}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mm}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Hg}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22232,'chemistry',58,172,380,'Three solutions are prepared by adding ‘w’ gram of ‘A’ into 1 kg of water, ‘w’ gram of ‘B’ into another 1 kg of water and ‘w’ gram of ‘C’ in another 1 kg of water (A, B, C are non electrolyte). Dry air is passed from these solutions in sequence $\\left( {A \\to B \\to C} \\right)$. The loss in weight of solution A was found to be 2g, while solution B gained 0.5 g and solution C lost 1 g. Then the relation between molar masses of A, B and C are','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{M}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}{\\\\rm{:}}{{\\\\rm{M}}_{\\\\rm{B}}}{\\\\rm{:}}{{\\\\rm{M}}_{\\\\rm{C}}}{\\\\rm{ = 4:3:5}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{M}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}{\\\\rm{:}}{{\\\\rm{M}}_{\\\\rm{B}}}{\\\\rm{:}}{{\\\\rm{M}}_{\\\\rm{C}}}{\\\\rm{ = }}\\\\frac{{\\\\rm{1}}}{{\\\\rm{4}}}{\\\\rm{:}}\\\\frac{{\\\\rm{1}}}{{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{:}}\\\\frac{{\\\\rm{1}}}{{\\\\rm{5}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{M}}_{\\\\rm{C}}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{M}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{M}}_{\\\\rm{B}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{M}}_{\\\\rm{B}}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{M}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{M}}_{\\\\rm{C}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The loss in water should be proportional to vapor pressure above that solution :<br>${\\rm{So,}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{P}}_{{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{A}}}}} \\propto {\\rm{2g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{P}}_{{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{B}}}}} \\propto {\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.5g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{P}}_{{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{C}}}}} \\propto {\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.5g}}$<br>So, maximum vapor pressure is above C solution; hence it is having minimum lowering and hence minimum mole fraction (hence minimum number of moles of solute). So, maximum molar mass of a substance.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22233,'chemistry',55,1237,2990,'Addition of HCl to 4,4 - dimethyl-2-pentene yields two products one of which has rearranged carbon skeleton. The ratio of number of carbon atoms in the final product to the degree of intermediate formed in the chemical reaction is ____ .','[{"text": "2.33", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.75", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5.65", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The reaction takes place as:  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_22233_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Ratio=7/3=2.33',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22237,'chemistry',54,1201,2869,'In the given compound, the number of ${\\mathrm{2^{\\circ}}}$ carbon atoms is ____.','[{"text": "Four", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "One", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Two", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A carbon atom that is attached with two other carbon atom is known as ${\\mathrm{2^{\\circ}}}$ carbon atom. In this compound there is one, ${\\mathrm{2^{\\circ}}}$ carbon atom.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_22237_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_22237_question_1.png'),
(22238,'chemistry',58,170,374,'Benzene and toulene form nearly ideal solutions. At ${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$, the vapour pressure of benzene is 75 torr and that of toulene is 22 torr. The partial vapour pressure of benzene at ${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$ for a solution containing 78 g of benzene and 46 g of toulene in torr is','[{"text": "50", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "25", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "37.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "53.5", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${P_B} = P_B^0{X_B}$<br>${\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{B}}^{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{ = 75}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{torr}}$<br>${{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{B}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\frac{{{\\rm{78}}}}{{{\\rm{78}}}}}}{{\\left( {\\frac{{{\\rm{78}}}}{{{\\rm{78}}}}} \\right){\\rm{ + }}\\left( {\\frac{{{\\rm{46}}}}{{{\\rm{92}}}}} \\right)}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{1 + 0}}{\\rm{.5}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.5}}}}$<br>${{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{B}}}{\\rm{ = 75 \\times }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.5}}}}{\\rm{ = 50}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{torr}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22240,'chemistry',54,1202,2870,'Bond line formula of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HOCH}(\\mathrm{CN})_{2}}}$ is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In bond line formula of an organic compound only heteroatoms and functional groups are shown. So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22241,'chemistry',58,170,374,'Which of the following plots does not represent the behaviour of an ideal binary liquid solution?','[{"text": "Plot of ${{\\\\rm{p}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{versus}}\\\\,\\\\,{{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}$ (mole fraction of A in liquid phase) is linear", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plot of ${{\\\\rm{p}}_{\\\\rm{B}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{versus}}\\\\,\\\\,{{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{B}}}$ is linear", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plot of ${{\\\\rm{P}}_{{\\\\rm{total}}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{total}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{versus}}\\\\,\\\\,{{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{(or}}\\\\,\\\\,{{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{B}}}{\\\\rm{)}}$ is linear", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plot of${{\\\\rm{p}}_{{\\\\rm{total}}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{versus}}\\\\,\\\\,{{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}$ is nonlinear", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Plot of ${{\\rm{p}}_{{\\rm{total}}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{versus}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{x}}_{\\rm{A}}}$ is linear',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22242,'chemistry',55,1237,2986,'How many distinct alkene products are possible when the alkyl iodide below undergoes E2 elimination? (Including stereoisomers)','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_22242_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/55_22242_question_1.png'),
(22244,'chemistry',58,170,376,'The vapour pressures of benzene, toluene and xylene are 75, 22 and 10 torr at ${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$. Which of the following is not a possible value of the vapour pressure of an equimolar binary/ternary solution of these at ${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$ ? Assume all form ideal solution with each other.','[{"text": "48.5 torr", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16 torr", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "35.66 torr", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "53.5 torr", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{T}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {{\\rm{75 + 22}}} \\right){\\rm{ = 48}}{\\rm{.5}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{torr}}$<br>${{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{T}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {{\\rm{75 + 10}}} \\right){\\rm{ = 42}}{\\rm{.5}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{torr}}$<br>${{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{T}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {{\\rm{22 + 10}}} \\right){\\rm{ = 16}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{torr}}$<br>${{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{T}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\left( {{\\rm{75 + 22 + 10}}} \\right)}}{{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ = 35}}{\\rm{.66}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{torr}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22246,'chemistry',54,1211,2901,'Match Column I (Pair of compounds) with Column II (Isomerism) Choose the correct answer from the options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A.n-propanol and isopropanal<br>B.Methoxypropane and ethoxyethane<br>C.Propanone and propanal<br>D.Neopentane and isopentane</div><div class="column-right">P.Metamerism<br>Q.Chain isomerism<br>R.Position isomerism<br>S.Functional isomerism</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q,B}} - {\\\\rm{P,C}} - {\\\\rm{S,D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R,B}} - {\\\\rm{P,C}} - {\\\\rm{S,D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R,B}} - {\\\\rm{P,C}} - {\\\\rm{Q,D}} - {\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P,B}} - {\\\\rm{R,C}} - {\\\\rm{S,D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}} - {\\rm{OH}}\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{and }}\\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{n - propanol}}\n\\end{array}$ <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_22246_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  are position isomers because these isomers have same molecular formula but difference in position of functional group.<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}} - {\\rm{O}} - {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\\\\n\\,\\,{\\rm{Methoxy}}\\,\\,{\\rm{propane}}\n\\end{array}$ and <br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{_5}} - {\\rm{O}} - {{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{_5}}\\\\\n\\,\\,{\\rm{Ethoxy}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ethane}}\n\\end{array}$<br> are metamers because these isomers have same molecular formula but difference in attachment of alkyl group around the functional group. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_22246_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  and<br> $\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{CHO}}\\\\\n{\\rm{Propanal}}\n\\end{array}$<br>are functional isomers because these isomers have same molecular formula but difference in the functional group.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_22246_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  and <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_22246_explanation_4.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  are chain isomers because these isomers have same molecular formula but difference in the chain length.<br>So,the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22247,'chemistry',58,170,374,'${{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{A}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{B}}}$ are vapour pressure of pure liquid components, A and B respectively of an ideal binary solution. If ${{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}$ represents the mole fraction of component A, the total pressure of the solution will be','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{P}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}{\\\\rm{ + }}{{\\\\rm{X}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}\\\\left( {{{\\\\rm{P}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}{\\\\rm{ - }}{{\\\\rm{P}}_{\\\\rm{B}}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{P}}_{\\\\rm{B}}}{\\\\rm{ + }}{{\\\\rm{X}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}\\\\left( {{{\\\\rm{P}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}{\\\\rm{ - }}{{\\\\rm{P}}_{\\\\rm{B}}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{P}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}{\\\\rm{ + }}{{\\\\rm{X}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}\\\\left( {{{\\\\rm{P}}_{\\\\rm{B}}}{\\\\rm{ - }}{{\\\\rm{P}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{P}}_{\\\\rm{B}}}{\\\\rm{ + }}{{\\\\rm{X}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}\\\\left( {{{\\\\rm{P}}_{\\\\rm{B}}}{\\\\rm{ - }}{{\\\\rm{P}}_{\\\\rm{A}}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{P = }}{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{A}}}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{B}}}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{B}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{A}}}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{B}}}\\left( {{\\rm{1 - }}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}} \\right)$<br>${\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{A}}}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{B}}}{\\rm{ - }}{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{B}}}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}{\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{B}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}\\left( {{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{A}}}{\\rm{ - }}{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{B}}}} \\right)$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22249,'chemistry',54,1209,2893,'Which of the following is metamer of the given compound ( X )?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_22249_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  and <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_22249_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  are  Metamers, Metamers are isomers having same molecular formula and same functional group but different alkyl or aryl groups on either side of functional group.<br> So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/54_22249_question_1.png'),
(22250,'chemistry',58,170,376,'Formation of a solution from two components can be considered as<br>(i) pure solvent $ \\to $ separated solvent molecules, ${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{1}}}$<br>(ii) pure solute $ \\to $ separated solute molecules, ${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>(iii) separated solvent and solute molecules $ \\to $ solution, ${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$<br>Solution so formed will be ideal if','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{{\\\\rm{Soln}}}}{\\\\rm{ = \\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\rm{ - \\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ - \\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{{\\\\rm{Soln}}}}{\\\\rm{ = \\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{ - \\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\rm{ - \\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{{\\\\rm{Soln}}}}{\\\\rm{ = \\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\rm{ + \\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ + \\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{{\\\\rm{Soln}}}}{\\\\rm{ = \\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\rm{ + \\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ - \\\\Delta }}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If net ${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{H}}_{{\\rm{so}}{\\kern 1pt} {\\kern 1pt} {\\rm{In}}}}$ is the sum of three steps, this means that solute-solvent interactions are similar to solvent - solvent and solute - solute interactions.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22252,'chemistry',54,1204,2875,'Functional group present in sulphonic acid is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{-\\\\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\\\mathrm{H}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{-\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{-\\\\mathrm{SO}_{3} \\\\mathrm{H}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{SO}_{3} \\mathrm{H}}}$ group is present in sulphonic acid. So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22253,'chemistry',58,170,374,'The total vapor pressure of a 4 mole% solution of ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$ in water at 293 K is 50 torr. The vapor pressure of pure water is 17 torr at this temperature. Applying Henry\'s law i.e., Solubility ${\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{H}}}{\\rm{ \\times }}$ Pressure and Raoult\'s law, calculate the total vapour pressure for a 5 mole % solution.','[{"text": "33 torr", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "42 torr", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "50.25 torr", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "58.25 torr", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to RLVP, $\\frac{{{{\\rm{P}}^{\\rm{0}}}{\\rm{ - P}}}}{{{{\\rm{P}}^{\\rm{0}}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{{\\rm{w}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ \\times M}}{{\\rm{W}}_{\\rm{1}}}}}{{{\\rm{m}}{{\\rm{w}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ \\times }}{{\\rm{W}}_{\\rm{1}}}}}$<br>4 mole% ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$ means 68 g ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$  in 1800 g of water.<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{17 - P}}}}{{\\rm{P}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\left( {{\\rm{68 \\times 18}}} \\right)}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{17 \\times 1800}}} \\right)}}$<br>P = 0.68 torr<br>Vapour pressure of ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O = 17 - 0}}{\\rm{.68 = 16}}{\\rm{.32}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{torr}}$<br>Vapour pressure of ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ = 50 - 16}}{\\rm{.32 = 33}}{\\rm{.68}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{torr}}$<br>From Henry\'s law,<br>${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{H}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{4}}}{{{\\rm{33}}{\\rm{.68}}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.118}}$<br>5 mole% ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$  means 85 g ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$ in 1800 g water<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{17 - P}}}}{{\\rm{P}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\left( {{\\rm{85 \\times 18}}} \\right)}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{17 \\times 1800}}} \\right)}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.85}}$<br>Vapour pressure of water ${\\rm{ =  17}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,\\,{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.85  =  16}}{\\rm{.15}}$<br>From Henry\'s law,<br>P = 42.1<br>Total vapor pressure = ${\\rm{16}}{\\rm{.51  +  42}}{\\rm{.1  =  58}}{\\rm{.25 torr}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22255,'chemistry',54,1207,2883,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left"><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_22255_qtext_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  IUPAC name of compound A  is  4-chloro-1,3-dinitrobenzene.</div><div class="column-right"><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_22255_qtext_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  IUPAC name of compound B is 4-ethyl-2-methylaniline.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/54_22255_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
