(22378,'chemistry',58,170,374,'The vapour pressure of pure liquid ‘A’ at ${\\rm{310^\\circ C}}$ is 120 torr. The vapour pressure of this liquid in solution with liquid B is 72 torr. Calculate the mole fraction of ‘A’ in solution if the mixture obeys Raoult’s law.','[{"text": "0.06", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.6", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to Raoult’s law,<br>${{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{A}}}{\\rm{ = P}}_{\\rm{A}}^{\\rm{0}}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}$<br>${\\rm{72 = }}\\left( {{\\rm{120}}} \\right)\\left( {{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}} \\right)$<br>${{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.6}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22379,'chemistry',59,373,697,'Impure copper containing Fe, Au, Ag as impurities is electrolytically refined. A current of 140 A for 482.5 s decreased the mass of the anode by 22.26 g and increased the mass of cathode by 22.011 g. Percentage of iron in impure copper is<br>(Given: molar mass Fe $ = 55.5\\,\\,g\\,\\,mo{l^{ - 1}},$ molar mass $Cu = 63.54\\,\\,g\\,\\,mo{l^{ - 1}}$)','[{"text": "0.95", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.85", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.97", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.90}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{u}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{Cu}}$<br>${\\rm{Equivalent}}\\,\\,{\\rm{wt}}{\\rm{. = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{63}}{\\rm{.54}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 31}}{\\rm{.77}}$<br>${\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{Fe}}$<br>${\\rm{Equivalent}}\\,\\,{\\rm{wt}}{\\rm{. = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{55}}{\\rm{.5}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 27}}{\\rm{.75}}$<br>Mass increased at cathode is due to deposition of Cu.<br>Hence, no. of gram equivalents of Cu deposited<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{22}}{\\rm{.011}}}}{{{\\rm{31}}{\\rm{.77}}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.6928}}$<br>Now, using Faraday’s first law,<br>${\\rm{Q = it = 140 A \\times 482}}{\\rm{.5 s = 67550}}\\,\\,{\\rm{C}}$<br>96,500 C gives 31.77 g Cu<br>67550 C will give =<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{31}}{\\rm{.77}}}}{{{\\rm{96,500}}}}{\\rm{ \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{67550}}}}{{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{ = 22}}{\\rm{.239}}\\,\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{pure}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Cu}}$<br>But, according to the question, the mass of cathode only increases = 22.011 g<br>Hence, ${\\rm{22}}{\\rm{.239  -  22}}{\\rm{.011  =  0}}{\\rm{.228 g}}$<br>Now, 31.77 g Cu $ \\equiv $ 0.228 g deposited<br>${\\rm{27}}{\\rm{.75g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{Fe}} \\equiv \\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.228}}}}{{{\\rm{31}}{\\rm{.77}}}}{\\rm{ \\times 27}}{\\rm{.75}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.199g}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{Fe}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{deposited}}$<br>${\\rm{\\% }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{Fe = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{Mass}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{Fe}}}}{{\\mathop {{\\rm{Mass}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{impurities}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{(at}}{\\kern 1pt} {\\kern 1pt} {\\rm{anode)}}} }}{\\rm{ \\times 100}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.199}}}}{{{\\rm{22}}{\\rm{.26}}}}{\\rm{ \\times 100 = 0}}{\\rm{.894}} \\approx {\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.90\\% }}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
