(22383,'chemistry',59,373,697,'Two electrolytic cells are connected in series containing ${\\rm{CuS}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ solution and molten ${\\rm{AlC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}}$. If in electrolysis 0.4 moles of ‘Cu’ are deposited on cathode of first cell. The number of moles of \'Al\' deposited on cathode of the second cell is','[{"text": "0.6 moles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.27 moles", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.18 moles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.4 moles", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'First find weight of copper deposited, by using the formula number of moles<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{weight}}}}{{{\\rm{molecular}}\\,\\,{\\rm{weight}}}}$<br>and then calculate weight of Al deposited and number of moles af Al by using second law of faraday’s.<br>Given,<br>Number of moles of Cu deposited = 0.4 moles<br>According to Faraday’s second law,<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{weight}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Cu}}\\,\\,{\\rm{deposited}}}}{{{\\rm{weight}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Al}}\\,\\,{\\rm{deposited}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{Eq}}\\,\\,{\\rm{wt}}{\\rm{.of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Cu}}}}{{{\\rm{Eq}}\\,\\,{\\rm{wt}}{\\rm{.of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Al}}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(1)}}$<br>${\\rm{No}}{\\rm{.}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{moles = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{weight}}}}{{{\\rm{molecular}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{weight}}}}$<br>${\\rm{Weight}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{Cu = 0}}{\\rm{.4 \\times 63}}{\\rm{.5}}$<br>Now, from Eq. (1).<br>$\\left( {\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.4 \\times 63}}{\\rm{.5}}}}{{{\\rm{weight}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{Al}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{deposited}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\frac{{{\\rm{63}}{\\rm{.5}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}}}{{\\frac{{{\\rm{27}}}}{{\\rm{3}}}}}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore $ Weight of Al deposited =<br>$\\frac{{0.4 \\times 63.5 \\times 9}}{{31.75}} = 7.2g$<br>Now, number of moles of Al deposited =<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{7}}{\\rm{.2}}}}{{{\\rm{27}}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.27}}\\,\\,{\\rm{moles}}{\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22386,'chemistry',59,371,693,'The conductivity of an electrolytic solution decreases on dilution due to','[{"text": "decrease in number of ions per unit volume", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "increase in ionic mobility of ions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increase in percentage ionisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increase in number of ions per unit volume", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Conductivity always decreases with increase in dilution of both the strong and weak electrolytes. This is due to the fact that the number of ions that carry current in a volume of solution always decreases upon dilution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22387,'chemistry',58,170,373,'Vapour pressure of a solution is','[{"text": "Inversely proportional to the mole fraction of the solvent.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Directly proportional to the mole fraction of the solvent.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Directly proportional to the mole fraction of the solute.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inversely proportional to the mole fraction of the solute.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For solutions containing non-volatile solutes, the Raoult’s law may be stated as at a given temperature, the vapour pressure of a solution containing non-volatile solute is directly proportional to the mole fraction of the solvent.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22390,'chemistry',58,170,374,'At ${\\rm{25^\\circ C}}$ the total pressure of an ideal solution obtained by mixing 3 mole of A and 2 mole of B, is 184 torr. What is the vapour pressure (in torr) of pure B at the same temperature (vapour pressure of pure A at ${\\rm{25^\\circ C}}$ is 200 torr)?','[{"text": "180", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "160", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "100", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{{\\rm{n}}_{\\rm{A}}}}}{{\\left( {{{\\rm{n}}_{\\rm{A}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{n}}_{\\rm{B}}}} \\right)}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{3}}}{{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.6}}$<br>${{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{B}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.4}}$<br>${\\rm{P = P}}_{\\rm{A}}^{\\rm{0}}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{A}}}{\\rm{ + P}}_{\\rm{B}}^{\\rm{0}}{{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{B}}}$<br>${\\rm{184 = 200 \\times 0}}{\\rm{.6 + P}}_{\\rm{B}}^{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{ \\times 0}}{\\rm{.4}}$<br>${\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{A}}^{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{ = 160}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{torr}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22391,'chemistry',59,373,697,'When a quantity of electricity is passed through ${\\rm{CuS}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ solution, 0.16 g of copper gets deposited. If the same quantity of electricity is passed through acidulated water, then the volume of ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ liberated at STP will be [given: at. wt. of Cu = 64]','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{4}}{\\\\rm{.0}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{56}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{604}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{8}}{\\\\rm{.0}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\frac{{{\\rm{wt}}{\\rm{.of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Cu}}\\,\\,{\\rm{deposited}}}}{{{\\rm{wt}}{\\rm{.of}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{produced}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{eq}}{\\rm{.wt}}{\\rm{.of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Cu}}}}{{{\\rm{eq}}{\\rm{.wt}}{\\rm{.of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{H}}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{64/2}}}}{{\\rm{1}}}$<br>${\\rm{or,}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.16}}}}{{{\\rm{wt}}{\\rm{.of}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{32}}}}{{\\rm{1}}}$<br>${\\rm{or,}}\\,\\,{\\rm{wt}}{\\rm{.}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.16}}}}{{{\\rm{32}}}}{\\rm{ = 5 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}{\\rm{g}}$<br>${\\rm{Vol}}{\\rm{.of}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{liberated}}\\,\\,{\\rm{at}}\\,\\,{\\rm{STP}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{22400}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ \\times 5 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 56}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22392,'chemistry',55,1237,2982,'Number of ${\\mathrm{\\sigma}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\pi}}$ bonds present in ethylene molecule is respectively','[{"text": "4 and 1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 and 1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5 and 1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5 and 2", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Ethylene, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{4}}}$ <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_22392_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Number of ${\\mathrm{\\sigma}}$ bonds ${\\mathrm{=5}}$<br>Number ${\\mathrm{\\pi}}$ bonds ${\\mathrm{=1}}$<br>So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
