(22408,'chemistry',55,1237,2984,'Which one of the following will show geometrical isomerism?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Conditions to show geometrical isomerism<br>(1) Restricted rotation around the atoms, e.g., ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{C}, \\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{N}, \\mathrm{N}=\\mathrm{N}}}$ and alicyclic compounds.<br>(2) Same group should not present on the same side of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{C}}}$. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_22408_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Geometrical isomers do not exist because two H (same group) are present on same carbon. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_22408_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Geometrical isomers exist as different groups are present on both carbon atoms. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/55_22408_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Geometrical isomers do not exist as carbon of the ring has same group of atoms.<br>So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
