(22475,'chemistry',58,168,368,'1 kg of NaOH is added to 10 ml of 0.1N HCl, the resulting solution will','[{"text": "red litumus paper turns blue", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "turn phenolphthalein solution pink", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "turn methyl orange red", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "will have no effect on red or blue litmus paper", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Number of moles of NaOH<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{Given}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mass}}}}{{{\\rm{Molar}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mass}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1000}}}}{{{\\rm{40}}}}{\\rm{ = 25moles}}$<br>Molarity of HCl = Normality of HCl = 0.1 M<br>Number of moles of HCl =<br>${\\rm{M \\times V}}\\left( {\\rm{L}} \\right){\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.1 \\times 0}}{\\rm{.01 = 0}}{\\rm{.001}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{moles}}$<br>Due to a greater number of moles of NaOH, solution is basic in nature. Hence, turns red litmus paper to blue.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22476,'chemistry',59,371,693,'An increases in equivalent conductivity of strong electrolyte with dilution is mainly due to','[{"text": "Increases in both, i.e., number of ions and ionic mobility", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increases in number of ions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase in ionic molility of ions", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100\\\\% $ ionisation of electrolyte at normal dilution", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${{\\rm{\\Delta }}_{{\\rm{eq}}}}. \\propto \\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{N}}}{\\rm{.}}$<br>On dilution, normality decreases, number of charge carriers per ${\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ decreases but the volume increases. Consequently, the ionic mobility increases, which increase equivalent conductance.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
