,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22942,'chemistry',58,174,382,'What is the normal b.p. of an aqueous solution whose freezing point is $-2.48^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ ?<br>$\\left(\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{f}}=1.86^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} . \\mathrm{kg} / \\mathrm{mol}, \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}}=0.512^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} . \\mathrm{kg} / \\mathrm{mol}\\right)$','[{"text": "$100.7^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$102.5^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$109.0^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$99.3^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\dfrac{\\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{f}}}{\\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{b}}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{f}}}{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{2.48}{\\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{b}}}=\\dfrac{1.86}{0.512}$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{b}}=\\dfrac{2.48 \\times 0.512}{1.86}=0.68$<br>$\\approx 0.7$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{Tb}=\\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{b}}^{0}+\\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{b}}=100+0.7$<br>$=100.7^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22945,'chemistry',59,372,694,'Kohlrausch’s law sates that at:','[{"text": "Infinite dilution, each ion makes definite contribution to equivalent conductance of an electrolyte, whatever be the nature of the other ion of the electrolyte.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Finite dilution, each ion makes definite contribution to equivalent conductance of an electrolyte, whatever be the nature of the other ion electrolyte", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Infinite dilution, each ion makes definite contribution to conductance of an electrolyte whatever be the nature of the other ion of the electrolyte", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Infinite dilution, each ion makes definite contribution to equivalent conductance of an electrolyte depending on the nature of the other ion of the electrolyte", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'It is definition of Kohlrausch’s law.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22946,'chemistry',58,176,385,'$\\mathrm{KBr}$ is $80 \\%$ dissociated in aqueous solution of 0.5 m concentration (given, $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{f}}$ for water ${\\text{ = 1}}{\\text{.86}}\\,{\\text{K}}\\,\\,{\\text{Kg }}\\,{\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$ ). The solution freezes at:','[{"text": "$271.326 \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$272 \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$270.5 \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$268.5 \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here degree of dissociation $=80 \\%$ or 0.8<br>$\\alpha  = \\frac{{{\\text{i  -  1}}}}{{{\\text{n  -  1}}}}$<br>${\\text{0}}{\\text{.8  =  }}\\frac{{{\\text{i  -  1}}}}{{{\\text{2  -  1}}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{i}=1.8$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{f}}=\\mathrm{i} \\mathrm{k}_{\\mathrm{f}} \\cdot \\mathrm{m}=1.8 \\times 1.86 \\times 0.5 \\Rightarrow \\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{f}}=1.674$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{f}}=\\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{f}}^{0}-\\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{f}} \\Rightarrow 1.674=273-\\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{f}}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{f}}=271.326 \\mathrm{~K}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22947,'chemistry',60,588,1184,'The following results were obtained during kinetic studies of the reaction; $2 A+B \\rightarrow$ products<br>The time (in minutes) required to consume half of $A$ is','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "100", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Assume ${\\text{r = k[A}}{{\\text{]}}^{\\text{x}}}{{\\text{[B]}}^{\\text{y}}}$<br>Taking exp. I and II, we get order of [B]<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\left(\\dfrac{0.20}{0.25}\\right)^{\\mathrm{y}}=\\dfrac{6.93 \\times 10^{-3}}{6.93 \\times 10^{-3}}=1 \\\\& \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\mathrm{y}}=\\left[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right]^{0} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{y}=0\\end{aligned}$<br>Taking expanision I and III,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{6.93 \\times 10^{-3}}{1.386 \\times 10^{-2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x} \\\\& x=1\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\therefore$ Overall order $=0+1=1$<br>Now, rate $=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{1}[\\mathrm{~B}]^{0}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& 6.93 \\times 10^{-3}=\\mathrm{k}[0.10] \\\\& \\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{6.93 \\times 10^{-3}}{0.10}=0.0693 \\mathrm{~min} \\\\& \\mathrm{t}_{1 / 2}=\\dfrac{0.693}{\\mathrm{k}}=\\dfrac{0.693}{0.0693}=10 \\mathrm{~min}\\end{aligned}$<br>To consume half of $A$ it becomes $\\dfrac{10}{2}=5 \\mathrm{~min}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_22947_question_1.png'),
(22949,'chemistry',59,371,693,'The equivalent conductance of NaCl at concentration C and at infinite dilution are ${{\\rm{\\lambda }}_{\\rm{c}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{\\lambda }}_{\\rm{o}}}$  is given as','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{\\\\lambda }}_{\\\\rm{c}}}{\\\\rm{ = }}{{\\\\rm{\\\\lambda }}_{\\\\rm{o}}}{\\\\rm{ - (B)}}\\\\sqrt {\\\\rm{C}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{\\\\lambda }}_{\\\\rm{c}}}{\\\\rm{ = }}{{\\\\rm{\\\\lambda }}_{\\\\rm{o}}}{\\\\rm{ + (B)}}\\\\sqrt {\\\\rm{C}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{\\\\lambda }}_{\\\\rm{c}}}{\\\\rm{ = }}{{\\\\rm{\\\\lambda }}_{\\\\rm{o}}}{\\\\rm{ + (B)C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{\\\\lambda }}_{\\\\rm{c}}}{\\\\rm{ = }}{{\\\\rm{\\\\lambda }}_{\\\\rm{o}}}{\\\\rm{ - (B)C}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Debye-Huckel Onsager equation can be written as<br>${{\\rm{\\lambda }}_{\\rm{c}}}{\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{\\lambda }}_\\infty }{\\rm{ - (B)}}\\sqrt {\\rm{C}} $<br>Where,<br>${{\\rm{\\lambda }}_{\\rm{c}}}{\\rm{ = }}$ Molar conductivity of the solution at certain concentration<br>${{\\rm{\\lambda }}_\\infty }{\\rm{ = }}$ Limiting molar conductivity<br>C = Concentration<br>B = Constant that depends on temperature, charges on the ions and dielectric constant as well as viscosity of the solution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22950,'chemistry',58,169,370,'A beaker contains a solution of substance $\\mathrm{A}$. Precipitation of substance A takes place when small amount of $\\mathrm{A}$ is added to the solution. The solution is:','[{"text": "Saturated", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Supersaturated", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Unsaturated", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Concentrated", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When small amount of solute is added to a super saturated solution, precipitation takes place.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22951,'chemistry',60,588,1184,'If $50 \\%$ of a reaction occurs in 100 seconds and $75 \\%$ of the reaction occurs in 200 seconds, the order of this reaction is:','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/60_22951_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> height=351&width=415&top_left_y=813&top_left_x=295 <br>First order reaction as half life is constant.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22952,'chemistry',59,372,694,'The ${\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{o}}\\,\\,{\\rm{for}}\\,\\,{\\rm{CaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{MgS}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$, respectively by using following data<br>${\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{a}}^{{\\rm{ + 2}}}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = 119}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{,\\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}^{\\rm{ - }}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = 76}}{\\rm{.3}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$<br>${\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{g}}^{{\\rm{ + 2}}}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = 106}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{,\\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{SO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{ - 2}}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = 160}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$','[{"text": "466, 266", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "366, 266", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "566, 271", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "271.6, 266", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{CaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}{\\rm{ = \\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{a}}^{{\\rm{ + 2}}}}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}{\\rm{ + 2\\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}^{\\rm{ - }}}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}$<br>${\\rm{ = 119 + }}\\left( {{\\rm{2 \\times 76}}{\\rm{.3}}} \\right){\\rm{ = 271}}{\\rm{.6}}\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$<br>${\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{MgS}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}{\\rm{ = \\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{g}}^{{\\rm{ + 2}}}}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}{\\rm{ + \\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{SO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{ - 2}}}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}$<br>${\\rm{ = 106 + 160 = 266}}\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22954,'chemistry',58,169,372,'Low concentration of oxygen in the blood and tissues of people living at high altitude is due to:','[{"text": "Low temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low atmospheric pressure", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "High atmospheric pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both low temperature and high atmospheric pressure", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Low concentration of oxygen in the blood and tissues of people living at high altitude is due to low atmospheric pressure. Because at high altitude, the partial pressure of oxygen is less than at the ground level.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22955,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'The rate of a reaction A doubles on increasing the temperature from 300 to $310 \\mathrm{~K}$. By how much, the temperature of reaction $\\mathrm{B}$ should be increased from $300 \\mathrm{~K}$ so that rate doubles if activation energy of the reaction $B$ is twice that of reaction $\\mathrm{A}$.','[{"text": "$4.92 \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9.84 \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$19.67 \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.45 \\\\mathrm{~K}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{k_{1}}{k_{2}}\\right)=\\dfrac{E_{a}}{R}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{T_{1}}-\\dfrac{1}{T_{2}}\\right]$<br>${\\rm{ln(2) = }}\\frac{{{{\\rm{E}}_{\\rm{a}}}}}{{\\rm{R}}}\\left\\{ {\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{300}}}}{\\rm{ - }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{310}}}}} \\right\\}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(1)}}$<br>${\\rm{ln}}\\,\\,{\\rm{(2) = 2}}\\frac{{{{\\rm{E}}_{\\rm{a}}}}}{{\\rm{R}}}\\left\\{ {\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{300}}}}{\\rm{ - }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{T}}}} \\right\\}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(2)}}$<br>From (1) and (2)<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{E}}_{\\rm{a}}}}}{{\\rm{R}}}\\left\\{ {\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{300}}}}{\\rm{ - }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{T}}}} \\right\\}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{{\\rm{E}}_{\\rm{a}}}}}{{\\rm{R}}}\\left\\{ {\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{300}}}}{\\rm{ - }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{310}}}}} \\right\\}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{300}}}}{\\rm{ + }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{310}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{T}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\rm{T = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{300 \\times 310}}}}{{{\\rm{610}}}}{\\rm{ \\times 2}}$<br>${\\rm{T = 304}}{\\rm{.92}}$<br>Incrase in $\\mathrm{T}=304.92-300=4.92 \\mathrm{~K}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22984,'chemistry',60,587,1180,'The half life period of a first order reaction is 15 minutes. The amount of substance left after one hour will be :','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{4}$ of the original amount", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{16}$ of the original amount", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{32}$ of the original amount", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{8}$ of the original amount", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Since $t_{1 / 2}=15 \\mathrm{~min}$<br>$\\therefore \\text { No. of half lives }=\\dfrac{6}{15}=4$<br>$\\therefore$ Amount of substance left after one hour<br>$=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{A}_{0}}{(2)^{\\mathrm{n}}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{A}_{0}}{(2)^{4}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{A}_{0}}{16}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22956,'chemistry',59,370,691,'Given ${\\rm{l/a = 0}}{\\rm{.5}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{,R = 50}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ohm,N = 1}}{\\rm{.0}}$. The equivalent conductance of the electrolyte is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{100 oh}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{gm}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{e}}{{\\\\rm{q}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{300 oh}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{gm}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{e}}{{\\\\rm{q}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{20 oh}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{gm}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{e}}{{\\\\rm{q}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{10 oh}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{gm}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{e}}{{\\\\rm{q}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{l/a = 0}}{\\rm{.5 c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{,R = 50 ohm}}$<br>${\\rm{\\rho  = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{Ra}}}}{{\\rm{l}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{50}}}}{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.5}}}}{\\rm{ = 100}}$<br>${\\rm{\\Lambda  = k \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1000}}}}{{\\rm{N}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{\\rho }}}{\\rm{ \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1000}}}}{{\\rm{N}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{\\rm{100}}}}{\\rm{ \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1000}}}}{{\\rm{1}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 10 oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{gm}}\\,\\,{\\rm{e}}{{\\rm{q}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22958,'chemistry',58,175,383,'Consider the figure and mark the correct option:','[{"text": "Water will move from side ${\\\\text{(a)}}$ to side ${\\\\text{(b)}}$ if a pressure lower than osmotic pressure is applied on piston ${\\\\text{(b)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Water will move from side ${\\\\text{(b)}}$ to side ${\\\\text{(a)}}$ if a pressure greater than osmotic pressure is applied on piston ${\\\\text{(b)}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Water will move from side ${\\\\text{(b)}}$ to side ${\\\\text{(a)}}$ if a pressure equal to osmotic pressure is applied on piston ${\\\\text{(b)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Water will move from side ${\\\\text{(a)}}$ to side ${\\\\text{(b)}}$  if pressure equal to osmotic pressure is applied on piston ${\\\\text{(b)}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know that, if a pressure higher than the osmotic pressure is applied on the solution side, the solvent will flow from the solution into the pure solvent through the semipermeable membrane. This process is called reverse osmosis.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/58_22958_question_1.png'),
(22959,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'The rate law for the reaction below is given by the expression, ${\\text{r = k[A][B]}}$<br>${\\text{A + B}} \\to {\\text{ Product}}$<br>If the concentration of B is increased from 0.1 to 0.3 mole, keeping the value of A at 0.1 mole, the rate constant will be :','[{"text": "$9 \\\\mathrm{k}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\mathrm{k}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\text{k}}}{{\\\\text{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{k}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Rate constant does not vary with concentration of reactants.<br>Rate of Constant wth the charge in the constant of the reactant but rate constant does not get affected by it.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2016,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22960,'chemistry',59,372,694,'What will be the molar conductivity of ${\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}$ ions at infinite dilution if molar conductivity of ${\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\left( {{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{3}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{is}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{858}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$ and ionic conductance of ${\\rm{SO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}\\,\\,{\\rm{is}}\\,\\,{\\rm{160}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$ at infinite dilution ?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{429}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{698}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{189}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1018}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{o}}\\left( {{\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{3}}}} \\right){\\rm{ = 2\\lambda }}_{{\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ + 3\\lambda }}_{{\\rm{SO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}}^{\\rm{o}}$<br>${\\rm{858 = 2 \\times \\lambda }}_{{\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ + }}\\left( {{\\rm{3 \\times 160}}} \\right)$<br>${\\rm{2\\lambda }}_{{\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = 189}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22982,'chemistry',58,167,360,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A solution is a homogeneous mixture of two or more chemically non-reacting substances.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Solutions can be made between any two states of matter.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A homogeneous mixture is a single-phase blend with uniformly distributed components, displaying consistent properties throughout. Solutions are homogeneous mixtures and can be made by mixing any two states of matter. Both statements are true, but reason is not the correct explanation. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22962,'chemistry',58,170,373,'Two beakers of capacity $500 \\mathrm{~mL}$ were taken. One of these beakers, labelled as " ${\\text{A}}$ ", was filled with $400 \\mathrm{~mL}$ water whereas the beaker labelled " ${\\text{B}}$ " was filled with $400 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of ${\\text{2}}\\,\\,{\\text{M}}$ solution of $\\mathrm{NaCl}$. At the same temperature both the beakers were placed in closed containers of same material and same capactiy as shown in figure: At a given temperature , which of the following statement is correct about the vapour pressure of pure water and that of NaCl solution?','[{"text": "Vapour pressure in container (A) is more than that in container $(\\\\mathrm{B})$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Vapour pressure in container $(\\\\mathrm{A})$ is less than that in container (B)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vapour pressure is equal in both the containers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vapour pressure in container (B) is twice the vapour pressure in container (A)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a non volatile solute is added to water, the vapour pressure of solution get decreases. This is due to decrease in surface covered by solvent molecule which lead to decrease in number of solvent molecules escaping from the surface corresponding to pure solvent.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/58_22962_question_1.png'),
(22963,'chemistry',60,587,1182,'The reaction of ozone with oxygen atoms in the presence of chlorine atoms can occur by a two step process shown below:<br>${{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{(}}\\,{\\rm{g) &nbsp;+ &nbsp;C}}{{\\rm{l}}^ \\bullet }{\\rm{(g)}} \\to {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(}}\\,{\\rm{g) &nbsp;+ &nbsp;Cl}}{{\\rm{O}}^ \\bullet }{\\rm{(g)}}{{\\rm{k}}_{\\rm{i}}}$<br>${\\text{ = 5}}.{\\text{2}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{\\text{9}}}{\\text{Lmo}}{{\\text{l}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}\\,{{\\text{s}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}{\\text{Cl}}{{\\text{O}}^ \\bullet }({\\text{g}}){\\text{ + }}{{\\text{O}}^ \\bullet }({\\text{g}}) \\to $<br>${{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}(\\,{\\text{g}}){\\text{ + C}}{{\\text{l}}^ \\bullet }({\\text{g}}){{\\text{k}}_{\\text{i}}}$<br>${\\rm{ &nbsp;= &nbsp; 2}}{\\rm{.6 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{10}}}}{\\rm{Lmo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ &nbsp; - &nbsp; 1}}}}\\,{{\\rm{s}}^{{\\rm{ &nbsp; - &nbsp; 1}}}}$<br>The closest rate constant for the overall reaction<br>$\\mathrm{O}_{3}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\mathrm{O}^{\\bullet}(\\mathrm{g}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$ is:','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.4 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{20}}}}{\\\\rm{L}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}\\\\,{{\\\\rm{s}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{5}}{\\\\rm{.2 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{\\\\rm{9}}}{\\\\rm{L}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}\\\\,{{\\\\rm{s}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{3}}{\\\\rm{.1 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{10}}}}{\\\\rm{L}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}\\\\,{{\\\\rm{s}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{2}}{\\\\rm{.6 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{10}}}}{\\\\rm{L}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}\\\\,{{\\\\rm{s}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The rate constant of overall reaction depends on slowest step. Hence equation(1) is slowest step. Option(2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2016,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22964,'chemistry',59,370,690,'Conductance of 0.1 M KCl (conductivity ${\\rm{ = X}}\\,\\,{\\rm{oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$) filled in a conductivity cell is ${\\rm{Y}}\\,\\,{\\rm{oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$. If the conductance of 0.1 M NaOH filled in the same cell is ${\\rm{Z}}\\,\\,{\\rm{oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$, the molar conductance of NaOH will be','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{XZ/Y}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{\\\\rm{4}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{XZ/Y}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{10}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{XZ/Y}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.1}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{XZ/Y}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Conductivity (X) = conductance $ \\times $ cell constant<br>Cell constant = X/Y<br>Conductivity of NaOH = XZ/Y<br>${{\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{\\rm{m}}}\\,\\,\\left( {{\\rm{NaOH}}} \\right){\\rm{ = 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{4}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{XZ/Y}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22966,'chemistry',58,176,385,'The freezing point depression constant for water is $1.86 \\mathrm{~K} \\mathrm{~kg} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$. If ${\\text{5}}{\\text{.00\\,g  N}}{{\\text{a}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}$ is dissolved in $45.0 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$, the freezing point is changed by $-3.82^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$.Calculate the van\'t Hoff factor for $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ :','[{"text": "0.381", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.05", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.63", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3.11", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'using; $\\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{f}}=\\mathrm{i} \\times \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{f}} \\times \\mathrm{m}$<br>$\\mathrm{i}=\\dfrac{\\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{f}} \\times \\mathrm{w}_{\\mathrm{A}}}{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{f}} \\times \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{B}} \\times 1000}$<br>$=\\dfrac{3.82 \\times 45}{1.86 \\times \\dfrac{5}{142} \\times 1000}=2.63$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22967,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'$\\mathrm{A}+2 \\mathrm{~B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}$, the rate equation for this reaction is given as, Rate $=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}]$.<br>If the concentration of $\\mathrm{A}$ is kept the same but that of $\\mathrm{B}$ is doubled what will happen to the rate itself?','[{"text": "Doubled", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Quadrupled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Halved", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Rate $=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}]=\\mathrm{r}$<br>$\\mathrm{r}^{\\prime}=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}][2 \\mathrm{~B}]$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{r}}{\\mathrm{r}^{\\prime}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}]}{\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}][2 \\mathrm{~B}]}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}]}{2 \\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}]}$$\\Rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{r}=\\mathrm{r}^{\\prime} \\text { i.e., rate become double. }$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2015,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22983,'chemistry',59,373,697,'How many coulombs of electricity are consumed when a 100 mA current is passed through a solution of ${\\rm{AgN}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}$ for half an hour during an electrolysis experiment?','[{"text": "108", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "18000", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "180", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3000", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{Q = It}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.1 \\times 1800 = 180}}\\,{\\rm{C}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22968,'chemistry',59,372,694,'The conductivity of a saturated solution of ${\\rm{BaS}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{is}}\\,\\,{\\rm{3}}{\\rm{.06 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 6}}}}{\\rm{oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$  and its equivalent conductance is ${\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.53}}\\,\\,{\\rm{oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{equi}}{{\\rm{v}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$. The ${{\\rm{K}}_{{\\rm{SP}}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{for}}\\,\\,{\\rm{BaS}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ will be','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{4 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 12}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{2}}{\\\\rm{.5 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 9}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{2}}{\\\\rm{.5 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 13}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 6}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{Solubility = }}$<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{k \\times 1000}}}}{{{{\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{eq}}}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{3}}{\\rm{.06 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 6}}}}{\\rm{ \\times 1000}}}}{{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.53}}}}{\\rm{ = 2 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}$<br>${\\rm{Solubility}}\\,\\,{\\rm{in}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mol/lit = 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}$<br>${{\\rm{K}}_{{\\rm{SP}}}}{\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{S}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 6}}}}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22970,'chemistry',58,176,385,'Calculate the number of moles of sodium sulphate required to be dissolved in 12 moles of water to lower its vapour pressure by $10 \\mathrm{mmHg}$ at a $300 \\mathrm{~K}$ temperature (V.P. of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$ $=50 \\mathrm{mmHg}$ ):','[{"text": "2 moles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 moles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 mole", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.5 moles", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{{{{\\text{P}}^{\\text{0}}}{\\text{ -  }}{{\\text{P}}_{\\text{s}}}}}{{{{\\text{P}}^{\\text{0}}}}} = \\frac{{{\\text{i}} \\cdot {{\\text{n}}_{\\text{B}}}}}{{{\\text{i}} \\cdot {{\\text{n}}_{\\text{B}}}{\\text{ +  }}{{\\text{n}}_{\\text{A}}}}}$<br>$\\dfrac{10}{50}=\\dfrac{3 \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{B}}}{3 \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{B}}+12}$<br>${\\text{3}}{{\\text{0}}_{{\\text{nB}}}}{\\text{ + 120 + 15}}{{\\text{0}}_{{\\text{nB}}}}$<br>${\\text{120  =  120}}{{\\text{n}}_{\\text{B}}}$<br>$\\dfrac{120}{120}=\\mathrm{n}_{\\text {B }}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22971,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'The rate constant $(\\mathrm{k})$ for a particular reaction is $1.3 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{M}^{-1} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$ at $\\quad 100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}, \\quad$ and $1.3 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}^{-1} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$ at $150^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. What is the energy of activation $\\left(\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}\\right)$ (in $\\mathrm{kJ}$ ) for the reaction? $\\left(\\mathrm{R}=8.314 \\mathrm{JK}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right)$','[{"text": "132", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "60", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "99", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\log \\dfrac{\\mathrm{k}_{2}}{\\mathrm{k}_{1}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{2.303 \\mathrm{R}}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{~T}_{1}}-\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{~T}_{2}}\\right]$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\log \\dfrac{1.3 \\times 10^{-3}}{1.3 \\times 10^{-4}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{2.303 \\mathrm{R}}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{373}-\\dfrac{1}{423}\\right] \\\\& 1=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{2.303 \\mathrm{R}}\\left[\\dfrac{50}{373 \\times 423}\\right] \\\\& \\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\dfrac{2.303 \\times 8.314 \\times 373 \\times 423}{1000 \\times 50} \\\\& =60.42 \\mathrm{~kJ}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22972,'chemistry',59,372,694,'The conductivity of ${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.001028}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mol}}\\,{{\\rm{L}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$ acetic acid is ${\\rm{4}}{\\rm{.95 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 5}}}}{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$. What will be the degree of dissociation of acetic acid ?<br>$\\left[ {{\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{o}}\\,\\,{\\rm{is}}\\,\\,{\\rm{390}}{\\rm{.5}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}} \\right]$','[{"text": "0.0123", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.23", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.123", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.23", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${{\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{\\rm{m}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{4}}{\\rm{.95 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 5}}}}{\\rm{ \\times 1000}}}}{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.001028}}}}{\\rm{ = 48}}{\\rm{.15}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{S}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$<br>${\\rm{\\alpha = }}\\frac{{{{\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{\\rm{m}}}}}{{{\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{o}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{48}}{\\rm{.15}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}}}{{{\\rm{390}}{\\rm{.5}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.1233}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22974,'chemistry',58,174,382,'An aqueous solution of a compound $A B$ freezes at $0.48^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. At this temperature, $\\mathrm{AB}$ behaves as a non-electrolyte. The same solution boils at $100.26^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ at a P of $1 \\mathrm{~atm}$ (for water, $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{f}}}{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}}}=3.7$ ).<br>We can conclude that compound $\\mathrm{AB}$ at the boiling point of solution,','[{"text": "behaves as a non-electrolyte", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ionises to the extent of $50 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "behaves as a strong electrolyte", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "dissociates in solution", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Apply, $\\Delta {{\\text{T}}_{\\text{f}}}{\\text{ =  }}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{f}}}{\\text{m}}$<br>$0.48 = {{\\text{K}}_{\\text{f}}} \\times {\\text{m}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(1)}}$<br>and $\\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{b}}=\\mathrm{iK}_{\\mathrm{b}} \\mathrm{m}$<br>${\\text{0}}{\\text{.26  =  i}}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{b}}}{\\text{m}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(2)}}$<br>from eq 1,2 ,<br>$\\dfrac{0.48}{0.26}=\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{i}} \\times 3.7 \\quad\\left(\\because \\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{f}}}{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{b}}}=3.7\\right)$<br>$\\mathrm{i}=\\dfrac{3.7}{1.84}=2$<br>Since vant hoff factor, $\\mathrm{i}>1$, its a case of dissociation. It behaves like an electrolyte.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22975,'chemistry',59,372,694,'Molar conductance of 0.1 M acetic acid is ${\\rm{7}}\\,\\,{\\rm{oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$. If the molar conductance of acetic acid at infinite dilution is ${\\rm{380}}{\\rm{.8}}\\,\\,{\\rm{oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$, the value of dissociation constant will be:','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{3}}{\\\\rm{.38 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 5}}}}{\\\\rm{mol}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{d}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 3}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{226 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 5}}}}{\\\\rm{mol}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{d}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 3}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.66 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 3}}}}{\\\\rm{mol}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{d}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.66 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 2}}}}{\\\\rm{mol}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{d}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 3}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{a}}}{\\rm{ = C}}{{\\rm{\\alpha }}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.1 \\times }}{\\left( {\\frac{{\\rm{7}}}{{{\\rm{380}}{\\rm{.8}}}}} \\right)^{\\rm{2}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 3}}{\\rm{.38 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 5}}}}{\\rm{mol}}\\,\\,{\\rm{d}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22976,'chemistry',60,585,1175,'For reaction, $2 \\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5} \\rightarrow 4 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}+\\mathrm{O}_{2}$, the rate equation can be expressed in two ways $-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\mathrm{k}\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}\\right]$ and $+\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\mathrm{k}^{\\prime}\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}\\right] . \\mathrm{k}$ and $\\mathrm{k}^{\\prime}$ are related','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{k}=4 \\\\mathrm{k}^{\\\\prime}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{k}=2 \\\\mathrm{k}^{\\\\prime}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{k}=\\\\mathrm{k}^{\\\\prime}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{k}=\\\\mathrm{k}^{\\\\prime}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$2 \\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5} \\rightarrow 4 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}+\\mathrm{O}_{2}$<br>$\\begin{gathered}-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{dt}}\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}\\right]=\\mathrm{k}\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}\\right] \\\\-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{dt}}\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}\\right]=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\mathrm{k}^{\\prime}\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}\\right] \\\\\\Rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{k}=\\mathrm{k}^{\\prime}\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22978,'chemistry',58,176,385,'The $\\mathrm{pH}$ of $1 \\mathrm{M}$ solution of a weak monobasic acid (HA) is 2. Then, what is its van\'t Hoff factor?','[{"text": "1.01", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{H}}{{\\text{A}}_{{\\text{(aq}}{\\text{.) }}}} \\rightleftharpoons {{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ + }}}{\\text{ + }}{{\\text{A}}^{\\text{ - }}}$<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {}&{{\\text{(aq}}{\\text{.)}}}&{{\\text{(aq}}{\\text{.)}}}&{{\\text{(aq}}{\\text{.)}}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{At t  =  0}}}&{\\text{C}}&{\\text{0}}&{\\text{0}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{At t  =  t}}}&{{\\text{ C -  C}}\\alpha }&{{\\text{C}}\\alpha }&{{\\text{C}}\\alpha } \n\\end{array}$<br>${\\text{pH}} = 2$<br>$\\therefore \\left( {{{\\text{H}}^ + }} \\right) = {10^{ - 2}}{\\text{M}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\text{C}}\\alpha  = {10^{ - 2}} \\Rightarrow 1 \\times \\alpha  = {10^{ - 2}}$<br>$\\alpha  = \\frac{{{\\text{i}} - 1}}{{{\\text{n}} - 1}} \\Rightarrow 0.01 = \\frac{{{\\text{i}} - 1}}{{2 - 1}}$<br>${\\text{i}} = 1.01$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22979,'chemistry',59,373,697,'A solution of ${\\rm{CuS}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$  is electrolysed for 10 minutes with a current of 1.5 amperes. What is the mass of copper deposited at the cathode?','[{"text": "0.6 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.3 g", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.9 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.2 g", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{t = 600}}\\,\\,{\\rm{s;}}$<br>${\\rm{Charge = current}} \\times {\\rm{time = 1}}{\\rm{.5}}\\,\\,{\\rm{A}}\\,\\, \\times \\,\\,{\\rm{600 s = 900 C}}$<br>According to the reaction:<br>${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{u}}^{2 + }}(aq) + 2{e^ - } \\to Cu(s)$<br>We require $2F\\,\\,or\\,\\,2 \\times 96487\\,\\,C$ to deposit 1 mol or 63 g of Cu.<br>For 900 C, the mass of Cu deposited<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\left( {{\\rm{63}}\\,\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{ \\times 900 C}}} \\right)}}{{{\\rm{2 \\times 96487 Cmo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.2938}}\\,\\,{\\rm{g}}{\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22980,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'For the reaction, $3 \\mathrm{~A}+2 \\mathrm{~B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}+\\mathrm{D}$, the differential rate law can be written as','[{"text": "$-\\\\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{A}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{C}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=\\\\mathrm{k}[\\\\mathrm{A}]^{\\\\mathrm{n}}[\\\\mathrm{B}]^{\\\\mathrm{m}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$+\\\\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{A}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=-\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{C}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=\\\\mathrm{k}[\\\\mathrm{A}]^{\\\\mathrm{n}}[\\\\mathrm{B}]^{\\\\mathrm{m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{A}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{C}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=\\\\mathrm{k}[\\\\mathrm{A}]^{\\\\mathrm{n}}[\\\\mathrm{B}]^{\\\\mathrm{m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{A}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{C}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=\\\\mathrm{k}[\\\\mathrm{A}]^{\\\\mathrm{n}}[\\\\mathrm{B}]^{\\\\mathrm{m}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Rate $=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{A}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{B}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{C}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{D}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { Rate }=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{\\mathrm{n}}[\\mathrm{B}]^{\\mathrm{m}} \\\\& \\begin{aligned}-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{A}]}{\\mathrm{dt}} & =-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{B}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{C}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{D}]}{\\mathrm{dt}} \\\\& =\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{\\mathrm{n}}[\\mathrm{B}]^{\\mathrm{m}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\text { So, }-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{A}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{C}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{\\mathrm{n}}[\\mathrm{B}]^{\\mathrm{m}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22985,'chemistry',58,174,382,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Larger the value of cryoscopic constant of the solvent, lesser will be the freezing point of the solution.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Extent of depression in the freezing point depends on the nature of the solvent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{f}}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{f}} \\mathrm{m}$<br>$\\Delta T_{f} \\propto K_{f}$<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{f}}=\\mathrm{T}_{\\text {solvent }}-\\mathrm{T}_{\\text {solution }}$<br>As $\\mathrm{T}_{\\text {solvent }}$ is constant, larger the value of $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{f}}$, lower the $\\mathrm{T}_{\\text {solution }}$. Also $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{f}}$ depends on the nature of the solvent. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22987,'chemistry',59,373,697,'During the preparation of ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{8}}}$  (per disulphuric acid) ${{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ gas is also released at anode as byproduct. When 9.72 L of ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ releases at cathode and 2.35 L ${{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ at anode, the weight ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{8}}}$ produced in gram is','[{"text": "51.74", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "87.12", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "43.65", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "83.42", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{2HSO}}_4^ -  \\to {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{8}}} + 2e$<br>${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\to {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, + \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4e$<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.35}}}}{{{\\rm{22}}{\\rm{.4}}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.105}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.42}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mole}}$<br>${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{H}}^ + }\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{ + }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2e\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\to \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>9.72/22.4 mole = 0.87 mole<br>\nmole of e for<br>${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{8}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.87 - 0}}{\\rm{.42 = 0}}{\\rm{.45 mole}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{mole}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{8}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.225}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mole}}$<br>$ \\equiv {\\rm{43}}{\\rm{.65}}\\,{\\rm{g}}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22988,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'In the reaction of formation of sulphur trioxide by contact process, $2 \\mathrm{SO}_{2}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{SO}_{3}$, the rate reaction was measured as $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=-2.5 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$. The rate of reaction in terms of $\\left[\\mathrm{SO}_{2}\\right]$ in ${\\rm{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{L}}^{{\\rm{  -  1}}}}\\,{{\\rm{s}}^{{\\rm{  -  1}}}}$ will be','[{"text": "$-2.25 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-5.00 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$-3.75 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-2.50 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{dt}}\\left[\\mathrm{SO}_{2}\\right]=-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{dt}}\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right]$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{dt}}\\left[\\mathrm{SO}_{2}\\right]=-2 \\times 2.5 \\times 10^{-4}=-5 \\times 10^{-4}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22990,'chemistry',58,168,367,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> One molal aqueous solution of urea contains $60 \\mathrm{~g}$ of urea in $1 \\mathrm{~kg}$ of water.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Solution containing one mole of solute in $1000 \\mathrm{~g}$ solvent is called one molal solution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Molecular weight of urea $\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{2} \\mathrm{CONH}_{2}\\right)$ is 60. $\\mathrm{g} / \\mathrm{mole}$.<br>Number of moles $=\\dfrac{\\text { Weight }}{\\text { Molecular weight }}=\\dfrac{60}{60}=1$<br>$\\mathrm{m}=\\dfrac{\\text { No.of moles of solute }}{\\text { Mass of solvent in } \\mathrm{kg}}=\\dfrac{1 \\mathrm{~mol}}{1 \\mathrm{~kg}}=1 \\mathrm{~m}$<br>$\\therefore 1 \\mathrm{~mol}$ (or) $60 \\mathrm{~g}$ of urea is present in $1 \\mathrm{~kg}$ of solvent.<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22991,'chemistry',59,373,697,'Three electrolytic cells A, B, C containing solutions of ${\\rm{ZnS}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{,}}\\,\\,{\\rm{AgN}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{CuS}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ respectively are connected in series. A steady current of 1.5 amperes was passed through them until 1.45 g of silver deposited at the cathode of cell B. What mass of zinc was deposited? (At. Mass of Ag = 108 amu, At. Mass of Zn = 65.3 amu)','[{"text": "1.45 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.5 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.438 g", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.348 g", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'By applying Faraday’s second law,<br>${\\left( {\\frac{{\\rm{w}}}{{\\rm{E}}}} \\right)_{{\\rm{Ag}}}}{\\rm{ = }}{\\left( {\\frac{{\\rm{w}}}{{\\rm{E}}}} \\right)_{{\\rm{Zn}}}}$<br>$\\left( {\\frac{{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.45}}}}{{{\\rm{108}}}}} \\right){\\rm{ = }}{\\left( {\\frac{{\\rm{w}}}{{{\\rm{65}}{\\rm{.3/2}}}}} \\right)_{{\\rm{Zn}}}}$<br>Mass of zinc deposited = 0.438 g',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22993,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'A radioactive isotope having a half-life period of 3 days was received after 12 days. If $3 \\mathrm{~g}$ of the isotope is left in the container, what would be the initial mass of the isotope?','[{"text": "$12 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{36 g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$48 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$24 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given $t_{1 / 2}=3$<br>Total time, $\\mathrm{T}=12$<br>No. of half lives $(\\mathrm{n})=\\dfrac{12}{3}=4$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\mathrm{n}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{N}}{\\mathrm{N}_{\\mathrm{o}}} \\\\& \\therefore\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4}=\\dfrac{3}{\\mathrm{~N}}\\end{aligned}$$\\dfrac{3}{\\mathrm{~N}}=\\dfrac{1}{16}, \\quad \\mathrm{~N}=48 \\mathrm{~g}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22994,'chemistry',58,170,376,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Dilute solution of benzene and toluene is an ideal solution.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Benzene and toluene form $\\mathrm{H}$-bonding with each other.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both benzene and toluene are non polar and do not show any $\\mathrm{H}$ - bonding. Also, upon mixing them, the intermolecular attractive forces before and after mixing are nearly equal. Thus, they form ideal solution. Hence, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22995,'chemistry',59,373,697,'When 0.1 mol ${\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}$ is oxidised the quantity of electricity required to completely oxidise ${\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}\\,\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,\\,{\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{2 \\\\times 96500 C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9650 C", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "96500 C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "96.50 C", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}\\,\\, \\to \\,\\,{\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}}$<br>${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.1}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mol}}\\,\\,{\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}$ will loose 0.1 mol electrons. The quantity of electricity required to oxidise ${\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}$ completely will be 0.1 faraday, i.e., 9650 C.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22997,'chemistry',58,168,369,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> One molar aqueous solution has always higher concentration than one molal.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The molality of a solution depends upon the density of the solution whereas molarity does not.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Molarity is one mole solute dissolved in 1L of solution. Whereas, molality is one mole solute disssolved in $1 \\mathrm{Kg}$ solvent. Since volume of solution includes volume of both solute and solvent, the solute is present in lesser quantity in molar solution than that present in molal solution. Molality depends upon the density of the solution whereas molarity does not. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22998,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'The decomposition of${{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}\\,\\,{\\text{into }}{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}$ and in presence gaseous argon follow second order kinetics with ${\\rm{k = }}\\left( {{\\rm{5}}{\\rm{.0 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{11}}}}\\,{\\rm{L}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{   -   1}}}}\\,{{\\rm{s}}^{{\\rm{   -   1}}}}} \\right){{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{   -   }}\\frac{{{\\rm{41570}}}}{{\\rm{T}}}}}{\\rm{K}}$ stands for Kelvin units). The energy of activation of the reaction is','[{"text": "$5.0 \\\\times 10^{11} J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$41570 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5000 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$345612.98 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${{\\rm{E}}_{\\rm{a}}}{\\rm{ = 41570 \\times R = 41570 \\times 8}}{\\rm{.31  =  345612}}{\\rm{.98J}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(22999,'chemistry',59,376,704,'Galvanization is applying a coating of:','[{"text": "Cr", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cu", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zn", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pb", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Galvanization is done by Zn.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23001,'chemistry',58,174,382,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Sodium chloride used to clear snow on the roads.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Sodium chloride depresses the freezing point of water.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The addition of a non-volatile solute to a volatile solvent lowers the freezing point of the solvent. Thus, addition of $\\mathrm{NaCl}$ decreases water\'s freezing point and prevents ice formation. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23002,'chemistry',60,590,1187,'In the presence of a catalyst, the heat evolved or absorbed during the reaction is','[{"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains unchanged", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "May increase or decrease", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In the presence of catalyst, the heat absorbed, or evolved during the reaction remains unchanged as there is no change.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25040,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'Chromyl chloride when dissolves in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaOH}}$ solution gives yellow solution. The yellow solution contains','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{5}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23003,'chemistry',59,373,697,'The equivalent conductance of two strong electrolytes at infinite dilution in ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$ (where ions move freely through a solution) at ${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{5}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{C}}$ are given below:<br>${\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{COONa}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = 91}}{\\rm{.0}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{/}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{equiv}}$<br>${\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{HCl}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = 426}}{\\rm{.2}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{/}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{equiv}}$<br>What additional information/quantity one needs to calculate ${{\\rm{\\Lambda }}^{\\rm{o}}}$ of an aqueous solution of acetic acid?','[{"text": "The limiting equivalent conductance of ${{\\\\rm{H}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{\\\\lambda }}_{{{\\\\rm{H}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}}^{\\\\rm{o}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{\\\\Lambda }}^{\\\\rm{o}}}$  of NaCl", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{\\\\Lambda }}^{\\\\rm{o}}}$ of chloroacetic acid $\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{ClC}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{COOH}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{\\\\Lambda }}^{\\\\rm{o}}}$ of ${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{COOK}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From Kohlrausch’s law,<br>${\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{COOH}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = \\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{COONa}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ + \\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{HCl}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = \\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{NaCl}}}^{\\rm{o}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23005,'chemistry',58,172,380,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The vapour pressure of a liquid decreases if some non-volatile solute is dissolved in it.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The relative lowering of vapour pressure of a solution containing a non-volatile solute is equal to the mole fraction of the solute in the solution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The introduction of a non-volatile solute reduces the vapor pressure of a liquid solution by displacing solvent molecules at the surface, hindering their escape into the vapor phase.<br>${\\text{RLVP}},{\\text{ }}\\frac{{\\Delta {{\\text{P}}_1}}}{{{\\text{P}}_1^0}} = {\\chi _2}\\,({\\text{Raoult\'s}}\\,{\\text{law}})$<br>Relative lowering of vapour pressure is equal to the mole dfraction of solute. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23006,'chemistry',60,588,1184,'Compounds \'$\\mathrm{A}$\' and \'$\\mathrm{B}$\' react according to the following chemical equation.<br>${\\text{A(g) + 2B(g)}} \\to {\\text{2C(g)}}$<br>Concentration of either ${\\text{\'A\'}}\\,\\,{\\text{or}}\\,\\,{\\text{\'B\'}}$ were changed keeping the concentrations of one of the reactants constant and rates were measured as a function of initial concentration. Following results were obtained. Choose the correct option for the rate equations for this reaction.','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{Rate}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ = }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{k}}{\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{A}} \\\\right]^{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{B}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Rate}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ = }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{k}}\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{A}} \\\\right]{\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{B}} \\\\right]^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Rate}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ = }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{k}}\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{A}} \\\\right]\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{B}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Rate}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ = }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{k}}{\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{A}} \\\\right]^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{B}} \\\\right]^{\\\\rm{0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Rate of reaction is change in concentration of reactant with respect to time.<br>${\\text{r = k[A}}{{\\text{]}}^{\\text{x}}}{{\\text{[B]}}^{\\text{y}}}$<br>$\\frac{{{\\text{ Rate of exp}}{\\text{. 1}}}}{{{\\text{ Rate of exp}}{\\text{.2}}}}{\\text{ = }}\\frac{{{{{\\text{[0}}{\\text{.30]}}}^{\\text{x}}}{{{\\text{[0}}{\\text{.30]}}}^{\\text{y}}}}}{{{{{\\text{[0}}{\\text{.30]}}}^{\\text{x}}}{{{\\text{[0}}{\\text{.60]}}}^{\\text{y}}}}}$<br>$\\frac{{{\\text{0}}{\\text{.10}}}}{{{\\text{0}}{\\text{.40}}}}{\\text{ = }}\\frac{{{{{\\text{[0}}{\\text{.30]}}}^{\\text{y}}}}}{{{{{\\text{[0}}{\\text{.60]}}}^{\\text{y}}}}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{4}}}{\\text{ = }}{\\left[ {\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{2}}}} \\right]^{\\text{y}}}$<br>${\\left[ {\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{2}}}} \\right]^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ = }}{\\left[ {\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{2}}}} \\right]^{\\text{y}}}$<br>${\\text{y = 2}}$<br>$\\frac{{{\\text{ Rate of exp}}{\\text{.3}}}}{{{\\text{ Rate of exp}}{\\text{.4}}}}{\\text{ = }}\\frac{{{{{\\text{[0}}{\\text{.30]}}}^{\\text{x}}}{{{\\text{[0}}{\\text{.30]}}}^{\\text{y}}}}}{{{{{\\text{[0}}{\\text{.60]}}}^{\\text{x}}}{{{\\text{[0}}{\\text{.30]}}}^{\\text{y}}}}}$<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.10}}}}{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.20}}}}{\\rm{  =  }}{\\left[ {\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.30}}}}{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.60}}}}} \\right]^{\\rm{x}}}{\\left[ {\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.30}}}}{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.30}}}}} \\right]^{\\rm{y}}}$<br>$\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{  =  }}{\\left[ {\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}} \\right]^{\\rm{x}}}{{\\rm{[1]}}^{\\rm{y}}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{  =  }}{\\left[ {\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}} \\right]^{\\rm{x}}}$<br>i.e., $\\quad x=1$<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ Rate $=k[A]^{x}[B]^{y} \\Rightarrow$ Rate $=k[A]^{1}[B]^{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_23006_question_1.png'),
(23007,'chemistry',59,367,680,'The potential of hydrogen electrode is${\\rm{  -  118 mV}}$. The ${{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}$ concentration of the solution is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 4}}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{M}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{2}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{M}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.01}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{M}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{M}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{ - 118}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mV =  - 0}}{\\rm{.118}}\\,\\,{\\rm{V}}$<br>${\\rm{E = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.0591}}}}{{\\rm{n}}}{\\rm{log}}\\left[ {{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}} \\right]$<br>${\\rm{ - 0}}{\\rm{.118 = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.059}}}}{{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{log}}\\left[ {{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}} \\right]$<br>${\\rm{log}}\\left[ {{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}} \\right]{\\rm{ =  - 2}}$<br>$\\left[ {{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}} \\right]{\\rm{ = 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 2}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23009,'chemistry',58,176,385,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Lowering of vapour pressure is directly proportional to the number of ions present in the solution.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The vapour pressure of $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ sugar solution is less than that of $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ potassium chloride solution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The greater the Van\'t Hoff factor (i) greater is the relative lowering of vapor pressure.<br>For $\\mathrm{KCl}$, \' $\\mathrm{i}$ \' value is 2 as it is an ionic compound of $\\mathrm{K}+$ and $\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$. For sugar, "i" value is 1 as it is not an electrolyte. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23010,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'Which of the following statements is incorrect about the collision theory of chemical reaction?','[{"text": "If considers reacting molecules or atoms to be hard spheres and ignores their structural features.<br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Number of effective collisions determines the rate of reaction.<br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Collision of atoms or molecules possessing sufficient threshold energy results into the product formation.<br>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Molecules should collide with sufficient threshold energy and proper orientation for the collision to be effective.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to the postulates of collision theory there are following neccessary conditions for any reaction to be occur<br>(i) Molecule should collide with sufficient threshold energy.<br>(ii) Their orientation must be proper.<br>(iii) The collision must be effective.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23011,'chemistry',59,373,696,'In the electrolysis of dilute ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ using platinum electrode','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$ is liberated at cathode", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$ is produced at cathode", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$  is obtained at cathode", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{O}}$ is produced at anode", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'During electrolysis of ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{,}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ liberates at cathode and ${{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ at anode.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23013,'chemistry',58,170,373,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The pressure exerted by the vapour over the surface of the liquid at the equilibrium at a given temperature is called the vapour pressure of the liquid.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> If a non-volatile solute is added to a solvent to give a solution, the vapour pressure of the solution is found to be greater than the vapour pressure of the pure solvent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The lower vapor pressure in a solution compared to the pure solvent is due to the presence of solute molecules, which reduce the number of solvent molecules at the surface, slowing down their escape into the vapor phase and decreasing the overall vapor pressure. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23014,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'A first order reaction is $50 \\%$ completed in $1.26 \\times 10^{14} \\mathrm{~s}$. How much time would it take for $100 \\%$ completion?','[{"text": "$1.26 \\\\times 10^{15} \\\\mathrm{~s}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.52 \\\\times 10^{14} \\\\mathrm{~s}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.52 \\\\times 10^{28} s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Infinite", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'It is impossible to perform $100 \\%$ of the reaction. Whole of the substance never reacts because in every half-life, $50 \\%$ of the substance reacts. Hence, time taken for $100 \\%$ completion of a reaction is infinite.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23015,'chemistry',59,373,697,'The electrochemical equivalent of a metal is “x” ${\\rm{g}}\\,\\,{\\rm{coulom}}{{\\rm{b}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$. The eq. wt of metal is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{x}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{x \\\\times 96500}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\rm{x}}}{{{\\\\rm{96500}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{E}}\\,{\\rm{ = }}\\,{\\rm{x}}\\,{\\rm{ \\times }}\\,{\\rm{96500}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23017,'chemistry',58,170,373,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> At the same temperature, water has higher vapour pressure than acetic acid.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Hydrogen bonding in water is weaker than in acetic acid.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Water\'s higher vapor pressure compared to acetic acid at the same temperature is due to water\'s weak intermolecular forces and extensive hydrogen bonding, facilitating a greater tendency for water molecules to escape into the vapor phase. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23026,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'Figure shows a graph in $\\log _{10} \\mathrm{k}$ vs $\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{~T}}$ where $\\mathrm{k}$ is rate constant and $\\mathrm{T}$ is temperature. The straight line $\\mathrm{BC}$ has slope, $\\tan \\theta=-\\dfrac{1}{2.303}$ and an intercept of 5 on ${\\text{y - axis}}$. Thus $E_{a}$, the energy of activation is','[{"text": "$2.303 \\\\times 2 \\\\mathrm{cal}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{2.303} \\\\mathrm{cal}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{cal}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\log _{10} \\mathrm{k}=\\log \\mathrm{A}-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{2.303 \\mathrm{RT}}$<br>Thus $-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{2.303 \\mathrm{R}}=\\tan \\theta=-\\dfrac{1}{2.303}$<br>$\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}=\\mathrm{R}=2 \\mathrm{cal}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_23026_question_1.png'),
(23018,'chemistry',60,587,1181,'The value of rate constant of a pseudo first order reaction is','[{"text": "Independent on the concentration of reactants present in small amount", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Depends on the concentration of reactants present in excess", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Is independent of the concentration of reactants", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depends only on temperature", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Pseudo first order reaction is a chemical reaction in which rate of reaction depends upon concentration of only one reactant while concentration of another reactant has no effect on rate of reaction.<br>e.g., hydrolysis of ethyl acetate in presence of excess of water $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOC}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}+\\underset{\\text { excess }}{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\stackrel{\\mathrm{H}^{+}}{\\longrightarrow}} \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}+\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$<br>$r=\\mathrm{K}\\left[\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOC}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right]$<br>Excess $\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right]$ can cause the independency of reaction on $\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right]$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23020,'chemistry',59,373,696,'Given below are the half - cell reactions<br>${\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{Mn,}}{{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{o}}}{\\rm{ =  - 1}}{\\rm{.18V}}$<br>${\\rm{2}}\\left( {{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}} \\right),{{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{o}}}{\\rm{ =  + 1}}{\\rm{.15V}}$<br>The ${{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{o}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{for}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{3M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}} \\to {\\rm{Mn + 2M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}$ will be','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 0}}{\\\\rm{.33 V}}$, the reaction will occur", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 2}}{\\\\rm{.69 V}}$, the reaction will not occur", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 2}}{\\\\rm{.69 V}}$, the reaction will occur", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 0}}{\\\\rm{.33 V}}$, the reaction will not occur", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{Mn;}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{o}}}{\\rm{ =  - 1}}{\\rm{.18V}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(1)}}$<br>${\\rm{2}}\\left( {{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}} \\right){\\rm{;}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{o}}}{\\rm{ =  + 1}}{\\rm{.15V}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(2)}}$<br>Subtracting Eq. (2) from Eq. (1), we get<br>${\\rm{3M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}} \\to {\\rm{Mn + 2M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}$<br>${{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{o}}}{\\rm{ =  - 1}}{\\rm{.18 - }}\\left( {{\\rm{ + 1}}{\\rm{.51}}} \\right){\\rm{ =  - 2}}{\\rm{.69V}}$<br>Since, the value of ${{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{o}}}$ is -ve, therefore the reaction is non-spontaneous.<br>Alternate method:<br>${\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{Mn;}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{o}}}{\\rm{ =  - 1}}{\\rm{.18V}}$<br>${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{G}}^{\\rm{o}}}{\\rm{ =  - nF}}{{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{o}}}$<br>[here, n = number of ${{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}}$ involved in the reaction]<br>${\\rm{\\Delta G}}_{\\rm{1}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ =  - 2F}}\\left( {{\\rm{ - 1}}{\\rm{.18}}} \\right){\\rm{ = 2}}{\\rm{.36F}}$<br>${\\rm{2M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{;}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{o}}}{\\rm{ =  + 1}}{\\rm{.51V}}$<br>${\\rm{\\Delta G}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ =  - 2F}}\\left( {{\\rm{ - 1}}{\\rm{.51}}} \\right){\\rm{ = 3}}{\\rm{.02F}}$<br>Subtracting Eq. (2) from Eq. (1), we get<br>${\\rm{3M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{ + Mn}} \\to {\\rm{2M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\rm{,}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\left[ {{\\rm{n = 2}}} \\right]$<br>${\\rm{\\Delta G}}_{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = \\Delta G}}_{\\rm{1}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ - \\Delta G}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = 5}}{\\rm{.38F}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{ - 2F}}{{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{o}}}{\\rm{ = 5}}{\\rm{.38F}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{o}}}{\\rm{ =  - 2}}{\\rm{.69V}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23021,'chemistry',58,170,377,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Solutions show deviations from Raoult\'s law.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The cause for these deviations lies in the nature of interactions at the molecular level.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Deviations from Raoult\'s law in non-ideal solutions occur when A-B interactions are weaker (positive deviation) or stronger (negative deviation) than A-A or B-B interactions.This leads to change in actual vapor pressures than expected. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23022,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'Which of the following graphs represents exothermic reaction?','[{"text": "(1) only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(2) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(3) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'During exothermic reaction, energy of reactants is more than the energy of the products.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_23022_question_1.png'),
(23023,'chemistry',59,366,679,'The position of some metals in the electrochemical series in decreasing electropositive character is given as Mg > Al > Zn > Cu > Ag. What will happen if a copper spoon is used to stir a solution of aluminium nitrate?','[{"text": "The solution becomes blue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There is no reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The spoon will get coated with aluminium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An alloy of aluminium and copper is formed", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Cu can’t displace ${\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}$ ion from aluminium nitrate.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23025,'chemistry',58,175,383,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Osmotic pressure is a colligative property.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Osmotic pressure depends only on the number of particles dissolved in solution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Colligative properties of a solution, like osmotic pressure and freezing point depression, depend solely on the number of solute particles, regardless of the solute\'s identity, as long as the particles remain undissociated and undergo no chemical changes in the solution. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23028,'chemistry',59,368,683,'A lead storage battery is charged','[{"text": "Sulphuric acid is regenerated", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The conc. Of ${{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{S}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lead dioxide dissolves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lead electrode becomes coated with lead sulphate.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When lead accumulator is charging it acts as electrolytic cell during discharging it acts as electrochemical cell.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23029,'chemistry',58,173,381,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Water boils at $373 \\mathrm{~K}$ as the vapour pressure at this temperature becomes equal toatmospheric pressure.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Vapour pressure of water is less than 1.013 bar at $373 \\mathrm{~K}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Water boils at $100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ when its vapor pressure equals atmospheric pressure ( 1 atmosphere or 1.013 bar) at $373 \\mathrm{~K}$, marking the boiling point where the transition from liquid to vapor phase occurs. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23030,'chemistry',60,588,1184,'Consider the reaction $\\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}$, graph between half-life $t_{1 / 2}$ and initial concentration (a) of the reactant is .<br>Hence graph between ${\\text{ - }}\\frac{{{\\text{d}}\\left[ {\\text{A}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}}$ and time will be','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The reaction is of zero order',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_23030_question_1.png'),
(23031,'chemistry',58,172,380,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If a liquid solute, more volatile than the solvent, is added to the solvent, the vapour pressure of the solution may increase i.e., $\\mathrm{p}_{\\mathrm{s}}>\\mathrm{p}^{\\circ}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In the presence of a more volatile liquid solute, only the solute will form the vapours and solvent will not.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In a solution, as it evaporates, the vapor phase becomes enriched with the more volatile component, characterized by a higher vapor pressure or lower boiling point, due to its greater tendency of transition from the liquid to the gas phase. But along with solute molecules, solvent molecules also evaporates. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23032,'chemistry',59,374,701,'Following are some of the facts about dry cell<br>i) It is also called Leclanche cell<br>ii) It is also called Daniel cell<br>iii) Electrolyte is a moist paste of ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{Cl}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ZnC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>iv) Cathodic reaction is<br>${\\rm{Mn}}{{\\rm{O}}_2}(s) + NH_4^ + (aq) + {e^ - } \\to MnO(OH) + N{H_3}$ Select correct facts:','[{"text": "i, ii, iii", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, iii, iv", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ii, iii, iv", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, iv", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Dry cell is also called as Leclanche cell but not Daniel cell.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23034,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'The graph plotted between concentration versus time','[{"text": "It gives rate of disappearance of reactant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rate ${\\\\text{ =  - }}\\\\frac{{{\\\\text{d}}\\\\left[ {{{\\\\text{C}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{ - }}{{\\\\text{C}}_{\\\\text{1}}}} \\\\right]}}{{{{\\\\text{t}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{ - }}{{\\\\text{t}}_{\\\\text{1}}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It predicts the order of reaction", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Concentration of reactants decreases with time and concentration of products increases with time.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_23034_question_1.png'),
