(23044,'chemistry',59,373,695,'An electric current is passed through an aqueous solution of the following compounds which one shall decompose?','[{"text": "Urea", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glucose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Silver nitrate", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Propyl alcohol", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Silver nitrate is an example of electrolyte which can easily undergo decomposition. Remaining compounds are non-electrolytes.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23045,'chemistry',58,175,383,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If blood cells are placed in pure water, they swell and burst.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Due to osmosis, the movement of water molecules into the cell, dilutes the salt content.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Osmosis involves the movement of solvent from a region of its higher concentration to lower concentration. The concentration of water been high in pure water, water enters the blood cell from outside and this bursts the cell. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23046,'chemistry',60,587,1178,'Which of the following curve represent zero order reaction of $A \\rightarrow$ products?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For zero-order reaction<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\dfrac{-d[A]}{d t}=k \\\\\\text { or }[A]_{0}-[A]=k t \\\\\\text { or }[A]=\\left[A_{0}\\right]-k t\\end{gathered}$<br>It means concentration of A linearly decreases with $t$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23048,'chemistry',59,373,695,'Which of the following processes makes use of electrolysis?','[{"text": "Electrorefining", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electroplating", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Electrorefining and electroplating are electrolysis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23049,'chemistry',58,175,383,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Solutions having the same osmotic pressure are called isotonic solutions.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{K}^{+}$ions are responsible for maintaining proper osmotic pressure balance in the cells of organism.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Sodium and potassium ions play essential roles in maintaining osmotic pressure balance in cells but not $\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$. Isotonic solutions have same osmotic pressure. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23050,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'The energy profile diagrams of two reactions are shown in the figure. Then','[{"text": "Reaction $\\\\mathrm{A} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{B}$ is faster than and more exothermic than reaction $\\\\mathrm{C} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{D}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reaction $\\\\mathrm{C} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{D}$ is faster than reaction $\\\\mathrm{A} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{B}$ but less exothermic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reaction $\\\\mathrm{C} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{D}$ is faster and more exothermic than the reaction $\\\\mathrm{A} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{B}$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Reaction ${\\\\text{C}} \\\\to {\\\\text{D}}\\\\,{\\\\text{2}}\\\\frac{{\\\\text{1}}}{{\\\\text{2}}}$ times faster than reaction $A \\\\rightarrow B$ at the same temperature", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Due to lower activation energy or threshold energy level.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_23050_question_1.png'),
(23052,'chemistry',59,373,696,'The two aqueous solutions ${\\rm{A(AgN}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{)}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{B(LiCl)}}$, were electrolysed using Pt electrodes. The pH of the resulting solutions will','[{"text": "Decrease in A and increase in B", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increase in A and decrease in B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decrease in both A and B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase in both A and B", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{(A)}}\\,\\,{\\rm{AgN}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}$<br>${\\rm{At}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{cathode:}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{g}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{Ag(s)}}$<br>${\\rm{At}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{anode:}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}} \\to {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 4}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{ + 4}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}}$<br>Due to the formation of  ${{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{,pH}}$ value decreases.<br>${\\rm{(B)}}\\,\\,{\\rm{LiCl}}$<br>${\\rm{At}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{cathode:}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{2}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O + 2e}} \\to {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 2O}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ - }}}$<br>${\\rm{At}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{anode:}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{2}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 2e}}$<br>Due to the formation of  ${\\rm{O}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ - }}}$ion, pH value increase.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23053,'chemistry',58,175,384,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Reverse osmosis is used in the desalination of sea water.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> When pressure more than osmotic pressure is applied, pure water is squeezed out of the sea water through the membrane.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Reverse osmosis occurs when higher pressure than osmotic pressure is applied, causing movement of pure solvent molecules from the solution side to the solvent side through a semi-permeable membrane. It is a process commonly used in water purification to remove contaminants by selectively allowing solvent molecules to pass through. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23054,'chemistry',60,588,1184,'What will be the order of reaction and rate constant for a chemical change having ${\\text{log}}\\,{{\\text{t}}_{{\\text{50\\% }}}}$ versus log concentration of ( 1$)$ curves as?','[{"text": "$0, \\\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1,1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3,1", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'By graph, we can say,<br>$\\log t_{1 / 2}=\\log a ; t_{1 / 2}=a$<br>$t_{1 / 2} \\propto a$ then zero order reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{k} \\times \\mathrm{t}_{1 / 2}=a / 2$<br>then $k=\\dfrac{1}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_23054_question_1.png'),
(23055,'chemistry',59,367,680,'For the reaction ${{\\text{M}}^{{\\text{n + }}}}\\left( {{\\text{aq}}} \\right){\\text{ + n}}{{\\text{e}}^{\\text{ - }}} \\to {\\text{M}}\\left( {\\text{s}} \\right)$ select the best suitable representation of Nernst equation when the solid M is taken','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\left. {{{\\\\rm{M}}^{{\\\\rm{n + }}}}} \\\\right|{\\\\rm{M}}}}{\\\\rm{ = E}}_{\\\\left( {\\\\left. {{{\\\\rm{M}}^{{\\\\rm{n + }}}}} \\\\right|{\\\\rm{M}}} \\\\right)}^{\\\\rm{o}}{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{RT}}}}{{{\\\\rm{nF}}}}{\\\\rm{ln}}\\\\frac{{\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{M}} \\\\right]}}{{\\\\left[ {{{\\\\rm{M}}^{{\\\\rm{n + }}}}} \\\\right]}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\left. {{{\\\\rm{M}}^{{\\\\rm{n + }}}}} \\\\right|{\\\\rm{M}}}}{\\\\rm{ = E}}_{\\\\left( {\\\\left. {{{\\\\rm{M}}^{{\\\\rm{n + }}}}} \\\\right|{\\\\rm{M}}} \\\\right)}^{\\\\rm{o}}{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{RT}}}}{{{\\\\rm{nF}}}}{\\\\rm{ln}}\\\\frac{{\\\\rm{1}}}{{\\\\left[ {{{\\\\rm{M}}^{{\\\\rm{n + }}}}} \\\\right]}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\left. {{{\\\\rm{M}}^{{\\\\rm{n + }}}}} \\\\right|{\\\\rm{M}}}}{\\\\rm{ = E}}_{\\\\left( {\\\\left. {{{\\\\rm{M}}^{{\\\\rm{n + }}}}} \\\\right|{\\\\rm{M}}} \\\\right)}^{\\\\rm{o}}{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{RT}}}}{{{\\\\rm{nF}}}}{\\\\rm{ln}}\\\\frac{{\\\\left[ {{{\\\\rm{M}}^{{\\\\rm{n + }}}}} \\\\right]}}{{\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{M}} \\\\right]}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\left. {{{\\\\rm{M}}^{{\\\\rm{n + }}}}} \\\\right|{\\\\rm{M}}}}{\\\\rm{ = E}}_{\\\\left( {\\\\left. {{{\\\\rm{M}}^{{\\\\rm{n + }}}}} \\\\right|{\\\\rm{M}}} \\\\right)}^{\\\\rm{o}}{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{RT}}}}{{{\\\\rm{nF}}}}{\\\\rm{ln}}\\\\left[ {{{\\\\rm{M}}^{{\\\\rm{n + }}}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23057,'chemistry',58,173,381,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Camphor is used as a solvent in the determination of molecular masses of naphthalene, anthracene, etc.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Camphor has high molal elevation constant.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Camphor has high molal depression constant. Given assertion is a correct statement. So option (3) is correct .',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23058,'chemistry',60,587,1178,'Which graph represents zero order reaction $[\\mathrm{A}(\\mathrm{g}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}(\\mathrm{g})] ?$','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In case of Zero order reaction, ${{\\text{t}}_{\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{2}}}}}\\,{\\text{or }}{{\\text{t}}_{\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{4}}}}}$ is directly proportional to the initial concentration of reactants.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23059,'chemistry',59,368,681,'${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{G}}^{\\rm{o}}}$ of the cell reaction ${\\text{AgCl(s) + }}\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{Ag(s) + }}{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ + }}}{\\text{ + C}}{{\\text{l}}^{\\text{ - }}}$ ${\\rm{is}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ - 21}}{\\rm{.52kJ}}$.<br>${\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\text{G}}^{\\text{o}}}\\,\\,{\\text{of}}\\,\\,{\\text{2AgCl(s) + }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2Ag(s) + 2}}{{\\text{H}}^{{\\text{ + }}}}{\\text{ + 2C}}{{\\text{l}}^{{\\text{ - }}}}$ is','[{"text": "-21.52 kJ", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "43.04 kJ", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "-10.76 kJ", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "-43.04 kJ", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${{\\rm{K}}_{{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{\\rm{ = K}}_{{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{1}}}}^{\\rm{2}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{\\rm{\\Delta G = - nF}}{{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{o}}}{\\rm{ = - RT}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{ln}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{C}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{\\rm{\\Delta }}{{\\rm{G}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 2 \\times }}\\left( {{\\rm{ - 21}}{\\rm{.52}}} \\right){\\rm{ = - 43}}{\\rm{.04}}\\,\\,{\\rm{kJ}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23061,'chemistry',58,176,385,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The mass of acetic acid molecules in benzene is more than the actual value of the solute,<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Molecules of acetic acid dimerise in benzene due to hydrogen bonding.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Acetic acid (molar mass $60 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{mol}$ ) dimerises in benzene due to hydrogen bonding between its molecules. Hence the molecular weight becomes double than that of normal value. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23062,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'For an exothermic chemical process occurring in two steps as<br>(i) ${\\text{A + B}} \\to {\\text{X}}$ (slow)<br>(ii) ${\\text{X}} \\to {\\text{AB}}\\,\\,{\\text{(fast)}}$<br>The progress of the reaction can be best described by ( $\\mathrm{X}$ is considered as intermediate)','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In case of exothermic reactions, energy of reactants is more than the energy of products.<br>As the reaction takes place in two steps, two maxima must be mentioned.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23063,'chemistry',58,176,385,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $1 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{KCl}$ in one $\\mathrm{kg}$ of water increases the boiling point by $2 \\times 0.52 \\mathrm{~K}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $1 \\mathrm{~mol}$ of $\\mathrm{KCl}$ in water releases $1 \\mathrm{~mol}$ of $\\mathrm{K}^{+}$and $1 \\mathrm{~mol}$ of $\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ions.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Ionic compounds, like $\\mathrm{KCl}$, undergo dissociation in water, yielding twice the number of ions ( 2 moles) compared to the moles of the original compound added. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23064,'chemistry',59,367,680,'The standard emf of the cell, $\\left. {{\\rm{Cd(s)}}} \\right|\\left. {{\\rm{CdC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(aq)}}} \\right\\|\\left. {{\\rm{AgCl(s)}}} \\right|{\\rm{Ag(s)}}$ in which the cell reaction is,<br>$\\begin{array}{l}<br>{\\rm{Cd}}({\\rm{s}}) + {\\rm{2AgCl}}({\\rm{s}}) \\to \\\\<br>{\\rm{2Ag}}({\\rm{s}}) + {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{d}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}({\\rm{aq}}) + {\\rm{2C}}{{\\rm{l}}^ - }({\\rm{aq}})<br>\\end{array}$ is ${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.6915}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{V}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{at}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{0^\\circ C}}{\\mkern 1mu} $ and ${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.6753}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{V}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{at}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{25}}^\\circ {\\rm{C}}$. The ${\\rm{\\Delta H}}$ reaction at ${\\rm{25^\\circ C}}$ is&nbsp;','[{"text": "&ndash; 167.26 kJ", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "&ndash; 176 kJ", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+ 123.5 kJ", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "&ndash; 234.7 kJ", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\Delta H = - nFE + nFT{\\left( {\\frac{{\\partial E}}{{\\partial T}}} \\right)_P}$<br>${\\rm{ = - 2 \\times 96500 \\times 0}}{\\rm{.6753 + 2 \\times 96500 \\times 298}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\left( {\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.6753 - 0}}{\\rm{.6915}}}}{{{\\rm{25}}}}} \\right){\\rm{ = }}\\,\\,{\\rm{ - 167}}{\\rm{.6}}\\,\\,{\\rm{kJ}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23066,'chemistry',60,589,1185,'The rate of a chemical reaction doubles for every $10^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ rise of temperature. If the temperature is raised by $50^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, the rate of the reaction increases by about','[{"text": "24 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "64 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "32 times", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For every $10^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ rise of temperature, rate is doubled.<br>Thus, temperature coefficient of the reaction $=$ 5<br>When temperature is increased by $50^{\\circ}$, rate becomes $=2^{(50 / 10)}=2^{5}$ times $=32$ times',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23067,'chemistry',59,366,679,'Given,<br>${\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}{\\rm{/Cr}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ =  - 0}}{\\rm{.74V;E}}_{{\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{/M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = 1}}{\\rm{.5V}}$ <br>${\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{7}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{/C}}{{\\rm{r}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = 1}}{\\rm{.33V;E}}_{{\\rm{Cl/C}}{{\\rm{l}}^{\\rm{ - }}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = 1}}{\\rm{.36V;}}$<br>Based on the data given above, strongest oxidizing agent will be','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{MnO}}_{\\\\rm{4}}^{\\\\rm{ - }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Cl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{r}}^{{\\\\rm{3 + }}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{M}}{{\\\\rm{n}}^{{\\\\rm{2 + }}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Higher the SRP (Standard Reduction Potential) stronger is the oxidizing agent. Among the given electrode potentials, ${\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}{\\rm{/M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}}^{\\rm{o}}$ is highest.<br>Hence, ${\\rm{MnO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{\\rm{ - }}$ is the strongest oxidizing agent.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23068,'chemistry',58,173,381,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The boiling point of $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ urea solution is less than that of $0.1 \\mathrm{M} \\mathrm{KCl}$ solution.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Elevation of boiling point is directly proportional to the number of species present in the solution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{KCl}$, an electrolyte, contributes more particles to the solution upon dissociation compared to urea, a non-electrolyte, which remains as individual molecules. Elevation in boiling point is a colligative property and depends on the number of particles. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23070,'chemistry',60,588,1184,'Consider the reaction, ${\\text{2A + B}} \\to $ Products When concentration of ${\\text{B}}$ alone was doubled, the half-life did not change. When the concentration of ${\\text{A}}$ alone was doubled, the rate increased by two times. The unit of rate constant for this reaction is:','[{"text": "No unit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{mol}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {{\\\\text{L}}^{{\\\\text{  -  1}}}}\\\\,{{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{  -  1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{L}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\text{mo}}{{\\\\text{l}}^{{\\\\text{  -  1}}}}\\\\,{{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{  -  1}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$2 A+B \\rightarrow$ Product<br>When concentration of $B$ is doubled, the halflife did not change, hence, reaction is of firstorder w.r.t. B. When concentration of $A$ is doubled, reaction rate is doubled, hence, reaction is of first-order w.r.t. A. Hence, overall order of reaction is $1+1=2$<br>So, unit of rate constant is $\\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ litre $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23071,'chemistry',59,367,680,'The oxidation potential of ${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.05}}\\,\\,{\\rm{M}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 2 \\\\times 0}}{\\\\rm{.0591}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 0}}{\\\\rm{.01 \\\\times 0}}{\\\\rm{.0591}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ - 2}}{\\\\rm{.321 \\\\times 0}}{\\\\rm{.0591}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.0591}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{Red}}}}{\\rm{ = E}}_{{\\rm{Red}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ - }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.059}}}}{{\\rm{n}}}{\\rm{log}}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{{\\left[ {{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}} \\right]}^{{\\rm{ - 2}}}}}}{\\rm{;}}$<br>${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{Red}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\,\\,{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.059}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{log}}\\,\\,\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{{{{\\rm{(0}}{\\rm{.1)}}}^{\\rm{2}}}}}{\\rm{;}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{Red}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\,\\,{\\rm{ - 0}}{\\rm{.059V,}}$<br>${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{axi}}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.059 V}}{\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23073,'chemistry',58,171,379,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Elevation in boiling point and depression in freezing point are colligative properties.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> All colligative properties are used for the calculation of molecular masses.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Colligative properties like elevation in boiling point and depression in freezing point, dependent on solute particles, and all colligative Properties are used to measure molar mass. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23084,'chemistry',58,174,382,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Increasing pressure on pure water decreases its freezing point.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Density of water is maximum at  $273{\\text{ K}}.$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Water reaches maximum density at $4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ due to the arrangement of molecules. When pressure increases, water molecules cannot be properly arranged, so water cannot freeze at the freezing point at $273 \\mathrm{~K}$. That means water freezes at below $273 \\mathrm{~K}$. Hence, the given statement "Increasing pressure on pure water decreases its freezing point" is true. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23074,'chemistry',59,367,680,'The emf of the following cell at 298 K is $\\left. {{\\rm{Fe(s)}}} \\right|\\left. {{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}\\left( {{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.001M}}} \\right)} \\right\\|\\left. {{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{(1M)}}} \\right|\\left. {{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g)(1}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{bar)}}} \\right|{\\rm{Pt(s)}}$ ${\\rm{Given}}\\,\\,{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{(F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{/Fe)}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ =  - 0}}{\\rm{.44V}}$','[{"text": "0.44 V", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.53 V", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.35 V", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Data insufficient", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{(aq) + Fe(s)}} \\to {\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{(aq) + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g)}}$<br>Nernst equation :<br>${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell}}}}{\\rm{ = E}}_{{\\rm{cell}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ - }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.0591}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{log}}\\frac{{\\left[ {{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}} \\right]}}{{{{\\left[ {{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}} \\right]}^{\\rm{2}}}}}$<br>${\\rm{EMF}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{the}}\\,\\,{\\rm{cell}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell}}}}{\\rm{ = }}$<br>$\\left[ {{\\rm{0 - ( - 0}}{\\rm{.44)}}} \\right]{\\rm{ - }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.0591}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{log}}\\frac{{\\left[ {{\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}} \\right]}}{{{{\\left[ {\\rm{1}} \\right]}^{\\rm{2}}}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.44 - }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.591}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ \\times }}\\left( {{\\rm{ - 3}}} \\right)$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.44 + 0}}{\\rm{.0887}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\,\\,{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.5287V}}\\,\\, \\approx \\,\\,{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.53V}}$<br>${\\rm{EMF = 0}}{\\rm{.53}}\\,\\,{\\rm{V}}{\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23076,'chemistry',58,176,385,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{NaCl}$ in water and organic acids in benzene show abnormal molecular mass.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Abnormal molecular mass is obtained when the substance in the solution undergoes dissociation or association.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Dissociation increases the number of particles, raising colligative property values, and decreases effective molecular mass. Similarly association decreases particle number, lowering colligative property values and increases effective molecular mass. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23077,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'According to collision theory, not all collisions between molecules lead to reaction. Which of the following statements provide reasons for the same?<br>\n(i) The total energy of the two colliding molecules is less than some minimum amount of energy.<br>\n(ii) Molecules cannot react with each other unless a catalyst is present.<br>\n(iii) Molecule that are improperly oriented during collision will not react.<br>\n(iv) Molecules in different states of matter cannot react with each other.','[{"text": "(i) and (ii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) and (iii)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(ii) and (iii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) and (iv)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23078,'chemistry',59,367,680,'Given that ${\\rm{E}}_{\\left. {{\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}} \\right|{\\rm{Zn}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ =  - 0}}{\\rm{.763}}\\,\\,{\\rm{V}}$ and ${\\rm{E}}_{\\left. {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{d}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}} \\right|{\\rm{Cd}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ =  - 0}}{\\rm{.403}}\\,\\,{\\rm{V}}$, the emf of the following cell: ${\\rm{Zn/Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{,0}}{\\rm{.004M//C}}{{\\rm{d}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{,0}}{\\rm{.2M/Cd}}$','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{E =  + 0}}{\\\\rm{.36 + }}\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.059}}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}} \\\\right]\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{log}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.2}}}}{{{\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.004}}}}} \\\\right)} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{E =  - 0}}{\\\\rm{.36 + }}\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.059}}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}} \\\\right]\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{log}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.2}}}}{{{\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.004}}}}} \\\\right)} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{E =  + 0}}{\\\\rm{.36 - }}\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.059}}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}} \\\\right]\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{log}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.004}}}}{{{\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.2}}}}} \\\\right)} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{E =  - 0}}{\\\\rm{.36 + }}\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.059}}}}{{\\\\rm{2}}}} \\\\right]\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{log}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.004}}}}{{{\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.2}}}}} \\\\right)} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Since ${\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{d}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{/Cd}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ > E}}_{{\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{/Zn}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{,}}$ therefore Zn electrode acts as anode and Cd electrode as cathode.<br>$\\frac{{\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}{{\\rm{Anode}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{:}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{Zn(s)}} \\to {\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{(aq) + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Cathode}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{:}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{d}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{Cd(s)}}}\\end{array}}}{{{\\rm{Zn(s) + C}}{{\\rm{d}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{(aq)}} \\to {\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{(aq) + Cd(s)}}}}$<br>${\\rm{Q = }}\\left[ {{\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}} \\right]\\left[ {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{d}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}} \\right]{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.004/0}}{\\rm{.2}}$<br>${\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ = E}}_{{\\rm{cathode}}}^{\\rm{o}}{\\rm{ - E}}_{{\\rm{anode}}}^{\\rm{o}}$<br>${\\rm{ =  - 0}}{\\rm{.403 V - ( - 0}}{\\rm{.763 V)}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.36 V}}$<br>${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell}}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.36 - }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.059}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{log}}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.004}}}}{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.2}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23080,'chemistry',58,168,369,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When a concentrated solution is diluted by adding more water, the number of moles of solute in the solution remains unchanged.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Number of moles of a solute is equal to the product of molarity and volume of solution in litres.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The moles of solute in a solution remain constant when additional solvent is added, as the increase in volume proportionally decreases the molarity to maintain a consistent product of moles and molarity. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
