(23208,'chemistry',60,585,1176,'The rate of the reaction $\\mathrm{A}+2 \\mathrm{~B} \\rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{C}$ gets increased by 72 times when the concentration of $\\mathrm{A}$ is tripled and that of $\\mathrm{B}$ is doubled. The order of the reaction with respect to $A$ and $B$ are respectively','[{"text": "1,2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3,2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{r = k[A}}{{\\rm{]}}^{\\rm{\\alpha }}}{{\\rm{[B]}}^{\\rm{\\beta }}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(1)}}$<br>${\\rm{72r = k[3A}}{{\\rm{]}}^{\\rm{\\alpha }}}{{\\rm{[2B]}}^{\\rm{\\beta }}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(2)}}$<br>Dividing eqn. (2) by eqn. (1), $72=3^{\\alpha} 2^{\\beta}$<br>Test by substituting the given values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$.<br>we get, $3^{2} \\times 2^{3}=9 \\times 8=72$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
