(23241,'chemistry',60,588,1184,'At $518^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, the rate of decomposition of a sample of gaseous acetaldehyde, initially at a pressure of 363 Torr, was 1.00 Torr ${{\\text{s}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$ when 5% had reacted and 0.5 torr ${{\\text{s}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$ when 33% had reacted. The order of the reaction is :','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CHO} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CH}_{4}+\\mathrm{CO}$<br>$r_{1}=1$ torr $\\sec ^{-1}$, when $5 \\%$ reacted $(95 \\%$ left $)$<br>$r_{2}=0.5$ torr $\\sec ^{-1}$, when $33 \\%$ reacted $(67 \\%$<br>left)<br>$r \\propto(a-x)^{m} \\mathrm{~m}=$ order of reaction $\\mathrm{a}-\\mathrm{x}=$ unreacted<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}=\\left[\\dfrac{\\left(a-x_{1}\\right)}{\\left(a-x_{2}\\right)}\\right]^{m} \\\\& \\dfrac{1}{0.5}=\\left(\\dfrac{0.95}{0.67}\\right)^{m} \\\\& 2=(1.41)^{m} \\Rightarrow 2=(\\sqrt{2})^{m} \\\\& \\Rightarrow m=2\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23279,'chemistry',58,168,366,'How much volume of $3.0\\,\\,{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ is required for the preparation of 1.0 litre of 1.0 M solution?','[{"text": "300 mL", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "320 mL", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "333.3 mL", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "350.0 ml", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Dilution law,<br>$\\mathrm{M}_{1} \\mathrm{~V}_{1}=\\mathrm{M}_{2} \\mathrm{~V}_{2}$<br>$3 \\times V_{1}=1 \\times 1$<br>$\\mathrm{V}_{1}=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ litre $\\approx 333.3 \\mathrm{~mL}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23244,'chemistry',59,370,691,'The resistance of a N/10 KCl solution is ${\\rm{245}}\\,\\,{\\rm{\\Omega }}$. The equivalent conductance of the solution if the electrodes in the cell are 4 cm apart and each having an area of 7.0 sq. cm. is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{232}}{\\\\rm{.3}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{e}}{{\\\\rm{q}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{2332}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{e}}{{\\\\rm{q}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{23}}{\\\\rm{.23}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{e}}{{\\\\rm{q}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{2}}{\\\\rm{.323}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{e}}{{\\\\rm{q}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{245 = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{\\Lambda }}}{\\rm{ \\times }}\\frac{{\\rm{4}}}{{\\rm{7}}}{\\rm{ \\times }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1000}}}}{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.1}}}}$<br>${\\rm{\\Lambda  = 23}}{\\rm{.23}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{S}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{e}}{{\\rm{q}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23245,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'Rate of a reaction can be expressed by Arrhenius equation as: ${\\text{k = A}}{{\\text{e}}^{{\\text{ - E/RT}}}}$<br>In this equation, ${{\\text{e}}^{{\\text{ - E/RT}}}}$ represents','[{"text": "The fraction of molecules with energy greater than the activation energy of the reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The energy above which all the colliding molecules will react", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The energy below which colliding molecules will not react", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The total energy of the reacting molecules at a temperature, $\\\\mathrm{T}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Arrhenius equation',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23247,'chemistry',58,175,383,'Evaluate the following statements for their correctness.<br>A. The elevation in boiling point temperature of water will be same for 0.1 M NaCl and 0.1 M urea.<br>B. Azeotropic mixtures boil without change in their composition.<br>C. Osmosis always takes place from hypertonic to hypotonic solution.<br>D. The density of  $32\\,\\% \\,\\,{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_4}$  solution havingmolarity 4.09 M is approximately $1.26 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mL}^{-1}$.<br>E. A negatively charged sol is obtained when KI solution is added to silver nitrate solution.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "B and D only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A, B and D only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A and C only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B, D and E only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'(A)  $\\Delta {{\\text{T}}_{\\text{b}}} \\propto {\\text{i}} \\times {\\text{C}}$<br>(B)  Azeotropic mixtures have same composition in both liquid and vapour phase.<br>(C)  Osmosis always takes place from hypotonic to hypertonic solution.<br>(D)  The density of  $32\\,\\% \\,\\,{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_4}$  solution having molarity 4.09 M is approximately $1.26 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mL}^{-1}$.<br>(E)  When KI solution is added to $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ solution, positively charged sol is formed due to adsorption of $\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}$ions from dispersion medium. Thus, only statements B and D are correct. Hence, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23248,'chemistry',59,370,690,'The conductivity of 0.20 M solution of KCl at 298 K is ${\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.0248}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$. Its molar conductivity is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{124}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{124}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{12}}{\\\\rm{.4}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{12}}{\\\\rm{.4}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Conductivity $\\left( {\\rm{\\kappa }} \\right){\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.0248}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.0248}}\\,\\,{\\rm{oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$<br>Molar concentration $\\left( {\\rm{C}} \\right){\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.20}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mol}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{L}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\left( {{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.2}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mol}}} \\right)}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{1000c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}} \\right)}}{\\rm{ = 2}}{\\rm{.0 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 4}}}}{\\rm{mol}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}$<br>${\\rm{Molar}}\\,\\,{\\rm{conductivity}}\\,\\,\\left( {{{\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{\\rm{m}}}} \\right){\\rm{ = }}$<br>$\\frac{{\\rm{\\kappa }}}{{\\rm{C}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\left( {{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.0248}}\\,\\,{\\rm{oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}} \\right)}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.0 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 4}}}}{\\rm{mol}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}} \\right)}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 124}}\\,\\,{\\rm{oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{or}}\\,\\,{\\rm{124}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}$<br>Molar conductivity ${\\rm{ = 124}}\\,\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23249,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'In the equilibrium $\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{C}+\\mathrm{D}$, the activation energy for forward reaction is 25 $\\mathrm{K} . \\mathrm{cal} / \\mathrm{mol}$ and that of backward reaction is 15 $\\mathrm{K} . \\mathrm{Cal} / \\mathrm{mol}$. Which of the following statement is correct.','[{"text": "It is exothermic process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is endothermic process", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is reaction for which $\\\\Delta H=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is a sublimable process", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Activation energy of forward reaction is greater than that backward reaction. Hence, the reaction is endothermic.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23262,'chemistry',60,586,1177,'A chemical reaction proceeds into the following steps<br>Step I, $2 \\mathrm{~A} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{X}$ (fast)<br>Step II, ${\\rm{X + B}} \\to {\\rm{Y(slow)}}$<br>Step III, $Y+B \\rightleftharpoons$ Product (fast)<br>The rate law for the overall reaction is','[{"text": "Rate $=k[A]^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rate $=k[B]^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rate $=k[A][B]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rate $=k[A]^{2}[B]$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From slowest step i.e., rds step,<br>Rate $=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{X}][\\mathrm{B}]$ $\\mathrm{X}$ is an intermediate, need to be replaced with A by using fast step $I$.<br>$K=\\dfrac{[X]}{[A]^{2}} \\Rightarrow[X]=K[A]^{2}$<br>substitute $[X]$ in eq. (1)<br>Rate $=\\mathrm{kK}[\\mathrm{A}]^{2}[\\mathrm{~B}]$<br>Rate $=\\mathrm{k}^{1}[\\mathrm{~A}]^{2}[\\mathrm{~B}]$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23251,'chemistry',58,170,374,'Vapour pressure of a solution containing 18 g of glucose and 178.2 g of water at ${\\mathrm{100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}}}$ is: (Vapour pressure of pure water at${\\mathrm{100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}=760}}$ torr)','[{"text": "76.0 torr", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "752.4 torr", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7.6 torr", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3207.6 torr", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}=\\dfrac{178.2}{18}=9.9 ; \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{12} \\mathrm{O}_{6}}=\\dfrac{18}{180}=0.1}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\chi_{\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{12} \\mathrm{O}_{6}}=\\dfrac{0.1}{10}=0.01}}$<br>Raoult\'s Law, ${\\mathrm{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}_{0}-\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{s}}}{\\mathrm{P}_{0}}=\\chi_{\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{12} \\mathrm{O}_{6}}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\dfrac{760-P_{s}}{760}=0.01}}$<br>${\\mathrm{760-P_{s}=7.6}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{s}}=752.4}}$ torr',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23252,'chemistry',59,369,685,'Conductivity of a strong electrolyte','[{"text": "may increase or decrease with dilution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases on dilution", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increases on dilution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Does not change with dilution", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Strong electrolyte ionizes completely on dilution and the number of ions does not increase on dilution. A small increase in ${{\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{\\rm{m}}}$ with dilution is due to the weakening of electrostatic attraction between the ions on dilution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23253,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'Consider the given figure and mark the correct option.','[{"text": "Activation energy of forward reaction is ${{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\rm{ + }}{{\\\\rm{E}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$ and product is less stable than reactant.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Activation energy of forward reaction is ${{\\\\text{E}}_{\\\\text{1}}}{\\\\text{ + }}{{\\\\text{E}}_{\\\\text{2}}}$ and product is more stable than reactant.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Activation energy of both forward and backward reaction is ${{\\\\text{E}}_{\\\\text{1}}}{\\\\text{ + }}{{\\\\text{E}}_{\\\\text{2}}}$ and reactant is more stable than product.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Activation energy of backward reaction is ${{\\\\text{E}}_{\\\\text{1}}}$ and product is more stable than reactant.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From the graph, It is clear that<br>Activation energy of forward reaction $=\\mathrm{E}_{1}+$ $\\mathrm{E}_{2}$<br>The given reaction is an example of endothermic reaction so products are less stable than reactants.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_23253_question_1.png'),
(23255,'chemistry',58,170,377,'A mixture of phenol and aniline shows negative deviation from Raoult\'s law. This is due to the formation of:','[{"text": "Polar covalent bond", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Non-polar covalent bond", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Intermolecular Hydrogen bond", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Intramolecular Hydrogen bond", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Due to intermolecular hydrogen bond between phenol and aniline, $-$ve deviation from Raoult\'s law is observed.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23256,'chemistry',59,369,685,'Select the solution that has highest conductivity.','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.1}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{M}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{NaCl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.1}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{M}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{HCl}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.1}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{M}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{KN}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.1}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{M}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{COOH}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{NaCl,}}\\,\\,{\\rm{KN}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{,}}\\,\\,{\\rm{HCl}}$ are strong electrolytes but size of hydrated ${{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}$  ion is small. Smaller the size of ion, greater is the conductivity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23258,'chemistry',60,586,1177,'Consider the following reactions,<br>${{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}} \\rightleftharpoons {{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{   +   O( fast )}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} $<br>${\\text{O   +   }}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}} \\to {\\text{2}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(slow)}}$<br>The rate law expression should be','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{r}=\\\\mathrm{k}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{O}_{3}\\\\right]^{2}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\\\right]^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{r}=\\\\mathrm{k}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{O}_{3}\\\\right]\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{r}=\\\\mathrm{k}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{O}_{3}\\\\right]^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{r}=\\\\mathrm{k}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\\\right]^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From Eq. (1), $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{eq}}=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{O}]\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{3}\\right]}$<br>or $[\\mathrm{O}]=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{eq}}\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{3}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right]}$<br>From Eq. (2), Rate law $=\\mathrm{k}\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{3}\\right][\\mathrm{O}]$<br>or $\\mathrm{r}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{k}\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{3}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{3}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right]} \\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{eq}}$<br>or $\\mathrm{r}=\\mathrm{k}^{\\prime}\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{3}\\right]^{2}\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right]^{-1}\\left[\\mathrm{k}^{\\prime} \\mathrm{k}=\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{eq}}\\right]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23259,'chemistry',58,170,377,'Which one of the following pairs will show positive deviation from Raoult\'s Law?','[{"text": "Water - HCl", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Benzene-Methanol", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Water ${\\\\mathrm{-\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acetone - Chloroform", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Benzene ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}}}$ system shows +ve deviation as benzene breaks H -bonds among ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23260,'chemistry',59,372,694,'At Infinite dilution, each ion makes definite contribution to conductance of an electrolyte, whatever be the nature of the other ion of the electrolyte. This law states that','[{"text": "Kohlrausch’s law", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Debye Huckel rule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Faraday’s law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Arrhenius law", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given is the statement of Kohlrausch law',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23263,'chemistry',58,169,371,'The Henry\'s law constant ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{H}}\\right)}}$ values of three gases ${\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{B}, \\mathrm{C})}}$ in water are ${\\mathrm{145,2 \\times 10^{-5}}}$ and 35 kbar respectively. The solubility of these gases in water follows the order:','[{"text": "B ${\\\\mathrm{>}}$ A ${\\\\mathrm{>}}$ C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{B>C>A}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A ${\\\\mathrm{>}}$ C ${\\\\mathrm{>}}$ B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A ${\\\\mathrm{>}}$ B ${\\\\mathrm{>}}$ C", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Higher the value of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{H}}}}$ at a given pressure, lower is the solubility of the gas in the liquid.<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{H}}}}$ value of gases (given) : ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A}>\\mathrm{C}>\\mathrm{B}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\therefore \\quad}}$ Order of solubility of gases in water :<br>${\\mathrm{B>C>A}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23264,'chemistry',59,372,694,'Equivalent conductance at infinite dilution of ${\\rm{BaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{,}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{HCl}}$ aqueous solutions are ${{\\rm{x}}_{\\rm{1}}}{\\rm{,}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{x}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{x}}_{\\rm{3}}}$ respectively. Equivalent conductance of ${\\rm{BaS}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ solution is','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\rm{ + }}{{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ - }}{{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\rm{ - }}{{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ - }}{{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\rm{ + }}{{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ - 2}}{{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\rm{ - 2}}{{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ - }}{{\\\\rm{x}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{BaS}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}{\\rm{ = \\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{BaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}{\\rm{ + \\Lambda }}_{{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}{\\rm{ - \\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{HCl}}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}$<br>$ = {{\\rm{x}}_{\\rm{1}}} + {{\\rm{x}}_{\\rm{2}}} - {{\\rm{x}}_{\\rm{3}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23266,'chemistry',60,586,1177,'Nitric oxide (NO) reacts with oxygen to produce nitrogen dioxide<br>${\\text{2N}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{(g)}}}}{\\text{ &nbsp;+ &nbsp;}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}}\\xrightarrow{{\\text{K}}}{\\text{2N}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{2(g)}}}}$<br>If the mechanism of reaction is<br>${\\text{NO &nbsp;+ &nbsp;}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}\\mathop &nbsp;\\rightleftharpoons \\limits_{\\text{K}} {{\\text{N}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{( fast )}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ &nbsp;+ &nbsp;NO}}\\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{1}}}}}$<br>${\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ &nbsp;+ &nbsp;N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(slow)}}$<br>then rate law is','[{"text": "Rate $=K^{\\\\prime}[N O]\\\\left[O_{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rate $=K^{\\\\prime}[\\\\mathrm{NO}]\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\\\right]^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rate $=K^{\\\\prime}[N O]^{2}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Rate $=K^{\\\\prime}[N O]^{3}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From slowest step i.e., rds step,<br>Rate $=\\mathrm{k}_{1}\\left[\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right][\\mathrm{NO}]$<br>$\\mathrm{NO}_{3}$ is an intermediate, need to be replaced with $\\mathrm{NO}$ and $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ by using fast step.<br>$K=\\dfrac{\\left[N O_{3}\\right]}{[N O]\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right]} \\Rightarrow\\left[N O_{3}\\right]=K[N O]\\left[O_{2}\\right]$<br>Substitute $\\left[\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right]$ in eq. (1)<br>Rate $=k_{1} K[N O]\\left[O_{2}\\right][N O]$<br>Rate $=k^{\\prime}[N O]^{2}\\left[O_{2}\\right] \\quad\\left[k^{\\prime}=k_{1} K\\right]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23267,'chemistry',58,175,383,'The plot of osmotic pressure ( ${\\mathrm{\\Pi}}$ ) vs concentration $({\\rm{mol}}\\,{{\\rm{L}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}})$ for a solution gives a straight line with slope ${\\mathrm{25.73 \\mathrm{~L}\\, \\mathrm{bar} \\,\\mathrm{mol}^{-1}}}$. The temperature at which the osmotic pressure measurement is done is (Use ${\\text{R}}\\,{\\text{ = }}\\,{\\text{0}}.{\\text{083}}\\,{\\text{L}}\\,{\\text{bar}}\\,{\\text{mol}}\\,{{\\text{K}}^{ - 1}})$','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{37^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{310^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{25.73^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{12.05^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}}}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Osmotic pressure, ${\\mathrm{\\Pi=\\mathrm{CRT}}}$ <br>and slope ${\\mathrm{=\\mathrm{RT}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\therefore 25.73=0.083 \\times \\mathrm{T}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{T}=\\dfrac{25.73}{0.083}=309.47 \\approx 310 \\mathrm{~K}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\therefore}}$ Temperature in ${\\mathrm{{ }^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}=310-273}}$<br>${\\mathrm{=37^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23268,'chemistry',59,372,694,'The equivalent conductivity of 0.1 M weak acid is 100 times less than that at infinite dilution. The degree of dissociation is','[{"text": "100", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.01", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.001", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Degree of dissociation<br>$ = \\frac{{\\Lambda _{eq}^C}}{{\\Lambda _{eq}^o}} = \\frac{{0.1}}{{100}} = 0.001$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23270,'chemistry',60,585,1176,'Higher order $(>3)$ reactions are rare due to:','[{"text": "Loss of active species on collision", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "shifting of equilibrium towards reactants due to elastic collisions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low probability of simultaneously collision of all the reacting species", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increase in entropy and activation energy as more molecules are involved.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Higher order $(>3)$ reactions are rare due to low probability of simultaneous collision of all the reacting species.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23272,'chemistry',59,372,694,'Molar ionic conductivities at infinite dilution of bivalent ions of an electrolytes ${{\\rm{x}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{y}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}}$ are 57 and ${\\rm{73}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$ respectively. The molar conductivity of the solution formed by them will be','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{130}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{65}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{260}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{187}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{S}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{c}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\Lambda _{\\rm{m}}^\\infty \\,\\,{\\rm{ = }}\\,\\,{\\rm{57}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ + }}\\,\\,{\\rm{73}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ = }}\\,\\,{\\rm{130}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{S}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23274,'chemistry',60,588,1184,'The following data are for the decomposition of ammonium nitrate in aqueous solution<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}{{\\rm{Volume}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{in}}\\,\\,{\\rm{cc}}}&{{\\rm{Time}}\\,\\,{\\rm{(minutes)}}}\\\\{{\\rm{6}}{\\rm{.25}}}&{{\\rm{10}}}\\\\{{\\rm{9}}{\\rm{.50}}}&{{\\rm{15}}}\\\\{{\\rm{11}}{\\rm{.42}}}&{{\\rm{20}}}\\\\{{\\rm{13}}{\\rm{.65}}}&{{\\rm{25}}}\\\\{{\\rm{35}}{\\rm{.05}}}&{{\\rm{Finally}}}\\end{array}$<br>The order of the reaction is','[{"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "One", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Two", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$k=\\dfrac{2.303}{t} \\log \\dfrac{V_{\\infty}}{V_{\\infty}-V_{t}}$ gives constant value of $\\mathrm{k}$ by using given data. Hence it is $1^{s t}$ order.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23275,'chemistry',58,168,366,'A solution of $\\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$ is $0.5 \\mathrm{~mol} /$ liter ; then the moles of chloride ion in 500 mL will be','[{"text": "0.5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.25", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.75", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'1 litre solution $\\rightarrow 0.5 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$<br>0.5 litre solution<br>$\\frac{{0.5}}{2}\\,{\\rm{mol}}\\,\\,{\\rm{CaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}$.<br>Moles of $\\mathrm{C} \\overline{1}$ ion $=\\dfrac{0.5}{2} \\times 2=0.5$ mole ${\\rm{C\\bar l}}$ ions.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23276,'chemistry',59,372,694,'The ionic conductivity of ${\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{a}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}^{\\rm{ - }}}$  at infinite dilution are ${\\rm{127}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{76}}\\,\\,{\\rm{oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{e}}{{\\rm{q}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$ respectively. The equivalent conductivity of ${\\rm{BaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ at infinity dilution $\\left( {{\\rm{in}}\\,\\,{\\rm{oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{e}}{{\\rm{q}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}} \\right)$ would be:','[{"text": "203", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "279", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "101.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "139.5", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Equivalent conductivity of ${\\rm{BaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>$\\Lambda _{\\rm{m}}^\\infty \\,\\,\\left( {{\\rm{BaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right)\\,\\,{\\rm{ = }}\\,\\,\\lambda _{\\rm{m}}^\\infty \\,\\,\\left( {{\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{a}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}} \\right)\\,\\,{\\rm{ + }}\\,\\,{\\rm{2}}\\lambda _{\\rm{m}}^\\infty \\,\\,\\left( {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}^{\\rm{ - }}}} \\right)$<br>${\\rm{127 + 2 \\times 76 = 279}}\\,\\,{\\rm{oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{e}}{{\\rm{q}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23278,'chemistry',60,588,1184,'For the non-stoichiometric reaction, ${\\rm{2A + B}} \\to {\\rm{C + D}}$, the following kinetic data were obtained in three separate experiments, all at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$.<br>The rate law for the formation of C is :','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{dc}}}}{{{\\\\rm{dt}}}}{\\\\rm{ = k[A][B]}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{dc}}}}{{{\\\\rm{dt}}}}{\\\\rm{ = k[A}}{{\\\\rm{]}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{[B]}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{dc}}}}{{{\\\\rm{dt}}}}{\\\\rm{ = k[A][B}}{{\\\\rm{]}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{dc}}}}{{{\\\\rm{dt}}}}{\\\\rm{ = k[A]}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Rate law equation is given by<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{d c}{d t}=k[A]^{x}[B]^{y} \\\\& \\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{d c}{d t} \\text { for } \\mathrm{C} \\text { at various concentration are } \\\\& 1.2 \\times 10^{-3}=k(0.1)^{x}(0.1)^{y} \\\\& 1.2 \\times 10^{-3}=k(0.1)^{x}(0.2)^{y} \\\\& 2.4 \\times 10^{-3}=k(0.2)^{x}(0.1)^{y}\\end{aligned}$<br>Solving for $\\mathrm{x}$ [divide Eq. (iii) by Eq. (i)]<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{2.4 \\times 10^{-3}}{1.2 \\times 10^{-3}}=\\dfrac{k(0.2)^{x}(0.1)^{y}}{k(0.1)^{x}(0.1)^{y}} \\\\& 2=(2) x \\\\\\Rightarrow & x=1\\end{aligned}$<br>Solving by y [divide Eq. (3) by Eq. (1)] <br>$\\begin{gathered}\\dfrac{2.4 \\times 10^{-3}}{1.2 \\times 10^{-3}}=\\dfrac{k(0.2)^{x}(0.1)^{y}}{k(0.1)^{x}(0.1)^{y}} \\\\2=(2)^{x}\\left(\\dfrac{0.1}{0.2}\\right)^{y} \\\\1=(0.5)^{y} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\quad(1)^{0}=(0.5)^{y} \\\\y=0 \\\\\\text { Thus } \\dfrac{d c}{d t}=k[A]^{1}[B]^{0} \\\\\\dfrac{d c}{d t}=k[A]^{1}\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_23278_question_1.png'),
