(23401,'chemistry',58,168,366,'An aqueous solution of glucose is $10 \\%$ in strength. The volume in which $1 \\mathrm{~g}$ mole of it is dissolved will be','[{"text": "$18 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.9 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.8 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$10 \\%$ glucose solution $=10 \\mathrm{~g}$<br>$=10 / 180 \\mathrm{~mol} \\text { in } 100 \\,\\mathrm{cc} \\text { i.e. } 0.1 \\mathrm{~L}$ <br>Hence, 1 mole of glucose will be present in<br>$=\\dfrac{0.1 \\times 180}{10}=1.8 \\mathrm{~L}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
