(23419,'chemistry',58,172,380,'The vapour pressure lowering caused by the addition of $100 \\mathrm{~g}$ of sucrose (molecular mass $=342$) to $1000 \\mathrm{~g}$ of water, if the vapour pressure of water at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ is $23.8 \\mathrm{~mm} \\,\\mathrm{Hg}$ is','[{"text": "$0.12 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\,\\\\mathrm{Hg}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.125 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\,\\\\mathrm{Hg}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.15 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\,\\\\mathrm{Hg}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.25 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\,\\\\mathrm{Hg}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Number of moles of $\\mathrm{C}_{12} \\mathrm{H}_{22} \\mathrm{O}_{11}$<br>$ = \\frac{{100}}{{342}}$<br>$ = 0.292\\,{\\rm{mol}}$<br>Number of moles of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}=\\dfrac{1000}{18}$<br>$=55.5 \\mathrm{~mol}$<br>$(\\because$ Molecular mass of sucrose $=342$ ),<br>(Molecular mass of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}=18$ )<br>Vapour pressure of pure water ${\\rm{p}}^\\circ  = 23.8\\,{\\rm{mm\\,Hg}}$ <br>According to Raoult\'s law,$\\frac{{{\\rm{\\Delta p}}}}{{{\\rm{p}}^\\circ }} = \\frac{{\\rm{n}}}{{{\\rm{n}} + {\\rm{N}}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{\\rm{\\Delta p}}}}{{{\\rm{23}}{\\rm{.8}}}} = \\frac{{0.292}}{{0.292 + 55.5}}$<br>${\\rm{\\Delta p}} = \\frac{{23.8 \\times 0.292}}{{55.79}} = 0.125\\,{\\rm{mm\\,Hg}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
