(23781,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'For the reaction ${\\rm{A + B}} \\to {\\rm{C}}$; starting with different initial concentrations of A and B, initial rate of reaction were determined graphically in four experiments.<br>Rate law of the reaction from above data is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{r = k[A}}{{\\\\rm{]}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{[B]}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{r = k[A}}{{\\\\rm{]}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{[B]}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{r = k[A][B}}{{\\\\rm{]}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{r = k[A][B]}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From expt. no. 1 and 2, keeping [B] constant and doubling $x=1$, the rate is increased by 4 times.<br>The order w.r.t $\\mathrm{A}$ is 2 .<br>From expt. no. 1 and 3, keeping [A] constant and doubling concentration of $\\mathrm{B}$, the rate increases by 2 times. The order w.r.t B is 1<br>So, the rate law is<br>rate $=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{2}[\\mathrm{~B}]^{1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_23781_question_1.png'),
