(23784,'chemistry',59,367,680,'Consider the following four electrodes:<br>${\\text{A}} = {\\text{C}}{{\\text{u}}^{2 + }}(0.0001{\\text{M}})\\mid {\\text{Cu}}({\\text{s}}){\\text{ }}$<br>${\\text{B}} = {\\text{C}}{{\\text{u}}^{2 + }}(0.1{\\text{M}})\\mid {\\text{Cu}}({\\text{s}})$<br>${\\text{C}} = {\\text{C}}{{\\text{u}}^{2 + }}(0.01{\\text{M}})\\mid {\\text{Cu}}({\\text{s}})$<br>${\\text{D}} = {\\text{C}}{{\\text{u}}^{2 + }}(0.001{\\text{M}})\\mid {\\text{Cu}}({\\text{s}})$<br>If the standard reduction potential of $\\mathrm{Cu}^{+2} / \\mathrm{Cu}$ is $+0.34 \\mathrm{~V}$, then the reduction potentials (in volts) of the above electrodes follow the order','[{"text": "A $>$ D $>$ C $>$ B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B $>$ C $>$ D $>$ A", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "C $>$ D $>$ B $>$ A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A $>$ B $>$ C $>$ D", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For metal electrodes,<br>$\\mathrm{E}=\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}+\\dfrac{0.059}{\\mathrm{n}} \\log \\left[\\mathrm{Cu}^{+2}\\right]$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{E} \\alpha$ concentration',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23785,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'Select the rate law that corresponds to the data shown for the following reaction:<br>$A+B \\rightarrow C$<br>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{rate = k}}{\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{B}} \\\\right]^{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{rate = k}}{\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{B}} \\\\right]^{\\\\rm{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{rate = k}}\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{A}} \\\\right]{\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{B}} \\\\right]^{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{rate = k}}{\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{A}} \\\\right]^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\left[ {\\\\rm{B}} \\\\right]^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From expt. no. 1 and 4, keeping [A] constant and doubling $[\\mathrm{B}]$ concentration, the rate is increased by 8 times.<br>The order w.r.t $\\mathrm{B}$ is 3 .<br>From expt. no. 1 and 3, keeping [B] constant and doubling $[\\mathrm{A}]$ concentration, the rate remains constant.<br>The order w.r.t $\\mathrm{A}$ is 0<br>$\\therefore$ Rate $=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{B}]^{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_23785_question_1.png'),
(23788,'chemistry',59,365,677,'Deduce from the following $\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}$ values of half cells, what combination of two half cells would result in a cell with the largest potential?<br>I. $\\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{A}^{+}+\\mathrm{e} ; \\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}=-0.24 \\mathrm{~V}$<br>II. $\\mathrm{B}^{+} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}+\\mathrm{e} ; \\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}=-2.1 \\mathrm{~V}$<br>III. $\\mathrm{C} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}^{2+}+2 \\mathrm{e}^{-} ; \\mathrm{E}^{0}=-0.38 \\mathrm{~V}$<br>IV. $\\mathrm{D}^{2-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{D}^{-}+\\mathrm{e}^{-} ; \\mathrm{E}^{0}=-0.59 \\mathrm{~V}$','[{"text": "I and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {cell }}^{\\mathrm{o}}=\\mathrm{RHS}-\\mathrm{LHS}=0.24-(-2.1)$<br>$=2.34 \\mathrm{~V}$ for (i)  & (ii) reactions.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23789,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'The reaction ${\\text{2NO(g) + }}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}}$ is of first order. If volume of reaction vessel is reduced to ${\\raise0.5ex\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle {\\rm{1}}$}\\kern-0.1em/\\kern-0.15em\\lower0.25ex\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle {\\rm{3}}$}}$, the rate of reaction would be','[{"text": "$1 / 3$ times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 / 3$ times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 times", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6 times", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For the following reaction,<br>$2 \\mathrm{NO}(g)+\\mathrm{O}_{2}(g) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}(g)$, when the volume of vessel change into $\\dfrac{1}{3}$, then concentration of reactant becomes three times $\\left(\\because C \\alpha \\dfrac{1}{V}\\right)$.<br>The rate of reaction $\\propto$ Concentration. So, rate of reaction will increase three times.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23792,'chemistry',59,373,697,'A certain quantity of electricity is passed through an aqueous solution of $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ and cupric salt solution connected in series. The amount of silver deposited is $1.08 \\mathrm{~g}$. The amount of copper deposited is $(\\mathrm{AW}$. of $\\mathrm{Cu}=63.54 ; \\mathrm{Ag}=108)$','[{"text": "$0.6454 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.354 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.3177 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.177 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Acc. to Faraday\'s second law,<br>$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{m}_{1}}{\\mathrm{~m}_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{E}_{1}}{\\mathrm{E}_{2}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1.08}{\\mathrm{~m}_{2}}=\\dfrac{108}{63.54 / 2} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{m}_{2}=0.3177 \\mathrm{~g}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23793,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'For the gaseous reaction ${\\rm{2A + B}} \\to {\\rm{C + D}}$ the differential rate law is $\\frac{{{\\rm{dx}}}}{{{\\rm{dt}}}}{\\rm{ = k[A]}} \\cdot {\\rm{[B]}}$. The volume of the container containing the reaction mixture is reduced to$\\frac{{{{\\rm{1}}^{{\\rm{th }}}}}}{{\\rm{4}}}$ of the original volume. The rate of reaction will change by','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{16}$ times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{8}$ times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16 times", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8 times", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If the volume of the container is reduced to $1 / 4$ th of the original volume, concentration increases by 4 times.<br>$\\dfrac{d x}{d t}=k 4[A] .4[B]=16(k[A][B])$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23794,'chemistry',58,176,385,'The ratio of the value of any colligative property for ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}}}$ solution of urea solution under similar condition is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For urea, ${\\mathrm{i=1}}$, while for ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}, i=3}}$, therefore, the ratios of any colligative property for ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}}}$ solution to urea solution is 3 .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23796,'chemistry',59,372,694,'Degree of dissociation of pure $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ is $1.9 \\times 10^{-9}$. Molar ionic conductances of $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$& $\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ions at infinite dilution are ${\\text{200 S c}}{{\\text{m}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$<br>/ ${\\text{350}}\\,\\,{\\text{S c}}{{\\text{m}}^{\\text{2}}} {\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$respectively. Molar conductance of water is:','[{"text": "$3.8 \\\\times 10^{-7} \\\\mathrm{Scm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.7 \\\\times 10^{-7} \\\\mathrm{Scm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.5 \\\\times 10^{-7} \\\\mathrm{Scm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.045 \\\\times 10^{-6} \\\\mathrm{Scm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\alpha=\\dfrac{\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{m}}^{\\mathrm{c}}}{\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{m}}^{\\infty}}$<br>$\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{m}}^{\\infty}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)=\\lambda_{\\mathrm{m}}^{\\infty}\\left(\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right)+\\lambda_{\\mathrm{m}}^{\\infty}\\left(\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right)$ $=200+350=550$<br>$\\Rightarrow 1.9 \\times 10^{-9}=\\dfrac{\\wedge_{\\mathrm{m}}^{\\mathrm{c}}}{550 \\mathrm{Scm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}$<br>$\\wedge_{\\mathrm{m}}^{\\mathrm{c}}=550 \\times 1.9 \\times 10^{-9} \\mathrm{Scm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$<br>$=1.045 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{scm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23807,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'In an experiment to study the reaction ${\\text{A + 2B}} \\to {\\text{C + 2D}}$, the initial rate $\\frac{{{\\text{ - d[A]}}}}{{{\\text{dt}}}}$ at $\\mathrm{t}=$ 0 was found to be ${\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.6 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{  -  2}}}}{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{s}}^{{\\rm{  -  1}}}}$. What is the value of $\\frac{{{\\text{ - d[B]}}}}{{{\\text{dt}}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{at t  =  0}}\\,\\,{\\text{in M}}{{\\text{s}}^{{\\text{  -  1}}}}$ ?','[{"text": "$2.6 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.2 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.3 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.0 \\\\times 10^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\frac{{{\\rm{ - d[B]}}}}{{{\\rm{dt}}}}{\\rm{  =  2}}\\left[ {\\frac{{{\\rm{ - d[A]}}}}{{{\\rm{dt}}}}} \\right]$<br>${\\rm{ = 2 \\times 2}}{\\rm{.6 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 2}}}}$<br>${\\rm{ = 5}}{\\rm{.2 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 2}}}}\\,{\\rm{M}}\\,{{\\rm{s}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23797,'chemistry',60,585,1175,'The rate constant of the reaction ${\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(aq)}} \\to {\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O(l) + }}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g)}}$<br>\nAt what concentration of ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{,the}}\\,\\,{\\rm{rate}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{reaction}}\\,\\,{\\rm{will}}\\,\\,{\\rm{be}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{2 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 4}}}}\\,{\\rm{M}}\\,{{\\rm{s}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$','[{"text": "$6.67 \\\\times 10^{-3} M$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.08 \\\\mathrm{M}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\text{ - }}\\frac{{{\\text{d}}\\left[ {{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}}{\\text{ = k}}{\\left[ {{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]^{\\text{1}}}$<br>${\\rm{2 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{  -  4}}}}{\\rm{  =  }}\\frac{{{\\rm{3 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{  -  3}}}}}}{{{\\rm{60}}}}{\\rm{ \\times }}\\left[ {{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right]$<br>or $\\left[ {{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right]{\\rm{  =  4M}}{\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23800,'chemistry',59,370,690,'The resistance of $0.5 \\mathrm{M}$ solution of an electrolyte in a cell was found to be $50 \\Omega$. If the electrodes in the cell are $2.2 \\mathrm{~cm}$ apart ${\\rm{\\& }}$ having an area of $4.4 \\mathrm{~cm}^{2}$, then the molar conductivity (in $\\mathrm{Sm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ ) of solution is:','[{"text": "0.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.02", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.002", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Apply, $K=\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{R}} \\dfrac{\\ell}{\\mathrm{a}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{1}{50} \\times \\dfrac{2.2 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{~m}}{4.4 \\times\\left(10^{-2} \\mathrm{~m}\\right)^{2}}$.<br>${\\text{K}} = 1{\\Omega ^{ - 1}}\\,{{\\text{m}}^{ - 1}}^\\prime {\\text{;C}} = 0.5\\,{\\text{mol}}/{\\text{d}}{{\\text{m}}^3} = 0.5\\,{\\text{mol}}/{10^{ - 3}} {{\\text{m}}^3}$<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{k}}{\\mathrm{c}}=\\dfrac{1 \\mathrm{~s} \\cdot \\mathrm{m}^{-} \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~m}^{3}}{0.5 \\mathrm{~mol}}$<br>$=2 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~s} \\cdot \\mathrm{m}^{2} \\cdot \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23801,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'A gaseous reaction, ${{\\rm{A}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g)}} \\to {\\rm{B(g) + }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C(g)}}$ shows increase in pressure from${\\rm{100}}\\,{\\rm{\\,mm}}\\,\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,\\,{\\rm{120 mm}}$ in 5 minutes. The rate of disappearance of ${{\\rm{A}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{4}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{mm}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{mi}}{{\\\\rm{n}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{8}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{mm}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{mi}}{{\\\\rm{n}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{16\\\\,mm\\\\,mi}}{{\\\\rm{n}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{2}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{mm}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{mi}}{{\\\\rm{n}}^{{\\\\rm{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{A}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}+(1 / 2) \\mathrm{C}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{t}=0 \\quad 100 \\quad 0 \\quad 0 \\\\& \\mathrm{t}=5 \\quad 100-\\mathrm{p} \\quad \\mathrm{p} \\quad \\mathrm{p} / 2 \\\\& \\mathrm{p}_{\\text {Total }}=100+\\mathrm{p} / 2=120 \\\\& \\therefore \\mathrm{p}=40 \\mathrm{~mm}\\end{aligned}$<br>Rate of disappearance of $\\mathrm{A}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})=\\dfrac{-(60-100)}{5}$ $=8 \\mathrm{~mm} \\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23804,'chemistry',59,367,680,'The emf of the cell<br>$\\mathrm{M} \\mid \\mathrm{M}_{(0.02 \\mathrm{M})}^{\\mathrm{n}^{+}} \\| \\mathrm{H}^{+}(1 \\mathrm{M}), \\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) 1$ atm, $\\mathrm{Pt}$ at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ is $0.81 \\mathrm{~V}$. The valency of the metal, if the standard oxidation potential of the metal is 0.76 $\\mathrm{V}$  with is','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{M}+\\mathrm{ne}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{M}^{\\mathrm{n}+}(0.02 \\mathrm{M}) ; \\mathrm{E}^{+}=0.76 \\mathrm{~V}$<br>$\\mathrm{nH}^{+}+\\mathrm{ne}^{-} \\rightarrow \\dfrac{\\mathrm{n}}{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2} ; \\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}=0 \\mathrm{~V}$<br>$\\mathrm{M}+\\mathrm{nH}^{+} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{M}^{\\mathrm{n}+}+\\dfrac{\\mathrm{n}}{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2} ; \\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}=0.76 \\mathrm{~V}$<br>$\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {cell }}=\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}-\\dfrac{0.0591}{\\mathrm{n}} \\log \\dfrac{0.02}{1}$<br>$0.81=0.76-\\dfrac{0.0591}{\\mathrm{n}} \\log \\dfrac{0.02}{1} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{n}=2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23806,'chemistry',58,171,379,'The total number of colligative properties are','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Total colligative properties are four.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23808,'chemistry',59,372,694,'The molar conductance of acetic acid at infinite dilution is 390.7 and for $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ acetic acid solution is $5.2\\,\\,{\\text{ohm c}}{{\\text{m}}^2} {\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{ - 1}}$. The degree of dissociation of $0.1\\,\\,{\\text{M C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{COOH}}$  solution is:','[{"text": "$13.3 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.0133 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.33 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$133 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\alpha=\\dfrac{\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{M}}}{\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{M}}^{\\infty}}=\\dfrac{5.2}{390.7}=0.0133$ (or) $1.33 \\%$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23811,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'The rate of a chemical reaction','[{"text": "Increases as the reaction proceeds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "May increase or decrease during the reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases as the reaction proceeds", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Remains constant as the reaction proceeds", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'During the course of the chemical reaction, rate of the reaction decreases as the reaction proceeds.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23812,'chemistry',59,368,681,'Following two half cells form a complete cell which has $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}$ (in $\\mathrm{kJ}$ ) value<br>${\\text{2}}{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ + }}}{\\text{ + 1/2}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ + 2}}{{\\text{e}}^{\\text{ - }}} \\to {{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O; E}}^\\circ \\,{\\text{ =   + 1}}{\\text{.23 V }}$<br>${\\text{F}}{{\\text{e}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}}{\\text{ + 2}}{{\\text{e}}^{\\text{ - }}} \\to {\\text{Fe(s);E}}^\\circ {\\text{ =   -  0}}{\\text{.44 V}}$','[{"text": "$-122 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-222 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-322 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$-422 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\Delta {\\text{G}}^\\circ {\\text{  =   - nFE}}^\\circ $<br>$=-2 \\times 96500 \\times[1.23-(-0.44)] \\mathrm{J}$<br>$=-322310 \\mathrm{~J}=-322.31 \\mathrm{~kJ}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23815,'chemistry',60,584,1173,'Which of the following statements is correct?','[{"text": "The rate of a reaction decreases with passage of time as the concentration of reactants decreases.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The rate of a reaction is same at any time during the reaction.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The rate of a reaction is independent of temperature change.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The rate of a reaction decreases with increase in concentration of reactant(s).", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The rate of a reaction decreases with passage of time as the concentration of reactants decreases and the concentration of products increases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23816,'chemistry',59,371,693,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The conductivity of solution is greater than pure solvent.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Conductivity depends upon number of the ions present in solution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When electrolytes are dissolved in a solvent, they release their constituent ions into the solution. The presence of these ions enhances the solution\'s conductivity. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23817,'chemistry',58,175,384,'The osmotic pressure of a solution containing 40 g of solute (molecular mass 246) per litre at ${\\mathrm{27^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}}}$ is ( ${\\mathrm{R=0.0822 \\mathrm{~atm} \\mathrm{~L}}}$ ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{mol}^{-1}}}$ )','[{"text": "5.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>${\\mathrm{\\pi=C R T=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{W_{2}}{M w_{2}}\\right) R T}{V}}}$<br>Given ${\\mathrm{W_{2}=40 \\mathrm{~g}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{M w_{2}=246}}$<br>${\\mathrm{T=27^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}=300 \\mathrm{~K}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{V=1 \\mathrm{~L}}}$<br>Substituting all the values, we get<br>${\\mathrm{\\pi=\\dfrac{40}{246} \\times 0.082 \\times 300=4 \\mathrm{~atm}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23819,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'For the reaction, $4 \\mathrm{~A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{C}+2 \\mathrm{D}$ which of the following statements is not correct?','[{"text": "Rate of disappearance of B is 1/4th of rate of disappearance of $\\\\mathrm{A}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rate of formation of $\\\\mathrm{C}$ is $1 / 2$ of rate of consumption of A.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rate of appearance of $\\\\mathrm{D}$ is $1 / 2$ of rate of disappearance of $\\\\mathrm{B}$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Rate of formation of $\\\\mathrm{C}$ and $\\\\mathrm{D}$ are equal.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Rate of appearance of D is twice the rate of disappearance of B.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23820,'chemistry',59,369,685,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Conductance of a substance increases with decrease in resistance.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The inverse of resistance is called conductance.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Conductance is a property of a conductor that promotes the flow of electricity through it, facilitating the movement of electrical charge. On the other hand, resistance is the ability to resists the flow of electricity, So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23821,'chemistry',58,170,377,'A solution of two miscible liquids showing negative deviation from Raoult\'s law will have :','[{"text": "Increased vapour pressure, increased boiling point", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increased vapour pressure, decreased boiling point", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreased vapour pressure, increased boiling point", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Decreased vapour pressure, decreased boiling point.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In the solutions which show ${\\rm{ }} - {\\rm{ve}}$ deviation from Raoult\'s law, more attractions are there between particles. As a result vapour pressure decreases and boiling point increases. So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23824,'chemistry',59,369,685,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Substances like glass, ceramics, etc. having very low conductivity are known as insulators.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> They do not allow the passage of electric current through them.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The substances which do not allow the flow of electric current through them are termed as insulators. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23825,'chemistry',58,168,369,'The quantity which changes with temperature','[{"text": "Mole fraction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mass percentage", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Molarity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Molality", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Molarity is number of moles of solute per litre of solution. As, it is volume dependent, depends on temperature also. Remaining are independent of temperature as there is no volume term. So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23828,'chemistry',59,371,693,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Specific conductance decreases with dilution whereas equivalent conductance increases.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> On dilution, number of ions per millilitre decreases but total number of ions increases considerably.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'On dilution, number of ions per millilitre decreases. Total number of ions increases only in case of weak electrolytes. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23832,'chemistry',59,370,689,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The observed conductance depends upon the nature of the electrolyte and the concentration of the solution<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The cell constant of a cell depends upon the nature of the material of the electrodes.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The cell constant depends upon the distance between the electrodes and their area of cross section. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23834,'chemistry',58,168,366,'The molarity (M) of an aqueous solution containing 5.85 g of NaCl in 500 mL of water is (Given : Molar mass Na: 23 and $\\left.\\mathrm{Cl}: 35.5 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right)$','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{M}=\\dfrac{\\operatorname{mass}(\\mathrm{g})}{\\text { gram molar mass }} \\times \\dfrac{1000}{\\mathrm{~V}(\\mathrm{~mL})}}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\dfrac{5.85}{58.5} \\times \\dfrac{1000}{500} \\\\& =0.2 \\mathrm{M}\\end{aligned}$<br>So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23836,'chemistry',59,373,696,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> At the end of electrolysis using platinum electrodes, an aqueous solution of copper sulphate turns colourless.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Copper in $\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}$ is converted to<br>$\\mathrm{Cu}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ during the electroysis.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>Aq. $\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}$ contains $\\mathrm{H}^{+}, \\mathrm{Cu}^{+2}, \\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{-2}, \\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ions.<br>According to preferential discharge theory, $\\mathrm{Cu}^{+2}$ reduces to $\\mathrm{Cu}$; $\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$oxidises to $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ is left in the solution. As $\\mathrm{Cu}^{+2}$ ions undergo reaction, $\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}$ solution turns to colourless. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23837,'chemistry',58,168,369,'The density of \' ${\\mathrm{x}}$ \' ${\\mathrm{M}}$ solution (\' ${\\mathrm{x}}$ \' molar) of NaOH is ${\\mathrm{1.12 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mL}^{-1}}}$, while in molality, the concentration of the solution is 3 m ( 3 molal).<br> Then x is (Given : Molar mass of NaOH is ${\\mathrm{40 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{mol}}}$ )','[{"text": "3.8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.0", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Molality ${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{1000}}\\,{\\rm{M}}}}{{{\\rm{1000\\, d}} - \\left( {{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{M}}^\\prime }} \\right)}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\dfrac{1000 \\times 3}{(1000 \\times 1.12)-(3 \\times 40)} \\\\& =3 \\mathrm{~m}\\end{aligned}$<br>So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23839,'chemistry',59,369,686,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The conductivity depends on the charge and size of the ions in which they dissociate, the concentration of ions or ease with which the ions move under potential gradient.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The conductivity of solutions of different electrolytes in the same solvent and at a given temperature is same.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At a given temperature and in the same solvent, the conductivity of solutions containing various electrolytes differs. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23842,'chemistry',58,168,369,'Molality (m) of 3 M aqueous solution of NaCl is (Given : Density of solution ${\\mathrm{=1.25 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mL}^{-1}}}$, atomic mass in ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{g} \\,\\mathrm{mol}^{-1}: \\mathrm{Na}-23, \\mathrm{Cl}-35.5}}$ )','[{"text": "2.79 m", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.90 m", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.85 m", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.90 m", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\mathrm{m=\\dfrac{1000 \\mathrm{M}}{1000 \\mathrm{~d}-\\left(\\mathrm{MM}^{\\prime}\\right)}}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\dfrac{1000 \\times 3}{(1000 \\times 1.25)-(3 \\times 58.5)} \\\\& =2.79 \\mathrm{~m}\\end{aligned}$<br>So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23843,'chemistry',59,369,684,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The electrical resistance of a column of ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.05}}\\,\\,{\\text{M NaOH}}$  solution of diameter $1 \\mathrm{~cm}$ and length $50 \\mathrm{~cm}$ is $5.55 \\times 10^{3} \\mathrm{ohm}$. Its resistivity is equal to $76.234 \\Omega \\mathrm{Cm}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Resistivity directly propotional to $\\left( {\\frac{{\\text{A}}}{{\\text{I}}}} \\right)$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\text{R}} = \\rho {\\text{ }}(1/{\\text{A}})$<br>$\\rho  = {\\text{RA}}/1$<br>$\\rho  = \\frac{{5.55 \\times {{10}^3} \\times 3.14 \\times 0.5 \\times 0.5}}{{50}} = 87.135\\Omega {\\text{cm}}$<br>Assertion is not true. Reason is true. Hence, the option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23845,'chemistry',58,170,377,'Identify the mixture that shows positive deviations from Raoult\'s Law.','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}+\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{CO}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{CO}+\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{NH}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{6}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{CO}+\\\\mathrm{CS}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When a solution is formed by adding carbon disulphide ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{CS}_{2}\\right)}}$ to acetone ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COCH}_{3}\\right)}}$, the dipolar interactions among the solute - solute and solvent - solvent molecules are stronger than solute - solvent interactions. Hence, this solution shows positive deviation from Raoult\'s law. So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23847,'chemistry',59,373,697,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In electrolysis, the quantity of electricity needed for depositing 1 mole of silver is different from that required for 1 mole of copper.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The atomic weight of silver and copper are different.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As per Faraday\'s law of electrolysis, the mass of the substances deposited are proportional to their respective equivalent weights. Equivalent weight of $\\mathrm{Ag}=108$ and equivalent weight of $\\mathrm{Cu}=63.5 / 2=31.75$.<br>Atomic weights of $\\mathrm{Ag}$ and $\\mathrm{Cu}$ are different, but the correct reason for the assertion is the difference in their equivalent weights, not molecular weights. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23851,'chemistry',59,370,692,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Equivalent conductance of all electrolytes decreases with increasing concentration.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Lesser number of ions are available per gram equivalent at higher concentration.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Equivalent conductance of all electrolytes decreases with increasing concentration or with decrease in dilution due to the less number of ions are available per equivalent at higher concentration. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23855,'chemistry',59,369,686,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The conductivity of metals decreases with increase of temperature whereas that of electrolytic solution increases.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electrons in metals are very tightly held by the nucleus and are not free to move.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In materials, electrons can move freely, but the vibrations of atomic nuclei, known as lattice vibrations, hinder their smooth flow, causing electrical resistance. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23859,'chemistry',59,368,681,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> An electrochemical cell can be set up only if the redox reaction is spontaneous.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A reaction is spontaneous if standard free energy change is negative.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If a redox reaction is spontaneous, it means that the change in standard Gibbs free energy $\\left(\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}\\right)$ for the reaction is negative $\\left(\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ} < 0\\right)$. As a result, the standard cell potential $\\left(\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}\\right)$ for the reaction is positive. This relationship between $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}$ and $\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}$ is described by the equation:<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}=-\\mathrm{nFE}^{\\circ}$<br>Here, $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}$ is the change in standard Gibbs free energy, $\\mathrm{n}$ is the number of moles of electrons transferred in the redox reaction, $\\mathrm{F}$ is the Faraday constant, and $\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}$ is the standard cell potential. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23863,'chemistry',59,372,694,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Molar conductivity of a weak electrolyte at infinite dilution cannot be determined experimentally.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Kohlrausch law helps to find the molar conductivity of a weak electrolyte at infinite dilution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Extrapolating molar conductivity to zeroconcentration is challenging due to complex ion-ion interactions and solvent effects. Researchers determine the limiting molar conductivity from experimental data at low concentrations, crucial for characterizing electrolyte conductance, while Kohlrausch\'s law is employed for weak electrolytes. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23866,'chemistry',58,175,383,'${\\mathrm{0.05 \\mathrm{M} \\,\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}}}$ when treated with ${\\mathrm{0.01 \\mathrm{M}\\, \\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}}$ gives green colour solution of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}}$. The two solutions are separated as shown below: [SPM : Semi Permeable Membrane]<br>Due to osmosis<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Side X SPM Side ${\\mathrm{Y}}$[SPM : Semi Permeable Membrane]Due to osmosis undefined</div><div class="column-right">undefined undefined</div></div>','[{"text": "Green colour formation observed on side Y.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Green colour formation observed on side ${\\\\mathrm{X}}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Molarity of ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}}}$ solution is lowered.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Molarity of ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}}}$ solution is lowered.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Only solvent molecules pass through the semipermeable membrane from a low concentrated solution to a more concentrated solution. The conc. of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}}$ is less, so water molecules move from ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}}$ to ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}}}$ solution side, As a result conc. of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}}}$ decreases. So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/58_23866_question_1.png'),
(23867,'chemistry',59,373,696,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Electrolysis of an aqueous solution of KI gives $\\mathrm{I}_{2}$ at the anode but that of KF gives $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ at the anode not $\\mathrm{F}_{2}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{I}^{-}$ions have much higher oxidation potential than water while $\\mathrm{F}^{-}$ions have much lower oxidation potential than water.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The products of electrolysis in aqueous solutions depend on the standard reduction potentials. In aq. KI electrolysis, iodine gas is liberated at the anode due to the more favorable oxidation of $\\mathrm{I}^{-}$ions, while in aq. $\\mathrm{KF}$ electrolysis, oxygen gas is produced as $\\mathrm{F}^{-}$ions are less easily oxidized than water. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23870,'chemistry',58,174,382,'What happens to freezing point of benzene when small quantity of naphthalene is added to benzene?','[{"text": "First decreases and then increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When a non-volatile solute is added, freezing point of solution decreases. So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23871,'chemistry',59,372,694,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If  ${\\lambda ^{\\text{o}}}_{{\\text{N}}{{\\text{a}}^ + }}$  and  ${\\lambda ^{\\text{o}}}_{{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}^ - }}$ are molar limiting conductivity of the sodium and chloride ions respectively, then the limiting molar conductivity for sodium chloride is given by the equation, $\\mathrm{A}^{\\circ}{ }_{\\mathrm{NaCl}}=\\lambda^{\\circ}{ }_{\\mathrm{Na}^{+}}+\\lambda^{\\circ}{ }_{\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}}$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> This is according to Kohlrausch law of independent migration of ions','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Kohlrausch\'s law states that the limiting molar conductivity of an electrolyte at infinite dilution is the sum of the molar conductivities of its constituent ions, providing insights into the conductance of individual ions.<br>$\\therefore \\lambda^{\\circ}{ }_{\\mathrm{NaCl}}=\\lambda^{\\circ}{ }_{\\mathrm{Na}^{+}}+\\lambda^{\\circ}{ }_{\\mathrm{Cl}}$<br>So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23872,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'For a certain reaction, the rate ${\\text{ =  k [A}}{{\\text{]}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ [B]}}$, when the initial concentration of A is tripled keeping concentration of B constant, the initial rate would','[{"text": "Increase by a factor of six", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase by a factor of nine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increase by a factor of three", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decrease by a factor of nine", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Rate ${{\\text{r}}_{\\text{1}}}{\\text{  =  k[A}}{{\\text{]}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{[B]}}$<br>If ${\\text{[A]}}$ is tripled and ${\\text{[B]}}$ is kept constant.<br>$\\mathrm{r}_{2}=\\mathrm{k}[3 \\mathrm{~A}]^{2}[\\mathrm{~B}]$<br>$\\mathrm{r}_{2}=9 \\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{2}[\\mathrm{~B}]$<br>$\\mathrm{r}_{2}=9 \\mathrm{r}_{1}$<br>$\\therefore$ increased by a factor of nine.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23875,'chemistry',59,373,697,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> One coulomb of electric charge deposits weight equal to the electrochemical equivalent of the substance.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> One Faraday deposits one mole of the substance.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'One Faraday (F) of electric charge deposits or liberates one gram equivalent of a substance, as per Faraday\'s laws of electrolysis. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23876,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'For a reaction, the value of rate constant at 300 $\\mathrm{K}$ is $6.0 \\times 10^{5} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$. The value of Arrhenius factor $\\mathrm{A}$, at infinitely high temperature is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{6 \\\\times 10^{-5}}{300}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times 10^{5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times 10^{5} \\\\times \\\\mathrm{e}^{-\\\\mathrm{Ea} / 300 \\\\mathrm{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{e}^{-\\\\mathrm{Ea} / 300 \\\\mathrm{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{k}=\\mathrm{A} \\mathrm{e}^{\\frac{-\\mathrm{Ea}}{\\mathrm{RT}}}$<br>At $\\mathrm{T}=\\infty ; \\mathrm{k}=\\mathrm{A}$<br>$=6 \\times 10^{5} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23879,'chemistry',59,373,697,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $96500\\,\\,{\\text{C}}$  charge is required for the reduction of one mole of silver ions.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The amount of electricity (or charge) required for oxidation or reduction depends on the stoichiometry of the electrode reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{Ag}_{(\\text {aq })}^{+}+\\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Ag}_{(\\mathrm{s})}$<br>Charge on one mole of electrons<br>$=6.02 \\times 10^{23} \\times 1.6021 \\times 10^{-19}$<br>$=96487 \\mathrm{Cmol}^{-1} \\approx 96500 \\mathrm{C} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$<br>So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23880,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'The rate constants $\\mathrm{k}_{1}$ and $\\mathrm{k}_{2}$ for two different reactions are $10^{16} \\times \\mathrm{e}^{-2000 / \\mathrm{T}}$ and $10^{15} \\times \\mathrm{e}^{-1000 / \\mathrm{T}}$ respectively. The temperature at which $\\mathrm{k}_{1}=\\mathrm{k}_{2}$ is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1000}{2.303} \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1000 \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2000}{2.303} \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2000 \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{k}_{1}=10^{16} \\times \\mathrm{e}^{\\frac{-2000}{\\mathrm{~T}}}$<br>$\\mathrm{k}_{2}=10^{15} \\times \\mathrm{e}^{\\frac{-1000}{\\mathrm{~T}}}$<br>Given, $\\mathrm{k}_{1}=\\mathrm{k}_{2}$<br>$10^{16} \\times \\mathrm{e}^{\\frac{-2000}{\\mathrm{~T}}}=10^{15} \\times \\mathrm{e}^{\\frac{-1000}{\\mathrm{~T}}}$<br>${\\text{10  =  }}{{\\text{e}}^{\\frac{{{\\text{1000}}}}{{{\\text{T}}}}}}$<br>$\\ln 10=\\dfrac{1000}{\\mathrm{~T}}$<br>$2.303 \\log 10=\\dfrac{1000}{\\mathrm{~T}}$<br>$\\mathrm{T}=\\dfrac{1000}{2.303} \\mathrm{k}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23883,'chemistry',59,366,679,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Auric chloride $\\left(\\mathrm{AuCl}_{3}\\right)$ solution cannot be stored in a vessel made of copper, iron, nickel, chromium, zinc or tin.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Gold is a very precious metal.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Gold has higher reduction potential than copper, iron, nickel, zinc or tin. Thus, gold chloride solution cannot be stored in a vessel made of copper, iron, nickel, chromium, zinc or tin. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23884,'chemistry',60,585,1176,'In which one of the following reactions, rate constant has the unit $\\mathrm{molL}^{-1} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$ ?','[{"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{NO}_{(\\\\mathrm{g})}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2(\\\\mathrm{~g})}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decomposition of $\\\\mathrm{HI}$ on the surface of Gold", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Acid catalysed hydrolysis of $\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COOCH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{CCl}_{4}+\\\\mathrm{HCl}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Decomoposition of HI on the surface of gold follows zero order kinetics.  Zero order reaction rate constant units are $\\mathrm{molL}^{-1} \\mathrm{sec}^{-1}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23888,'chemistry',60,588,1184,'For reaction, $\\mathrm{aA} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{xp}$, when $[\\mathrm{A}]=2.2 \\mathrm{mM}$ the rate was found to be $2.4 \\mathrm{mMs}^{-1}$. On reducing concentration of $\\mathrm{A}$  to half, the rate changes to $0.6 \\mathrm{mMs}^{-1}$. The order of reaction with respect to $\\mathrm{A}$ is','[{"text": "1.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.0", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.0", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{aA} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{xP}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { Rate } \\propto[\\mathrm{A}]^{\\mathrm{a}}=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{\\mathrm{a}} \\\\& \\text { Initially }[\\mathrm{A}]_{1}=2.2 \\mathrm{mM}, \\\\& \\mathrm{r}_{1}=2.4 \\mathrm{mM} / \\mathrm{s} \\\\& \\text { If }[\\mathrm{A}]_{2}=\\dfrac{2.2}{2}=1.1 \\mathrm{mM}, \\\\& \\mathrm{r}_{2}=0.6 \\mathrm{mM} / \\mathrm{s}\\end{aligned}$<br>We have, $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{r}_{1}}{\\mathrm{r}_{2}}=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{1}^{\\mathrm{a}}}{[\\mathrm{A}]_{2}^{\\mathrm{a}}}(\\mathrm{a}$ is the order $)$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{2.4}{0.6}=\\dfrac{(2.2)^{\\mathrm{a}}}{(1.1)^{\\mathrm{a}}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{2.4}{0.6}=(2)^{\\mathrm{a}}\\end{aligned}$<br>or $4=2^{\\mathrm{a}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow 2^{2}=2^{\\mathrm{a}} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{a}=2$<br>$\\therefore$ Order is 2 .<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23891,'chemistry',59,365,676,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The potential difference between the two electrodes of a galvanic cell is called the cell potential.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Cell potential is equal to the emf of a cell.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The difference between the electrode potential of two electrodes when the cell is sending current through the circuit is called cell potential or potential difference, The difference between electrode potential of two electrodes when no current is drawn through the cell is called cell emf (electromotive force). So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23892,'chemistry',60,587,1180,'Which of the following graphs represent relation between initial concentration of reactants and half-life for third order reaction?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\because \\mathrm{t}_{1 / 2} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{a}^{\\mathrm{n}-1}}$,<br>Graph between ${{\\text{t}}_{{\\text{1/2}}}}$  and inital concentration represents for<br>(1) Zero order reaction<br>(2) First order reaction<br>(3) Second order reaction<br>(4) Third order reaction<br>Thus, plot given in option (4) represents third order reaction.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23893,'chemistry',58,176,385,'We have three aqueous solutions of NaCl labelled as ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{B}}}$ and C with concentrations ${\\mathrm{0.1 \\mathrm{M}, 0.01 \\mathrm{M}}}$ and 0.001 M respectively. The value of van\'t Hoff factor (i) for these solutions will be in the order','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{i_{A}=i_{B}=i_{C}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{i_{A} < i_{B} < i_{C}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{i}_{\\\\mathrm{A}}>\\\\mathrm{i}_{\\\\mathrm{B}}>\\\\mathrm{i}_{\\\\mathrm{C}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{i}_{\\\\mathrm{A}} < \\\\mathrm{i}_{\\\\mathrm{C}} < \\\\mathrm{i}_{\\\\mathrm{B}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\mathrm{i_{A}=i_{B}=i_{C}}}$<br>To account for the extent of dissociation or association van\'t Hoff factor is defined.van\'t Hoff factor is independent of concentration. So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23905,'chemistry',60,587,1181,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The hydrolysis of methyl acetate by dilute $\\mathrm{HCl}$ is a pseudo first order reaction.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{HCl}$ acts as a catalyst for the hydrolysis.','[{"text": "If both Assertion & Reason are true and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "If both Assertion & Reason are true but the reason is not the correct explanation of the assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "If Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are false<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Both the statements are correct and the correct reason is that during hydrolysis of methyl acetate, water is taken in large excess, therefore, its concentration does not change during hydrolysis.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23895,'chemistry',59,365,677,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For a cell reaction,<br>${\\text{Z}}{{\\text{n}}_{{\\text{(s)}}}}{\\text{ +  Cu}}_{{\\text{(aq)}}}^{{\\text{2 + }}} \\to {\\text{Zn}}_{{\\text{(aq)}}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}{\\text{ +  C}}{{\\text{u}}_{{\\text{(s)}}}}{\\text{;}}$<br>at the equilibrium, voltmeter gives zero reading.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> At the equilibrium, there is no change in the concentration of $\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ ions.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the electrochemical cell, as $\\mathrm{Zn}$ undergoes oxidation and $\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions are reduced, leading to changing ion concentrations, the cell\'s emf decreases over time due to ongoing reactions until reaching dynamic equilibrium, where concentrations stabilize, and the voltmeter reads zero, signifying a balanced state and the cessation of electrical energy production. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23896,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'For a first order reaction, to obtain a positive slope, we need to plot $\\{$ where $[\\mathrm{A}]$ is the concentration of reactant $\\mathrm{A}$ }','[{"text": "$-\\\\log _{10}[\\\\mathrm{~A}]$ vs $\\\\mathrm{t}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\log _{\\\\mathrm{e}}[\\\\mathrm{A}] \\\\mathrm{vst}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\log _{10}[\\\\mathrm{~A}] \\\\mathrm{vs} \\\\log \\\\mathrm{t}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$[\\\\mathrm{A}] \\\\mathrm{vst}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For a first order reaction to obtain a positive slope, a graph is plotted between $-\\log _{\\mathrm{e}}[\\mathrm{A}] \\mathrm{vst}$.<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23897,'chemistry',58,176,385,'The solution from the following with highest depression in freezing point/lowest freezing point is','[{"text": "180 g of acetic acid dissolved in benzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "180 g of benzoic acid dissolved in benzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "180 g of glucose dissolved in water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "180 g of acetic acid dissolved in water.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\mathrm{\\Delta T_{f}=i \\times K_{f} \\times m}}$ or ${\\mathrm{\\Delta T_{f} \\propto i m}}$ or<br>${\\mathrm{\\Delta \\mathrm{T}_{\\mathrm{f}} \\propto \\mathrm{i} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{w}}{\\mathrm{M}}}}$<br>Where, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{w}=}}$ Mass of compound and, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{M}=}}$ Molar mass of compound<br>For acetic acid in benzene, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{i} < 1}}$ (association takes place)<br>For benzoic acid in benzene, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{i} < 1}}$ (association takes place)<br>For glucose in water, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{i}=1}}$<br>For acetic acid in water, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{i}>1}}$ (diassociation takes place)<br>Molecular mass of acetic acid ${\\mathrm{=60}}$ g. ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{mol}^{-}}}$<br>Molecular mass of glucose ${\\mathrm{=180 \\mathrm{~g} \\cdot \\mathrm{mol}^{-}}}$<br>Molecular mass of benzoic acid ${\\mathrm{=122 \\mathrm{~g} \\cdot \\mathrm{mol}^{-}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\therefore \\dfrac{\\mathrm{w}}{\\mathrm{m}}}}$ value is highest for acetic acid.<br>Hence, 180 g of accetic acid dissolved in water will have highest depression in freezing point or lowest freezing point.So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23899,'chemistry',59,374,701,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A battery is used as a source of electrical energy.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A battery may have one or more than one cell connected in series.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A battery, functioning as a galvanic cell, converts chemical energy from redox reactions into electrical energy, creating an electromotive force (emf) that, when harnessed across terminals, supplies voltage to power diverse devices. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23901,'chemistry',60,585,1176,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The order of a reaction can have fractional value.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The order of a reaction cannot be written from balanced equation of a reaction','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Order is an experimentally determined value and depends upon the concentration change of reactants. It changes with pressure, temperature and concentration and it can have fractional values also. But it must not be written from a balanced chemical equation.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23902,'chemistry',59,374,701,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Dry cell is an example of primary battery.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In the primary batteries, the reaction occurs again and again.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Dry cell is an example of primary battery. In primary batteries, the chemical reaction occurs only once, and the battery becomes depleted or "dead" after a period of use. Primary batteries are non-rechargeable, So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23907,'chemistry',59,374,701,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The cell potential of mercury cell is $1.35 \\mathrm{~V}$ which remains constant<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In mercury cell, the electrolyte is a paste of $\\mathrm{KOH}$ and $\\mathrm{ZnO}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In a mercury cell, represented by the reaction<br>$\\mathrm{Zn}(\\mathrm{Hg})+\\mathrm{HgO} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{ZnO}+\\mathrm{Hg}$<br>A unique characteristic is that the cell potential remains constant throughout its operational life. Because the overall redox reaction does not involve any ions in solution. The electrolyte is a paste of $\\mathrm{KOH}$ and $\\mathrm{ZnO}$. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23909,'chemistry',60,585,1176,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The order of reaction may be defined as the sum of the powers which the concentration terms are raised in order to determine the rate of reaction gives the total order of reaction<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The number of molecules whose concentrations determine the rate of reaciton at a given temperature is called order of reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'It is true that order of reaction is defined as the sum of powers of concentrations present in the rate law.<br>Rate $=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{\\mathrm{m}}[\\mathrm{B}]^{\\mathrm{n}}$<br>Order of reaction $=\\mathrm{m}+\\mathrm{n}$, and given reason is a wrong statement.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2012,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23911,'chemistry',59,374,702,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Lead storage battery is a secondary cell.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Lead storage battery is used in automobiles and invertors.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A lead-acid storage battery, is a secondary cell, as it is rechargeable by passing a current opposite to discharge, reversing chemical reactions and restoring reactants It is used as automotive batteries and uninterruptible power supplies. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23913,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Acid catalysed hydrolysis of ethyl acetate is a first order reaction<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Water does not take part in the reaction','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and the Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but the Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'It is a first order reaction. In this reaction, water takes part in the reaction but it is taken in large amount, so the concentration change in water is not measurable. So, assertion is correct but reason is wrong.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2012,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23914,'chemistry',59,365,677,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The standard electrode potential of a half cell has a fixed value.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A negative value of standard reduction potential means that reduction takes place on this electrode with reference to standard hydrogen electrode.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A negative standard reduction potential  implies that, with respect to the Standard Hydrogen Electrode (SHE), the electrode will undergo oxidation, indicating a higher likelihood of losing electrons rather than gaining them. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23917,'chemistry',60,587,1180,'Among the following statements, the correct statement about the half-life period for a first order reaction is','[{"text": "Independent of concentration", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Proportional to concentration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inversely proportional to concentration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inversely proportional to the square of the concentration.<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${{\\text{t}}_{{\\text{1/2}}}}{\\text{ = }}\\frac{{{\\text{0}}{\\text{.693}}}}{{\\text{k}}}$  (for first order reactions)<br>Here,  ${{\\text{t}}_{{\\text{1/2}}}}$ does not depend on concentration.<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23918,'chemistry',59,365,675,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{KCl}, \\mathrm{NaCl}$ and $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}$ cannot be used in the salt bridge of a cell containing silver.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A salt bridge contains concentrated solution of an inert electrolyte like $\\mathrm{KCl}, \\mathrm{KNO}_{3}$ $\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ or solidified solution of such an electrolyte in agar-agar and gelatine.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{KCl}, \\mathrm{NaCl}$, and $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{NO}_{3}$ are unsuitable as salt bridges in a cell with silver $(\\mathrm{Ag})$ electrodes due to their potential to react with silver ions $\\left(\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\\right)$forming silver chloride precipitate $(\\mathrm{AgCl})$, which can block the salt bridge and hinder ionic conductivity. A non-reactive electrolyte is essential for proper cell functioning. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23920,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'The temperature dependence of rate constant $(\\mathrm{k})$ of a chemical reaction is written in terms of Arrhenius equation $\\mathrm{k}=\\mathrm{Ae}^{-\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}} / \\mathrm{RT}}$. Activation energy $\\left(E_{a}\\right)$ of the reaction can be calculated by plotting.','[{"text": "${\\\\text{log kvs T}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{log kvs}}\\\\frac{{\\\\text{1}}}{{{\\\\text{ T}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{kvs T}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{kvs}}\\\\frac{{\\\\text{1}}}{{{\\\\text{logT}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{k}=\\mathrm{Ae}^{-\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}} / \\mathrm{RT}}$<br>$\\therefore \\log \\mathrm{k}=\\log \\mathrm{A}-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{2.303 \\mathrm{RT}}$<br>Comparing this equation with straight line<br>$\\mathrm{y}=\\mathrm{mx}+\\mathrm{c}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{y}=\\log \\mathrm{k}$ and $\\mathrm{x}=1 / \\mathrm{T}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}$ is calculated with the help of slope by plotting the curve between $\\log \\mathrm{k}$ and $\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{~T}}$.<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23921,'chemistry',59,375,703,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In thermal plants, the chemical energy (heat of combustion) of fossil fuels (coal,  gas or oil) is used to produce electricity.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Production of electricity by thermal plants is a very efficient method.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Thermal power plants, relying on fossil fuel combustion, are deemed inefficient and environmentally harmful due to low energy conversion efficiencies, substantial waste heat generation, and emissions like ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{, S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{, N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{x}}}$, and particulate matter. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23923,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> According to transition state theory, for the formation of an activated complex, one of the vibrational degree of freedom is converted into a translational degree of freedom.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Energy of the activated complex is higher than energy of reactant molecules.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The formation of an activated complex takes place when vibrational degree of freedom converts into a translational degree of freedom. This statement is given by transistion state theory. Also, the energy of activated complex is higher than the energy of reactant molecule, is true but it is not the correct explanation of the assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23924,'chemistry',59,375,703,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Galvanic cells containing hydrogen,  methane, methanol, etc. as fuels are called fuel cells.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> They are designed to convert the energy of combustion of fuels directly into electrical energy.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Fuel cells directly convert the chemical energy of fuels like hydrogen into electrical energy through controlled chemical reactions. They offer high efficiency and minimal pollution. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23926,'chemistry',59,375,703,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{H}_{2}-\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ fuel cell is generally used in Apollo space programme.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The fuel cell uses the reaction of hydrogen with oxygen to provide electrical power and water.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The hydrogen-oxygen fuel cell, used in the Apollo space program, not only efficiently converts chemical energy to electrical power but also recycles water vapor. It generates power and a clean, drinkable water source. So,option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23927,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'An endothermic reaction with high activation energy for the forward reaction is given by the diagram<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In an endothermic reaction, the overall energy is absorbed by the system and therefore the energy of products is higher than reactants.<br>Option 3 is correct for endothermic reaction with high activation energy.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23929,'chemistry',59,375,703,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{H}_{2}-\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ fuel cell gives a constant voltage throughout its life.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In this fuel cell, $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ reacts with $\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ions yet the overall concentration of $\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ions does not change.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Fuel cells maintain continuous and controlled flows of hydrogen and oxygen to the electrodes, facilitating electrochemical reactions for steady electrical energy generation, ensuring efficient reactant utilization, minimizing waste, and enhancing reliability in various applications. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23931,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'The potential energy diagram for a reaction \\(\\mathrm{R} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{P}\\) is given below. \\[\\Delta {\\text{HO}}\\] of the reaction corresponding to the energy.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{a}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{b}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{c}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{a + b}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Activation energy for forward reaction ${\\text{ =  a}}$  Activation energy for backward reaction ${\\text{ =  b}}$<br>$\\because$ Reaction is exothermic<br>$\\therefore$ Heat of reaction $=\\mathrm{b}-\\mathrm{a}=\\mathrm{c}(\\mathrm{b}>\\mathrm{a})$.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2014,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_23931_question_1.jpg'),
(23932,'chemistry',59,376,704,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Corrosion of iron is commonly known as rusting.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Corrosion of iron occurs in presence of water and air.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In the presence of water and air, iron undergoes corrosion. It is oxidizied to form hydrated ferric oxide (rust), a reddish-brown, flaky substance. It is commonly called rusting of iron. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23949,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'Instantaneous rate of a reaction is $-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{x}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{y}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{z}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$, identify the reaction','[{"text": "${\\\\text{x - 2y}} \\\\to {\\\\text{2z}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{2x  +  y}} \\\\to {\\\\text{2z}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{2z  +  y}} \\\\to {\\\\text{2x}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{2x  -  2y}} \\\\to {\\\\text{z}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For ${\\text{2x  +  2y}} \\to {\\text{2z}}$,<br>$-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{x}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{-\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{y}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{z}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23934,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'Rate constant $(\\mathrm{k})$ varies with temperature as given by equation $\\log \\mathrm{k}\\left(\\mathrm{min}^{-1}\\right)=5-\\dfrac{2000}{\\mathrm{~T}}$. Consider the following about this equation<br>I. Pre-exponential factor is $10^{5}$.<br>II. $\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}$ is $9.212 \\mathrm{kcal}$.<br>III. Variation of $\\log \\mathrm{k}$ with $\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{~T}}$ is linear.<br>Select the correct statements.','[{"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and III<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given,<br>$\\log {\\text{k}}\\left( {{{\\min }^{ - 1}}} \\right) = 5 - \\frac{{2000}}{{\\,{\\text{T}}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(1)}}$<br>$\\because {\\log _{10}}{\\text{k}} = {\\log _{10}}{\\text{A}} - \\frac{{{{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}}}}{{2.303{\\text{RT}}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(2)}}$<br>On comparing equations (1) and (2)<br>I. $\\log \\mathrm{A}=5$ or $\\mathrm{A}=10^{5}$<br>II. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{2.303 \\mathrm{R}}=2000$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}}{\\text{ =  2}}{\\text{.303}} \\times {\\text{2000}} \\times {\\text{0}}{\\text{.002 kcal}}$<br>$\\therefore {{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}} = {\\text{9}}{\\text{.212 k cal}}$<br>III. From Eq. (2), it is clear that,<br>$\\log \\mathrm{k} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{~T}}$<br>$\\therefore$ All given statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23935,'chemistry',59,376,704,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Corrosion causes enormous damage to buildings, bridges, ships and to all objects made of metals.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Corrosion changes the chemical composition of metals.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Corrosion is a natural process where metal surfaces transform into compounds like oxides, sulfides, and carbonates due to exposure to atmospheric gases, such as oxygen, sulfur, and carbon dioxide, leading to less stable and protective compounds that can weaken metal structures over time. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23937,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'The rate constant for the first order decomposition of a certain reaction is described by the equation<br>$\\ln \\mathrm{k}\\left(\\mathrm{s}^{-1}\\right)=14.34-\\dfrac{1.25 \\times 10^{4} \\mathrm{~K}}{\\mathrm{~T}}$. The energy of activation for this reaction is','[{"text": "${\\\\text{1}}.{\\\\text{26}} \\\\times {\\\\text{1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{\\\\text{4}}}{\\\\text{cal mo}}{{\\\\text{l}}^{{\\\\text{  -  1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{4}}{\\\\text{.29}} \\\\times {\\\\text{1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{\\\\text{4}}}{\\\\text{cal mo}}{{\\\\text{l}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{3}}{\\\\text{.12}} \\\\times {\\\\text{1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{\\\\text{4}}}{\\\\text{cal mo}}{{\\\\text{l}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{2}}{\\\\text{.50}} \\\\times {\\\\text{1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{\\\\text{4}}}{\\\\text{cal mo}}{{\\\\text{l}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given that,<br>In   ${\\text{k}}\\left( {{{\\text{s}}^{ - 1}}} \\right) = 14.34 - \\frac{{1.25 \\times {{10}^4}}}{{\\,{\\text{T}}}} {\\text{K}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(1)}}$<br>We know that,<br> In  ${\\text{k}}\\left( {{{\\text{s}}^{ - 1}}} \\right) = \\ln {\\text{A}} - \\frac{{{{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}}}}{{{\\text{RT}}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(2)}}$<br>On comparing equation (1) and (2) we get<br>$\\frac{{{{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}}}}{{\\text{R}}} = 1.25 \\times {10^4} {\\text{K}}$<br>$\\therefore {{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}} = 1.25 \\times {10^4} {\\text{K}} \\times {\\text{R cal }}{{\\text{K}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}{\\text{ mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$<br>$ = 1.25 \\times {10^4}\\,{\\text{K}} \\times {\\text{2 cal }}{{\\text{K}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}{\\text{ mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$  $\\left[\\because \\mathrm{R}=2 \\mathrm{cal} \\mathrm{K}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right]$<br>$ = 2.50 \\times {10^4}{\\text{cal mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23939,'chemistry',59,366,679,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Zinc displaces copper from copper sulphate solution.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}$ of zinc is $-0.76 \\mathrm{~V}$ and that of copper is $+0.34 \\mathrm{~V}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The standard reduction potential of zinc is $-0.76 \\mathrm{~V}$ and that of copper is $+0.34 \\mathrm{~V}$. Due to more oxidation potential or less reduction potential of zinc than copper, zinc can reduce copper ions in copper sulphate to copper. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23940,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The reaction<br>$2 \\mathrm{NO}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ and $2 \\mathrm{CO}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ proceeds at the same rate becuase they are similar.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Both the reactions have same activation energy<br>','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are  incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both the reactions $2 \\mathrm{NO}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ and $2 \\mathrm{CO}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ proceed at the different rates and have different activation energies.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23941,'chemistry',59,365,675,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For the Daniell cell,<br>$\\mathrm{Zn}\\left|\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\\right|\\left|\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\\right| \\mathrm{Cu}$ with $\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {cell }}^{\\mathrm{o}}=1.1 \\mathrm{~V}$, the application of opposite potential greater than ${\\text{1}}{\\text{.1 V}}$ results into flow of electrons from cathode to anode.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Zn}$ is deposited at anode and $\\mathrm{Cu}$ is dissolved at cathode.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When the opposing potential surpasses the cell potential, causing a reversal in current flow, the electrochemical cell undergoes a change in the direction of the overall cell reaction, converting zinc ions to solid zinc at the anode and oxidizing copper to produce copper ions at the cathode. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23943,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'What is the activation energy $(\\mathrm{kJ} / \\mathrm{mol})$ for a reaction, if its rate constant doubles when the temperature is raised from $300 \\mathrm{~K}$ to $400 \\mathrm{~K}$ $\\left(\\mathrm{R}=8.314 \\mathrm{Jmol}^{-1} \\mathrm{~K}^{-1}\\right)$','[{"text": "68.8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6.88", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "34.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.44", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\log \\dfrac{\\mathrm{k}_{2}}{\\mathrm{k}_{1}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{2303 \\mathrm{R}}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{~T}_{1}}-\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{~T}_{2}}\\right]$ where $\\mathrm{T}_{1}$ and $\\mathrm{T}_{2}$ are initial and final temperature; $\\mathrm{k}_{1}$ and $\\mathrm{k}_{2}$ are initial and final rate constants; $\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}=$ activation energy and $\\mathrm{R}=$ gas constant.<br>Given, $\\mathrm{T}_{1}=300 \\mathrm{~K}, \\mathrm{~T}_{2}=400 \\mathrm{~K}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{R}=8.314 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\mathrm{~K}^{-1} \\\\& \\dfrac{\\mathrm{k}_{2}}{\\mathrm{k}_{1}}=\\dfrac{2}{1}\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\log \\dfrac{\\mathrm{k}_{2}}{\\mathrm{k}_{1}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{2303 \\times \\mathrm{R}}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{~T}_{1}}-\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{~T}_{2}}\\right]$<br>Thus, $\\log \\dfrac{2}{1}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}}{2.303 \\times 8.314}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{300}-\\dfrac{1}{400}\\right]$<br>$\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}=0.30 \\times 2.303 \\times 8.314 \\times 3 \\times 400$<br>$\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}=6.88 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23944,'chemistry',59,368,681,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A reaction is spontaneous if $\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {cell }}=+\\mathrm{ve}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> For $\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {cell }}=+\\mathrm{ve}, \\Delta \\mathrm{G}$ is always $-\\mathrm{ve}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For a redox reaction to be spontaneous, the EMF of the cell must be positive. As,<br>$\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}=-\\mathrm{nFE}_{\\text {cell }}^{\\circ}$<br>Therefore, for $\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {cell }}=+\\mathrm{ve}, \\mathrm{G}$ is always negative. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23946,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'For the reaction,<br>$3 \\mathrm{I}_{(\\text {aq) }}^{-}+\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{8 \\text { (aq) }}^{2-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{I}_{3(\\mathrm{aq})}^{-}+2 \\mathrm{SO}_{4(\\text { aq })}^{2-}$, rate of formation of $\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$ is ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.022 mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}{\\text{se}}{{\\text{c}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$<br>What is rate of formation of $\\mathrm{I}_{3(\\mathrm{aq})}^{-}$?','[{"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.022 mol d}}{{\\\\text{m}}^{{\\\\text{ - 3}}}}{\\\\text{se}}{{\\\\text{c}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.11 mol d}}{{\\\\text{m}}^{{\\\\text{ - 3}}}}{\\\\text{se}}{{\\\\text{c}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.011 mol d}}{{\\\\text{m}}^{{\\\\text{ - 3}}}}{\\\\text{se}}{{\\\\text{c}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.033 mol d}}{{\\\\text{m}}^{{\\\\text{ - 3}}}}{\\\\text{se}}{{\\\\text{c}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$3 \\mathrm{I}_{(\\text {aq) }}^{-}+\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{8(\\text { (aq) }}^{2-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{I}_{3(\\text { aq })}^{-}+2 \\mathrm{SO}_{4(\\text { aq })}^{2-}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{I}_{3}^{-}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 0.022$<br>$ = 0.011\\,\\,{\\text{mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}{\\text{se}}{{\\text{c}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23950,'chemistry',59,376,704,'Which of the following statements is not correct about rusting of iron?','[{"text": "Dissolved acidic oxides $\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ in water act as catalyst in the process of rusting.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "When $\\\\mathrm{pH}$ lies above 9 or 10 , rusting of iron does not take place.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Coating of iron surface by tin prevents rusting, even if the tin coating is peeling off.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Rusting of iron is envisaged as setting up of electrochemical cell on the surface of iron object.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'While tin coatings can provide some protection against rusting, if the coating peels off or becomes damaged, it exposes the iron underneath, making it susceptible to rusting. So, the statement is not entirely correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23952,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'Half life for a first order reaction is 6.93 hour. What is the time required for $80 \\%$ completion of the reaction?','[{"text": "12 hours", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "18 hours", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6 hours", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16 hours", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For first order reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{0.693}{\\mathrm{t}_{\\frac{1}{2}}}=\\dfrac{0.693}{6.93}=0.1$ hour $^{-1}$<br>Here, $[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}=100,[\\mathrm{~A}]_{\\mathrm{t}}=100-80=20$<br>$\\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{2.303}{\\mathrm{t}} \\log _{10} \\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{[\\mathrm{~A}]_{\\mathrm{t}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{t}=\\dfrac{2.303}{0.1} \\log _{10} \\dfrac{100}{20}=23.03 \\times \\log _{10} 5$<br>$=23.03 \\times 0.699=16.10$ hours',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23955,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'For the reaction $2 \\mathrm{NO}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{NOCl}$<br>What is the relation between $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{NO}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$ and<br>$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{NOCl}]}{\\mathrm{dt}} ?$','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{NO}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=2 \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{NOCl}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{NO}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{NOCl}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{4} \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{NO}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{NOCl}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{NO}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}[\\\\mathrm{NOCl}]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{2NO  +  C}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}} \\to {\\text{2}}\\,\\,{\\text{NOCl}}$<br>Rate of reaction $=-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{NO}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{NOCl}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{NO}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{NOCl}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23958,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'In a first order reaction concentration of reactant decreases from ${\\text{20 m mol}}$  to ${\\text{10 m mol}}$ in $1.151 \\mathrm{~min}$. What is rate constant?','[{"text": "$1.15 \\\\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.0 \\\\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.50 \\\\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.60 \\\\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}=20 \\mathrm{~m} \\mathrm{~mol},[\\mathrm{~A}]_{\\mathrm{t}}=10 \\mathrm{~m} \\mathrm{~mol}$<br>$\\mathrm{t}=1.15 \\min , \\mathrm{k}=$ ?<br>For first order reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{2.303}{\\mathrm{t}} \\log _{10} \\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{[\\mathrm{~A}]_{\\mathrm{t}}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore \\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{2.303}{1.151 \\mathrm{~min}} \\log _{10}\\left(\\dfrac{20}{10}\\right)=\\dfrac{2.303 \\times \\log 2}{1.151} \\\\& =\\dfrac{2.303 \\times 0.3010}{1.151}=0.60 \\mathrm{~min}^{-1}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23964,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'What is the rate of appearance of ${\\text{Z}}$  in following reaction? $3 \\mathrm{x} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{y}+\\mathrm{z}$, if rate of disappearance of $\\mathrm{x}$ is ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.072 mol}}{{\\text{s}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$.','[{"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.072 mol }}{{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{  -  1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.048 mol }}{{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{  -  1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.024 mol}}{{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{  -  1}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.096 mol }}{{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{  -  1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\text{3x}} \\to {\\text{2y  +  z}}$<br>Rate of formation $ =  - \\frac{1}{3}\\frac{{{\\text{d}}[{\\text{x}}]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{{\\text{d}}[{\\text{y}}]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = \\frac{{{\\text{d}}[{\\text{z}}]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}}$<br>$\\frac{{{\\text{d}}[{\\text{z}}]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = \\frac{1}{3}(0.072) = 0.024\\,\\,{\\text{mol }}{{\\text{s}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23967,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'Slope of the graph between rate $(\\mathrm{Y}-$ axis $)$ and $[\\mathrm{A}](\\mathrm{X}$-axis $)$ for the first order reaction is equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{k}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2.303}{\\\\mathrm{k}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{k}}{2.303}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{k}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A plot of rate versus  ${{\\text{[A]}}_{\\text{t}}}$  is a straight line passing through origin. This is shown in Fig. The slope of straight line $=\\mathrm{k}$.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/60_23967_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23968,'chemistry',59,365,677,'The standard electrode potential of  $\\mathrm{M}^{+} / \\mathrm{M}$ in aqueous solution does not depend on','[{"text": "ionisation of a gaseous metal atom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sublimation of a solid metal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "hydration of a gaseous metal ion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ionisation of a solid metal atom.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The standard electrode potential of  $\\mathrm{M}^{+} / \\mathrm{M}$ in aqueous solution is independent of the ionisation of a solid metal atom.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23970,'chemistry',60,587,1180,'In a first order reaction, concentration of reactant is reduced to $(1 / 8)^{\\text {th }}$ of concentration in 23.03 minutes. What is half-life period of reaction?','[{"text": "$25 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.7 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$15 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}=1,[\\mathrm{~A}]_{\\mathrm{t}}=\\dfrac{1}{8}, \\mathrm{t}=23.03 \\mathrm{~min}$<br>For first order reaction,<br>(i) $\\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{2.303}{\\mathrm{t}} \\log _{10} \\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{[\\mathrm{~A}]_{\\mathrm{t}}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{2.303}{23.03} \\log _{10} \\dfrac{1}{(1 / 8)}=0.1 \\times \\log 8$<br>$=0.1 \\times 0.9030$<br>$=0.0903 \\mathrm{~min}^{-}$<br>(ii) $\\mathrm{t}_{1 / 2}=\\dfrac{0.693}{\\mathrm{k}}=\\dfrac{0.693}{0.0903 \\mathrm{~min}^{-1}}=7.7 \\mathrm{~min}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23971,'chemistry',59,373,696,'The product, which is not obtained during the electrolysis of brine solution is','[{"text": "HCl", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "NaOH", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The electrolysis of brine solution results in the following reaction:<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/59_23971_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>HCl is not a product of electrolysis of brine solution.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23973,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'For a first order reaction, intercept of the graph between $\\log \\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{[\\mathrm{~A}]_{1}}$ (Y-axis) and conc (X-axis) is equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{k}}{2.303 \\\\mathrm{~K}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\log [\\\\mathrm{A}]_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2.303}{\\\\mathrm{~K}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'It starts from origin, so intercept is zero.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23988,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'For the reaction $2 \\mathrm{~A}+2 \\mathrm{~B} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{C}+\\mathrm{D}$ if $\\mathrm{r}=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{2}[\\mathrm{~B}]^{0}$, then rate of reaction is','[{"text": "Inversely porportional to square of concentration of A.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Independent of concentration of A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Indepenedent of concentration of B", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Directly proportional to concentration of B", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to given rate equation, rate is independent of conc. of B.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23976,'chemistry',60,587,1180,'What is the half - life of a first order reaction if time required to decrease concentration of reactant from $1.0 \\mathrm{M}$ to $0.25 \\mathrm{M}$ is 10 hour?','[{"text": "12 hour", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4 hour", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5 hour", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10 hour", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{t}=10$ hour, $[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}=1.0 \\mathrm{M},[\\mathrm{A}]_{\\mathrm{t}}=0.25 \\mathrm{M}$ For first order reaction,<br>(i) $\\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{2.303}{\\mathrm{t}} \\log _{10} \\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{[\\mathrm{~A}]_{\\mathrm{t}}}=\\dfrac{2.303}{10 \\mathrm{~h}} \\log _{10} \\dfrac{1.0}{0.25}$<br>$=\\dfrac{2.303}{10 \\mathrm{~h}} \\log _{10} 4 \\quad=\\dfrac{2.303 \\times 0.6020}{10 \\mathrm{~h}}$<br>$=0.139 \\mathrm{~h}^{-1}$<br>(ii) $\\mathrm{t}_{1 / 2}=\\dfrac{0.693}{\\mathrm{k}}=\\dfrac{0.693}{0.139 \\mathrm{~h}^{-1}}$<br>$=4.98$ hour $\\approx 5$ hours',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23977,'chemistry',59,365,678,'The reaction<br>$\\frac{1}{2}{{\\text{H}}_{2({\\text{g}})}} + {\\text{AgC}}{{\\text{l}}_{({\\text{s}})}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{H}}_{({\\text{aq}})}^ +  + {\\text{Cl}}_{({\\text{aq}})}^ -  + {\\text{A}}{{\\text{g}}_{({\\text{s}})}}$ <br> occurs in which of the given galvanic cell?','[{"text": "${\\\\text{Ag }}\\\\left| {{\\\\text{AgC}}{{\\\\text{l}}_{({\\\\text{s}})}}} \\\\right|{\\\\text{KC}}{{\\\\text{l}}_{\\\\left( {{\\\\text{so}}{{\\\\text{l}}^{\\\\text{n}}}} \\\\right)}}\\\\left| {{\\\\text{AgN}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{3\\\\left( {{\\\\text{so}}{{\\\\text{l}}^{\\\\text{n}}}} \\\\right)}}} \\\\right|{\\\\text{Ag}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{Pt}}\\\\left| {{{\\\\text{H}}_{2({\\\\text{g}})}}} \\\\right|{\\\\text{HC}}{{\\\\text{l}}_{\\\\left( {{\\\\text{so}}{{\\\\text{l}}^{\\\\text{n}}}} \\\\right)}}\\\\left| {{\\\\text{AgN}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{3\\\\left( {{\\\\text{so}}{{\\\\text{l}}^{\\\\text{n}}}} \\\\right)}}} \\\\right|{\\\\text{Ag}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{Pt}}\\\\left| {{{\\\\text{H}}_{2(\\\\,{\\\\text{g}})}}} \\\\right|\\\\left. {{\\\\text{HC}}{{\\\\text{l}}_{\\\\left( {{\\\\text{so}}{{\\\\text{l}}^{\\\\text{n}}}} \\\\right)}}} \\\\right)\\\\left| {{\\\\text{AgC}}{{\\\\text{l}}_{({\\\\text{s}})}}} \\\\right|{\\\\text{Ag}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{Pt}}\\\\left| {{{\\\\text{H}}_{2(\\\\,{\\\\text{g}})}}} \\\\right|{\\\\text{HC}}{{\\\\text{l}}_{\\\\left( {{\\\\text{so}}{{\\\\text{l}}^{\\\\text{n}}}} \\\\right)}}\\\\left| {{\\\\text{AgC}}{{\\\\text{l}}_{({\\\\text{s}})}}} \\\\right|{\\\\text{Ag}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{Pt}\\left|\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right| \\mathrm{HCl} \\| \\mathrm{AgCl} \\mid \\mathrm{Ag}$<br>The given reaction is<br>$1 / 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+\\mathrm{AgCl}_{(\\mathrm{aq})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{+}+\\mathrm{Ag}_{(\\mathrm{s})}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{-}$<br>Silver is the cathode and undergoes reduction Hydrogen electrode is the anode. The half cell reactions are as follows:<br>At anode: $1 / 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{H}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{+}+\\mathrm{e}^{-}$<br>At cathode: $\\mathrm{AgCl}_{(\\mathrm{s})}+\\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Ag}_{(\\mathrm{s})}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{-}$<br>The correct representation is  $\\mathrm{Pt}\\left|\\mathrm{H}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}\\right| \\mathrm{HCl}_{\\left(\\mathrm{sol}^{\\mathrm{n}}\\right)}\\left|\\mathrm{AgCl}_{(\\mathrm{s})}\\right| \\mathrm{Ag}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23979,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'What is the rate of disappearance of  ${\\text{B}}$  in following reaction? $2 \\mathrm{~A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{C}$, if rate of appearance of $\\mathrm{C}$ is $1.3 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$.','[{"text": "$4.33 \\\\times 10^{-5} \\\\mathrm{~mol} \\\\mathrm{~L}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8.6 \\\\times 10^{-5} \\\\mathrm{~mol} \\\\mathrm{~L}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}{\\\\text{mol }}{{\\\\text{L}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}{\\\\text{ s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.2 \\\\times 10^{-5} \\\\mathrm{~mol} \\\\mathrm{~L}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{2A +  B}} \\to {\\text{3C}}$<br>Rate $=-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{A}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{B}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{1}{3} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{C}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{B}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times 1.3 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$<br>$=4.33 \\times 10^{-5} \\mathrm{molL}^{-1} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23982,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'For a reaction $\\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow$ product, rate constant is $2 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$. The initial concentration of $\\mathrm{A}$ is ${\\text{1}}{\\text{.0 mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}$. What is the value of $\\log \\dfrac{1}{[\\mathrm{~A}]_{\\mathrm{t}}}$ after 100 seconds?','[{"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.430 mol d}}{{\\\\text{m}}^{{\\\\text{ - 3}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.135 mol d}}{{\\\\text{m}}^{{\\\\text{ - 3}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.270 mol d}}{{\\\\text{m}}^{{\\\\text{ - 3}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.868 mol d}}{{\\\\text{m}}^{{\\\\text{ - 3}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\text{k  =  2}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 2}}}}{\\text{}}{{\\text{s}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}{\\text{,[ A}}{{\\text{]}}_{\\text{0}}}{\\text{ =  1}}{\\text{.0 mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}$<br>$\\mathrm{t}=100 \\mathrm{~s}, \\log \\dfrac{1}{[\\mathrm{~A}]_{\\mathrm{t}}}=$ ?<br>For first order reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{2.303}{\\mathrm{t}} \\log _{10} \\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{[\\mathrm{~A}]_{\\mathrm{t}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\log _{10} \\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{[\\mathrm{~A}]_{\\mathrm{t}}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{k} \\times \\mathrm{t}}{2.303}$<br>$\\therefore {\\log _{10}}\\frac{1}{{{{[\\,{\\text{A}}]}_{\\text{t}}}}} = \\frac{{2 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}} \\times 100}}{{2.303}} = 0.868\\,\\,{\\text{mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23991,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'What is rate constant of a first order reaction if 0.08 mole of reactant reduces to 0.02 mole in 23.03 minute?','[{"text": "$0.2303 \\\\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6021 \\\\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.4031 \\\\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.06021 \\\\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For first order reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{2.303}{\\mathrm{t}} \\log _{10} \\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{[\\mathrm{~A}]_{\\mathrm{t}}}$<br>$\\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{2.303}{23.03 \\min } \\log _{10} \\dfrac{0.08}{0.02}$<br>$=\\dfrac{2.303}{23.03 \\min } \\log _{10} 4=0.1 \\mathrm{~min}^{-1} \\times 0.6021$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{k}=0.06021 \\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23994,'chemistry',60,585,1176,'Which of the following statement is NOT true for a reaction having rate law $\\mathrm{r}=\\mathrm{k}\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{I}_{2}\\right]$ ?','[{"text": "The reaction is first order in $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Overall order of reaction is 1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The reaction is first order in ${{\\\\text{I}}_{\\\\text{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Overall order of reaction is 2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{r}=\\mathrm{k}\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{I}_{2}\\right]$<br>The reaction is first order with respect to $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{I}_{2}$. The overall order of reaction is 2 .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23995,'chemistry',59,374,702,'For lead storage battery, pick the correct statements.<br>a. During charging of battery, $\\mathrm{PbSO}_{4}$ on anode is converted into $\\mathrm{PbO}_{2}$.<br>b.  During charging of battery, $\\mathrm{PbSO}_{4}$ on cathode is converted into $\\mathrm{PbO}_{2}$.<br>c.  Lead storage battery consists of grid of lead packed with $\\mathrm{PbO}_{2}$ as anode.<br>d.  Lead storage battery has $38 \\%$ solution of sulphuric acid as an electrolyte.<br>Choose the correct answer from the option given below:','[{"text": "a,b,d only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "b,d only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "b,c,d only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "b,c only", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In each lead storage cell, the anode is a grid of lead packed with finely divided spongy lead and cathode is a grid of lead packed with $\\mathrm{PbO}_{2}$. The electrolyte is aqueous solution of sulphuric acid $38 \\%$ by mass.<br>During charging, lead is deposited on anode and  $\\mathrm{PbO}_{2}$ on the cathode. Thus, statements b and d are correct.<br>So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(23997,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'For the reaction $\\mathrm{N}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3(\\mathrm{~g})}$, what is the relation between $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$ ?','[{"text": "$3 \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\\\right]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\\\right]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\\\right]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=3 \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\\\right]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\\\right]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=2 \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\\\right]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\\\right]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{d}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\\\right]}{\\\\mathrm{dt}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{N}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>Rate of reaction $=-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=3 \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24000,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'The rate law equation for a reaction between $\\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{B}$ and $\\mathrm{C}$ is $\\mathrm{r}=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}][\\mathrm{C}]^{2}$, what will be new rate of reaction if concentration of both $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{B}$ are doubled.','[{"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{r}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 r$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6 \\\\mathrm{r}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 r$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{r}=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}][\\mathrm{C}]^{2}$ (given)<br>If concentration of both $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{B}$ are doubled, then new rate of reaction is<br>$\\mathrm{r}^{\\prime}=\\mathrm{k}[2 \\mathrm{~A}][2 \\mathrm{~B}][\\mathrm{C}]^{2}$<br>$=4 \\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}][\\mathrm{C}]^{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{r}^{\\prime}=4 \\mathrm{r}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24003,'chemistry',60,585,1176,'The order of reaction for which the units of rate constant are ${\\text{mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{ - 3}}\\,{{\\text{s}}^{ - 1}}$ is','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For zero order reactions, ${\\text{K}}$  units mol.dm ${ }^{-3} \\mathrm{sec}^{-}$<br>In general \' $\\mathrm{K}$ \' units $\\mathrm{mol}^{(1-\\mathrm{n})} \\cdot \\mathrm{Lit}^{(\\mathrm{n}-1)} \\cdot \\mathrm{sec}^{-}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24006,'chemistry',60,587,1178,'Which of the following equations represents integrated rate law for zero order reaction?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{k}=\\\\dfrac{[\\\\mathrm{A}]_{\\\\mathrm{t}}-[\\\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{\\\\mathrm{t}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{k}=\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\mathrm{t}} \\\\log _{10} \\\\dfrac{[\\\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{[\\\\mathrm{~A}]_{\\\\mathrm{t}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{k}=\\\\dfrac{[\\\\mathrm{A}]_{0}-[\\\\mathrm{A}]_{\\\\mathrm{t}}}{\\\\mathrm{t}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{k}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{t}}{2.303} \\\\times \\\\log _{10} \\\\dfrac{[\\\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{[\\\\mathrm{~A}]_{\\\\mathrm{t}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For zero order reactions, $K=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}-[\\mathrm{A}]_{\\mathrm{t}}}{\\mathrm{t}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24010,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'Ammonia and oxygen react at high temperature as $4 \\mathrm{NH}_{3(\\mathrm{~g})}+5 \\mathrm{O}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow 4 \\mathrm{NO}_{(\\mathrm{g})}+6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(\\mathrm{g})}$.<br>If rate of formation of $\\mathrm{NO}_{(\\mathrm{g})}$ is $3.6 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1} \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$, then rate of disappearance of ammonia is','[{"text": "$7.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}} {\\\\text{mol }}{{\\\\text{L}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}{\\\\text{ }}{{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}{\\\\text{mol }}{{\\\\text{L}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}{\\\\text{}}{{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.4 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}{\\\\text{mol }}{{\\\\text{L}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}{\\\\text{}}{{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}{\\\\text{mol }}{{\\\\text{L}}^{{\\\\text{  -  1}}}}{{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{  -  1}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Rate of reaction $=-\\dfrac{1}{4} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$<br>$=-\\dfrac{1}{5} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{NO}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{1}{6} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}$<br>$\\therefore$ Rate of disappearance of $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}=$ Rate of formation of $\\mathrm{NO}$<br>$ = 3.6 \\times {10^{ - 3}}{\\text{mol }}{{\\text{L}}^{ - 1}} {{\\text{s}}^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24012,'chemistry',59,366,679,'The standard electrode potential $\\left(\\mathrm{M}^{3+} / \\mathrm{M}^{2+}\\right)$ for $\\mathrm{V}, \\mathrm{Cr}, \\mathrm{Mn}$ and Co are  $-0.26 \\mathrm{~V},-0.41 \\mathrm{~V},+1.57 \\mathrm{~V}$ and +1.97 V  respectively. The metal ions which can liberate $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from a dilute acid are','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{V}^{2+}$ and $\\\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ and $\\\\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}$ and $\\\\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{V}^{2+}$ and $\\\\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Metals having negative reduction potential can reduce hydrogen from dilute acid.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24013,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'Which of the following represents integrated rate law equation for gas phase first order reaction, $\\mathrm{A}_{(\\mathrm{g})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}_{(\\mathrm{g})}+\\mathrm{C}_{(\\mathrm{g})}$<br>if $\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{i}}=$ Initial pressure of $\\mathrm{A}$ $\\mathrm{P}=$ Total pressure of reaction mixture at time?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{k}=2.303 \\\\times \\\\log _{10} \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{P}_{\\\\mathrm{i}}}{2 \\\\mathrm{P}_{\\\\mathrm{i}}-\\\\mathrm{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{k}=\\\\dfrac{2.303}{\\\\mathrm{t}} \\\\times \\\\log _{10} \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{P}_{\\\\mathrm{i}}}{2 \\\\mathrm{P}_{\\\\mathrm{i}}-\\\\mathrm{P}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{k}=\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\mathrm{t}} \\\\ln \\\\dfrac{2 \\\\mathrm{P}_{\\\\mathrm{i}}-\\\\mathrm{P}}{\\\\mathrm{P}_{\\\\mathrm{i}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{k}=\\\\dfrac{2.303}{\\\\mathrm{t}} \\\\times \\\\log _{10} \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{P}_{\\\\mathrm{i}}-\\\\mathrm{P}}{\\\\mathrm{P}_{\\\\mathrm{i}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {}&{\\mathop {\\text{A}}\\limits_{{\\text{(g)}}} }&{ \\to \\mathop {\\text{B}}\\limits_{{\\text{(g)}}} }&{{\\text{ + }}\\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits_{{\\text{(g)}}} } \\\\ \n  {{\\text{ Initial }}}&{{{\\text{P}}_{\\text{i}}}}&{\\text{ - }}&{\\text{ - }} \\\\ \n  {}&{{{\\text{P}}_{\\text{i}}}{\\text{ - x}}}&{\\text{x}}&{\\text{x}} \n\\end{array}$<br>$\\therefore$ Final total pressure,<br>$\\mathrm{P}=\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{i}}-\\mathrm{x}+\\mathrm{x}+\\mathrm{x}=\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{i}}+\\mathrm{x} \\text { (or) }$<br>$\\mathrm{x}=\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{i}}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{A}}=\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{i}}-\\mathrm{x}=\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{i}}-\\left(\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{i}}\\right)=2 \\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{i}}-\\mathrm{P}$<br>Acc. to first order kinetics,<br>$\\mathrm{K}=\\dfrac{2.303}{\\mathrm{t}} \\log _{10} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{i}}}{\\mathrm{P}_{\\text {final }}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{2.303}{\\mathrm{t}} \\log _{10} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{i}}}{2 \\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{i}}-\\mathrm{P}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24016,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'If decomposition of hydrogen peroxide is a first order reaction, it\'s rate law equation can be represented as','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{r}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{k}}{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\\\right]}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{r}=\\\\mathrm{k}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{r}=\\\\mathrm{k} \\\\dfrac{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right]\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\\\right]^{1 / 2}}{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\\\right]}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{r}=\\\\mathrm{k} \\\\dfrac{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right]}{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\\\right]\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\\\right]^{1 / 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2(1)} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(1)}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}$<br>It is first order reaction (given)<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{r}=\\mathrm{k}\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24019,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'A reaction is first order with respective to $\\mathrm{A}$ and second order with respective to $\\mathrm{B}$. What is the effect on reaction rate if concentration of $\\mathrm{B}$ is increase 3 times?','[{"text": "Rate increases 6 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rate increases 9 times", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Rate increases 2 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rate increases 3 times", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{r  =  k[A][B}}{{\\text{]}}^{\\text{2}}}$ (given condition)<br>If concentration of $\\mathrm{B}$ is increased 3 times<br>$\\mathrm{r}^{\\prime}=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}][3 \\mathrm{~B}]^{2}$<br>$=9 \\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}]^{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{r}^{\\prime}=9 \\mathrm{r}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24022,'chemistry',60,585,1175,'For the reaction $\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow$ product, rate of reaction is $3.6 \\times {10^{ - 2}}{\\text{mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}{\\text{se}}{{\\text{c}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$. When $[{\\text{A}}] = 0.2 {\\text{mol  d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}$  and$[{\\text{B}}] = 0.1 {\\text{mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}$, find rate constant of reaction if it is second order with respective to both reactants.','[{"text": "$18 \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-3} \\\\mathrm{dm}^{9} \\\\mathrm{sec}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90 \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-3} \\\\mathrm{dm}^{9} \\\\mathrm{sec}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$72 \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-3} \\\\mathrm{dm}^{9} \\\\mathrm{sec}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$36 \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-3} \\\\mathrm{dm}^{9} \\\\mathrm{sec}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow$ Products<br>$\\mathrm{r}=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{2}[\\mathrm{~B}]^{2} \\quad$ (given condition)<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{r}}{[\\mathrm{A}]^{2}[\\mathrm{~B}]^{2}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{\\text{3}}{\\text{.6}} \\times {\\text{1}}{{\\text{0}}^{{\\text{ - 2}}}}{\\text{mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}{\\text{se}}{{\\text{c}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}}}{{{{{\\text{[0}}{\\text{.2]}}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 6}}}} \\times {{{\\text{[0}}{\\text{.1]}}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 6}}}}}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{3.6 \\times 10^{-2}}{0.04 \\times 0.01} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-3} \\mathrm{dm}^{9} \\mathrm{sec}^{-1}$<br>$=90 \\mathrm{~mol}^{-3} \\mathrm{dm}^{9} \\mathrm{sec}^{-1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24025,'chemistry',60,587,1180,'In a first order reaction $60 \\%$ of reactant decomposes in $4.606 \\mathrm{~min}$. What is half life of reaction? $\\left(\\mathrm{k}=0.1989 \\mathrm{~min}^{-1}\\right)$','[{"text": "$3.48 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.4 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.0 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.74 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{k}=0.1989 \\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$<br>For first order reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{t}_{1 / 2}=\\dfrac{0.693}{\\mathrm{k}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{0.693}{0.1989 \\mathrm{~min}^{-1}}=3.48 \\mathrm{~min}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24027,'chemistry',59,373,696,'Which one of the following statements is correct for electrolysis of brine solution?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$  is formed at cathode.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ is formed at cathode.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is formed at cathode.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is formed at anode.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Statement 4 is incorrect. $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is formed at cathode .<br>$\\mathrm{NaCl}_{(\\mathrm{aq})} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Na}^{+}+\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$<br>Cathode reaction:$2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+2 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\xrightarrow{\\text { reduction }} \\mathrm{H}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+2 \\mathrm{OH}^{-}$<br>Anode reaction :<br>$2 \\mathrm{Cl}^{-} \\xrightarrow{\\text { reduction }} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+2 \\mathrm{e}^{-}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24029,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'For the reaction $2 \\mathrm{~A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{C}$, rate of disappearance of $\\mathrm{A}$ is ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.076 mol }}{{\\text{s}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$. What is the rate of disappearance of $\\mathrm{B}$ ?','[{"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.076 mol }}{{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.038 mol }}{{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.019 mol }}{{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0}}{\\\\text{.095 mol }}{{\\\\text{s}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{2A + B}} \\to {\\text{2C}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { Rate }=-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{A}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{B}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{C}]}{\\mathrm{dt}} \\\\& \\therefore \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{B}]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}[\\mathrm{A}]}{\\mathrm{dt}} \\\\& =\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 0.076 \\mathrm{mols}^{-1}=0.038 \\mathrm{mols}^{-1}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24032,'chemistry',60,585,1175,'For the reaction $2 \\mathrm{~A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow$ product, rate of the reaction is $15 \\times {10^{ - 2}}{\\text{mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}{\\text{se}}{{\\text{c}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$. When $\\left( {{\\text{[A]  =  0}}{\\text{.3 mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}{\\text{and}}\\,\\,{\\text{[B] = 0}}{\\text{.05 mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}} \\right)$  What is the value of rate constant if reaction is first order in both the reactants?','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\text{r  =  k[A][B]}}$<br>( $\\because$ Reaction is first order in both the reactants) rate $=15 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{~mol} \\cdot \\mathrm{dm}^{-3} \\mathrm{sec}^{-}$;<br>$\\begin{aligned}& {[\\mathrm{A}]=0.3 \\mathrm{~mol} \\cdot \\mathrm{dm}^{-3}} \\\\& {[\\mathrm{~B}]=0.05 \\mathrm{~mol} \\cdot \\mathrm{dm}^{-3}}\\end{aligned}$<br>or, $\\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{r}}{[\\mathrm{A}][\\mathrm{B}]}=\\dfrac{15 \\times 10^{-2}}{0.3 \\times 0.05}=10$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24033,'chemistry',59,373,697,'A current of 3 A is passed through a molten calcium salt for 1 hr 47 min 13 sec . The mass of calcium deposited is:<br>(Molar mass of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ca}=40 \\,\\mathrm{g\\,mol}^{-1}}}$ )','[{"text": "6.0 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.0 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8.0 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.0 g", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Equivalent mass of \' Ca \' ${\\mathrm{=20}}$ gram<br>${\\mathrm{1 \\mathrm{hr} 47 \\mathrm{~min} 13 \\,\\mathrm{sec}=6433 \\,\\mathrm{sec}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{w}=\\dfrac{\\text { Eit }}{96500}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{20 \\times 3 \\times 6433}{96500}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{=3.999 \\approx 4}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24035,'chemistry',60,587,1181,'${{\\text{C}}_{{\\text{12}}}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{22}}}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{11}}}}{\\text{ + }}\\mathop {{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ (excess) }}}  \\to \\mathop {{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{6}}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{12}}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{6}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ (glucose) }}} {\\text{ + }}\\mathop {{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{6}}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{12}}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{6}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ (fructose) }}} $ Rate law is expressed as','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{r}=\\\\mathrm{k}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{C}_{12} \\\\mathrm{H}_{22} \\\\mathrm{O}_{11}\\\\right]\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{r}=\\\\mathrm{k}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{C}_{12} \\\\mathrm{H}_{22} \\\\mathrm{O}_{11}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{r}=\\\\mathrm{k}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{r}=\\\\mathrm{k}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{C}_{12} \\\\mathrm{H}_{22} \\\\mathrm{O}_{11}\\\\right]\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right]^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ester hydrolysis in acid medium is of first order. Here rate does not depend on water as it is in excess quantity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24036,'chemistry',59,372,694,'The value of \' ${\\mathrm{A}}$ \' in the equation ${\\mathrm{\\lambda_{m}=\\lambda_{m}^{0}-A \\sqrt{C}}}$ is same for the pair','[{"text": "NaCl and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MgSO}_{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "NaCl and KBr", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}}}$ and NaCl", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaCl}, \\mathrm{KBr}}}$ produce same no. of ions.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24038,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'On increasing the pressure three fold, the rate of reaction of $2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow$ products, would increase','[{"text": "3 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "27 times", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow$ products<br>Rate  ${\\text{ =  p}}_{{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ s}}}^{\\text{2}} \\cdot {{\\text{p}}_{{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}}}$<br>$=\\left[3 \\mathrm{p}_{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}}\\right]^{2}\\left(3 \\mathrm{p}_{2}\\right)=27\\left[\\mathrm{p}_{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}}\\right]\\left(\\mathrm{p}_{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}\\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24039,'chemistry',59,373,697,'Mass in grams of copper deposited by passing 9.6487 A current through a voltmeter containing copper sulphate solution for 100 seconds is<br>(Given : Molar mass of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}: 63 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}, 1 \\mathrm{~F}=}}$ 96487 C)','[{"text": "3.15 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.315 g", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "31.5 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.0315 g", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From Faraday\'s first law,<br>Mass of Cu deposited ${\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{w})=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{M} \\times \\mathrm{i} \\times \\mathrm{t}}{\\mathrm{nF}}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{63 \\times 9.6487 \\times 100}{2 \\times 96487}=0.315 \\mathrm{~g}}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24041,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'The value of rate constant for a first order is $2.303 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{sec}^{-1}$. What will be the time required to reduce the concentration to $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ th of the initial concentration?','[{"text": "$100 \\\\mathrm{sec}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10 \\\\mathrm{sec}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2303 \\\\mathrm{sec}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$23.03 \\\\mathrm{sec}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{t}=\\dfrac{2.303}{\\mathrm{k}} \\log \\dfrac{\\mathrm{a}}{\\mathrm{a}-\\mathrm{x}}$<br>$\\mathrm{t}=\\dfrac{2.303}{2.303 \\times 10^{-2}} \\log \\dfrac{\\mathrm{x}}{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{x}}{10}}=100 \\log 10=100 \\mathrm{sec}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24042,'chemistry',59,373,697,'Match Column I (Conversion) with Column II (Number of Faraday required).<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A.${\\rm{1 mol}}\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{{\\rm{2}}\\,}}{\\rm{O}}\\,\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>B.${\\rm{1 mol}}\\,{\\rm{MnO}}_4^ - \\,\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,\\,{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}$<br>C.$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.5 \\,mol}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{Ca}}\\,{\\rm{from}}\\,\\,\\\\\n{\\rm{molten}}\\,{\\rm{CaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}\n\\end{array}$<br>D.${\\rm{1 mol}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{FeO}}\\,\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}$</div><div class="column-right">P.3F<br>Q.2F<br>R.1F<br>S.5F<br></div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A }} - {\\\\rm{ Q; B }} - {\\\\rm{ S; C }} - {\\\\rm{P; D }} - {\\\\rm{ R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A }} - {\\\\rm{R; }}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B }} - {\\\\rm{ S; C}} - {\\\\rm{ P;  D}} - {\\\\rm{ Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A }} - {\\\\rm{ Q; B }} - {\\\\rm{ R; C }} - {\\\\rm{P; D }} - {\\\\rm{ S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A }} - {\\\\rm{R; }}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B }} - {\\\\rm{ S; C}} - {\\\\rm{ Q;  D}} - {\\\\rm{ P}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\mathrm{4 \\mathrm{OH}^{-} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{O}_{2}+4 \\mathrm{e}^{-}}}$<br>for 2 mole of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}, 4 \\mathrm{~F}}}$ is required<br>for 1 mole of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}=\\dfrac{4 \\mathrm{~F}}{2}=2 \\mathrm{~F}}}$ is required<br>$\\mathop {{\\rm{Mn}}{{\\rm{O}}_4}^ - }\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 7}}}  \\to \\mathop {{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 2}}} $<br>For 1 mole ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}, 5 \\mathrm{~F}}}$ charge is required<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+} \\xrightarrow{+2 e^{-}} \\mathrm{Ca}}}$<br>${\\rm{For 1}}\\,{\\rm{mole}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{a}}^{2 + }}\\,{\\rm{ion}}\\,{\\rm{ = }}\\,{\\rm{2\\,F}}\\,{\\rm{is}}\\,{\\rm{required}}$<br>${\\mathrm{1.5 \\,\\mathrm{~mole} \\,\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}}}$ ion ${\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{2}{1} \\times 1.5=3 \\mathrm{~F}}}$ is required<br>$\\mathop {{\\rm{FeO}}}\\limits^{^{2 + }}  \\to \\mathop {{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}_2}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}}\\limits^{3 + } $<br>for ${\\mathrm{1 \\mathrm{~mole}\\, \\mathrm{FeO}, 1 \\mathrm{~F}}}$ is required.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24044,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'${\\text{k}}$ \' represents the rate constant of a reaction when $\\log \\mathrm{k}$ is plotted against $1 / \\mathrm{T}$<br>$(\\mathrm{T}=$ temperature) the graph obtained is a','[{"text": "Curve", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A straight line with a constant positive slope", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A straight line with a constant negative slope", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A straight line with no slope", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/60_24044_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24045,'chemistry',59,365,675,'Which occurs at the anode of any voltaic cell?<br> P.     A metal is deposited on electrode.<br>Q.  A substance undergoes oxidation.<br>R.  Positive ions are deposited from the solution.','[{"text": "Only P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "P and Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "P and R", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At anode, oxidation takes place.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24047,'chemistry',60,585,1176,'For a reaction, $\\mathrm{I}^{-}+\\mathrm{OCl}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{IO}^{-}+\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$in an aqueous medium, the rate of reaction is given by<br>$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}\\left[\\mathrm{IO}^{-}\\right]}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{k}\\left[\\mathrm{I}^{-}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{OCl}^{-}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]}$<br>The overall order of reaction is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Rate = $\\frac{{{\\text{k}}\\left[ {{{\\text{I}}^{\\text{ - }}}} \\right]\\left[ {{\\text{OC}}{{\\text{l}}^{\\text{ - }}}} \\right]}}{{\\left[ {{\\text{O}}{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ - }}}} \\right]}}{\\text{  =  k}}\\left[ {{{\\text{I}}^{\\text{ - }}}} \\right]\\left[ {{\\text{OC}}{{\\text{l}}^{\\text{ - }}}} \\right]{\\left[ {{\\text{O}}{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ - }}}} \\right]^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24048,'chemistry',59,366,679,'Red hot carbon will remove oxygen from the oxides AO and BO but not from MO , while B will remove oxygen from AO. The activity of metals $\\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{B}$ and M in decreasing order is','[{"text": "A $>$ B $>$ M", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B $>$ A $>$ M", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{M}>\\\\mathrm{B}>\\\\mathrm{A}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{M}>$ A $>$ B", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As carbon can reduce AO, BO but not MO. It means \' M \' is more reactive than \' A \' and \' B \', Similarly as \' B \' can reduce AO, \' B \' is more reactive than A.<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Reactivity order $\\mathrm{M}>\\mathrm{B}>\\mathrm{A}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24050,'chemistry',60,586,1177,'In the sequence of reaction<br>${\\text{A}}\\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}}}{\\text{ B}}\\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{2}}}}}{\\text{C}}\\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{3}}}}}{\\text{D; }}{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ >  }}{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ >  }}{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}$<br>Then the rate determining step of the reaction is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{B}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{D}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{B} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{D}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Slowest step is the rate determining step for which \' $\\mathrm{k}_{1}$ \' is least.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24051,'chemistry',59,366,679,'The $\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{M}^{3+} / \\mathrm{M}^{2+}}^{\\circ}$ values for $\\mathrm{Cr}, \\mathrm{Mn}, \\mathrm{Fe}$ and Co are $-0.41,+1.57,+0.77$ and +1.97 , respectively. For which one of these metals the change in oxidation state from +2 to +3 is easiest?','[{"text": "Cr", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mn", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fe", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Co", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'RP of \' Cr \' is minimum means oxidation potential of Cr is maximum,<br>(i.e., 0.41 V ) so it can be easily oxidized from <br>+2 to +3 .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24053,'chemistry',60,587,1180,'The rate equation for a reaction, ${\\text{A}} \\to {\\text{B}}$  is $\\mathrm{r}=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{\\circ}$. If the initial concentration of the reactant is \'${\\text{a}}$\' ${\\text{mol d}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{ - 3}}}}$, the half period of the reaction is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{k}}{\\\\mathrm{a}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{a}}{\\\\mathrm{k}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\mathrm{a}}{\\\\mathrm{k}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{a}}{2 \\\\mathrm{k}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to the rate equation given, the reaction is of zero order reaction,<br>for zero order reactions, ${{\\text{t}}_{{\\text{1/2}}}}{\\text{ = }}\\frac{{\\text{a}}}{{{\\text{2k}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24054,'chemistry',59,366,679,'Given that, $\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+} / \\mathrm{Fe}}^{\\circ}=-0.44 \\mathrm{~V}$;<br>${\\rm{E}}{^\\circ _{{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{3 + }}/{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{2 + }}}} = 0.77\\,{\\rm{V}}$. If $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and Fe solid are kept together then','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+} / \\\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ remains unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ decreases", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'SRP of $\\mathrm{Fe}^{+2} / \\mathrm{Fe}$ is –ve , so reduction of $\\mathrm{Fe}^{+2}$ to Fe is non - spontaneous. SRP of $\\mathrm{Fe}^{+3} / \\mathrm{Fe}^{+2}$ is + ve, so reduction of $\\mathrm{Fe}^{+3}$ to $\\mathrm{Fe}^{+2}$ is spontaneous. When $\\mathrm{Fe}^{+3}$ and solid Fe are kept together $\\mathrm{Fe}^{+3}$ changes to $\\mathrm{Fe}^{+2}$. $\\mathrm{So}, \\mathrm{Fe}^{+3}$ concentration decreases and $\\mathrm{Fe}^{+2}$ conc. increases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24056,'chemistry',59,368,681,'Given that, $\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}+8 \\mathrm{H}^{+}+5 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow$<br>${\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{2 + }} + 4{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}\\,;{\\rm{E}}^\\circ  = 1.51\\,{\\rm{V}}$<br>${\\rm{Mn}}{{\\rm{O}}_2} + 4{{\\rm{H}}^ + } + 2{{\\rm{e}}^ - } \\to $<br>${\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{2 + }} + 2{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}\\,;{\\rm{E}}^\\circ  = 1.23\\,{\\rm{V}}$<br>${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{MnO}}_4^ - /{\\rm{Mn}}{{\\rm{O}}_2}}}$  is','[{"text": "1.70 V", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.91 V", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.37 V", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.548 V", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We need to use $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}=\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}{ }_{1}+\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}{ }_{2}$ and $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}=-\\mathrm{nFE}^{\\circ}{ }_{\\text {cell }}$ principles here.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/59_24056_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\therefore(1 \\cdot 51 \\mathrm{~V}) 5=3 \\mathrm{x}+[2(1 \\cdot 23 \\mathrm{~V})]$<br>$\\mathrm{x}=1 \\cdot 70 \\mathrm{~V}$<br>[Note: In the expression \' F \' is not considered]',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(24057,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'For the first order reaction half-life is $14 \\mathrm{sec}$, the time required for the initial concentration to reduce to $1 / 8$ of its value is:','[{"text": "${(14)^3}{\\\\text{ sec}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$28\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{sec}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$42\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{sec}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${(14)^2}{\\\\text{ sec}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${{\\text{t}}_{{\\text{1/2}}}}{\\text{ =  14sec, N = }}\\frac{{{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{0}}}}}{{\\text{8}}}$<br>$\\frac{{\\text{N}}}{{{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{o}}}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{\\text{n}}}$<br>$\\frac{{\\text{N}}}{{8\\,{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{o}}}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{\\text{n}}} \\Rightarrow {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^3} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{\\text{n}}} \\Rightarrow {\\text{n}} = 3$<br>$\\mathrm{t}=\\mathrm{nt}_{1 / 2}=3 \\times 14=42 \\mathrm{sec}$<br>(or) $1-\\dfrac{1}{2}-\\dfrac{1}{4}-\\dfrac{1}{8} ; 3$ half-lives $3 \\times 14 = 42\\,\\,{\\text{sec}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24059,'chemistry',59,370,692,'Which of the following expressions correctly represents the equivalent conductance at infinite dilution of $\\mathrm{Al}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}$. Given that $\\Lambda {^\\circ _{{\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}}}$ and $\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}}^{\\circ}$ are the equivalent conductance at infinite dilution of the respective ions?','[{"text": "$2\\\\Lambda {^\\\\circ _{{\\\\rm{A}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{3 + }}}} + 3\\\\Lambda {^\\\\circ _{{\\\\rm{SO}}_4^{2 - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Lambda {^\\\\circ _{{\\\\rm{A}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{3 + }}}} + \\\\Lambda {^\\\\circ _{{\\\\rm{SO}}_4^{2 - }}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {\\\\Lambda {^\\\\circ _{{\\\\rm{A}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{3 + }}}} + \\\\Lambda {^\\\\circ _{{\\\\rm{SO}}_4^{2 - }}}} \\\\right) \\\\times 6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{3}\\\\Lambda {^\\\\circ _{{\\\\rm{A}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{3 + }}}} + \\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\Lambda {^\\\\circ _{{\\\\rm{SO}}_4^{2 - }}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Lambda_{\\mathrm{eq}}^{\\circ}\\left(\\mathrm{Al}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}\\right)=\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{eq}}^{\\circ}\\left(\\mathrm{Al}^{3+}\\right)+\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{eq}}^{\\circ}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\\right) \\\\& =\\Lambda^{\\circ}\\left(\\mathrm{Al}^{+3}\\right)+\\Lambda^{\\circ}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\\right)\\end{aligned}$<br>e.q. conductance dose not depend on no. of ions in compound',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24060,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'$\\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}$ is a first order reaction. The initial concentration of ${\\text{A}}$ is $0.2 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}$. After $10 \\mathrm{~min}$, the concentration of ${\\text{B}}$ is found to be $0.18 \\mathrm{~mol}$ $\\mathrm{L}^{-1}$. The rate constant (in $\\min ^{-1}$ ) for the reaction is:','[{"text": "0.2303", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.303", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.693", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.01", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{2.303}{\\mathrm{t}} \\log \\dfrac{\\mathrm{a}}{(\\mathrm{a}-\\mathrm{x})}$<br>$\\mathrm{a}=0.2 ;(\\mathrm{a}-\\mathrm{x})=0.02$<br>$\\mathrm{k}=\\dfrac{2.303}{10} \\log \\dfrac{0.2}{0.02}=\\dfrac{2.303}{10} \\log 10=0.2303$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24062,'chemistry',59,373,697,'How many electrons flow when a current of 5 amperes is passed through a conductor for 200 seconds?','[{"text": "$6.241 \\\\times 10^{21}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.0241 \\\\times 10^{21}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.241 \\\\times 10^{22}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.0241 \\\\times 10^{22}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'q = it<br>$\\mathrm{q}=5 \\times 200=1000$ coulombs<br>96500 coulombs $=6 \\cdot 023 \\times 10^{23} \\mathrm{e}^{-}$<br>1000 coulombs $=$ ?<br>$=\\dfrac{6 \\cdot 023 \\times 10^{23} \\times 1000}{96500}$<br>$=6 \\cdot 241 \\times 10^{23}$ electrons',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24064,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'The rate constant for a first order reaction at $300^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ for which $\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}$ is $35 \\mathrm{Kcal}^{\\mathrm{mol}^{-1}}$ and frequency constant \' $A$ \' is $1.45 \\times 10^{11} \\mathrm{sec}^{-1}$ is:','[{"text": "$10 \\\\times 10^{-2} \\\\mathrm{~S}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.37 \\\\times 10^{10} \\\\mathrm{~S}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times 10^{-4} \\\\mathrm{~S}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.94 \\\\times 10^{-3} \\\\mathrm{~S}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{k}=\\mathrm{Ae}^{-\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}} / \\mathrm{RT}}$<br>$\\log \\mathrm{k}=\\log \\left(1.45 \\times 10^{11}\\right)-\\dfrac{35 \\times 10^{3}}{2 \\times 2.303 \\times 573}$<br>$\\left[ {\\because {\\text{R}} = 2\\,\\,{\\text{cal}}\\,\\,{\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{ - 1}}{{\\text{k}}^{ - 1}}} \\right]$<br>$\\log \\mathrm{k}=11.16-13.26=-2.1$<br>Taking antilog, $\\mathrm{k}=7.94 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~S}^{-1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24065,'chemistry',59,373,697,'A certain current liberated 0.504 g of hydrogen gas in 2 hours. How many grams of copper can be deposited by the same current flowing for the same time in copper sulphate solution?','[{"text": "31.8 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16.0 g", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "12.7 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "63.5 g", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Faraday\'s second low,<br>$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{W}_{1}}{\\mathrm{~W}_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{E}_{1}}{\\mathrm{E}_{2}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{0 \\cdot 504}{\\mathrm{~W}_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\dfrac{63.5}{2}}$<br>$\\mathrm{W}_{2}=16 \\cdot 0$ gram',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24067,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'According to Arrhenius hypothesis, rate of reaction increases with:','[{"text": "rise in temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "decrease in temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "rise in pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "decrease in pressure", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Arrhenius theory, with rise in temperature the rate of reaction also increases as the number of activated reactant molecules increase.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24068,'chemistry',59,373,697,'The mass of metal deposited by the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of metal ions increases in direct proportion to which property?<br>(P) Electrolysis current<br>(Q) Electrolysis time<br>(R) Metal ion charge','[{"text": "P only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "R only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "P and Q only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "P, Q and R", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'m α Q or m α  it<br>So, when amount of current (i) and its passage time (t) increase, more deposition of metal takes place.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24070,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'A reaction having equal energies of activation for forward and reverse reaction has:','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{G}=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}=0$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{H}=\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{G}=\\\\Delta \\\\mathrm{S}=0$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Energy  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/60_24070_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  When Ea=Eb,<br>$\\Delta {\\text{H  =  0}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24071,'chemistry',59,373,696,'Which products are formed by the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of $\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$ ?<br>(P) $\\mathrm{Al}(\\mathrm{s})$<br>(Q) $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$<br>(R) $\\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$<br>(S) $\\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$','[{"text": "P and R only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "P and S only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Q and R only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Q and S only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'aq. $\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$, produces $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas at cathode and $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ gas at anode.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24073,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'The rate constant of a first order reaction at $27^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ is $10^{-3} \\mathrm{~min}^{-1}$. The rate constant (in $\\min ^{-1}$ ) at $17^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ for this reaction is','[{"text": "$10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Temperature Coefficient,<br>$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{k}_{\\mathrm{t}}+10}{\\mathrm{k}_{\\mathrm{t}}}=2($ in general $)$<br>$2=\\dfrac{10^{-3}}{\\mathrm{~K}_{\\mathrm{t}}} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{k}_{\\mathrm{t}}=\\dfrac{10^{-3}}{2}=5 \\times 10^{-4}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24076,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'Chemical reaction with high ${{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}}$ values are generally','[{"text": "Very fast", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Very slow", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Moderately fast", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Spontaneous", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Reaction having high ${{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}}$  are slow reactions.<br>If $\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}$ is high, then the number of effective collisions will be less and the reaction will takes place slowly.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24077,'chemistry',59,374,701,'The most common dry cell is the Leclanche cell. It consists of a carbon (graphite) rod as cathode which is surrounded by powdered ____ and carbon black.','[{"text": "MnO", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mn}_{3} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The cathode is surrounded by a paste of manganese dioxide $\\left(\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}\\right)$, ammonium chloride and zinc chloride.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24079,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'The activation energy of a reaction at a given temperature is found to be ${\\text{2}}{\\text{.303 RT J mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$. The ratio of rate constant $(\\mathrm{k})$ to the arrhenius factor (A) is:','[{"text": "0.01", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.02", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.001", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{k}=\\mathrm{Ae}^{-\\mathrm{Ea} / \\mathrm{RT}}$<br>$\\log \\mathrm{k}=\\log \\mathrm{A}-\\dfrac{\\mathrm{Ea}}{\\mathrm{RT} \\times 2.303}$<br>$\\log \\mathrm{k}-\\log \\mathrm{A}=\\dfrac{-\\mathrm{Ea}}{2.303 \\mathrm{RT}}$<br>$\\log \\dfrac{\\mathrm{k}}{\\mathrm{A}}=\\dfrac{-\\mathrm{Ea}}{2.303 \\mathrm{RT}}=\\dfrac{-2.303 \\mathrm{RT}}{2.303 \\mathrm{RT}}$<br>$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{k}}{\\mathrm{A}}=\\operatorname{anti} \\log -1=1 \\times 10^{-1}=0.1$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24080,'chemistry',59,374,701,'Read Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct option given below.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Sodium ions are discharged in preference to hydrogen ions at a mercury cathode.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The nature of the cathode can affect the order of discharge of ions.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The nature of the cathode can affect the order of discharge of ions, that\'s why $\\mathrm{Na}^{+}$ions are discharged at mercury in preference to $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$ ions.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24082,'chemistry',60,587,1181,'In the reaction $\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow$ Products, if $\\mathrm{B}$ is taken in excess, then it is an example of','[{"text": "Second order reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero order reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fractional order reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First order reaction", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Excess substance is not considered in rate law. Here, rate $=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{1}[\\mathrm{~B}]^{0}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24083,'chemistry',59,366,679,'Match Column I with Column II and choose the correct combination from the options given.<br>Given \\(\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+} / \\mathrm{Cu}}=0.34 \\mathrm{~V}\\),<br>\\(\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} / \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}}^{\\circ}=1.36 \\mathrm{~V}\\),<br>\\(\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{Br}_{2} / \\mathrm{Br}^{-}}^{\\circ}=1.08 \\mathrm{~V}\\),<br>\\(\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{I}_{2} / \\mathrm{I}^{-}}^{\\circ}=0.54 \\mathrm{~V}\\),<br><div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - R,\\tB - P,\\tC - S,\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q,\\tB - P,\\tC - S,\\tD - R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - P,\\tB - R,\\tC - Q,\\tD - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S,\\tB - P,\\tC - R,\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'(A-Q) ${\\rm{E}}{^\\circ _{{\\rm{red}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}/{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}^ - }}}\\, > \\,\\,{\\rm{E}}{^\\circ _{{\\rm{red}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{u}}^{2 + }}/{\\rm{Cu}}}}$<br>So reduction of $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ to $\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$will occur at cathode and oxidation of Cu to $\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ will occur at anode.So reverse reaction is possible. It means such reverse reaction can be made to occur in an electrolysis cell.<br>(B-P) As mentioned in (A), the reaction is feasible and hence can produce electricity in the galvanic cell.<br>(C-S) $2 \\mathrm{I}^{-}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{I}_{2}$ (violet) $+2 \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$<br>Since $\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {red of } \\mathrm{Cl}_{2} / \\mathrm{Cl}^{+}}^{\\circ}>\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {red of } \\mathrm{I}_{2} / \\mathrm{I}^{-}}^{\\circ}$<br>(D-R) $2 \\mathrm{Br}^{-}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ (brown) $+2 \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$<br>Since ${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{red}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}/{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}^ - }}} > {{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{red}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}/{\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{r}}^ - }}}$',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/59_24083_question_1.png'),
(24085,'chemistry',60,585,1176,'The rate constant of a reaction is ${\\text{175}}\\,\\,{\\text{li}}{{\\text{t}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{{\\text{ - 2}}}}{\\text{ se}}{{\\text{c}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$. What is the order of reaction?','[{"text": "First", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Second", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Third", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From units of rate constant, the reaction is third order. \' ${\\text{k}}$ \' units $\\Rightarrow$ lit $^{\\mathrm{n}-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{1-\\mathrm{n}} \\mathrm{sec}^{-}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24086,'chemistry',59,366,679,'Match the columns on the basis of data given below:<br>\\[{\\rm{E}}_{{{\\rm{F}}_2}/{{\\rm{F}}^ - }}^\\Theta = 2.87\\;{\\rm{V}},\\;\\;\\;{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{L}}{{\\rm{i}}^ + }/{\\rm{Li}}}^\\Theta = - 3.5\\;{\\rm{V}}\\]<br>\\[{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{u}}^{3 + }}/{\\rm{Au}}}^\\Theta = 1.4\\;{\\rm{V}},\\;\\;\\;{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{r}}_2}/{\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{r}}^ - }}^\\Theta = 1.09\\;{\\rm{V}}\\]<br><div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - R,B - P, C - V, D - T, E - S,F - Q,G - U", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - P, B - Q,C - R, D - S,E - T,F - U,G - V", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,B - P, C - Q,D - S,E - T,F - V,G - U", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - V,B - U,C - T, D - S,E - R,F - Q,G - P", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Higher SRP, stronger oxidizing agent<br>Lowest SRP, stronger reducing agent<br>$\\mathrm{F}_{2}$ - Non metal, strong oxidizing agent.<br>Li - Strongest reducing metal.<br>$\\mathrm{Au}^{+3}$ - Oxidizing agent, metal ion.<br>$\\mathrm{Br}^{-}$- Oxidized by $\\mathrm{Au}^{+3}$<br>Au - Unreactive metal (Noble metal)<br>$\\mathrm{Li}^{-}$- Weakest oxidizing metal ion.<br>$\\mathrm{F}^{-}-$Weakest reducing anion.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/59_24086_question_1.png'),
(24088,'chemistry',63,805,1731,'I : As, Sb, Bi are found mainly as sulphide minerals.<br>II : Phosphoproteins are present in milk and eggs.<br>Which of the above statement(s) is/are correct?','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neither I nor II", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{As}, \\mathrm{Sb}, \\mathrm{Bi}$ are found mainly in sulphide minerals, Phosphoproteins are present in milk and eggs.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24089,'chemistry',60,585,1176,'What is the order of a reaction which has a rate expression: rate $=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{3 / 2}[\\mathrm{~B}]^{-1}$','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'order $=\\dfrac{3}{2}-1=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ (order is sum of the powers of conc. terms in rate law).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
