(24109,'chemistry',60,587,1182,'Consider the following statements<br>The rate law for the acid catalysed hydrolysis of an ester being given is-<br>rate${\\text{ =  k}}\\left[ {{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ + }}}} \\right]\\,{\\text{[ester ]  =  k\' [ester ]}}$, if the acid concentration is doubled at constant ester concentration.<br>I. The second order rate constant, $\\mathrm{k}$ is doubled<br>II. The pseudo first order rate constant, ${\\text{k\'}}$ is doubled<br>III. The rate of the reaction is doubled<br>Which of the above statements are correct?','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As rate of reaction is doubled then pseudo first order rate constant is doubled as well. It may be noted that in presence of acid hydrolysis of ethyl acetate, a pseudo-unimolecular reaction by the actual value of $\\mathrm{k}$ depends upon the concentration of $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$ions, otherwise rate constant of a reaction is constant at constant temperature.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24110,'chemistry',59,373,697,'A current is passed through 2 voltmeters connected in series. The first voltmeter contains $\\mathrm{XSO}_{4}(\\mathrm{aq})$ and second has $\\mathrm{Y}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}(\\mathrm{aq})$. The relative atomic masses of X and Y are in the ratio of 2: 1 . The ratio of the mass of X  liberated to the mass of Y liberated is','[{"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "none of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{X}^{+2}+2 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{X} ; \\mathrm{Y}^{+}+\\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Y}$<br>So, 2 mole $\\mathrm{e}^{-}$produce 1 mole ${\\rm{X}} = \'1 \\times {\\rm{M}}\'{\\rm{g}}$ <br>and 2 mole $\\mathrm{e}^{-}$ produce 2 mole $\\mathrm{Y}=2 \\times \\dfrac{\\mathrm{M}}{2} \\mathrm{~g}$<br>Hence, ratio of the masses of $\\mathrm{X}: \\mathrm{Y}=1: 1$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24112,'chemistry',63,807,1747,'In the preparation of $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$, we get $\\mathrm{NO}$ gas by catalytic oxidation of ammonia. The moles of $\\mathrm{NO}$ produced by the oxidation of two moles of $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ will be','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}{{\\text{4N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ + 5}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}}&{\\xrightarrow{{}}}&{{\\text{4NO + 6}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}} \\\\ {{\\text{4}}\\,\\,{\\text{moles}}}&{}&{{\\text{4}}\\,\\,{\\text{moles}}} \\\\ {{\\text{Given}}\\,\\,{\\text{:}}\\,\\,{\\text{2}}\\,\\,{\\text{moles}}}&{}&{{\\text{2}}\\,\\,{\\text{moles}}} \\end{array}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
