(24114,'chemistry',59,373,697,'Solutions of $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}, \\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}$ and $\\mathrm{AuCl}_{3}$ are electrolyzed in the apparatus depicted. If the electrolysis is stopped before any of the ions are deposited completely, how do the number of moles ( n ) of $\\mathrm{Ag}, \\mathrm{Cu}$ and Au deposited compare?<br>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{n}_{\\\\mathrm{Ag}}=\\\\mathrm{n}_{\\\\mathrm{Cu}}=\\\\mathrm{n}_{\\\\mathrm{Au}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{n}_{\\\\mathrm{Ag}} < \\\\mathrm{n}_{\\\\mathrm{Cu}} < \\\\mathrm{n}_{\\\\mathrm{Au}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{n}_{\\\\mathrm{Ag}}>\\\\mathrm{n}_{\\\\mathrm{Cu}}>\\\\mathrm{n}_{\\\\mathrm{Au}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{n}_{\\\\mathrm{Ag}}=\\\\mathrm{n}_{\\\\mathrm{Cu}}>\\\\mathrm{n}_{\\\\mathrm{Au}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}+\\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Ag}$<br>$\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+2 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}$<br>$\\mathrm{Au}^{3+}+3 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Au}$<br>So, for a fixed amount of charge flow, deposition of Ag will be maximum and Au will be minimum, because of charge required per mole deposition is minimum in silver and maximum in gold.<br>So, orders of deposition $\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{Ag}}>\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{Cu}}>\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{Au}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/59_24114_question_1.png'),
(24116,'chemistry',63,807,1747,'The correct statements about $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ are<br>I. $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ acts as a dehydrating agent<br>II. The proteins are converted into xanthoproteins<br>III. $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ acts as an oxidising agent<br>IV. It exists in two canonical forms<br>The correct option is','[{"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ is an oxidising not dehydrating agent.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24117,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'At $300 \\mathrm{~K}$, rate constant for $\\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow$ product at $\\mathrm{t}=50 \\mathrm{~min}$ is $0.02 \\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$, then rate constant at $\\mathrm{t}=$ $75 \\mathrm{~min}$ and $310 \\mathrm{~K}$ will be (in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ )','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{0.04}{25}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.04 \\\\times 25$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.04", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{0.02}{25}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At $\\mathrm{t}=50 \\mathrm{sec}, 300 \\mathrm{~K}, \\mathrm{k}=0.02 \\mathrm{sec}^{-}$<br>$\\mathrm{t}=75 \\mathrm{sec}, 310 \\mathrm{~K}, \\mathrm{k}=$ ?<br>Rate constant does not change with time but changes with temperature for every $10^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ rise in temperature, rate constant doubles (in general). So, $2 \\times 0.02=0.04 \\mathrm{sec}^{-}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24119,'chemistry',59,373,696,'In the electrolysis of $\\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ solution (aq) with Cu electrodes, the weight of cathode increased by 3.2 g . In the anode,','[{"text": "0.05 mole of $\\\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ will go into the solution", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "560 ml of $\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ will liberate.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "112 ml of $\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ will liberate.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.2 mole of $\\\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ will go into the solution", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Products of electrolysis of $\\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$, using copper electrodes:<br>At anode: $\\mathrm{Cu} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+2 \\mathrm{e}^{-}$<br>At cathode: $\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+2 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}$<br>If 3.2 g Cu deposited at cathode, $3.2\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{u}}^{ + 2}}$ will go into the solution.<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Moles of $\\mathrm{Cu}, \\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{Cu}}=\\dfrac{3.2}{63.5}=0.05$ mole<br>$\\therefore 0.05\\,{\\rm{mole}}\\,\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{u}}^{2 + }}$ will go into the solution.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24120,'chemistry',63,807,1747,'Nitric acid (conc.) oxidises phosphorus to :','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{P}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{PO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{4} \\\\mathrm{P}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{P}_{4}+20 \\mathrm{HNO}_{3} \\rightarrow 4 \\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}+20 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}+4 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24121,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'For the reaction system<br>$2 \\mathrm{NO}(\\mathrm{g})+\\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$, volume is suddenly reduced to half of its value by increasing the pressure on it. If the reaction is of first order with respect to $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and second order with respect to $\\mathrm{NO,}$ then the rate of reaction will','[{"text": "Diminish to one - eight of its initial value", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase to eight times of its initial value", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increase to four times of its initial value", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Diminish to one fourth of its initial value", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When volume decreases, conc. increases. Here volume is made to half. So conc. becomes double. Overall order is 3 , so rate becomes<br>$(2)^{3}=8$ times',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24123,'chemistry',59,366,679,'According to international convention, standard reduction potentials are now called standard electrode potentials. With the decrease of standard electrode potential,<br>(I) The oxidizing power of the species on the left side of the reaction decreases<br>(II) The reducing power of the species on the right-hand side of the reaction increases<br>(III) The oxidizing power of the species on the left side of the reaction increases<br>(IV) The reducing power of the species on the right side of the reaction decreases','[{"text": "(III) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (II)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(I) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Oxidized form $+\\mathrm{ne}^{-} \\rightarrow$ Reduced form With decrease of $\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}$, the tendency to undergo forward reaction decreases while the tendency to undergo backward reaction increases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
