(24127,'chemistry',59,365,676,'By electromotive force we mean','[{"text": "The potential difference of a cell measured when there is a flow of current from the anode to the cathode and the cell is operating irreversibly.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The potential difference of a cell measured when there is a flow of current from the cathode to the anode and the cell is operating reversibly.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The potential difference of a cell when there is no flow of current and the cell is operating irreversibly", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The potential difference of a cell when there is no flow of current and the cell is operating reversibly<br>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The potential difference of a cell when there is no flow of current and the cell is operating reversibly is the EMF.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24128,'chemistry',63,807,1746,'Which one of the following properties is not shown by NO?','[{"text": "Its bond order is 2.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is diamagnetic in gaseous state", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It combines with oxygen to form nitrogen dioxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is a neutral oxide", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The electronic configuration of ${\\rm{NO}}\\left[ {{\\rm{15}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ - }}}} \\right]$ is given by<br>${\\rm{\\sigma 1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{,}}{{\\rm{\\sigma }}^{\\rm{*}}}{\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{,\\sigma 2}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{,}}{{\\rm{\\sigma }}^{\\rm{*}}}{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{,\\sigma 2p}}_{\\rm{z}}^{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{,\\pi 2p}}_{\\rm{x}}^{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{ = \\pi 2p}}_{\\rm{y}}^{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{,}}{{\\rm{\\pi }}^{\\rm{*}}}{\\rm{2p}}_{\\rm{z}}^{\\rm{1}}$<br>Thus, NO possess one unpaired electron hence it is paramagnetic in nature. However, it dimerises at low temperature to become diamagnetic.<br>${\\rm{2NO}} \\to {{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>Bond order of ${\\rm{NO = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{[10 - 5] = 2}}{\\rm{.5}}$<br>Thus, bond order of NO is 2.5 and it combines with $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to give $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24129,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'For the elementary reaction $2 \\mathrm{~A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}$, the concentration of A after 30 minutes was found to be $0.01 \\mathrm{~mole} / \\mathrm{lit}$. If the rate constant of the reaction is $2.5 \\times 10^{-2}$ lit mole $^{-1} \\mathrm{sec}^{-1}$, the rate of the reaction at 30 minutes is','[{"text": "$2.5 \\\\times 10^{-4}$ mole lit $^{-1} \\\\mathrm{sec}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times 10^{-6}$ mole lit $^{-1} \\\\mathrm{sec}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times 10^{-2}$ mole lit $^{-1} \\\\mathrm{sec}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times 10^{-8}$ mole lit ${ }^{-1} \\\\mathrm{sec}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From units of \' ${\\text{k}}$ \', it is clear that it is second order reaction.<br>Rate $=\\mathrm{k} \\times(\\mathrm{a})^{2}$<br>$=2.5 \\times 10^{-2} \\times\\left(10^{-2}\\right)^{2}$<br>$=2.5 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L} \\mathrm{sec}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24131,'chemistry',59,372,694,'If molar conductivity of $\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ions are 119 and $71 \\mathrm{~S} \\mathrm{~cm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ respectively, then the molar conductivity of $\\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$ at infinite dilution is','[{"text": "$215 \\\\mathrm{~S} \\\\mathrm{~cm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$340 \\\\mathrm{~S} \\\\mathrm{~cm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$126 \\\\mathrm{~S} \\\\mathrm{~cm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$261 \\\\mathrm{~S} \\\\mathrm{~cm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given,$\\Lambda {^\\circ _{{\\text{C}}{{\\text{a}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}}}}{\\text{ = 119}}\\,{\\text{S}}\\,{\\text{c}}{{\\text{m}}^{\\text{2}}}\\,{\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$<br>$\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}}^{\\circ}=71 \\mathrm{~S} \\mathrm{~cm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$<br>As we know that,<br>$\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}}^{\\circ}=\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}}^{\\circ}+2 \\times \\Lambda_{\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}}^{\\circ}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\Lambda {^\\circ _{{\\rm{CaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_2}}} = 119 + 2 \\times 71$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\Lambda {^\\circ _{{\\rm{CaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_2}}} = 261\\,{\\rm{S}}\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^2}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24132,'chemistry',63,808,1752,'Consider the following statements regarding phsophorus,<br>I. Phosphorus exists in different allotropic forms.<br>II. Yellow phosphorus exists as tetrahedral molecular solid.<br>III. Yellow phosphorus is less reactive than red phosphorus.<br>IV. Black phosphorus has layer type structure.<br>The correct statement are','[{"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I,III and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Yellow phosphorus is more reactive than red phosphorus.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24134,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'For the process $2 \\mathrm{~A} \\rightarrow$ products, rate of reaction w.r.t ${\\text{A}}$  at 10 seconds is $2 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{Ms}^{-1}$ then rates of same process at 5 and 15 seconds $\\quad($ order $\\neq 0$ ) respectively are (in $\\mathrm{M} / \\mathrm{s}$ )','[{"text": "${\\\\text{1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{{\\\\text{  -  1}}}}{\\\\text{&  4 }} \\\\times {\\\\text{1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{{\\\\text{  -  2}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{2}}{\\\\text{.7}} \\\\times {\\\\text{1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{{\\\\text{ - 2}}}}{\\\\text{ &  1}}{\\\\text{.6}} \\\\times {\\\\text{1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{{\\\\text{ - 2}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{1}}{\\\\text{.6}} \\\\times {\\\\text{1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{{\\\\text{ - 2}}}}{\\\\text{ &  2}}{\\\\text{.7}} \\\\times {\\\\text{1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{{\\\\text{ - 2}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{2}} \\\\times {\\\\text{1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{{\\\\text{ - 2}}}}{\\\\text{ &  2}} \\\\times {\\\\text{1}}{{\\\\text{0}}^{{\\\\text{ - 2}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As time passes, rate decreases for all reactions except zero order reactions. So at 5 seconds it is more and at 15 seconds it is less than at $10\\,\\,{\\text{sec}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24135,'chemistry',59,372,694,'At $18^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, the conductance of $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$and $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COO}^{-}$at infinite dilution are 315 and $35\\,{\\rm{mho}}\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^2}\\,{\\rm{e}}{{\\rm{q}}^{ - 1}}$ respectively. The equivalent conductivity of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}$ at infinite dilution is ${\\rm{mho}}\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^2}\\,{\\rm{e}}{{\\rm{q}}^{ - 1}}$','[{"text": "350", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "280", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "30", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "315", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\Lambda_{\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}\\right)}^{\\circ}=\\Lambda_{\\left(\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right)}^{\\circ}+\\Lambda_{\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COO}^{-}\\right)}^{\\circ}$<br>$ = (315 + 35)\\,{\\text{mho}}\\,{\\text{c}}{{\\text{m}}^{\\text{2}}}\\,{\\text{e}}{{\\text{q}}^{ - 1}}$<br>$ = 350{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{mho}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{e}}{{\\rm{q}}^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24136,'chemistry',63,808,1754,'$\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ forms bubbles when passed slowly in water but $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ dissolves because','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ is lighter gas so it bubbles out", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ hates water hence it bubbles out", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ cannot form hydrogen bond with water so it bubbles out.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ is covalent hence it bubbles out.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ forms hydrogen bonds with water therefore it is soluble in it but $\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ cannot form hydrogen bond with water so it escapes as gas. 15. (4) In solid state, $P C l_{5}$ exists as<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{PCl}_{4}\\right]^{+} \\&\\left[\\mathrm{PCl}_{6}\\right]^{-}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24138,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'For $\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}+\\mathrm{D}, \\Delta \\mathrm{H}=-20 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mole}^{-1}$. The activation energy for the forward reaction is 85 $\\mathrm{kJ}$. Then the activation energy for the backward reaction is ____.','[{"text": "$65 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$105 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$85 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40 \\\\mathrm{~kJ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Activation energy for backward reaction = $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}^{+}$activation energy for forward reaction. For exothermic reaction  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/60_24138_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24139,'chemistry',59,370,691,'The compound exhibiting maximum value of equivalent conductance in a fused state is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SrCl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BeCl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'On moving downward in a group, atomic size increases, and, hence ionic character increases. Thus, $\\mathrm{SrCl}_{2}$ is most ionic among the given compounds, i.e. it will give ion readily in fused state. Hence, $\\mathrm{SrCl}_{2}$ exhibit the maximum value of equivalent conductance in fused state.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24140,'chemistry',63,808,1755,'In solid state $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ is a','[{"text": "Covalent solid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Octahedral structure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ionic solid with $\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{6}\\\\right]^{+}$octahedral and $\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{4}\\\\right]^{-}$tetrahedral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ionic solid with $\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{4}\\\\right]^{+}$tetrahedral and $\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{6}\\\\right]^{-}$octahedral", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In solid state, ${\\rm{PC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{5}}}$ exits as ${\\left[ {{\\text{PC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{4}}}} \\right]^{\\text{ + }}}$ & ${\\left[ {{\\text{PC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{6}}}} \\right]^{\\text{ - }}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24142,'chemistry',60,588,1184,'For the reaction $\\mathrm{aA} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{xP}$ when ${\\text{[A]  =  2}}{\\text{.2 mM}}$  the rate was found to be ${\\text{2}}{\\text{.4 m M}}{{\\text{s}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$. On reducing the concentration of A to half, the rate changes to ${\\text{0}}{\\text{.6 mM}}{{\\text{s}}^{{\\text{ - 1}}}}$. The order of reaction with respect to $\\mathrm{A}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\text{1}}{\\\\text{.5}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{2}}{\\\\text{.0}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{2}}{\\\\text{.5}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{3}}{\\\\text{.0}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{r  =  k[A}}{{\\text{]}}^{\\text{a}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(1)}}$<br>$\\frac{{\\text{r}}}{{\\text{4}}}{\\text{ =  k}}{\\left[ {\\frac{{\\text{A}}}{{\\text{2}}}} \\right]^{\\text{a}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(2)}}$<br>$\\dfrac{(2)}{(1)} \\Rightarrow 4=2^{\\mathrm{a}}$<br>$a=2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24143,'chemistry',59,369,686,'Which of the following is not a non-electrolyte?','[{"text": "Acetic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Glucose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethanol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Urea", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The substance whose aqueous solution allows the passage of electric current and is chemically decomposed, is termed as electrolyte. Electrolytic substances are classified as strong or weak, according to acid base concept. Acetic acid is a weak electrolyte while glucose, ethanol and urea are non-electrolytes.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24148,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'For a reaction following first-order kinetics, which of the following statements are correct?','[{"text": "The time taken for the completion of $75 \\\\%$ of the reaction is twice ${{\\\\text{t}}_{{\\\\text{1/2}}}}$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A plot of the reciprocal of the concentration of the reactants against time gives a sraight line.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The degree of dissociation is equal to $1-\\\\mathrm{e}^{-\\\\mathrm{kt}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A plot of $[\\\\mathrm{A}]_{0} /[\\\\mathrm{A}]$ versus time gives a straight line", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{1}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{1/2}}}}\\mathop {\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{50 %  over }}} \\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{t}}_{{\\text{1/2}}}}}}\\mathop {\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{4}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{75 %  over }}} $<br>$\\therefore {{\\text{t}}_{1/2}}:{{\\text{t}}_{75\\% }} = 1:2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24150,'chemistry',59,373,697,'The weight of silver (atomic weight $=108$ ) displaced by a quantity of electricity which displaces $5600 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ at ${\\rm{STP}}$ will be','[{"text": "$5.4 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.8 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$54.0 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$108.0 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Since, $22400 \\mathrm{~mL}$ volume is occupied by 1 mole of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ at STP,<br>  thus, $5600\\,\\,{\\rm{mL}}\\,{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ means<br>$ = \\frac{{5600}}{{22400}}\\,{\\rm{mol}}\\,{{\\rm{O}}_2} = \\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\rm{mol}}\\,{{\\rm{O}}_2}$<br>$\\therefore$ Weight of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times 32=8 \\mathrm{~g}$<br>According to problem,<br>equivalents of $\\mathrm{Ag}=$ equivalents of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{\\rm{weight}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{Ag}}}}{{{\\rm{equivalent}}\\,{\\rm{weight}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{Ag}}}}$<br>${\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{ = }}\\frac{{{{\\text{w}}_{{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}}}}}{{{\\text{equivalent weight of }}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{{\\text{w}}_{{\\text{Ag}}}}}}{{\\frac{{{{\\text{M}}_{{\\text{Ag}}}}}}{{\\text{1}}}}} = \\frac{{{{\\text{w}}_{{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}}}}}{{\\frac{{{{\\text{M}}_{{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}}}}}{{\\text{4}}}}}$  $\\left[ {\\because 2{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}} \\to {{\\text{O}}_2} + 4{{\\text{H}}^ + } + 4{{\\text{e}}^ - }} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {{\\text{w}}_{{\\text{Ag}}}} = {\\text{108}}\\,{\\text{g}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24151,'chemistry',63,811,1767,'Why is dioxygen a gas but sulphur a solid?','[{"text": "Sulphur has strong tendency to form ${\\\\rm{p\\\\pi  - p\\\\pi }}$ bond", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Due to small size oxygen has strong tendency to form ${\\\\rm{p\\\\pi  - p\\\\pi }}$ bond and exist as diatomic gas", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Sulphur is larger in size hence exists as solid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxygen has less reactivity than sulphur", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24152,'chemistry',60,588,1184,'Using the data given below, the order and rate constant for the reaction:<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CHO}(\\mathrm{g}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CH}_{4}(\\mathrm{~g})+\\mathrm{CO}(\\mathrm{g})$ would be','[{"text": "${\\\\text{2, [k  =  2}}{\\\\text{.01 / mol  sec]}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{0,[k  =  2}}{\\\\text{.0 mol / 1sec]}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{2,[k  =  1}}{\\\\text{.51/mol sec]}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{1,}}\\\\left[ {{\\\\text{k  =  1}}{\\\\text{.5 se}}{{\\\\text{c}}^{{\\\\text{ - 1}}}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{r  =  k}}{\\left[ {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CHO}}} \\right]^{\\text{n}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(1)}}$<br>${\\text{4r  =  k}}{\\left[ {{\\text{2C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CHO}}} \\right]^{\\text{n}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(2)}}$<br>$\\dfrac{(2)}{(1)} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{n}=2$, then rate $=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{2}$<br>$0.02 = {\\text{k}}{(0.1)^2}$<br>${\\text{k  =  2 lit}}{\\text{.mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{\\text{ - }}}{\\text{ se}}{{\\text{c}}^{\\text{ - }}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_24152_question_1.png'),
(24154,'chemistry',59,373,697,'On passing $3 \\mathrm{~A}$ of electricity for $50 \\mathrm{~min}, 1.8 \\mathrm{~g}$ metal deposits. The equivalent mass of metal is','[{"text": "9.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "19.3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "38.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "39.9", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${{\\text{w}}_{{\\text{metal }}}} = \\frac{{{\\text{Eit}}}}{{{\\text{96500}}}} = \\frac{{{\\text{E}} \\times 3 \\times 50 \\times 60}}{{96500}}$<br>${\\text{E}} = \\frac{{96500 \\times {\\text{w}}}}{{3 \\times 50 \\times 60}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad E=\\dfrac{96500 \\times 1.8}{3 \\times 50 \\times 60}=19.3 \\text { equiv }$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24155,'chemistry',63,811,1769,'The elements of group- 16 which show negative oxidation state are<br>I. Oxygen<br>II. Polonium<br>III. Tellurium<br>IV. Selenium<br>Choose the correct option.','[{"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II,III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Except polonium, all the other three, i.e., $\\mathrm{O}, \\mathrm{Te}, \\mathrm{Se}$, show negative oxidation state $(-2)$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24156,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'Consider an endothermic reaction ${\\text{X}} \\to {\\text{Y}}$ with the activation energies  ${{\\text{E}}_{\\text{b}}}$ and ${{\\text{E}}_{\\text{f}}}$ for the backward and forward reactions respectively. In general','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{E}_{\\\\mathrm{b}} < \\\\mathrm{E}_{\\\\mathrm{f}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{E}_{\\\\mathrm{b}}>\\\\mathrm{E}_{\\\\mathrm{f}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{E}_{\\\\mathrm{b}}=\\\\mathrm{E}_{\\\\mathrm{f}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There is no definite relation between $\\\\mathrm{E}_{\\\\mathrm{b}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{E}_{\\\\mathrm{f}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For endothermic reactions, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/60_24156_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\therefore \\mathrm{Ea}_{\\mathrm{fwd}}>\\mathrm{Ea}_{\\mathrm{bkwd}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24158,'chemistry',59,373,696,'In electrolysis of dil. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ using platinum electrodes','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is evolved at cathode", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ is produced at anode", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is obtained at cathode", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ is produced at cathode", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Dilute sulphuric acid contains $\\mathrm{H}^{+}, \\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$ and $\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ions.<br>At cathode <br>$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$ions undergo reduction by taking up electrons to form $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$.<br>$2{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}({\\rm{aq}}) + {{\\rm{e}}^ - } \\to {{\\rm{H}}_2}({\\rm{g}})$<br>At anode: <br>The concentration of $\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ions is too low to maintain a reaction and sulphate ions are not oxidised but remain in the solution at the end. Thus, $\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ions are discharged in preference over $\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$ giving off $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ gas.<br>$4 \\mathrm{OH}^{-}(a q) \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{O}_{2}(g)+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}(l)+4 e^{-}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24159,'chemistry',63,811,1770,'Oxygen shows anomalous behaviour from other members of its family because<br>I. Oxygen shows maximum valency of two.<br>II. Among chalcogens oxygen is of the smallest<br>III. Oxygen exhibits allotropy. <br>IV. Oxygen has no vacant d-orbital.<br>The correct option explaining the reasons is','[{"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Among chalcogens, oxygen has smallest size. It shows maximum valency of two. It has no vacant d-orbital.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24160,'chemistry',60,586,1177,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Chemical kinetics deals with the rate of reaction, the factors affecting the rates of the reactions and the mechanism by which the reaction proceeds.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Nature of reactants, concentration of reactants and products and catalyst affect the rate of reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Chemical kinetics is the branch of physical chemistry that focuses on the study of reaction rates, the factors influencing those rates, and the mechanisms by which chemical reactions occur. Rate of reaction depends upon the experimental conditions such as concentration of reactants (pressure in case of gases), temperature and catalyst. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24162,'chemistry',59,373,697,'${\\rm{4}}{\\rm{.5\\,g}}$ of aluminium (atomic mass $27{\\rm{u}}$ ) is deposited at cathode from a molten electrolyte containing ${\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{l}}^{3 + }}$ ions by a certain quantity of electric charge. The volume of hydrogen produced at STP from ${{\\rm{H}}^ + }$ ions in a solution by the same quantity of electric charge will be','[{"text": "$44.8 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11.2 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22.4 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.6 \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Equivalent mass of $\\mathrm{Al}=\\dfrac{27}{3}=9 \\mathrm{~g}$ eq<br>Equivalent mass of $\\mathrm{H}=1$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\frac{{{{\\rm{w}}_{{\\rm{Al}}}}}}{{{{\\rm{w}}_{{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{equivalent}}\\,{\\rm{mass}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{Al}}}}{{{\\rm{equivalent}}\\,{\\rm{mass}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\frac{{4.5}}{{{{\\rm{w}}_{{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}}} = \\frac{9}{1} \\Rightarrow {{\\rm{w}}_{{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}}} = 0.5{\\rm{\\,g}}$<br>$\\because 2 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at STP occupies volume$=22.4 \\mathrm{~L}$<br>$\\therefore 0.5 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at STP will occupy<br>$\\text { volume }=\\dfrac{22.4 \\times 0.5}{2}=5.6 \\mathrm{~L}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24163,'chemistry',63,813,1776,'Which of the following does not react with oxygen directly?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Pt}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Zn}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ti}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Noble metals like gold, platinum and noble gases do not react directly with oxygen.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24164,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Kinetics explains the reaction mechanism.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Kinetics explains the formation of products.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Kinetics in chemistry explores reaction mechanisms, deciphering how atoms rearrange within reactant molecules during a chemical reaction, offering insights into the pathway, energy changes, and molecular transformations leading to the formation of products. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24166,'chemistry',59,373,697,'How many Coulombs of electricity are required for the reduction of 1 mole of $\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$ to $\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ ?','[{"text": "$96500 \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.65 \\\\times 10^{6} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.83 \\\\times 10^{5} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.93 \\\\times 10^{5} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathop {{\\rm{MnO}}_4^ - }\\limits_{1{\\rm{ mole}}}  + 8{{\\rm{H}}^ + } + \\mathop {5{{\\rm{e}}^ - }}\\limits_{5\\,{\\rm{mol}}}  \\to {\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{2 + }} + 4{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>i.e. $5\\,{\\rm{F}}$ of electricity is required.<br>Hence, electricity required.<br>$ = 5 \\times 96500\\,{\\rm{C}} = 482500\\,{\\rm{C}}$<br>$ = 4.83 \\times {10^5}\\,{\\rm{C}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24167,'chemistry',63,813,1778,'Ozone is thermodynamically less stable than oxygen<br>(A) Its decomposition into oxygen results in the liberation of heat<br>(B) Increase in entropy<br>(C) Large negative Gibbs free energy change for its conversion into oxygen.<br>The correct reason is/are','[{"text": "A, B, C", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A, B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B,C", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24168,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For the reaction, $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{PCl}_{3}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$, the concentration of $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ decreases with the increases in concentration of $\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> During the course of a reaction, the concentration of the reactants decreases while that of products increases.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The reaction demonstrates the fundamental principle of chemical reactions, wherein the consumption of $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ leads to a decrease in its concentration, while the production of $\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ results in an increase in their concentrations. So option (1) is correct .',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24170,'chemistry',59,373,697,'In the electrolysis of water, one Faraday of electrical energy would evolve','[{"text": "one mole of oxygen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "one gram atom of oxygen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\mathrm{~g}$ of oxygen", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{22}}{\\\\rm{.4 L}}$ of oxygen", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Hydrolysis of water is given as,<br>$2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{O}_{2}+4 \\mathrm{H}^{+}+4 e^{-}$<br>$\\because 4 \\mathrm{~F}$ of charge liberate $\\mathrm{O}_{2}=32 \\mathrm{~g}$<br>$\\therefore 1 \\mathrm{~F}$ of charge liberate $\\mathrm{O}_{2}=\\dfrac{32}{4}=8 \\mathrm{~g}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24171,'chemistry',63,813,1779,'Uses of ozone $\\left(\\mathrm{O}_{3}\\right)$ includes<br>I. As an oxidising agent in the manufacture of $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$<br>II. As a germicide, disinfectant and for sterilisation.<br>III. For bleaching oils, flour, ivory, starch, etc.<br>The correct set of uses of ozone is','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24172,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The rate of the reaction is the rate of change of concentration of a reactant or a product.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Rate of reaction remains constant during the complete reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The rate of a chemical reaction varies due to its dependence on reactant concentrations. It decreases as the reaction progresses and reactants are consumed. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24174,'chemistry',59,367,680,'Given, ${\\rm{E}}{^\\circ _{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}^{3 + }}/{\\rm{Cr}}}} =  - 0.72\\,{\\rm{V}},{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{E}}{^\\circ _{{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{2 + }}/{\\rm{Fe}}}} =  - 0.42{\\rm{V}}$, <br>The potential for the cell<br>${\\rm{Cr}}\\left| {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}^{3 + }}(0.1{\\rm{M}})} \\right|\\left| {{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{2 + }}(0.01{\\rm{M}})} \\right|{\\rm{Fe}}$ is','[{"text": "$-0.339 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-0.26 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.26 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.3 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell }}}^{\\rm{0}} = {{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cathode }}}} - {{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{anode }}}}$<br>$=-0.42-(-0.72)=0.3 \\mathrm{~V}$<br>At anode ${\\rm{Cr}} \\to {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}^{3 + }} + 3{e^ - } \\times 2$<br>At cathode ${\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{2 + }} + 2{e^ - } \\to {\\rm{Fe}} \\times 3$<br>$2{\\rm{Cr}} + 3{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{2 + }} \\to 2{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}^{3 + }} + 3{\\rm{Fe}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {\\rm{E}} = {\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell }}}^0 - \\frac{{0.0591}}{6}{\\rm{log}}\\frac{{{{\\left[ {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}^{{\\rm{3 + }}}}} \\right]}^2}}}{{{{\\left[ {{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{2 + }}} \\right]}^3}}}$<br>$ = 0.3 - \\frac{{0.0591}}{6}\\log \\frac{{{{(0.1)}^2}}}{{{{(0.01)}^3}}}$<br>$ = 0.26\\,{\\rm{V}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24175,'chemistry',63,813,1778,'Starch paper moistened with solution of KI turns to blue in ozone because','[{"text": "Alkali is formed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Iodine is liberated", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ozone reacts with litmus paper", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxygen is liberated", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Starch paper moistened with solution of KI turns blue in ozone because iodine is liberated.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24176,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Instantaneous rate of reaction is equal to ${\\text{dx / dt}}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It is the rate of reaction at any particular instant of time.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The instantaneous rate of a reaction is the precise measurement of concentration change (dx) over an infinitesimally small time interval (dt) at a specific moment. Thus, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24178,'chemistry',59,367,680,'If ${\\rm{E^\\circ }}\\left( {{\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{,Zn}}} \\right) =  - 0.763{\\rm{V}}$ and ${\\rm{E^\\circ }}\\left( {{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{,Fe}}} \\right) =  - 0.44{\\rm{V}}$, then the emf of the cell<br>${\\rm{Zn}}\\left| {{\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{2 + }}(a = 0.001)} \\right|\\left| {{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{2 + }}({\\rm{a}} = 0.005)} \\right|{\\rm{Fe}}$ is','[{"text": "equal to $0.323 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "less than $0.323 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "greater than $0.323 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "equal to $1.103 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The cell reaction is<br>$\\mathrm{Zn}+\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}+\\mathrm{Fe}$<br>From Nernst equation<br>${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell }}}} = {\\rm{E}}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{{\\rm{cell }}}} - \\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.0591}}}}{{\\rm{n}}}\\log \\frac{{{{\\rm{a}}_{{\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}}}}}{{{{\\rm{a}}_{{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}}}}}$<br>$ = (0.763 - 0.44) - \\frac{{0.0591}}{2}\\log \\frac{{0.001}}{{0.005}}$<br>$ = 0.344\\,{\\rm{V}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24179,'chemistry',63,814,1781,'Which form of sulphur shows paramagnetic behaviour?','[{"text": "Monoclinic sulphur", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plastic sulphur", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rhombic sulphur", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}_{2}$ in vapour state", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In vapour state sulphur partly exists as $\\mathrm{S}_{2}$ molecule which has two unpaired electrons in the antibonding $\\pi^{*}$ orbitals like $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and hence exhibits paramagnetism.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24180,'chemistry',60,584,1172,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $50 \\%$ of a zero order reaction is completed in ${\\text{100 sec}}$. therefore, $75 \\%$ of the reaction will be completed in ${\\text{150 sec}}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The rate constant of a zero order reaction depends upon time.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Rate constant of any reaction is constant at constant temperature and does not change with time or concentration.<br>$\\mathrm{t}_{50 \\%}=\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{k}}\\left\\{\\left[\\mathrm{A}_{0}\\right]-[\\mathrm{A}]\\right\\}$<br>For zero order raection,<br>At $\\mathrm{t}_{50 \\%}[\\mathrm{~A}]=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{A}_{0}}{2}$<br>$\\mathrm{t}_{50 \\%}=\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{k}}\\left\\{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}-\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{2}\\right\\}=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{2}=100 \\mathrm{sec}$<br>For Completion<br>$75 \\%, \\mathrm{t}_{75 \\%}=\\dfrac{1}{\\mathrm{k}}\\left\\{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}-\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{2}\\right\\}=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{\\mathrm{k}} \\times \\dfrac{3}{4}$<br>$=\\dfrac{[\\mathrm{A}]_{0}}{2 \\mathrm{k}} \\times \\dfrac{3}{2}=100 \\times \\dfrac{3}{2}=150 \\mathrm{sec}$<br>Thus, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24182,'chemistry',59,365,676,'For the electrochemical cell,<br>$\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}|\\mathrm{AgCl}| \\mathrm{KCl}|| \\mathrm{AgNO}_{3} \\mid \\mathrm{Ag}^{+}$, the overall cell reaction is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}+\\\\mathrm{KCl} \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{AgCl}(s)+\\\\mathrm{K}^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ag}+\\\\mathrm{AgCl} \\\\longrightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{Ag}+\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{AgCl}(s) \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}+\\\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}+\\\\mathrm{Cl}^{-} \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{AgCl}(\\\\mathrm{s})$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For the electrochemical cell, <br>$\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}|\\mathrm{AgCl}| \\mathrm{KCl} \\| \\mathrm{AgNO}_{3} \\mid \\mathrm{Ag}^{+}$<br>The cell reaction is, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/59_24182_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Overall reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{AgCl}(s) \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Ag}^{+}+\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24183,'chemistry',63,814,1782,'Two methods of preparation of $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ are given below.<br>I. ${\\rm{4Fe}}{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(s) + 11}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g)}} \\to {\\rm{2F}}{{\\rm{e}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{(s) + 8S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g)}}$<br>II.${\\rm{SO}}_{\\rm{3}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{(aq) + 2}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{(aq)}} \\to {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O(l) + S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g)}}$<br>Categorise these reactions into laboratory method and industrial method of preparing $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ with the help of options given below:','[{"text": "I - industrial; II - laboratory", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I - industrial; II - industrial", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I - laboratory; II - industrial", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I - laboratory; II - laboratory", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Industrial method of preparation of $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ is<br>${\\rm{4Fe}}{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {\\rm{s}} \\right){\\rm{ + 11}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {\\rm{g}} \\right) \\to {\\rm{2F}}{{\\rm{e}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\left( {\\rm{s}} \\right){\\rm{ + 8S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {\\rm{g}} \\right)$<br>Laboratory method of preparation of $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ is<br>${\\rm{SO}}_{\\rm{3}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}\\left( {{\\rm{aq}}} \\right){\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}\\left( {{\\rm{aq}}} \\right) \\to {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}\\left( {\\rm{l}} \\right){\\rm{ + S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\left( {\\rm{g}} \\right){\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24184,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Rate of reaction can be expressed as rate of change in partial pressure of the gaseous reactants or product<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Partial pressure of a gas is equal to its concentration.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In gaseous reactions at constant temperature, the rate can be conveniently expressed in terms of partial pressures. But partial pressure is not equal to concentration. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24186,'chemistry',59,368,682,'The value of reaction quotient ${\\rm{(Q)}}$, for the following cell<br>${\\rm{Zn}}({\\rm{s}})\\left| {{\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{2 + }}(0.01{\\rm{M}})\\left\\| {{\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{g}}^ + }(1.25{\\rm{M}})} \\right.} \\right|{\\rm{Ag}}({\\rm{s}})$is','[{"text": "156", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "125", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.25 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.4 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The cell reaction is <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/59_24186_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\mathrm{Zn}(s)+2 \\mathrm{Ag}^{+}(1.25 \\mathrm{M}) \\longrightarrow$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{Z}}{{\\text{n}}^{2 + }}(0.01{\\text{M}}) + 2{\\text{Ag}}({\\text{s}})$<br>$\\therefore \\quad Q=\\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\\right]^{2}}=\\dfrac{0.01}{(1.25)^{2}}=6.4 \\times 10^{-3}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ Value of $Q=6.4 \\times 10^{-3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24187,'chemistry',63,814,1782,'Which statement is correct?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ dissolve in water & forms sulphurous acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ has ${\\\\rm{4p\\\\pi  - d\\\\pi }}$ bonds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ has pungent odour", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{SO}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24188,'chemistry',60,586,1177,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The presence of a catalyst increases the speed of the forward and backward reaction to the same extent.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Activation energy for both the forward and the backward reactions is lowered to the same extent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Catalysts accelerate chemical reactions by providing an alternative pathway with significantly lower activation energy, making it easier for reactant molecules to reach the transition state and facilitating product formation, thereby expediting the overall reaction rate. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24190,'chemistry',59,365,676,'The formal potential of $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+} / \\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ in a sulphuric acid and phosphoric acid mixture$\\left( {{\\rm{E}}^\\circ  =  + 0.16\\,{\\rm{V}}} \\right)$ is much lower than the standard potential$\\left( {{\\rm{E}}^\\circ  =  + 0.77{\\rm{V}}} \\right)$. This is due to','[{"text": "formation of the species $\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{FeHPO}_{4}\\\\right]^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "lowering of potential upon complexation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "formation of the species $\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{FeSO}_{4}\\\\right]^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "high acidic of the medium", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Since, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ both are strong acids so, their presence makes the medium more acidic. The $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions form complex, $\\left[\\mathrm{FeHPO}_{4}\\right]^{+}$with these acids and hence, the concentration of $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ion reduces which results in lowering in the formal reduction potential.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24191,'chemistry',63,814,1784,'The oxoacids of $\\mathrm{S}$ forms two series of salts, namely','[{"text": "Normal and acid salts", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Normal and basic salts", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acid and basic salts", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The acid forms two series of salts i.e., normal sulphates (like sodium sulphate and copper sulphate) and acid sulphates (e.g., sodium hydrogen sulphates).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24192,'chemistry',60,586,1177,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Molecularity has no meaning for a complex reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The overall molecularity of a complex reaction is equal to the molecularity of the slowest step.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Molecularity, a concept applicable only to elementary reactions, signifies the number of reactant molecules colliding simultaneously in a single, straightforward step. A complex reactions, which involve multiple consecutive steps lacks a clear determination of molecularity due to the intricate nature of intermediate species and simultaneous encounters. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24193,'chemistry',59,368,682,'The equilibrium constant of the reaction,<br>${\\rm{Cu(s)}} + 2{\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{g}}^ + }({\\rm{aq}}) \\to {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{u}}^{2 + }}({\\rm{aq}}) + 2{\\rm{Ag(s)}}$<br>${\\rm{E}}^\\circ {\\rm{ }} = 0.46\\,{\\rm{V}}\\,{\\rm{at}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,298\\,{\\rm{K}}\\,is$','[{"text": "$2.4 \\\\times 10^{10}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0 \\\\times 10^{10}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.0 \\\\times 10^{10}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.0 \\\\times 10^{15}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{Cu}(s)+2 \\mathrm{Ag}^{+}(a q) \\longrightarrow$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{C}}{{\\text{u}}^{2 + }}({\\text{aq}}) + 2{\\text{Ag}}({\\text{s}})$<br>${{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}} = 0.46\\,{\\rm{V}}\\,{\\rm{at}}\\,298\\,{\\rm{K}}$<br>${\\rm{E^\\circ }} = \\frac{{0.059}}{2}\\,{\\rm{log}}\\,{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{C}}}\\,,{\\rm{at}}\\,298\\,{\\rm{K}}$<br>$0.46 = \\frac{{0.059}}{2}\\log {{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{C}}}$<br>${\\rm{log }}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{C}}} = 15.59$<br>${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{C}}} = {\\rm{antilog }}15.59$<br>${{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{C}}} = 3.89 \\times {10^{15}} \\approx 4 \\times {10^{15}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24195,'chemistry',63,814,1786,'Which of the following oxacids of sulphur contains a sulphur-sulphur single bond?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{6}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_24195_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24196,'chemistry',60,586,1177,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The molecularity of the reaction.<br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2}+\\mathrm{Br}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{HBr}$ is 2<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The rate of reaction is given by<br>$\\mathrm{k}\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{Br}_{2}\\right]$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Molecularity is 2 as two molecules are colliding. Rate of reaction for given reactions is<br>Rate $=\\mathrm{k}\\left[\\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{Br}_{2}\\right]^{1 / 2}$. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24198,'chemistry',59,365,675,'Saturated solution of $\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}$ is used to make salt bridge because','[{"text": "velocity of $\\\\mathrm{K}^{+}$is greater than that of $\\\\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "velocity of $\\\\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$is greater than that of $\\\\mathrm{K}^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "velocity of both $\\\\mathrm{K}^{+}$and $\\\\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$are nearly same", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}$ is soluble in water", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Saturated solution of $\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}$ is used to make salt bridge because the ions have same ionic mobility.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24199,'chemistry',63,814,1785,'In the preparation of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ by Contact Process, why is $\\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ not absorbed directly in water to form $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ ?','[{"text": "Acid fog is formed, which is difficult to condense.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It does not dissolve in water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is explosive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gets reduced", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Acid fog is formed, which is difficult to condense.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24200,'chemistry',60,586,1177,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The order of the reaction<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COO}}{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{5}}}{\\text{ +  }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}} \\to $<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COOH  +  }}{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{5}}}{\\text{OH}}$  is 1.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The molecularity ot this reaction is 2 .','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The hydrolysis of an ester, with consistently high water concentration, is effectively a pseudo-first-order reaction. But molecularity is 2 for this reaction. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24202,'chemistry',59,366,679,'The  ${\\rm{E}}{^\\circ _{{{\\rm{M}}^{3 + }}/{{\\rm{M}}^{2 + }}}}$ values for $\\mathrm{Cr}, \\mathrm{Mn}, \\mathrm{Fe}$ and Co are -0.41 , $+1.57,+0.77$ and $+1.97 \\mathrm{~V}$ respectively. For which one of these metals, the change in oxidation state from +2 to +3 is easiest?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mn}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Co}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'More the negative value of standard reduction potential, higher is the reduction power and higher is the tendency to get oxidised. The ${{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{c}}}$ value most negative is for $\\mathrm{Cr}$ among the given, hence it has higher tendency to change from +2 to +3 state',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24203,'chemistry',63,814,1785,'Hot conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ acts as moderately strong oxidising agent. It oxidises both metals and nonmetals. Which of the following element is oxidised by conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ into two gaseous products?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Zn}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\mathrm{C} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{SO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24204,'chemistry',60,590,1187,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The addition of catalyst lowers the activation barrier, yet there is no change in the enthalpy.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enthalpy change is equal to the differenee in the activation energy for the forward and the backward reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Catalysts lower the activation energy barrier, increasing reaction rates, but they do not alter the overall energy change $(\\Delta \\mathrm{H})$ associated with the reaction. Thus, the thermodynamic characteristics, whether exothermic or endothermic, remain unchanged. So option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24206,'chemistry',59,365,675,'Which of the following statements is true for the electrochemical Daniell cell?','[{"text": "Electrons flow from copper electrode to zinc electrode", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Current flows from zinc electrode to copper electrode", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cations move towards copper electrode", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cations move towards zinc electrode", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In Daniell cell, following reaction occurs.<br>$\\mathrm{Zn}+\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}+\\mathrm{Cu}$<br>${\\rm{At}}\\,{\\rm{cathode}}:{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{u}}^{2 + }} + 2{e^ - } \\to {\\rm{Cu}}$<br>${\\rm{At}}\\,{\\rm{anode}}:{\\rm{Zn}} \\to {\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{2 + }} + 2{e^ - }$<br>Electrons moves from anode $(\\mathrm{Zn})$ to cathode $(\\mathrm{Cu})$ and electricity flows from $\\mathrm{Cu}$ to $\\mathrm{Zn}$. Here, cations move towards cathode (copper) electrode.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24207,'chemistry',63,814,1785,'${{\\text{C}}_{{\\text{12}}}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{22}}}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{11}}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{conc}}{\\text{. }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}}}$ Products. Product(s) of the reaction is/are','[{"text": "$12 \\\\mathrm{C}+11 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$11 \\\\mathrm{C}+12 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\mathrm{CO}+7 \\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{C}_{12} \\mathrm{H}_{22} \\mathrm{O}_{11} \\stackrel{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}{\\longrightarrow} 12 \\mathrm{C}+11 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24208,'chemistry',60,587,1178,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The decomposition of $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ on finely divided platinum surface is first order when the concentration is low, however at higher concentration, the reaction becomes zero order.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In first order reaction, the rate of reaction is proportional to the first power of the concentration of the reactant.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In heterogeneous systems, the reaction rate initially follows first-order kinetics proportional to the reactant concentration. However, at higher concentrations, it becomes a zero-order reaction, where the rate depends solely on the availability of active sites on the catalyst surface. Given reason statement is true, but not the correct explantion for the assertion. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24210,'chemistry',59,368,682,'The logarithm of the equilibrium constant of the cell reaction corresponding to the cell<br>${\\rm{X(s)}}\\left| {{{\\rm{X}}^{2 + }}({\\rm{aq}})} \\right|\\left| {{{\\rm{Y}}^ + }({\\rm{aq}})} \\right|{\\rm{Y(s)}}$ with standard cell potential, ${\\rm{E}}{^\\circ _{{\\rm{cell}}}} = 1.2\\,{\\rm{V}}$ is given by','[{"text": "12.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "21.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "40.6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "47.2", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{X(s)}} \\to {{\\rm{X}}^{2 + }} + 2{{\\rm{e}}^ - }$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left[ {{{\\rm{Y}}^ + } + {{\\rm{e}}^ - }} \\right. \\to {\\rm{Y}}] \\times 2$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{{\\rm{Y}}^ + } + 2{{\\rm{e}}^ - } \\to 2{\\rm{Y}}$<br>${\\rm{Overall \\, reaction :}}$<br>${\\rm{X(s) + 2}}{{\\rm{Y}}^{\\rm{ + }}} \\to {{\\rm{X}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{ + 2Y}}$<br>Thus, ${\\rm{n}} = 2$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{E}}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{{\\rm{cell}}}} = \\frac{{0.0591}}{{\\rm{n}}}{\\rm{log }}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{C}}},$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,1.2 = \\frac{{0.0591}}{2}{\\rm{log }}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{C}}}$<br>${\\rm{log }}{{\\rm{K}}_{\\rm{C}}} = \\frac{{1.2 \\times 2}}{{0.0591}} = 40.6$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24211,'chemistry',63,814,1785,'The one that liberates $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ with dil. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NaHSO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}\\left( {{\\rm{dil}}{\\rm{.}}} \\right) \\to {\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{ + S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24212,'chemistry',60,586,1177,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Formation of $\\mathrm{HI}$ is a bimolecular reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Two molecules of reactants are involved in this reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A bimolecular reaction involves two molecules coming together to interact. Formation of $\\mathrm{HI}$ is bimolecular in which one molecule of hydrogen interacts with one molecule of iodine.<br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2}+\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{HI} .$<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24213,'chemistry',59,365,676,'If the half-cell reactions are given as<br>${\\rm{ I}}{\\rm{. F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{2 + }}({\\rm{aq}}) + 2{{\\rm{e}}^ - } \\to {\\rm{Fe(s)}},{\\rm{E}}^\\circ  =  - 0.44\\,{\\rm{V}}$<br>${\\rm{II}}{\\rm{. }}2{{\\rm{H}}^ + }{\\rm{(aq)}} + \\frac{1}{2}{{\\rm{O}}_2}{\\rm{(g)}} + 2{{\\rm{e}}^ - } \\to {{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}(l),$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{E}}^\\circ  =  + 1.23\\,{\\rm{V}}$<br>then ${\\rm{E}}^\\circ $ for the reaction, <br>${\\rm{Fe(s)}} + 2{{\\rm{H}}^ + } + \\frac{1}{2}{{\\rm{O}}_2}{\\rm{(g)}} \\to {\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{2 + }}{\\rm{(aq)}} + {{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}({\\rm{l}})$','[{"text": "$+1.67 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$-1.67 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$+0.79 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-0.79 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{E}}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{{\\rm{cell}}}} = {\\rm{E}}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{{\\rm{cathode}}}} - {\\rm{E}}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{{\\rm{anode}}}}$<br>$ =  + 1.23 - ( - 0.44)$<br>$ =  + 1.67{\\rm{\\,V}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24237,'chemistry',59,374,702,'Which of the following is used widely in the manufacture of lead storage battery?','[{"text": "Arsenic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lithium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bismuth", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Antimony", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Antimony is used in making lead storage batteries because lead containing antimony is harder and more resistant towards the action of acids than ordinary lead.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24214,'chemistry',63,816,1792,'Following are statements related to group - 17 elements<br>I. Sea water contains chlorides, bromides and calcium, but mainly sodium chloride solution.<br>II. The deposits of dried up seas contain carnallite, $\\mathrm{KCl}$. $\\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}$. $6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>III. Certain forms of marine life contain iodine in their system.<br>Which of the above statements are correct?','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Sea water contains chlorides, bromides and iodides of sodium, potassium, magnesium and calcium but is mainly sodium chloride solution. Fluorine is present mainly as insoluble fluorides (fluorspar $\\mathrm{CaF}_{2}$, cryolite $\\mathrm{Na}_{3} \\mathrm{AlF}_{6}$ and fluoroapatite $\\left.3 \\mathrm{Ca}_{3}\\left(\\mathrm{PO}_{4}\\right)_{2} \\cdot \\mathrm{CaF}_{2}\\right)$ and small quantities are available in soil, river, water plants and bones and teeth of animals.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24216,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If the activation energy of a reaction is zero, temperature will have no effect on the rate constant.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Lower the activation energy, faster is the reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to the Arrhenius equation, the rate constant $(\\mathrm{k})$ of a chemical reaction is related to the activation energy $(\\mathrm{Ea})$, the gas constant $(\\mathrm{R})$, the absolute temperature $(\\mathrm{T})$, and the pre-exponential factor (A). The equation is expressed as follows:<br>$\\mathrm{K}=\\mathrm{Ae}^{-\\mathrm{Ea} / \\mathrm{RT}}$<br>At an absolute temperature of 0 Kelvin $(\\mathrm{E}=0)$, the rate constant is equal to the pre-exponential factor, A. This represents the lowest possible temperature at which a chemical reaction can occur. As the Ea decreases, no of molecules with Ea increases, as a result rate increases. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24217,'chemistry',59,366,679,'A gas ${\\rm{X}}$ at $1 \\mathrm{~atm}$ is bubbled through a solution containing a mixture of$1{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{Y}}^ - }\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,1{\\rm{MY}}{{\\rm{Z}}^ - }\\,{\\rm{at}}\\,25^\\circ \\,{\\rm{C}}$. If the order of reduction potential of ${\\rm{Z,Y}}$ and ${\\rm{X}}$ is ${\\rm{Z}} > {\\rm{Y}} > {\\rm{X}}$, then','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{Y}}$ will oxidise ${\\\\rm{X}}$ but not ${\\\\rm{Z}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Y}}$ will oxidise both ${\\\\rm{X}}$ and ${\\\\rm{Z}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Y}}$ will oxidise ${\\\\rm{Z}}$ but not ${\\\\rm{X}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Y}}$ will reduce both ${\\\\rm{X}}$ and ${\\\\rm{Z}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Reducing nature depends on the tendency of losing electron or electrons. Lesser is the negative value of reduction potential, more is the tendency to lose electrons. Then, reducing nature decreases from top to bottom. So, ${\\rm{Y}}$ cannot reduce ${\\rm{X}}$ but can reduce ${\\rm{Z}}$, or ${\\rm{Y}}$ will oxidise ${\\rm{X}}$ but not ${\\rm{Z}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24218,'chemistry',63,816,1793,'Halogens have maximum negative electron gain enthalpy in the respective periods of the periodic table. Why?','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{Z}}_{{\\\\rm{eff }}}}$ is high and require one electron for octet", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Due to large size", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Energy of halogen increases by accepting electron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They are non metals", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Halogens have the smallest size in their respective periods and therefore high effective nuclear charge. As a consequence, they readily accept one electron to acquire noble gas electronic configuration.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24220,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Formation of activated complex by reactant molecules is called transition state.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Transition state is the configuration of atoms in the activated complex, which if attained, leads to the formation of the products.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Transition state theory posits that, in a chemical reaction, reactant molecules must first converge to create an activated complex - a temporary, high-energy state between reactants and products. Transistion state formation leads to product formation. Therefore option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24221,'chemistry',59,368,681,'The potential of standard hydrogen electrode is zero. This implies that','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\Delta G}}_{\\\\rm{f}}^{\\\\rm{^\\\\circ }}\\\\left( {{{\\\\rm{H}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}{\\\\rm{,aq}}} \\\\right) = 0$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\Delta H}}_{\\\\rm{f}}^{\\\\rm{^\\\\circ }}\\\\left( {{{\\\\rm{H}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}{\\\\rm{,aq}}} \\\\right) = 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\Delta G}}_{\\\\rm{f}}^{\\\\rm{^\\\\circ }}\\\\left( {{{\\\\rm{H}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}{\\\\rm{,aq}}} \\\\right) < 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\Delta G}}_{\\\\rm{f}}^{\\\\rm{^\\\\circ }}\\\\left( {{{\\\\rm{H}}^{\\\\rm{ + }}}{\\\\rm{,aq}}} \\\\right) > 0$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As ${\\rm{\\Delta G}}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{\\rm{f}}} =  - {\\rm{nFE^\\circ }}$<br>if  ${\\rm{E^\\circ }} = 0$<br>then, ${\\rm{\\Delta G}}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{\\rm{f}}} = 0$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24222,'chemistry',63,816,1793,'Among the following, the one that is relatively most electropositive is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{F}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Br}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{I}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'On moving down the group ${\\rm{F}}\\,\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,\\,{\\rm{I}}$ electronegativity decreases and electropositive nature increases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24224,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> According to steady state hypothesis, in a multistep reaction, the change in concentration with time for reactive intermediates is zero.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The intermediates are so reactive that after a brief initial period their concentrations rise from zero to a small value and remains constant for most of the duration of the reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The steady-state hypothesis, often applied to multistep reactions, states that the change in concentration with time for reactive intermediates is essentially zero. This hypothesis is based on the idea that certain reaction intermediates are formed and consumed at nearly equal rates during the course of the reaction, resulting in a state of dynamic equilibrium in terms of their concentrations. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24225,'chemistry',59,367,680,'${\\rm{Zn}}({\\rm{s}}) + {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}({\\rm{1}}\\,{\\rm{atm}}) \\to {\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{2 + }} + 2{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}^ - };{\\rm{E}}{^\\circ _{{\\rm{cell}}}}$ of the cell is $2.12\\,{\\rm{V}}$. To increase ${\\rm{E}}$','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\\\\right]$ should be increased", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\\\\right]$ should be decreased", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\\\\right]$should be increased", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{p}}_{{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}}}$ should be decreased", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to Nernst equation,<br>${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell }}}} = {\\rm{E}}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{{\\rm{cell}}}} - \\frac{{{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.303RT}}}}{{{\\rm{nF}}}}{\\rm{log}}\\frac{{{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{1}}}}}{{{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}$<br>For, ${\\rm{Zn(s)}} + {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(1atm)}} \\to $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{2 + }} + 2{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}^ - }$<br>${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell}}}} = {\\rm{E}}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{{\\rm{cell}}}} - \\frac{{{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.303RT}}}}{{{\\rm{nF}}}}{\\rm{log}}\\frac{{\\left[ {{\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{2 + }}} \\right]{{\\left[ {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}^ - }} \\right]}^2}}}{{{{\\rm{p}}_{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}}}$<br>Hence, to increase ${\\rm{E,}}{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{1}}}$ should be decreased and ${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ should be increased, i.e. $\\left[ {{\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}} \\right]$ and $\\left[ {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}^ - }} \\right]$ should be decreased and ${{\\rm{p}}_{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}$ should be increased.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24226,'chemistry',63,816,1796,'The example/s to show the anomalous behaviour of fluorine is','[{"text": "HF is liquid while other hydrohalides are gases.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fluorine exhibits only (-1) oxidate state in its compounds.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Flourine reacts with water gives oxygen while other halogens do not", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24228,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Hydrolysis of ethyl acetate in presence of acid is a reaction of first order whereas in presence of alkali, it is a reaction of second order.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Acid only acts as a catalyst whereas alkali acts as one of the reactants.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOC}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{H}^{+}}$<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}+\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$<br>Rate $\\propto\\left[\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOC}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right]$<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOC}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}+\\mathrm{NaOH} \\rightarrow$<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONa}+\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$<br>Rate $\\propto\\left[\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOC}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right][\\mathrm{NaOH}]$<br>Thus option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24261,'chemistry',63,818,1806,'${\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O + C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\to {\\rm{A + B}}$<br>${\\text{NaCl + }}\\mathop {\\text{A}}\\limits_{{\\text{( Conc}}{\\text{.) }}} \\xrightarrow{{{\\text{420K}}}}{\\text{B + Sodiu}}{{\\text{m}}_{{\\text{(Acidic) }}}}{\\text{ salt }}$<br>Compound (A) is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{A}: \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\mathrm{~B}: \\mathrm{HCl}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24229,'chemistry',59,367,680,'Two concentration cells of ${\\rm{Ag}}$ with ${\\rm{Ag}}$ electrode are dipped in solution of $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$. In first cell, concentration of one electrode is $1 \\mathrm{M}$ and other electrode is 0.1 and emf is $0.06 \\mathrm{~V}$. In second cell, concentration of one electrode is $1 \\mathrm{M}$ and other electrode is $0.01 \\mathrm{M}$. Calculate the emf of second cell.','[{"text": "$0.12 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.06 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.09 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.16 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell }}}} = {{\\rm{E}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}} - \\frac{{0.059}}{{\\rm{n}}}{\\rm{log}}\\,\\frac{{{\\rm{[ Product ]}}}}{{{\\rm{[ Reactant ]}}}}$<br>${\\left( {{{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell }}}}} \\right)_1} = 0 - \\frac{{0.059}}{1}\\log \\frac{{0.1}}{1}$<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n0.06 =  - \\frac{{0.059}}{1}{\\rm{log}}\\frac{1}{{10}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} (1)\\\\\n{\\left( {{{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell}}}}} \\right)_2} = 0 - \\frac{{0.059}}{1}{\\rm{log}}\\frac{{0.01}}{1}\\\\\n{\\left( {{{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell}}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{2}}} =  - \\frac{{0.059}}{1}{\\rm{log}}\\frac{1}{{100}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} (2)\n\\end{array}$<br>Dividing Eq. (1) by Eq. (2)<br>$\\frac{{0.06}}{{{{\\left( {{{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell }}}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{2}}}}} = \\frac{{\\log 1 - \\log 10}}{{\\log 1 - \\log 100}}$<br>$\\frac{{0.06}}{{{{\\left( {{{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell }}}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{2}}}}} = \\frac{{0 - 1}}{{0 - 2\\log 10}}$<br>$\\frac{{0.06}}{{{{\\left( {{{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell }}}}} \\right)}_2}}} = \\frac{{ - 1}}{{ - 2}} = \\frac{1}{2}$<br>${\\left( {{{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell }}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{2}}} = 2 \\times 0.06$<br>$ = 0.12\\,{\\rm{V}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24230,'chemistry',63,826,1829,'Explain why inspite of nearly the same electronegativity, nitrogen forms hydrogen bonding while chlorine does not.','[{"text": "Due to small size of Nitrogen", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Half filled p-orbital configuration in $\\\\mathrm{N}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chlorine has high electron affinity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Due to more electronegativity of $\\\\mathrm{N}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24232,'chemistry',60,586,1177,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For the reaction,<br>$2 \\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5} \\rightarrow 4 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}+\\mathrm{O}_{2}$, Rate $=\\mathrm{k}\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}\\right]$<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Rate of decomposition of $\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}$ is determined by slow step.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Decomposition of $\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}$ occurs as follows  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/60_24232_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Generally, in a complex reaction, the rate of the overall reaction is determined by the slow step reaction. Also, it follows first order kinetics. Therefore, Rate $=\\mathrm{k}\\left[\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}\\right]$. So option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24233,'chemistry',59,367,680,'If hydrogen electrode dipped in two solutions of $\\mathrm{pH}=3$ and $\\mathrm{pH}=6$ and salt bridge is connected. The emf of resulting cell is','[{"text": "$0.177 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.3 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.052 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.104 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For concentration cells,<br>${\\rm{E}}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{{\\rm{cell}}}} = \\frac{{0.0591}}{1}\\log \\left[ {\\frac{{{\\rm{ anode }}}}{{{\\rm{ cathode }}}}} \\right]$<br>Given, ${\\rm{pH}} = 3$ for first solution and<br>${\\rm{pH}} = 6$ for second solution<br>$\\therefore\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]$ for first solution $=10^{-\\mathrm{pH}}$<br>$=10^{-3} \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$ and $\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]$for second solution<br>$=10^{-\\mathrm{pH}}=10^{-6} \\mathrm{~mol} / \\mathrm{L}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {\\rm{E}}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{{\\rm{cell}}}} =  - \\frac{{0.0591}}{1}\\log \\left[ {\\frac{{{{10}^{ - 6}}}}{{{{10}^{ - 3}}}}} \\right]$<br>$=-0.0591 \\log 10^{-3}$<br>$ =  - 0.0591 \\times ( - 3\\log 10)$<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n =  - 0.0591 \\times ( - 3 \\times 1)\\\\\n =  - 0.0591 \\times ( - 3)\n\\end{array}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {\\rm{E}}^\\circ \\,{\\rm{ = }}\\,{\\rm{0}}.{\\rm{1773}};{\\rm{V}} \\sim {\\rm{0}}.{\\rm{177}}\\,{\\rm{V}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24234,'chemistry',63,818,1806,'Name poisonous gases which can be prepared from chlorine gas.','[{"text": "Phosgene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mustard gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chloropicrin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24236,'chemistry',60,587,1180,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Half-life period of a reaction of first order is independent of initial concentration.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Half-life period for a first order<br>reaction, $\\mathrm{t}_{1 / 2}=\\dfrac{2.303}{\\mathrm{k}} \\log 2$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For first order reaction,<br>$\\mathrm{t}_{1 / 2}=\\dfrac{2.303}{\\mathrm{k}} \\log 2=\\dfrac{0.693}{\\mathrm{k}}$<br>Thus option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24238,'chemistry',63,818,1807,'When $\\mathrm{HCl}$ reacts with finely powdered iron, it forms ferrous chloride and not ferric chloride. Why?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCl}$ is not strong enough to oxidise $\\\\mathrm{Fe}$ to $\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ produced in reaction if escapes prevents formation of ferric chloride", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Iron prefer to be in $\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{+2}$ state", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(1) and (3) are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{HCl}$ reaction with iron produces $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$.<br>$\\mathrm{Fe}+2 \\mathrm{HCl} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2}$<br>Liberation of hydrogen prevents the formation of ferric chloride.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24240,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A reaction takes place because the reacting molecules collide with each other.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The rate of reaction depends on the total number of collisions per second per unit volume.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The rate of a chemical reaction hinges on the frequency and effectiveness of high-energy collisions between reactant molecules, as only collisions with energy surpassing the activation barrier lead to effective reactions and product formation. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24241,'chemistry',59,374,702,'When lead storage battery is charged','[{"text": "lead dioxide dissolves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sulphuric acid is regenerated", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "the lead electrode becomes coated with lead sulphate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the amount of sulphuric acid decreases", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'During the charging of a lead storage battery, the reactions at the anode and cathode are :<br>${\\rm{At}}\\,{\\rm{anode}}$<br>$\\mathrm{PbSO}_{4}+2 e^{-} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Pb}+\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$<br>${\\rm{At}}\\,{\\rm{cathode}}$<br>${\\rm{PbS}}{{\\rm{O}}_4} + 2{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}} \\to {\\rm{Pb}}{{\\rm{O}}_2}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, + {\\rm{4}}{{\\rm{H}}^ + } + {\\rm{SO}}_4^{2 - } + 2{\\rm{e}}_2^ - $<br>In both the reactions, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ is regenerated.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24242,'chemistry',63,818,1806,'I. $\\mathop {{\\rm{2NaOH}}}\\limits_{\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}{{\\rm{(cold}}\\,{\\rm{and}}}\\\\{{\\rm{dilute}})}\\end{array}} {\\rm{ + C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\to {\\rm{A + B + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>II. $\\mathop {{\\rm{NaOH}}}\\limits_{\\scriptstyle{\\rm{ (hot}}\\,{\\rm{and }}\\atop\\scriptstyle{\\rm{ conc) }}} {\\rm{ + 3C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\to {\\rm{C + D + 3}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>Here, $\\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{B}, \\mathrm{C}$ and $\\mathrm{D}$ refer to','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{NaCl} ; \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{NaClO}_{3} ; \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{NaOCl} ; \\\\mathrm{D}-5 \\\\mathrm{NaCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-5 \\\\mathrm{NaCl} ; \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{NaClO}_{3} ; \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{NaOCl} ; \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{NaCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{NaCl} ; \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{NaOCl} ; \\\\mathrm{C}-5 \\\\mathrm{NaCl} ; \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{NaClO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-5 \\\\mathrm{NaCl} ; \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{NaOCl} ; \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{NaCl} ; \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{NaClO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'I. $\\mathop {2{\\rm{NaOH}}}\\limits_{\\scriptstyle{\\rm{ cold}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and }}\\atop\\scriptstyle{\\rm{ dilute }}}  + {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_2} \\to {\\rm{NaCl}} + {\\rm{NaOCl}} + {{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}$<br>$\\text { II. } \\underset{\\substack{\\text { (hot and } \\\\ \\text { conc) }}}{2 \\mathrm{NaOH}}+3 \\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\rightarrow 5 \\mathrm{NaCl}+\\mathrm{NaClO}_{3}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24244,'chemistry',59,374,702,'When an acid cell is charged then','[{"text": "voltage of cell increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "electrolyte of cell dilutes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "resistance of cell increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The discharged acid cell (lead storage battery or cell) can be recharged by passing a direct current through in opposite direction. During recharging of cell, the electrode material are restored in their original forms.Due to formation of $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$and $\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$ ions,the specific gravity of sulphuric acid increases and it attains the original value $\\left(1.3 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{cm}^{3}\\right)$ and also its voltage increases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24269,'chemistry',63,820,1814,'Why is $\\mathrm{ICl}$ more reactive than $\\mathrm{I}_{2}$ ?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{I}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{I}}$ bond is very difficult to break", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{I}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{I}}$ bond is easy to break", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{I}-\\\\mathrm{Cl}$ bond is weak", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{I}-\\\\mathrm{Cl}$ bond is strong", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In general, interhalogen compounds are more reactive than halogens due to weaker ${\\rm{X\\_}}{{\\rm{X}}^{\\rm{1}}}$ bonding than ${\\rm{X - X}}$ bond. Thus $\\mathrm{ICl}$ is more reactive than ${{\\rm{I}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24245,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Most probable velocity is the velocity possessed by maximum dfraction of molecules at the same temperature.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> On collision, more and more molecules acquire higher speed at the same temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'On collision, the velocities of the individual particles changes such that the total kinetic energy remains conserved. More and more molecules can acquire higher speed only when the temperature is raised. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24246,'chemistry',63,826,1829,'A black compound of manganese reacts with a halogen acid to give greenish yellow gas. When excess of this gas reacts with $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ an unstable trihalide is formed. In this process, the oxidation state of nitrogen changes from','[{"text": "-3 to +3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "-3 to 0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "-3 to +5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0 to -3", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Greenish yellow gas is chlorine which reacts with ammonia to give unstable $\\mathrm{NCl}_{3}$. Oxidation state of $\\mathrm{N}$ in ammonia is - 3 which changes to +3 in $\\mathrm{NCl}_{3}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24248,'chemistry',59,373,695,'Which of the following will form the cathode with respect to iron anode in an electrolyte cell?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mg}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Al}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Zn}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In electrolytic cell the cathode is of higher reduction potential.Out of the given metals, Mg has higher reduction potential , hence, act as cathode.<br>So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24249,'chemistry',60,585,1176,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The order of a reaction may be negative.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In some cases, the rate of reaction decreases as the concentration of the reactant increases.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A negative order with respect to a substance in a chemical reaction indicates that as the concentration of the substance increases, the reaction rate decreases. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24250,'chemistry',63,817,1801,'On reaction with water,<br>I. Fluorine oxidises water to oxygen.<br>II. Chlorine and bromine react with water to form corresponding hydrohalic and hypohalous acids.<br>III. Reaction of iodine with water is spontaneous.<br>Which of the above mentioned statement(s) is/are correct?','[{"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Fluorine oxidises water to oxygen whereas chlorine and bromine react with water to form corresponding hydrohalic and hypohalous acids. The reaction of iodine with water is non-spontaneous.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24252,'chemistry',59,373,696,'When the sample of copper with zinc impurity is to be purified by electrolysis, the appropriate electrodes are<br> $\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\bf{Cathode}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}&{{\\bf{Anode}}}\n\\end{array}$','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{Pure}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\rm{zinc}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\, - {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{Pure}}{\\\\mkern 1mu}\\\\, {\\\\rm{copper}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Impure}}{\\\\mkern 1mu}\\\\, {\\\\rm{sample}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,\\\\, - \\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{Pure}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{copper}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Impure}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\rm{zinc}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\, - {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{Impure}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{sample}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Pure}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\rm{copper}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\, - {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{Impure}}{\\\\mkern 1mu}\\\\, {\\\\rm{sample}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In electrolytic purification cathode is of pure metal and anode is of impure metal.<br>${\\text{At}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{anode:M}} \\to {{\\text{M}}^{{\\text{n + }}}}{\\text{ + n\\bar e}}$<br>${\\text{At}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{cathode:}}{{\\text{M}}^{{\\text{n + }}}}{\\text{ + n\\bar e}} \\to {\\text{M}}.$<br>The pure metal is thus deposited at cathode.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24381,'chemistry',63,808,1753,'On heating with concentrated $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ solution in an inert atmosphere of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$, white phosphorus gives a gas. Which of the following statement is incorrect about the gas?','[{"text": "It is highly poisonous and has smell like rotten fish.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It\'s solution in water decomposes in the<br>presence of light.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is more basic than $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is less basic than $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Gas is $\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$. It is less basic than $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24253,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The rate law equation can be found only by experiment.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It can be written from stoichiometric equation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The rate law equation, expressing the relationship between reaction rate and reactant concentrations, is determined experimentally through kinetic studies, considering factors like reaction mechanisms and orders, and cannot be directly inferred from the stoichiometric equation. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24254,'chemistry',63,818,1809,'Explain why the stability of oxoacids of chlorine increases in the order given below :<br>$\\mathrm{HClO} < \\mathrm{HClO}_{2} < \\mathrm{HClO}_{3} < \\mathrm{HClO}_{4}$','[{"text": "Dispersal of negative charge on Chlorine increases with number of oxygens", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "More oxygen more stability", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxygen forms double bonds with chlorine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chlorine donates electrons to oxygen", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Oxygen is more electronegative than chlorine, therefore dispersal of negative charge present on chlorine increases from $\\mathrm{ClO}^{-}$to $\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ ion because number of oxygen atoms attached to chlorine is increasing. Therefore, stability of ions will increase in the order given below:<br>$\\mathrm{ClO}^{-} < \\mathrm{ClO}_{2}^{-} < \\mathrm{ClO}_{3}^{-} < \\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$<br>Thus, due to increase in stability of conjugate base, acidic strength of corresponding acid increases in the following order:<br>$\\mathrm{HClO} < \\mathrm{HClO}_{2} < \\mathrm{HClO}_{3} < \\mathrm{HClO}_{4}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24256,'chemistry',59,370,692,'The conductivity of a saturated solution of<br> $\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}$ is $3.06 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{ohm}^{-1} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$ and its<br> equivalent conductance is ${\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.53}}\\,{\\rm{oh}}{{\\rm{m}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}{\\rm{\\,c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{2}}}$<br> equiv ${ }^{-1}$. The $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{sp}}$ for $\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}$ will be','[{"text": "$4 \\\\times 10^{-12}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times 10^{-9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times 10^{-13}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times 10^{-6}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{Solubility = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{conductivity \\times 1000}}}}{{{{\\rm{\\Lambda }}_{{\\rm{eq}}}}}}$<br> ${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{3}}{\\rm{.06 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 6}}}}{\\rm{ \\times 1000}}}}{{{\\rm{1}}{\\rm{.53}}}}{\\rm{ = 2 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}$<br> ${{\\rm{K}}_{{\\rm{sp }}}}{\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{S}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 4 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 6}}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24257,'chemistry',63,817,1799,'Among the following, the one that has the highest heat of vaporization is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HF}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HBr}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HI}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'HF shows hydrogen bonding. So high heat of vaporization.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24258,'chemistry',60,587,1181,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Hydrolysis of cane sugar is a pseudo - first order reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Water is present in large excess during hydrolysis.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The hydrolysis of cane sugar is considered as a pseudo-first-order reaction due to the excess and constant concentration of water. Hence, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24260,'chemistry',59,371,693,'In a cell that utilises the reaction<br> $\\mathrm{Zn}(\\mathrm{s})+2 \\mathrm{H}^{+}(\\mathrm{aq}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}(\\mathrm{aq})+\\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$<br> addition of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ to cathode compartment, will','[{"text": "increase the E and shift equilibrium to the right.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "lower the $E$ and shift equilibrium to the right.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "lower the E and shift equlibrium to the left.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increase the $\\\\mathrm{E}$ and shift equilibrium to the left.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{Zn(s) + 2}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{ + (aq)}}\\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{(aq) + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{(g)}}\n\\end{array}$<br> ${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell}}}}{\\rm{ = E}}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{{\\rm{cell}}}}{\\rm{ - }}\\frac{{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.059}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{log}}\\frac{{{\\rm{[Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{][}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{]}}}}{{{{{\\rm{[}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}{\\rm{]}}}^{\\rm{2}}}}}$<br> Addition of ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ will increase $\\left[ {{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}} \\right]$ and <br>${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell }}}}$ will also increase and the equilibrium will shift towards RHS',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24262,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> According to transition state theory, for the formation of an activated complex, one of the vibrational degrees of freedom is converted into a translational degree of freedom.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Energy of the activated complex is higher than the energy of reactant molecules.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{B} \\rightleftharpoons \\underset{\\text { Intermediate }}{(\\mathrm{AB})} \\rightarrow$ Product<br>The activated complex, representing a unique chemical species, can be viewed as an unstable molecule in equilibrium with reactants, experiencing a conversion of vibrational to translational degrees of freedom along the reaction coordinate. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24264,'chemistry',59,373,697,'How long a current of 3 amperes has to be passed through a solution of $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ to coat a metal surface of $80 \\mathrm{~cm}^{2}$ and $0.005 \\mathrm{~mm}$ thick layer. Density of Ag is $10.5 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-3}$.','[{"text": "125.1 seconds", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "12.5 seconds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "155.2 seconds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "200 seconds", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{Volume = Area \\times thickness}}$<br> ${\\rm{Mass = Volume \\times density}}$<br> $\\therefore {\\rm{Mass}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{Ag}}\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,{\\rm{be}}\\,{\\rm{deposited}}$<br> ${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{80 \\times 0}}{\\rm{.005}}}}{{{\\rm{10}}}}{\\rm{ \\times 10}}{\\rm{.5 = 0}}{\\rm{.42\\,g}}$<br> ${\\rm{Amount}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{deposited}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{E}}.{\\rm{wt}} \\times {\\rm{i}} \\times {\\rm{t}}}}{{96500}}$<br> $\\therefore {\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.42 = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{108 \\times 3 \\times t}}}}{{{\\rm{96500}}}}$<br> $\\therefore {\\rm{t = 125}}{\\rm{.1}}\\,\\,{\\rm{seconds}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24265,'chemistry',63,820,1814,'The iodine atom in $\\mathrm{ICl}_{2}^{-}$involves','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{s}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{d}}$ hybridisation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{s}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{d}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$ hybridisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{s}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$ hybridisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{s}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{3}}}$ hydridisation", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{2bp + 3lp}} \\to {\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{d}}$ hybridisation',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24266,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The kinetics of the reaction, ${\\text{mA +  nB  +  pC}} \\to {m^\\prime }X + {n^\\prime }Y + {p^\\prime }Z$  obeys the rate expression as $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{dx}}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{\\mathrm{m}}[\\mathrm{B}]^{\\mathrm{n}}$<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The rate of reaction does not depend upon the concentration of $\\mathrm{C}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From the rate expression, represented as $\\mathrm{dx} / \\mathrm{dt}=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{A}]^{\\mathrm{m}} \\times[\\mathrm{B}]^{\\mathrm{n}}$, it is evident that the total order of the reaction is ${\\text{m  +  n}}$. The key point to note is that the rate of the reaction is entirely independent of the concentration of $\\mathrm{C}$, as indicated by the zero order with respect to $\\mathrm{C}$. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24268,'chemistry',59,365,677,'Two electrochemical cell $\\mathrm{Fe} / \\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\\left\\|\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\\right\\| \\mathrm{Cu}$<br> and $\\mathrm{Zn} / \\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\\left\\|\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\\right\\| \\mathrm{Cu}$ are connected in series.<br>$\\left( \\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}{\\rm{/Fe}} =  - \\,{\\rm{0}}.{\\rm{41}}\\,{\\rm{V}},{\\rm{Z}}{{\\rm{n}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}/{\\rm{Zn}} =  - \\,{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.076}}\\,{\\rm{V}},\\\\\n{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{u}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}/{\\rm{Cu}} =  + \\,{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.34}}\n\\end{array} \\right)$.\nThe net emf of this cell is','[{"text": "$1.10 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.75\\\\,V}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.35 \\\\mathrm{~V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.85\\\\,V}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For Fe & Cu cell, Fe is anode and Cu is cathode,<br>${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell }}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.34 - ( - 0}}{\\rm{.41) = 0}}{\\rm{.75\\,V}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{for}}$<br> Zn & Cu cell, Zn is anode and Cu is cathode,<br>  ${{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{cell }}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.34 - ( - 0}}{\\rm{.76) = 1}}{\\rm{.10\\,V}}$.<br> So net emf ${\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.75\\,V + 1}}{\\rm{.10\\,V =  + 1}}{\\rm{.85\\,V}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24270,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In rate law, unlike in the expression for equilibrium constants, the exponents for concentrations do not necessarily match the stoichiometric coefficients.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It is the mechanism and not the balanced chemical equation for the overall change that governs the reaction rate.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The rate law, distinct from equilibrium constants, delineates the relationship between reaction rate and reactant concentrations based on the specific mechanism, experimentally determining reaction orders, and accommodating catalyst impact on rate. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24272,'chemistry',59,372,694,'At $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, the molar conductances at infinite dilution for the strong electrolytes $\\mathrm{NaOH}$, $\\mathrm{NaCl}$ and $\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}$ are $248 \\times 10^{-4}, 126 \\times 10^{-4}$and $280 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~S} \\mathrm{~m}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ respectively. $\\lambda {^\\circ _{\\text{m}}}{\\text{Ba}}{({\\text{OH}})_2}$ in ${\\text{S}}{\\mkern 1mu} {{\\text{m}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{ - 1}}$ is','[{"text": "$52.4 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$524 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$402 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$262 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{NaOH} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Ba}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}+2 \\mathrm{NaCl}$<br>${\\rm{\\lambda }}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{{\\rm{Ba(OH}}{{\\rm{)}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{\\rm{ = \\lambda }}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{{\\rm{BaC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}{\\rm{ + }}\\,{\\rm{2\\lambda }}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{{\\rm{NaOH}}}}{\\rm{ - 2\\lambda }}{{\\rm{^\\circ }}_{{\\rm{NaCl}}}}$<br>$ = 280 \\times {10^{ - 4}} + 2 \\times 248 \\times {10^{ - 4}} - 2 \\times 126 \\times {10^{ - 4}}$<br>$ = (280 + 496 - 252) \\times {10^{ - 4}}$<br>$ = 524 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,{\\text{S}}\\,{{\\text{m}}^2}\\,{\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
