(24311,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'Consider the following reaction that goes from A to B in three steps as shown below:<br> <br>Choose the correct option.','[{"text": "Number of Intermediates : 2<br>Number of Activated complexes : 3<br>Rate determining step : III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Number of Intermediates : 3<br>Number of Activated complexes : 2<br>Rate determining step : II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Number of Intermediates : 2<br>Number of Activated complexes : 3<br>Rate determining step : I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Number of Intermediates : 2<br>Number of Activated complexes : 3<br>Rate determining step : II", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/60_24311_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The species formed at the end of the in-between steps (step I and II in this case) are the reaction intermediates. They are depicted in the graph using points 2 and 4 . Each step has an activated complex. Thus, there are 3 activation complexes (points 1,3 and 5 in the graph). Slowest step is the rate determining step. Higher the activation energy, slower the step.<br>${{\\rm{E}}_{{{\\rm{a}}_2}}}$ is greater than ${{\\rm{E}}_{{{\\rm{a}}_1}}}$ and ${{\\rm{E}}_{{{\\rm{a}}_3}}}$ Thus, step II is the rate determining step.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_24311_question_1.png'),
