(24481,'chemistry',60,587,1180,'Under the same reaction conditions, initial concentration of  $1.386\\,{\\text{mol}}\\,\\,{\\text{d}}{{\\text{m}}^{ - 3}}$  of a substance becomes half in 40 seconds and 20 seconds through first order and zero order kinetics, respectively. Ratio $\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{k}_{1}}{\\mathrm{k}_{0}}\\right)$ of rate constant for first order $\\left(k_{1}\\right)$ and zero order $\\left(k_{0}\\right)$ of the reaction is','[{"text": "$0.5\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{d}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.0\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{d}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{d}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0\\\\,{\\\\rm{mo}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{d}}{{\\\\rm{m}}^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\left( {{{\\rm{t}}_{1/2}}} \\right)_1} = \\frac{{0.693}}{{{{\\rm{k}}_{\\rm{1}}}}} = 40\\,\\,{\\rm{sec}}$<br>${\\left( {{{\\rm{t}}_{1/2}}} \\right)_0} = \\frac{{{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{0}}}}}{{2{{\\rm{k}}_{\\rm{0}}}}} = 20\\,\\,{\\rm{sec}}$<br>given, $\\mathrm{a}_{0}=1.386$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\left(\\mathrm{t}_{1 / 2}\\right)_{1}}{\\left(\\mathrm{t}_{1 / 2}\\right)_{0}}=\\dfrac{0.693 \\times 2 \\mathrm{k}_{0}}{\\mathrm{k}_{1} \\times 1.386}=\\dfrac{40}{20}$<br>on solving<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\mathrm{k}_{1}}{\\mathrm{k}_{0}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24482,'chemistry',59,371,693,'The conductivity of ${\\mathrm{0.1 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{~L}^{-1}\\, \\mathrm{KCl}}}$ solution is ${\\mathrm{1.41 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{~S} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}}}$. What is its molar conductivity (in ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S} \\mathrm{~cm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}}$ )?','[{"text": "1.41", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "14.1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "141", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.141", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Molar conductivity ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\wedge_{\\mathrm{m}}\\right)=\\dfrac{1000 \\mathrm{~K}}{\\mathrm{C}}}}$$\\begin{align*}& =\\dfrac{1000 \\times 1.41 \\times 10^{-3}}{0.1} \\\\& =14.1 \\mathrm{~S} \\mathrm{~cm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\end{align*}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24485,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'For a given reaction, energy of activation for forward reaction $\\left(\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{af}}\\right)$ is $80 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ and $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}=40 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$. A catalyst lowers $\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{af}}$ to $20 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$. The ratio of energy of activation for reverse reaction before and after addition of catalyst is','[{"text": "1.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/60_24485_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Before adding catalyst,<br>$\\left(\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}\\right)_{\\mathrm{b}}=80+40=120 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-}$<br>After adding catalyst,<br>$\\left(\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}\\right)_{\\mathrm{b}}^{\\prime}=20+40=60 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{\\left(\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}\\right)_{\\mathrm{b}}}{\\left(\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{a}}\\right)_{\\mathrm{b}}^{\\prime}}=\\dfrac{120}{60}=2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24486,'chemistry',63,814,1785,'The key step in the manufacture of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ is','[{"text": "Burning of sulphur or sulphide ores in air to generate $\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Catalytic oxidation of $\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ with $\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to give $\\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ in the presence of $\\\\mathrm{V}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{5}$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Absorption of $\\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ in $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ to give oleum.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Either (2) or (3)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The key step in the manufacture of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ is<br>${\\text{2S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}({\\text{g}}){\\text{ + }}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}({\\text{g}})\\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{v}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{5}}}}}{\\text{2S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}({\\text{g}});$<br>${{\\rm{\\Delta }}_{\\rm{r}}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{ =  - 196}}{\\rm{.6\\,kJ\\,mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$<br>The reaction is exothermic, reversible and the forward reaction leads to a decrease in volume. Thus, low temperature and high pressure are the favourable conditions for maximum yield. But the reaction should not be very low otherwise rate of reaction will become slow.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
