(24490,'chemistry',60,591,1188,'Match the column I with column II and mark the appropriate choice.<br><div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q,B}} - {\\\\rm{P,C}} - {\\\\rm{T,D}} - {\\\\rm{S,E}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P,B}} - {\\\\rm{Q,C}} - {\\\\rm{T,D}} - {\\\\rm{S,E}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R,B}} - {\\\\rm{S,C}} - {\\\\rm{Q,D}} - {\\\\rm{T,E}} - {\\\\rm{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S,B}} - {\\\\rm{Q,C}} - {\\\\rm{R,D}} - {\\\\rm{P,E}} - {\\\\rm{T}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Collision theory is for bimolecular reactions.Zero order reaction, rate constant units are mol. lit $^{-}$sec $^{-}$photo chemical reaction is an example of zero order reaction. Intercept in Arrhenius plot is $\\ln \\mathrm{A}$.<br>Order of reaction can be $+\\mathrm{ve},-\\mathrm{ve}$, zero or dfractional.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_24490_question_1.png'),
(24492,'chemistry',60,588,1184,'One proposed mechanism of the reaction of HBr with $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is given here<br>${\\rm{HBr}} + {{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\to {\\rm{HOOBr}}\\quad \\quad \\quad \\,\\,({\\rm{slow}})$<br>${\\rm{HOOBr}} + {\\rm{HBr}} \\to 2{\\rm{HOBr}}\\,\\quad \\,\\,({\\rm{fast}})$<br>${\\rm{HOBr}} + {\\rm{HBr}} \\to {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}} + {\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\quad ({\\rm{fast}})$<br>What is the equation for the overall reaction?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HBr}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{HOOBr}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{HBr}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\mathrm{HBr}+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}+2 \\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{HOBr} \\\\rightarrow 2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}+\\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Multiply $3^{\\text {rd }}$ equation with 2 and add all equations.<br>$\\mathrm{HBr}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{HOOBr}$<br>$\\mathrm{HOOBr}+\\mathrm{HBr} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{HOBr}$<br>$\\underline{2 \\mathrm{HOBr}+2 \\mathrm{HBr} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+2 \\mathrm{Br}_{2}}$<br>$4 \\mathrm{HBr}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+2 \\mathrm{Br}_{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24493,'chemistry',59,366,679,'Standard reduction potentials of some species ${\\mathrm{P}}$, Q. R. S, T, U, W are ${\\mathrm{-2.90 \\mathrm{~V},+0.34 \\mathrm{~V}}}$, ${\\mathrm{+1.20 \\mathrm{~V},-0.76 \\mathrm{~V},-1.35 \\mathrm{~V},+0.11 \\mathrm{~V},-0.40 \\mathrm{~V}}}$, respectively. How many of these can oxidize hydrogen gas?','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The standard reduction potential of hydrogen electrode is zero. Hence, any species having electrode potential greater than zero can oxidize hydrogen gas. Thus, ${\\mathrm{Q, R}}$ and ${\\mathrm{U}}$ can oxidize hydrogen gas.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
