(24505,'chemistry',59,373,697,'How many Faradays of electricity are required to oxidize 1 mol of water to dioxygen?','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{H}^{+}+2 \\mathrm{e}^{-}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$<br>Hence, for the oxidation of one mole of water to dioxygen, 2 moles of electrons ${\\mathrm{=2 \\mathrm{~F}}}$ electricity is required.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24506,'chemistry',60,592,1189,'In the reversible reaction, $2{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_2}\\mathop  \\rightleftharpoons \\limits_{{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{2}}}}^{{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}} {{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}$<br> the rate of disappearance of $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ is equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{2}}{{\\\\rm{k}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}}}{{{{\\\\rm{k}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}}}{\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}} \\\\right]^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2{{\\\\rm{k}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}} \\\\right]^{\\\\rm{2}}} - 2{{\\\\rm{k}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{\\\\,}}{{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2{{\\\\rm{k}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}{\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}} \\\\right]^2} - {{\\\\rm{k}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{\\\\,}}{{\\\\rm{N}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {2{{\\\\rm{k}}_1} - {{\\\\rm{k}}_2}} \\\\right)\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2} \\underset{k_{2}}{\\stackrel{k_{1}}{\\rightleftharpoons}} \\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$<br>Rate of reaction $ =  - \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{{\\text{d}}\\left[ {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = {{\\text{k}}_1}{\\left[ {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]^2} - {{\\text{k}}_2}\\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}} \\right]$<br>$\\therefore$ Rate of disappearance of $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$<br>i.e. $\\frac{{ - {\\text{d}}\\left[ {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = {\\text{2}}{{\\text{k}}_1}{\\left[ {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]^2} - 2{{\\text{k}}_2}\\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}} \\right]$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24508,'chemistry',63,816,1792,'Sea is the greatest source of','[{"text": "Only Chlorides", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only Bromides", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only Iodides", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Sea water contains chlorides, bromides and iodides of sodium, potassium, magnesium and calcium but is mainly sodium chloride solution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24510,'chemistry',60,592,1189,'For the set of reactions<br>${\\text{(i) A   +   B}}\\mathop  \\rightleftharpoons \\limits_{{k_2}}^{{k_1}} C,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{(ii) C}} + {\\text{B}}\\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{3}}}}}{\\text{D}}$<br>${{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}{\\text{[A][B]}} - {{\\text{k}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{[C]}} - {{\\text{k}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{[C][B]}}$ is equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{ - {\\\\text{d[A]}}}}{{{\\\\text{dt}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{ - {\\\\text{d[B]}}}}{{{\\\\text{dt}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\text{d[C]}}}}{{{\\\\text{dt}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\text{d[D]}}}}{{{\\\\text{dt}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For equilibrium reaction (i),<br>Rate of forward reaction $ = {{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}{\\text{[A][B]}}$<br>Rate of backward reaction $ = {{\\text{k}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{[C]}}$<br>Hence, ${{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}{\\text{[A][B]}} - {{\\text{k}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{[C]}}$; represent the production of ${\\text{[C]}}$.<br>For reaction (ii), $\\frac{{{\\text{d[D]}}}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = {{\\text{k}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{[C][B]}}$<br>Production of ${\\text{[D]}}$ is governed by production of reactant ${\\text{[C]}}$ in reaction (i).<br>Hence, ${{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}{\\text{[A][B]}} - {{\\text{k}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{[C]}} - {{\\text{k}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{[C][B]}} = \\frac{{{\\text{d[D]}}}}{{{\\text{dt}}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24512,'chemistry',63,816,1793,'Consider the following statements.<br>I. Due to maximum effective nuclear charge, the halogens have the smallest atomic radii in their respective periods.<br>II. These have low ionisation enthalpy.<br>III. Halogens have maximum negative electron gain enthalpy.<br>IV. Fluorine is the most electronegative element in the periodic Table.<br>The correct statements are','[{"text": "I, II, III, IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III, IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II, III, IV", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Due to maximum effective nuclear charge, the halogens have the smallest atomic radii in their respective periods. These have high ionisation enthalpies, due to their little tendency to lose electron. Halogens have maximum electron gain enthalpy and fluorine is the most electronegative element in the Periodic Table.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24513,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'Consider the decomposition of $\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}$ as<br>$\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5} \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}$<br>The rate of reaction is given by<br>$\\frac{{ - {\\text{d}}\\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{5}}}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{{\\text{d}}\\left[ {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = 2\\frac{{{\\text{d}}\\left[ {{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = {{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}\\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{5}}}} \\right]$<br>Therefore, $\\frac{{ - {\\text{d}}\\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_5}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = {{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}\\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{5}}}} \\right]$<br>$\\frac{{{\\text{  +  d}}\\left[ {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = 2{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}\\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{5}}}} \\right] = {\\text{k}}_1^\\prime \\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{5}}}} \\right]$<br>$\\frac{{{\\text{  +  d}}\\left[ {{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = \\frac{1}{2}{{\\text{k}}_1}\\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{5}}}} \\right] = {\\text{k}}_1^{\\prime \\prime }\\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{5}}}} \\right]$<br>Choose the correct option.','[{"text": "${{\\\\text{k}}_1} = {\\\\text{k}}_1^\\\\prime  = {\\\\text{k}}_1^{\\\\prime \\\\prime }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{k}}_1} = 2{\\\\text{k}}_1^\\\\prime  = {\\\\text{k}}_1^{\\\\prime \\\\prime }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4{{\\\\text{k}}_1} = {\\\\text{k}}_1^\\\\prime  = 2{\\\\text{k}}_1^{\\\\prime \\\\prime }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2{{\\\\text{k}}_1} = {\\\\text{k}}_1^\\\\prime  = 4{\\\\text{k}}_1^{\\\\prime \\\\prime }$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given,  $\\frac{{ - {\\text{d}}\\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{5}}}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{{\\text{d}}\\left[ {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{\\text{2d}}\\left[ {{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = {{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}\\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{5}}}} \\right]$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{{\\text{k}}_1}\\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{5}}}} \\right] = \\frac{{{\\text{k}}_1^\\prime }}{2}\\left[ {\\,{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_5}} \\right] = 2{\\text{k}}_1^{\\prime \\prime }\\left[ {{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_5}} \\right]$<br>or  $2{k_1} = k_1^\\prime  = 4{\\text{k}}_1^{\\prime \\prime }$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24516,'chemistry',63,817,1799,'Affinity for hydrogen decreases in the group from fluorine to iodine. Which of the halogen acids should have the highest bond dissociation enthalpy?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HF}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HBr}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HI}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Due to less bond length and strong hydrogen bond',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24517,'chemistry',60,587,1180,'Formula for half- life of a zero order reaction is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{{\\\\text{C}}_{\\\\text{0}}}}}{{\\\\text{k}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{{\\\\text{C}}_{\\\\text{0}}}}}{{{\\\\text{2k}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\text{2}}{{\\\\text{C}}_{\\\\text{0}}}}}{{\\\\text{k}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\text{2}}{{\\\\text{C}}_{\\\\text{0}}}}}{{{\\\\text{2k}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For zero order reaction, if concentration of product is $\\left[ {{{\\text{C}}_0}} \\right]$ at time ${{\\text{t}}_{\\text{1}}}$ and $\\frac{{\\left[ {{{\\text{C}}_0}} \\right]}}{2}$ at time ${{\\text{t}}_{\\text{2}}}$, then<br>${\\text{k}} = \\frac{{\\left[ {\\frac{{{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{0}}}}}{{\\text{2}}}} \\right] - \\left[ {{{\\text{C}}_0}} \\right]}}{{{{\\text{t}}_2} - {{\\text{t}}_1}}} = \\frac{{\\left[ {{{\\text{C}}_0}} \\right] - \\left[ {\\frac{{{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{0}}}}}{{\\text{2}}}} \\right]}}{{{{\\text{t}}_{1/2}}}}$<br>$\\therefore {{\\text{t}}_{1/2}} = \\frac{{\\left[ {{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{0}}}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{2k}}}}$<br>Thus,${{\\text{t}}_{{\\text{1/2}}}}$ for zero order reaction is directly proportional to the initial concentration of the reactant.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24518,'chemistry',59,373,698,'How many of the following metals can be obtained by electrolysis of aqueous solutions of their salts?<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ag}, \\mathrm{Ca}, \\mathrm{Cu}, \\mathrm{Mg}, \\mathrm{Na}, \\mathrm{Al}}}$','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ca}, \\mathrm{Mg}, \\mathrm{Na}}}$ and Al have lower reduction potential than water ${\\mathrm{\\left(E^{\\circ}=-0.83 \\mathrm{~V}\\right)}}$. Hence, they cannot be reduced in their aqueous solution. Instead water will get reduced to liberate ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
