(24579,'chemistry',60,585,1175,'For the following homogeneous reaction, the unit of rate constant is<br>${\\text{A}} + {\\text{B}}\\xrightarrow{{\\text{k}}}{\\text{C}}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{mol}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{Ls}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{s}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{s}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~mol} \\\\mathrm{~L}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For the homogeneous gaseous reaction,<br>${\\text{A}} + {\\text{B}}\\xrightarrow{{\\text{k}}}{\\text{C}}$,<br>the rate law is, $\\frac{{{\\text{dx}}}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = {\\text{k[A][B]}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\text{k}} = \\frac{{{\\text{dx}}}}{{{\\text{dt}} \\times {\\text{[A][B]}}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{  =  }}{\\mkern 1mu} \\frac{{{\\rm{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{L}}^{ - 1}}}}{{{\\rm{s}} \\times {\\rm{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{L}}^{ - 1}} \\times {\\rm{mol}}{\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{L}}^{ - 1}}}}$<br>$ = {\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{ - 1}}\\,{\\rm{L}}\\,{{\\rm{s}}^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24565,'chemistry',59,367,680,'The concentration of $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\\right]$ at equilibrium when potassium iodide is added to a solution of $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ initially $0.50\\,{\\rm{M}}$ until $[{{\\rm{I}}^ - }] = 1.0{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{M}}$  is ____$({\\rm{Given}}\\,{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{3 + }}\\mid {\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{2 + }}}^0 = 0.770\\,{\\rm{V}},{\\rm{E}}{^\\circ _{{{\\rm{I}}_2}{\\rm{/}}{{\\rm{I}}^ - }}} = 0.535\\,{\\rm{V}})$','[{"text": "0.01 M", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{M}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.535\\\\,{\\\\rm{M}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{M}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The concerned cheminal reaction is<br>$2{\\text{F}}{{\\text{e}}^{3 + }} + 2{{\\text{I}}^ - } \\rightleftharpoons 2{\\text{F}}{{\\text{e}}^{2 + }} + {{\\text{I}}_{\\text{2}}}({\\text{s}})$<br>Now, ${\\text{E}}{^\\circ _{{\\text{cell}}}} = {\\text{E}}{^\\circ _{{\\text{F}}{{\\text{e}}^{3 + }}/{\\text{F}}{{\\text{e}}^{2 + }}}} - {\\text{E}}{^\\circ _{{{\\text{I}}_{\\text{2}}}/{{\\text{I}}^ - }}} = \\frac{{0.059}}{{\\text{n}}}{\\text{log}}\\,\\,{{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{eq}}}}$<br>or, $0.770 - 0.535 = \\frac{{0.059}}{2}{\\text{log}}\\,\\,{{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{eq}}}}$<br>${{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{eq}}}} = 9.25 \\times {10^7}$<br>As the equilibrium constant is very large, the equilibrium concentration may be calculated by completing the reaction first in forward direction and then approaching equilibrium from backward direction  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/59_24565_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Now,<br>${{\\text{K}}_{{\\text{eq}}}} = \\frac{{{{\\left[ {{\\text{F}}{{\\text{e}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}}} \\right]}^2}}}{{{{\\left[ {{\\text{F}}{{\\text{e}}^{{\\text{3 + }}}}} \\right]}^2}{{\\left[ {{{\\text{I}}^ - }} \\right]}^2}}}$<br>or, $9.25 \\times {10^7} = \\frac{{{{(0.5)}^2}}}{{{{\\text{x}}^2} \\times {{(1.0)}^2}}}$<br>$\\,\\left[ {{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{{\\rm{3  +  }}}}} \\right] = {\\rm{x}} = 5.2 \\times {10^5}{\\rm{M}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24566,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'For a first order reaction, the time required for $99.9 \\%$ of the reaction to take place is nearly','[{"text": "10 times that required for half of the reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "100 times that required for two-thirds of the reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10 times that required for one-fourth of the reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20 times that required for half of the reaction", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Time required to complete $99.9 \\%$ of reaction is given as:<br>${{\\text{t}}_{{\\text{99}}{\\text{.9}}}} = \\frac{{2.303}}{{\\text{k}}}\\log \\frac{{100}}{{100 - 99.9}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{.303}}}}{{\\rm{k}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{log}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} 1000 = \\frac{{{\\rm{6}}{\\rm{.909}}}}{{\\rm{k}}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {\\because \\log {{10}^3} = 3} \\right)$<br>${\\text{and }}{{\\text{t}}_{{\\text{1/2}}}} = \\frac{{{\\text{2}}{\\text{.303}}}}{{\\text{k}}}\\log \\frac{{100}}{{100 - 50}}\\quad $<br>$ = \\frac{{{\\text{2}}{\\text{.303}}}}{{\\text{k}}}\\log 2 = \\frac{{{\\text{0}}{\\text{.693}}}}{{\\text{k}}}$<br>So, ${{\\text{t}}_{{\\text{99}}{\\text{.9}}}}$ is 10 times that required for half of the reaction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24568,'chemistry',63,817,1798,'The element which can displace three other halogens from their compounds is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Br}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{I}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{F}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24571,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'The reaction ${\\text{2NO(g)}} + {{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{2N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}}$ is of first order. If volume of reaction vessel is reduced to $1 / 3$ the rate of reaction would be','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{3}$ times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{3}$ times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 times", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6 times", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$2{\\text{NO(g)}} + {{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}} \\rightleftharpoons 2{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{(g)}}$<br>When the volume of vessel is reduced to $1 / 3$, then concentration of reactant become three times, as concentration $\\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\text { volume }}$. The rate of reaction for first order reaction is directly proportional to concentration. Therefore, rate of reaction will increase three times.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24572,'chemistry',63,820,1814,'Interhalogen compounds are','[{"text": "Covalent molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Diamagnetic in nature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Volatile solids/liquid at $298 \\\\mathrm{~K}$ except $\\\\mathrm{ClF}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Interhalogen compounds are covalent molecule and diamagnetic in nature. These are volatile solids or liquids at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$ except $\\mathrm{Cl} \\mathrm{F}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24575,'chemistry',60,587,1181,'Consider the following statements:<br>The rate law for the acid catalysed hydrolysis of an ester being given as<br>Rate $ = {\\text{k}}\\left[ {{{\\text{H}}^ + }} \\right][{\\text{ester}}] = {{\\text{k}}^\\prime }[{\\text{ester}}]$. If the acid concentration is doubled at constant ester concentration <br>I. The second order rate constant,  k is doubled.<br>II. The pesudo first order rate constant,${\\text{k}}$ is doubled.<br>III. The rate of the reaction is doubled.<br>Which of the above statements are correct?','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Pseudo first order rate constant is doubled as well as rate of reaction is doubled. It may be noted that in presence of acid, hydrolysis of ethyl acetate is a pseudo-unimolecular reaction but the actual value of ${\\text{k}}$ depends upon the concentration of $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$ions, otherwise rate constant of a reaction is constant at constant temperature.Thus, \'II\' and \'III\' are the correct statements.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24576,'chemistry',63,820,1814,'Interhalogen compounds can be used as<br>I. Non-aqueous solvents<br>II. Fluorinating agents<br>The correct use(s) is/are','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neither I nor II", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Interhalogen compounds can be used as non-aqueous solvents and fluorinating agents.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24577,'chemistry',59,373,697,'If an aqueous $\\mathrm{NaCl}$ solution is electrolysed using a current of $5 \\mathrm{~A}$ for $200 \\mathrm{~min}$. Volume of $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$ in litres produced under STP condition is $({\\rm{F}} = 96000\\,\\,{\\rm{C}}).$','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\text{I}} = 5\\,{\\text{A}}\\,\\,\\,{\\text{t}} = 200\\,{\\text{min}} = 200 \\times 60\\,\\,{\\text{sec}}{\\text{.}}$<br>Equivalent volume of $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} ; \\mathrm{Ve}=11.2 \\mathrm{~L}$<br>${\\text{V}} = \\frac{{{\\text{It}} \\cdot {\\text{Ve}}}}{{96500}} = \\frac{{5 \\times 200 \\times 60 \\times 11.2}}{{96500}}$<br>${\\text{V}} = 6.96\\,{\\text{L}} \\approx 7\\,{\\text{L}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24583,'chemistry',60,587,1179,'For a reaction,<br>${\\text{X(g)}} \\to {\\text{Y(g)}} + {\\text{Z(g)}}$<br>the half-life period is $10 \\mathrm{~min}$. In what period of time would the concentration of ${\\text{X}}$ be reduced to $10 \\%$ of original concentration?','[{"text": "$20 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$33 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$15 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{X(g)}} \\to {\\text{Y(g)}} + {\\text{Z(g)}}$<br>The reaction is a first order reaction.<br> Hence,<br>${\\text{k}} = \\frac{{0.693}}{{{{\\text{t}}_{{\\text{1/2}}}}}} = \\frac{{{\\text{2}}{\\text{.303}}}}{{\\text{t}}}\\log \\frac{{\\text{a}}}{{({\\text{a}} - {\\text{x}})}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\frac{{0.693}}{{10}} = \\frac{{{\\text{2}}{\\text{.303}}}}{{\\text{t}}}\\log \\frac{{\\text{a}}}{{{\\text{a}}/10}}{\\text{ }}$<br>${\\text{or}}\\,{\\text{ t}} = \\frac{{2.303 \\times 10}}{{0.693}} \\times \\log 10 = 33\\,{\\text{min}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\because \\log 10 = 1]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24584,'chemistry',63,820,1814,'I. ${\\rm{IF}}$ is detected spectroscopically and is very unstable.<br>II. $\\mathrm{BrCl}$ is pure solid at room temperature<br>III. $\\mathrm{ICl}_{3}$ dimerises as Cl-bridged dimer $\\left(\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{6}\\right)$.<br>The correct set of statements is','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'IF is detected spectroscopically and is very unstable $\\mathrm{BrCl}$ is pure solid at room temperature.<br>$\\mathrm{ICl}_{3}$ dimerises as Cl-bridged dimer $\\left(\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{6}\\right)$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24587,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'According to law of mass action, rate of a chemical reaction is proportional to','[{"text": "concentration of reactants", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "molar concentration of reactants", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "concentration of products", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "molar concentration of products", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Guldberg and Waage (1864) suggested relationship between rate of reaction and the concentration of the reacting species known as law of mass action. According to this "at constant temperature, the rate of a chemical reaction is directly proportional to the product of the molar concentrations of reacting species, with each concentration term raised to the power equal to the numerical coefficient of that species in that chemical equation". For any general reaction,<br>${\\text{aA}} + {\\text{bB}} \\to {\\text{ Products }}$<br>${\\text{ }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{Rate }} = {\\text{k[A}}{{\\text{]}}^{\\text{a}}}{{\\text{[B]}}^{\\text{b}}}$<br>${\\text{  i}}{\\text{.e}}{\\text{.}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{Rate }} \\propto {{\\text{[A]}}^{\\text{a}}}{{\\text{[B]}}^{\\text{b}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24588,'chemistry',63,823,1817,'Why are the elements of Group 18 known as noble gases?','[{"text": "They react with all elements", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They do not react at all", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "React with few elements under certain conditions", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Discovered by Davy", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The elements present in Group 18 have their valence shell orbitals completely filled and, therefore, react with a few elements only under certain conditions. Therefore, they are now known as noble gases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24589,'chemistry',59,373,697,'How many grams of water will be electrolysed by 96500 coulomb?','[{"text": "96", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4.5", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\text{W}} = \\frac{{{\\text{E}} \\times {\\text{Q}}}}{{96500}}$ Given  ${\\text{Q}} = 96500\\,\\,{\\text{C}}$ and ${\\text{E}}\\left( {{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}} \\right) = 9\\,{\\text{g}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\text{W}} = \\frac{{9 \\times 96500}}{{96500}} = 9\\,{\\text{g}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24591,'chemistry',60,589,1185,'A chemical reaction was carried out at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$ and $280 \\mathrm{~K}$. The rate constant were found to be ${{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}$  and  ${{\\text{k}}_{\\text{2}}}$ respectively, then','[{"text": "${{\\\\text{k}}_2} = {\\\\text{4}}{{\\\\text{k}}_{\\\\text{1}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{k}}_{\\\\text{2}}} = 0.25{{\\\\text{k}}_{\\\\text{1}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{k}}_{\\\\text{2}}} = 2{{\\\\text{k}}_{\\\\text{1}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{k}}_{\\\\text{2}}} = 0.5{{\\\\text{k}}_{\\\\text{1}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For every $10^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ rise, rate constant gets doubled. Hence, for every $20^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ rise, rate constant will be 4 times. Therefore, according to the given statement,<br>Temperature$\\quad 280\\,{\\text{K}}\\quad 300\\,{\\text{K}}$<br>$Rateconstant{\\rm{ }}\\,\\,{{\\rm{k}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{k}}_{\\rm{1}}}\\left( {{\\rm{4}}{{\\rm{k}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right)$<br>${\\rm{Thus,}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{k}}_{\\rm{1}}} = 4{{\\rm{k}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\Rightarrow {{\\rm{k}}_2} = 0.25{{\\rm{k}}_{\\rm{1}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24592,'chemistry',63,823,1817,'All noble gases have general electronic configuration " $A$ " except helium which has " $B$ ". Here, A and B refer to','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A - n}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{n}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{4}}}{\\\\rm{;B - 1}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - n}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{n}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{6}}}{\\\\rm{;B - 1}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - n}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{n}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{6}}}{\\\\rm{;B - 1}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{1}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - n}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{n}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{4}}}{\\\\rm{;B - 1}}{{\\\\rm{s}}^{\\\\rm{1}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'All noble gases have general electronic configuration ${\\rm{n}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{n}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{6}}}$ except helium which has ${\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}$ configuration. Many of the properties of noble gases including their inactive nature are ascribed to their closed shell structures.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24834,'chemistry',64,193,1933,'The inner transition elements are the elements in which the added electrons go to','[{"text": "(n-1)d-orbitals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{(n - 2)}}$f-orbitals", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(n-1)d-orbitals and (n-2)f-orbitals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(n-1)d-orbitals and ns orbitals", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Elements in which differentiating electron enters into $\\mathrm{(n-2) f}$ orbitals are known as inner transition elements.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24595,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'In the following graph.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "value of $\\\\frac{{{{\\\\text{E}}_{\\\\text{a}}}}}{{{\\\\text{2}}{\\\\text{.303}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "value of $\\\\frac{{{\\\\text{2}}{\\\\text{.303}}}}{{{{\\\\text{E}}_{\\\\text{a}}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "value of  $ - \\\\frac{{{{\\\\text{E}}_{\\\\text{a}}}}}{{{\\\\text{2}}{\\\\text{.303R}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "value of  $ - \\\\frac{{{{\\\\text{E}}_{\\\\text{a}}}}}{{{\\\\text{2}}{\\\\text{.303RT}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\because \\ln {\\text{k}} = \\ln {\\text{A}} - \\frac{{{{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}}}}{{{\\text{RT}}}}$<br>$\\ln {\\text{k}} = \\ln {\\text{A}} - \\frac{{{{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}}}}{{\\text{R}}} \\cdot \\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{T}}}$<br>Thus,  ${\\log _{10}}{\\text{k}} = \\log {\\text{A}} - \\frac{{{{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}}}}{{{\\text{2}}{\\text{.303RT}}}}$<br>or $\\quad \\log {\\text{k}} = \\log {\\text{A}} - \\frac{{{{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}}}}{{{\\text{2}}{\\text{.303R}}}} \\cdot \\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{T}}}$<br>On comparing the above equation with equation of straight line, i.e. y=mx + c ,<br> a slope $ = \\frac{{ - {{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}}}}{{2.303{\\text{R}}}}$ is obtained.',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/60_24595_question_1.png'),
