(24601,'chemistry',59,373,697,'$\\mathrm{M}^{n+}$ solution was electrolysed for $75 \\mathrm{~min}$ by a current of $0.30 \\mathrm{~A}$. Mass of the metal $\\mathrm{M}$ deposited at the cathode was $0.958 \\mathrm{~g}$. $\\mathrm{M}^{n+}$ contains 68 protons and 69 neutrons. What is the value of $n$ ?','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${{\\text{M}}^{{\\text{n + }}}} + {\\text{n}}{{\\text{e}}^ - } \\to {\\text{M}}$<br>By Faraday\'s first law<br>$\\mathrm{W}=$ ZIt<br>where, Z = electrochemical equivalent $=\\dfrac{\\text { Atomic mass }}{\\text { Electrons } \\times 96500}$<br>${\\text{Z}} = \\frac{{(68 + 69)}}{{{\\text{n}} \\times 96500}}$<br>${\\text{W}} = 0.958\\,{\\text{g}}$<br>${\\text{t}} = 75 \\times 60\\,{\\text{s}}$<br>$\\mathrm{I}=0.30 \\mathrm{~A}$<br>$\\therefore 0.958=\\dfrac{137}{\\mathrm{n} \\times 96500} \\times 0.3 \\times 75 \\times 60$<br>$\\therefore {\\text{n}} = 2.0$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
