(24663,'chemistry',60,585,1174,'In the reversible reaction, $2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2} \\underset{k_{2}}{\\stackrel{k_{1}}{\\rightleftharpoons}} \\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$ the rate of disappearance of $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ is equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{2{{\\\\text{k}}_1}}}{{{{\\\\text{k}}_2}}}{\\\\left[ {{\\\\text{N}}{{\\\\text{O}}_2}} \\\\right]^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2{{\\\\text{k}}_1}{\\\\left[ {{\\\\text{N}}{{\\\\text{O}}_2}} \\\\right]^2} - 2{{\\\\text{k}}_2}\\\\left[ {\\\\,{{\\\\text{N}}_2}{{\\\\text{O}}_4}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2{{\\\\text{k}}_1}{\\\\left[ {{\\\\text{N}}{{\\\\text{O}}_2}} \\\\right]^2} - {{\\\\text{k}}_2}\\\\left[ {\\\\,{{\\\\text{N}}_2}{{\\\\text{O}}_4}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {2{{\\\\text{k}}_1} - {{\\\\text{k}}_2}} \\\\right)\\\\left[ {{\\\\text{N}}{{\\\\text{O}}_2}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{2N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}\\mathop  \\rightleftharpoons \\limits_{{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{2}}}}^{{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}} {{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}$<br>Rate of reaction $ =  - \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{{\\text{d}}\\left[ {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}}$<br>$ = {{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}{\\left[ {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]^2} - {{\\text{k}}_{\\text{2}}}\\left[ {\\,{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}} \\right]$<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ Rate of disappearance of $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ i.e.,<br>$ - \\frac{{{\\text{d}}\\left[ {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]}}{{{\\text{dt}}}} = 2{{\\text{k}}_1}{\\left[ {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]^2} - 2{{\\text{k}}_2}\\left[ {\\,{{\\text{N}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}} \\right]$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24664,'chemistry',59,373,697,'When electrolysis of KCl is done in alkaline medium. 10 g of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KClO}_{3}}}$ is produced as follows:${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}^{\\ominus}+6 \\stackrel{\\ominus}{\\mathrm{O}} \\mathrm{H} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{ClO}_{3}{ }^{\\ominus}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+6 e^{-}}}$<br>A  current of 2A is passed for 10.941 hours.Calculate the <br>${\\mathrm{\\left(\\dfrac{\\text { Percentage current efficiency }}{10}\\right)}}$ used in the process.${\\mathrm{\\left(M w\\right.}}$ of ${\\mathrm{\\left.\\mathrm{KClO}_{3}=122.5\\right)}}$','[{"text": "0.6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let ${\\mathrm{x \\%}}$ is the current efficiency of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KClO}_{3}=}}$ Number of Faradays.<br>${\\mathrm{\\dfrac{10 \\mathrm{~g}}{122.5 / 6}=\\dfrac{2 \\times x \\times 10.941 \\times 3600}{100 \\times 96500}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\therefore x=60 \\%}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\therefore \\dfrac{\\text { Percentage current efficiency }}{10}=\\dfrac{60}{10}=6}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24665,'chemistry',63,807,1751,'Among the following the incorrect statement is','[{"text": "nitrogen can form strong ${\\\\rm{p\\\\pi  - p\\\\pi }}$ multiple bonds, whereas phosphorus cannot", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{5}$ are monomeric whereas trioxide and pentoxide of phosphorus are dimeric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "nitrite and nitrate of ammonia give nitrogen on heating whereas chloride and suphate of ammonia give ammonia gas on heating", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "oxidation state of nitrogen in hydrazine is (-2) and in hydroxylamine is (-1).", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Ammonium nitrite on heating gives ${{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}}$.<br>${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\mathop  \\to \\limits^\\Delta  {{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{  +  2}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>Ammonium nitrate on heating gives $\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$. $\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{NO}_{3} \\stackrel{\\Delta}{\\longrightarrow} \\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24666,'chemistry',60,589,1185,'A chemical reaction was carried out at $300 \\mathrm{~K}$ and $280 \\mathrm{~K}$. The rate constants were found to be ${{\\text{k}}_1}$ and ${{\\text{k}}_2}$ respectively. Then','[{"text": "${{\\\\text{k}}_2} = 4{{\\\\text{k}}_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{k}}_2} = 2{{\\\\text{k}}_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{k}}_2} = 0.25{{\\\\text{k}}_1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{k}}_2} = 0.5{{\\\\text{k}}_1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Rate constant becomes double for every $10^\\circ $ rise in temperature. Hence, for $20^\\circ $ rise in temperature, rate constant will become four times. i.e.,<br>${{\\text{k}}_1} = 4{{\\text{k}}_2} \\Rightarrow {{\\text{k}}_2} = \\frac{1}{4}{{\\text{k}}_1} \\Rightarrow {{\\text{k}}_2} = 0.25\\,{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24667,'chemistry',59,366,679,'Reduction potential of ions are given below<br>$\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-} & \\mathrm{IO}_{4}^{-} & \\mathrm{BrO}_{4}^{-} \\\\\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}=1.19 \\mathrm{~V} & \\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}=1.65 \\mathrm{~V} & \\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}=1.74 \\mathrm{~V}\\end{array}$<br>The correct order of their oxidising power is','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{BrO}_{4}^{-}>\\\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}>\\\\mathrm{IO}_{4}^{-}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{BrO}_{4}^{-}>\\\\mathrm{IO}_{4}^{-}>\\\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{IO}_{4}^{-}>\\\\mathrm{BrO}_{4}^{-}>\\\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}>\\\\mathrm{IO}_{4}^{-}>\\\\mathrm{BrO}_{4}^{-}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Higher the standard reduction potential, stronger of the given ions is the oxidising power. Hence, the order of oxidising power of the given ions is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BrO}_{4}^{-}>\\mathrm{IO}_{4}^{-}>\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}}}$.<br>So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24668,'chemistry',63,808,1752,'Which one of the following properties of white phosphorus are shown by red phosphorus?','[{"text": "It dissolves in $\\\\mathrm{CS}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It burns when heated in air", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It reacts with $\\\\mathrm{NaOH}$ to give $\\\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It phosphorescences in air", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Both forms ${{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{5}}}$ or ${{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{4}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{{\\rm{10}}}}$<br>$\\mathop {{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{4}}}}\\limits_{\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}{{\\rm{White}}}\\\\{\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}{{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{or}}}\\\\{{\\rm{Red}}}\\end{array}}\\end{array}} {\\rm{ + 5}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\to {{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{4}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{{\\rm{10}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24669,'chemistry',60,589,1186,'The rate constant of a reaction at temperature $200 \\mathrm{~K}$ is 10 times less than the rate constant at $400 \\mathrm{~K}$. What is the activation energy of the reaction?','[{"text": "$1842.4\\\\,{\\\\text{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$460.6\\\\,{\\\\text{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$230.3\\\\,{\\\\text{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$921.2\\\\,{\\\\text{R}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'At ${{\\text{T}}_{\\text{1}}} = 200\\,{\\text{K}},\\,\\,{\\text{if}}\\,\\,{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{1}}} = {\\text{k}}$<br>then at ${{\\text{T}}_2} = 400\\,{\\text{K}},{{\\text{k}}_{\\text{2}}} = 10{\\text{k}}$<br>${\\text{log}}\\frac{{10{\\text{k}}}}{{\\text{k}}} = \\frac{{{{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}}}}{{2.303{\\text{R}}}}\\left( {\\frac{{400 - 200}}{{400 \\times 200}}} \\right)$<br>${{\\text{E}}_{\\text{a}}} = 921.2{\\text{R}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24670,'chemistry',59,371,693,'For a strong electrolyte, a plot of molar conductivity against (concentration) ${\\mathrm{{ }^{1 / 2}}}$ is a straight line, with a negative slope, the correct unit for the slope is','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{S} \\\\,\\\\mathrm{cm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~L}^{1 / 2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{S}\\\\, \\\\mathrm{cm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-3 / 2} \\\\mathrm{~L}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{S}\\\\, \\\\mathrm{cm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-3 / 2} \\\\mathrm{~L}^{-1 / 2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{S} \\\\,\\\\mathrm{cm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-3 / 2} \\\\mathrm{~L}^{1 / 2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For strong electrolyte, ${\\mathrm{\\Lambda_{m}=\\Lambda_{m}^{\\circ}-A \\sqrt{C}}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { Slope, } \\mathrm{A}=\\dfrac{\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{m}}^{\\circ}-\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{m}}}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{C}}} \\\\& \\begin{aligned}\\therefore \\text { Units of } \\mathrm{A} & =\\dfrac{\\mathrm{S} \\cdot \\mathrm{cm}^{2} \\cdot \\mathrm{mol}^{-}}{\\left.\\left(\\mathrm{mol}^{-} \\cdot \\mathrm{lit}\\right)^{-}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}} \\\\& =\\mathrm{S\\,cm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-3 / 2} \\mathrm{~L}^{1 / 2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}$<br>So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24671,'chemistry',63,808,1761,'Phosphorus is manufactured by heating in an electric furnace along with a mixture of','[{"text": "Bone ash, silica and coke", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bone ash and silica", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bone ash and coke", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{2C}}{{\\text{a}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\left( {{\\text{P}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}} \\right)_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{  +  6Si}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{1770}}\\,\\,{\\text{K}}}}{\\text{6CaSi}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{  +  }}{{\\text{P}}_{\\text{4}}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{10}}}}$<br>${\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {{\\text{P}}_{\\text{4}}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{10}}}}{\\text{ + 10C}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{1770}}\\,{\\text{K}}}}\\mathop {{{\\text{P}}_{\\text{4}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ White }}} {\\text{ + 10CO}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24672,'chemistry',59,372,694,'Molar ionic conductivities of divalent cation and anion are ${\\mathrm{57 \\,\\mathrm{S\\,cm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{73 \\,\\mathrm{S\\,cm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}}$ respectively. The molar conductivity of solution of an electrolyte with the above cation and anion will be','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{65\\\\, \\\\mathrm{S\\\\,cm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{130 \\\\,\\\\mathrm{S\\\\,cm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{260 \\\\,\\\\mathrm{S\\\\,cm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{187 \\\\,\\\\mathrm{S\\\\,cm}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'On applying Kohlrausch\'s law<br>${\\mathrm{\\Lambda_{\\text {solution }}=\\lambda_{\\mathrm{c}^{2+}}+\\lambda_{\\mathrm{A}^{2-}}=57+73=130 \\,\\mathrm{S\\,cm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}}$<br>So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24689,'chemistry',60,587,1180,'The half life period of cobalt-60 is 5.26 years. Calculate the percentage activity after 4 years.','[{"text": "$75 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$59 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$15 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$72 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given, ${{\\rm{t}}_{{\\rm{1 / 2}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\,{\\rm{5}}{\\rm{.26}}$ years <br>${\\rm{\\lambda }} = \\frac{{0.693}}{{5.26}}$<br>If m % is the activity, then $\\frac{{{\\rm{a}} - {\\rm{x}}}}{{\\rm{a}}} = \\frac{{\\rm{m}}}{{100}}$<br>Now, ${\\rm{t}} = \\frac{{2.303}}{{\\rm{\\lambda }}}{\\rm{log}}\\frac{{\\rm{a}}}{{{\\rm{a}} - {\\rm{x}}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 4 = \\frac{{2.303}}{{0.693}} \\times 5.26\\,{\\rm{log}}\\,\\,\\frac{{{\\rm{100}}}}{{\\rm{m}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\rm{m}} = 59.04\\% $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24673,'chemistry',60,585,1175,'For a reaction of type ${\\text{A}} + {\\text{B}} \\to $ products, it is observed that doubling concentration of ${\\text{A\'}}$ causes the reaction rate to be four times but doubling amount of $\'{\\text{B\'}}$ does not affect the rate. The unit of rate constant is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{s}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{s}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~mol} \\\\mathrm{~L}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{s}^{-1} \\\\mathrm{~mol}^{-2} \\\\mathrm{~L}^{-2}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let the initial rate is ${\\text{R}}$ and order with respect to ${\\text{A}}$ is ${\\text{x}}$ and order with respect to ${\\text{B is y}}.$ Thus, rate law is<br>${\\text{R}} = {[{\\text{A}}]^{\\text{x}}}{[{\\text{B}}]^{\\text{y}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>On doubling the concentration of ${\\text{A}}$, rate becomes 4R<br>${\\text{4R}} = {[2{\\text{A}}]^{\\text{x}}}{[{\\text{B}}]^{\\text{y}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} (2)$<br>On doubling the concentration of ${\\text{B}}$, rate remains R<br>${\\rm{R}} = {[{\\rm{A}}]^{\\rm{x}}}{[{\\rm{2B}}]^{\\rm{y}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>From eqns (1) and (2), we get<br>$\\frac{R}{{4R}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^x} \\Rightarrow {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^2} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^x}$<br>${\\kern 1pt} \\therefore x = 2$<br>From eqns (1) and (3), we get<br>$\\frac{{\\text{R}}}{{\\text{R}}} = {\\left[ {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right]^{\\text{y}}} \\Rightarrow 1 = {\\left[ {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right]^{\\text{y}}}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\therefore {\\text{ y}} = 0$<br>Thus, actual rate law is, rate, ${\\rm{R}} = {[{\\rm{A}}]^2}{[{\\rm{B}}]^0}$.<br>The order of this reaction is 2 and for second order reaction unit of rate constant are ${s^{ - 1}}\\,{\\text{mo}}{{\\text{l}}^{ - 1}}\\,{\\text{L}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(24674,'chemistry',63,808,1761,'White phosphorus reacts with chlorine and the product hydrolyses in the presence of water. The mass of $\\mathrm{HCl}$ obtained by the hydrolysis of the product formed by the reaction of $62 \\mathrm{~g}$ of white phosphorus with chlorine in the presence of water is','[{"text": "$0.219 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$21.9 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.19 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$219 \\\\mathrm{~g}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{P}_{4}+6 \\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\rightarrow 4 \\mathrm{PCl}_{3}$<br>$\\left( {{\\rm{PC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ + 3}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}} \\to {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{P}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ + 3HCl}}} \\right){\\rm{ \\times 4}}$<br>$\\overline {{{\\rm{P}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{ + 6C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 12}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}} \\to {\\rm{4}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{P}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ + 12HCl}}} $<br>1 mol of white phosphorus produces $12 \\mathrm{~mol}$ of $\\mathrm{HCl}$<br>$62 \\mathrm{~g}$ of white phosphorus has been taken which is equivalent to $\\frac{{{\\rm{62}}}}{{{\\rm{124}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{1}}}{{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{\\,mol}}$.<br>Therefore $6 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{HCl}$ will be formed<br>Mass of $6 \\mathrm{~mol} \\mathrm{HCl}=6 \\times 36.5=219.0 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{HCl}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
