(25115,'chemistry',64,188,1904,'Among the following, the one that is not considered as a typical transition element.','[{"text": "chromium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "manganese", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "cobalt", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zinc", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'By definition, a transition element contain an incompletely filled d-subshell. With its $\\mathrm{3 d^{10}}$ configuration, zinc does not fall under this definition. Transition elements are metallic solids with high melting and boiling points. Zinc has a melting point of only $\\mathrm{420^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}}$ and a relatively low boiling point of $\\mathrm{907^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}}$. Zinc is also a metallic solid, which forms a variety of complexes like other transition elements. Hence zinc is not considered as a typical transition element.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25116,'chemistry',63,806,1740,'$\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ is well known but, $\\mathrm{NCl}_{5}$ is not because','[{"text": "nitrogen is less reactive than phosphorous.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "nitrogen doesn\'t have d - orbitals in its valence shell.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "catenation tendency is weaker in nitrogen than phosphorous.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "size of phosphorous is larger than nitrogen.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ forms five bonds by using the $\\mathrm{d}$ - orbitals to " expand the octet". But $\\mathrm{NCl}_{5}$ does not exist because there are no $\\mathrm{d}$ - orbitals in the valence shell $\\left( {{{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{nd }}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{shell}}} \\right)$. Therefore there is no way to expand the octet.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
