(25303,'chemistry',66,1029,2423,'Following statements are given regarding the preparation of arylhalides from toluene. Read the following statements and choose the correct option.<br>(i) Aryl chlorides and bromides can be easily prepared by this method.<br>(ii) The ortho and para isomers formed in the reaction can not be separated easily due to small difference in their melting point.<br>(iii) Reactions with iodine are reversible in nature and require the presence of an oxidising agent.<br>(iv) Fluoro compounds are not prepared by this method due to low reactivity of fluorine.','[{"text": "(i) and (iii) are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(ii) and (iv) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i), (ii) and (iii) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All statements are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Aryl chlorides and bromides can be easily prepared by electrophilic substitution of arenes with chlorine and bromine respectively in the presence of Lewis acid catalysts like iron or iron (III) chloride.<br>The ortho and para isomers can be easily separated due to large difference in their melting points.<br>Reaction with iodine are reversible in nature and require the presence of an oxidising agent $$\\left(\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}, \\mathrm{HIO}_{4}\\right)$$ to oxidise the $$\\mathrm{HI}$$ formed during iodination.<br>Fluoro compounds are not prepared by this method due to high reactivity of fluorine.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25304,'chemistry',63,814,1784,'Which of the following oxoacids of sulphur contains a sulphur-sulphur single bond?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{6}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{5}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_25304_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25305,'chemistry',64,191,1914,'The $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}\\left(\\mathrm{M}^{2+} / \\mathrm{M}\\right)}$ value for copper is positive $\\mathrm{(+0.34 \\mathrm{~V})}$. What is possible reason for this?','[{"text": "Low $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta_{a} \\\\mathrm{H}^{\\\\circ}}$ and high $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta_{\\\\text {hyd }} \\\\mathrm{H}^{\\\\circ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "High $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta_{a} \\\\mathrm{H}^{\\\\circ}}$ and Low $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta_{\\\\text {hyd }} \\\\mathrm{H}^{\\\\circ}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Low $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta_{a} \\\\mathrm{H}^{\\\\circ}}$ and Low $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta_{\\\\text {hyd }} \\\\mathrm{H}^{\\\\circ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "High $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta_{a} \\\\mathrm{H}^{\\\\circ}}$ and High $\\\\mathrm{\\\\Delta_{\\\\text {hyd }} \\\\mathrm{H}^{\\\\circ}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Due to high enthalpy of atomisation and low hydration enthalpy.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25306,'chemistry',65,960,2238,'Amongst the following, the most stable complex is','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}\\\\right)_{3}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{FeCl}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Polydentate ligands result in chelation which makes complex more stable. Only $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}$ is bidentate ligand while all others are monodentate. Hence option 3 is a chelated complex hence more stable.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25307,'chemistry',66,1031,2432,'A set of compounds in which the reactivity of halogen atom towords ${{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{N}}}{\\rm{1}}$ reaction in the ascending order is','[{"text": "Chlorobenzene, vinyl chloride, chloroethane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Chloroethane, chlorobenzene, vinylchloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vinyl chloride, chloroethane, chloroethane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vinyl chloride, chloroethane, chlorobenzene", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'On the same basis as above<br>$$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}>\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\mathrm{CHCl}>\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{Cl}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25308,'chemistry',63,814,1781,'Which is the most stable allotrope of sulphur?','[{"text": "Octahedral sulphur", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Monoclinic sulphur", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plastic sulphur", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Octahedral sulphur (rhombic or $\\alpha$ - sulphur) is the most stable allotrope of sulphur. It is stable at temperature below $95.6^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. Its specific gravity is 2.06 . It melts at $114^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. It is soluble in carbon disulphide. Its crystal structure contains cyclic $\\mathrm{S}_{8}$ rings packed in a manner which leads to the formation of Rhombic Crystals. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_25308_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> (Rhombic or $\\alpha$ or octahedral sulphur)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2016,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25309,'chemistry',64,191,1917,'Which of the following weighs less when weighed in magnetic field ?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{VCl}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{ScCl}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{TiCl}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{ScCl}_{3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Sc}^{3+}+3 \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}, \\mathrm{Sc}^{3+}=[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 d^{0}}$<br>Since there is no unpaired electron, it will show diamagnetic character and will be repelled. Hence, it will weigh less.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25310,'chemistry',65,960,2239,'The complex with highest stability constant at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$ is :','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CdCl}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CdBr}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CdI}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cd}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Ligands like  ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{N}}^{\\text{ - }}}{\\text{,CO,}}{{\\text{R}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{P}}$  etc. which are capable to form $\\pi$ bonds give more stable complexes.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
