(25337,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'When acidified $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}$ solution is added to $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}}$ salts then $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}}$ changes to','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Sn}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Sn}^{3+}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Sn}^{+}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}+14 \\mathrm{H}^{+}+3 \\mathrm{Sn}^{2+} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}+3 \\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}+7 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25315,'chemistry',66,1030,2428,'Boiling points of alkyl chlorides and alkyl iodides have been compared with alkane<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{}&{}&{{\\rm{Boiling}}\\,\\,{\\rm{points}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{n  -  butylchloride}}}&{\\left( {{\\rm{92}}{\\rm{.5}}\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{  -  1}}}}} \\right)}&{{\\rm{78}}{\\rm{.4^\\circ C}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{n  -  hexane}}}&{\\left( {{\\rm{86}}{\\rm{.2}}\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{  -  1}}}}} \\right)}&{{\\rm{68}}{\\rm{.7^\\circ C}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{methyliodide}}}&{\\left( {{\\rm{142}}\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{  -  1}}}}} \\right)}&{{\\rm{42}}{\\rm{.5^\\circ C}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{n  -  decane}}}&{\\left( {{\\rm{142}}\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,{\\rm{mo}}{{\\rm{l}}^{{\\rm{  -  1}}}}} \\right)}&{{\\rm{164^\\circ C}}}\n\\end{array}$<br>Boiling point of ${\\rm{n}}$-hexane is about same as of ${\\rm{n}}$ butyl chloride (of same molar mass), while boiling point of n-decane is higher than that of methyl iodide (of same molar mass). This is due to which of the following?','[{"text": "Greater molecular volume of alkanes than alkyl iodides.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Compensation of polar effects and molecular volumes in alkyl chlorides and alkanes.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25316,'chemistry',63,814,1786,'What is the $\\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{S}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond angle in $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ ?','[{"text": "$104.5^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$92.1^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$91^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "thionyl chloride.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The $\\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{S}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond angle in $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ is $92.1^{\\circ}$ which is slightly lesser than the tetrahedral angle. As sulphur is less electronegative and hence less repulsion is present <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_25316_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25317,'chemistry',64,191,1917,'Among the following, the incorrect statement is','[{"text": "The fourth period atom having a total of six \' $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{d}}$ \' electrons is $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Fe}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}}$ has a smaller radius than $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{K}^{+}}$, because it has a higher nuclear charge", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The magnetic moment for $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Co}^{3+}}$ is ${\\\\rm{4}}{\\\\rm{.90}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{BM}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Among $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}}$, the smaller ion is less paramagnetic.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}}$ is smaller in size. Its electronic configuration is $\\mathrm{[A r] 3 d^{5}}$. It has 5 unpaired electrons and thus is more paramagnetic than $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}}$ ion (which has only 4 unpaired electrons)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
