(25498,'chemistry',65,956,2225,'$\\left[\\mathrm{Co}(\\mathrm{en})_{3}\\right]^{3+}$ ion is expected to show','[{"text": "two optically active isomers: ${\\\\rm{d}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{l}}$ forms", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{d, l}}$ and meso forms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "four optically active isomers: cis, ${\\\\rm{d}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{l}}$ isomers and trans ${\\\\rm{d}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{l}}$ isomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "none of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\left[ {{\\text{Co(en}}{{\\text{)}}_{\\text{3}}}} \\right]^{{\\text{3 + }}}}$  ion due to absence of one of the symmetry of elements it has two $d$ and $l$ optically active forms. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_25498_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25499,'chemistry',64,191,1913,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Transition metals show variable valency.<br></div><div class="column-right">Transition metals have a large energy difference between the $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{ns}^{2}}$ and $\\mathrm{(n-1) d}$ electrons.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The Statement A is correct but the Statement $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{B}}$ is incorrect. Transition metal show variable valency due to very small energy difference between the $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{ns}^{2}}$ and (n-1)d electrons.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25500,'chemistry',63,826,1829,'In which of the following arrangements, the order is not strictly according to the property written against it?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2} < \\\\mathrm{SiO}_{2} < \\\\mathrm{SnO}_{2} < \\\\mathrm{PbO}_{2}$ (oxidising power)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HF} < \\\\mathrm{HCl} < \\\\mathrm{HBr} < \\\\mathrm{HI}$ (acidic strength)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{PH}_{3} < \\\\mathrm{AsH}_{3} < \\\\mathrm{SbH}_{3}$ (basic strength)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "B $ < $ C $ < $ O $ < N$ (first ionisaiton enthalpy)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The correct decreasing order of basic strength is<br>$\\mathrm{NH}_{3}>\\mathrm{PH}_{3}>\\mathrm{AsH}_{3}>\\mathrm{SbH}_{3}$<br>$\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is most basic because of its small size, the electron density of electron pair is concentrated over a small region. As the size increases, the electron density gets diffused over a large surface area and the ability to donate the electron pair (basicity) decreases.<br>The other three statements given are correct.<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25501,'chemistry',66,1031,2433,'The rate of $$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{2}$$ reaction is maximum when the solvent is','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{DMSO}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Benzene", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Polar aprotic solvents such as DMSO increase the rate of the $$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{2}$$ reactions.<br>(DMSO is dimethyl sulphoxide).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25502,'chemistry',65,956,2223,'Different hydrated isomer of $\\mathrm{CrCl}_{3} \\cdot 6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ can not be differentiated by:','[{"text": "Conductivity measurements", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Precipitation by $\\\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dipole moment", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Magnetic moment", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Hydrated isomers of $\\mathrm{CrCl}_{3} \\cdot 6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ are:<br>(i) $\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{3}$<br>(ii) $\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{5} \\mathrm{Cl}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>(iii) $\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl} \\cdot 2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>They all have different conductivity, dipole moment and precipitation by $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$. They all have similar magnetic moment because in all complexes, ligands are weak field. $\\mathrm{Cr}^{+3}$ has 3 unpaired $e^{-}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25503,'chemistry',64,191,1917,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Magnetic moment values of actinides are lesser than the theoretically predicted values.<br></div><div class="column-right">Actinide elements are strongly paramagnetic.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The magnetic moment is less as the $\\mathrm{5 \\mathrm{f}}$ electrons of actinides are less effectively shielded which results in quenching of orbital contribution, they are strongly paramagnetic due to presence of unpaired electrons',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25519,'chemistry',64,192,1925,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Permanganate at $\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right]=1}$ should oxidise water but in practice the reaction is extremely slow.<br></div><div class="column-right">The above reaction is practically feasible only when either manganese (II) ions are present or the temperature is raised.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25504,'chemistry',63,807,1747,'Which of the following products will be obtained when copper metal is reacted with $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ ?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}$ and $\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}$ and $\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ and $\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{2}$ and $\\\\mathrm{N}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The products of copper reaction with $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ depends upon the concentration of $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ used.<br>Copper metal reacts with dilute $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ to form nitrogen (II) oxide (NO).<br>$\\begin{aligned}& 3 \\mathrm{Cu}+8 \\mathrm{HNO}_{3}(\\text { dil. }) \\rightarrow \\\\& 3 \\mathrm{Cu}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}+2 \\mathrm{NO}+4 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\end{aligned}$<br>Copper metal reacts with conc. $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ to form nitrogen (IV) oxide or nitrogen dioxide $\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right)$.$\\mathrm{Cu}+4 \\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ (conc.) $\\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}+2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25505,'chemistry',66,1031,2435,'The number of steps involved in ${{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{N}}}{\\rm{1}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{N}}}{\\rm{2}}$ mechanisms are given by the set','[{"text": "1, 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3, 1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2, 1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2, 2", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25506,'chemistry',65,956,2224,'Among the following coordination compounds/ ions<br>(i) $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]^{3-}$<br>(ii) $\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right]$<br>(iii) $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$<br>(iv) $\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{3}$<br>Which species exhibit geometrical isomerism?','[{"text": "(ii) only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(i) and (ii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(ii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) and (iii)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Geometrical isomerism is possible only in square planar complexes of the type ${\\text{M}}{{\\text{A}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{B}}_{{\\text{2}}\\,\\,}}{\\text{and}}\\,\\,{\\text{M}}{{\\text{A}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{BC}}$  and for octahedral complexes of the type ${\\text{M}}{{\\text{A}}_{\\text{4}}}{{\\text{B}}_{\\text{2}}}\\,\\,{\\text{and}}\\,\\,{\\text{M}}{{\\text{A}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{BC}}$. Hence only (ii) will show geometrical isomerism.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25507,'chemistry',64,188,1906,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">${\\rm{Cr}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}}$ are extra stable elements in the $\\mathrm{3 \\mathrm{~d}}$-series<br></div><div class="column-right">Half and completely filled set of orbitals are relatively more stable.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Half and completely filled set of orbitals are relatively more stable. Thus, electronic configuration of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}}$ is ${\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{d}}^{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{4}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{1}}}$ instead of ${\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{d}}^{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{4}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}$, the energy gap between the two sets ${\\rm{3d}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{4s}}$ of orbitals is small enough to prevent electron entering the 3d-orbitals. Similarly, in the case of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}}$, the configuration is ${\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{d}}^{{\\rm{10}}}}{\\rm{4}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{1}}}$ and not ${\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{d}}^{\\rm{9}}}{\\rm{4}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25508,'chemistry',63,808,1757,'Which of the following oxyacid does not contain $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{P}$ bond?','[{"text": "Isohypophosphoric acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pyrophosphorus acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Diphosphoric acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hypophosphoric acid", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Hypophosphoric acid $\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{4} \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{6}\\right)$ does not contain $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{P}$ bond whereas isohypophosphoric acid $\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{4} \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{6}\\right)$, diphosphorus acid $\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{4} \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}\\right)$ and diphosphoric acid $\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{4} \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}\\right)$ all contain $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{P}$ bonds. The structures of oxyacids of phosphorus are  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_25508_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25509,'chemistry',66,1031,2437,'Ethyl bromide and isopropyl chloride can be distinguished by','[{"text": "Alc. $$\\\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Comparing their colours", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Burning the compounds on spatula", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aq. $$\\\\mathrm{KOH}$$ solution", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'With alcoholic $$\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$$, ethyl bromide gives a light yellow and isopropyl chloride gives white ppt.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25510,'chemistry',65,956,2224,'For which value of the ${\\rm{x}}\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,{\\rm{y,}}$ the following square planar compound shows geometrical isomers ?<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}(\\mathrm{Cl})_{x}(\\mathrm{Br})_{y}\\right]^{2-}$','[{"text": "1,3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3,1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1,1", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Geometrical isomers of following type of square planar complexes is possible.<br>${\\text{M}}{{\\text{a}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{b}}_{\\text{2}}}\\,\\,{\\text{type,}}\\,\\,{\\text{M}}{{\\text{a}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{bc}}$ type and Mabcd type.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25511,'chemistry',64,196,1943,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Actinoids form relatively less stable complexes as compared to lanthanoids.<br></div><div class="column-right">Actinoids can utilise their $\\mathrm{5 \\mathrm{f}-}$ orbitals along with ${\\rm{6d}}$-orbitals in bonding but lanthanoids do not use their 4f-orbital for bonding.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct. Actinoids form relatively more stable complexes as compared to lanthanoids because of actinoids can utilise their 5 -orbitals along with ${\\rm{5d}}$-orbitals in bonding but lanthanoids do not use their $\\mathrm{4 \\mathrm{f}}$-orbitals for bonding.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25512,'chemistry',63,814,1782,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Reaction of $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ in the presence of $\\mathrm{Fe}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ catalyst gives elemental sulphur.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ is a reducing agent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ and $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ react as follows:   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_25512_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  In this reaction, $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ is oxidising agent and oxidises $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ into sulphur.<br>Thus, Assertion  is correct but Reason is incorrect.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25513,'chemistry',66,1031,2436,'Reaction of ethyl bromide and silver acetate gives','[{"text": "Ethyl ethanoate", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Methyl ethanoate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethanoic anhydride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-butanone", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Reaction of ethyl bromide and silver acetate gives ethyl ethanoate. It is an ester formation and has got a fruity smell.<br>$$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOAg}+\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{Br} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOC}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}+$$ $$\\mathrm{AgBr}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25514,'chemistry',65,956,2223,'Which of the following pairs represents linkage isomers?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cu}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{4}\\\\right]\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}\\\\right]$ and<br>$$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Pt}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{4}\\\\right]\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CuCl}_{4}\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[ {{\\\\text{Pd}}{{\\\\left( {{\\\\text{PP}}{{\\\\text{h}}_{\\\\text{3}}}} \\\\right)}_{\\\\text{2}}}{{{\\\\text{(NCS)}}}_{\\\\text{2}}}} \\\\right]$ and<br>$\\\\left[ {{\\\\text{Pd}}{{\\\\left( {{\\\\text{PP}}{{\\\\text{h}}_{\\\\text{3}}}} \\\\right)}_{\\\\text{2}}}{{{\\\\text{(SCN)}}}_{\\\\text{2}}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{5}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{NO}_{3} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and<br>$$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{5} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{NO}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{4}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Br}$ and<br>$$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PtBr}_{2}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\\\right)_{3}\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Linkage isomers are caused due to the presence of ambidentate ligands $\\left[\\mathrm{Pd}(\\mathrm{PPh})_{2}(\\mathrm{NCS})_{2}\\right]$ and<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{Pd}\\left(\\mathrm{PPh}_{3}\\right)_{2}(\\mathrm{SCN})_{2}\\right]$ is a linkage isomers due to $\\mathrm{SCN}$, an ambidentate ligand. Thus, $\\mathrm{SCN}$ can linked to $\\mathrm{Pd}$ by both $\\mathrm{N}$ as well as $\\mathrm{S}$ through their lone pairs',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25515,'chemistry',64,193,1933,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The three series of the transition metals are 3d-series ( $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Sc}}$ to $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Zn}}$ ), 4d series ( $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Y}}$ to $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cd}}$ ) and 5d-series. (La, Hf to $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Hg}}$ )<br></div><div class="column-right">The two series of the inner transition metals (4f and $\\mathrm{5 \\mathrm{f}}$ ) are known as actinoids and lanthanoids, respectively.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The three main series of the transition metals are ${\\rm{3d}}$ series (${\\rm{Sc}}\\,\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Zn}}$), ${\\rm{4d}}$ series ( ${\\rm{Y}}\\,\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Cd}}$ ) and ${\\rm{5d}}$ series (La to $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Hg}}$ omitting $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ce}}$ to $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Lu}}$ ). The fourth ${\\rm{6d}}$ series [ Ac, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Rf}}$ to $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cn}}$ ]. The two series of the inner-transition metals, (4f and 5f) are known as lanthanoids and actinoids respectively.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25516,'chemistry',63,813,1778,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Ozone is powerful oxidising agent in comparison to $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Ozone is diamagnetic but $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is paramagnetic.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ozone is a powerful oxidising agent next to fluorine. It is much stronger oxidising agent because it forms nascent oxygen on easy decomposition.<br>$\\mathrm{O}_{3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{O}_{2}+[\\mathrm{O}]$<br>It is also true that $\\mathrm{O}_{3}$ is diamagnetic while $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is paramagnetic. But it is not the explanation for assertion.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25517,'chemistry',66,1036,2451,'$$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{I}+\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{MgI} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{X}$$. The product " $$\\mathrm{X}$$ " is','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right) \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'It is an example of ${{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{N}}}^{\\rm{2}}$ reaction.<br>$$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2}^{-}$$in Grignard reagent acts as a nucleophile.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25518,'chemistry',65,956,2226,'Consider the following reaction and statements<br>$$\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{Br}_{2}\\right]^{+}+\\mathrm{Br}^{-}$$<br>$$\\rightarrow\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{Br}_{3}\\right]+\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$$<br>(I) Two isomers are produced if the reactant complex ion is a cis-isomer.<br>(II) Two isomers are produced if the reactant complex ion is a trans-isomer.<br>(III) Only one isomer is produced if the reactant complex ion is a trans-isomer.<br>(IV)Only one isomer is produced if the reactant complex ion is a cis-isomer.<br>The correct statements are:','[{"text": "(I) and (III)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(III) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (II)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_25518_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25520,'chemistry',63,811,1768,'The incorrect statement among the following is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{60}$ is an allotropic form of carbon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$ is an allotropic form of oxygen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}_{8}$ is only allotropic form of sulphur", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Red phosphorus is more stable in air than white phosphorus<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'There are five allotropic forms of sulphur<br>1. Rhombic, octahedral or $\\alpha $ - sulphur<br>2. Monoclinic, prismatic or $\\beta $ - sulphur<br>3. Plastic or $\\gamma $ - sulphur<br>4. Milk of sulphur<br>5. Colloidal or $\\delta $ - sulphur',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25521,'chemistry',66,1031,2436,'Treatment of ammonia with excess ethyl chloride gives','[{"text": "Diethylamine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Methylamine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tetraethyl ammonium chloride", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Treatment of ammonia with excess ethyl chloride will give quarternary ammonium salt.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25522,'chemistry',65,956,2224,'The number of geometric isomers that can exist for square planar  $\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}(\\mathrm{Cl})(\\mathrm{py})\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}\\right)\\right]^{+}$ is (py $=$ pyridine):','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The complex is of the type [Mabed] $\\mathrm{M}=$ metal; $\\quad \\mathrm{a}, \\mathrm{b}, \\mathrm{c}, \\mathrm{d}=$ Monodentate ligands. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_25522_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25523,'chemistry',64,189,1907,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Radii of the members of the third transition series is very similar to those of the corresponding members of the second series.<br></div><div class="column-right">It is due to lanthanoid contraction.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25524,'chemistry',63,826,1829,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Moelcular nitrogen is less reactive than molecular oxygen.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The bond length of $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ is shorter than that of oxygen.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ is less reactive than that of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ because nitrogen has high dissociation energy in comparison to oxygen so, its reactivity is less. Also, bond length of nitrogen is shroter than oxygen because of presence of triple bond between nitrogen atoms. Hence, both Assertion and Reason  are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25525,'chemistry',66,1031,2437,'Rank the following species in the order of decreasing nucleophilicity in a polar protic solvent (Most to least nucleophilic):<br>(A) $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{O}^{-}$$<br>(B) $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}^{-}$$<br>(C) $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{COO}^{-}$$','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}>\\\\mathrm{A}>\\\\mathrm{B}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B $$>$$ C $$>$$ A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{A}>\\\\mathrm{C}>\\\\mathrm{B}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B $$>$$ A $$>$$ C", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Due to symmetrical resonance present in $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COO}^{-}$$, it is less nucleophilic than (A) and (B).<br>Due to more electronegativity of oxygen, (A) is less nucleophilic than (B)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25526,'chemistry',65,956,2225,'Which of the following complex species is not expected to exhibit optical isomerism?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\mathrm{en})\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right]^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{3}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\text { en })_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right]^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\text { en })_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right]^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Optical isomerism is exhibited by only those complexes which lack elements of symmetry.<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{Cl}_{3}\\right]$ shows facial as well as meridional isomerism.<br>But the forms contain plane of symmetry. Thus, this complex does not exhibit optical isomerism.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25527,'chemistry',64,189,1907,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The order of atomic radii of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}, \\mathrm{Ag}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Au}}$ is $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu} < \\mathrm{Ag} \\approx \\mathrm{Au}}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The atomic radii of $\\mathrm{4 d}$ series elements are larger than those of $\\mathrm{3 d}$ series elements but generally the radii of $\\mathrm{4 d}$ and $\\mathrm{5 d}$ series elements are almost identical.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The atomic radii, in general, increase down the group. The atomic radii of second series are larger than those of first transition series. In the atoms of the second transition series, the number of shells are more than those of the first transition series. As a result, the atoms of second transition series, the number of shells are more than those of the first transition series. As a result, the atoms of second transition series are larger than those of the elements of the first transition series. But the atomic radii of the second and third transition series are almost the same. This phenomenon is associated with the intervention of the $\\mathrm{4 \\mathrm{f}}$ orbitals which must be filled before the ${\\rm{5d}}$ series of elements begin. The filling of $\\mathrm{4 \\mathrm{f}}$ before ${\\rm{5d}}$ orbital results in a regular decrease in atomic radii called Lanthanoid contraction which essentially compensates for the expected increase in atomic size with increasing atomic number.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25529,'chemistry',66,1031,2435,'Among the given halides, which one will give same product in both $$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{1}$$ and $$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{2}$$ reactions?<br>(A) $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right) \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}(\\mathrm{Br}) \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$$<br>(B) $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}(\\mathrm{Br}) \\mathrm{Et}$$<br>(C) Cyclohexyl chloride','[{"text": "(A) & (B)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(B) & (C)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(C) & (A)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$2^{\\circ}$$ halides can undergo both $$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{1}$$ and $$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{2}$$ reactions.<br>In case of compound (1), rearranged product is possible which differ from ${{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{N}}}^{\\rm{2}}$ product.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25530,'chemistry',65,956,2224,'Which one of the following complex ions has geometrical isomers?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cr}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{4}(\\\\mathrm{en})\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2}(\\\\mathrm{en})_{2}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{5} \\\\mathrm{Br}\\\\right]^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\text { en })_{3}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_25530_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25531,'chemistry',64,194,1937,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{C e^{4+}}$ is a good oxidising agent.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Sm}^{2+}}$ is a good reducing agent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In +4 state, Ce is most oxidizing because it can give an electron to become more stable at +3 state. Whereas in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Sm}^{+2}}$, the lanthanides are more stable in +3 state and hence in +2 state it gets oxidized easily (good reducing agent) to get to +3 oxidation state.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25532,'chemistry',63,814,1789,'$\\mathrm{S}^{2-}$ and $\\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{2-}$ can be distinguished by using','[{"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COO}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{~Pb}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{5} \\\\mathrm{NO}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{S}^{2-}+\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COO}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{~Pb} \\rightarrow \\underset{\\text { Black ppt }}{\\mathrm{PbS}} \\downarrow+2 \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COO}^{-}\\end{aligned}$<br>${{\\text{S}}^{{\\text{2 - }}}}{\\text{ +  N}}{{\\text{a}}_{\\text{2}}}\\left[ {{\\text{Fe(CN}}{{\\text{)}}_{\\text{5}}}{\\text{NO}}} \\right] \\to $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mathop {\\left[ {{\\text{Fe(CN}}{{\\text{)}}_{\\text{5}}}{\\text{NOS}}} \\right]}\\limits_{{\\text{Violet}}\\,\\,{\\text{ppt}}} ^{{\\text{4 - }}}}{\\text{ +  2N}}{{\\text{a}}^{\\text{ + }}}$<br>Hence, they can be distinguished by both the reagents as only $\\mathrm{S}^{2-}$ gives precipitate with these reagents, while $\\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{2-}$ does not.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25533,'chemistry',66,1032,2440,'The order of reactivity of alkyl halides towards elimination reaction is','[{"text": "$$3^{\\\\circ}>2^{\\\\circ}>1^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{2}}^{\\\\rm{^\\\\circ }}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{1}}^{\\\\rm{^\\\\circ }}}{\\\\rm{ > }}{{\\\\rm{3}}^{\\\\rm{^\\\\circ }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3^{\\\\circ}>1^{\\\\circ}>2^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1^{\\\\circ}>2^{\\\\circ}>3^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Elimination reactions of alkyl halides involve carbocations, hence the order of reactivity is $$3^{\\circ}>2^{\\circ}>1^{o}$$ alkyl halide.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25534,'chemistry',64,191,1914,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}}$ is the only ion $\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{M}^{2+}\\right)}$ which has positive $\\mathrm{E_{r e d}^{o}\\left(M^{2+} / M\\right)}$ in ${\\rm{3d}}$ series.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}}$ has lower hydration enthalpy as comparision to its I.E. and $\\mathrm{\\Delta H_{\\text {atomisation }}}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25535,'chemistry',65,956,2225,'Which one of the following has an optical isomer? (en = ethylenediamine)','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Zn}(\\\\text { en })_{2}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{4}(\\\\text { en })\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Zn}(\\\\text { en })\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\text { en })_{3}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Complex $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}(\\mathrm{en})_{3}\\right]^{3+}$ has no plane of symmetry and centre of symmetry. That\'s why, it is optically active.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_25535_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25536,'chemistry',63,814,1781,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The $\\mathrm{S}-\\mathrm{S}-\\mathrm{S}$ bond angle in $\\mathrm{S}_{8}$ molecule is $107^{\\circ}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{S}_{8}$ has V - shape.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{S}$ - atoms in $\\mathrm{S}_{8}$ molecule are $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ hybridised and contain two lone pairs of electrons on each sulphur atom and exist as staggered eight atom ring. Tetrahedral angle is $109^{\\circ}$ but due to lone pairof electrons, it reduces to $107^{\\circ} . \\mathrm{S}_{8}$ ring is puckered and has a crown shape, but each \' $\\mathrm{S}$ \' atom is in V-shape only.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
