(25543,'chemistry',65,960,2239,'Which of the following statements is incorrect?','[{"text": "Greater the stability constant of a complex ion, greater is its stability.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Greater the charge on the central metal ion, greater is the stability of the complex.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Greater the basic character of the ligand, the greater is the stability of the complex.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Complexes have low stability constants.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25544,'chemistry',63,814,1784,'Which of the following statement is correct for oleum?','[{"text": "It is prepared by absorption of $\\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ in conc. $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It contains $\\\\mathrm{O}-\\\\mathrm{O}$ groups", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It has six $\\\\mathrm{OH}$ groups", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Oleum, i.e. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ can be prepared by absorption of $\\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ in conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ as follows:<br>$\\mathrm{SO}_{3}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow \\underset{\\text { Oleum }}{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}$<br>It\'s structure is as follows:  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_25544_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Thus, it has no $\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{O}$ bond and has $2-\\mathrm{OH}$ groups.<br>Thus, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25545,'chemistry',66,1032,2439,'In which of the following reactions Saytzeff alkene is major product?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Quaternary ammonium salts, weak leaving groups and in presence of bulky groups, Hoffmann product is major product. Saytzeff product is major other than the above mentioned conditions.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25546,'chemistry',64,196,1943,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Magnetic properties of Actinoids are more complex than Lanthanoids.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The variation in the magnetic susceptibility of the actinoids with the number of unpaired $\\mathrm{5 \\mathrm{f}}$ electrons is roughly parallel to the corresponding results for the lanthanoids, the latter have higher values.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25547,'chemistry',63,819,1813,'Which two of the following salts are used for preparing iodised salt?<br>I. $\\mathrm{KIO}_{3}$<br>II. KI<br>III. $\\mathrm{I}_{2}$<br>IV. HI','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'To prepare iodised salt, $\\mathrm{KIO}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{KI}$ are used.<br>',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25548,'chemistry',65,960,2239,'In a complex ${\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{L}}_{\\rm{2}}}$, stepwise formation constants for<br>${\\text{M + L}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{ML}}$<br>${\\text{ML + L}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{M}}{{\\text{L}}_{\\text{2}}}$<br>are 4 and 3 . Hence, overall stability constant for the reaction, $M+2 L \\rightleftharpoons M L_{2}$ is,','[{"text": "12", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.33", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.75", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\beta {\\text{ overall = }}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{1}}} \\times {{\\text{K}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ = 4}} \\times {\\text{3 = 12}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25549,'chemistry',66,1032,2440,'Which one of the following compound will be least susceptible to elimination of hydrogen bromide?','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Br}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Br}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Br}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CN}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Br}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Et}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Due to $$+\\mathrm{IE}$$ character of $$-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$$ group',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25550,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'Which one of the following statements is correct when $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{2}}$ is passed through acidified $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}$ solution?','[{"text": "The solution is decolourised.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}}$ is reduced.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Green $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\\\right)_{3}}$ is formed.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The solution turns blue.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}$ is reduced to green solution of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}}$ whereas $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{2}}$ oxidises into sulphate $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}+\\mathrm{SO}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\right.}$ $\\mathrm{\\left.\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25551,'chemistry',63,817,1800,'Which one of the following is the true covalent oxide of iodine?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{9}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In general, higher the oxidation state, more is the covalent character of the oxide. $\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ and $\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{9}$ do not exist. $\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is ionic in nature. It is infact iodyl iodates $[\\mathrm{IO}]^{+}\\left[\\mathrm{IO}_{3}\\right]^{-}$. The only covalent oxide of iodine is $\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}$.<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25552,'chemistry',65,960,2238,'In the complexes $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+},\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]^{3-}$ $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)_{3}\\right]^{3-}$ and $\\left[\\mathrm{FeCl}_{6}\\right]^{3-}$, more stability is shown by','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}\\\\right)_{3}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{FeCl}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)_{3}\\right]^{3-}$. The iron is present in the highest oxidation state $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}$ is a chelating ligand. Chelates are always more stable complexes.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25553,'chemistry',66,1032,2440,'The conversion of 2,3-dibromobutane to 2 butene with $$\\mathrm{Zn}$$ is','[{"text": "Redox reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\alpha$$-elimination", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\beta$$-elimination", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both $$\\\\alpha$$-elimination and redox reaction", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Removal of bromine molecule in presence of $$\\mathrm{Zn}$$ is an example of $$\\beta$$-elimination.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
