(25584,'chemistry',63,818,1807,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{NaCl}$ reacts with concentrated $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ to give colourless fumes with pungent smell. But on adding $\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ the fumes become greenish yellow.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ oxidises $\\mathrm{HCl}$ to chlorine gas which is greenish yellow.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{NaCl}$ reacts with concentrated $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ to give colourless fumes with pungent smell. This pungent smell is due to foramtion of $\\mathrm{HCl}$.<br>$\\mathrm{NaCl}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{HCl}$<br>But on adding $\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$, the fumes become greenish yellow due to formation of chlorine gas.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25585,'chemistry',64,191,1921,'Identify the incorrect statement','[{"text": "The transition metals and their compounds are known for their catalytic activity due to their ability to adopt multiple oxidation states and to form complexes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Interstitial compounds are those that are formed when small atom like $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}, \\\\mathrm{C}}$ or $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{N}}$ are trapped inside the crystal lattices of metals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The oxidation states of chromium in $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2^{-}}}$are not the same", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}\\\\left(d^{4}\\\\right)}$ is a stronger reducing agent than $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\\\\left(d^{6}\\\\right)}$ in water", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Chromate $\\left( {{\\rm{CrO}}_{\\rm{4}}^{{\\rm{ - 2}}}} \\right) \\Rightarrow $ oxidation state $\\mathrm{=+6}$<br>Dichromate$\\left( {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{7}}^{{\\rm{ - 2}}}} \\right) \\Rightarrow $ oxidation state $\\mathrm{=+6}$ oxidation state are same',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25586,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'An excess of $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ is added to $100 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of a 0.01 M solution of dichlorotetraaquachromium (III) chloride. The number of moles of $\\mathrm{AgCl}$ precipitated would be','[{"text": "0.01", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.001", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.002", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.003", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}+\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl} \\rightarrow$<br>$$\\mathrm{AgCl}+\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right] \\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right.$$<br>millimole of $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ added is<br>$$100 \\times 0.1=1$$<br>$\\therefore$ Mole of $\\mathrm{AgCl}=1 \\times 10^{-3}=0.001$<br>(As $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ causes ppt of $\\mathrm{AgCl}$ so $\\mathrm{AgCl}$ ppt is $10^{-3}$ moles)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25587,'chemistry',66,1031,2433,'Which of the following compounds can undergo substitution under ${{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{N}}}{\\rm{2}}$ condition only?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{1}$ is not possible for (1) because the nitro group destablises the carbocation. In the case of (2) $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{1}$ is also possible. (3) undergoes substitution by $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{1}$ only because it is tertiary. In (4), $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{1}$ and $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{2}$ are not possible. Only $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{\\text {Ar }}$ takes place.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25588,'chemistry',63,813,1778,'What is $\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{O}$ bond length in resonance hybrid of ozone?','[{"text": "${\\\\text{131 pm}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{121 pm}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{128 pm}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{148 pm}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_25588_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Resonance hybrid of ozone<br>$\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{O}$ bond length in resonance hybrid of ozone $=128 \\mathrm{pm}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25589,'chemistry',64,196,1943,'The incorrect statement among the following is:','[{"text": "Most of the trivalent Lanthanoid ions are colourless in the solid state.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lanthanoids are good conductors of heat and electricity.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Actinoids are highly reactive metals, especially when finely divided.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Actinoid contraction is greater for element to element than Lanthanoid contraction.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Most of the trivalent Lanthanoid ions are coloured both in solid state and in aqueous solution.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25590,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'On treatment of $100 \\mathrm{~mL}$ of $0.1 \\mathrm{M}$ solution of $\\mathrm{CoCl}_{3} \\cdot 6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ with exces $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3} ; 1.2 \\times 10^{22}$ ions are precipitated . The complex is :-','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl} \\\\cdot 2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{3}\\\\right] \\\\cdot 3 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{5} \\\\mathrm{Cl}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\\\cdot \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { Moles of complex }=\\dfrac{\\text { Molarity } \\times \\text { volume }(\\mathrm{ml})}{1000} \\\\& =\\dfrac{100 \\times 0.1}{1000}=0.01 \\mathrm{~mole}\\end{aligned}$$<br>Moles of ions precipitated with excess of<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}=\\dfrac{1.2 \\times 10^{22}}{6.02 \\times 10^{23}} \\\\& =0.02 \\text { moles }\\end{aligned}$$<br>Number of $\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$present in ionization sphere<br>${\\text{ = }}\\frac{{{\\text{Mole of ion precipitated with excess AgN}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}}}{{{\\text{mole}}\\,\\,{\\text{of}}\\,\\,{\\text{complex}}}}$<br>$$=\\dfrac{0.02}{0.01}=2$$<br>It means $2 \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ions present in ionization sphere<br>$$\\therefore \\text { complex is }\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{5} \\mathrm{Cl}_{3}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\cdot \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25611,'chemistry',65,954,2214,'Select the incorrect statement:','[{"text": "Hydrazine can act as bidentate ligand.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Oxalate is a chelating ligand.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All bidentate ligands are chelating ligands.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Number of chelate rings $=$ (denticity-1)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Hydrazine $\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~N}-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}\\right)$ is a monodentate ligand. All bidentate ligands are chelating ligands, oxalate is a bidentate ligand.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25591,'chemistry',66,1031,2433,'Neopentyl alcohol,  reacts with $$\\mathrm{HX}$$ according to:','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{N}}}1$ mechanism", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{N}}}{\\\\rm{2}}$ mechanism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{N}}}{\\\\rm{i}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Neopentyl alcohol, though is a $$1^{\\circ}$$ alcohol, it undergoes substitution through $$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{1}$$ mechanism because of steric hindrance due to bulky alkyl group.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25591_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_25591_question_1.png'),
