(25664,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'When sulphur dioxide is passed in an acidified $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}$ solution, the oxidation state of sulphur is changed from','[{"text": "+4 to +6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "+6 to +4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+4 to 0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+4 to +2", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}$ acts as a strong oxidising agent. It oxidises other compounds and gets itself reduced. In $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{2}, \\mathrm{~S}}$ is in +4 state so it gets oxidised to +6 state which is maximum group oxidation state.<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}+4 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}+4 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$<br>$\\rm{\\begin{array}{r}\\dfrac{\\left[\\mathrm{SO}_{2}+[\\mathrm{O}]+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right] \\times 3}{\\mathrm{~K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+3 \\mathrm{SO}_{2} \\rightarrow} \\\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\end{array}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
