(25720,'chemistry',65,956,2224,'On treatment of $\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4}\\right]^{2+}$ with concentrated $\\mathrm{HCl}$, two compounds (I) and (II) having the same formula, $\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right]$ are obtained, (I) can be converted into (II) by boiling with dilute $\\mathrm{HCl}$. A solution of (I) reacts with oxalic acid to form $\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)\\right]$ whereas (II) does not react. Point out the correct statement of the following.','[{"text": "(I) cis, (II) trans; both tetrahedral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) cis, (II) trans; both square planar", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(I) trans, (II) cis; both tetrahedral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) trans, (II) cis; both square planar", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Chelate ligands i.e., oxalato being too small to span the trans positions and thus prefer to occupy the cis-position. So (I) is cis and (II) is trans. Platinum is in +2 oxidation state with ${\\text{5}}{{\\text{d}}^{\\text{8}}}$ valence shell electron configuration. This configuration has higher CFSE and thus favours square planar geometry.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
