(25769,'chemistry',64,189,1908,'Match the column I with column II and mark the appropriate choice.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Element with highest second ionisation enthalpy<br>B. Element with highest third ionisation enthalpy<br>C. M in $\\left[ {{\\rm{M}}{{\\left( {{\\rm{CO}}} \\right)}_{\\rm{6}}}} \\right]$ is<br>D. Element with highest heat of atomisation</div><div class="column-right">P. Cr<br>Q. Cu<br>R. Zn<br>S. V</div></div>','[{"text": "A - Q, B - R, C - P, D - S", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - S, B - R, C - P, D - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - P, C - Q, D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P, B - Q, C - R, D - S", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A. Chromium (Cr) is known for having a high second ionization enthalpy because it achieves a stable half-filled electronic configuration after losing one electron.<br>B. Among the given options, Vanadium (V),<br>being in the middle of the transition series, has a relatively high third ionization enthalpy.<br>${\\rm{C}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{M}}{\\left( {{\\rm{CO}}} \\right)_{\\rm{6}}}$ implies a metal bonded with six CO molecules. Chromium can exist in the coordination number 6, so M would be Cr.<br>D. Copper (Cu), given its electron configuration and bonding characteristics, has the highest heat of atomization among the options listed.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
