(25801,'chemistry',66,1028,2419,'A hydrocarbon of molecular mass $$72 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$$ gives a single monochloro derivative and two dichloro derivatives on photo chlorination. Hydrocarbon is','[{"text": "n - Butane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "n - Pentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isopentane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neo pentane", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$$\\mathrm{C}_{5} \\mathrm{H}_{12}$$, pentane has molecular mass $$72 \\mathrm{~g}$$ $$\\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$$, i.e., the isomer of pentane which yields single monochloro derivative should have all the 12 hydrogens equivalent.<br>The hydrocarbon is <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25801_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Monochlorod derivative <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25801_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Dichloro derivatives <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25801_explanation_3.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25802,'chemistry',64,194,1934,'The electronic configuration of $\\mathrm{G d^{2+}}$ is (at.no.of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Gd}}$ is 64)','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{[X e] 4 f^{7}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{[X e] 4 f^{7} 5 d^{1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{[\\\\mathrm{Xe}] 4 f^{8}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{[X e] 4 f^{7} 5 d^{1} 6 s^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25803,'chemistry',63,824,1827,'Which of the following is a false statement?','[{"text": "Radon is obtained by the decay of radium.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Helium is an inert gas.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Xenon is the most reacting among rare gases.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The most abundant rare gas in the atmosphere is helium.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The most abundant rare gas in atmosphere is Argon.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25805,'chemistry',64,196,1943,'Identify the correct statement among the following.','[{"text": "the earlier members of lanthanoid series resemble calcium in their chemical properties", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "the extent of actinoid contraction is not same as lanthanoid contraction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In general, lanthanoids and actinoids shows variable oxidation states", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C e^{4+}}$ in aqueous solution is not known", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Except option (1) remaining options are incorrect statements. The correct statements of option<br>2: The extent of actinoid contraction is not same as lanthanoid contraction option<br>3: In general lanthanoids and actinoids shows variable oxidation states option<br>4: $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}}$ in aqueous solution exist.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25806,'chemistry',66,1035,2445,'The product of 1-bromo-3-chloro cyclobutane with $$\\mathrm{Na}$$ in presence of dioxane is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Bond energy of $$\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{Br} < $$ Bond energy of $$\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{Cl}$$.<br>Only $$\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{Br}$$ is affected by wurtz reaction <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25806_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25806_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25809,'chemistry',64,194,1937,'Which of the following statements is wrong regarding lanthanoids?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{Ln}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{(III)}}$ compounds are predominantly ionic in character.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Ln (III)}}$ hydroxides are mainly basic in nature.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The ionic size of ${\\\\rm{Ln (III)}}$ ions decreases with increasing atomic number.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Ln (III)}}$ compounds are generally colourless.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Many of the trivalent ions of lanthanoids are coloured both in the solid state as well as in solution as they contain unpaired electrons in $\\mathrm{4 \\mathrm{f}}$ and $\\mathrm{5 \\mathrm{~d}}$ subshells.. The colour of lanthanoid ions arises due to absorption of light from visible region of spectrum resulting in $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{f}}$ - $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{f}}$ transition as lanthanoids have partly filled f- orbitals.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25811,'chemistry',63,814,1785,'On addition of conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ to a chloride salt, colourless fumes are evolved but in case of iodide salt, violet fumes come out. This is because:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ reduces $\\\\mathrm{HI}$ to $\\\\mathrm{I}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HI}$ is of violet colour", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "HI gets oxidised to $\\\\mathrm{I}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "HI changes to $\\\\mathrm{HIO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Hydrogen iodide (HI) is more stronger oxidising agent than $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$. So, it reduces $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ to $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ and itself oxidises to $\\mathrm{I}_{2}$.<br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{HI} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{SO}_{2}+\\underset{\\substack{\\text { Violet } \\\\ \\text { colour }}}{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25812,'chemistry',66,1031,2435,'Which of the following statements are correct about the reactivities of (I) n-propylchloride and (II) Allyl chloride?','[{"text": "Rate of ${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{N}}}{\\\\rm{1}}$ reaction of (I) $$>$$ (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rate of ${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{N}}}{\\\\rm{1}}$ reaction of (II) $$>$$ (I)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Rate of ${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{N}}}{\\\\rm{2}}$ reaction of (I) $$>$$ (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rate of ${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{N}}}{\\\\rm{2}}$ reaction of (II) = (I)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Allyl chloride is much more reactive than n-propyl chloride, although it is $$1^{\\circ} \\mathrm{RX}$$, due to resonance stabilisation. Allyl chloride is reactive by both $$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{1}$$ and $$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{2}$$ meachanisms, both more reactive by $$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}^{1}$$ mechanism.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25813,'chemistry',64,194,1937,'Identify the incorrect statement with reference to $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ce}(\\mathrm{Z}=58)}$.','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C e^{4+}}$ is a reducing agent.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ce in +3 oxidation state is more stable than in +4 .", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atomic size of $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Ce}}$ is more than that of $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Lu}}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ce shows common oxidation states of +3 and +4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{C e^{4+}}$ is an oxidising agent.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25815,'chemistry',63,808,1757,'In a cyclotrimetaphosphoric acid molecule, how many bonds are present?','[{"text": "3 double bonds; 9 single bonds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6 double bonds; 6 single bonds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 double bonds; 12 single bonds", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Zero double bonds; 12 single bonds.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{CuS}+2 \\mathrm{HNO}_{3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$<br>$\\mathrm{Cu}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}+4 \\mathrm{NH}_{3} \\rightarrow \\underset{\\text { deep blue solution }}{\\left[\\mathrm{Cu}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4}\\right]^{+2}}+2 \\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$<br>Cyclic form of meta phosphoric acid <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_25815_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25816,'chemistry',66,1031,2435,'Arrange the following alkyl halides in the increasing reactivity towards nucleophilic substitution reaction','[{"text": "A $$>$$ B $$>$$ D $$>$$ C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A $$>$$ C $$>$$ B $$>$$ D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B $$>$$ C $$>$$ A $$>$$ D", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{D}>\\\\mathrm{A}>\\\\mathrm{C}>\\\\mathrm{B}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Stability of formed carbocation and weak bond strength of $$\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{X}(\\mathrm{X}=\\mathrm{Br}, \\mathrm{Cl})$$ favour the $$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}$$ reaction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_25816_question_1.jpg');
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(25817,'chemistry',64,194,1938,'What is the general molecular formula of the products obtained on heating lanthanoids ( $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ln})}$ with sulphur?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\operatorname{LnS}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{LnS}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{L}}{{\\\\rm{n}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{L}}{{\\\\rm{n}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When lanthanoids ( $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ln})}$ are heated with sulphur, then ${\\rm{L}}{{\\rm{n}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{3}}}$ are formed. Their sulphides $\\left( {{\\rm{L}}{{\\rm{n}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{3}}}} \\right)$ are highly stable because $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ln}}$ is in +3 oxidation state.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2016,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25819,'chemistry',63,806,1738,'Bond dissociation enthalpies of $\\mathrm{E}-\\mathrm{H}(\\mathrm{E}=$ element) bonds are given below. Which of the compounds will act as strongest reducing agent?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{AsH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SbH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Less bond enthalpy of hydride - strong is the reducing nature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/63_25819_question_1.png'),
(25820,'chemistry',66,1038,2461,'Identify ${\\rm{Z}}$ in the following series:<br>${{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{5}}}{\\text{I}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{AlC}} \\cdot {\\text{KOH}}}}{\\text{X}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{B}}{{\\text{r}}_{\\text{2}}}}}{\\text{Y}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{KCN}}}}{\\text{Z}}$','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CN}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{NCCH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CN}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{BrCH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CN}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{BrCH}=\\\\mathrm{CHCN}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{I} \\stackrel{\\text { alc. } \\mathrm{KOH}}{\\longrightarrow} \\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\stackrel{\\mathrm{Br}_{2}}{\\longrightarrow}$$<br>$$\\mathrm{BrCH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{Br} \\stackrel{\\mathrm{KCN}}{\\longrightarrow} \\mathrm{CNCH}_{2} \\cdot \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CN}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46134,'physics',42,322,590,'The valency of impurity element for making $n$-type semiconductor is','[{"text": "$3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25821,'chemistry',64,194,1937,'The most basic hydroxide from following is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\operatorname{Pr}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{3}(\\\\mathrm{Z}=59)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Sm}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{3}(\\\\mathrm{Z}=62)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Ho}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{3}(\\\\mathrm{Z}=67)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{La}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{3}(\\\\mathrm{Z}=57)}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Due to lanthanoid contraction, the basic character of hydroxides decreases from $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{La}^{3+}}$ to $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Lu}^{3+}}$.<br>As a result, covalent nature of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ln}-\\mathrm{OH}}$ bond also increases. Thus, the most basic hydroxide among the given option is $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{La}(\\mathrm{OH})_{3}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25823,'chemistry',63,808,1752,'On heating with concentrated $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ solution in an inert atmosphere of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$, white phosphorus gives a gas. Which of the following statement is incorrect about the gas?','[{"text": "It is highly poisonous and smells like rotten fish.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It\'s solution in water decomposes in the presence of light.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is more basic than $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is less basic than $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{P}_{4}+3 \\mathrm{NaOH}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{PH}_{3}+\\underset{\\text { (sodium hypophosphite) }}{3 \\mathrm{NaH}_{2} \\mathrm{PO}_{2}}$<br>Phosphine gas is less basic than $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25824,'chemistry',66,1032,2440,'Compound \'$$\\mathrm{X}$$\' reacts with alcoholic $$\\mathrm{KOH}$$ to yield compound \'$$\\mathrm{Y}$$\' with on ozonolysis followed by reaction with $$\\mathrm{Zn} / \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$$ gives methanal and propanal. Compound \' $$\\mathrm{X}$$ \' is','[{"text": "1-Propanol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1-Butanol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1-Chlorobutane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1-Chloropentane", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathop {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{Cl}}}\\limits_{\\left( {\\rm{X}} \\right)} $ $\\xrightarrow[\\Delta ]{{{\\text{alc}}{\\text{.}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{KOH}}}}$ $\\mathop {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{CH  =  C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits_{\\left( {\\rm{Y}} \\right)} $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{ + }}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ZnH,O}}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{CHO + HCHO}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25825,'chemistry',64,195,1941,'Which of the following actinoids exhibits only +3 oxidation state?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\operatorname{Lr}(Z=103)}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{U(Z=92)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\operatorname{Th}(Z=90)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Pa}(\\\\mathrm{Z}=91)}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Lawrencium (Lr) shows only +3 oxidation state, that has electronic configuration $\\mathrm{[\\mathrm{Rn}] 5 \\mathrm{f}^{14} 7 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 7 \\mathrm{p}^{1}}$. This element may easily give up three valence electron from the $\\mathrm{7 \\mathrm{~s}}$ and $\\mathrm{7 \\mathrm{p}}$ subshell to form a stable +3 oxidation state. The state is especially stable in case of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Lr}}$ because it contain full filled $\\mathrm{\\left(5 f^{14}\\right) \\mathrm{f}}$ - subshell.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25827,'chemistry',63,807,1746,'Lead nitrate on heating forms oxides of nitrogen and lead. The oxides formed are:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}, \\\\mathrm{PbO}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{PbO}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}, \\\\mathrm{PbO}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}, \\\\mathrm{PbO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$2 \\mathrm{~Pb}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} 4 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{PbO}+\\mathrm{O}_{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25828,'chemistry',66,1037,2458,'Which of the following is known as freon which is used as a refrigerant ?','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CCl}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~F}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~F}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CF}_{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{CC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ called Freon.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25830,'chemistry',65,958,2234,'The CFSE for tetrahedral $\\left[\\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}\\right]^{2-}$ is 20,000 $\\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$. The CFSE for octahedral $\\left[\\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}\\right]^{2-}$ will be','[{"text": "$8,000 \\\\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20,000 \\\\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45,000 \\\\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$16,000 \\\\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to question, $\\Delta_{o}=20,000 \\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}\\therefore \\quad \\Delta_{o}=\\dfrac{9}{4} \\Delta_{t} & =\\dfrac{9}{4} \\times 20,000 \\mathrm{~cm}^{-1} \\\\& =45,000 \\mathrm{~cm}^{-1} .\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25831,'chemistry',63,807,1751,'Which of the following statements is wrong?','[{"text": "Single N-N bond is stronger than the single $\\\\mathrm{P}-\\\\mathrm{P}$ bond", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ can act as a ligand in the formation of coordination compound with transition elements", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ is paramagnetic in nature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Covalency of nitrogen in $\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{5}$ is four", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Single N-N bond is weaker than the single $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{P}$ bond. This is why phosphorous show allotropy but nitrogen does not.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25832,'chemistry',66,1034,2444,'How many moles of ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{OH}}$ required to react completely with ‘B’?','[{"text": "One", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Two", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Four", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25832_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_25832_question_1.jpg'),
(25844,'chemistry',66,1030,2429,'Arrange the following compounds in the increasing order of their densities.','[{"text": "(A) $$ < $$ (B) $$ < $$ (C) $$ < $$ (D)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(A) $$ < $$ (C) $$ < $$ (D) $$ < $$ (B)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(D) $$ < $$ (C) $$ < $$ (B) $$ < $$ (A)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(B) $$ < $$ (D) $$ < $$ (C) $$ < $$ (A)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Molecular weight is directly proportional to the density.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_25844_question_1.jpg'),
(27233,'chemistry',67,1088,2612,'Diethyl ether mixed with alcohol is used as a petrol substitute. It is used under the name','[{"text": "Bakelite", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Natalite", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hypnotite", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hostalene", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Natalite is industrial name for dichloro diethyl ether + alcohol, a petrol substitute.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25834,'chemistry',65,960,2239,'Which of the following complexes formed by $\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions is most stable?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+4 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} \\\\longrightarrow\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cu}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{4}\\\\right]^{2+}, \\\\log \\\\mathrm{K}=8.9$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+4 \\\\mathrm{NH}_{3} \\\\longrightarrow\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cu}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{4}\\\\right]^{2+}, \\\\log \\\\mathrm{K}=11.6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+4 \\\\mathrm{CN}^{-} \\\\longrightarrow\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cu}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}, \\\\log \\\\mathrm{K}=27.3$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+2 \\\\mathrm{en}^{-} \\\\longrightarrow\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cu}(\\\\text { en })_{2}\\\\right]^{2+}, \\\\log \\\\mathrm{K}=15.4$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Greater the value of $\\log K$, greater will be stability of complex compound formed.<br>For reaction,<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+4 \\mathrm{CN}^{-} \\longrightarrow\\left[\\mathrm{Cu}(\\mathrm{CN})_{4}\\right]^{2-} \\\\& K=\\dfrac{\\left[\\left(\\mathrm{Cu}(\\mathrm{CN})_{4}\\right)^{2-}\\right]}{\\left[\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{CN}^{-}\\right]^{4}} \\text { and } \\log \\mathrm{K}=27.3\\end{aligned}$$<br>For this reaction, $\\log \\mathrm{K}$ has the highest value among the given four reactions. Hence, $\\mathrm{K}$ will also be higher among these four. i.e., stability of the complexes will be the highest among these four complexes.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25835,'chemistry',63,814,1785,'Which of the following element is oxidised by conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ into two gaseous products?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Zn", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{Cu}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ (conc) $\\rightarrow \\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}+\\underset{(\\mathrm{g})}{\\mathrm{SO}_{2}}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>${\\text{S  +  2}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{(conc}}) \\to \\mathop {{\\text{3S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{\\left( {\\text{g}} \\right)} {\\text{ +  2}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}$<br>$\\mathrm{C}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ (conc) $\\rightarrow \\underset{(\\mathrm{g})}{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}}+\\underset{(\\mathrm{g})}{2 \\mathrm{SO}_{2}}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
