(25968,'chemistry',63,806,1743,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ is less reactive than $\\mathrm{P}_{4}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Nitrogen has more electron gain enthalpy than phosphorus.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{P}$ single bond in $\\mathrm{P}_{4}$ molecules is much weaker $\\left(213 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right)$ than $\\mathrm{N} \\equiv \\mathrm{N}$ triple bond $\\left(941.4 \\mathrm{~kJ} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right)$ in $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$. So $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ is less reactive than $\\mathrm{P}_{4}$. Electron gain enthalpy of nitrogen is less than phosphorus. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25969,'chemistry',64,194,1934,'Which element from the following lanthanoids has half-filled $\\mathrm{f}$-orbitals, in observed and expected electronic configuration?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Eu}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Sm}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ce}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Pm}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Europium $\\left(\\mathrm{Eu}_{63}\\right)=[\\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \\mathrm{f}^{7} 6 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25970,'chemistry',66,1030,2431,'Arrange the following in the decreasing order of dipole moment.','[{"text": "I $$>$$ II $$>$$ III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II $$>$$ I $$>$$ III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III $$>$$ II $$>$$ I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I $$>$$ III $$>$$ II", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'I $$>$$ II $$>$$ III<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25970_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>There is intramolecular hydrogen bonding which decreases the bond angle $$\\left( < 60^{\\circ}\\right)$$ hence the dipole is decreased.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25970_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Dipole-dipole repulsion of two chlorine atoms in ortho-position increases the bond angle $$\\left(>60^{\\circ}\\right)$$ hence the dipole is decreased.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_25970_question_1.jpg'),
(25971,'chemistry',65,956,2224,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Tetrahedral complexes do not show geometrical isomerism.<br></div><div class="column-right">The relative positions of the unidentate ligands attached to the central metal atom are the same with respect to each other.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Tetrahedral complexes do not show geometridcal isomerism because the relative positions of the unidentate ligands attached to the central metal atom are same with respect to $\\mathrm{e} \\mathrm{a} \\mathrm{ch}$ other.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25972,'chemistry',64,191,1917,'Identify ferromagnetic element from the following','[{"text": "Iron", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Vanadium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chromium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Manganese", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Among transition metals $\\mathrm{Fe}, \\mathrm{Co}, \\mathrm{Ni}$ are Ferromagnetic.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25973,'chemistry',63,817,1799,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> HI cannot be prepared by the reaction of $\\mathrm{KI}$ with concentrated $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{HI}$ has lowest $\\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{X}$ bond strength among halogen acids.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'HI being a strong reducing agent reduces $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ to $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ and itself gets oxdised to $\\mathrm{I}_{2} . \\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{I}$ has lowest bond length among hydrides of halogen due to large size of iodine. But it is not the correct reason for assertion. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25974,'chemistry',66,1037,2460,'Which amongst the following has the highest percentage of chlorine?','[{"text": "Chloral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gammaxene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethyl chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pyrene", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$$\\%$$ of chlorine $$=\\dfrac{\\text { Mas of chlorine }}{\\text { Mass of organic compound }} \\times 100$$<br>Chloral $$\\left(\\mathrm{CCl}_{3} \\mathrm{CHO}\\right)=\\dfrac{106.5}{147.5} \\times 100=72.20$$<br>Pyrene $$\\left(\\mathrm{CCl}_{4}\\right)=\\dfrac{142}{154} \\times 100=92.20$$ Highest<br>Gammexene $$\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{6} \\mathrm{Cl}_{6}\\right)=\\dfrac{123}{291} \\times 100=73.19$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25975,'chemistry',65,954,2214,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Glycinate ion is an unsymmetrical bidentate ligand.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Glycinate ion is a conjugate base of glycine.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25976,'chemistry',63,812,1774,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{SF}_{6}$ cannot be hydrolysed but $\\mathrm{SF}_{4}$ can be.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Six $\\mathrm{F}$ atoms in $\\mathrm{SF}_{6}$ prevent the attack of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ on sulphur atom of $\\mathrm{SF}_{6}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{SF}_{6}$ resists hydrolysis due to its symmetrical and stable structure, preventing interaction with water. In contrast, $\\mathrm{SF}_{4}$ can undergo hydrolysis as its trigonal bipyramidal shape allows interaction with water, leading to the breakdown into $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ and HF. Therefore, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25978,'chemistry',66,1037,2456,'Iodoform can be prepared from all except','[{"text": "Ethyl methyl ketone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isopropyl alcohol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3-Methyl-2-butanone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isobutyl alcohol", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Iodoform reaction is given by alcohols and ketones containing <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25978_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> and <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25978_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> group, respectively. Thus, it does not given by isobutyl alcohol as it does not contain <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25978_explanation_3.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> group. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25978_explanation_4.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25978_explanation_5.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25978_explanation_6.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25978_explanation_7.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Hence, compounds (1), (2) and (3) will give iodoform while compound (4) (isobutyl alcohol) does not give any iodoform reaction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25979,'chemistry',65,954,2214,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}$  is an ambidentate ligand.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}$  is a Lewis base.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25980,'chemistry',63,811,1769,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Dinegative anion of oxygen $\\left(\\mathrm{O}^{-2}\\right)$ is quite common but dinegative anion of sulphur $\\left(\\mathrm{S}^{2-}\\right)$ is less common.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Covalency of oxygen is two.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Oxygen ranks as the second most electronegative element, following fluorine. This characteristic allows oxygen to readily acquire electrons and form a doubly negative anion $\\left(\\mathrm{O}^{-2}\\right)$, a trait not as prominent in sulfur, which is less electronegative compared to oxygen. Covalency of oxygen is 2 . So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25981,'chemistry',64,191,1917,'What is the value of spin only magnetic moment of $\\mathrm{Ni}(\\mathrm{Z}=28)$ in +2 oxidation state?','[{"text": "$3.9 \\\\mathrm{BM}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.0 \\\\mathrm{BM}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.8 \\\\mathrm{BM}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.7 \\\\mathrm{BM}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\text{N}}{{\\text{i}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}}{\\text{(Z = 28)}} \\Rightarrow {\\text{1}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{2}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{6}}}{\\text{3}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{3}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{6}}}{\\text{3}}{{\\text{d}}^{\\text{8}}}$<br>$\\therefore$ It has 2 unpaired electrons, (in $3 \\mathrm{~d}$ orbitals)<br>$\\mu=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{n}(\\mathrm{n}+2)}$<br>$=\\sqrt{2(2+2)}=2.8 \\mathrm{BM}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25982,'chemistry',66,1038,2461,'Identify the compound \'C\'.','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mixture of (1) & (2)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_25982_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_25982_question_1.jpg'),
(25983,'chemistry',65,957,2227,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ and $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ are reducing in nature.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Unpaired electrons are present in their d-orbitals.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ and $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ are reducing in nature due to the formation of more stable complex ion after losing of electron.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25984,'chemistry',64,191,1917,'Which of the following elements in their respective oxidation states does not develop spin only magnetic moment?<br>${\\text{[Ti(Z = 22), Zn(Z = 30),V(Z = 23), Cu(Z = 29)]}}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{V}^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+} \\rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{9} \\quad$  -1 unpaired electron<br>$\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+} \\rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{10} \\quad$-No unpaired electrons<br>$\\mathrm{Ti}^{3+} \\rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{1} \\quad-1$ unpaired electron<br>$\\mathrm{V}^{3+} \\quad \\rightarrow 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{2} \\quad-2$ unpaired electrons<br>$\\therefore \\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ does not develop spin only magnetic moment due to absence of unpaired electrons.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25985,'chemistry',63,816,1796,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{F}-\\mathrm{F}$ bond has low bond dissociation energy.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The fluorine has lower reactivity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The lower bond dissociation energy in fluorine $(\\mathrm{F}-\\mathrm{F})$ is due to heightened interelectronic repulsions in its small-sized 2p-orbital. This makes $\\mathrm{F}-\\mathrm{F}$ bond weaker compared to<br>$\\mathrm{Cl}$ $-\\mathrm{Cl}$ and $\\mathrm{Br}-\\mathrm{Br}$ bonds. Bond dissociation energies are: ${\\text{F   -   F(38 k cal/mol),Cl   -   Cl(57 k cal/mol)}}$  ${\\text{Br   -   Br (45}}{\\text{.5 k cal/mol) ,}}$ and ${\\text{I   -   I (35}}{\\text{.6 k cal/mol)}}.$  ${{\\text{F}}_2}$ is highly reactive non metal. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25989,'chemistry',63,807,1747,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{BaCO}_{3}$ is more soluble in $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ than in plain water.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Carbonate is a weak base and reacts with the $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$from the strong acid, causing the barium salt to dissociate.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{BaCO}_{3}+2 \\mathrm{HNO}_{3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Ba}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$<br>So option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25991,'chemistry',63,810,1763,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{Sb}$ (III) is not precipitated as sulphide when in its alkaline solution $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ is passed.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The concentration of $\\mathrm{S}^{2-}$ ion in alkaline medium is inadequate for precipitation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{Sb}$ (III) serves as a basic radical of the IIB group, and it can be precipitated using hydrogen sulfide $\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}\\right)$ in the presence of dilute hydrochloric acid $(\\mathrm{HCl})$. Maintaining the appropriate hydrogen ion concentration is essential for precipitating the second group cations and for examining the precipitation of the fourth group cations. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25992,'chemistry',64,190,1909,'Which element from the following is a soft element?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mn}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Zn}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Co}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ni}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{Cu}, \\mathrm{Zn}$ are soft elements in $3 \\mathrm{~d}$-series.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25993,'chemistry',65,958,2234,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> ${{\\rm{\\Delta }}_{\\rm{t}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{4}}}{{\\rm{9}}}{{\\rm{\\Delta }}_{\\rm{o}}}{\\rm{.}}$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> ${{\\rm{\\Delta }}_{\\rm{O}}}$   is always greater than ${{\\rm{\\Delta }}_{\\rm{t}}}$  for same metal ion and ligand.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25995,'chemistry',64,191,1918,'Which among the following cations will not form coloured compounds? (Atomic number $\\mathrm{Cu}=29, \\mathrm{Ti}=22, \\mathrm{~V}=23, \\mathrm{Mn}=25$ )','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{V}^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{V}^{3+}: 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{2} \\rightarrow 2$ unpaired electrons <br>$\\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}: 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{1} \\rightarrow 1$ unpaired electron <br>$\\mathrm{Cu}^{+}: 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{10} \\rightarrow$ No unpaired electron <br>$\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}: 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5} \\rightarrow 5$ unpaired electrons <br>Due to absence of unpaired electron, $\\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$will not form coloured compounds.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25996,'chemistry',63,818,1809,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{HClO}_{4}$ is a stronger acid than $\\mathrm{HClO}_{3}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Oxidation state of $\\mathrm{Cl}$ in $\\mathrm{HClO}_{4}$ is +8 and in $\\mathrm{HClO}_{3}$ is +5 .','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Acid strength of oxyacids of the same halogen increases with increase in oxidation number of the halogens. Oxidation number of $\\mathrm{Cl}$ in $\\mathrm{HClO}_{4}$ is +7 and in $\\mathrm{HClO}_{3}$ is +5 . So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25997,'chemistry',65,956,2224,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Complexes of $\\mathrm{MX}_{6}$ and $\\mathrm{MX}_{5} \\mathrm{~L}$ type (X and $\\mathrm{L}$ are unidentate) do not show geometrical isomerism.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Geometrical isomerism is not shown by complexes of coordination number 6 .','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(25999,'chemistry',63,813,1778,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Ozone is an allotrope of oxygen. <br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Oxygen is bluish colour liquid and in singlet state it is more paramagnetic.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Oxygen is a colourless, odourless and tasteless gas. It is paramagnetic in gaseous, liquid and solid states. It can be liquified to a pale blue liquid by compressing the gas at a very low temperature. Singlet state of oxygen is diamagnetic in nature. Its allotropic modification is ozone. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26011,'chemistry',64,192,1925,'$\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ can be converted into $\\mathrm{Mn}^{7+}$ by reacting with','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PbO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Strong oxidising agents such as $\\mathrm{PbO}_{2}$ or sodium bismuthate $\\left(\\mathrm{NaBiO}_{3}\\right)$ oxidise $\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ to $\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$or $\\mathrm{Mn}^{7+}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26000,'chemistry',64,191,1918,'Which element from the following in +3 oxidation state forms colourless compounds?','[{"text": "${\\\\text{Sc(Z  =  21)}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{Ti(Z  =  22)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{V(Z  =  23)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{Fe(Z  =  26)}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{Sc}^{3+}: 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{0} \\rightarrow$ No unpaired electron<br>$\\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}: 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{1} \\rightarrow 1$ unpaired electron<br>$\\mathrm{V}^{3+}: 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{2} \\rightarrow 2$ unpaired electrons<br>$\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}: 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5} \\rightarrow 5$ unpaired electrons<br>Due to absence of unpaired electron, Sc in +3 oxidation state forms colourless compounds.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26002,'chemistry',65,960,2238,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{[Ni(EDTA)]}}$  is more stable than ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{3}}}\\left[ {{\\text{Al}}{{\\left( {{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}} \\right)}_{\\text{3}}}} \\right]$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Nickel (Ni) is a transition element while aluminium (Al) is non-transistion element.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Both assertion and reason are true but the greater stability is due to greater number of chelate ring (five) formed by EDTA than by oxalate.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26003,'chemistry',64,194,1936,'Which among the following lanthanoids forms most stable +2 oxidation state?','[{"text": "${\\\\text{Eu(Z  =  63)}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{Pm(Z  =  61)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{Ce(Z  =  58)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{Gd(Z  =  64)}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {{\\text{Ion}}}&{{\\text{Valence shell}}} \\\\ \n  {}&\\begin{gathered}\n  {\\text{   electronic}} \\hfill \\\\\n  {\\text{ configuration}} \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered}  \\\\ \n  {{\\text{E}}{{\\text{u}}^{{\\text{2   +   }}}}}&{{\\text{4}}{{\\text{f}}^{\\text{7}}}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{P}}{{\\text{m}}^{{\\text{2   +   }}}}}&{{\\text{4}}{{\\text{f}}^{\\text{5}}}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{e}}^{{\\text{2   +   }}}}}&{{\\text{4}}{{\\text{f}}^{\\text{2}}}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{G}}{{\\text{d}}^{{\\text{2   +   }}}}}&{{\\text{4}}{{\\text{f}}^{\\text{7}}}{\\text{5}}{{\\text{d}}^{\\text{1}}}} \n\\end{array}$<br>Due to stable half filled 4 f orbitals, $\\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$ will be most stable, among given.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26004,'chemistry',63,818,1811,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Bleaching powder reacts with dilute acids to evolve chlorine.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The chlorine liberated by the action of dilute acids on bleaching powder is called available chlorine.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The reaction involved is<br>$\\mathrm{CaOCl}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CaSO}_{4}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$<br>The available chlorine may be defined as the $\\%$ of chlorine liberated when one mole of bleaching powder is treated with excess of dilute $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26006,'chemistry',65,960,2240,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{K}_{4}\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]$ is more stable than<br>$$\\mathrm{K}_{3}\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]$$<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\ln \\mathrm{K}_{4}\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]$, the EAN of $\\mathrm{Fe}$ is 36.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26007,'chemistry',64,194,1936,'What is the number of unpaired electrons present in f-orbital at +3 oxidation state of $\\mathrm{Lu}(\\mathrm{Z}=71)$ ?','[{"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${ }_{71} \\mathrm{Lu}:[\\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \\mathrm{f}^{14} 5 \\mathrm{~d}^{1} 6 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}$<br>$\\mathrm{Lu}^{3+}:[\\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \\mathrm{f}^{14}$ (fulfilled f-orbitals)<br>Hence, number of unpaired electrons in f-orbital of $\\mathrm{Lu}^{3+}$ is zero.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26008,'chemistry',63,805,1736,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{R}_{3} \\mathrm{P}=\\mathrm{O}$ exists but $\\mathrm{R}_{3} \\mathrm{~N}=\\mathrm{O}$ does not exist.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> P is more electronegative than $\\mathrm{N}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Due to small size and absence of d-orbital, $\\mathrm{N}$ cannot form $\\mathrm{R}_{3} \\mathrm{~N}=\\mathrm{O}$. The electronegativity of $\\mathrm{N}(3.0)$ is much higher than that of $\\mathrm{P}(2.1)$. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26010,'chemistry',65,962,2242,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Toxic metal ions are removed by the chelating ligands.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Chelate complexes tend to be more stable.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Toxic metal ions are removed by chelating ligands. When a solution of chelating ligand is added to solution containing toxic metals ligands chelates the metal ions by formation of stable complex.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28623,'chemistry',69,1178,2830,'The final product R is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_28623_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_28623_question_1.jpg'),
(26012,'chemistry',63,807,1745,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{AgCl}$ is more soluble in $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ than in water.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Ammonia is more polar than water.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Solubility of $\\mathrm{AgCl}\\left(\\right.$ at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ ) in $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>$=0.0020 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{AgCl}$ per litre of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>Solubility of $\\mathrm{AgCl}$ in $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ (at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ )<br>${\\text{ = 14}}{\\text{.00 g  AgCl}}$ dissolved per $\\mathrm{kg}$ of $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$<br>Solubility of $\\mathrm{AgCl}$ is more in $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ due to formation of soluble stable complex<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{2}\\right]^{+}$.Ammonia is less polar than water, as oxygen is more electronegative than nitrogen. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26015,'chemistry',64,191,1916,'Which transition metal reduces steam to evolve hydrogen?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mg}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Sc}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Pt}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When steam is passed over red hot iron, it is decomposed to hydrogen.<br>$2 \\mathrm{Fe}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Fe}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\uparrow$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26016,'chemistry',63,824,1826,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Helium is used in diving apparatus.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Solubility of helium is less in blood.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In diving apparatus, a blend of oxygen and helium is employed to achieve an equivalent partial pressure of oxygen as that found in normal air at sea level $(1 \\mathrm{~atm})$. Nitrogen has increased solubility in blood and can lead to the formation of bubbles, causing decompression sickness or "the bends." To mitigate this risk, nitrogen is substituted with helium, as it is significantly less soluble in biological fluids, reducing the likelihood of bubble formation during decompression. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26019,'chemistry',64,192,1929,'Which of the following is a constituent of lithopone?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{ZnSO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{ZnS}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{ZnO}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Lithopone is $\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4} \\cdot \\mathrm{ZnS}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26020,'chemistry',63,818,1806,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Chlorine undergoes disproportionation reaction in alkaline medium.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ is an oxidising agent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}_2} + 2{\\text{NaO}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{(dil) }}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ cold }}}}{\\text{NaCl}} + {\\text{NaOCl}} + {{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{O}}$<br>$3 \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+6 \\mathrm{NaOH}_{\\text {(conc) }} \\xrightarrow{\\text { heat }}$<br>$5 \\mathrm{NaCl}+\\mathrm{NaClO}_{3}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>Chlorine is simultaneously reduced to<br>chloride ion $\\left(\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\\right)$and is oxidised to either $\\mathrm{ClO}^{-}$<br>or $\\mathrm{ClO}_{3}{ }^{-}$ion. Halogens (e.g., $\\mathrm{Cl}$ ) have a strong tendency to accept electrons. Thus, they act as strong oxidising agents. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26036,'chemistry',66,1030,2428,'Which is the correct increasing order of boiling points of the following compounds?<br>1-Iodobutane, 1-Bromobutane, 1-Chlorobutane, Butane','[{"text": "Butane $$ < 1$$-Chlorobutane $$ < 1$$-Bromobutane<br>$$ < $$ 1-Iodobutane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1-Iodobutane $$ < 1$$ - Bromobutane $$ < 1$$ Chlorobutane $$ < $$ Butane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Butane $$ < 1$$-Iodobutane $$ < 1$$-Bromobutane $$ < 1$$-Chlorobutane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Butane $$ < 1$$-Chlorobutane $$ < 1$$-Iodobutane $$ < 1$$-Bromobutane", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Higher the surface area, higher will be the intermolecular forces of attraction and thus boilingpoint too. Boiling point increases with increase in molecular mass of halogen atom for the similar type of alkyl halide. Butane has no halogen atom and rest of all three compounds are halo derivatives of butane.<br>Atomic mass of iodine is the highest so the boiling point of 1-Iodobutane is maximum among all the given compounds and hence, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26022,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'The correct order of the stoichiometries of $\\mathrm{AgCl}$ formed when $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ in excess is treated with the complexs:<br>$\\mathrm{CoCl}_{3} .6 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}, \\mathrm{CoCl}_{3} .5 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}, \\mathrm{CoCl}_{3} .4 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ respectively is :-','[{"text": "$3 \\\\mathrm{AgCl}, 1 \\\\mathrm{AgCl}, 2 \\\\mathrm{AgCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\mathrm{AgCl}, 2 \\\\mathrm{AgCl}, 1 \\\\mathrm{AgCl}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{AgCl}, 3 \\\\mathrm{AgCl}, 1 \\\\mathrm{AgCl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 \\\\mathrm{AgCl}, 3 \\\\mathrm{AgCl}, 2 \\\\mathrm{AgCl}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\left[ {{\\text{Co}}{{\\left( {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}} \\right)}_{\\text{6}}}} \\right]{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{3}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{AgN}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}}}{\\text{3}}\\,{\\text{mol AgCl}}$<br>$\\left[ {{\\text{Co}}{{\\left( {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}} \\right)}_{\\text{5}}}{\\text{Cl}}} \\right]{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{AgN}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}}}{\\text{2}}\\,{\\text{mol  AgCl}}$<br>$\\left[ {{\\text{Co}}{{\\left( {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}} \\right)}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]{\\text{Cl}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{AgN}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}}}{\\text{1}}\\,{\\text{mol AgCl}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26023,'chemistry',64,191,1913,'In which one of the following, the first is more stable than the second?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}, \\\\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}, \\\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}, \\\\mathrm{Na}^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}, \\\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\text{T}}{{\\text{i}}^{3 + }} = 22 = [{\\text{Ar}}]3\\,{{\\text{d}}^1}4\\,{{\\text{s}}^0}\\quad \\mathop {{\\text{T}}{{\\text{i}}^{4 + }} = 3\\,{{\\text{d}}^0}4\\,{{\\text{s}}^0}}\\limits_{{\\text{(Noble}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{gas)}}} $<br>${\\text{M}}{{\\text{n}}^{3 + }} = 25 = [{\\text{Ar}}]3\\,{{\\text{d}}^4}\\,{{\\text{s}}^0}\\quad \\mathop {{\\text{M}}{{\\text{n}}^{2 + }} = [{\\text{Ar}}]3\\,{{\\text{d}}^5}}\\limits_{{\\text{(half}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{filled)}}} $<br>${\\text{Na}} = 1\\,{{\\text{s}}^2}2\\,{{\\text{s}}^2}2{{\\text{p}}^6}3\\,{{\\text{s}}^1}\\quad \\mathop {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{a}}^ + } = 1\\,{{\\text{s}}^2}2\\,{{\\text{s}}^2}2{{\\text{p}}^6}}\\limits_{{\\text{(Noble}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{gas)}}} $<br>$\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}=[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5}$ (half field) $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}=[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{6}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26024,'chemistry',63,808,1757,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{2}$ has strong reducing property but $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ does not.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond is not present in $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26024_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Oxoacids of phosphorus that contain $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{H}$ bonds serve as reducing agents, enabling them to reduce metal salts to their elemental forms.<br>Consequently, $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{2}$ acts as a reducing agent,<br>while $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ does not possess this reducing ability. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26025,'chemistry',66,1031,2435,'Which graph is incorrect?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26026,'chemistry',63,812,1772,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{Se}$ is more acidic than $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{S}$ is less electronegative than $\\mathrm{Se}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Down the group, electronegativity decreases. The bond dissociation energy also decreases due to increased atomic size, and the release of $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$ ion becames easier. Thus $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{Se}$ is more acidic than $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$. S is more electronegative than Se. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26027,'chemistry',64,191,1913,'The stability of ferric ion is due to:','[{"text": "Half-filled f-orbitals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Half-filled d-orbitals", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Completely filled f-orbitals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Completely filled d-orbitals", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{Fe}} = [{\\text{Ar}}]4\\,{{\\text{s}}^2}3\\,{{\\text{d}}^6}\\quad \\mathop {{\\text{F}}{{\\text{e}}^{3 + }} = ({\\text{Ar}})3\\,{{\\text{d}}^5}}\\limits_{{\\text{(half}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{filled}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{3d - orbitals)}}} $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26028,'chemistry',65,957,2228,'Pick out the correct statement with respect to $\\left.\\left[\\mathrm{Mn}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right)\\right]^{3-}:-$','[{"text": "It is ${\\\\text{s}}{{\\\\text{p}}^{\\\\text{3}}}{{\\\\text{d}}^{\\\\text{2}}}$  hybridised and tetrahedral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is  ${{\\\\text{d}}^{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{s}}{{\\\\text{p}}^{\\\\text{3}}}$  hybridised and octahedral", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is ${\\\\text{ds}}{{\\\\text{p}}^{\\\\text{2}}}$  hybridised and square planar", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is ${\\\\text{s}}{{\\\\text{p}}^{\\\\text{3}}}{{\\\\text{d}}^{\\\\text{2}}}$  hybridised and octahedral", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\left.\\left[\\mathrm{Mn}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right)\\right]^{3-} \\rightarrow O . S$. of $\\mathrm{Mn}$ is (+3)<br>C.N. $=6$<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26028_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Presence of SFL (Pairing is possible)<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26028_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> octahedral)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26029,'chemistry',66,1031,2433,'Free energy profile for given reaction is<br>\\[{\\rm{H}}{{\\rm{O}}^ - } + {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_3} - {\\rm{Br}} \\to {\\rm{HO}} - {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_3} + {\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{r}}^ - }\\]<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given reaction is an example of ${{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{N}}}^{\\rm{2}}$ reaction which follows one step mechanism.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26030,'chemistry',63,808,1759,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Bond lengths of $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{Cl}$ bonds in gaseous $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ and solid $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ are not equal.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Because in solid state two $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ molecules are associated.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the solid state, $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ forms an ionic solid $\\left[\\mathrm{PCl}_{4}\\right]^{+}\\left[\\mathrm{PCl}_{6}\\right]^{-}$with tetrahedral and octahedral structures, respectively. In the gaseous state, $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ adopts a trigonal bipyramidal shape, in which equatorial bonds are equivalent and axial bonds are longer resulting from increased repulsion between additional bond pairs. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26031,'chemistry',64,192,1925,'Potassium permanganate acts as an oxidant in neutral, alkaline as well as in acidic media. The final products obtained from it in the three conditions are, respectively','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{2-}, \\\\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}, \\\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-2}, \\\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Mn}^{+2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{MnO}, \\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}, \\\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In neutral medium:<br>$2 \\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}+3 \\mathrm{MnSO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+5 \\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$<br>In alkaline medium:<br>$2 \\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{KOH}+3[\\mathrm{O}]$<br>In acidic medium:<br>$2 \\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+2 \\stackrel{(+2)}{\\mathrm{MnSO}_{4}}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+5[\\mathrm{O}]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26032,'chemistry',65,959,2237,'Correct increasing order for the wavelengths of absorption in the visible region the complexes of $\\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ is :-','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+},\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\text { en })_{3}\\\\right]^{3+},\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+},\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+},\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\text { en })_{3}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+},\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\mathrm{en})_{3}\\\\right]^{3+},\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\text { en })_{3}\\\\right]^{3+},\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+},\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\left[\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{a}} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{a}}}\\right]$<br>where $\\varepsilon \\Rightarrow$ absorbed energy (splitting energy)<br>$\\lambda_{\\mathrm{a}} \\Rightarrow$ absorbed wavelength<br>Presence of SFL $\\Rightarrow \\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{a}}(\\uparrow) \\lambda_{\\mathrm{a}}(\\downarrow)$<br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} < \\mathrm{NH}_{3} < $ en ligand strength $\\uparrow$ splitting energy $\\uparrow$ so absorbed $\\lambda \\downarrow$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26033,'chemistry',66,702,2415,'The position of $$\\mathrm{Br}$$ in the compound, $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}=\\mathrm{CHC}(\\mathrm{Br})\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2}$$ can be classified as____.','[{"text": "Allyl", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Aryl", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vinyl", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Secondary", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Allyl halides are those compounds in which the halogen atom is bonded to ${\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{3}}}$ hybridised carbon atom next to carbon, carbondouble bond.<br>e.g., $$\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\mathrm{CH}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{X}$$ and<br>$$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}=\\mathrm{CHC}(\\mathrm{Br})\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2}$$.<br>Aryl halides are the compounds in which the halogen atom is bonded to the ${\\rm{s}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}$ hybridised carbon atom of an aromatic ring. e.g., <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26033_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Vinyl halides are the compounds in which the halogen atom is bonded to the $$s p^{2}$$ hybridised carbon atom of a carbon carbon-double bond.<br>e.g., $$\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\mathrm{CH}-\\mathrm{X}$$<br>Secondary alkyl halides are the compounds in which the halogen atom is bonded to the $$s p^{2}$$ hybridised carbon atom which is further bonded to two alkyl groups and one hydrogen atom. e.g., <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26033_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26034,'chemistry',63,808,1757,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Phosphoric acid has no reducing properties<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Phosphoric acid does not contain $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Phosphoric acid is <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26034_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Due to the absence of $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{H}$ bonds within its structure, phosphoric acid does not exhibit reducing properties. So option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26035,'chemistry',64,192,1923,'Which oxide of manganese is amphoteric.?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{MnO}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ is amphoteric oxide.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26038,'chemistry',64,191,1917,'Which of the following will show increase in weight when kept in magnetic field?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\\\right)_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{ScCl}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{Fe}^{+3}-3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5}$ (5 unpaired electrons).<br>Fe will be attracted in magnetic field so it shows increase in weight.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26039,'chemistry',63,808,1757,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{3}$ is dibasic acid.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Two hydrogen atoms are directly attached to the $\\mathrm{P}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Structure of $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{3}$ is  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26039_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Due to the presence of two hydroxyl protons, $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{3}$ is dibasic acid. It has only one $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26040,'chemistry',66,1031,2435,'Reaction of $$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{Br}$$ with aqueous sodium hydroxide follows','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{N}}}1$ mechanism", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{N}}}2$ mechanism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Any of the above two depending upon the temperature of reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Saytzeff rule", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{1}$$ mechanism depends upon the stability of carbocation. Higher the stability of carbocation, higher will be the possibility of $$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{1}$$ mechanism to take place. In the given compound $$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{Br}$$, carbocation is $$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}_{2}$$. This carbocation<br>$$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}_{2}$$ is a stable carbocation due to resonance, therefore, it shows $$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{1}$$ mechanism.<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26040_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26041,'chemistry',65,956,2224,'The type of isomerism shown by the complex<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}(\\mathrm{en})_{2}\\right]$ is','[{"text": "Ionization isomerism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Linkage isomerism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Coordination isomerism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Geometrical isomerism", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26041_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Geometrical isomerism',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26042,'chemistry',63,826,1829,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Nitrogen and oxygen are the main components in the atmosphere but these do not react to form oxides of nitrogen.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The reaction between nitrogen and oxygen requires high temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Nitric oxide is formed when dinitrogen and dioxygen are heated in an electric arc to temperatures ranging from 2273 to $3273 \\mathrm{~K}$ Therefore, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26043,'chemistry',64,192,1923,'$\\mathrm{CrO}_{3}$ dissolves in aqueous $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ to give','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}(\\\\mathrm{OH})$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{CrO}_{3}+2 \\mathrm{NaOH} \\rightarrow \\underset{\\text { Yellow solution }}{\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(26044,'chemistry',66,1028,2421,'$$\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{Cl}+\\mathrm{AgF} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{~F}+\\mathrm{AgCl}$$<br>The above reaction is called','[{"text": "Hunsdiecker", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Swart", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Strecker", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wurtz", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Swartz reaction',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26046,'chemistry',64,191,1916,'Pure conc. $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ makes iron passive as the surface is covered with protective layer of','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{FeO}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}_{3} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\\\right)_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The passivity of iron is due to the formation of a thin insoluble and invisible iron film on surface which prevents its further reactions. The film is due to the formation of $\\mathrm{Fe}_{3} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26047,'chemistry',63,824,1826,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Charcoal is used in separation of noble gases.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Charcoal has porous structure.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Inert gases are adsorbed on the charcoal. Porous structure of charcoal makes it good adsorbent. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26048,'chemistry',66,1035,2446,'Which of the following is Wurtz-Fittig reaction?','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{I}+2 \\\\mathrm{Na}+\\\\mathrm{ICH}_{3} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}+2 \\\\mathrm{NaI}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{I}+\\\\mathrm{Cu}+\\\\mathrm{IC}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}+\\\\mathrm{CuI}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{I}+2 \\\\mathrm{Na}+\\\\mathrm{ICH}_{3} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}+2 \\\\mathrm{NaI}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{I}+2 \\mathrm{Na}+\\mathrm{ICH}_{3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$$ This reaction is Wurtz-Fittig reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26049,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'The geometry and magnetic behaviour of the complex $\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}(\\mathrm{CO})_{4}\\right]$ are','[{"text": "Square planar geometry and paramagnetic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tetrahedral geometry and paramagnetic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tetrahedral geometry and diamagnetic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Square planar geometry and diamagnetic", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Configuration of $\\mathrm{Ni}$ is $[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{8} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}$<br>Oxidation no. of $\\mathrm{Ni}$ is \' $\\mathrm{O}$ \' <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26049_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  CO being strong field ligand it will pair up $e^{-} s$  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26049_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Tetrahedral and diamagnetic.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26050,'chemistry',63,826,1829,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ is stronger acid than $\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{S}$ is more electronegative than $\\mathrm{P}$, conjugate base $\\mathrm{HS}^{-}$is more stable than $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{P}^{-}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}_{(\\mathrm{g})}+\\mathrm{aq} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{HS}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{-}+\\mathrm{H}_{\\text {(aq) }}^{+}$<br>$\\mathrm{PH}_{3(\\mathrm{~g})}+\\mathrm{aq} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{P}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{-}+\\mathrm{H}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{+}$<br>As $\\mathrm{S}$ is more electronegative than $\\mathrm{P}$, conjugate base $\\mathrm{HS}^{-}$is more stable than $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{P}^{-}$. Hence, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ is stronger acid than $\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26051,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'In acidic medium potassium dichromate acts as an oxidant according to the equation,<br>$\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}+14 \\mathrm{H}^{+}+6 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}+7 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$. What is the equivalent weight of $\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ ? (mol.<br>Wt. $=$ M)','[{"text": "${\\\\text{M}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{M / 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{M / 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{M / 6}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'No. of electrons involved are 6.<br>So, equivalent weight ${\\text{ = }}\\frac{{\\text{M}}}{{\\text{6}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26052,'chemistry',66,1037,2460,'Ethylene chloride and ethylidene chloride are isomers. Identify the correct statements.','[{"text": "Both the compounds form same product on treatment with alcoholic $$\\\\mathrm{KOH}$$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both the compounds form same product on treatment with aqueous $$\\\\mathrm{KOH}$$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both the compounds form different product on reduction.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both the compounds are optically active.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'(1) ${\\text{ClC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{Cl}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{AlcKOH(Excess)}}}}{\\text{HC}} \\equiv {\\text{CH}}$<br>Ethylene chloride<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CHC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{Alc}}\\,{\\text{KOH(Excess)}}}}{\\text{HC}} \\equiv {\\text{CH}}$<br>Ethylidene chloride<br>(2) ${\\text{ClC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{Cl}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{Aq}}{\\text{.KOH}}}}{\\text{HO}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C - C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{OH}}$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CHC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{AlcKOH(Excess)}}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CH}}{\\left( {{\\text{OH}}} \\right)_{\\text{2}}}$<br>$\\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CHO}}$<br>(3) ${\\text{ClC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{Cl}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{Reduction}}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C - C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ + 2HCl}}$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CHC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{Reduction}}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ + 2HCl}}$<br>(4) Both these compounds are optically inactive',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26053,'chemistry',65,958,2232,'What is the correct electronic configuration of the central atom in $\\mathrm{K}_{4}\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]$ based on crystal field theory?','[{"text": "$t_{2 g}^{4} e_{g}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$t_{2 g}^{6} e_{g}^{0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$e^{3} t_{2}^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$e^{4} t_{2}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{4}}}\\left[ {{\\text{Fe(CN}}{{\\text{)}}_{\\text{6}}}} \\right]$<br>Fe ground state : ${\\text{[Ar]3}}{{\\text{d}}^{\\text{6}}}{\\text{4}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}$<br>${\\text{F}}{{\\text{e}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}}{\\text{:3}}{{\\text{d}}^{\\text{6}}}{\\text{4}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{0}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26054,'chemistry',63,811,1769,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Oxygen is always present in -2 oxidation state.</div><div class="column-right">Stability of oxidation state of group 16 for +4 and +6 decreases down the group.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Oxygen can show $-2,-1,-\\dfrac{1}{2},+1,+2$ and 0 oxidation states. But -2 is the most stable state, Therefore, statement A is false. Stability of +6 oxidation state decreases down the group due to inert pair effect. Thus, statement B is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26055,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'Acidified potassium dichromate is treated with hydrogen sulphide. In the reaction, the oxidation number of chromium','[{"text": "increases from +3 to +6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "decreases from +6 to +3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "remains unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "decreases from +6 to +2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Decreases from +6 to +3 .<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}+4 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S} \\rightarrow$<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3}+7 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+3 \\mathrm{~S}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26056,'chemistry',66,1025,2416,'The IUPAC name of isobutyl bromide is','[{"text": "1 - Bromo-3-methylbutane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 - Bromo-2-methylpropane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-Bromo-2-methylpropane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1-Bromo-2-methylpropane", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26056_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> 1 - Bromo-2-methyl-propane',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26057,'chemistry',65,954,2214,'Ethylene diamineteraacetate (EDTA) ion is :','[{"text": "Unidentate ligand", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bidentate ligand with two \\" $\\\\mathrm{N}$ \\" donor atoms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tridentate ligand with three \\"N\\" donor atoms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hexadentate ligand with four \\"O\\" and two \\"N\\" donor atoms", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'EDTA is a hexadentate ligand with 4 \' $\\mathrm{O}$ \' and two \' $\\mathrm{N}$ \' donor atoms.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26057_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26058,'chemistry',63,817,1800,'Read Statement A and Statement B carefully and mark the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Chlorine can easily combine with oxygen to form oxides, and the product has a tendency to explode.</div><div class="column-right">Chemical reactivity of an element can be determined by its reaction with oxygen and halogens.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both the Statements A and B are false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both the Statements A and B are true.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement  A  is false but Statement  B  is true.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is true but Statement B is false.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Chlorine oxides, $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\mathrm{O}, \\mathrm{ClO}_{2}, \\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{6}$ and  $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ are highly reactive oxidizing agents and tend to explode.  <br>$\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and  $\\mathrm{F}_{2}$ react with most of the elements, thus can be used to determine the chemical reactivity order. Thus both the statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26059,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'$4 \\mathrm{~K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7} \\xrightarrow{\\text { heat }} 4 \\mathrm{~K}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}+3 \\mathrm{O}_{2}+\\mathrm{X}$. In the above reaction \' $\\mathrm{X}$ \' is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{5}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Potassium dichromate, on heating gives $\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}$, oxygen and chromic oxide $\\left(\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}\\right)$. $4 \\mathrm{~K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7} \\stackrel{\\Delta}{\\longrightarrow} 4 \\mathrm{~K}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+3 \\mathrm{O}_{2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26060,'chemistry',66,1037,2455,'Auto-oxidation of chloroform in air and sunlight produces a poisonous gas known as','[{"text": "Chloropicrin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mustard gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lewisite", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phosgene", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$$\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3} \\stackrel{\\mathrm{Air}}{\\longrightarrow} \\mathrm{COCl}_{2}$$ Phosgene)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26061,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'Match List - I with List - II.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A.${\\left[ {{\\text{Fe(CN}}{{\\text{)}}_{\\text{6}}}} \\right]^{{\\text{3 - }}}}$<br>B $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{+3}$<br>C ${\\left[ {{\\text{Fe(CN}}{{\\text{)}}_{\\text{6}}}} \\right]^{{\\text{ - 4}}}}$<br>D $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{+2}$</div><div class="column-right">P $5.92\\,\\,{\\text{BM}}$<br> Q  ${\\text{0}}\\,\\,{\\text{BM}}$<br>R $4.90\\,\\,{\\text{BM}}$<br>S  $1.73\\,\\,{\\text{BM}}$</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A - Q,   B - S,   C - R,   D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - P,   B - R,   C - S,   D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - S,   B - P,   C - Q,   D - R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - S,   B - Q,   C - P,   D - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26061_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26062,'chemistry',63,807,1749,'Match Column I with Column II.<br> <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - Q ,\\tB - S,\\tC - R\\t\\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,\\tB - P,\\tC - S,\\t\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q ,\\tB - P,\\tC - R,\\tD - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,\\tB - P,\\tC - Q ,\\tD - S", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'(A - R) <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26062_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>It has one $\\mathrm{N}-\\mathrm{N}$ bond<br>$(\\mathrm{B}-\\mathrm{P})$  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26062_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>It has one $\\mathrm{N}=\\mathrm{O}$ bond <br> $({\\rm{C}} - {\\rm{Q}})$  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26062_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>It has one  ${\\rm{N}} - {\\rm{O}} - {\\rm{N}}$  bond  <br>$({\\rm{D}} - {\\rm{S}})$  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26062_explanation_4.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br> It has  $\\mathrm{N}=\\mathrm{N}$ or $\\mathrm{N} \\equiv \\mathrm{N}$ bond.<br>Thus, the correct match is option 4.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/63_26062_question_1.png'),
(26063,'chemistry',64,189,1907,'Metallic radii of some transition elements are given below. Which of these elements will have highest density:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Metallic radii/pm 126 <br>  undefined</div><div class="column-right">125 125 <br> undefined undefined</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ni}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Co}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Density $\\propto$ mass and density $\\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\text { volume }}$. Density increases down the group and also across a period from left to right. Among the given four choices, $\\mathrm{Cu}$ belongs to right side of periodic table in transition metal series and it has highest density $\\left(89 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{cm}^{3}\\right)$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26064,'chemistry',66,1034,2442,'A major component of Brosch reagent is obtained by reacting hydrazine hydrate with which of the following?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The major component of Brosch reagent is 2, 4-dinitrophenyl hydrazine which can be obtained by reaction of 2,4-dinitrochloro benzene and hydrazine <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26064_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26065,'chemistry',63,817,1803,'Which halogen is known to cause the reaction given below?<br>$2 \\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+4 \\mathrm{X}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}_{2} \\mathrm{X}_{2(s)}+\\mathrm{X}_{2}$','[{"text": "Only chlorine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only bromine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only iodine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All halogens", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The product ${{\\rm{X}}_2}$ is molecular iodine  $\\left( {{{\\rm{I}}_2}} \\right)$<br>  where X represents an iodide ion $\\mathrm{I}^{-}$.<br>$2 \\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}+4 \\mathrm{I}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}_{2} \\mathrm{I}_{2}+\\mathrm{I}_{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26066,'chemistry',64,192,1925,'Why is $\\mathrm{HCl}$ not used to make the medium acidic in oxidation reactions of $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ in acidic medium?','[{"text": "Both $\\\\mathrm{HCl}$ and $\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ act as oxidising agents.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ oxidises $\\\\mathrm{HCl}$ into $\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ which is also an oxidising agent.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ is a weaker oxidising agent than $\\\\mathrm{HCl}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ acts as a reducing agent in the presence of $\\\\mathrm{HCl}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ oxidises $\\mathrm{HCl}$ to $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$. So, $\\mathrm{HCl}$ is not used for acid medium.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26067,'chemistry',65,955,2222,'The IUPAC name of the complex -<br>$$\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}(\\mathrm{CN})_{2}\\right] \\text { is: }$$','[{"text": "diaquasilver(II) dicyanidoargentate(II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "dicyanidosilver(I) diaquaargentate(I)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "diaquasilver(I) dicyanidoargentate(I)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "dicyanidosilver(II) diaquaargentate(II)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'IUPAC name of<br>$$\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{2}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}(\\mathrm{CN})_{2}\\right] \\text { is }$$<br>Diaquasilver(I) dicyanidoargentate (I)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26068,'chemistry',66,1035,2446,'The Wurtz-Fitting reaction involves condensation of:','[{"text": "Two molecules of aryl halides", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "One molecule of each of aryl halide and alkyl halide", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "One molecule of each of aryl halide and phenol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two molecules of aralkyl halide", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Reaction between alkyl halide, aryl halide and sodium in presence of ether is known as Wurtz-Fitting reaction<br>${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{Cl + 2Na + ClC}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}} \\to \\mathop {{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}}\\limits_{\\scriptstyle{\\rm{Toluene}}\\atop\\scriptstyle{\\rm{  }}} {\\rm{ + 2NaCl}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26069,'chemistry',64,191,1914,'${\\text{E}}^\\circ $ values for the couples  ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{r}}^{{\\text{3 + }}}}{\\text{/C}}{{\\text{r}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}}{\\text{  &  M}}{{\\text{n}}^{{\\text{3 + }}}}{\\text{/M}}{{\\text{n}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}}$ are ${\\text{ - 0}}{\\text{.41 &   + 1}}{\\text{.51V}}$ respectively. Considering these values select the correct option:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}$ acts as a reducing agent and $\\\\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}$ acts as an oxidising agent in their aqueous solutions.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}{ }_{\\\\text {(aq) }}$ is more stable than $\\\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}{ }_{\\\\text {(aq) }}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{Mn}}_{(aq)}^{3 + }$ is more stable than ${\\\\text{M}}{{\\\\text{n}}^{2 + }}_{{\\\\text{(aq) }}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}$ acts as reducing agent because it is itself oxidised to $\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ giving it $\\mathrm{t}_{2 \\mathrm{~g}}^{3}$ configuration, whereas $\\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}$ acts as oxidising agent giving it $3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5}$ configuration $\\left(\\mathrm{Mn}^{+2}\\right)$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26070,'chemistry',65,959,2237,'The order of energy absorbed which is responsible for the color of complexes<br>(A) $\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{2}(\\text { en })_{2}\\right]^{2+}$<br>(B) $\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{4}(\\text { en })\\right]^{2+}$ and<br>(C) $\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}(\\text { en })_{3}\\right]^{2+}$','[{"text": "$($ C $)>($ B $)>($ A $)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$($ C $)>($ A $)>($ B $)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$($ B $)>($ A $)>($ C $)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(\\\\mathrm{A})>(\\\\mathrm{B})>(\\\\mathrm{C})$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'(A) ${\\left[ {{\\text{Ni}}{{\\left( {{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}} \\right)}_{\\text{2}}}{{{\\text{( en )}}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right]^{{\\text{2 + }}}}$<br>(B) ${\\left[ {{\\text{Ni}}{{\\left( {{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}} \\right)}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{(en)}}} \\right]^{{\\text{2 + }}}}$<br>(C)${\\left[ {{\\text{Ni( en }}{{\\text{)}}_{\\text{3}}}} \\right]^{{\\text{2 + }}}}$<br>en is SFL (strong field ligand)<br>As the number of en (strong ligand) increase splitting also increases.<br>So, $\\Delta_{0}$ increases.<br>i.e. ,maximum energy will be absorbed in case of option C.<br>So the order is $\\mathrm{C}>\\mathrm{A}>\\mathrm{B}$<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26072,'chemistry',66,1031,2437,'The order of reactivity of the given haloalkanes towards nucleophile is:','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{RI}>\\\\mathrm{RBr}>\\\\mathrm{RCl}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{RCl}>\\\\mathrm{RBr}>\\\\mathrm{RI}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{RBr}>\\\\mathrm{RCl}>\\\\mathrm{RI}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{RBr}>\\\\mathrm{RI}>\\\\mathrm{RCl}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For a given alkyl group, the order of reactivity is<br>$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{I}>\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{Br}>\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{Cl}>\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{F}}{\\text { Decreasing bond energy }}$$<br>This order depends on the carbon-halogen bond energy; the carbon-fluorine bond energy is maximum and thus fluorides are least reactive while carbon-iodine bond energy is minimum hence iodides are most reactive.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26073,'chemistry',64,192,1925,'Pyrolusite is $\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$, and is used to prepare $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4.}$ The Steps are<br>$\\mathrm{MnO}_{2} \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{I}} \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{2-} \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{II}} \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$<br>Steps I and II are respectively:','[{"text": "Fuse with $\\\\mathrm{KOH} /$ air and electrolytic oxidation.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fuse with $\\\\mathrm{KOH} / \\\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}$ and electrolytic oxidation.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2).", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Dissolve in $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$ and oxidation.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ fused with $\\mathrm{KOH}$ in presence of air or oxidising agents like $\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}, \\mathrm{KClO}_{3}$ etc.<br>$2 \\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+4 \\mathrm{KOH}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\xrightarrow{\\text { fuse }} 2 \\mathrm{~K}_{2} \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>${\\text{MnO}}_4^{2 - }\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ electrolytic oxidation }}}}{\\text{MnO}}_4^ -  + {{\\text{e}}^ - }$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26075,'chemistry',66,1029,2426,'Aryl fluoride may be prepared from arene diazonium chloride using:','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{HBF}_{4} / \\\\Delta$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{HBF}_{4} / \\\\mathrm{NaNO}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Cu}, \\\\Delta$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CuF} / \\\\mathrm{HF}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{Cu} / \\\\mathrm{HF}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26075_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28675,'chemistry',70,1258,3070,'Which of the following are purine bases ?<br>I. Guanine<br>II. Adenine<br>III. Thymine<br>IV. Uracil','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Adenine and guanine are purine bases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26076,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'Which complex cannot ionise in solution?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoCl}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{4}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{2}\\\\left[\\\\operatorname{Pt}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{F}_{6}\\\\right)\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Pt}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\left[\\mathrm{CoCl}_{3}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{3}\\right]$ cannot ionise in solution as it is a non-electrolyte and do not furnish ions in solution.<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26077,'chemistry',64,191,1913,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ is more stable than $\\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ has half-filled configuration.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Electronic configuration of $\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5}$<br>Electronic configuration of $\\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{4}$<br>A half-filled or completely filled orbital demonstrates higher stability compared to an incompletely filled orbital. This stability is primarily due to the balanced distribution of electrons in these orbitals. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26078,'chemistry',63,807,1748,'Prolonged heating is avoided during the preparation of ferrous ammonium sulphate to','[{"text": "prevent reduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "prevent  oxidation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "prevent hydrolysis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "prevent breaking.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Prolonged heating results in oxidation of  $\\mathrm{Fe}^{+2}$  to  $\\mathrm{Fe}^{+3}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26079,'chemistry',66,1034,2443,'Chlorobenzene reacts with trichloro acetaldehyde in the presence of $$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$$ to give ____.','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Chloral on reaction with chlorobenzene in the presence of a catalytic amount of sulphuric acid forms DDT (dichlorodiphenyl Trichloro ethane).<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26079_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26079_question_1.jpg'),
(26080,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'What is the secondary valence of $\\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ ion according to Werner\'s theory in  $$\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right]^{+} \\text {? }$$','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In the complex $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right]^{+}$, the secondary valence of $\\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ ion according to Werner\'s theory is 6 . The secondary valences are non-ionisable. These are satisfied by neutral molecules or negative ions. The secondary valence is equal to the coordination number and is fixed for a metal.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26081,'chemistry',64,192,1925,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ is stored in dark bottles.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> On heating with alkalies, $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ is converted to manganate.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Potassium permanganate $\\left(\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}\\right)$ is stored in opaque containers to prevent photochemical decomposition induced by light, particularly UV radiation, which can degrade the compound. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26082,'chemistry',66,1027,2418,'For the compounds $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Cl}, \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Br}, \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{I}$$ and $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{~F}$$, the correct order of increasing $$\\mathrm{C}$$-halogen bond length is','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~F} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Br} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{I}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~F} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Br} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{I}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~F} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{I} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Br} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Br} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~F} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{I}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The correct order of increasing bond length is $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{~F} < \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Cl} < \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Br} < \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$$ I Bond length increases with increase in size of halogen.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26084,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'Which complex among the following gives a white precipitate on treatment with an aqueous solution of barium chloride?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Pt}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{4} \\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{5} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{5} \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Pt}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Among the given coordination compounds, $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{5} \\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right] \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ gives a white precipitate on treatment with an aqueous solution of barium chloride. This can be explained by the following reaction.<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& {\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{5} \\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right] \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}(\\mathrm{aq})} \\\\& \\quad \\longrightarrow\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{5} \\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+\\underset{\\text { Whiteppt. }}{\\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}(\\mathrm{~s})}\\end{aligned}$$<br>The other given compounds do not give white ppt. due to absence of $\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$ ions.<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26118,'chemistry',65,955,2222,'The IUPAC name of $\\mathrm{K}_{2}\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}(\\mathrm{CN})_{4}\\right]$ is','[{"text": "potassium tetracyanonickelate (II)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "potassium tetracyanatonickelate (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "potassium tetracyanatonickel (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "potassium tetracyanonickel (III)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In anionic complexes metal name ends with "-ate".',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26085,'chemistry',64,189,1907,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Members of 4d  and  5d series of transition elements have nearly same atomic radii.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Atomic and ionic radii for transition elements are smaller than their corresponding s-block elements.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Members of ${\\text{4d}}$ and ${\\text{5d}}$ series of transition elements have nearly same atomic radii due to lanthanide contraction. This results from ineffective electron shielding by the \' $\\mathrm{f}$ \' orbitals. Atomic and ionic radii decrease accross a period. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26086,'chemistry',66,1031,2437,'The major product formed when 1,1,1-trichloropropane is treated with aqueous potassium hydroxide is:','[{"text": "Propyne", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propan-1-ol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propan-2-ol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propionic acid", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26086_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26087,'chemistry',63,808,1752,'One mole of  $\\mathrm{P}_{4}$ reacts with 8 moles of $\\mathrm{SOCl}_{2}$ to give 4 moles of A , x mole $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ and 2 moles of B. A, B and  x  respectively are','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}, \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ and 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}, \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ and 4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{POCl}_{3}, \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ and 4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{POCl}_{3}, \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ and 2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${{\\rm{P}}_4} + 8{\\rm{SOC}}{{\\rm{l}}_2} \\to 4{\\rm{A}} + {\\rm{xS}}{{\\rm{O}}_2} + 2{\\rm{B}}$<br>$\\mathrm{P}_{4}+8 \\mathrm{SOCl}_{2} \\rightarrow 4 \\mathrm{PCl}_{3}+4 \\mathrm{SO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$<br>$\\mathrm{A}=\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}, \\mathrm{~B}=\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}, \\mathrm{x}=4$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26088,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'According to Werner\'s theory, the geometry of the complex is determined by','[{"text": "only from the primary valence in space", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "number and position of the primary valency  in space", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "number and position of the secondary valency  in space", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "only from the position of secondary valence in space", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Werner\'s theory was used to describe the structure and formation of complex compounds or coordination compounds. According to this theory the primary valency gives the oxidation number and the secondary valency gives the coordination number. Also, the geometry of the complex is determined by number and position of secondary valences in space as the ligand satisfying seconday valences are always directed towards fixed position in space.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26089,'chemistry',64,191,1920,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Transition metals are good catalysts.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{V}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}$ or Pt is used in the preparation of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ by Contact process.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Transition metals, owing to their expansive surface area and the capacity for variable valencies, readily create intermediate activated complexes. This unique property makes them highly effective catalysts. $\\mathrm{V}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}$ or Pt is used in Contact process. But it is not the exact reason for the given assertion. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26090,'chemistry',66,1033,2441,'Which one of the following reagents is not suitable for the elimination reaction?','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{NaI}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{NaOEt} / \\\\mathrm{EtOH}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{NaOH} / \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{NaOH} / \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}-\\\\mathrm{EtOH}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Alkyl chloride or bromide undergo substitution and get converted to any alkyl iodide on treatment with a solution of sodium iodide in acetone. e.g.<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{Br + NaI}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{acetone}}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{I + NaBr}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2016,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26090_question_1.jpg'),
(26091,'chemistry',63,820,1814,'$\\mathrm{ClF}_{5}$ at room temperature is a','[{"text": "colourless liquid with square pyramidal geometry", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "colourless gas with square pyramidal geometry", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "colourless gas with trigonal bipyramidal geometry", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "colourless liquid with trigonal bipyramidal geometry.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At room temperature, $\\mathrm{ClF}_{5}$ is a colourless liquid with square pyramidal geometry.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26091_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26092,'chemistry',65,954,2218,'The number of unidentate ligands in the complex ion is called','[{"text": "oxidation number", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "primary valency", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "coordination number", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "EAN", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Coordination number of ligand is the total number of monodentate or unidentate ligands surrounding the central atom in the coordination sphere or in the complex ion.<br>e.g. $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$ - coordination number $=6$<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26150,'chemistry',66,1028,2419,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">$$\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}$$ is used during the reaction of alcohol with $$\\mathrm{KI}$$.<br></div><div class="column-right">$$\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}$$ is a non-oxidising agent.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}$$ is used during the reaction of alcohol with KI because $$\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}$$ is a nonoxidising agent.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26093,'chemistry',64,191,1922,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Transition metals form substitutional alloys.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Alloys are made to develop some useful properties which are absent in the constituent elements.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Transition metals, due to their closely matched sizes, readily form substitutional alloys by seamlessly substituting one another within the crystal lattice structure. Alloys have properties that are not present in the parent metals. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26094,'chemistry',66,1033,2441,'The major product of the following reaction is :','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26094_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26094_question_1.jpg'),
(26095,'chemistry',63,808,1759,'The number of  $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{P}$ bonds in $\\mathrm{H}_{4} \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7},\\left(\\mathrm{HPO}_{3}\\right)_{3}$ and  $\\mathrm{P}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{10}$ are respectively','[{"text": "$1,2,4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1,3,6$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0,3,6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0,3,4$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In $\\mathrm{H}_{4} \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$, <br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26095_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Number of  $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{P}$ bonds $=1$<br>In $\\left(\\mathrm{HPO}_{3}\\right)_{3}$,<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26095_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Number of  $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{P}$ bonds $=3$.<br>In $\\mathrm{P}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{10}$,<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26095_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Number of  $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{P}$ bonds $=6$<br>Thus, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26096,'chemistry',64,191,1916,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Zinc becomes dull in moist air.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Zinc is coated by a thin film of its basic carbonate in moist air.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$4 \\mathrm{Zn}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+2 \\mathrm{O}_{2}+\\mathrm{CO}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{ZnCO}_{3} \\cdot 3 \\mathrm{Zn}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$<br>When exposed to moist air, zinc undergoes a reaction resulting in the formation of a thin film of basic carbonates on its surface, causing it to lose its luster and appear dull. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26097,'chemistry',65,954,2218,'The co-ordination number of $\\mathrm{Fe}$ and $\\mathrm{Co}$ in the complex ions, $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)_{3}\\right]^{3-}$ and $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}(\\mathrm{SCN})_{4}\\right]^{2-}$ are respectively','[{"text": "6 and 8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4 and 6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6 and 4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3 and 4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Coordination number is the number of coordinate covalent bonds formed between central metal atom and Ligand.<br>In $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)_{3}\\right]^{3-}, \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}$ is a bidentate ligand<br>$\\therefore$ Coordination number $=6$<br>In $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}(\\mathrm{SCN})_{4}\\right]^{2-}, \\mathrm{SCN}^{-}$is a monodentate ligand.<br>$\\therefore$ Coordination number $=4$<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26098,'chemistry',66,1032,2440,'The major product of the following reaction is :','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CHCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\mathrm{CHCH}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CHCH}=\\\\mathrm{CHCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\mathrm{C}=\\\\mathrm{CHCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\mathrm{CH}-\\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\mathrm{CHCH}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26098_question_1.jpg'),
(26099,'chemistry',63,823,1819,'Inert gases have positive electron gain enthalpy. Its correct order is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{He} < \\\\mathrm{Kr} < \\\\mathrm{Xe} < \\\\mathrm{Ne}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{He} < \\\\mathrm{Xe} < \\\\mathrm{Kr} < \\\\mathrm{Ne}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{He} < \\\\mathrm{Ne} < \\\\mathrm{Kr} < \\\\mathrm{Xe}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Xe} < \\\\mathrm{Kr} < \\\\mathrm{Ne} < \\\\mathrm{He}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Noble gases have large positive electron gain enthalpies since, they have no tendency to accept electron due to stable configuration.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26099_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26100,'chemistry',64,191,1917,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When $\\mathrm{Zn}$ is placed in a magnetic field, it is feebly magnetised in a direction opposite to that of the magnetising field.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Zn}$ has completely filled atomic orbitals.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A diamagnetic material gets magnetized in the direction opposite to the applied magnetic field. With all its electrons paired in its electronic configuration of $[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{10} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}$, zinc exhibits diamagnetic properties due to the absence of unpaired electrons. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26184,'chemistry',63,807,1746,'Match Column I with Column II and choose the correct combination from the options given.<br><div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - P,\\tB - Q,\\tC - S,\\t\\tD - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S,\\tB - R,\\tC - Q,\\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,\\tB - S,\\tC - Q,\\tD - P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - R,\\tB - Q,\\tC - S,\\t\\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26184_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/63_26184_question_1.png'),
(26101,'chemistry',65,954,2215,'Which among the following is a neutral complex?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Pt}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ag}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A neutral complex is a complex ion which have no charge on it. Among the given options $\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right]$ is a neutral complex. The type of complexes in other options are as follows:<br>(1) $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{3}$ - cationic complexes<br>(2) $\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{6}$ - cationic complexes<br>(4) $\\mathrm{K}\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}\\left(\\mathrm{CN}_{2}\\right)\\right]$ - anionic complexes<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26102,'chemistry',63,808,1752,'Reaction of thionyl chloride with white phosphorus forms a compound [A], which on hydrolysis gives [B], a dibasic acid. [A] and  [B]  are respectively','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{POCl}_{3}$  and  $\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$  and  $\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}$  and  $\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{PO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{P}_{4} \\\\mathrm{O}_{6}$  and  $\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{PO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'White phosphorus reacts with thionyl chloride to form $\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}$ :<br>$\\mathrm{P}_{4}+8 \\mathrm{SOCl}_{2} \\longrightarrow \\underset{[A]}{4 \\mathrm{PCl}_{3}}+4 \\mathrm{SO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$<br>$\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}$ hydrolyses in presence of moisture:<br>${\\rm{PC}}{{\\rm{l}}_3} + 3{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}} \\to \\mathop {{{\\rm{H}}_3}{\\rm{P}}{{\\rm{O}}_3}}\\limits_{[{\\rm{B}}]}  + 3{\\rm{HCl}}$<br>Thus, option (3) is the correct choice.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26103,'chemistry',66,1032,2440,'Which of the following will most readily give the dehydrohalogenation product?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26104,'chemistry',64,191,1914,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Zinc is used in the galvanisation of iron<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Its coating on iron articles increases their life by protecting them from rusting.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Zinc exhibits remarkable resistance to reactions with both air and water, making it an excellent protective coating for iron articles. Its application creates a barrier that shields the iron surface, significantly extending its lifespan by preventing rust formation. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26105,'chemistry',63,816,1797,'The bond dissociation energy is highest for','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{F}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{I}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26105_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\mathrm{F}_{2}$ has less bond dissociation energy due to small size and high interelectronic repulsions between lone pairs. $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ and  $\\mathrm{I}_{2}$ have less bond dissociation energies than $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ due to large sizes.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26106,'chemistry',65,954,2217,'What is the oxidation number of gold in the complex $\\left[\\mathrm{AuCl}_{4}\\right]^{1-}$ ?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{ + 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{  4}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let ${\\text{x}}$  be the oxidation number of gold (Au) in the complex $\\left[\\mathrm{AuCl}_{4}\\right]^{-1}$<br>$$\\begin{array}{ll}\\therefore & \\mathrm{x}+4(-1)=-1 \\\\\\Rightarrow & \\mathrm{x}=-1+4 \\\\\\Rightarrow & \\mathrm{x}=+3\\end{array}$$<br>Hence, the oxidation number of gold is +3 .<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26107,'chemistry',66,1038,2461,'The compounds ${\\rm{A}}$ and ${\\rm{B}}$ in the following reaction are respectively','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}}$ benzyl chloride, ${\\\\rm{B}}$ benzyl isocyanide", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}}$ = benzyl alcohol, ${\\\\rm{B}}$ benzyl isocyanide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}}$ benzyl alcohol, ${\\\\rm{B}}$ benzyl cyanide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}}$ = benzyl chloride, ${\\\\rm{B}}$ benzyl cyanide", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26107_question_1.jpg'),
(26108,'chemistry',64,191,1917,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ is paramagnetic.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ contains four unpaired electrons.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Electronic configuration of $\\mathrm{Fe}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{6} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}$<br>Electronic configuration of $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{6}$<br>which has four unpaired electrons and is paramagnetic. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26119,'chemistry',66,1032,2440,'Consider the following alkyl halides<br>$\\mathop {{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{I,}}}\\limits_{\\rm{I}} \\,\\,\\,\\mathop {{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{Cl}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{II}}} {\\rm{,}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\mathop {{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{Br}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{III}}} {\\rm{,}}\\,\\,\\,\\mathop {\\,{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{\\,F}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{IV}}} $<br>(I) I, undergoes faster dehydrohalogenation than (II) and (IV).<br>(II) II, undergoes lower dehydrohalogenation than (I).<br>(III) IV, has least ability to undergo dehydrohalogenation.<br>Choose the correct option.','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The reactivity order or alkyl halides towards dehydrohalogenation can be explained on the basis of $$\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{X}$$ bond strength. Lower the bond strength, higher is the reactivity. Hence, order is $$\\mathrm{I}>\\mathrm{III}>\\mathrm{II}>\\mathrm{IV}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26109,'chemistry',63,807,1747,'"A " obtained by Ostwald\'s method involving air oxidation of  $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$, upon further air oxidation produces " B ".  " B " on hydration forms an oxoacid of nitrogen along with evolution of "  A  ". The oxoacid also produces "A" and gives positive brown ring test.<br>Identify  A and B , respectively.','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}, \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}, \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Ostwald process involves the following equations;<br>$4{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{3({\\text{g}})}} + \\mathop {5{{\\text{O}}_{2({\\text{g}})}}}\\limits_{{\\text{(From}}{\\kern 1pt} \\,{\\kern 1pt} {\\text{air)}}} \\xrightarrow[\\begin{subarray}{l} \n  500\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{K}}, \\\\ \n  9{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\text{bar}} \n\\end{subarray} ]{\\begin{subarray}{l} \n  {\\text{Pt}}/{\\text{Rh}} \\\\ \n  {\\text{gauge}} \n\\end{subarray} }\\mathop {4{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{({\\text{g}})}}}\\limits_{({\\text{A}})}  + 6{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{({\\text{g}})}}$<br>$2{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{({\\text{g}})}} + {{\\text{O}}_{2({\\text{g}})}} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathop {2{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{2(\\,{\\text{g}})}}}\\limits_{({\\text{B}})} $<br>$3 \\mathrm{NO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(\\mathrm{l})} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{HNO}_{3(\\mathrm{aq})}+\\mathrm{NO}_{(\\mathrm{g})}$<br>Brown ring test involves the following equations;<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}+\\mathrm{NO} \\rightarrow$<br>$\\mathop {{{\\left[ {{\\text{Fe}}{{\\left( {{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{O}}} \\right)}_5}({\\text{NO}})} \\right]}^{2 + }}}\\limits_{{\\text{ Brown}}\\,\\,{\\text{ring }}}  + {{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}$<br>Hence, A is NO and B is $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26110,'chemistry',65,954,2214,'What is the number of donor atoms in dimethylglyoximato ligand?','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Dimethylglyoximato ligand is a bidendate ligand, i.e. it can bind with metal ion through two donor atoms. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26110_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26112,'chemistry',64,191,1916,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> On heating $\\mathrm{HgCl}_{2}$ sublimes.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{HgCl}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}$ exist together in aqueous solution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{HgCl}_{2}$ sublimes on heating. $\\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}$ is strong reducing agent and reduces $\\mathrm{HgCl}_{2}$ first to $\\mathrm{Hg}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ (white) and then to $\\mathrm{Hg}$ (black).<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{HgCl}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{SnCl}_{4}+\\underset{\\text { (White) }}{\\mathrm{Hg}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}} \\\\& \\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}+\\mathrm{Hg}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{SnCl}_{4}+\\underset{\\text { (Black) }}{2 \\mathrm{Hg}} .\\end{aligned}$<br>So, $\\mathrm{HgCl}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}$ cannot exist together in an aqueous solution. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26114,'chemistry',66,1029,2424,'For the below reaction<br>I.${\\rm{ X  =  Cl}}$; ortho and para-isomers can be separated out<br>II. ${\\rm{X  =  I}}$; reaction does not occur due to high reactivity of iodine<br>III. ${\\rm{X  =  F}}$; reaction with fluorine is reversible.<br>Select the most appropriate option with respect to correct statements','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The ortho and para-isomers of chlorotoluene can be easily separated out due to large difference in their melting points. Reactions with iodine are reversible in nature and require the presence of an oxidising agent $$\\left(\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}\\right.$$ or $$\\left.\\mathrm{HIO}_{4}\\right)$$ to oxidise $$\\mathrm{HI}$$ formed during iodination. Fluoro compounds are not prepared by this method due to high reactivity of fluorine.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26114_question_1.png'),
(26115,'chemistry',65,954,2214,'Match the column I with column II and choose the correct option<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A Triphenylphosphine<br>B $\\mathrm{BF}_{3}$<br>C  Ethylene  diamine<br>D Ethylenediamine  tetracetate ion</div><div class="column-right">P Unidentate<br>Q  Didentate<br> R Not a ligand<br>S  Hexadentate</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A - S,   B - R,   C - Q,   D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - P,   B - Q,   C - R,   D - S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - P,   B - R,   C - Q,   D - S}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - P,   B - Q,   C - S,   D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26116,'chemistry',64,191,1918,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Hydrated $\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}$ turns white on heating.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Hydrated $\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}$ is also known as blue vitriol.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Hydrated copper sulphate $\\left(\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4} \\cdot 5 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)$ is blue which on heating loses water of crystallization to form anhydrous $\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}$, a white compound.<br>$\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4} \\cdot 5 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\xrightarrow{\\text { heat }} \\underset{\\text { (White) }}{\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}}+5 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>$\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4} .5 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ is commonly called blue vitriol.<br>It is blue in colour due to the presence of 5 molecules of water of crystallization. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26117,'chemistry',63,812,1772,'Bond dissociation energy of " ${\\rm{E}} - {\\rm{H}}$ " bond of the "  $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{E}$ " hydrides of group 16 elements (given below) follows order<br>A. O<br>B.  S<br>C. Se<br>D. Te<br>Choose the correct from the options given below.','[{"text": "A $>$ B $>$ D $>$ C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B $>$ A $>$ C $>$ D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "D $>$ C $>$ B $>$ A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A $>$ B $>$ C $>$ D", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Bond dissociation energy of group 16 elements has the following order :<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26117_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Thus, option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26120,'chemistry',64,191,1916,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Rusting of iron is an example of corrosion.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Rusting of iron is decreased by acids and electrolyte.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Corrosion is a process through which refined metals are converted into more stable compounds such as metal oxides, metal sulfides, or metal hydroxides. Rusting of iron is a common example. During rusting, iron oxidizes to $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ ions. These ions combine to form $\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$, which further oxidizes to produce $\\mathrm{Fe}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3} \\cdot \\mathrm{xH}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ (rust). Acids aid in the dissolution of pure iron to ferrous ions, while electrolytes enhance conductivity, facilitating the electrochemical reactions involved in rust formation. Therefore option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26121,'chemistry',63,817,1800,'Identify $\\mathrm{X}, \\mathrm{Y}, \\mathrm{Z}$ in the following reaction.<br>$\\mathrm{ClO}+\\mathrm{NO}_{2} \\rightarrow \\underline{\\mathrm{X}} \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}} \\underline{\\mathrm{Y}}+\\underline{\\mathrm{Z}}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{X}=\\\\mathrm{ClNO}_{3}, \\\\mathrm{Y}=\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Z}=\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{X}=\\\\mathrm{ClONO}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Y}=\\\\mathrm{HOCl}, \\\\mathrm{Z}=\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{X}=\\\\mathrm{ClONO}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Y}=\\\\mathrm{HOCl}, \\\\mathrm{Z}=\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{X}=\\\\mathrm{ClNO}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Y}=\\\\mathrm{HCl}, \\\\mathrm{Z}=\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26121_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26122,'chemistry',65,955,2222,'The IUPAC name of the complex $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}$ is','[{"text": "dichlorotetraamminecobalt (III) chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "tetraamminedichlorocoblat (III) chloride", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "tetraamminedichlorocobalt (II) chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "teraamminedichlorocobalt (IV) chloride", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26123,'chemistry',66,1037,2455,'Consider the following reaction,<br>$$\\underset{\\mathrm{A}}{2 \\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\stackrel{\\text { hv }}{\\longrightarrow} \\underset{\\mathrm{B}}{2} \\mathrm{COCl}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{HCl}$$<br>I. B is phosgene gas and is poisonous<br>II. A is stored in closed dark coloured bottle.<br>III. A is used for the production of the freon refrigerant R-22<br>Choose the correct option.','[{"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathop {{\\text{2CHC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{3}}}}\\limits_{\\text{A}} {\\text{ + }}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{hv}}}}\\mathop {{\\text{2COC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{\\text{B}} {\\text{ + 2HCl}}$<br>$\\begin{array}{l}{\\rm{A = chloroform}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{*stored}}\\,\\,{\\rm{in}}\\,\\,{\\rm{closed}}\\,\\,{\\rm{dark}}\\,\\,{\\rm{vessels}}\\\\\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{*Used}}\\,\\,{\\rm{in}}\\,\\,{\\rm{the}}\\,\\,{\\rm{production}}\\,\\,\\\\\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{freon,}}\\,\\,{\\rm{R - 22}}{\\rm{.}}\\end{array}$<br>$$\\mathrm{B}=$$ Phosgene $$=$$ Poisonous gas.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26124,'chemistry',64,192,1931,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{AgBr}$ is used in photography.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{AgBr}$ undergoes photochemical reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{AgBr}$ is the most sensitive silver halide to photoreduction. Hence, it is used as the light sensitive material in photographic films. The unchanged $\\mathrm{AgBr}$ is dissolved in hypo solution to cast an image on photographic plate.<br>$2 \\mathrm{AgBr} \\xrightarrow{\\text { hu }} 2 \\mathrm{Ag}+\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$.<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26126,'chemistry',65,955,2222,'As per IUPAC norms, the name of the complex $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}(\\mathrm{en})_{2}(\\mathrm{ONO}) \\mathrm{Cl}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}$ is','[{"text": "Chlorido bis(ethane-1,2-diamine) nitro-Ocobalt (III) chloride", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Chlorobis (ethylenediamine) nitro-O- cobalt(III) chloride.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chloridodi (ethylenediamine) nitrocobalt (III) chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chloroethylenediaminenitro-O-cobalt(III) chloride", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26127,'chemistry',66,1028,2419,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In the preparation of alkyl halides from alcohols, thionyl chloride is preferred over other reagents.<br></div><div class="column-right">The other two products produced during the reaction except alkyl halide, are escapable gases</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$\\mathrm{ROH}+\\mathrm{SOCl}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{RCl}+\\underset{\\text { escapable gas }}{\\mathrm{SO}_{2}}+\\mathrm{HCl}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26128,'chemistry',64,191,1916,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Copper dissolves in conc. nitric acid (a strong oxident) but not in dil. $\\mathrm{HCl}$<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Standard oxidation potential of $\\mathrm{Cu}$ is below $\\mathrm{H}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Copper has its standard oxidation potential below $\\mathrm{H}$ and thus cannot liberate $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from $\\mathrm{HCl}$. However, it dissolves in concentrated nitric acid because conc. $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ is strong oxidising agent.<br>$\\mathrm{Cu}+4 \\mathrm{HNO}_{3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{3}\\right)_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+2 \\mathrm{NO}_{2}$<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26130,'chemistry',66,1038,2461,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Alkyl benzene is not prepared by Friedel - crafts alkylation of benzene.<br></div><div class="column-right">Alkyl halides are more reactive than aryl halides towards $$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}$$ reactions.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Statement-A is incorrect,because aryl halides do not undergo nucleophilic substitution under ordinary conditions. This is due to resonance, because of which the carbonchlorine bond acquires partial double bond character, hence it becomes shorter and stronger and thus cannot be replaced by nucleophiles.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26131,'chemistry',65,955,2222,'The IUPAC name of<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right)\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}$ is','[{"text": "tetraaminechloridonitrito-N-cobalt(III) chloride", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "tetraaminechloridonitrocobalt(II) chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "tetraaminechloridonitritocobalt(I) chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "tetraaminechloridodinitrocobalt(III) chloride", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}$, donor atom is $\\mathrm{N}$ so it is represented as Nitro - N.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26132,'chemistry',64,191,1913,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ is more stable than $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ ions are easily oxidised to $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ is more stable than $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$. The electronic configuration of $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ has half- filled \'d \'orbitals and thus becomes more stable.<br>${ }_{26} \\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}: 1 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 2 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 2 \\mathrm{p}^{6} 3 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 3 \\mathrm{p}^{6} 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{6}$<br>${ }_{26} \\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}: 1 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 2 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 2 \\mathrm{p}^{6} 3 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 3 \\mathrm{p}^{6} 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5}$.<br>$\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ ions are easily oxidised to more stable $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26134,'chemistry',66,1028,2419,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Yield of an alkyl halide which is formed by the free radical halogenation is low.<br></div><div class="column-right">Free radical halogenation lead to formation of mixture of isomeric mono and polyhaloalkanes.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Free radical chlorination or bromination of alkanes gives a complex mixture of isomeric mono and polyhaloalkanes which is difficult to separate as pure compounds. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26134_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26135,'chemistry',65,955,2222,'IUPAC name of $\\mathrm{Na}_{3}\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right)_{6}\\right]$ is','[{"text": "sodium hexanitritocobaltate (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sodium hexanitritocobalt (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sodium hexanitrocobaltate (III)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "sodium cobaltnitrite", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'IUPAC name of $\\mathrm{Na}_{3}\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right)_{6}\\right]$ is sodium hexanitrocobaltate (III)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26136,'chemistry',64,192,1926,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Aqueous solution of $\\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ is acidic.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The hydrated form of $\\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ consists<br>of six water molecules, $\\mathrm{FeCl}_{3} \\cdot 6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ in water shows acidic nature because of cationic hydrolysis of $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$.<br>$\\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{OH})_{3}+3 \\mathrm{HCl}$<br>Given reason is also correct, but it is not the correct explanation of the assertion. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26137,'chemistry',63,807,1746,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}}$ gas is :','[{"text": "Colourless, neutral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Colourless, acidic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Brown, acidic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Brown neutral", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}}$ is brown coloured gas and is acidic in nature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26138,'chemistry',66,1037,2455,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">$$\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$$ is stored in dark bottles. <br></div><div class="column-right">$$\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$$ is oxidised in dark.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$$ is stored in dark bottles to prevent oxidation of $$\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$$ in presence of sunlight.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26147,'chemistry',64,190,1911,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Tungsten filament is used in electric bulb.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Tungsten is a metal of high melting point.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Tungsten boasts an exceptionally high melting point, making it an ideal material for filaments used in light bulbs. When an electric current passes through the tungsten filament, it emits brilliant light, contributing to its efficacy in lighting applications. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26139,'chemistry',64,192,1926,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A solution of ferric chloride on standing gives a brown precipitate.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ possesses covalent bonds and chlorine bridge structure.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Hydrated solution of $\\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ gets converted into brown $\\mathrm{Fe}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ due to hydrolysis.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\underset{\\text { Brown ppt }}{\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{OH})_{3}}+3 \\mathrm{HCl} \\\\& \\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{OH})_{3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Fe}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} .\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ structure is <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/64_26139_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26140,'chemistry',65,955,2222,'IUPAC name of the complex $\\mathrm{Ba}\\left[\\mathrm{CuCl}_{4}\\right]$ is','[{"text": "barium tetrachlorocuprate (II)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "tetrachlorobariumcuprate (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "barium tetrachlorocuprate (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "tetrachlorobarium copper (II)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'IUPAC name of the complex $\\mathrm{Ba}\\left[\\mathrm{CuCl}_{4}\\right]$ is barium tetrachlorocuprate (II), While naming a complex, the cation is named first and ligands are named in an alphabetical order before the name of the central atom or ion.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26141,'chemistry',63,817,1799,'The correct decreasing order of boiling points of hydrogen halides is:','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HF}>\\\\mathrm{HCl}>\\\\mathrm{HBr}>\\\\mathrm{HI}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HI}>\\\\mathrm{HBr}>\\\\mathrm{HCl}>\\\\mathrm{HF}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HF}>\\\\mathrm{HI}>\\\\mathrm{HBr}>\\\\mathrm{HCl}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{HI}>\\\\mathrm{HF}>\\\\mathrm{HBr}>\\\\mathrm{HCl}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'HF has exceptionally high B.P. due to hydrogen bonding. Down the group, in general HX boiling points increase.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26142,'chemistry',66,1031,2436,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Reaction between<br>\n$$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{ONa}$$ and $$\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{Cl}$$ produces $$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OC}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}$$ and $$\\mathrm{NaCl}$$.</div><div class="column-right">Number of enantiomers formed during the conversion of $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{Br}$$ to<br>\n$$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{NC}$$ by reacting with $$\\mathrm{AgCN}$$ are 2.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Zero enantiomers observed during the conversion of $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{Br}$$ to $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{NC}$$ due to the absence of chiral carbon.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26143,'chemistry',64,191,1918,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\left[\\mathrm{Ti}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$ is a coloured ion.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Ti shows $+2,+3,+4$ oxidation states.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The presence of the +3 oxidation state in titanium results in a configuration of $[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{1}$, allowing for d-d transitions. This characteristic gives rise to its capacity to exhibit coloration $\\mathrm{Ti}$ shows $+2,+3,+4$ oxidation states but it is not the explanation for assertion. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26144,'chemistry',65,955,2222,'The correct IUPAC name of $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{3}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right)_{3}\\right]$ is','[{"text": "Triammine trinitro - $\\\\mathrm{N}$ cobalt (III)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Triammine trinitro- $\\\\mathrm{N}$ cobalt (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Triammine cobalt (III) nitrite", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Triammine trinitro-N cobaltate (III)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The IUPAC name of $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{3}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right)_{3}\\right]$ is triammine trinitro- $\\mathrm{N}$ cobalt (III).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26145,'chemistry',63,823,1817,'The synthetically produced radioactive noble gas by the collision of  ${\\mathrm{{ }_{98}^{249} \\mathrm{Cf}}}$ with ${\\mathrm{{ }_{20}^{48} \\mathrm{Ca}}}$ is :','[{"text": "Radon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Radium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oganesson", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Xenon", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Oganesson ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Z}=118}}$<br>${ }_{98}^{249} \\mathrm{Cf}+{ }_{20}^{48} \\mathrm{Ca} \\rightarrow{ }_{18}^{294} \\mathrm{Og}+3{ }_{0}^{1} \\mathrm{n}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26146,'chemistry',66,1034,2442,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Chlorobenzene is reactive towards Aromatic nucleophilic substitution reaction than o-Nitrochlorobenzene.<br></div><div class="column-right">$$-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$$ is stronger EWG than $$-\\mathrm{Cl}$$</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Chlorobenzene is less reactive than onitrochlorobenzene towards ArSN reactions.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26148,'chemistry',65,957,2228,'An octahedral complex is formed when hybrid orbitals of the following type are involved','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{d}^{2} \\\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{sp}^{2} \\\\mathrm{~d}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{dsp}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Depending upon the nature of ligand, octahedral complex is of two types<br>i. Inner orbital complex $\\left(\\mathrm{d}^{2} \\mathrm{sp}^{3}\\right) \\rightarrow$ formed under the influence of strong ligands.<br>ii. Outer orbital complex $\\left(\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}^{2}\\right) \\rightarrow$ formed under the influence of weak ligands.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26151,'chemistry',64,191,1921,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Transition metals form a large number of interstitial compounds.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> They have high melting point and boiling point.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Certain non-metallic atoms such as hydrogen $(\\mathrm{H})$, boron $(\\mathrm{B})$, carbon $(\\mathrm{C})$, nitrogen $(\\mathrm{N})$, among others, possess the ability to occupy interstitial sites within the lattices of transition metals, thereby forming interstitial compounds. High melting points of these metals is due to the involvement of greater number of electrons from (n-1)d in addition to the ns electrons in the interatomic metallic bonding. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26152,'chemistry',63,811,1769,'Among Group 16 elements, which one doesNOT show -2 oxidation state?','[{"text": "O", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Se", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Te", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Po", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Polonium being metal can not show -2 oxidation state. It shows ${\\mathrm{+2,+4}}$ oxidation states',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26153,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'A correct statement is','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}$ is diamagnetic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{MnBr}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}$ is tetrahedral", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoBr}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{en})_{2}\\\\right]^{-}$exhibits linkage isomerism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}$ is an inner orbital complex", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'(1) $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}:{ }_{27} \\mathrm{Co}^{2+}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{7} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{0}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26153_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\mathrm{n}=3$ (where $\\mathrm{n}=$ no. of unpaired electrons)<br>Hence, complex is paramagnetic in nature.<br>(2) $\\left[\\mathrm{MnBr}_{4}\\right]^{2-}:{ }_{25} \\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26153_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ - hybridization<br>(Tetrahedral)<br>(3) $\\left[\\mathrm{CoBr}_{2}(\\mathrm{en})_{2}\\right]^{-}$exhibit geometrical isomerism.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26153_explanation_3.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>(4) $\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}: \\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{8}$<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26153_explanation_4.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26154,'chemistry',66,1030,2428,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Boiling points of chlorides, bromides and iodides are considered to be higher than hydrocarbons.</div><div class="column-right">Due to greater polarity and higher molecular mass, the intermolecular forces of attraction are stronger in halogen derivative as compared to hydrocarbon.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Due to greater polarity as well as higher molecular mass as compared to the parent hydrocarbon, the intermolecular forces of attraction (diple-dipole and van der Waal\'s) are stronger in the halogen derivatives that is why the boiling points of chlorides, bromides and iodides are considerably higher than those of the hydrocarbons of comparable molecular mass.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26155,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ is not a primary standard in volumetric analysis.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ is hygroscopic.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Sodium chromate $\\left(\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}\\right)$ is unsuitable as a primary standard in volumetric analysis due to its hygroscopic nature, lack of stability in solution, and potential purity concerns. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26156,'chemistry',63,812,1772,'Given below are two statements.\n choose the correct answer from the options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">: The boiling point of hydrides of Group 16 elements follow the order<br>${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}\\,{\\rm{ > }}\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{Te}}\\,{\\rm{ > }}\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{Se}}\\,{\\rm{ > }}\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{\\,S}}{\\rm{.}}$</div><div class="column-right">On the basis of molecular mass, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$ is expected to have lower boiling point than the other members of the group but due to the presence of extensive H -bonding in ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$, it has higher boiling point.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Boiling points of hydrides of group 16 follow the order ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}>\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{Te}>\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{Se}>\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}}}$.<br>Due to intermolecular H -bonding ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$ shows higher boiling point than respective hydrides of group 16 .<br>So both statements are true.<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26157,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'Which one of following is an outer orbital complex and exhibits paramagnetic behaviour?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Zn}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cr}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${ }_{28} \\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ in $\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}$ has ${\\text{1}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{,2}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{,2}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{6}}}{\\text{,3}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{3}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{6}}}{\\text{3}}{{\\text{d}}^{\\text{8}}}$, configuration. It uses 4 th orbital to show ${\\text{s}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{3}}}{{\\text{d}}^{\\text{2}}}$-hybridisation to form outer complex with 2 unpaired electrons in  ${\\text{3d}}$ -orbital.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26158,'chemistry',66,1030,2430,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Haloalkanes are very slightly soluble in water.</div><div class="column-right">New intermolecular attraction between haloalkanes and solvent molecules have much more strength than intermolecular attraction between haloalkanes molecules.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Haloalkanes are slightly soluble in water. Less energy is released when attraction between haloalkanes and water molecules takes place. As a result solubility of haloalkane in water is low.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26159,'chemistry',64,190,1910,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Chromium is hard but mercury is soft.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Chromium is a 3d transition element.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Chromium stands out for having the maximum number of unpaired dectrons among the elements, contributing to its classification as a hard metal. On the contrary, mercury $(\\mathrm{Hg})$ lacks any unpaired delectrons, placing it in the category of soft metals $\\mathrm{Cr}$ belongs to $3 \\mathrm{~d}$ series. Thus, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26160,'chemistry',63,806,1740,'The decreasing order of rate of reaction of the following haldies with water is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{AsCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{SbCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{BiCl}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{NCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{AsCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{SbCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{BiCl}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{AsCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{NCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{SbCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{BiCl}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SbCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{BiCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{AsCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{NCl}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Down the group, group 15 elements trichlorides stability increases, as a result reaction rate with water decreases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26161,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'Of the following complex ions, which is diamagnetic in nature?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CuCl}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{NiCl}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ has two unpaired $\\left( {{\\text{3}}{{\\text{d}}^{\\text{8}}}} \\right)$ electrons.<br>$\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$is strong field ligand and thus all the eight electrons are paired giving ${\\text{ds}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ - }}$hybridisation.   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26161_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26162,'chemistry',66,1031,2433,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">${{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{N}}}{\\rm{2}}$ mechanism is accompanied by racemisation.</div><div class="column-right">Nucleophile attaches itself on the side opposite to one where halogen atom is present.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In case of optically active alkyl halides, the product formed as a result of $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ mechanism has inverted configuration as compared to the reactant. This is because the nucleophile attaches itself on the side opposite to the one where the halogen atom is present.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26163,'chemistry',64,191,1918,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ is a coloured compound.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Colour of $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ is due to charge transfer.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The purple color of $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ arises from electronic transitions involving manganese $(\\mathrm{Mn})$ $\\mathrm{d}$ orbitals, absorbing visible light $(500-600 \\mathrm{~nm})$ due to ligand-induced splitting, resulting in its distinctive color. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26164,'chemistry',63,814,1782,'Which of the following reaction cannot change$\\mathrm{SO}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-} ?$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-} \\\\xrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-} \\\\xrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+} \\\\xrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{Ba}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{2} \\\\rightarrow$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$2 \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}+5 \\mathrm{SO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\longrightarrow$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{\\rm{M}}{{\\rm{n}}^{2 + }} + 5{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{SO}}_4^{2 - } + 4{{\\rm{H}}^ + }$<br>${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_7^{2 - } + 3\\,{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_2} + 2{{\\rm{H}}^ + } \\to $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}^{3 + }} + 3{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{SO}}_4^{2 - } + {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>$2{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{3 + }} + {\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_2} + 2{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}} \\to $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{\\rm{F}}{{\\rm{e}}^{2 + }} + 4{{\\rm{H}}^ + } + {\\rm{SO}}_4^{2 - }$<br>$\\boxed{{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_2} + {\\text{Ba}}{{({\\text{OH}})}_2} \\to {\\text{BaS}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}} + {{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}$<br>So, $\\mathrm{Ba}\\left(\\mathrm{OH}_{2}\\right)$ cannot oxidise $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ to $\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{-2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26173,'chemistry',66,1035,2448,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Chlorobenzene on reaction with sodium metal in the presence of dry ether gives diphenyl.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> This reaction is called Ullmann reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26165,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'The $d$-electronic configuration of $\\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}, \\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}, \\mathrm{Fe}^{2+} \\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ are $3 d^{4}, 3 d^{5}, 3 d^{6}$ and $3 d^{8}$ respectively.<br>Which of the following complex will show minimum paramagnetic behaviour?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cr}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Mn}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}, \\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}, \\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ have $4,5,4$ and 2 unpaired electrons. No of unpaired electrons $\\alpha $ paramagnetism.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26166,'chemistry',66,1035,2445,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">tert-butylbromide undergoes Wurtz reaction to give 2,2,3,3 tetramethylbutane.</div><div class="column-right">In Wurtz reaction, alkyl halides react with sodium in dry ether to give hydrocarbon containing double the carbon atoms that present in the halide.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Alkyl halides reacts with sodium in dry ether to give hydrocarbons containing double the number of carbon atoms as that present in the parent halide. This reaction is known as Wurtz reaction.<br>$$2 \\mathrm{RX}+2 \\mathrm{Na} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{R}+2 \\mathrm{NaX}$$ <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26166_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26167,'chemistry',64,191,1919,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Transition metals form complexes.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Transition metals have unpaired electrons.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Transition metals form complexes due to the comparatively smaller sizes of the metal ions, their high ionic charges and the availability of d orbitals for bond formation. Transition metal ions do contain unpaired electrons. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26168,'chemistry',63,814,1784,'The oxyacid of sulphur that contains a lone pair of electrons on sulphur is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{8}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ has pyramidal structure due to a lone pair of electrons on sulphur atom.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26168_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26169,'chemistry',66,1034,2443,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Chlorine is an ortho-para directing group in electrophilic aromatic substitution reaction.</div><div class="column-right">Chlorine is an electron withdrawing group.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Chlorine is an electron withdrawing group, yet it acts as ortho, para-directing group in electrophilic aromatic substitution reaction due to its $$+\\mathrm{R}$$-effect.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26170,'chemistry',65,957,2228,'Match the column I with column II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A  Tetracarbonyl  nickel (0)<br>B  Pentacarbonly  iron (0)<br>C  Hexacarbonyl  chromium (0)</div><div class="column-right">P Octahedral<br>Q Trigonal  bipyramidal<br>R Tetrahedral</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A - P,   B - Q,   C - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - R,   B - Q,   C - P}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - Q,   B - R,   C - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - Q,   B - P,   C - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26171,'chemistry',64,192,1926,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{FeSO}_{4} \\cdot 7 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ is a green crystalline compound.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Due to atmospheric oxidation, these crystals acquire brown yellow colour.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathop {4{\\text{FeS}}{{\\text{O}}_4}}\\limits_{{\\text{(green)}}}  + 2{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{O}} + {{\\text{O}}_2} \\to \\mathop {4{\\text{Fe}}({\\text{OH}}).{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_4}}\\limits_{\\mathop {{\\text{Basic ferric sulphate}}}\\limits_{{\\text{(brownish yellow)}}} } $<br>Light green crystals of ferrous sulphate lose water molecule and turn brown on exposure to air. This is due to its oxidation to basic ferric sulphate. Both statements are true but not actual reason for the assertion. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26172,'chemistry',63,814,1784,'Which of the following compound has maximum $\\mathrm{S}-\\mathrm{S}$ linkages ?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{4} \\\\mathrm{O}_{6}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26172_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>It has \' $\\mathrm{S}-\\mathrm{S}$ \' linkages.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26174,'chemistry',65,958,2231,'According to crystal field theory, the M-L bond in a complex is','[{"text": "purely ionic bond", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "purely coordinate bond", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "purely covalent bond", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "partially covalent bond", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to crystal field theory, the bonding in a complex is purely electrostatic or ionic.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26175,'chemistry',64,192,1926,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Mohr salt is used as a primary standard in volumetric analysis.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Mohr salt contains both $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions in the crystalline salt.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Mohr salt $\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{2} \\cdot 6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ contains only $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ ions without any trace of $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions. Thus, a standard solution of $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ ions can be obtained directly by weighing a known amount of the Mohr salt. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26176,'chemistry',63,817,1805,'Select the periodic property which is not regular in halogen family on moving down the group?<br>(I) Ionization energy<br>(II) Electron gain enthalpy<br>(III) Size<br>(IV) Electronegativity','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III, and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Electron gain enthalpy shows an irregular trend in halogens. Chlorine has higher electron gain enthalpy than fluorine. The correct order is $\\mathrm{Cl}>\\mathrm{F}>\\mathrm{Br}>\\mathrm{I}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26177,'chemistry',66,1031,2433,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In presence of DMSO solvent, the rate of ${{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{N}}}{\\rm{2}}$ reaction increases.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> DMSO is a polar protic solvent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26178,'chemistry',65,956,2223,'Which of the following can participate in linkage isomerism?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\ddot{\\\\mathrm{NCH}_{2}} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\ddot{\\\\mathrm{N}} \\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$: \\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}$may be bonded to metal through nitrogen (nitro) or through oxygen (nitrito). Hence $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}$can participate in linkage isomerism.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26178_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26179,'chemistry',64,191,1916,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> On adding $\\mathrm{KI}$ to blue $\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}$ solution, the colour changes to dark brown.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> This is due to the formation of $\\mathrm{CuO}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The dark brown colour is due to the evolution of iodine.<br>$2 \\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}+4 \\mathrm{KI} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{CuI}+2 \\mathrm{~K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\underset{\\text { (dark brown) }}{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}$<br>So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26180,'chemistry',63,807,1746,'Match Column I with Column II and choose the correct combination from the options given.<br><div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - P,\\tB - Q,\\tC - R,\\tD - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q,\\tB - P,\\tC - S,\\t\\tD - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S,\\tB - Q,\\tC - R,\\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S,\\tB - R,\\tC - Q,\\tD - P", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{NO}, \\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ are colourless neutral gases.<br>$\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ is blue solid (acidic).<br>$\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ is brown gas (acidic).<br>$\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}$ is colourless solid (acidic).',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/63_26180_question_1.png'),
(26181,'chemistry',66,1031,2436,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When alkyl halide is reacted with $$\\mathrm{AgCN}$$ then alkyl isocyanide is formed.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $$\\mathrm{AgCN}$$ is a covalent compound and only the site of nitrogen is available for the reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26182,'chemistry',64,188,1904,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Promethium is a manmade element.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It is radioactive and has been prepared by artificial means.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Promethium, primarily produced in nuclear reactors due to its scarcity, is a manmade element discovered after technetium. Its artificial synthesis involves bombarding elements in reactors to obtain isotopes used in research, nuclear batteries, and specific industries. Thus, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26183,'chemistry',65,956,2223,'$\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}(\\mathrm{SCN})_{6}\\right]$ and $\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{2}(\\mathrm{SCN})_{4}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4}(\\mathrm{SCN})_{2}\\right]$ are the examples of what type of isomerism ?','[{"text": "Ionisation isomerism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Linkage isomerism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Coordination isomerism", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Solvate isomerism", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Here, in given quesiton, two coordination spheres in a compound are given which indicates that this is an example of coordination isomerism.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2015,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26185,'chemistry',66,1028,2419,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Thionyl chloride is perferred over Phosphorus chlorides (tri and penta) for the preparation of alkyl chlorides from alcohols.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Phosphours chlorides give pure alkyl halides.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Assertion is correct but Reason is false. Correct reason thionyl chloride gives pure alkyl halide as other two products $$\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{2}+\\mathrm{HCl}\\right)$$ are escapable gases.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26186,'chemistry',64,194,1937,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Most of the trivalent lanthanide ions are coloured both in the solid state and in aqueous solution.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The elements with ${\\rm{xf}}$ electrons have a similar colour to those of  ${\\rm{(14 - x) f}}$ electrons.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Lanthanide ions exhibit distinct colors owing to the partially filled f-orbitals within their electronic configurations. This unique characteristic allows for $\\mathrm{f}$-f transitions, leading to the absorption of specific wavelengths of light and the manifestation of various colors in these ions. As  f  subshell can have a maximum of 14 electrons,  xf  and (14-x) f, both will have the same number of unpaired electrons for transition .Hence,they both show same color. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26187,'chemistry',65,956,2224,'Square planar complex of the type MABXL (where A, B, X and L are unidentate ligands) shows following set of isomers','[{"text": "two cis and one trans", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "two trans and one cis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "two cis and two trans", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "three cis and one trans.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Square planar complex of type MABXL will show two-cis and one-transisomers  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26187_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  These above three isomers are formed by fixing the position of one ligand, A.<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26188,'chemistry',63,820,1814,'Match Column I with Column II and choose the correct combination from the options given.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A.${\\text{CI}}{{\\text{F}}_{\\text{3}}}$<br>B.${\\rm{I}}{{\\rm{F}}_7}$<br>C.${\\rm{CI}}{{\\rm{F}}_5}$<br>D.${\\rm{Br}}$</div><div class="column-right">P.Trigonal planar<br>Q.Bent T-shape<br>R.Pentagonal bipyramidal<br>S.Square pyramidal</div></div>','[{"text": "A - S,\\tB - R,\\tC - Q,\\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P,\\tB - R,\\tC - S,\\t\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q,\\tB - R,\\tC - S,\\t\\tD - P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Q,\\tB - S,\\tC - P,\\t\\tD - R", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{ClF}_{3}-\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}$ hybridization -2 1.P- Bent -T- shape <br>$\\mathrm{IF}_{7}-\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}^{3}$ hybridization -no 1.P. - Pentagonal bipyramidal.<br>$\\mathrm{ClF}_{5}-\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}^{2}$ hybridization - 1 1.P. - Square pyramidal<br>$\\mathrm{BF}_{3}-\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ hybridization - no 1.P. - Trigonal planar',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26190,'chemistry',64,194,1937,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Europium (II) is more stable than ceriurn (II).<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Cerium salts are used as a catalyst in petroleurn cracking.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The electronic configurations of europium (II) and cerium (II) are as follows: $\\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$ is $[\\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \\mathrm{f}^{7}$ and $\\mathrm{Ce}^{2+}$ is $[\\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \\mathrm{f}^{1} 5 \\mathrm{~d}^{1}$. In the case of $\\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$, the f-subshell is half-filled, rendering it particularly stable due to the half-filled electron configuration. Given reason is a correct statement but not an explanation to the assertion. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26191,'chemistry',65,956,2224,'Which of the following will be able to show geometrical isomerism?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{MA}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~B}_{2}$-Tetrahedral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "MABCD - Tetrahedral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{MA}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~B}$-Square planar", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "MABCD - Square planar", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Tetrahedral complexes do not show geometrical isomerism because due to symmetrical structure, relative positions of the ligands is same with respect to each other. Square planar complexes of the type $\\mathrm{MA}_{3} \\mathrm{~B}$ do not show geometrical isomerism because the possible spatial arrangements are equivalent.<br>MABCD type square planar complexes show three isomers which can be obtained by fixing the position of one ligand and placing any of the remaining three ligands at the trans position one by one.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26191_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26192,'chemistry',63,816,1796,'Which among the following factors is the most important in making fluorine the strongest oxidizing agent?','[{"text": "Electron affinity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ionization enthalpy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydration enthalpy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bond dissociation energy", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$F_{2}$ has the most negative $\\Delta {\\rm{G}}^\\circ $ value which is dependent on hydration enthalpy.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26193,'chemistry',66,1030,2428,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The boiling points of alkyl halides decrease in the order : $$\\mathrm{RI}>\\mathrm{RBr}>\\mathrm{RCl}>\\mathrm{RF}$$<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The boiling points of alkyl chlorides, bromides and iodides are considerably higher than that of the hydrocarbon of comparable molecular mass.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26194,'chemistry',64,195,1942,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Actinides are highly electropositive metals.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Actinides have same valency.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Actinides exhibit variable valency owing to the extremely small energy differences between their $5 \\mathrm{f}, 6 \\mathrm{~d}$, and $7 \\mathrm{~s}$ orbitals. This proximity in energy levels allows for facile changes in oxidation states, contributing to the wide range of valencies observed in these elements. Therefore, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26195,'chemistry',65,956,2224,'Which one of the following complexes is not expected to exhibit geometrical isomerism?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Pt}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{4}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{2}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Pt}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right]$ is tetrahedral.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26196,'chemistry',63,807,1745,'$\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ produces dark blue solution when reacts with $\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}$ solution, if $\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ is used in place of $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$, then','[{"text": "No reaction takes place.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dark blue solution is formed.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Light blue ppt is formed.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Black precipitate is formed.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{Cu}_{3} \\mathrm{P}_{2}$ (black ppt.) is formed.<br>${\\text{3CuS}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}} + 2{\\text{P}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}} \\to \\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{u}}_3}}\\limits_{{\\text{Black ppt }}} {{\\text{P}}_{\\text{2}}} \\downarrow  + {{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26197,'chemistry',66,1029,2424,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Aryl iodides can be prepared by reaction of arenes with iodine in the presence of an oxidising agent.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Oxidising agent oxidises $$\\mathrm{I}_{2}$$ into $$\\mathrm{HI}$$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Oxidising agent oxidises $$\\mathrm{HI}$$ to $$\\mathrm{I}_{2}$$',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26198,'chemistry',64,191,1918,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Cuprous ion $\\left(\\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\\right)$is colourless whereas cupric ion $\\left(\\mathrm{Cu}^{++}\\right)$is blue in aqueous solution.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Cuprous ion $\\left(\\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\\right)$has unpaired electrons while cupric ion $\\left(\\mathrm{Cu}^{++}\\right)$does not.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{Cu}^{+}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{10} \\\\& \\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{9}\\end{aligned}$<br>Cuprous ion $\\left(\\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\\right)$is colourless because it has no unpaired electrons in the subshell $3 \\mathrm{~d}$ as it has $3 \\mathrm{~d}^{10}$ configuration. Cupric ion $\\left(\\mathrm{Cu}^{++}\\right)$has one unpaired electron as it has<br>$3 \\mathrm{~d}^{9}$ configuration. By absorbing the visible light, the unpaired electron can migrate from one set of d-orbitals to another. Hence, cupric ion  $\\left(\\mathrm{Cu}^{++}\\right)$in aqueous solution shows blue colour. So option (3) is correct.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26199,'chemistry',63,810,1763,'Read Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct option given below.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Although, $\\mathrm{PF}_{5}, \\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ and $\\mathrm{PBr}_{5}$ are known, the pentahalides of nitrogen have not been observed.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Phosphorus has lower electronegativity than nitrogen.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Maximum covalency of nitrogen is 4 due to non - availability of vacant d - orbitals, so it can not form pentahalides.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26200,'chemistry',65,956,2225,'Which of the following will give a pair of enantiomers?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Pt}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cr}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\mathrm{en})_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26200_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    Stability of Co-ordination Compounds',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46926,'physics',42,323,592,'Which circuit will not show current in ammeter?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'This circuit is reverse bias.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26201,'chemistry',66,1031,2436,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> KCN reacts with methyl chloride to give methyl isocyanide<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $$\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$$is an ambident nucleophile.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'More is the steric hindrance at the carbon bearing the halogen, lesser is the ${{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{N}}}^{\\rm{2}}$ reactivity.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26202,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Solution of $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}$ in water is intensely coloured.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Oxidation state of $\\mathrm{Cr}$ in $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}$ is $+\\mathrm{VI}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}$ solution in water is yellow in colour, when acidified it changes to orange colour (due to the formation of $\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}{ }^{2-}$ ions).<br>$2 \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\rightarrow$<br>$\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}+\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>Let oxidation state of $\\mathrm{Cr}$ in $\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$ be x.<br>$\\Rightarrow 2(+1)+\\mathrm{x}+4(-2)=0 \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x}=+6$.<br>So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26203,'chemistry',63,817,1805,'With respect to halogens, four statements are given below:<br>(I) The bond dissociation energies for halogens are in the order: $\\mathrm{I}_{2} < \\mathrm{F}_{2} < \\mathrm{Br}_{2} < \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$.<br>(II) The only oxidation state is –1 .<br>(III) The amount of energy required for the excitation of electrons to first excited state decreases progressively as we move from F to I.<br>(IV)They form $\\mathrm{HX}_{2}{ }^{-}$species in their aqueous solutions $(x=$ halogen).<br>The correct statement are','[{"text": "I, II, and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III, and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Only I and III are correct.<br>$\\mathrm{Cl}, \\mathrm{Br}$, I show positive oxidation states.<br>$\\mathrm{HX}_{2}^{-}$ is formed due to hydrogen bond, only by fluorine. So, II, IV statements are incorrect.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26204,'chemistry',65,960,2240,'What is the effective atomic number of $\\mathrm{Zn}$ in $\\left[\\mathrm{Zn}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4}\\right] \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ ?','[{"text": "30", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "27", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "28", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "36", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\left[\\mathrm{Zn}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4}\\right] \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$<br>EAN $=$ [atomic number of metal atom - oxidation number of metal atom) $+2 \\times$ number of ligands $\\left[\\mathrm{Zn}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right]$ can be written as $\\left[\\mathrm{Zn}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4}\\right]^{2+} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}\\text { EAN } & =[30-(+2)+(2 \\times 4)] \\\\& =[28+8] \\\\& =36\\end{aligned}$$<br>Thus. EAN of $\\mathrm{Zn}$ in $\\left[\\mathrm{Zn}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4}\\right] \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ is 36 .<br>',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26205,'chemistry',66,1034,2442,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Presence of a nitro group at ortho or para position increases the reactivity of haloarenes towards nucleophilic substitution.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Nitro group, being an electron withdrawing group decreases the electron density over the benzene ring.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26206,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ is used as primary standard in volumetric analysis.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It has a good solubility in water.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ is less soluble in water .<br>$\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ can be found in pure state and can be accurately weighed. It is not hygroscopic in nature. So, $\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ is used as a primary standard solution. Thus, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26207,'chemistry',63,818,1807,'Concentrated hydrochloric acid when kept in open air sometimes produces a cloud of white fumes. The explanation for it is that','[{"text": "Concentrated hydrochloric acid emits strongly smelling HCl gas all the time", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxygen in air reacts with the emitted HCl gas to form a cloud of chlorine gas", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Strong affinity of HCl gas for moisture in air results in forming of droplets of liquid solution which appears like a cloudy smoke", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Due to strong affinity for water, concentrated hydrochloric acid pulls moisture of air towards itself. This moisture forms droplets of water and hence the cloud.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$4 \\mathrm{HCl}+\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>Appearance of cloud is due to formation of chlorine.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26208,'chemistry',65,960,2240,'The effective atomic number of $\\operatorname{Iron}(Z=26)$ in $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]^{-3}$ is','[{"text": "36", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "33", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "35", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "34", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In the given complex ion $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]^{3-}$, the $\\mathrm{Fe}$ is in +3 oxidation state. As we know that atomic number of iron is 26 . The number of electron in $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ is 23 . Each of the six cyanide molecules donates a pair of electrons so that EAN becomes $23+2 \\times 6 \\Rightarrow 35$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26209,'chemistry',66,1034,2443,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Nitration of chlorobenzene leads to the formation of m-nitrochlorobenzene<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $$\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$$ group is a m-directing group.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Reactivity decreases as the number of $$-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$$ group w.r.t. $$-\\mathrm{Br}$$ decreases.<br>m - Nitrobromobenzene is, however, less reactive than the ${\\rm{p}}$-Nitrobromobenzene since the $$-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$$ group at the m-position cannot stabilise the intermediate carbanion by resonance.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26210,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Change in colour of acidic solution of potassium dichromate by breath is used to test drunk drivers.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Change in colour is due to the complexation of alcohol with potassium dichromate.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Change in colour is due to the oxidation of alcohol with potassium dichromate, $\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ in the presence of dilute $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$. It is converted into chromic sulphate which is green in colour.<br>$\\mathop {\\mathop {{{\\text{K}}_2}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{r}}_2}{{\\text{O}}_7}}\\limits_{{\\text{ (orange) }}}  + 4{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_4}}\\limits_{}  \\to {{\\text{K}}_2}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_4}{\\text{ }}$<br>$ + \\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{r}}_2}{{\\left( {{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_4}} \\right)}_3}}\\limits_{{\\text{ (green) }}}  + 4{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{O}} + \\frac{3}{2}{{\\text{O}}_2}$<br>So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26211,'chemistry',63,806,1738,'Which of the following pair behaves as strong and weak base hydride respectively? <br>$\\mathrm{NH}_{3}, \\mathrm{PH}_{3}, \\mathrm{AsH}_{3}, \\mathrm{SbH}_{3}, \\mathrm{BiH}_{3}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}, \\\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}, \\\\mathrm{BiH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PH}_{3}, \\\\mathrm{BiH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BiH}_{3}, \\\\mathrm{AsH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The basic strength of given hydrides decreases in the order as follows :$\\mathrm{NH}_{3}>\\mathrm{PH}_{3}>\\mathrm{AsH}_{3}>\\mathrm{SbH}_{3}>\\mathrm{BiH}_{3}$This is due to the decrease in the availability of the lone pair of electrons which depends on the size of central atom that increases from $\\mathrm{N}$ to $\\mathrm{Bi}$. Therefore, $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{BiH}_{3}$ acts as a strong and weak base hydride respectively.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26212,'chemistry',66,1029,2424,'Among the following, the reaction that proceeds through an electrophilic substitution, is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26212_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26212_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26213,'chemistry',65,962,2242,'Match the column I with column II  and choose the correct option<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A Chlorophyll<br>B  Blood pigment<br>C  Wilkinson catalyst<br>D Vitamin B $_{12}$</div><div class="column-right">P Rhodium<br>Q Cobalt<br>R Calcium<br>S Iron<br>T Magnesium</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A - T,   B - S,   C - P,   D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - S,   B - Q,   C - P,   D - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - P,   B - Q,   C - R,   D - S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - R,   B - T,   C - P,   D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26214,'chemistry',64,191,1916,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Copper sulphate solution is not stored in zinc vessel.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Zinc forms complex with $\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Electropositive element like zinc precipitates copper from a solution of copper sulphate and finally forms a complex.<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4(\\mathrm{aq})} & +\\mathrm{Zn} \\rightarrow \\\\\\mathrm{Cu} & +\\mathrm{ZnSO}_{4(\\mathrm{aq})} \\text { or }\\left[\\mathrm{Zn}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{4}\\right] \\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\end{aligned}$<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26215,'chemistry',63,808,1757,'Which of the following statements is not valid for oxoacids of phosphorus?','[{"text": "Orthophosphoric acid is used in the manufacture of triple super phosphate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hypophosphorous acid is a diprotic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All oxoacids contain tetrahedral four coordinated phosphorus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All oxo-acids contain at least one $\\\\mathrm{P}=\\\\mathrm{O}$ unit and one P-OH group", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Hypophosphorous acid $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{2}$ has the following structure. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26215_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  As, it contains only one replaceable $\\mathrm{H}$-atom (that is attached with $\\mathrm{O}$, not with $\\mathrm{P}$ directly) so it is a monoprotic acid.All other given statements are true.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26216,'chemistry',66,1032,2440,'Elimination reaction of 2-Bromo-pentane to form pent-2-ene is:<br>(a) $$\\beta$$-Elimination reaction<br>(b) Follows Zaitsev rule<br>(c) Dehydrohalogenation reaction<br>(d) Dehydration reaction','[{"text": "(a), (c), (d)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(b), (c), (d)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(a), (b), (d)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(a), (b), (c)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26216_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Sec.alkylhaide<br>This reaction is an example of $$\\beta$$-elimination<br>Hydrogen is removed from $$\\beta$$-carbon and halogen from $$\\alpha$$-carbon, hence dehydrohalgoenation reaction. Generally in $$\\mathrm{E}_{2}$$ reaction Zaitsev alkene is formed as major product (more stable alkene).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26217,'chemistry',65,952,2211,'How many ions per molecule are produced in the solution when Mohr\'s salt is dissolved in excess of water?','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\begin{gathered}\n  \\mathop {{\\text{FeS}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Mohr\'ssalt}}} {\\text{.}}{\\left( {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}} \\right)_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{.6}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{Excess}}\\,\\,{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}} \\hfill \\\\\n  {\\text{F}}{{\\text{e}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}}{\\text{ + 2NH}}_{\\text{4}}^{\\text{ + }}{\\text{ + 2SO}}_{\\text{4}}^{{\\text{2 - }}}{\\text{ + 6}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}} \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26218,'chemistry',64,194,1937,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Magnetic moment of Dy is the highest among the lanthanoids.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Orbital motion contributes magnetic moment.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Dysprosium (Dy) exhibits the highest magnetic moment among lanthanoids due to its configuration with seven unpaired electrons in the $4 \\mathrm{f}$ orbitals, contributing to significant magnetic properties. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26219,'chemistry',63,807,1745,'Ammonia, on reaction with excess of chlorine, gives','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NCl}_{3}$ and $\\\\mathrm{HCl}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}_{4}$ and $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NCl}_{3}$ and $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\\\\mathrm{HCl}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}} + \\mathop {{\\rm{3C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{Excess}}}  \\to \\mathop {{\\rm{NC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{Explosive}}}  + 3{\\rm{HCl}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26220,'chemistry',66,1027,2418,'The correct sequence of bond enthalpy of \' $$\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{X}$$ \' bond is :','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{F}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{Cl}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{Br}$$ $$>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{I}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{F} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{Cl}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{Br}$$ $$>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{I}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{Cl}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{F}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{Br}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$$ $$-\\\\mathrm{I}^{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{F} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{Cl} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{Br}$$ $$ < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{I}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathop {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ - F}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{485}}} {\\rm{ > }}\\mathop {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{328}}} {\\rm{ - Cl > }}\\mathop {\\mathop {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}}\\limits_{\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{276}}} }\\limits_{\\,\\,} {\\rm{ - Br > }}\\mathop {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ - I}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{240\\,kJ/mol}}} $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26221,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'The secondary valency of platinum in tetrammine dichloroplatinum(IV) chloride is','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Secondary valency corresponds to the coordination number of the central metal atom or ion. Coordination number of $\\mathrm{Pt}$ in $\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}$ is 6 .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26222,'chemistry',64,191,1922,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Transition metals are poor reducing agents.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Transition metals form numerous alloys with other metals.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In actual practice, transition metals react with acids very slowly and act as poor reducing agents. This is due to the protection of metal as a result of formation of thin oxide protective film. Further, their poor tendency as reducing agent is due to high ionisation energy, high heat of vapourisation and low heat of hydration. Transition metals form alloys due to similar size. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26223,'chemistry',63,808,1757,'The maximum number of $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{H}$ bonds are contained in which of the following molecules?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{PO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{PO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{4} \\\\mathrm{P}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The structures of the given oxyacids of phosphorus are as follows : <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26223_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26223_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Thus, $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{2}$ contains the maximum number of $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{H}$ bonds, i.e. 2 .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26224,'chemistry',66,1031,2435,'The incorrect statement regarding chirality is:','[{"text": "The product obtained by ${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{N}}}1$ reaction of haloalkane having chirality at the reactive site shows inversion of configuration,", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Enantiomers are superimposable mirror images of each other.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A racemic mixture shows zero optical rotation.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{N}}}1$ reaction yields $$1: 1$$ mixture of both enantiomers.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Enantiomers are non-superimposable mirror images of each other.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26225,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'Addition of excess of $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ to an aqueous solution of 1 mole of $\\mathrm{PdCl}_{2} \\cdot 4 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ gives 2 moles of $\\mathrm{AgCl}$. The conductivity of this solution corresponds to','[{"text": "$1: 1$ electrolyte", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 3$ electrolyte", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 2$ electrolyte", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 4$ electrolyte", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& {\\left[\\mathrm{Pd}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3} \\rightarrow\\left[\\mathrm{Pd}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4}\\right]^{+2}} \\\\& +2 \\mathrm{AgCl}\\end{aligned}$$<br>So 1:2 ratio of electrolyte responsible for conductance.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26226,'chemistry',63,808,1757,'For ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{P}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}$, the correct choice is','[{"text": "dibasic and reducing", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "dibasic and non-reducing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "tribasic and reducing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "tribasic and non-reducing", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26226_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{3}$ is dibasic because it has two $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{OH}$ bonds and reducing due to the presence of one $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26227,'chemistry',64,195,1941,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The degree of complex formation in actinides decreases in the order<br>$\\mathrm{M}_{4}^{+}>\\mathrm{MO}_{2}^{2+}>\\mathrm{M}^{3+}>\\mathrm{MO}_{2}^{+}$<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Actinides form complexes with $\\pi$ bonding ligands such as alkyl phosphines and thioethers.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Higher the charge on the metal ion, smaller is the ionic size and more is the complex forming ability. Thus, the degree of complex formation decreases in the order:<br>$\\mathrm{M}^{4+}>\\mathrm{MO}_{2}^{2+}>\\mathrm{M}^{3+}>\\mathrm{MO}_{2}^{+}$<br>So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26228,'chemistry',66,1029,2424,'Which of the following is suitable to synthesize chlorobenzene?','[{"text": "Phenol, $$\\\\mathrm{NaNO}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{HCl}, \\\\mathrm{CuCl}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Benzene, $$\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$$, anhydrous $$\\\\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$$<br>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26228_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26229,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'Match the column I with column II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A $\\mathrm{CoCl}_{3} \\cdot 6 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ <br>B  $\\mathrm{CoCl}_{3} \\cdot 5 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$<br>C $\\mathrm{CoCl}_{3} \\cdot 4 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$<br>D $\\mathrm{CoCl}_{3} \\cdot 3 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$</div><div class="column-right">P Violet <br>Q  Green<br>R  Purple<br>S Yellow</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A - P,   B - Q,   C - R,   D - S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - Q,   B - P,   C - S,   D - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - R,   B - P,   C - S,   D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - S,   B - R,   C - Q,   D - P}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\text{A - S,}}\\,\\,\\,{\\text{B - R,}}\\,\\,\\,{\\text{C - Q,}}\\,\\,\\,{\\text{D - P}}$',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26230,'chemistry',63,807,1744,'By which of the following processes, pure nitrogen gas is prepared?','[{"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7} \\\\xrightarrow{\\\\Delta}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}+\\\\mathrm{NaNO}_{2} \\\\xrightarrow{\\\\Delta}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ba}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{N}_{3}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\xrightarrow{\\\\Delta}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}+\\\\mathrm{Cu} \\\\xrightarrow{\\\\Delta}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Very pure nitrogen can be obtained by the thermal decomposition of barium azide as. <br>$\\mathrm{Ba}\\left(\\mathrm{N}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Ba}+3 \\mathrm{~N}_{2}$<br>In other reactions, impurities are also obtained. Hence, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26231,'chemistry',64,191,1914,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Reduction potential of ${\\text{Mn( - 3}}\\,\\,{\\text{to 2)}}$  is more positive than ${\\text{Fe( + 3}}\\,\\,{\\text{to + 2)}}{\\text{.}}$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Ionisation potential of $\\mathrm{Mn}$ is more than that of $\\mathrm{Fe}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}=[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5}, \\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}=[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{4}$<br>$\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}=[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{6}, \\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}=[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5}$<br>Thus, $\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ has more stable configuration than $\\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}$ while $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ has more stable configuration than $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$. Hence, reduction potential for $\\mathrm{Mn}^{3+} / \\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ couple is more positive than $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+} / \\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$.<br>As we move across the period, ionisation potential increases, thus, ionisation potential of $\\mathrm{Fe}(762 \\mathrm{KJ} / \\mathrm{mol})$ is more than that of $\\mathrm{Mn}(717$ $\\mathrm{KJ} / \\mathrm{mol}$ ). So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26232,'chemistry',66,1028,2421,'Which reaction is useful in exchange of halogen in alkyl chloride by iodide?','[{"text": "Wurtz reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Finkelstein reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Reimer-Tiemann reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Williamson synthesis", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The reaction is useful in exchange of halogen in alkyl chloride by iodide is Finkelstein reaction. This reaction is used in preparation of alkyl iodide by the reaction of alkyl chloride, bromide with $$\\mathrm{Nal}$$ in dry acetone.<br>e.g.<br>${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{Br  +  Nal}}$ $\\xrightarrow[\\Delta ]{{{\\text{Acetone}}}}$ ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{l  +  NaBr}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26233,'chemistry',65,954,2217,'A complex compound in which the oxidation number of a metal is zero is','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}(\\\\mathrm{CO})_{4}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Pt}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{4}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{4}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{3}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{CO}}$  is a neutral ligand, hence it has zero oxidation state in the complex, $\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}(\\mathrm{CO})_{4}\\right]$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26249,'chemistry',66,1035,2445,'In the reaction, ${\\text{2R - X}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{Dry ether }}}]{{{\\text{2Na}}}}\\mathop {{\\text{product }}}\\limits_{\\text{ }} $<br>The product obtained is','[{"text": "2n-alkene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "n-sodium halide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "n-alcohol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "n-alkane", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\text{2nR  -  X}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{Dryether}}}]{{{\\text{2Na}}}}\\mathop {{\\text{R  -  R}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Alkane}}} {\\text{  +  2NaX}}$<br>The above given reaction is known as Wurtz reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2015,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26234,'chemistry',63,808,1757,'Sodium pyrophosphate is represented by which of the following formula?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{P}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{4} \\\\mathrm{P}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{4} \\\\mathrm{P}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{P}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{5}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Sodium pyrophosphate is represented by $\\mathrm{Na}_{4} \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$. It is sodium salt of pyrophosphoric acid $\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{4} \\mathrm{P}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}\\right)$, which may be considered to be made up by two molecules of ortho phosphoric acid $\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}\\right)$, eliminating one molecule of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26234_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26234_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26235,'chemistry',64,194,1937,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ is used as an oxidising agent in volumetric analysis.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ has the tendeney of attaining+3 oxidation state.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The element which can reduce itself acts as an oxidising agent. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26236,'chemistry',66,1028,2419,'Which among the following methods is not suitable for the preparation of alkyl chlorides?','[{"text": "Addition of $$\\\\mathrm{HCl}$$ to alkene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Treating alcohols with Lucas reagent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "By heating alcohols with thionyl chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chlorination of alkanes in presence of sunlight", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Chlorination of alkanes in presence of sunlight is not suitable for the preparation of alkyl chloride because chlorination of alkanes gives a complex mixture of isomeric mono and polyhaloalkanes, which is difficult to separate as pure compounds. Consequently, the yield of any one compound is low.<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{r}}{{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{/UVlight}}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{Cl}}} \\\\ {{\\text{ + C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{CHClC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}} \\end{array}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26237,'chemistry',65,954,2218,'The coordination number and the oxidation state of the element \' $\\mathrm{M}$ \' in the complex<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{M}(\\text { en })_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)\\right] \\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ (where, en is ethane-<br>1,2-diamine) are respectively','[{"text": "6 and 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4 and 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6 and 3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4 and 3", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let the oxidation state of metal ${\\text{ M \' be x}}$.<br>For complex, $\\left[\\mathrm{M}(\\text { en })_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)\\right] \\mathrm{NO}_{2}$<br>$$\\mathrm{x}+0-2-1=0 \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x}=+3$$<br>Also, ethylenediamine and $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}$ both are bidentate ligands. Thus, coordination number will be \'6\'. <br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26238,'chemistry',63,808,1757,'As the number of $-\\mathrm{OH}$ groups increases in hypophosphorus acid, phosphorus acid and phosphoric acid, the acidic strength','[{"text": "increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "remain nearly same", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "remain appropriately same", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{2}$ has $1-\\mathrm{OH}$ group, $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{3}$ has $2-\\mathrm{OH}$ groups and $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ has 3 $\\mathrm{OH}$ groups. In these acid although the number of $-\\mathrm{OH}$ groups (ionisable hydrogen) increases, yet the acidity does not increase very much.This is due to the fact that the number of unprotonated oxygen, responsible for the enhancement of acidity due to inductive effect, remains the same with the result dissociation constant also remain nearly same.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26239,'chemistry',64,194,1937,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{La}(\\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ is more basic than $\\mathrm{Lu}(\\mathrm{OH})_{3}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Size of $\\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions increases along the lanthanoid series and shows less covalent character','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Due to lanthanide contraction, the size of $\\mathrm{Lu}^{3+}$ is decreased, then, covalent character of hydroxides increases and hence the basic strength decreases. Thus, $\\mathrm{La}(\\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ is more ionic and thus, more basic. Thus, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26250,'chemistry',63,814,1788,'If the supply of oxygen is limited, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ reacts with $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to form','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}+\\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}+\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}+\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}+\\\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In limited supply of oxygen, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ burns with blue flame and $\\mathrm{S}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ are the main products<br>$2{{\\rm{H}}_2}\\,{\\rm{S}} + {{\\rm{O}}_2} \\to 2{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}} + 2\\,{\\rm{S}}$<br>Note  In excess of oxygen, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ gives $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ and water as main product.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26260,'chemistry',65,954,2214,'Which among the following is an ambidentate ligand?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\ddot{\\\\mathrm{N}} \\\\mathrm{H}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}$is an ambidentate ligand. Ligand, which has two different donor atoms and either of the two ligands in the complex is called ambidentate ligand.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26260_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}$ion can coordinate either through nitrogen or through oxygen to a central metal atom/ion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26240,'chemistry',66,1028,2419,'Match the column I with column II and mark the appropriate choice.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. $\\begin{gathered}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\left( {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right)_{\\text{3}}} \\hfill \\\\{\\text{OH}}\\xrightarrow[{{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}},\\Delta }]{{{\\text{NaBr}}}} \\hfill \\\\ \\end{gathered} $<br>B. ${\\left( {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}} \\right)_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COH}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{room}}\\,{\\text{temp}}{\\text{.}}}]{{{\\text{Conc}}{\\text{.}}\\,{\\text{HCl}}}}$<br>C. $\\begin{gathered}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CH}}\\left( {{\\text{OH}}} \\right){\\left( {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right)_{\\text{2}}} \\hfill \\\\{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{PB}}{{\\text{r}}_{\\text{3}}}}} \\hfill \\\\ \\end{gathered} $<br>D. $\\begin{gathered}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}} \\hfill \\\\{\\text{OH}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{SOC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}}} \\hfill \\\\ \\end{gathered} $</div><div class="column-right">P. $\\begin{array}{l}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{CH}}\\left( {{\\rm{Br}}} \\right)\\\\{\\left( {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\end{array}$<br>Q. ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{Cl}}$<br>R. ${\\left( {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{CCl}}$<br>S. ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\left( {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right)_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{Br}}$</div></div>','[{"text": "A - S, B - R, C - P, D - Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - S, B - R, C - Q, D - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - S, C - P, D - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - S, C - Q, D - P", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26241,'chemistry',65,954,2218,'The correct charge and co-ordination number of \' $\\mathrm{Fe}$ \' in $\\mathrm{K}_{3}\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]$ is','[{"text": "$+2,4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$+3,6$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$+2,6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$+3,3$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Coordination number of $\\mathrm{Fe}$ in $\\mathrm{K}_{3}\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]$ is 6 because it is bonded with six $\\mathrm{CN}$ ligands. Let $\\mathrm{x}$ be the oxidation state of $\\mathrm{Fe}$<br>$$\\begin{array}{ll}\\therefore & \\mathrm{x}+6(-1)=-3 \\\\\\therefore & \\mathrm{x}=+3\\end{array}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26242,'chemistry',63,807,1747,'Iron is dropped in dil. $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$, it gives','[{"text": "ferric nitrate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ferric nitrate and $\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ferrous nitrate and ammonium nitrate", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ferrous nitrate and nitric oxide", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26242_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26243,'chemistry',64,192,1923,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The correct order of oxidising power is :<br>$\\mathrm{VO}_{2}^{+} < \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-} < \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The oxidation state of $\\mathrm{Mn}$ is +7 .','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The oxidation states of the given compounds are<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{VO}_{2}^{+} ; \\mathrm{x}+2(-2)=+1 \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x}=+5 \\\\& \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2}: 2 \\mathrm{x}+7(-2)=-2 \\\\& \\Rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{x}=+12 \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{x}=+6 \\\\& \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}: \\mathrm{x}+4(-2)=-1 \\\\& \\mathrm{x}=-1+8=+7\\end{aligned}$<br>The correct order of oxidising power is :<br>$\\mathrm{VO}_{2}^{+} < \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{-2} < \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}$<br>So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26244,'chemistry',66,1026,2417,'How many chiral carbon atoms are present in 2,3,4-trichloropentane?','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26244_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Two chiral carbon atoms are present in the given compound.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26245,'chemistry',65,954,2214,'Which of the following is a negatively charged bidentate ligand?','[{"text": "Dimethyl glyoximato", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cyano", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethylene diammine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acetato", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26245_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26246,'chemistry',64,196,1943,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Chemistry of actinoids is more complicated than lanthanoids.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Actinoid elements are radioactive.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Chemistry of actinoids is more complicated due to the possibility of large number of oxidation states. Actinoid elements are radioactive, but it is not the explanation for assertion. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26247,'chemistry',63,807,1747,'When tin is treated with concentrated nitric acid','[{"text": "it is converted into stannous nitrate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "it is converted into stannic nitrate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "it is converted into metastannic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "it becomes passive", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26247_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26248,'chemistry',66,1026,2417,'The number of optical isomers of the compound,<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}(\\mathrm{Br})-\\mathrm{COOH}$ is','[{"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Number of optical isomers $=2^{\\mathrm{n}}$ (when the molecule has no symmetry).<br>Where, ${\\rm{n = }}$ no.of chiral carbon atoms.<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3} - \\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits^* {\\text{H}}({\\text{Br}}) - {\\text{COOH}}$<br>No.of chiral carbons $=1$<br>$\\therefore$ No. of optical isomers are $=2^{\\mathrm{n}}=2^{1}=2$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26251,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'$\\mathrm{NaCl}$ reacts with conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and $\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$ to give reddish fumes (B), which react with<br>$\\mathrm{NaOH}$ to give yellow solution (C) . (B) and<br>(C) respectively are :','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}, \\\\mathrm{CrO}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{KHSO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Chloride ion $\\left(\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\\right)$is confirmed by chromyl chloride test, the reactions of which are as follows :<br>$4 \\mathrm{NaCl}(\\mathrm{s})+\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}(\\mathrm{~s})+6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ (conc.)<br>$\\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{CrO}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{NaHSO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{KHSO}_{4}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ (Reddish brown)<br>$\\mathrm{CrO}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+4 \\mathrm{NaOH} \\rightarrow \\underset{(\\text { Yellow) }}{\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{NaCl}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(26252,'chemistry',65,954,2214,'Identify the tetradentate ligand from the following.','[{"text": "Triethylene tetramine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ethylene diamine tetraacetate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dimethyl glyoximato", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxalato", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Triethylene tetramine is a tetradentate ligand with the formula $\\left[\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{NHCH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}\\right]_{2}$. This bind with four donor atoms to a central atom to form a coordination complex. This number of donor atoms that bind is called denticity and is a way to classify ligands<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26252_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Triethylene tetramine (trien)<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26253,'chemistry',66,1031,2436,'Nitroalkanes are obtained in laboratory from primary or secondary alkyl halides by the action of','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{AgNO}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{NaNO}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Nitroalkanes are obtained in laboratory from primary or secondary alkyl halides by the action of silver nitrite $$\\left(\\mathrm{AgNO}_{2}\\right)$$.<br>This method of preparation is an example of nucleophilic substitution reaction. The reaction is as follow;<br>${\\rm{Br}}\\,{\\rm{/}}\\,{\\rm{Rl}}\\, + $ $\\mathop {{\\rm{Ag}}}\\limits_{\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}{{\\rm{Alkyl}}}\\\\{{\\rm{halide}}}\\end{array}} {\\mkern 1mu} $ $ - \\,{\\rm{O}}\\, - {\\rm{N}}\\, = \\,{\\rm{O}}$ $\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{Alcohol}}}}$<br>${\\text{R - }}\\mathop {{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ + AgBr}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Nitroalkane}}} \\mathop {{\\text{/Agl}}}\\limits_{\\text{ }} $<br>( $$\\mathrm{R}$$ can be $$1^{\\circ}$$ or $$2^{\\circ}$$ )',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26254,'chemistry',64,191,1917,'Which of the following electronic configuration would be associated with the highest magnetic moment?','[{"text": "$[A r] 3 \\\\mathrm{~d}^{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$[\\\\operatorname{Ar}] 3 \\\\mathrm{~d}^{6}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$[\\\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\\\mathrm{~d}^{8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$[\\\\operatorname{Ar}] 3 \\\\mathrm{~d}^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/64_26254_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Magnetic moment $(\\mu)=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{n}(\\mathrm{n}+2)}$<br>where \' $n$ \' is no. of unpaired $e^{-}$.<br>More are the no. of unpaired electrons, more is the magnetic moment (spin only)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26255,'chemistry',63,814,1782,'Bromine water reacts with $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ to form','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HBr}$ and $\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$ and $\\\\mathrm{HBr}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}$ and $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and $\\\\mathrm{HBr}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When bromine water reacts with $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$, it oxidises $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ to sulphuric acid and itself gets reduced to $\\mathrm{HBr}$. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26255_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26256,'chemistry',65,954,2214,'What is the number of $=\\mathrm{N}-\\mathrm{OH}$ groups present in dimethyl glyoximato?','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{Two}=\\mathrm{N}-\\mathrm{OH}$ groups present in dimethyl glyoximato.diagramDimethyl glyoximato is a bidentate ligand. It is commonly known as DMG. Chemical formula is $\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{8} \\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26257,'chemistry',66,1031,2436,'Match the alkyl halide reacting with reagent given in Column I with their respective substituted product given in Column II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. ${\\rm{KCN}}$<br>B. ${\\rm{LiAl}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}$<br>C. ${\\rm{KN}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>D. ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$</div><div class="column-right">P. Primary amine $\\left( {{\\rm{RN}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}} \\right)$<br>Q. Nitrile $\\left( {{\\rm{RCN}}} \\right)$<br>R. Alkane $\\left( {{\\rm{RH}}} \\right)$<br>S. Alkyl nitrite $\\left( {{\\rm{RONO}}} \\right)$</div></div>','[{"text": "A - S, B - R, C - Q, D - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - Q, C - P, D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q, B - R, C - S, D - P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - R, B - S, C - Q, D - P", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26258,'chemistry',63,814,1784,'Caro\'s acid is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{5}$ peroxomonosulphuric acid is known as Caro\'s acid.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26259,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'Yellow compound of lead chromate gets dissolved on treatment with hot $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ solution. The product of lead formed is a :','[{"text": "Tetraanionic complex with coordination number six.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neutral complex with coordination number four.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dianionic complex with coordination number six.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dianionic complex with coordination number four.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Yellow ppt. of $\\mathrm{PbCrO}_{4} \\downarrow$ is soluble in hot $\\mathrm{NaOH}$, forming $\\left[\\mathrm{Pb}(\\mathrm{OH})_{4}\\right]^{2-}$, as $\\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ is amphoteric in nature. $\\left[\\mathrm{Pb}(\\mathrm{OH})_{4}\\right]^{2-}$ is divalent with coordination number 4 .',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26261,'chemistry',66,1035,2445,'An alkyl bromide ${\\rm{X}}$ reacts with sodium in ether to form 4, 5-diethyloctane, the compound \' $$\\mathrm{X}$$ \' is','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{Br}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{5} \\\\mathrm{Br}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}(\\\\mathrm{Br}) \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}(\\\\mathrm{Br})-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The given reaction is an example of Wurtz reaction. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26261_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26262,'chemistry',63,814,1785,'Which of the following salt would give $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ with hot and dil. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and also decolourises $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ water?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NaHSO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~S}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ reacts with hot and diluted $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ to give $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ gas which decolourises bromine water.<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{3}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\longrightarrow & \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\\\& +\\mathrm{SO}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\\\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\longrightarrow & 2 \\mathrm{HBr}+[\\mathrm{O}]\\end{aligned}$<br>Decolourisation of bromine water.<br>$\\mathrm{SO}_{2}+[\\mathrm{O}] \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{SO}_{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26263,'chemistry',64,188,1906,'In which option all have $\\mathrm{d}^{10}$ configuration in their ground state?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu}, \\\\mathrm{Zn}, \\\\mathrm{Cd}, \\\\mathrm{Ag}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cd}, \\\\mathrm{Au}, \\\\mathrm{Hg}, \\\\mathrm{Ni}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Sc}, \\\\mathrm{Ti}, \\\\mathrm{Fe}, \\\\mathrm{Zn}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}, \\\\mathrm{Cr}, \\\\mathrm{Co}, \\\\mathrm{Ni}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{Cu}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{10} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{1}$<br>$\\mathrm{Zn}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{10} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}$ $\\mathrm{Cd}:[\\mathrm{Kr}] 4 \\mathrm{~d}^{10} 5 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}$<br>$\\mathrm{Ag}:[\\mathrm{Kr}] 4 \\mathrm{~d}^{10} 5 \\mathrm{~s}^{1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
