(26266,'chemistry',63,817,1799,'The increasing order of reducing power of halogen acid is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCl} < \\\\mathrm{HBr} < \\\\mathrm{HF} < \\\\mathrm{HI}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HF} < \\\\mathrm{HCl} < \\\\mathrm{HBr} < \\\\mathrm{HI}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HI} < \\\\mathrm{HBr} < \\\\mathrm{HCl} < \\\\mathrm{HF}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HBr} < \\\\mathrm{HCl} < \\\\mathrm{HF} < \\\\mathrm{HI}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The increasing order of reducing power of halogen acid is<br>$\\mathrm{HF} < \\mathrm{HCl} < \\mathrm{HBr} < \\mathrm{HI}$<br>The reason is that, by increasing the size of halogen atom, the bond strength of $\\mathrm{H}-X$ decreases. Thus, the ease of hydrogen donation increases and hence, reducing power increases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
