(26288,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'Which of the following complexes exhibits the highest paramagnetic behaviour?<br>where gly $=$ glycine, en $=$ ethlyenediamine and bipy $=$ bipyridyl (At. no. $\\mathrm{Ti}=22, \\mathrm{~V}=23, \\mathrm{Fe}=$ $26, \\mathrm{Co}=27$ )','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ti}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{V}(\\\\mathrm{gly})_{2}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{2}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2}\\\\right]^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}(\\\\text { en })(\\\\text { bipy })\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\mathrm{ox})_{2}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Greater is the number of unpaired electrons, larger is the paramagnetism.<br>In $\\left[\\mathrm{Ti}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$, $\\mathrm{Ti}$ is in +3 oxidation state, thus it contains 1 unpaired electron.<br>In $\\left[\\mathrm{V}(\\mathrm{gly})_{2}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{2}\\right]^{+}, \\mathrm{V}$ is in +5 oxidation state, thus it contains 0 unpaired electrons<br>In $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{en})\\left(\\text { bipy) }\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{2}\\right]^{2+}\\right.$, $\\mathrm{Fe}$ is in +2 oxidation state.<br>Configuration of $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}=[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{6}$<br>But en, bipy and $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ are all strong field ligands, so pairing occurs, thus no unpaired electrons<br>In $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}(\\mathrm{ox})_{2}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}\\right]^{-}$, Co is in +5 oxidation state, thus it has 4 unpaired electrons. Hence, $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}(\\mathrm{ox})_{2}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}\\right]^{-}$has highest paramagnetic behaviour.<br>',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26289,'chemistry',66,1037,2460,'Match the column I with column II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Chloroform<br>B. Iodoform<br>C. Trichloromethane<br>D. DDT</div><div class="column-right">P. Antiseptic<br>Q. Insecticide<br>R. Aneasthetic<br>S. Propellant</div></div>','[{"text": "A - S, B - P, C - R, D - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - P, C - S, D - Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Q, B - S, C - P, D - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - S, C - P, D - Q", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26290,'chemistry',64,191,1915,'Strong reducing and oxidizing agents among the following, respectively are','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$  and   $\\\\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$  and   $\\\\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$  and   $\\\\mathrm{Tb}^{4+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$  and   $\\\\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The stable oxidation state of lanthanoids is $+3 . \\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ accepts an electron to change to $+ 3$ state. Hence, it acts as a good oxidising agent.<br>$\\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$ has a strong tendency to lose an electron to attain the more stable $+ 3$ state and is a strong reducing agent.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26291,'chemistry',63,819,1813,'Fluorine is not prepared by general methods because','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{HF}}$ can be easily oxidised.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{HF}}$ cannot be easily oxidised.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{HF}}$ is highly poisonous.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{HF}}$ is a good conductor of electricity.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Fluorine is not prepared by general methods as used for the preparation of $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ because $\\mathrm{HF}$ cannot be easily oxidised to $\\mathrm{F}_{2}$. (Fluorine is the most electronegative element)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26292,'chemistry',65,958,2232,'Among the following complexes, the one which shows zero crystal field stabilisation energy (CFSE) is','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Mn}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ is a weak ligand<br>${\\text{CFSE = ( - 0}}{\\text{.4x + 0}}{\\text{.6y)}}{\\Delta _0}$<br>where $\\mathrm{x}$ and $\\mathrm{y}$ are electrons present in ${{\\text{t}}_{{\\text{2g}}}}\\,{\\text{and}}\\,\\,{{\\text{e}}_{\\text{g}}}$ orbitals.<br>For $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$ complex ion<br>${\\text{F}}{{\\text{e}}^{{\\text{3  +  }}}}\\left( {{\\text{3}}{{\\text{d}}^{\\text{5}}}} \\right){\\text{  =  t}}_{{\\text{2g}}}^{\\text{3}}{\\text{e}}_{\\text{g}}^{\\text{2}}{\\text{  =    -  0}}.{\\text{4}} \\times {\\text{3 + 0}}{\\text{.6}} \\times {\\text{2  =  0}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
