(26379,'chemistry',66,1031,2432,'Which one is most reactive towards ${{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{N}}}{\\rm{1}}$ reactions?','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{C}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}\\\\right) \\\\mathrm{Br}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Br}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{CH}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}\\\\right) \\\\mathrm{Br}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{CH}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right) \\\\mathrm{Br}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{1}$$ reaction involves the formation of carbocations, hence higher the stability of carbocation, more will be the reactivity of the parent alkyl halide. Thus tertiary carbocation formed from (3) is stabilised by two phenyl groups and one methyl group, hence most stable. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26379_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26427,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'$\\mathrm{CrO}_{3}$ dissolves in aq. NaOH to give','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{CrO}_{3}+2 \\mathrm{OH}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{-2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26380,'chemistry',64,191,1917,'Spin only \' magnetic moment is same for which of the following ions?<br>(A) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}}}$(B) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}}}$(C) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}}}$(D) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}}}$(E) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Sc}^{3+}}}$<br>Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.','[{"text": "B and D only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A and E only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B and C  only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A and D only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Spin only magnetic momentum depends on number of unpaired electrons.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/64_26380_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Spin only magnetic moment is given by<br>${\\mathrm{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{n}(\\mathrm{n}+2)} \\mathrm{BM}}}$.<br>${\\mathrm{\\therefore \\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}}}$ will have same spin only magnetic moment, as they have same no. of unpaired electrons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26381,'chemistry',63,818,1806,'Which of the following is used in the preparation of chlorine?','[{"text": "Only $\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only $\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both $\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ and $\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Either $\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ or $\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both ${\\rm{Mn}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ and ${\\rm{KMn}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ used for the preparation of chlorine by the action of<br> conc. HCl<br> ${\\rm{Mn}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 4HCl}} \\to {\\rm{MnC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 2}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O + C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br> $\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{2KMn}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{ + 16HCl}} \\to \\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{2KCl + 2MnC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 8}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O + 5C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}\n\\end{array}$<br> Chlorine is not obtained by dil. HCl<br> ${\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{g}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}} \\to \\mathop {{\\rm{A}}{{\\rm{g}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{ Black ppt}}{\\rm{. }}} $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26383,'chemistry',66,1029,2424,'Which of the following pairs of aryl halides cannot be prepared directly by electrophilic substitution?','[{"text": "Aryl chloride and aryl bromide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aryl fluoride and aryl chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aryl bromide and aryl iodide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aryl iodide and aryl fluoride", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Aryl iodide and fluoride cannot be prepared directly by electrophilic substitution because reaction with iodine are reversible in nature and required the presence of an oxidisign agent<br>$$\\left(\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}, \\mathrm{HlO}_{4}\\right)$$ to oxidise the $$\\mathrm{Hl}$$ formed. Fluoro compounds are not prepared by this method due to high reactivity of fluorine.<br>Aryl iodide + aryl fluoride $$\\longrightarrow$$ no reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
