(26459,'chemistry',65,960,2238,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Toxic metal ions are removed by the chelating ligands.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Chelate complexes tend to be more stable.<br>','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Assertion and reason both are correct and reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.<br>Toxic metal ions are removed by chelating ligands. When a solution of chelating ligand is added to a solution containing toxic metal, ligands chelate the metal ions by formation of stable complex.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26461,'chemistry',66,1030,2428,'Which of the following compounds has lower boiling point?','[{"text": "Fluoromethane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Iodomethane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bromomethane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chloromethane", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'B.P. $\\alpha$ molar mass, and the decreasing order of b.p. is<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{I}>\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Br}>\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Cl}>\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{~F}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26462,'chemistry',64,191,1918,'Which of the following imparts green colour to the glass?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "CdS", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "FeO", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{Cr}^{+3}$ ions are green in colour and are used to impart green colour to glass in the form of $\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26464,'chemistry',65,959,2237,'The correct order for the wavelength of absorption in the visible region is','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{4-} < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+} < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{4-} < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+} < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+} < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+} < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{4-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+} < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+} < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{4-}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When central metal ion is same the absorption of colour depends on the strength of the ligand. According to spectrochemical series, strength of various ligands is as follows:<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{I}^{-} < \\mathrm{Br}^{-} < \\mathrm{Cl}^{-} < \\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-} &  < \\mathrm{F}^{-} < \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} < \\mathrm{NH}_{3} \\\\&  < \\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-} < \\mathrm{CN}^{-} < \\mathrm{CO}\\end{aligned}$<br>Thus, $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ is the weakest ligand among given, therefore, the absorbed energy will be lowest in $\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}$, so it will absorb highest wavelength (red light) $\\left(\\because \\mathrm{E} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}\\right)$<br>$\\therefore$ The order of increasing wavelength is, $\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{4-} < \\left[\\mathrm{Ni}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+} < \\left[\\mathrm{Ni}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}$<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26465,'chemistry',66,1028,2421,'Which of the following reactions is used for the conversion of alkyl chloride to alkyl iodide?','[{"text": "Fittig reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Finkelstein reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Swarts reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Friedel Craft\'s reaction", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathop {{\\text{R  -  Cl}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ Alkylchloride }}} {\\text{ -  NaI}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ acetone }}}}\\mathop {{\\text{R  -  I}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ Alkyliodide }}} {\\text{ +  NaCl}} \\downarrow $<br>This reaction is known as Finkelstein reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26466,'chemistry',64,193,1933,'Which of the following is a synthetic Lanthanoid?','[{"text": "Samarium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Europium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cerium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Promethium", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Promethium is a synthetic lanthanoid.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26467,'chemistry',63,807,1749,'The total number of atoms in a molecule of nitrogen sesquioxide is ____.','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Nitrogen sesquioxide is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}}}$. Hence, there are 5 atoms in one molecule of nitrogen sesquioxide.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26468,'chemistry',65,958,2232,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Potassium ferrocyanide is diamagnetic, whereas potassium ferricyanide is paramagnetic.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Crystal field splitting in ferrocyanide ion is greater than that of ferricyanide ion.<br>','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{3}}}\\left[ {^{{\\text{3 + }}}{\\text{Fe(CN}}{{\\text{)}}_{\\text{6}}}} \\right]$ (Potassium ferricyanide)<br>$\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ in the presence of $\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26468_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{4}}}\\left[ {^{{\\text{3 + }}}{\\text{Fe(CN}}{{\\text{)}}_{\\text{6}}}} \\right]$  (Potassium ferrocyanide)<br>$\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ in the presence of $\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26468_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Crystal field splitting in ferrocyanide is less than that of ferricyanide ion because higher the oxidation state of the metal, greater is the crystal field splitting.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26469,'chemistry',66,1034,2443,'Identify reactant (A) used in the following conversion.<br>Chlordeanene $+\\mathrm{A} \\underset{\\text { anhydrous }}{\\stackrel{\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}}{\\longrightarrow}} 1$<br>-Chloroacetophenone +4 -Chloroacetophenone','[{"text": "Ethyl acetate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acetophenone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acetic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acetyl chloride", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26469_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
