(26627,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'In the dichromate dianion,','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{4}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{Cr}} - {\\\\rm{O}}$ bonds are equivalent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{6}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{Cr}} - {\\\\rm{O}}$ bonds are equivalent", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "all $\\\\mathrm{Cr}-\\\\mathrm{O}$ bonds are equivalent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "all $\\\\mathrm{Cr}-\\\\mathrm{O}$ bonds are nonequivalent", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The structure of ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{7}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}$ <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/64_26627_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> There are six normal Cr - O bonds and two bridged Cr - O bond.<br>The six normal Cr - O bonds are expected to be equivalent and different from those of the bridged Cr - O bond.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26628,'chemistry',65,952,2211,'Which of the following is not a double salt but is a complex salt?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{KCl} \\\\cdot \\\\mathrm{MgCl}_{2} \\\\cdot 6 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{FeSO}_{4} \\\\cdot\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\\\cdot 6 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\\\cdot \\\\mathrm{Al}_{2}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\cdot 24 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\mathrm{KCN} \\\\cdot \\\\mathrm{Fe}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$4 \\mathrm{KCN} \\cdot \\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{2}$ is $\\mathrm{K}_{4}\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]$. It is a complex salt.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26629,'chemistry',66,1031,2432,'Identify the correct reactivity order for $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1$ reaction.','[{"text": "I $>$ II $>$ III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II $>$ I $>$ III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I $>$ III $>$ II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I $=$ III $=$ II", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Stability of carbocation is the major concern here . <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26629_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  carbocation in case II is more stable because $\\mathrm{N}$ shows more $+\\mathrm{M}$ (being less electronegative).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26629_question_1.jpg'),
(26631,'chemistry',64,195,1939,'The electronic configuration of actinoids cannot be assigned with degree of certainty because of','[{"text": "small energy difference between 5f and 6d levels", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "overlapping of inner orbitals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "free movement of electrons over all the orbitals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "none of the above.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For the first four actinide elements, $\\mathrm{Th}, \\mathrm{Pa}, \\mathrm{U}$ and $\\mathrm{Np}$, the difference in energy between $5\\,{\\text{f}}$ and $6\\,{\\text{d}}$-orbitals is small. Thus, in these elements (and their ions) electrons may occupy the $5\\,{\\text{f}}$ or the $6\\,{\\text{d}}$ levels or sometimes both. Later in the actinide series, the 5 -orbitals become appreciably lower in energy. Thus, from $\\mathrm{Pu}$ onwards the 5 -shell fills in a regular way and the elements become very similar.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26632,'chemistry',65,954,2218,'In sodium nitroprusside, the oxidation number, coordination number and EAN of iron are, respectively.','[{"text": "$+3,6,36$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$+2,6,36$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$+3,3,36$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$+3,6,35$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Sodium nitroprusside is $\\mathrm{Na}_{2}\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{5} \\mathrm{NO}\\right]$.<br>$\\left(\\mathrm{As} \\mathrm{Na}^{+}, \\mathrm{CN}^{-}, \\mathrm{NO}^{+}\\right.$). The oxidation state of \' $\\mathrm{Fe}$ \' will be +2 .<br>Coordination number of $\\mathrm{Fe}$ is 6<br>$\\mathrm{EAN}=26-2+6(2)=36$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26633,'chemistry',66,1031,2436,'What is the major product obtained in the following reaction?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$2^{\\circ}$ halide with acetylide ion mainly undergoes elimination and gives an alkene with conjugation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26633_question_1.jpg'),
(26634,'chemistry',63,820,1814,'The total number of lone pairs of electrons in an ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{IF}_{7}}}$ molecule is ____ .','[{"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "42", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "21", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'the structure of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{IF}_{7}}}$ is as follows: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26634_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> There is no lone pair of electrons on central ${\\text{I}}$ atom. However, each of seven ${\\mathrm{F}}$ atoms contains 3 lone pairs of electrons. Hence, total number of lone pairs of electrons in an ${\\rm{I}}{{\\rm{F}}_{\\rm{7}}}$ molecule is 21.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26635,'chemistry',64,188,1906,'Which of the following has the maximum number of unpaired  d-electrons?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Zn}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{F}}{{\\text{e}}^{2 + }} = [{\\text{Ar}}]{\\text{3}}{{\\text{d}}^6}{\\text{4}}{{\\text{s}}^0}$<br>$\\Rightarrow 4$ unpaired electrons<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{u}}^{\\text{ + }}} = [{\\text{Ar}}]3{{\\text{d}}^{10}}4{{\\text{s}}^0} \\Rightarrow 0$ unpaired electron<br>${\\text{Zn}} = [{\\text{Ar}}]3{{\\text{d}}^{10}}4{{\\text{s}}^2} \\Rightarrow 0$ unpaired electron<br>${\\text{N}}{{\\text{i}}^{3 + }} = [{\\text{Ar}}]3{d^7}4{{\\text{s}}^0} \\Rightarrow 3$ unpaired electrons',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26636,'chemistry',66,1031,2436,'In the reactions given below<br>${\\text{R -  Cl}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{(i) KC}}{{\\text{N}}^{\\text{ - }}}{\\text{(ii)LiAl}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}}}{\\text{Product}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{A}}$<br>${\\text{R -  Cl}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ (i) AgCN(ii)LiAl}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}}}{\\text{Product}}\\,\\,{\\text{B}}$<br>The compounds $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{B}$ are:','[{"text": "Chain isomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Position isomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Functional isomers", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Metamers", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A and B are $\\mathrm{RCH}_{2} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{RNHCH}_{3}$. So these are functional isomers.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
