(26926,'chemistry',64,197,1944,'The metals that are employed in the battery industries are<br>${\\rm{(I) Fe}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(II) Mn}}\\,\\,{\\rm{(III) Ni}}\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(IV) Cr}}\\,\\,\\,$<br>${\\rm{(V) Cd}}$<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below','[{"text": "(II), (III) and (V) only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(II), (IV) and (V) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (II), (III) and (IV) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (II), (III), (IV) and (V)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Metals like ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}, \\mathrm{Ni}, \\mathrm{Cd}, \\mathrm{Zn}, \\mathrm{Pb}}}$ and Hg are employed in the battery industries. So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26928,'chemistry',66,1038,2461,'Major products ${\\mathrm{A}}$ and ${\\mathrm{B}}$ formed in the following reaction sequence, are<br>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26928_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26928_question_1.png'),
(26929,'chemistry',64,192,1923,'When ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}}$ is added to a salt (A), the greenish yellow gas liberated as salt (A) is','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{KNO}_{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "NaBr", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CaI}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Greenish yellow gas is chlorine, obtained from chloride salt, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}+\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\\\& \\quad \\mathrm{MnSO}_{4}+\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\end{aligned}$<br>So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26930,'chemistry',63,811,1770,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct option given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Oxygen being the first member of group 16 exhibits only -2 oxidation state.</div><div class="column-right">Down the group 16, stability of +4 oxidation state decreases and +6 oxidation state increases.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Statement ${\\mathrm{A}}$ is incorrect as along with $-$2 oxidation state, oxygen shows<br>${\\mathrm{-1\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right), 0\\left(\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right),-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\mathrm{KO}_{2}\\right),+1\\left(\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\mathrm{~F}_{2}\\right)}}$ and${\\mathrm{+2\\left(\\mathrm{OF}_{2}\\right)}}$ oxidation states.<br>Statement ${\\mathrm{B}}$ is also incorrect as down the group stability of +4 oxidation state increases and +6 oxidation state decreases due to inert pair effect. So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26931,'chemistry',65,958,2235,'Which of the following cannot be explained by crystal field theory?','[{"text": "Magnetic properties of transition metal complexes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The order of spectrochemical series", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Stability of metal complexes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Colour of metal complexes", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Crystal field theory explains about the structure, magnetic properties and colour of metal complexes. It also explains the stability of metal complexes.<br>Crystal field theory introduces spectrochemical series based upon the experimental values of $\\Delta$ but can\'t explain it\'s order.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26932,'chemistry',66,702,2415,'Hybridization of β-carbon atom in allyl halide is','[{"text": "sp", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{sp}^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{sp}^{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\\\mathrm{~d}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Allyl halide is:  $\\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits^\\gamma  {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}} = \\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits^\\beta  {\\rm{H}} - \\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits^\\alpha  {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}} - {\\rm{x}}$<br>Here β-carbon is with double bond. So ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{sp}^{2}}}$ hybridised.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26933,'chemistry',64,192,1925,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Fusion of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}}$ with KOH and an oxidising agent gives dark green\n${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}}}$.</div><div class="column-right">Manganate ion on electrolytic oxidation in alkaline medium gives permanganate ion.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both statements are true.<br>${\\text{Mn}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{with}}\\,\\,{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}}]{{{\\text{KOH}}\\,{\\text{,oxidised}}}}\\mathop {{\\text{MnO}}_{\\text{4}}^{2 - }}\\limits_{{\\text{Manganate}}\\,{\\text{ion}}} $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\xrightarrow[\\begin{subarray}{l} \n  {\\text{oxidation}}\\,\\,{\\text{in}}\\,\\,{\\text{alkaline}} \\\\ \n  {\\text{solution}} \n\\end{subarray} ]{{{\\text{Electrolytic}}}}\\mathop {{\\text{MnO}}_4^ - }\\limits_{{\\text{permanganate}}\\,\\,{\\text{ion}}} $<br>So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26934,'chemistry',63,811,1770,'Anomalous behaviour of oxygen is due to its','[{"text": "large size and low electronegativity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "small size and low electronegativity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "small size and high electronegativity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "large size and high electronegativity.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Anomalous behaviour of oxygen is due to its small size, high electronegativity and non availability of vacant d- orbitals. So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26935,'chemistry',66,1025,2416,'IUPAC name of<br>','[{"text": "4-Chloro-1, 3-dinitrobenzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1-Chloro-2, 4-dinitrobenzene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1-Chloro-3, 6-dinitrobenzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "o, p-dinitrochlorobenzene", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Lowest location rule is to be followed.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26935_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26935_question_1.png'),
(26936,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'The hybridisation and magnetic behaviour of cobalt ion in $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$ complex, respectively is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\\\mathrm{~d}^{2}$ and paramagnetic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\\\mathrm{~d}^{2}$ and diamagnetic.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{d}^{2} \\\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ and diamagnetic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{d}}^2}{\\\\rm{s}}{{\\\\rm{p}}^3}$  and paramagnetic", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$<br>$\\mathrm{Co}^{3+}: 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{6}, 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{0}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26936_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>It is diamgnetic as there are no unpaired electrons.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26937,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'The equilibrium ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}}}$ is shifted to the right in','[{"text": "a weakly acidic medium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a neutral medium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "an acidic medium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a basic medium.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/64_26937_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The equilibrium shifts right side in a basic medium and left side in acidic medium.<br>So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26938,'chemistry',63,806,1738,'Choose the correct statements about the hydrides of group 15 elements.<br>(I) The stability of the hydrides decreases in the order ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}>\\mathrm{PH}_{3}>\\mathrm{AsH}_{3}>\\mathrm{SbH}_{3}>\\mathrm{BiH}_{3}}}$.<br>(II) The reducing ability of the hydrides increases in the order ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3} < \\mathrm{PH}_{3} < \\mathrm{AsH}_{3} < \\mathrm{SbH}_{3} < \\mathrm{BiH}_{3}}}$<br> (III) Among the hydrides, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$ is strong reducing agent while ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BiH}_{3}}}$ is mild reducing agent.<br>(IV) The basicity of the hydrides increases in the order ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3} < \\mathrm{PH}_{3} < \\mathrm{AsH}_{3} < \\mathrm{SbH}_{3} < \\mathrm{BiH}_{3}}}$.<br>Choose the most appropriate from the options given below:','[{"text": "(II) and (III) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(III) and (IV) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (IV) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (II) only", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$ to ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BiH}_{3}}}$, thermal stability decreases (as bond energy decreases), basicity decreases (as electronegativity decreases), reducing ability increases (as bond energy decreases). ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BiH}_{3}}}$ is strong reducing agent and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$ is mild. This is because as nitrogen being unable to easily accommodate a lone pair due to its small size while the larger atom can do so. So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26939,'chemistry',66,1028,2419,'Identify the major product in the reaction given below.<br>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The reaction follows ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2}}$ mechanism which would give inverted product (i.e., ${\\mathrm{S \\rightarrow R}}$ ).<br>Thus,<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26939_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26939_question_1.png'),
(26940,'chemistry',65,958,2234,'Which of the following complex will show largest splitting of d - orbitals?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{FeF}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}\\\\right)_{3}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Higher the strength of ligand, higher will be the splitting energy.<br>$\\mathrm{I}^{-} < \\mathrm{Br}^{-} < \\mathrm{Cl}^{-} < \\mathrm{F}^{-} < \\mathrm{OH}^{-} < \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-} < \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>$ < \\mathrm{EDTA}^{4} < \\mathrm{NH}_{3} < \\mathrm{en} < \\mathrm{CN}^{-} < \\mathrm{CO}$<br>$\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$  being strongest ligand among given options will lead to largest splitting of  d  - orbitals for  $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]^{3-}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26941,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'In acidic medium, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}}$ shows oxidising action as represented in the half reaction<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}+\\mathrm{XH}^{+}+\\mathrm{Ye}^{-} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{~A}+\\mathrm{ZH}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$ <br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{X}, \\mathrm{Y}, \\mathrm{Z}}}$ and A respectively are','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{8,4,6}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{14,7,6}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{14,6,7}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{8,6,4}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The balanced half reaction will be<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}+14 \\mathrm{H}^{+}+6 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}+7 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$<br>So, ${\\mathrm{X=14, Y=6, Z=7, A=\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}}}$<br>So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26942,'chemistry',63,812,1772,'Read the Assertion  and  Reason carefully to mark the correct option out of the options given below.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{Te}}$ is more acidic than ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Bond dissociation enthalpy of ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{Te}}$ is lower than ${{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{S}}$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$ to ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{Te}}}$, acidic strength increases as ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{E}-\\mathrm{H}}}$ bond energy decreases ( E is group 16 element) due to increased atomic size of E.<br> So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26943,'chemistry',66,1031,2434,'Total number of di-brominated products of  ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{10}}}$ (including stereoisomers) is','[{"text": "12", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "13", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "14", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "15", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Di-brominated products of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{10}}}$ are<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26943_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26944,'chemistry',65,952,2211,'Which of the following are the examples of double salt?<br>A. $\\mathrm{FeSO}_{4} \\cdot\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\cdot 6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>B. $\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4} \\cdot 4 \\mathrm{NH}_{3} \\cdot \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>C. $\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\cdot \\mathrm{Al}_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)_{3} \\cdot 24 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>D. $\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{2} \\cdot 4 \\mathrm{KCN}$<br>Choose the correct answer.','[{"text": "B and D only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, B and D only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A and C only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A and B only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A double salt is a crystalline salt made up of two simple salts.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26944_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Double salt contains two or more types of salts.   $\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4} \\cdot 4 \\mathrm{NH}_{3} \\cdot \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ and $\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{2} \\cdot 4 \\mathrm{KCN}$ are complex compounds.<br>So, A and C are double salts.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26945,'chemistry',63,812,1775,'Choose the correct statements from the following.<br>(I) All group 16 elements form oxides of general formula ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{EO}_{2}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{EO}_{3}}}$, where ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{E}=\\mathrm{S}, \\mathrm{Se}, \\mathrm{Te}}}$ and Po. Both the types of oxides are acidic in nature.<br>(II) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{TeO}_{2}}}$ is an oxidising agent while ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{2}}}$ is reducing in nature.<br>(III) The reducing property decreases from ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}}}$ to ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{Te}}}$ down the group.<br>(IV) The ozone molecule contains five lone pairs of electrons.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "(III) and (IV) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (IV) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (II) only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(II) and (III) only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'All the hydrides of group 16 elements except water possess reducing property and this character increases from ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}}}$ to ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{Te}}}$. <br>Ozone molecule contains six lone pairs of electrons. So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26946,'chemistry',64,191,1918,'Which of the following compounds show colour due to d - d transition?','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4} \\\\cdot 5 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Due to presence of d-electrons in 3d-orbital of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}\\left(3 \\mathrm{~d}^{9}\\right) \\mathrm{CuSO}_{4} \\cdot 5 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$ undergoes ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{d}-\\mathrm{d}}}$ transition and shows colour. ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}^{+7}, \\mathrm{Cr}^{+6}}}$ show colour due to charge transfer phenomenon.So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26947,'chemistry',66,1030,2431,'Which of the following is incorrect with respect to physical properties of alkyl halides?','[{"text": "Alkyl halides are colourless but alkyl bromide and iodides develop colour when exposed to light.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Though alkyl halides are polar, still they are very slightly soluble in water.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The nature of intermolecular forces in alkyl halides are dipole-dipole and van der Waals\' forces.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The boiling point of isomeric haloalkanes increase with increase in branching.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'B.P. decreases with branching. As the branching increases for isomeric haloalkanes, there will be decrease in van der Waals\' forces and thus boiling point also decreases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26948,'chemistry',65,956,2223,'The complex cation which has two isomers is','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{5} \\\\mathrm{Cl}\\\\right]^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{5} \\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{5} \\\\mathrm{Cl}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}$  is an ambidentate ligand. Hence, it can show the following two isomers:<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{5} \\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right]^{2+} \\text { and }\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{5} \\mathrm{ONO}\\right]^{2+}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26950,'chemistry',64,191,1920,'Identify the incorrect pair from the following','[{"text": "Wacker process - ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Photography - AgBr", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Haber process - Iron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Polythene preparation - ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}, \\\\mathrm{Al}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In Wacker process, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}}}$ is used as catalyst, not ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}}}$. So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26951,'chemistry',66,1038,2461,'Read the following statements and choose the correct answer.<br>(i) Alkyl iodide gives yellow ppt. with aq. ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}}}$ while aryl iodide does not.<br>(ii) Among isomeric dihalobenzenes, the paraisomer have higher melting point than their ortho and meta isomers.<br>(iii) The isomeric dihalobenzenes have large difference in their boiling and melting points.<br>(iv) The isomeric dihalobenzenes have nearly same boiling point.','[{"text": "(i), (ii) and (iii) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) and (iii) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(ii) and (iv) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i), (ii), and (iv) are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Isomeric dihalobenzenes have nearly same boiling points but different melting points, hence, statements iii is incorrect.<br>    <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26951_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26952,'chemistry',64,192,1925,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}}$ decomposes on heating at 513 K to form ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$ along with','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mn and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{KO}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}}}$ and Mn", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{KMnO}_{4} \\xrightarrow{513 \\mathrm{~K}} \\mathrm{~K}_{2} \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}+\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+\\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$ <br>So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26953,'chemistry',63,826,1829,'Match Column I (Molecules /Species) with Column II (Property/shape).<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A.${\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>B.${\\rm{NO}}$<br>C.${\\rm{NO}}_2^ - $<br>D.${\\rm{I}}_3^ - $</div><div class="column-right">P.Paramagnetic<br>Q.Dimagnetic<br>R.Tetrahedral<br>S.Linear</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R,B}} - {\\\\rm{S,C}} - {\\\\rm{Q,D}} - {\\\\rm{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S,B}} - {\\\\rm{P,C}} - {\\\\rm{R,D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q,B}} - {\\\\rm{R,C}} - {\\\\rm{P,D}} - {\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R,B}} - {\\\\rm{P,C}} - {\\\\rm{Q,D}} - {\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}}$ is tetrahedral. A $-$ R <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26953_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  NO is paramagnetic with an odd electron. ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{B}-\\mathrm{P}}}$ <br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}}}$is diamagnetic.${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{Q}}}$<br>  ${\\rm{I}}_{\\rm{3}}^ - $  is linear.    D-S <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/63_26953_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26955,'chemistry',66,1031,2435,'Read the following statements and choose the correct code.<br>(I) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2}}$ reactions follow a second order kinetics where as ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1}}$ reactions follow the first order kinetics.<br>(II) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1}}$ reactions follow the second order kinetics whereas ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2}}$ reactions follow the first order kinetics.<br>(III) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2}}$ reactions take place in a single step whereas ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1}}$ reactions take place in two steps.<br>(IV) Tertiary alkyl halides are least reactive towards ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2}}$ reactions but they show high reactivity towards ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1}}$ reaction.','[{"text": "(II) and (IV) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (III) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (II) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (III) and (IV) are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Rate of reaction for ${\\mathrm{S_{N} 1 \\propto[}}$ Reactant <br>${\\mathrm{]\\left(1^{\\text {st }}\\right.}}$ order)<br>Rate of reaction for ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2 \\propto}}$ [Reactant] [: Nu${\\mathrm{]}}$<br>( ${\\mathrm{2^{\\text {nd }}}}$ order). So statements (ii) is incorrect.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26956,'chemistry',64,192,1925,'In alkaline medium, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}}}$oxidises ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{I}^{-}}}$to','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{IO}_{3}^{-}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{I}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{IO}_{4}^{-}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{IO}^{-}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}+\\mathrm{I}^{-}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{MnO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{OH}^{-}+\\mathrm{IO}_{3}^{-}}}$<br>So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26957,'chemistry',63,817,1798,'Thiosulphate reacts differently with iodine and bromine in the reactions given below:<br>${\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{~S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}^{2-}+\\mathrm{I}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{S}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{6}^{2-}+2 \\mathrm{I}^{-}}}$ <br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}^{2-}+5 \\mathrm{Br}_{2}+5 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}+10 \\mathrm{Br}^{-}+10 \\mathrm{H}^{+}}}$<br>Which of the following statement justifies the above dual behaviour of thiosulphate?','[{"text": "Bromine is a weaker oxidant than iodine.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thiosulphate undergoes oxidation by bromine and reduction by iodine in these reactions.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bromine undergoes oxidation and iodine undergoes reduction in these reactions.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bromine is a stronger oxidant than iodine.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Br}_{2}}}$ oxidises ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}^{2-}(+2 \\,\\mathrm{O} . \\mathrm{S})}}$ to <br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}(+6}}$ O.S. ${\\mathrm{)}}$ while ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}}$ oxidises<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}^{2-}(+2}}$ O.S ${\\mathrm{)}}$ to ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{6}^{2-}(+2.5}}$ O.S. ${\\mathrm{)}}$,<br> hence  ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Br}_{2}}}$ is stronger oxidant than iodine. So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26958,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'Read Assertion and Reason carefully and mark correct option.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> The spin only magnetic moment value for $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]^{3-}$ is 1.74 B.M., whereas for $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$ is 5.92 B.M.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In both complexes, Fe is present in $ + \\,3$ oxidation state.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is false but Reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$ contains $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ with $3{{\\text{d}}^5}$ configuration<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26958_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ is a weak field ligand.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26958_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Thus, $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$ has 5 unpaired electrons.<br>Hence, $\\mathrm{n}=5$<br>$\\mu  = \\sqrt {{\\rm{n}}({\\rm{n}} + 2)}  = \\sqrt {5\\,(5 + 2)} $<br>$=\\sqrt{35}=5.92 \\mathrm{~B} . \\mathrm{M}$.<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]^{3-}$ contains $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ with $3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5}$configuration<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26958_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>$\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$  is a strong field ligand. Pairing of unpaired electrons will takes place.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26958_explanation_4.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Thus, $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]^{3-}$ has only one unpaired electron, $\\mathrm{n}=1$<br>$\\mu=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{n}(\\mathrm{n}+2)}=\\sqrt{1(1+2)}=\\sqrt{3}=1.732$ B.M.<br>Thus, the values of magnetic moment are due to the number of unpaired electrons present and not because of the oxidation states of Fe. Hence, both assertion and reason are true, but reason is not the correct explanation.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26959,'chemistry',66,1031,2435,'Read the following statements and choose the correct option.<br>(I) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1}}$ reactions are carried out through formation of carbocation as an intermediate.<br>(II) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1}}$ reactions are two step reactions in which step 1 is fast and irreversible.<br>(III) In ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SN}_{1}}}$, Step 1 involves breaking of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{X}}}$ bond which obtain energy through solvation of halide.<br>(IV) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1}}$ reactions are two step reactions in which step 2 is slow and reversible.<br>(V) Allylic and benzylic halides show high reactivity towards ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SN}_{1}}}$ reactions.','[{"text": "(I), (III) and (V) are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(II), (III) and (V) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (III) and (IV) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (II) and (IV) are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Statements (ii) and (iv) are incorrect as ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1}}$ reaction proceed as<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{X} \\xrightarrow{\\text { slow }} \\mathrm{R}^{+}+\\mathrm{X}^{-} \\quad}}$ (step I)<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{R}^{+}+: \\mathrm{Nu} \\xrightarrow{\\text { fast }} \\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{Nu}}}$ (step II)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26960,'chemistry',64,191,1920,'Match Column I (Substances) with Column II(Element present).<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A.Ziegler catalyst<br>B.Blood pigment<br>C.Wilkinson catalyst<br>D.Vitamin ${{\\rm{B}}_{{\\rm{12}}}}$</div><div class="column-right">P.Rhodium<br>Q.Cobalt<br>R.Iron<br>S.Titanium</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S,B}} - {\\\\rm{R,C}} - {\\\\rm{P,D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R,B}} - {\\\\rm{Q,C}} - {\\\\rm{S,D}} - {\\\\rm{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q,B}} - {\\\\rm{R,C}} - {\\\\rm{S,D}} - {\\\\rm{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q,B}} - {\\\\rm{S,C}} - {\\\\rm{P,D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Ziegler catalyst is a complex of titanium. (A-S)<br>Blood pigment contains iron. (B-R)<br>Wilkinson catalyst contains rhodium. (C-P)<br>Vitamin ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{B}_{12}}}$ includes cobalt. (D-Q)<br>So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26961,'chemistry',63,817,1798,'Among the following halogens, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{F}_{2}, \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}, \\mathrm{Br}_{2}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}}$, which can undergo disproportionation reactions?','[{"text": "Only ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{I}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{I}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{F}_{2}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{F}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{F}_{2}}}$ cannot undergo disproportionation as it shows only $-$1 oxidation state and hence, has a tendency to reduce only. Remaining ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}, \\mathrm{Br}_{2}, \\mathrm{I}_{2}}}$ can undergo disproportionation. So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26963,'chemistry',66,1034,2442,'Aryl halides are extremely less reactive towards nucleophilic substitution than alkyl halides.<br>Which of the following accounts for this?<br>(I) Due to resonance in aryl halides.<br>(II) In alkyl halides, carbon atom in ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{X}}}$ bond is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{sp}^{2}}}$ hybridized whereas in aryl halides carbon atom in ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{X}}}$ bond is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{sp}^{3}}}$ hybridized.<br>(III) Due to stability of phenyl cation.<br>(IV)Due to possible repulsion there are less chances of nucleophile to approach electron rich arenes.','[{"text": "(I), (II) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (II) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (IV)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(II), (III) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'(ii) and (iii) are incorrect as carbon of alkyl halide and aryl halide is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{sp}^{3}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{sp}^{2}}}$ hybridized, respectively. Also phenyl cation is highly unstable. Aryl halides are less reactive towards SN reactions due to resonance and possible repulsion there are less chances of nucleophile to approach ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{e}^{-}}}$ rich arenes.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26964,'chemistry',64,192,1925,'Alkaline oxidative fusion of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}}$ gives " ${\\mathrm{A}}$ " which on electrolytic oxidation in alkaline solution produces B. A and B respectively are','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{2-}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{3}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{2-}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{2-}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>${\\text{Mn}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{Oxidised}}{\\kern 1pt} {\\text{with}}{\\kern 1pt} {\\text{air}}{\\kern 1pt} {\\text{or}}{\\kern 1pt} {\\text{KN}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}}]{{{\\text{Fused with KOH}}}}\\mathop {{\\text{MnO}}_{\\text{4}}^{2 - }}\\limits_{{\\text{Manganate}}{\\kern 1pt} {\\text{ion}}} $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{Oxidation}}\\,\\,{\\text{in}}\\,\\,{\\text{alkaline}}\\,\\,{\\text{solution}}}]{{{\\text{Electrolytic}}}}\\mathop {{\\text{MnO}}_{\\text{4}}^ - }\\limits_{{\\text{Permanganate}}\\,\\,{\\text{ion}}} $<br>So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26965,'chemistry',63,816,1792,'The ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{F}^{-}}}$ions make the enamel on teeth much harder by converting hydroxyapatite (the enamel on the surface of teeth) into much harder fluoroapatite having the formula','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[3\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{Ca}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}\\\\right)_{2}\\\\right) \\\\cdot \\\\mathrm{Ca}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{2}\\\\right]}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[3\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{Ca}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}\\\\right)_{3}\\\\right) \\\\cdot \\\\mathrm{CaF}_{2}\\\\right]}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[3\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{Ca}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}\\\\right)_{2}\\\\right) \\\\cdot \\\\mathrm{CaF}_{2}\\\\right]}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[3\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{Ca}_{2}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}\\\\right)_{2}\\\\right) \\\\cdot \\\\mathrm{Ca}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{2}\\\\right]}}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Fluoroapatite formula is<br>${\\mathrm{\\left[3\\left(\\mathrm{Ca}_{3}\\left(\\mathrm{PO}_{4}\\right)_{2}\\right) \\cdot \\mathrm{CaF}_{2}\\right]}}$.<br> So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26966,'chemistry',65,955,2222,'The IUPAC name of   $\\mathrm{K}_{3}\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)_{3}\\right]$ is','[{"text": "potassium trioxalatocobalt  (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "potassium tris (oxalato) cobaltate  (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "potassium tris (oxalato) cobalt  (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "potassium trioxalatocobaltate  (III).", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The IUPAC name of   $\\mathrm{K}_{3}\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)_{3}\\right]$ is potassium trioxalatocobaltate (III).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26967,'chemistry',66,1034,2444,'Read the statements given below.<br>(A) Haloarenes require drastic conditions for nucleophilic substitution reaction (NSR).<br>(B) Presence of electron withdrawing group increases the reactivity of haloarenes towards NSR.<br>(C) Electrophilic substitution of haloarenes is slower than benzene.<br>Choose the option containing correct set of statements.','[{"text": "A and B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B and C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A and C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, B and C", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All the statements are correct.Haloarenes are very less rective towards NSR due to double bond nature of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{X}}}$ bond. But EWG increases the reactivity towards NSR.Reactivity towards NSR increases by ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}}$ (EWG). Also, electrophilic substitution of haloarene is slower than benzene as halogen ${\\mathrm{(-\\mathrm{I}>+\\mathrm{M})}}$ decreases the electron density of benzene.<br>So, the correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26968,'chemistry',63,826,1829,'Match Column I with Column II.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A.N<br>B.S<br>C.Br<br>D.Kr</div><div class="column-right">P.${\\rm{[Ar]3}}{{\\rm{d}}^{{\\rm{10}}}}{\\rm{4}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{4}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{5}}}$<br>Q.${\\rm{[Ne]3}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{3}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{4}}}$<br>R.${\\rm{[He]2}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{3}}}$<br>S.${\\rm{[Ar]3}}{{\\rm{d}}^{{\\rm{10}}}}{\\rm{4}}{{\\rm{s}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{4}}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{6}}}$</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S,B}} - {\\\\rm{R,C}} - {\\\\rm{Q,D}} - {\\\\rm{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R,B}} - {\\\\rm{Q,C}} - {\\\\rm{P,D}} - {\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P,B}} - {\\\\rm{S,C}} - {\\\\rm{R,D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q,B}} - {\\\\rm{P,C}} - {\\\\rm{S,D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{N}(7):[\\mathrm{He}] 2 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 2 \\mathrm{p}^{3}(\\mathrm{~A}-\\mathrm{R})}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}(16):[\\mathrm{Ne}] 3 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 3 \\mathrm{p}^{4} \\quad(\\mathrm{~B}-\\mathrm{Q})}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\operatorname{Br}(35):[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{10} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 4 \\mathrm{p}^{5}(\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{P})}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\operatorname{Kr}(36):[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{10} 4 \\mathrm{~s}^{2} 4 \\mathrm{p}^{6}(\\mathrm{D}-\\mathrm{S})}}$<br>So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26969,'chemistry',64,191,1918,'The orange colour of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}}$ and purple colour of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}}$ is due to','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{d} \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathrm{d}}}$ transitions in ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}}}$ and charge transfer transitions in ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "charge transfer transition in both.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{d \\\\rightarrow d}}$ transitions in both.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{d \\\\rightarrow d}}$ transitions in ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}}$ and charge transfer transitions in ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}}}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}}$, the colour is due to charge transfer phenomenon in which momentary transfer of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{e}^{-}}}$ from oxygen to metal atoms takes place. So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26971,'chemistry',66,1036,2453,'Grignard reagent is a very versatile initiating compound for a variety of organic reactions. Choose the correct option regarding Grignard reagent.','[{"text": "It is prepared by treating alkyl halide with magnesium metal in dry ether.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The carbon atom in Grignard reagent is less electronegative than Mg-atom.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Grignard reagent reacts with ethyl alcohol to give corresponding alkene as a major product.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Mg}}}$ bond in Grignard reagent is nonpolar and ionic.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26971_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Carbon atom is more electronegative then Mg . RMgX reacts with alcohol to produce alkane. ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{Mg}}}$ bond is polar covalent.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26972,'chemistry',64,192,1924,'A and ${\\mathrm{B}}$ formed in the following reactions are <br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CrO}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+4 \\mathrm{NaOH} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{A}+2 \\mathrm{NaCl}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A}+2 \\mathrm{HCl}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}=\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}, \\\\mathrm{~B}=\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}=\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}, \\\\mathrm{~B}=\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}=\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}, \\\\mathrm{~B}=\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{5}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}=\\\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}, \\\\mathrm{~B}=\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{5}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{Cr}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 4NaOH}} \\to \\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_2}{\\rm{Cr}}{{\\rm{O}}_4}}\\limits_{({\\rm{A}})}  + 2{\\rm{NaCl}} + 2{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}\n\\end{array}$<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{Cr}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{ + 2HCl + 2}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}} \\to \\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{Cr}}{{\\rm{O}}_5}}\\limits_{({\\rm{B}})}  + 2{\\rm{NaCl}} + 3{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}\n\\end{array}$<br>So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26973,'chemistry',63,816,1795,'Element not showing variable oxidation states is','[{"text": "bromine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "chlorine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iodine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "fluorine.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Fluorine is the most electronegative element and shows only $-$1 oxidation state, while other halogens show variable oxidation states like +1 , ${\\mathrm{+3,+5,+7,-1}}$. So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26974,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'Which of the following complex is octahedral, diamagnetic and the most stable?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}_{3}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoCl}_{6}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{3}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{K}_{3}\\left[\\mathrm{Co}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]$  is octahedral, diamagnetic and most stable. The  $\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$  is a strong field ligand and causes pairing of  $\\mathrm{d}-$ electrons of Co .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26975,'chemistry',66,1035,2445,'Read Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct option given below.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Isobutylbromide undergoes Wurtz reaction to give 2, 5-dimethylhexane.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In Wurtz reaction, alkyl halide reacts with sodium in dry ether to give hydrocarbon containing double the carbon atoms that present in the halide.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is false but reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26975_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Both statements are true but reason is not the explanation of assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26976,'chemistry',64,192,1925,'Identify the correct statements from below.<br>(I) The chromate ion is square planar.<br>(II) Dichromates are generally prepared from chromates.<br>(III) The green manganate ion is diamagnetic.<br>(IV) Dark green coloured ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{MnO}_{4}}}$disproportionates in a neutral or acidic medium to give permanganate.<br>(V) With increasing oxidation number of transition metal, ionic character of the oxides decreases.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below','[{"text": "(I),(IV), (V) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II), (IV), (V) only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(II), (III), (IV) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (II), (III) only", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Chromate ion ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}\\right)}}$ has tetrahedral structure.<br>The green manganate ion is paramagnetic because of one unpaired electron.So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26977,'chemistry',63,816,1792,'Identify the incorrect pair from the following.','[{"text": "Cryolite ${\\\\mathrm{-\\\\mathrm{Na}_{3} \\\\mathrm{AlF}_{6}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fluoroapatite ${\\\\mathrm{-3 \\\\mathrm{Ca}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{PO}_{4}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\cdot \\\\mathrm{CaF}_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fluorspar ${\\\\mathrm{-\\\\mathrm{BF}_{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Carnallite ${\\\\mathrm{-\\\\mathrm{KCl}_{2} \\\\cdot \\\\mathrm{MgCl}_{2} \\\\cdot 6 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Fluorspar is calcium fluoride, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CaF}_{2}}}$. So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26978,'chemistry',66,1031,2437,'About the product A and B , which set of statements is correct?<br>I. \'A\' is inactive racemic mixture.<br>II. \'B\' is inactive racemic mixture.<br>III. \'A\' is substitution product whereas \'${\\mathrm{B}}$\' is addition product.','[{"text": "I, II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26978_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Here, both the products form racemic mixture; thus, are inactive.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26978_question_1.png'),
(26979,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'The correct order of spin only magnetic moments for the following complex ions is','[{"text": "${\\\\left[ {{\\\\text{Fe}}{{({\\\\text{CN}})}_6}} \\\\right]^{3 - }} < {\\\\left[ {{\\\\text{Mn}}{{({\\\\text{CN}})}_6}} \\\\right]^{3 - }}$  $ < {\\\\left[ {{\\\\text{Co}}{{\\\\text{F}}_6}} \\\\right]^{3 - }} < {\\\\left[ {{\\\\text{MnB}}{{\\\\text{r}}_4}} \\\\right]^{2 - }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-} < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{MnBr}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}$ $ < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\\\right]^{3-} < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Mn}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\\\right]^{3-} < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}$  $ < {\\\\left[ {{\\\\text{MnB}}{{\\\\text{r}}_4}} \\\\right]^{2 - }} < {\\\\left[ {{\\\\text{Mn}}{{({\\\\text{CN}})}_6}} \\\\right]^{3 - }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{MnBr}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-} < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}$ $ < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\\\right]^{3-} < \\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Mn}(\\\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26979_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26980,'chemistry',64,192,1923,'Choose the correct statements from the following.<br>(I) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mn}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}}}$ is an oil at room temperature.<br>(II) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{V}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}}}$ reacts with acid to give ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{VO}_{2}^{2+}}}$.<br>(III) CrO is a basic oxide.<br>(IV) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{V}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}}}$ does not react with acid.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below','[{"text": "(I) and (III) only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(I), (II) and (III) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II) and (III) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (II) and (IV) only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{V}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}}}$ dissolves in acids to give ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{VO}^{2+}}}$ salts. ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{V}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{5}}}$ is amphoteric in nature, reacts with acids as well as alkalies to give ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{VO}_{4}^{3-}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{VO}_{4}^{+}}}$. So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26981,'chemistry',63,823,1820,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct option given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Noble gases have very high boiling points.</div><div class="column-right">Noble gases are monoatomic gases. They are held together by strong dispersion forces. Because of this they are liquefied at very low temperature. Hence, they have very high boiling points.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All the noble gases are monoatomic. They have very low melting and boiling points due to weak dispersion forces (van der Waals forces). So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26982,'chemistry',66,702,2415,'Match the halides in column I with their types given in column II and mark the appropriate choice.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - P,\\tB - Q,\\tC - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, \\tB - P,\\tC - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,\\tB - Q,\\tC - P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Q,\\tB - P,\\tC - R", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26982_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26982_question_1.png'),
(26983,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'Match Column I with Column II.<br> <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - R,\\tB - S,\\tC - P,\\t\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q ,\\tB - S,\\tC - P,\\t\\tD - R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Q ,\\tB - P,\\tC - S,\\t\\tD - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S,\\tB - R,\\tC - Q ,\\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]^{3-}: \\mathrm{d}^{2} \\mathrm{sp}^{3} ; \\mathrm{n}=3$<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}: \\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}^{2} ; \\mathrm{n}=4$<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}: \\mathrm{d}^{2} \\mathrm{sp}^{3} ; \\mathrm{n}=0$<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}: \\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}^{2} ; \\mathrm{n}=2$<br>$(\\mathrm{n}=$ no.of unpaired electrons)',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/65_26983_question_1.png'),
(26985,'chemistry',63,826,1829,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{8}}}$ solid undergoes disproportionation reaction under alkaline conditions to form ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}^{2-}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}^{2-}}}$.</div><div class="column-right">${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}}}$can undergo disproportionation reaction under acidic conditions.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathop {\\,{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{8}}}}\\limits^{\\rm{0}} {\\rm{ + 12O}}{{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ - }}} \\to {\\rm{4}}\\,\\mathop {\\rm{S}}\\limits^{ - 2} {\\rm{ + 2}}\\mathop {\\,{{\\rm{S}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits^{{\\rm{ + 2}}} {\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}^{{\\rm{2 - }}}{\\rm{ + 6}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}$<br>So, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{8}}}$ disproportionates in alkaline medium.<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}}}$does not show disproportionation reaction in any medium because here chlorine is present in its highest O.S. i.e., +7 . So, the correct option is ${\\mathrm{(1)}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26986,'chemistry',66,1030,2428,'Consider the following haloarenes,<br> <br>(I) B.P. of II is more than that of I.<br>(II) B.P. of II is least among the given.<br>(III) M.P. of III is highest among the given.<br>(IV) M.P. of I is greater than that of II.<br>Choose the correct option.','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III, and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'(III) is symmetrical; thus, due to excellent packaging, it has the highest melting and boiling points, while (I) has more dipole than (II)<br>Hence, melting and boiling points follow the order: III ${\\mathrm{>}}$ I ${\\mathrm{>}}$ II. So statements (ii) is incorrect.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_26986_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26986_question_1.png'),
(26989,'chemistry',63,816,1797,'The correct group of halide ions which can be oxidised by oxygen in acidic medium is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Br}^{-}$  and  $\\\\mathrm{I}^{-}$only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$, $\\\\mathrm{Br}^{-}$  and  $\\\\mathrm{I}^{-}$only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{B}}{{\\\\text{r}}^ - }$ only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\text{I}}^ - }$ only", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Only ${{\\text{I}}^ - }$ can be oxidised by oxygen in acidic medium as follows :<br>$4 \\mathrm{I}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}^{-}+4 \\mathrm{H}_{\\text {(aq) }}^{+}+\\mathrm{O}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{I}_{2(\\mathrm{~s})}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{(\\mathrm{l})}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26990,'chemistry',66,1034,2444,'Match the reactions in column I with their reaction conditions given in column II and mark the appropriate choice.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - P,\\tB - Q,\\tC - R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - R, \\tB - Q,\\tC - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,\\tB - P,\\tC - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q,\\tB - P,\\tC - R", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The presence of EWG ${\\mathrm{\\left(-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right)}}$ increases the NSR tendency on halo benzene. Thus, as the number of ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}}$ group increases at ortho and para positions, the reaction gets favourable even in normal conditions.<br>So, the correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_26990_question_1.png'),
(26991,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'The octahedral diamagnetic low spin complex among the following is','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{NiCl}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\left[ {{\\\\rm{CoC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_6}} \\\\right]^{3 - }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ acts as a strong field ligand.<br>Cobalt (Co) is in +3 oxidation state.<br>Hence, $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$ is a low spin diamagnetic complex, with no unpaired electrons.<br>Rest of the given complexes are high spin and paramagnetic. As $\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$and $\\mathrm{F}^{-}$  are weak field ligands, they do not cause pairing of electrons in sthe 3d orbitals of the central metal atom.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(26993,'chemistry',66,1031,2433,'When the concentration of alkyl halide is tripled and the concentration of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{OH}^{-}}}$ion is reduced to half, the rate of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2}}$ reaction increases by','[{"text": "3 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.5 times", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6 times", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Rate of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2 \\propto[\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{X}][\\mathrm{Nu}]}}$<br>If ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{X}}}$ becomes 3 times and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{OH}^{\\ominus}}}$ is half<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{\\mathrm{r}_{2}}{\\mathrm{r}_{\\mathrm{i}}}=\\dfrac{[3 \\mathrm{RX}]\\left[\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]}{[\\mathrm{RX}]\\left[\\mathrm{OH}^{-}\\right]} \\\\& \\dfrac{\\mathrm{r}_{2}}{\\mathrm{r}_{1}}=1.5\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26996,'chemistry',66,1037,2458,'When freon is manufactured by tetrachloremethane, the reaction involved in this process is called','[{"text": "Sandmeyer reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Swarts reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Finkelstein reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Swarts reaction is used to prepare fluorides.Replacement of ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{Cl},-\\mathrm{Br}}}$ from RX by -F is called swarts reaction.<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CCl}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{HF} \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{SbCl}_{5}} 2 \\mathrm{HCl}+\\mathrm{CCl}_{2} \\mathrm{~F}_{2}}}$ (Freon)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26997,'chemistry',65,957,2228,'The correct order of the number of unpaired electrons in the given complexes is<br>A. $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]^{3-}$<br>B. $\\left[\\mathrm{FeF}_{6}\\right]^{3-}$<br>C. ${\\left[ {{\\rm{Co}}{{\\rm{F}}_6}} \\right]^{3 - }}$<br>D. $\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}(\\text { oxalate })_{3}\\right]^{3-}$<br>E. $\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}(\\mathrm{CO})_{4}\\right]$<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "A $ < $ E $ < $ C $ < $ B $ < $ D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "E $ < $ A $ < $ D $ < $ C $ < $ B", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "E $ < $ A $ < $ B $ < $ D $ < $ C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A $ < $ E $ < $ D $ < $ C $ < $ B", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26997_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>In this complex, $\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$  is a strong field ligand, that causes pairing of electrons.<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ It has one unpaired electron.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26997_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>In this complex as $\\mathrm{F}^{-}$  is a weak field ligand, it does not cause pairing of electrons.<br>$\\therefore$   It has 5 unpaired electrons.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26997_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>As $\\mathrm{F}^{-}$  is a weak field ligand, it does not cause pairing of electrons.<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ It has 4 unpaired electrons.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26997_explanation_4.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>As oxalate is a weak field ligand, it does not cause pairing of electrons.<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ It has 3 unpaired electrons.<br>In $\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}(\\mathrm{CO})_{4}\\right]$, Ni is in zero oxidation state. CO is a strong field ligand. Hence, causes pairing of electrons.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_26997_explanation_5.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Hence, 0 unpaired electrons.<br>Thus, the correct order of unpaired electrons is $\\mathrm{E} < \\mathrm{A} < \\mathrm{D} < \\mathrm{C} < \\mathrm{B}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(26999,'chemistry',66,1037,2459,'Which of the following statements is/are correct for DDT?<br>I. It is very effective against the mosquito that spreads malaria.<br>II. It is easily metabolized by animals.<br>III. It is highly toxic for fish.<br>Choose the correct option.','[{"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'DDT is not easily metabolized by animals, and it is very effective against mosquitoes. DDT is toxic to fishes.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27000,'chemistry',65,958,2234,'Match Column I with Column II<br> <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - S,\\tB - P,\\tC - R,\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q ,\\tB - S,\\tC - R,\\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q ,\\tB - S,\\tC - P,\\t\\tD - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S,\\tB - P,\\tC - Q ,\\tD - R", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A. In $\\left[\\mathrm{Ti}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+} ; \\mathrm{Ti}$ is in +2 oxidation state. Hence, its electronic configuration is ${\\text{t}}_{2{\\text{g}}}^2\\,{\\text{e}}_{\\text{g}}^0$ as $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ does not cause pairing of electrons.<br>$\\mathrm{CFSE}=[-0.4(2)+0.6(0)] \\Delta_{\\mathrm{o}}=-0.8 \\Delta_{\\text {o }}$<br>B. In $\\left[\\mathrm{V}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+} ; \\mathrm{V}$ is in $ + \\,2$ oxidation state. Hence its electronic configuration is $\\mathrm{t}_{2 \\mathrm{~g}}^{3} \\mathrm{e}_{\\mathrm{g}}^{0}$ as $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ does not cause pairing of electrons. $\\mathrm{CFSE}=[-0.4(3)+0.6(0)] \\Delta_{\\mathrm{o}}=-1.2 \\Delta_{\\text {o }}$ <br>C. In $\\left[\\mathrm{Mn}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}, \\mathrm{Mn}$ is in $ + \\,3$ oxidation state. Hence, electronic configuration is  ${\\rm{t}}_{2\\,{\\rm{g}}}^3{\\rm{e}}_{\\rm{g}}^1$  as $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ does not cause pairing of electrons.CFSE $=[-0.4(3)+0.6(1)] \\Delta_{\\text {o }}=-0.6 \\Delta_{\\text {。 }}$<br>D. In $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$ : Fe is in $ + \\,3$ oxidation state.Hence, its electronic configuration is $\\mathrm{t}_{2 \\mathrm{~g}}^{3} \\mathrm{e}_{\\mathrm{g}}^{2}$  CFSE $=[-0.4(3)+0.6(2)] \\Delta_{\\text {。 }}$ $=(-1.2+1.2) \\Delta_{\\mathrm{o}}=0$<br>Thus, the correct match is  ${\\rm{A}} - {\\rm{S}},{\\rm{ B}} - {\\rm{P}},{\\rm{ C}} - {\\rm{Q}},{\\rm{ D}} - {\\rm{R}}$',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/65_27000_question_1.png'),
(27002,'chemistry',66,1032,2440,'Identify the rate of reaction of given compounds with respect to dehydrohalogenation.','[{"text": "$1>2>3>4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1>3>2>4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2>1>3>4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2>4>1>3$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For dehydrohalogeneration, the reactivity order is $\\mathrm{RI}>\\mathrm{RBr}>\\mathrm{RCl}>\\mathrm{RF}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_27002_question_1.png'),
(27003,'chemistry',65,954,2214,'The set which does not have ambidentate ligand(s) is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}$, ethylene diammine, $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{EDTA}^{4-}, \\\\mathrm{NCS}^{-}, \\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}, \\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}, \\\\mathrm{EDTA}^{4-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{C}}_2}{\\\\rm{O}}_4^{2 - },{\\\\rm{NO}}_2^ - ,{\\\\rm{NC}}{{\\\\rm{S}}^ - }$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}, \\mathrm{NCS}$ are ambidentate ligands.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27003_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27005,'chemistry',66,1028,2419,'If react with $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ in presence of light and then react with $\\mathrm{Na}$ metal in dry ether, final product is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When cyclobutane reacts with $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ in presence of light, substitution reaction takes place. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27005_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The reaction of product with Na metal in dry ether takes place as, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27005_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_27005_question_1.png'),
(27006,'chemistry',65,956,2224,'Which of the following complex has a possibility to exist as meridional isomer?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\\\right)_{3}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\mathrm{en})_{3}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}(\\\\mathrm{en})_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Pt}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Compound of  $\\left[\\mathrm{MA}_{3} \\mathrm{~B}_{3}\\right]$ can form facial (fac) and meridional (mer) isomers.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27006_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27007,'chemistry',66,1031,2435,'Which of the following is not correct?','[{"text": "$\\\\begin{gathered}\\n  {\\\\text{PhC}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{Br}} > {\\\\text{PhCHBrC}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{3}}} \\\\hfill \\\\\\\\\\n  {\\\\mkern 1mu}  > {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\text{PhCBr}}{\\\\left( {{\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{3}}}} \\\\right)_{\\\\text{2}}}\\\\left( {\\\\,{{\\\\text{S}}_{\\\\text{N}}}{\\\\text{1}}} \\\\right) \\\\hfill \\\\\\\\ \\n\\\\end{gathered} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{R}} - {\\\\rm{I}} > {\\\\rm{R}} - {\\\\rm{Br}} > {\\\\rm{R}} - {\\\\rm{Cl}}\\\\left( {{{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{N}}}{\\\\rm{2}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_2} = {\\\\rm{CH}} - {\\\\rm{Cl}} < {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_2} = {\\\\rm{CH}} - {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}} - {\\\\rm{Cl}}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\, < {\\\\rm{PhC}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}} - {\\\\rm{Cl}}\\\\left( {{{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{N}}}{\\\\rm{1}}} \\\\right)\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{R}} - {\\\\rm{Cl}} < {\\\\rm{R}} - {\\\\rm{Br}} < {\\\\rm{R}} - {\\\\rm{I}}\\\\left( {{{\\\\rm{S}}_{\\\\rm{N}}}{\\\\rm{1}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1$ reactions, carbocations are the intermediates. Greater the stability of carbocation, more easily it is formed and, hence faster will be the rate of reaction.<br>The stability of carbocation decreases in the order:<br>${{\\rm{3}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{carbocation}}\\,{\\rm{ > }}{{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{carbocation}}\\,{\\rm{ > }}{{\\rm{1}}^{\\rm{^\\circ }}}{\\rm{carbocation}}{\\rm{.}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{ph}}\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{C(}}}\\limits^{\\rm{ + }} {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{{\\rm{)}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{ > }}\\,{\\rm{ph}}\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{CH}}}\\limits^{\\rm{ + }} \\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{{\\rm{3}}\\,}}{\\rm{ > }}\\,{\\rm{ph}}\\mathop {\\,{\\rm{C}}}\\limits^{\\rm{ + }} {{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3^\\circ  > 2^\\circ  > 1^\\circ $<br> Thus, option (1) is incorrect, while trends in other options are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
