(27017,'chemistry',64,194,1934,'The electronic configuration for neodymium is<br> [Atomic number for neodymium 60]','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{[\\\\mathrm{Xe}] 5 \\\\mathrm{f}^{7} 7 \\\\mathrm{~s}^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{[\\\\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \\\\mathrm{f}^{6} 6 \\\\mathrm{~s}^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{[\\\\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \\\\mathrm{f}^{1} 5 \\\\mathrm{~d}^{1} 6 \\\\mathrm{~s}^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{[\\\\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \\\\mathrm{f}^{4} 6 \\\\mathrm{~s}^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The electronic configuration for neodymium is [Xe] ${\\mathrm{4 \\mathrm{f}^{4} 6 \\mathrm{~s}^{2}}}$. So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27018,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'If $\\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ is replaced by $\\mathrm{Pt}^{2+}$ in the complex $\\left[\\mathrm{NiCl}_{2} \\mathrm{Br}_{2}\\right]^{2-}$, which of the following properties are expected to get changed?<br>A. Geometry<br>B. Geometrical isomerism<br>C. Optical isomerism<br>D. Magnetic properties','[{"text": "B and C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, B and D", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A and D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, B and C", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\left[\\mathrm{NiCl}_{2} \\mathrm{Br}_{2}\\right]^{2-}$ is tetrahedral with $\\mathrm{d}^{8}$ system (with 2 unpaired electrons). It has no stereo isomers. $\\left[\\mathrm{PtCl}_{2} \\mathrm{Br}_{2}\\right]^{2-}$ is square planar with $\\mathrm{d}^{8}$ system (with no unpaired electrons).It shows cis-trans (geometrical isomerism).<br>Thus, $\\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{B}$, and D are expected to get changed.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27019,'chemistry',66,1033,2441,'Identify the major product that is obtained on treatment of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}$ (F) $\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ with $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{-} / \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}\\\\right) \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\mathrm{CHCH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to Saytzeff\'s rule, dehydrohalogenation occurs in such a way that the major product is highly substituted. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27019_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27020,'chemistry',64,194,1936,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In the Lanthanoids, the formation of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ce}^{+4}}}$ is favoured by its noble gas configuration.</div><div class="column-right">${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ce}^{+4}}}$ is a strong oxidant reverting to the common +3 state.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both statements are true. As ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ce}^{+3}}}$ is more stable than ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ce}^{+4}, \\mathrm{Ce}^{+4}}}$ has tendency to accept an electron and thus, it behaves as an strong oxidising agent. So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27021,'chemistry',66,1036,2451,'What is the product \'P\' in the above reaction?','[{"text": "Methyl ethanoate", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ethanoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propanal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Methyl propanoate", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The reaction is as follows: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27021_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Hence, the product \'P\' is methyl-1-ethanoate,',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_27021_question_1.png'),
(27022,'chemistry',64,194,1936,'Which of the following acts as a strong reducing agent?(Atomic number: ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ce}=58, \\mathrm{Eu}=63}}$, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Gd}=64, \\mathrm{Lu}=71)}}$','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Lu}^{3+}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Gd}^{3+}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}}}$ oxidizes readily to give more stable ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}}}$. So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27023,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'The magnetic moment is measured in Bohr Magneton (B.M.). Spin only magnetic moment of Fe in $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$ and   $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]^{3-}$ complexes respectively is','[{"text": "5.92 B.M. and   1.732 B.M.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3.87 B.M. and   1.732 B.M.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.89 B.M. and   6.92 B.M.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6.92 B.M. in both.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In both the complexes, Fe is in $ + \\,3$ oxidation state. $\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$  is a strong field ligand, but $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ is a weak field ligand.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27023_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>There are 5 unpaired electrons. Spin only magnetic moment value $=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{n}(\\mathrm{n}+2)}=\\sqrt{5(5+2)}=\\sqrt{35}=5.92$ B.M.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27023_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>There is only one unpaired electron.<br>Spin only magnetic moment value $=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{n}(\\mathrm{n}+2)}=\\sqrt{1(1+2)}=\\sqrt{3}=1.732$ B.M.<br>Thus, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27024,'chemistry',66,1035,2445,'Among the following, the suitable reagent for Wurtz reaction is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na} /$ alcohol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na} / \\\\mathrm{ether}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Zn} /$ ether", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Zn} /$ alcohol", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The suitable reagent for Wurtz reaction is $\\mathrm{Na} /$ ether. An ethereal solution of an alkyl halide is treated with sodium to produce higher alkanes. This reaction involves the formation of new $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}$ bond.e.g.<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n  {{\\text{2C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{Br}} + {\\text{2Na}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ether}}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{Ethyl}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{bromide}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{Butane}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} } \n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27025,'chemistry',64,194,1937,'Diamagnetic lanthanoid ions are','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{La}^{3+}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Lu}^{3+}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The lanthanoid ions other than the ${\\mathrm{4 \\mathrm{f}^{0}\\left(\\mathrm{La}^{3+}\\right.}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\left.\\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}\\right)}}$ and the ${\\mathrm{4 \\mathrm{f}^{14}}}$ type ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{Yb}^{2+}\\right.}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\left.\\mathrm{Lu}^{3+}\\right)}}$ are all paramagnetic. So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27074,'chemistry',65,956,2223,'The complex compounds ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{5} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right] \\mathrm{Br}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{5} \\mathrm{Br}\\right] \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}}$ are','[{"text": "Coordination isomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Geometrical isomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Optical isomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ionisation isomers", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{5} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right] \\mathrm{Br}}}$ and${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{5} \\mathrm{Br}\\right] \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}}$ are ionisation isomers, as they form different ions on ionisation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27026,'chemistry',65,958,2234,'Match the Column I with Column II.<br> <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - Q ,\\tB - R,\\tC - P,\\t\\tD - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P,\\tB - S,\\tC - Q ,\\tD - R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - P,\\tB - Q ,\\tC - S,\\t\\tD - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,\\tB - S,\\tC - P,\\t\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\left[\\mathrm{Cu}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}$;<br>Oxidation state of Cu is $ + \\,2$ and  $\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ has  ${\\text{t}}_{2{\\text{g}}}^6{\\text{e}}_{\\text{g}}^3$  configuration<br>CFSE $=(-0.4 \\times 6)+(0.6 \\times 3)=-0.6 \\Delta_{0} ;(\\mathrm{A}-\\mathrm{P})$<br>${\\left[ {{\\rm{Ti}}{{\\left( {{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}} \\right)}_6}} \\right]^{3 + }};{\\rm{T}}{{\\rm{i}}^{3 + }}:{\\rm{t}}_{2\\,{\\rm{g}}}^1{\\rm{e}}_{\\rm{g}}^0$<br>CFSE $=(-0.4 \\times 1)=-0.4 \\Delta_{0}:$ $\\quad ({\\rm{B}} - {\\rm{S}})$<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]^{3-} ; \\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}: \\mathrm{t}_{2 \\mathrm{~g}}^{5} \\mathrm{e}_{\\mathrm{g}}^{0}$<br>CFSE $=(-0.4 \\times 5)=-2.0 \\Delta_{0} ;$  $\\quad ({\\rm{C}} - {\\rm{Q}})$<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{NiF}_{6}\\right]^{4-} ; \\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}: \\mathrm{t}_{2 \\mathrm{~g}}^{6} \\mathrm{e}_{\\mathrm{g}}^{2}$<br>CFSE  $ = ( - 0.4 \\times 6) + (2 \\times 0.6) =  - 1.2{\\Delta _0};{\\rm{  }}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} ({\\rm{D}} - {\\rm{R}})$',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/65_27026_question_1.png'),
(27027,'chemistry',66,1031,2436,'When alkyl halide is heated with dry $\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$, it produces','[{"text": "ester", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ether", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ketone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "alcohol", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Alkyl halide on reaction with dry silver oxide $\\left(\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)$ gives ether <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27027_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27029,'chemistry',65,962,2242,'The mismatched combinations are<br>A. Chlorophyll - Co<br>B. Water hardness - EDTA<br>C. Photography \\(-\\left[\\operatorname{Ag}(\\mathrm{CN})_{2}\\right]\\)<br>D. Wilkinson\'s catalyst - \\(\\left[\\left(\\mathrm{Ph}_{3} \\mathrm{P}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{RhCl}\\right]\\)<br>E. Chelating ligand -D-Penicillamine<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below :<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "D and E only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A and E only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, C and E only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A and C only", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Chlorophyll : Mg<br>Photography : $\\left[\\mathrm{Ag}\\left(\\mathrm{S}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}\\right)_{2}\\right]^{3-}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27030,'chemistry',66,1036,2451,'Given reaction, \' Y \' in the reaction is','[{"text": "hexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "cyclohexane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "cyclohexylcyclohexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "cyclohexyl ether", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27030_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_27030_question_1.png'),
(27031,'chemistry',64,192,1925,'Out of the 5 different products formed in the following reaction, how many contains O -atom(s)? ____ .<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}+\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\longrightarrow}}$','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The chemical reaction is<br>${\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}+5 \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}+8 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}}$<br>$\\begin{array}{r}\\longrightarrow \\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}+2 \\mathrm{MnSO}_{4}+5 \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\\\+8 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+5 \\mathrm{O}_{2}\\end{array}$<br>Hence, number of products containing O -atom(s) is 5 .',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27032,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'Which of the following complexes will exhibit maximum attraction to an applied magnetic field?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\operatorname{Co}(\\\\mathrm{en})_{3}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Zn}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'More is the number of unpaired electrons, greater is the paramagnetism and thus, higher is the attraction towards magnetic field.<br>The number of unpaired electrons in $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+},\\left[\\mathrm{Co}(\\mathrm{en})_{3}\\right]^{3+},\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}$  and  $\\left[\\mathrm{Zn}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}$  are respectively  $3,0,2,0$. So, $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{2+}$ will exhibit maximum attraction towards magnetic field.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27033,'chemistry',66,1031,2433,'Which of the following alkyl halides is hydrolysed by $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ mechanism?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Br}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Br}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CHCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Br}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{CBr}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${{\\text{S}}_{\\text{N}}}2$ mechanism involves the back side attack and formation of a transition state, thus less hindered alkyl halide readily undergoes $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ mechanism. Among the given, $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Br}$ (methyl bromide) is less hindered, thus it is hydrolysed by $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ mechanism.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27035,'chemistry',65,956,2226,'The total number of stereoisomers for the complex  $\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}(\\mathrm{ox})_{2} \\mathrm{ClBr}\\right]^{3-}$  (where ox = oxalate) is','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Total number of stereoisomers will be three.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27035_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>It has plane of symmetry, so it is optically inactive.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27035_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>It has no plane of symmetry. Thus, can show two optical isomers: d-form and $1-$ form. Therefore, there can be a total of three stereoisomers.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27142,'chemistry',67,1074,2563,'Which of the following is the best preparation of meta cresol, starting from toluene?','[{"text": "$\\\\xrightarrow[{{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{S}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{4}}}}]{{{\\\\text{HN}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{3}}}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\xrightarrow[{{\\\\text{HCl}}}]{{{\\\\text{Zn}}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\xrightarrow[{0^\\\\circ {\\\\text{C}}}]{{{\\\\text{HN}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{2}}}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\xrightarrow{{{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{(v)}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\xrightarrow[{{\\\\text{FeB}}{{\\\\text{r}}_{\\\\text{3}}}}]{{{\\\\text{B}}{{\\\\text{r}}_{\\\\text{2}}}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\xrightarrow[{{\\\\text{Heat}}}]{{{\\\\text{KOH}}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\xrightarrow{{{\\\\text{HCl}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\xrightarrow[{{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{S}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{4}}}}]{{{\\\\text{HN}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{3}}}}}\\\\xrightarrow[{{\\\\text{(ii)(C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{3}}}{\\\\text{CO}}{{\\\\text{)}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{O}}}]{{{\\\\text{(i)Zn/HCl}}}}\\\\xrightarrow[{{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{S}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{4}}}}]{{{\\\\text{HN}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{3}}}}}$<br>$\\\\xrightarrow[{{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{O}}}]{{{{\\\\text{H}}^{{\\\\text{    +    }}}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\xrightarrow[{0^\\\\circ {\\\\text{C}}}]{{{\\\\text{HN}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{2}}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\xrightarrow{{{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{3}}}{\\\\text{P}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{2}}}}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\xrightarrow[{{\\\\text{HCl}}}]{{{\\\\text{Zn}}}}$<br>$\\\\xrightarrow[{0^\\\\circ {\\\\text{C}}}]{{{\\\\text{HN}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{2}}}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\xrightarrow{{{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{O(v)}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\xrightarrow[{{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{S}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{4}}}}]{{{\\\\text{HN}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{3}}}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\xrightarrow[{{\\\\text{(ii)}}\\\\,{{{\\\\text{(C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{3}}}{\\\\text{CO)}}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{O}}}]{{{\\\\text{(i)Zn/HCl}}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\xrightarrow[{{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{S}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{4}}}}]{{{\\\\text{HN}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{3}}}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\xrightarrow[{{\\\\text{HCl}}}]{{{\\\\text{Zn}}}}$<br>$\\\\xrightarrow[{0^\\\\circ {\\\\text{C}}}]{{{\\\\text{HN}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{2}}}}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\xrightarrow{{{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}{\\\\text{(O)(v)}}}}\\\\,\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_27142_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27036,'chemistry',66,1031,2435,'The reactivities of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Cl}, \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}$ and $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{Cl}$ are in the order','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}>\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}>\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{Cl}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}>\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{Cl}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Bulky group causes steric hindrance in the<br> formation of transition state. <br>Therefore, higher homologous are less reactive than  lower homologous.<br>$\\therefore $Therefore correct order is<br>${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{Cl}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{ > }}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{Cl}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{ > }}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{6}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{Cl}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27037,'chemistry',65,958,2234,'The complex with highest magnitude of crystal field splitting energy $\\left(\\Delta_{0}\\right)$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Mn}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{OH}_{2}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cr}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{OH}_{2}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Fe}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{OH}_{2}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ti}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{OH}_{2}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{CFSE}=(-0.4 \\mathrm{x}+0.6 \\mathrm{y}) \\Delta_{\\text {o }}$<br>where, x =  No. of electrons in $t_{2 g}$<br>y =  No. of electrons in $e_{g}$<br>Here, water is a weak field ligand.<br>$ \\bullet {\\text{ In }}{\\left[ {\\operatorname{Mn} {{\\left( {{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}} \\right)}_6}} \\right]^{3 + }},$<br>Electronic configuration of  ${\\text{M}}{{\\text{n}}^{3 + }}:[{\\text{Ar}}]\\,3{{\\text{d}}^4}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27037_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>${\\rm{CFSE}} = ( - 0.4 \\times 3) + (0.6 \\times 1){\\Delta _0}$<br>$=-0.6 \\Delta_{\\text {o }}$<br>$ \\bullet $   $\\operatorname{In}\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathbf{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$,<br>Electronic configuration of   $\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{3}$<br>CFSE $=(-0.4 \\times 3)+(0.6 \\times 0) \\Delta_{\\text {o }}=-1.2 \\Delta_{\\text {o }}$<br>$ \\bullet $   In $\\left[\\mathbf{F e}\\left(\\mathbf{H}_{2} \\mathbf{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$,<br>Electronic configuration of  $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5}$<br>$\\operatorname{CFSE}=(-0.4 \\times 3)+(0.6 \\times 2) \\Delta_{\\text {o }}=0$<br>$ \\bullet $   $\\operatorname{In}\\left[\\mathbf{T i}\\left(\\mathbf{H}_{2} \\mathbf{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$,<br>Electronic configuration of $\\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{1}$<br>CFSE $=(-0.4 \\times 1)+(0.6 \\times 0)=-0.4 \\Delta_{\\text {o }}$ <br>Hence, $\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$ has the highest magnitude of crystal field splitting energy.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27038,'chemistry',66,1031,2433,'Which one of the following species will be most reactive in $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ reaction?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ is an inversion reaction, so will be favoured by least sterically hindered carbon atom or a primary carbon atom. Hence, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27038_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   will be most reactive in $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27040,'chemistry',66,1028,2419,'The alkyl halides that can be made by free radical halogenation of alkanes are','[{"text": "RCl and RBr but not RF or RI", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "RF, RCl and RBr but not RI", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RF, RCl, RBr, RI", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RF, RCl and RI but not RBr", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'RCl and RBr can be prepared by free radical halogenation of alkanes while RF and RI cannot be prepared. With $\\mathrm{F}_{2}$, the reaction is not only explosive but also brings cleavage of $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}$ bonds while with $\\mathrm{I}_{2}$, the reaction is too slow that it does not have any practical value.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27042,'chemistry',66,1032,2440,'A mixture of 1-chlorobutane and 2-chlorobutane when treated with alcoholic $\\mathrm{KOH}$ gives','[{"text": "1-butene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-butene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iso-butylene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "mixture of 1-butene +2 -butene", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'On treatment with alcoholic $\\mathrm{KOH}$, <br>1-chlorobutane gives 1-butene while 2-chlorobutane gives 2-butene (major) + 1-butene (minor). Therefore, a mixture of 1-butene +2 -butene is formed.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27044,'chemistry',66,1030,2427,'Which of the following is liquid at room temperature?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{I}-66^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}, 42^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Br}-94^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}, 3^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{Cl}-139^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}, 12^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~F}-115^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C},-78^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Br}\\left(\\mathrm{b} \\cdot \\mathrm{p} \\cdot=3^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}\\right), \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{~F}\\left(\\mathrm{~b} \\cdot \\mathrm{p} \\cdot=-78^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}\\right)$ and $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{Cl}$ ( $\\mathrm{bp}=12^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ ) are all gases at room temperature as their boiling point is much less than room temperature, i.e. they boil (vaporise) at a temperature much less than room temperature. But $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{I}$ has boiling point $42^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ which is above the room temperature so, it is liquid at room temperature and solidifies at around $-66^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27045,'chemistry',65,959,2237,'Match Column I with Column II :<br> <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - R,\\tB - Q ,\\tC - P,\\t\\t    D - S", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Q ,\\tB - R,\\tC - S,\\t\\t    D - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S,\\tB - P,\\tC - R,\\t    D - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,\\tB - P,\\tC - Q ,\\t    D - S", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Stronger the ligand, higher will be the CFSE value thus, order of energy is<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{Co}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]^{3-}>\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}>$<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{CoCl}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{5}\\right]^{2+}>\\left[\\mathrm{Cu}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{4}\\right]^{2+}$<br>As energy is inversely proportional to wavelength, hence order of wavelength will be<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27045_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/65_27045_question_1.png'),
(27215,'chemistry',67,1084,2603,'Ethers are made free from peroxide linkage on distilling impure sample with','[{"text": "Conc. $$\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conc. $$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Conc. $$\\\\mathrm{HCl}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Peroxides are decomposed on heating with $$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27046,'chemistry',66,1036,2450,'The order of reactivities of methyl halide in the formation of Grignard reagent is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Br}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{I}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{I}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Br}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Br}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{I}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Br}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{I}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Order of reactivities of alkyl halides depends upon ${\\rm{C}} - {\\rm{X}}$ bond length. As, on going down the group, the size of halogen atom increases. Therefore, ${\\rm{C}} - {\\rm{X}}$ bond becomes weaker.Hence, the order of reactivities of methyl halide in the formation of Grignard reagent is, <br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{I}>\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Br}>\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{Cl}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27048,'chemistry',66,1031,2436,'Hydrolysis of trichloromethane with aqueous $\\mathrm{KOH}$ gives','[{"text": "methanol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "acetic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ethanol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "formic acid", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Trichloromethane on hydrolysis with aqueous $\\mathrm{KOH}$ give formic acid.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27048_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27049,'chemistry',65,956,2225,'Chiral complex from the following is <br>(Here, en $=$ ethylene diamine)','[{"text": "cis - $\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "trans $-\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}(\\\\text { en })_{2}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "trans - $\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right]^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "cis $-\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}(\\\\mathrm{en})_{2}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The chiral complex is cis $ - $ $\\left[\\mathrm{PtCl}_{2}(\\mathrm{en})_{2}\\right]^{2+}$ as there is no element of symmetry.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27049_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27050,'chemistry',66,1037,2455,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ is stored in closed, dark coloured bottles completely filled so that air is kept out.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $1^{\\circ}$ amines on reaction with ethanolic $\\mathrm{KOH}$ and $\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ gives carbylamine.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Both $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{R}$ are correct and $\\mathrm{R}$ is not the correct explanation of $\\mathrm{A}$.<br>$\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ is stored in dark coloured bottles and properly closed to keep out air because in the presence of air or light, it gets slowly oxidised to poisonous gas phosgene (carbonyl chloride).<br>${{\\text{CHC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{3}}} + \\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_2}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{light}}}]{{{\\text{Air}}\\,{\\text{or}}}}\\mathop {{\\text{COC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{\\,{\\text{(Phosgene)}}} {\\text{ + }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{HCl}}}$<br>The reaction of $1^\\circ $ amine with ethanolic KOH and  ${\\text{CHC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{3}}}$ is shown below.<br>${\\rm{RN}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + }}\\,{\\rm{alc}}{\\rm{.KOH}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{ + }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{CHC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}} \\to \\mathop {{\\rm{RNC}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{Carbylamine}}} $',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27052,'chemistry',66,1037,2455,'Which of the following is added to chloroform to slow down its aerial oxidation in presence of light?','[{"text": "Carbonyl chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethyl alcohol", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Sodium hydroxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nitric acid", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Chloroform is oxidised to a poisonous gas, by aerial oxidation.<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{CHC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}} + [{\\rm{O}}] \\to \\mathop {{\\rm{COC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{Phosgene}}}  + {\\rm{HCl}}}\\\\\n{{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} }\n\\end{array}$<br>Ethyl alcohol is used as a negative catalyst for the aerial oxidation of chloroform in presence of light. It converts $\\mathrm{COCl}_{2}$ to non-poisonous ethyl carbonate.<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{COC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\,{\\rm{ + }}\\,\\,{\\rm{2}}{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{OH}} \\to {\\rm{O}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ = }}\\,\\,{\\rm{C(O}}{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{{\\rm{)}}_{\\rm{2}}}\\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{ + }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{2HCl}}\n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27053,'chemistry',66,1036,2450,'Chloropicrin is obtained by the reaction of','[{"text": "steam on carbon tetrachloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "nitric acid on chlorobenzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "chlorine on picric acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "nitric acid on chloroform", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Chloroform on reaction with nitric acid give chloropicrin (nitro chloroform) according to following reaction.<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{CHC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ + HN}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{3}}} \\to {\\rm{C(N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{)C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ + }}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}\\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{Nitrochloroform}}\\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(chloropicrin)}}\n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27075,'chemistry',66,1034,2442,'Reactivity order of the following towards $\\mathrm{NaOEt}, \\mathrm{EtOH}$','[{"text": "III $>$ II $>$ I", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II $>$ I $>$ III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I $>$ II $>$ III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III $>$ I $>$ II", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Nucleophilic substitution of aryl halides is favoured by electron withdrawing substituents attached at ortho and para positions to the halide atom. So, reactivity order of given compounds towards $\\mathrm{NaOEt}$ and $\\mathrm{EtOH}$ is <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27075_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_27075_question_1.png'),
(27216,'chemistry',68,1108,2642,'Aspirin is known as','[{"text": "Methyl salicylic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acetyl salicylate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acetyl salicylic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Phenyl salicylate", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Aspirin is acetyl derivative of salicyclic acid.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27054,'chemistry',65,955,2222,'Correct order of spin only magnetic moment of the following complex ions is','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{FeF}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}>\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}\\\\right)_{3}\\\\right]^{3-}>\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}\\\\right)_{3}\\\\right]^{3-}>\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}>\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{FeF}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{FeF}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}>\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}>\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}\\\\right)_{3}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}>\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{FeF}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}>\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}\\\\right)_{3}\\\\right]^{3-}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\bullet \\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{5}$<br>In presence of weak field ligand,<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{FeF}_{6}\\right]^{3-}:$<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27054_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\mu=\\sqrt{\\mathrm{n}(\\mathrm{n}+2)}=\\sqrt{5(5+2)}=\\sqrt{35}=5.916$ B.M.<br>$ \\bullet $   $\\mathrm{Co}^{3+}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{6}$<br>In the presence of strong field ligand,<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)_{3}\\right]^{3}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27054_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\mathrm{n}=0, \\quad \\mu=0$<br>$\\bullet \\mathrm{Co}^{3+}:[\\mathrm{Ar}] 3 \\mathrm{~d}^{6}$ the presence of weak field ligand, $\\left[\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\right]^{3-}$ :<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27054_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\mathrm{n}=4$$\\mu=\\sqrt{4(4+2)}=\\sqrt{24}=4.89$B.M.<br>Hence, the correct order of spin only magnetic moments is<br>$\\left[\\mathrm{FeF}_{6}\\right]^{3-}>\\left[\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\right]^{3-}>\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)_{3}\\right]^{3-}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27055,'chemistry',66,1037,2458,'Freon used as refrigerant is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CF}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CF}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~F}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CCl}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~F}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CF}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Freon used as refrigerant is $\\mathrm{CCl}_{2} \\mathrm{~F}_{2}$.<br>${{\\text{CC}}{{\\text{l}}_4} + 2{\\text{HF}}\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{F}}_2}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{l}}_2}}\\limits_{\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {{\\text{Freon}}} \\\\ \n  {\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {({\\text{dichlorodifluoro}}} \\\\ \n  {{\\text{methane}})} \n\\end{array}} \n\\end{array}}  + 2{\\text{HCl}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27057,'chemistry',65,958,2234,'Which of the following is correct order of ligand field strength?','[{"text": "S$^{2-} < \\\\mathrm{NH}_{3} < $ en $ < \\\\mathrm{CO} < \\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3} < $ en $ < \\\\mathrm{CO} < \\\\mathrm{S}^{2-} < \\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CO} < \\\\mathrm{en} < \\\\mathrm{NH}_{3} < \\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-} < \\\\mathrm{S}^{2-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}^{2-} < \\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{4}^{2-} < \\\\mathrm{NH}_{3} < $ en $ < \\\\mathrm{CO}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{I}^{-} < \\mathrm{Br}^{-} < \\mathrm{S}^{2}- < \\mathrm{Cl}^{-} < \\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-} < \\mathrm{F}^{-}$<br>$ < \\mathrm{OH}^{-} < \\mathrm{EtOH} < $ oxalate $ < \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} < $ EDTA<br>$ < \\mathrm{NH}_{3} < $ en $ < \\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-} < \\mathrm{CN}^{-} < \\mathrm{CO}$<br>As per the given spectrochemical series, the correct order is option (4).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27058,'chemistry',66,1034,2442,'Which one is least reactive towards nucleophilic substitution reaction?','[{"text": "o-aminochlorobenzene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Chlorobenzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "o-nitrochlorobenzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2, 4-dinitrochlorobenzene", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In nucleophilic substitution, for aromatic compounds like benzene, nucleophile displaces a good leaving group like halide on aromatic ring. Electron withdrawing groups also activate the ring towards nucleophilic attack. For e.g. nitro group at ortho and para-position, halide in 2, 4-dinitrochlorobenzene. In 2, 4-dinitrochlorobenzene, nitro group is present at ortho and para-position and activates the ring. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27058_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  But electron donating groups like- $\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (if at ortho,para-position) will decrease the nucleophilic substitution reaction. Hence, (1) is the correct option.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
