(27067,'chemistry',65,959,2237,'Cobalt chloride when dissolved in water forms pink coloured complex X which has octahedral geometry. This solution on treating with conc. HCl forms deep blue complex Y which has a Z geometry. $\\mathrm{X}, \\mathrm{Y}$ and Z , respectively are','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{X}=\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right]^{+}, \\\\mathrm{Y}=\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoCl}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}, \\\\mathrm{Z}=$ Tetrahedral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{X}=\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}, \\\\mathrm{Y}=\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoCl}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}, \\\\mathrm{Z}=$Octahedral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{X}=\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{2+}, \\\\mathrm{Y}=\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoCl}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}, \\\\mathrm{Z}=$ Tetrahedral", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{X}=\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Co}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right]^{3+}, \\\\mathrm{Y}=\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{CoCl}_{6}\\\\right]^{3-}, \\\\mathrm{Z}=$ Octahedral", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27067_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27068,'chemistry',66,1034,2443,'Chlorobenzene is ____ reactive than benzene towards electrophilic substitution and directs the incoming electrophile to the ____  position.','[{"text": "more, ortho/para", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "less, ortho/para", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "more, meta", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "less, meta", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'ortho-para directing groups activate the benzene ring towards electrophilic substitution while meta directing groups deactivate the benzene ring towards electrophilic substitution. $-\\mathrm{F},-\\mathrm{Cl}$, $-\\mathrm{Br}$ and -I (i.e. halogens) are exceptions to the above rule. These groups are o-, p-directing but deactivate the benzene ring.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27069,'chemistry',66,1027,2418,'Among the following, which one has the weakest carbon-halogen bond?','[{"text": "Benzyl bromide", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bromobenzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vinyl bromide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Benzyl chloride", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{C}} - {\\rm{X}}$ bond in benzyl bromide is much weaker than in vinyl bromide and bromobenzene since the benzyl cation left after the removal of the bromide ion is stabilised by resonance. Further, $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{Br}$ bond is weaker than $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{Cl}$ bond due to larger size of $-\\mathrm{Br}$. Therefore, $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{Br}$ has the weakest ${\\rm{C}} - {\\rm{X}}$ bond.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27071,'chemistry',66,1025,2416,'Write the IUPAC name of given structure:','[{"text": "para-dibromobenzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1,4-dibromobenzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2) are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "meta-dibromo benzene", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The IUPAC name of the given structure <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27071_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  is 1,4-dibromobenzene or para dibromobenzene.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_27071_question_1.png'),
(27072,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'On treating 100 mL of 0.1 M aqueous solution of the complex ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CrCl}_{3} \\cdot 6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$ with excess of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}, 2.86 \\mathrm{~g}}}$ of AgCl was obtained. The complex is','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cr}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{3}\\\\right] \\\\cdot 3 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cr}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl} \\\\cdot 2 \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cr}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{5} \\\\mathrm{Cl}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\\\cdot \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Cr}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}\\\\right)_{6}\\\\right] \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'No. of moles of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{AgCl}=\\dfrac{2.86}{143}=0.02}}$ moles <br>0.01 moles of an aqueous solution of${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CrCl}_{3} \\cdot 6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$ gives 0.02 moles AgCl means, 1 mole of aqueous solution gives 2 moles of AgCl .<br>so, ${\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}}}$ions should satisfy only primary valency and the compound will be ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{5} \\mathrm{Cl}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{2} \\cdot \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27073,'chemistry',66,1036,2449,'Which of the following does not have a metal-carbon bond?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Al}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{OC}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}\\\\right)_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{MgBr}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Pt}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{4}\\\\right) \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{3}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ni}(\\\\mathrm{CO})_{4}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{Al}\\left(\\mathrm{OC}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{3}$ does not have metal-carbon bond, i.e. it is not an example of organometallic compound. Its structure is  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27073_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27076,'chemistry',65,957,2228,'Which of the following statements are true about${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\right]^{3-}}}$ ion?<br>(I) The complex has octahedral geometry.<br>(II) Coordination number of Co is 3 and oxidation state is +6 .<br>(III) The complex is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}^{2}}}$ hybridised<br>(IV) It is a high spin complex','[{"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Coordination number is 6 and oxidation state is +3 for Cobalt.<br>I, III and IV statements are true.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27077,'chemistry',66,1031,2433,'Which one of the following undergoes $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ reaction faster?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27077_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> is a primary halide and therefore undergoes faster $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ reaction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27078,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Both ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}}}$ and\n${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\right]^{3-}}}$ complexes are octahedral but differ in their magnetic behaviour.</div><div class="column-right">${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}}}$ is diamagnetic\nwhereas ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\right]^{3-}}}$ is paramagnetic.\n choose the correct answer from the options given below:</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}, \\mathrm{Co}^{3+}}}$ ion is having ${\\mathrm{3 \\mathrm{~d}^{6}}}$ configuration.<br>Electronic configuration of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Co}^{3+}}}$ :<br>  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27078_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   In presence of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$ ligand, pairing of electrons takes place and it becomes diamagnetic complex ion.<br>In presence of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$ ligand:  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27078_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  ${\\mathrm{\\therefore\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}}}$ is octahedral with ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{d}^{2} \\mathrm{sp}^{3}}}$ hybridisation and it is diamagnetic in nature.<br>In case of ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\right]^{3-}}}$, Co is in +3 oxidation state and it is having ${\\mathrm{3 \\mathrm{~d}^{6}}}$ configuration.<br>In presence of weak field ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{F}^{-}}}$ligand, pairing does not take place.<br>In presence of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{F}^{-}}}$ligands: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27078_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  ${\\mathrm{\\therefore \\ln \\left[\\mathrm{CoF}_{6}\\right]^{3-}, \\mathrm{Co}^{3+}}}$ is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}^{2}}}$ hybridised with four unpaired electrons, so it is paramagnetic in nature.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27079,'chemistry',66,1038,2461,'Which one of the following alkylbromides undergoes most rapid solvolysis in methanol solution to give corresponding methyl ether?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'1-bromo-3-methylbut-2-ene reacts most readily by $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1$ because the bromine is allylic.$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}$ is a weak nucleophile and weak nucleophile favours $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1$. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27079_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27080,'chemistry',65,956,2223,'Match Column I (Complex) with Column II (Types of isomerism).<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A.${\\rm{[Co(N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{{\\rm{)}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{(N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{)]C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{2}}}$<br>B.${\\rm{[Co(N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{{\\rm{)}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{(S}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{)]Br}}$<br>C.${\\rm{[Co(N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{{\\rm{)}}_{\\rm{6}}}{\\rm{][Cr(CN}}{{\\rm{)}}_{\\rm{6}}}{\\rm{]}}$<br>D.${\\rm{[Co(}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{O}}{{\\rm{)}}_{\\rm{6}}}{\\rm{]C}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}}$</div><div class="column-right">P.Solvate isomerism<br>Q.Linkage isomerism<br>R.Ionisation isomerism<br>S.Coordination isomerism</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q,B}} - {\\\\rm{R,C}} - {\\\\rm{S,D}} - {\\\\rm{P}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P,B}} - {\\\\rm{R,C}} - {\\\\rm{S,D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P,B}} - {\\\\rm{S,C}} - {\\\\rm{R,D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q,B}} - {\\\\rm{S,C}} - {\\\\rm{R,D}} - {\\\\rm{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'(A) ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{5}\\left(\\mathrm{NO}_{2}\\right)\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}-(\\mathrm{Q})}}$ Linkage isomerism as ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}}$ ligand can be attached as ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}}$ or -ONO .<br>(B) ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{5}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}\\right)\\right] \\mathrm{Br}-(\\mathrm{R})}}$ Ionization isomerism due to ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{-2} \\& \\mathrm{Br}^{-}}}$ ions.<br>(C) ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]-}}$(S) Coordination isomerism, as both cation and anion are complexes.<br>(D) ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{3}}}$ - (P) Solvate isomerism as ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$ is acting as ligand.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27081,'chemistry',66,1031,2433,'Which will undergo fastest $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ substitution reaction when treated with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ ?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Primary alkyl halides undergo bimolecular nucleophilic substitution most readily. Thus, $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{Br}$ undergoes fastest  $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ substitution reaction with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27082,'chemistry',65,954,2220,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}}}$ is a homoleptic complex whereas ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right]^{+}}}$is a heteroleptic complex.</div><div class="column-right">Complex ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}}}$ has only one kind of ligands but ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right]^{+}}}$ has more than one kind of ligands.\n choose the correct answer from the options given below.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Only one type of ligands - homoleptic complex.<br>More than one type of ligands - heteroleptic complex.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27092,'chemistry',65,954,2217,'What is the oxidation number of Cr in the dimeric hydroxo bridged species?','[{"text": "+6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ + 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right.}}$ is neutral and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{OH}^{-}}}$ is with – 1 charge.)<br>$2{\\rm{ x}} + 2( - 1) =  + \\,4$<br>$2{\\rm{ x}} - 2 =  + \\,4{\\rm{ }}$<br>$2{\\rm{ x}} =  + \\,6{\\rm{ }}$<br>${\\rm{x}} =  + \\,3$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/65_27092_question_1.png'),
(31227,'physics',18,124,444,'Magnifying power of simple microscope is (when final image is formed at  $D = 25\\,cm$ from eye)','[{"text": "$1 - \\\\frac{D}{f}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 + \\\\frac{D}{f}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{D}{f}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 + \\\\frac{f}{D}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27083,'chemistry',66,1031,2432,'The $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1$ reactivity of the following halides will be in the order<br>I. $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{CBr}$<br>II. $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{CHBr}$<br>III. $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{C}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right) \\mathrm{Br}$<br>IV. $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{CHBr}$<br>V. $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{Br}$','[{"text": "II $>$ I $>$ III $>$ V $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I $>$ III $>$ V $>$ II $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "V $>$ I $>$ II $>$ IV $>$ III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III $>$ II $>$ I $>$ IV $>$ V", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1$ (unimolecular nucleophilic substitution) reactions,<br>Rate $\\propto$ [substrate]<br>Rate determining step is the formation of carbocation and depends on the stability of carbocation formed. The stability of carbocations follow the order  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27083_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Thus, the order of reactivity towards reaction is <br>(III) $>$(II) $>$ (I) $>$ (IV) $>$ V',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27084,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'Select CORRECT statements about $\\mathrm{PtCl}_{4}$. $4 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ complex salt<br>I. All the chlorides are bonded by primary valency.<br>II. All the chlorides are bonded by secondary valency.<br>III. All the $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ are bonded by secondary valency.<br>IV. Half of the chlorides are bonded with secondary valency.','[{"text": "I, III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III, IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II, III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}}$ So, all four ammonia molecules and half of chloride ions (2) satisfy secondary valency.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27085,'chemistry',66,1031,2432,'$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1$ reaction of alkyl halides leads to','[{"text": "retention of configuration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "racemisation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "inversion of configuration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1$ reactions involve racemisation, i.e. when an optically active $\\mathrm{d}$ (act as reactant) form is subjected to $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1$ reaction, the product obtained is a racemic mixture of  d and  l (forms).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27086,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'Which of the following has the highest molar conductivity under similar conditions?','[{"text": "Diamminedichloroplatinum(II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tetraamminedichlorocobalt(III) chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Potassium hexacyanoferrate(II)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hexaaquachromium(III) chloride", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right] \\rightarrow}}$ No.of ions in aq. solution is 0.<br>${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\right] \\rightarrow}}$ No. of ions in aq. solution is 2 .<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{4}\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right] \\rightarrow}}$ No. of ions in aq. solution is 5 <br>${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Cr}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{6}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}_{3} \\rightarrow}}$ No. of ions in aq. solution is 4 .<br>So, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{K}_{4}\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{CN})_{6}\\right]}}$ will show highest conductivity, as it possesses highest no. of ions.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27087,'chemistry',66,1031,2437,'The following reaction is described as','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}_{\\\\mathrm{E}} 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}_{\\\\mathrm{N}} 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}_{\\\\mathrm{N}} 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}_{\\\\mathrm{N}} 0$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27087_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> In this reactions, inversion takes place.Hence, it is an example of $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ reaction. In this mechanism, the attack of $\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ion takes place from the back side, while the ${\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{r}}^ - }{\\rm{ion}}\\,{\\rm{leaves}}\\,{\\rm{from}}$ the front side.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_27087_question_1.png'),
(27088,'chemistry',67,1074,2562,'In Dow\'s process haloarene is converted to phenol with fused $$\\mathrm{NaOH}$$. The most reactive compound is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All are equally reactive", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27089,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'In the compound, $\\mathrm{CoCl}_{3} .5 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$','[{"text": "All the Cl atoms show primary valency only.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two Cl atoms show primary valency and one Cl atom shows secondary valency.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two Cl atoms show primary valency and one Cl atom shows primary valency as well as secondary valency.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All the Cl atoms show secondary valency.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Werner representation:<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27089_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>two ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}^{-} \\rightarrow}}$ primary valency<br>one ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}^{-} \\rightarrow}}$ primary as well as secondary',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27090,'chemistry',66,1031,2432,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> (A) In $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1$ reaction on 2 bromobutane, product formed is a racemic mixture.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> (R) Carbocation is the intermediate of ${\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{N}}^{\\rm{1}}}$ reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'2-bromobutane is an optically active alkyl halide. When it undergoes $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1$ reaction, the intermediate carbocation is formed which is a planar species, therefore the attack of nucleophile can take place with equal ease from both the faces.Thus, the product formed is a racemic mixture ( $\\pm$ ) which is optically inactive. Hence, Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27091,'chemistry',67,1083,2600,'In the Liebermann\'s nitroso reaction, sequential changes in the colour of phenol occurs as','[{"text": "Red $$\\\\rightarrow$$ green $$\\\\rightarrow$$ white", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Brown or red $$\\\\rightarrow$$ green $$\\\\rightarrow$$ red $$\\\\rightarrow$$ deep blue", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Red $$\\\\rightarrow$$ deep blue $$\\\\rightarrow$$ green", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Red $$\\\\rightarrow$$ green $$\\\\rightarrow$$ white", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27093,'chemistry',66,1034,2442,'Order of reactivity towards nucleophilic substitution reaction of the compounds','[{"text": "I >II > III > IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II $>$ I $>$ III $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV $>$ III $>$ II $>$ I", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "III > IV > II > I", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Nucleophile will preferentially attack on the compound which has lower electron density. Out of the given options, (IV) has four electron withdrawing groups and is the most preferred substrate.As, the number of electrons withdrawing groups decreases or the number of electron donating groups increases, the electron density of the compound increases. Hence, the reactivity towards nucleophilic substitution reaction decreases.So, the order is <br>(IV)  > (III) > (II) >  (I)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/66_27093_question_1.png'),
(27094,'chemistry',67,1077,2572,'$$0.037 \\mathrm{~g}$$ of an alcohol, $$\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{OH}$$ was added to $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{MgBr}$$ and the gas evolved measured 11.2 $$\\mathrm{mL}$$ at STP. The molecular mass of $$\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{OH}$$ will be','[{"text": "47", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "79", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "74", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "77", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathop {{\\rm{R - OH}}}\\limits_{{\\rm{1}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{mol}}} {\\rm{ + C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{MgBr}} \\to \\mathop {{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}}\\limits_{\\scriptstyle{\\rm{1 mol }}\\atop\n{\\scriptstyle{\\rm{ or }}\\atop\n\\scriptstyle{\\rm{22400}}\\,{\\rm{mL at STP}}}} {\\rm{ + Mg(Br)OR}}$<br>$11.2\\,{\\text{mL  C}}{{\\text{H}}_4}$ at STP is formed from<br>$$0.037 \\mathrm{~g}$$ $$\\mathrm{ROH}$$.<br>$\\therefore 22400\\,{\\text{mL  C}}{{\\text{H}}_4}$ at STP will be formed from $\\frac{{{\\text{0}}{\\text{.037}}}}{{{\\text{11}}{\\text{.2}}}} \\times {\\text{22400 g ROH  =  74 g ROH}}$<br>$$\\therefore \\quad$$ Molecular mass of $$\\mathrm{ROH}=74$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27095,'chemistry',65,954,2217,'The oxidation state of Mo in its oxo-complex species $\\left[\\mathrm{Mo}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{4}\\right)_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{2}\\right]^{-2}$ is','[{"text": "+2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "+3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "+4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ + 5$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Mo}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{4}\\right)_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)_{2}\\right]^{2-}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{x}+(-2) 4+(0) 2+(0) 2=-2}}$<br>${\\mathrm{2 \\mathrm{x}-8=-2}}$<br>${\\mathrm{2 x=-2+8}}$<br>${\\mathrm{x=+3}}$<br>Oxidation number of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Mo}=+3}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27096,'chemistry',66,1031,2435,'Which of the following is least reactive in nucleophilic substitution reaction?','[{"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CHCl}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CHCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Chlorine of vinyl chloride $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\mathrm{CHCl}\\right) \\mathrm{s}$ non-reactive (less reactive) towards nucleophile in nucleophilic substitution reaction because it shows the following resonating structures due to + M-effect of $-\\mathrm{Cl}$ atom. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27096_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  In structure $\\mathrm{II}, \\mathrm{Cl}$-atom has positive charge and partial double bond character with $\\mathrm{C}$ of vinyl group, so it is more strongly attracted towards the nucleus and it does not get replaced by nucleophile in $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}$ reaction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27097,'chemistry',67,1077,2577,'A compound of the formula ${{\\text{C}}_{\\text{4}}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{10}}}}{\\text{O}}$  reacts with and undergoes oxidation to give a carbonyl compound which does not reduce Tollen\'s reagent, the original compound is','[{"text": "Diethyl ether", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "n-Butyl alcohol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isobutyl alcohol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sec-Butyl alcohol", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Since the compound $$\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}$$ reacts with sodium, it must be alcohol (option 2, 3 or 4). As it is oxidised to carbonyl compound which does not reduce Tollen\'s reagent, the carbonyl compound should be a ketone and thus $$\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}$$ should be a secondary alcohol, i.e. sec-butyl alcohol; other two given alcohols are $$1^{\\circ}$$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27098,'chemistry',66,1030,2431,'The correct decreasing order of dipole moment ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{Cl}}$ , ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_3}B{r_3}$ and $C{H_3}F$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{F}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ > }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{Cl}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ > }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{B}}{{\\\\rm{r}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{F}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{ > }}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{Br}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{ > }}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{Cl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{Cl}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{ > }}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{F}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{ > }}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{Br}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{Cl}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{ > }}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{Br}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{ > }}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{F}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{F}}$ is expected to have higest dipole moment But     bon length is smaller than  bond length. Hence ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{F}}$ dipole moment is less than ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{Cl}}$. Hence the correct decreasing order of dipole moment in ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{Cl,C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{,C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{F}}\\,\\,{\\rm{is}}$<br>${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{Cl}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{ > }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{F}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{ > }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{r}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27099,'chemistry',65,955,2222,'$\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}^{\\mathrm{II}}\\left(\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right)(\\mathrm{CN})_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}\\right]^{-4}$ is named as','[{"text": "Chlorotetracyanodioxo ferrate (II) ion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chlorotetracyanoperoxoferrate (II) ion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chloridotetracyanosuperoxoferrate (II) ion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None is correct", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'First oxidation state of ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right)}}$ is needed to know the nature of ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right)}}$ group.<br>${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}^{\\mathrm{II}}\\left(\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right)(\\mathrm{CN})_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}\\right]^{4-}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{(+2)+2 x+4(-1)+(-1)=-4}}$<br>${\\mathrm{2 x-3=-4 ; 2 x=-1}}$ and ${\\mathrm{x=\\dfrac{-1}{2}}}$<br>So, the ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{O}_{2}\\right)}}$ is superoxide Chlorotetracyanosuperoxoferrate(II) ion',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27100,'chemistry',67,1077,2576,'The reagent used for dehydration of an alcohol is','[{"text": "phosphorus pentachloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "calcium chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "aluminium oxide", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "sodium chloride", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ = C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}\\xleftarrow[{{\\text{650K}}}]{{{\\text{A}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{OH}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{525K}}}]{{{\\text{A}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}}}$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{OC}}{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27101,'chemistry',66,1028,2419,'In which of the following conversions,<br> phosphorus pentachloride is used as the<br> reagent?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{C}=\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH} \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{O}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH} \\\\equiv \\\\mathrm{CH} \\\\longrightarrow \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CHCl}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When ethyl alcohol is treated with ${\\rm{PC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{5}}}$,<br> then ethyl chloride is formed.<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ -  OH  +  PC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{5}}}\\mathop  \\to \\limits_\\Delta  \\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ -  Cl  +  HCl  +  POC}}{{\\rm{l}}_{\\rm{3}}}\n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
