(27220,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'The strongest acid amongst the following compounds is','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{ClCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}(\\\\mathrm{Cl}) \\\\mathrm{CO}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{HCOOH}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{ - I}}$ effect exerting (electron withdrawing) groups increases the acidic strength of an acid by withdrawing electron density toward itself, thereby weakening $$\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{H}$$ bond; thus, the release of $$\\mathrm{H}^{+}$$ion by an acid becomes easier.<br>However, +I effect exerting ( $$e^{-}$$releasing) groups decreases the acidic strength by donating electron density to O-atom. Further, -I effect decreases with distance. Thus, the acidic strength of the given acids would be <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_27220_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_27220_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Hence, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_27220_explanation_3.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> is the most acidic and the strongest acid than other given compounds.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27221,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'Consider the following statements:<br>According to the Werner\'s theory.<br>(I) Ligands are connected to the metal ions by ionic bonds.<br>(II) Secondary valencies have directional properties<br>(III) Secondary valencies are non-ionizable. Of these statements:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "I, II and III are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and III are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'(I) Ligands are connected to the metal ion by coordinate covalent bond (dative bond).<br>(II) Secondary valences have directional properties.<br>(III) Secondary valences are non- ionizable and primary valences are ionizable.<br>So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
