(27243,'chemistry',65,956,2225,'Which can exist both as diastereoisomer and enantiomer?','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\operatorname{Pt}(\\\\mathrm{en})_{3}\\\\right]^{4+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Pt}(\\\\mathrm{en})_{2} \\\\mathrm{ClBr}\\\\right]^{2+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ru}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\\\right)_{4} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\\\\right]^{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{PtCl}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}\\\\right]^{0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}(\\mathrm{en})_{2} \\mathrm{ClBr}\\right]^{2+}$ can exist both as diastereoisomer and enantiomer. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27243_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27243_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The $(+)$ and $(-)$ forms of cis $\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}(\\mathrm{en})_{2} \\mathrm{ClBr}\\right]^{2+}$ are enantiomers.<br>The cis and trans forms of $\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}(\\mathrm{en})_{2} \\mathrm{ClBr}\\right]^{2+}$ are diastereoisomers.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27245,'chemistry',67,1084,2604,'$$\\mathrm{HBr}$$ reacts with $$\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\mathrm{CH}-\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$$ under anhydrous conditions at room temperature to give:','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{CHBr}-\\\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{BrCH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$$ and $$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Br}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{BrCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$$ and $$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{CH}_{2} & =\\mathrm{CH}-\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}+\\mathrm{H}^{+} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}+\\mathrm{Br}^{-} \\\\& \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}(\\mathrm{Br}) \\mathrm{OCH}_{3} .\\end{aligned}$$<br>The formed Carbocation, $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$$ is stabilised by resonance with oxygen so its formation is more favourable than $$\\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27247,'chemistry',65,957,2227,'In the complex ion $\\left[\\mathrm{Co}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{6}\\right]^{3+}$, the $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ molecules are linked to the central metal ion by','[{"text": "ionic bonds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "covalent bonds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "coordinate bonds", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "hydrogen bonds", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A complex is formed, when ligand(s) donates its (their) electrons to the vacant d-orbital of transition metals. Since, the electrons are given by only ligands and used up by metals as well as ligands, the bond formed is called coordinate bond.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
