(27450,'chemistry',65,956,2224,'The number of geometric isomers that can exist for square planar ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}(\\mathrm{Cl})(\\mathrm{py})\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}\\right)\\right]^{+}}}$is <br>____. (py = pyridine)','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For square planar complexes of the type ${{\\rm{[Mabcd]}}^{{\\rm{n}} \\pm }}$ where all the four ligands are different, three geometrical isomers are possible.So, for the given square planar complex ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Pt}(\\mathrm{Cl})(\\mathrm{py})\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}\\right)\\right]^{+}}}$, following three geometrical isomers are possible: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/65_27450_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> where ${\\mathrm{a=\\mathrm{Cl}, \\mathrm{b}=}}$ py, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{c}=\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{d}=\\mathrm{NH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}}}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
