(27486,'chemistry',66,1031,2436,'Alkyl halide is converted into alkyl isocyanide by reaction with','[{"text": "NaCN", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\\\mathrm{CN}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AgCN", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "KCN .", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{X}+\\mathrm{AgCN} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{NC}+\\mathrm{Ag} \\mathrm{X}}}$<br>(Where, R is alkyl group).<br>AgCN is mainly covalent in nature and nitrogen is free to donate electron pair forming isocyanide as the main product. So, the correct option is (3) .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27488,'chemistry',67,1076,2568,'$$\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$$ cannot be dried by anhydrous $$\\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$$, because','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{OH}$$ is soluble in water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Explosion takes place", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{OH}$$ forms adduct with $$\\\\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$\\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}+3 \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CaCl}_{2} \\cdot 3 \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27489,'chemistry',68,1115,2672,'Which of the following statements is/ are correct?','[{"text": "The two carbon-oxygen bond lengths in molecular formic acid are different.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The two carbon-oxygen bond lengths in sodium formate are equal.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Partial resonance is there in formate.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27490,'chemistry',66,1031,2435,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">High concentration of strong nucleophilic reagent with secondary alkyl halides which do not have bulky substituents will follow ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2}}$ mechanism.</div><div class="column-right">A secondary alkyl halide when treated with a large excess of ethanol follows ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1}}$ mechanism.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Rate ${\\mathrm{=\\mathrm{k}[\\mathrm{Nu}]}}$ [Substrate ]<br>High concentration of strong nucleophilic reagent with secondary alkyl halides which do not have bulky substituents will follow ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2}}$ mechanism. Ethanol is a weak nucleophile. So, it favours ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1}}$ mechanism. Ethanol present in excess amount so there is no significance change in rate of reaction. So reaction depends only on concentration of alkyl halide hence favour ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 1}}$ mechanism. So, the correct option is (3) .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27492,'chemistry',67,1076,2569,'Boiling point will be least for','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Alkanes are non polar in nature and possess weak VanderWaal\'s forces,<br>hence, have low B.P.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27493,'chemistry',68,1116,2673,'When benzyl chloride is treated with ethanolic $$\\mathrm{KCN}$$, followed by acidification, the major product formed is:','[{"text": "Benzoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Benzyl alcohol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Benzyl cyanide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phenyl acetic acid", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_27493_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27494,'chemistry',66,1038,2461,'The product (C) in the below mentioned reaction is<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}} - {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}} - {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}} - {\\text{Br}}\\xrightarrow[\\Delta ]{{{\\text{KO}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{(alc)}}}}}}{\\text{A}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\xrightarrow[\\Delta ]{{{\\text{HBr}}}}{\\text{B}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{KO}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{(aq)}}}}}]{\\Delta }{\\text{C}}$','[{"text": "propan-1-ol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "propyne", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "propene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "propan-2-ol.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/66_27494_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (4) .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27513,'chemistry',68,1113,2657,'Addition of alcohols to aldehydes and ketones takes place in presence of dry $$\\mathrm{HCl}$$ gas because it<br>(i) Protonates the oxygen of the carbonyl compounds<br>(ii) Increase the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon<br>(iii) Removes the excess moisture from the reaction<br>(iv) Helps the reaction to move in the forward direction','[{"text": "(i), (ii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(ii), (iii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i), (iii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Dry hydrogen chloride protonates the oxygen of the carbonyl compounds and therefore, increases the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon facilitating the nucleophilic attack by the alcohol molecule. Dry $$\\mathrm{HCl}$$ gas also absorbs the water produced in these reactions thereby shifting the equilibrium in the forward direction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27495,'chemistry',65,953,2212,'Palladium(II) tends to form complexes with a coordination number of 4 . One such compound was originally formulated as ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{PdCl}_{2} \\cdot 3 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$. Suppose an aqueous solution of the compound is treated with excess ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3(\\mathrm{aq})}}}$, how many moles of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{AgCl}_{(\\mathrm{s})}}}$ are formed per mole of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{PdCl}_{2} \\cdot 3 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$ ?','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Because of their charge, the ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}}}$ions can be either in the coordination sphere, where they are bonded directly to the metal, or outside the coordination sphere, where they are bonded ionically to the complex. Because the ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{3}}}$ ligands are neutral, they must be in the coordination sphere.<br>So, the correct formula is ${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Pd}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{Cl}\\right] \\mathrm{Cl}}}$.<br>The chloride ion that serves as a ligand will not be precipitated as ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{AgCl}_{(\\mathrm{s})}}}$. Thus, only the single ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}^{\\text {}}}}$, which is outside the coordination sphere can react with ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}}}$.<br>Therefore, 1 mol of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{AgCl}_{(\\mathrm{s})}}}$ is formed per mole of the complex.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
