(27859,'chemistry',67,1075,2566,'In Williamson synthesis if tertiary alkyl halide is used then','[{"text": "ether is obtained in good yield", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ether is obtained in poor yield", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "alkene is the only reaction product", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "a mixture of alkene as a major product and ether as a minor product forms.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If a tertiary alkyl halide is used, an alkene is the only reaction product and no ether is formed. For example, the reaction of $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{ONa}$$ with $$\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{Br}$$ gives exclusively 2 Methylpropene.   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_27859_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   It is because alkoxides are not only nucleophiles but strong bases as well. They react with alkyl halides leading to elimination reactions.<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(27860,'chemistry',65,957,2229,'Select the option with correct property','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}(\\\\mathrm{CO})_{4}\\\\right]}}$ diamagnetic, ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{NiCl}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}}}$ paramagnetic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}(\\\\mathrm{CO})_{4}\\\\right]}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{NiCl}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}}}$ both diamagnetic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}(\\\\mathrm{CO})_{4}\\\\right]}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{NiCl}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}}}$ both paramagnetic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{NiCl}_{4}\\\\right]^{2-}}}$ diamagnetic, ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\left[\\\\mathrm{Ni}(\\\\mathrm{CO})_{4}\\\\right]}}$ paramagnetic", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{NiCl}_{4}\\right]^{2-}}}$ is paramagnetic since it contains two unpaired electrons ( ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{sp}^{3}}}$ hybridization).<br>${\\mathrm{\\left[\\mathrm{Ni}(\\mathrm{CO})_{4}\\right]}}$ has tetrahedral geometry but it is diamagnetic because nickel is in zero oxidation state and contains no unpaired electron ( ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{sp}^{3}}}$ hybridization). So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
