(27946,'chemistry',67,1083,2591,'The correct order of acid strength of the following compounds is<br>(I) Phenol<br>(II) $$\\mathrm{p}$$-cresol<br>(III) m-nitrophenol<br>(IV) p-nitrophenol','[{"text": "I $$>$$ II $$>$$ IV $$>$$ III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III $$>$$ II $$>$$ I $$>$$ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II $$>$$ IV $$>$$ I $$>$$ III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV $$>$$ III $$>$$ I $$>$$ II", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_27946_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Acidic because of resonance stabilisation of phenoxide ion.<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_27946_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  ${\\rm{ - C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$  group release electrons, destabilises the phenoxide ion thus acidic nature is decreased ,hence II < I (acidic).    <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_27946_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   $$-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$$ withdraws electrons, stabilizes the phenoxide ion, hence acidity is increased.<br>(IV) Lower acidity in case of m-isomer as compared to $$\\mathrm{p}$$-isomer is explained by the fact that m-nitrophenoxide is stabilised by inductive effect only and no resonance effect operates with $$-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$$ group.<br>Thus, m-nitorphenol  <  p-nitrophenol.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
