(27984,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'An organic compound X is oxidised by using acidified $$\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$$. The product obtained reacts with phenyl hydrazine but does not answer silver mirror test. The possible structure of $$\\mathrm{X}$$ is','[{"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COCH}_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{CHOH}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The oxidation product of X reacts with phenyl hydrazine, thus it contains <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_27984_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> The same product does not answer silver mirror test thus, it is a ketone, because only aldehydes gives this test.<br>Thus the compound $$\\mathrm{X}$$ must be a $$2^{\\circ}$$ alcohol, as only secondary alcohols give ketones on oxidation and hence $$\\mathrm{X}$$ is $$\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{CHOH}$$. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_27984_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
