(28014,'chemistry',68,1109,2643,'Which of the following statements is true for carbonyl group?','[{"text": "The carbonyl bond is weaker as compared to double bond in alkene.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C-C-O bond angle is $$90^{\\\\circ}$$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carbon atom forms three sigma bonds.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Carbon atom is $$\\\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$$ hybridised.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In carbonyl group, carbon atom forms three sigma bonds. It can be easily shown as below: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28014_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> The carbonyl carbon atom is $$\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$$-hybridised contributes one electron to each of three hybrid orbitals, forming three sigma $$(\\sigma)$$ bonds. The $$\\pi$$ - electron cloud is above and below the plane. The bond angles are approximately $$120^{\\circ}$$ as expected of a trigonal planar structure. Because of the greater electronegativity of oxygen, the carbonyl group is polar and form stable bond in comparison with non-polar double bond of alkene.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
