(28092,'chemistry',68,1117,2676,'Carboxylic acids have higher boiling points than aldehydes, ketones and even alcohols of comparable molecular mass. It is due to their','[{"text": "More extensive association of carboxylic acid via vander Waals force of attraction.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Formation of intermolecular hydrogen bonding", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Formation of carboxylate ion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Formation of intramolecular hydrogen bonding", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Carboxylic acids have quite high boiling points due to the presence of intermolecular hydrogen bonding which results in the formation of dimeric structure. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28092_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
