(28112,'chemistry',67,1083,2596,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In case of phenol bromination takes place even in absence of lewis acid whereas bromination of benzene takes place in presence of Lewis acid like $$\\mathrm{FeBr}_{3}$$.<br></div><div class="column-right">- OH group attached to benzene ring is highly deactivating.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The usual halogenation of benzene takes place in the presence of a Lewis acid. such as $$\\mathrm{FeBr}_{3}$$, which polarises the halogen molecule. In case of phenol, the polarisation of bromine molecule takes place even in the absence of Lewis acid.<br>It is due to the highly activating effect of $$-\\mathrm{OH}$$ group attached to the benzene ring.<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
