(28481,'chemistry',68,1120,2687,'${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COOH}}\\xrightarrow[{\\left( {{\\text{Step}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{  -  }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{1}}} \\right)}]{{{\\text{PC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{3}}}}}{\\text{A}}\\xrightarrow[\\begin{subarray}{l} \n{\\text{Pd  -  BaS}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}} \\\\ \\left( {{\\text{Step}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{  -  }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{2}}} \\right) \\end{subarray} ]{{{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}}{\\text{B}}{\\text{.}}$<br>Step - 2 reaction is not suitable to prepare a compound (P). Which of the following test is used to distinguish compound B and P ?','[{"text": "Tollen\'s reagent test", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fehling\'s solution test", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Iodoform test", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Schiff\'s test", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$\\mathrm{A}: \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COCl}$$<br>B: $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CHO}$$ (Rosenmund reduction)<br>HCHO cannot be prepared by using step - 2 .<br>So, $$\\mathrm{HCHO}$$ and $$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CHO}$$ can be distinguished by iodoform test <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28481_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
