(28483,'chemistry',67,1079,2584,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}$ can be distinguished by haloform test.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Haloform test is given by $2^{0}$ alcohol.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{OH}}\\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{I}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{/O}}{{\\text{H}}^\\Theta }}}\\mathop {{\\text{CH}}{{\\text{I}}_{\\text{3}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ Yellow ppt}}{\\text{. }}} $<br>The above test is iodoform test. It is given by $\\alpha$-methyl ketones, aldehydes. It can also be given by $1^{\\circ}, 2^{\\circ}$ alcohols, if they have $\\beta$-methylgroup. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_28483_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> can give iodoform test but not by <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_28483_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  (Both these are $2^{\\circ}$ alcohols) So. all $2^{\\circ}$ alcohols do not give this test.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
