(28498,'chemistry',67,1077,2576,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The ease of dehydration of the following alcohols is  https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764954892/preptracker/questions/q_28498_col1_1764954891710.jpg<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Alcohols leading to conjugated alkenes are dehydrated to a greater extent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Phenol is least reactive towards dehydration due to resonance stabilisation. 2-Cyclohexenol undergoes dehydration more readily as it gives conjugated alkene. (Alcohols leading to conjugated alkenes are dehydrated more readily). Thus, both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28499,'chemistry',69,1174,2810,'Which of the following statement(s) is/are incorrect in case of Hofmann bromamide degradation?','[{"text": "Reaction is useful for decreasing length of carbon chain by one carbon atom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It gives tertiary amine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It gives primary amine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aqueous or alc. $\\\\mathrm{KOH}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Statement (B) is incorrect in case of Hofmann bromanide degradation. This is used to synthesise primary amine.<br>In this reaction, amide on reaction with $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ in an aqueous or ethanolic solution of $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ give $1^{\\circ}$ amine with one $\\mathrm{C}$ atom less than that present in the amine.<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\mathrm{RCONH}_{2}+\\mathrm{Br}_{2}+4 \\mathrm{KOH} \\stackrel{\\Delta}{\\longrightarrow} \\underset{1^{\\circ} \\mathrm{a} \\text { mine }}{\\mathrm{RNH}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{KBr}} \\\\+\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28500,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'${\\rm{Ph   -   CHO   +   C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{CHO}}$ $\\xrightarrow[\\Delta ]{{{\\text{Dil}}{\\text{.NaOH}}}}{\\text{P}}$<br>${\\text{P + }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}\\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{/Zn - }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}}{\\text{Q + Ph - CHO}}$<br>Compound \'Q\' can undergo which of the<br>following reaction?','[{"text": "Aldol condensation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cannizaro reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Perkin\'s reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Intramolecular aldol reaction", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28500_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> compound \'Q\' can undergo cannizaro rx.<br>as it an example of no $$\\alpha-\\mathrm{H}$$ molecule',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
