(28602,'chemistry',67,1076,2569,'Which of the following alcohols has lowest boiling point?','[{"text": "sec-Butyl alcohol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "tert-Butyl alcohol", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "iso- Butyl alcohol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "n-Butyl alcohol", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As branching increases, boiling point decreases.<br>$\\therefore$ tert-butyl alcohol has lowest boiling point, due to minimum surface area.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28612,'chemistry',69,1176,2823,'Identify the product \' $\\mathrm{C}$ \' in the following reaction.<br>${\\text{Aniline}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{Pyridine}}}]{{{{\\left( {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CO}}} \\right)}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}}{\\text{A}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COOH}}}]{{{\\text{B}}{{\\text{r}}_{\\text{2}}}}}{\\text{B}}\\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ + }}}\\,\\,{\\text{or}}\\,\\,{\\text{O}}{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ - }}}}}{\\text{C}}$','[{"text": "Acetanilide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "p-bromoacetanilide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "p-bromoaniline", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "o-bromoaniline", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28603,'chemistry',68,1113,2663,'${\\text{HCHO  +  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CHO}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ Conc}}{\\text{. NaOH}}}}$<br>Find out the products of reaction','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CO}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Na}$ and $\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}+\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}$ and $\\\\mathrm{HCO}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Na}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CO}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Na}+\\\\mathrm{HCO}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Na}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The given reaction (as per the options) is crossed Cannizzaro reaction. A simple Cannizzaro reaction is shown by those aldehydes in which $\\alpha$ - hydrogen is absent. These aldehydes in the presence of a strong base like $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ show disproportionation, i.e., simultaneous oxidation and reduction of the same molecule. Thus, the product includes an acid alongwith an alcohol.<br>In crossed Cannizzaro reaction however, two different molecules participate, out of which the one more prone to nucleophilic attack will show oxidation, while the other shows reduction<br>Here, out of the given two subtrates, $\\mathrm{HCHO}$ is more prone to nucleophilic attack hence, this will undergo oxidation while $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CHO}$ will undergo reduction. Hence, the possible products are $\\mathrm{HCOO}^{-} \\mathrm{Na}^{+}+\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}$. Reaction involved is as follows:<br>${\\text{HCHO   +   C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CHO}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{ NaOH }}}]{{{\\text{ Conc }}}}$<br>${\\text{HCO}}\\overline {\\text{O}} \\mathop {{\\text{Na}}}\\limits^{{\\text{  +  }}} {\\text{  +  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{OH}}$<br>${\\text{Note}}$  Same combination of substrates give crossed aldol as well, in the presence of<br>$\\mathrm{NaOH}$ to form  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28603_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> But as, it is not among the option as the product, hence we are considering option (3) as the most appropriate option<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28604,'chemistry',69,1176,2819,'The amine \'A\' when treated with nitrous acid gives yellow oily substance. The amine $\\mathrm{A}$ is','[{"text": "triethylamine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "trimethylamine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "aniline", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "methylphenylamine", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Secondary amines when treated with nitrous acid give yellow oily substance. So, amine \' $\\mathrm{A}$ \' is methylphenyl amine.<br>Reaction of nitrous acid with aliphatic amines liberate $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas along with alcohol.<br>$\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}+\\mathrm{HNO}_{2} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{R}-\\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{N}} \\equiv \\mathrm{NCl}^{-}$<br>$\\stackrel{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}{\\longrightarrow} \\mathrm{ROH}+\\mathrm{N}_{2}+\\mathrm{HCl}$<br>Reaction of nitrous acid with aromatic amines liberates $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas along with phenol.<br>$\\mathrm{PhNH}_{2}+\\mathrm{HNIO}_{2}+\\mathrm{HCl} \\stackrel{273-276 \\mathrm{~K}}{\\longrightarrow}$<br>$\\mathrm{Ph}-\\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{N}} \\equiv \\mathrm{NCl}^{-1} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{PhOH}+\\mathrm{HCl}+\\mathrm{N}_{2}$<br>Reaction of nitrous acid with $2^{\\circ}$ amines with<br>$\\mathrm{HNO}_{2}$, they give nitroso amines, which being insoluble in dilute acids separte as yellow oily compound.<br>$\\mathrm{R}_{2} \\mathrm{NH}+\\mathrm{HONO} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{R}_{2}-\\mathrm{N}-\\mathrm{N}=\\mathrm{O}$<br>$\\mathrm{N}$-nitroso dialkyl amine (yellow Oil)<br>Reaction of nitrous acid with $3^{\\circ}$ amines<br>Aliphatic $3^{\\circ}$ amines on reaction with $\\mathrm{HONO}$ form soluble nitrite salts, while aromatic $3^{\\circ}$ amines form green coloured P-nitroso amines.<br>${{\\rm{R}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{\\,N + HONO}} \\to \\mathop {\\left[ {{{\\rm{R}}_{\\rm{3}}}\\mathop {\\rm{N}}\\limits^{\\rm{ + }} {\\rm{H}}} \\right]{\\rm{NO}}_{\\rm{2}}^{\\rm{ - }}}\\limits_{\\left( {{\\rm{Soluble}}\\,\\,{\\rm{nitrite}}} \\right)} $<br>${\\rm{Ph - NM}}{{\\rm{e}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + HONO}} \\to $ <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_28604_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2016,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
