(28796,'chemistry',67,1076,2570,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Alcohols are soluble in water.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Alcohols show H-bonding.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_28796_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Solubility of alcohols in water is due to their ability to form hydrogen bonds with water molecules. The solubility decreases with increase in size of alkyl/aryl (hydroophobic) groups. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28797,'chemistry',70,1251,3051,'<br>The above polysacchride is called&nbsp;','[{"text": "Amylose", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Amylopectin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cellulose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glycogen", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/70_28797_question_1.png'),
(28798,'chemistry',68,1111,2646,'Propanone can be prepared from ethyne by:','[{"text": "Passing a mixture of ethyne and steam over a catalyst, magnesium at $420^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Passing a mixture of ethyne and ethanol over a catalyst zinc chromite", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Boiling ethyne with water and $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Treating ethyne with Iodine and $\\\\mathrm{NaOH}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$2 \\mathrm{CH} \\equiv \\mathrm{CH}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\xrightarrow[\\mathrm{Mg}]{420^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}}$<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COCH}_{3}+\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28799,'chemistry',69,1176,2819,'Which among the following reagents is used to distinguish primary, secondary and tertiary amines according to solubility in alkaline medium?','[{"text": "Benzenesulphonyl chloride", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Iodoform", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethylene glycol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acetyl chloride", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Benzenesulphonyl chloride $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{SO}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}\\right)$, (Hinsberg reagent) is used to distinguish primary, secondary and tertiary amines.<br>The reaction of benzenesulphonyl chloride with primary amine yields $\\mathrm{N}$-alkylbenzene sulphonamide which is soluble in alkali.<br>$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{SO}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}+\\mathrm{RNH}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{SO}_{2} \\mathrm{NHR}$<br>(Soluble in alkali due to presence of acidic hydrogen atom attached to nitrogen atom.<br>With secondary amine, it yields N, N  dialkylbenzene sulphonamide which is insoluble in alkali.<br>$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{SO}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}+\\mathrm{R}_{2} \\mathrm{NH} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{SO}_{2} \\mathrm{NR}_{2}$<br>(Insoluble in alkali due to absence hydrogen atom attached to nitrogen atom.<br>Tertiary amines do not react with benzenesulphonyl chloride.<br>$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{SO}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl},+\\mathrm{R}_{3} \\mathrm{~N} \\rightarrow$ No reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29271,'chemistry',70,1254,3059,'In a protein molecule various amino acids are linked together by:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\beta}$-glycosidic bond", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "peptide bond", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "dative bond", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\alpha}$-glycosidic bond", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29271_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28800,'chemistry',67,1080,2586,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Alcoholic fermentation involves conversion of sugar into ethyl alcohol by yeast.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Fermentation involves the slow decomposition of complex organic compounds into simpler substances through the agency of complex nitrogenous compounds called enzymes.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Ethanol, $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$, is obtained commercially by fermentation. The sugar in molasses, sugarcane or fruits such as grapes is converted to glucose and fructose, (both of which have the formula ${{\\text{C}}_6}{{\\text{H}}_{12}}{{\\text{O}}_6})$, in the presence of an enzyme, invertase. Glucose and fructose undergo fermentation in the presence of another enzyme, zymase, which is found in yeast.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{C}_{12} \\mathrm{H}_{22} \\mathrm{O}_{11}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\text { invertase } \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{12} \\mathrm{O}_{6} \\\\& \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{12} \\mathrm{O}_{6}(\\mathrm{aq})+\\text { zymase } \\rightarrow \\\\& 2 \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}_{(\\mathrm{aq})}+\\mathrm{CO}_{2(\\mathrm{~g})}\\end{aligned}$<br>So the option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28801,'chemistry',70,1251,3049,'Gun cotton is','[{"text": "Nitrosucrose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nitrocellulose", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Nitroglucose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nitropicrin", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Gun cotton is a nitrocellulose or cellulose trinitrate which is used on explosives and as a blinder for dolid rocket propellant.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28802,'chemistry',69,1173,2806,'What is the IUPAC name of<br>$\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{NCH}_{3}$ ?','[{"text": "Trimethylamine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,2-Dimethylmethanamine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "N, N-Dimethylmethanamine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "N-Methyldimethanamine", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_28802_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Trimethylamine (common name)<br>$\\mathrm{N}, \\mathrm{N}$-Dimethylmethanamine (IUPAC name)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28803,'chemistry',68,1113,2656,'Which among the following is most reactive towards nucleophilic addition?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{FCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{ClCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BrCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CO}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{ICH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In ${\\text{FC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{CHO,}}$ fluroine is a strong electron withdrawing in nature. This make - $\\mathrm{CO}$ group more polar and makes this compound more reactive towards $\\mathrm{Nu}^{\\Theta}$ addition.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28804,'chemistry',67,1088,2612,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Grignard reagents are alkyl or aryl magnesium halides.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Alcohols can be used as solvent for Grignard reagent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Alcohols are unsuitable solvents for Grignard reagents due to their reactivity, as the acidic hydrogen in alcohols can undergo undesired side reactions with Grignard reagents, leading to hydrocarbon formation and reduced reagent effectiveness. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
