(28811,'chemistry',68,1113,2662,'When propanal reacts with 2-Methyl propanal in presence of $\\mathrm{NaOH}$, four different products (excluding stereo isomers) are formed. The reaction is known as:','[{"text": "Aldol condensation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Crossed aldol condensation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cannizzaro reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "HVZ condensation", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28811_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28812,'chemistry',67,1077,2578,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The hydrolysis of ethyl acctate is catalysed by mineral acid.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Esterification takes place between ethyl alcohol and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ at $170^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Hydrolysis of ethyl acetate is acid catalysed<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOC}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{H}^{+}}$<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}+\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}$<br>Excess concentrated sulfuric acid at elevated temperatures catalyzes the dehydration of alcohols, resulting in the elimination of water and the formation of alkenes.<br>$\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH} \\xrightarrow[170^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}]{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}} \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{CH}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28813,'chemistry',70,1250,3047,'In disaccharides, if the reducing groups of monosaccharides i.e., aldehydic or ketonic groups are bonded, these are non-reducing sugars. Which of the following disaccharide is a non-reducing sugar?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28814,'chemistry',69,1174,2810,'The source of nitrogen in Gabriel synthesis of amines is:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NaN}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NaNO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{KCN}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_28814_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28815,'chemistry',68,1113,2662,'Which of the following compound will undergo self-aldol condensation in the presence of cold dilute alkali?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH} \\\\equiv \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{CHO}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CHCHO}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For aldol condensation, $\\alpha-\\mathrm{H}$ is must  present on $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ hybrid carbon. ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{CHO}}$ undergo self-aldol condensation due to the presence of $\\alpha-\\mathrm{H}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28816,'chemistry',67,1080,2586,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Absolute alcohol is $100 \\%$ ethanol.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The absolute alcohol boils at $78.3^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Absolute alcohol is $100 \\%$ ethanol and is free from water or other impurities. Given reason is also true, but not the actual explanation. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28817,'chemistry',70,1249,3042,'When an aqueous solution of D-glucose is treated with a base, it is converted into D-fructose and D-mannose, this conversion (isomerisation) involves','[{"text": "enolisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "tautomerisation.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "none of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When an aqueous solution of D-glucose, D-mannose or D-fructose is treated with a base, it undergoes enolisation and a series of ketoenol tautomerisation to form a mixture of the three monosaccharides (Lobry de Bruyn-van Ekenstein transformation).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28819,'chemistry',68,1113,2662,'${\\text{2C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ dry HCl}}}}{\\text{\' X \'}}$.<br>\' $\\mathrm{X}$ \' is:','[{"text": "Mesityl oxide", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Diacetone alcohol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acetic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mesitylene", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28819_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28820,'chemistry',67,1076,2568,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Ethanol is liquid but dimethyl ether is a gas.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In alcohols, carbon as well as oxygen are $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ hybridised.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ethanol is liquid at room temperature because molecules are strongly associated with each other due to the presence of hydrogen bonding.<br>Whereas ethers like dimethyl ether have weaker dipole-dipole attractions and is a gas. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28821,'chemistry',70,1247,3033,'Optical rotation of a freshly prepared solution of $\\mathrm{\\alpha}$-D-glucopyranose is $\\mathrm{+111^{\\circ}}$ while that of $\\mathrm{\\beta}$ - D-glucopyranose is $\\mathrm{+19.2^{\\circ}}$. In solution, an equilibrium mixture of these two anomers is $\\mathrm{52.5^{\\circ}}$. The percentage of $\\mathrm{\\alpha}$-form in the equilibrium mixture is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{36 \\\\%}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{64 \\\\%}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{52.5 \\\\%}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{19.2 \\\\%}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\alpha \\,{\\text{ -  D  -  Glucopyranose}} \\rightleftharpoons {\\text{Equilibrium}}\\,\\,$<br>${\\text{mixture}}\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{ + 111}}^\\circ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{ + 52}}.{\\text{5}}^\\circ $<br>$\\mathrm{\\rightleftharpoons \\beta}$-D-Glucopyranose<br>$\\mathrm{+19.2^{\\circ}}$<br>Ratio of $\\mathrm{\\alpha: \\beta-}$ form $\\mathrm{=(52.5-19.2):(111-52.5)}$<br>$\\rm{=33.3: 58.5}$<br>$\\mathrm{\\therefore \\%}$ of $\\mathrm{\\alpha}$-D-glucopyranose<br>$\\rm{=\\dfrac{33.3}{(33.3+58.5)} \\times 100=36.27 \\%}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28822,'chemistry',69,1176,2823,'Amino groups are present in which of biological compounds?','[{"text": "Proteins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vitamins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alkaloids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Proteins, vitamins, alkaloids all contain amino groups.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28823,'chemistry',68,1113,2662,'Acetone on reaction with chlorine normally gives:','[{"text": "Monochloro acetone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dichloro acetone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Trichloro acetone", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hexachloro acetone", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28823_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28824,'chemistry',67,1083,2594,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The reaction between phenol and benzoyl chloride in presence of $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ is called Schotten Baumann reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Phenol on reaction with $\\mathrm{Zn}$ dust gives benzene.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Schotten-Baumann reaction is the reaction of benzoyl chloride with phenol in presence of $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ to give Phenyl benzoate. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_28824_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> When phenol reacts with $\\mathrm{Zn}$ dust, benzene is formed  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_28824_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28825,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'The most reactive amine towards dilute hydrochloric acid is:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The order of basicity of amines in aqueous medium: $2^{\\circ}>3^{\\circ}>1^{\\circ}>\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$. So $2^{\\circ}$ amine is most reactive towards $\\mathrm{HCl}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28826,'chemistry',68,1108,2641,'Write the IUPAC name of the following compound.','[{"text": "2-Amino - 4 - hydroxybenzoic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6 - Amino - 4 hydroxy benzoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 - Amino - 4 - carboxyphenol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 - Carboxy - 4 - hydroxy aniline", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Priority order of functional group for naming is<br>$-\\mathrm{COOH}>-\\mathrm{OH}>-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$<br>$\\therefore$ IUPAC name of the compound is  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28826_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  2 - Amino - 4 - hydroxy benzoic acid',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_28826_question_1.png'),
(28827,'chemistry',70,1247,3033,'Glucose is found to exist in two different $\\mathrm{\\alpha}$ and $\\mathrm{\\beta}$ crystalline forms. These forms can be obtained by.<br>(i) The $\\mathrm{\\alpha -}$ form of glucose is obtained by crystallisation from concentrated solution of glucose at $\\mathrm{303 \\mathrm{~K}}$.<br>(ii) The $\\mathrm{\\beta -}$ form of glucose is obtained by crystallisation from concentrated solution of glucose at $\\mathrm{303 \\mathrm{~K}}$.<br>(iii) The $\\mathrm{\\beta-}$ form is obtained by crystallisation from hot and saturated aqueous solution at $\\mathrm{371 \\mathrm{~K}}$.<br>(iv) The $\\mathrm{\\alpha -}$ form is obtained by crystallisation from hot and saturated aqueous solution at $\\mathrm{371 \\mathrm{~K}}$.','[{"text": "(i) and (iii)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(ii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(ii) and (iii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28828,'chemistry',67,1077,2578,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}$ undergoes esterification faster than $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{COH}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The reaction between an acid and alcohol in presence of dry $\\mathrm{HCl}$ gas to give ester is known as esterification process.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'During esterification, when the size of the alkyl group in either the acid or the alcohol increases, the rate of esterification tends to decrease. This is due to steric hindrance. $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}$ has a smaller $-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ group which offers less steric hindrance. Whereas, $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{COH}$ has a bulky t-butyl group which offers more steric hindrance. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28837,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'Increasing $\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}$ values of $\\mathrm{o}-, \\mathrm{m}$ - and $\\mathrm{p-}$ toluidines is','[{"text": "p $ < $ m $ < $ o", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "o $ < $ m $ < $ p", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "p $ < $ o $ < $ m", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "m $ < $ o $ < $ p", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The basicity of o-, m-and p-toluidines decreases in the order $\\mathrm{p}>\\mathrm{m}>\\mathrm{o}$. In other words, their $\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}$ values will increase in the reverse order, i.e., $\\mathrm{p} < \\mathrm{m} < $ o.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28829,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'An organic compound ${\\text{X}}$ with molecular formula $\\mathrm{C}_{9} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}$ gives positive 2,4 - DNP and Tollen\'s test. It undergoes Cannizarro reaction and on vigorous oxidation, it gives 1,4 Benzenedicarboylic acid. Compound $(\\mathrm{X})$ is:','[{"text": "2,3-Dimethyl Benzaldehyde", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "o - methyl benzaldehyde", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "p - ethyl benzaldehyde", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2,2 - dimethyl hexanal", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>(i) +ve 2,4-DNP test $\\rightarrow$ carbonyl group is present<br>(ii) +ve Tollen\'s test $\\rightarrow$ aldehyde group is present<br>(iii) +ve Cannizzaro reaction $\\rightarrow$ No $\\alpha-$ hydrogen is present<br>(iv) Gives 1,4-Benzenedicarboxylic acid means benzene with 1,4 substitution is present in which one group is aldehyde. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28829_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28830,'chemistry',69,1177,2825,'Consider the following compounds:<br>(i) p-methyl aniline         (ii) N, N-dimethylaniline<br>(iii) N-ethyl aniline           (iv) $\\mathrm{N}$-ethyl-N-methyl aniline<br>The compounds which do not form diazonium salt with ice-cold $\\mathrm{NaNO}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{HCl}$ are:','[{"text": "(i), (ii) and (iii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(ii), (iii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(i), (iii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(ii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Diazonium salt is formed when the $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group on benzene is free and not substituted. So, only p-methyl aniline forms.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28831,'chemistry',67,1078,2579,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Tertiary alcohols give turbidity immediately with Lucas reagent.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A mixture of conc. $\\mathrm{HI}+$ anhydrous<br>$\\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$ is called Lucas reagent.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A mixture of concentrated hydrochloric acid (conc. $\\mathrm{HCl}$ ) and anhydrous zinc chloride<br>$\\left(\\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}\\right)$ is commonly referred to as the Lucas reagent. Given assertion is true. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28832,'chemistry',70,1253,3058,'Which of the following statements is correct?','[{"text": "All amino acids are optically active.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All amino acids except lysine are optically active", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All amino acids except glutamic acid are optically active.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All amino acids except glycine are optically active.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Glycine is optically inactive while all other amino acids are optically active.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28833,'chemistry',68,1116,2673,'Which of the following can\'t form $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}$ from $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$ ?<br>${\\text{(A) PCC}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(B)PDC}}$<br>${\\text{(C) }}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{r}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{/}}{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ + }}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(D)Micoderma}}\\,\\,{\\text{aceti}}$','[{"text": "A and B only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "C and D only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All A,B,C,D", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{5}}}{\\text{OH}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{ (mild oxidation) }}}]{{{\\text{ PCC or PPC }}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CHO}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28834,'chemistry',69,1174,2809,'Identify the compounds from the following which form primary amines under suitable reduction conditions:<br>(I) $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{NC}$                    (II) $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{6}$<br>(III) $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CONH}_{2}$                    (IV) $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{NO}_{2}$','[{"text": "I, IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III, IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, III, IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III, IV", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Hofmann bromamide degradation reaction gives primary amines.<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CONH}_{2}+\\mathrm{Br}_{2}+ & 4 \\mathrm{KOH} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{NH}_{2} \\\\& +2 \\mathrm{KBr}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\end{aligned}$<br>Primary amines may also be prepared by the reduction of nitroalkanes, nitrobenzene etc.<br>${\\text{RN}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{  +  3}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}\\xrightarrow[\\substack{ {\\text{ or}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{LAH}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{or}}{\\mkern 1mu} {{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{/Pt }} \\\\ {\\text{ or Metal}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{acid }} } ]{{{\\text{Ni/250}}^\\circ {\\text{C}}}}{\\text{RN}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{  +   2}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}$<br>${{\\text{C}}_6}{{\\text{H}}_5}{\\text{N}}{{\\text{O}}_2} + 3{{\\text{H}}_2}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{ LAH }}}]{{{\\text{Ni}}/250^\\circ {\\text{C}}}}{{\\text{C}}_6}{{\\text{H}}_5}{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_2} + 2{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{O}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28835,'chemistry',67,1083,2592,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Phenols cannot be converted into esters by direct reaction with carboxylic acids.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electron withdrawing groups increase the acidity of phenols.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Phenols, being less nucleophilic than alcohols, do not directly react with carboxylic acids to form esters, requiring alternative methods for esterification. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28836,'chemistry',68,1116,2673,'In the reaction of<br>Ethanol $\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{PI}_{3}} \\mathrm{~A} \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{KCN}} \\mathrm{B} \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}} \\mathrm{C}$;<br>Cis:','[{"text": "Acetamide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propionamide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propanoic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Acetic acid", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28836_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28838,'chemistry',70,1251,3049,'Which one of the following forms the constituent of cell wall of plant cell?','[{"text": "Cellulose", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Glycogen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Amylose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Starch", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Cellulose is the constituent of cell wall of plant cells.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28839,'chemistry',67,1078,2581,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> tert-Butyl alcohol undergoes acid catalysed dehydration readily than propanol.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $3^{\\circ}$ Alcohols do not give Victor-<br>Meyer\'s test.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The dehydration of tert-butyl alcohol to form an alkene involves the formation of a highly stable tertiary carbocation, facilitating the reaction more readily than other alcohols. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28840,'chemistry',68,1112,2653,'Which of the following statements regarding aldehydes is not correct?','[{"text": "The carbonyl functional group is planar", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The boiling points of aldehydes is greater than those of alkanes of comparable molar masses", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The boiling points of aldehydes is greater than those of alcohols of comparable molar masses.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Formaldehyde is gas at room temperature", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Alcohols have higher B.P than carbonyl compounds due to $\\mathrm{H}$-bonding',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28841,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'Which among the following is the strongest conjugate acid?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\stackrel{+}{\\\\mathrm{N}} \\\\mathrm{H}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A weak base has strong conjugate acid. Since aniline is the weakest base, therefore, its conjugate acid is the strongest acid.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28842,'chemistry',67,1089,2613,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The ionisation constant of phenol is higher than ethanol.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Phenoxide ion is stabilised through delocalisation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The hydroxyl group, in phenol is directly attached to the $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ hybridised carbon of benzene ring which acts as an electron withdrawing group. The resonance in phenol causes the oxygen of -OH group to be positive. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_28842_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  This increases the polarity of $\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond and results in an increase in ionisation constant of phenols. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28843,'chemistry',70,1253,3057,'${\\text{Aminoacid }}\\xrightarrow[\\Delta ]{{{\\text{NaOH &nbsp;+ &nbsp;CaO}}}}{\\text{gas}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{evolved }}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{ Lime water }}}]{{{\\text{ pass in }}}}\\mathop {{\\text{ Salt }}}\\limits_{{\\text{0}}{\\text{.1999kg}}} $<br>Amino acid having','[{"text": "Three - $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{COOH}}$ groups", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ groups", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "One - $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{COOH}}$ groups", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two - $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{COOH}}$ groups", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Gas evolved by amino acid is $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}}$ and salt is $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CaCO}_{3}}$.<br>${\\text{1}} {\\text{mol = 100}}\\,{\\text{kg CaC}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}$ is obtained from $\\mathrm{1 \\mathrm{~mol}=}$ $\\mathrm{44 \\mathrm{~kg}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}}$<br>$\\mathrm{0.1999 \\mathrm{~kg}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CaCO}_{3}}$ is obtained from $\\mathrm{44 \\mathrm{x}}$ $\\mathrm{0.1999 / 100=0.088 \\mathrm{~kg}=88 \\mathrm{~g}}$<br>In order to get $\\mathrm{88 \\mathrm{~g}=2}$ moles of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}}$, amino acid must contained two - $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{COOH}}$ groups',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28844,'chemistry',69,1175,2814,'Which of the following should be most volatile?<br>(I) $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}$<br>(II) $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{~N}$<br>(III) (IV) $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$','[{"text": "II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Due to weak Vander Waals forces, alkanes have low boiling points. i.e., ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}$ has low  B.P., so, most volatile.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_28844_question_1.png');
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(28845,'chemistry',68,1115,2672,'(C-O) bond length designated by $\\gamma$ and $\\delta$ are in order -','[{"text": "$\\\\gamma>\\\\delta$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\delta>\\\\gamma$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\delta=\\\\gamma$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Due to resonance, both bonds are of same length.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_28845_question_1.png'),
(28846,'chemistry',67,1077,2578,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Alcohols may act as Lewis base.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> 2-Butanol can exist in optically active form.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Alcohols act as lewis base by donating a pair of electrons to acids, forming oxonium ions. They also act as Bronsted acids in the presence of bases by donating protons to form alkoxide ions and water. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28874,'chemistry',69,1177,2825,'Which will not go for diazotisation?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Diazotisation is given by an aromatic primary amines ($-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ attached to benzene)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28847,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'A mixture contains three amino acids $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A}(\\mathrm{pH}=}$ 3. (2), $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{B}(\\mathrm{pH}=5.7)}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}(\\mathrm{pH}=9.7)}$<br>When it is subjected to electrophoresis at $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{pH}}$ 7.7, in which direction will each component of the mixture move?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}}$ to anode, $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{B}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}}$ to cathode", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}}$ to cathode, $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{B}}$ no movement, $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}}$ to anode", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}}$ to anode, $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{B}}$ no movement, $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}}$ to anode", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{B}}$ to anode and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}}$ to cathode", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Since $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{pH}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A}}$ and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{B}}$ is less than $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{pH}}$ (7.7), so they will exist in anionic form and will migrate towards anode. Since $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{pI}}$ of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}}$ is greater than the $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{pH}}$ of medium, so it will exist in cationic form and migrate towards cathode.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28848,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'Which of the following statements is wrong?','[{"text": "Aniline is more acidic than ammonia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Amines are less acidic than alcohols of <br>comparable molecular masses", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{RCONH}_{2}$ is more acidic than $\\\\mathrm{RCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Due to -I effect of ${\\text{F}}$, electron density in the $\\mathrm{N}-\\mathrm{H}$ decreases and hence release of a proton becomes easier from ${\\text{p}}$-fluoranilinium ion than form anilinium ion.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28849,'chemistry',68,1113,2657,'Which will form two oximes with $\\mathrm{NH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}$ ?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COCH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Syn  & anti are geometrical isomers possible only with $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{COCH}_{3}$.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28849_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28850,'chemistry',67,1077,2572,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Alcohols react with acid anhydride and acid chloride to form esters.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Dissociation constants of alcohols are higher than the dissociation constant of water.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Alcohols exhibit lower dissociation constants than water, indicating their weaker acidity compared to water. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28851,'chemistry',68,1113,2660,'What are the correct steps to convert acetaldehyde into acetone?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{MgBr}, \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$, oxidation by<br>$\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4} / \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}, \\\\Delta$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxidation, $\\\\mathrm{Ca}(\\\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$, Heat", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Reduction, KCN,hydrolysis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxidation, $\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{ONa}$,Heat", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28851_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28852,'chemistry',69,1174,2809,'Which one of the following reagent will convert acetamide to ethanamine?','[{"text": "Phophorous pentoxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lithium aluminium hydride", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Potassium cyanide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thionyl chloride", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CON}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Acteamide}}} {\\text{ +  4[H]}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{LiAl}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}}}$<br>$\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Ethanamine}}} {\\text{ + }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28853,'chemistry',70,1254,3065,'The most important contribution to the stability of a protein conformation appears to be the','[{"text": "sum of free energies of formation of many weak interactions among the hundreds of amino acids in a protein", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "sum of free energies of formation of many weak interactions between its polar amino acids and surrounding water molecules.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "maximum entropy increase from ionic interactions between the ionised amino acids in a protein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "entropy increase from the decrease in ordered water molecules forming a solvent shell around it", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Several weak electrostatic attraction exist between the amino acid units of protein. These interactions lower the free energy to a very great extent.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28854,'chemistry',67,1077,2572,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Acidic character of alcohols follows the order primary $>$ secondary $>$ tertiary.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Acidic character of alcohols is due to the presence of polar $-\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{H}$ group.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The acidity of alcohols depends on the $+\\mathrm{I}$ effect of alkyl groups, making tertiary alcohols less acidic due to stronger electron-releasing effects than primary alcohols. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28855,'chemistry',69,1174,2811,'Acetoxime on reduction with $\\mathrm{Na} / \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$ would give','[{"text": "Isopropylamine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "n-Propylamine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethylmethylamine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Diethylamine", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_28855_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28856,'chemistry',68,1113,2662,'In the following sequence of reactions, the final product \' $\\mathrm{B}$ \' is:','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28856_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_28856_question_1.png'),
(28857,'chemistry',67,1083,2601,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Dipole moment of phenol is 1.54 D.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The dipole moment of phenol is due to the electron withdrawing effect of phenyl group in contrast to the electron releasing effect of alkyl group in alcohols.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In phenol, the carbon atom is $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ hybridized and due to this reason benzene ring is producing electron withdrawing effect. On the other hand,carbon of methanol is $\\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ hybridized and produces electron releasing effect ( $+\\mathrm{I}$ effect). Thus, $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{O}$ bond in phenol is less polar than $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{O}$ bond in methanol. The dipole moment of phenol of $1.54 \\mathrm{D}$. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
