(28935,'chemistry',68,1113,2662,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Acetaldehyde undergoes aldol condensation with dilute $\\mathrm{NaOH}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Aldehydes which do not contain $\\alpha$ hydrogen undergo aldol condensation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Acetaldehyde which has $\\alpha$-hydrogen undergoes aldol condensation with dilute $\\mathrm{NaOH}$. Aldehydes lacking $\\alpha$ - hydrogens undergo Cannizzaro reaction, involving self oxidation and reduction upon heating with concentrated alkali. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28936,'chemistry',70,1247,3035,'D-Glucose reacts with phenylhydrazine to make osazone. How many molecules of phenylhydrazine are used for this reaction per molecule of D-glucose?','[{"text": "One", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Four", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28938,'chemistry',69,1176,2823,'The correct match of  Column I (Amines) and Column II (Property) is<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}$<br>B.${{\\text{C}}_{\\text{6}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{5}}}{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}$<br>C. ${{\\text{(C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{)}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{NH}}$<br>D. ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CON}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}$</div><div class="column-right">P. Undergoes Lieberman nitrosoamine<br>Q. Undergoes Hoffmann bromamide reaction<br>R. Gives azo dye test<br>S. With alcoholic KOH and ${\\text{CHC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{3}}}$  producesbad smell</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{P}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{P}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$\\mathrm{A}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}\\right)-\\mathrm{S}$ (with alcoholic $\\mathrm{KOH}$ and $\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ produces bad smell);<br>$\\mathrm{B}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}\\right) \\quad-\\mathrm{R}$ (gives azo dye test);<br>$\\left.\\left[\\mathrm{C}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{NH}\\right)\\right]-\\mathrm{P}($ undergoes Liebermann nitrosoamine reaction)<br>D $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CONH}_{2}\\right)$ - Q (undergoes Hofmann bromamide reaction)',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29409,'chemistry',68,1118,2679,'In which of the following reactions, carbon dioxide is not released?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In this reaction (4) dehydration takes place<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29409_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28939,'chemistry',68,1113,2668,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Fehling\'s solution can be used to distinguish benzaldehyde from acetaldehyde.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond of $\\mathrm{CHO}$ group in benzaldehyde is stronger than $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond of $\\mathrm{CHO}$ group in acetaldehyde.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Upon heating acetaldehyde with Fehling\'s solution it undergoes oxidation to form acetic acid. Aromatic aldehydes do not exhibit this reaction.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\underset{\\text { Acetaldehyde }}{\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CHO}}+\\underset{\\text { Fehling\'s solution }}{2 \\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}}+4 \\mathrm{OH}^{-} \\rightarrow \\\\& \\\\& \\underset{\\text { Acetic acid }}{\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}}+\\underset{\\text { Red ppt }}{\\mathrm{Cu}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\end{aligned}$<br>Resonance in benzaldehyde reduces the electrophilicity of its carbonyl carbon, making it less reactive than acetaldehyde. Consequently, the $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond in the aldehydic group of benzaldehyde is stronger than in acetaldehyde. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28939_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28940,'chemistry',67,1075,2566,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Di-tert-butyl ether cannot be prepared by Williamson\'s synthesis.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> tert-Butyl bromide on treatment with sodium tert-butoxide preferentially undergoes elimination to form isobutylene and tert-butyl alcohol.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_28940_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Di-tert-butyl ether (DTBE) cannot be synthesized via Williamson\'s synthesis due to steric hindrance from bulky tert-butyl groups, hindering effective nucleophilic attack by the alkoxide ion on tert-butyl halide. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28941,'chemistry',69,1177,2829,'The correct match of  Column I (Reagent /Name reaction) and Column II (Replacement of diazo group by) is<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. ${{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{P}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}$<br>B. Sandmeyer reaction<br>C. Boiling with dil.${{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}$<br>D. Gomberg reaction</div><div class="column-right">P. Hydroxy group<br>Q. Aryl group<br>R. Hydrogen atom<br>S. Chlorine atom</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{P}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{P}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$\\mathrm{A}\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{2}\\right) \\quad-\\mathrm{R}$ (hydrogen);<br>B (Sandmeyer reaction ) - S (chlorine);<br>$\\mathrm{C}$ (boiling with dil.<br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ ) - $\\mathrm{P}$ (hydroxyl group);<br>D (Gomberg reaction ) - Q (aryl group)',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28942,'chemistry',70,1246,3029,'Three cyclic structures of monosaccharides are given below which of these are anomers.','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III is anomer of I and II", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/70_28942_question_1.png'),
(28943,'chemistry',68,1113,2657,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{NaHSO}_{3}$ is used for the purification of carbonyl compounds.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> They are used in the blending of perfumes and flavouring agents.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Carbonyl compounds form solid bisulfite adducts that enable easy separation, and upon subsequent hydrolysis with dilute acid, the original carbonyl compounds are regenerated, facilitating pure compound recovery without impurities. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28943_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28944,'chemistry',67,1084,2604,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> tert-Butyl methyl ether on cleavage with $\\mathrm{HI}$ at $373 \\mathrm{~K}$ gives tert-butyl iodide and methanol.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The reaction occurs by $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ mechanism.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If the reaction takes place via an ${{\\text{S}}_{\\text{N}}}{\\text{2}}$  mechanism, it would lead to the formation of methyl iodide and tert-butyl alcohol due to direct displacement of the leaving group by the nucleophilic alkoxide ion, resulting in inversion of configuration at the carbon center. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29030,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Ammonia is more basic than water.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Nitrogen is less electronegative than oxygen.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The higher basicity of ammonia compared to water stems from the lower electronegativity of nitrogen, promoting a greater tendency to donate electrons and form ammonium ions. Thus, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28945,'chemistry',69,1174,2809,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Alkyl cyanides on reduction with $\\mathrm{H}_{2} / \\mathrm{Ni}$ give $1^{\\circ}$ amine.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Cyanides and isocyanides are functional isomers of each other.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{RC} \\equiv \\mathrm{N} \\xrightarrow[\\mathrm{Na} / \\mathrm{Hg} / \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}]{\\stackrel{\\mathrm{H}_{2} / \\mathrm{i}}{\\longrightarrow}} \\mathrm{RCH}_{2} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}$<br>Nitriles on reduction with lithium aluminium hydride $\\left(\\mathrm{LiAlH}_{4}\\right)$ or catalytic hydrogenation produce primary amines. Cyanides $(\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{CN})$ and isocyanides (R-NC) are indeed functional isomers. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28946,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Ketones are less reactive than aldehydes.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Ketones do not give Schiff\'s test.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The positive inductive effect of alkyl groups in ketones reduces carbonyl carbon\'s electrophilicity, making them less reactive than aldehydes. Additionally, steric hindrance from bulky alkyl groups in ketones prevents Schiff\'s test. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28947,'chemistry',70,1247,3032,'The spatial arrangement of different $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{OH}}$ groups in the molecules is given. Choose the option with correct names for I, II and III.','[{"text": "I $\\\\mathrm{\\\\rightarrow}$ Gluconic acid; II $\\\\mathrm{\\\\rightarrow}$ Glucose; III $\\\\mathrm{\\\\rightarrow}$ Saccharic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I $\\\\mathrm{\\\\rightarrow}$ Saccharic acid; II $\\\\mathrm{\\\\rightarrow}$ Glucose; III $\\\\mathrm{\\\\rightarrow}$ Guconic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I $\\\\mathrm{\\\\rightarrow}$ Glucose; II $\\\\mathrm{\\\\rightarrow}$ Gluconic acid; III $\\\\mathrm{\\\\rightarrow}$ Saccharic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I $\\\\mathrm{\\\\rightarrow}$ Glucose; II $\\\\mathrm{\\\\rightarrow}$ Saccharic acid; III $\\\\mathrm{\\\\rightarrow}$ Gluconic acid.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/70_28947_question_1.png'),
(28948,'chemistry',67,1089,2613,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Sodium metal can be used for dying diethyl ether and benzene but not ethanol.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Ethanol reacts with sodium metal to form sodium ethoxide and $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Sodium drying is a process involving the use of sodium metal to remove moisture from non-polar solvents like diethyl ether and benzene by selectively reacting with traces of water. In polar solvents like ethanol, a more vigorous reaction occurs forming sodium ethoxide and hydrogen gas So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28950,'chemistry',68,1112,2653,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Boiling point of aldehydes lie in between parent alkanes, and corresponding alcohols.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Intermolecular forces of attraction in aldehydes is stronger than that in found in parent alkanes, but weaker than that in alcohols.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Boiling point of aldehydes is lower than parent alkane because they contain polar carbonyl group. The carbonyl group undergoes weak intermolecular dipole-dipole interactions between the opposite ends of the $=\\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{O}$ dipoles. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28950_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  However, these dipole interactions are weaker than the intermolecular hydrogen bonding found in alcohols. Thus, aldehydes have lesser b.p than alcohols. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28951,'chemistry',67,1075,2566,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Both symmetrical and unsymmetrical ethers can be prepared by Williamson\'s synthesis<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Williamson\'s synthesis is an example of nucleophilic substitution reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Williamson\'s synthesis (nucleophilic substitution) produces symmetrical ethers when the alkyl halide and alkoxide ion have the same alkyl group and unsymmetrical ethers when they have different alkyl groups. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28954,'chemistry',68,1112,2654,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Lower members of aldehydes and ketones are miscible with water.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Solubility of carbonyl compounds increases with increase in molecular weight.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The solubility of carbonyl compounds in water relies on hydrogen bonding, facilitated by hydrogen bond donating groups. However, as molecular weight increases and alkyl chains lengthen, hydrophobic interactions dominate, diminishing solubility due to the resistance of non-polar alkyl chains to interact with polar water molecules. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28955,'chemistry',67,1088,2612,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Grignard reagents are prepared in ethers but not in benzene.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Grignard reagents are soluble in benzene.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The solubility of Grignard reagents in anhydrous ether is crucial for their reactivity, ensuring a water-free environment, easy preparation, and stable reaction conditions in organic synthesis. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28957,'chemistry',70,1247,3032,'Which of the following properties of glucose cannot be explained by its open chain structure?<br>(i) Glucose does not form hydrogen sulphite with $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaHSO}_{3}}$<br>(ii) On oxidation with $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}}$ glucose gives saccharic acid.<br>(iii) Glucose is found to exist in two different crystalline forms which are named as $\\mathrm{\\alpha}$ and $\\mathrm{\\beta}$.','[{"text": "(ii) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) and (iii)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(ii) and (iii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) and (ii)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'To explain the properties which can not be explained by open chain structure of glucose it was proposed that one of the -OH groups may add to the -CHO group and form a cyclic hemiacetal structure as shown below.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_28957_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28958,'chemistry',68,1113,2657,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Oximes of aldehydes and ketones show geometrical isomerismn.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Oximes are less acidic than hydroxyl amine.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Oximes exhibit greater acidity than hydroxylamines due to resonance stabilization in the anion formed after deprotonation, facilitated by the double bond between nitrogen and oxygen in oximes. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29292,'chemistry',69,1175,2815,'$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is soluble in','[{"text": "Dilute HCl", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}$ solution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Amines being basic in nature dissolve in dilute HCl . They can also coordinate with ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}}}$ions to form soluble complexes as they act as good ligands.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28960,'chemistry',67,1084,2603,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Etherates are coordination complexes of ethers with Lewis acids.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Ethers are easily cleaved by mineral acids such as $\\mathrm{HCl}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ at $373 \\mathrm{~K}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Ethers, act as Lewis bases. Ethers, with their lone pairs of electrons on the oxygen atom, can readily donate electron pairs to Lewis acids. When they interact with Lewis acids, the resulting complexes are commonly referred to as "ethers" or "etherates." Ethers are not easily cleaved by sulphuric acid or $\\mathrm{HCl}$. Thus assertion is true but reason is false. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28961,'chemistry',68,1113,2656,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Halogen acids do not add on to carbonyl bond.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Addition depends upon the polarisation of HX and carbonyl bond.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The electrophilic carbon in carbonyl compounds attracts nucleophilic halide ions, leading to nucleophilic addition reactions. But in alkenes, the double bond serves as the electrophilic site for halide ion attack, resulting in alkyl halide formation. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28962,'chemistry',70,1254,3059,'Which of the following statment is not correct?','[{"text": "amino acid can exist as inner salt.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "each polypeptide has one C- terminal and other N-terminal.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "enzymes are naturally occuring simple proteins.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the union of two amino acids produces two peptide linkages.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The union of two amino acids produces one peptide linkage.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28964,'chemistry',67,1083,2596,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> o-Nitrophenol is more volatile than p-nitrophenol.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Intramolecular hydrogen bonding is present in o-nitrophenol while intermolecular $\\mathrm{H-}$ bonding is in p-nitrophenol.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_28964_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Intermolecular hydrogen bonding in p-nitrophenol enhances stability, reducing vapor pressure, while o-nitrophenol lacks such bonding, resulting in higher volatility and vapor pressure. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28965,'chemistry',68,1113,2657,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Rate of addition of $\\mathrm{HCN}$ on carbonyl compounds increases in presence of $\\mathrm{NaCN}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Reaction involves the addition of $\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$ as the rate determining step.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{NaCN}$ catalyzes the nucleophilic addition of $\\mathrm{HCN}$ to carbonyl compounds by continuously generating cyanide ions, enhancing the reaction rate and efficiency to yield higher amounts of cyanohydrin products. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28965_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28966,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'${\\text{p}}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}}$ of the sites $\\alpha ,\\,\\,\\beta \\,\\,\\,and\\,\\,\\gamma $ are 10.79,2.18 and 8.95, isoelectric point will be','[{"text": "2.18", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9.87", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5.07", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6.5", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Isoelectric point $\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{(10.79+8.95)}{2}=9.87}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/70_28966_question_1.png'),
(28968,'chemistry',67,1076,2569,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$ has comparable molecular weight but boiling point of $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$ is more than dimethyl ether.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$ forms intermolecular $\\mathrm{H}$ -<br>bonding while $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$ forms intramolecular $\\mathrm{H}$-bonding.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The difference in boiling points between ethanol and dimethyl ether is due to hydrogen bonding in ethanol, resulting in stronger intermolecular forces and a higher boiling point. But dimethyl ether lacks such bonding, leading to weaker forces and a lower boiling point. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28969,'chemistry',68,1113,2666,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{C} \\equiv \\mathrm{O}^{+}$is more stable than $\\mathrm{R}-\\stackrel{+}{\\mathrm{C}}=\\mathrm{O}^{\\ominus}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Resonance in carbonyl compound provides $\\mathrm{C}^{+}$and $\\mathrm{O}^{-}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In a triple bond between carbon and oxygen octet rule for both atoms is followed. Whereas double bond between them leaves the carbon atom with an incomplete octet, resulting in an electrophilic site due to its electron deficiency . So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28971,'chemistry',67,1084,2603,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Ethers behave as bases in the presence of mineral acids.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It is due to the presence of lone pair of electrons on the oxygen.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Ethers exhibit weak Lewis base nature in the presence of strong mineral acids by donating lone pairs to form oxonium ions. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28972,'chemistry',70,1253,3058,'Pick out the incorrect statement.','[{"text": "p-aminobenzenesulphonic acid as a dipolar ion; while p-aminobenzoic acid does not", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In a electrolysis experiment, Alpha - amino<br>acids migrate at the isoelectric point towards electrodes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Sulphanilic acid is soluble in base, but not in acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~N}^{+} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}}$ is more acidic than $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{RCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28973,'chemistry',68,1113,2668,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Benzaldehyde forms black precipitate or silver mirror with Tollen\'s reagent.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Benzaldehyde gives a positive test with Benedict\'s and Fehling solution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Benzaldehyde is, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_28973_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Benzaldehyde, an aromatic aldehyde, does not react positively with Fehling\'s or Benedict\'s reagents due to its lack of hydrogens for enolate formation, in contrast to Tollen\'s reagent which oxidizes the carbonyl group and reduces to form a black mirror. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29293,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29293_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29293_question_1.png'),
(28975,'chemistry',67,1084,2604,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The major products formed by heating $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$ with $\\mathrm{HI}$ are  $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{I}$ and $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Benzyl cation is more stable than methyl cation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_28975_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The reaction follows ${{\\text{S}}_{\\text{N}}}{\\text{1}}$ mechanism. The benzyl cation produced is more stable than methyl carbocation. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28976,'chemistry',70,1253,3058,'Which of the following statements is not correct?','[{"text": "Proteins are poly amides formed from amino acids.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Except glycine, all other amino acids show optical activity.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Natural proteins are made up of L-isomers of amino acids.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In $\\\\alpha {\\\\text{  -  amino acids  -  N}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{-\\\\mathrm{COOH}}$ groups are attached to different carbon atoms.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In amino acids $\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ and $\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{COOH}}$ groups are attached to the same carbon atoms.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28977,'chemistry',68,1112,2653,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Carbonyl compounds do not show intermolecular hydrogen bonding.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Boiling points of carbonyl compounds are lower than alkanes.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Higher boiling points of carbonyl compounds (aldehydes, ketones) compared to corresponding alkanes arise from stronger dipole-dipole interactions between polar carbonyl groups, surpassing London dispersion forces in non-polar molecules, necessitating more energy for vaporization. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28979,'chemistry',67,1083,2596,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Phenol is more reactive than benzene towards electrophilic substitution reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In the case of phenol, the intermediate carbocation is more resonance stabilised.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The $-\\mathrm{OH}$ group, attached to the benzene ring, in phenol activates it towards electrophilic substitution and directs the incoming group to the ortho and para positions as these positions become electron rich due to the resonance. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_28979_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  In contrast, benzene lacks the electron-donating $-\\mathrm{OH}$ group. As a result, electrophilic aromatic substitution reactions are less efficient and slower in benzene compared to phenol. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28980,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'Amino acids exist in zwitter ion form. What is the structure of glycine at $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{pH}=4}$ ?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~N}^{\\\\oplus}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{COO}^{-}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~N}^{\\\\oplus}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{COOH}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~N}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{COOH}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~N}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{COO}^{-}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_28980_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28981,'chemistry',68,1113,2657,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Carbonyl compounds react with phenyl hydrazine to form phenyl hydrazones.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Hydrazones of aldehydes and ketones are not prepared in highly acidic medium.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In highly acidic conditions, protonation of hydrazine reduces its nucleophilic character by engaging lone pairs in bonding with protons, limiting accessibility for nucleophilic attacks in chemical reactions. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29219,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">At isoelectric point, the amino group does not migrate under the influence of electric field.</div><div class="column-right">At isoelectric point, amino acid exista as a zwitterion.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Statement $\\mathrm{B}$ is the correct explanation of Statement A.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28982,'chemistry',69,1174,2810,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In order to convert $\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{Cl}$ to pure $\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$. Gabriel pthalimide synthesis can be used.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> With proper choice of alkyl halides, pthalimide synthesis can be used to prepare $1^{\\circ}, 2^{\\circ}$ and $3^{\\circ}$ amines.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_28982_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The Gabriel phthalimide reaction is a method used for the synthesis of primary aliphatic amines. This reaction is specifically suitable for the preparation of primary amines and is not applicable to the synthesis of secondary or tertiary amines.<br>So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
